(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"ethics","chapters":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An unnamed First One, known only through epithets, is made the ground of learning, freedom from anxiety and the crossing of the sea. The chapter's recurring claim is exclusive: without reaching those feet, these things are out of reach."},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"title":"வான்சிறப்பு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Rain is the unacknowledged condition of everything: food, farming, the sea's life, worship, giving, austerity and conduct. The chapter makes virtue and the gods depend on a natural cause."},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"title":"நீத்தார் பெருமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The greatness of those who have renounced: immeasurable, grounded in mastery of the senses, and shown in the force of their word and anger. It ends by saying that the name அந்தணர் belongs to those whose conduct is cool grace toward all beings."},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"title":"அறன்வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Virtue's power: nothing brings more gain, and forgetting it is the worst loss. The chapter defines it inwardly, as a clean mind free of four faults, and presses that it must be practised now and continually."},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"title":"இல்வாழ்க்கை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The household life as the support of everyone else and as the fullest form of virtue. Having just exalted renunciants in chapter 3, the book now asks what they could gain by leaving home, and the commentators work hard to limit that challenge."},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"title":"வாழ்க்கைத் துணைநலம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The wife as the household's real wealth. The chapter praises her steadfastness and even grants her power over the rain, yet it is told largely from the husband's side, and it measures her by what she makes possible for his life."},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"title":"புதல்வரைப் பெறுதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Children are the greatest thing one can obtain: first for the fruit they bring beyond this life, then for sensuous joy in them, and finally for their worth in the world. It moves from possessing them to being known by them, and the last verse has the father judged by the son."},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"title":"அன்புடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Love cannot be hidden, gives itself away to the bone, and is what makes a body alive. Without it, virtue burns a person and a life is a withered tree."},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"title":"விருந்தோம்பல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Hospitality is the reason for having a home and wealth at all. Hosting does not impoverish, refusing it makes a rich person poor, and it depends finally on the face with which a guest is met."},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"title":"இனியவைகூறல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sweet speech is defined as loving, guileless and true, then ranked above generous giving. Its closing argument is almost one of common sense: why would anyone choose the unripe fruit?"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"title":"செய்ந்நன்றி அறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Gratitude: help is measured by its timing, its motive and the worth of the one who receives it, not by its size. Memory is ordered accordingly, so that good is kept and harm is let go, and ingratitude is called the one sin with no escape."},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"title":"நடுவு நிலைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impartiality: holding to what is due, whoever is involved, and giving up any gain that requires leaving it. The chapter's central image is the balance, and it treats loss incurred by being fair as no real loss."},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"title":"அடக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Self-restraint is the treasure that decides one's end. It works through body, speech and mind, and the chapter is most vivid on the tongue, whose burn alone never heals."},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"title":"ஒழுக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Conduct is to be guarded above life itself. It is what high birth really consists of, it outranks study, and its final test is living in accord with the world."},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"title":"பிறனில் விழையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Desire for another man's wife is the height of folly and a loss in every register at once. The chapter speaks to men, frames the woman as belonging to another, and calls restraint here the greatest manhood."},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance as strength: it wins the victory, it outlasts revenge's one day of pleasure, and for those who can bear harsh words it outranks fasting. Beyond it lies forgetting the wrong entirely."},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"title":"அழுக்காறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Envy is a self-inflicted ruin that needs no enemy. The chapter insists that the envious never rise and the unenvious never fall, and yet it admits the world sometimes shows otherwise, which it says is to be pondered."},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"title":"வெஃகாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Coveting another's goods destroys one even in the wanting. Not wanting is presented as the real security of wealth, and it ends as a kind of pride and victory."},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"title":"புறங்கூறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Backbiting is placed below open wrongdoing because it adds falsity to harm: the smile to the face after the slander behind the back. The remedy is simple and hard: to see one's own faults as one sees a stranger's."},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"title":"பயனில சொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Empty speech, especially before many, ruins the speaker's standing and even his claim to being fully human. The chapter ranks uselessness of speech as a graver failing than unkindness or lack of grace, and ends with a bare rule."},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fear of evil deeds is presented as a mark of excellence and as enlightened self-interest. Harm done to others does not stay with them: it pursues the doer as surely as a shadow, so whoever loves himself will refrain."},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"title":"ஒப்புரவறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"ஒப்புரவு is the obligation to make one's means serve the community, expecting nothing back as rain expects nothing. It is treated as the mark of being alive, and it holds even in poverty and at the cost of ruin."},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"True giving is giving to those who have nothing, and everything else is a kind of loan. The chapter ranks giving above reward, above ascetic endurance and even above life itself, and it argues from the giver's own joy and safety as well as from virtue."},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"title":"புகழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renown, earned above all through giving, is the one gain that outlasts the body. The chapter treats a life without it as a disgrace and a burden on the earth, indeed as no life at all."},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"title":"அருளுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"அருள், compassion extended to all living beings, is the highest wealth and the basis of other virtue. The chapter promises its possessor freedom from suffering and denies its lack any recovery, then closes by showing how it can be cultivated."},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"title":"புலான்மறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Eating flesh is incompatible with அருள், and the chapter argues this like a debate, answering those who say that intent is what matters and those who say that the guilt lies only with the killer. It ends by exalting the one who neither kills nor eats."},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"title":"தவம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"தவம் is defined simply as bearing pain and harming no one, and is then credited with immense powers, over enemies, wishes, and even death. The chapter makes it the one truly self-regarding work and the reason so few prosper."},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"title":"கூடாவொழுக்கம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The false renunciant, whose outward form hides an unrenounced heart, is exposed through a run of precise images. The chapter insists that conduct, not appearance, is the test, and ends by declaring the outward marks of renunciation unnecessary."},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"title":"கள்ளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Theft begins as a thought, and its wish alone is an offence. The chapter sets களவு against அளவு, stealth against measure, as two settled dispositions that can't live together, and promises ruin to the one and heaven to the other."},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"title":"வாய்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Truthfulness is defined by what speech does rather than by whether it matches facts: speech without harm is truth, and a falsehood yielding faultless good counts as truth. The chapter then raises this virtue above austerity, giving, and every other good."},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"title":"வெகுளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Anger is the self-destroying fire: it kills smiles and joy, burns the raft that would save a person, and returns on the one who holds it. Restraint counts only where anger could have had its way, and the one who drops it equals those who have renounced everything."},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"title":"இன்னாசெய்யாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Doing no hurt holds even when hurt would bring great wealth and even toward those who hurt first. The chapter's ground is self-knowledge, since a person who knows pain in his own life has no reason to inflict it, and it adds that hurt returns to its doer by the same day's end."},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"title":"கொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not killing is the first of all goods, ranked explicitly above truthfulness and above all renunciation. It is absolute, holding even at the cost of one's own life and regardless of any gain, and the chapter ends with the fate of those who kill."},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"title":"நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Nothing lasts: wealth disperses like a theatre crowd, each day is a blade, and life is a lodger in the body. The error is mistaking this for permanence, and the practical conclusion is urgency: do good now."},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation is the removal of attachment to what is 'mine' and even to the 'I'. It begins with freedom from each thing's pain and reaches release, while those who keep any hold stay in the net. The chapter ends in the paradox that one must cling to get free of clinging."},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"title":"மெய்யுணர்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Seeing what is really so is what ends suffering and birth: mistaking the unreal for the real starts it, and stripping off appearances ends it. The chapter never says what மெய்ப்பொருள் is, and this silence is where its commentators divide most."},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"title":"அவாவறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Craving is the seed of all birth and the sorrow of sorrows, and its removal, not any other act, is the direct cause of an unchanging state. The whole section on renunciation ends here, by locating release at the moment when wanting stops."},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"title":"ஊழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fate as the force behind wealth, understanding, and pleasure and pain. It reaches even the qualities a person might rely on against it, such as effort and intelligence."},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"title":"இறைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a king excellent: not power alone but a set of resources and qualities that must be held without lapse, and above all how he is to the people who approach him. His standing is conferred from below, by those who praise him, shelter under him, and set him apart."},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning as the thing that makes a person see, belong anywhere, and possess what can't be lost. It begins and is only complete in conduct: to learn is also to stand by what is learned."},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"title":"கல்லாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The emptiness of being unlearned: the unlearned person's speech, native wit, looks, wealth and rank all fail to count. The chapter is the harshest in the book on this point, and its contempt is largely about assemblies and public standing."},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"title":"கேள்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Listening to the wise as a form of learning open even to those who haven't studied. The chapter treats the ear as an organ of nourishment and taste, rivalling the mouth, and it softens the verdict of the previous chapter."},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding as protection and as practice: it steers the mind, tests what is heard, moves with the world, foresees what is coming and knows what to fear. After three chapters on acquiring knowledge, this one asks what it is to have it."},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"title":"குற்றங்கடிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's real enemy is his own faults: arrogance, anger, desire, avarice, misplaced pride and self-regard. Small ones must be treated as large and dealt with before they arrive, and only one who has cleared his own is fit to judge others."},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"title":"பெரியாரைத் துணைக்கோடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's power depends on those greater than himself whom he can win and keep, and above all on those who will correct him. The chapter counts this as the highest strength and calls its lack self-destruction."},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"title":"சிற்றினஞ்சேராமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding and goodness are not sealed within a person; they take on the nature of the company one keeps, as water takes on its soil. The chapter gradually grants the mind its goodness while insisting that company still strengthens or spoils it."},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"title":"தெரிந்துசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Acting well begins before the act: weighing cost and return, taking counsel, and matching the means to the person and to one's own position. The chapter's vocabulary is that of a careful ledger, and its fear is less of failure than of being laughed at for failing."},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"title":"வலியறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing strength: the deed's, one's own, the opponent's and the allies', and treating wealth as a form of strength with a limit. The theme is limits, and most of the chapter warns against zeal, generosity and pride that outrun them."},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"title":"காலமறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Strength is not a fixed quantity but depends on when it is used. The chapter's real teaching is about waiting: holding back, even bowing and hiding one's anger, then striking without hesitation when the moment comes. Its stance is cold and tactical."},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"title":"இடனறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Power belongs to the ground it stands on. Through animals and vehicles, the chapter shows the strong undone off their own ground and the weak made strong on theirs, and it warns against attacking even people with nothing but their home."},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"title":"தெரிந்துதெளிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How a ruler comes to trust someone: test first and trust only after that, and once trust is settled, don't doubt it. The chapter doesn't expect the flawless. It looks at what predominates, and deeds are its final test."},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"title":"தெரிந்துவினையாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Having tested someone, how to use them: match person, means, task and time, hand the work over fully, and don't resent the familiarity it brings, yet look at their conduct every day. Trust and scrutiny are both required, and the chapter doesn't entirely settle how."},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding kin close as a political and material matter: kin stay when wealth goes, their love multiplies gain, and wealth's use lies in being surrounded by them. They have to be kept through giving, soft speech and absence of anger, and they can be won back once estranged."},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"title":"பொச்சாவாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Heedlessness, especially the forgetting that comes with joy and success, is more dangerous than rage. To keep in mind what one has resolved is the skill on which everything else depends, and it is an inward fort or an inward leak."},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"title":"செங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Justice as the root of a king's power: the people live by his sceptre as the world lives by rain, and it wins him battles, brings rain and protects him in turn. The chapter ends by defending punishment, even death, as part of just rule."},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"title":"கொடுங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Oppressive rule as a crime and a self-destruction: the king who extorts is a murderer and a highwayman, and his people's tears wear away his wealth. Its effects reach everything, turning wealth into a burden, and in the older reading failing the rain, the cows and the sacred books."},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"title":"வெருவந்தசெய்யாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Punishment belongs to rule, but dread does not. Disproportion, inaccessibility, a harsh face and harsh words turn subjects' fear into the slow or sudden ruin of the one who causes it."},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"title":"கண்ணோட் டம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Kindly regard is what eyes are for and what keeps the world's dealings going; eyes without it are wounds or dead wood. Yet the chapter also sets a limit on it, that it must not undo one's duty, and then praises enduring poison for its sake."},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"title":"ஒற்றாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Intelligence-gathering is treated as the sight of the state and as a discipline of evidence: a spy must watch everyone and hold firm, and the king must not trust any one spy."},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"title":"ஊக்கமுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energy of mind is the only possession that truly belongs to a person. Wealth leaves and returns, but this inner drive sets how high one rises, and without it strength and even humanity count for little."},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"title":"மடியின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sloth is not a private weakness but the ruin of a lineage: it puts out the family's light, hands a person to enemies, and can be reversed only by the one who harbours it."},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"title":"ஆள்வினையுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sustained labour builds the doer, funds the virtue of giving and supports one's kin. Faced with fate, the chapter shifts the question: failure of fortune carries no blame, labour always earns its wage, and persistence can outlast what opposes it."},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"title":"இடுக்கணழியாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Trouble need not break a person: it can be laughed at, thought away, outlasted, or expected as the body's lot, until suffering itself seems to suffer. The chapter is a series of stances, not one remedy, and it closes the book's treatment of the king."},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"title":"அமைச்சு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The minister is defined by what he can do: judge means and time, split and join alliances, unite innate acuity with learning and with the ways of the world. The chapter also assigns him a duty to speak even to a king who won't listen, and warns that a false minister is worse than a multitude of enemies."},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"title":"சொல்வன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech is an excellence in its own right and a source of gain and ruin. Its mastery means fitting the hearer, being unanswerable, being brief when one can and expansive when one must; learning that can't be spoken has no fragrance."},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"title":"வினைத்தூய்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The worth of an action lies in its cleanness: no gain, necessity or even a mother's hunger justifies what the worthy condemn, because tainted gain departs as it came and leaves ruin behind."},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"title":"வினைத்திட்பம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Firmness in action is firmness of mind, and nothing else substitutes for it. Speaking is easy and doing is hard, but one who stays firm gets what he has thought of, however small he looks and whatever pain lies on the way."},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"title":"வினைசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Deliberation ends in decision and decision must not stall; beyond that, the chapter gives a practical method of action and alliance, measured to whether one is stronger, an equal, or weaker than the other side."},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"title":"தூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"An envoy must combine love, knowledge, learning and presence with exact speech, courage and incorruptibility, and must carry his sovereign's interest to a foreign court even at the cost of his life."},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"title":"மன்னரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Serving a king is like warming oneself at a fire: useful, dangerous, and demanding constant awareness of distance. The chapter gives rules for courtiers who can't presume on favour, age, kinship or old intimacy, and is candid about the danger behind the courtesy."},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Reading what is unspoken from face and eye is the highest social faculty. The chapter places this skill near the divine, treats the eyes that can't do it as useless, and holds that the face betrays what the heart holds."},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech can't be judged apart from its hearers. One must read the assembly and adjust: shine among the bright, stay silent or whitewashed among the dull, step carefully among the great, and never spill nectar where it can't be received."},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"title":"அவையஞ்சாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fearlessness before an assembly is rarer than courage in battle, and it's the test of learning: knowledge that can't be spoken there is a sword in a frightened hand, and its holder counts for less than the unlearned and little more than the dead."},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"title":"நாடு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a land deserve the name: people and yield that do not fail, resilience under burden and damage, and freedom from hunger, disease and faction. All of it is void if land and ruler are not in accord."},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"title":"அரண்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The fort: what makes it hard to take and easy to hold, from terrain and walls to food, arms and fighters. Its last verse says all of this depends on the people inside knowing how to act."},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"title":"பொருள்செயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The making of wealth: it is the great social force that raises the despised, drives out enemies and brings virtue and pleasure within reach, but only wealth rightly gained deserves pursuit."},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"title":"படைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes an army: inherited courage, unity before death, honour, and discipline rather than numbers. It fails when the king neglects it or when it lacks leaders."},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"title":"படைச்செருக்கு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The warrior's pride, spoken largely from within: boast, disdain for easy prey, mercy as courage's edge, and death sought as honour. Its ethic is one of glory and is not softened by the rest of the Kural."},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"title":"நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship as a rare achievement and the strongest guard. It grows with use, lives in the heart rather than on the face, corrects as well as comforts, and moves to help as instinctively as a hand to a slipping garment."},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Testing friends before committing, because a friendship once formed should not be left. It asks what to test for, finds that ruin is the most reliable test, and accepts that bad friendships must sometimes be paid to end."},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"title":"பழைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Old friendship as the freedom to take liberties with each other. It teaches welcoming what a friend presumes to do, reading hurt charitably, and staying even through harm the friend causes."},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"title":"தீ நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A catalogue of false friendship: the self-serving, the unreliable, fools, jesters and the two-faced. Its rhetoric of comparison ranks all of them below loneliness and even below enemies."},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"title":"கூடாநட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship with one who is inwardly hostile: smiles, soft words, folded hands and even tears can hide a weapon. In the end the chapter tells the reader to answer such a person with the same outward show."},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly as choosing loss over gain. It is not ignorance as such: its worst form belongs to the learned who do not restrain themselves, and it spoils whatever the fool touches, whether work, wealth, friendship or an assembly."},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty knowledge that thinks itself wise. It is the poverty that counts most, it does worse harm than any enemy, and it can't be taught, because the person who has it is already sure that he knows."},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"title":"இகல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Hostility, as a disease of the disposition to quarrel. Giving it up is shown as gain and strength more than as saintliness, and pursuing it as a way to one's own ruin."},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"title":"பகைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy worth having, seen coldly: pick the weak, and notice who makes himself easy to beat through anger, ignorance, stinginess and isolation. Read another way, its list of vices is a portrait of how not to become someone's easy enemy."},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"title":"பகைத்திறந்தெரிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How to handle enmity as a strategic matter. Don't seek it, avoid certain enemies above all, turn some into friends and others into neutrals, hide your weakness, and cut the rest down while they are still young."},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"title":"உட்பகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy within the circle: kin and companions whose hostility is hidden. It is worse than an open foe because it is unseen, it wears a family down slowly, and even a sesame-sized amount of it holds ruin."},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not offending the great: to slight those with real power is to call down one's own ruin, and no wealth, fort or ally protects against them. Who the great are is the key question, and the verses themselves mostly leave it open."},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"title":"பெண்வழிச்சேறல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A man who lets his wife's will or his desire for her govern him loses virtue, wealth, standing and even pleasure. The chapter treats household subordination as a failure of his own agency and of his duties to others, and it speaks throughout from the husband's side."},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"title":"வரைவின்மகளிர்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Women who sell intimacy are portrayed as a danger to men because their words and embrace are divided from their hearts. The chapter's argument is that the falseness is detectable, and that who touches them reveals the quality of the man. It gives no view from the women's side."},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"title":"கள்ளுண்ணாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Drink destroys standing, shame and understanding, can't be hidden, and can't be argued away. The only exit the chapter offers is the drinker's own recognition during a sober moment."},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"title":"சூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Gambling is ruin even when it pays, because a win is the bait that holds the player. It takes wealth first, then truthfulness, compassion and the basics of living, and the attachment grows with each loss."},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"title":"மருந்து","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Medicine as a matter of measure. Illness comes from imbalance, the best remedy is eating with judgment so that no remedy is needed, and when illness does come, healing depends on careful diagnosis and on a whole system of people."},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"title":"குடிமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"High birth as a set of obligations and marks: integrity, shame, truthfulness, generous speech and giving, held to even in poverty. The chapter speaks as if birth produced these qualities, while its own tests make conduct the evidence of birth, and the commentators pull hard in opposite directions on that point."},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour ranks above profit, fame and survival itself: one abandons even indispensable acts that would lower one, and one who has lost standing is as worthless as fallen hair. The chapter ends by praising those who would rather die than live degraded."},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness is not given by birth, rank or wealth. It consists of high aim, self-guarding, doing the rare thing, freedom from conceit and covering others' weakness, and smallness is the same set of features reversed."},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"title":"சான்றாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fullness of character: the worthy hold all good qualities as their own, rest on five pillars, don't tell others' faults, and do good to those who harm them. This fullness doesn't depend on poverty or wealth, and the chapter finally places the earth's stability upon it."},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"title":"பண்புடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Courtesy of character, பண்பு, is what makes one fully human and keeps the world going. It reaches even to enemies, begins in being approachable, and without it sharp intelligence and great wealth come to nothing."},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"title":"நன்றியில்செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that is neither enjoyed nor given is effectively no wealth. It does nothing for the holder, the needy or the holder's heirs, and it turns a person into something the world doesn't want. The chapter measures wealth entirely by its use."},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"title":"நாணுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Shame over wrongdoing is the distinguishing mark of a human being and the seat of worth. It ranks above life, conduct and the whole earth, and those without it only imitate being alive."},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"title":"குடிசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Raising one's people is the highest greatness. It requires ceaseless effort and knowledge, draws divine help and willing kin, ignores season and pride, and costs the one who does it his own bodily ease. A family with no one to carry it falls."},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"title":"உழவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Farming is the world's first occupation because everyone, including kings and renouncers, ends up depending on it. Those who live by it alone owe nothing to anyone, and the land itself responds to attention, as a wife or a mocking woman would."},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"title":"நல்குரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty as the worst suffering, one that has no comparison. It strips lineage, speech, learning and even a mother's regard, it returns day after day, and the chapter's last verse asks whether such a person should go on as they are."},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"title":"இரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Asking is permitted, even graceful, when the giver is one who can't conceal. The chapter then goes further and says that the world needs askers, since without them giving, fame and even being alive would lose their point. It closes by telling the asker not to be angry at refusal."},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Dread of begging: never asking, even from the best givers, is worth ten million times what asking could bring. The chapter's fury goes so far as a curse on whoever ordered the world so that begging is needed, and it holds the withholder guiltier still."},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A bitterly ironic portrait of the base: they look perfectly human, are as free as gods, and are happier than the good because nothing troubles them. Only fear and greed move them, and at the first trouble they sell themselves. The chapter ends the whole Book of Wealth."},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"title":"தகையணங்குறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's first sight of the woman, told as an injury: her beauty and returned gaze attack him like a goddess, an army, Death and a rutting elephant. The chapter treats love's beginning as the defeat of a strong man, and ends by claiming that love, unlike liquor, intoxicates through sight alone."},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Reading desire that won't declare itself. The lovers communicate through lowered eyes, half-smiles and feigned indifference, until spoken words are declared useless."},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"title":"புணர்ச்சிமகிழ்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's joy in union, in which one person holds all five senses, cures the sickness she caused, and rivals heaven and the virtuous householder's meal. Its last verse compares this desire to knowledge, which only reveals how much more there is."},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"title":"நலம்புனைந்துரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man praises her beauty through deliberate hyperbole, defeating the standard comparisons: flowers, the moon, swan's down. As the praise goes on, it becomes a claim of exclusivity, and what is seen by many ceases to compare with what he alone sees."},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"title":"காதற்சிறப்புரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Each lover declares what this love is. He makes it a bond of body and life, and she makes it an indwelling so complete that separation is impossible, which leaves the townspeople's talk of an absent, unloving man simply mistaken."},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"title":"நாணுத்துறவுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Declaring that shame has been given up. The man threatens to ride the palmyra horse, a public humiliation that forces the issue, while the woman, who may not do so, watches her love go into the streets on its own."},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"title":"அலரறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The lovers turn the town's gossip around. Talk meant to shame and stop them feeds the love, keeps the man alive, and can even press for marriage or elopement. The chapter treats public scandal as an unintended gift."},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"title":"பிரிவாற்றாமை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The inability to bear parting. Mostly in the woman's voice, the chapter argues against his leaving before it happens: promises of a quick return are useless to one who won't survive, and a broken 'fear not' makes trust itself hard."},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"title":"படர்மெலிந்திரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman wastes away in grief through the night, caught between a love she can't hide and a shame that forbids telling him. Love is measured against the sea and against the night, and the only company that remains is the night itself."},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"title":"கண்விதுப்பழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman puts her own eyes on trial. They showed her the man, so they caused the sickness; now they weep, can't sleep and betray her secret, and she answers with bitter laughter and a curse. Blaming a body part makes her suffering speakable."},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"title":"பசப்புறுபருவரல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The pallor of longing (பசலை), the woman's most visible symptom, personified as a creature that rides her body, moves in the instant he moves away, and earns her the town's blame while he escapes it. She ends by offering her body as the price of his good name."},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"title":"தனிப்படர்மிகுதி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The surfeit of lonely grief turns into an argument about reciprocity. Mutual love is the seedless fruit and the timely rain; one-sided love is a carrying pole weighted at one end. The chapter asks what love is worth if it isn't returned."},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"title":"நினைந்தவர்புலம்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Remembering the absent lover as both the only thing keeping one alive and a burning pain. The chapter starts with a man's claim that memory itself gives joy, and turns to a woman who wonders whether she exists in his thoughts at all."},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"title":"கனவுநிலையுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dreams as the woman's way of staying alive. She argues that a lover who won't come when she is awake comes when she sleeps, and so waking life is the villain and the gossips are simply people who have never dreamed of a beloved."},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"title":"பொழுதுகண்டிரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Evening, once a friend, returns as an executioner. The woman speaks to the hour, quarrels with it and maps her sickness through the day, ending on the man whose nature, she says, has become wealth itself."},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"title":"உறுப்புநலனழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman's body as a public record of his absence. Eyes, shoulders and forehead lose their beauty, and each speaker reads that record differently: the friend sees an accusation, the woman refuses to let it be one, and the man, far away and turning for home, sees a reason to hurry."},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுகிளத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman argues with her own heart about what to do: find a remedy, stop suffering, go to him, or forget him. The heart is a separate party that can be scolded, sent away and accused, and the chapter ends without any option having won."},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"title":"நிறையழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The breaking of self-containment (நிறை). Desire is an axe against a door, a sneeze that can't be held back, a tyrant working her at midnight. The chapter ends with a woman who sets out to sulk and finds her heart has already gone over to him."},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"title":"அவர்வயின்விதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impatience for reunion on both sides of the distance. She has worn down her fingers counting days and rehearses how she will greet him, and, on Parimelazhagar's reading, he is stuck on campaign and starting to fear he may come back too late."},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"title":"குறிப்பறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Communicating what can't be said. Thread within crystal, scent within a bud, and the movement of her gaze from bangles to shoulders to feet: unspoken signs carry the fear of a new parting and the resolve to go with him. It is the book's mature counterpart to the first signs of chapter 110."},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"title":"புணர்ச்சிவிதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The eagerness for union overrides every plan to sulk. She can't see his faults once he is in view, and sets out to quarrel but her heart betrays her. The chapter's most quoted verse states why: love is more delicate than a flower, and few catch its moment."},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுபுலத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Quarrelling with one's own heart for taking the other side. The woman charges it with disloyalty for loving him when she wants to sulk, and the chapter ends in the man's voice, on Parimelazhagar's reading, with both lovers facing the same discovery: one's own heart may not be on one's side."},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"title":"புலவி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sulking and its right measure. The quarrel is salt: just enough makes love tasty and a little more spoils it. Nothing holds it in check except that the other person cares, as the chapter's closing verses, in which the man finds her sulk has gone too far, make clear."},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"title":"புலவி நுணுக்கம்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The fine points of sulking, a comedy of jealous logic. Every word he says, every sneeze, whether he hides it or not, whether he pleads or looks, is turned into evidence. It is the most purely entertaining chapter in the Kural, and it also shows how love trains two people in close reading."},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"title":"ஊடலுவகை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The joy of the quarrel, and the end of the Kural. Sulking is here set above heaven, and above union itself as digestion is above eating; the loser wins, and a man prays for the night to last. The whole work ends not on virtue or God but on a condition about an embrace."},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"title":"செல்வம் நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Wealth does not stay with anyone: the feasting rich end up begging and kings lose their wives to enemies. Because death is near and certain, wealth should be shared and spent on virtue now, and the hoarder who neither enjoys nor gives has in effect already lost it."},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"title":"இளமை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Youth does not last: old age, the loss of friends and the decay of the beloved's body come to everyone, and death can take the young as well as the old. The wise therefore renounce early and do virtue while they have the means."},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"title":"யாக்கை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body does not last: kings die like everyone, wedding drums turn into funeral drums, and the body is a bubble, a passing cloud or dew on grass that the life within leaves behind. Because of this, the body should be used for its proper fruit, virtue and service, while it lasts."},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"title":"அறன் வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An urging to virtue: human birth is rare and the body brief, so its fruit, virtue, must be gathered now. Those who live on the merit of past births without adding to it end at others' gates; those who have gathered it face death without grief, and even a small gift to the worthy grows vast."},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"title":"தூய்தன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body is impure: beneath skin, perfume and garlands it is a vessel of flesh, fluids and filth, and in death it is food for vultures and a grinning skull. Desire for a woman's body rests on not seeing this, and the renunciant who has seen it is not moved by the conventional praise of eyes and teeth."},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation, argued from impermanence: the wise see that home, youth, wealth and pleasure do not last and that pleasure is mixed with pain, so they leave household life without delay, keep their vows under hardship, pity those who insult them, and master the senses to reach release."},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"title":"சினம் இன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Freedom from anger: the worthy let honour and insult pass alike, guard their tongues, bear harsh words from those beneath them and do not return harm, while their anger, when it comes, cools of itself. The base nurse and broadcast theirs."},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance carried beyond insult into friendship and privation: avoid fools, bear inferiors, value a friend's harsh words, bear with or quietly leave an erring friend, starve rather than beg from the graceless, and refuse blameworthy pleasure, harm and falsehood even at the greatest cost."},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"title":"பிறர்மனை நயவாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not desiring another man's wife, argued mostly from consequences: constant fear, small pleasure, the king's punishment, family disgrace, hell and a degraded rebirth. The last quatrains turn from the act to desire itself, a fire worse than any outer fire, which the strong suppress in silence."},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Giving: give gladly and daily within one's means, even when poor and even a grain's worth, to those who cannot repay, because death and age are near and wealth comes and goes by past merit, not by clutching. The fame of giving outlasts all else, and one who cannot give should at least not beg."},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"title":"பழவினை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Old deeds (பழவினை) find their doer and settle each person's share of joy and suffering. Wishing, effort, holiness and learning cannot move that share, so the chapter ends by asking why anyone should grieve over it."},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"title":"மெய்ம்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Things as they truly are (மெய்ம்மை): the hard facts of the world, as opposed to how people would like it to be. Natures do not change, kin come with fortune and leave with it, the rich are heard and the poor are not, and in the end only one's deeds go with one."},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dread of evil deeds (தீவினையச்சம்): harm done to creatures returns in kind, and bad company is itself a source of evil, turning even the upright. The chapter ends by urging the heart to give up household attachment, since only good done profits the soul."},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning (கல்வி) is the only true beauty, a medicine for delusion and the best legacy, and it outranks birth. Because life is short, one must choose what to learn, keep learned company, and prefer the books of wisdom that free one from confusion to merely worldly study."},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"title":"குடிப்பிறப்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Noble birth (குடிப்பிறப்பு) shows in conduct that holds through poverty: the well-born keep their principles, courtesy and generosity when they have nothing, while the low-born fail to do good even when they can. The chapter treats these qualities as belonging to birth."},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"title":"மேன்மக்கள்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Portraits of the noble (மேன்மக்கள்): they cannot bear a stain, aim high whatever the outcome, stay within bounds in want, keep their speech clean under abuse and welcome those who come to ask. Wealth draws the base, but meeting the well-born is the real treasure."},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not wronging the great: their goodwill, once lost, cannot be restored, their anger reaches into any fortress, and their esteem is the only esteem worth having. Time with them is precious, and their bond grows where the bond of the small shrinks."},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"title":"நல்லினம் சேர்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Joining good company: it washes out the faults of youth, raises the lowly as river water sanctifies sewage, protects the weak and hides meanness. Life's pain makes the company of the wise the thing to seek, and bad company ruins even the pure."},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness of character: it renounces when the season of giving and loving has passed, sees home and its supports as passing, and keeps giving even in decline. Its faults show more than others' crimes, it stays unshaken by slander, and it feeds others before itself."},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"title":"தாளாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energetic effort (தாளாண்மை): it makes a person, as a twig hardens into an elephant post, supports kin and an ageing father, and is what lineage really consists of. Manliness is persisting when things fail, and food won by one's own effort tastes better than a patron's feast."},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding one's own close: the sympathy of kin dissolves suffering, the worthy bear everyone who leans on them without sorting people into ours and others, and plain food from those who love is sweeter than a feast from those who don't. The bond is proved when it goes into the fire with you."},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Test a friend before taking him: by learning, by family, by loyalty under injury, by constancy, and by whether he actually helps. The reason is given at the end: once a bond is formed, parting hurts even when the friend was a snake."},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"title":"நட்பிற் பிழை பொறுத்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Once a friend has been chosen, bear their faults: everything has its husk, the patience of one keeps two in friendship, and one who exposes or pries into a friend's faults is worse than the friend."},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"title":"கூடா நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendships that don't fit: with those who stay only for their own ends, whose bond is shallow, who are of another nature, or who lack discernment. Such friendship fades, pains, and drags down even the discerning, while the company of the wise is like heaven."},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding shown in conduct: restraint toward fallen foes and in poverty, judging people by mind and conduct rather than birthplace or kind, steadiness in friendship, and knowing that a person raises or lowers himself. It ends with the picture of a life that completes work, pleasure and virtue."},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"title":"அறிவின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Want of understanding (அறிவின்மை) is the real poverty. The unlearned and the base cannot take in good counsel, show themselves for what they are among the learned, and set up petty assemblies of their own, while the learned keep silent before those who would not absorb what they say."},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"title":"நன்றியில் செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that does no good (நன்றியில் செல்வம்): the riches of the base and ungiving are of no use to anyone near them, and the poor turn away to givers however far. The chapter also faces the scandal that wealth goes to the senseless while the good are poor, and explains it by former deeds."},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"title":"ஈயாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not giving (ஈயாமை): to cook and eat is to share; the miser who neither gives nor enjoys is mocked by his own hoard, loses it to strangers and heirs, and is worse off than the poor. Giving must be done now, in whatever measure one can, and wealth held without giving is a house of suffering."},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"title":"இன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty (இன்மை): the world honours a few coins over noble birth, kin and flatterers leave with wealth, and poverty strips away lineage, valour, learning, goodness and even sharpness of mind. For one who cannot help those who ask, the chapter says it is better to leave home and live as a guest or a beggar elsewhere."},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour (மானம்): those who have it burn at the arrogance and contempt of the rich, will not lay their need before the unworthy, keep away from patrons who demean them, and would rather die, or refuse heaven itself, than incur sin, blame or loss of honour. Honour is set in a scale of what people fear and is said to bring good in this world and the next."},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The dread of begging (இரவச்சம்): a person of worth would rather go hungry and poor than ask, even of willing givers, because the mind melts at the thought of asking and a refusal kills. The only givers the chapter allows are those who welcome one in, and the last quatrain turns to the help owed to an old friend."},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing the assembly (அவையறிதல்): where learning should be spoken and before whom. The worthy keep silent before the ignorant and the quarrelsome, while those who memorise texts without understanding them expose themselves. A book's meaning is harder than its letter, and only those who can explain it, by the full method of commentary, count as scholars."},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty understanding (புல்லறிவாண்மை): the small-minded scorn the teaching of virtue, see life slipping away and still do no good, nurse quarrels, hoard and put things off, until old age or death leaves them nothing. Folly is measured here by wasted time and wasted wealth."},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly (பேதைமை): the fool enjoys a world in which death is already at work, puts off virtue, relies on birth and status, clings to the rich and the unworthy, praises himself and quarrels for nothing. Most quatrains are comparisons that make his position look absurd."},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"title":"கீழ்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Baseness (கீழ்மை): the base keep their nature whatever is done for them. Teaching, invitation, honour, wealth and care do not change them, and the chapter sets them beside the worthy, whom prosperity does not change either."},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The base (கயமை): their nature is not changed by age, learning or wealth. They give only under force, remember insults and forget kindnesses, speak ill of others to their faces, and hold their wealth without softening."},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"title":"பன்னெறி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Various ways (பன்னெறி): a closing miscellany of the Book of Wealth. It weighs the wife as what makes a house a home or a ruin, ranks the lives a person may lead with austerity first, and observes that sons inherit their fathers' minds, that the world turns upside down, and that the unfeeling are better dead."},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"title":"பொது மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Courtesans (பொது மகளிர்): their love lasts as long as a man's money and turns hostile when it ends. The chapter blames the men who trust them as much as the women, calling them beasts, dupes and people whose bodies are full of sin."},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"title":"கற்புடை மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The chaste wife (கற்புடை மகளிர்): her worth is measured by self-guarding, hospitality in poverty, modesty that never wears off, and timely sulking. In the last four quatrains she speaks herself, as a wife wronged by a husband who goes to courtesans."},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"title":"காமநுதலியல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The nature of love (காமநுதலியல்): love needs both union and the sulking that interrupts it, and its pain is separation, felt most at evening and in the rains. The chapter ends with a girl's flight with her lover, seen by her mother and herself, and a woman's grief at a lover gone to seek wealth."},"ac:1":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":1,"title":"ஆசாரக்கோவை 1-20","section":"ஆசாரக்கோவை","theme":"The book opens by rooting ஆசாரம் in eight ethical virtues and promising its rewards, then sets out the rules of the period for the day: rising and worship, the kinds of எச்சில் and what they bar, the occasions and manner of bathing, reverence owed to elders and sacred things, and the purifying acts and posture of the meal."},"ac:2":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":2,"title":"ஆசாரக்கோவை 21-40","section":"ஆசாரக்கோவை","theme":"The chapter reports the period's rules for the meal (whom to feed first, direction, posture, order of tastes, rinsing the mouth), for the evening and for sleep, and for spitting and excretion, and it sets among them two ethics verses that bar even the sight and the thought of the gravest wrongs."},"ac:3":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":3,"title":"ஆசாரக்கோவை 41-60","section":"ஆசாரக்கோவை","theme":"The third twenty move from the body to the household and the road: rules of touch and of marital union timed by the wife's cycle and the calendar, the cleansing and auspicious ordering of the house, days without Vedic recitation, festival days and the welcome of guests, the measure of dress and speech, sights and places to avoid, omens at departure, and deference to elders on the road."},"ac:4":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":4,"title":"ஆசாரக்கோவை 61-80","section":"ஆசாரக்கோவை","theme":"Verses 61-80 set out reverence to Veda-knowers, elders and the vulnerable, and then a long code of conduct in the presence of the great: in the great house, before the lord and in his counsel, the attendant governs gaze, gesture, speech and the body itself."},"ac:5":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":5,"title":"ஆசாரக்கோவை 81-100","section":"ஆசாரக்கோவை","theme":"The book's last twenty verses turn to conduct before the king, elders and other households, with a few rules of the funeral rite, of leavings and of the day of a rite, and close by naming the persons freed from ஆசாரம்."},"el:1":{"book":"elathi","num":1,"title":"ஏலாதி 0-20","section":"ஏலாதி","theme":"After a payiram that says the book joins the householder's and the renouncer's codes and an invocation to the twenty-four, twenty verses count five to eight things under one predicate, moving between the household's giving and hospitality, the king's and minister's duties, and a Jain code of non-killing, truth and restraint; three verses close by setting the books of learning aside for conduct."},"el:2":{"book":"elathi","num":2,"title":"ஏலாதி 21-40","section":"ஏலாதி","theme":"Verses 21 to 40 move from Death and impermanence as the reason to seek release (21 to 24, 37), through faults sorted by mind, speech and body (27 to 29) and an envoy's excellences (26), to the twelve kinds of legal son (30, 31) and rewards in heaven or kingship for restrained speech and for feeding the needy (32 to 36, 40), closing with what is easy and what is hard (39)."},"el:3":{"book":"elathi","num":3,"title":"ஏலாதி 41-60","section":"ஏலாதி","theme":"Nineteen of the twenty verses name about six marks of a giver (refusals of killing, flesh, toddy, lies and theft, and gifts of food, goods, water works and cures to the ruined, the sick, mothers and ascetics) and pay each with a reward, mostly kingship in a later life; the run closes with a learner's six habits and the six evils of bad company."},"el:4":{"book":"elathi","num":4,"title":"ஏலாதி 61-80","section":"ஏலாதி","theme":"Twenty verses mix the renouncer's code (the defects of the soul, the path and timetable of release, the high state and the seven hells, the teacher's fitness) with the householder's giving, naming in sixes those to be fed, the gifts to students and ascetics, and the marks of love."},"iv:1":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":1,"title":"இனியவை நாற்பது 0-20","section":"இனியவை நாற்பது","theme":"The invocation and first twenty verses set the book's form, three or four things each named sweet, often ranked 'foremost' (முன்) or capped with 'nothing nobler', and spread it across the king's pleasures of horse and elephant, the householder's joys of children, kin and harvest, and restraints such as not killing, not eating flesh and not looking at another's wife, which the list counts as sweetnesses rather than duties."},"iv:2":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":2,"title":"இனியவை நாற்பது 21-40","section":"இனியவை நாற்பது","theme":"The book's second twenty verses keep its form of three sweet things, often ranked from 'foremost sweet' to 'nothing sweeter', and spread it over giver and asker, king and servant, householder and renouncer, so that restraint of speech and desire, honour, and faithfulness to what others entrust are presented as pleasures rather than duties."},"in:1":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":1,"title":"இன்னா நாற்பது 0-20","section":"இன்னா நாற்பது","theme":"The invocation and the first twenty verses each count four painful things, setting failures of worship, kingship, friendship, learning and the household beside plain images of things without their ground (a scentless flower, an unfenced cane field, a weapon in weak hands), so that a fault of conduct is read as one more thing cut off from what makes it work."},"in:2":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":2,"title":"இன்னா நாற்பது 21-40","section":"இன்னா நாற்பது","theme":"The last twenty verses of Innaa Narpathu each count four painful things, mixing bodily dangers, failures of skill and learning, faults of speech and friendship, and the pains of poverty and patronage, so that a moral fault is weighed on the same scale as a snake in the house or a stone in the areca nut."},"mk:1":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":1,"title":"சிறந்த பத்து 1-10","section":"சிறந்த பத்து","theme":"The first ten ranks one good above another in each line, and repeatedly puts what is kept or practised (conduct, retention, truthfulness, health, shame, chastity, reverence, not dwindling) above what is possessed or displayed (study, brilliance, liberality, youth, beauty, lineage, a large beginning)."},"mk:2":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":2,"title":"அறிவுப் பத்து 11-20","section":"அறிவுப் பத்து","theme":"The ten of knowing (அறிவுப் பத்து) names, line by line, the outward sign by which a hidden quality is recognised: great or small birth by kindness or pride, kindness by giving, friendship by help, learning by discernment, theft by trickery, all lapses by a lapse of speech or understanding, and ability by deeds."},"mk:3":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":3,"title":"பழியாப் பத்து 21-30","section":"பழியாப் பத்து","theme":"The ten that people do not blame: inborn temper in the untrained, the lowly's lack of eminence, the difficulty of great things and the esteem of rare ones, an uncompletable shortfall, an unjust king (by one living in his land), good kin's omissions, a strange land's customs, the poor man's lack of generosity, and the conduct of the small."},"mk:4":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":4,"title":"துவ்வாப் பத்து 31-40","section":"துவ்வாப் பத்து","theme":"The ten of துவ்வாப் பத்து set ten things that look like goods (wealth, daring, a life, a gift, an undertaking, help, a bond, a companion, old age, pleasure) each against a plain lack or a vice and find them no better, so that each good is emptied by the fault that comes with it: blame, excess, shamelessness, disregard, failure to act, falsity, ingratitude, folly, disgrace and not sharing."},"mk:5":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":5,"title":"அல்ல பத்து 41-50","section":"அல்ல பத்து","theme":"The அல்ல ten denies a thing its name when one inner condition is missing: a spouse who does not know the other's nature, a house without an excellent wife, kin without tenderness, a friend without consent, learning without submission, giving off aram's road, penance not rightly undertaken, and age that does not know rebirth."},"mk:6":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":6,"title":"இல்லைப் பத்து 51-60","section":"இல்லைப் பத்து","theme":"The ten of இல்லைப் பத்து names, line by line, the thing beyond which there is no greater of its kind: children as gain, knowing ஒப்புரவு as a fitting acquisition, attainable and unattainable desire as the best and worst desire, withholding as cruelty, lack of understanding as death, desire as poverty, renown as legacy, begging as disgrace and giving to askers as distinction."},"mk:7":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":7,"title":"பொய்ப் பத்து 61-70","section":"பொய்ப் பத்து","theme":"The ten of பொய்ப் பத்து calls ten things false, each a state or wish of a person who lacks what it needs: good life for the wise man aside, the eminent without anger, the drinker without lapse, gain without timing, safety without foresight, rising without bearing one's lot, greatness without bearing lowliness and its mirror, justice from the greedy, and penance from the impure."},"mk:8":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":8,"title":"எளிய பத்து 71-80","section":"எளிய பத்து","theme":"The ten of எளிய பத்து pairs a desire with what comes easily to it: the love of fame, kindness, hardship and bearing a load brings heaven, giving, pleasure and shared food, while the love of strife, tale-bearing, pleasure, food and women brings battle, betrayed secrets, pain, disease and blame. The last line breaks the pattern with a lack, not a desire: those without support easily meet killing."},"mk:9":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":9,"title":"நல்கூர்ந்த பத்து 81-90","section":"நல்கூர்ந்த பத்து","theme":"The ninth ten calls things poor (நல்கூர்ந்தன்று): goods and acts that lose their worth because the condition that would give them force is missing, such as a land without a just king, desire and adornment in the very old, sulking where one is not cherished, words where words do not carry, anger that inspires no awe, and visits where there is no friendship."},"mk:10":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":10,"title":"தண்டாப் பத்து 91-100","section":"தண்டாப் பத்து","theme":"The last ten pairs each wish with the practice its seeker never leaves off: rising with lofty speech, growth with renown, learning with reverence, steadfastness with penance, a good life with deliberation, plenty with toil, safety with just rule and love with hinted speech, and in its centre it sets pleasure and pain each as the price of the other."},"nm:1":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":1,"title":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை 0-20","section":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை","theme":"Two invocations to Vishnu, by likeness and by deed, open twenty verses that each set four 'gems' under one predicate: where things are born, how their worth is tested, what adorns, binds, harms or lays waste, and what each thing ends in. Natural images (gems, beasts, crops, fire) sit in the same row as conduct, so that a virtue is shown to have a source, a test and an end like a thing."},"nm:2":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":2,"title":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை 21-40","section":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை","theme":"Twenty four-gem verses on what ruins and what sustains (disunity, bad company, enmity, the giving hand), the stages of learning, kinds of joy and refuge, and a birth-ranking set against verses that deny birth any hold on fortune."},"nm:3":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":3,"title":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை 41-60","section":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை","theme":"Twenty verses of four gems each, many defining a common word anew (stranger, mother, the sweet eater) or naming what thrives, withers, burns or dies before an unfit receiver, ranging from the king's army to household giving, karma and the constant order of the world."},"nm:4":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":4,"title":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை 61-80","section":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை","theme":"Verses 61 to 80 each set four things in one frame (what thrives by being drawn on, what is known by what, what is better than what, what lies within a kind), so that household, learning, wealth and speech are judged by the same patterns of sign, measure and increase."},"nm:5":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":5,"title":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை 81-104","section":"நான்மணிக்கடிகை","theme":"The last twenty-four verses keep the four-gem form: each names four things under one verb or likeness (dies beneath, is death to, stays, grows, is a fault, is evil, is the light of). They range over learning and its absence, speech, the base and the worthy, women's conduct, kin and friendship, and close with chains from speech or the wife to understanding and release."},"pz:1":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":1,"title":"கல்வி payiram-10","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading கல்வி, with the payiram and the invocation, ten verses on learning: it must be had in youth, the learner feels his ignorance grow, learning that does not clear confusion is worthless, the learned are at home everywhere, hearing excels solitary study, a young Chola judges by inherited craft, only the learned or the better can judge learning and goodness, the learned are restrained, and their faults are seen by all."},"pz:2":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":2,"title":"கல்லாதார் 11-16","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading கல்லாதார், six verses on the unlearned and the learned in company: the unlearned man's right judgements are luck, his insights falter when spoken before the learned and count for nothing unspoken, and the learned man who speaks among the unlearned turns his good words bad and lapses in conduct; the unlearned who challenge the learned want what they cannot do."},"pz:3":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":3,"title":"அவையறிதல் 17-25","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading அவையறிதல், nine verses on knowing the assembly: the skilled speaker reads his hearers' desire, does not answer back or speak out of turn, and wastes no counsel on fools, while the unlearned and shameless who push into the company of the wise talk emptily, mock the restraint of the learned, and are silent among the good but boastful among the mean."},"pz:4":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":4,"title":"அறிவுடைமை 26-33","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading அறிவுடைமை, eight verses on what understanding is worth: it is the garment that all other excellence decorates, one wise man outshines a crowd of fools, the senseless cannot be set right, the wise win the wise through the wise and keep out of company that does not value them, real ability cannot be hidden, and understanding stays where wealth comes and goes with fortune."},"pz:5":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":5,"title":"ஒழுக்கம் 34-42","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading ஒழுக்கம், nine verses on keeping one's own conduct: conduct as richness added to good birth, taking a good word from any mouth, walking by one's own way, the limits of advice to the fixed, mending one's own faults before others', choosing ruin before a lasting scar, curing undeserved reproach, the shameless untouched by blame, and the evil tongue that spares no one."},"pz:6":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":6,"title":"இன்னா செய்யாமை 43-50","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading இன்னா செய்யாமை, eight verses on not hurting others: harm returns on the one who causes it whatever the victim does (43, 46), the self-measure of pain answers the sense of the other as different (44), the scorned dependant and the defenceless strike back by bite or by tears (45, 47), the great cannot be pinched like stone (48), and the worthy do not retaliate against the base, as beneath them and as a waste of their strength (49, 50)."},"pz:7":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":7,"title":"வெகுளாமை 51-59","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading வெகுளாமை, nine verses on not answering provocation with anger: the great are not heated by the small, the man of unquenched anger is to be avoided, a feud dies as one tends one's own good, a mocking jest rebounds on the jester, the talk of strangers and the lies of the hired cannot be stopped and need not be grieved, the learned do not gore back the fool any more than one gores a cow, abuse returned sets one's own house on fire, and nobility comes from enduring rather than punishing."},"pz:8":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":8,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை 60-64","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை, five verses on offending the great: belittling them is no wisdom and leaves their standing whole, the insolence of the small is a blind walk to death, the unlearned who needle them hide behind a shrub, a repeated offence is not forgiven, and long association does not save one who strays."},"pz:9":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":9,"title":"புகழ்தலின் கூறுபாடு 65-68","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading புகழ்தலின் கூறுபாடு, four verses on praise given out of place: praising oneself before the work is done, accepting praise even from one's own people, praising boasters who have nothing to boast of, and withholding the good word from a poor kinsman set apart among many kin."},"pz:10":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":10,"title":"சான்றோர் இயல்பு 69-80","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading சான்றோர் இயல்பு, twelve verses on the nature of the worthy: their worth shines through ruin, poverty and mud and is seen by the discerning; they keep their station in hunger, do not covet, give with knowing folly, speak no wounding word in company, judge their own hidden faults, shun slanderers, and pay for their height, since their faults are seen far and remembered long."},"pz:11":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":11,"title":"சான்றோர் செய்கை 81-89","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading சான்றோர் செய்கை, nine verses on how the worthy act: they keep up ஒப்புரவு in want, even by borrowing, speak truth though beholden, do not cast off those they took in or friends who prove faulty, pity an enemy in distress, and, though great by birth, depend on and keep company with the lesser; their poverty outranks the wealth of the small."},"pz:12":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":12,"title":"கீழ்மக்கள் இயல்பு 90-106","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading கீழ்மக்கள் இயல்பு, seventeen verses argue that the base cannot be changed: good company, instruction, a ruler's grant, punishment, favour and wealth all leave them as they were, and the wise neither trust them, counsel them nor honour them in place of the great. Three verses (96, 103, 106) tie baseness to a way of life, a line of descent or family rank."},"pz:13":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":13,"title":"கீழ்மக்கள் செய்கை 107-123","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading கீழ்மக்கள் செய்கை, seventeen verses on the conduct of base men: their insolence to the great, their denial of the next world, the danger of helping, reforming, provoking or living with them, their self-betraying speech and backbiting past shame, their deafness to counsel, their hidden ill will and whims, and the inverted world in which they rise."},"pz:14":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":14,"title":"நட்பின் இயல்பு 124-134","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading நட்பின் இயல்பு, eleven verses on what friendship is: a union closer than the god's half-body, kept by taking a friend's words at their meaning, by not parting, by help in deed, by silence about and patience with faults, by telling one's want to the friend who will cure it; one verse takes friendship from anyone for its yield, and the last says the prosperous man's thousand guests vanish in ruin."},"pz:15":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":15,"title":"நட்பில் விலக்கு 135-141","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading நட்பில் விலக்கு, seven verses on the friends to be shunned: those once dear as the eye's pupil who turn to plotting one's life, those kept as a reserve who fail at need, the friend who withdraws to piety when the battle comes, the fools who heed slander, the shameless on whom love is wasted, the friend who breaks and must be broken with, and the double-dealer who will hold to neither side."},"pz:16":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":16,"title":"பிறர் இயல்பைக் குறிப்பால் உணர்தல் 142-148","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading பிறர் இயல்பைக் குறிப்பால் உணர்தல், seven verses on knowing others by signs: a small part seen stands for the whole, hidden wrong is seen by others' eyes and leaks through the face, the face mirrors intent, and a person's nature is read from their kin, their birth-group and the company they keep."},"pz:17":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":17,"title":"முயற்சி 149-161","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading முயற்சி, thirteen verses on effort: one is one's own physician and no helper does one's work, the doer outranks the learned and the old, neither the smallness of a task nor the lack of kin excuses sloth, action must be weighed and done by means that succeed, delegation from sloth and dependence on kings fail, and folded hands before fate end no trouble."},"pz:18":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":18,"title":"கருமம் முடித்தல் 162-176","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading கருமம் முடித்தல், fifteen verses on getting a task done through others: fitting one's manner to each person, winning help by affection rather than force, choosing the capable over kin, the senior or the base, allowing the agent his share, and keeping watch after the work is handed over."},"pz:19":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":19,"title":"மறை பிறர் அறியாமை 177-182","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading மறை பிறர் அறியாமை, six verses on keeping secrets: kinship and friendship are no ground for disclosure, a mean confidant chosen over worthy kin is a drum struck, love must be known before one confides, everyone passes on what he hears, only the noble keep a secret, and even the affectionate must be examined first."},"pz:20":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":20,"title":"தெரிந்து தெளிதல் 183-195","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading தெரிந்து தெளிதல், thirteen verses on examining before trusting or acting: the giver who does not weigh the asker, the witness who weighs even what he saw, the go-between caught in a quarrel, the near small gain over the far large one, water taken for shallow, the loser who suspects even gods, fair-seeming dependants who rob, and private scrutiny joined to going with the town."},"pz:21":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":21,"title":"பொருள் 196-204","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading பொருள், nine verses on what wealth does: it overcomes the unreasonable better than force, makes every affair go well, draws the needy and the praise of all, guards the one who guards it, grows from small daily gains, is untouched by petty theft, and leaves the fallen great their standing; one verse warns against scorning a man for his small beginnings."},"pz:22":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":22,"title":"பொருளைப் பெறுதல் 205-212","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading பொருளைப் பெறுதல், eight verses on keeping wealth rather than winning it: look ahead to one's own wealth and kin's, guard it oneself and not through untested kin, the base or the man of no family, keep it in a fit place, do not neglect it and hope to recover it later, and know that wealth let go or lent out does not come back without anger."},"pz:23":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":23,"title":"நன்றியில் செல்வம் 213-226","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading நன்றியில் செல்வம், fourteen verses on wealth that does no good: ill-gotten gain that perishes as it seems to grow, wealth in weak hands that draws attack, the hoard neither given nor enjoyed, the miser's piety and his lost next birth, giving that passes over kin, the feigned kindness and the false plea of poverty, the honest refusal set against killing hope, and the gift wasted on the ungrateful."},"pz:24":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":24,"title":"ஊழ் 227-240","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading ஊழ், fourteen verses argue that fate rules effort, understanding, guarding, service to kings and escape: what is due befalls the highest and the unwilling, gain falls to those whose earlier good deeds or penance earned it, the fortunate have no evil day, the foe is only the agent of one's old deed, and survivors of fire come to escape or to rule."},"pz:25":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":25,"title":"அரசியல்பு 241-257","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading அரசியல்பு, seventeen verses on the nature of kingship: punishing one's own and sparing no age, even justice to rich and poor, gentle taxation and the futility of favours paid for by extortion, the helplessness of subjects under an unjust king, the need for a measure of severity and a firm mind, keeping no rival power or equal close, not handing office to the unrestrained, forbearance as a ground of rule, and the gain of joining the great."},"pz:26":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":26,"title":"அமைச்சர் 258-265","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading அமைச்சர், eight verses on the king's counsellors: the royal assembly shows their learning, the king's faults are laid on those who shaped him, a king without many counsellors for eyes is helpless in trouble, the king should test and hold the wise, restrained guardians keep his life, good ministers press unwelcome good on him, the wise may even beguile him at the right moment, and the experienced take care never to provoke his anger."},"pz:27":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":27,"title":"மன்னரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகல் 266-284","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading மன்னரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகல், nineteen verses on serving kings: work owed for food already eaten and reward trusted to the commander, no contradiction and no flinching, strength borrowed from the king, not asking and not seeking elsewhere, access and timing above rank, the danger of royal love, a fallen king still a snake, no meddling between kings, no treachery, resentment kept asleep and forbearance not presumed on, and steadiness before slander."},"pz:28":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":28,"title":"பகைத்திறம் தெரிதல் 285-297 (1/2)","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading பகைத்திறம் தெரிதல் (first part), thirteen verses on dealing with enemies: fear empties every fort, a young enmity must be cut off from its allies, retaliation must not wait, favours do not win a settled foe, the great and their dependents must not be provoked, the weak should flee and live, an old enemy never becomes a friend, and a false friend close by is worse than a flood afar."},"pz:29":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":29,"title":"பகைத்திறம் தெரிதல் 298-310 (2/2)","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"The second part of the heading பகைத்திறம் தெரிதல் (verses 298-310) treats the craft of enmity: an open enemy is better than a false intimate, anger should stop when the enemy is dead, the petty are not worth fighting, allies in number outweigh one's own prowess, and above all an enemy is beaten by turning others against him, a rear threat, an outside power, the division within his own camp, and finally by the four means of words, gifts, division and measured force."},"pz:30":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":30,"title":"படைவீரர் 311-326","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading படைவீரர், sixteen verses on the soldier: deeds over words, loyalty that cannot join the enemy, silence about one's own valour, courage that weighs the match and is not the fool's ignorance, scorn for those who bully the weak, hide behind walls, kin or reputation, or eat the king's food without fighting, and the refusal of a daughter to a king that can end only in war."},"pz:31":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":31,"title":"இல்வாழ்க்கை 327-337 (1/2)","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"The first half of the heading இல்வாழ்க்கை, eleven verses on the household: money as the seed of its work, cherishing the wife who shared its toil, keeping up appearances in want, the wife who tends guests unbidden, the father's charge over his children, a mother's favour, the bard who pleads for a straying husband, a glance that hides modesty, not entering a friend's house alone, the futility of confining women, and the great sparing the poor as hosts."},"pz:32":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":32,"title":"இல்வாழ்க்கை 338-347 (2/2)","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"The second part of இல்வாழ்க்கை, ten verses: a gift spoiled by withholding a sweet word, penance as an even mind not a sect's mark, prompt return of a deposit, the use of even a small companion, the bar on flesh for renouncers and for the graceless, service to the one who feeds one, bearing the anger of kin one has fed, and ingratitude as cutting one's own branch and burning the house one ate in."},"pz:33":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":33,"title":"உறவினர் 348-356","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading உறவினர், nine verses on kin: one must feel dependants' trouble as one's own and feed them, good kin remove distress, stand by in trouble even after enmity and help at last like late rain, their hurtful counsel is to be borne, and one taken as kin follows his kinsman; but even brothers should not share a roof long, and dice among kin, as the Bharata shows, make them strangers."},"pz:34":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":34,"title":"அறம் செய்தல் 357-371","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading அறம் செய்தல், fifteen verses on doing aram: the rich give easily, aram rather than the hoard is the reserve for need, it serves both worlds, it must be done before illness and death and while the rare human birth lasts, begun aram must not be dropped, a few days of the best will do, guarded wealth is lost anyway, grace is its root, and aram or penance practised falsely binds the deceiver."},"pz:35":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":35,"title":"ஈகை 372-386","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading ஈகை, fifteen verses on giving: giving to get more is angling, the hoard ends with the king while a small gift to the starving is rain in the waste, discerning giving neither drains nor dwindles wealth but renews it like tank and well, the best giver reads the need, the habitual giver gives even in want as Pari's daughter did in drought, a gift measures the patron's worth, giving for fame is wage work, crowds of askers and incapable patrons defeat the asker, and wealth not given in time is lost past remedy."},"pz:36":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":36,"title":"வீட்டு நெறி 387-399","section":"பழமொழி நானூறு","theme":"Under the heading வீட்டு நெறி, thirteen verses on the way to release: self-mastery outranks wealth and birth, the senses must be guarded early, the finished household should give way to renunciation and the undecided lose both lives, no fortune escapes Death, desire and pride in town life are costly, the renouncer must cut even the smallest tie, and birth ends when its supports are gone."},"sp:1":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":1,"title":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் 0-20","section":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம்","theme":"After an invocation and a verse offering the book as rain against famine, the chapter counts things under one predicate (amrita, beauty, laughable, poison, painful, death, the tongue's death) across the household, the king's service and a Jain code whose crown is not being born again by giving up lying, theft, women and meat."},"sp:2":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":2,"title":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் 21-40","section":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம்","theme":"Twenty verses of about five things each, opening with a matched pair on the precocious and the dull, then moving through renunciation in youth, a bad wife, teacher and pupil, penance defined by conduct, a ladder of giving, penance and knowledge, beauty as speech, and the soldier who spares the surrendered."},"sp:3":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":3,"title":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் 41-60","section":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம்","theme":"The chapter gathers counsel on the household and the realm: which women a man avoids and which men guard a woman, a wife's excellence and her hold on her husband, the farmer's care and ruin, the fortress and the minister, and the rewards of learning; several verses on women and marriage state period norms of gender and astrology."},"sp:4":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":4,"title":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் 61-80","section":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம்","theme":"Most of these twenty verses count acts of giving and care (water and shade, tanks and wells, alms to fasters, food to the maimed, the dying and the hungry low, care of mothers and newborns) and attach a fruit to each list, from kingship and heaven to long life and health, set among verses that strip the ascetic's outward forms, spread the sin of killing along the chain to the eater, and divide what company governs from what the mind does."},"sp:5":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":5,"title":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம் 81-96","section":"சிறுபஞ்சமூலம்","theme":"These sixteen closing verses mostly grade conduct and learning: five marks of the good giver and the lay vows set above scripture, the trick of the calf-skin placed beside killing, two tiers of the learned, five dogs answered by five easy virtues, a ladder from sweet speech to release, virtues fitted to the station where they cost most, and a ranking that puts Ganga worship below restraint, ending with worth proved only at death."},"tk:1":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":1,"title":"திரிகடுகம் 0-20","section":"திரிகடுகம்","theme":"The invocation counts three deeds of the god's feet, and the twenty verses that follow each count three things under one predicate (a medicine, the ruin of ignorance, a road to grief, a mute's dream, an undying body), mixing household, social and royal conduct so that unlike acts are judged as one kind."},"tk:2":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":2,"title":"திரிகடுகம் 21-40","section":"திரிகடுகம்","theme":"Twenty verses each count three things under one predicate (a rope to merit, a sinking mire, those called the world, the pure, husk-pounders, an army that breaks wealth), ranging from household giving and learning to statecraft and release, with a pointed attack on flesh-eating and sacrificial killing."},"tk:3":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":3,"title":"திரிகடுகம் 41-60","section":"திரிகடுகம்","theme":"Verses 41 to 60 count, three at a time, what heads instruction, what makes a family fair or brings it down, what leads to hell or stops the rain, what the wise avoid and what is wasted, and close on the sorrow proper to every kind of birth; household, royal and inward conduct are judged on one scale."},"tk:4":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":4,"title":"திரிகடுகம் 61-80","section":"திரிகடுகம்","theme":"Twenty verses of three things each range from the minister's daily practice to the wife's debts, the passions that consume their holder, what counts as wealth and purity, and gifts wasted on those who lack their inner condition; again and again a good is measured by the giving, restraint or trust within the person rather than by outward rank."},"tk:5":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":5,"title":"திரிகடுகம் 81-100","section":"திரிகடுகம்","theme":"Verses 81 to 100 count threes under predicates of loss and power: those who sink in craving, who are born yet unborn, who are utter fools or miss the good world, set against the wife, ascetic, king and priest whose keeping of their station brings rain, with the spy and the king's limbs closing the book on rule."}},"verses":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"t":["அகர முதல எழுத்தெல்லாம் ஆதி","பகவன் முதற்றே உலகு"],"p":"As the letter A is the first of all letters, so the eternal God is first in the world.","r":"As all letters have 'a' as their first, the world has the Primal Lord as its first.","n":"The verse gives a simile of being first and says nothing of creating. Parimelazhagar reads it as establishing that the unseen deity exists, arguing from the visible world, which is more than the couplet states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"t":["கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல் வாலறிவன்","நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"What Profit have those derived from learning, who worship not the good feet of Him who is possessed of pure knowledge ?","r":"What has learning achieved if the learned do not bow at the good feet of the one of pure knowledge?","n":"The question assumes that learning has a purpose beyond itself. It is the chapter's first conditional, and it is put to the learned, not to the ignorant.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one of pure knowledge is God, and scriptural knowledge bears fruit by worship that cuts off rebirth.","ஆகம அறிவிற்குப் பயன் அவன் தாளைத் தொழுது பிறவியறுத்தல்"],["kalaignar","The one meant is any person greater in knowledge, and the failing is a lack of humility before such people.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"t":["மலர்மிசை ஏகினான் மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்","நிலமிசை நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"They who are united to the glorious feet of Him who passes swiftly over the flower of the mind, shall flourish long above all worlds.","r":"Those who reach the noble feet of the one who walked upon the flower will live long upon the earth.","n":"The Tamil says நிலமிசை, on the earth, so reading it as a world above is an interpretation. Parimelazhagar also rejects an identification of 'the one who walked on the flower' with another deity, which shows the epithet was already disputed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The long life is deathless life in the world of release, above all worlds.","எல்லா உலகிற்கும் மேலாய் வீட்டு உலகின்கண் அழிவின்றி வாழ்வார்"],["manakkudavar","The general word 'land' covers this world and the next, with enjoyment named before release.","இவ்வுலகின்கண்ணும் மேலுலகின்கண்ணு மென்று கொள்ளப்படும்"],["kalaignar","The long life is the lasting renown of those who follow such a person.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"t":["வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலானடி சேர்ந்தார்க்கு","யாண்டும் இடும்பை இல"],"p":"To those who meditate the feet of Him who is void of desire or aversion, evil shall never come.","r":"Those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering, anywhere or at any time.","n":"The one sought is defined by what he lacks. Parimelazhagar's explanation makes the benefit mimetic: those who reach him also lose liking and aversion, and so lose the suffering these cause.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","idumbai","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering removed is that of rebirth, in its three classical kinds.","எக்காலத்தும் பிறவித் துன்பங்கள் உளவாகா."],["kalaignar","The one meant is a selfless human example, and following such a person keeps ordinary suffering away.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"t":["இருள்சேர் இருவினையும் சேரா இறைவன்","பொருள்சேர் புகழ்புரிந்தார் மாட்டு"],"p":"The two-fold deeds that spring from darkness shall not adhere to those who delight in the true praise of God.","r":"The two kinds of deed born of darkness do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","n":"The verse says both kinds, so good deeds are also among what must not cling. That is a stronger claim than 'worship removes sin'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even good deeds cause birth, and the praise is true only of the one who has all divine qualities.","நல்வினையும் பிறத்தற்கு ஏதுவாகலான்"],["kalaignar","Those who understand what 'the Lord' means and seek renown meet good and bad with equal measure.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"t":["பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான் பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க","நெறிநின்றார் நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"Those shall long proposer who abide in the faultless way of Him who has destroyed the five desires of the senses.","r":"Those who stand firm on the truthful path of conduct of the one who quenched the five senses' desires will live long.","n":"This is the only verse in the chapter that asks for a way of living instead of reaching or praising. Parimelazhagar says the path was taught by the one who quenched the five, as a poem belongs to its poet, which makes him a teacher and not only an object of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"t":["தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"Anxiety of mind cannot be removed, except from those who are united to the feet of Him who is incomparable.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the incomparable one, it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety.","n":"The verse says அரிது, hard. Parimelazhagar explicitly takes this as impossibility, which hardens the claim beyond its plain wording.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"t":["அறவாழி அந்தணன் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது"],"p":"None can swim the sea of vice, but those who are united to the feet of that gracious Being who is a sea of virtue.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the gracious one who is a sea of virtue, the other seas are hard to swim.","n":"The sea of virtue set against 'other seas' is the image's whole point. Pope's gloss, 'sea of vice', is not in the Tamil.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other seas are wealth and pleasure, set apart from virtue in the three aims of life.","ஏனைப் பொருளும் இன்பமும் 'பிற' எனப்பட்டன."],["kalaignar","The அந்தணன் is a worthy human being, whose example carries others across seas of trouble.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"t":["கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே எண்குணத்தான்","தாளை வணங்காத் தலை"],"p":"The head that worships not the feet of Him who is possessed of eight attributes, is as useless as a sense without the power of sensation.","r":"A head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is as useless as a sense organ that cannot sense.","n":"The verse never lists the eight. Parimelazhagar gives a list from Saiva scripture and records rival lists, so any particular list is the commentator's. Manakkudavar goes further and says such bodies lack life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The eight qualities are those taught in the Saiva agamas, though others list them differently.","இவ்வாறு சைவாகமத்துக் கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"t":["பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர் நீந்தார்","இறைவன் அடிசேரா தார்"],"p":"None can swim the great sea of births but those who are united to the feet of God.","r":"Those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births, and those who do not reach them cannot.","n":"The chapter closes with both outcomes in one couplet. Verses 7 and 8 hedge with 'hard', and this one drops the hedge. Parimelazhagar's 'reach' comes from the verse's own closing negative, since the positive verb is left implied.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The sea is the shoreless chain of cause and effect in rebirth.","காரண காரியத் தொடர்ச்சியாய் கரை இன்றி வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The sea is life in this world, which is hard to cross without following a leading person.",""]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"t":["வான்நின்று உலகம் வழங்கி வருதலால்","தான்அமிழ்தம் என்றுணரற் பாற்று"],"p":"By the continuance of rain the world is preserved in existence; it is therefore worthy to be called ambrosia.","r":"Because the world keeps going through the rain's constancy, rain deserves to be understood as amrita.","n":"The word உணரற்பாற்று makes this a matter of recognition, since rain is ordinary and easily taken for granted. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying it should not be thought of as a mere element.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"t":["துப்பார்க்குத் துப்பாய துப்பாக்கித் துப்பார்க்குத்","துப்பாய தூஉம் மழை"],"p":"Rain produces good food, and is itself food.","r":"Rain produces the food that eaters eat, and rain is itself food to those who drink it.","n":"Five uses of துப்பு pack the couplet, and the wordplay enacts the idea: the same substance is the maker, the made and the eater's need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"t":["விண்இன்று பொய்ப்பின் விரிநீர் வியனுலகத்து","உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி"],"p":"If the cloud, withholding rain, deceive (our hopes) hunger will long distress the sea-girt spacious world.","r":"If the sky fails, hunger stays and torments this wide world, though it is surrounded by spreading water.","n":"The irony is in விரிநீர்: the world is ringed by water and still starves. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit. பொய்ப்பின் also treats the sky as something that can break its word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"t":["ஏரின் உழாஅர் உழவர் புயல்என்னும்","வாரி வளங்குன்றிக் கால்"],"p":"If the abundance of wealth imparting rain diminish, the labour of the plough must cease.","r":"If the bounty of the rains declines, ploughmen will not plough.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the verse as explaining why food runs out. The human labour is real but placed downstream of a yield the farmer doesn't control.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"t":["கெடுப்பதூஉம் கெட்டார்க்குச் சார்வாய்மற் றாங்கே","எடுப்பதூஉம் எல்லாம் மழை"],"p":"Rain by its absence ruins men; and by its existence restores them to fortune.","r":"Rain is what ruins, and then it is rain again that supports and lifts up the ruined.","n":"The verse says only that rain ruins. The commentators here take the ruin as failure to fall, though a reader might expect flood too. The power to destroy and restore belongs to one impersonal agent, with no moral reason given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"t":["விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்மற் றாங்கே","பசும்புல் தலைகாண்பு அரிது"],"p":"If no drop falls from the clouds, not even the green blade of grass will be seen.","r":"Unless drops fall from the sky, it is hard to see even the head of a green blade of grass.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the implied 'even': if the grass is gone, not even a life with one sense remains. The argument descends to the smallest thing that lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"t":["நெடுங்கடலும் தன்நீர்மை குன்றும் தடிந்தெழிலி","தான்நல்கா தாகி விடின்"],"p":"Even the wealth of the wide sea will be diminished, if the cloud that has drawn (its waters) up gives them not back again (in rain).","r":"Even the vast sea would lose its nature if the cloud held back after taking from it.","n":"The source of rain needs rain, so the dependence runs in a circle. Parimelazhagar makes this the point, and Kalaignar turns the same cycle into a social lesson.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தடிந்து means the cloud scoops up the sea's water, and the sea's 'nature' is its living creatures and gems.","ஈண்டுக் 'குறைத்தல்' என்றது முகத்தலை."],["manakkudavar","He takes the word as the cloud flashing, and reports that others read it as dividing.","மின்னி மழையானது பெய்யாவிடின்"],["kalaignar","The verse also teaches that those who rise from a society must serve it for it to live.",""]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"t":["சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது வானம்","வறக்குமேல் வானோர்க்கும் ஈண்டு"],"p":"If the heaven dry up, neither yearly festivals, nor daily worship will be offered in this world, to the celestials.","r":"If the sky dries up, festival and worship will cease here, even for the gods.","n":"The word 'even' reverses the usual order: the celestials depend on what the sky provides through human offering. Kalaignar's comment adds scepticism with 'those said to live in the sky', which the verse does not express.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"t":["தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா வியன்உலகம்","வானம் வழங்கா தெனின்"],"p":"If rain fall not, penance and alms-deeds will not dwell within this spacious world.","r":"If the sky does not give, neither giving nor austerity will last in this wide world.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps giving broadly to the householder and austerity to the renunciant, so this verse quietly announces the two paths that chapters 3 and 5 go on to compare. Even austerity, which might seem independent of plenty, is included.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","thavam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"t":["நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகெனின் யார்யார்க்கும்","வான்இன்று அமையாது ஒழுக்கு"],"p":"If it be said that the duties of life cannot be discharged by any person without water, so without rain there cannot be the flowing of water.","r":"If no one's world can do without water, then its flow can't exist without the sky.","n":"The whole dispute rests on ஒழுக்கு, which can mean a flow or conduct. On the second reading, the chapter ends by saying morality depends on rain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒழுக்கு is the unceasing flow of water, and he records the conduct reading as someone else's.","அந்நீர் இடையறாது ஒழுகும் ஒழுக்கும் வானை இன்றி அமையாது."],["kalaignar","ஒழுக்கு is conduct, which may decay if there is no rain.",""]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை விழுப்பத்து","வேண்டும் பனுவல் துணிவு"],"p":"The end and aim of all treatise is to extol beyond all other excellence, the greatness of those who, while abiding in the rule of conduct peculiar to their state, have abandoned all desire.","r":"The settled view of the texts is to place the greatness of those who renounce within their discipline above all other excellence.","n":"The verse says only ஒழுக்கத்து, in discipline. Parimelazhagar's ladder from duties of varna to release is his own construction. The commentators also give very different reasons for the agreement among texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a shared conclusion of all religious texts, though they conflict on everything else.","ஒன்றையொன்று ஒவ்வாத சமய நூல்கள் எல்லாவற்றிற்கும் இஃது ஒத்த துணிவு"],["manakkudavar","Even a false text praises renunciants, so that people will honour it.","யாதானுமொரு பொய்யைச் சொல்லும் நூலும்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"t":["துறந்தார் பெருமை துணைக்கூறின் வையத்து","இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண் டற்று"],"p":"To describe the measure of the greatness of those who have forsaken the two-fold desires, is like counting the dead.","r":"Trying to state the measure of renouncers' greatness is like counting all the dead who have ever lived.","n":"It's a strange image of praise. The dead aren't only numerous but past counting in principle. My reading, offered tentatively, is that comparing renouncers to the dead also suits people who have left the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"t":["இருமை வகைதெரிந்து ஈண்டுஅறம் பூண்டார்","பெருமை பிறங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The greatness of those who have discovered the properties of both states of being, and renounced the world, shines forth on earth (beyond all others).","r":"The greatness of those who discern the two ways and take on virtue here stands highest in the world.","n":"ஈண்டு, here, is important: this choice is made in this life. Parimelazhagar uses an implied 'only' to place them above even emperors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","perumai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two are birth and release, weighed for their suffering and joy, and the virtue taken up is renunciation.","பிறப்பு வீடு என்னும் இரண்டனது துன்ப இன்பக் கூறுபாடுகளை ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்து"],["kalaignar","The two are good and evil, and anyone who chooses the good is great.",""]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"t":["உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்","வரனென்னும் வைப்பிற்கோர் வித்து"],"p":"He who guides his five senses by the hook of wisdom will be a seed in the world of heaven.","r":"One who guards the five with the goad of firmness is a seed for the finest ground.","n":"The goad implies the elephants, which the verse doesn't mention, and then the image shifts to farming. வரன் leaves the 'field' open: Parimelazhagar makes it release, while Kalaignar makes it renunciation itself. The word உரன் suggests strength, though Parimelazhagar says it refers here to understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"t":["ஐந்தவித்தான் ஆற்றல் அகல்விசும்பு ளார்கோமான்","இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி"],"p":"Indra, the king of the inhabitants of the spacious heaven, is himself, a sufficient proof of the strength of him who has subdued his five senses.","r":"Of the strength of one who has quenched the five, Indra, lord of the wide heavens, is witness enough.","n":"The verse does not say how Indra testifies. The commentators supply two opposed stories, one in which Indra failed and another in which he fears.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Indra, cursed for failing to quench the five, shows by his own failure what the power is worth.","தான் ஐந்து அவியாது சாபம் எய்தி நின்று, அவித்தவனது ஆற்றல் உணர்த்தினான்"],["manakkudavar","Indra trembles for his throne when anyone does great penance, so the ascetic is stronger than the gods.","அவன் தன் பதம் இழக்கின்றானாக நடுங்குமாதலான்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"t":["செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர் சிறியர்","செயற்கரிய செய்கலா தார்"],"p":"The great will do those things which is difficult to be done; but the mean cannot do them.","r":"Those who do what is hard to do are great, and those who can't do it are small.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is hard, so it reads as general. In its context the commentators narrow it to yoga. Manakkudavar turns it into a warning within the chapter: renouncing alone does not make one great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is hard is the eight limbs of yoga; what is easy is following the senses.","செயற்கு அரிய ஆவன, இமயம், நியமம் முதலாய எண்வகை யோக உறுப்புக்கள்."],["manakkudavar","Those who have renounced are still small if they can't do these hard things.","துறந்த மாத்திரத்தானே பெரியரென்று கொள் ளப்படார்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"t":["சுவைஒளி ஊறுஓசை நாற்றமென ஐந்தின்","வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு"],"p":"The world is within the knowledge of him who knows the properties of taste, sight, touch, hearing and smell.","r":"The world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five: taste, light, touch, sound and smell.","n":"கட்டே is the crux. Pope's 'owns his sway' and Parimelazhagar's 'within his knowledge' suggest different kinds of mastery. Parimelazhagar's whole twenty-five principles are not in the couplet, which names only the five.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is knowledge of the twenty-five principles of Samkhya, beyond which there is no world.","சாங்கிய நூலுள் ஓதியவாற்றான் ஆராய்தல்."],["kalaignar","This is the ability to understand and master the senses, which the world honours.",""]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"t":["நிறைமொழி மாந்தர் பெருமை நிலத்து","மறைமொழி காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"The hidden words of the men whose words are full of effect, will shew their greatness to the world.","r":"The greatness of those whose word is full is shown on earth by their hidden words.","n":"நிறைமொழி and மறைமொழி echo each other: a complete word and a concealed one. The verse doesn't say whether the proof is what the words do or what they teach.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renunciants' words take effect, and their commanding mantras prove it visibly.","அவர் ஆணையாகச் சொல்லிய மந்திரங்களே கண்கூடாகக் காட்டும்."],["manakkudavar","'Full' describes people of complete learning, whose mantras circulate as proof.","நிரம்பிய கல்வியையுடைய மாந்தரது"],["kalaignar","The proof is the enduring ethical books of the worthy.",""]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"t":["குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார் வெகுளி","கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The anger of those who have ascended the mountain of goodness, though it continue but for a moment, cannot be resisted.","r":"The anger of those who have climbed the hill of good qualities, even for a moment, is hard to withstand.","n":"The crux is whose capacity காத்தல் refers to, and the commentators turn the verse in opposite directions. Pope sides with the threatening reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their anger lasts only a moment, yet its target cannot ward it off.","தான் உள்ள அளவு கணமே ஆயினும், வெகுளப்பட்டாரால் தடுத்தல் அரிது."],["manakkudavar","The harm from their anger can't be avoided, as in Nahusha's becoming a serpent.","நகுஷன் பெரும்பாம்பாயினான்."],["kalaignar","Such people can't hold on to anger, even for a moment.",""]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"t":["அந்தணர் என்போர் அறவோர்மற் றெவ்வுயிர் க்கும்","செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுக லான்"],"p":"The virtuous are truly called Anthanar; because in their conduct towards all creatures they are clothed in kindness.","r":"Those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous, because they live clothed in cool kindness toward every living being.","n":"The verse defines a status word by conduct, and its logic is a reason (ஒழுகலான்), so the name follows from the behaviour. Placed right after 29, its coolness is the counterweight to the sage's terrifying anger.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The name's reason restricts it to those with such grace, namely those in renunciation.","அந்தணரென்று சொல்லப்படுவார் துறவறத்தில் நின்றவர்."],["manakkudavar","Having said some renunciants are small, the verse says some non-renunciants are great.","துற வாதாரினும் பெரியாருளரென்று கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","Any worthy person with love for all beings is an அந்தணர்.",""]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"t":["சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும் அறத்தினூஉங்கு","ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Virtue will confer heaven and wealth; what greater source of happiness can man possess ?","r":"It yields distinction and it yields wealth: what gain is greater for a living being than virtue?","n":"The appeal is to self-interest, as virtue is praised for what it brings. Parimelazhagar takes சிறப்பு as release and செல்வம் as heaven, while Manakkudavar takes the second more plainly as wealth and sees the verse as answering those who say prosperity comes from wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"t":["அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை அதனை","மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு"],"p":"There can be no greater source of good than (the practice of) virtue; there can be no greater source of evil than the forgetfulness of it.","r":"No gain surpasses virtue, and no ruin surpasses forgetting it.","n":"The opposite of virtue here is மறத்தல், forgetting, not doing wrong. Ruin comes through inattention, so the danger is ordinary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"t":["ஒல்லும் வகையான் அறவினை ஓவாதே","செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாஞ் செயல்"],"p":"As much as possible, in every way, incessantly practise virtue.","r":"As far as you are able, without stopping, do the work of virtue wherever it can go.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையான் sets the measure by capacity, so the demand is relentless in duration but not impossible in scale. Parimelazhagar gives 'wherever' as mind, speech and body, while Kalaignar turns it into a requirement that all one's acts be virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"t":["மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்","ஆகுல நீர பிற"],"p":"Let him who does virtuous deeds be of spotless mind; to that extent is virtue; all else is vain show.","r":"To be spotless in mind is the whole of virtue, and everything else is noisy show.","n":"ஆகுல means clamour, so the charge against outward virtue is that it wants an audience. Parimelazhagar reads the verse with 33, so it concerns the mind of someone already doing good, not the mind in place of deeds. Manakkudavar's comment says the four faults were named earlier, which implies his text placed 35 before this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"t":["அழுக்காறு அவாவெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல் நான்கும்","இழுக்கா இயன்றது அறம்"],"p":"That conduct is virtue which is free from these four things, viz, malice, desire, anger and bitter speech.","r":"Virtue is what proceeds without slipping into these four: envy, craving, anger and harsh speech.","n":"Virtue is defined by exclusion. The verse only lists the faults, and Parimelazhagar supplies a causal chain in which craving leads to anger and anger to harsh speech. Three of the four are inward and one is spoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","azhukkaaru","avaa","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"t":["அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது அறஞ்செய்க மற்றது","பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை"],"p":"Defer not virtue to another day; receive her now; and at the dying hour she will be your undying friend.","r":"Do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it later': when you perish, it is a companion that doesn't perish.","n":"The pun பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றா turns on one verb: you perish and it doesn't. The verse doesn't say how virtue survives, and that is where the commentators part.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When the body dies, virtue stays joined to the soul and travels into the next body.","செய்த உடம்பு அழியவும் உயிரோடு ஒன்றி ஏனை உடம்பினுட் சேறலின்"],["kalaignar","Virtue survives as lasting renown after death.",""]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"t":["அறத்தாறு இதுவென வேண்டா சிவிகை","பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை"],"p":"The fruit of virtue need not be described in books; it may be inferred from seeing the bearer of a palanquin and the rider therein.","r":"There is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who bears the palanquin and the one who rides in it.","n":"The couplet is very compressed, and it never says what difference we are to see. Taken at face value, it treats social rank as visible evidence of moral merit, which is one of the harder claims in the book.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Rider and bearer are direct perceptual evidence of virtue's fruit, so scripture needn't be cited.","சிவிகையைக் காவுவானோடு செலுத்துவானிடைக் காட்சியளவை தன்னானே உணரப்படும்."],["kalaignar","This is an analogy of attitude: the virtuous ride through joy and sorrow lightly, and others carry life as a burden.",""]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"t":["வீழ்நாள் படாஅமை நன்றாற்றின் அஃதொருவன்","வாழ்நாள் வழியடைக்கும் கல்"],"p":"If one allows no day to pass without some good being done, his conduct will be a stone to block up the passage to other births.","r":"If you do good so that no day falls away unused, that is a stone blocking the road of your days.","n":"வீழ்நாள் and வாழ்நாள் are set against each other, so fallen days are contrasted with living days. The stone's job depends entirely on what வாழ்நாள் means here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The stone blocks the road by which embodied days would return, which means it ends rebirth.","அச்செயல் அவன் யாக்கையோடு கூடும் நாள் வரும் வழியை வாராமல் அடைக்குங் கல்லாம்."],["kalaignar","The stone is a paving stone that smooths the road of this life.",""]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"t":["அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம் மற் றெல்லாம்","புறத்த புகழும் இல"],"p":"Only that pleasure which flows from domestic virtue is pleasure; all else is not pleasure, and it is without praise.","r":"Only what comes through virtue is joy; everything else lies outside it and has no renown either.","n":"Pope's gloss restricts this to 'domestic virtue', which follows Parimelazhagar and is not in the Tamil. The verse has a strong implication: joy is not separate from virtue and can't be got apart from it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is sensual enjoyment that accords with household life; illicit pleasure turns to pain.","இல்லறத்தோடு பொருந்தி வருவதே இன்பம் ஆவது"],["manakkudavar","All enjoyment of any kind comes through virtue.","இஃது எல்லாப் போக நுகர்ச்சியும் இதனானே வருமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The joy arises from the renown a virtuous life brings.",""]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"t":["செயற்பால தோரும் அறனே ஒருவற்கு","உயற்பால தோரும் பழி"],"p":"That is virtue which each ought to do, and that is vice which each should shun.","r":"What a person should do is virtue, and what a person should escape is blame.","n":"Virtue's counterpart here is பழி, blame, not sin. Parimelazhagar explains it as what is blamed, and takes it to mean bad deeds. The term gives virtue a social measure as well as a cosmic one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"t":["இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் இயல்புடைய மூவர்க்கும்","நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற துணை"],"p":"He will be called a (true) householder, who is a firm support to the virtuous of the three orders in their good path.","r":"The one called a householder is the steady support, on their good path, of the three who keep their proper way.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three, and what is meant by இயல்புடைய decides who is included.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The three are the other life-stages, and he says Valluvar takes the first two from other people's doctrine.","இவருள் முன்னை இருவரையும் பிறர் மதம் மேற்கொண்டு கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","The three are one's parents, spouse and children.",""]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"t":["துறந்தார்க்கும் துவ்வாதவர்க்கும் இறந்தார்க்கும்","இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை"],"p":"He will be said to flourish in domestic virtue who aids the forsaken, the poor, and the dead.","r":"The householder is the support of those who have let go, those who have nothing and those who have died.","n":"A single support reaches even the dead. Chapter 3 uses துறந்தார் for renouncers, so Parimelazhagar's different reading here gives the same word a very different sense.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துறந்தார் are people abandoned by those who should support them, and இறந்தார் are those who die alone at the door.","களைகண் ஆனவரால் துறக்கப்பட்டார்க்கும்"],["manakkudavar","துறந்தார் are renouncers who have given up caste and name.","வருணத்தினையும் நாமத்தினையுந் துறந்தார்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","They are ascetics, the hungry and the unprotected, so there is no duty to the dead here.",""]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"t":["தென்புலத்தார் தெய்வம் விருந்தொக்கல் தானென்றாங்கு","ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல் தலை"],"p":"The chief (duty of the householder) is to preserve the five-fold rule (of conduct) towards the manes, the Gods, his guests, his relations and himself.","r":"To tend properly the five, namely ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself, is the chief duty.","n":"The self is last on a list of duties. Parimelazhagar's reason is that one must exist in order to do any virtue, so self-care is a moral requirement. Kalaignar replaces ancestors and gods with memory of the dead and honour for those who live well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"t":["பழியஞ்சிப் பாத்தூண் உடைத்தாயின் வாழ்க்கை","வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்"],"p":"His descendants shall never fail who, living in the domestic state, fears vice (in the acquisition of property) and shares his food (with others).","r":"If a life fears blame and shares its food, its line never fails at any time.","n":"Fear of blame comes before sharing because generosity can't launder what was wrongly got. Parimelazhagar gives a striking account of how merit and fault split in that case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","pazhi","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","If someone shares wealth that was wrongly taken, the merit goes to the owner and the sin stays with the sharer.","அறம் பொருளுடையார் மேலும், பாவம் தன் மேலுமாய் நின்று வழி எஞ்சும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"t":["அன்பும் அறனும் உடைத்தாயின் இல்வாழ்க்கை","பண்பும் பயனும் அது"],"p":"If the married life possess love and virtue, these will be both its duty and reward.","r":"If household life has love and virtue, these are its character and its fruit.","n":"The verse makes the reward internal: the fruit of having love and virtue is having them. Parimelazhagar directs love to the wife and virtue to sharing, whereas Manakkudavar directs love to everyone and says no further reward is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"t":["அறத்தாற்றின் இல்வாழ்க்கை ஆற்றின் புறத்தாற்றில்","போஒய்ப் பெறுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"What will he who lives virtuously in the domestic state gain by going into the other, (ascetic) state ?","r":"If one conducts household life in the way of virtue, what would one gain by going off along the outside way?","n":"The rhetorical question challenges chapter 3 directly. Everything depends on how far 'outside way' reaches.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The outside way is only the forest stage, which leaves full renunciation out of the comparison.","இல்லை விட்டு வனத்துச் செல்லும் நிலை."],["manakkudavar","The outside way is austerity in general, and the householder gets the fruit of austerity too.","புறநெறியாகிய தவத்திற் போய்ப் பெறுவது யாதோ?"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"t":["இயல்பினான் இல்வாழ்க்கை வாழ்பவன் என்பான்","முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Among all those who labour (for future happiness) he is greatest who lives well in the household state.","r":"One who lives the household life according to its nature stands first among all who strive.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they strive for, so the comparison group changes with each commentator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those striving are ascetics trying to give up the senses, and the householder gets the result without such effort.","புலன்களை விட முயல்வார் எல்லாருள்ளும் மிக்கவன்."],["manakkudavar","Those striving are people striving for wealth.","முயறல் - பொருட்கு முயறல்."]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"t":["ஆற்றின் ஒழுக் கி அறனிழுக்கா இல்வாழ் க்கை","நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து"],"p":"The householder who, not swerving from virtue, helps the ascetic in his way, endures more than those who endure penance.","r":"A household life that keeps others on their way and doesn't itself slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance.","n":"The word chosen, நோன்மை, is the ascetic's own virtue claimed for the householder. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic bears only their own pain, while the householder also bears that of others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thavam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The householder also endures the troubles that come upon others.","பிறரை உற்ற நோயும் பொறுத்தல் இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"t":["அறன் எனப் பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை அஃதும்","பிறன்பழிப்ப தில்லாயின் நன்று"],"p":"The marriage state is truly called virtue. The other state is also good, if others do not reproach it.","r":"What is called virtue is household life, and that too is good if there is nothing in it others can blame.","n":"The whole verse turns on what அஃதும் points to. On one reading it is a rebuke to renunciants, and on another it is a condition on householders. Manakkudavar's explanation also shows a caste assumption of his own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aram","pazhi","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'That too' is renunciation, which is good only if it is free of hypocrisy.","ஏனைத் துறவறமோ எனின், அதுவும் பிறனால் பழிக்கப்படுவது இல்லையாயின்"],["manakkudavar","'That too' is household life, and the blame in question would be a wife of low birth.","பழிக்கப்படுவதென்றது இழிகுலத்தாளாகிய மனையாளை."],["kalaignar","A blameless household life is what is honoured as the household dharma.",""]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"t":["வையத்துள் வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன் வான்உநற்யும்","தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who on earth has lived in the conjugal state as he should live, will be placed among the Gods who dwell in heaven.","r":"One who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky.","n":"வைக்கப்படும், 'will be placed', could mean a destiny or an estimation. Manakkudavar treats it as being honoured by all, while Parimelazhagar also expects actual godhood later on. The Tamil word for how to live, வாழ்வாங்கு, is left undefined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"t":["மனைக்தக்க மாண்புடையள் ஆகித்தற் கொண்டான்","வளத்தக்காள் வாழ்க்கைத் துணை"],"p":"She who has the excellence of home virtues, and can expend within the means of her husband, is a help in the domestic state.","r":"She who has the excellence the home needs, and lives within the means of the man who took her, is the companion of life.","n":"Income literacy is half the definition, and it comes before chastity is mentioned. Parimelazhagar makes her skills concrete: knowing what is needed, cooking and neighbourly help. Manakkudavar instead reads மனை as her birth family, which shifts the standard from her work to where she comes from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"t":["மனைமாட்சி இல்லாள்கண் இல்லாயின் வாழ்க்கை","எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்"],"p":"If the wife be devoid of domestic excellence, whatever (other) greatness be possessed, the conjugal state, is nothing.","r":"If the wife lacks household excellence, the life has nothing, whatever other distinction it may have.","n":"The final word இல், nothing, is blunt. The verse doesn't say the life is worse, it says the life isn't there at all, and Parimelazhagar says this is because it bears no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"t":["இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால் உள்ளதென்","இல்லவள் மாணாக் கடை?"],"p":"If his wife be eminent (in virtue), what does (that man) not possess ? If she be without excellence, what does (he) possess ?","r":"What is lacking if the wife has worth, and what is present if she does not?","n":"Two questions with no answers given. Manakkudavar supplies the sharp version: though the man has nothing, nothing is lacking, and though he has everything, nothing is there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"t":["பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க யாவுள கற்பென்னும்","திண்மைஉண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"What is more excellent than a wife, if she possess the stability of chastity ?","r":"What is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு?","n":"The word திண்மை presents கற்பு as a strength, not as mere purity. Whether the verse is ranking a possession or a form of life is what divides the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A wife is ranked among the things a man can obtain, and none is better.","ஒருவன் எய்தும் பொருள்களுள் இல்லாளின் மேம்பட்ட பொருள்கள் யாவை உள"],["manakkudavar","The comparison is about being born a woman, which nothing exceeds if it has this firmness.","பெண் பிறப்புப்போல் மேம்பட்டன யாவையுள்?"],["kalaignar","The firmness is womanhood's own strength of character.",""]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"t":["தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்","பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை"],"p":"If she, who does not worship God, but who rising worships her husband, say, \"let it rain,\" it will rain.","r":"She who rises worshipping her husband and not a god: if she says 'rain', it rains.","n":"The verse says flatly that she doesn't worship a god. Parimelazhagar softens this by adding 'other' gods. The line is both the strongest claim of a woman's power in the book and the most troubling, and Kalaignar's entry reads the rain as an image of fearful servitude, not of power.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her husband is her own god, and her steadfastness is so powerful that the deities obey her.","பிற தெய்வம் தொழாது தன் தெய்வம் ஆகிய கொழுநனைத் தொழாநின்று துயிலெழுவாள்"],["manakkudavar","She regards all gods as being her husband.","எல்லாத் தெய்வ முந் தன்கணவனென்றே கருதி"],["kalaignar","Such a wife is like rain falling in fear at a command: she has made herself a slave.",""]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"t":["தற்காத்துத் தற்கொண்டாற் பேணித் தகைசான்ற","சொற்காத்துச் சோர்விலாள் பெண்"],"p":"She is a wife who unweariedly guards herself, takes care of her husband, and preserves an unsullied fame.","r":"She is a woman who guards herself, cares for the man who took her, keeps their good name and does not flag.","n":"பெண் here is a title that has to be earned, as the verse defines who counts as a woman. Parimelazhagar takes சொல் as a shared reputation for wife and husband, which means she is responsible for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pugazh","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"t":["சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பெவன் செய்யும் மகளிர்","நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை"],"p":"What avails the guard of a prison ? The chief guard of a woman is her chastity.","r":"What use is the guarding of women by confinement? The guard that their own steadfastness keeps is the best.","n":"The verse rejects confinement because it does not work, and it doesn't speak of the woman's rights. It leaves in place the assumption that she needs guarding, but it moves that guard inside her.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Walls and door-guards are useless without நிறை, the holding of her heart to chastity.","காவல் இரண்டினும் நிறைக் காவல் இல்வழி ஏனைச் சிறைக்காவலால் பயன் இல்லை"],["kalaignar","The verse is a charge against men: treating such women as slaves is ignorance.",""]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"t":["பெற்றாற் பெறின்பெறுவர் பெண்டிர் பெருஞ்சிறப்புப்","புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு"],"p":"If women shew reverence to their husbands, they will obtain great excellence in the world where the gods flourish.","r":"If women obtain the one who obtained them, they will gain great distinction in the world where the gods live.","n":"The word play பெற்றாற் பெறின் leaves open what 'obtaining' a husband means. Who has to be worthy depends on how the gap is filled.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The missing word is worship: women who serve their husbands are honoured by the gods.","தற்கொண்டாற் பேணிய மகளிர் புத்தேளிரால் பேணப்படுவர்"],["kalaignar","If a woman obtains a virtuous husband, household life becomes a new world for her.",""]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"t":["புகழ்புரிந்த இல்லிலோர்க்கு இல்லை இகழ்வார்முன்","ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை"],"p":"The man whose wife seeks not the praise (of chastity) cannot walk with lion-like stately step, before those who revile them.","r":"Those whose home does not seek renown cannot walk with a great beast's proud gait before those who deride them.","n":"The man's public bearing is said to depend on his wife's conduct. ஏறு needn't mean a lion as Pope and Parimelazhagar have it, since the word covers male animals generally, and Manakkudavar describes the gait without naming an animal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"t":["மங்கலம் என்ப மனைமாட்சி மற்று அதன்","நன்கலம் நன்மக்கட் பேறு"],"p":"The excellence of a wife is the good of her husband; and good children are the jewels of that goodness.","r":"They call a home's excellence its blessing, and good children its fine ornament.","n":"Parimelazhagar says this verse prepares for the next chapter by naming the wife's ornament. Children are ornaments here and do not yet have the value they get in chapter 7. Kalaignar takes மனைமாட்சி as the family's culture and not specifically the wife's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"t":["பெறுமவற்றுள் யாமறிவது இல்லை அறிவறிந்த","மக்கட்பேறு அல்ல பிற"],"p":"Among all the benefits that may be acquired, we know no greater benefit than the acquisition of intelligent children.","r":"Of all that can be gained, we know of nothing else like children who come to know what should be known.","n":"The first-person யாம் அறிவது is unusual and it sounds personal. The word அறிவறிந்த makes this a conditional praise: not children as such, but children who come to understand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The epithet of understanding restricts மக்கள் to sons, excluding daughters.","'மக்கள்' என்னும் பெயர் பெண் ஒழித்து நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The verse is about intelligent children in general, with no such exclusion.",""]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"t":["எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா பழிபிறங்காப்","பண்புடை மக்கட் பெறின்"],"p":"The evils of the seven births shall not touch those who abtain children of a good disposition, free from vice.","r":"Evils will not touch one for seven births, if one has children of good character who bring no blame.","n":"The verse assumes a child's character can protect a parent across births. Kalaignar's reading turns seven births into seven generations, which reverses the direction from the parent's future to the descendants', but no aligned Parimelazhagar or Manakkudavar comment is available to set against it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"t":["தம்பொருள் என்பதம் மக்கள் அவர்பொருள்","தம்தம் வினையான் வரும்"],"p":"Men will call their sons their wealth, because it flows to them through the deeds which they (sons) perform on their behalf.","r":"People call their children their wealth, because the wealth of those children comes by way of their own deeds.","n":"தம்தம் வினை is the crux: whose deeds, and whose wealth? Pope's couplet and his own gloss take opposite sides of this.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Sons' good deeds done on the parents' behalf bring what they earned to the parents in the next world.","அப்புதல்வர் செய்த பொருள் தம்மை நோக்கி அவர் செய்யும் நல்வினையானே தம்பால் வரும்"],["kalaignar","Each child's wealth comes from that child's own actions.",""]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"t":["அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதேதம் மக்கள்","சிறுகை அளாவிய கூழ்"],"p":"The rice in which the little hand of their children has dabbled will be far sweeter (to the parent) than ambrosia.","r":"Far sweeter than amrita is the plain gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children.","n":"The food is கூழ், a humble gruel, and it is sweet because of the mess. Parimelazhagar illustrates the mess by citing Purananuru 188, which makes this verse a real thread across books.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"t":["மக்கள்மெய் தீண்டல் உடற்கின்பம் மற்று அவர்","சொற்கேட்டல் இன்பம் செவிக்கு"],"p":"The touch of children gives pleasure to the body, and the hearing of their words, pleasure to the ear.","r":"Touching one's children is joy to the body, and hearing their words is joy to the ear.","n":"The chapter dwells on the senses here, after verses that praise restraining them. Parimelazhagar notes that சொல் is deliberately general, so it covers grown learned speech as well as baby talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"t":["குழல் இனிது யாழ்இனிது என்பதம் மக்கள்","மழலைச்சொல் கேளா தவர்"],"p":"\"The pipe is sweet, the lute is sweet,\" say those who have not heard the prattle of their own children.","r":"'The flute is sweet, the lute is sweet', say those who haven't heard their own children's prattle.","n":"The verse never actually says prattle is sweeter. It leaves that to be inferred, and Parimelazhagar notes this implied ending. Such praise comes in the form of mild scorn for the musically refined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"t":["தந்தை மகற்காற்று நன்றி அவையத்து","முந்தி இருப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"The benefit which a father should confer on his son is to give him precedence in the assembly of the learned.","r":"The good a father does for a son is to make him sit at the front in the assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar rules out nepotism: the son must actually surpass the assembly in learning. He adds that making him rich is no real good, since wealth brings pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"t":["தம்மின்தம் மக்கள் அறிவுடைமை மாநிலத்து","மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம் இனிது"],"p":"That their children should possess knowledge is more pleasing to all men of this great earth than to themselves.","r":"Their children's understanding is sweet to all beings on the great earth, more than to themselves.","n":"தம்மின், than themselves, can attach to two different things. Pope's couplet and gloss again split, one on each side.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is sweeter to all the world, especially the assembly, than it is to the parents.","மன்உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம் தம்மின் இனிது"],["kalaignar","It concerns children who are wiser than their parents, which gladdens everyone.",""]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"t":["ஈன்ற பொழுதின் பெரிதுவக்கும் தன்மகனைச்","சான்றோன் எனக்கேட்ட தாய்"],"p":"The mother who hears her son called \"a wise man\" will rejoice more than she did at his birth.","r":"A mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than at the hour she bore him.","n":"The verse says she hears. Parimelazhagar's explanation is his own and is telling: he says women cannot know this for themselves. The verse may simply show that public recognition is what is wanted. It sets the birth's joy as the baseline and goes beyond it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has to hear it because, by a woman's nature, she cannot judge it herself.","பெண்ணியல்பால் தானாக அறியாமையின் 'கேட்ட தாய்'"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"t":["மகன்தந்தைக்கு ஆற்றும் உதவி இவன்தந்தை","என்நோற்றான் கொல் எனும் சொல்"],"p":"(So to act) that it may be said \"by what great penance did his father beget him,\" is the benefit which a son should render to his father.","r":"The help a son gives his father is the saying, 'What penance did this one's father do?'","n":"The repayment is a sentence spoken by others. The son can't deliver it directly, only live so that others say it. The verse uses the language of austerity (நோற்றான்), so fatherhood is framed as a kind of penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"t":["அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ் ஆர்வலர்","புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்"],"p":"Is there any fastening that can shut in love ? Tears of the affectionate will publish the love that is within.","r":"Is there any bolt that can shut in even love? The small tears of the loving will proclaim it.","n":"A tiny tear (புன்கணீர்) produces an uproar (பூசல்), and the contrast in scale is the point. Parimelazhagar specifies that the tears fall at the beloved's pain, and he treats them as logical inference: love is unseen but can be inferred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"t":["அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர் அன்புடையார்","என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are destitute of love appropriate all they have to themselves; but those who possess love cons ider even their bones to belong to others.","r":"The loveless belong to themselves in everything, and the loving belong to others even to their bones.","n":"Love is framed as a question of ownership. Parimelazhagar glosses the bones by citing Purananuru 43, on the king who gave his own flesh for a dove, which is a direct thread to the Sangam corpus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"t":["அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கென்ப ஆருயிர்க்கு","என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு"],"p":"They say that the union of soul and body in man is the fruit of the union of love and virtue (in a former birth).","r":"They say the bond of the precious life with the bone is the way that came to be joined with love.","n":"The syntax is compressed, and the verse could give embodiment a purpose or a past cause. On Parimelazhagar's reading, the body exists for loving, which is an unusually positive view of embodiment. Pope's gloss follows the past-birth view.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul is joined to a body for the purpose of love, since love can't be done without one.","உடம்போடு இயைந்தல்லது அன்பு செய்யலாகாமையின், அது செய்தற் பொருட்டு இத்தொடர்ச்சி உளதாயிற்று"],["manakkudavar","The present embodiment results from love practised in a previous birth.","முற்பிறப்பின்கண் அன்போடு பொருந்தச் சென்ற செலவென்று சொல்லுவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is an analogy: love and action should fit as soul and body do.",""]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"t":["அன்பு ஈனும் ஆர்வம் உடைமை அதுஈனும்","நண்பு என்னும் நாடாச் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Love begets desire: and that (desire) begets the immeasureable excellence of friendship.","r":"Love gives rise to eagerness for others, and that gives rise to the unsought distinction called friendship.","n":"Love is a seed with a sequence. Parimelazhagar widens the final term a great deal: love for one's own extends to everyone, until the person has neither enemies nor neutrals. நாடா, unsought, suggests this distinction arrives without being chased.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"t":["அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கென்ப வையகத்து","இன்புற்றார் எய்தும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"They say that the felicity which those who, after enjoying the pleasure (of the conjugal state) in this world, obtain in heaven is the result of their domestic state imbued with love.","r":"They say that the distinction won by those who have known joy in this world is the outcome of a life settled in love.","n":"The commentators disagree about the timeline. Manakkudavar puts the love in a past birth, Parimelazhagar puts the distinction in heaven after this life, and Kalaignar keeps both in this life. Parimelazhagar's contrast is a pointed one: ascetics reach heaven through pain, while householders reach it through joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"t":["அறத்திற்கே அன்புசார் பென்ப அறியார்","மறத்திற்கும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"The ignorant say that love is an ally to virtue only, but it is also a help to get out of vice.","r":"The ignorant say that love supports only virtue, but it is also the ally against, or of, மறம்.","n":"The word மறம் is the problem. It can mean sin or valour, and the ambiguous dative 'for மறம்' leaves open whether love helps to remove it or to do it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறம் is hostile feeling, and love helps remove it when one treats an enemy as a friend.","மறத்தை நீக்குதற்கும் துணையாம்"],["manakkudavar","Love also stands by those who do மறம்.","அவ்வன்பு மறஞ் செய்வார்க்குத் துணையாம்,"],["kalaignar","மறம் is heroism, and heroic action is also backed by love.",""]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"t":["என்பி லதனை வெயில்போலக் காயுமே","அன்பி லதனை அறம்"],"p":"So loveless beings virtue's power to nothing brings.","r":"Virtue burns what has no love, as the sun burns what has no bones.","n":"It is a chilling image, and on Parimelazhagar's explanation the sun bears no malice: it shines equally, and the boneless creature perishes by its own nature. Virtue is thus not vindictive, which makes this verse more unsettling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aram","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is அறம் personified as a deity, impersonal like the sun, and the loveless soul perishes before it.","அன்பில்லாத உயிரை அறக்கடவுள்."],["kalaignar","What burns is one's own conscience, in someone who knows virtue but doesn't follow it.",""]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"t":["அன்பகத் தில்லா உயிர்வாழ்க்கை வன்பாற்கண்","வற்றல் மரந்தளிர்த் தற்று"],"p":"The domestic state of that man whose mind is without love is like the flourishing of a withered tree upon the parched desert.","r":"A life without love inside is like a dried tree putting forth shoots on hard ground.","n":"The image is an impossibility, as Parimelazhagar says and as Manakkudavar explains: there is nothing in the tree to cause sprouting. The verse says உயிர்வாழ்க்கை, a living being's life, and Parimelazhagar and Pope narrow it to household life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"t":["புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்பு ம் யாக்கை","அகத்துறுப்பு அன்பி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Of what avail are all the external members (of the body) to those who are destitute of love, the internal member.","r":"What use are all the outer parts to those without love, the inner part of the body?","n":"Love is classed as an organ, which is a striking way to make it non-optional. Parimelazhagar explicitly refutes the bodily-organs reading that Kalaignar's comment follows.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anbu","manam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Outer 'parts' are household means such as place, wealth and servants, because the body reading has no connection to household life.","புறத்து உறுப்பாவன: இடனும், பொருளும், ஏவல் செய்வாரும் முதலாயின."],["kalaignar","Outer parts are the body's beauty, which is pointless without love.",""]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"t":["அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை அஃதிலார்க்கு","என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு"],"p":"That body alone which is inspired with love contains a living soul: if void of it, (the body) is bone overlaid with skin.","r":"The seat of life goes by way of love; for those without it, a body is bones wrapped in skin.","n":"The final verse is about what counts as alive, and it isn't an exaggeration about ethics. Manakkudavar connects உயிர்நிலை to release, whereas Parimelazhagar connects it to the household virtue that makes a body live.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"t":["இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வ தெல்லாம் விருந்தோம்பி","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"The whole design of living in the domestic state and laying up (property) is (to be able) to exercise the","r":"All of staying at home and living by guarding one's goods is for the purpose of caring for guests and doing them good.","n":"The verse gives household life a purpose outside itself. Parimelazhagar draws the consequence: without this, the toil of home and earning has no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"t":["விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல் சாவா","மருந்தெனினும் வேண் டற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"It is not fit that one should wish his guests to be outside (his house) even though he were eating the food of immortality.","r":"Even if it were the medicine of immortality, eating it alone while the guest stays outside is not to be desired.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a second, sharper reading as acceptable: if someone calls even one meal eaten without a guest a medicine of immortality, reject it. Either way, the argument sets a refusal to eat alone over the prospect of not dying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"t":["வருவிருந்து வைகலும் ஓம்புவான் வாழ்க்கை","பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று"],"p":"The domestic life of the man that daily entertains the guests who come to him shall not be laid waste by poverty.","r":"The life of one who daily tends the guest who comes will not suffer and fall into ruin.","n":"It answers the obvious worry that hosting every day would be ruinous. Parimelazhagar goes further than the verse's 'not ruined': he says wealth will keep branching out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"t":["அகனமர்ந்து செய்யாள் உறையும் முகனமர்ந்து","நல்விருந்து ஓம்புவான் இல்"],"p":"Lakshmi with joyous mind shall dwell in the house of that man who, with cheerful countenance, entertains the good as guests.","r":"The fair goddess dwells with a glad heart in the home of one who tends good guests with a glad face.","n":"A chiasm links the two gladnesses: the host's face and the goddess's heart. Parimelazhagar's reason is that she's pleased when her wealth goes the right way. He also takes நல், 'good', to limit the guests to those of high knowledge and conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"t":["வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ விருந்தோம்பி","மிச்சில் மிசைவான் புலம்"],"p":"Is it necessary to sow the field of the man who, having feasted his guests, eats what may remain ?","r":"Must one even sow the field of the person who feeds guests and then eats what's left?","n":"மிச்சில், leftovers, is a low word for the host's own food, as if the guest's plate comes first. The rhetorical question doesn't say what the answer is, and the commentators answer it in opposite ways.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No sowing is needed, since the field will yield by itself.","'தானே விளையும்' என்பது குறிப்பெச்சம்."],["kalaignar","Such a host would use even the seed set aside for the field to feed guests.",""]],"ch":"k:9"},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"t":["செல்விருந்து ஓம்பி வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்","நல்வருந்து வானத் தவர்க்கு"],"p":"He who, having entertained the guests that have come, looks out for others who may yet come, will be a welcome guest to the inhabitants of heaven.","r":"One who tends the guest who is leaving and watches for the guest to come will be a good guest to those in the sky.","n":"The host becomes the guest in heaven, and the verse has an elegant reciprocity. Manakkudavar gives 'watching' a concrete rule: not eating without a guest. Kalaignar's 'those in the sky of renown' removes the literal heaven that Parimelazhagar assumes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"t":["இனைத்துணைத் தென்பதொன் றில்லை விருந்தின்","துணைத்துணை வேள்விப் பயன்"],"p":"The advantages of benevolence cannot be measured; the measure (of the virtue) of the guests (entertained) is the only measure.","r":"The fruit of this sacrifice has no set measure: it is as great as the guest's worth.","n":"Calling hospitality வேள்வி claims the language of ritual for a household meal. The measure is the guest and not the cost, and Parimelazhagar supports this with a Naladiyar verse. This also introduces a hierarchy among guests, which sits awkwardly with welcoming whoever comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிப் பற்றற்றேம் என்பர் விருந்தோம்பி","வேள்வி தலைப்படா தார்"],"p":"Who cherish not their guests, nor kindly help supply.","r":"Those who don't take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods anxiously and then say 'we have no support'.","n":"It is a late regret: the wealth they guarded with such pain is gone. பற்றற்றேம் can also be read as the renouncer's boast, 'we're without attachment', and that echo seems to me to sharpen the irony, though no commentator here says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"t":["உடைமையுள் இன்மை விருந்தோம்பல் ஓம்பா","மடமை மடவார்கண் உண்டு"],"p":"That stupidity which excercises no hospitality is poverty in the midst of wealth. It is the property of the stupid.","r":"Poverty in the midst of having is the folly that neglects hospitality, and it is found only among fools.","n":"The verse redefines poverty as a failure of use, not a lack of goods. Parimelazhagar explains: since folly takes away the benefit one gets from wealth, it makes having into not having.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"t":["மோப்பக் குழையும் அனிச்சம் முகந்திரிந்து","நோக்கக் குநழ்யும் விருந்து"],"p":"As the Anicham flower fades in smelling, so fades the guest when the face is turned away.","r":"The anicham flower wilts when smelled, and a guest wilts when the host's face changes on seeing them.","n":"Parimelazhagar's analysis has the guest more delicate than the flower, as the flower needs to be touched but the guest wilts at a distance. He gives a sequence of pleasant face, pleasant word and good deed, which leads directly into the next chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈரம் அளைஇப் படிறுஇலவாம்","செம்பொருள் கண்டார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"Sweet words are those which imbued with love and free from deceit flow from the mouth of the virtuous.","r":"Sweet words are those from the mouths of people who see the true thing, moist with love and free of deceit.","n":"The definition rules out flattery. Sweetness needs truthfulness and love, and ஈரம், moisture, makes love a physical quality of the words. Whether it allows rebuke is what the commentators bring out.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","anbu","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செம்பொருள் is virtue, and the speakers are those who understand it.","அறத்தினை உணர்ந்தார் வாயிற்சொற்கள்."],["manakkudavar","The speakers judge disputes between two parties, and they must rebuke sweetly too.","இஃது ஒருவனைக் கடிந்து சொல்லவேண்டு மிடத்தும் இன்சொல்லாலே கடிய வேண்டுமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"t":["அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே முகனமர்ந்து","இன்சொலன் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Sweet speech, with a cheerful countenance is better than a gift made with a joyous mind.","r":"Better than giving with a glad heart is to be, with a glad face, a person of sweet speech.","n":"This is a surprising ranking in a book so committed to giving. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that sweet speech is in one's own power, where a gift depends on wealth, yet it comes naturally only to a virtuous heart, so it's the rarer thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"t":["முகத்தான் அமர்ந் துஇனிது நோக்கி அகத்தானாம்","இன்சொ லினதே அறம்"],"p":"Sweet speech, flowing from the heart (uttered) with a cheerful countenance and a sweet look, is true virtue.","r":"Virtue lies in sweet words from the heart, after looking kindly with a glad face.","n":"The sequence is face, then eye, then heart, then word, and அகத்தானாம் is what keeps it from being mere manners. Parimelazhagar takes the emphatic ending as 'not in giving'. That's a strong inference: the verse may only be emphasizing speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"t":["துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை இல்லாகும் யார்மாட்டும்","இன்புறூஉம் இன்சொ லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Sorrow-increasing poverty shall not come upon those who use towards all, pleasure-increasing sweetness of speech.","r":"Grievous want won't come to those whose sweet speech gives everyone joy.","n":"The two forms உறூஉம் balance grief and joy in matching words. The promise seems to be social, not magical: Parimelazhagar reasons that one who has only friends comes to all wealth.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துவ்வாமை is material poverty, in which the senses have nothing to enjoy.","துன்பத்தை மிகுவிக்கும் நல்குரவு இல்லையாம்."],["kalaignar","The want removed is a poverty of friendship.",""]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"t":["பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல் ஒருவற்கு","அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற"],"p":"Humility and sweetness of speech are the ornaments of man; all others are not (ornaments).","r":"Being humble and sweet-spoken is a person's ornament, and nothing else is.","n":"The verse states humility without limit. Parimelazhagar limits it: one is to be humble toward those who deserve deference and sweet-spoken toward everybody. That's a notable narrowing by the commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"t":["அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும் நல்லவை","நாடி இனிய சொலின்"],"p":"If a man, while seeking to speak usefully, speaks also sweetly, his sins will diminish and his virtue increase.","r":"If one seeks out good things and speaks them sweetly, what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows.","n":"நாடி, having sought out, makes sweetness conditional on content. Parimelazhagar adds the converse: good counsel spoken harshly isn't virtue. For him the effect is on the speaker's own merit, whereas Kalaignar makes it a method of guiding others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"t":["நயன் ஈன்று நன்றி பயக்கும் பயன்ஈன்று","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்"],"p":"That speech which, while imparting benefits ceases not to please, will yield righteousness (for this world) and merit (for the next world).","r":"Speech that yields benefit and never parts from gentleness produces justice and brings forth good.","n":"பயன் and பண்பு are held together, so usefulness alone doesn't suffice and neither does charm. Parimelazhagar defines நயன் strikingly as being in accord with the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"t":["சிறுமையுவு நீங்கிய இன்சொல் மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்பம் தரும்"],"p":"Sweet speech, free from harm to others, will give pleasure both in this world and in the next.","r":"Sweet speech free of pettiness gives joy in the next life and in this one.","n":"The next life is named first, and Parimelazhagar says this is because its joy is greater. Kalaignar reads this as renown during and after life, which removes the afterlife. Pope's and Parimelazhagar's 'harm to others' is more specific than சிறுமை, smallness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","inbam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"t":["இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான் எவன்கொலோ","வன்சொல் வழங்கு வது?"],"p":"Why does he use harsh words, who sees the pleasure which sweet speech yields ?","r":"Why would one who sees how sweetly sweet speech yields use harsh words?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this an argument from experience: you know how others' sweet words feel to you, and harsh words are just as painful to them. The verse doesn't command, it only asks what the point is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"t":["இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்","கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந் தற்று"],"p":"To say disagreeable things when agreeable are at hand is like eating unripe fruit when there is ripe.","r":"Speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are there is like grabbing raw fruit when ripe fruit is at hand.","n":"The final charge is stupidity, not wickedness. Parimelazhagar makes the stakes much higher by reading the fruit as Avvai's nelli, which is like amrita, and the raw one as poison. He adds that harsh speech ends up hurting the speaker, and this allusion is a thread to the Sangam story of Avvai and the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"t":["செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவிக்கு வையகமும்","வானகமும் ஆற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"(The gift of) heaven and earth is not an equivalent for a benefit which is conferred where none had been received.","r":"For help given when no help was given first, earth and heaven together are hardly repayment.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason: all repayment has a cause, and this help has none, so nothing can match it. He also records a variant reading, in which the helped person could not return it, which changes the focus from the giver's freedom to the receiver's helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He reports another reading of the text, about help given to those unable to return it.","மறித்து உதவமாட்டாமை உள்ளவிடத்துச் செய்த உதவி என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"t":["காலத்தி னாற்செய்த நன்றி சிறிதெனினும்","ஞாலத்தின் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"A favour conferred in the time of need, though it be small (in itself), is (in value) much larger than the world.","r":"Help given in its time, though small, is far greater than the earth.","n":"Value here is relative to the need of the moment. Parimelazhagar's phrase is striking: it's the moment of mortal crisis that is being weighed, not the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"t":["பயன்தூக்கார் செய்த உதவி நயன்தூக்கின்","நன்மை கடலின் பெரிது"],"p":"If we weigh the excellence of a benefit which is conferred without weighing the return, it is larger than the sea.","r":"If you weigh the good of help given by those who don't weigh the return, it is greater than the sea.","n":"The verb தூக்கு is used twice: the giver doesn't weigh, and the recipient should. The three opening comparisons go from earth and heaven to earth to sea, and the verses seem interested in measure as such.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"t":["தினைத்துணை நன்றி செயினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பயன்தெரி வார்"],"p":"Though the benefit conferred be as small as a millet seed, those who know its advantage will consider it as large as a palmyra fruit.","r":"Though the good done is only as much as a millet, those who understand its fruit take it as a palm.","n":"The enlargement happens in the recipient's mind, and it isn't an error, it's a discipline. Parimelazhagar says the fruit in question is what comes to the one who regards it this way, so gratitude also benefits the grateful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"t":["உதவி வரைத்தன்று உதவி உதவி","செயப்பட்டார் சால்பின் வரைத்து"],"p":"The benefit itself is not the measure of the benefit; the worth of those who have received it is its measure.","r":"Help is not bounded by the help given: its measure is the worth of those to whom it was done.","n":"The verse repeats உதவி three times and it isn't clear which help is being measured.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This concerns repayment: what the receiver gives back is measured by the receiver's own worth, not by the original help.","கைம்மாறான உதவி, காரணத்தானும் பொருளானும் காலத்தானும் ஆகிய மூவகையானும் முன் செய்த உதவியளவிற்று அன்று;"],["kalaignar","This concerns the help itself: its value is judged by the quality of whoever receives it.",""]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"t":["மறவற்க மாசற்றார் கேண்மை துறவற்க","துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு"],"p":"Forsake not the friendship of those who have been your staff in adversity. Forget not be benevolence of the blameless.","r":"Do not forget the bond of the stainless, and do not abandon the friendship of those who were your strength in sorrow.","n":"Two different people are owed loyalty: those of good character and those who helped when it was needed. Parimelazhagar says the first serves the next life and the second this one. The second debt holds whatever the helper's character is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"t":["எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர் தங்கண்","விழுமந் துடைத்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"(The wise) will remember throughout their seven-fold births the love of those who have wiped away their affliction.","r":"For all seven births they will remember the friendship of those who wiped away their sorrow.","n":"துடைத்தவர், those who wiped, has a physical sense of wiping tears away. Parimelazhagar notes the past tense conveys speed, and he cites the lost Valaiyapathi for the phrase, which is a valuable thread.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seven births are meant literally: affection for the helper carries on in each birth.","பிறப்புதோறும் தொடர்ந்து அன்புடையராதல்."],["kalaignar","Seven births is a figure of exaggeration meaning that there is no time limit.",""]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"t":["நன்றி மறப்பது நன்றன்று நன்றல்லது","அன்றே மறப்பது நன்று"],"p":"It is not good to forget a benefit; it is good to forget an injury even in the very moment (in which it is inflicted).","r":"It's not good to forget a good done; what's good is to forget, that very day, what isn't good.","n":"நன்று repeats through the verse, so 'good' is both the thing remembered and the act of letting go. Parimelazhagar says this is about one person who has done both good and harm, which is narrower than a general call to forget all wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"t":["கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் அவர்செய்த","ஒன்றுநன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"Though one inflict an injury great as murder, it will perish before the thought of one benefit (formerly) conferred.","r":"Even if they do something as painful as killing, it vanishes when one thinks of a single good they did.","n":"This is a hard saying, as it places the burden on the injured person. Parimelazhagar explains it through 104: the millet taken as a palm outweighs everything. Kalaignar makes it more therapeutic, a way to keep from being wounded inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"t":["எந்நன்றி கொன்றார்க்கும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்வில்லை","செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு"],"p":"He who has killed every virtue may yet escape; there is no escape for him who has killed a benefit.","r":"Those who kill any good may have an escape, but there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him.","n":"Ingratitude is ranked as the sin with no remedy. Parimelazhagar's list of lesser crimes comes from Purananuru 34, whose speaker makes the same comparison, so this verse is a direct thread to that poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even the great crimes, cutting a cow's udder, destroying a womb and killing a Brahmin, can be expiated, as Purananuru 34 lists.","'ஆன்முலை அறுத்தலும், மகளிர் கருவினைச் சிதைத்தலும், பார்ப்பார்த்தபுதலும்' (புறநா 34)"]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"t":["தகுதி எனவொன்று நன்றே பகுதியால்","பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறின்"],"p":"That equity which consists in acting with equal regard to each of (the three) divisions of men [enemies, strangers and friends] is a pre-eminent virtue.","r":"That one thing called rightness is good if one can keep to it, part by part, in what is due.","n":"Parimelazhagar says பெறின் conveys how rare this is. The whole verse depends on what பகுதி divides.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The parts are enemy, stranger and friend, toward each of whom one keeps the same rule.","பகை, நொதுமல், நட்பு எனும் பகுதிதோறும், தன் முறைமையை விடாது ஒழுகப் பெறின்,"],["manakkudavar","The parts are each person's own station, so what is due varies with position.","அவரவர் நிலைமைப்பகுதியோடே அறத்தின் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறுமாயின்,"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"t":["செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கஞ் சிதைவின்றி","எச்சத்திற் கேமாப்பு உடைத்து"],"p":"The wealth of the man of rectitude will not perish, but will bring happiness also to his posterity.","r":"The wealth of a person of rectitude doesn't decay and is a strength to those who come after.","n":"The claim concerns durability, not amount. Parimelazhagar explains that it lasts because it comes together with virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"t":["நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை","அன்றே யொழிய விடல்"],"p":"Forsake in the very moment (of acquisition) that gain which, though it should bring advantage, is without equity.","r":"Even if it brings nothing but good, let go that very day of gain that comes from leaving what is fair.","n":"The commentators differ on the force of 'even if'. For Parimelazhagar it's only a concession, because such gain can't really bring good. Kalaignar's wording allows that it may, which makes giving it up a costlier demand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"t":["தக்கார் தகவிலர் என்பது அவரவர்","எச்சத்தாற் காணப்ப படும்"],"p":"The worthy and unworthy may be known by the existence or otherwise of good offsprings.","r":"Whether people are worthy or unworthy will be seen from what each leaves behind.","n":"எச்சம், what remains, is left unspecified, and the choice has large consequences. Parimelazhagar's claim that the unworthy have no good children is a very confident generalisation, and it isn't in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","pugazh","makkal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What remains is good offspring, whose presence or absence is a sure mark.","தக்கார்க்கு எச்சம் உண்டாதலும் தகவிலார்க்கு இல்லையாதலும் ஒரு தலையாகலின்,"],["kalaignar","What remains is the praise or blame left after a person is gone.",""]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"t":["கேடும் பெருக்கமும் இல்லல்ல நெஞ்சத்துக்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"Loss and gain come not without cause; it is the ornament of the wise to preserve evenness of mind (under both).","r":"Loss and increase are not absent from life; not letting the heart bend is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"The verse doesn't explain where loss and gain come from, and Pope's 'not mere accident' assumes one view. The important move is that bias is presented as useless as well as wrong.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They are already laid down by past deeds, so bending to get or avoid them achieves nothing.","'அவற்றிற்குக் காரணம் பழவினையே; கோடுதல் அன்று'"],["kalaignar","Rise and fall are simply the nature of the world.",""]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"t":["கெடுவல்யான் என்பது அறிகதன் நெஞ்சம்","நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்"],"p":"Let him whose mind departing from equity commits sin well consider thus within himself, \"I shall perish.\"","r":"If one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know: 'I will perish'.","n":"The subject is the heart, so the act is inward. Parimelazhagar draws this out: thinking is the same as doing, and the very thought is an omen to be read. The first-person கெடுவல் யான் makes the warning a sentence one says to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"t":["கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம் நடுவாக","நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு"],"p":"The great will not regard as poverty the low estate of that man who dwells in the virtue of equity.","r":"The world won't count as ruin the low state of one who remains in virtue by standing fair.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses உலகம் as the noble, as commentators often do, and adds that they regard such poverty as wealth. That's a real narrowing, since the actual world may well despise it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"t":["சமன்செய்து சீர்தூக்குங் கோல்போல் அமைந்தொருபால்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"To incline to neither side, but to rest impartial as the even-fixed scale is the ornament of the wise.","r":"To be settled and lean to no side, like the balance that first makes itself level and then weighs, is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"A balance isn't indifferent: it first levels itself, then registers exactly what's put on it. Fairness here is a precise response, not neutrality. Parimelazhagar carries the image into assembly practice, with weighing as hearing both sides and not leaning as stating the result equally to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"t":["சொற்கோட்டம் இல்லது செப்பம் ஒருதலையா","உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"F reedom from obliquity of speech is rectitude, if there be (corresponding) freedom from bias of mind.","r":"Rectitude is speech without bend, provided one surely has no bend within.","n":"Parimelazhagar's corollary is subtle: a correct statement made from a biased mind isn't impartiality. Manakkudavar adds that it excludes clever words which suit both sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"t":["வாணிகம் செய்வார்க்கு வாணிகம் பேணிப்","பிறவும் தமபோல் செயின்"],"p":"The true merchandize of merchants is to guard and do by the things of others as they do by their own.","r":"For traders, true trade is caring for others' goods as if they were their own.","n":"Manakkudavar glosses வாணிகம் as profit, so honesty is what actually pays. Parimelazhagar's phrasing, taking no more and giving no less, closely resembles a description of merchants in Pattinappalai, as I recall it. That is my own observation, and it should be checked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"t":["அடக்கம் அமரருள் உய்க்கும் அடங்காமை","ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Self-control will place (a man) among the Gods; the want of it will drive (him) into the thickest darkness (of hell).","r":"Restraint leads one among the immortals, and lack of restraint drives one into thick darkness.","n":"Both verbs are of driving, so restraint is itself a force that carries a person. The final உய்த்துவிடும் has a sense of finality.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The destinations are literal: the gods' world and a particular hell called darkness.","'இருள்' என்பது ஓர் நரக விசேடம்."],["kalaignar","The results are this-worldly: lasting renown and a darkened life.",""]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"t":["காக்க பொருளா அடக்கத்தை ஆக்கம்","அதனினூஉங் கில்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Let self-control be guarded as a treasure; there is no greater source of good for man than that.","r":"Guard restraint as your wealth, for life has no greater gain than it.","n":"Restraint is pictured as something to guard, as misers guard money, so the language of acquisition is turned to its opposite. Parimelazhagar notes this concerns humans, who can understand it and benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"t":["செறிவறிந்து சீர்மை பயக்கும் அறிவறிந்து","ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Knowing that self-control is knowledge, if a man should control himself, in the prescribed course, such self-control will bring him distinction among the wise.","r":"If one understands and keeps restrained in the right way, the restraint, recognised, will yield distinction.","n":"The syntax is knotted, and the commentators parse the repeated அறிந்து differently: Parimelazhagar says one learns that restraint is itself knowledge, and Manakkudavar says one learns the five objects and the five organs. I'm unsure which is closest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"t":["நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான் தோற்றம்","மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"More lofty than a mountain will be the greatness of that man who without swerving from his domestic state, controls himself.","r":"The stature of one who is restrained and doesn't stray from his station is much greater than a mountain's.","n":"தோற்றம், how one looks or appears, makes this a visual comparison. The dispute over நிலை decides whether the verse has a social hierarchy in it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The station is household life, and the restraint is exercised while enjoying sense objects.","பொறிகளால் புலன்களை நுகராநின்றே அடங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The station is the order of varna and stage of life.","நிலை - வன்னாச்சிரம தன்மம்."],["kalaignar","It's a matter of firmness of mind joined to unostentatious restraint.",""]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல் அவருள்ளும்","செல்வர்க்கே செல்வம் தகைத்து"],"p":"Humility is good in all; but especially in the rich it is (the excellence of) higher riches.","r":"Bowing is good for all, and among them, for the rich it is a further wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar asks why the rich are singled out. Those with learning or birth may be humble without it being notable, but for the rich it stands out. Manakkudavar reads செல்வம் simply as abundance, which weakens the pun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","selvam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"t":["ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல் ஆற்றின்","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Should one throughout a single birth, like a tortoise keep in his five senses, the fruit of it will prove a safe-guard to him throughout the seven-fold births.","r":"If in one birth one can draw in the five like a tortoise, it is a protection for seven births.","n":"The tortoise withdraws out of self-protection, and the image suggests retreat from danger, not conquest. The numbers one and seven make for an argument about return on effort. Kalaignar translates the seven births as all time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"t":["யாகாவா ராயினும் நாகாக்க காவாக்கால்","சோகாப்பர் சொல்லிழுக்குப் பட்டு"],"p":"Whatever besides you leave unguarded, guard your tongue; otherwise errors of speech and the consequent misery will ensue.","r":"Whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue; if they don't, they'll suffer from the fault of their words.","n":"This is a rare concession in a demanding chapter: it sets priorities for someone who cannot do everything. The opening யாகாவாராயினும் accepts failure in advance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"t":["ஒன்றானுந் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்","நன்றாகா தாகி விடும்"],"p":"If a man's speech be productive of a single evil, all the good by him will be turned into evil.","r":"If even one bad word has its effect, it turns out that nothing is good.","n":"The verse doesn't say what stops being good, and the commentators offer three scopes, from one's speech to one's whole moral record.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One harm caused by evil words turns the good of the speaker's other virtues to evil.","அவனுக்குப் பிற அறங்களான் உண்டான நன்மை தீதாய்விடும்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns a word sweet to the hearer but harmful in effect, so flattery is also bad.","இது சால மொழி கூறினாலுந் தீதாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","One bad word in a speech spoils all of that speech's good words, like poison in milk.",""]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"t":["தீயினாற் சுட்டபுண் உள்ளாறும் ஆறாதே","நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு"],"p":"The wound which has been burnt in by fire may heal, but a wound burnt in by the tongue will never heal.","r":"A wound burned by fire heals inside, but a scar burned by the tongue doesn't heal.","n":"புண் and வடு are carefully chosen: Parimelazhagar notes the first heals and is a wound, while the second stays and is a scar. For him the fire's mark may stay on the body but is healed in the mind, which reverses what one expects to be permanent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"t":["கதங்காத்துக் கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான் செவ்வி","அறம்பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து"],"p":"Virtue, seeking for an opportunity, will come into the path of that man who, possessed of learning and self-control, guards himself against anger.","r":"Virtue itself looks for its moment and enters by the path to the one who has guarded against anger and has learned restraint.","n":"The usual relation is reversed: virtue is a seeker waiting on a person. Parimelazhagar's gloss of செவ்வி pictures virtue as a petitioner awaiting a good moment to present its plea. Manakkudavar adds what must be restrained: the pride that comes from learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","adakkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் விழுப்பந் தரலான் ஒழுக்கம்","உயிரினும் ஓம்பப் படும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct leads to eminence, it should therefore be preserved more carefully than life.","r":"Since conduct gives excellence, conduct is to be guarded more than life.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reasoning is careful: life is the best of all things, but it doesn't confer distinction the way conduct does, so conduct gets greater care. He adds that this holds for high and low alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிக் காக்க ஒழுக்கம் தெரிந்தோம்பித்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"Let propriety of conduct be laboriously preserved and guarded; though one know and practise and excel in many virtues, that will be an eminent aid.","r":"Guard conduct with great effort; however much you search and weigh, it alone is the help.","n":"The three forms of ஓம்பி, தெரிந்து and தேர்ந்து make it sound like a long investigation with one result. Parimelazhagar makes it a survey of all virtues, of which only this one helps in both lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம்","இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is true greatness of birth, and impropriety will sink into a mean birth.","r":"Having conduct is what noble birth is, and failing in it becomes low birth.","n":"The verse redefines birth by behaviour. Even the conservative commentators allow that one's status can really change, though for Parimelazhagar it is within a varna frame that the verse doesn't state.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Status really changes: a low-born person of good conduct becomes high, and a high-born person who fails falls.","உயர்ந்த வருணத்துப் பிறந்தாராயினும் ஒழுக்கத்தில் தவறத் தாழ்ந்த வருணத்தராவர்"],["kalaignar","Conduct is the evidence of good birth, and failures are regarded as low-born whoever they are.",""]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:134":{"book":"kural","num":134,"t":["மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும் பார் ப்பான்","பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"A Brahman though he should forget the Vedas may recover it by reading; but, if he fail in propriety of conduct even his high bi rth will be destroyed.","r":"Even if forgotten, the Veda can be learned again, but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines.","n":"This is a pointed ranking, because study is what the Brahmin is supposed to be defined by. It makes birth a thing that can be lost. Parimelazhagar says the rule extends from this high example to the other varnas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ozhukkam","pirappu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:135":{"book":"kural","num":135,"t":["அழுக்கா றுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம்போன்று இல்லை","ஒழுக்க மிலான்கண் உயர்வு"],"p":"Just as the envious man will be without wealth, so will the man of destitute of propriety of conduct be without greatness.","r":"As there is no prosperity in the envious, so there is no elevation in one who lacks conduct.","n":"The simile takes as given what chapter 17 has yet to argue, that envy destroys wealth. Parimelazhagar uses verse 166 to extend it: as an envious man's kin suffer, so do a person's kin when conduct fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","azhukkaaru","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:136":{"book":"kural","num":136,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் ஒல்கார் உரவோர் இழுக்கத்தின்","ஏதம் படுபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"Those firm in mind will not slacken in their observance of the proprieties of life, knowing, as they do, the misery that flows from the transgression from them.","r":"The strong don't slacken in conduct, knowing what harm arises from a lapse.","n":"The motive given is prudence, not love of virtue. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ a little on உரவோர், as strength of mind and understanding respectively, the same question raised by உரன் in verse 24.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:137":{"book":"kural","num":137,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் எய்துவர் மேன்மை இழுக்கத்தின்","எய்துவர் எய்தாப் பழி"],"p":"From propriety of conduct men obtain greatness; from impropriety comes insufferable disgrace.","r":"From conduct people attain high standing, and from lapses they attain blame not properly theirs.","n":"எய்தாப் பழி is the sharp part. Parimelazhagar sees that after one slip, even a false charge from an enemy is believed. Conduct is therefore a shield as well as a good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When enemies make false charges, the world believes them because of the earlier lapse.","பகை பற்றி அடாப்பழி கூறியவழி, அதனையும் இழுக்கம்பற்றி உலகம் அடுக்கும் என்று கொள்ளுமாகலின்,"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:138":{"book":"kural","num":138,"t":["நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம் தீயொழுக்கம்","என்றும் இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is the seed of virtue; impropriety will ever cause sorrow.","r":"Good conduct is a seed of good, and bad conduct always gives suffering.","n":"The two halves are asymmetrical: one is a seed with a future harvest, the other suffers 'always'. Parimelazhagar shows each half implies what the other leaves out. Manakkudavar makes the seed one of release, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:139":{"book":"kural","num":139,"t":["ஒழுக்க முடையவர்க்கு ஒல்லாவே தீய","வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்"],"p":"Those who study propriety of conduct will not speak evil, even forgetfully.","r":"Speaking evil with their mouths, even by a slip, is not possible for those who have conduct.","n":"It's a strong claim: conduct has become so ingrained that the slip itself can't occur. Parimelazhagar adds speech improper to one's varna to the list of evil words, which is his addition. He also says the redundant 'with the mouth' implies that it's a mouth used to good words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:140":{"book":"kural","num":140,"t":["உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் பலகற்றும்","கல்லார் அறிவிலா தார்"],"p":"Those who know not how to act agreeably to the world, though they have learnt many things, are still ignorant.","r":"Those who haven't learned to live in accord with the world have studied much and still lack understanding.","n":"All three commentators narrow உலகம் to the noble. Yet Parimelazhagar makes an unusual concession in explaining why the verse says 'world': scripture contains things that no longer suit the present, and it omits things that do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Living with the world includes setting aside what the moral texts say that doesn't suit this age, and taking up what suits it.","அறநூல் சொல்லியவற்றுள் இக்காலத்திற்கு ஏலாதன ஒழிந்து, சொல்லாதனவற்றுள் ஏற்பன கொண்டு வருதலான்"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:141":{"book":"kural","num":141,"t":["பிறன்பொருளாள் பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை ஞாலத்து","அறம்பொருள் கண்டார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The folly of desiring her who is the property of another will not be found in those who know (the attributes of) virtue and (the rights of) property.","r":"The folly of desiring a woman who is another's possession doesn't exist in those who have seen virtue and wealth in this world.","n":"பிறன்பொருளாள், the verse's own word, treats the woman as property. Parimelazhagar explains why only two of the three aims are named: the science of pleasure has a term for this very act, so its experts can't be cited against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:142":{"book":"kural","num":142,"t":["அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம் பிறன்கடை","நின்றாரின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"Among all those who stand on the outside of virtue, there are no greater fools than those who stand outside their neighbour's door.","r":"Among all who stand outside virtue, none is a greater fool than those who stand outside another man's door.","n":"The repeated கடை sets one threshold on another: outside virtue and outside the door. Parimelazhagar's reason is telling: other sinners at least get their pleasure, whereas this man is too afraid even for that. So the charge is stupidity, and even pleasure is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:143":{"book":"kural","num":143,"t":["விளிந்தா஡஢ன் வேறல்லர் மன்ற தெளிந்தாரில்","தீமை புரிந்து ஒழுகு வார்"],"p":"Certainly they are no better than dead men who desire evil towards the wife of those who undoubtingly confide in them.","r":"Those who take to evil in the home of people who trust them are surely no different from the dead.","n":"The wrong here is defined by trust, as தெளிந்தார் shows: Parimelazhagar points out that the act is only possible because the husband trusts him. It's betrayal of a friend as much as sexual wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","natpu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:144":{"book":"kural","num":144,"t":["எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம் தினைத்துணையும்","தேரான் பிறனில் புகல்"],"p":"However great one may be, what does it avail if, without at all considering his guilt, he goes unto the wife of another ?","r":"However great they are, what does it come to, when one enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth?","n":"A millet's worth of thought would be enough to stop it. Parimelazhagar names Indra as the example of greatness lost. He also reports a reading of தேரான் பிறன் as 'the unsuspecting husband'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:145":{"book":"kural","num":145,"t":["எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான் எய்துமெஞ் ஞான்றும்","விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி"],"p":"He who thinks lightly of going into the wife of another acquires guilt that will abide with him imperishably and for ever.","r":"One who crosses into a home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies.","n":"The contrast is in timescale: 'easy' describes one moment and the blame lasts forever. Parimelazhagar makes it the family's blame, so the cost falls on others too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:146":{"book":"kural","num":146,"t":["பகைபாவம் அச்சம் பழியென நான்கும்","இகவாவாம் இல்லிறப்பான் கண்"],"p":"Hatred, sin, fear, disgrace; these four will never leave him who goes in to his neighbour's wife.","r":"Enmity, sin, fear and blame: these four never leave one who crosses into a home.","n":"The four cover different kinds of consequence: social, moral, psychological and reputational. 'Home' throughout this chapter stands for the wife, an equation that the Tamil title itself makes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:147":{"book":"kural","num":147,"t":["அறனியலான் இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் பிறனியலாள்","பெண்மை நயவா தவன்"],"p":"He who desires not the womanhood of her who should walk according to the will of another will be praised as a virtuous house-holder.","r":"The one called a householder in the way of virtue is he who doesn't desire the womanhood of the one who belongs to another's way.","n":"It's a negative definition of the householder, after chapter 5's positive ones. Parimelazhagar makes it exclusive: he alone is such a householder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:148":{"book":"kural","num":148,"t":["பிறன்மனை நோக்காத பேராண்மை சான்றோர்க்கு","அறனொன்றோ ஆன்ற வொழுக்கு"],"p":"That noble manliness which looks not at the wife of another is the virtue and dignity of the great.","r":"The great manhood that doesn't look at another's wife is, for the worthy, both virtue and complete conduct.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is a fine one: those who can subdue outer enemies can still find this inner enemy hard, so this is the greater manhood. He takes நோக்காத beyond looking, to not holding her in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:149":{"book":"kural","num":149,"t":["நலக்குரியார் யாரெனின் நாமநீர் வைப்பின்","பிறர்க்குரியாள் தோள்தோயா தார்"],"p":"Is it asked, \"who are those who shall obtain good in this world surrounded by the terror-producing sea ?\" Those who touch not the shoulder of her who belongs to another.","r":"Who deserve good on this world ringed by the awesome sea? Those who don't touch the shoulders of a woman belonging to another.","n":"The question-and-answer form and the immense sea are grand for such a plain answer. தோள் is the vocabulary of love poetry. The commentators differ on நாமநீர், as Parimelazhagar has fearful water and Manakkudavar has water that grants wishes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:150":{"book":"kural","num":150,"t":["அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும் பிறன்வரையாள்","பெண்மை நயவாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though a man perform no virtuous deeds and commit (every) vice, it will be well if he desire not the womanhood of her who is within the limit (of the house) of another.","r":"Even if one doesn't practise virtue and does what is wrong, it's good not to desire the womanhood of one within another's bounds.","n":"The verse sets a minimum for someone who has failed at everything else. Parimelazhagar says this quality alone stands out, and Manakkudavar says that it brings good even with no other virtue. It doesn't say the person is thereby virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:151":{"book":"kural","num":151,"t":["அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போலத் தம்மை","இகழ்வார்ப் பொறுத்தல் தலை"],"p":"To bear with those who revile us, just as the earth bears up those who dig it, is the first of virtues.","r":"Like the earth that holds up those who dig into it, bearing with those who scorn you is the highest thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss brings out the image: the earth keeps the digger from falling, so the one injured is still supporting the one who injures. Forbearance here is more than not responding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:152":{"book":"kural","num":152,"t":["பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை","மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று"],"p":"Bear with reproach even when you can retaliate; but to forget it will be still better than that.","r":"Always bear with excess against you; forgetting it is better still.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains why forgetting ranks higher: to forbear, one must still hold the wrong in mind. He adds that one should forbear even when one has the power to punish, which is what makes this more than helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:153":{"book":"kural","num":153,"t":["இன்நம்யுள் இன்மை விருந்தொரால் வன்மையுள்","வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை"],"p":"To neglect hospitality is poverty of poverty. To bear with the ignorant is might of might.","r":"The poverty of poverties is turning away a guest, and the strength of strengths is forbearance toward fools.","n":"The verse is an analogy, as Parimelazhagar notes: just as turning a guest away isn't wealth, bearing with fools isn't weakness. The first half is there to change what we think the second half means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","virundhu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விருந்து means a guest, and turning one away is the worst poverty.","வறுமையுள் வைத்து வறுமையாவது விருந்தினரை ஏற்றுக் கொள்ளாது நீக்குதல்;"],["manakkudavar","விருந்து means novelty: the weakest weakness is failing to reject the strange and unheard-of.","புதுமை யென்றது கேட்டறியாதது."]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:154":{"book":"kural","num":154,"t":["நிறையுடைமை நீங்காமை வேண்டின் பொநற்யுடைமை","போற்றி யொழுகப் படும்"],"p":"If you desire that greatness should never leave, you preserve in your conduct the exercise of patience.","r":"If you want நிறை never to leave you, keep and live by forbearance.","n":"Forbearance is presented as the means, and நிறை is what is at stake. Parimelazhagar takes the latter as worth of character, and Manakkudavar as a guarded discipline: keeping what should be kept and rejecting what should be rejected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:155":{"book":"kural","num":155,"t":["ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையாரே வைப்பர்","பொறுத்தாரைப் பொன்போற் பொதிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) will not at all esteem the resentful. They will esteem the patient just as the gold which they lay up with care.","r":"They don't count at all those who retaliate, but those who forbear they keep wrapped up like gold.","n":"It's a claim about social estimation, which is why the motive is reputational. Parimelazhagar gives the reason: the person who retaliates has become the wrongdoer's equal. He reads 'wrapping up' as constantly bearing them in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:156":{"book":"kural","num":156,"t":["ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம் பொறுத்தார்க்குப்","பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்"],"p":"The pleasure of the resentful continues for a day. The praise of the patient will continue until (the final destruction of) the world.","r":"Those who retaliate have one day's joy, and those who forbear have renown until they perish.","n":"The verse honestly concedes that revenge is a pleasure. Parimelazhagar plays this down with 'false joy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The word 'world' is supplied, so the renown lasts until the world itself ends.","பொறுத்தார்க்கு உலகம் அழியுமளவும் புகழ் உண்டாம்."],["manakkudavar","The renown lasts as long as the forbearing person lives.","பொறுத்த வர்க்குத் தாம் சாமளவும் புகழுண்டாம்,"]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:157":{"book":"kural","num":157,"t":["திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும் நோநொந்து","அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though others inflict injuries on you, yet compassionating the evil (that will come upon them) it will be well not to do them anything contrary to virtue.","r":"Though others do you wrong, it's good to feel the pain of it and not do what is contrary to virtue.","n":"நோநொந்து, grieving at the pain, doesn't say whose pain. On the first reading below, the wronged person pities the wrongdoer, which is a remarkable demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","aram","arul"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One grieves for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoer, who may fall into hell.","அவர்க்கு அதனால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு நொந்து,"],["kalaignar","One feels hurt by the base act done to oneself, and still does not take revenge.",""]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:158":{"book":"kural","num":158,"t":["மிகுதியான் மிக்கவை செய்தாரைத் தாந்தம்","தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்"],"p":"Let a man by patience overcome those who through pride commit excesses.","r":"Those who do wrong out of their excess, defeat them by your own rightness.","n":"The language is of winning. Manakkudavar says it most directly: forbearing isn't defeat, it is victory. மிகுதி and தகுதி, excess and fitness, are set against each other as rival kinds of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:159":{"book":"kural","num":159,"t":["துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை உடையர் இறந்தார்வாய்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Those who bear with the uncourteous speech of the insolent are as pure as the ascetics.","r":"Those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds are as pure as those who have renounced.","n":"Parimelazhagar spells out the implication: this is someone still in household life. The purity of renunciation can therefore be reached without renouncing. Here இறந்தார் means those who overstep, not the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thuravu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:160":{"book":"kural","num":160,"t":["உண்ணாது நோற்பார் பெரியர் பிறர்சொல்லும்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பாரின் பின்"],"p":"Those who endure abstinence from food are great, next to those who endure the uncourteous speech of others.","r":"Those who fast are great, but only after those who bear what others say in harshness.","n":"The verse first grants greatness and then takes the top rank away. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is subtle: bearing insult is done while one's sense of 'I' and 'mine' is still in place, the very things that make it hard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thavam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:161":{"book":"kural","num":161,"t":["ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க ஒருவன்தன் நெஞ்சத்து","அழுக்காறு இலாத இயல்பு"],"p":"Let a man esteem that disposition which is free from envy in the same manner as propriety of conduct.","r":"Let one take as one's rule of conduct a nature whose heart has no envy.","n":"ஒழுக்காறு and அழுக்காறு differ by one sound, so the sound of the verse sets conduct and envy against each other. Coming right after chapter 14, it makes an inner state into a rule of conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","ozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:162":{"book":"kural","num":162,"t":["விழுப்பேற்றின் அஃதொப்பது இல்லையார் மாட்டும்","அழுக்காற்றின் அன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"Amongst all attainable excellences there is none equal to that of being free from envy towords others.","r":"Among choice gains nothing equals it, if one can be free of envy toward anyone at all.","n":"யார்மாட்டும், toward anyone at all, is what makes it hard. Parimelazhagar says this includes one's enemies. It's a further 'nothing greater' claim to be read along with those for virtue, restraint and children.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:163":{"book":"kural","num":163,"t":["அறன்ஆக்கம் வேண்டாதான் என்பான் பிறனாக்கம்","பேணாது அழுக்கறுப் பான்"],"p":"Of him who instead of rejoicing in the wealth of others, envies it, it will be said \"he neither desires virtue not wealth.\"","r":"He who doesn't care for another's prosperity and envies it is called one who wants neither virtue nor gain.","n":"The verse argues that envy works against what the envier wants. பேணாது goes beyond 'not envying': Parimelazhagar glosses it as failing to help, so what's required is positive support for another's prosperity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:164":{"book":"kural","num":164,"t":["அழுக்காற்றின் அல்லவை செய்யார் இழுக்காற்றின்","ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) knowing the misery that comes from transgression will not through envy commit unrighteous deeds.","r":"They do no wrong out of envy, because they know the harm that arises from a wrong path.","n":"The second line nearly repeats verse 136, and here too the motive is prudence. Parimelazhagar's list of wrongs from envy covers thought, speech and action against those with wealth or learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:165":{"book":"kural","num":165,"t":["அழுக்காறு உடையார்க்கு அதுசாலும் ஒன்னார்","வழுக்காயும் கேடீன் பது"],"p":"To those who cherish envy that is enough. Though free from enemies that (envy) will bring destruction.","r":"For the envious that's enough: even without enemies, it brings about their ruin.","n":"The verse is sardonic, and அதுசாலும், 'that is enough', sounds almost like a verdict. The envious person's enemy is within, and no outside enemy is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:166":{"book":"kural","num":166,"t":["கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான் சுற்றம் உடுப்பதூஉம்","உண்பதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"He who is envious at a gift (made to another) will with his relations utterly perish destitute of food and rainment.","r":"The kin of one who envies what is given will perish, with nothing to wear or eat.","n":"The cost falls on those near him. Parimelazhagar says his own ruin needn't be stated. Manakkudavar makes the fault active, that he stops another's giving, whereas Parimelazhagar makes it the feeling itself. Either way, the verse punishes innocents, and it doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:167":{"book":"kural","num":167,"t":["அவ்வித்து அழுக்காறு உடையானைச் செய்யவள்","தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"Lakshmi envying (the prosperity) of the envious man will depart and introduce him to her sister.","r":"The fair goddess, grudging the envious man, will show him to her elder sister.","n":"The verse describes the goddess's act with the word for envy's own attitude, அவ்வித்து, so the envier gets what he gives. The elder sister is misfortune. Kalaignar distances himself by saying this is how people describe it. Parimelazhagar also records a different reading of the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddess too can't bear it, and leaves after showing him to her elder sister.","திருமகள் தானும் பொறாது, தன் தவ்வைக்கு காட்டி நீங்கும்."]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:168":{"book":"kural","num":168,"t":["அழுக்காறு எனஒரு பாவி திருச்செற்றுத்","தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Envy will destroy (a man's) wealth (in his world) and drive him into the pit of fire (in the world to come.)","r":"The one evil called envy destroys good fortune and drives one to the bad place.","n":"பாவி personifies a quality as a villain. Parimelazhagar acknowledges it's a figure of speech: a quality isn't a person, but this one is so cruel to its maker that it's called one. Kalaignar has 'evil path' where the older commentators have hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:169":{"book":"kural","num":169,"t":["அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் செவ்வியான்","கேடும் நினைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"The wealth of a man of envious mind and the poverty of the righteous will be pondered.","r":"The prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are matters to be pondered.","n":"This verse admits what the next one denies. It doesn't state a resolution, only that it must be thought about. Parimelazhagar cites Silappatikaram 15.91-93 as an example of someone pondering this in the story of Kovalan's suffering, a direct thread between the two works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These results can't come from crookedness or uprightness, so the wise ask what past deed caused them.","'இதற்கு ஏது ஆகிய பழவினை யாது?' என்று ஆராயப்படுதலின்,"],["kalaignar","This is simply a matter for astonishment, with no appeal to past deeds.",""]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:170":{"book":"kural","num":170,"t":["அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை அஃதுஇல்லார்","பெருக்கத்தில் தீர்ந்தாரும் இல்"],"p":"Never have the envious become great; never have those who are free from envy been without greatness.","r":"None have grown great through envy, and none free of it have lost out on increase.","n":"It's an absolute claim, with 'none' twice. Parimelazhagar takes this as the moral cause of gain and ruin, immediately after his past-deeds reading of 169, without explaining how the two fit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:171":{"book":"kural","num":171,"t":["நடுவின்றி நன்பொருள் வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றிக்","குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்"],"p":"If a man departing from equity covet the property (of others), at that very time will his family be destroyed and guilt be incurred.","r":"If one covets another's good things and leaves aside fairness, the family perishes, and faults come at once.","n":"The condition is only வெஃகின், if one covets, and ஆங்கே, right then, makes the penalty immediate. Parimelazhagar gives நடுவு a specific meaning: the recognition that what belongs to others isn't ours to take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:172":{"book":"kural","num":172,"t":["படுபயன் வெஃகிப் பழிப்படுவ செய்யார்","நடுவன்மை நாணு பவர்"],"p":"Those who blush at the want of equity will not commit disgraceful acts through desire of the profit that may be gained.","r":"Those ashamed of unfairness won't do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had.","n":"நாணு, shame, is the operative word and it means this person is restrained from inside. Parimelazhagar glosses it with fear, which is a less interior motive than the verse's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:173":{"book":"kural","num":173,"t":["சிற்றின்பம் வெஃகி அறனல்ல செய்யாரே","மற்றின்பம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire the higher pleasures (of heaven) will not act unjustly through desire of the trifling joy. (in this life.)","r":"Those who want the other joy won't do what's contrary to virtue out of desire for a small joy.","n":"சிற்றின்பம் here refers to pleasure from taking others' goods, which isn't its common later meaning of sexual pleasure. The verse leaves 'the other joy' unnamed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","inbam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other joy is the lasting joy that comes from virtue.","அறத்தான் வரும் நிலையுடைய இன்பத்தைக் காதலிப்பவர்."],["manakkudavar","The other joy is the great joy of release.","பேரின்பமாகிய வீடுபேற்றைக் காமிப்பவர்,"],["kalaignar","It's a lasting benefit that one wants in place of an immediate one.",""]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:174":{"book":"kural","num":174,"t":["இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார் புலம்வென்ற","புன்மையில் காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who have conquered their senses and are free from crime, will not covet (the things of others), with the thought \"we are destitute.\"","r":"Those of faultless sight, who have conquered the senses, won't covet at the thought 'we have nothing'.","n":"Need is the hardest case, and the verse rules out that excuse. Parimelazhagar goes a step further and says that such people don't count as poor at all, which removes the case instead of answering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:175":{"book":"kural","num":175,"t":["அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவென்னாம் யார்மாட்டும்","வெஃகி வெறிய செயின்"],"p":"What is the advantage of extensive and accurate knowledge if a man through covetousness act senselessly towards all ?","r":"What is the use of fine and wide understanding if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone?","n":"The sound of அஃகி, to be fine, is played against வெஃகி, to covet, and a learned mind is shown undone by the same sound. Parimelazhagar repeats a principle from 140: the purpose of understanding is what one refrains from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:176":{"book":"kural","num":176,"t":["அருள்வெஃகி ஆற்றின்கண் நின்றான் பொருள்வெஃகிப்","பொல்லாத சூழக் கெடும்"],"p":"If he, who through desire of the virtue of kindness abides in the domestic state i.e., the path in which it may be obtained, covet (the property of others) and think of evil methods (to obtain it), he will perish.","r":"One standing on the path out of desire for grace will perish if, desiring wealth, he plots wrongs.","n":"வெஃகி is used twice: he desires grace and he desires wealth, in the one verb. The other notable word is சூழ, to plot: Parimelazhagar notes that it says the plotting alone destroys him, let alone the doing.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arul","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is household life, called a path because one can't renounce until understanding matures in it.","இல்லற நெறியில் அறிவு முதிர்ந்துழி அல்லது துறக்கப் படாமையின், அதனைத் துறவறத்திற்கு 'ஆறு' என்றார்."],["manakkudavar","The path is simply the way of virtue, and even one with grace can fall.","அருளை விரும்பி யறனெறியிலே நின்றவனும்,"]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:177":{"book":"kural","num":177,"t":["வேண்டற்க வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம் விளைவயின்","மாண்டற் கரிதாம் பயன்"],"p":"Desire not the gain of covetousness. In the enjoyment of its fruits there is no glory.","r":"Don't seek gain that comes by coveting: when it ripens, its fruit is hard to make good.","n":"The word விளைவயின் brings in a harvest image: the wrong shows only when the crop comes in, so the problem is one of delayed consequences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:178":{"book":"kural","num":178,"t":["அஃகாமை செல்வத்திற்கு யாதெனின் வெஃகாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கைப் பொருள்"],"p":"If it is weighed, \"what is the indestructibility of wealth,\" it is freedom from covetousness.","r":"What keeps wealth from shrinking? Not coveting what's in another's hand, which that person wants.","n":"The chapter's sound play, அஃகாமை and வெஃகாமை, is itself the answer. It offers a security for wealth that the wealthy might not expect: leaving others' goods alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:179":{"book":"kural","num":179,"t":["அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும்","திறன்அறிந் தாங்கே திரு"],"p":"Lakshmi, knowing the manner (in which she may approach) will immediately come to those wise men who, knowing that it is virtue, covet not the property of others.","r":"Fortune, knowing the way to come, approaches those who understand virtue and do not covet.","n":"Here the goddess has to work out the right way to approach, as if she were the supplicant. Parimelazhagar's gloss explains the 'way' as time, place and the right moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:180":{"book":"kural","num":180,"t":["இறலீனும் எண்ணாது வெஃகின் விறல்ஈனும்","வேண்டாமை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"To covet (the wealth of another) regardless of consequences will bring destruction. That greatness (of mind) which covets not will give victory.","r":"Coveting without thought yields destruction, and the pride called not-wanting yields victory.","n":"செருக்கு normally means arrogance, and here it's a virtue. Parimelazhagar explains why it's a victory: it puts beneath it all those who scramble after wealth. Not wanting is a form of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","perumai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:181":{"book":"kural","num":181,"t":["அறங்கூறான் அல்ல செயினும் ஒருவன்","புறங்கூறான் என்றல் இனிது"],"p":"Though one do not even speak of virtue and live in sin, it will be well if it be said of him \"he does not backbite.\"","r":"Though one doesn't speak of virtue and does wrong, it's pleasing if it is said, 'he doesn't backbite'.","n":"The merit here is what's said about a person, which fits the chapter: a backbiter is judged by talk, and this man is praised by it. Virtue need not even be spoken of, which sets a very low floor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:182":{"book":"kural","num":182,"t":["அறனழீஇ அல்லவை செய்தலின் தீதே","புறனழீஇப் பொய்த்து நகை"],"p":"To smile deceitfully (in another's presence) after having reviled him to his destruction (behind his back) is a greater evil than the commission of (every other) sin and the destruction of (every) virtue.","r":"Worse than denying virtue and doing wrong is destroying someone behind their back and then smiling falsely.","n":"The ranking is what's striking. Parimelazhagar takes அறனழீஇ as openly saying there's no such thing as virtue, so the moral denier is rated above the smiling gossip. Honest wickedness is treated as less bad than a hidden betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:183":{"book":"kural","num":183,"t":["புறங்கூறிப் பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்","அறங்கூற்றும் ஆக்கத் தரும்"],"p":"Death rather than life will confer upon the deceitful backbiter the profit which (the treatises on) virtue point out.","r":"Dying brings more of the gain that virtue speaks of than living on by backbiting and falsehood.","n":"Death is compared favourably here, and the terms are moral accounting. Parimelazhagar's reason is that death at least stops the backbiting. Manakkudavar pictures a person who refuses to gossip and dies poor, which suggests that gossip may be a livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:184":{"book":"kural","num":184,"t":["கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும் சொல்லற்க","முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்"],"p":"Though you speak without kindness before another's face speak not in his absence words which regard not the evil subsequently resulting from it.","r":"Though you speak harshly to a person's face, do not speak words that have no regard for what follows, when they are not there.","n":"The verse is a concession, as open harshness is the lesser evil, and it goes against chapter 10's standards. Manakkudavar takes care to add that this isn't permission for harsh speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","inchol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The forbidden word disregards the harm that will come later, in the person's absence.","அவன் எதிரின்றிப்பின் வரும் குற்றத்தை நோக்காத சொல்லைச் சொல்லாதொழிக."],["manakkudavar","The forbidden word is one that will leave you unable to look the person in the eye later.","பிற்காலத்து அவன் முன்னே நின்று எதிர்முகம் நோக்க வொண் ணாத சொல்லைச் சொல்லா தொழிக,"]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:185":{"book":"kural","num":185,"t":["அறஞ்சொல்லும் நெஞ்சத்தான் அன்மை புறஞ்சொல்லும்","புன்மையாற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"The emptiness of that man's mind who (merely) praises virtue will be seen from the meanness of reviling another behind his back.","r":"That someone's heart doesn't really speak virtue will be seen from the meanness of their backbiting.","n":"It's a test: talk about virtue is checked against talk about others. Parimelazhagar makes it specific: a backbiter can praise virtue, but because his mind is bad, what he says shouldn't be accepted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","manam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:186":{"book":"kural","num":186,"t":["பிறன்பழி கூறுவான் தன்பழி யுள்ளும்","திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப் படும்"],"p":"The character of the faults of that man who publishes abroad the faults of others will be sought out and published.","r":"One who talks of another's faults will have his own faults picked out and talked about.","n":"The passive voice doesn't say who will do the talking. Parimelazhagar makes it the injured party, who picks out from the speaker's many faults the ones that would hurt most, and says them to his face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:187":{"book":"kural","num":187,"t":["பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர் நகச்சொல்லி","நட்பாடல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those who know not to live in friendship with amusing conversation will by back-biting estrange even their relatives.","r":"Those who don't know how to make friends through words that delight will divide even their own people with words that divide.","n":"பகச்சொல்லி and நகச்சொல்லி are one sound apart, like divide and delight. The verse gives a diagnosis, that gossip is a social failure: those who can't please must separate. Parimelazhagar says even kin are affected, and strangers all the more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu","inchol","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:188":{"book":"kural","num":188,"t":["துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும் மரபினார்","என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு"],"p":"What will those not do to strangers whose nature leads them to publish abroad the faults of their intimate friends ?","r":"Those whose way is to spread abroad even the faults of those closest to them, what would they do to strangers?","n":"மரபினார் suggests a settled habit or even a family tradition, not a lapse. Parimelazhagar reports a variant reading, 'what sort of people are they?'","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:189":{"book":"kural","num":189,"t":["அறன்நோக்கி ஆற்றுங்கொல் வையம் புறன்நோக்கிப்","புன்சொல் உரைப்பான் பொறை"],"p":"The world through charity supports the weight of those who reproach others observing their absence.","r":"Is it out of regard for virtue that the earth bears the weight of one who watches for a person's back and speaks mean words?","n":"The earth, the model of forbearance, finds this one hard. Parimelazhagar imagines the earth telling itself that bearing this is its duty. Manakkudavar adds darkly that otherwise it would shake him off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","porai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:190":{"book":"kural","num":190,"t":["ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","தீதுண்டோ மன்னும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"If they observed their own faults as they observe the faults of others, would any evil happen to men ?","r":"If people could see their own faults as they see strangers' faults, what harm would come to any living being?","n":"The whole chapter resolves into a problem of seeing. காண்கிற்பின் means 'if they were able', and Parimelazhagar notes that this impartial comparison is rare.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seeing the fault ends it, so there's no sin, and thus no suffering in the births to come.","கண்டவழி ஒழிதலின் பாவம் இன்றாம், ஆகவே வரும் பிறவிகளினும் துன்பம் இல்லை என்பது நோக்கி,"],["kalaignar","The habit of backbiting goes and one's life becomes peaceful.",""]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:191":{"book":"kural","num":191,"t":["பல்லார் முனியப் பயனில சொல்லுவான்","எல்லாரும் எள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"He who to the disgust of many speaks useless things will be despised by all.","r":"A person who talks emptily to the irritation of many ends up despised by all.","n":"The verse says only 'many'. Parimelazhagar narrows them to the discerning, which turns the move from 'many' to 'all' into a causal chain: once the wise are repelled, everyone else follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:192":{"book":"kural","num":192,"t":["பயனில பல்லார்முன் சொல்லல் நயனில","நட்டார்கண் செய்தலிற் றீது"],"p":"To speak useless things in the presence of many is a greater evil than to do unkind things towards friends.","r":"Saying useless things before many is worse than doing what is unwelcome to one's friends.","n":"The ranking is startling: a word to an audience outweighs a deed against a friend. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that the talk brings more disgrace, so his measure is the speaker's standing and not the harm done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:193":{"book":"kural","num":193,"t":["நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும் பயனில","பாரித் துரைக்கும் உரை"],"p":"That conversation in which a man utters forth useless things will say of him \"he is without virtue.\"","r":"Expansive talk about useless things itself declares that the speaker is one without நயன்.","n":"No one needs to accuse the talker, because the speech testifies against him. நயன் shifts through this chapter: Parimelazhagar takes it as liking in 192 and as justice here, so the word should not be given a single translation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:194":{"book":"kural","num":194,"t":["நயன்சாரா நன்மையின் நீக்கும் பயன்சாராப்","பண்பில்சொல் பல்லா ரகத்து"],"p":"The words devoid of profit or pleasure which a man speaks will, being inconsistent with virtue, remove him from goodness.","r":"Graceless words that serve no purpose, spoken among many, lack நயன் and take a person away from what is good.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words fall short of justice toward the listeners, and cost the speaker his good qualities.","அவை அவர்மாட்டு நீதியோடு படாவாய், அவனை நற்குணங்களின் நீக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is even-handedness, and it is the speaker who loses that balance and falls from goodness.","அவன் நடு சாராது நன்மையி னீங்கும்"],["kalaignar","நயன் is the gladness of the gathering, and such words destroy it along with good.",""]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:195":{"book":"kural","num":195,"t":["சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும் பயனில","நீர்மை யுடையார் சொலின்"],"p":"If the good speak vain words their eminence and excellence will leave them.","r":"If those of gentle nature speak useless things, their worth departs and the regard it earned goes with it.","n":"The verse aims at the good, not the habitual babbler of 196. Standing already earned is not secure against this one lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:196":{"book":"kural","num":196,"t":["பயனில் சொல் பராட்டு வானை மகன்எனல்","மக்கட் பதடி யெனல்"],"p":"Call not him a man who parades forth his empty words. Call him the chaff of men.","r":"Don't call a person who keeps up useless talk a man; call him chaff among people.","n":"For Parimelazhagar the image means a husk without the kernel of understanding, so the talk reveals emptiness and doesn't merely offend.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பாராட்டுவான் is one who says such things repeatedly: the fault is the habit.","பயன் இல்லாத சொற்களைப் பலகாலுஞ் சொல்லுவானை"],["manakkudavar","He is one who prizes and celebrates useless speech: the fault is the relish.","பயனில்லாத சொல்லைக் கொண்டாடுவானை"],["kalaignar","He is one who hopes to profit from empty talk: the fault is the motive.",""]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:197":{"book":"kural","num":197,"t":["நயனில சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக சான்றோர்","பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let the wise if they will, speak things without excellence; it will be well for them not to speak useless things.","r":"Let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must; it is good that they not say what lacks use.","n":"The concession is rhetorical, as Parimelazhagar observes: 'even if they say it' implies they should not. Its force is the comparison, which keeps the sting whichever sense of நயன் is taken.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is justice, so even speech at odds with justice is ranked less bad than useless speech.","நயன் இலவற்றினும் பயன் இல தீய என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","நயன் is pleasantness, so the worthy may say what is unsweet, which is a much milder claim.",""]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:198":{"book":"kural","num":198,"t":["அரும்பயன் ஆயும் அறிவினார் சொல்லார்","பெரும்பயன் இல்லாத சொல்"],"p":"The wise who seek after rare pleasures will not speak words that have not much weight in them.","r":"Those whose understanding searches out rare ends do not say what lacks great use.","n":"The bar rises here from useless to not greatly useful, and Parimelazhagar draws this out: words of small use are excluded too. His identification of the rare ends with release and higher destinies is his own; Manakkudavar says only 'rare matters'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rare ends are release and passage to higher worlds.","அறிதற்கு அரிய பயன்களாவன: வீடு பேறும் மேற்கதிச் செலவும் முதலாயின."]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:199":{"book":"kural","num":199,"t":["பொருள்தீர்ந்த பொச்சாந்துஞ் சொல்லார் மருள்தீர்ந்த","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those wise men who are without faults and are freed from ignorance will not even forgetfully speak things that profit not.","r":"Those of stainless sight, freed from delusion, do not say meaningless things even by a lapse.","n":"பொச்சாந்தும் moves the claim from choice to nature: for such people the restraint survives inattention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:200":{"book":"kural","num":200,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லிற் பயனுடைய சொல்லற்க","சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்"],"p":"Speak what is useful, and speak not useless words.","r":"Say what among words has use; do not say what among words has none.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that சொல்லில் is grammatically surplus and kept for the figure of repeated words. It may be a quiet wit that a chapter against waste in speech ends on a deliberate surplus, though that is my reading and no commentator's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல்லில் means 'among words': the verse sorts words into two kinds.","சொற்களில் பயன் உடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"],["manakkudavar","The first சொல்லில் means 'if you speak', which leaves silence as an option.","சொல்லுவனாயின் பயனுடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:201":{"book":"kural","num":201,"t":["தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்","தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have experience of evil deeds will not fear, but the excellent will fear the pride of sin.","r":"Those given to evil deeds don't fear the intoxication called evil deed; the excellent do.","n":"Fear is the sign of moral standing here, not a weakness. Parimelazhagar adds an insight about habit: long practice has removed the fear in one group, and never having done such things keeps it alive in the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is delusion: the evil deed is called by the name of the confusion it brings on.","தீவினை என்று சொல்லப்படும் மயக்கத்தை"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is the elation of wrongdoing.","தீவினை யாகிய களிப்பை"],["kalaignar","செருக்கு is a pleasure the deed may yield, and the wise fear the deed even so.",""]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:202":{"book":"kural","num":202,"t":["தீயவை தீய பயத்தலால் தீயவை","தீயினும் அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"Because evil produces evil, therefore should evil be feared more than fire.","r":"Since evil deeds yield evil, they are to be feared more than fire.","n":"The verse's reason is only that evil begets evil, sounded in the play of தீயவை on தீ. Parimelazhagar supplies a further reason, that fire does not burn across time, place and bodies, which brings in rebirth that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fire can't burn in another time, another place or another body, and an evil deed can.","பிறிதொரு காலத்தும், பிறிதொரு தேயத்தும், பிறிதோர் உடம்பினும் சென்று சுடுதல் தீக்கு இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:203":{"book":"kural","num":203,"t":["அறிவினுள் எல்லாந் தலையென்ப தீய","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்"],"p":"To do no evil to enemies will be called the chief of all virtues.","r":"They say the chief of all understanding is to leave off doing harm even to those who hate you.","n":"The word is அறிவு, not virtue as Pope's gloss has it. Forbearance appears here as the height of intelligence, and Parimelazhagar makes the logic prudential: refraining keeps suffering from coming back to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is the chief understanding because one infers that refraining, even when able to strike, keeps suffering away.","செய்யத் தக்குழியுஞ் செய்யாது ஒழியவே தமக்குத் துன்பம் வாராது என உய்த்துணர்தலின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:204":{"book":"kural","num":204,"t":["மறந்தும் பிறன்கேடு சூழற்க சூழின்","அறஞ்சூழம் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு"],"p":"Even though forgetfulness meditate not the ruin of another. Virtue will meditate the ruin of him who thus meditates.","r":"Don't plot another's ruin even by a lapse; if you do, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter.","n":"The same verb, சூழ், serves both parties, so the plotting already sets the reply in motion and harm need never be carried out. Parimelazhagar draws the point: even thinking of it is forbidden.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறம் is a deity of virtue, and its 'plotting' is resolving to leave the man to his ruin.","அறக்கடவுள் எண்ணுதலாவது, அவன் கெடத் தான் நீங்க நினைத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","அறம் actively returns harm for harm, and gets in before the intended victim can respond.","தீமை செய்தார்க்குத் தீமை பயக்கும் அறந்தானே சூழும்"],["kalaignar","சூழும் means surrounds: moral order closes in on him like a siege.",""]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:205":{"book":"kural","num":205,"t":["இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க செய்யின்","இலனாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"Commit not evil, saying, \"I am poor\": if you do, you will become poorer still.","r":"Don't do evil saying 'I have nothing'; if you do, you'll have nothing all over again.","n":"The verse answers the strongest excuse for wrongdoing on its own terms: it doesn't defend the poor man's honour but says the plan won't work. Parimelazhagar also reports other glossators who take the second poverty as poverty of good qualities.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The poverty returns across lives, birth after birth.","அத் தீவினையால் பிறவிதோறும் இலன் ஆம் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The deed simply fails to produce wealth, with no appeal to rebirth.","பின்பும் நல்குரவினனாவன்; அது செல் வம் பயவாது"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:206":{"book":"kural","num":206,"t":["தீப்பால தான்பிறர்கண் செய்யற்க நோய்ப்பால","தன்னை அடல்வேண்டா தான்"],"p":"Let him not do evil to others who desires not that sorrows should pursue him.","r":"One who doesn't want afflicting things to overpower him should not do evil things to others.","n":"The balance of தீப்பால and நோய்ப்பால gives the rule its shape: what one portions out as evil returns as a portion of pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:207":{"book":"kural","num":207,"t":["எனைப்பகை யுற்றாரும் உய்வர் வினைப்பகை","வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்"],"p":"However great be the enmity men have incurred they may still live. The enmity of sin will incessantly pursue and kill.","r":"However great the enmity they've incurred, people survive it; the enmity of deeds doesn't die and follows them to kill.","n":"The deed is made an enemy of a special kind, one that can't be outrun because it travels with the doer. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss it as entering wherever one enters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:208":{"book":"kural","num":208,"t":["தீயவை செய்தார் கெடுதல் நிழல்தன்னை","வீயாது அஇஉறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will dwell at the heels of those who commit evil even as their shadow that leaves them not.","r":"The ruin of those who do evil is like a person's shadow, which never perishes and stays at the foot.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a shadow that stretches far but comes back to rest at the foot, so a consequence can seem distant and still be right there. He expressly rejects another reading, in which the shadow stands for a deed latent until its time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is the shadow's return to the foot, not a deed lying hidden until its season.","ஒருவன் நிழல் நொடிதாகப் போயும், அவன்றன்னை விடாது வந்து அடியின்கண் தங்கி தன்மைத்து."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:209":{"book":"kural","num":209,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காதல னாயின் எனைத்தொன்றும்","துன்னற்க தீவினைப் பால்"],"p":"If a man love himself, let him not commit any sin however small.","r":"If a person loves himself, let him not go near the portion of evil deeds, however small.","n":"The appeal rests on self-love, using காதலன், a word of real attachment. The verse doesn't ask a person to overcome self-regard: it asks him to think it through, and on Parimelazhagar's reading harm to others is harm to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:210":{"book":"kural","num":210,"t":["அருங்கேடன் என்பது அறிக மருங்கோடித்","தீவினை செய்யான் எனின்"],"p":"Know ye that he is freed from destruction who commits no evil, going to neither side of the right path.","r":"Know that one who doesn't stray aside and do evil is a person for whom ruin is rare.","n":"அருங்கேடன் on its face says ruin is 'rare' or 'hard'. Parimelazhagar argues that அருமை here means absence, which turns a strong probability into a guarantee. Kalaignar also says no ruin at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருமை means absence, so the man has no ruin at all.","அருமை:இன்மை."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:211":{"book":"kural","num":211,"t":["கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு மாரிமாட்டு","என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு"],"p":"Benevolence seeks not a return. What does the world give back to the clouds ?","r":"Obligation seeks no return: what does the world do for the rain in exchange?","n":"The word chosen is கடப்பாடு, what is owed, and not gift. Parimelazhagar points out that the name itself implies it can't be dropped. Giving with no return is therefore framed as debt, a nicely paradoxical idea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:212":{"book":"kural","num":212,"t":["தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருளெல்லாம் தக்கார்க்கு","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"All the wealth acquired with perseverance by the worthy is for the exercise of benevolence.","r":"All the wealth got through exertion exists for doing வேளாண்மை, with தக்கார் standing in some relation to it.","n":"The grammar of தக்கார்க்கு decides whether this sets a limit on recipients or describes the owner, so I have left it open in the reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தக்கார் are the owners: for those who are worthy, all wealth is for benevolence, not for private consumption or hoarding.","தக்கார்க்கு தகுதி உடையார்க்கு ஆயின்;"],["manakkudavar","தக்கார் are the recipients: the wealth is for helping those who deserve it.","ஒருவன் முயன்று ஈட்டிய பொருளெல்லாம் தகுதியுடையார்க்கு உபகரித்தற்காகவாம்"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:213":{"book":"kural","num":213,"t":["புத்தே ளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும் பெறலரிதே","ஒப்புரவின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"It is difficult to obtain another good equal to benevolence either in this world or in that of the gods.","r":"Whether in the world of புத்தேள் or here, it is hard to find any other thing as good as ஒப்புரவு.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a striking reason for the gods' world: all there are alike, with no giver and no receiver, so this good can't exist there. On that view the human condition of need is what makes the highest good possible.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புத்தேள் உலகு is the world of the gods.","தேவர் உலகத்தும் இவ்வுலகத்தும்"],["kalaignar","It is a new world yet to come, which removes the gods from the verse.",""]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:214":{"book":"kural","num":214,"t":["ஒத்த தறவோன் உயிர்வாழ்வான் மற்றையான்","செத்தாருள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He truly lives who knows (and discharges) the proper duties (of benevolence). He who knows the m not will be reckoned among the dead.","r":"One who knows what is fitting truly lives; anyone else is counted among the dead.","n":"Knowing here includes doing: Parimelazhagar glosses it as knowing and acting, because the dead neither know nor act.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is fitting is the way of the world as a whole, and lapses in it can't be repaired as lapses in Vedic practice can.","உலகநடை வழு வேதநடை வழுப்போலத் தீர்திறன் உடைத்து அன்று"],["manakkudavar","What is fitting is specifically ஒப்புரவு, and those who lack it are like corpses.","ஒப்புரவறியாதார் பிணத்தோ டொப்ப ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:215":{"book":"kural","num":215,"t":["ஊருணி நீர்நிறைந் தற்றே உலகவாம்","பேரறி வாளன் திரு"],"p":"The wealth of that man of eminent knowledge who desires to exercise the benevolence approved of by the world, is like the full waters of a city-tank.","r":"The wealth of a greatly wise person connected with the world's desiring is like water filling the village's drinking tank.","n":"The image puts the wealth in a common place, and the owner disappears from the picture: people simply draw from it. This verse calls such a person பேரறிவாளன, wise, not merely generous.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This person desires and practises the world's way.","உலகநடையை விரும்பிச் செய்யும் பெரிய அறிவினை யுடையவனது செல்வம்"],["manakkudavar","This person is the one desired by all the world.","உலகத்தா ரெல்லாராலும் நச்சப்படுகின்ற"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:216":{"book":"kural","num":216,"t":["பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","நயனுடை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"The wealth of a man (possessed of the virtue) of benevolence is like the ripening of a fruitful tree in the midst of a town.","r":"Wealth falling to one who has நயன் is like a fruit-giving tree ripening in the heart of the town.","n":"Parimelazhagar says that நயன் here is ஒப்புரவு as the chief of worldly justice, Manakkudavar makes it being loved by others, and Kalaignar a tender heart. The tree's public position matters as much as its fruit, since no one has to go looking for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:217":{"book":"kural","num":217,"t":["மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","பெருந்தகை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"If wealth be in the possession of a man who has the great excellence (of benevolence), it is like a tree which as a medicine is an infallible cure for disease.","r":"Wealth falling to one of great nobility is like a tree that is medicine and never fails.","n":"This goes further than the fruit tree of 216. Parimelazhagar has every part of the tree serving as cure, with no regard for its own lack, which suggests giving that costs the giver something. He and Manakkudavar agree that தப்பா includes never being hidden from those who seek it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:218":{"book":"kural","num":218,"t":["இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்","கடனறி காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who know what is duty will not scant their benevolence even when they are without wealth.","r":"Even at a time without means, those whose sight knows what is due don't falter in ஒப்புரவு.","n":"The verse cuts ஒப்புரவு free from wealth. If it were a by-product of surplus it would lapse with the surplus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:219":{"book":"kural","num":219,"t":["நயனுடையான் நல்கூர்ந்தா னாதல் செயும்நீர","செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு"],"p":"The poverty of a benevolent man, is nothing but his inability to exercise the same.","r":"Poverty, for one with நயன், is just the condition of being unable to rest while what should be done goes undone.","n":"Poverty is redefined from the inside. Parimelazhagar makes the contrast explicit: it isn't going without one's own enjoyments. The deprivation is of the power to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","nayan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:220":{"book":"kural","num":220,"t":["ஒப்புரவி னால்வரும் கேடெனின் அஃதொருவன்","விற்றுக்கோள் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"If it be said that loss will result from benevolence, such loss is worth being procured even by the sale of one's self.","r":"If ruin is said to come through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth acquiring even by selling oneself.","n":"The verse deals with an objection: 'if it is said'. For Parimelazhagar nothing is normally worth selling oneself for, so this is meant to startle, and the reason he gives is the fame such loss yields.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Selling oneself is meant literally as the limit case: the loss is worth buying even at that price.","அக்கேடு தன்னை விற்றாயினும் கொள்ளும் தகுதியை உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","It is a commercial image, selling one thing to obtain another, so the 'loss' is really a trade for later benefit.","ஒன்றை விற்று ஒன்றைக் கொள்ளுகின்ற வாணிகமாகக் கொள்ளுந் தகுதி புடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:221":{"book":"kural","num":221,"t":["வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகைமற் றெல்லாம்","குறியெதிர்ப்பை நீர துடைத்து"],"p":"To give to the destitute is true charity. All other gifts have the nature of (what is done for) a measured return.","r":"Giving something to those who have nothing is what giving is; all the rest has the nature of a measured loan.","n":"குறியெதிர்ப்பு is a technical word: taking a stated measure and returning that same measure, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. All gifts among equals are thus reclassified, however generous they feel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:222":{"book":"kural","num":222,"t":["நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல்தீது மேலுலகம்","இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று"],"p":"To beg is evil, even though it were said that it is a good path (to heaven). To give is good, even though it were said that those who do so cannot obtain heaven.","r":"Even if it were called a good path, taking is bad; even if there were no higher world, giving alone is good.","n":"The verse cuts the value of giving loose from its reward by a hypothetical. How strong that hypothetical is, is exactly what the commentators disagree about.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both 'even ifs' are purely hypothetical: no one actually says such things, and heaven isn't in doubt.","'எனினும்' என்பது இரு வழியும் அங்ஙனம் கூறுவார் இன்மை விளக்கி நின்றது."],["kalaignar","Giving won't obtain any higher world, and the giving life is best regardless.",""]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:223":{"book":"kural","num":223,"t":["இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை ஈதல்","குலனுடையான் கண்ணே யுள"],"p":"(Even in a low state) not to adopt the mean expedient of saying \"I have nothing,\" but to give, is the characteristic of the mad of noble birth.","r":"Not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, are found only in one of good family.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists several rival interpretations, including giving before the asker has to speak, and giving so that he need not go on to ask anyone else.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are two virtues: not begging oneself, and not refusing those who beg.","யான் வறியன் என்று இரப்பான் சொல்லும் இளிவரவைத் தான் பிறர்கண் சொல்லாமையும்"],["manakkudavar","It is one act: giving without telling the suppliant 'I have nothing'.","இரந்து வந்தார்க்கு இல னென்னாகின்ற துன்பத்தைக் கூறாது ஈதலும்"],["kalaignar","It means giving without showing the distress of one's own poverty.",""]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:224":{"book":"kural","num":224,"t":["இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல் இரந்தவர்","இன்முகங் காணும் அளவு"],"p":"To see men begging from us in disagreeable, until we see their pleasant countenance.","r":"Being begged from is itself painful, until one sees the glad face of the one who begged.","n":"A candid observation: being asked is uncomfortable even for the generous. Parimelazhagar attributes this to fear of not being able to give all that is sought.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இரக்கப்படுதல் is being the one asked, and resolving to give.","இரக்கப்படுதல்-'இரப்பார்க்கு ஈவல்' என்று இருத்தல்."],["kalaignar","It is feeling pity (இரக்கம்) for the supplicant, a different root.",""]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:225":{"book":"kural","num":225,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பசிஆற்றல் அப்பசியை","மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்"],"p":"The power of those who perform penance is the power of enduring hunger. It is inferior to the power of those who remove the hunger (of others).","r":"The strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that ranks behind the strength of those who remove hunger.","n":"The verse says only ஆற்றுவார், those with strength. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both understand ascetics, which makes this a direct ranking of the giver over the renunciant. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic hungers and can't relieve others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:226":{"book":"kural","num":226,"t":["அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல் அஃதொருவன்","பெற்றான் பொருள்வைப் புழி"],"p":"The removal of the killing hunger of the poor is the place for one to lay up his wealth.","r":"Removing the ruinous hunger of the destitute is where a person who has come into wealth should store it.","n":"அழிபசி is hunger that destroys: Parimelazhagar says all goods, Manakkudavar says a person's qualities. The financial image is made pointed by Parimelazhagar: the wealth comes back to help the giver. He supplies 'for virtue's sake' himself, so the verse is plainer about the bank than he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:227":{"book":"kural","num":227,"t":["பாத்தூண் மரீஇ யவனைப் பசியென்னும்","தீப்பிணி தீண்டல் அரிது"],"p":"The fiery disease of hunger shall never touch him who habitually distributes his food to others.","r":"The evil disease called hunger is unlikely to touch one who is habituated to sharing his food.","n":"Manakkudavar reads this as a reply to the miser's fear that giving will lead to hunger. Understood that way, the verse is a piece of argument rather than a promise. மரீஇ makes the claim about habit, not about any one gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers the fear that giving will reduce wealth and bring on hunger.","பகுத்துண்ணப் பசி வாராதென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:228":{"book":"kural","num":228,"t":["ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம் அறியார்கொல் தாமுடைமை","வைத்திழக்கும் வன்க ணவர்"],"p":"Do the hard-eyed who lay up and lose their possessions not know the happiness which springs from the pleasure of giving ?","r":"Do those hard-eyed people who hoard what they have and then lose it not know the joy of giving and gladdening?","n":"The appeal is to pleasure, not to duty: the hoarder has simply missed out. 'Keep and lose' in one word, வைத்திழக்கும், makes the loss seem part of keeping. Parimelazhagar's own wording suggests the joy belongs to those with அருள், so this reaches forward to chapter 25.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:229":{"book":"kural","num":229,"t":["இரத்தலின் இன்னாது மன்ற நிரப்பிய","தாமே தமியர் உணல்"],"p":"Solitary and unshared eating for the sake of filling up one's own riches is certainly much more unpleasant than begging.","r":"Eating alone to fill up one's own store is surely more bitter than begging.","n":"Parimelazhagar's comparison: begging has only a moment's humiliation and no lasting poverty, while the solitary eater has both. The verse takes the worst thing most people can think of and names something worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நிரப்பிய refers to filling a fixed hoard target, so the fault is accumulation.","ஒரோ எண்களைக்குறித்து அத்துணை ஈட்டுதும் என ஈட்டத்தையே மேற்கொண்டு இவறக் கூட்டுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It refers to the food one has obtained, and தமியர் to eating out of everyone's sight.","தேடின வுணவைத தாமே தமியராயிருந் துண்டல்"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:230":{"book":"kural","num":230,"t":["சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை இனிததூஉம்","ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை"],"p":"Nothing is more unpleasant than death: yet even that is pleasant where charity cannot be exercised.","r":"Nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach.","n":"The chapter's last ranking sets giving above life. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it frees one from a bodily burden no use to anyone. This is hyperbole about value, not advice to die, and Kalaignar softens it to a comparison of pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:231":{"book":"kural","num":231,"t":["ஈதல் இசைபட வாழ்தல் அதுவல்லது","ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Give to the poor and live with praise. There is no greater profit to man than that.","r":"Give, and live so that renown arises: there is no other profit for a living being.","n":"ஊதியம is a word for profit, so the verse puts its ethics in the language of a balance sheet. Parimelazhagar notes that learning and valour can also win fame, and that giving is named as the highest route to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:232":{"book":"kural","num":232,"t":["உரைப்பார் உரைப்பவை எல்லாம் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று","ஈவார்மேல் நிற்கும் புகழ்"],"p":"Whatsoever is spoken in the world will abide as praise upon that man who gives alms to the poor.","r":"All that speakers have to say amounts to praise resting on those who give something to those who ask.","n":"Parimelazhagar extends speech to poets' song, and he dismisses a reading that lets others have praise too ('say what you will, fame stays with the giver'), because it misses fame's excellence. The verse's sweep ('all that speakers say') is a large claim, even as hyperbole.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:233":{"book":"kural","num":233,"t":["ஒன்றா உலகத்து உயர்ந்த புகழல்லால்","பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன் றில்"],"p":"There is nothing that stands forth in the world imperishable, except fame, exalted in solitary greatness.","r":"Apart from towering, unmatched renown, there is nothing in the world that stands undying.","n":"The verse doesn't say what makes fame unmatched. Parimelazhagar specifies gifts that are hardest to give, of life, limb and property, which raises the bar far above ordinary repute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:234":{"book":"kural","num":234,"t":["நிலவரை நீள்புகழ் ஆற்றின் புலவரைப்","போற்றாது புத்தேள் உலகு"],"p":"If one has acquired extensive fame within the limits of this earth, the world of the Gods will no longer praise those sages who have attained that world.","r":"If someone achieves far-reaching renown within the earth's bounds, the world of the gods doesn't honour the புலவர்.","n":"The verse is elliptical: it doesn't say whom heaven honours in their place, and everything hinges on the identity of புலவர். I'm not confident any reading here is the verse's own. Parimelazhagar cites Purananuru 27 for the idea that the famous gain both worlds.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புலவர் are sages who have reached that world, and it prefers the famous to them.","புத்தேள் உலகம் அவனையல்லது தன்னை எய்தி நின்ற ஞானிகளைப் பேணாது."],["manakkudavar","புலவர் are the gods themselves, so named because they have wisdom.","புலவரென்றார் தேவரை; அவர் புலனுடையாராதலான்."],["kalaignar","புலவர் are the learned and the world is a future one: it will not honour intellect alone over the selfless and famous.",""]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:235":{"book":"kural","num":235,"t":["நத்தம்போல் கேடும் உளதாகும் சாக்காடும்","வித்தகர்க் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Prosperity to the body of fame, resulting in poverty to the body of flesh and the stability to the former arising from the death of the latter, are achievable only by the wise.","r":"A ruin that is really growth and a death that is really existence are hard to come by except for the skilled.","n":"வித்தகர் praises skill, not goodness: for Parimelazhagar, the art of turning what doesn't last into what does. The reading depends on his derivation of நத்தம் from நந்து, growth, and on taking போல் as a filler, so its foundation is a philological argument.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a doctrine of two bodies: the elemental body grows poor and dies while the body of fame grows rich and stands.","ஆக்கமாகும் கேடாவது, புகழ் உடம்பு செல்வம் எய்தப் பூத உடம்பு நல்கூர்தல்."],["kalaignar","It speaks of strength of character that grows fame amid hardship and establishes it even in dying, with no second body.",""]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:236":{"book":"kural","num":236,"t":["தோன்றின் புகழொடு தோன்றுக அஃதிலார்","தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று"],"p":"If you are born (in this world), be born with qualities conductive to fame. From those who are destitute of them it will be better not to be born.","r":"If you appear, appear with renown; for those without it, not appearing is better than appearing.","n":"Taken as birth, a command about how to be born can't be obeyed. Each commentator has to resolve that oddity, and how they do it shapes their positions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means being born human, and for those lacking the qualities that lead to fame, an animal birth is better, since no one reviles animals.","அக்குணம் இல்லாதார் மக்களாய்ப் பிறத்தலின் விலங்காய்ப் பிறத்தல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","It means plainly that not being born is better.","அஃதிலார் பிறக்குமதிற் பிறவாமை நன்று"],["kalaignar","தோன்று means entering a field of activity: excel there, or stay out of it.",""]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:237":{"book":"kural","num":237,"t":["புகழ்பட வாழாதார் தந்நோவார் தம்மை","இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்?"],"p":"Why do those who cannot live with praise, grieve those who despise them, instead of grieving themselves for their own inability.","r":"Those who cannot live with renown: why do they resent the people who scorn them, instead of resenting themselves?","n":"The scorn is accepted as a fair verdict. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that a good name was within reach, so the failure is one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:238":{"book":"kural","num":238,"t":["வசையென்ப வையத்தார்க் கெல்லாம் இசையென்னும்","எச்சம் பெறாஅ விடின்"],"p":"Not to beget fame will be esteemed a disgrace by the wise in this world.","r":"They say it is a disgrace for everyone in the world not to obtain the legacy called renown.","n":"எச்சம் is what remains after one dies, and it also means offspring, which Pope's 'childless' seizes on. Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar make a severe point: the lack of fame needs no other fault to justify disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:239":{"book":"kural","num":239,"t":["வசையிலா வண்பயன் குன்றும் இசையிலா","யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்"],"p":"The ground which supports a body without fame will diminish in its rich produce.","r":"Land that bears a body without renown will decline in its blameless rich yield.","n":"யாக்கை, 'body', is pointed: for Parimelazhagar, what lacks renown is merely a body, a load the land must carry. Moral failure has an effect on the soil here, which Kalaignar weakens to how the land is regarded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:240":{"book":"kural","num":240,"t":["வசையொழிய வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் இசையொழிய","வாழ்வாரே வாழா தவர்"],"p":"Those live who live without disgrace. Those who live without fame live not.","r":"Those who live free of blame are the ones who live; those who live free of renown are the ones who don't.","n":"The balanced form leaves no neutral ground, and Parimelazhagar states it outright: to lack blame is to have renown and to lack renown is to have blame. A quiet, harmless, obscure life has no place in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:241":{"book":"kural","num":241,"t":["அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம் பொருட்செல்வம்","பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள"],"p":"The wealth of kindness is wealth of wealth, in as much as the wealth of property is possessed by the basest of men.","r":"The wealth of அருள் is the wealth among wealths, because the wealth of goods is found even among the base.","n":"The argument is about distribution: what the worst can have can't be the best thing. It doesn't say goods are bad, and 247 will concede how much depends on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:242":{"book":"kural","num":242,"t":["நல்லாற்றாள் நாடி அருளாள்க பல்லாற்றால்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"(Stand) in the good path, consider, and be kind. Even considering according to the conflicting tenets of the different sects, kindness will be your best aid, (in the acquisition of heavenly bliss.)","r":"Examine by sound means and hold to அருள்; however many ways you investigate, that alone is the companion.","n":"Compassion here is what inquiry concludes, not an alternative to it. Parimelazhagar reads பல்லாற்றால் as the competing religious systems, which disagree on means of knowledge and objects but converge here. The verse itself mentions no sects, so this ecumenical reading is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Mutually opposed schools agree on this one point.","அன்னவாயினும், அருள் துணை என்றற்கண் ஒக்கும் என்பதாம்."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:243":{"book":"kural","num":243,"t":["அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கில்லை இருள்சேர்ந்த","இன்னா உலகம் புகல்"],"p":"They will never enter the world of darkness and wretchedness whose minds are the abode of kindness.","r":"Those whose hearts are joined with அருள் will not enter the painful world joined with darkness.","n":"அருள் and இருள் rhyme across the verse, and the same word, சேர்ந்த, joins each to its noun.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal hell, a place in the lower world.","தன்கண்ணே புக்கார்க்குத் துன்பம் செய்வதோர் நரகத்தை"],["kalaignar","The darkness is ignorance, and the suffering is of this life.",""]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:244":{"book":"kural","num":244,"t":["மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வார்க் கு இல்லென்ப","தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை"],"p":"(The wise) say that the evils, which his soul would dread, will never come upon the man who exercises kindness and protects the life (of other creatures)","r":"They say that one who sustains living beings and holds to அருள் has no deeds his own life need fear.","n":"Care for other lives removes the fear for one's own. Parimelazhagar takes மன் as making souls eternal, a doctrine not required by the word, and gives a causal link: such a person won't kill, and hence won't go to hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:245":{"book":"kural","num":245,"t":["அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை வளிவழங்கும்","மல்லன்மா ஞாலங் கரி"],"p":"This great rich earth over which the wind blows, is a witness that sorrow never comes upon the kind-hearted.","r":"Those who hold to அருள் have no distress, and the wide rich earth where the wind moves is the witness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's witness is negative: no one has ever seen such a person suffer, so the whole world can testify. Kalaignar instead makes the wind an analogy for அருள் moving the mind. The claim of no distress is bold, and should be read with the reservation of 169.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:246":{"book":"kural","num":246,"t":["பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார் என்பர் அருள்நீங்கி","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"(The wise) say that those who neglect kindness and practise cruelties, neglected virtue (in their former birth), and forgot (the sorrows which they must suffer.)","r":"Those who abandon அருள் and carry on doing what should not be done, they say, have parted from பொருள் and forgotten.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொருள் is virtue: they failed to practise it before and have forgotten the suffering they are in as a result.","முன்னும் உறுதிப் பொருளைச் செய்யாது தாம் துன்புறுகின்றமையை மறந்தவர்"],["manakkudavar","பொருள் is wealth, and the verse spans lives: lacking அருள் in a former birth, they are now both poor and forgetful.","இப்பிறப்பின்கண் பொருளினின்று நீங்கி மறவியு முடையவ"],["kalaignar","With no former birth in view, the cruel become poor and forget their duty.",""]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:247":{"book":"kural","num":247,"t":["அருளில்லார்க்கு அவ்வுலகம் இல்லை பொருளில்லார்க்கு","இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு"],"p":"As this world is not for those who are without wealth, so that world is not for those who are without kindness.","r":"As this world is not for those without wealth, that world is not for those without அருள்.","n":"The comparison gets its force from its blunt premise about money, which the verse states as obvious fact. A chapter that opened by belittling goods grants them complete authority over this world.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","That world is release.","உயிர்கள்மேல் அருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு வீட்டுலகத்து இன்பம் இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","That world is heaven.","இஃது அருளில்லாதார் சுவர்க்கம் புகாரென்றது."],["kalaignar","It isn't another world at all: wealth is to the household life as compassion is to the renouncer's life.",""]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:248":{"book":"kural","num":248,"t":["பொருளற்றார் பூப்பர் ஒருகால் அருளற்றார்","அற்றார்மற் றாதல் அரிது"],"p":"Those who are without wealth may, at some future time, become prosperous; those who are destitute of kindness are utterly destitute; for them there is no change.","r":"Those without wealth may flower one day; those without அருள் are finished, and it is hard for them to become anything again.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees this as withdrawing the parallel just drawn in 247, since the two lacks aren't alike after all. His addition of 'through fate' to the poor man's state is not in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:249":{"book":"kural","num":249,"t":["தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்றால் தேரின்","அருளாதான் செய்யும் அறம்"],"p":"If you consider, the virtue of him who is without kindness is like the perception of the true being by him who is without wisdom.","r":"Virtue done by one without அருள், if examined, is like the muddled person seeing the true reality.","n":"Parimelazhagar concludes that அருள் is the root of all other virtues. That goes beyond the comparison the verse draws, but it is a natural development.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Such virtue can occur, as a passing glimpse can, but the person's own lack destroys it.","அருளாதான் இடையே அறஞ்செய்தால் அதனைத் தன் அருளாமையால் தானே அழித்துவிடும்"],["manakkudavar","Such people can't practise virtue at all.","இஃது அறஞ்செய்யவும் மாட்டாரென்றது,"]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:250":{"book":"kural","num":250,"t":["வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க தான் தன்னின்","மெலியார்மேல் செல்லு மிடத்து"],"p":"When a man is about to rush upon those who are weaker than himself, let him remember how he has stood (trembling) before those who are stronger than himself.","r":"When you go against those weaker than yourself, think of yourself before those stronger.","n":"The chapter's only instruction on how compassion arises doesn't appeal to a principle: it asks you to remember your own fear. Parimelazhagar calls this the means by which அருள் is born, and extends the weaker to animals too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:251":{"book":"kural","num":251,"t":["தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்குத் தான்பிறிது ஊனுண்பான்","எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்?"],"p":"How can he be possessed of kindness, who to increase his own flesh, eats the flesh of other creatures.","r":"How can one who eats another's flesh to swell his own hold to அருள்?","n":"ஊன் appears on both sides, so the eater is flesh too and the act is flesh feeding flesh. Parimelazhagar adds that fattening the body is itself useless, so the cruelty buys nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:252":{"book":"kural","num":252,"t":["பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை அருளாட்சி","ஆங்கில்லை ஊன்தின் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those possess no property who do not take care of it, so those possess no kindness who feed on flesh.","r":"Holding wealth isn't for those who don't guard it, and likewise holding அருள் isn't for those who eat flesh.","n":"ஆட்சி, holding and using, is the operative word: compassion is a possession that lapses through neglect. For Parimelazhagar the verse answers those who say a flesh-eater who wishes no creature harm hasn't compromised அருள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse rebuts the claim that good intention makes flesh-eating harmless to அருள்.","ஊன் தின்றாராயினும் உயிர்கட்கு ஒரு தீங்கும் நினையாதார்க்கு அருள் ஆள்தற்கு இழுக்கு இல்லை என்பாரை மறுத்து,"]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:253":{"book":"kural","num":253,"t":["படைகொண்டார் நெஞ்சம்போல் நன்னூக்காது ஒன்றன்","உடல்சுவை உண்டார் மனம்"],"p":"Like the (murderous) mind of him who carries a weapon (in his hand), the mind of him who feasts with pleasure on the body of another (creature), has no regard for goodness.","r":"The mind of one who has relished another creature's body doesn't incline to good, like the heart of one holding a weapon.","n":"The argument concerns attention rather than guilt: what the hand holds, or the tongue relishes, takes over the mind. சுவை matters here, since the verse speaks of savouring and not merely of eating.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:254":{"book":"kural","num":254,"t":["ருளல்லது யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பொருளல்லது அவ்வூன் தினல்"],"p":"If it be asked what is kindness and what its opposite, the answer would be preservation and destruction of life; and therefore it is not right to feed on the flesh (obtained by taking away life).","r":"If asked what is அருள் and what isn't, it comes down to not killing and killing; eating that flesh is not பொருள்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","arul","pulaal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருள் is not killing and its opposite is killing, and பொருள் here means virtue, so eating is sin. He also reports another division of the words.","அக்கோறலான் வந்த ஊனைத் தின்கை பாவம்."],["manakkudavar","Non-அருள் is destroying the vow of not killing, and பொருள் means gain, so eating harms அருள் and gets one nothing.","அருள் கெடுதலேயன்றி, பெறுவதொரு பயனுமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:255":{"book":"kural","num":255,"t":["உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை ஊனுண்ண","அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது அளறு"],"p":"Not to eat flesh contributes to the continuance of life; therefore if a man eat flesh, hell will not open its mouth (to let him escape out, after he has once fallen in).","r":"The standing of life lies in not eating flesh; if one eats, hell doesn't open its mouth.","n":"The image is of a mouth that has swallowed and won't open to release. Parimelazhagar offers a population argument for உயிர்நிலை: creatures that are eaten dwindle while others multiply. Kalaignar takes அளறு as a mud pit, not the hell of the older commentators.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:256":{"book":"kural","num":256,"t":["தினற்பொருட்டால் கொல்லாது உலகெனின் யாரும்","விலைப்பொருட்டால் ஊன்றருவா ரில்"],"p":"If the world does not destroy life for the purpose of eating, then no one would sell flesh for the sake of money.","r":"If the world didn't kill for the sake of eating, nobody would bring flesh for the sake of a price.","n":"This is a demand-and-supply argument. The buyer who says he killed nothing is told that his purchase causes the killing. Parimelazhagar formalizes the reply as inference by necessary implication.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers those who ask what fault there is in buying and eating without having killed.","கொன்று தின்னாது விலைக்குக் கொண்டு தின்பார்க்குக் குற்றமென்னை யென்றார்க்கு , அதனாலுங் கொலைப்பாவம் வருமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:257":{"book":"kural","num":257,"t":["உண்ணாமை வேண்டும் புலாஅல் பிறிதொன்றன்","புண்ணது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If men should come to know that flesh is nothing but the unclean ulcer of a body, let them abstain from eating it.","r":"If there were people who perceived flesh as another creature's wound, it would not be eaten.","n":"பெறின் suggests such perceivers are rare. The commentators stress impurity, yet 'another's wound' can equally appeal to fellow-feeling, as the wound belongs to someone. That second emphasis is my own reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:258":{"book":"kural","num":258,"t":["செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார் உண்ணார்","உயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த ஊன்"],"p":"The wise, who have freed themselves from mental delusion, will not eat the flesh which has been severed from an animal.","r":"Those whose vision has parted from fault don't eat flesh that has parted from life.","n":"The two lines mirror each other with தலைப்பிரிந்த: a person parted from defect faces flesh parted from life. Parimelazhagar says they see for themselves that it is a corpse, so no rule is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:259":{"book":"kural","num":259,"t":["அவிசொரிந் தாயிரம் வேட்டலின் ஒன்றன்","உயிர்செகுத் துண்ணாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to kill and eat (the flesh of) an animal, is better than the pouring forth of ghee etc., in a thousand sacrifices.","r":"Better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices is not taking one creature's life and eating.","n":"A thousand rites weigh less than one abstention. The commentators treat this as a comparison of merit, but the verse can also be read as a challenge to ritual, and that sharper reading isn't one they give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:260":{"book":"kural","num":260,"t":["கொல்லான் புலாலை மறுத்தானைக் கைகூப்பி","எல்லா உயிருந் தொழும்"],"p":"All creatures will join their hands together, and worship him who has never taken away life, nor eaten flesh.","r":"All living beings fold their hands and worship the one who doesn't kill and has refused flesh.","n":"The beings that have been spared do the worshipping, an image rather than a report. Parimelazhagar insists that the two abstentions are only fruitful together, which closes the loophole of the previous verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:261":{"book":"kural","num":261,"t":["உற்றநோய் நோன்றல் உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை","அற்றே தவத்திற் குரு"],"p":"The nature of religious discipline consists, in the endurance (by the ascetic) of the sufferings which it brings on himself, and in abstaining from giving pain to others.","r":"Bearing the pain that comes and causing no pain to living beings: such alone is the form of தவம்.","n":"The definition mentions no gods, rites or forest, and half of it is ethical. Parimelazhagar takes the pain as self-imposed through such things as reduced food, while Manakkudavar says only pain that has come to one. Kalaignar turns it into a heart that can bear anything, which would make tapas available in any life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:262":{"book":"kural","num":262,"t":["தவமும் தவமுடையார்க்கு ஆகும் அதனை","அஃதிலார் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"Austerities can only be borne, and their benefits enjoyed, by those who have practised them (in a former birth); it will be useless for those who have not done so, to attempt to practise them (now).","r":"தவம் too comes to those who have தவம்; for those without it, taking it up is futile.","n":"The form is circular, a discouraging one for a would-be beginner. The chapter's praise of effort in the following verses sits awkwardly with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One needs தவம் from an earlier birth, which gives the familiarity needed to complete it.","தவந்தானும் உண்டாவது முன்தவம் உடையார்க்கே"],["manakkudavar","It requires earlier good deeds of any kind, not earlier austerity as such.","தவஞ்செய்தலும் முன்பு நல்வினை செய்தார்க்கு வரும்"],["kalaignar","It requires present firmness and self-control, with no former birth involved.",""]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:263":{"book":"kural","num":263,"t":["துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி மறந்தார்கொல்","மற்றை யவர்கள் தவம்"],"p":"It is to provide food etc, for the ascetics who have abandoned (the desire of earthly possessions) that other persons have forgotten (to practise) austerity ?","r":"Is it in wanting to provide for those who have renounced that the others have forgotten தவம்?","n":"The chiding question implies that தவம் isn't reserved for renunciants. Both older commentators draw the conclusion that தவம் is greater than giving, but the verse only implies it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Householders, drawn by love of giving, have forgotten what is greater.","தானத்தினும் தவம் மிக்கது என்பது பெற்றாம்."]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:264":{"book":"kural","num":264,"t":["ஒன்னார்த் தெறலும் உவந்தாரை ஆக்கலும்","எண்ணின் தவத்தான் வரும்"],"p":"If (the ascetic) desire the destruction of his enemies, or the aggrandizement of his friends, it will be effected by (the power of) his austerities.","r":"Crushing foes and raising those who rejoice with one come through தவம், if one so intends.","n":"எண்ணின், 'if one thinks of it', is doing much here. Parimelazhagar takes it to imply that such people naturally don't think of it. An ascetic with enemies is a problem for him, and he solves it by moving the power to தவம் itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The foes are enemies of virtue, and the friends are those who delight in it.","அறத்திற்குப் பகையாய் அழிவு செய்தாரைக் கெடுத்தலும்"],["manakkudavar","These are ordinary enemies and friends, and the power comes from austerity done earlier.","பகைவரைத் தெறுதலும், நட்டோரையாக்குதலு மாகிய வலி ஆராயின், முன்செய்த தவத்தினாலே வரும்"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:265":{"book":"kural","num":265,"t":["வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங் கெய்தலால் செய்தவம்","ஈண்டு முயலப் படும்"],"p":"Religious dislipline is practised in this world, because it secures the attainment of whatever one may wish to enjoy (in the world to come).","r":"Because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, தவம் is striven for here.","n":"The motive offered is getting what you want, in a chapter that condemns desire in the very next verse. Parimelazhagar infers from ஈண்டு, 'here', that the results come in the next life and include release, but the verse doesn't say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:266":{"book":"kural","num":266,"t":["தவஞ் செய்வார் தங்கருமஞ் செய்வார்மற் றல்லார்","அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு"],"p":"Those discharge their duty who perform austerities; all others accomplish their own destruction, through the entanglement of the desire (of riches and sensual pleasure).","r":"Those who do தவம் are doing their own work; the rest, caught in desire, do what is futile.","n":"தங்கருமம் is telling: austerity is presented as true self-interest, and worldly activity as neglect of one's real business. Parimelazhagar adds 'having renounced' and identifies 'the rest' as those pursuing wealth and pleasure, which sweeps in most of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:267":{"book":"kural","num":267,"t":["சுடச்சுடரும் பொன்போல் ஒளிவிடும் துன்பஞ்","சுடச்சுட நோற்கிற் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Just as gold is purified as heated in the fire, will those shine, who have endured the burning of pain (in frequent austerities).","r":"For those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine, as gold shines the more it is burned.","n":"The doubled சுடச்சுட appears in both lines and makes the image of repeated firing audible. What the shining stands for is left open: for Parimelazhagar sin leaves and knowledge grows, for Manakkudavar deeds leave, and for Kalaignar it is fame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:268":{"book":"kural","num":268,"t":["தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை ஏனைய","மன்னுயி ரெல்லாந் தொழும்"],"p":"All other creatures will worship him who has attained the control of his own soul.","r":"All other living beings worship the one who has completely gained his own self.","n":"The disagreement is nearly a reversal: for one reading the person comes to own the self, and for the other he is rid of it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He has obtained his own self as truly his own, and அற intensifies this: nothing is harder to obtain.","தன் உயிரைத் தான் தனக்கு உரித்தாகப் பெற்றவனை"],["manakkudavar","அற marks cessation: he has got rid of the thought of 'I', and தான் is the ego.","தானறு தலாவது அகங்கார மறு தல்."],["kalaignar","He has neither attachment to 'my life' nor the pride of 'I'.",""]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:269":{"book":"kural","num":269,"t":["கூற்றம் குதித்தலும் கைகூடும் நோற்றலின்","ஆற்றல் தலைப்பட் டவர்க்குல்"],"p":"Those who have attained the power which religious discipline confers, will be able also to pass the limit of Yama, (the God of death).","r":"Even leaping past death becomes possible for those who have attained the power of austerity.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the 'even' can signal how nearly impossible it is. Manakkudavar illustrates with Markandeya, which is his example and isn't in the verse. Kalaignar takes it as conquering death through steadfastness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:270":{"book":"kural","num":270,"t":["இலர்பல ராகிய காரணம் நோற்பார்","சிலர்பலர் நோலா தவர்"],"p":"Because there are few who practise austerity and many who do not, there are many destitute and few rich in this world.","r":"The reason the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who don't are many.","n":"The verse says just 'those who lack', without saying what they lack. As an explanation of poverty it is arresting and severe, and the commentators each soften or redirect it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth and poverty here include having and lacking understanding.","செல்வம் நல்குரவு என்பன ஈண்டு அறிவினது உண்மை இன்மைகளையும் குறித்து நின்றன;"],["manakkudavar","It is about those without goods, plainly.","பொருளில்லாதார் உலகத்துப் பலராதற்குக் காரணம், தவஞ் செய்வார் சிலராதல்;"],["kalaignar","It is about those lacking capability, and the few are simply those with firm resolve.",""]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:271":{"book":"kural","num":271,"t":["வஞ்ச மனத்தான் படிற்றொழுக்கம் பூதங்கள்","ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்"],"p":"The five elements (of his body) will laugh within him at the feigned conduct of the deceitful minded man.","r":"The five elements laugh inwardly at the concealed conduct of one with a deceitful mind.","n":"A hypocrite can't escape witnesses, because his own body is one of them. Parimelazhagar identifies the hidden conduct as sexual, and he carries this through 273 and 274, but the verse doesn't specify.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elements themselves are the cosmic witnesses, mingled in his body and laughing without his knowing.","உடம்பாய் அவனோடு கலந்து நிற்கின்ற பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் கண்டு தம்முள்ளே நகும்."],["manakkudavar","'Elements' stands for the senses, their products, which know what he does.","பூதங்களென்றது அவையிற்றின் காரியமாகிய பொறிகளை ."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:272":{"book":"kural","num":272,"t":["வானுயர் தோற்றம் எவன்செய்யும் தன்னெஞ்சம்","தான்அறி குற்றப் படின்"],"p":"What avails an appearance (of sanctity) high as heaven, if his mind suffers (the indulgence) of conscious sin.","r":"What will an appearance as high as the sky do, if one's own heart sinks into a fault one knows to be a fault?","n":"தான்அறி, 'that one knows', is the crux. Parimelazhagar's point is that a fault done unknowingly can be washed away, but one done knowingly can't, so the outward habit only frightens others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:273":{"book":"kural","num":273,"t":["வலியில் நிலைமையான் வல்லுருவம் பெற்றம்","புலியின்தோல் போர்த்துமேய்ந் தற்று"],"p":"The assumed appearance of power, by a man who has no power (to restrain his senses and perform austerity), is like a cow feeding on grass covered with a tiger's skin.","r":"A person without strength taking on the form of the strong is like a cow grazing wrapped in a tiger's skin.","n":"Parimelazhagar presses the image hard. The guards don't chase the 'tiger' because tigers don't eat grass, and because they are afraid, and the cow leaves its own grass for another's crop. So the renouncer leaves his wife and pursues other men's. The strength lacking is mastery of one's own mind; the rest is his extrapolation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:274":{"book":"kural","num":274,"t":["தவமறைந்து அல்லவை செய்தல் புதல்மறைந்து","வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த் தற்று"],"p":"He who hides himself under the mask of an ascetic and commits sins, like a sportsman who conceals himself in the thicket to catch birds.","r":"Hiding in austerity and doing what isn't austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush and snaring birds.","n":"With 273 this gives two opposed animals: the cow is harmless inside a predator's skin, and the hunter is a predator behind harmless leaves. The hypocrite is both, weak before his desire and dangerous to others. That pairing is my own observation. Manakkudavar gives Arjuna as an example.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:275":{"book":"kural","num":275,"t":["பற்றற்றேம் என்பார் படிற்றொழுக்கம் எற்றெற்றென்று","ஏதம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"The false conduct of those who say they have renounced all desire will one day bring them sorrows that will make them cry out, \"Oh! what have we done, what have we done.\"","r":"The hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many evils, with cries of 'what, what!'","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', quietly marks the claim as words only, and Parimelazhagar says that the attachment was only cut in speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cry belongs to the hypocrites, who later lament what they have done.","பின் 'என் செய்தோம்! என் செய்தோம்! என்று தாமே இரங்கும்வகை,"],["manakkudavar","The cry belongs to everyone, who exclaim at such a person, and எற்று is a regional word.","எல்லாரும் எற்றெற்றென்று சொல்லும்படி பலகுற்றமும் உண்டாக்கும்,"]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:276":{"book":"kural","num":276,"t":["நெஞ்சின் துறவார் துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"Amongst living men there are none so hard-hearted as those who without to saking (desire) in their heart, falsely take the appearance of those who have forsaken (it).","r":"None are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced in heart, live by deceiving as though they had renounced.","n":"Why is deceit the height of cruelty? Parimelazhagar has a remarkable answer: the victims are donors, whose gifts to the unworthy earn them a low birth, so the fraud returns harm for help. He supports this from the Civakacintamani, not from the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They harm those who feed them, since gifts to the unrestrained bring donors a low birth.","தமக்கு ஆவன செய்தார்க்கு ஆகாதன விளைத்தலின் 'வன்கணார் இல்' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:277":{"book":"kural","num":277,"t":["புறங்குன்றி கண்டனைய ரேனும் அகங்குன்றி","முக்கிற் கரியார் உடைத்து"],"p":"(The world) contains persons whose outside appears (as fair) as the (red) berry of the Abrus, but whose inside is as black as the nose of that berry.","r":"The world holds those who, though looking outwardly like the red kunri seed, are inwardly black as its tip.","n":"The kunri is a closely observed choice, since its red and black belong to a single small seed. உடைத்து, 'it holds', says only that such people exist. Manakkudavar takes this as a warning to the wise not to judge ascetics by form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:278":{"book":"kural","num":278,"t":["மனத்தது மாசாக மாண்டார் நீராடி","மறைந்தொழுகு மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"There are many men of masked conduct, who perform their ablutions, and (make a show) of greatness, while their mind is defiled (with guilt).","r":"Many people, with impurity in their minds, bathe as though distinguished and go about concealed.","n":"பலர், 'many', comes right after the 'some' of 277 and is a social observation. Water cleans what isn't dirty and leaves what is. Parimelazhagar specifies the impurity as desire, anger and delusion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They literally bathe, to appear distinguished by austerity, and hide behind this.","பிறர்க்குத் தவத்தான் மாட்சிமையுடையராய் நீரின் மூழ்கிக் காட்டி;"],["manakkudavar","நீராடி isn't bathing: they put on the நீர்மை, the nature, of the distinguished.","மாட்சிமைப் பட்டாரது நீர்மையைப் பூண்டு,"],["kalaignar","Water is an analogy: they hide under the name of greatness as a diver hides under water.",""]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:279":{"book":"kural","num":279,"t":["கணைகொடிது யாழ்கோடு செவ்விதுஆங் கன்ன","வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்"],"p":"As, in its use, the arrow is crooked, and the curved lute is straight, so by their deeds, (and not by their appearance) let (the uprightness or crookedness of) men be estimated.","r":"The arrow is cruel and the curved lute is straight, so know people by the nature of their deeds.","n":"The verse makes shape deceive in both directions: the straight kills and the curved delights. It does more than warn that some who look good are bad, since it also suggests that some who look wrong are good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:280":{"book":"kural","num":280,"t":["மழித்தலும் நீட்டலும் வேண்டா உலகம்","பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்"],"p":"There is no need of a shaven crown, nor of tangled hair, if a man abstain from those deeds which the wise have condemned.","r":"Neither shaving nor long hair is needed, if one gives up what the world condemns.","n":"The test of renunciation is set by what the ordinary world condemns, and not by a special ascetic code. Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the high-minded, but Manakkudavar leaves it as people generally. The verse removes the outward marks, not renunciation itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:281":{"book":"kural","num":281,"t":["எள்ளாமை வேண்டுவான் என்பான் எனைத்தொன்றும்","கள்ளாமை காக்கதன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Let him, who desires not to be despised, keep his mind from (the desire of) defrauding another of the smallest thing.","r":"One who is called a person wanting not to be scorned should guard his own heart against stealing anything at all.","n":"The verse guards the heart, not the hand, and so the offence is relocated. Parimelazhagar uses எள்ளாமை for a long attack on Lokayata materialism, and this polemic is entirely his.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means not scorning release: the person wants liberation and is respected by ascetics for that.","வீட்டினை இகழாது விரும்புவான் இவன் என்று தவத்தோரான் நன்கு மதிக்கப்படுவான்;"],["manakkudavar","It means not being scorned by other people, a matter of ordinary repute.","பிறரா லிகழப்படாமையை வேண்டுவா னிவனென்று சொல் லப்படுமவன்,"]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:282":{"book":"kural","num":282,"t":["உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும் தீதே பிறன்பொருளைக்","கள்ளத்தால் கள்வேம் எனல்"],"p":"Even the thought (of sin) is sin; think not then of crafiily stealing the property of another.","r":"Even thinking it in the mind is wrong, saying 'we will steal another's goods by stealth'.","n":"Parimelazhagar limits this high standard to renunciants, whose minds he says are special, but the verse names no such group. The distinction matters, as a universal thought-crime is a stronger claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:283":{"book":"kural","num":283,"t":["களவினால் ஆகிய ஆக்கம் அளவிறந்து","ஆவது போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The property, which is acquired by fraud, will entirely perish, even while it seems to increase.","r":"Gain got by stealth will perish beyond all measure, even as it seems to grow.","n":"ஆவது போல, 'as if growing', places the ruin inside the appearance of success. Parimelazhagar gives அளவிறந்து a sharp sense: as it goes, stolen wealth leaves sin and blame behind and takes earlier virtue along with it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","selvam","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:284":{"book":"kural","num":284,"t":["களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல் விளைவின்கண்","வீயா விழுமம் தரும்"],"p":"The eager desire of defrauding others will, when it brings forth its fruit, produce undying sorrow.","r":"Love that has ripened in stealth yields, at harvest, a sorrow that doesn't die.","n":"The verb and noun suggest a crop: கன்றிய, then விளைவு. Using காதல் for the urge to steal treats theft as an attachment, and Parimelazhagar makes the point that the craving breeds repetition, which is why the sorrow doesn't end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:285":{"book":"kural","num":285,"t":["அருள்கருதி அன்புடைய ராதல் பொருள்கருதிப்","பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The study of kindness and the exercise of benevolence is not with those who watch for another's forgetfulness, though desire of his property.","r":"Having a loving nature with அருள் in view isn't found in those who, with goods in view, watch for others' lapses.","n":"The rhyme of அருள்கருதி and பொருள்கருதி sets two objects of attention side by side as if a mind could hold only one. பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார் is a precise picture of the thief's patient watching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:286":{"book":"kural","num":286,"t":["அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார் களவின்கண்","கன்றிய காத லவர்"],"p":"They cannot walk steadfastly, according to rule, who eagerly desire to defraud others.","r":"Those whose love has ripened in stealth can't keep to the path of அளவு.","n":"The rhyme அளவு and களவு runs through the next several verses, so the chapter has built its argument into one letter's difference.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அளவு is reasoned inquiry measuring the soul, karma and release, which he identifies with a Jain term.","உயிர் முதலியவற்றை அளத்தலாகிய நெற்றியின்கண் நின்று அதற்கு ஏற்ப ஒழுகமாட்டார்;"],["manakkudavar","அளவு is simply rectitude.","களவின்கண்ணே மிக்க ஆசையையுடையவர் நேரின் கணின்று ஒழுகுதலைச் செய்யமாட்டார்,"],["kalaignar","அளவு is living within limits, and failing to do so leads to theft, which reverses the causal direction.",""]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:287":{"book":"kural","num":287,"t":["களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை அளவென்னும்","ஆற்றல் புரிந்தார்கண்ட இல்"],"p":"That black-knowledge which is called fraud, is not in those who desire that greatness which is called rectitude.","r":"The dark knowledge called stealth isn't found in those who have set their love on the strength called அளவு.","n":"Stealth is granted to be a kind of intelligence, just a dark one, so cleverness alone is not a virtue in the Kural. Parimelazhagar says stealth and renunciation are opposed as dark and light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:288":{"book":"kural","num":288,"t":["அளவறாந்தார் நெஞ்சத் தறம்போல நிற்கும்","களவறிந்தார் நெஞ்சில் கரவு"],"p":"Deceit dwells in the mind of those who are conversant with fraud, even as virtue in the mind s of those who are conversant with rectitude.","r":"Deceit stands in the heart of those who know stealth, just as virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு.","n":"The comparison gives vice the same stability as virtue. Manakkudavar draws the discouraging conclusion that once learned, stealth can't be given up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:289":{"book":"kural","num":289,"t":["அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர் களவல்ல","மற்றைய தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those, who are acquainted with nothing but fraud, will perish in the very commission of transgression.","r":"Those who know nothing but stealth perish right there, having done what is beyond measure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruin is immediate and inward: they are lost the moment they think such thoughts, and 'other things' are the renunciant's permitted food and lay gifts.","அவ்வளவல்லாத தீய நினைவுகளை நினைத்த பொழுதே கெடுவர்;"],["manakkudavar","The ruin is civil: kings execute thieves.","இது கள் வாரை அரசர் கொல்வரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:290":{"book":"kural","num":290,"t":["கள்வார்க்குத் தள்ளும் உயிர்நிலை கள்வார்க்குத்","தள்ளாது புத்தே ளுளகு"],"p":"Even their body will fail the fraudulent; but even the world of the gods will not fail those who are free from fraud.","r":"For those who steal, உயிர்நிலை falls away; for those who don't, the world of the gods doesn't fall away.","n":"The shared verb தள்ளு balances the near against the far, in Parimelazhagar's phrase: even what is closest fails the one, and even what is remote doesn't fail the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்நிலை is the body, lost when the king punishes.","களவினைப் பயில்வார்க்குத் தம்மின் வேறல்லாத உடம்பும் தவறும்;"],["manakkudavar","உயிர்நிலை is release, so thieves miss liberation just as non-thieves gain heaven.","பிறர் பொருளைக் கள்வார்க்கு உயிர்நிலையாகிய வீடு பெறுதல் தப்பும்;"],["kalaignar","It is life itself, and the gods' world becomes a world of fame.",""]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:291":{"book":"kural","num":291,"t":["வாய்மை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","தீமை இலாத சொலல்"],"p":"Truth is the speaking of such words as are free from the least degree of evil (to others).","r":"If you ask what truth is, it is speech containing no evil at all.","n":"A surprising definition, because nothing here refers to facts. Parimelazhagar spells out the consequence: stating what happened isn't the definition, and even an accurate report is falsehood if it harms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","sol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Reporting what actually occurred is excluded as the definition of truth.","இதனான் நிகழ்ந்தது கூறல் என்பது நீக்கப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:292":{"book":"kural","num":292,"t":["பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த புரைதீர்ந்த","நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்"],"p":"Even falsehood has the nature of truth, if it confer a benefit that is free from fault.","r":"Even falsehood is in the place of truth, if it yields good that is free of fault.","n":"புரைதீர்ந்த does the limiting work: this excuses no convenient lie. Parimelazhagar's example is a falsehood that saves a creature from ruin or death, and he builds 291 and 292 into a four-way scheme crossing fact with effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","sol","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truth and falsehood are classified by effect: useful non-fact is truth, and harmful or useless fact is falsehood.","'தீங்கு பயவாத நிகழ்ந்தது கூறலும், நன்மை பயக்கும் நிகழாதது கூறலும் மெய்ம்மை'"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:293":{"book":"kural","num":293,"t":["தன்நெஞ் சறிவது பொய்யற்க பொய்த்தபின்","தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்"],"p":"Let not a man knowingly tell a lie; for after he has told the lie, his mind will burn him (with the memory of his guilt).","r":"Don't lie about what your own heart knows; once you lie, your own heart will burn you.","n":"The harm returns by the same organ of knowing. Parimelazhagar has the heart as a witness that brings on the sin's result, and Manakkudavar has it as remorse on seeing what the lie did to others. Kalaignar's 'conscience' is a modern word for the same thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:294":{"book":"kural","num":294,"t":["உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யா தொழுகின் உலகத்தார்","உள்ளத்து ளெல்லாம் உளன்"],"p":"He who, in his conduct, preserves a mind free from deceit, will dwell in the minds of all men.","r":"If one conducts oneself without being false in one's heart, one lives in the hearts of all the world.","n":"உள்ளம் appears on both sides of the verse: inner truth gives a place in others' inner lives. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' to the elevated, and says it is speech that has to match the heart. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar make it not even thinking a lie.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:295":{"book":"kural","num":295,"t":["மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின் தவத்தொடு","தானஞ்செய் வாரின் தலை"],"p":"He, who speaks truth with all his heart, is superior to those who make gifts and practise austerities.","r":"If one speaks truth together with one's heart, one is above those who practise austerity along with giving.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is about levels of the person: acts of body are outward, but acts of mind and speech are inward. Note that truth is ranked here above the two virtues that chapters 23 and 27 have just exalted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:296":{"book":"kural","num":296,"t":["பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை எய்யாமை","எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்"],"p":"There is no praise like the praise of never uttering a falsehood: without causing any suffering, it will lead to every virtue.","r":"There is no renown like not lying, and it brings all virtue without எய்யாமை.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pugazh","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without bodily strain: it spares the toil that wealth-gathering and fasting demand.","மறுமைக்கு மெய் வருந்தாமல் அவனுக்கு எல்லா அறங்களையும் தானே கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","Without his knowing: virtue accrues to him unawares.","பொய்யாமையானது அவனறியாமல் தானே எல்லா அறங்களையுங் கொடுக்குமாதலான்,"],["kalaignar","It brings benefits that never leave.",""]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:297":{"book":"kural","num":297,"t":["பொய்யாமை பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற","செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"If a man has the power to abstain from falsehood, it will be well with him, even though he practise no other virtue.","r":"If one keeps up not lying, not lying, then not doing other virtues, not doing them, is good.","n":"The commentators' opposed readings depend on where the doubled words are divided. Parimelazhagar knows and rejects a reading like Manakkudavar's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truthfulness alone suffices, and it is better to skip other virtues than to attempt many and fail.","அவன் பிற அறங்களைச் செய்யாமையே செய்யாமையே நன்று."],["manakkudavar","The opposite stress: other virtues are good only if truthfulness is unfailing, and otherwise they turn bad.","பொய்யாமையைப் பொய்யாமல் செலுத்துவனாயின், பிற அறங் களைச் செய்தல் நன்றாம்; அல்லது தீதாம்,"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:298":{"book":"kural","num":298,"t":["புறள்தூய்மை நீரான் அமையும் அகந்தூய்மை","வாய்மையால் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Purity of body is produced by water and purity of mind by truthfulness.","r":"Outer purity comes about through water; inner purity is seen through truth.","n":"காணப்படும் is more ambiguous than it looks. Parimelazhagar explains that what is seen exists, so truth produces purity, whereas Manakkudavar says purity is known by truthful speech, which makes truth its evidence. Parimelazhagar also infers that renunciants need both purities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:299":{"book":"kural","num":299,"t":["எல்லா விளக்கும் விளக்கல்ல சான்றோர்க்குப்","பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு"],"p":"All lamps of nature are not lamps; the lamp of truth is the lamp of the wise.","r":"Not all lamps are lamps; for the worthy, the unfailing lamp alone is the lamp.","n":"பொய்யா, 'unfailing' and also 'unlying', makes truth a kind of light that shows things as they are.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rejected lamps are worldly lights such as sun, moon and fire, which dispel outer darkness only.","புறத்து இருள் கடியும் உலகத்தார் விளக்குகள் எல்லாம் விளக்கு ஆகா;"],["manakkudavar","விளக்கு is the radiance of a good person, and what is rejected is radiance earned by other virtues.","சான்றோர்க்கு எல்லாவறத்தினாலும் உண்டான ஒளியும் ஒளி யல்ல ;"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:300":{"book":"kural","num":300,"t":["யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை எனைத்தொன்றும்","வாய்மையின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"Amidst all that we have seen (described) as real (excellence), there is nothing so good as truthfulness.","r":"Among the things we have seen as true, there is nothing at all better than truthfulness.","n":"The first person, யாம், is a rare moment when the poet speaks as himself. Parimelazhagar turns 'what we have seen' into the scriptures revealed by the Lord, but Manakkudavar keeps it simple as what we have found to be true, and nothing in the verse mentions texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:301":{"book":"kural","num":301,"t":["செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான் அல்லிடத்துக்","காக்கின்என் காவாக்கால் என்?"],"p":"He restrains his anger who restrains it when it can injure; when it cannot injure, what does it matter whether he restrain it, or not ?","r":"One who guards his anger where it can take effect truly guards it; where it can't, what does guarding or not guarding matter?","n":"Virtue is located only where power is. Patience toward those who could crush you may be just caution, and the verse declines to count it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:302":{"book":"kural","num":302,"t":["செல்லா இடத்துச் சினந்தீது செல்லிடத்தும்","இல்அதனின் தீய பிற"],"p":"Anger is bad, even when it cannot injure; when it can injure; there is no greater evil.","r":"Anger is bad where it can't take effect; where it can, nothing else is worse.","n":"This closes the gap left by 301, which could be misread as permission to rage where it doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar sets out why: at the strong it rebounds, and at the weak it brings both blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:303":{"book":"kural","num":303,"t":["மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும் தீய","பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்"],"p":"Forget anger towards every one, as fountains of evil spring from it.","r":"Forget anger at anyone at all; evils are born from it.","n":"The verb is மறத்தல், to forget, not to suppress: the instruction asks for anger to go out of mind and not to be held down. Pope's 'trespass' moves the forgetting from the anger to the offence, which the Tamil doesn't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:304":{"book":"kural","num":304,"t":["நகையும் உவகையும் கொல்லும் சினத்தின்","பகையும் உளவோ பிற"],"p":"Is there a greater enemy than anger, which kills both laughter and joy ?","r":"Is there any other enemy than the anger that kills both the smile and the joy?","n":"Parimelazhagar assigns the smile to the face and the joy to the heart, so anger destroys both outer and inner. He adds that renunciants have no outer enemies, which is why this inner one stands alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:305":{"book":"kural","num":305,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால்","தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்"],"p":"If a man would guard himself, let him guard against anger; if he do not guard it, anger will kill him.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against anger; if you don't, it is yourself that anger kills.","n":"தன்னையே, 'yourself and no other', turns anger's target around. Parimelazhagar reads the verse in light of curses: the ascetic who spends his power cursing loses it, so 'kills' means extreme suffering. Manakkudavar takes it as a threat to life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Kills' means intense suffering: the ascetic loses his austerity by cursing and is back in the misery of births.","கொலைச்சொல் ஈண்டுத் துன்பமிகுதி உணர்த்தி நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","Anger endangers life itself.","இஃது உயிர்க்கேடு வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:306":{"book":"kural","num":306,"t":["சினமென்னும் சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி இனமென்னும்","ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்"],"p":"The fire of anger will burn up even the pleasant raft of friendship.","r":"Anger, the fire that kills what it comes near, burns even the safe raft of one's own people.","n":"The name சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி is a coinage for fire, 'killer of those it joins'. Parimelazhagar finds a contrast with ordinary fire, which burns only where it touches. It also matters that a raft is being burned by someone who needs it to survive.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இனம் means fellow renunciants who teach, the raft that crosses the sea of births to release.","முற்றத் துறந்து தவஞானங்களால் பெரியயராய்க் கேட்டார்க்கு உறுதி பயக்கும் மொழிகளை இனியவாகச் சொல்லுவாரை."],["manakkudavar","இனம் means one's friends, who bring one ashore from a sea of sorrow.","தன்னைக் கரையேற விடுகின்ற நட்டோராகிய புணையைச் சுடும்."],["kalaignar","இனம் means one's kin.",""]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:307":{"book":"kural","num":307,"t":["சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன் கேடு","நிலத்தறைந்தான் கைபிழையா தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will come upon him who ragards anger as a good thing, as surely as the hand of him who strikes the ground will not fail.","r":"The ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which can't miss.","n":"பொருள் also varies: Parimelazhagar makes it a quality that displays one's power, citing Vaisesika usage, and Manakkudavar seems to take it as wealth when he speaks of loss of goods. The image is a fine one because the ground is untouched by the blow.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is about certainty: the ruin can no more miss than a hand can miss the ground.","நிலத்தின்கண் அறைந்த அவன் கை அந்நிலத்தையுறுத்தல் தப்பாதவாறு போலத் தப்பாது."],["kalaignar","The image is about rebound: it is the striker's own hand that hurts.",""]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:308":{"book":"kural","num":308,"t":["இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்","புணரின் வெகுளாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though one commit things against you as painful (to bear) as if a bundle of fire had been thrust upon you, it will be well, to refrain, if possible, from anger.","r":"Even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames, not being angry, if you can manage it, is good.","n":"புணரின், 'if it can be managed', is a rare concession in such a chapter. Parimelazhagar notes that such treatment would anger even sages. The commandment admits its own difficulty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:309":{"book":"kural","num":309,"t":["உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும் உள்ளத்தால்","உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்"],"p":"If a man never indulges anger in his heart, he will at once obtain whatever he has thought of.","r":"All one has thought of comes together at once, if one doesn't think of anger in the heart.","n":"The root உள் runs through the whole verse: thought, heart, not thinking. It sets one kind of thinking against another. The promise is enormous, and Parimelazhagar assigns it to the ascetic, though the verse itself doesn't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:310":{"book":"kural","num":310,"t":["இறந்தார் இறந்தார் அனையர் சினத்தைத்","துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை"],"p":"Those, who give way to excessive anger, are no better than dead men; but those, who are freed from it, are equal to those who are freed (from death).","r":"Those who go to excess in anger are like the dead; those who renounce anger are equal to those who have renounced.","n":"Wordplay carries the argument: இறந்தார் is both 'those who exceed' and 'those who have died'. துறந்தார் doubles in the same way, so a single renunciation is set equal to total renunciation.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They equal those who have attained release, since knowledge is certain for them though they still have a mortal body.","'வீடு பெற்றாரோடு ஒப்பர்'"],["manakkudavar","They equal those who have given up all possessions.","அதனை யொழிந் தார் எல்லாப் பொருளையுந் துறந்தாரோடு ஒப்பர்,"]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:311":{"book":"kural","num":311,"t":["சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும் பிறர்க்கு இன்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to cause sorrow to others, although they could (by so causing) obtain the wealth which confers greatness.","r":"Even if it would bring wealth that gives eminence, not hurting others is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"கோள் is a stance held, not a rule obeyed. The verse puts the temptation first and at its strongest, then refuses it in a word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The prize is the yogic powers such as becoming minute, so the temptation is an ascetic one.","யோகமாகிய சிறப்புத் தரும் அணிமா முதலிய செல்வங்களைப்"],["manakkudavar","The prize is ordinary wealth, refused even when it would bring no blame.","இது பழி வாராத செல்வம் பெறினும் தவிர வேண்டு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:312":{"book":"kural","num":312,"t":["கறுத்துஇன்னா செய்தவக் கண்ணும் மறுத்தின்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to do evil, even in return, to those who have cherished enmity and done them evil.","r":"Even when someone in hatred has done them hurt, not hurting in return is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies a detail that alters the verse's pairing with 313: here the other person's anger was aroused by one's own fault, whereas 313 treats enmity with no cause. On his view, the two verses together cover both provoked and unprovoked enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","samanilai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The attacker has been angered by a fault of one's own.","தாஞ்செய்த குற்றத்தினாலே வெகுண்டு,"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:313":{"book":"kural","num":313,"t":["செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும் இன்னாத செய்தபின்","உய்யா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"In an ascetic inflict suffering even on those who hate him, when he has not done them any evil, it will afterwards give him irretrievable sorrow.","r":"Doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing, brings sorrow there is no escaping.","n":"The verse chooses the case where retaliation looks most justified, an unprovoked enemy, and says it still rebounds. Parimelazhagar explains the sorrow by an ascetic's loss of austerity and takes the actor to be a renunciant, as Pope's gloss follows him in doing, but the verse doesn't name one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:314":{"book":"kural","num":314,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தாரை ஒறுத்தல் அவர்நாண","நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்"],"p":"The (proper) punishment to those who have done evil (to you), is to put them to shame by showing them kindness, in return and to forget both the evil and the good done on both sides.","r":"The way to punish those who have hurt you is to shame them by doing them good, and then let it go.","n":"ஒறுத்தல், to punish, is kept: retribution isn't abolished but redefined, and shame remains its instrument, so this isn't quite unconditional love. விடல் adds that one should leave the matter there, without savouring the effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nayan","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:315":{"book":"kural","num":315,"t":["அறிவினான் ஆகுவ துண்டோ பிறிதின்நோய்","தந்நோய்போல் போற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"What benefit has he derived from his knowledge, who does not endeavour to keep off pain from another as much as from himself ?","r":"What comes of understanding, if one doesn't guard against another's pain as one's own?","n":"பிறிதின், 'of another', is neuter and so can reach beyond humans. போற்று is active guarding, so understanding that doesn't produce care is judged to have no result at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","innaa","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:316":{"book":"kural","num":316,"t":["இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை துன்னாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்"],"p":"Let not a man consent to do those things to another which, he knows, will cause sorrow.","r":"What one has perceived to be painful, one must not come near doing to another.","n":"A negative golden rule, but with உணர்ந்தவை, what has been felt or perceived, as the basis rather than a principle. Pope's couplet says 'his own soul has felt', and Kalaignar too says known through experience, while Manakkudavar only says known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:317":{"book":"kural","num":317,"t":["எனைத்தானும் எஞ்ஞான்றும் யார்க்கும் மனத்தானாம்","மாணாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"It is the chief of all virtues not knowingly to do any person evil, even in the lowest degree, and at any time.","r":"Not doing ill of any degree, at any time, to anyone, with the mind, is the highest thing.","n":"Three universals pile up before the verse arrives at மனத்தானாம், which pulls in two directions. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar extend the ban to harm even in thought, while Pope's 'wilful' and 'knowingly' limit it to harm that is intended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:318":{"book":"kural","num":318,"t":["தன்னுயிர்ககு ஏன்னாமை தானறிவான் என்கொலோ","மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"Why does a man inflict upon other creatures those sufferings, which he has found by experience are sufferings to himself ?","r":"Why does one who knows what is hurtful to his own life do hurtful things to other living beings?","n":"This is posed as a real question and isn't answered. Manakkudavar's touch is that a person differs from unconscious objects in knowing his pain, which is exactly why this conduct is puzzling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","arul","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:319":{"book":"kural","num":319,"t":["பிறர்க்கின்னா முற்பகல் செய்யின் தமக்கு இன்னா","பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்"],"p":"If a man inflict sorrow upon others in the morning, it will come upon him unsought in the very evening.","r":"If you do hurt to others in the forenoon, hurts to you will come in the afternoon, of themselves.","n":"தாமே, 'of themselves', removes any agent: as Manakkudavar says, nobody else need do it. The speed is rhetorical, since the verse is not a report on timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:320":{"book":"kural","num":320,"t":["நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம் நோய்செய்யார்","நோயின்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Sorrow will come upon those who cause pain to others; therfore those, who desire to be free from sorrow, give no pain to others.","r":"All pains rest on those who caused pain; those who want to be without pain cause none.","n":"நோய் occurs four times. Taken strictly, 'all pains' has a hard consequence: anyone who suffers must have caused suffering. Manakkudavar accepts it by a time scheme in which present pain derives from past harm, and in that way this chapter leads into the claim of 330.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Present suffering comes from past harm, and harming no one now protects the future.","இக் காலத்து நுகர்கின்ற துன்பமெல்லாம் முற்காலத்துப் பிறர்க் குத் துன்பம் செய்தார் மாட்டே யுளவாம் ;"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:321":{"book":"kural","num":321,"t":["அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பிறவினை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"Never to destroy life is the sum of all virtuous conduct. The destruction of life leads to every evil.","r":"If you ask what virtuous action is, it is not killing; killing brings all the other kind of deed.","n":"பிறவினை, 'other deeds', leaves the wrongness to be inferred. Parimelazhagar takes it as a claim about results: the suffering killing yields exceeds that of all other sins combined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:322":{"book":"kural","num":322,"t":["பகுத்துண்டு பல்லுயிர் ஓம்புதல் நூலோர்","தொகுத்தவற்றுள் எல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"The chief of all (the virtues) which authors have summed up, is the partaking of food that has been shared with others, and the preservation of the mainfold life of other creatures.","r":"Sharing one's food and sustaining many lives is the chief of all that the authors have gathered.","n":"Inside a chapter on not killing, the crown goes to a positive act of feeding, so non-harm here isn't only refraining. Parimelazhagar assigns the summary to renunciants, Manakkudavar says it is good for all faiths, and Kalaignar reads an egalitarian creed into it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:323":{"book":"kural","num":323,"t":["ஒன்றாக நல்லது கொல்லாமை மற்றதன்","பின்சாரப் பொய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to destroy life is an incomparably (great) good next to it in goodness ranks freedom from falsehood.","r":"Good beyond compare is not killing; following behind it, not lying is good.","n":"A rare moment of the Kural ranking two of its own highest claims. Parimelazhagar recognizes this: since 297 and 300 raised doubt about which is greater, this verse settles it, and it can do so because truth depends on results and therefore shifts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse exists to settle a doubt that the chapter on truth raised.","இரண்டு அறந்துள்ளும் யாது சிறந்தது என்று ஐயம் நிகழுமன்றே!"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:324":{"book":"kural","num":324,"t":["நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி"],"p":"Good path is that which considers how it may avoid killing any creature.","r":"If you ask what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything at all.","n":"The question form follows 291 and 321, as if the Kural is defining terms. யாதொன்றும் is wide, and Parimelazhagar says it takes in even one-sensed life. சூழும் makes it a matter of constant attention rather than a single vow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:325":{"book":"kural","num":325,"t":["நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள் எல்லாம் கொலைஅஞ்சிக்","கொல்லாமை சூழ்வான் தலை"],"p":"Of all those who, fearing the permanence of earthly births, have abandoned desire, he is the chief who, fearing (the guilt of) murder, considers how he may avoid the destruction of life.","r":"Among all who renounced out of fear of their condition, the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief.","n":"Two fears are set side by side with the repeated அஞ்சி, one about one's own state and the other about another's life. The second outranks the first.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","thuravu","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is the highest within the class of renunciants, who differ by sect.","கொலைப் பாவத்தை அஞ்சிக் கொல்லாமை ஆகிய அறத்தை மறவாதவன் உயர்ந்தவன்."],["manakkudavar","He may stay a householder and still be above all who renounce.","கொல்லாமையைச் சிந்திப்பான் தலைமை யுடையவன்; இல் வாழ்க்கையில் நிற்பினும்,"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:326":{"book":"kural","num":326,"t":["கொல்லாமை மேற்கொண் டொழுகுவான் வாழ்நாள்மேல்","செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று"],"p":"Yama, the destroyer of life, will not attack the life of him, who acts under the determination of never destroying life.","r":"Death, the devourer of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing.","n":"The symmetry is exact: the one who won't kill is not killed, and the killer of all refrains here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It promises a full allotted span without interruption, which gives time for knowledge and release.","இப் பேரறம்செய்தான் தானும் கொல்லப்படான்; படானாகவே, அடியிற்கட்டிய வாழ்நாள் இடையூறின்றி எய்தும்"],["manakkudavar","Death doesn't come because there will be no further birth.","பிறவாமை யுண்டாமாதலால் கூற்றுச் செல்லாது என்றார்."],["kalaignar","It is a tribute: death itself hesitates, in awe.",""]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:327":{"book":"kural","num":327,"t":["தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க தான்பிறிது","இன்னுயிர் நீக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Let no one do that which would destroy the life of another, although he should by so doing, lose his own life.","r":"Even if your own life should go, do not do the deed that takes away another's sweet life.","n":"இன்னுயிர் gives the other's life the word for sweetness, while one's own is just உயிர். This is the Kural's hardest statement of the rule, and it allows no exception of necessity.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The case is a creature that would kill you: even then, because killing increases sin. He rejects a reading about ritual expiation.","'தன்னை அது கொல்லினும் தான் அதனைக் கொல்லற்க'"],["manakkudavar","The case is killing for medicine in mortal illness, which some people hold is no fault.","உயிர்க்குக் கேடுவருங் காலத்து நோய்க்கு மருந்தாகக் கொல்லுதல் குற்ற மன்று என் பார்க்கு, இது கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:328":{"book":"kural","num":328,"t":["நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும் சான்றோர்க் குக்","கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கங் கடை"],"p":"The advantage which might flow from destroying life in sacrifice, is dishonourable to the wise (who renounced the world), even although it should be said to be productive of great good.","r":"However great the gain that would bring good, gain that comes by killing is the lowest for the worthy.","n":"The verse mentions no sacrifice. That whole issue comes from the older commentators and through them Pope's gloss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is Vedic sacrifice, valid for householders seeking heaven and base only for those seeking release, so injunction and prohibition don't clash.","துறக்கம் எய்துவார்க்கு ஆம் ஆயினும், வீடு எய்துவார்க்கு ஆகாது"],["manakkudavar","Killing in sacrifice is also not acceptable, as the great seek release and let ritual go.","வேள்வி யின் வரும் கொலையும் ஆகாதென்றது."],["kalaignar","It is any killing that would produce great benefit, and sacrifice isn't mentioned.",""]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:329":{"book":"kural","num":329,"t":["கொலைவினைய ராகிய மாக்கள் புலைவினையர்","புன்மை தெரிவா ரகத்து"],"p":"Men who destroy life are base men, in the estimation of those who know the nature of meanness.","r":"People whose work is killing are of base occupation in the eyes of those who discern what baseness is.","n":"Baseness is assigned by work, not birth, as both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar stress, though the word புலை itself carries caste weight. Parimelazhagar's claim here that sacrifice isn't killing at all fits awkwardly with his note on 328.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The phrase 'whose work is killing' shows that killing in sacrifice isn't killing.","'கொலை வினையர்' என்றதனான், வேள்விக் கண் கொலையன்மை அறிக."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:330":{"book":"kural","num":330,"t":["உயிர் உடம்பின் நீக்கியார் என்ப செயிர் உடம்பின்","செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கை யவர்"],"p":"(The wise) will say that men of diseased bodies, who live in degradation and in poverty, are those who separated the life from the body of animals (in a former birth).","r":"Those in wretched bodies, leading an evil and failing life, they say, are people who separated life from body.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is the verse's own hedge. It doesn't mention a former birth, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply. Its logic runs backward from a sufferer to his guilt, and that makes it one of the more troubling claims in this section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:331":{"book":"kural","num":331,"t":["நில்லாத வற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்","புல்லறி வாண்மை கடை"],"p":"That ignorance which considers those things to be stable which are not so, is dishonourable (to the wise).","r":"The paltry understanding that takes what doesn't last for what lasts is the lowest.","n":"The fault is cognitive before it is moral: a mistake of knowledge, sarcastically dignified with ஆண்மை, mastery. Parimelazhagar limits 'lowest' to those seeking release, for whom this error feeds attachment, and the verse doesn't state that limit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:332":{"book":"kural","num":332,"t":["கூத்தாட்டு அவைக் குழாத் தற்றே பெருஞ்செல்வம்","போக்கும் அதுவிளிந் தற்று"],"p":"The acquisition of wealth is like the gathering together of an assembly for a theatre; its expenditure is like the breaking up of that assembly.","r":"The coming of great wealth is like a crowd gathering at a dance hall, and its going is like that crowd breaking up.","n":"A crowd has its own reason for being there, and Parimelazhagar presses this: wealth comes because of good deeds, of its own accord, and leaves when that cause is spent. So the owner is no more than the hall. His addition that this includes heavenly wealth is his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:333":{"book":"kural","num":333,"t":["அற்கா இயல்பிற்றுச் செல்வம் அதுபெற்றால்","அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்"],"p":"Wealth is perishable; let those who obtain it immediately practise those (virtues) which are imperishable.","r":"Wealth has the nature of not staying; if you get it, do straight away what stays.","n":"அற்கா and அற்குப play on one root, so what doesn't stay can be converted into what does. For Parimelazhagar 'what stays' means worship and giving without expecting fruit, leading to release, though the verse doesn't name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:334":{"book":"kural","num":334,"t":["நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி உயிர் ஈரும்","வாளது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"Time, which shows itself (to the ignorant) as if it were something (real) is in the estimation of the wise (only) a saw which cuts down life.","r":"It shows itself as a thing called a day, but it is a blade that saws through life, if one can find those who perceive it.","n":"Parimelazhagar: the fool rejoices that time is passing, and doesn't realize it is his own time. He grammatically dismisses a rival division of the words at length. உணர்வார்ப் பெறின் admits how few will ever see it this way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:335":{"book":"kural","num":335,"t":["நாச்செற்று விக்குள்மேல் வாராமுன் நல்வினை","மேற்சென்று செய்யப் படும்"],"p":"Let virtuous deeds be done quickly, before the biccup comes making the tongue silent.","r":"Before the hiccup rises and stops the tongue, good deeds must be rushed to.","n":"The image is the plain physical detail of the death rattle, with no metaphor. Parimelazhagar notes that it is the tongue being stopped, meaning that one can't even instruct others to act, and that no one knows when it will come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:336":{"book":"kural","num":336,"t":["நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை என்னும்","பெருமை உடைத்துஇவ் வுலகு"],"p":"This world possesses the greatness that one who yesterday was is not today.","r":"This world has the greatness that one who was here yesterday isn't here today.","n":"பெருமை is bitter here. Parimelazhagar takes it as excess, an overflow of impermanence, while Manakkudavar simply repeats 'greatness'. Kalaignar makes it the world's arrogance, boasting of having killed. All three hear the irony, in different keys.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:337":{"book":"kural","num":337,"t":["ஒருபொழுதும் வாழ்வது அறியார் கருதுப","கோடியும் அல்ல பல"],"p":"Innumerable are the thoughts which occupy the mind of (the unwise), who know not that they shall live another moment.","r":"Those who can't know they will live one moment more have thoughts in the many crores.","n":"The disproportion, a moment against crores, is the joke. Parimelazhagar gives a delightful catalogue of what such people plan: acquiring, guarding, overcoming, and raising friends and ruining rivals.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The crores are thoughts and schemes.","கோடியளவும் அன்றி அதனினும் பலவாய நினைவுகளை நினையா நிற்பர் அறிவிலாதார்."],["manakkudavar","The crores are what they assume their lifespan to be.","தமது வாழ்நாளைக் கோடியுமல்ல, பலவாகக்கருதுவர் உலகத்தார்,"]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:338":{"book":"kural","num":338,"t":["குடம்பை தனித்து ஒழியப் புள்பறந் தற்றே","உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love of the soul to the body is like (the love of) a bird to its egg which it flies away from and leaves empty.","r":"The friendship between body and life is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone.","n":"நட்பு, friendship, is heavily ironic: for Parimelazhagar there is none, only a union forced by deeds.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடம்பை is the egg, since a bird grows within it and never returns, and he explicitly rejects 'nest'.","முன் தனியாத முட்டை தனித்துக் கிடப்ப அதனுள் இருந்த புள்ளுப் பருவம் வந்துழிப் பறந்து போன தன்மைத்து,"],["manakkudavar","குடம்பை is the nest, and the point is that life leaves whenever it will.","கூடு தனியே கிடக்கப் புள்ளுப் பறந்து போனாற்போலும்;"],["kalaignar","It is the egg and the chick.",""]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:339":{"book":"kural","num":339,"t":["உறங்கு வதுபோலுஞ் சாக்காடு உறங்கி","விழிப்பது போலும் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Death is like sleep; birth is like awaking from it.","r":"Death is like falling asleep, and birth is like waking after sleep.","n":"The verse is very short and calm, with nothing about what happens in between. Its tone can be taken as reassuring or as a further turn of the screw. Parimelazhagar says birth is included to show that impermanence is itself what is permanent.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Death and birth alternate naturally and quickly, as sleep and waking do.","சாக்காடும் பிறப்பும் இயல்பாய்க் கடிதின் மாறிமாறி வரும்"],["manakkudavar","It is a statement of rebirth: the life that went is born again.","போன உயிர் மீண்டும் பிறக்கு மென்பதூஉம்,"],["kalaignar","There is no cycle: birth is the waking and death is a sleep with no waking.",""]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:340":{"book":"kural","num":340,"t":["புக்கில் அமைந்தின்று கொல்லோ உடம்பினுள்","துச்சில் இருந்த உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"It seems as if the soul, which takes a temporary shelter in a body, had not attained a home.","r":"Has the life that sits as a lodger in the body not found a home of its own?","n":"துச்சில், a lodger's corner in another's house, is homely and sad, and the verse is a soft question rather than a declaration.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No body has ever been a permanent home to it, given how life comes and goes among bodies.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் இருப்பதோர் இல் இதுகாறும் அமைந்ததில்லை போலும்!"],["manakkudavar","The home is specifically the state of release.","புக்கில் என்பது முத்தி ஸ்தானம்."],["kalaignar","Once it leaves the body, life has no other shelter, which comes close to the reverse.",""]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:341":{"book":"kural","num":341,"t":["யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல்","அதனின் அதனின் இலன்"],"p":"Whatever thing, a man has renounced, by that thing; he cannot suffer pain.","r":"From whatever, whatever one has left, from that, from that, one has no pain.","n":"The doubled words make this piecemeal, and Parimelazhagar accepts that: giving up everything at once is best, but each thing left removes its own pain. So renunciation isn't all or nothing in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:342":{"book":"kural","num":342,"t":["வேண்டின் உண் டாகத் துறக்க துறந்தபின்","ஈண்டுஇயற் பால பல"],"p":"After a man has renounced (all things), there will still be many things in this world (which he may enjoy); if he should desire them, let him, while it is time abandon. (the world).","r":"If you want it, renounce while there is time; after renouncing, there are many things that come about here.","n":"The verse is elliptical. Parimelazhagar concedes that he has supplied both 'joys' and 'time', so much of what appears to be said is commentary. Its surprise, on his reading, is that renunciation brings joys in this life and not only the end of pain. It also starts with வேண்டின், 'if you want'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renounce in time, while young, so as to have these joys for longer.","அவ் இன்பங்களை வேண்டின், அவற்றைக் காலம் பெறத் துறக்க."],["manakkudavar","Renounce if you want gain for your own soul.","தன்னுயிர்க்கு ஆக்கம் உண்டாக வேண்டின்,"],["kalaignar","Renounce while everything is still there to give up.",""]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:343":{"book":"kural","num":343,"t":["அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை விடல்வேண்டும்","வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Let the five senses be destroyed; and at the same time, let everything be abandoned that (the ascetic) has (formerly) desired.","r":"The domain of the five must be crushed, and all that one has wanted must be let go at once.","n":"புலம் is the field of the senses, their enjoyment, and not the organs that Pope's gloss has destroyed. Parimelazhagar gives the reason for 'at once': sense enjoyment fed with objects only burns higher, like fire with fuel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:344":{"book":"kural","num":344,"t":["இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை உடைமை","மயலாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"To be altogether destitute is the proper condition of those who perform austerities; if they possess anything, it will change (their resolution) and bring them back to their confused state.","r":"Having nothing at all is natural to austerity; having anything brings back confusion all over again.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading is psychological: keep one thing and everything given up returns through it. Manakkudavar links the confusion to further birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:345":{"book":"kural","num":345,"t":["மற்றும் தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல் பிறப்பறுக்கல்","உற்றார்க்கு உடம்பும் மிகை"],"p":"What means the addition of other things those who are attempting to cut off (future) births, when even their body is too much (for them).","r":"For those set on cutting off births, even the body is excess; why then any further ties?","n":"The body is the one tool needed for the work and still counts as excess. Parimelazhagar divides it into gross and subtle bodies, a doctrine the verse doesn't state, and makes this the renunciation of the inner 'I'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:346":{"book":"kural","num":346,"t":["யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு அறுப்பான் வானோர்க்கு","உயர்ந்த உலகம் புகும்"],"p":"He who destroys the pride which says \"I\", \"mine\" will enter a world which is difficult even to the Gods to attain.","r":"One who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods'.","n":"செருக்கு is glossed as delusion by the older commentators, as Parimelazhagar also does at 201: for them, the problem is a false identification and not arrogant manners. The body isn't 'I' and goods aren't 'mine'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is the world of release, beyond even the gods' reach.","வானோர்க்கும் எய்தற்கு அரிய வீட்டுலகத்தை எய்தும்."],["kalaignar","This is earthly fame greater than heaven-high fame.",""]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:347":{"book":"kural","num":347,"t":["பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள் பற்றினைப்","பற்றி விடாஅ தவர் க்கு"],"p":"Sorrows will never let go their hold of those who give not up their hold of desire.","r":"Griefs that hold and won't let go hold those who hold attachment and won't let go.","n":"The two lines use one verb for the person and the griefs, so that the grip one keeps and the grip one suffers are shown to be the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:348":{"book":"kural","num":348,"t":["தலைப்பட்டார் தீரத் துறந்தார் மயங்கி","வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have entirely renounced (all things and all desire) have obtained (absorption into God); all others wander in confusion, entangled in the net of (many) births.","r":"Those who have thoroughly renounced have got to the head; the rest, in confusion, are in the net.","n":"தலைப்பட்டார் doesn't say what has been reached, and the past tense suggests it is already done. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar say release, Kalaignar says a high state, and Pope's gloss has 'absorption into God', which adds a theology the Tamil doesn't have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:349":{"book":"kural","num":349,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும் மற்று","நிலையாமை காணப் படும்"],"p":"At the moment in which desire has been abandoned, (other) births will be cut off; when that has not been done, instability will be seen.","r":"The moment attachment ends it cuts off births; otherwise impermanence is what is seen.","n":"கண்ணே, 'at the very moment', makes release immediate, as an effect stops with its cause. Parimelazhagar takes மற்று as 'if not', and Pope's couplet and gloss diverge on it, the couplet making impermanence what is seen after the release, the gloss what is seen without it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:350":{"book":"kural","num":350,"t":["பற்றுக பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை அப்பற்றைப்","பற்றுக பற்று விடற்கு"],"p":"Desire the desire of Him who is without desire; in order to renounce desire, desire that desire.","r":"Cling to the clinging of the one who has no clinging; cling to that clinging, in order to let clinging go.","n":"பற்று comes six times in seven words, and the chapter on letting go ends by commanding a holding on. A useful attachment is the ladder that lets one drop the others.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unattached one is the Lord, and what is held is the path to release that he taught.","எல்லாப் பொருளையும் பற்றி நின்றே பற்றற்ற இறைவன் ஓதிய வீட்டு நெறியை,"],["manakkudavar","What is held is a practice: meditative absorption.","பற்றற்றான் பற்றாவது தியான சமாதி."],["kalaignar","The unattached one is any person so unattached, and it is him one should hold to.",""]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:351":{"book":"kural","num":351,"t":["பொருளல்ல வற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Inglorious births are produced by the confusion (of mind) which considers those things to be real which are not real.","r":"A joyless birth comes from the confusion that perceives what isn't real as real.","n":"The verse doesn't say which things are unreal. Parimelazhagar fills this in as denying rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God, adding the stump mistaken for a man and shell for silver. Kalaignar has no birth in view, only a life that goes poorly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The false view is to deny rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God.","அவ் விபரீத உணர்வாவது, மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும், கடவுளும் இல்லை எனவும்,"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:352":{"book":"kural","num":352,"t":["இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும் மருள்நீங்கி","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்க்கு"],"p":"A clear, undimmed vision of things will deliver its possessors from the darkness of future births, and confer the felicity (of heaven).","r":"For those of stainless sight, who have left confusion behind, it removes darkness and brings joy.","n":"What is the darkness? Parimelazhagar says hell, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar say ignorance, and Pope's gloss says the darkness of future births. The verse leaves it as a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:353":{"book":"kural","num":353,"t":["ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு வையத்தின்","வானம் நணிய துடைத்து"],"p":"Heaven is nearer than earth to those men of purified minds who are freed from from doubt.","r":"For those who have left doubt and become clear, the sky is nearer than the earth they stand on.","n":"Doubt here is the obstacle in its own right. Parimelazhagar makes an unusually candid point: every school's texts refute the others, so which is true can only be settled through one's own experience. The image of the far as nearer than the near is very bold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:354":{"book":"kural","num":354,"t":["ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","மெய்யுணர்வு இல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"Even those who have all the knowledge which can be attained by the five senses, will derive no benefit from it, if they are without a knowledge of the true nature of things.","r":"Even having gained the five-fold awareness, there is no benefit for those who lack true perception.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The five-fold awareness is the mind itself, gathered into concentration, and even this falls short.","ஐந்தாகிய உணர்வு: மனம்."],["manakkudavar","It is all that can be known by the five senses.","மெய் முதலாகிய பொறிகளைந்தினானும் அறியப்படுவனவெல்லாம் அறிந்தவிடத்தும்,"],["kalaignar","It is having subdued the five senses.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:355":{"book":"kural","num":355,"t":["எப்பொருள் எத்தன்மைத் தாயினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"(True) knowledge is the perception concerning every thing of whatever kind, that that thing is the true thing.","r":"Whatever a thing is and of whatever nature, seeing the true thing in that thing is understanding.","n":"One of the Kural's best-known definitions, and it is doctrinally open. Parimelazhagar removes a Chera king's rank, lineage, looks and names as conventions, down to the elements and beyond, while Kalaignar asks only that we not be fooled by appearances. It is both a metaphysical and a practical line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One discards the world's imputed conceptions and sees what remains.","பொருள் தோறும் உலகத்தார் கற்பித்துக் கொண்டு வழங்குகின்ற கற்பனைகளைக் கழித்து, நின்ற உண்மையைக் காண்பது"],["kalaignar","It is about not being taken in by outward looks.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:356":{"book":"kural","num":356,"t":["கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார் தலைப்படுவர்","மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி"],"p":"They, who in this birth have learned to know the True Being, enter the road which returns not into this world.","r":"Those who have learned here and seen the true thing will reach the path of not coming back here.","n":"Here learning is an actual route to the truth, in contrast to 373. For Parimelazhagar this is hearing from an experienced teacher, the first of his three means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Not coming back' means no return to birth.","மீண்டு இப்பிறப்பின்கண் வாராத நெறியை எய்துவர்."],["kalaignar","It means no return to the household life from renunciation.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:357":{"book":"kural","num":357,"t":["ஓர்த்துள்ளம் உள்ளது உணரான் ஒருதலையாப்","பேர்த்துள்ள வேண்டா பிறப்பு"],"p":"Let it not be thought that there is another birth for him whose mind having thoroughly considered (all it has been taught) has known the True Being.","r":"If the mind ponders and knows with certainty what is, one needn't think again of birth.","n":"உள்ளது, 'what is', is the plainest of the chapter's names for the goal. This reading assumes the commentators' உணரின் over the verse text's உணரான், as noted in source_issues.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","There is no need to expect a further birth for such a person.","அவனுக்கு மாறிப் பிறப்புளதாக நினைக்க வேண்டா."],["kalaignar","Those who truly investigate won't believe in rebirth at all, which reverses the claim into a denial of the doctrine.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:358":{"book":"kural","num":358,"t":["பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை நீங்கச் சிறப்பென்னும்","செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"True knowledge consists in the removal of ignorance; which is (the cause of) births, and the perception of the True Being who is (the bestower of) heaven.","r":"Understanding is seeing the perfect thing called the excellent, so that the folly called birth departs.","n":"The verse calls birth itself a folly, an astonishing metaphor that all three commentators have to unpack, each differently. Pope's 'man's dignity' for சிறப்பு goes in a different direction from all of them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means the ignorance that is the first cause of birth.","பிறப்பிற்கு முதற்காரணமாய அவிச்சை கெட;"],["manakkudavar","The folly is thinking that oneself is born and dies, when one's real standing has neither.","தான் பிறந்தானாக வும் செத்தானாகவும் கருதுகின்ற அறியாமையை விட்டுத்,"],["kalaignar","The ignorance is the very belief in a next birth.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:359":{"book":"kural","num":359,"t":["சார்புணர்ந்து சார்பு கெடஒழுகின் மற்றழித்துச்","சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்"],"p":"He who so lives as to know Him who is the support of all things and abandon all desire, will be freed from the evils which would otherwise cleave to him and destroy (his efforts after absorption).","r":"If one knows the support and lives so that supports perish, the pains due to cling won't destroy that life and cling.","n":"One word, சார்பு, does double service. Parimelazhagar adds that the fruit even of good deeds counts as 'pain' here, because it causes birth, which is a hard saying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","vinai","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The support to be known is the supreme reality on which all rests, and the supports to end are the attachments.","எல்லாப் பொருட்கும் சார்பாய அச்செம்பொருளை உணர்ந்து,"],["manakkudavar","The support is what attaches to oneself, the dependence on deeds.","சார்பு - வினைச் சார்பு;"],["kalaignar","It simply means finding out the causes of one's troubles and letting go of them.",""]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:360":{"book":"kural","num":360,"t":["காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் இநவ்முன்றன்","நாமம் கெடக்கெடும் நோய்"],"p":"If the very names of these three things, desire, anger, and confusion of mind, be destroyed, then will also perish evils (which flow from them).","r":"When even the names of the three, desire, anger and confusion, perish, pain perishes.","n":"நாமம், 'the name', is what must go, so they aren't merely suppressed. This triad resembles the three poisons in Buddhist teaching, a comparison that is mine and no commentator's. Parimelazhagar derives it by reducing a Sanskrit list of five faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","kaamam","vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The Sanskrit list of five faults reduces to these three, since ego falls under ignorance and craving under desire.","இவர் அவற்றுள் அகங்காரம் அவிச்சைக்கண்ணும் அவாவுதல் ஆசைக்கண்ணும் அடங்குதலான், 'மூன்று' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:361":{"book":"kural","num":361,"t":["அவாஎன்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் எஞ் ஞான்றும்","தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து"],"p":"(The wise) say that the seed, which produces unceasing births, at all times, to all creatures, is desire.","r":"They say craving is the seed that, for all beings at all times, produces unceasing birth.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is read by Parimelazhagar as marking agreement among all schools. He supplies a mechanism: at death, craving carries the life to its next state. Kalaignar keeps the seed but removes birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:362":{"book":"kural","num":362,"t":["வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை மற்றது","வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"If anything be desired, freedom from births should be desired; that (freedom from births) will be attained by desiring to be without desire.","r":"If you must want, want not to be born; that comes when you want not to want.","n":"A paradox as neat as 350. The verb வேண்டு occurs in every clause, and the verse doesn't pretend desire can simply be stopped. It redirects desire until it undoes itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Non-birth comes of itself to one who wants to want nothing.","அப்பிறவாமைதான் ஒரு பொருளையும் அவாவாமையை வேண்ட அவனுக்குத் தானே உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a warning: the misery that makes one wish one had never been born comes if desires aren't eliminated.",""]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:363":{"book":"kural","num":363,"t":["வேண்டாமை அன்ன விழுச்செல்வம் ஈண்டில்லை","ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is in this world no excellence equal to freedom from desire; and even in that world, there is nothing like it.","r":"There is no fine wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either.","n":"It is pointed to name wanting nothing as wealth. Parimelazhagar explains why it is the top: human and divine wealth always have something above them, so only not wanting has no superior.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:364":{"book":"kural","num":364,"t":["தூஉய்மை என்பது அவாவின்மை மற்றது","வாஅய்மை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"Purity (of mind) consists in freedom from desire; and that (freedom from desire) is the fruit of the love of truth.","r":"Purity is the absence of craving, and that comes by wanting வாய்மை.","n":"The word தூய்மை also differs: for Parimelazhagar it is release itself, as the soul's stains are gone, and for Manakkudavar it is the removal of defilement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வாய்மை here stands for the Supreme, who has truth, and wanting it means continuous contemplation.","மெய்ம்மையுடைய பரத்தை ஆகுபெயரால் 'மெய்ம்மை' என்றும் கூறினார்."],["manakkudavar","வாய்மை means speaking truth, and those who speak truth come to have no desire.","அவ்வாசை யின்மை மெய் சொல்லுதலை விரும்ப வரும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:365":{"book":"kural","num":365,"t":["அற்றவர் என்பார் அவாஅற்றார் மற்றையார்","அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்"],"p":"They are said to be free (from future birth) who are freed from desire; all others (who, whatever else they may be free from, are not freed from desire) are not thus free.","r":"Those called 'freed' are those freed of craving; the rest are not so freed.","n":"அற்றார் is left without an object, and that is the wit. Being cut free of other things is a partial matter, which Parimelazhagar makes explicit: others have only avoided some pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:366":{"book":"kural","num":366,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தோரும் அறனே ஒருவனை","வஞ்சிப்ப தோரும் அவா"],"p":"It is the chief duty of (an ascetic) to watch against desire with (jealous) fear; for it has power to deceive (and destroy) him.","r":"It is virtue alone to fear; what deceives a person is craving.","n":"Craving is a confidence trick: Manakkudavar has it stand in front like a benefactor and then lead one down. Parimelazhagar has it slip in through a moment's inattention, even for someone close to release. So vigilance has no end point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","acham","aram"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It deceives by acting first as a friend.","வஞ்சனை செய்தல் - நன்றி செய்வாரைப் போல முன்னே நின்று , பின்னே தீக்கதியுள் உய்த்தல்."]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:367":{"book":"kural","num":367,"t":["அவாவினை ஆற்ற அறுப்பின் தவாவினை","தான்வேண்டு மாற்றான் வரும்"],"p":"If a man thoroughly cut off all desire, the deeds, which confer immortality, will come to him, in the path in which he seeks them.","r":"If one cuts off craving thoroughly, the deeds that don't fail come in the way one wishes.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold antinomian comment: once craving is fully cut, no other virtue need be done, and everything one does is virtue. The verse says only that the right deeds arrive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:368":{"book":"kural","num":368,"t":["அவாஇல்லார்க் கில்லாகுந் துன்பம் அஃதுண்டேல்","தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்"],"p":"There is no sorrow to those who are without desire; but where that is, (sorrow) will incessantly come, more and more.","r":"Those without craving have no sorrow; if it exists, sorrow keeps coming, unfailing, on and on.","n":"The claim seems too simple, since those without craving still get sick. Parimelazhagar sees this and limits it: the suffering that came with the body is already earned, and the verse means suffering created now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:369":{"book":"kural","num":369,"t":["இன்பம் இடையறா தீண்டும் அவாவென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"Even while in this body, joy will never depart (from the mind, in which) desire, that sorrow of sorrows, has been destroyed.","r":"Joy will be unbroken ஈண்டும், if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes.","n":"Craving is not just the cause of sorrow but is itself the worst sorrow. Parimelazhagar explains that it hurts both at its source and in its fruit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஈண்டும் means 'even here', so joy comes while still in the body.","அவன் வீடு பெற்ற வழியே அன்றி உடம்போடு நின்ற வழியும் இன்பம் இடையறாது."],["manakkudavar","ஈண்டும் means 'it swells', so joy increases without break, and Parimelazhagar notes this view.","இன்ப மானது இடையறாமல் வந்து மிகும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:370":{"book":"kural","num":370,"t":["ஆரா இயற்கை அவாநீப்பின் அந்நிலையே","பேரா இயற்கை தரும்"],"p":"The removal of desire, whose nature it is never to be satisfied, will immediately confer a nature that can never be changed.","r":"If one gives up craving, whose nature is never to be filled, right then it gives a nature that never shifts.","n":"The rhyme of ஆரா and பேரா sets an insatiable nature against an immovable one, and அந்நிலையே makes it immediate. Parimelazhagar calls this person liberated while living and quotes the Tiruvaymoli. The verse says only that the nature doesn't move.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:371":{"book":"kural","num":371,"t":["ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை கைப்பொருள்","போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி"],"p":"Perseverance comes from a prosperous fate, and idleness from an adverse fate.","r":"A fate of gain produces tireless effort and a fate of loss produces sloth.","n":"Effort is presented as fate's product, not its rival, so the verse removes the usual escape route from fate.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate itself generates the effort and sloth that help make or lose wealth.","முயற்சி மடிகளையும் தானே தோற்றுவிக்கும்"],["kalaignar","A natural condition, not fate, gives rise to enthusiasm or laziness.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:372":{"book":"kural","num":372,"t":["பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ் அறிவகற்றும்","ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"An adverse fate produces folly, and a prosperous fate produces enlarged knowledge.","r":"A fate of loss makes a person foolish, and a fate of gain, when it arrives, enlarges their understanding.","n":"Intelligence is not a fixed possession here; circumstances of fortune change it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate alters even the understanding one is born with.","இயற்கையானாகிய அறிவையும் வேறுபடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Natural conditions of ruin and growth produce ignorance and understanding.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:373":{"book":"kural","num":373,"t":["நுண்ணிய நூல்பல கற்பினும் மற்றுந்தன்","உண்மை யறிவே மிகும்"],"p":"Although (a man) may study the most polished treatises, the knowledge which fate has decreed to him will still prevail.","r":"However many subtle books a person studies, their own understanding still prevails.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose understanding wins or why. The commentators fill that gap differently, and தன் உண்மை அறிவு carries the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns one fated to folly, whose fate-given foolishness overrides what study taught.","தன் ஊழான் ஆகிய பேதைமை உணர்வே மேற்படும்"],["manakkudavar","Learning may be present, but understanding that comes from fate is stronger.","கல்வியுண்டாயினும் ஊழானாய்வறிவு வலியுடைத் தென்றார்"],["kalaignar","Natural intelligence rises above study, with no appeal to fate.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:374":{"book":"kural","num":374,"t":["இருவேறு உலகத்து இயற்கை திருவேறு","தெள்ளிய ராதலும் வேறு"],"p":"There are (through fate) two different natures in the world, hence the difference (observable in men) in (their acquisition of) wealth, and in their attainment of knowledge.","r":"The world has two distinct natures: being wealthy is one thing, and being wise is another.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws a pointed conclusion: since the wise can be poor and others rich, wealth does not need wisdom as a cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:38"},"k:375":{"book":"kural","num":375,"t":["நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம் தீயவும்","நல்லவாம் செல்வம் செயற்கு"],"p":"In the acquisition of property, every thing favourable becomes unfavourable, and (on the other hand) everything unfavourable becomes favourable, (through the power of fate).","r":"In making wealth, all that is favourable can turn out badly and all that is unfavourable can turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'good' and 'bad' are favourable conditions such as time, place, means and action, which fate overturns.","'நல்லவை' 'தீயவை' என்பன காலமும், இடனும், கருவியும், தொழிலும் முதலாயவற்றை."],["kalaignar","Good and bad actions can end in the opposite result, which is simply the natural course.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:376":{"book":"kural","num":376,"t":["பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல உய்த்துச்","சொரியினும் போகா தம"],"p":"Whatever is not conferred by fate cannot be preserved although it be guarded with most painful care; and that, which fate has made his, cannot be lost, although one should even take it and throw it away.","r":"What is not yours will not stay however carefully you guard it, and what is yours will not leave though you throw it away.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Possession and loss follow fate, not care or neglect.","பொருள்களின் நிலையும் போக்கும் ஊழினான் ஆவதல்லது, காப்பு இகழ்ச்சிகளான் ஆகா"],["kalaignar","The verse is about what one has a right to, not what fate allots.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:377":{"book":"kural","num":377,"t":["வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் கோடி","தொகுத்தார்க்கு துய்த்தல் அரிது"],"p":"Even those who gather together millions will only enjoy them, as it has been determined by the disposer (of all things).","r":"Even those who have amassed millions can enjoy them only as the one who apportions has apportioned.","n":"வகுத்தான் gives fate a personal agent. This is one of the chapter's clearest points of disagreement about whether a cosmic agent is present.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The apportioner is a deity who gives each soul the fruit of its own deeds.","ஓர் உயிர் செய்த வினையின் பயன் பிறிதோர் உயிரின்கண் செல்லாமல் அவ்வுயிர்க்கே வகுத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","What is apportioned is an ordered way of life, and wealth is hard to enjoy without following it.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:378":{"book":"kural","num":378,"t":["துறப்பார்மன் துப்புர வில்லார் உறற்பால","ஊட்டா கழியு மெனின்"],"p":"The destitute will renounce desire (and become ascetics), if (fate) do not make them suffer the hindrances to which they are liable, and they pass away.","r":"The destitute would renounce the world, if only what they are bound to suffer would pass without afflicting them.","n":"The irony is dry: the poor already lack what renunciation gives up, yet fate withholds even that peace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They could let go of what they lack anyway, but fate's power keeps them suffering through their attitude.","கருத்து வேறுபாட்டால் துன்பமுறுகின்றது ஊழின் வலியான்"],["kalaignar","If afflicting feelings left them, they would renounce; the obstacle is psychological.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:379":{"book":"kural","num":379,"t":["நன்றாங்கால் நல்லவாக் காண்பவர் அன்றாங்கால்","அல்லற் படுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"How is it that those, who are pleased with good fortune, trouble themselves when evil comes, (since both are equally the decree of fa te) ?","r":"Those who accept things as good when good comes: why do they grieve when it doesn't?","n":"After nine verses on fate's power, this one asks for a response. Fate here is the premise of equanimity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both are one's own earlier deeds and can't be escaped, so accepting one and resisting the other is unwise.","தாமே முன் செய்து கொண்டமையானும், ஊட்டாது கழியாமையானும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:380":{"book":"kural","num":380,"t":["ஊழிற் பெருவலி யாவுள மற்றொன்று","சூழினுந் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"What is stronger than fate ? If we think of an expedient (to avert it), it will itself be with us before (the thought).","r":"What is stronger than fate? Even when one devises another way, it gets there first.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can come through the very plan meant to avert it.","தான் அவ்வுபாயமேயானும் பிறிதொன்றானும் வழியாக வந்து"],["kalaignar","The natural state reasserts itself over artificial arrangements.",""]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:381":{"book":"kural","num":381,"t":["படைகுடி கூழ்அமைச்சு நட்பரண் ஆறும்","உடையான் அரசருள் ஏறு"],"p":"He who possesses these six things, an army, a people, wealth, ministers, friends and a fortress, is a lion among kings.","r":"One who has all six, an army, a people, resources, ministers, allies and a fort, stands among kings as the male leads its herd.","n":"ஏறு names the male of a species, not specifically a lion. Pope's and Kalaignar's lion is an interpretive choice. Parimelazhagar presses ஆறும்: lacking even one limb, royal policy doesn't function.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:382":{"book":"kural","num":382,"t":["அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவூக்கம் இந்நான்கும்","எஞ்சாமை வேந்தர்க் கியல்பு"],"p":"Never to fail in these four things, fearlessness, liberality, wisdom, and energy, is the kingly character.","r":"Fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive, these four never failing, are the nature of a king.","n":"The weight is on எஞ்சாமை. Parimelazhagar explains that when one quality surfaces in a person others recede, and a king can't afford that, so the verse asks for constancy as well as possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","eegai","arivu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:383":{"book":"kural","num":383,"t":["தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்","நீங்கா நிலனான் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"These three things, viz., vigilance, learning, and bravery, should never be wanting in the ruler of a country.","r":"Promptness, learning and resolve: these three do not leave those who rule the land.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","kalvi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணிவு here stands for manly swiftness, acting quickly without regard to anything else.","ஆண்மையாவது, ஒன்றனையும் பாராது கடிதில் செய்வது"],["manakkudavar","துணிவு is a settled decision reached after examining the matter.","ஒரு பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து துணிதலுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:384":{"book":"kural","num":384,"t":["அறனிழுக்கா தல்லவை நீக்கி மறனிழுக்கா","மானம் உடைய தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who, with manly modesty, swerves not from virtue, and refrains from vice.","r":"A king does not stray from virtue, removes what is not virtue, and keeps an honour that does not stray from valour.","n":"The verse pairs அறன் with மறன், so virtue and martial valour stand side by side as parts of one honour. Parimelazhagar illustrates this honour with rules of war, such as not striking the young, the old or one who flees.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is removed is wrongdoing in his realm, such as killing and theft. It is a public duty.","அறனல்லவை தன் நாட்டின் கண்ணும் நிகழாமல் கடிந்து"],["manakkudavar","What is removed is desire and anger, the king's own inner faults.","அறமல்லாத காம வெகுளியைக் கடிந்து"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:385":{"book":"kural","num":385,"t":["இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலுங் காத்தலும் காத்த","வகுத்தலும் வல்ல தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who is able to acquire (wealth), to lay it up, to guard, and to distribute it.","r":"A king is one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it, and apportion what is guarded.","n":"Four verbs form a cycle that ends in distribution, not in holding. The verse doesn't say to whom. Parimelazhagar spreads the giving over virtue, wealth and pleasure, while Manakkudavar's example is equipping the army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:386":{"book":"kural","num":386,"t":["காட்சிக் கெளியன் கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்","மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்"],"p":"The whole world will exalt the country of the king who is easy of access, and who is free from harsh language.","r":"If a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others.","n":"The grammar praises the land, not the king. Parimelazhagar notes that praising the land makes praising the king unnecessary, though he has to supply the world as the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:387":{"book":"kural","num":387,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்குத் தன்சொலால்","தான்கண் டனைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world will praise and submit itself to the mind of the king who is able to give with affability, and to protect all who come to him.","r":"For those who can give and protect with pleasant words, this world falls in with their word and their intent.","n":"The second line is compressed, and my reading of தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து is tentative. Pleasant speech is named as what makes the giving work, not as an extra.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["arasu","inchol","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல் means fame: the world, joined to his renown, becomes as much as he conceives.","இவ்வுலகம் தன் புகழோடு மேவித் தான் கருதிய அளவிற்றாம்"],["manakkudavar","சொல் means his command: by it the world lies in his control.","தன்னேவலாலே இவ்வுலகம் தான் கண்டாற்போலும் தன் வசத்தே கிடக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:388":{"book":"kural","num":388,"t":["முறைசெய்து காப்பாற்றும் மன்னவன் மக்கட்கு","இறையென்று வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"That king, will be esteemed a God among men, who performs his own duties, and protects (his subjects).","r":"The ruler who does justice and protects is set apart as இறை for the people.","n":"வைக்கப்படும் is passive and conditional: the status is awarded for doing justice, not held by right of office.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Though a man by birth, by his deeds he is placed apart as a god for humans.","பிறப்பான் மகனேயாயினும், செய்வான் மக்கட்குக் கடவுள் என்று வேறு வைக்கப்படும்"],["manakkudavar","He is reckoned the lord of people, a political headship with no deification.","மனிதர்க்கு நாயக னென்று எண்ணப்படுவான்"],["kalaignar","He is honoured as the people's leader.",""]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:389":{"book":"kural","num":389,"t":["செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும் பண்புடை வேந்தன்","கவிகைக்கீழ்த் தங்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The whole world will dwell under the umbrella of the king, who can bear words that embitter the ear.","r":"The world rests under the parasol of a king with the character to bear words that are bitter to the ear.","n":"செவிகைப்ப moves a taste onto the ear, as Parimelazhagar points out. Tolerance of bitterness is presented as what brings the world under the parasol, which reverses the usual picture of power as not having to listen.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words are the rebukes of counsellors, borne for the good they will produce.","இடிக்கும் துணையாயினார் சொற்களைத் தன் செவி பொறாதாகவும்."],["manakkudavar","The words are reports of what others have done wrong, and the king forgives those offences. He also records a view that they are a priest's words.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றங்களைக் கேட்டு வைத்தும், அதனைப் பொறுக்கவல்ல குணமுடைய"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:390":{"book":"kural","num":390,"t":["கொடையளி செங்கோல் குடியோம்பல் நான்கும்","உடையானாம் வேந்தர்க் கொளி"],"p":"He is the light of kings who has there four things, beneficence, benevolence, rectitude, and care for his people.","r":"One who has these four, giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people, is a light among kings.","n":"Manakkudavar reads all four as relief for struggling farmers, including seed and plough and deferred or waived dues. That makes the verse far more concrete than its abstract nouns suggest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:391":{"book":"kural","num":391,"t":["கற்க கசடறக் கற்பவை கற்றபின்","நிற்க அதற்குத் தக"],"p":"Let a man learn thoroughly whatever he may learn, and let his conduct be worthy of his learning.","r":"Learn what is to be learned without flaw, and having learned, stand in keeping with it.","n":"நிற்க gives conduct a posture: the chapter's first verse makes learning unfinished until one stands by it. Parimelazhagar narrows கற்பவை to works on the aims of life, on the grounds that life is too short for the rest, but the verse leaves the scope open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:392":{"book":"kural","num":392,"t":["எண்ணென்ப ஏனை எழுத்தென்ப இவ்விரண் டும்","கண்ணென்ப வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Letters and numbers are the two eyes of man.","r":"What are called numbers and what are called letters, these two, are called the eyes of living beings.","n":"The verse says வாழும் உயிர்க்கு and doesn't restrict it. Parimelazhagar limits it to beings of special understanding and treats the two as tools for seeing the aims of life; Kalaignar goes further and makes them the test of being alive at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:393":{"book":"kural","num":393,"t":["கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர் முகத்திரண்டு","புண்ணுடையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The learned are said to have eyes, but the unlearned have (merely) two sores in their face.","r":"Those called people with eyes are the learned; the unlearned have two sores on their face.","n":"The insult works because the sore is in the same place and looks similar. Parimelazhagar adds that a merely physical eye gives pain through disease. Kalaignar draws an inference absent from the verse, that a blind person who is learned counts as having eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:394":{"book":"kural","num":394,"t":["உவப்பத் தலைக்கூடி உள்ளப் பிரிதல்","அனைத்தே புலவர் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the part of the learned to give joy to those whom they meet, and on leaving, to make them think (Oh! when shall we meet them again.)","r":"To meet with delight and part leaving others thinking of you: such is the way of the learned.","n":"The measure of learning here is purely social, with no mention of knowledge. Manakkudavar makes it a comparison with two people who meet in joy and part in longing, an image close to love. Parimelazhagar's விளக்கம் opens the அத்தன்மைத்து to include right conduct and counsel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:395":{"book":"kural","num":395,"t":["உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல் ஏக்கற்றுங் கற்றார்","கடையரே கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are inferior to the learned, before whom they stand begging, as the destitute before the wealthy.","r":"Those who learned while yearning like the poor before the rich are the foremost; those who did not learn are the lowest.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","kallaamai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The learner stands humbled before the teacher and is thereby foremost. Those ashamed of that posture stay low for good, having lost true learning to false pride.","செல்வர்முன் நல்கூர்ந்தார் நிற்குமாறு போலத் தாமும் ஆசிரியர்முன் ஏக்கற்று நின்றும் கற்றார் தலையாயினார்"],["manakkudavar","It is the unlearned who stand yearning like the poor, and they rank beneath the learned.","அதனைக் காதலித்து நிற்றலுமன்றிக் கற்றாரிடத்தாவர் கல்லாதார்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:396":{"book":"kural","num":396,"t":["தொட்டனைத் தூறும் மணற்கேணி மாந்தர்க்குக்","கற்றனைத் தூறும் அறிவு"],"p":"Water will flow from a well in the sand in proportion to the depth to which it is dug, and knowledge will flow from a man in proportion to his learning.","r":"A well in sand gives water as far as it is dug; for people, understanding wells up as far as they have learned.","n":"ஊறும் matters: understanding seeps up from below rather than being poured in, so study opens something rather than simply filling a store. Parimelazhagar openly reconciles this with 373, saying it holds where fate doesn't oppose it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse holds where fate does not stand against it, and so it doesn't conflict with 373.","இஃது ஊழ் மாறு கொள்ளாவழியாகலின்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:397":{"book":"kural","num":397,"t":["யாதானும் நாடாமால் ஊராமால் என்னொருவன்","சாந்துணையுங் கல்லாத வாறு"],"p":"How is it that any one can remain without learning, even to his death, when (to the learned man) every country is his own (country), and every town his own (town) ?","r":"Any land becomes one's own, any town one's own; why then does a person stay unlearned until death?","n":"The verse states the belonging without naming who has it; the commentators supply the learned. Parimelazhagar's reason is reception: others' lands give the learned gifts and honour. A reader today may hear something more cosmopolitan than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:398":{"book":"kural","num":398,"t":["ஒருமைக்கண் தான் கற்ற கல்வி ஒருவற்கு","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"The learning, which a man has acquired in one birth, will yield him pleasure during seven births.","r":"The learning one acquires in a single span serves as protection through seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒருமை and எழுமை are births: learning stays with the soul as deeds do, and follows it into each birth.","வினைகள்போல உயிரின்கண் கிடந்து அது புக்குழிப் புகும்"],["kalaignar","They are generations: what is learned in one generation protects seven generations of descendants.",""]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:399":{"book":"kural","num":399,"t":["தாமின் புறுவது உலகின் புறக் கண்டு","காமுறுவர் கற்றறிந் தார்"],"p":"The learned will long (for more learning), when they see that while it gives pleasure to themselves, the world also derives pleasure from it.","r":"Seeing that what delights them delights the world, the learned come to love it all the more.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What they desire further is learning itself, because it delights the world too. Unlike wealth, its pleasure gains from being shared.","கற்றறிந்தார் பின்னும் அதனையே விரும்புவர்"],["manakkudavar","The learned are glad when others enjoy what they enjoy. The verse is about freedom from envy, a virtue that learning brings.","இஃது அழுக்காறு செய்யாது இன்புறுதல் அறமாதலின், அது கல்வியானே வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:400":{"book":"kural","num":400,"t":["கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம் கல்வி யொருவற்கு","மாடல்ல மற்றை யவை"],"p":"Learning is the true imperishable riches; all other things are not riches.","r":"Learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth for a person; other things are not wealth.","n":"மாடு is a plain word for property and cattle, and the verse flatly denies that other possessions deserve it. Parimelazhagar defines the imperishability as more than safety from thieves and kings: learning doesn't decrease when given away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:401":{"book":"kural","num":401,"t":["அரங்கின்றி வட்டாடி யற்றே நிரம்பிய","நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To speak in an assembly (of the learned) without fullness of knowledge, is like playing at chess (on a board) without squares.","r":"Holding forth in an assembly without complete learning is like playing a board game with no marked board.","n":"The image is exact: a game without a board isn't a bad game, because no move can mean anything. The fault is the absence of a shared frame, not lack of cleverness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:402":{"book":"kural","num":402,"t":["கல்லாதான் சொற்கா முறுதல் முலையிரண்டும்","இல்லாதாள் பெண்காமுற் றற்று"],"p":"The desire of the unlearned to speak (in an assembly), is like a woman without breasts desiring (the enjoyment of ) woman-hood.","r":"For the unlearned to set their heart on speech is like desiring womanhood, or a woman, in one who lacks both breasts.","n":"A crude comparison that rests on a narrow view of women's bodies. It is one of the verses where the book's period shows, and it shouldn't be softened. The commentators disagree on who is desiring.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unlearned man himself longs to speak, and this is like such a woman longing for womanhood: it gets nowhere, or brings laughter.","கல்வியில்லாதவன் ஒருவன் அவையின்கண் ஒன்று சொல்லுதலை அவாவுதல்"],["kalaignar","The fault lies with the listener who wants to hear the unlearned, and this is like being infatuated with such a woman.",""]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:403":{"book":"kural","num":403,"t":["கல்லா தவரும் நனிநல்லர் கற்றார்முன்","சொல்லா திருக்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"The unlearned also are very excellent men, if they know how to keep silence before the learned.","r":"Even the unlearned are very good, if only they can stay silent before the learned.","n":"Dry praise. Parimelazhagar takes பெறின் seriously: it marks the condition as hard to meet, since those who don't know themselves can't manage it. The verse's sting is that knowing when to be silent would itself take the knowledge they lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:404":{"book":"kural","num":404,"t":["கல்லாதான் ஒட்பம் கழியநன் றாயினும்","கொள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Although the natural knowledge of an unlearned man may be very good, the wise will not accept for true knowledge.","r":"However excellent an unlearned person's brightness may be, the wise do not accept it.","n":"The verse grants the brightness and refuses it recognition, so this is a claim about what the wise will credit and not about what the person has.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It isn't real understanding: any good result is a chance hit, like letters traced by accident.","ஏரலெழுத்துப் போல்வதோர் விழுக்காடு"],["kalaignar","The natural intelligence may be real. The wise simply won't accept him as distinguished in learning.",""]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:405":{"book":"kural","num":405,"t":["கல்லா ஒருவன் தகைமை தலைப்பெய்து","சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The self-conceit of an unlearned man will fade away, as soon as he speaks in an assembly (of thelearned).","r":"An unlearned person's standing wilts once he meets and speaks.","n":"The verse doesn't say with whom; the commentators supply a learned person. தகைமை is taken three ways: Parimelazhagar as his self-estimate, Manakkudavar as real greatness, and Kalaignar as a false pose. The first and third make the loss self-delusion ending, while the second makes it a real loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:406":{"book":"kural","num":406,"t":["உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர் அல்லால் பயவாக்","களரனையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are like worthless barren land: all that can be said of them is, that they exist.","r":"The unlearned can only be said to exist; beyond that they are like barren saline land that yields nothing.","n":"உளர் என்னும் மாத்திரையர் reduces a person to the bare fact of being said to exist. Kalaignar's walking corpses is stronger than the verse's image, which is about yield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:407":{"book":"kural","num":407,"t":["நுண்மாண் நுழைபுலம் இல்லான் எழில்நலம்","மண்மாண் புனைபாவை யற்று"],"p":"The beauty and goodness of one who is destitute of knowledge by the study of great and exquisite works, is like (the beauty and goodness) of a painted earthen doll.","r":"The fine looks of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll.","n":"Parimelazhagar concedes that a fine body is itself a rare fruit of birth, which makes the point less a scorn of beauty than a statement that it needs learning to be worth anything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:408":{"book":"kural","num":408,"t":["நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமையின் இன்னாதே","கல்லார்கண் பட் ட திரு"],"p":"Wealth, gained by the unlearned, will give more sorrow than the poverty which may come upon the learned.","r":"Wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good.","n":"The verse says நல்லார், the good, and opposes it to கல்லார், the unlearned. The pairing makes the learned and the good one group by sound and by implication. Parimelazhagar glosses நல்லார் as கற்றார் outright.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth ruins the unlearned owner, while poverty doesn't ruin the good. That is why it is more painful.","திருகல்லாரைக் கெடுக்க, வறுமை நல்லாரைக் கெடாது நிற்றலான்"],["manakkudavar","The harm falls on others: given wealth, the unlearned make other people suffer.","இது செல்வமுண்டாயின், பிறரைத் துன்பமுறுவிப்பரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:409":{"book":"kural","num":409,"t":["மேற்பிறந்தா ராயினும் கல்லாதார் கீழ்ப்பிறந்தும்","கற்றார் அனைத்திலர் பாடு"],"p":"The unlearned, though born in a high caste, are not equal in dignity to the learned; though they may have been born in a low caste.","r":"Though born high, the unlearned do not have the worth of those who are learned though born low.","n":"The verse overturns the ranking but keeps the words மேல் and கீழ், so birth rank remains as something to be overturned. The argument is not that the distinction is false, only that learning outweighs it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Caste rank ends with the body while learning goes with the soul, so learning is superior. Caste rank itself is assumed.","உடலோடு ஒழியும் சாதி உயர்ச்சியினும், உயிரோடு செல்லும் கல்வி உயர்ச்சி சிறப்புடைத்து"],["kalaignar","The honour of learning removes the distinction of high and low altogether.",""]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:410":{"book":"kural","num":410,"t":["விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர் இலங்குநூல்","கற்றாரோடு ஏனை யவர்"],"p":"As beasts by the side of men, so are other men by the side of those who are learned in celebrated works.","r":"As beasts stand to people, so do others stand to those who have studied the shining books.","n":"The chapter ends by removing the unlearned from the human as such. Parimelazhagar's logic is that the surplus of awareness which marks humans over animals is found in the learned, so the two aren't of one birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:411":{"book":"kural","num":411,"t":["செல்வத்துட் செல்வஞ் செவிச்செல்வம் அச்செல்வம்","செல்வத்து ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"Wealth (gained) by the ear is wealth of wealth; that wealth is the chief of all wealth.","r":"The wealth among wealths is the wealth of the ear; that wealth stands first among all wealth.","n":"The sound does the work, with செல்வம் in every half-line. After 400 has already denied other wealth the name, this verse places the ear at the head of that revalued wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:412":{"book":"kural","num":412,"t":["செவுக்குண வில்லாத போழ்து சிறிது","வயிற்றுக்கும் ஈயப் படும்"],"p":"When there is no food for the ear, give a little also to the stomach.","r":"When there is no food for the ear, a little may be given to the stomach too.","n":"The verse is witty, not ascetic. Parimelazhagar catches the two loaded words: சிறிது, and ஈயப்படும், which treats the stomach as a beggar to be given alms. It also says the food is given so that one can go on listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:413":{"book":"kural","num":413,"t":["செவியுணவிற் கேள்வி யுடையார் அவியுணவின்","ஆன்றாரோ டொப்பர் நிலத்து"],"p":"Those who in this world enjoy instruction which is the food of the ear, are equal to the Gods, who enjoy the food of the sacrifices.","r":"Those who have listening as food for the ear, though on earth, are equal to those fulfilled by the food of oblation.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆன்றார் here are the gods who receive sacrificial offerings. The listeners are their earthly equals.","நிலத்தின்கண்ணர் ஆயினும் அவியுணவினையுடைய தேவரொடு ஒப்பர்."],["kalaignar","ஆன்றார் are human sages who eat little and are full of understanding, with no sacrifice or gods.",""]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:414":{"book":"kural","num":414,"t":["கற்றில னாயினுங் கேட்க அஃதொருவற்கு","ஒற்கத்தின் ஊற்றாந் துணை"],"p":"Although a man be without learning, let him listen (to the teaching of the learned); that will be to him a staff in adversity.","r":"Even if you haven't studied, listen; it will be a staff to lean on in one's faltering.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the உம்மை in ஆயினும் keeps study as the first duty. Listening is not offered as an equal path, only as what remains. He also makes the faltering inward: a mind shaken by poverty or ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:415":{"book":"kural","num":415,"t":["இழுக்கல் உடையுழி ஊற்றுக்கோல் அற்றே","ஒழுக்க முடையார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"The words of the good are like a staff in a slippery place.","r":"The words from the mouths of those of good conduct are like a staff on slippery ground.","n":"The authority comes from the speaker's conduct, not learning. Parimelazhagar makes the exclusion explicit: the learned without conduct are not to be heard at all. So the chapter's listening is listening to a kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","ozhukkam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:416":{"book":"kural","num":416,"t":["எனைத்தானும் நல்லவை கேட்க அனைத்தானும்","ஆன்ற பெருமை தரும்"],"p":"Let a man listen, never so little, to good (instruction), even that will bring him great dignity.","r":"Listen to good things, however little; that much too will give full greatness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even a little brings full greatness. Like drops of rain, the listening accumulates, so don't despise the small.","அக்கேள்வி மழைத் துளைபோல வந்து ஈண்டு எல்லா அறிவுகளையும் உள ஆக்கலின்"],["kalaignar","Greatness comes in proportion: as much as one listens, that much greatness.",""]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:417":{"book":"kural","num":417,"t":["பிழைத் துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லா ரிழைத்துணர்ந்","தீண்டிய கேள்வி யவர்"],"p":"Not even when they have imperfectly understood (a matter), will those men speak foolishly, who have profoundly studied and diligently listened (to instruction).","r":"Those whose listening is gathered upon their own close investigation do not speak folly, even when they have misunderstood.","n":"A subtle claim: being wrong and speaking foolishly are different things. Such a person can err and still not talk nonsense. இழைத்துணர்ந்து comes before கேள்வி, and Parimelazhagar reads this as one's own investigation first, confirmed by consulting those who know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:418":{"book":"kural","num":418,"t":["கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே கேள்வியால்","தோட்கப் படாத செவி"],"p":"The ear which has not been bored by instruction, although it hears, is deaf.","r":"Ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear.","n":"தோட்கப்படாத plays on pierced ears, ornament and all. Parimelazhagar's closing remark is apt: the old hole isn't a hole. Listening must make a new opening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:419":{"book":"kural","num":419,"t":["நுணங்கிய கேள்விய ரல்லார் வணங்கிய","வாயின ராதல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing to find modesty, a reverend mouth- with those who have not received choice instruction.","r":"It is hard for those without fine listening to have a mouth that bows.","n":"The bowing is done by the mouth, so this is humility as a way of speaking. The causal claim is striking: humility comes from having listened, because those who haven't heard much admire themselves, as Parimelazhagar explains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:420":{"book":"kural","num":420,"t":["செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்","அவியினும் வாழினும் என்?"],"p":"What does it matter whether those men live or die, who can judge of tastes by the mouth, and not by the ear ?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, these beings who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear?","n":"The verse doesn't say to whom it doesn't matter; both older commentators supply the world, which makes it a social judgment of uselessness, not a cosmic one. மாக்கள் rather than மக்கள் also seems to me a deliberate demotion. It may also echo அவி of 413, oblation there and perishing here, though that is my suggestion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:421":{"book":"kural","num":421,"t":["அறிவற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி செறுவார்க்கும்","உள்ளழிக்க லாகா அரண்"],"p":"Wisdom is a weapon to ward off destruction; it is an inner fortress which enemies cannot destroy.","r":"Understanding is the tool that guards against அற்றம், and an inner fortress enemies can't destroy.","n":"The fort is one of the six limbs of a kingdom (381). This verse puts a fort inside the person, with உள் able to mean either inward or impenetrable from within, as Parimelazhagar notes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றம் is final ruin, which understanding foresees and prevents.","அரசர்க்கு அறிவு என்பது இறுதி வாராமல் காக்கும் கருவியாம்"],["manakkudavar","அற்றம் is one's own weakness, and understanding covers one's faults, while the fort handles harm from others.","இது தனக்குள்ள குற்றத்தை மறைக்கு மென்றும், பிறரால் வரும் தீமை யைக் காக்குமென்றும்"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:422":{"book":"kural","num":422,"t":["சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா தீதொரீஇ","நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்ப தறிவு"],"p":"Not to permit the mind to go where it lists, to keep it from evil, and to employ it in good, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding does not let one go wherever things have gone, but turns away from evil and steers toward good.","n":"The verse doesn't name what wanders. Both commentators supply the mind, but they disagree on what is held back and how far.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mind itself is driven through the senses, as a groom drives a horse. Understanding is a separate quality of the soul.","குதிரையை நிலமறிந்து செலுத்தும் வாதுவன்போல வேறாக்கி மனத்தைப் புலமறிந்து செலுத்துவது அறிவு"],["manakkudavar","The mind may go, but the body must not follow it, and the verse chiefly concerns sexual pleasure.","உள்ளஞ் சென்ற விடத்தே உடம்பையுஞ் செல்லவிடாது"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:423":{"book":"kural","num":423,"t":["எப்பொருள் யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To discern the truth in every thing, by whomsoever spoken, is wisdom.","r":"Whatever is heard, from whoever's mouth, understanding is seeing the true substance of that very thing.","n":"The repeated யார்யார் removes the speaker from the test. Parimelazhagar gives the sharp consequence: high matters may come from low mouths, harmful ones from friends. He takes மெய்ப்பொருள் as the thing's true use, not its truth in the abstract, which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar prefer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:424":{"book":"kural","num":424,"t":["எண்பொருள வாகச் செலச்சொல்லித் தான்பிறர்வாய்","நுண்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To speak so as that the meaning may easily enter the mind of the hearer, and to discern the subtlest thought which may lie hidden in the words of others, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding is saying things so they go in easily, while seeing the subtle meaning in what comes from others.","n":"The two directions are asymmetric: speech should make the hard simple, and listening should find the fine in the plain. Parimelazhagar goes further, saying to take the use from what others say even if it is flawed in expression.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:425":{"book":"kural","num":425,"t":["உலகம் தழீஇய தொட்பம் மலர்தலும்","கூம்பலும் இல்ல தறிவு"],"p":"To secure the friendship of the great is true wisdom; it is (also) wisdom to keep (that friendship unchanged, and) not opening and closing (like the lotus flower).","r":"Embracing the world is brightness; not opening and closing is understanding.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the worthy and contrasts a pond flower that closes with a tree flower that stays open.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What must not open and close is friendship with the worthy. He records another view that it is about wealth and poverty.","அந்நட்பின்கண் முன் மலர்தலும் பின் கூம்புதலும் இன்றி ஒரு நிலையினாவது அறிவாம்."],["kalaignar","What must not open and close is one's response to joy and sorrow, which is equanimity.",""]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:426":{"book":"kural","num":426,"t":["எவ்வ துறைவது உலகம் உலகத்தோடு","அவ்வ துறைவ தறிவு"],"p":"T o live as the world lives, is wisdom.","r":"As the world lives, so to live with the world is understanding.","n":"This verse needs reading with 423, because the same chapter tells the reader to test everything and to go along.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It restrains a king: he must not think that because he commands the world, no one commands him.","உலகத்தையெல்லாம் யான் நியமித்தலான் என்னை நியமிப்பாரில்லை,"],["manakkudavar","உலகம் means the elevated of earlier times, so this is following the worthy, not the crowd.","முற்பட்ட உயர்ந்தாரோடு பொருந்த ஒழுகுதல் அறிவு என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:427":{"book":"kural","num":427,"t":["அறிவுடையார் ஆவ தறிவார் அறிவிலார்","அஃதறி கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The wise are those who know beforehand what will happen; those who do not know this are the unwise.","r":"Those with understanding know what will come to be; those without it cannot.","n":"ஆவது could be what will happen or what is good for oneself; Parimelazhagar records both. His gloss makes foreknowledge an act of reasoning ahead, எண்ணி அறிதல், and not prophecy. Not knowing is defined as finding out once it arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:428":{"book":"kural","num":428,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சாமை பேதைமை அஞ்சுவது","அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்"],"p":"Not to fear what ought to be feared, is folly; it is the work of the wise to fear what should be feared.","r":"Not fearing what is to be feared is folly; fearing what is to be feared is the work of the knowing.","n":"Fear is treated as a skill, தொழில். Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both notice the conflict with the kingly fearlessness of 382 and resolve it. Parimelazhagar defines fear here as considering and refraining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:429":{"book":"kural","num":429,"t":["எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்க் கில்லை","அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்"],"p":"No terrifying calamity will happen to the wise, who (foresee) and guard against coming evils.","r":"Those whose understanding guards against what is coming have no affliction that comes to shake them.","n":"அதிர வருவது is careful: the claim may be that no calamity shakes them, not that no calamity comes. Parimelazhagar's guarding means removing the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","oozh"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Even suffering from past deeds does not come harshly if it is guarded against early.","இது முன்னை வினையால் வருந் துன்பமும் முற்பட்டுக் காக்கின், கடிதாக வாராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:430":{"book":"kural","num":430,"t":["அறிவுடையார் எல்லா முடையார் அறிவிலார்","என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்"],"p":"Those who possess wisdom, possess every thing; those who have not wisdom, whatever they may possess, have nothing.","r":"Those with understanding have everything; those without it have nothing, whatever they have.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a practical reason, not a pious one: all wealth is made and kept by understanding, so the fool has no means of keeping what he has or rebuilding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:431":{"book":"kural","num":431,"t":["செருக்குஞ் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்","பெருக்கம் பெருமித நீர்த்து"],"p":"Truly great is the excellence of those (kings) who are free from pride, anger, and lust.","r":"The prosperity of those without arrogance, anger and smallness has a dignified quality.","n":"The verse puts the virtue in the quality of the prosperity: the same wealth is dignified or not, depending on who holds it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is intoxication from wealth, and சிறுமை is excessive desire, which he calls small because it is what small people do.","மதமும் வெகுளியும் காமமும் ஆகிய குற்றங்கள்"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is coveting another man's wife, and சிறுமை is any behaviour of petty people.","பிறர்மனை நயத்தலும், வெகுளியும், சிறியார் செய்வன செய் தொழுகுதலும்"]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:432":{"book":"kural","num":432,"t":["இவறலும் மாண்பிறந்த மானமும் மாணா","உவகையும் ஏதம் இறைக்கு"],"p":"Avarice, undignified pride, and low pleasures are faults in a king.","r":"Stinginess, honour that has passed beyond dignity, and joy that lacks dignity are faults in a ruler.","n":"The surprising items are positive feelings gone wrong. Parimelazhagar makes the undignified joy excessive leniency, கழிகண்ணோட்டம், and the wrong honour includes refusing to bow to elders and carrying a thing through just because one intended it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","ivaral","maanam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:433":{"book":"kural","num":433,"t":["தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம் வரினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பழிநாணு வார்"],"p":"Those who fear guilt, if they commit a fault small as a millet seed, will consider it to be as large as a palmyra tree.","r":"Though a fault comes as small as a millet seed, those who are ashamed of blame take it to be as large as a palmyra.","n":"The motive is shame at blame, பழிநாணுவார், so this is social conscience. Parimelazhagar makes the scale practical: treating it as large is how they ensure it doesn't come again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:434":{"book":"kural","num":434,"t":["குற்றமே காக்க பொருளாகக் குற்றமே","அற்றந் த்ரூஉம் பகை"],"p":"Guard against faults as a matter (of great consequence; for) faults are a deadly enemy.","r":"Guard against fault itself as your aim; fault alone is the enemy that brings ruin.","n":"The double ஏ, குற்றமே twice, refuses any other candidate. Parimelazhagar draws a strong conclusion from a Sanskrit view: external enemies can only bring ruin through such faults. பொருளாக is 'as your aim' for him; Pope's 'wealth' is a different and weaker reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:435":{"book":"kural","num":435,"t":["வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான் வாழ்க்கை எரிமுன்னர்","வைத்தூறு போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who does not timely guard against faults, will perish like straw before fire.","r":"The life of one who doesn't guard before it comes will perish like straw set before a fire.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is coming is fault, supplied from the chapter's subject; even a small fault burns away great wealth.","குற்றம் வரக் கடவதாகின்ற முற்காலத்திலே அதனைக் காவாத அரசன் வாழ்க்கை"],["manakkudavar","What is coming is suffering, and failing to prepare against it is itself the fault.","இது முந்துற்றுக் காவல் செய்வன செய்யாமையும் குற்ற மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:436":{"book":"kural","num":436,"t":["தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","என்குற்ற மாகும் இறைக்கு?"],"p":"What fault will remain in the king who has put away his own evils, and looks after the evils of others.","r":"If he removes his own fault and can then see others' faults, what fault can attach to a ruler?","n":"Parimelazhagar turns this into a condition of legitimacy: punishing others' faults is itself a fault until one has cleared one's own, after which it becomes justice. The verse's order, தன்... பிறர், supports him, though the claim about the right to judge is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:437":{"book":"kural","num":437,"t":["செயற்பால செய்யா திவறியான் செல்வம்","உயற்பால தன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"The wealth of the avaricious man, who does not expend it for the purposes for which he ought to expend it will waste away and not continue.","r":"The wealth of one who clings to it, not doing what should be done with it, does not last but perishes.","n":"Stinginess doesn't keep wealth, on this view; it loses it. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that wealth left unused for virtue and growth has nothing to sustain it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:438":{"book":"kural","num":438,"t":["பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை எற்றுள்ளும்","எண்ணப் படுவதொன் றன்று"],"p":"Griping avarice is not to be reckoned as one among other faults; (it stands alone - greater than all).","r":"The miserliness called the clinging heart is not a thing to be counted among any.","n":"பற்றுள்ளம் uses a word close to the attachment renunciation speaks of, here applied to wealth in the Porul section.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It can't be counted among faults because it exceeds them all: it can dominate even when every good quality is present.","குற்றத் தன்மைகள் எல்லாவற்றுள்ளும் வைத்து எண்ணப்படுவது ஒன்று அன்று; மிக்கது."],["manakkudavar","It isn't counted because it is contemptible: useless to self and others, it is reckoned as nothing.","இஃது உலோபம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாமையால் ஒரு பொருளாக மதிக்கப்படாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:439":{"book":"kural","num":439,"t":["வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை நயவற்க","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Let no (one) praise himself, at any time; let him not desire to do useless things.","r":"Never admire yourself, and never set your heart on a deed that brings no good.","n":"The two clauses could be separate, or the second a consequence of the first, as Manakkudavar and Kalaignar take it. Parimelazhagar connects them too: self-admiration blinds one to place, time and strength, the subjects of chapters 48 to 50.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kutram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:440":{"book":"kural","num":440,"t":["காதல காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்","ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்"],"p":"If (a king) enjoys, privately the things which he desires, the designs of his enemies will be useless.","r":"If you can enjoy what you love without others knowing of the love, your enemies' schemes come to nothing.","n":"A striking end. After a chapter on removing faults, the last verse manages one by concealing it. Parimelazhagar concedes that desire, anger and joy aren't to be wholly removed. The verse is a matter of security: a known desire is a door for the enemy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:441":{"book":"kural","num":441,"t":["அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார் கேண்மை","திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) ponder well its value, and secure the friendship of men of virtue and of mature knowledge.","r":"Knowing their worth and the way to go about it, choose the friendship of those of understanding who have matured in knowledge of virtue.","n":"மூத்த is not simply age: Parimelazhagar counts maturity in understanding, character and years. The verse doesn't say what office such people hold, though Manakkudavar takes them to be priests. For Parimelazhagar திறன் is the way of winning them, and for Manakkudavar it is their capacities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:442":{"book":"kural","num":442,"t":["உற்றநோய் நீக்கி உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்","பெற்றியார்ப் பேணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) procure and kindly care for men who can overcome difficulties when they occur, and guard against them before they happen.","r":"Cherish and take as support those who have the quality of removing the affliction that has come and guarding before against what has not.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are priests and ministers: priests handle afflictions from the gods by rites, and ministers those from people by the four policies.","புரோகிதரையும் அமைச்சரையும் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","These are ministers. He takes the verse's general word to refer to them, leaving priests to 441.","இது மந்திரிகளைக் கூட் மாறு கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:443":{"book":"kural","num":443,"t":["அரியவற்று ளெல்லாம் அரிதே பெரியாரைப்","பேணித் தமராக் கொளல்"],"p":"To cherish great men and make them his own, is the most difficult of all difficult things.","r":"To cherish the great and make them one's own is the rarest of all rare things.","n":"அரிது can mean hard to do, as Manakkudavar has it, or rare to attain, as Parimelazhagar does. The second adds his observation that a king who can have every rare thing still prizes this one, because the others come through it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:444":{"book":"kural","num":444,"t":["தம்மிற் பெரியார் தமரா ஒழுகுதல்","வன்மையு ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"So to act as to make those men, his own, who are greater than himself is of all powers the highest.","r":"To conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the chief of all strengths.","n":"A paradox of power: the supreme strength is standing under someone. Parimelazhagar's தாம் அவர் வழிநின்று makes the king the follower, and he ranks this above wealth, army and fortress on the ground that it reaches afflictions they can't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","vali","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:445":{"book":"kural","num":445,"t":["சூழ்வார்கண் ணாக ஒழுகலான் மன்னவன்","சூழ்வாரைக் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"As a king must use his ministers as eyes (in managing his kingdom), let him well examine their character and qualifications before he engages them.","r":"Since counsellors serve as his eyes, a ruler must take his counsellors after counsel.","n":"The verse plays on சூழ், which means to deliberate, to plot and to surround, and the commentators split along those senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king should examine his counsellors before taking them on, because they are his eyes.","அரசன் அத்தன்மையராய அமைச்சரை ஆராய்ந்து தனக்குத் துணையாகக் கொள்க."],["manakkudavar","The king should be able to outplot and kill those who plot to kill him.","அரசன் தன்னைச் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்ல நினைப்பாரைத் தானுஞ் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்லவல்லவனாதல்"],["kalaignar","The ruler benefits by keeping the wise around him.",""]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:446":{"book":"kural","num":446,"t":["தக்கா ரினத்தனாய்த் தானொழுக வல்லானைச்","செற்றார் செயக்கிடந்த தில்"],"p":"There will be nothing left for enemies to do, against him who has the power of acting (so as to secure) the fellowship of worthy men.","r":"There is nothing enemies can do against one able to carry himself as one of the company of the worthy.","n":"தான் ஒழுக வல்லான்: having good company isn't enough; he too must act accordingly. It is the ability that protects, not only the associates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:447":{"book":"kural","num":447,"t":["இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வரை யாரே","கெடுக்குந் தகைமை யவர்"],"p":"Who are great enough to destroy him who has servants that have power to rebuke him ?","r":"Who has the standing to ruin those who keep as their support people who will rebuke them?","n":"ஆள்வாரை: such people must actually be employed and valued. Parimelazhagar adds that the rebuker needs to be free of the same faults and to love the king, so rebuke alone doesn't qualify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:448":{"book":"kural","num":448,"t":["இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்","கெடுப்பா ரிலானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"The king, who is without the guard of men who can rebuke him, will perish, even though there be no one to destroy him.","r":"A ruler without the guard of those who rebuke will perish even with no one to ruin him.","n":"Parimelazhagar's image is an elephant with no mahout, going off the path and ruining itself. கெடுப்பார் இலானும் makes enemies unnecessary to the story, and 447 and 448 form a pair, protection and exposure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:449":{"book":"kural","num":449,"t":["முதலிலார்க ஊதிய மில்லை மதலையாஞ்","சார்பிலார்க் கில்லை நிலை"],"p":"There can be no gain to those who have no capital; and in like manner there can be no permanence to those who are without the support of adherents.","r":"Those without capital have no profit; those without the support of a pillar have no standing.","n":"A merchant's image for a king's court. Allies are the principal rather than the return: everything else is earned on them. The verse doesn't limit itself to kings; the older commentators supply that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:450":{"book":"kural","num":450,"t":["பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்தே","நல்லார் தொடர்கை விடல்"],"p":"It is tenfold more injurious to abandon the friendship of the good, than to incur the hatred of the many.","r":"To let go one's tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many.","n":"Pope and Kalaignar read கைவிடல் as abandoning a tie, whereas Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it as failing to take the great as support. His reason is practical: there are ways out of many enemies, but none out of having no good company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:451":{"book":"kural","num":451,"t":["சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்"],"p":"(True) greatness fears the society of the base; it is only the low - minded who will regard them as friends.","r":"Greatness fears the small company; smallness at once takes it as kin.","n":"The verse gives the fear to greatness, not to a person lacking resolve. Parimelazhagar explains it through self-knowledge: the wise know their judgment can bend, while the small see only likeness and suspect nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:452":{"book":"kural","num":452,"t":["நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர்திரிந் தற்றாகும் மாந்தர்க்கு","இனத்தியல்ப தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"As water changes (its nature), from the nature of the soil (in which it flows), so will the character of men resemble that of their associates.","r":"As water changes by the nature of the land, so a person's understanding becomes the nature of their company.","n":"Note the subject: it is அறிவு that changes, not behaviour or reputation. Parimelazhagar sharpens the image, with water pure in the sky and only turning on contact, and so with understanding held alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:453":{"book":"kural","num":453,"t":["மனத்தானாம் மாந்தர்க் குணர்ச்சி இனத்தானாம்","இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்"],"p":"The power of knowing is from the mind; (but) his character is from that of his associates.","r":"A person's perception comes from the mind; the word 'such a one' comes from their company.","n":"A clean division of labour: faculty is inner and reputation is social. Parimelazhagar assigns the mind only basic sense-awareness and gives all cultivated judgment to company, a step further than the verse says. Manakkudavar makes the word specifically blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:454":{"book":"kural","num":454,"t":["மனத்து ளதுபோலக் காட்டி ஒருவற்கு","இனத்துள தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"Wisdom appears to rest in the mind, but it really exists to a man in his companions.","r":"Understanding, while showing itself as though in the mind, lies for a person in their company.","n":"The verse is one of the Kural's most radical on the self: where understanding appears to be is not where it is. Parimelazhagar's 'the mind is where it shows, the company is its root' makes it explicit. The verse doesn't explain why.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:455":{"book":"kural","num":455,"t":["மனந்தூய்மை செய்வினை தூய்மை இரண்டும்","இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்"],"p":"Chaste company is the staff on which come, these two things, viz, purity of mind and purity of conduct.","r":"Purity of mind and purity of deeds, these two, come leaning on purity of company.","n":"தூவா is a rare word, which Parimelazhagar glosses as a support and defends with a citation. The claim is causal: company is the thing the other two rest on, not a third kind of purity alongside them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:456":{"book":"kural","num":456,"t":["மனந்தூயார்க் கெச்சம்நன் றாகும் இனந்தூயார்க்கு","இல்லைநன் றாகா வினை"],"p":"To the pure-minded there will be a good posterity. By those whose associates are pure, no deeds will be done that are not good.","r":"Those pure of mind have a good எச்சம்; for those pure of company there is no deed that doesn't turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","எச்சம் is children: effects don't differ from their causes.","மனம் தூயராயினார்க்கு மக்கட்பேறு நன்று ஆகும்;"],["manakkudavar","எச்சம் is the wealth that remains after one.","மனநல்லார்க்குப் பின்பு நிற்கும் காண முதலான பொருள்கள் நல்லவாம்;"],["kalaignar","எச்சம் is fame.",""]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:457":{"book":"kural","num":457,"t":["மனநலம் மன்னுயிர்க் காக்கம் இனநலம்","எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்"],"p":"Goodness of mind will give wealth, and good society will bring with it all praise, to men.","r":"Goodness of mind gains a person prosperity; goodness of company gives all praise as well.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason for the second clause is neat: those able to give praise are the good themselves, and they are the company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:458":{"book":"kural","num":458,"t":["மனநலம் நன்குடைய ராயினும் சான்றோர்க்கு","இனநலம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Although they may have great (natural) goodness of mind, yet good society will tend to strengthen it.","r":"Though the worthy have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a protection to them.","n":"The verse makes its concession to the mind here, then refuses to let it stand alone. Even சான்றோர் are not exempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Good company keeps nurturing a goodness of mind that came from earlier good deeds.","அந் நல்வினை உள்வழியும் மனநலத்தை வளர்த்து வருதலின்"],["manakkudavar","The protection is social: good company keeps the worthy from being looked down on.","இனநலம் இகழ்ச்சி வாராமற் காக்குமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:459":{"book":"kural","num":459,"t":["மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமைமற் றஃதும்","இனநலத்தின் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Future bliss is (the result) of goodness of mind; and even this acquires strength from the society of the good.","r":"From goodness of mind comes the next world, and even that gains strength from goodness of company.","n":"The verse names மறுமை in plain terms in the book on polity. Parimelazhagar notes that goodness of mind can slip from time to time, and good company holds it steady.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns joy in the next life, which the mind's goodness yields and good company secures.","ஒருவற்கு மனநன்மையானே மறுமை இன்பம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","He doesn't mention the next life. The verse is about the support that company gives to a firm mind.",""]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:460":{"book":"kural","num":460,"t":["நல்லினத்தி னூங்குந் துணையில்லை தீயினத்தின்","அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"There is no greater help than the company of the good; there is no greater source of sorrow than the company of the wicked.","r":"There is no greater support than good company and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the chapter's title, avoiding small company, has been argued mainly from the reverse: by praising good company. Only this last verse states both sides together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:461":{"book":"kural","num":461,"t":["அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ஆகி வழிபயக்கும்","ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let a man reflect on what will be lost, what will be acquired and (from these) what will be his ultimate gain, and (then, let him) act.","r":"Weigh what will be lost, what will arise, and the gain that comes of it later, and then act.","n":"The verse separates the immediate return from later gain. Parimelazhagar draws out the consequence: a deed that only breaks even now may still be worth doing if the later gain is there. So this is not a plain cost-benefit rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:462":{"book":"kural","num":462,"t":["தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு","அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to (be attained by) those who, before they act, reflect well themselves, and thoroughly consider (the matter) with chosen friends.","r":"Nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining it with chosen company and considering it themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the two verbs in order: first with the company, then alone. So consulting doesn't replace judgment. தெரிந்த also makes the counsellors themselves the first thing chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","inam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:463":{"book":"kural","num":463,"t":["ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை","ஊக்கார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Wise men will not, in the hopes of profit, undertake works that will consume their principal.","r":"Those with understanding don't undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is political: marching to take another's land and losing one's own. He also reads செய்வினை as covering even work already begun, so sunk effort is no excuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:464":{"book":"kural","num":464,"t":["தெளிவி லதனைத் தொடங்கார் இளிவென்னும்","ஏதப்பாடு அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"Those who fear reproach will not commence anything which has not been (thoroughly considered) and made clear to them.","r":"Those who fear the affliction called disgrace don't begin what isn't clear to them.","n":"The stated motive is shame, not loss. Parimelazhagar explains தொடங்கார்: once begun, a thing can't be dropped midway, so scorn would be added to the failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:465":{"book":"kural","num":465,"t":["வகையறச் சூழா தெழுதல் பகைவரைப்","பாத்திப் படுப்பதோ ராறு"],"p":"One way to promote the prosperity of an enemy, is (for a king) to set out (to war) without having thoroughly weighed his ability (to cope with its chances).","r":"Rising up without thinking through every aspect is one way of bedding your enemy in good soil.","n":"பாத்தி is a garden bed. The sting is that the hasty attacker does the enemy's cultivating for them: a failed attack strengthens its target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:466":{"book":"kural","num":466,"t":["செய்தக்க அல்ல செயக் கெடும் செய்தக்க","செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"He will perish who does not what is not fit to do; and he also will perish who does not do what it is fit to do.","r":"One perishes by doing what is unfit to do, and perishes too by not doing what is fit.","n":"Omission gets the same verdict as commission. தக்க is not obviously moral: Parimelazhagar's list of the unfit includes the too-laborious, the useless and the doubtful, which makes it prudential.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:467":{"book":"kural","num":467,"t":["எண்ணித் துணிக கருமம் துணிந்தபின்","எண்ணுவம் என்பது இழுக்கு"],"p":"Consider, and then undertake a matter; after having undertaken it, to say \"We will consider,\" is folly.","r":"Think and then resolve on a task; to say 'we'll think after resolving' is a lapse.","n":"What is to be thought about isn't stated. For Parimelazhagar it is the means, the four strategies; for Manakkudavar it is the result. The first assumes the deed is already chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:468":{"book":"kural","num":468,"t":["ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம் பலர்நின்று","போற்றினும் பொத்துப் படும்"],"p":"The work, which is not done by suitable methods, will fail though many stand to uphold it.","r":"Effort not made in the right way will collapse, even if many stand by to protect it.","n":"The verse corrects the chapter 45 picture of allies as near-sufficient: support can't save effort that is on the wrong path. Parimelazhagar spells out which means suit which kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","muyarchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:469":{"book":"kural","num":469,"t":["நன்றாற்ற லுள்ளுந் தவுறுண்டு அவரவர்","பண்பறிந் தாற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"There are failures even in acting well, when it is done without knowing the various dispositions of men.","r":"Even in doing good there is error, when it is done without knowing each person's character.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்று is the good stratagem, gifts and sweet words, used on rival kings, and the error is that the task fails.","வேற்று வேந்தர் மாட்டு நன்றான உபாயம் செய்தற்கண்ணும் குற்றம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","நன்று is kindness to anyone, and misjudged kindness comes back as harm.",""]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:470":{"book":"kural","num":470,"t":["எள்ளாத எண்ணிச் செயல்வேண்டும் தம்மோடு","கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு"],"p":"Let a man reflect, and do things which bring no reproach; the world will not approve, with him, of things which do not become of his position to adopt.","r":"Think and do what won't be scorned; the world doesn't accept what doesn't accord with oneself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","pugazh","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is tactical fit: the strong shouldn't use the means of the weak, such as gifts, and the weak shouldn't use force.","தாம் வலியராய் வைத்து மெலியார்க்கு உரிய கொடுத்தல் முதலிய மூன்றனைச் செய்தலும்,"],["manakkudavar","The issue is propriety: even if a thing can be accomplished, do only what others won't despise.","முடியுமாயினும், பிறராலிகழப்படாதவற்றை எண்ணிச் செய் தல் வேண்டும்;"]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:471":{"book":"kural","num":471,"t":["வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும்","துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Let (one) weigh well the strength of the deed (he purposes to do), his own strength, the strength of his enemy, and the strength of the allies (of both), and then let him act.","r":"Weigh the strength of the deed, one's own strength, the opponent's strength and the strength of allies, and then act.","n":"The first item is surprising: the deed itself has strength. Parimelazhagar adds a strict rule the verse doesn't state, that one should act only if one's own side is greater, and avoid even an equal contest, since victory there is doubtful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:472":{"book":"kural","num":472,"t":["ஒல்வ தறிவது அறிந்ததன் கண்தங்கிச்","செல்வார்க்குச் செல்லாதது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing which may not be accomplished by those who, before they attack (an enemy), make themselves acquainted with their own ability, and with whatever else is (needful) to be known, and apply themselves wholly to their object.","r":"For those who know what they can do and what must be known, and proceed resting in that, nothing fails.","n":"The chapter's only verse of confidence, and it hangs on அதன்கண் தங்கி, where the commentators pull in nearly opposite directions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Resting means total commitment: fixing mind, speech and body on the task while marching.","எப்பொழுதும் மன, மொழி, மெய்களை அதன் கண் வைத்துப் பகைமேல் செல்லும் அரசர்க்கு;"],["manakkudavar","Resting means restraint: staying within the measure of what one knows one can do.","அதன் பின்பு அவ்வளவிலே நின்று, ஒழுகுவராயின்,"]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:473":{"book":"kural","num":473,"t":["உடைத்தம் வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி","இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்"],"p":"There are many who, ignoran t of their (want of) power (to meet it), have haughtily set out to war, and broken down in the midst of it.","r":"Many, not knowing the strength they have, rose on enthusiasm and were broken midway.","n":"A rare verse appealing to history, பலர். ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி uses the very quality demanded of kings in 382 as the cause of the fall. Not knowing runs both ways too, as உடைத்தம் வலி means the strength they do have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:474":{"book":"kural","num":474,"t":["அமைந்தாங் கொழுகான் அளவறியான் தன்னை","வியந்தான் விரைந்து கெடும்"],"p":"He will quickly perish who, ignorant of his own resources flatters himself of his greatness, and does not live in peace with his neighbours.","r":"One who doesn't live in accord, doesn't know his measure and admires himself perishes quickly.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes ஆங்கு அமைந்து about neighbouring kings: one must be either a friend or an enemy who knows his strength. Others read it as a general composure, or as respect for people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:475":{"book":"kural","num":475,"t":["பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும் அப்பண்டஞ்","சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்"],"p":"The axle tree of a bandy, loaded only with peacocks' feathers will break, if it be greatly overloaded.","r":"Even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle, if that load is piled too high.","n":"The two உம் make both the cart strong and the load light. The verse speaks only of the cart, and the allegory is supplied by the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The feathers are weak enemies: even the strong fail when many combine against them.","'எளியர்' என்று பலரோடு பகைகொள்வான். தான் வலியனே ஆயினும் அவர் தொக்க வழி வலியழியும்;"],["manakkudavar","The cart is a fortress: those who trust in strong forts learn that defences fail if the enemy grows too much.","இஃது அரண் மிகுதல் நன் றென்றிருப்பார்க்குப் பகைமிகின் அரண் நில்லா தென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:476":{"book":"kural","num":476,"t":["நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார் அஃதிறந் தூக்கின்","உயிர்க்கிறுதி ஆகி விடும்"],"p":"There will be an end to the life of him who, having climbed out to the end of a branch, ventures to go further.","r":"Those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and strive further will end their life.","n":"The climber has done well to get there, and that is the danger: this is a warning to the successful. The verse is purely image. Parimelazhagar applies it to a king who has gone as far as he can on a campaign, and Manakkudavar has him turn back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:477":{"book":"kural","num":477,"t":["ஆற்றின் அறவறிந்து ஈக அதுபொருள்","போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி"],"p":"Let a man know the measure of his ability (to give), and let him give accordingly; such giving is the way to preserve his property.","r":"Give in the proper way, knowing the measure; that is the way of guarding wealth and bestowing it.","n":"The verse fuses the two usually opposed, போற்றி and வழங்கு, into one way. Parimelazhagar's fixed ratio, two parts for spending, one for reserves and one for giving, comes from Tirikadugam, not the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:478":{"book":"kural","num":478,"t":["ஆகாறு அளவிட்டி தாயினுங் கேடில்லை","போகாறு அகலாக் கடை"],"p":"Even though the income (of a king) be small, it will not cause his (ruin), if his outgoings be not larger than his income.","r":"Though the channel of income be narrow, there is no ruin, if the channel of outgo isn't wider.","n":"ஆறு is a path or channel, which gives wealth a hydraulic image. Income's size doesn't determine ruin; only the ratio does. Parimelazhagar adds that even exact balance is safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:479":{"book":"kural","num":479,"t":["அளவற஧ந்து வாழாதான் வாழ்க்கை உளபோல","இல்லாகித் தோன்றாக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who lives without knowing the measure (of his property), will perish, even while it seems to continue.","r":"The life of one who doesn't live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without a trace.","n":"The verse has three stages: seeming, not being and not even seeming. Parimelazhagar points out that ruin isn't visible at the start, which is why the first stage is a seeming and the warning needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:480":{"book":"kural","num":480,"t":["உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புர வாண்மை","வளவரை வல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The measure of his wealth will quickly perish, whose liberality weighs not the measure of his property.","r":"Helpfulness that doesn't weigh the limit of what one has quickly ends the limit of one's wealth.","n":"Even the most praised social virtue gets a limit. Manakkudavar sees the objection coming: if spending must stay below income, how can one be helpful? This is presented as the answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:481":{"book":"kural","num":481,"t":["பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை இகல்வெல்லும்","வேந்தர்க்கு வேண்டும் பொழுது"],"p":"A crow will overcome an owl in the day time; so the king who would conquer his enemy must have (a suitable) time.","r":"By day the crow beats the owl; kings who would win a contest need the right time.","n":"The image makes strength relational, as the same two birds reverse by night, a symmetry Manakkudavar makes explicit. Parimelazhagar's definition of time is unexpectedly concrete: weather that lets an army travel without disease and with food and water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:482":{"book":"kural","num":482,"t":["பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் திருவினைத்","தீராமை ஆர்க்குங் கயிறு"],"p":"Acting at the right season, is a cord that will immoveably bind success (to a king).","r":"Acting in accord with the season is the rope that binds fortune so that it doesn't leave.","n":"A rope only makes sense for something that tends to run. Parimelazhagar draws this from தீராமை: fortune's nature is to leave. Kalaignar takes திருவினை differently, as the success of a good deed, but the older commentators read wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:483":{"book":"kural","num":483,"t":["அருவினை யென்ப உளவோ கருவியான்","காலம் அறிந்து செயின்"],"p":"Is there anything difficult for him to do, who acts, with (the right) instruments at the right time ?","r":"Are there things said to be hard, if one acts with the right means and knows the time?","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that கருவியான் is there to say that even with the means, time is still needed: the tools don't make timing unnecessary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:484":{"book":"kural","num":484,"t":["ஞாலம் கருதினுங் கைகூடுங் காலம்","கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்"],"p":"Though (a man) should meditate (the conquest of) the world, he may accomplish it if he acts in the right time, and at the right place.","r":"Even the world will come to hand if one thinks of the time and acts by the place.","n":"This verse joins time and place and forms a bridge into the next chapter. The promise is grand, and the chapter's next verse immediately makes the same ambition the reason for doing nothing yet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:485":{"book":"kural","num":485,"t":["காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது","ஞாலம் கருது பவர்"],"p":"They who thoughtfully consider and wait for the (right) time (for action), may successfully meditate (the conquest of) the world.","r":"Those who have their mind on the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time.","n":"Greater ambition means more waiting, not less. Parimelazhagar adds that this applies even if one's strength is already superior, and identifies இருப்பு as a recognised policy, one of six, the opposite of marching out. கலங்காது could go with either the waiting or the aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:486":{"book":"kural","num":486,"t":["ஊக்க முடையான் ஒடுக்கம் பொருதகர்","தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து"],"p":"The self-restraint of the energetic (while waiting for a suitable opportunity), is like the drawing back of a fighting-ram in order to butt.","r":"The holding back of the person of energy is like a fighting ram drawing back in order to charge.","n":"It reframes restraint as part of attack, so ஒடுக்கம் doesn't signal a lack of ஊக்கம். Kalaignar adds that this is silence in the face of cruelty and that it isn't from fear, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:487":{"book":"kural","num":487,"t":["பொள்ளென ஆங்கே புறம்வேரார் காலம்பார்த்து","உள்வேர்ப்பர் ஒள்ளி யவர்"],"p":"The wise will not immediately and hastily shew out their anger; they will watch their time, and restrain it within.","r":"The bright don't flare up outwardly at once; watching the time, they burn within.","n":"The anger isn't removed, only stored. Parimelazhagar gives the unusual reason that it must continue within: if it died away, one could not later stop the offender. Anger here is a resource.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaalam","vekuli","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:488":{"book":"kural","num":488,"t":["செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை","காணின் கிழக்காம் தலை"],"p":"If one meets his enemy, let him show him all respect, until the time for his destruction is come; when that is come, his head will be easily b rought low.","r":"Seeing those who would harm you, bear them; when you see their final limit, the head goes down.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is to be carried or whose head falls. Manakkudavar's தலையினாற் சுமக்க is the literal image behind Parimelazhagar's reading: carry them on the head, then tip them off.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Actually bow to the enemy until his time comes. Your submission makes him careless, and that is why his head falls.","பணியவே, அக்காலம் வந்திறும் வழி அவர் தகைவின்றி இறுவர்."],["kalaignar","Bear one's own feelings of enmity patiently, and the enemy falls by himself.",""]],"ch":"k:49"},"k:489":{"book":"kural","num":489,"t":["எய்தற் கரியது இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே","செய்தற் கரிய செயல்"],"p":"If a rare opportunity occurs, while it lasts, let a man do that which is rarely to be accomplished (but for such an opportunity).","r":"When what is hard to get has come, do at that very point what is hard to do.","n":"The double அரிய pairs them: rare time, rare deed. Parimelazhagar makes the best observation on அந்நிலையே: the moment won't stay. All the patience of the preceding verses exists for this one instant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:490":{"book":"kural","num":490,"t":["கொக்கொக்க கூம்பும் பருவத்து மற்றதன்","குத்தொக்க சீர்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"At the time when one should use self-control, let him restrain himself like a heron; and, let him like it, strike, when there is a favourable opportunity.","r":"When it is the season to fold up, be like the heron; when the moment is right, be like its strike.","n":"One image holds both modes, and the verse compares the person first to the bird and then only to the bird's stab. Parimelazhagar notes that the heron looks as though lifeless while it waits, which adds a touch of menace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:491":{"book":"kural","num":491,"t":["தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும் எள்ளற்க முற்றும்","இடங்கண்ட பின்அல் லது"],"p":"Let not (a king) despise (an enemy), nor undertake any thing (against him), until he has obtained (a suitable) place for besieging him.","r":"Begin no deed and scorn nothing until you have seen the ground for it in full.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","முற்றும் is a verb, to besiege: don't act against an enemy until you have ground for a siege.","பகைவரை முற்றுதற்கு ஆவதோர் இடம் பெற்றபின் அல்லது;"],["manakkudavar","முற்றும் is to complete: don't begin any deed until you see where it can be finished, and don't treat it as easy.","முடியுமிடங் கண்டாலல்லது, யாதொரு வினையுந் தொடங்கா தொழிக; எளிதென்றிகழாதொழிக,"],["kalaignar","முற்றும் is 'fully': choose a completely right place.",""]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:492":{"book":"kural","num":492,"t":["முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பி னவர்க்கும் அரண்சேர்ந்தாம்","ஆக்கம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"Even to those who are men of power and expedients, an attack in connection with a fortification will yield many advantages.","r":"Even for those of contentious strength, the gain that comes by joining a fortress gives many benefits.","n":"Even the strong: உம் concedes the objection. Manakkudavar takes the fort as one's own place, and Parimelazhagar, who thinks of a king on campaign, as a siege position. Either way, it is a protection for those who think they don't need it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:493":{"book":"kural","num":493,"t":["ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப இடனறிந்து","போற்றார்கண் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"Even the powerless will become powerful and conquer, if they select a proper field (of action), and guard themselves, while they make war on their enemies.","r":"Even those without strength become strong and win, if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves.","n":"The ஆற்றி in ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி plays on the same root: place does not merely help the weak but turns them into the strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:494":{"book":"kural","num":494,"t":["எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர் இடனறிந்து","துன்னியார் துன்னிச் செயின்"],"p":"If they who draw near (to fight) choose a suitable place to approach (their enemy), the latter, will have to relinquish the thought which they once entertained, of conquering them.","r":"Those who had planned will lose their plan, if those who approach know the place and hold to it.","n":"The loss is of a thought: the enemy's victory has been defeated before fighting starts.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துன்னியார் are the kings who have marched there and who stay close to their fort.","தாம் வினை செய்தற்கு ஏற்ற இடத்தினை அறிந்து சென்ற அரசர்;"],["manakkudavar","துன்னியார் are allies acting with one mind, so the verse is about concord among friends as well as place.","வினைசெய்யும் இடமறிந்து நட்டோரானவர் மனம் பொருந்திச் செய்வாராயின்."]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:495":{"book":"kural","num":495,"t":["நெடும்புனலுள் வெல்லும் முதலை அடும்புனலின்","நீங்கின் அதனைப் பிற"],"p":"In deep water, a crocodile will conquer (all other animals); but if it leave the water, other animals will conquer it.","r":"A crocodile wins in deep water; if it leaves the water, others kill it.","n":"The very same animal, and the reversal is total: பிற, anything at all. Parimelazhagar's maxim is that everyone is strong on their own ground, and the practical point is to fight where the enemy can't stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:496":{"book":"kural","num":496,"t":["கடலோடா கால்வல் நெடுந்தேர் கடலோடும்","நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து"],"p":"Wide chariots, with mighty wheels, will not run on the ocean; neither will ships that the traverse ocean, move on the earth.","r":"Long chariots with strong wheels don't run on the sea; ships that run on the sea don't run on land.","n":"The verse states an obvious fact without comment, and the plainness is the point. Parimelazhagar notes the qualities listed, strong wheels and great size, as just those that don't transfer. He and Manakkudavar read it as being about fitting equipment to the terrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:497":{"book":"kural","num":497,"t":["அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா எஞ்சாமை","எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்"],"p":"You will need no other aid than fearlessness, if you thoroughly reflect (on what you are to do), and select (a suitable) place for your operations.","r":"No support is needed but fearlessness, if you think everything through and act by the place.","n":"This verse suggests an answer to how 382's fearlessness can sit with 428's fear of what should be feared: fearlessness is what remains once the thinking is complete, எஞ்சாமை எண்ணி. Parimelazhagar glosses அஞ்சாமை as firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","therithal","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:498":{"book":"kural","num":498,"t":["சிறுபடையான் செல்லிடம் சேரின் உறுபடையான்","ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்"],"p":"The power of one who has a large army will perish, if he goes into ground where only a small army can act.","r":"If one with a large army enters where one with a small army can move, his zeal is destroyed.","n":"The large army's size becomes the problem. Parimelazhagar explains that it can't act together and has no practice on that ground. What is destroyed is ஊக்கம், the fighting spirit, not only the force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:499":{"book":"kural","num":499,"t":["சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும் மாந்தர்","உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a hazardous thing to attack men in their own country, although they may neither have power nor a good fortress.","r":"Even if they have no fortress or standing, it is hard to take on people on the land where they live.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the reason in psychology: attacked at home, such people won't resolve to flee; they will resolve to die, and even a large army breaks on that. The verse says மாந்தர், people, not soldiers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:500":{"book":"kural","num":500,"t":["காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும் கண்ணஞ்சா","வேலாள் முகத்த களிறு"],"p":"A fox can kill a fearless, warrior-faced elephant, if it go into mud in which its legs sink down.","r":"The jackal kills, in mud where feet sink, the unflinching elephant with a warrior's face.","n":"The chapter closes with its most extreme reversal, the jackal over the elephant. கண் அஞ்சா also splits the commentators, between a beast unafraid at a glance and one that won't obey its handlers.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elephant is one that strikes down spearmen. He rejects the variant வேலாழ், because a wounded elephant makes the jackal's success unremarkable.","'வேலாழ் முகத்த' என்று பாடம் ஓதுவாரும் உளர்; வேற்படை குளித்த முகத்தவாயின் அதுவும் நரி அடுதற்கு ஏதுவாய் முடிதலின், அது பாடம் அன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The elephant's face has spears sunk in it: he follows the variant reading.","கண்ணஞ்சாத வேலழுத்தப்பட்ட முகத்தினையுடைய களிற்றைக்"]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:501":{"book":"kural","num":501,"t":["அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம் நான்கின்","திறந்தெரிந்து தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let (a minister) be chosen, after he has been tried by means of these four things, viz,-his virtue, (love of) money, (love of) sexual pleasure, and tear of (losing) life.","r":"A person is to be trusted after their nature has been examined through four things: virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life.","n":"My observation: the usual fourth term after virtue, wealth and pleasure is liberation, and here it is fear for life, the most worldly substitute possible. Parimelazhagar defends the wording against a variant, உயிரெச்சம், saying others changed it for not knowing the Sanskrit source.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thelivu","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are four entrapment tests from Sanskrit polity, in which agents tempt the minister with staged plots, wealth, a woman and threats. Passing shows he won't change in future.","உபதை நான்கின் திறத்தான் மனவியல்பு ஆராய்ந்தால் பின்பு தெளியப்படும்."],["kalaignar","These are qualities to look for: firm in virtue, honest with money, not lost in pleasure and unafraid for his life.",""]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:502":{"book":"kural","num":502,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி வடுப்பரியும்","நாணுடையான் சுட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"(The king's) choice should (fall) on him, who is of good family, who is free from faults, and who has the modesty which fears the wounds (of sin).","r":"Trust lies in one born of good family, free of faults and with a shame that recoils from any blemish.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kudi","kutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","High birth is one of the qualifications in its own right, alongside the others.","உயர்ந்த குடியில் பிறந்து; குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி"],["kalaignar","He reverses the logic: one who is faultless and ashamed of wrong is the one to be considered high-born.",""]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:503":{"book":"kural","num":503,"t":["அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார் கண்ணும் தெரியுங்கால்","இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு"],"p":"When even men, who have studied the most difficult works, and who are free from faults, are (carefully) examined, it is a rare thing to find them without ignorance.","r":"Even in those who have studied the hard books and are flawless, it is rare, when examined, to find no blankness.","n":"வெளிறு is whiteness or emptiness, a striking word for ignorance: a pale spot in a person otherwise solid. Coming right after the demand for faultlessness, this verse admits no one fully passes, which prepares 504. Parimelazhagar takes it as occasional, due to an unsteady mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:504":{"book":"kural","num":504,"t":["குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி அவற்றுள்","மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) consider (a man's) good qualities, as well as his faults, and then judge (of his character) by that which prevails.","r":"Examine the qualities, examine the faults too, examine which of these predominates, and take that.","n":"நாடி three times. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is what makes the verse humane: no one in the world is only good or only bad. Dominance can be either in weight or in number, so this isn't simple counting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:505":{"book":"kural","num":505,"t":["பெருமைக்கும் ஏனைச் சிறுமைக்கும் தத்தம்","கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்"],"p":"A man's deeds are the touchstone of his greatness and littleness.","r":"For greatness and for smallness alike, one's own deeds are the touchstone.","n":"The ஏ in கருமமே excludes other tests, which sits awkwardly with 502's demand for birth. Parimelazhagar says explicitly that birth, quality and understanding produce the greatness and that deeds are only the final test of it. Manakkudavar turns it into practice: give a small task first, then promote.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:506":{"book":"kural","num":506,"t":["அற்றாரைத் தேறுதல் ஓம்புக மற்றவர்","பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி"],"p":"Let (a king) avoid choosing men who have no relations; such men have no attachment, and thereforehave no fear of crime.","r":"Beware of trusting those who are cut off: they have no attachments and aren't ashamed of blame.","n":"The logic is that attachment makes a person accountable: without ties there is nothing that shame can work on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","sutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றார் are those with no kin. Without them a person lacks the worldly custom that restrains him from blame.","சுற்றம் இல்லாரைத் தெளிதலை ஒழிக;"],["manakkudavar","அற்றார் are those without moral conduct.","ஒழுக்கமற்றாரைத் தேறுதலைத் தவிர்க;"],["kalaignar","They are those without principles, not those without kin.",""]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:507":{"book":"kural","num":507,"t":["காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்","பேதைமை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"To choose ignorant men, through partiality, is the height of folly.","r":"To trust the ignorant with fondness as your support brings every kind of folly.","n":"Manakkudavar shows this is an argument, naming Parasara as holding that a king may trust those who love him. The verse refutes that. Parimelazhagar notes the folly is the king's, not only the appointee's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:508":{"book":"kural","num":508,"t":["தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான் வழிமுறை","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Sorrow that will not leave even his posterity will come upon him chooses a stranger whose character he has not known.","r":"One who trusts an outsider without examining him brings trouble that doesn't end, even down his line.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","therithal","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழிமுறை means descendants: the suffering passes to them, as the kin fall under the enemy.","அத்தெளிவு தன் வழிமுறையினும் நீங்காத துன்பத்தைக் கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","வழிமுறை means hereditary succession: the error is trusting an outsider as if he were of the ministerial line, and those from such lines may be trusted.","பிறனை ஆராயாதே வழிமுறையென்று தெளிந்தவனுக்கு அத் தெளிவு,"]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:509":{"book":"kural","num":509,"t":["தே றற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின்","தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்"],"p":"Let (a king) choose no one without previous consideration; after he has made his choice, let him unhesitatingly select for each such duties as are appropriate.","r":"Trust no one without examining them; having examined, trust what is to be trusted.","n":"The commentators limit the second half differently. For Parimelazhagar, trust comes for tasks suited to each person's capacity, while Manakkudavar has trust in one matter extend to all matters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:510":{"book":"kural","num":510,"t":["தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To make choice of one who has not been examined, and to entertain doubts respecting one who has been chosen, will produce irremediable sorrow.","r":"Trusting without examining, and doubting one who has been trusted: both bring endless trouble.","n":"The second error is the unusual one. Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: the person senses it and thinks 'this won't last', so he slackens and becomes easy for enemies to detach. Doubt creates the very betrayal it fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:511":{"book":"kural","num":511,"t":["நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி நலம்புரிந்த","தன்மையான் ஆளப் படும்"],"p":"He should be employed (by a king), whose nature leads him to choose the good, after having weighed both the evil and the good in any undertaking.","r":"One whose nature, having looked at good and bad, has settled on the good is to be employed.","n":"Note the sequence: this person also looks at the bad. The verse doesn't ask for innocence. Parimelazhagar finds in the past tense புரிந்த a probationary stage: a trial task given first, then a more important one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:512":{"book":"kural","num":512,"t":["வாரி பெருக்கி வளம்படுத்து உற்றவை","ஆராய்வான் செய்க வினை"],"p":"Let him do (the king's) work who can enlarge the sources (of revenue), increase wealth and considerately prevent the accidents (which would destroy it).","r":"Let him do the work who enlarges the sources, increases the yield and looks into what befalls them.","n":"A purely administrative definition of a good servant, with no moral term. உற்றவை includes, for Parimelazhagar, what comes from the king himself, whose interference counts among the hazards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:513":{"book":"kural","num":513,"t":["அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை இந்நான்கும்","நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"Let the choice (of a king) fall upon him who largely possesses these four things, love, knowledge, a clear mind and freedom from covetousness.","r":"Trust lies in one who firmly has these four: love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire.","n":"Love comes first, even after the warnings of 507. Parimelazhagar directs it to the king and places the absence of desire at the moment when money comes into his hands, which is a very specific temptation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:514":{"book":"kural","num":514,"t":["எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும் வினைவகையான்","வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"Even when (a king) has tried them in every possible way, there are many men who change, from the nature of the works (in which they may be employed).","r":"Even when trusted after every kind of test, many people turn different through the nature of the work.","n":"The cause of change is the job itself, வினைவகையான். Office alters the person. Parimelazhagar's example is Kattiyankaran, the treacherous minister of Jivaka Chintamani, and he says it is rare for people to resist the pleasures of royal power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:515":{"book":"kural","num":515,"t":["அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு அல்லால் வினைதான்","சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"(A king's) work can only be accomplished by a man of wisdom and patient endurance; it is not of a nature to be given to one from mere personal attachment.","r":"Work itself isn't such as to be given to anyone except one who knows and endures and can do it, just because he is favoured.","n":"The grammar makes வினை the subject. Work has its own nature and doesn't admit of favourites. Parimelazhagar takes சிறந்தான் as someone dear to the king, and Kalaignar as someone merely thought excellent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:516":{"book":"kural","num":516,"t":["செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு","எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let (a king) act, after having considered the agent (whom he is to employ), the deed (he desires to do), and the time which is suitable to it.","r":"Examine the doer, examine the deed, and act understanding how they fit with the time.","n":"The doer comes before the deed, which is not the expected order. Parimelazhagar's last step is a judgment on the conjunction: at this time, one of this quality doing a deed of this nature will succeed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:517":{"book":"kural","num":517,"t":["இதனை இதனால் இவன்முடிக்கும் என்றாய்ந்து","அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்"],"p":"After having considered, \"this man can accomplish this, by these means\", let (the king) leave with him the discharge of that duty.","r":"Having reasoned 'this one will finish this by this means', leave that to him.","n":"The verse is all pointing, with இதனை, இதனால், இவன், then அதனை, அவன். The distal pronouns of the second line themselves enact the handing over: the thing moves away from the king. விடல் is a real letting go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:518":{"book":"kural","num":518,"t":["வினைக் குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை அவனை","அதற்குரிய னாகச் செயல்"],"p":"Having considered what work a man is fit for, let (the king) employ him in that work.","r":"After seeing a person is fit for the work, make him fully entitled to it.","n":"Recognition isn't enough: authority has to be conferred. Parimelazhagar makes it elevation so that he can carry the work out himself, and warns that without it the work fails. Kalaignar reads this as simply examining then appointing, which leaves it close to 517.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:519":{"book":"kural","num":519,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மைவே றாக","நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு"],"p":"Prosperity will leave (the king) who doubts the friendship of the man who steadily labours in the discharge of his duties.","r":"Fortune leaves one who takes differently the closeness of a person devoted to the work.","n":"A rare observation about what success at work produces: familiarity. Parimelazhagar's setting is sharp: rivals tell the king this man acts as if he were family, and the king takes it as disrespect. If he punishes it, no one will commit themselves again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:520":{"book":"kural","num":520,"t":["நாடோ றும் நாடுக மன்னன் வினைசெய்வான்","கோடாமை கோடா துலகு"],"p":"Let a king daily examine the conduct of his servants; if they do not act crookedly, the world will not act crookedly.","r":"Let the ruler look into it daily: if the one doing the work doesn't bend, the world won't bend.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the difficulty with trust and adds that the inspection should be done privately, without harming the person's entitlement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruler inspects the officer's conduct. The officer is the one point through which the whole world can be checked.","வினை செய்வான் கோடாது ஒழிய உலகம் கோடாது;"],["kalaignar","The state checks on workers' condition. If those who labour don't wither, the world's prosperity doesn't wither.",""]],"ch":"k:52"},"k:521":{"book":"kural","num":521,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமைபா ராட்டுதல்","சுற்றத்தார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Even when (a man's) property is all gone, relatives will act towards him with their accustomed (kindness).","r":"Even when all is gone, to keep honouring old ties is found only among kin.","n":"பழைமை points back to shared history. Parimelazhagar takes it as help the fallen man gave when he had means, which turns kin loyalty into a kind of remembered debt. Kalaignar turns the description into a definition: those with this trait are kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:522":{"book":"kural","num":522,"t":["விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின் அருப்பறா","ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If (a man's) relatives remain attached to him with unchanging love, it will be a source of ever-increasing wealth.","r":"If kin whose love doesn't lapse come together, they yield many gains that keep on branching.","n":"Pope's flower and fadeless blossom come from reading அருப்பு as a bud. The commentators read it as branching, கிளைத்தல், which gives a neat play: கிளை also means kin. So kin make wealth put out kin of its own, though that last step is my reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:523":{"book":"kural","num":523,"t":["அளவளா வில்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை குளவளாக்","கோடின்றி நீர்நிறைந் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of one who does not mingle freely with his relatives, will be like the filling of water in a spacious tank that has no banks.","r":"The life of one who doesn't mingle freely is like a broad tank filling with water, with no bank.","n":"The tank does fill: the failure isn't in earning. Without kin, the wealth has nothing to hold it and spreads away uselessly. அளவளாவு is intimacy, and Parimelazhagar explains it through mutual sharing of mind, so mere proximity isn't enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:524":{"book":"kural","num":524,"t":["சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படஒழுகல் செல்வந்தான்","பெற்றத்தால் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"To live surrounded by relatives, is the advantage to be derived from the acquisition of wealth.","r":"The fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin.","n":"It is striking how far this goes: a whole purpose for wealth in four words. Parimelazhagar's reason keeps it in Porul: it is what enables ruling free of enemies. Read without him, it is more tender than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:525":{"book":"kural","num":525,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் அடுக்கிய","சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படும்"],"p":"He will be surrounded by numerous relatives who manifests generosity and affability.","r":"One who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layer upon layer.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies these with the Sanskrit policies of dāna and sāma. So the tender acts of the household are also two of the four instruments of statecraft, which he used in 467 against enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","eegai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:526":{"book":"kural","num":526,"t":["பெருங்கொடையான் பேணான் வெகுளி அவனின்","மருங்குடையார் மாநிலத்து இல்"],"p":"No one, in all the world, will have so many relatives (about him), as he who makes large gift, and does not give way to anger.","r":"No one on earth has more of a following than one who gives greatly and doesn't cherish anger.","n":"பேணான் doesn't mean he has no anger, only that he doesn't tend it. Parimelazhagar even takes it as not exceeding what a king needs. In 487 anger is nursed within against enemies. The verses may simply be about different audiences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","eegai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:527":{"book":"kural","num":527,"t":["காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும் ஆக்கமும்","அன்னநீ ரார்க்கே உள"],"p":"The crows do not conceal (their prey), but will call out for others (to share with them) while they eat it; wealth will be with those who show a similar disposition (towards their relatives).","r":"Crows don't hide, they call and eat; gain too belongs only to those of that nature.","n":"A humble bird is the model for royal conduct. The verse's உம் says gain too comes this way, and Parimelazhagar concludes that sharing yields wealth as well as virtue and pleasure. Hiding, கரவா, is what is excluded, so a person's private hoard is the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:528":{"book":"kural","num":528,"t":["பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்","அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"Many relatives will live near a king, when they observe that he does not look on all alike, but that he looks on each man according to his merit.","r":"If a king looks at people not in common but by rank, many will live looking to that.","n":"A real problem: equal treatment drives away those who see themselves as higher. Parimelazhagar openly gives that as the reason.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Each kinsman is to be regarded according to his own standing; treating all alike would make the eminent leave.","எல்லாரையும் ஒரு தன்மையராக நோக்காது அரசன் தத்தம் தகுதிக்கு ஏற்ப நோக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about singling out one person: it is the advice to appoint a crown prince.","இஃது ஒருவனை இளவரசாக்க வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","All people are equal, and regard should follow ability, not rank.",""]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:529":{"book":"kural","num":529,"t":["தமராகிக் தற்றுறந்தார் சுற்றம் அமராமைக்","காரணம் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"Those who have been friends and have afterwards forsaken him, will return and join themselves (to him), when the cause of disagreement is not to be found in him.","r":"Those who were kin and left will return to kinship when the cause of discord is gone.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cause was in the king. If he removes it, they come back by themselves, as natural affection returns without being won again.","அவ்வமராமைக் காரணம் தன் மாட்டு இல்லையாகத் தானே உளதாம்."],["kalaignar","The cause was their pretext. They come back on realising it doesn't hold up.",""]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:530":{"book":"kural","num":530,"t":["உழைப்பிரிந்து காரணத்தின் வந்தானை வேந்தன்","இழைத் திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"When one may have left him, and for some cause has returned to him, let the king fulfil the object (for which he has come back) and thoughtfully receive him again.","r":"One who left and came back for a reason, the king should accept with care after attending to it.","n":"The contrast with 529 is subtle, as Parimelazhagar notes: there a reason for leaving is removed, and here there was none for leaving, only one for returning. He advises to grant what the person came for, but stay cautious: otherwise he may go to the enemy, but he has also shown no love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:531":{"book":"kural","num":531,"t":["இறந்த வெகுளியின் தீதே சிறந்த","உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு"],"p":"More evil than excessive anger, is forgetfulness which springs from the intoxication of great joy.","r":"The slackness that comes of the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger.","n":"A surprising ranking, because joy isn't usually the danger. சோர்வு is a letting go, and it comes when things go well. Parimelazhagar's reason is exact: anger sometimes at least kills an enemy, while this kills only oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:532":{"book":"kural","num":532,"t":["பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை அறிவினை","நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன் றாங்கு"],"p":"Forgetfulness will destroy fame, even as constant poverty destroys knowledge.","r":"Heedlessness kills fame as unremitting poverty kills understanding.","n":"The comparison is the striking claim. நிச்ச நிரப்பு is not poverty but chronic poverty, and Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar make it daily begging to fill the stomach. Understanding is treated as something that material conditions can destroy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:533":{"book":"kural","num":533,"t":["பொச்சாப்பார்க் கில்லை புகழ்மை அதுஉலகத்து","எப்பால்நூ லோர்க்கும் துணிவு"],"p":"Thoughtlessness will never acquire fame; and this tenet is upheld by all treatises in the world.","r":"The heedless have no fame: that is the settled view of writers of every kind in the world.","n":"The Kural rarely appeals to an all-schools consensus. Parimelazhagar explains why it can: every aim of life fails when one is heedless, so the claim isn't limited to polity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:534":{"book":"kural","num":534,"t":["அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை ஆங்கில்லை","பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு"],"p":"Just as the coward has no defence (by whatever fortifications ha may be surrounded), so the thoughtless has no good (whatever advantages he may possess).","r":"The fearful have no fortress; likewise the heedless have no good.","n":"A fort is useless to one whose fear is inside it: the verse places the failure in the mind, not in the walls. So the heedless may have resources, and they do them no good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:535":{"book":"kural","num":535,"t":["முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான் தன்பிழை","பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The thoughtless man, who provides not against the calamities that may happen, will afterwards repent for his fault.","r":"One who slipped by not guarding beforehand will later, when struck, grieve over his own error.","n":"Regret is presented as the penalty. தன்பிழை matters: there is no one else to blame. Parimelazhagar adds that the things that could have been guarded against are enemies' moves, made by watching for such lapses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:536":{"book":"kural","num":536,"t":["இழுக்காமை யார்மாட்டும் என்றும் வழுக்காமை","வாயின் அதுவொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing comparable with the possession of unfailing thoughtfulness at all times; and towards all persons.","r":"If not slipping, with anyone, at any time, is had without fail, nothing equals it.","n":"Three universals. Parimelazhagar assigns each: toward all people, in good times and bad, in every matter. The condition வாயின் concedes how seldom this is met. Manakkudavar takes it as not making errors in giving justice, a narrower sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:537":{"book":"kural","num":537,"t":["அரியஎன்று ஆகாத இல்லைபொச் சாவாக்","கருவியால் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to be accomplished, if a man set about it carefully, with unflinching endeavour.","r":"Nothing fails to be done on the ground of being hard, if done with the tool of unforgetting care.","n":"Attention is called a கருவி, a tool. Parimelazhagar notes that it is an inner instrument, the mind, set beside the external means of 483.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:538":{"book":"kural","num":538,"t":["புகழ்ந்தவை போற்றிச் செயல்வேண்டும் செய்யாது","இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு எழுமையும் இல்"],"p":"Let (a man) observe and do these things which have been praised (by the wise); if he neglects and fails to perform them, for him there will be no (happiness) throughout the seven births.","r":"What has been praised must be carefully done; those who slight it and don't do it have nothing through seven births.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","piravi","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is praised is what polity texts prescribe to kings, and lapsing means hell, as with neglect of caste duty.","நீதி நூலுடையார் இவை அரசர்க்கு உரியன என்று உயர்த்துக் கூறிய செயல்களைக் கடைப்பிடித்துச் செய்க;"],["manakkudavar","What is praised is virtue, as praised by the exalted, and the verse is about not neglecting it.","இஃது அறத்தின்கண் இகழாமற் செய்வது கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","They are one's duties, and neglect means no rise in life. He doesn't mention births.",""]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:539":{"book":"kural","num":539,"t":["இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக தாந்தம்","மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து"],"p":"Let (a king) think of those who have been ruined by neglect, when his mind is elated with joy.","r":"When swollen with one's own joy, think of those who were ruined by negligence.","n":"A practical remedy: history is used deliberately against one's own mood, at the moment it is hardest to think of it. Parimelazhagar notes that negligence and ruin arise together.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The memory is a corrective to mood: thinking 'we too would fall that way' stops one exulting.","கெட்டாரை உள்ளவே, 'நாமும் அவ்வாறே கெடுதும்' என்று அதன்கண் மைந்துறார் என்பது கருத்து"],["manakkudavar","It leads to a specific precaution: a king should have what he eats, wears and smears checked, because past kings died that way.","இஃது உண்பவை, உடுப்பவை, பூசுபவை சோதித்துக் கொள்க என்றது."]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:540":{"book":"kural","num":540,"t":["உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிதுமன் மற்றுந்தான்","உள்ளியது உள்ளப் பெறின்"],"p":"It is easy for (one) to obtain whatever he may think of, if he can again think of it.","r":"To gain what one has thought of is easy, if one can keep thinking of what one has thought of.","n":"The verse is built on repetition of உள்ளியது, which enacts its own lesson. Parimelazhagar catches the particle மன்: it hints what remains unsaid, that this is just what people can't do. So 'easy' is an ironic word. It is easy only in the way the conditional describes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","manam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:541":{"book":"kural","num":541,"t":["ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது இறைபுரிந்து யார்மாட்டும்","தேர்ந்துசெய் வஃதே முறை"],"p":"To examine into (the crimes which may be committed), to show no favour (to any one), to desire to act with impartiality towards all, and to inflict (such punishments) as may be wisely resolved on, constitute rectitude.","r":"To investigate, show no favour to anyone, hold to what is fitting for a ruler and act after consideration: that is justice.","n":"The definition is mainly procedural, four steps with no mention of outcome. கண்ணோடாது is a strong word for a book with a whole chapter praising கண்ணோட்டம். Parimelazhagar extends யார்மாட்டும் to those dearer than life, and Manakkudavar to one's own kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:542":{"book":"kural","num":542,"t":["வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம் மன்னவன்","கோல் நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி"],"p":"When there is rain, the living creation thrives; and so when the king rules justly, his subjects thrive.","r":"All the world lives looking to the sky; the people live looking to the ruler's sceptre.","n":"நோக்கி has the sense of dependence as well as looking up. Parimelazhagar narrows the point: the sky gives food and the sceptre gives security, and without security the food is no use to the people. So the sceptre is the more basic of the two for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:543":{"book":"kural","num":543,"t":["அந்தணர் நூற்கும் அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய்","நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்"],"p":"The sceptre of the king is the firm support of the Vedas of the Brahmin, and of all virtues therein described.","r":"The ruler's sceptre stands as the origin of the books of the அந்தணர் and of virtue.","n":"ஆதி is a bold word. It makes politics the source of learning and morals, the opposite of what might be expected. Parimelazhagar notices this, because the Vedas are eternal for him, and explains ஆதி as meaning they don't function without the sceptre.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","aram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அந்தணர் நூல் is the Veda: beginningless itself, but it needs the sceptre in order to operate.","வேதமும் அறனும் - அநாதியாயினும், செங்கோல் இல்வழி நடவா ஆகலின்,"],["kalaignar","They are books of the virtuous, with no reference to the Veda or to Brahmins.",""]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:544":{"book":"kural","num":544,"t":["குடிதழீஇக் கோலோச்சும் மாநில மன்னன்","அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will constantly embrace the feet of the great king who rules over his subjects with love.","r":"The world stands embracing the feet of the great ruler who embraces his people and wields the sceptre.","n":"The same word தழீஇ appears twice, and the reciprocity is the point: the king embraces his people and the world embraces his feet. The embrace from above must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:545":{"book":"kural","num":545,"t":["இயல்புளிக் கோலோச்சும் மன்னவன் நாட்ட","பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு"],"p":"Rain and plentiful crops will ever dwell together in the country of the king who sways his sceptre with justice.","r":"Rain and harvest gather together in the land of the ruler who wields the sceptre according to its nature.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal effect: sky and earth work together and yield abundance where rule is just.","வானும் நிலனும் சேரத் தொழிற்பட்டு வளம் சுரக்கும் என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a comparison: just government is like seasonal rain and a fine harvest.",""]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:546":{"book":"kural","num":546,"t":["வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது மன்னவன்","கோலதூஉங் கோடா தெனின்"],"p":"It is not the javelin that gives victory, but the king's sceptre, if it do no injustice.","r":"It is not the spear that gives victory but the ruler's sceptre, if it doesn't bend.","n":"வேல் and கோல் rhyme and look alike as long shafts. The comparison also lets the sceptre be read as itself a weapon. The verse makes the claim without saying how justice wins battles. Parimelazhagar only says a spear strikes true when the sceptre is straight, and quotes a Sangam poem in support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:547":{"book":"kural","num":547,"t":["இறைகாக்கும் வையகம் எல்லாம் அவனை","முறைகாக்கும் முட்டாச் செயின்"],"p":"The king defends the whole world; and justice, when administered without defect, defends the king.","r":"The ruler guards all the world; justice guards him, if he doesn't falter in it.","n":"முட்டாச் செயின் means even when it costs. Parimelazhagar's examples are the most extreme ones in Tamil memory: the king who gave judgment on his own son and the king who cut off his own hand, both from the Silappatikaram. That shows what 'not faltering' is taken to require.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:548":{"book":"kural","num":548,"t":["எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","தண்பதத்தான் தானே கெடும்"],"p":"The king who gives not facile audience (to those who approach him), and who does not examine and pass judgment (on their complaints), will perish in disgrace.","r":"A ruler who isn't easily approached and who doesn't examine and do justice falls low and perishes by himself.","n":"தானே கெடும் is close to 448: the ruin needs no enemy. Parimelazhagar's 'low state' is sin and blame, and he supports it by quoting that virtue is death to those who have done wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:549":{"book":"kural","num":549,"t":["குடிபுறங் காத்தோம்பிக் குற்றம் கடிதல்","வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"In guarding his subjects (against injury from others), and in preserving them himself; to punish crime is not a fault in a king, but a duty.","r":"Guarding the people, caring for them and removing wrongdoing are no stain on a king: they are his work.","n":"வடு அன்று answers a charge that must have been in the air. Parimelazhagar makes it explicit: punishing one's subjects could seem against virtue, and he replies that purifying them of wrong is the king's caste duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:550":{"book":"kural","num":550,"t":["கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல் பைங்கூழ்","களைகட் டதனொடு நேர்"],"p":"For a king to punish criminals with death, is like pulling up the weeds in the green corn.","r":"A king's dealing with the cruel by death is equal to weeding out the young crop.","n":"The weeding image makes killing seem an act of care for the crop. It says nothing about who decides which are the weeds, and the next chapter opens on a king who may be one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king is to punish by death those who burn, poison, rob, and those who desire another's wife. Not doing so harms the world.","அரசன் கொடியவர்களைக் கொலையான் ஒறுத்துத் தக்கோரைக் காத்தல்;"],["manakkudavar","The cruel are a narrower list: thieves, highway robbers and plunderers.","கொடியாராவார் கள்வர், ஆறலைப்பார், சூறைகொள்வார்."],["kalaignar","கொலை is the crime and not the penalty: the state simply punishes those who commit murder and similar cruelty.",""]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:551":{"book":"kural","num":551,"t":["கொலைமேற்கொண் டாரிற் கொடிதே அலைமேற்கொண்டு","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகும் வேந்து"],"p":"The king who gives himself up to oppression and acts unjustly (towards his subjects) is more cruel than the man who leads the life of a murderer.","r":"A king who takes up harassing people and goes on doing wrong is crueller than those who have taken up murder.","n":"Both are given the same verb மேற்கொண்டு, taken up as a trade. Parimelazhagar's reason concerns duration: the killer's harm is for a moment, the oppressor's all the time. He adds the reason is wanting wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:552":{"book":"kural","num":552,"t":["வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென் றதுபோலும்","கோலொடு நின்றான் இரவு"],"p":"The request (for money) of him who holds the sceptre is like the word of a highway robber who stands with a weapon in hand and says \"give up your wealth\".","r":"The asking of one who stands with a sceptre is like one standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'.","n":"The cutting word is இரவு: the king is begging, or calls it that. A request backed by the power to punish isn't a request. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree that this is about demands beyond the fixed dues. Manakkudavar adds it is like robbery even when worded as a request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:553":{"book":"kural","num":553,"t":["நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்"],"p":"The country of the king who does not daily examine into the wrongs done and distribute justice, will daily fall to ruin.","r":"A ruler who doesn't examine daily and do justice loses his land day by day.","n":"The repeated நாடொறும், and the play of நாடி and நாடு, make the decline as gradual as the neglect. Parimelazhagar notes two ways to read it: that the king loses the land's fruits or that the land itself decays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:554":{"book":"kural","num":554,"t":["கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும் கோல்கோடிச்","சூழாது செய்யும் அரசு"],"p":"The king, who, without reflecting (on its evil consequences), perverts justice, will lose at once both his wealth and his subjects.","r":"A ruler who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together.","n":"சூழாது is the failure here, not malice. Pope's 'who counsel doth refuse' is more specific than the Tamil. Parimelazhagar makes the people the source of future wealth, so a bad king loses past and future income at a stroke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:555":{"book":"kural","num":555,"t":["அல்லற்பட்டு ஆற்றாது அழுதகண் ணீரன்றே","செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை"],"p":"Will not the tears, shed by a people who cannot endure the oppression which they suffer (from their king), become a saw to waste away his wealth ?","r":"Are not the tears shed by those who can't bear their affliction the weapon that wears away wealth?","n":"தேய்க்கும் is slow wearing: tears work like an abrasive. Parimelazhagar sees a saw and a tree in it, and says the real agent is the sin of causing affliction, shifted onto the tears. So he supplies a moral mechanism the verse leaves unstated. Kalaignar has the tears destroy the regime, though the verse says wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:556":{"book":"kural","num":556,"t":["மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை அஃதின்றேல்","மன்னாவாம் மன்னர்க் கொளி"],"p":"Righteous government gives permanence to (the fame of) kings; without that their fame will have no endurance.","r":"For rulers, enduring is by just rule; without it, the lustre of rulers doesn't endure.","n":"The verse plays on மன்னர், மன்னுதல், மன்னா, ruler and enduring in one root, as if a ruler is by definition one who lasts. Parimelazhagar takes ஒளி as fame and adds the implication that the ruler himself doesn't endure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:557":{"book":"kural","num":557,"t":["துளியின்மை ஞாலத்திற்கு எற்றற்றே வேந்தன்","அளியின்மை வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"As is the world without rain, so live a people whose king is without kindness.","r":"As the lack of rain is to the earth, so the king's lack of tender regard is to those who live.","n":"The negative of 542, as Parimelazhagar points out. But note the shift: 542 needs the sceptre, and 557 fears the lack of அளி, kindness. The failure of a king here is one of feeling as well as of law.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","ali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:558":{"book":"kural","num":558,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை முறைசெய்யா","மன்னவன் கோற்கீழ்ப் படின்"],"p":"Property gives more sorrow than poverty, to those who live under the sceptre of a king without justice.","r":"Under the sceptre of a ruler who doesn't do justice, having is more painful than having nothing.","n":"Pope's couplet, adding a sting to poverty, misses this, though his gloss has it. Parimelazhagar spells out the reason with grim realism: only those who have something get shackled and tortured for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:559":{"book":"kural","num":559,"t":["முறைகோடி மன்னவன் செய்யின் உறைகோடி","ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்"],"p":"If the king acts contrary to justice, rain will become unseasonable, and the heavens will withhold their showers.","r":"If the ruler bends justice in what he does, the sky, bending the rain, won't pour.","n":"கோடி is used of both king and cloud, so the same bending passes between them, and the grammar itself does the work of linking them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is literal: rain in fact fails to come in season, and the next verse gives the cause.","அவன் நாட்டுப் பருவமழை இன்றாம் வகை மேகம் பொழிதலைச் செய்யாது."],["kalaignar","It is a practical chain: bad government lets reservoirs fall into ruin, so rain can't be stored.",""]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:560":{"book":"kural","num":560,"t":["ஆபயன் குன்றும் அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்","காவலன் காவான் எனின்"],"p":"If the guardian (of the country) neglects to guard it, the produce of the cows will fail, and the men of six duties viz., the Brahmins will forget the vedas.","r":"If the guardian doesn't guard, the yield of cows declines and those of the six occupations forget their books.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kalvi","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those of the six duties are Brahmins. Without milk and mantras there is no sacrifice, and that is why rain fails, which completes 559.","பசுக்கள் பால் குன்றியவழி அவியின்மையானும், அது கொடுத்தற்குரியார் மந்திரம் கற்பம் என்பன ஓதாமையானும், வேள்வி நடவாதாம்;"],["kalaignar","They are not a caste: the verse means that productive work and key occupations dwindle.",""]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:561":{"book":"kural","num":561,"t":["தக்காங்கு நாடித் தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்","ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து"],"p":"He is a king who having equitably examined (any injustice which has been brought to his notice), suitably punishes it, so that it may not be again committed.","r":"A king is one who weighs a case fairly and then punishes in proportion, so that the wrong does not go further.","n":"The chapter opens by defining the king through punishment, not against it. As I read it, dread is set apart from penalty by inquiry and proportion; the two matching words தக்காங்கு and ஒத்தாங்கு carry the whole standard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","kol","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:562":{"book":"kural","num":562,"t":["கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக நெடிதாக்கம்","நீங்காமை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let the king, who desires that his prosperity may long remain, commence his preliminary enquires wi th strictness, and then punish with mildness.","r":"Those who want their prosperity to last long should raise the rod sharply and bring it down lightly.","n":"The rod image gives two audiences to one act: the raising is seen by would-be wrongdoers and the light landing by everyone else. If Manakkudavar is right, this verse softens the exact proportion that 561 has just asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Begin as if going beyond measure, then act within measure: the penalty remains proportionate and only looks mild beside its start.","அளவிறப்பச் செய்வார்போல் தொடங்கி; மெல்ல எறிக-செய்யுங்கால் அளவிறவாமல் செய்க"],["manakkudavar","The penalty should actually be less than the offence deserves.","இது குற்றத்திற்குத் தக்க தண்டத்தைக் குறையச் செய்யவேண்டு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The severity lies in admonishing to prevent wrongs, and the gentleness in the punishing itself.",""]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:563":{"book":"kural","num":563,"t":["வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோல னாயின்","ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The cruel-sceptred king, who acts so as to put his subjects in fear, will certainly and quickly come to ruin.","r":"A cruel-sceptred ruler who goes on doing what terrifies people will certainly and quickly perish.","n":"The verse supplies no mechanism: it asserts certainty (ஒருவந்தம்) and speed (ஒல்லை) and leaves the how to the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:564":{"book":"kural","num":564,"t":["இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல் வேந்தன்","உறைகடுகி ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The king who is spoken of as cruel will quickly perish; his life becoming shortened.","r":"A king about whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' are spoken will quickly perish, his tenure cut short.","n":"The harmful speech here is arguably the subjects', not the king's. A phrase people say of him can do what no enemy has done.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kadunjol","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful word is what wounded subjects say of the king, and உறை means his days: his life shrinks and his wealth goes.","ஆயுளும் குறைந்து செல்வமும் கடிதின் இழக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","உறை is where he dwells, which turns against him; even if land and residence bear it, divine force ruins him.","இது நாடும் தான் உறையும் இடமும் பொறுப்பினும் தெய்வத்தினாற் கெடுவ னென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speech is the ruler's own, and what is lost is the regime's standing.",""]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:565":{"book":"kural","num":565,"t":["அருஞ்செவ்வி இன்னா முகத்தான் பெருஞ்செல்வம்","பேஎய்கண் டன்னது உடைத்து"],"p":"The great wealth of him who is difficult of access and possesses a sternness of countenance, is like that which has been obtained by a devil.","r":"The great wealth of one who is hard to get an audience with and grim of face has something of the ghost about it.","n":"Inaccessibility and a grim face count as violence here, though no one has been touched.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth is as if a ghost had seized it: since no one approaches, it benefits neither him nor others.","அவனது செல்வம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாது"],["manakkudavar","The wealth is frightening, like seeing a ghost: no one will take from it, so no army gathers.","இது செல்வத்தை வாங்குவார் இன்மையின் படை சேராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:566":{"book":"kural","num":566,"t":["கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன் ஆயின் நெடுஞ்செல்வம்","நீடின்றி ஆங்கே கெடும்"],"p":"The abundant wealth of the king whose words are harsh and whose looks are void of kindness, will instantly perish instead of abiding long, with him.","r":"If a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard, even vast wealth is lost on the spot instead of lasting.","n":"நெடுஞ்செல்வம் and ஆங்கே are set against each other: length of holdings gives no length of time. This verse's instant loss sits oddly beside the slow filing of 567, so the chapter seems to offer no single speed of ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kannottam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:567":{"book":"kural","num":567,"t":["கடுமொழியும் கையிகந்த தண்டமும் வேந்தன்","அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்"],"p":"Severe words and excessive punishments will be a file to waste away a king's power for destroying","r":"Harsh words and punishment past all bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength.","n":"A file works by small repeated strokes, and here the king holds it against his own iron. Parimelazhagar assigns the harm: harsh words lose the army, and excessive punishment loses the country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","oruththal","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:568":{"book":"kural","num":568,"t":["இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன் சினத்தாற்றிச்","சீறிற் சிறுகும் திரு"],"p":"The prosperity of that king will waste away, who without reflecting (on his affairs himself), commits them to his ministers, and (when a failure occurs) gives way to anger, and rages against them.","r":"The fortune of a king shrinks if he does not think matters through with his circle and then lashes out in anger.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","veruvu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He leaves deliberation to his ministers without sharing it, then rages at them when it fails, and they withdraw from him in fear.","அமைச்சர்மேல் வைத்து அவரோடு தானும் எண்ணிச் செய்யாத அரசன்"],["manakkudavar","The context is justice: he does not examine others' offences together with his council, and acts from anger.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றத்தைத் தனக்கு இனமானாரோடே அமைந்து ஆராயாத அரசன்"],["kalaignar","The fault is a failure to consult, followed by anger and crooked conduct, and the regime falls by itself.",""]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:569":{"book":"kural","num":569,"t":["செருவந்த போழ்திற் சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்","வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்"],"p":"The king who has not provided himself with a place of defence, will in times of war be seized with fear and quickly perish.","r":"A king who has not made his defences will, when war comes, be seized by fear and quickly perish.","n":"வெரு returns here pointed the other way: in a chapter on causing dread, the king himself ends up dreading. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying that a king must also not do what brings fear on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:570":{"book":"kural","num":570,"t":["கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும் கடுங்கோல் அதுவல்லது","இல்லை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The earth bears up no greater burden than ignorant men whom a cruel sceptre attaches to itself (as the ministers of its evil deeds).","r":"The harsh sceptre binds the unlearned to itself, and the earth carries no burden heavier than this.","n":"Cruelty is presented as having a staffing logic: it gathers those who lack the learning to resist it. Parimelazhagar puts the weight on the alliance and Manakkudavar on the king who forms it, which comes to much the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kol","kalvi","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:571":{"book":"kural","num":571,"t":["கண்ணோட்டம் என்னும் கழிபெருங் காரிகை","உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world exists through that greatest ornament (of princes), a gracious demeanour.","r":"Because that surpassingly great beauty called kindly regard exists, this world exists.","n":"A word of ornament (காரிகை) is given the weight of a cosmic condition. The chapter's elevated claim starts by calling kindness a beauty, not a duty.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The beauty is the king's, and without it his subjects live like deer beside a tiger.","கண்ணோட்டம் என்று சொல்லப்படுகின்ற சிறப்பு உடைய அழகு அரசர்மாட்டு உண்டு ஆகலான்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about anyone who has this quality, and they bring the world its greatness.",""]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:572":{"book":"kural","num":572,"t":["கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல் அஃதிலார்","உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The prosperity of the world springs from the kindliness, the existence of those who have no (kindliness) is a burden to the earth.","r":"The world's ordinary workings rest on kindly regard, and the mere existence of those without it is a weight on the earth.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists what உலகியல் consists of: mutual helpfulness, protection, and forgiving lapses. On that reading the grace of the eye is social infrastructure, not private sentiment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:573":{"book":"kural","num":573,"t":["பண்என்னாம் பாடற்கு இயைபின்றேல் கண்என்னாம்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Of what avail is a song if it be inconsistent with harmony ? what is the use of eyes which possess no kindliness.","r":"Of what use is melody that does not fit the song? Of what use is an eye with no kindly regard?","n":"The analogy treats kindness as attunement: the eye is related to regard as melody is related to singing, so it needs the other to fulfil itself. Parimelazhagar's long list of musical terms is a resource for the music tradition more than for the verse's argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:574":{"book":"kural","num":574,"t":["உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் அளவினால்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Beyond appearing to be in the face, what good do they do, those eyes in which is no well-regulated kindness ?","r":"What do eyes that lack measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face?","n":"அளவினால் is easy to skip: even while condemning the lack, the verse measures the virtue, and Parimelazhagar takes this as excluding excess of it. This prepares for 578.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:575":{"book":"kural","num":575,"t":["கண்ணிற்கு அணிகலம் கண்ணோட்டம் அஃதின்றேல்","புண்ணென்று உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Kind looks are the ornaments of the eyes; without these they will be considered (by the wise) to be merely two sores.","r":"Kindly regard is the eye's only jewel, and without it the eye is known for a wound.","n":"The wound is what the observer perceives (உணரப் படும்), so the verse judges the eye by how it meets others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:576":{"book":"kural","num":576,"t":["மண்ணோ டியைந்த மரத்தனையர் கண்ணோ","டியைந்துகண் ணோடா தவர்"],"p":"They resemble the trees of the earth, who although they have eyes, never look kindly (on others).","r":"Those who have eyes yet do not turn them kindly on others are like trees fixed in the soil.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means a real tree, which stands joined to earth: they walk about but are as unresponsive as trees. He rejects the plaster-puppet reading.","அதனைச் சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடிய மரப்பாவை என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர். அஃது உரையன்மை"],["manakkudavar","It means a wooden doll made with plaster: people whose eyes have met another's and still show no regard are like the doll.","சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடச் செய்த மாப்பாவையோடு ஒப்பார்"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:577":{"book":"kural","num":577,"t":["கண்ணோட் டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர் கண்ணுடையார்","கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்"],"p":"Men without kind looks are men without eyes; those who (really) have eyes are also not devoid of kind looks.","r":"Those without kindly regard have no eyes, and those who truly have eyes do not lack it.","n":"The verse redefines possession of eyes by function. The second half turns the first around, closing off the idea of a sighted person who merely chooses not to look kindly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:578":{"book":"kural","num":578,"t":["கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்க்கு","உரிமை உடைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world is theirs (kings) who are able to show kindness, without injury to their affairs, (administration of justice).","r":"The world belongs to those able to extend kindly regard without damaging the task at hand.","n":"வல்லார் marks this as a skill and a difficult one. After seven verses of unqualified praise, the condition here gives the whole chapter its hard edge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The task is the king's justice, which fails if he spares associates who have harmed others.","முறை செய்தல் ஆகிய தன் தொழில் அழியாமல்"],["manakkudavar","The task is the king's own interests: kindness must not cost him material loss.","அரசர்க்குப் பொருட்கேடு வாராமல் கண்ணோட வேண்டுமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:579":{"book":"kural","num":579,"t":["ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்கண் ணோடிப்","பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை"],"p":"Patiently to bear with, and show kindness to those who grieve us, is the most excellent of all dispositions.","r":"The highest disposition is to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt you.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பண்பினார் implies familiarity: those meant are people close to the king, and this is the top quality in kings.","'பண்பினார்' என்றதனான், அவர் பயிற்சி பெற்றாம்."],["kalaignar","It extends to anyone at all who is inclined to destroy you.",""]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:580":{"book":"kural","num":580,"t":["பெயக்கண்டும் நஞ்சுண் டமைவர் நயத்தக்க","நாகரிகம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to cultivate that degree of) urbanity which all shall love, even after swallowing the poison served to them by their friends, will be friendly with them.","r":"Those who want a refinement worth loving will see poison poured, drink it, and stay at ease.","n":"The verse names no pourer: the 'friends' in Pope and the associates in Parimelazhagar are supplied. Parimelazhagar's support comes from Natrinai 355, which offers a thread beyond the Kural, and it is where the identification of நாகரிகம with kindly regard rests.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நாகரிகம is kindly regard, and the poison comes from associates whom one's eyes cannot refuse.","நாகரிகம் என்பது கண்ணோட்டமாதல்"],["manakkudavar","நாகரிகம is the broader possession of good qualities across virtue, wealth and pleasure.","நாகரிகம் - அறம் பொருள் இன்பத்தின்கண் நற்குணங்கள் பலவும் உடைமை"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:581":{"book":"kural","num":581,"t":["ஒற்றும் உரைசான்ற நூலும் இவையிரண்டும்","தெற்றென்க மன்னவன் கண்"],"p":"Let a king consider as his eyes these two things, a spy and a book (of laws) universally esteemed.","r":"Let a ruler hold spies and the well-regarded treatise, these two, firmly as his eyes.","n":"Immediately after a chapter that defines the eye by kindness, the eye is redefined by information. Parimelazhagar's pairing makes the spy the eye of flesh, which sees what is happening, and the treatise the eye of wisdom, which says what to do about it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:582":{"book":"kural","num":582,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","வல்லறிதல் வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the duty of a king to know quickly (by a spy) what all happens, daily, amongst all men.","r":"To know quickly, at all times, everything happening to everyone is the king's work.","n":"Knowing is called the work itself. Parimelazhagar explains that this is a figure, since both favour and punishment depend on timely knowledge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Everyone' is the three classes named in 584, and what is known is their good and bad words and acts.","'எல்லார்க்கும்' என்றது மூன்று திறத்தாரையும்."],["manakkudavar","'Everyone' is the surrounding kings, whether enemy, ally, middle or neutral, and both their circle and his own.","பகைவராகியும் நட்டாராகியும் மத்திமராகியும் உதாசீனராகியும் இருக்கின்ற அரசர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:583":{"book":"kural","num":583,"t":["ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா மன்னவன்","கொற்றங் கொளக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"There is no way for a king to obtain conquests, who knows not the advantage of discoveries made by a spy.","r":"A ruler who does not gather through spies and discern what it all means has no victory lying ready to take.","n":"The verse has two conditions: spying and discerning (பொருள் தெரியா). Gathering reports without weighing them fails as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:584":{"book":"kural","num":584,"t":["வினைசெய்வார் தம்சுற்றம் வேண்டாதார் என்றாங்கு","அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who watches all men, to wit, those who are in the king's employment, his relatives, and his enemies.","r":"A spy is one who examines everyone: those doing the king's work, his kin, and those who wish him ill.","n":"The list begins with the king's own servants and relatives; enemies come last. Surveillance turns inward first, and Kalaignar draws out of this a professional ethic: the spy must not sort people by favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:585":{"book":"kural","num":585,"t":["கடாஅ உருவொடு கண்ணஞ்சாது யாண்டும்","உகாஅமை வல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to assume an appearance which may create no suspicion (in the minds of others), who fears no man's face, and who never reveals (his purpose).","r":"A spy is one who can take an appearance that raises no suspicion, not flinch before eyes that probe him, and never let anything spill.","n":"உகாஅமை is the image of not spilling. Parimelazhagar's gloss shows his own social world: the unsuspected forms are those of Brahmins, merchants and the like.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:586":{"book":"kural","num":586,"t":["துறந்தார் படிவத்த ராகி இறந்தாராய்ந்து","என்செயினும் சோர்விலது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who, assuming the appearance of an ascetic, goes into (whatever place he wishes), examines into (all, that is needful), and never discovers himself, whatever may be done to him.","r":"A spy goes in the guise of renunciants, pushes through to examine what must be examined, and gives nothing away whatever is done to him.","n":"என்செயினும் quietly admits torture. Parimelazhagar sees this: having mentioned the four methods already, he says force is raised again because it is the hardest to bear.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He lives as complete renunciants and vow-keepers such as pilgrims do, and this commentator records separately that others read படிவம் as costume.","இதனுள் 'படிவம்' என்றதனை வேடமாக்கி, 'துறந்தார் வேடத்தாராகி' என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."],["manakkudavar","He wears the costume of an ascetic and crosses the country's border to gather news.","தவஞ் செய்வார் வேடத்தராகி, நாட்டெல்லையைக் கடந்து போய்"],["kalaignar","No renunciant is involved: he simply seems unconnected with the affair.",""]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:587":{"book":"kural","num":587,"t":["மறைந்தவை கேட்கவற் றாகி அறிந்தவை","ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to discover what is hidden and who retains no doubt concerning what he has known.","r":"A spy is one able to hear what has been concealed and to be free of doubt about what he has learned.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: a king can't act on a report delivered with doubt. The next verse then refuses to accept the spy's certainty, so in this chapter confidence is required of the source but not trusted by the user.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:588":{"book":"kural","num":588,"t":["ஒற்றொற்றித் தந்த பொருளையும் மற்றுமோர்","ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let not a king receive the information which a spy has discovered and made known to him, until he has examined it by another spy.","r":"Even what a spy has found and brought back should be confirmed through another spy.","n":"The commentators explain why from a suspicion of corruption: Parimelazhagar thinks of collusion with those being watched, and Manakkudavar of a spy taking money from rival kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:589":{"book":"kural","num":589,"t":["ஒற்றெற் றுணராமை ஆள்க உடன்மூவர்","சொற்றொக்க தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let a king employ spies so that one may have no knowledge of the other; and when the information of three agrees together, let him receive it.","r":"Run spies so that one does not know of another, and accept what three say in agreement.","n":"This is a statement about independent evidence: agreement counts only among witnesses who could not have conferred. Parimelazhagar adds that agreement need only be in substance (பயனால்), and that if the accounts differ, the inquiry continues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:590":{"book":"kural","num":590,"t":["சிறப்பறிய ஒற்றின்கண் செய்யற்க செய்யின்","புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை"],"p":"Let not a king publicly confer on a spy any marks of his favour; if he does, he will divulge his own secret.","r":"Do not honour a spy where others can see it; if the king does, he has himself let out the secret.","n":"The exposure comes from the patron, not the spy, and from gratitude, not carelessness: the honour itself is the leak. The verse does not forbid the reward, only its being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:591":{"book":"kural","num":591,"t":["உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம் அஃ தில்லார்","உடையது உடையரோ மற்று"],"p":"Energy makes out the man of property; as for those who are destitute of it, do they (really) possess what they possess ?","r":"What makes someone 'one who has' is energy of mind; without it, do people really have even what they have?","n":"Ownership is put in question, not merely wealth's worth. Parimelazhagar supplies a practical reason: without the power to guard it, they will lose it too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:592":{"book":"kural","num":592,"t":["உள்ளம் உடைமை உடைமை பொருளுடைமை","நில்லாது நீங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The possession of (energy of) mind is true property; the possession of wealth passes away and abides not.","r":"Possession of the inner drive is real possession; possession of goods does not stay and slips away.","n":"The verse's word is உள்ளம், not ஊக்கம். Parimelazhagar makes the identification by metonymy, and Pope's '(energy of)' imports it, so the verse itself may be claiming somewhat more broadly that the mind is the true holding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","selvam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:593":{"book":"kural","num":593,"t":["க்கம் இழந்தேமென்று அல்லாவார் ஊக்கம்","ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடை யார்"],"p":"They who are possessed of enduring energy will not trouble themselves, saying, \"we have lost our property.\"","r":"Those who hold steady energy as their cash in hand do not fret, 'We have lost our gains'.","n":"கைத்து is money in the hand, and the image is the verse's point: energy is liquid in a way that lost property is not. Their calm is a calculation of assets, not detachment, and this differs from the equanimity grounded in fate at 379.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:594":{"book":"kural","num":594,"t":["க்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா","ஊக்க முடையா னுழை"],"p":"Wealth will find its own way to the man of unfailing energy.","r":"Prosperity itself asks the way and goes to the one whose energy does not waver.","n":"Wealth is personified as a traveller who asks directions, so the seeker has become the sought. Parimelazhagar glosses அசைவின்மை as not flagging in hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:595":{"book":"kural","num":595,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய மலர்நீட்டம் மாந்தர்தம்","உள்ளத் தனையது உயர்வு"],"p":"The stalks of water-flowers are proportionate to the depth of water; so is men's greatness proportionate to their minds.","r":"The length of a water-flower's stem matches the depth of the water, and a person's height matches their inner drive.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the point of the image: the stem keeps lengthening as long as the water rises, so there is no fixed ceiling. The analogy has a gap, though: water is outside the plant, whereas the mind is within. 'Lotus' is Pope's and Kalaignar's addition; the verse says only மலர்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","manam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The height gained is worldly: increase in wealth and military force.","உயர்தல் - பொருள் படைகளான் மிகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","What is measured by the mind is energy itself, so the verse says how energy arises.","மாந்தரது உள்ளத்தின் அளவினது ஊக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:596":{"book":"kural","num":596,"t":["உள்ளுவ தெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல் மற்றது","தள்ளினுந் தள்ளாமை நீர்த்து"],"p":"In all that a king thinks of, let him think of his greatness; and if it should be thrust from him (by fate), it will have the nature of not being thrust from him.","r":"Whatever you think about, think of rising; even if it is thrust aside, it has the quality of not having been thrust aside.","n":"The verse names nothing that thrusts the aim aside. 'Fate' comes from Pope and from Parimelazhagar's பால்வகை, so this is less clearly a verse about fate than it appears in English.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even if fate denies kings the height, the aim stands: good people will not blame it, since their exertion did not fail.","அவ்வுயர்ச்சி பால்வகையாற் கூடிற்றில்லையாயினும், அக்கருத்துத் தள்ளாமை நீர்மையுடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","A failed high aim is equal to having achieved it, and is beyond blame.","முயன்று பெற்றதனோடு ஒக்கும்"],["kalaignar","This is an instruction: even if it does not succeed, do not give up the thought.",""]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:597":{"book":"kural","num":597,"t":["சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார் உரவோர் புதையம்பிற்","பட்டுப்பா டூன்றுங் களிறு"],"p":"The strong minded will not faint, even when all is lost; the elephant stands firm, even when wounded by a shower of arrows.","r":"The strong in mind do not give way when things fall apart, as a bull elephant holds its ground under a sheaf of arrows.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads a crossover: 'not giving way' belongs to the elephant and 'upholding one's greatness' to the strong, so that each half lends the other its verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:598":{"book":"kural","num":598,"t":["உள்ளம் இலாதவர் எய்தார் உலகத்து","வள்ளியம் என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have no (greatness of) mind, will not acquire the joy of saying in the world, \"we have excercised liaberality\".","r":"Those without inner drive never reach, in this world, the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous'.","n":"செருக்கு is a fault in 431, yet here it is a joy worth wanting. Generosity is presented as something one may feel proud about, and this is not a usual picture of selfless giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","velanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உள்ளம் is energy: without it the chain of effort, wealth, giving and pride never starts.","ஊக்கம் இல்லையாகவே முயற்சி, பொருள், கொடை செருக்கு இவை முறையே இலவாம்"],["kalaignar","உள்ளம் is the eagerness to give: without it one can't take pride in being a giver.",""]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:599":{"book":"kural","num":599,"t":["பரியது கூர்ங்கோட்டது ஆயினும் யானை","஦வ்ருஉம் புலிதாக் குறின்"],"p":"Although the elephant has a large body, and a sharp tusk, yet it fears the attack of the tiger.","r":"Though its body is huge and its tusks are sharp, an elephant fears when a tiger attacks.","n":"The verse says nothing about energy or kings. Parimelazhagar names it an allegory (பிறிது மொழிதல்), and the chapter's setting is all that justifies the moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:600":{"book":"kural","num":600,"t":["உரமொருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கைஅஃ தில்லார்","மரம்மக்க ளாதலே வேறு"],"p":"Energy is mental wealth; those men who are destitute of it are only trees in the form of men.","r":"A person's real firmness is the abundance of the inner drive; those without it are trees, and being human is the only difference.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens the insult: trees bear fruit and serve in temples, houses, chariots and ships, so the only thing left separating such people from trees is that they are not as useful. He also reads உரம் as understanding, as in 24, which joins will and intelligence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:601":{"book":"kural","num":601,"t":["குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம் மடியென்னும்","மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்"],"p":"By the darkness, of idleness, the indestructible lamp of family (rank) will be extinguished.","r":"The unfailing lamp called lineage goes dark and dies when the grime called sloth creeps over it.","n":"A lamp said to be unfailing fails. Parimelazhagar explains மாசு through tamas (தாமத குணம்); that is his Samkhya vocabulary, which the verse does not use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:602":{"book":"kural","num":602,"t":["மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல் குடியைக்","குடியாக வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let those, who desire that their family may be illustrious, put away all idleness from their conduct.","r":"Those who want their family to be a family should conduct themselves with sloth treated as sloth.","n":"The repetition is the verse's device: மடியை மடியா and குடியைக் குடியாக. To be a family is an achievement here, not a birth fact.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மடியா means 'as sloth': regard it for what it is, as one says to treat fire as fire. He records the other reading.","நெருப்பிற் கொடியது பிறிதின்மை பற்றி, நெருப்பை நெருப்பாகவே கருதுக"],["manakkudavar","மடியா means killing: destroy the habit of sloth.","மடி செய்தலை மடித்து ஒழுகுக"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:603":{"book":"kural","num":603,"t":["மடிமடிக் கொண்டொழுகும் பேதை பிறந்த","குடிமடியும் தன்னினும் முந்து"],"p":"The (lustre of the) family of the ignorant man, who acts under the influence of destructive laziness will perish, even before he is dead.","r":"The family of a fool who carries deadly sloth inside him dies before he does.","n":"The cruelty is in the order: he survives to see it. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the family depends on him for support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:604":{"book":"kural","num":604,"t":["குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும் மடிமடிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Family (greatness) will be destroyed, and faults will increase, in those men who give way to laziness, and put forth no dignified exertions.","r":"For those sunk in sloth without any excellent effort, the family declines and faults multiply.","n":"The contrast is not sloth against mere activity but against மாண்ட உஞற்று, refined exertion. The verse thus sets a higher bar than being busy; 607 repeats the phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:605":{"book":"kural","num":605,"t":["நெடுநீர் மறவி மடிதுயில் நான்கும்","கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்"],"p":"Procrastination, forgetfulness, idleness, and sleep, these four things, form the vessel which is desired by those destined to destruction.","r":"Delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep, these four, are the pleasure-craft of those bound for ruin.","n":"The image lies in காமக் கலன்: a boat you board because you want to, and which then drops you mid-sea. Parimelazhagar records but does not accept a reading of கலன் as a desired ornament. Manakkudavar reads the four as one sequence: delaying a task, forgetting it, slacking, and sleeping instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:606":{"book":"kural","num":606,"t":["படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும் மடியுடையார்","மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing for the idle, even when possessed of the riches of kings who ruled over the whole earth, to derive any great benefit from it.","r":"Even when what world-rulers possess comes to them, the slothful rarely reach any great fruit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth of world-rulers comes to them unearned, but without effort to guard it, it perishes. He records another reading, that such kings become their allies.","நிலம் முழுவதும் ஆண்டாரது செல்வம் தானே வந்து எய்திய இடத்தும்"],["manakkudavar","It is the kings of the earth themselves who, however well provided, lose out if they are slothful.","பூமியையுடைய வேந்தர் பல்பொருளினாலும் அமைந்த விடத் தும் மடியுடையாராயின்"],["kalaignar","படி means worthiness: even those who hold the affection of worthy people get nothing.",""]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:607":{"book":"kural","num":607,"t":["இடிபுரிந்து எள்ளுஞ் சொல் கேட்பர் மடிபுரிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்"],"p":"Those who through idleness, and do not engage themselves in dignified exertion, will subject themselves to rebukes and reproaches.","r":"Those who cling to sloth and make no excellent effort end up hearing words of rebuke and scorn.","n":"கேட்பர் may hold the sting: they can only listen. Parimelazhagar builds a small story from இடி: friends scold first, see no result, and only then turn to contempt, and if friends do this, others need not be mentioned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:608":{"book":"kural","num":608,"t":["மடிமை குடிமைக்கண் தங்கின்தன் ஒன்னார்க்கு","அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"If idleness take up its abode in a king of high birth, it will make him a slave of his enemies.","r":"If sloth settles in one of a family-building nature, it delivers him into slavery to his enemies.","n":"மடிமை, குடிமை and அடிமை rhyme in sequence, and the sound performs the fall from sloth through standing to bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","kudi","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:609":{"book":"kural","num":609,"t":["குடியாண்மை யுள்வந்த குற்றம் ஒருவன்","மடியாண்மை மாற்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"When a man puts away idleness, the reproach which has come upon himself and his family will disappear.","r":"When a person ends the rule of sloth over him, the faults that have come into his family and his manhood are gone.","n":"Sloth is a ruler here (மடியாண்மை), and one's own effort deposes it. This is the chapter's one verse of repair. Parimelazhagar makes the promise wide, since even faults from other causes vanish; Manakkudavar limits the faults to neglecting what the family needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:610":{"book":"kural","num":610,"t":["மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான்","தாஅய தெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"The king who never gives way to idleness will obtain entire possession of (the whole earth) passed over by him who measured (the worlds) with His foot.","r":"A ruler free from sloth obtains, all at once, the whole expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one who measured is the deity who spanned all worlds with his stride, and the verse leaves him unnamed.","தன் அடியளவானே எல்லா உலகையும் அளந்த இறைவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a tireless human king who brings every place he goes within his footstep's range.",""]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:611":{"book":"kural","num":611,"t":["அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்","பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்"],"p":"Yield not to the feebleness which says, \"this is too difficult to be done\"; labour will give the greatness (of mind) which is necessary (to do it).","r":"One must not slacken by saying 'this is hard', because effort brings the greatness needed.","n":"Difficulty is measured against a self that labour itself enlarges, so the calculation 'this is too much for me' is wrong in its premise. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","perumai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:612":{"book":"kural","num":612,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல் வினைக்குறை","தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு"],"p":"Take care not to give up exertion in the midst of a work; the world will abandon those who abandon their unfinished work.","r":"Guard against letting a task die in the middle of the doing, for the world's bond with a person turns on how they have dealt with the work's unmet need.","n":"A short verse gives opposite readings of தீர்ந்தார்: those who left the work or those who finished it. Parimelazhagar glosses குறை from Purananuru 188, which is a thread beyond the Kural.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who abandoned the needed work, and the world abandons them.","வினையாகிய குறையைச் செய்யாது விட்டாரை உலகம் விட்டது"],["manakkudavar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who completed it, and the world does not leave them.","வினைக் குறையை முடித்தாரினின்றும் உலகம் விடப்பட்டதன்று"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:613":{"book":"kural","num":613,"t":["தாளாண்மை என்னும் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்றே","வேளாண்மை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"The lustre of munificence will dwell only with the dignity of laboriousness or efforts.","r":"The proud distinction called helping others stands on the quality called labour.","n":"Manakkudavar draws the implication plainly: even doing virtue comes from this. A moral excellence has an economic foundation, and தாளாண்மை and வேளாண்மை rhyme to tie them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:614":{"book":"kural","num":614,"t":["தாளாண்மை இல்லாதான் வேளாண்மை பேடிகை","வாளாண்மை போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The liberality of him, who does not labour, will fail, like the manliness of a hermaphrodite, who has a sword in its hand.","r":"The generosity of one who won't labour comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி.","n":"The image depends on a period contempt for a third-gender person, which commentators extend by saying the figure wishes to fight but is too afraid. Manakkudavar alone drops the word and says 'one who fears at the sight of arms'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:615":{"book":"kural","num":615,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான் தன்கேளிர்","துன்பம் துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்"],"p":"He who desires not pleasure, but desires labour, will be a pillar to sustain his relations, wiping away their sorrows.","r":"One who desires the work and not pleasure is a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles and bears them up.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds a principle of causes here: wanting the cause rather than the effect gets all effects, and wanting the effect without the cause gets nothing. The pillar stands under others' weight, so personal renunciation of pleasure is here for one's household, not for liberation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:616":{"book":"kural","num":616,"t":["முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும் முயற்றின்மை","இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"Labour will produce wealth; idleness will bring poverty.","r":"Effort builds prosperity, and the absence of effort ushers in the absence of everything.","n":"முயற்றின்மை இன்மை: absence brings absence. The verse's symmetry leaves no room for a third cause, and that is what 371 and 377 dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","திருவினை means wealth, and specifically a king's six limbs of state.","அரசர்மாட்டு உளதாய முயற்சி அவரது செல்வத்தினை வளர்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","It yields excellent deeds and achievements, not wealth as such.",""]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:617":{"book":"kural","num":617,"t":["மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி என்ப மடியிலான்","தாளுளான் தாமரையி னாள்"],"p":"They say that the black Mudevi (the goddess of adversity) dwells with laziness, and the Latchmi (the goddess of prosperity) dwells with the labour of the industrious.","r":"They say the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth, and she of the lotus lives in the labour of the unslothful.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', keeps a little distance from the claim: the verse reports it. தாள் can mean both foot and exertion, and so Lakshmi may be at the feet of the one who works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","aalvinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddesses are taken as they are, dwelling respectively in sloth and in effort, with sin's blackness assigned to the first.","பாவத்தின் கருமை அதன் பயனாக முகடிமேல் ஏற்றப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The names are mere words used to point at the energetic person and the idler.",""]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:618":{"book":"kural","num":618,"t":["பொறியின்மை யார்க்கும் பழியன்று அறிவறிந்து","ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி"],"p":"Adverse fate is no disgrace to any one; to be without exertion and without knowing what should be known, is disgrace.","r":"Lacking good fortune is no one's disgrace; failing to work, after learning what must be known, is the disgrace.","n":"The verse does not say effort defeats fate. It moves the question from causes to blame, and that is a different and more defensible claim than 620's. அறிவறிந்து adds a condition: blind labour isn't what is being asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொறி is fate: people blame present faults, not fruits of past deeds.","உலகம் பழவினை பற்றிப் பழியாது, ஈண்டைக் குற்றமுடைமை பற்றியே பழிப்பது"],["manakkudavar","பொறி is religious merit: lacking it is no fault, and this answers those who say effort is useless without merit.","யார்க்கும் புண்ணியமின்மை குற்றமாகாது"],["kalaignar","The idea of blame from fate is itself wrong.",""]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:619":{"book":"kural","num":619,"t":["தெய்வத்தான் ஆகா தெனினும் முயற்சிதன்","மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்"],"p":"Although it be said that, through fate, it cannot be attained, yet labour, with bodily exertion, will yield its reward.","r":"Though divine order may deny the end sought, effort still pays the wage of the body's strain.","n":"கூலி is a modest word: a labourer's wage, not the prize. The verse concedes the goal may be lost, and secures only a floor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெய்வம் is fate: where it withholds, one gets a wage, and where it favours, much more. So don't wait on it.","முயன்ற வினை பால்வகையால் கருதிய பயனைத் தாராதாயினும்"],["manakkudavar","தெய்வம் stands for merit: without it there's no gain, yet effort pays in proportion to strain.","புண்ணியம் இன்மையால் ஆக்கம் இல்லையாயினும்"],["kalaignar","It refers to God and prayer: what crying out to God won't achieve, labour will.",""]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:620":{"book":"kural","num":620,"t":["ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் உலைவின்றித்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்"],"p":"They who labour on, without fear and without fainting will see even fate (put) behind their back.","r":"Those who toil on without flagging or delay will see even fate's back.","n":"உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் is a battle image: to see an enemy's back as it retreats. Pope's rendering, with fate put 'behind their back', turns the image around.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can obstruct once or twice but not always, so repeated effort outlasts it.","ஊழ் ஒருகாலாக இருகாலாக அல்லது விலக்கலாகாமையின், பலகால் முயல்வார் பயன் எய்துவர்"],["manakkudavar","They actually conquer fate, turning back the bad karma that blocks the fruit.","இஃது ஊழ்தன்னையும் வெல்வ ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:621":{"book":"kural","num":621,"t":["இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக அதனை","அடுத்தூர்வது அஃதொப்ப தில்"],"p":"If troubles come, laugh; there is nothing like that, to press upon and drive away sorrow.","r":"When trouble comes, laugh; nothing else presses back on it as well.","n":"The laugh is treated as a tactic, not a sign of being unaffected. Parimelazhagar says it borrows the joy of a finished task in the middle of the task, and that the lift it gives supplies the strength to push through.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:622":{"book":"kural","num":622,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய இடும்பை அறிவுடையான்","உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"A flood of troubles will be overcome by the (courageous) thought which the minds of the wise will entertain, even in sorrow.","r":"Even a flood of suffering is gone when a person of understanding merely thinks it through in the mind.","n":"The verse doesn't say which thought. That gap is the whole dispute, and Pope's '(courageous)' fills it with one more answer.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Suffering is nothing but a stance in the mind, and it vanishes when that stance is reversed.","இடும்பையாவது உள்ளத்து ஒரு கோட்பாடேயன்றிப் பிறிதில்லை"],["manakkudavar","The thought is that this is the fruit of one's deeds.","நெஞ்சினாலே வினைப்பய னென்று நினைக்கக் கெடும்"],["kalaignar","The thought is a practical one: how to defeat the trouble.",""]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:623":{"book":"kural","num":623,"t":["இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படுப்பர் இடும்பைக்கு","இடும்பை படாஅ தவர்"],"p":"They give sorrow to sorrow, who in sorrow do not suffer sorrow.","r":"Those who do not suffer at suffering make suffering suffer.","n":"The verse enacts its turn in four occurrences of one word. This reversal of subject and object recurs in 624 and 625, so the chapter's signature is grammatical. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: persisting finishes the task, and finishing ends all the troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:624":{"book":"kural","num":624,"t":["மடுத்தவா யெல்லாம் பகடன்னான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடர்ப்பாடு உடைத்து"],"p":"Troubles will vanish (i.e., will be troubled) before the man who (struggles against difficulties) as a buffalo (drawing a cart) through deep mire.","r":"Trouble that meets one who pulls through every obstruction like a draught ox itself has trouble.","n":"Nothing about this image is heroic: Parimelazhagar cites Civakacintamani's picture of the ox on its side, nose to the ground. It sits beside the elephant of 597 and shows a humbler kind of strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:625":{"book":"kural","num":625,"t":["அடுக்கி வரினும் அழிவிலான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடுக்கட் படும்"],"p":"The troubles of that man will be troubled (and disappear) who, however thickly they may come upon him , does not abandon (his purpose).","r":"Though they come stacked one on another, the troubles of one who is not undone are themselves in trouble.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:626":{"book":"kural","num":626,"t":["அற்றேமென்று அல்லற் படுபவோ பெற்றேமென்று","ஓம்புதல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Will those men ever cry out in sorrow, \"we are destitute\" who, (in their prosperity), give not way to (undue desire) to keep their wealth.","r":"Would those who never knew how to clutch at wealth, saying 'we have got it', grieve, saying 'we have lost it'?","n":"Grief at losing is settled earlier, at the moment of having. The verse praises a lack of skill (தேற்றாதவர்), which is a wry way to describe a virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:627":{"book":"kural","num":627,"t":["இலக்கம் உடம்பிடும்பைக் கென்று கலக்கத்தைக்","கையாறாக் கொள்ளாதாம் மேல்"],"p":"The great will not regard trouble as trouble, knowing that the body is the butt of trouble.","r":"Knowing that the body is a target for suffering, they do not take trouble as a thing to be shaken by.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மேல் means 'upon them', and the verse concerns bodies in all four states of birth, targets for a sword called suffering.","நாற்கதியினும் உள்ள உடம்புகள் இடும்பை என்னும் வாளுக்கு இலக்கு என்று தெளிந்து"],["manakkudavar","மேல் means 'the superior', and the context is the hardship taken on in austerity.","இது மேல் நன்மையாற் றவஞ் செய்யுங்கால் வருந் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதா ரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:628":{"book":"kural","num":628,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"That man never experiences sorrow, who does not seek for pleasure, and who considers distress to be natural (to man).","r":"One who does not chase pleasure and calls suffering natural does not come to pain.","n":"Pain is prevented by an expectation, not a defence. Parimelazhagar sees two conditions: wanting pleasure brings pain, and so does trying to ward off suffering as if it were not natural.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:629":{"book":"kural","num":629,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான் துன்பத்துள்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"He does not suffer sorrow, in sorrow who does not look for pleasure in pleasure.","r":"One who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not feel the pain within pain.","n":"The experience is not denied: the phrasing இன்பத்துள், துன்பத்துள் places a person within each. Parimelazhagar says they undergo them, yet do not desire or grieve, so that the deeds bear no fruit. That karmic consequence is his addition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","idukkan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Pleasure and pain here are those arising through work and effort, and both are held in the same way.","இரண்டையும் ஒரு தன்மையாகக் கோடலின், பயன்களும் இலவாயின."],["manakkudavar","It concerns desire: one who doesn't lose himself in sensual pleasure isn't undone by the pain it brings.","இது காமத்தால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:630":{"book":"kural","num":630,"t":["இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின் ஆகுந்தன்","ஒன்னார் விழையுஞ் சிறப்பு"],"p":"The elevation, which even his enemies will esteem, will be gained by him, who regards pain as pleasure.","r":"If a person takes what hurts as pleasure, there comes to him a distinction even his enemies admire.","n":"The reward is a rival's respect, which is the one kind that can't be flattery. Parimelazhagar's reason is philosophical: pain is no part of the self but a passing mental stance (கணிகம், momentary), which the verse does not claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:631":{"book":"kural","num":631,"t":["கருவியும் காலமும் செய்கையும் செய்யும்","அருவினையும் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can make an excellent choice of means, time, manner of execution, and the difficult undertaking (itself).","r":"A minister is one excellent in means, timing, method and the difficult task itself.","n":"Manakkudavar makes this concrete in a military way: the hard task is seizing another's realm, the means elephants and horses, and the time a season with water and shade. The verse itself says nothing so specific.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:632":{"book":"kural","num":632,"t":["வன்கண் குடிகாத்தல் கற்றறிதல் ஆள்வினையோடு","ஐந்துடன் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who in addition to the aforesaid five things excels in the possession of firmness, protection of subjects, clearness by learning, and perseverance.","r":"A minister is one excellent in five things together, including resolve, protecting the people, learned knowing and labour.","n":"வன்கண் is hard-heartedness when it condemns impostors in 276, but here it is a qualification for office. The word's moral value depends on where it is pointed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","aalvinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This verse lists four, and 'the five' are those of 631. He criticises those who split items here to reach five.","மேற்சொல்லிய அங்கங்கள் ஐந்துடனே திருந்த உடையானே அமைச்சனாவான்"],["manakkudavar","The five are named in this verse, including the guarding of the senses, and are needed before one becomes a minister.","அஞ்சாமையும், குடிகாத்தலும், இந்திரியங்களைக் காத்தலும், நூல் முகத்தானறிதலும், முயற்சியும்"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:633":{"book":"kural","num":633,"t":["பிரித்தலும் பேணிக் கொளலும் பிரிந்தார்ப்","பொருத்தலும் வல்ல தமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can effect discord (among foes), maintain the good-will of his friends and restore to friendship those who have seceded (from him).","r":"A minister is one able to divide others, keep his own side close, and bring back those who have broken away.","n":"The verse states three skills of division and joining, with no moral comment. Kalaignar's '(for the nation's good)' and his 'those who reformed' are moral framing added by him. Parimelazhagar links two of them to Sanskrit terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:634":{"book":"kural","num":634,"t":["தெரிதலும் தேர்ந்து செயலும் ஒருதலையாச்","சொல்லலும் வல்லது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who is able to comprehend (the whole nature of an undertaking), execute it in the best manner possible, and offer assuring advice (in time of necessity).","r":"A minister is one able to discern, to act after weighing, and to speak in a way that settles the matter.","n":"ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் doesn't name its audience. For Parimelazhagar the settling speech is aimed at the parties being divided or joined, and for Manakkudavar it settles doubtful matters, so eloquence here is persuasion on one view and judgement on the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:635":{"book":"kural","num":635,"t":["அறனறிந்து ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்லான்எஞ் ஞான்றுந்","திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை"],"p":"He is the best helper (of the king) who understanding the duties, of the latter, is by his special learning, able to tender the fullest advice, and at all times conversant with the best method (of","r":"One who knows what is right, whose speech is rich and settled, and who always knows the ways of doing things is the companion for deliberation.","n":"Virtue comes first in the list, in a chapter otherwise about competence. But Parimelazhagar narrows it to 'virtues done by the king', which makes it knowledge of duties more than the minister's own goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","aram","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:636":{"book":"kural","num":636,"t":["மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு அதிநுட்பம்","யாவுள முன்நிற் பவை"],"p":"What (contrivances) are there so acute as to resist those who possess natural acuteness in addition to learning ?.","r":"What over-subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with treatises?","n":"Parimelazhagar names மதிநுட்பம் first since it must be given by the divine, leaving effort to the learning alone. His contrast of natural and artificial knowledge is a useful frame for the next verse. He also defends the text அதிநுட்பம் against அதினுட்பம், 'finer than that', the sense Manakkudavar's gloss gives; the verse's point survives either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:637":{"book":"kural","num":637,"t":["செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து","இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்"],"p":"Though you are acquainted with the (theoretical) methods (of performing an act), understand the ways of the world and act accordingly.","r":"Even after knowing the method of doing a thing, act with knowledge of how the world goes.","n":"செயற்கை and இயற்கை are opposed: the made and the given. In this verse a treatise's knowledge is the artifice and the world is the nature, which is a surprising way to rank them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Treatise methods must fit the prevailing ways of the world, or the world will blame them.","நூல் நெறியே ஆயினும் உலக நெறியோடு பொருந்தாதன செய்யற்க"],["manakkudavar","'The world' means the court: the character of kings and the standing of their retinue.","உலகநடை அறிதலாவது அரசர் சீலமும் பரிவாரத்திலுள்ளார் நிலைமையும் அறிதல்."]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:638":{"book":"kural","num":638,"t":["அறிகொன்று அறியான் எனினும் உறுதி","உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்"],"p":"Although the king be utterly ignorant, it is the duty of the minister to give (him) sound advice.","r":"Though the king destroys wise counsel and himself knows nothing, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is good for him.","n":"The word for the minister here is உழையிருந்தான், 'the one close by'. Duty arises from nearness, and Parimelazhagar adds that the world will lay the king's fall at the silent minister's door.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறிகொன்று means destroying the understanding of those who advise, even with contempt.","அறிந்து சொல்லியாரது அறிவையும் அழித்து அரசன் தானும் அறியானே ஆயினும்"],["manakkudavar","He splits the phrase differently: 'let the king know' what the minister said, and even if he knows nothing, the minister must speak.","அரசன் அமைச்சன் கூறிய பொருளை யறிக"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:639":{"book":"kural","num":639,"t":["பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரியின் பக்கததுள் தெவ்வோர்","எழுபது கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Far better are seventy crores of enemies (for a king) than a minister at his side who intends (his) ruin.","r":"Better seventy crore enemies than a minister at one's side who plots harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is that open enemies can be guarded against, but the one inside cannot. 'Seventy crore' is his example of a huge number, not a figure with meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:640":{"book":"kural","num":640,"t":["முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்","திறப்பாடு இலாஅ தவர்"],"p":"Those ministers who are destitute of (executive) ability will fail to carry out their projects, although they may have contrived aright.","r":"Though they plan things properly, those without practical capacity do only what comes to no conclusion.","n":"A good plan is not enough. Parimelazhagar splits capacity into knowing remedies for obstacles and being firm in oneself, so this anticipates the chapter on firmness in action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:641":{"book":"kural","num":641,"t":["நாநலம் என்னும் நலனுடைமை அந்நலம்","யாநலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The possession of that goodness which is called the goodness of speech is (even to others) better than any other goodness.","r":"Having the goodness called goodness of tongue is a good that isn't contained in any other good.","n":"The verse is about the excellence of speech as such; it does not mention ministers. Parimelazhagar draws them in from the chapter's position and also argues that if others need it, ministers need it more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:642":{"book":"kural","num":642,"t":["ஆக்கமுங் கேடும் அதனால் வருதலால்","காத்தோம்பல் சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு"],"p":"Since (both) wealth and evil result from (their) speech, ministers should most carefully guard themselves against faultiness therein.","r":"Since gain and ruin both come from it, guard against any lapse in speech.","n":"சோர்வு is a slip, not malice. Parimelazhagar widens the stakes: a minister's lapse falls on all living beings at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:643":{"book":"kural","num":643,"t":["கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும் தகையவாய்க் கேளாரும்","வேட்ப மொழிவதாம் சொல்"],"p":"The (minister's) speech is that which seeks (to express) elements as bind his friends (to himself) and is so delivered as to make even his enemies desire (his friendship).","r":"Speech is what is said so that it binds those who hear it and makes even those who have not heard want it.","n":"The second half is the striking one: speech is judged partly by its effect on those not present. Parimelazhagar lists good speech's qualities here as freedom from error, brevity, clarity, sweetness and valuable result.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Hearers are allies who accepted him and non-hearers are hostile parties won over. He criticises all others for reading தகையவாய் as 'having worth'.","நட்பாய் ஏற்றுக் கொண்டாரைப் பின் வேறுபடாமல் பிணிக்கும் குணங்களை அவாவி"],["manakkudavar","Those are the ones who asked and the ones who did not ask.","வினாவினாரைப் பிணித்துக் கொள்ளுந் தகையவாய், வினாவாதா ரும் விரும்புமாறு"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:644":{"book":"kural","num":644,"t":["திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை அறனும்","பொருளும் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்"],"p":"Understand the qualities (of your hearers) and (then) make your speech; for superior to it, there is neither virtue nor wealth.","r":"Speak with knowledge of what the moment and hearer require, for no virtue or gain goes beyond that.","n":"A surprising claim: fit speech ranks at the top of both virtue and gain. Parimelazhagar explains that the cause stands for the effect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'capacities' are differences of rank by birth, learning, conduct, wealth, form and age, and to speak according to them is the virtue.","குடிப்பிறப்பு, கல்வி, ஒழுக்கம், செல்வம், உருவம், பருவம் என்பவற்றான் வரும் தகுதி வேறுபாடுகள்."],["manakkudavar","It's the way of speaking: knowing this avoids slander, empty talk and lying, so it becomes virtue.","புறங்கூறாமையும் பயனில சொல்லாமையும் பொய்கூறா மையும் உளவாம்"],["kalaignar","It means knowing clearly the reason for saying something.",""]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:645":{"book":"kural","num":645,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லைப் பிறிதோர்சொல் அச்சொல்லை","வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"Deliver your speech, after assuring yourself that no counter speech can defeat your own.","r":"Speak your word once you know there is no other word that can defeat it.","n":"சொல் occurs five times, so the verse is a sort of closed argument. Parimelazhagar says winning means excelling in the qualities of speech, which is a broader standard than mere refutation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:646":{"book":"kural","num":646,"t":["வேட்பத்தாஞ் சொல்லிப் பிறர்சொல் பயன்கோடல்","மாட்சியின் மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the opinion of those who are free from defects in diplomacy that the minister should speak so as to make his hearers desire (to hear more) and grasp the meaning of what he hears himself.","r":"It is the way of those without blemish in excellence to speak so that others want more and to take the fruit from what others say.","n":"Mastery of speech includes a discipline of hearing. Parimelazhagar adds generosity: a master doesn't scorn others' faulty words, and takes what is useful in them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:647":{"book":"kural","num":647,"t":["சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான் அவனை","இகல்வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"It is impossible for any one to conquer him by intrique who possesses power of speech, and is neither faulty nor timid.","r":"It's hard for anyone to beat in a contest a person who can speak, doesn't slip, and isn't afraid.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss of 'speaking so that it is accepted' is uncomfortable: making people decide 'yes' even where the thing isn't actually in their interest. Eloquence, in that reading, isn't truthful by nature, and the chapter doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:648":{"book":"kural","num":648,"t":["விரைந்து தொழில்கேட்கும் ஞாலம் நிரந்தினிது","சொல்லுதல் வல்லார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If there be those who can speak on various subjects in their proper order and in a pleasing manner, the world would readily accept them.","r":"The world quickly responds if it gets those who can speak in order and sweetly.","n":"பெறின், 'if it gets', implies rarity. Parimelazhagar cites a Sanskrit saying that a true speaker is one in a hundred thousand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தொழில் is what is being said, and the world accepts these matters readily.","சொல்லப்படும் காரியங்களை நிரல்படக் கோத்து"],["manakkudavar","தொழில் is work to do: people rush up and ask what they must do.","உலகத்தார் மேவி விரைந்து சென்று செய்யுந் தொழில் யாது என்று கேட்பர்"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:649":{"book":"kural","num":649,"t":["பலசொல்லக் காமுறுவர் மன்றமா சற்ற","சிலசொல்லல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"They will desire to utter many words, who do not know how to speak a few faultless ones.","r":"Those who don't know how to say a few faultless words surely long to say many.","n":"Loquacity is explained as a substitute for an ability, not an excess of it. Parimelazhagar takes மன்ற as a firm answer to those who actually admire nonstop speech as eloquence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:650":{"book":"kural","num":650,"t":["இண்ருழ்த்தும் நாறா மலரனையர் கற்றது","உணர விரித்துரையா தார்"],"p":"Those who are unable to set forth their acquirements (before others) are like flowers blossoming in a cluster and yet without fragrance.","r":"Those who can't unfold what they've learned so that others understand are like flowers blooming in clusters without scent.","n":"Coming right after 649's praise of few words, this verse praises expansion. Manakkudavar notices this: brevity alone isn't enough, and one must also expand where needed. In the image, learning is already in bloom; what is missing is the part that travels to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["solvanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:651":{"book":"kural","num":651,"t":["துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் த்ருஉம் வினைநலம்","வேண்டிய எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"The efficacy of support will yield (only) wealth; (but) the efficacy of action will yield all that is desired.","r":"Good companions bring prosperity, while good action brings everything one could want.","n":"The verse does not say what 'everything' includes. Parimelazhagar expands it to virtue, wealth and pleasure here and desired stations hereafter, and he frames the contrast as between the visible good of allies and the inferred good of deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:652":{"book":"kural","num":652,"t":["என்றும் ஒருவுதல் வேண்டும் புகழொடு","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Ministers should at all times avoid acts which, in addition to fame, yield no benefit (for the future).","r":"One must at all times avoid action that yields neither renown nor good.","n":"என்றும் is pointed. Parimelazhagar says it holds in rising, shrinking and stable circumstances; Manakkudavar says it holds even when one has no means of doing anything else. The rule doesn't relax under pressure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:653":{"book":"kural","num":653,"t":["ஒஓதல் வேண்டும் ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை","ஆஅதும் என்னு மவர்"],"p":"Those who say, \"we will become (better)\" should avoid the performance of acts that would destroy (their fame).","r":"Those who say 'we shall rise' must refrain from the deed that dims their light.","n":"Ambition itself is the ground of the restraint. Parimelazhagar's summary is that gain that dims one's light isn't gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:654":{"book":"kural","num":654,"t":["இடுக்கண் படினும் இளிவந்த செய்யார்","நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those who have infallible judgement though threatened with peril will not do acts which have brought disgrace (on former ministers).","r":"Even when caught in trouble, those of unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading.","n":"Pope's 'ever-during vision' suggests mysticism; the Tamil நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி is steadiness of judgement. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is a calculation: trouble passes but disgrace doesn't, and what must come will come anyway.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","idukkan","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:655":{"book":"kural","num":655,"t":["எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க செய்வானேல்","மற்றன்ன செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let a minister never do acts of which he would have to grieve saying, \"what is this I have done\"; (but) should he do (them), it were good that he grieved not.","r":"Do nothing that makes you cry out 'What have I done!'; but if you do, it's good not to do the like.","n":"The second line turns on what அன்ன points to. Pope gives both answers: his couplet says sin no more, and his gloss says do not grieve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Once done in confusion, don't go on grieving: regret displays a lack of remedy and firmness and spreads one's own shame. He says all others miss the connection.","பின் இருந்து அவ்விரங்கல்களைச் செய்யாதொழிதல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","If one acts at all, one should not do anything like such deeds.","அவைபோல்வனவுஞ் செய்யாமையே நல்லது."],["kalaignar","Having done it, the good thing is not to continue it.",""]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:656":{"book":"kural","num":656,"t":["ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினுஞ் செய்யற் க","சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though a minister may see his mother starve; let him do not act which the wise would (treat with contempt).","r":"Even if you must watch the mother who bore you go hungry, don't do what the worthy condemn.","n":"The verse picks the hardest case it can imagine and refuses the exception. Its reach is the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","saandror","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This overrides a general dharma rule permitting wrong to feed aged parents, wife and infant, but only for ministers, who have standing and royal duties.","தீயன பலவுஞ் செய்தாயினும் புறந்தருக' என்னும் அறநூற்பொது விதி"],["kalaignar","It applies to anyone, with no exception: not even to relieve a mother in the throes of hunger.",""]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:657":{"book":"kural","num":657,"t":["பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை"],"p":"Far more excellent is the extreme poverty of the wise than wealth obtained by heaping up of sinful deeds.","r":"The utter poverty of the worthy is above wealth gained under a load of blame.","n":"Manakkudavar sees this as a reply to an objection: living by these rules will leave you poor. The verse concedes it. Poverty is not denied or softened by கழி, 'extreme'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:658":{"book":"kural","num":658,"t":["கடிந்த கடிந்தொரார் செய்தார்க்கு அவைதாம்","முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்"],"p":"The actions of those, who have not desisted from doing deeds forbidden (by the great), will, even if they succeed, cause them sorrow.","r":"Even when they succeed, the forbidden things people do without shunning them bring anguish.","n":"முடிந்தாலும் answers the practical objection that the end might come off. Parimelazhagar goes further and doubts it: such acts won't usually succeed, and even if somehow they do, pain follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:659":{"book":"kural","num":659,"t":["அழக் கொண்ட எல்லாம் அழப்போம் இழப்பினும்","பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பா லவை"],"p":"All that has been obtained with tears (to the victim) will depart with tears (to himself); but what has been by fair means; though with loss at first, will afterwards yield fruit.","r":"All that is taken with tears leaves with tears; what comes from good ways, even if lost, pays later.","n":"The verse names no one in அழ. The commentators divide the tears, so the victim weeps on taking and the taker on losing, and this moral symmetry is part of the sound of the line. பின் covers the next birth for Parimelazhagar, which the verse does not specify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:660":{"book":"kural","num":660,"t":["சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்தே மார்த்தல் பசுமண்","கலத்துள்நீர் பெய்திரீஇ யற்று"],"p":"(For a minister) to protect (his king) with wealth obtained by foul means is like preserving a vessel of wet clay by filling it with water.","r":"Making wealth by deceit and feeling secure in it is like pouring water into an unfired clay pot.","n":"Both pot and water are lost: the wrongly got wealth also destroys the one it was meant to protect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","thooimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமார்த்தல் means making the king safe with such wealth. He attacks those who read it as rejoicing, since that breaks the simile's form.","பிறரெல்லாம், 'ஏமாத்தல்' என்று பாடமோதி, அதற்கு மகிழ்தல் என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","It means delight: one enjoys what one gained by deception, and both person and wealth perish.","வஞ்சத்தாலே பொருள் தேடி மகிழ்ந்திருத்தல்"]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:661":{"book":"kural","num":661,"t":["வினைத்திட்பம் என்பது ஒருவன் மனத்திட்பம்","மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற"],"p":"Firmness in action is (simply) one's firmness of mind; all other (abilities) are not of this nature.","r":"What is called firmness in action is one's firmness of mind; all else is something other.","n":"Parimelazhagar points to the word choice: மற்றைய admits these others are related, and பிற denies that they count here. Manakkudavar takes 'the others' to be means and methods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:662":{"book":"kural","num":662,"t":["ஊறொரால் உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை இவ்விரண்டின்","ஆறென்பர் ஆய்ந்தவர் கோள்"],"p":"Not to perform a ruinous act, and not to be discouraged by the ruinous termination of an act, are the two maxims which, the wise say, from the principles of those who have investigated the subject.","r":"Avoiding harm beforehand and not giving way once it has come are, those who have studied it say, the path of these two.","n":"Parimelazhagar traces the maxim to the ministers of gods and demons, Jupiter and Venus, and so explains why clean action slips in here. The verse itself mentions only 'those who have studied'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Avoid deeds likely to fail, and don't flag if fate spoils a deed. He rejects the variant ஓரார் as ungrammatical.","பழுதுபடும் வினைகளைச் செய்யாமையும், செய்யும் வினை தெய்வத்தான் பழுதுபட்ட வழி அதற்குத் தளராமையும்"],["manakkudavar","Reading ஓரார்: don't dwell on the suffering that action involves, and if it comes, don't weaken.","வினை செய்யுங்கால் உறும் துன்பத்தை ஓரார் ஆதலும்"],["kalaignar","Work to remove obstacles before they come, and don't lose heart if they still do.",""]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:663":{"book":"kural","num":663,"t":["கடைக்கொட்கச் செய்தக்க தாண்மை இடைக்கொட்கின்","எற்றா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"So to perform an act as to publish it (only) at its termination is (true) manliness; for to announce it beforehand, will cause irremediable sorrow.","r":"True manliness is doing it so that it comes round only at the end; if it comes round midway, it brings unending trouble.","n":"The verb கொட்க carries the verse, and firmness comes out as secrecy on one reading and as persistence on the other. The two make different claims about what ஆண்மை is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கொட்க means becoming known: conceal the deed until its end, for enemies who learn of it midway will block it.","செய்யப்படும் வினையை முடிவின்க புலப்படும் வகை முன்னெல்லாம் மறைத்துச் செய்வதே திட்பமாவது"],["manakkudavar","கொட்க means turning back: go through to the end and return, for returning midway brings harm.","ஒரு வினையைத் தொடங்கினால், முடிவிலே சென்று மீளல் செய் வது ஆண்மையாவது"]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:664":{"book":"kural","num":664,"t":["சொல்லுதல் யார்க்கும் எளிய அரியவாம்","சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்"],"p":"To say (how an act is to be performed) is (indeed) easy for any one; but far difficult it is to do according to what has been said.","r":"Speaking is easy for anyone; doing as one has spoken is hard.","n":"Pope's 'shape one's life' turns it into a general moral. Both early commentators restrict it to the plan: saying 'this is how we will do this'. After a full chapter on eloquence, this comes as a quiet correction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","solvanmai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:665":{"book":"kural","num":665,"t":["வீறெய்தி மாண்டார் வினைத்திட்பம் வேந்தன்கண்","ஊறெய்தி உள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"The firmness in action of those who have become great by the excellence (of their counsel) will, by attaining its fulfilment in the person of the king, be esteemed (by all).","r":"The firmness in action of those great in excellence, once it takes effect on the king, comes to be held in everyone's regard.","n":"On Parimelazhagar's reading the fruits lodge in the king, wealth and fame being his; the minister's firmness earns only regard. Kalaignar seems to have the firmness attract the ruler instead, so what ஊறெய்தி describes is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","amaichu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:666":{"book":"kural","num":666,"t":["எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்து எண்ணியார்","திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"If those who have planned (an undertaking) possess firmness (in executing it) they will obtain what they have desired even as they have desired it.","r":"What people have thought of, they obtain as they thought of it, if those who thought it turn out firm.","n":"The first line sounds unconditional, and it is often quoted that way. The condition is in the second line: thinking must be backed by firmness. Parimelazhagar adds that such people don't think otherwise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thitpam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:667":{"book":"kural","num":667,"t":["உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும் உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு","அச்சாணி அன்னார் உடைத்து"],"p":"Let none be despised for (their) size; (for) the world has those who resemble the linch-pin of the big rolling car.","r":"Do not scorn anyone for how they look: the great rolling chariot has its linchpin.","n":"The image answers the appearance: the pin bears great loads because it is placed exactly at the point of strain. Parimelazhagar applies this to small-bodied ministers while Manakkudavar applies it to everyone (யாவரையும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:668":{"book":"kural","num":668,"t":["கலங்காது கண்ட வினைக்கண் துளங்காது","தூக்கங் கடிந்து செயல்"],"p":"An act that has been firmly resolved on must be as firmly carried out without delay.","r":"With the deed one has looked at clearly and decided, act unwaveringly and without delay.","n":"Sequence matters: கலங்காது before துளங்காது. Clarity comes first, then speed, and Parimelazhagar notes that a confused mind makes what should be dropped seem worth doing. தூக்கம் here is delay, not sleep as in Pope's 'unslumbering'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:669":{"book":"kural","num":669,"t":["துன்பம் உறவரினும் செய்க துணிவாற்றி","இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though it should cause increasing sorrow (at the outset), do with firmness the act that yield bliss (in the end).","r":"Even though pain closes in, do resolutely the deed that yields joy.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses technical language to weigh momentary pain of effort (கணிகம்) against the lasting pleasure of the result (பரிணாம இன்பம்). The verse only says 'joy-yielding'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:670":{"book":"kural","num":670,"t":["எனைத்திட்பம் எய் தியக் கண்ணும் வினைத்திட்பம்","வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு"],"p":"The great will not esteem those who esteem not firmness of action, whatever other abilities the latter may possess.","r":"Whatever other firmness they have attained, the world doesn't want those who don't want firmness of action.","n":"வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது mirrors the rejection. Parimelazhagar reads உலகு as 'the high', and Pope's gloss has 'the great', but the verse's own word is the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:671":{"book":"kural","num":671,"t":["சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல் அத்துணிவு","தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது"],"p":"Consultation ends in forming a resolution (to act); (but) delay in the execution of that resolve is an evil,”","r":"Deliberation ends in arriving at resolve, and it's a fault if that resolve lingers in delay.","n":"The verse gives thought a limit from both sides: it must run until resolve, as Parimelazhagar notes, and it has no right to run after. Manakkudavar supplies the risk: enemies learn of the delay and take precautions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:672":{"book":"kural","num":672,"t":["தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க","தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"Sleep over such (actions) as may be slept over; (but) never over such as may not be slept over.","r":"Sleep over what should be done slowly; do not sleep over what should be done without sleeping.","n":"தூங்கு means both to sleep and to delay. The verse repeats it so that the word itself has to be sorted, and it does not tell you how to tell the two kinds of task apart; Parimelazhagar says they are known through strength and timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:673":{"book":"kural","num":673,"t":["ஙல்லும்வா யெல்லாம் வினைநன்றே ஒல்லாக்கால்","செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Whenever it is possible to overcome your enemy the act {of fighting) is certainly good; if not, endeavour to employ some more ‘successful method,","r":"Wherever it's possible, the deed is good; where it isn't, act by looking for a way that will go.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வினை here means war: where stronger, fight, since force is what is feared; otherwise use one of the other three means (conciliation, division, gifts).","இயலுமிடத்தெல்லாம் போராற் செய்தல் நன்று"],["manakkudavar","No reference to war: act when possible, and when not, keep it in mind and wait for an opening.","இயலாத காலத்து அதனை நினைந்திருந்து, இயலும் இடம் பார்த்துச் செய்க"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:674":{"book":"kural","num":674,"t":["வினைபகை என்றிரண்டின் எச்சம் நினையுங்கால்","தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்"],"p":"When duly considered, the incomplete execution of an undertaking and hostility will grow and destroy one like the {unextinguished} remnant of a fire,","r":"If you think about it, what is left over from a task or an enmity scorches like what is left over from a fire.","n":"நினையுங்கால் suggests that this only becomes clear on reflection: a remainder seems small at the moment. Parimelazhagar assigns this to the stronger party, for whom leaving things unfinished seems safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:675":{"book":"kural","num":675,"t":["பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்","இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்"],"p":"Do an act alter a due consideration of the {following} five, viz., Money, means, time, execution and place.","r":"Act after thinking through until the darkness clears these five: resources, means, time, the deed and the place.","n":"The list recalls that of 631, but both early commentators double each item: one's own and the enemy's. The verse doesn't say this, and Kalaignar's checklist has only one side. If they're right, this is a list about comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:676":{"book":"kural","num":676,"t":["முடிவும் இடையூறும் முற்றியாங்கு எய்தும்","படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்"],"p":"An act is to be performed after considering the exertion required, the obstacles to be encountered, and the great to be gained {on its completion).","r":"Act after looking at how it would be finished, what will hinder it, and the great yield when complete.","n":"Parimelazhagar states it as a cost-benefit test: act if the yield outweighs the effort and obstacles. Manakkudavar reads முடிவு as the manner of ending, and Parimelazhagar as effort by metonymy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:677":{"book":"kural","num":677,"t":["செய்வினை செய்வான் செயன்முறை அவ்வினை","உள்ளறிவான் உள்ளம் கொளல்"],"p":"The method of performance for one who has begun an act is to ascertain the mind of him who knows the secret thereof,","r":"The proper way for someone doing a task is to learn the mind of the one who knows it from inside.","n":"After the preceding checklists, this verse says they aren't enough, and that someone who has done it should be consulted. Parimelazhagar's உள் அறிவான் is the person who has done this before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:678":{"book":"kural","num":678,"t":["வினையான் வினையாக்கிக் கோடல் நனைகவுள்","யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று"],"p":"To make one undertaking the means of accomplishing another (similar to it) is like making one rutting elephant the means of capturing arother.","r":"Using one task to accomplish another is like binding a rutting elephant with another elephant.","n":"The image takes for granted the practice of capturing elephants with an elephant, and பிணித்தல் is itself a skill of the deed. The elephants of 597 and 599 are about character; this one is about technique.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Plan each task so that it becomes a means to finishing the next similar one.","தொடங்கிய வினையானே பிறிதும் ஓர் வினையை முடித்தற்கு உபாயம் ஆமாறு எண்ணிச் செய்க."],["manakkudavar","It means setting an enemy's enemies against him, so that enmity is used against enmity.","தமக்கு ஒரு பகைவர் தோன்றினால் அவர்க்குப் பகையாயினாரை அவ ரோடு பகைக்குமாறு"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:679":{"book":"kural","num":679,"t":["நட்டார்க்கு நல்ல செயலின் விரைந்ததே","ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்"],"p":"One should rather hasten to secure the alliance of the foes (of one's foes} rather than perform good offices to one’s friends,","r":"Bringing the unallied over to one's side is more urgent than doing good to one's friends.","n":"Friends can wait because they are friends. This chilly ranking of urgency is about strategy, not love, and Parimelazhagar's reason is timing: it must be done before enemies see one's weakness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒட்டார் are those not allied with one's enemy, i.e. the enemy's enemies, to be made friends. He allows another sense: keeping one's own foes from joining others.","தன் பகைவரோடு ஒட்டாரைத் தனக்கு நட்பாக்கிக்கோடல்."],["manakkudavar","ஒட்டார் are simply one's enemies, to be reconciled, and this is put ahead of doing good for one's own kin.","தஞ் சுற்றத்திற்கு நல்லவை செய்தலினும், பகைவரைப் பொருந்தி நட்பாகக் கொள்ளுதலை"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:680":{"book":"kural","num":680,"t":["உறைசிறியார் உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சிக் குறைபெறின்","கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து"],"p":"Ministers of smali states, afraid of their people being frightened will yield to and acknowledge thelr superior foes, it the latter offer them a chance of reconciliation","r":"Those whose domain is small, afraid of the trembling within, will bow to the great and accept what they can get if it is offered.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the verse's tone: since bowing isn't what the honourable would choose, கொள்வர் reports what is done, without praising it. His reason is that if one's own side panics, all is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panivu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:681":{"book":"kural","num":681,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் வேந்தவாம்","பண்புடைமை தூதுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualification of an ambassador are affection (for his relations) a fitting birth, and the possession of attributes pleasing to royalty.","r":"Love, birth in an honourable family, and qualities kings desire make up the nature of one who carries a message.","n":"Whatever அன்பு means here, the envoy's list begins from the affections, not the skills. Parimelazhagar's 'qualities kings desire' also serve abroad: foreign kings too come under his sway.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The love is for one's own kin, which keeps them from harm, and the birth is in a family fit for ministry.","தன் சுற்றத்தார்மாட்டு அன்புடையனாதலும்"],["manakkudavar","The love is for the king, and 'qualities kings desire' means not desiring what the king desires.","வேந்தனால் விரும்பப்படும் குணமுடைமையாவது அவன் விரும்புவனவற் றைத் தான் விரும்பாமை."]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:682":{"book":"kural","num":682,"t":["அன்பறிவு ஆராய்ந்த சொல்வன்மை தூதுரைப்பார்க்கு","இன்றி யமையாத மூன்று"],"p":"Love (to his sovereign), knowledge (of his affairs), and a discriminating power of speech (before other sovereigns) are the three sine qua non qualifications of an ambassador.","r":"Love, knowledge and carefully considered eloquence are the three things an envoy can't do without.","n":"ஆராய்ந்த qualifies eloquence, and Parimelazhagar takes it as choosing words that will win assent. Parimelazhagar reads this verse's love as being for the king, so he has given the two adjacent verses two different objects of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:683":{"book":"kural","num":683,"t":["நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன் ஆகுதல் வேலாருள்","வென்றி வினையுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"To be powerful in politics among those who are learned (in ethics) is the character of him who speaks to lance-bearing kings on matters of triumph (to his own sovereign).","r":"It is the mark of one who speaks of victory among spear-bearers to be strong in the treatises among those who know them.","n":"The two audiences are set side by side: those with treatises and those with spears. Parimelazhagar sharpens the first: the ministers there merely understand the texts, while the envoy must be able to set them out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:684":{"book":"kural","num":684,"t":["அறிவுரு வாராய்ந்த கல்விஇம் மூன்றன்","செறிவுடையான் செல்க வினைக்கு"],"p":"He may go on a mission (to foreign rulers) who has combined in him all these three. viz., (natural) sense, an attractive bearing and well-tried learning.","r":"Let one who has these three together go on this work: understanding, presence and well-tested learning.","n":"உரு, bodily presence, counts as a requirement. Parimelazhagar says all three earn respect, on which the work depends, and that such a combination in one person is rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:685":{"book":"kural","num":685,"t":["தொகச் சொல்லித் தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி","நன்றி பயப்பதாந் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who (in the presence of foreign rulers) speaks briefly, avoids harshness, talks so as to make them smile, and thus brings good (to his own sovereign).","r":"The envoy is one who speaks in brief, removes what would offend, speaks so as to bring a smile, and thereby gets good done.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives each tactic a psychology: when told a compressed reason, hearers who would refuse many matters come round; when told in a pleasing way, they accept hard things without noticing they are hard. Courtesy is presented as technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:686":{"book":"kural","num":686,"t":["கற்றுக்கண் அஞ்சான் செலச்சொல்லிக் காலத்தால்","தக்கது அறிவதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who having studied (politics) talks impressively, is not afraid of angry looks, and knows (to employ) the art suited to the time.","r":"The envoy is one who is learned, does not fear hostile eyes, speaks so that it goes home, and knows what fits the time.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that காலத்தால் தக்கது allows for ends reached by means other than what the treatises taught. Manakkudavar makes the learning very specific: how to turn aside the enemy king's anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:687":{"book":"kural","num":687,"t":["கடனறிந்து காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து","எண்ணி உரைப்பான் தலை"],"p":"He is chief (among ambassadors) who understands the proper decorum (before foreign princes), seeks the (proper) occasion, knows the (most suitable) place, and delivers his message after (due) consideration.","r":"The highest is the one who knows the protocol, considers the moment, knows the place, and speaks after thinking it through.","n":"Parimelazhagar's எண்ணி is rehearsal in chains: what I say, what they answer, what I say next. His இடம் is a place where one's allies are present, so a place is political, not physical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:688":{"book":"kural","num":688,"t":["தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்","வாய்மை வழியுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualifications of him who faithfully delivers his (sovereign's) message are purity, the support (of foreign ministers), and boldness, with truthfulness in addition to the (aforesaid) three.","r":"Purity, support, resolve and truthfulness in these three mark the one who delivers the message faithfully.","n":"Parimelazhagar's purity is immunity to bribes of money and women, so that the message isn't altered. Truth here is a qualification for being believed. These are professional virtues as he explains them, not an inner holiness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","thooimai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணைமை is having the foreign court's ministers as support, which can calm a king's anger.","தமக்கு அவரமைச்சர் துணையாந்தன்மையும்"],["manakkudavar","துணைமை is having one's own retinue, and purity is of body and mind.","தூய்மையுடைமையும், சுற்றமுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:689":{"book":"kural","num":689,"t":["விடுமாற்றம் வேந்தர்க்கு உரைப்பான் வடுமாற்றம்","வாய்சேரா வன்க ணவன்"],"p":"He alone is fit to communicate (his sovereign's) reply, who possesses the firmness not to utter even inadvertently what may reflect discredit (on the latter).","r":"One who conveys the king's reply to kings is a hard man whose mouth will not let slip a word that shames.","n":"வன்கண் is again a virtue, as in 632, and Parimelazhagar shows why: the temptation comes from fear for oneself. The slip would be a phrase softening his king's standing in order to save his skin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:690":{"book":"kural","num":690,"t":["இறுதி பயப்பினும் எஞ்சாது இறைவற் கு","உறுதி பயப்பதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is the ambassador who fearlessly seeks his sovereign's good though it should cost him his life (to deliver his message).","r":"The envoy is one who, even if it brings his end, doesn't hold back and secures what is good for his lord.","n":"இறுதி is the limit, and the chapter stops there. The ideal is unconditional, and the verse gives no reason why the envoy should owe so much; it's taken for granted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:691":{"book":"kural","num":691,"t":["அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க","இகல்வேந்தர்ச் சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Ministers who serve under fickle-minded monarchs should, like those who warm themselves at the fire, be neither (too) far, nor (too) near.","r":"Those who attach themselves to quarrelsome kings should be like people warming at a fire: not going off, not going close.","n":"The image avoids praising or blaming the king: fire doesn't have intentions. Parimelazhagar spells out both halves: at a distance one gets nothing, and up close one is burned. இகல் doesn't say which kings, and may imply all of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:692":{"book":"kural","num":692,"t":["மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை மன்னரால்","மன்னிய ஆக்கந் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"Not desiring what kings desire brings, through those kings, lasting prosperity.","n":"A kind of worldly detachment that works as strategy. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: kings fear those who would rival them in pleasures, finery and rank, and they reward one who doesn't. So the restraint is prudence and may not be virtue at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:693":{"book":"kural","num":693,"t":["போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல் கடுத்தபின்","தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"Ministers who would save themselves should avoid (the commission of) serious errors for if the king's suspicion is once roused, no one can remove it.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against the gravest things; once suspicion has arisen, nobody can clear it.","n":"Parimelazhagar's list of grave faults is betrayal, intimacy with royal women and seizing treasure. His comparison is memorable: like a debt that comes to mind at each sight of the debtor, a cleared suspicion recurs each time the king sees you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:694":{"book":"kural","num":694,"t":["செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்","ஆன்ற பெரியா ரகத்து"],"p":"While in the presence of the sovereign, ministers should neither whisper to nor smile at others.","r":"In the presence of the great and established, suppress whispers and shared smiles.","n":"These small signs are dangerous because a ruler reads them: Parimelazhagar says he will assume they concern his own faults. The next chapter's interest in reading signs here falls on those who are read. The verse says 'great ones', and only commentators limit this to kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:695":{"book":"kural","num":695,"t":["எப்பொருளும் ஓரார் தொடரார்மற் றப்பொருளை","விட்டக்கால் கேட்க மறை"],"p":"(When the king is engaged) in secret counsel (with others), ministers should neither over-hear anything whatever nor pry into it with inquisitive questions, but (wait to) listen when it is divulged (by the king himself).","r":"Don't listen in to anything or press for anything; hear a secret when it has been let go.","n":"Manakkudavar says that even the desire to hear is a fault. The whole risk is that curiosity about power looks like a threat to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:696":{"book":"kural","num":696,"t":["குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி வெறுப்பில","வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்"],"p":"Knowing the (king's disposition and seeking the right time, (the minister) should in a pleasing manner suggest things such as are desirable and not disagreeable.","r":"Knowing the mood and considering the time, say what is needed and not disliked, so that it is wanted.","n":"Each word is doing work: வெறுப்பில, 'not disliked', is a test of the hearer, while வேண்டுப, 'needed', is a test of the matter. Parimelazhagar explains that a thing a king doesn't accept won't get done, so the point is practical, and that during lust or anger advice is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:697":{"book":"kural","num":697,"t":["வேட்பன சொல்லி வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கேட்பினும் சொல்லா விடல்"],"p":"Ministers should (always) give agreeable advice but on no occasion recommend useless actions, though requested (to do so).","r":"Say what is wanted, and never say what is useless even if you are asked.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Say what is of great benefit and desired, even if not asked, and don't say what is profitless, even if the king asks.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் பயனிலவாயவற்றைத் தானே கேட்டாலும் சொல்லாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","Say what the king wants; and if an enemy king questions an envoy about the size of the army, evade: 'Don't you know?'","பகையரசன் வினாவினால், நீ அறியாததொன்றோ வென்று அளவு கூறாமை."]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:698":{"book":"kural","num":698,"t":["இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார் நின்ற","ஒளியோடு ஒழுகப் படும்"],"p":"Ministers should behave in accordance with the (Divine) light in the person of kings and not despise them saying, \"He is our junior (in age) and connected with our family!\".","r":"Without belittling him as 'he's younger' or 'he's kin of such a degree', one should conduct oneself in keeping with the light he stands in.","n":"நின்ற ஒளி: the light is one he stands in, and this can be read as belonging to the office, whoever holds it. Pope's '(Divine)' comes from Parimelazhagar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The light is the king's divinity: he guards the world while asleep, and they must act as men before gods.","அவர் கடவுளரும் தாம் மக்களுமாய் ஒழுகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The light is simply the headship he has obtained.","அவர் பெற்று நின்ற தலைமையோடே பொருந்த ஒழுக வேண்டும்"],["kalaignar","It is the eminence of those in positions of government.",""]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:699":{"book":"kural","num":699,"t":["கொளப்பட்டேம் என்றெண்ணிக் கொள்ளாத செய்யார்","துளக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement is firm will not do what is disagreeable (to the sovereign) saying (within themselves) \"We are esteemed by the king\".","r":"Those of unshaken clarity won't, thinking 'we are in favour', do what is unacceptable.","n":"Parimelazhagar's diagnosis is one of changed self-image: those who fall think of themselves as new people after being favoured. Clarity, then, is seeing that one is the same person as before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:700":{"book":"kural","num":700,"t":["பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும்","கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்"],"p":"The (foolish) claim with which a minister does unbecoming acts because of his (long) familiarity (with the king) will ensure his ruin.","r":"The intimacy that, thinking 'we go back a long way', does what is improper brings ruin.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is the chapter's coldest note: when the king can no longer bear it, he takes the life without any kindly regard for the old bond. The chapter on kindly regard asks for exactly what the courtier here can't count on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:701":{"book":"kural","num":701,"t":["கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","மாறாநீர் வையக் கணி"],"p":"The minister who by looking (at the king) understands his mind without being told (of it), will be a perpetual ornament to the world which is surrounded by a never-drying sea.","r":"One who sees and knows what is meant before it is said is an ornament to the earth, whose waters never fail.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose mind is being read. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the king's, Kalaignar as anyone's. After the previous chapter, a minister's skill seems likely, though it is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:702":{"book":"kural","num":702,"t":["ஐயப் படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானைத்","தெய்வத்தோ டொப்பக் கொளல்"],"p":"He is to be esteemed a god who is able to ascertain without a doubt what is within (one's mind).","r":"Regard as equal to a god one who knows, without doubt, what is within.","n":"ஐயப்படாஅது is the claim's high bar: accurate conjecture isn't enough. Parimelazhagar locates the divinity narrowly in this one capacity, while the person stays human in body. Kalaignar's phrase 'if it is said that this power is god's' hedges the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:703":{"book":"kural","num":703,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணர் வாரை உறுப்பினுள்","யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்"],"p":"The king should ever give whatever (is asked) of his belongings and secure him who, by the indications (of his own mind) is able to read those of another.","r":"Give whatever part of what you have, and gain those who read a sign by a sign.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading contains a small theory of other minds: since inner workings are alike in all people, knowing one's own gives access to another's. The verse's குறிப்பிற் குறிப்பு is short enough to allow this but does not state it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","amaichu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They read another's sign through their own inner sign; 'parts' are outer possessions such as wealth, land, elephants.","உள் நிகழும் நெறி யாவர்க்கும் ஒத்தலின், பிறர் குறிப்பறிதற்குத் தம் குறிப்புக் கருவியாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","They read the mind's intent through the sign in the face, and 'parts' can be limbs of the body or of the realm.","முகக் குறிப்பினாலே உள்ளக்கருத்தை அறியுமவர்களை"],["kalaignar","They read the face, and the offer is any post in government.",""]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:704":{"book":"kural","num":704,"t":["குறித்தது கூறாமைக் கொள்வாரோ டேனை","உறுப்போ ரனையரால் வேறு"],"p":"Those who understand one's thoughts without being informed (thereof) and those who do not, may (indeed) resemble one another bodily; still are they different (mentally).","r":"Those who take what is meant without its being said and all the others are alike in limbs, yet they are different.","n":"Parimelazhagar completes the thought: lacking the highest knowing, the others are to be classed as beasts. The verse just says வேறு, 'different', and the understatement may be stronger than the gloss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:705":{"book":"kural","num":705,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணரா வாயின் உறுப்பினுள்","என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்?"],"p":"Of what use are the eyes amongst one's members, if they cannot by their own indications dive those of another ?.","r":"If they can't read a sign by a sign, of what use, among one's limbs, are eyes?","n":"Eyes are asked to do what is not seeing in the ordinary sense. Parimelazhagar says awareness itself is being attributed to the eye here because of how much it helps. Manakkudavar says such people are like the blind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:706":{"book":"kural","num":706,"t":["அடுத்தது காட்டும் பளிங்குபோல் நெஞ்சம்","கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்"],"p":"As the mirror reflects what is near so does the face show what is uppermost in the mind.","r":"As crystal shows what is near it, the face shows what is strongest in the heart.","n":"பளிங்கு is crystal, and it takes on a neighbouring colour. Pope and Kalaignar's 'mirror' changes the figure: a mirror shows what stands opposite, and crystal changes colour within itself. கடுத்தது is what is most intense, so the face shows peaks and not everything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:707":{"book":"kural","num":707,"t":["முகத்தின் முதுக்குறைந்தது உண்டோ உவப்பினும்","காயினும் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"Is there anything so full of knowledge as the face ? (No.) it precedes the mind, whether (the latter is) pleased or vexed.","r":"Is there anything as knowing as the face? Whether one is pleased or angry, it gets there first.","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a rejoinder to those who say a face, made of elements, can't know anything, and reads the verse's question as a mock refutation. The point isn't that it knows, but that it arrives before the words do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:708":{"book":"kural","num":708,"t":["முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும் அகம்நோக்கி","உற்ற துணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If the king gets those who by looking into his mind can understand (and remove) what has occurred (to him) it is enough that he stand looking at their face.","r":"If you have those who look within and understand what has happened, it's enough to stand and look them in the face.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It concerns a petitioner: he need only stand before those who will read his need and resolve it, since speaking shames both.","அவ்வெல்லையைக் கடந்து சொல்லுமாயின் இருவர்க்குஞ் சிறுமையாமாகலின்"],["manakkudavar","It's the reverse relation: the king, too, must read his ministers.","இஃது அமாத்தியர் குறிப்பை அரசனும் அறியவேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","It concerns the reader: if he has the skill, he only needs to stand in front of someone.",""]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:709":{"book":"kural","num":709,"t":["பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும் கண்ணின்","வகைமை உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If a king gets ministers who can read the movements of the eye, the eyes (of foreign kings) will (themselves) reveal (to him) their hatred or friendship.","r":"The eye itself will tell of enmity and friendship, if you have those who understand the eye's kinds.","n":"The eye is said to speak (உரைக்கும்), and speech itself isn't needed. Parimelazhagar applies this to foreign kings as read by ministers, so this is diplomatic intelligence at its most subtle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:710":{"book":"kural","num":710,"t":["நுண்ணியம் என்பார் அளக்குங்கோல் காணுங்கால்","கண்ணல்லது இல்லை பிற"],"p":"The measuring-rod of those (ministers) who say \"we are acute\" will on inquiry be found to be their (own) eyes and nothing else.","r":"When you look into it, the measuring rod of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye.","n":"என்பார் may carry a slight smile: 'those who say we are fine-minded'. Parimelazhagar explains why the eye: gesture, form, action and speech can all be hidden once one knows they're being read, but a gaze is mixed with the mind. He also records a variant reading, அலைக்குங்கோல்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:711":{"book":"kural","num":711,"t":["அவையறிநது ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the pure who know the arrangement of words speak with deliberation after ascertaining (the nature of) the court (then assembled).","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining the matter.","n":"Pope's 'pure in heart' gives the wrong impression. For Parimelazhagar, தூய்மை here is selection: discarding what doesn't suit and keeping what does. It's a purity of diction, not of character, though the verse doesn't rule out more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:712":{"book":"kural","num":712,"t":["இடைதெரிந்து நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","நடைதெரிந்த நன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the good who know the uses of words speak with a clear knowledge after ascertaining the time (suited to the court).","r":"Those with the goodness of knowing how words work should discern the opening, understand clearly, and then speak.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இடை is the moment: when the assembly is willing to hear.","செவ்வி; கேட்டற்கண் விருப்புடைமை."],["manakkudavar","இடை is the fault: think about what wrong a word could bring.","சொன்னால் அதற்கு வருங்குற்றத்தை ஆராய்ந்து"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:713":{"book":"kural","num":713,"t":["அவையறியார் சொல்லல்மேற் கொள்பவர் சொல்லின்","வகையறியார் வல்லதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the (nature of the) court are ignorant of the quality of words as well as devoid of the power (of learning).","r":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the assembly don't know the kinds of speech, and have no ability at all.","n":"A strong claim about what speech is. Parimelazhagar's reason is that kinds of speech follow from kinds of hearers' understanding, so if you don't know the hearer, you don't know the words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:714":{"book":"kural","num":714,"t":["ஒளியார்முன் ஒள்ளிய ராதல் வெளியார்முன்","வான்சுதை வண்ணம் கொளல்"],"p":"Ministers should be lights in the assembly of the enlightned, but assume the pure whiteness of mortar (ignorance) in that of fools.","r":"Before the bright be bright; before the empty, take on the colour of pure white plaster.","n":"The verse plays on வெளி, the blank and white. Parimelazhagar connects it to வெளிறு, wood without heart, and says one should appear even whiter than they are so they will respect you. It's unusually candid advice to feign emptiness; the commentators don't soften it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:715":{"book":"kural","num":715,"t":["நன்றென்ற வற்றுள்ளும் நன்றே முதுவருள்","முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு"],"p":"The modesty by which one does not rush forward and speak in (an assembly of) superiors is the best among all (one's) good qualities.","r":"The best of all things called good is the restraint of not speaking first among elders.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the exact limit: only speaking first is forbidden, so speaking with them or after them remains allowed. Manakkudavar extends முதுவர் beyond age to austerity, family and learning, and this matters because the verse gives only the one word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:716":{"book":"kural","num":716,"t":["ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந் தற்றே வியன்புலம்","ஏற்றுணர்வார் முன்னர் இழுக்கு"],"p":"(For a minister) to blunder in the presence of those who have acquired a vast store of learning and know (the value thereof) is like a good man stumbling (and falling away) from the path (of virtue).","r":"A slip before those who have taken in and understood wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is the path to liberation: it is like an ascetic falling away, with loss of fruit and scorn as well.","வீடு எய்தற்பொருட்டு நன்னெறிக்கண் நின்றானொருவன்"],["manakkudavar","It is simply someone who is on a path of life and loses his standing, and is scorned.","ஒரு நெறியின்கண் நின்றார் அந்நிலைமை குலைந்தாற்போல் இகழப்படும்"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:717":{"book":"kural","num":717,"t":["கற்றறிந்தார் கல்வி விளங்கும் கசடறச்","சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து"],"p":"The learning of those who have read and understood (much) will shine in the assembly of those who faultlessly examine (the nature of) words.","r":"The learning of those who have studied and understood shines where people can examine words faultlessly.","n":"Learning needs a qualified audience to appear at all. Manakkudavar puts this as a limit: only the learned can tell how good learning is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those with the skill to examine words are the assembly: speak in such a gathering.","வழுப்படாமல் சொற்களை ஆராய்தல் வல்லார் அவைக்கண் சொல்லின்"],["kalaignar","They are the speakers themselves: learning shows in those who choose flawless words.",""]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:718":{"book":"kural","num":718,"t":["உணர்வ துடையார்முன் சொல்லல் வளர்வதன்","பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Lecturing to those who have the ability to understand (for themselves) is like watering a bed of plants that are growing (of themselves).","r":"Speaking before those with understanding is like pouring water on a bed where the crop is already growing.","n":"The image could suggest redundancy, since the crop grows anyway. Parimelazhagar takes the opposite point: what would grow by itself grows all the more, and what grows is the speaker's own learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:719":{"book":"kural","num":719,"t":["புல்லவையுள் பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க நல்லவையுள்","நன்குசலச் சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who are able to speak good things impressively in an assembly of the good should not even forgetfully speak them in that of the low","r":"Those able to speak well and effectively in a good assembly should not, even by forgetting, speak in a mean one.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is double: the good assembly scorns you for not knowing your audience, and the mean one for not grasping the matter. பொச்சாந்தும், 'even by forgetting', treats it as a slip one must be on guard against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:720":{"book":"kural","num":720,"t":["அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்தற்றால் தங்கணத்தார்","அல்லார்முன் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To utter (a good word) in the assembly of those who are of inferior rank is like dropping nectar on the ground.","r":"Holding forth before those who aren't of one's own group is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard.","n":"The verse's division is தங்கணத்தார் அல்லார், those not of one's group, which is a matter of fit and not explicitly of worth. Pope's 'alien herd' and 'inferior rank' sharpen it. Parimelazhagar sees the waste as twofold: those who would know it as deathless food never get it, and the yard gains nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:721":{"book":"kural","num":721,"t":["வகையறிந்து வல்லவை வாய்சோரார் சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"The pure who know the classification of words having first ascertained the nature (of the court) will not (through fear) falter in their speech before the powerful body.","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words, having known the kinds, don't let their speech slip before the capable.","n":"The second line is identical to 711's. There the same people adjust to their hearers, and here they keep from faltering. Parimelazhagar brings in fear from the chapter's title; the verse only says வாய்சோரார்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","anjaamai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வகை is the kinds of assembly, and வல்லவை is a capable assembly. He records a reading as 'what they have mastered'.","கற்றுவல்ல அவை, அல்லா அவை என்னும் அவை வகையினை அறிந்து"],["manakkudavar","வகை is the kinds of harm that come from a slip.","தப்பினால் வருங் குற்றவகையை யறிந்து"]],"ch":"k:73"},"k:722":{"book":"kural","num":722,"t":["கற்றாருள் கற்றார் எனப்படுவர் கற்றார்முன்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who can agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned will be regarded as the most learned among the learned.","r":"Those who make what they've learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned.","n":"The verse is about reputation (எனப்படுவர்). Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that the world only knows the learned through such occasions, so public recognition depends on this one skill, not on how much is known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:723":{"book":"kural","num":723,"t":["பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர்","அவையகத்து அஞ்சா தவர்"],"p":"Many indeed may (fearlessly) die in the presence of (their) foes; (but) few are those who are fearless in the assembly (of the learned).","r":"Those who die amid enemies are easy to find; those who don't fear within an assembly are hard to find.","n":"பகையகத்து and அவையகத்து are parallel: the assembly is a battlefield with a higher casualty of nerve. This is one of the Kural's boldest reversals of martial values. Parimelazhagar reads எளியர் simply as 'many' and அரியர் as 'few', so the claim concerns numbers and isn't a ranking of worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","avai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:724":{"book":"kural","num":724,"t":["கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லித் தாம்கற்ற","மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"(Ministers) should agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned and acquire more (knowledge) from their superiors (in learning).","r":"Make what you have learned go home before the learned, and take the more from those who know more.","n":"Fearlessness has an epistemic pay-off. Parimelazhagar's reason: no one can learn everything, so speaking in a mixed assembly prompts others to speak. Manakkudavar goes so far as to define fearlessness of the assembly as this exchange.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:725":{"book":"kural","num":725,"t":["ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க அவையஞ்சா","மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"In order to reply fearlessly before a foreign court, (ministers) should learn logic according to the rules (of grammar).","r":"Learn by the proper path and with its measure, so as to give replies in the assembly without fear.","n":"Fear is treated as a gap in training, not of temperament. The verse's ஆற்றின் அளவு is brief; Parimelazhagar expands it into grammar followed by logic, listing Nyaya terms for disputation. Pope follows him. That learned apparatus isn't in the verse itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:726":{"book":"kural","num":726,"t":["வாளொடென் வன்கண்ணர் அல்லார்க்கு நூலொடென்","நுண்ணவை அஞ்சு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"What have they to do with a sword who are not valiant, or they with learning who are afraid of an intelligent assembly ?","r":"What is a sword to those who are not hard-eyed? What are treatises to those who fear a subtle assembly?","n":"வன்கண் is once again positive, as in 632 and 689. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that such people have no claim on the treatises, and this goes further than saying they fail to use them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:727":{"book":"kural","num":727,"t":["பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள் அவையகத்து","அஞ்சு மவன்கற்ற நூல்"],"p":"The learning of him who is diffident before an assembly is like the shining sword of an hermaphrodite in the presence of his foes.","r":"The treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி in the midst of enemies.","n":"The image relies on a period contempt, and Manakkudavar again drops the word, saying only 'one who fears'. Parimelazhagar works the comparison out carefully: field and sword are both good, so the failure lies wholly in the holder, as with the assembly and the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:728":{"book":"kural","num":728,"t":["பல்லவை கற்றும் பயமிலரே நல்லவையுள்","நன்கு செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who cannot agreeably speak good things before a good assembly are indeed unprofitable persons inspite of all their various acquirements.","r":"Though they learn many things, they are of no use if they can't speak well and tellingly in a good assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads பயமிலர் as of no use to the world, and records another view, that they have no fruit from their learning. Who is deprived is therefore not settled, the world or the scholar. The verse's own 'good assembly' is the same condition as 719.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:729":{"book":"kural","num":729,"t":["கல்லா தவரின் கடையென்ப கற்றறிந்தும்","நல்லா ரவையஞ்சு வார்"],"p":"They who, though they have learned and understood, are yet afraid of the assembly of the good, are said to be inferior (even) to the illiterate.","r":"They say that those who have studied and understood, and yet fear an assembly of the good, are lower than the unlearned.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', reports this as public opinion. Parimelazhagar makes it pointed: such people benefit neither themselves nor others and have obtained only the pain of study.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:730":{"book":"kural","num":730,"t":["உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர் களன்அஞ்சிக்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who through fear of the assembly are unable to set forth their learning in an interesting manner, though alive, are yet like the dead.","r":"Those who, fearing the arena, can't make what they've learned go home are like the absent, even though they exist.","n":"The chapter ends with a social death: to Parimelazhagar they are like the dead since the world doesn't count them. களன் can be a battlefield as well, so the word may close the chapter on the same comparison as 723, though Parimelazhagar glosses it as those seated there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:731":{"book":"kural","num":731,"t":["தள்ளா விளையுளும் தக்காரும் தாழ்விலாச்","செல்வரும் சேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that in which (those who carry on) a complete cultivation, virtuous persons, and merchants with inexhaustible wealth, dwell together.","r":"A land is where unfailing yield, worthy people and wealthy people whose wealth does not sink all come together.","n":"The definition opens with who and what gathers in a place, not with territory or borders. A land here is a convergence.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விளையுள் stands for the cultivators, தக்கார் for renunciants and brahmins, and செல்வர் for merchants, so the verse lists three kinds of people.","'விளையுள்' என்பது உழவர்மேல் நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","விளையுள் is land itself, fields that do not fail even when rain does.","தள்ளா விளையுள் - மழையில்லாத காலத்தினும் சாவிபோகாத நிலம்."],["kalaignar","The wealthy who count are those who do not spend their wealth on evil.",""]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:732":{"book":"kural","num":732,"t":["பெரும்பொருளால் பெட்டக்க தாகி அருங்கேட்டால்","ஆற்ற விளைவது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is desire for its immense wealth, and which grows greatly in prosperity, being free from destructive causes.","r":"A land is desirable for its great wealth and yields abundantly because ruin rarely touches it.","n":"The verse names no king. Parimelazhagar lists six calamities and credits their absence to the king's virtue and his might, which is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:733":{"book":"kural","num":733,"t":["பொறையொருங்கு மேல்வருங்கால் தாங்கி இறைவற்கு","இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which can bear any burden that may be pressed on it (from adjoining kingdoms) and (yet) pay the full tribute to its sovereign.","r":"A land bears burdens that fall on it all at once and still gives its ruler his full due.","n":"நேர்வது suggests consent as well as payment. Parimelazhagar's gloss உடம்பட்டு makes the land a willing payer, not a squeezed one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The burdens are refugees and herds driven from other lands by war, misrule or famine, whom this land absorbs.","பாரங்கள் - மக்கள் தொகுதியும் ஆன் எருமை முதலிய விலங்குத் தொகுதியும்."],["manakkudavar","The burdens are the land's own, the extra exactions laid on subjects beyond the fixed tax.","குடிமையாவது கடமையொழிய வருவது."]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:734":{"book":"kural","num":734,"t":["உறுபசியும் ஓவாப் பிணியும் செறுபகையும்","சேரா தியல்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which continues to be free from excessive starvation, irremediable epidemics, and destructive foes.","r":"A land is one that goes on without severe hunger, unceasing disease or destructive enemies coming to it.","n":"சேரா answers சேர்வது in 731. The chapter defines a land by what gathers in it and then by what does not. Each ill also carries an intensifier, so the verse excludes the crushing forms and not all hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:735":{"book":"kural","num":735,"t":["பல்குழுவும் பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகையும் வேந்தலைக்கும்","கொல்குறும்பும் இல்லது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is without various (irregular) associations, destructive internal enemies, and murderous savages who (sometimes) harass the sovereign.","r":"A land is free of many factions, of ruinous enemies within, and of murderous marauders who harass the king.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the factions are united by caste or by god, a striking gloss on பல்குழு, and his 'enemies within' include robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. Chapter 89 uses உட்பகை much more intimately.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:736":{"book":"kural","num":736,"t":["கேடறியாக் கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா","நாடென்ப நாட்டின் தலை"],"p":"The learned say that the best kingdom is that which knows no evil (from its foes), and, if injured (at all), suffers no diminution in its fruitfulness.","r":"The foremost of lands is one that does not know ruin, and whose richness does not fall even if ruin comes.","n":"The verse does not rest on safety alone. It adds a second test for after the first has failed, so the highest praise goes to resilience. The commentators disagree about the ruin: Parimelazhagar means enemies, Manakkudavar pests and storms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:737":{"book":"kural","num":737,"t":["இருபுனலும் வாய்ந்த மலையும் வருபுனலும்","வல்லரணும் நாட்டிற்கு உறுப்பு"],"p":"The constituents of a kingdom are the two waters (from above and below), well situated hills and an undestructible fort.","r":"The limbs of a land are the two waters, a well-placed hill, the water that comes from it, and a strong fort.","n":"Three of the four limbs involve water or its source, and the fort comes last. Parimelazhagar's hill stores rain and releases it in summer, a piece of water reasoning.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two waters are ground water and surface water, and வருபுனல் is what flows down from the hill.","'கீழ் நீர், மேல் நீர்' எனப்பட்ட தன்கண் நீரும்"],["kalaignar","The two waters are river and sea, and வருபுனல் is rain.",""]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:738":{"book":"kural","num":738,"t":["பிணியின்மை செல்வம் விளைவின்பம் ஏமம்","அணியென்ப நாட்டிவ் வைந்து"],"p":"Freedom from epidemics, wealth, produce, happiness and protection (to subjects); these five, the learned, say, are the ornaments of a kingdom.","r":"Freedom from disease, wealth, yield, happiness and protection: these five, they say, are a land's ornaments.","n":"Health stands ahead of wealth. Calling these ornaments rather than foundations is odd, and Parimelazhagar's explanation is that such beauty makes people of other lands desire it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:739":{"book":"kural","num":739,"t":["நாடென்ப நாடா வளத்தன நாடல்ல","நாட வளந்தரு நாடு"],"p":"The learned say that those are kingdom whose wealth is not laboured for, and those not, whose wealth is only obtained through labour.","r":"They call those lands whose riches need no seeking; lands that give riches only when sought are no lands.","n":"The verse puns on நாடு as land and as seek. The verdict is on the land, not on the person who toils, but it still sits awkwardly with a text that honours labour. Parimelazhagar treats seeking as hardship, and the verse does not say no work at all is done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:740":{"book":"kural","num":740,"t":["ஆங்கமை வெய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","வேந்தமை வில்லாத நாடு"],"p":"Although in possession of all the above mentioned excellences, these are indeed of no use to a country, in the absence of harmony between the sovereign and the sujects.","r":"Even with all these attained, a land is without use if it has no accord with its king.","n":"After nine verses of conditions, the tenth makes them insufficient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேந்து அமைவு is mutual: the people's love for the king and his grace toward them.","குடிகள் அவன்மாட்டு அன்புடையராதலும், அவன்தான் இவர்மாட்டு அருளுடையனாதலும் அடங்கின."],["manakkudavar","The requirement falls on the king, who must himself be of good character.","இது நாட்டுக்கு அரசனும் பண்புடையனாகல் வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The point is the absence of good government.",""]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:741":{"book":"kural","num":741,"t":["ஆற்று பவர்க்கும் அரண்பொருள் அஞ்சித்தற்","போற்று பவர்க்கும் பொருள்"],"p":"A fort is an object of importance to those who march (against their foes) as well as to those who through fear (of pursuers) would seek it for shelter.","r":"A fort matters to those strong enough to march out, and it matters as much to those who, afraid, look to their own safety.","n":"The verse sets no shame on அஞ்சி. Fear is a normal condition of statecraft here. Parimelazhagar names the strong first because even they perish without a fort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:742":{"book":"kural","num":742,"t":["மணிநீரும் மண்ணும் மலையும் அணிநிழற்","காடும் உடைய தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has everlasting water, plains, mountains and cool shady forests.","r":"A fort has jewel-clear water, open ground, a hill, and a forest of dense shade.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fort is ringed by all four defences, natural and built, and the ground is a bare waste left so enemies find no foothold.","நீரரண், நிலவரண், மலையரண், காட்டரண் என இயற்கையும் செயற்கையுமாய இந்நான்கு அரணும் சூழப்படுவது அரண் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","Any one of these is enough, whether water, bare land, hill or forest.","மலையாயினும், அழகிய நிழலினையுடைய காடாயினும் உடையது அரணாம்"],["kalaignar","The water is a deep, wide moat.",""]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:743":{"book":"kural","num":743,"t":["உயர்வகலம் திண்மை அருமைஇந் நான்கின்","அமைவரண் என்றுரைக்கும் நூல்"],"p":"The learned say that a fortress is an enclosure having these four (qualities) viz., height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility.","r":"The treatises say a fort is a wall with height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility in full.","n":"The verse appeals openly to a technical literature (நூல்), which Valluvar is summarizing. Parimelazhagar fills in அருமை with a long catalogue of war machines from the Silappatikaram, which the verse doesn't list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:744":{"book":"kural","num":744,"t":["சிறுகாப்பிற் பேரிடத்த தாகி உறுபகை","ஊக்கம் அழிப்ப தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has an extensive space within, but only small places to be guarded, and such as can destroy the courage of besieging foes.","r":"A fort has little to guard and much room within, and it breaks the spirit of the enemy who comes against it.","n":"The final test is psychological. For Parimelazhagar the enemy who comes meaning to take it at once loses heart merely on seeing it, so a good fort wins some of its battle before it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:745":{"book":"kural","num":745,"t":["கொளற்கரிதாய்க் கொண்டகூழ்த் தாகி அகத்தார்","நிலைக்கெளிதாம் நீரது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured, which abounds in suitable provisions, and affords a position of easy defence to its inmates.","r":"A fort is hard to seize, holds stored food, and is easy for those inside to hold their ground in.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நீரது means of such a nature: it is built so the defenders' position is easy, with their missiles reaching out and the enemy's not reaching in.","அகத்தாரது போர்நிலைக்கு எளிதாய நீர்மையையுடையதே அரணாவது."],["manakkudavar","நீரது means having water: there is water easy for those inside and scarce for those outside.","அகத்துறைவார்க்கு நிற்றற்கு எளிதாகும் நீரை யுடைத்தாயிருப்பது அரணாவது"]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:746":{"book":"kural","num":746,"t":["எல்லாப் பொருளும் உடைத்தாய் இடத்துதவும்","நல்லாள் உடையது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has all (needful) things, and excellent heroes that can help it against destruction (by foes).","r":"A fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place.","n":"நல்லாள் is not merely a strong man. Parimelazhagar reads the 'good' as love of the king, honour, valour and vigilance, so the defenders' character is already part of the fort's definition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:747":{"book":"kural","num":747,"t":["முற்றியும் முற்றா தெறிந்தும் அறைப்படுத்தும்","பற்றற் கரியது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured by blockading, assaulting, or undermining it.","r":"A fort is hard to take by encircling it, by storming it without encircling, or by working on those inside.","n":"Pope's 'mine beneath the ground' turns அறைப்படுத்தல் into tunnelling, while Parimelazhagar makes it subversion, turning trusted insiders so they open the gates. On his reading the third danger is treachery. He says stores answer the first threat and good men the other two, so walls answer none of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:748":{"book":"kural","num":748,"t":["முற்றாற்றி முற்றி யவரையும் பற்றாற்றிப்","பற்றியார் வெல்வது அரண்"],"p":"That is a fort whose inmates are able to overcome without losing their ground, even abler men who have besieged it.","r":"A fort lets those who hold it stand fast and defeat even those strong enough to besiege it.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that a small force does not merely endure a large one but wins, which raises the claim from survival to victory. He also says others explain this verse differently, and his text doesn't give their view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:749":{"book":"kural","num":749,"t":["முனைமுகத்து மாற்றலர் சாய வினைமுகத்து","வீறெய்தி மாண்ட தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which derives excellence from the stratagems made (by its inmates) to defeat their enemies in the battlefield.","r":"A fort gains its distinction from the actions by which its people bring down enemies as soon as battle begins.","n":"The chapter's language shifts here from what the fort has to what is done in it, preparing for 750.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:750":{"book":"kural","num":750,"t":["எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும் வினைமாட்சி","இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்"],"p":"Although a fort may possess all (the above-said) excellence, it is, as it were without these, if its inmates possess not the excellence of action.","r":"Whatever excellences it has, a fort is nothing in the hands of those without excellence in action.","n":"Pope's 'craven souls' narrows this to cowardice. வினைமாட்சி is broader, and Parimelazhagar includes sitting idle, acting without measure and doing what is unsuitable. A brave incompetent would fail this verse as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:751":{"book":"kural","num":751,"t":["பொருளல் லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்","பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்"],"p":"Besides wealth there is nothing that can change people of no importance into those of (some) importance.","r":"Apart from wealth, nothing makes persons of no account into persons of account.","n":"பொருள் appears four times, playing on 'wealth' and 'something that counts'. Whether this is approval or a dry observation is left open. Parimelazhagar names the unlearned and low-born and concludes that wealth alone should be amassed, while Kalaignar calls those raised unworthy of regard, which has an edge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:752":{"book":"kural","num":752,"t":["இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர் செல்வரை","எல்லாரும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"All despise the poor; (but) all praise the rich.","r":"Everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich.","n":"The verse states a social fact without saying it is just. Parimelazhagar sharpens it, adding that the poor are scorned though they have every good and the rich honoured though they have every evil, words which aren't in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:753":{"book":"kural","num":753,"t":["பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம் இருளறுக்கும்","எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று"],"p":"The imperishable light of wealth goes into regions desired (by its owner) and destroys the darkness (of enmity therein).","r":"The unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind and cuts away the darkness.","n":"Unlike a lamp, this one travels to where it is intended.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The darkness is enmity, and the lamp reaches enemy lands on its owner's behalf.","தன்னைச் செய்தவர்க்கு அவர் நினைத்த தேயத்துச் சென்று பகை என்னும் இருளைக் கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The darkness is enmity, and a wealthy king's command runs wherever he intends.","அரசன் பொரு ளுடையனானால், தான் கருதிய தேசமெல்லாம் தன்னாணை நடத்துவானென்றது."],["kalaignar","The darkness is ordinary suffering, which money in hand drives away anywhere one goes.",""]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:754":{"book":"kural","num":754,"t":["அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும் திறனறிந்து","தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்"],"p":"The wealth acquired with a knowledge of the proper means and without foul practices will yield virtue and happiness.","r":"Wealth that comes with knowledge of the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure.","n":"The commentators differ on the audience. Parimelazhagar makes 'without wrong' a matter of a king not being tyrannical, while Manakkudavar means no harm to anyone else, and the verse itself names no king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:755":{"book":"kural","num":755,"t":["அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்","புல்லார் புரள விடல்"],"p":"(Kings) should rather avoid than seek the accumulation of wealth which does not flow in with mercy and love.","r":"Accumulation of wealth that does not come with grace and love should not be embraced, but let go.","n":"புல்லார் புரள விடல் is more than refusing. It is the gesture of letting something roll away. Coming right after 754 and before 756, it is the chapter's clearest limit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arul","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Coming with grace and love means, for a king, taxing no more than the rightful one-sixth.","அவற்றொடு கூடி வருதலாவது, ஆறிலொன்றாய் வருதல்."],["manakkudavar","It applies to any earner: not profiting from others' distress and not losing affection for one's intimates while earning.","பொருள் தேடுங்கால் பிறர் வருத்தத்திற்கு உபகரியாதும், பயின்றார் மாட்டுக் காதலில்லாமலும்"]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:756":{"book":"kural","num":756,"t":["உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும்தன் ஒன்னார்த்","தெறுபொருளும் வேந்தன் பொருள்"],"p":"Unclaimed wealth, wealth acquired by taxes, and wealth (got) by conquest of foes are (all) the wealth of the king.","r":"Wealth that falls in on its own, wealth from customs, and wealth from crushing his enemies are the king's wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes this as the income beyond the one-sixth of 755. Without that bridge, an income by force would sit oddly next to wealth that comes with grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உறுபொருள் is ownerless wealth: long-buried treasure and estates without heirs.","வைத்தார் இறந்துபோக நெடுங்காலம் நிலத்தின்கண் கிடந்து பின் கண்டெடுத்தூஉம், தாயத்தார் பெறாததூஉமாம்."],["manakkudavar","உறுபொருள் is revenue connected with protection.","உறு பொருள் - காவற் பொருள்."]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:757":{"book":"kural","num":757,"t":["அருளென்னும் அன்பீன் குழவி பொருளென்னும்","செல்வச் செவிலியால் உண்டு"],"p":"The child mercy which is borne by love grows under the care of the rich nurse of wealth.","r":"The child called grace, born of love, is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called wealth.","n":"This gives a genealogy of compassion. Parimelazhagar distinguishes love, which runs along ties, from grace, which goes to any sufferer. The mother gives birth but cannot rear alone, so wealth is necessary without being the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:758":{"book":"kural","num":758,"t":["குன்றேறி யானைப் போர் கண்டற்றால் தன்கைத்தொன்று","உண்டாகச் செய்வான் வினை"],"p":"An undertaking of one who has wealth in one's hands is like viewing an elephant-fight from a hill-top.","r":"Undertaking a task with money in hand is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop.","n":"The picture's point is the safety of distance. Parimelazhagar says the funded person commissions the able and finishes the work without fear or strain, which is a view of capital as insulation from risk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:759":{"book":"kural","num":759,"t":["செய்க பொருளைச் செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்","எஃகதனிற் கூரிய தில்"],"p":"Accumulate wealth; it will destroy the arrogance of (your) foes; there is no weapon sharper than it.","r":"Make wealth; there is no steel sharper than it for cutting down the pride of enemies.","n":"The target is செருக்கு and not the enemy's body. Parimelazhagar seizes on this: other blades can't cut an intangible. The verse is one of the chapter's few bald imperatives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:760":{"book":"kural","num":760,"t":["ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு எண்பொருள்","ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"To those who have honestly acquired an abundance of riches, the other two, (virtue and pleasure) are things easy (of acquisition).","r":"For those who have made bright wealth in full measure, the other two are easily had, together.","n":"ஒண் does a lot of work: the claim is for bright wealth and not for any wealth. Parimelazhagar reads ஒண் as wealth gained by the right path, while Manakkudavar simply glosses it as bright. Everything else in the claim hinges on that adjective.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:761":{"book":"kural","num":761,"t":["உறுப்பமைந்து ஊறஞ்சா வெல்படை வேந்தன்","வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"The army which is complete in (its) parts and conquers without fear of wounds is the chief wealth of the king.","r":"A conquering army complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds is the chief of all a king's wealth.","n":"வெறுக்கை calls the army wealth, directly after the chapter on wealth. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it guards the other limbs of the state and the king himself. The commentators count the parts differently: he has four, Manakkudavar five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:762":{"book":"kural","num":762,"t":["உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண் தொலைவிடத்துத்","தொல்படைக் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Ancient army can alone have the valour which makes it stand by its king at the time of defeat, fearless of wounds and unmindful of its reduced strength.","r":"The hardness that fears no wound when the king is faltering and the force itself is reduced is rare except in an ancient army.","n":"The verse locates courage in continuity. Only தொல் is the verse's own; Parimelazhagar makes it a technical term, ranking the ancestral force first among six kinds, above hired, allied and enemy-derived troops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:763":{"book":"kural","num":763,"t":["ஒலித்தக்கால் என்னாம் உவரி எலிப்பகை","நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்"],"p":"What if (a host of) hostile rats roar like the sea ? They will perish at the mere breath of the cobra.","r":"What if a host of hostile rats roar like the sea? They perish when the cobra merely breathes.","n":"The verse doesn't mention armies at all. Parimelazhagar reads it as an allegory recommending one hero over many who are not. The cobra doesn't even strike: its breath is enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:764":{"book":"kural","num":764,"t":["அழிவின்றி அறைபோகா தாகி வழிவந்த","வன்க ணதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which has stood firm of old without suffering destruction or deserting (to the enemy).","r":"An army is one that does not collapse, cannot be turned by subversion, and has courage that has come down to it.","n":"அறைபோகாது repeats the danger of 747: the same threat of being turned from inside. Parimelazhagar's supporting verse is a woman's boast of the men of her house who fell in battle, so the inheritance includes grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:765":{"book":"kural","num":765,"t":["கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும் கூடி எதிர்நிற்கும்","ஆற்ற லதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which is capable of offering a united resistance, even if Yama advances against it with fury.","r":"An army is one that, even when Death comes raging at it, has the power to stand together and face it.","n":"The operative word is கூடி. The courage asked for is collective. Parimelazhagar says the many are of one heart because they love the king, and that ஆற்றல் here is strength of mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:766":{"book":"kural","num":766,"t":["மறமானம் மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு தேற்றம்","எனநான்கே ஏமம் படைக்கு"],"p":"Valour, honour, following in the excellent-footsteps (of its predecessors) and trust-worthiness; these four alone constitute the safeguard of an army.","r":"Valour, honour, walking the proven path and தேற்றம் are the four things that protect an army.","n":"Parimelazhagar's 'path' includes not striking those who have turned their backs, so honour limits valour. He concludes that such men need no other fort.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தேற்றம் is being trusted by the king.","அரசனால் தேறப்படுதலும்"],["manakkudavar","தேற்றம் is the army's own clarity, and the good path is steadiness in fighting.","நல்லவழிச்சேறலாவது மறஞ் செய்யுங் காலத்துக் கலக்கமின்மை."],["kalaignar","It is winning the trust of the leader, close to Parimelazhagar but placed on the leader.",""]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:767":{"book":"kural","num":767,"t":["தார்தாங்கிச் செல்வது தானை தலைவந்த","போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"That is an army which knowing the art of warding off an impending struggle, can bear against the dust-van (of a hostile force).","r":"An army is one that knows how to meet the battle coming at it and advances bearing the தார்.","n":"This is the chapter's one verse about skill. Parimelazhagar sends readers to Sanskrit treatises on battle formation and declines to describe them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தார் is the enemy's vanguard, held off before pressing forward.","அவர் தூசியைத் தன்மேல் வாராமல் தடுத்துத்தான் அதன்மேற்செல்வதே படையாவது."],["kalaignar","தார் is the garland of victory that the capable army carries.",""]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:768":{"book":"kural","num":768,"t":["அடல்தகையும் ஆற்றலும் இல்லெனினும் தானை","படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"Though destitute of courage to fight and strength (to endure), an army may yet gain renown by the splendour of its appearance.","r":"Even without striking power or endurance, an army gains stature through its martial bearing.","n":"A surprising concession that appearance has real force. Parimelazhagar's பாடு is the fear an army causes at first sight, so display works on the enemy's mind as 744's fort does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Display gives some standing, though the wording itself shows striking power and endurance remain indispensable.","'இல்லெனினும்' எனவே, அவற்றது இன்றியமையாமை பெறப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The army's parade appearance should be more distinguished than either valour or endurance.",""]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:769":{"book":"kural","num":769,"t":["சிறுமையும் செல்லாத் துனியும் வறுமையும்","இல்லாயின் வெல்லும் படை"],"p":"An army can triumph (over its foes) if it is free from diminution; irremediable aversion and poverty.","r":"An army wins if it is free of shrinking, of a resentment that will not leave, and of poverty.","n":"The verse states an army's conditions, but Parimelazhagar assigns the blame upward. Desertion and poverty come from a king who doesn't pay, and resentment from outrages such as seizing their women. A verse on the army becomes a verse on the king's conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:770":{"book":"kural","num":770,"t":["நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும் தானை","தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்"],"p":"Though an army may contain a large number of permanent soldiers, it cannot last if it has no generals.","r":"Even with plenty of steadfast men, there is no army where there are no leaders.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is striking. If leaders go, the men say there is no one to witness and leave too. On this view courage needs an audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:771":{"book":"kural","num":771,"t":["என்னைமுன் நில்லன்மின் தெவ்விர் பலரென்னை","முன்நின்று கல்நின் றவர்"],"p":"O my foes, stand not before my leader; (for) many are those who did so but afterwards stood (in the shape of) statues.","r":"Enemies, do not stand before my lord; many who stood before him now stand in stone.","n":"The verse puns on நில்: to stand in battle and to stand as a hero-stone. The stone is both a threat and an honour, a fact that makes the boast double-edged. It is a voice in the Kural that isn't Valluvar's own, and Parimelazhagar notes the warrior places his valour on his king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:772":{"book":"kural","num":772,"t":["கான முயலெய்த அம்பினில் யானை","பிழைத்தவேல் ஏந்தல் இனிது"],"p":"It is more pleasant to hold the dart that has missed an elephant than that which has hit hare in the forest.","r":"Carrying a spear that missed an elephant is sweeter than carrying an arrow that hit a forest hare.","n":"The verse measures an act by its target, not its result. Parimelazhagar supplies a dramatic setting, a warrior ashamed that the opposing force fled, but the verse itself gives only the comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:773":{"book":"kural","num":773,"t":["பேராண்மை என்ப தறுகண்ஒன் றுற்றக்கால்","ஊராண்மை மற்றதன் எஃகு"],"p":"The learned say that fierceness (incontest with a foe) is indeed great valour; but to become a benefactor in case of accident (to a foe) is the extreme (limit) of that valour.","r":"They call fierceness great manhood; but when something befalls the foe, generous help is its cutting edge.","n":"The image is exact: the kindness isn't an exception to valour but its sharpest point. Parimelazhagar's example is Rama telling the defeated Ravana to go and come back tomorrow, an epic illustration that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","arul","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:774":{"book":"kural","num":774,"t":["கைவேல் களிற்றொடு போக்கி வருபவன்","மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்"],"p":"The hero who after casting the lance in his hand on an elephant, comes (in search of another) will pluck the one (that sticks) in his body and laugh (exultingly).","r":"Having sent his spear away with an elephant, he comes on seeking another, and laughs as he pulls one from his own body.","n":"The laugh is the verse's whole shock. For Parimelazhagar he is happy because he has found what he was seeking, and rage had kept him from noticing the wound. A wound registers as supply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:775":{"book":"kural","num":775,"t":["விழித்தகண் வேல்கொண டெறிய அழித்திமைப்பின்","ஒட்டன்றோ வன்க ணவர்க்கு"],"p":"Is it not a defeat to the valiant to wink and destroy their ferocious look when a lance in cast at them (by their foe) ?","r":"When the eye that glares at the enemy gives way and blinks at a flung spear, is that not flight, to the fierce?","n":"Valour is carried to the reflex. Parimelazhagar's line is that even withdrawing the angry gaze counts as retreat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The spear is thrown at the man, and giving way means ending the glare by blinking.","அவர் வேலைக்கொண்டு எறிய அஃது ஆற்றாது அந்நோக்கை அழித்து இமைக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The spear is thrust into the eye itself, and blinking again at that is equal to defeat.","மாற்றாரோடு எதிர்த்துச் சிவந்து நோக்கின கண்ணிலே ஒரு வேலினாலே எறிய"]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:776":{"book":"kural","num":776,"t":["விழுப்புண் படாதநாள் எல்லாம் வழுக்கினுள்","வைக்கும்தன் நாளை எடுத்து"],"p":"The hero will reckon among wasted days all those on which he had not received severe wounds.","r":"Going over his days, he sets among the wasted all those on which he took no noble wound.","n":"விழுப்புண் is a wound of a certain kind. Parimelazhagar restricts it to face and chest, so that it marks one who did not turn. A ledger of days is a sober image for a very unsober ethic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:777":{"book":"kural","num":777,"t":["சுழலும் இசைவேண்டி வேண்டா உயிரார்","கழல்யாப்புக் காரிகை நீர்த்து"],"p":"The fastening of ankle-ring by those who disire a world-wide renown and not (the safety of) their lives is like adorning (themselves).","r":"For those who want fame that circles the world and do not want life, the tying on of the war-anklet is an adornment.","n":"The verse names only fame. Parimelazhagar adds heaven (துறக்கம்), saying fame remains encircling the earth while the hero goes on, so the afterlife is his reading. காரிகை turns a mark of danger into jewellery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:778":{"book":"kural","num":778,"t":["உறின்உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர் இறைவன்","செறினும் சீர்குன்றல் இலர்"],"p":"The heroes who are not afraid of losing their life in a contest will not cool their ardour, even if the king prohibits (their fighting).","r":"Warriors who do not fear for their lives when battle comes will not lessen in their distinction even if their lord is angry.","n":"This verse makes a real space between warrior and king, which the army chapter's language of loyalty doesn't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They press on to battle even if the king angrily forbids it, because their ardour doesn't diminish.","தம் இறைவன் அது வேண்டா என்று முனியினும் அவ் வீரமிகுதி குன்றார்."],["kalaignar","The true warriors are those who go on doing their duty though their leader is angry with them.",""]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:779":{"book":"kural","num":779,"t":["இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவாரை யாரே","பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Who would reproach with failure those who seal their oath with their death ?","r":"Who could reproach those who die so that what they vowed is not broken, for having failed?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold move: dying in the attempt counts as fulfilling the vow. The verse's rhetorical question closes off blame for one who failed, without calling the failure a success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:780":{"book":"kural","num":780,"t":["புரந்தார்கண் நீர்மல்கச் சாகிற்பின் சாக்காடு","இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"If (heroes) can so die as to fill with tears the eyes of their rulers, such a death deserves to be obtained even by begging.","r":"If one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death is worth begging for.","n":"The measure of the death is another's tears, and the one who weeps is the patron. Parimelazhagar sets it against dying sick at home among weeping kin, which only spends old deeds, whereas this earns heaven. The verse itself mentions neither of those.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:781":{"book":"kural","num":781,"t":["செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின் அதுபோல்","வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு"],"p":"What things are there so difficult to acquire as friendship ? What guards are there so difficult to break through by the efforts (of one's foes) ?","r":"What is as hard to make as friendship, and what guard is as hard for hostile deeds to get past?","n":"The two questions are linked: the difficulty of getting it is the reason for its value as protection. Parimelazhagar opens by classifying friendship as natural or made and says this chapter treats the made kind, kin being treated elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:782":{"book":"kural","num":782,"t":["நிறைநீர நீரவர் கேண்மை பிறைமதிப்","பின்னீர பேதையார் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of the wise waxes like the new moon; (but) that of fools wanes like the full moon.","r":"Friendship with the good grows like the crescent moon filling, and that of fools shrinks like the moon after it is full.","n":"The moon's phases make growth depend on the starting point: the good begin small and fools begin large. Parimelazhagar's reason is knowledge. Each pair at first doesn't know the other and later does, and it is that knowing which makes one friendship grow and the other shrink.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:783":{"book":"kural","num":783,"t":["நவில்தொறும் நூல்நயம் போலும் பயில்தொறும்","பண்புடை யாளர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Like learning, the friendship of the noble, the more it is cultivated, the more delightful does it become.","r":"The more it is practised, the bond with people of character is like the more a book is studied, its nayam.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Nayam is delight: both friendship and study give increasing pleasure.","நூற் பொருள் கற்குந்தோறும் கற்றார்க்கு இன்பஞ் செய்தலை ஒக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The gain is understanding: a friend of good character teaches as a book does.","இது குணவானோடு நட்புக் கொள்ளின், அறிவுண்டா மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:79"},"k:784":{"book":"kural","num":784,"t":["நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல் மிகுதிக்கண்","மேற்செனறு இடித்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"Friendship is to be practised not for the purpose of laughing but for that of being beforehand in giving one another sharp rebukes in case of transgression.","r":"Friendship is not for laughing together, but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps.","n":"மேற்சென்று has timing in it. The rebuke goes out before the wrong is done, says Parimelazhagar, and it is harsh because sweet words will not turn the person back. This is not a claim that friends should never laugh, only that laughter isn't the purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:785":{"book":"kural","num":785,"t":["புணர்ச்சி பழகுதல் வேண்டா உணர்ச்சிதான்","நட்பாங் கிழமை தரும்"],"p":"Living together and holding frequent intercourse are not necessary (for friendship); (mutual) understanding can alone create a claim for it.","r":"Neither meeting nor long acquaintance is needed; a shared feeling alone gives the claim of friendship.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is Kopperuncholan and Pisirandaiyar, who never met and whose bond was so close that one died with the other. The verse itself offers no example and says nothing of dying together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:786":{"book":"kural","num":786,"t":["முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று நெஞ்சத்து","அகநக நட்பது நட்பு"],"p":"The love that dwells (merely in the smiles of the face is not friendship; (but) that which dwells deep in the smiles of the heart is true friendship.","r":"Friendship that makes the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is friendship.","n":"The verse sounds as if the face doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar says that an implied 'also' is present, so both should bloom together. That softens a sharper opposition in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:787":{"book":"kural","num":787,"t":["அழிவி னவைநீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து அழிவின்கண்","அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship turns aside from evil (ways) makes (him) walk in the (good) way, and, in case of loss if shares his sorrow (with him).","r":"Friendship turns one away from what destroys, sets one on the path, and in ruin shares the suffering.","n":"The order is a plan of action: prevent, then guide, then, if all that fails, suffer alongside. Parimelazhagar adds that ruin in the third stage comes from a divine cause and so can't be averted. The verse names no such cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:788":{"book":"kural","num":788,"t":["உடுக்கை இழந்தவன் கைபோல ஆங்கே","இடுக்கண் களைவதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship hastens to the rescue of the afflicted (as readily) as the hand of one whose garment is loosened (before an assembly).","r":"Like the hand of a man whose garment slips, friendship clears away distress there and then.","n":"The simile is about speed and also about no deliberation. Parimelazhagar notes that the hand gets there before the mind does. The garment also implies that the friend's trouble is a shame to be covered, and that the friend is as close as one's own limb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:789":{"book":"kural","num":789,"t":["நட்பிற்கு வீற்றிருக்கை யாதெனின் கொட்பின்றி","ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை"],"p":"Friendship may be said to be on its throne when it possesses the power of supporting one at all times and under all circumstances, (in the practice or virtue and wealth).","r":"Where does friendship sit enthroned? In standing firm without wavering, supporting however one can.","n":"ஒல்லும்வாய் sets a realistic limit: as far as one is able. The throne is unwavering support, not unlimited power. Parimelazhagar specifies support in virtue and wealth, which Pope follows in parentheses, while the verse itself gives no object.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:790":{"book":"kural","num":790,"t":["இனையர் இவரெமக்கு இன்னம்யாம் என்று","புனையினும் புல்லென்னும் நட்பு"],"p":"Though friends may praise one another saying, \"He is so intimate with us, and we so much (with him)\"; (still) such friendship will appear mean.","r":"'They are this to us and we are that to them': even when so adorned, friendship looks shabby.","n":"A subtle point. For Parimelazhagar friendship has no 'I' and 'they', so that listing what each is to the other brings in a difference that did not exist. The praise itself is what damages it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:791":{"book":"kural","num":791,"t":["நாடாது நட் டலிற் கேடில்லை நட்டபின்","வீடில்லை நட்பாள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those who are of a friendly nature will not forsake (a friend) after once loving (him), there is no evil so great as contracting a friendship without due inquiry.","r":"Nothing brings ruin like befriending without inquiry, since for those devoted to friendship there is no leaving once it is formed.","n":"The logic turns on faithfulness: the reason for caution is precisely the commitment to stay. Parimelazhagar adds that with no distinction between friends, the friend's sins and blame become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:792":{"book":"kural","num":792,"t":["ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை கடைமுறை","தான்சாம் துயரம் தரும்"],"p":"The friendship contracted by him who has not made repeated inquiry will in the end grieve (him) to death.","r":"The friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends by bringing grief that kills him.","n":"The doubled ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து asks for repeated testing and not a single check. For Parimelazhagar no enemy is needed, for one brings it on oneself, and the bad friend's enemies become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:793":{"book":"kural","num":793,"t":["குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் குன்றா","இனனும் அறிந்தியாக்க நட்பு"],"p":"Make friendship (with one) after ascertaining (his) character, birth, defects and the whole of one's relations.","r":"Know his qualities, his family, his faults and his inan, and only then form a friendship.","n":"Faults are on the list to be known, not to be absent. Parimelazhagar's point is that no one is faultless, so the question is whether the faults can be borne.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kudi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்றா இனன் is a friend's undiminished kin, since those bound to kin bring that bond along with them.","சுற்றப் பிணிப்புடையார் நட்டாரோடும் பிணிப்புண்டு வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The phrase means qualities that don't decline, not relatives.",""]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:794":{"book":"kural","num":794,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணு வானைக்","கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of one who belongs to a (good) family and is afraid of (being charged with) guilt, is worth even purchasing.","r":"One of good family who feels shame at blame on himself: that friendship should be had even by giving.","n":"பழிநாணுவான் is a person who is governed by shame. The commercial phrase is startling for something the previous chapter praises so highly. Kalaignar softens it to 'by any means', while Parimelazhagar says plainly: buy it for a price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:795":{"book":"kural","num":795,"t":["அழச்சொல்லி அல்லது இடித்து வழக்கறிய","வல்லார்நடபு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"You should examine and secure the friendship of those who can speak so as to make you weep over a crime (before its commission) or rebuke you severely (after you have done it) and are able to teach you (the ways of) the world.","r":"Inquire and take the friendship of those able to speak until one weeps, to rebuke, and to teach what is proper.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idithal","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse describes friends, who dissuade before the act, press hard after it, and make one do what is proper.","தாம் உலக வழக்கல்லது செய்யக் கருதின்சோகம் பிறக்கும்வகை சொல்லி விலக்கியும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about which ministers a ruler should befriend.","இது மந்திரிகளுள் நட்பாக்கற் பாலாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:796":{"book":"kural","num":796,"t":["கேட்டினும் உண்டோ ர் உறுதி கிளைஞரை","நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்"],"p":"Even in ruin there is some good; (for) it is a rod by which one may measure fully (the affection of one's) relations.","r":"Even in ruin there is one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length.","n":"The benefit is knowledge rather than comfort. Parimelazhagar says the true extent of each friend's bond stays hidden in wealthy times, and that ruin measures it for choosing better afterward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:797":{"book":"kural","num":797,"t":["ஊதியம் என்பது ஒருவற்குப் பேதையார்","கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"It is indead a gain for one to renounce the friendship of fools.","r":"Gain for a person means giving up and leaving the friendship of fools.","n":"The verse calls a loss a gain. Parimelazhagar says one must also move away, because like sandal and vengai burning together, nearness alone brings harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:798":{"book":"kural","num":798,"t":["உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ கொள்ளற்க","அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு"],"p":"Do not think of things that discourage your mind, nor contract friendship with those who would forsake you in adversity.","r":"Do not think on what shrinks the spirit, and do not take the friendship of those who cut away in distress.","n":"The verse pairs a rule about thought and a rule about company, and Parimelazhagar treats the first as an illustration of the second. Fair-weather friends are thus a thing that shrinks the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:799":{"book":"kural","num":799,"t":["கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார் கேண்மை அடுங்காலை","உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்"],"p":"The very thought of the friendship of those who have deserted one at the approach of adversity will burn one's mind at the time of death.","r":"Friendship with those who forsake one in ruin burns the heart, even to think of, at the moment of death.","n":"Betrayal is measured against death and is what burns. Parimelazhagar goes further: the mere thought of it is worse than Death, and even for an unconnected person who thinks of it. He also records another reading in which the thought is of the harm one brought on oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:800":{"book":"kural","num":800,"t":["மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மைஒன் றீத்தும்","ஒருவுக ஒப்பிலார் நட்பு"],"p":"Continue to enjoy the friendship of the pure; (but) renounce even with a gift, the friendship of those who do not agree (with the world).","r":"Keep close to the friendship of the stainless; give something if you must, but leave the friendship of those who are oppilar.","n":"The cost of a bad friendship is paid at the end as well. It balances 794: one may pay to get a friend and pay to be rid of one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒப்பிலார் are those out of accord with the world.","உலகோடு ஒத்தலில்லார் நட்பினை அறியாது கொண்டாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","ஒப்பிலார் are those not one's equals.","தனக்கு நிகரில்லாதார் நட்பினின்று நீங்குக"],["kalaignar","They are simply those who carry a stain, the contrary of the first half.",""]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:801":{"book":"kural","num":801,"t":["பழைமை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதும்","கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு"],"p":"Imtimate friendship is that which cannot in the least be injured by (things done through the) right (of longstanding intimacy).","r":"What is called old friendship? It is friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties that intimacy gives.","n":"The definition is not about time, and Parimelazhagar says so outright. His list of permitted liberties is concrete: acting without asking, even acting to one's harm, taking what one needs, showing neither deference nor fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:802":{"book":"kural","num":802,"t":["நட்பிற் குறுப்புக் கெழுதகைமை மற்றதற்கு","உப்பாதல் சான்றோர் கடன்"],"p":"The constituents of friendship are (things done through) the right of intimacy; to be pleased with such a right is the duty of the wise.","r":"Intimate liberty is a limb of friendship, and being as salt to it is the duty of the worthy.","n":"உப்பு: the worthy person doesn't merely tolerate liberties but gives them savour. Manakkudavar gives a homely comparison: a bitter gourd stays bitter however much seasoning goes in, so without consent no sweetness comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:803":{"book":"kural","num":803,"t":["பழகிய நட்பெவன் செய்யுங் கெழுதகைமை","செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை"],"p":"Of what avail is long-standing friendship, if friends do not admit as their own actions done through the right of intimacy ?","r":"What use is long-practised friendship if one does not accept a friend's liberty as if one had done it oneself?","n":"செய்தாங்கு is the core: treat the act as your own. Parimelazhagar adds that this holds even for what one wouldn't agree to, which is where the test really lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:804":{"book":"kural","num":804,"t":["விழைதகையான் வேண்டி இருப்பர் கெழுதகையாற்","கேளாது நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends, through the right of friendship, do (anything) without being asked, the wise will be pleased with them on account of its desirability.","r":"When friends act unasked out of intimacy, those who are wise welcome it for how desirable it is.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The act is one's own business done for one by a friend unasked, and nothing is better than finding it done.","ஒருவர்க்குத் தம் கருமம் தாம் அறியாமல் முடிந்திருத்தலின் ஊங்கு நன்மை யின்மையின்"],["manakkudavar","The act is something one doesn't approve, and one should still welcome it and not only permit it.","கேளாது இசைவில்லாதவற்றைச் செய்வாராயின், அதற்கு முனியாது"]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:805":{"book":"kural","num":805,"t":["பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க","நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends should perform what is painful, understand that it is owing not only to ignorance, but also to the strong claims of intimacy.","r":"If friends do something that hurts, understand it as either folly or great intimacy.","n":"The verse is a rule of interpretation: two readings are allowed, and lack of love isn't one of them. Parimelazhagar brings in fate, saying the folly comes from a fate of loss. The verse does not. Pope's 'not only... but also' blurs the either-or that the commentators find.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:806":{"book":"kural","num":806,"t":["எல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் துறவார் தொலைவிடத்தும்","தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Those who stand within the limits (of true friendship) will not even in adversity give up the intimacy of long-standing friends.","r":"Those who hold to the bounds will not give up the bond of those who stand in old friendship, even when ruin comes.","n":"For Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar the ruin comes from that friend, which makes this harder than loyalty in hard times. The verse's தொலைவிடத்தும் doesn't itself say who caused the ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:807":{"book":"kural","num":807,"t":["அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார் அன்பின்","வழிவந்த கேண்மை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have (long) stood in the path of affection will not give it up even if their friends cause (them) their ruin.","r":"Even when destructive things are done to them, those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love.","n":"The verse does not say the friendship goes unaltered in every way, only that அன்பு does not end. It locates the constancy in love and not in the deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:808":{"book":"kural","num":808,"t":["கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை வல்லார்க்கு","நாளிழுக்கம் நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"To those who understand that by which they should not listen to (tales about) the faults of their friends, that is a (profitable) day on which the latter may commit a fault.","r":"For those who have the strength of intimacy not to listen to reports of a friend's lapse, the day the friend lapses has its own meaning.","n":"The verse has a sting: the lapse is what reveals the strength. Parimelazhagar says the bond only shows when it is tested, so other days don't count.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The day a friend lapses is a day of real worth, for it alone shows such intimacy.","செய்து போந்துழியல்லது அவ்வுரிமை வெளிப்படாமையின், செய்யாதன நாளல்லவாயின."],["manakkudavar","Such a day is a good day.","நட்டோர் தப்புச் செய்யின், அந்த நாள் நல்ல நாளாம்"],["kalaignar","The reverse: if they do wrong to one so trusting, all the days of that friendship were wasted.",""]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:809":{"book":"kural","num":809,"t":["கெடாஅ வழிவந்த கேண்மையார் கேண்மை","விடாஅர் விழையும் உலகு"],"p":"They will be loved by the world, who have not forsaken the friendship of those with whom they have kept up an unbroken long-standing intimacy.","r":"The world loves those who do not give up the friendship of friends whose bond has come down unbroken.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a motive: everyone reasons that such a person would be like this to them too, and comes to seek friendship. Loyalty to one is an advertisement to all. Manakkudavar's வழிவந்த points rather to friendship inherited through family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:810":{"book":"kural","num":810,"t":["விழையார் விழையப் படுப பழையார்கண்","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியா தார்"],"p":"Even enemies will love those who have never changed in their affection to their long-standingfriends.","r":"Those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them.","n":"The commentators take விழையார் as enemies. The verse's own word is softer, 'those who don't desire', and the tight wordplay விழையார் விழையப் works without naming any foe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","panpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:811":{"book":"kural","num":811,"t":["பருகுவார் போலினும் பண்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது"],"p":"The decrease of friendship with those who look as if they would eat you up (through excess of love) while they are really destitute of goodness is far better than its increase.","r":"Though they seem to drink you up, the friendship of those without character is better shrinking than growing.","n":"பருகுவார் is the language of passion. Parimelazhagar cites a matching line of poetry and Manakkudavar adds 'with the eyes'. The verse warns that intensity is not evidence. The advice is modest too, since it only says that shrinking is better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:812":{"book":"kural","num":812,"t":["உறின்நட்டு அறின்ஙருஉம் ஒப்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்?"],"p":"Of what avail is it to get or lose the friendship of those who love when there is gain and leave when there is none ?","r":"What does it matter whether one gains or loses the friendship of misfits who are friends when there is profit and leave when there is none?","n":"The rhetorical shrug is the point. Parimelazhagar concludes that one should simply be neutral toward them (நொதுமல்). They are neither to be sought nor fought, which sets them apart from the verses that say to run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:813":{"book":"kural","num":813,"t":["உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பும் பெறுவது","கொள்வாரும் கள்வரும் நேர்"],"p":"Friendship who calculate the profits (of their friendship), prostitutes who are bent on obtaining their gains, and thieves are (all) of the same character.","r":"Friends who weigh what they stand to gain, those who take what they get, and thieves are all alike.","n":"The middle term in the verse says only 'those who take what they get'. It is the commentators who identify them as prostitutes. In the verse's shape, the calculation of friends is the real target and the other two are comparisons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:814":{"book":"kural","num":814,"t":["அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா அன்னார்","தமரின் தனிமை தலை"],"p":"Solitude is more to be desired than the society of those who resemble the untrained horses which throw down (their riders) in the fields of battle.","r":"Better solitude than having as one's own those like an untrained horse that throws its rider in battle.","n":"The horse has been carrying its rider well until the moment that counts. Parimelazhagar also notes the verse doesn't praise solitude, which is only less bad. Valluvar isn't recommending it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:815":{"book":"kural","num":815,"t":["செய்தேமஞ் சாராச் சிறியவர் புன்கேண்மை","எய்தலின் எய்தாமை நன்று"],"p":"It is far better to avoid that to contract the evil friendship of the base who cannot protect (their friends) even when appointed to do so.","r":"It is better not to get than to get the poor friendship of the small, who are no protection even after one has made them friends.","n":"ஏமம் brings in the guard of 781 again: a friend who doesn't protect has failed the definition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The small are base people in general, whose failure is that they leave in ruin.","அரணாகாமை - தொலைவின்கண் விட்டு நீங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The small are particular kinds of people, gamblers, hunters and women among them, a gendered list which the verse doesn't contain.","சிறியார் - சூதர், வேட்டைக் காரர், பெண்டிர் போல்வார்."]],"ch":"k:82"},"k:816":{"book":"kural","num":816,"t":["பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பின் அறிவுடையார்","ஏதின்மை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"The hatred of the wise is ten-million times more profitable than the excessive intimacy of the fool.","r":"Being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool.","n":"ஏதின்மை is literally not-belonging. Pope and the commentators read it as enmity. Parimelazhagar's argument is that the enmity of the wise does no harm while the fool's friendship does every harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:817":{"book":"kural","num":817,"t":["நகைவகைய ராகிய நட்பின் பகைவரால்","பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்"],"p":"What comes from enemies is a hundred million times more profitable than what comes from the friendship of those who cause only laughter.","r":"Ten crore times better than friendship of the kind that is all laughter is what comes from enemies.","n":"The multiplier goes up ten times from 816. Parimelazhagar says even the pains that enemies cause exceed the pleasures of these friends, identifies them as hangers-on who amuse for profit, and dismisses other commentators' readings as ungrammatical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:818":{"book":"kural","num":818,"t":["ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்று பவர்கேண்மை","சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்"],"p":"Gradually abandon without revealing (beforehand) the friendship of those who pretend inability to carry out what they (really) could do.","r":"With those who make hard work of what they could easily do, let the friendship slip without a word.","n":"A practical subtlety. Parimelazhagar says speaking would only lead them to patch things up and go on, so the silence is tactical. It is the opposite of a formal break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:819":{"book":"kural","num":819,"t":["கனவினும் இன்னாது மன்னோ வினைவேறு","சொல்வேறு பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those whose actions do not agree with their words will distress (one) even in (one's) dreams.","r":"Even in a dream, the bond with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is exact: enemies in deed and friends in word. The dream adds that the harm follows into sleep, a place where in other verses love is what lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:820":{"book":"kural","num":820,"t":["எனைத்தும் குறுகுதல் ஓம்பல் மனைக்கெழீஇ","மன்றில் பழிப்பார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Avoid even the least approach to a contraction of friendship with those who would love you in private but ridicule you in public.","r":"Take care not to come near at all to the bond of those who are friendly at home and defame you in the assembly.","n":"The contrast is between private and public, and it's the public that matters: a friend is tested by what is said in the மன்று. எனைத்தும் leaves no room for degree, unlike the 'better to shrink' of 811.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:821":{"book":"kural","num":821,"t":["சீரிடம் காணின் எறிதற்குப் பட் டடை","நேரா நிரந்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those who behave like friends without inward affection is a weapon that may be thrown when a favourable opportunity presents itself.","r":"The friendship of those who come close without inner consent is an anvil for striking, once they find the right place.","n":"A பட்டடை seems to hold a thing up and in fact is what makes the blow cut, so Pope's gloss 'weapon' loses the image. The false friend need not be the blade. The support he gives is what enables it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:822":{"book":"kural","num":822,"t":["இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார் கேண்மை மகளிர்","மனம்போல வேறு படும்"],"p":"The friendship of those who seem to be friends while they are not, will change like the love of women.","r":"The friendship of those who seem one's own but aren't will change the way women's minds change.","n":"The verse itself makes a general comparison about women, and the commentators' responses show how uneasy it is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Women's minds are changeable once there is an opportunity, and he supports it from another text.","இடம் பெற்றால் பெண்பாலார் மனம் போல வேறுபடும்."],["kalaignar","The comparison concerns only prostitutes, who pretend to the virtue that women have, so women in general are not the target.",""]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:823":{"book":"kural","num":823,"t":["பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்து மனநல்லர்","ஆகுதல் மாணார்க் கரிது"],"p":"Though (one's) enemies may have mastered many good books, it will be impossible for them to become truly loving at heart.","r":"Though they have studied many good things, it is hard for the unworthy to become good at heart.","n":"The verse separates learning from moral change. This limit matters to a text that elsewhere praises study so highly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kalvi","vanjam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மாணார் are enemies, and learning won't make them friends, so don't trust them because they are learned.","அதனான் மனம் திருந்தி நட்பாதல் பகைவர்க்கு இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","மாணார் are simply those lacking excellence, and the point is about what learning can reveal.","மனநல்லாராகு தல் மாட்சிமையில்லார்க்கு அரிது"]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:824":{"book":"kural","num":824,"t":["முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா","வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"One should fear the deceitful who smile sweetly with their face but never love with their heart.","r":"Fear the deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while within they are bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason for fear: the face is the instrument for reading intentions, and here the malice doesn't show on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:825":{"book":"kural","num":825,"t":["மனத்தின் அமையா தவரை எனைத்தொன்றும்","சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"In nothing whatever is it proper to rely on the words of those who do not love with their heart.","r":"Those not in accord with you in mind are not to be trusted on the strength of their words in anything.","n":"The verse states no authority, but Parimelazhagar supplies one: 'the treatises on policy' say so. He reads the line as counsel on whom to trust in practical affairs (கருமங்கள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["manam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:826":{"book":"kural","num":826,"t":["நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும் ஒட்டார்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though (one's) foes may utter good things as though they were friends, once will at once understand (their evil, import).","r":"Though they say good things as friends do, the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through.","n":"This verse is more optimistic than its neighbours about detection. Parimelazhagar reads the concession to mean they rarely say good things at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:827":{"book":"kural","num":827,"t":["சொல்வணக்கம் ஒன்னார்கண் கொள்ளற்க வில்வணக்கம்","தீங்கு குறித்தமை யான்"],"p":"Since the bending of the bow bespeaks evil, one should not accept (as good) the humiliating speeches of one's foes.","r":"Do not take humility in an enemy's speech as humility, for the bending of a bow means harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar marks it as 'word-bowing' and not real bowing, since the man himself doesn't bow. The bend is what stores the force, so the humility may be the very preparation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:828":{"book":"kural","num":828,"t":["தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும் ஒன்னார்","அழுதகண் ணீரும் அனைத்து"],"p":"A weapon may be hid in the very hands with which (one's) foes adore (him) (and) the tears they shed are of the same nature.","r":"A weapon hides even inside hands folded in worship, and an enemy's shed tears are the same.","n":"The chapter moves from words to the body, and it picks the gestures hardest to doubt, reverence and grief. Parimelazhagar's line is that those able to read intent see the weapon even in the moment of reassurance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:829":{"book":"kural","num":829,"t":["மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளு வாரை நகச்செய்து","நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லற் பாற்று"],"p":"It is the duty of kings to affect great love but make it die (inwardly); as regard those foes who shew them great friendship but despise them (in their heart).","r":"Those who overdo friendship and despise you inwardly should be made to smile, and embraced in friendship so that it dies.","n":"The verse itself gives no audience, but Parimelazhagar supplies 'royal policy' and says plainly that having one thing inside and another outside is wrong in general but proper toward enemies. He treats it as a licensed exception. சாப்புல்லல் is a hard phrase: this reading follows the commentators, and Pope's couplet ('slap') goes elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:830":{"book":"kural","num":830,"t":["பகைநட்பாம் காலம் வருங்கால் முகநட்டு","அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"When one's foes begin to affect friendship, one should love them with one's looks, and, cherishing no love in the heart, give up (even the former).","r":"When the time comes that an enemy turns friend, befriend with the face, let go in the heart, and then let it go altogether.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows the permission to a moment when open rejection isn't possible, and stresses a final exit. So the mask is a temporary means, not a settled way of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:831":{"book":"kural","num":831,"t":["பேதைமை என்பதொன்று யாதெனின் ஏதங்கொண்டு","ஊதியம் போக விடல்"],"p":"Folly is one (of the chief defects); it is that which (makes one) incur loss and forego gain.","r":"What is the one thing called folly? It is taking hold of harm and letting gain go.","n":"This is a definition by behaviour rather than by lack of knowledge. Parimelazhagar lists poverty, blame and sin as the harm and wealth, fame and virtue as the gain, so he reads the choice as ruining both worlds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:832":{"book":"kural","num":832,"t":["பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை காதன்மை","கையல்ல தன்கட் செயல்"],"p":"The greatest folly is that which leads one to take delight in doing what is forbidden.","r":"Of all follies the worst is to set one's love on what is not for one's hand.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means conduct forbidden by the treatises, loved and done anyway.","இருமைக்கும் ஆகாதென்று நூலோர் கடிந்த செயல்களை விரும்பிச் செய்தல் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","It means something out of reach, loved even though effort won't get it.","தனக்குக் கைவாராத பொருளின்கண் காதன்மை செய்தல்"],["kalaignar","It means tasks beyond one's ability, and meddling in them.",""]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:833":{"book":"kural","num":833,"t":["நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும்","பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்"],"p":"Shamelessness indifference (to what must be sought after), harshness, and aversion for everything (that ought to be desired) are the qualities of the fool.","r":"No shame, no searching, no tenderness, no care for anything: this is what a fool does.","n":"The fool is described by four absences, in a chain of words ending in -மை. தொழில் suggests something habitual: Parimelazhagar says these are his constant nature. நார் is a fibre, a bond, and also fellow-feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:834":{"book":"kural","num":834,"t":["ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் பிறர் க்குரைத்தும் தானடங்காப்","பேதையின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"There are no greater fools than he who, though he has read and understood (a great deal) and even taught it to others, does not walk according to his own teaching.","r":"There is no greater fool than one who studies, understands and teaches others but does not restrain himself.","n":"All three stages of learning are granted, and restraint alone is missing. Parimelazhagar makes it medical: this folly isn't cured by its own remedy, and no other remedy exists.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:835":{"book":"kural","num":835,"t":["ஒருமைச் செயலாற்றும் பேதை எழுமையும்","தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு"],"p":"A fool can procure in a single birth a hell into which he may enter and suffer through all the seven births.","r":"A fool can, in one life's doing, make the mire he will enter and sink into for seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mire is hell, and all future births are lived in hell's suffering because of one life's cruel deeds.","வரும் பிறவிகள் எல்லாம் தான் புக்கு அழுந்தும் நிரயத்தினை"],["kalaignar","ஒருமை is acting on one's own whim, and எழுமை simply means always. The mire is suffering with no births or hell involved.",""]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:836":{"book":"kural","num":836,"t":["பொய்படும் ஒன்றோ புனைபூணும் கையறியாப்","பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்"],"p":"If the fool, who knows not how to act undertakes a work, he will (certainly) fail. (But) is it all ? He will even adorn himself with fetters.","r":"If a fool who doesn't know how takes up a task, it comes to nothing, and more than that, he wears fetters.","n":"பூணும் is what one does with jewellery, so the fetters are worn like ornaments. Manakkudavar takes பொய்படும் to mean he himself gets called false, whereas Parimelazhagar means the work collapses inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:837":{"book":"kural","num":837,"t":["ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர் பேதை","பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"If a fool happens to get an immense fortune, his neighbours will enjoy it while his relations starve.","r":"When a fool gets great wealth, strangers feast and his own people go hungry.","n":"The verse says உற்றக்கடை, 'when it has come to him'. Parimelazhagar takes this as wealth got by fate, on the argument that a fool couldn't have made it himself. The next verse's பெறின் gets the same treatment. Both are his additions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:838":{"book":"kural","num":838,"t":["மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்றால் பேதைதன்","கையொன்று உடைமை பெறின்"],"p":"A fool happening to possess something is like the intoxication of one who is (already) giddy.","r":"When a fool gets something into his hand, it is like a man already deranged getting drunk.","n":"The image is of something added to an existing state. Wealth doesn't produce the folly but compounds it, and ஒன்று, 'some one thing', suggests it doesn't take much.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:839":{"book":"kural","num":839,"t":["பெரிதினிது பேதையார் கேண்மை பிரிவின்கண்","பீழை தருவதொன் றில்"],"p":"The friendship between fools is exceedingly delightful (to each other): for at parting there will be nothing to cause them pain.","r":"The friendship of fools is very sweet, for there is nothing painful about parting from them.","n":"பிரிவு is a word that carries great weight in the Book of Love, so the 'sweetness' measured by painless parting reads like the opposite of what love is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is mock praise, blame dressed as praise: the friendship wanes daily, so parting hurts no one.","புகழ்வார் போன்று பழித்தவாறு."],["manakkudavar","It concerns women's relations with foolish men, and how a fool enjoys desire.","இது பேதை காமந் துய்க்குமாறு கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","He gives the literal sense, with no irony marked: it is sweet because parting brings no sorrow.",""]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:840":{"book":"kural","num":840,"t":["கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்றால் சான்றோர்","குழாஅத்துப் பேதை புகல்"],"p":"The appearance of a fool in an assembly of the learned is like placing (one's) unwashed feet on a bed.","r":"A fool's entering an assembly of the worthy is like placing an unwashed foot on a bed.","n":"The harm passes to the place: the bed and the assembly are both defiled, not the foot or the fool. Parimelazhagar notes that 'unwashed' is a polite cover for what the foot has trodden, which Kalaignar spells out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:841":{"book":"kural","num":841,"t":["அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை பிறிதின்மை","இன்மையா வையா துலகு"],"p":"The want of wisdom is the greatest of all wants; but that of wealth the world will not regard as such.","r":"Lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties; other lack the world does not count as lack.","n":"வையாது உலகு attributes the valuation to the world. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the wise keep this world's and the next world's fruit even in poverty, whereas the petty-minded don't even with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:842":{"book":"kural","num":842,"t":["அறிவிலான் நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல் பிறிதியாதும்","இல்லை பெறுவான் தவம்"],"p":"(The cause of) a fool cheerfully giving (something) is nothing else but the receiver's merit (in a former birth).","r":"When a person without understanding gives with a glad heart, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's merit.","n":"A strange and rather cold claim: a good act by such a person earns him nothing. Parimelazhagar raises and rebuts the objection that fools also do good. Kalaignar has simply good luck (பெற்றபேறு), which removes தவம் from the picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:843":{"book":"kural","num":843,"t":["அறிவிலார் தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The suffering that fools inflict upon themselves is hardly possible even to foes.","r":"The suffering those without understanding do to themselves is hard even for their enemies to do.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a clever reason: an enemy has to watch for a moment and knows only so much, whereas self-injury never pauses and reaches poverty, blame and sin all at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:844":{"book":"kural","num":844,"t":["வெண்மை எனப்படுவ தியாதெனின் ஒண்மை","உடையம்யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"What is called want of wisdom is the vanity which says, \"We are wise\".","r":"What is called வெண்மை? It is the pride that says 'we have brightness.'","n":"வெண்மை, whiteness, is rawness or unripeness. ஒண்மை is brightness. The two words sound alike and describe opposite things, and the fool can't tell whether he is lit or blank. Parimelazhagar calls it a delusion because such people persist knowing the world mocks them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:845":{"book":"kural","num":845,"t":["கல்லாத மேற்கொண் டொழுகல் கசடற","வல்லதூஉம் ஐயம் தரும்"],"p":"Fools pretending to know what has not been read (by them) will rouse suspicion even as to what they have thoroughly mastered.","r":"Taking on what one hasn't learned brings doubt even on what one has mastered without flaw.","n":"The verse is about how credibility works. The pretence costs real knowledge too. This is not a moral claim but a practical one about reputation, and it grants that the pretender may have actual skill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:846":{"book":"kural","num":846,"t":["அற்றம் மறைத்தலோ புல்லறிவு தம்வயின்","குற்றம் மறையா வழி"],"p":"Even to cover one's nakedness would be folly, if (one's) faults were not covered (by forsaking them).","r":"Is covering one's nakedness not itself petty, when one's faults aren't covered?","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To cover a fault means to get rid of it. Covering the lesser thing while leaving the greater and thinking oneself decent is the folly.","குற்றம் மறைத்தலாவது, அவற்றை இலவாக்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","To cover a fault means keeping it from others' notice, as clothing hides the body from view.","தம்பாலுள்ள குற்றத்தைப் பிறரறியாமல் தாம் மறையாத காலத் து"]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:847":{"book":"kural","num":847,"t":["அருமறை சோரும் அறிவிலான் செய்யும்","பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு"],"p":"The fool who neglects precious counsel does, of his own accord, a great injury to himself.","r":"One without understanding who lets slip the precious மறை does great harm to himself by himself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kedu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறை is rare teaching which the fool doesn't take in. He rejects the other reading explicitly, since leaking a secret would be carelessness and not this fault.","அது பேணாமை என்னும் பேதைமையாவதன்றிப் புல்லறிவாண்மையன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","மறை is a closely held secret, and letting it slip means telling others.","சோரவிடுதல் - பிறர்க்குச் சொல்லுதல்."]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:848":{"book":"kural","num":848,"t":["ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான்தேறான் அவ்வுயிர்","போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்"],"p":"The fool will not perform (his duties) even when advised nor ascertain them himself; such a soul is a burden (to the earth) till it departs (from the body).","r":"Not doing it when told, not seeing it himself, that life is a disease until it departs.","n":"The verse doesn't say for whom. Parimelazhagar says for the earth that bears him, Manakkudavar for the people of the world, and Kalaignar for himself. These are three very different diseases. The verse's two failures also cover both routes to knowledge, one's own and another's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:849":{"book":"kural","num":849,"t":["காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான் காணாதான்","கண்டானாம் தான்கண்ட வாறு"],"p":"One who would teach a fool will (simply) betray his folly; and the fool would (still) think himself \"wise in his own conceit\".","r":"One who shows things to a person who doesn't see becomes himself unseeing, and the unseeing one goes on having seen what he saw.","n":"The compact wordplay on காண் is the content: the teacher fails and the pupil remains satisfied. Parimelazhagar says the teacher is blamed by the pupil and ends up the ignorant one, and concludes that good sense can't be instilled here by any means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:850":{"book":"kural","num":850,"t":["உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான் வையத்து","அலகையா வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who denies the existence of what the world believes in will be regarded as a demon on earth.","r":"One who says 'it isn't' to what the world says is will be set down on this earth as a demon.","n":"The verse names neither what's denied nor who 'the world' is, and everything turns on those blanks.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is denied is God, rebirth, and the fruit of deeds, as affirmed by the eminent.","கடவுளும், மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும் முதலாக அவர் உள என்பன பலவேனும்"],["manakkudavar","What is denied is what the many wise affirm, with no specific doctrine named.","உலகத்தில் அறிவுடையார் பலர் உண்டென்பதாகிய ஒரு பொருளை இல்லையென்று"],["kalaignar","What is denied is what's been shown clearly by evidence, and the denial is deliberate.",""]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:851":{"book":"kural","num":851,"t":["இகலென்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் பகலென்னும்","பண்பின்மை பாரிக்கும் நோய்"],"p":"The disease which fosters the evil of disunion among all creatures is termed hatred by the wise.","r":"They call hostility the disease that spreads, among all living beings, the ill nature of division.","n":"Hostility here is not an event but a disease that spreads a disposition. Parimelazhagar sees in எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் a judgement: quarrelling humans are being put with the animals.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:852":{"book":"kural","num":852,"t":["பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும் இகல்கருதி","இன்னாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"Though disagreeable things may be done from (a feeling of) disunion, it is far better that nothing painful be done from (that of) hatred.","r":"Even if someone does what you can't bear, intending division, it is best not to do harm in return, intending hostility.","n":"The reason Parimelazhagar gives is strategic rather than saintly. Retaliate and enmity grows and you sink, abstain and it dies down and you rise. This is non-retaliation argued in the Book of Wealth's terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:853":{"book":"kural","num":853,"t":["இகலென்னும் எவ்வநோய் நீக்கின் தவலில்லாத்","தாவில் விளக்கம் தரும்"],"p":"To rid one-self of the distressing dtsease of hatred will bestow (on one) a never-decreasing imperishable fame.","r":"If the painful disease called hostility is removed, it gives a light that doesn't diminish and has no flaw.","n":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ on விளக்கம்: fame for one, radiance for the other. His route to fame is a long chain. Everyone becomes a friend, then every wealth comes, then giving, then renown. The verse takes no such steps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:854":{"book":"kural","num":854,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் பயக்கும் இகலென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"If hatred which is the greatest misery is destroyed, it will yield the greatest delight.","r":"If the suffering of sufferings called hostility is destroyed, it brings the joy of joys.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is worldly: with everyone a friend, one obtains every fruit.","யாவரும் நட்பாகலின் எல்லாப் பயனும் எய்தியுறுவது."],["manakkudavar","The joy is liberation, the highest of all joys.","எல்லா இன்பத்தின் மிக்க வீடுபேற்றின்பம் எய்தும் என்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:855":{"book":"kural","num":855,"t":["இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை யாரே","மிக்லூக்கும் தன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Who indeed would think of conquering those who naturally shrink back from hatred ?","r":"Who would set out to defeat those who are able to lean away from hostility?","n":"வல்லாரை marks it as an ability, not a weakness. Manakkudavar faces the objection directly: wouldn't giving way be a defeat? The verse answers that no one can defeat them. Parimelazhagar adds it is particularly hard for kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:856":{"book":"kural","num":856,"t":["இகலின் மிகலினிது என்பவன் வாழ்க்கை","தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து"],"p":"Failure and ruin are not far from him who says it is sweet to excel in hatred.","r":"The life of one who says it is sweet to excel in hostility is close to failure and to ruin.","n":"The problem is the relish: என்பவன் indicts the person who enjoys conflict and not one drawn into it. Kalaignar gives 'easy' (எளிது) for the verse's 'sweet' (இனிது), which changes the charge from pleasure to overconfidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:857":{"book":"kural","num":857,"t":["மிகல்மேவல் மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார் இகல்மேவல்","இன்னா அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement brings misery through its connection with hatred cannot understand the triumphant nature of truth.","r":"Those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing that holds victory.","n":"Parimelazhagar's point: hostility itself clouds the mind, so that the very quarrel blinds one to how to win. A combative person is therefore bad at conflict.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மெய்ப்பொருள் here is the content of the treatises on policy, which brings success to those who follow them.","வெற்றி பொருந்துதலையுடைய நீதி நூற்பொருளை அறியமாட்டார்."],["manakkudavar","It is truth as such, which those in conflict can't perceive.","இது மாறுபடுவார்க்கு மெய்ப்பொருள் தோன்றாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:858":{"book":"kural","num":858,"t":["இகலிற்கு எதிர்சாய்தல் ஆக்கம் அதனை","மிக்லூக்கின் ஊக்குமாம் கேடு"],"p":"Shrinking back from hatred will yield wealth; indulging in its increase will hasten ruin.","r":"Leaning away from hostility is gain; if one drives on to excel at it, ruin drives on to come.","n":"Ruin mirrors what one does: ஊக்கின் ஊக்கும். Parimelazhagar locates the turning point in one's own mind, at the moment when hostility arises and isn't taken up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:859":{"book":"kural","num":859,"t":["இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால் அதனை","மிகல்காணும் கேடு தரற்கு"],"p":"At the approach of wealth one will not think of hatred (but) to secure one's ruin, one will look to its increase.","r":"When gain is coming one doesn't look at hostility, and to bring on ruin one looks to excel at it.","n":"This complicates 858. Hostility may not be the cause of ruin but the sign of it. Parimelazhagar says these are forerunners of gain and loss, and Manakkudavar links them to one's merit, which reduces how much the choice in 858 is really free.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:860":{"book":"kural","num":860,"t":["இகலானாம் இன்னாத எல்லாம் நகலானாம்","நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"All calamities are caused by hatred; but by the delight (of friendship) is caused the great wealth of good virtues.","r":"From hostility come all ills, and from friendly gladness comes the pride of good and fitting ways.","n":"செருக்கு is usually a fault in the Kural (844) and here it is praise. Parimelazhagar reads it as great wealth, and நன்னயம் as good policy. So the virtue is presented as a prosperous kind of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:861":{"book":"kural","num":861,"t":["வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக ஓம்பா","மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை"],"p":"Avoid offering resistance to the strong; (but) never fail to cherish enmity towards the weak.","r":"Avoid opposing the strong, and without holding back, favour enmity against the weak.","n":"Whatever this is, it is not a moral teaching, which is why the commentators' disagreement matters so much.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a plain rule of statecraft: avoid the strong, and keep enmity with the weak, since beating them doesn't drain one's strength.","ஏனை மெலியார்க்குப் பகையாதலை ஒழியாது விரும்புக."],["manakkudavar","The weak in view are those who don't respect one, which narrows the rule.","தம்மைப் போற்றாத எளியார்மாட்டுப் பகைகோடலை மேவுக"],["kalaignar","The opposite: true excellence in enmity is leaving the weak alone and taking on the strong.",""]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:862":{"book":"kural","num":862,"t":["அன்பிலன் ஆன்ற துணையிலன் தான்துவ்வான்","என்பரியும் ஏதிலான் துப்பு"],"p":"How can he who is unloving, destitute of powerful aids, and himself without strength overcome the might of his foe ?","r":"With no love, no worthy allies and no strength of his own, how can he wear down an enemy's might?","n":"அன்பு appears in a list of military assets. For Parimelazhagar it is love of one's kin, so without it there's no kin, and for an attacker such a person only adds strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:863":{"book":"kural","num":863,"t":["அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்","தஞ்சம் எளியன் பகைக்கு"],"p":"In the estimation of foes miserably weak is he, who is timid, ignorant, unsociable and niggardly.","r":"He fears, doesn't know, doesn't fit in with others, doesn't give: he is utterly easy for an enemy.","n":"The list includes not giving, which is an ethical failing elsewhere and here is a military one. Kalaignar's 'compassion' softens the word. Parimelazhagar says அஞ்சும் means fearing what needn't be feared, the mirror of 428.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:864":{"book":"kural","num":864,"t":["நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","யாங்கணும் யார்க்கும் எளிது"],"p":"He who neither refrains from anger nor keeps his secrets will at all times and in all places be easily conquered by all.","r":"Not free from rage and without நிறை: at any time, in any place, he is easy for anyone.","n":"The commentators differ on நிறை. Parimelazhagar means keeping secrets and Kalaignar means self-control. Parimelazhagar's logic is neat: the enemy no longer needs to watch for the right time, place or strength because the angry man's leaked plans give it all away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:865":{"book":"kural","num":865,"t":["வழிநோக்கான் வாய்ப்பன செய்யான் பழிநோக்கான்","பண்பிலன் பற்றார்க்கு இனிது"],"p":"(A) pleasing (object) to his foes is he who reads not moral works, does nothing that is enjoined by them cares not for reproach and is not possessed of good qualities.","r":"He doesn't look to the வழி, doesn't do what is suitable, doesn't look to blame, has no character: he is a pleasure to his enemies.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழி is the treatise on policy, handed down from the ancients, which he doesn't study.","ஒருவன் நீதிநூலை ஓதான்"],["manakkudavar","வழி is the consequence of an action, which he doesn't consider when he starts it.","ஒருவினை செய்யத் தொடங்குங்கால் பின் வருவன பாரான்"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:866":{"book":"kural","num":866,"t":["காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்","பேணாமை பேணப் படும்"],"p":"Highly to be desired is the hatred of him whose anger is blind, and whose lust increases beyond measure.","r":"One of blinding anger and ever-growing lust: his not caring for you is something to care for.","n":"பேணாமை பேணப்படும் is the wordplay and the whole point. Such a person's hostility is an asset, since he will destroy himself. Parimelazhagar says all become his enemies through the anger, and in the lust he loses sight of his affairs. Kalaignar turns காமம் into greed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:867":{"book":"kural","num":867,"t":["கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற அடுத்திருந்து","மாணாத செய்வான் பகை"],"p":"It is indeed necessary to obtain even by purchase the hatred of him who having begun (a work) does what is not conductive (to its accomplishment).","r":"One who stays close and does what is unbecoming: it is truly necessary to get his enmity, even by giving something.","n":"Even the enmity has a price worth paying. The verse's hardest word is அடுத்திருந்து, and the commentators each supply a different object.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is a ruler who has begun a task and does what doesn't fit it, so losing a little to fight him pays many times over.","வினையைத் தொடங்கியிருந்து அதற்கு ஏலாதன செய்வான் பகைமையை"],["manakkudavar","He has taken up enmity and does what doesn't serve it, so he will not actually hurt you.","இஃது இவன் பகைசெய்யமாட்டானாதலால், அப்பகை கோடலாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","He is someone staying at your side and doing wrong, and you must pay to make him an open enemy.",""]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:868":{"book":"kural","num":868,"t":["குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின் மாற்றார்க்கு","இனனிலனாம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"He will become friendless who is without (any good) qualities. and whose faults are many; (such a character) is a help to (his) foes.","r":"With no qualities and many faults he has no one around him, and this is a protection to his enemies.","n":"ஏமாப்பு, the word for protection, has been turned around. A man's faults become his enemies' shield. Parimelazhagar lists what deserts him: kin, friends, wealth, army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:869":{"book":"kural","num":869,"t":["செறுவார்க்குச் சேணிகவா இன்பம் அறிவிலா","அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"There will be no end of lofty delights to the victorious, if their foes are (both) ignorant and timid.","r":"Joy doesn't go far from those fighting, if they get enemies who are unknowing and afraid.","n":"பெறின் sounds like luck. Parimelazhagar says that's because foes with neither knowledge nor resolve are rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:870":{"book":"kural","num":870,"t":["கல்லான் வெகுளும் சிறுபொருள் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","ஒல்லானை ஒல்லா தொளி"],"p":"The light (of fame) will never be gained by him who gains not the trifling reputation of having fought an unlearned (foe).","r":"Fame will never come to one who cannot manage the small thing of being angry at the unlearned.","n":"The syntax is hard and the commentaries don't converge. The reading given follows Parimelazhagar, who says others have not kept to the chapter's subject.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pugazh","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fighting the untaught is cheap in effort, and one who can't manage even that won't win fame.","நீதி அறியாதானை வேறல் எளிதாயிருக்கவும், அது மாட்டாதானை வெற்றியான் வரும் புகழ் கூடாது என்பதாம்"],["manakkudavar","The words describe one enemy who is unlearned, angry and poor, and it is he whom light never reaches.","கல்லாதானுமாய், வெகுளியுடையனுமாய், சிறு பொருளனுமாகிய பகைவனை எல்லா நாளும் ஒளி பொருந்தாது"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:871":{"book":"kural","num":871,"t":["பகைஎன்னும் பண்பி லதனை ஒருவன்","நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"The evil of hatred is not of a nature to be desired by one even in sport.","r":"The thing called enmity, which has no good in it, shouldn't be wanted by anyone, even in jest.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a psychological reason for நகையேயும்: desired in play, the resentment grows and it becomes real. The verse sits at odds with the chapter that comes just before it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:872":{"book":"kural","num":872,"t":["வில்லேர் உழவர் பகைகொளினும் கொள்ளற்க","சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை"],"p":"Though you may incur the hatred of warriors whose ploughs are bows, incur not that of ministers whose ploughs are words.","r":"Though you take on the enmity of ploughmen whose plough is the bow, do not take on that of ploughmen whose plough is the word.","n":"A famous line, often read now as about writers and poets, but the older commentators don't read it that way. Parimelazhagar means those with counsel and policy, whose harm reaches one's descendants, and Manakkudavar means ministers. Kalaignar's 'learned' is the broadest. Both groups are 'ploughmen', borrowing the honour chapter 104 gives to that work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:873":{"book":"kural","num":873,"t":["ஏமுற் றவரினும் ஏழை தமியனாய்ப்","பல்லார் பகைகொள் பவன்"],"p":"He who being alone, incurs the hatred of many is more infatuated than even mad men.","r":"One who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more wretched than those who are ஏமுற்றவர்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the mad, who at least aren't harmed by their delusion, while this one is.","மயக்கத்தால் ஒப்பாராயினும் ஏமுற்றவர் அதனால் தீங்கு எய்தாமையின்"],["kalaignar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the arrogant, and this person is better called a fool than arrogant.",""]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:874":{"book":"kural","num":874,"t":["பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடை யாளன்","தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The world abides in the greatness of that good-natured man who behaves so as to turn hatred into friendship.","r":"The world rests in the greatness of a person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship.","n":"நட்பாக் கொண்டு is ambiguous: making them friends, or treating them as friends? The whole verse turns on it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A king who, when it is needed, wins enemies over, and whose power therefore holds the world.","வேண்டியவழிப் பகையை வேறுபடுத்துத் தனக்கு நட்பாகச் செய்து கொண்டொழுகும் இயல்பினையுடைய அரசனது"],["manakkudavar","One able to behave toward enemies as if they were friends.","பகைவரை நட்புபோலக் கொண்டொழுக வல்ல பண்புடையவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a moral nobility in anyone who thinks of enemies as friends, and the world praises it.",""]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:875":{"book":"kural","num":875,"t":["தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால் தான்ஒருவன்","இன்துணையாக் கொள்கவற்றின் ஒன்று"],"p":"He who is alone and helpless while his foes are two should secure one of them as an agreeable help (to himself).","r":"If you have no ally and two enemies, being alone, take one of them as a pleasing ally.","n":"The word is இன்துணை, a sweet ally, which is warm for what it describes. Parimelazhagar adds 'for that time', and picks the one suited to defeating the other. The temporariness is his addition, though it's probably correct as politics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:876":{"book":"kural","num":876,"t":["தேறினும் தேறா விடினும் அழிவின்கண்","தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்"],"p":"Though (one's foe is) aware or not of one's misfortune one should act so as neither to join nor separate (from him).","r":"Whether or not you have trusted him, when ruin comes neither join with him nor break from him; let him be.","n":"A rule for neutrality. Parimelazhagar explains the two halves separately: don't join, because he could harm from inside; don't break, because he could help your ruin along. In adversity an enemy is best left unprovoked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:877":{"book":"kural","num":877,"t":["நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு மேவற்க","மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து"],"p":"Relate not your suffering even to friends who are ignorant of it, nor refer to your weakness in the presence of your foes.","r":"Don't complain to those who don't know of your pain, and don't show softness in front of enemies.","n":"The pairing is the surprise. Parimelazhagar takes those who don't know to be friends who haven't noticed on their own, which is itself a judgement on them. Pain should be read and not announced, which ties this to the chapters on signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:878":{"book":"kural","num":878,"t":["வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப மாயும்","பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு"],"p":"The joy of one's foes will be destroyed if one guards oneself by knowing the way (of acting) and securing assistance.","r":"Knowing the way, building oneself up and guarding oneself will end the swagger on the enemy's side.","n":"All three verbs are about oneself and none about the enemy, yet the enemy is what changes. Parimelazhagar's enemy is celebrating an expected win. Manakkudavar calls this 'the way to kill enemies', which shows how much he thinks self-strengthening does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:879":{"book":"kural","num":879,"t":["இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க களையுநர்","கைகொல்லும் காழ்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"A thorny tree should be felled while young, (for) when it is grown it will destroy the hand of the feller.","r":"Cut a thorn tree while it is young; grown hard, it will destroy the hand that cuts it.","n":"The verse itself doesn't mention enemies, and Parimelazhagar labels it allegory. Its logic of acting before a threat matures has no room in it for what a sapling might have become, or for whether it is really a thorn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:880":{"book":"kural","num":880,"t":["உயிர்ப்ப உளரல்லர் மன்ற செயிர்ப்பவர்","செம்மல் சிதைக்கலா தார்"],"p":"Those who do not destroy the pride of those who hate (them) will certainly not exist even to breathe.","r":"Those who don't break the pride of those hostile to them are, surely, not alive even to breathe.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds that this is about one who could do it and neglects it out of contempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their enemies will grow and destroy them, so they won't live even long enough to draw breath. He allows that it may be the enemy's breath that kills.","அவர் உயிர்த்த துணையானே தாம் இறப்பர் எனினும் அமையும்."],["manakkudavar","It is the enemy's breath that kills, and the lesson is not to take on the strong as enemies at all.","இது பகை கொள்ளுங்கால் வலியாரோடு பகைகோடலாகா தென்றது."],["kalaignar","They can't be said to live merely because they are breathing.",""]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:881":{"book":"kural","num":881,"t":["நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா தமர்நீரும்","இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்"],"p":"Shade and water are not pleasant, (if) they cause disease; so are the qualities of (one's) relations not agreeable, (if) they cause pain.","r":"Even shade and water are bad if they do harm, and the நீர் of one's own people too is bad, if it does harm.","n":"நீர் does double work, as water and as nature: தமர்நீர் is the nature of kin, sitting right next to actual water. Parimelazhagar's kin are specifically the relatives who could become inner enemies, and his illnesses from bad water are quite concrete. Manakkudavar reads நிழல்நீர் as one thing, the water in the shade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:882":{"book":"kural","num":882,"t":["வாள்போல பகைவரை அஞ்சற்க அஞ்சுக","கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Fear not foes (who say they would cut) like a sword; (but) fear the friendship of foes (who seemingly act) like relations.","r":"Don't fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the bond with enemies who are like kin.","n":"The verse says to fear the தொடர்பு and not the person. Parimelazhagar explains that the danger already lies in the existing bond, the one that has been made, and that open foes can be guarded against because they are known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:883":{"book":"kural","num":883,"t":["உட்பகை அஞ்சித்தற் காக்க உலைவிடத்து","மட்பகையின் மாணத் தெறும்"],"p":"Fear internal enmity and guard yourself; (if not) it will destroy (you) in an evil hour, as surely as the tool which cuts the potter's clay.","r":"Fear the enemy within and guard yourself; when you falter, it will cut surely, like the potter's cutter.","n":"A மட்பகை is what the potter uses to take a pot off the wheel. Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's point is that the cut is clean and unnoticed. உலைவிடத்து adds patience, as this enemy doesn't need to attack until you are already down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:884":{"book":"kural","num":884,"t":["மனமாணா உட்பகை தோன்றின் இனமாணா","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"The secret enmity of a person whose mind in unreformed will lead to many evils causing disaffection among (one's) relations.","r":"If an inner enmity of unreformed mind appears, it brings many ills of a circle that has gone bad.","n":"The damage is secondary: it spoils the other relationships. Parimelazhagar's analysis is that the enemy works from within to turn others, the king's trust then fails too, and the whole thing compounds. Kalaignar places the mind that won't reform in the person himself, not in another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:885":{"book":"kural","num":885,"t":["உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை தோன்றின் இறல்முறையான்","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If there appears internal hatred in a (king's) family; it will lead to many a fatal crime.","r":"If an inner enmity appears that has the form of kinship, it brings many ills that have the form of death.","n":"The verse's two lines balance on முறையான், so the closer the tie, the closer to death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is about a king: an outwardly related enemy who may kill him, aid outside foes, and erode his ministers.","புறப்பகைத் துணையாய் நின்றே அது தோன்றாமல் கோறல் முதலிய வஞ்சனை செய்தலும்"],["kalaignar","It is about enmity among close relatives in general, who all then suffer.",""]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:886":{"book":"kural","num":886,"t":["ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பொன்றாமை ஒன்றல் அரிது"],"p":"If hatred arises among (one's) own people, it will be hardly possible (for one) to escape death.","r":"If not-being-one arises among those who are one, it is ever hard to reach not-dying.","n":"The wordplay runs through three lines: ஒன்றாமை, ஒன்றியார், ஒன்றல். Oneness is what is broken and also what is needed. Parimelazhagar's எஞ்ஞான்றும் means even when one is at the peak of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:887":{"book":"kural","num":887,"t":["செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"Never indeed will a family subject to internal hatred unite (really) though it may present an apparent union like that of a casket and its lid.","r":"Though joined like a casket and its lid, a family that has inner enmity is not joined.","n":"The image shows a perfect fit with a seam all the same. The lid and the box remain two objects.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The members look united but are not, and will split when an outside enemy comes. He notes other readings with the enmity itself as what doesn't join.","உட்பகையான் மனம் வேறுபட்டமையின், புறப்பகை பெற்றுழி வீற்றுவீற்றாவர் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The enmity and the family are what fit together, and the lid moves aside for the one who comes to take what's inside, as the insider assists the thief.","செப்பகத் துப் பொருள் வாங்குவார்க்கு மூடி துணையாய் அகன்று நிற்குமதுபோல அகன்று நிற்பரென்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:888":{"book":"kural","num":888,"t":["அரம்பொருத பொன்போலத் தேயும் உரம்பொருது","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"A family subject to internal hatred will wear out and lose its strength like iron that has been filed away.","r":"As பொன் that is rubbed by a file wears away, a family with inner enmity wears down its strength by rubbing against itself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொன் is iron, the hard metal worn down by a file, and the insider seems to help while slowly separating people.","அரத்தாற் பொரப்பட்ட இரும்பு போல அதனால் பொரப்பட்டு வலி தேயும்."],["manakkudavar","பொன் is gold, which is polished as it is filed. So inner enmity seems to beautify while it wastes strength.","இஃது உட்பகை அழகு செய்வது போலப் பலத்தைக் கெடுக்கு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:889":{"book":"kural","num":889,"t":["எட்பக வன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்","உட்பகை உள்ளதாங் கேடு"],"p":"Although internal hatred be as small as the fragment of the sesamum (seed), still does destruction dwell in it.","r":"Though only as small as a split sesame seed, inner enmity has ruin within it.","n":"உள்ளதாம் lets the word play with உட்பகை: the ruin is inside what is inside. Parimelazhagar notes the smallness is relative to a king's greatness, and that the large ruin stays contained until its hour comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:890":{"book":"kural","num":890,"t":["உடம்பாடு இலாதவர் வாழ்க்கை குடங்கருள்","பாம்போடு உடனுறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Living with those who do not agree (with one) is like dwelling with a cobra (in the same) hut.","r":"Living with those not in accord with you is like living with a snake inside a hut.","n":"Pope's 'domestic life' narrows what the verse leaves open. It says only 'those not in accord', which could be anyone one lives with. Parimelazhagar's point is about smallness and familiarity: in a confined space with long proximity, the bite is a certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:891":{"book":"kural","num":891,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் இகழாமை போற்றுவார்","போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Not to disregard the power of those who can carry out (their wishes) is more important than all the watchfulness of those who guard (themselves against evil).","r":"Not slighting the power of those able to act is the highest of all safeguards for those who guard themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a large claim from this: fort, army, wealth and friends can all be undone by such people, so this safeguard outranks them. It closes a stretch of chapters that were all about those other safeguards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:892":{"book":"kural","num":892,"t":["பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகிற் பெரியாரால்","பேரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To behave without respect for the great (rulers) will make them do (us) irremediable evils.","r":"Going on without regard for the great will, through the great, bring ills that do not leave.","n":"The grammar makes the conduct itself the agent, and the great are only the instrument. Parimelazhagar says this was meant to show people do it to themselves. He has the king as the one at fault, whereas Manakkudavar has a person failing to honour sages.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:893":{"book":"kural","num":893,"t":["கெடல்வேண்டின் கேளாது செய்க அடல்வேண்டின்","ஆற்று பவர்கண் இழுக்கு"],"p":"If a person desires ruin, let him not listen to the righteous dictates of law, but commit crimes against those who are able to slay (other sovereigns).","r":"If you want to be ruined, don't listen, and commit a fault against those able to destroy.","n":"A piece of grim irony, phrased as an instruction. The commentators differ on what isn't listened to: Parimelazhagar means the treatises on policy, and Manakkudavar means the great themselves. His Purananuru citation makes such kings swifter than Death, who at least waits for the time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:894":{"book":"kural","num":894,"t":["கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்றால் ஆற்றுவார்க்கு","ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"The weak doing evil to the strong is like beckoning Yama to come (and destroy them).","r":"For those who can't do harm to those who can is like beckoning Death with the hand.","n":"The word is விளி, to call, so the gesture does it. Parimelazhagar adds that Death would have come anyway, so the action only brings it sooner, and that the hand gesture itself carries a scornful tone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:895":{"book":"kural","num":895,"t":["யாண்டுச்சென் றியாண்டு முளராகார் வெந்துப்பின்","வேந்து செறப்பட் டவர்"],"p":"Those who have incurred the wrath of a cruel and mighty potentate will not prosper wherever they may go.","r":"Wherever they go, they won't exist anywhere, those who've drawn the anger of a king of fierce might.","n":"Parimelazhagar is shrewdly political: other kings won't shelter the fugitive and may kill him to win favour or his goods. The verse's point is not the king's reach but what everyone around him will do because of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:896":{"book":"kural","num":896,"t":["எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார்","பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Though burnt by a fire (from a forest), one may perhaps live; (but) never will he live who has shown disrespect to the great (devotees).","r":"Even if burned by fire, there can be survival; those who go on offending the great will not survive.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The great are ascetics, whose wrath strikes at the life itself in an instant where fire only reaches the body first.","தவத்தாற் பெரியாரைப் பிழைத்து ஒழுகுவார், எவ்வாற்றானும் உயிருய்யார்."],["manakkudavar","The great are sages, with the same conclusion.","இது முனிவரைப் பிழைத்தலினால் வருங்குற்றம் கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","The great are simply those mighty in capacity, with no asceticism involved.",""]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:897":{"book":"kural","num":897,"t":["வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும் என்னாம்","தகைமாண்ட தக்கார் செறின்"],"p":"If a king incurs the wrath of the righteous great, what will become of his government with its splendid auxiliaries and (all) its untold wealth ?","r":"What are a fully appointed life and lofty wealth, if worthy ones of great stature are angered?","n":"Parimelazhagar says தகை means greatness that can curse and bless. Note his remark that செறின் implies they usually don't get angry, which holds for the verses after this. The verse's question doesn't state a mechanism, while he describes royal wealth burning up in a moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:898":{"book":"kural","num":898,"t":["குன்றன்னார் குன்ற மதிப்பின் குடியொடு","நின்றன்னார் மாய்வர் நிலத்து"],"p":"If (the) hill-like (devotees) resolve on destruction, those who seemed to be everlasting will be destroyed root and branch from the earth.","r":"If hill-like people are held low or themselves turn to bringing low, those who seemed to stand forever will vanish from earth with their line.","n":"நின்றன்னார், those who 'seem to stand', and குன்றன்னார், those who 'are like hills': the verse compares two kinds of standing. குன்ற is also a verbal pun on the hill.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்ற மதித்தல் is what the hill-like ascetics do, resolving on someone's ruin, and the rich die with their kin at once.","குன்றத்தை ஒக்கும் அருந்தவர் கெட நினைப்பாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","It is done to them: someone thinks little of those who are great as hills.","குன்ற மதித்தல் - அவமதித்தல்."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:899":{"book":"kural","num":899,"t":["ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின் இடைமுரிந்து","வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்"],"p":"If those of exalted vows burst in a rage, even (Indra) the king will suffer a sudden loss and be entirely ruined.","r":"If those of exalted resolve flare up, even the வேந்தன் breaks midway and loses his kingship.","n":"ஏந்திய கொள்கை and வேந்தன் carry all the weight in this verse, and the disagreement could hardly be wider, from gods and vows to politics.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The வேந்தன் is Indra, himself toppled by ascetics of great vows, with Nahusha's fall from Indra's seat through Agastya's anger in mind.","அவராற்றலான் இந்திரனும் இடைய தன் பதம் இழந்து கெடும்."],["manakkudavar","It is also Indra, who loses his realm, and this is a loss of wealth.","உயர்ந்த கோட்பாட்டையுடையார் வெகுள்வராயின், இந்திரனும் இடையிலே இற்றுத் தன்னரசு இழக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Those with firm principles rise up and a repressive regime collapses, with no god or vow in it.",""]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:900":{"book":"kural","num":900,"t":["இறந்தமைந்த சார்புடையர் ஆயினும் உய்யார்","சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்"],"p":"Though in possession of numerous auxiliaries, they will perish who are-exposed to the wrath of the noble whose penance is boundless.","r":"Even those with overwhelming support will not survive if those of supreme distinction are angered.","n":"சார்பு gets a precise list from Parimelazhagar: fort, army, wealth, friends, which are the chapters just passed through. His example is the burning of the three cities. The verse's own சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் doesn't specify asceticism or any cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:901":{"book":"kural","num":901,"t":["மனைவிழைவார் மாண்பயன் எய்தார் வினைவிழையார்","வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது"],"p":"Those who lust after their wives will not attain the excellence of virtue; and it is just this that is not desired by those who are bent on acquiring wealth.","r":"Men who fix their desire on the wife do not reach the fine fruit of life, and this same desire is what men intent on action refuse to want.","n":"The verse says only மனை, 'home', and மாண்பயன், 'fine fruit', without naming either. Parimelazhagar supplies virtue as the fruit and wealth-making as the action, so the claim that the household's own goal is lost is partly his construction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","kaamam","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:902":{"book":"kural","num":902,"t":["பேணாது பெண்விழைவான் ஆக்கம் பெரியதோர்","நாணாக நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The wealth of him who, regardless (of his manliness), devotes himself to his wife's feminine nature will cause great shame (to ali men) and to himself;","r":"The prosperity of one who desires his woman without caring for something else brings him shame, and a great shame in the world besides.","n":"பேணாது has no object in the verse, so what is being neglected is exactly what the commentators must decide.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","selvam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What he abandons is his manliness, and his wealth shames all men because she controls its use.","தன் ஆண்மையை விட்டு மனையாளது பெண்மையை விழைவான்"],["manakkudavar","What he fails to tend is virtue and wealth, with no appeal to manliness.","அறத்தினையும் பொருளினையும் பேணாது மனையாளைக் காதலிப் பானது செல்வம்"],["kalaignar","What he neglects is the principle he adopted, as he trails after a woman.",""]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:903":{"book":"kural","num":903,"t":["இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லாருள் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The frailty that stoops to a wife will always make (her husband) feel ashamed among the good.","r":"The unnatural condition of stooping before one's wife brings a man shame among good people at all times.","n":"The shame is social and comparative. It arises among நல்லார், not in the house.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fault is a husband's fear of any wife: fear is natural to her, so in him it is unnatural and leaves her uncontrolled.","அவள் தான் அஞ்சி ஒழுகுதல் இயல்பாகலின், அவளை அஞ்சுதல் இயல்பின்மையாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The fault is yielding to a wife of bad character instead of trying to correct her.",""]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:904":{"book":"kural","num":904,"t":["மனையாளை அஞ்சும் மறுமையி லாளன்","வினையாண்மை வீறெய்த லின்று"],"p":"The undertaking of one, who fears his wife and is therefore destitute of (bliss), will never be applauded.","r":"A man who fears his wife and has no share in the other life gets no distinction from his capacity for action.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","vinai","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுமை is the next world, whose fruit he forfeits because he cannot perform household virtue.","தன் மனையாளை அஞ்சி ஒழுகுகின்ற மறுமைப்பயன் இல்லாதானுக்கு"],["kalaignar","The man fears running a family and misses the fruit of the new life that marriage opens; no next world is involved.",""]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:905":{"book":"kural","num":905,"t":["இல்லாளை அஞ்சுவான் அஞ்சுமற் றெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லார்க்கு நல்ல செயல்"],"p":"He that fears his wife will always be afraid of doing good deeds (even) to the good.","r":"One who fears his wife will always be afraid to do good to good people.","n":"The harm named is the loss of generosity to outsiders, so fear in the house is treated as a public failure of giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He fears giving to guests, ascetics and elders, even out of wealth he earned himself.","தான் தேடிய பொருளே யாயினும் அதனால் நல்லார்க்கு நல்லன செய்தலை"],["kalaignar","He reverses the sense: a man anxious to do good without error heeds the wife who watches for error.",""]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:906":{"book":"kural","num":906,"t":["இமையாரின் வாழினும் பாடிலரே இல்லாள்","அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"They that fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives will be destitute of manliness though they may flourish like the Gods.","r":"Even if they live like the gods, those who fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives have no worth.","n":"The bamboo epithet is usually praise, but here it makes the fear ridiculous: Parimelazhagar takes it to show how slight the object of terror is, and contrasts it with warriors' shoulders. Kalaignar again limits the verse, to a wife lacking wisdom and character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:907":{"book":"kural","num":907,"t":["பெண்ணேவல் செய்தொழுகும் ஆண்மையின் நாணுடைப்","பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து"],"p":"Even shame faced womanhood is more to be esteemed than the shameless manhood that performs the behests of a wife.","r":"A woman who has shame holds more greatness than the manhood of one who goes about doing a woman's bidding.","n":"The verse uses gender as a rank and then overturns it: நாண் outranks manhood. Its force still depends on the assumption that being below a woman is a disgrace, so it cuts both ways.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","perumai","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:908":{"book":"kural","num":908,"t":["நட்டார் குறைமுடியார் நன்றாற்றார் நன்னுதலாள்","பெட் டாங்கு ஒழுகு பவர்"],"p":"Those who yield to the wishes of their wives will neither relieve the wants of (their) friends nor perform virtuous deeds.","r":"Those who act as the woman of the fine brow wishes neither meet their friends' needs nor do good.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: she can neither issue the needed orders on her own nor hand over the wealth, so the household's decision-making stalls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","sutram","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:909":{"book":"kural","num":909,"t":["அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்","பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்"],"p":"From those who obey the commands of their wives are to be expected neither deeds of virtue, nor those of wealth nor (even) those of pleasure.","r":"Deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits are absent in those who do a woman's bidding.","n":"பிறவினை is left open. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as pleasure, which gives the verse its sting: the man who surrenders for the sake of pleasure ends up losing pleasure too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:910":{"book":"kural","num":910,"t":["எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத் திடனுடையார்க்கு எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை இல்"],"p":"The foolishness that results from devotion to a wife will never be found in those who possess a reflecting mind and a prosperity (flowing) therefrom.","r":"Those whose minds hold deliberation and who have strength of heart will never have the foolishness that comes of attachment to a woman.","n":"The positive turns the chapter into a claim about mind, not women: the fault is called பேதைமை and is the man's own. Commentators split on இடன், which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth in kings and Manakkudavar as breadth of mind, while Kalaignar hears firmness of heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:911":{"book":"kural","num":911,"t":["அன்பின் விழையார் பொருள்விழையும் ஆய்தொடியார்","இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்"],"p":"The sweet words of elegant braceleted (prostitutes) who desire (a man) not from affection but from avarice, will cause sorrow.","r":"The sweet words of bangled women who desire a man for his wealth and not from love will bring him harm.","n":"The harm is located in the sweet speech, not the sex. The chapter opens as a chapter on deceptive language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:912":{"book":"kural","num":912,"t":["பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும் பண்பின் மகளிர்","நயன்தூக்கி நள்ளா விடல்"],"p":"One must ascertain the character of the ill-natured women who after ascertaining the wealth (of a man) speak (as if they were) good natured-ones, and avoid intercourse (with them).","r":"Weigh the conduct of faithless women who weigh the profit and then speak of their own virtue, and keep away from them.","n":"தூக்கி is repeated: she weighs the gain, so he must weigh her in return. Parimelazhagar calls this behaviour their சாதி தருமம், which is a commentator's social classification and not in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is their conduct, examined so that one can leave them.","ஒழுகலாற்றினை ஆராய்ந்தறிந்து அவரைப் பொருந்தாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is the pleasure they offer, which on examination is not worth approaching.","இன்பத்தை யாராய்ந்து பார்த்து, அவரைச் சாராதொழிக"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:913":{"book":"kural","num":913,"t":["பொருட்பெண்டிர் பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம் இருட்டறையில்","ஏதில் பிணந்தழீஇ அற்று"],"p":"The false embraces of wealth-loving women are like (hired men) embracing a strange corpse in a dark room.","r":"The false embrace of women of wealth is like embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room.","n":"A quick reader takes the client to be holding a corpse, as Pope's couplet does. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar make the woman the embracer: she is the hired bearer, and the unwanted client is the corpse she handles for pay. On that reading the disgust is hers, which complicates the chapter's usual stance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image describes her inner state: outwardly embracing, inwardly recoiling for the sake of money, like a paid corpse-bearer.","அகத்தால் அருவராநின்றும் பொருள் நோக்கிப் புறத்தால் தழுவுவர்"],["kalaignar","She embraces someone for money and acts out false love.",""]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:914":{"book":"kural","num":914,"t":["பொருட்பொருளார் புன்னலந் தோயார் அருட்பொருள்","ஆயும் அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"The wise who seek the wealth of grace will not desire the base favours of those who regard wealth (and not pleasure) as (their) riches.","r":"Those whose understanding weighs the wealth of grace will not plunge into the base charms of women whose wealth is only wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam","varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருட்பொருள் is wealth joined to grace: the wise still pursue wealth, but examine how to use it graciously.","அருளொடு கூடிய பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து செய்யும் அறிவினையுடையார்"],["manakkudavar","அருட்பொருள் treats grace itself as the wealth one seeks.","அருளைப் பொருளாக ஆராயும் அறிவுடையார்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:915":{"book":"kural","num":915,"t":["பொதுநலத்தார் புன்னலம் தோயார் மதிநலத்தின்","மாண்ட அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose knowledge is made excellent by their (natural) sense will not covet the trffling delights of those whose favours are common (to all).","r":"Those whose knowledge has been refined by the goodness of their intellect will not plunge into the base charms of those whose goodness belongs to everyone.","n":"பொதுநலம், 'common goodness', is a pointed phrase, since நலம் elsewhere means personal excellence. Parimelazhagar explains மதிநலம் as clarity earned in former births; the verse names no births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:916":{"book":"kural","num":916,"t":["தந்நலம் பாரிப்பார் தோயார் தகைசெருக்கிப்","புன்னலம் பாரிப்பார் தோள்"],"p":"Those who would spread (the fame of) their own goodness will not desire the shoulders of those,who rejoice in their accomplishments and bestow their despicable favours (on all who pay).","r":"Those who would spread their own goodness will not touch the shoulders of women who, proud of their charm, spread a base goodness.","n":"The same verb பாரிப்பார் is used for both parties: each is spreading a நலம், and the verse sets reputation against availability as two kinds of publicity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:917":{"book":"kural","num":917,"t":["நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர் தோய்வார் பிறநெஞ்சிற்","பேணிப் புணர்பவர் தோள்"],"p":"Those who are destitute of a perfectly (reformed) mind will covet the shoulders of those who embrace (them) while their hearts covet other things.","r":"Those with no firmness of heart touch the shoulders of women who join with them while tending other things in their hearts.","n":"A divided heart on one side is paired with an unguarded heart on the other. The verse's பிற is unspecified; the commentators fill it with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:918":{"book":"kural","num":918,"t":["ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு அணங்கென்ப","மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு"],"p":"The wise say that to such as are destitute of discerning sense the embraces of faithless women are (as ruinous as those of) the celestail female.","r":"For those lacking discerning understanding, the embrace of deceiving women is said to be an அணங்கு.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அணங்கு is a divine woman who takes life through desire, so the embrace is sweet and then fatal.","காமநெறியான் உயிர் கொள்ளும் தெய்வமகள்."],["manakkudavar","அணங்கு simply means affliction, with no divine being.","வருத்தமாமென்று சொல்லுவர்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:919":{"book":"kural","num":919,"t":["வரைவிலா மாணிழையார் மென்தோள் புரையிலாப்","பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு"],"p":"The delicate shoulders of prostitutes with excellent jewels are a hell into which are plunged the ignorant base.","r":"The soft shoulders of women of fine jewels who draw no line are the mire in which base men with no worth sink.","n":"வரைவு, 'limit', gives the chapter its title. Parimelazhagar says it means no line between high and low clients, and insists பூரியர் here means low in understanding, not in caste. அளறு means hell and mire at once, and softness of shoulder fits sinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:920":{"book":"kural","num":920,"t":["இருமனப் பெண்டிரும் கள்ளும் கவறும்","திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Treacherous women, liquor, and gambling are the associates of such as have forsaken by Fortune.","r":"Double-minded women, liquor and dice are the company of those whom Fortune has cast off.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These companions are signs by which one can tell that the goddess has already abandoned a man.","திரு நீக்கிப் பட்டமை இக்குறிகளான் அறியப்படும்"],["kalaignar","Consorting with them is what causes life's distinction to depart.",""]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:921":{"book":"kural","num":921,"t":["உட்கப் படாஅர் ஒளியிழப்பர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Those who always thirst after drink will neither inspire fear (in others) nor retain the light (of their fame).","r":"Those who go on loving liquor are never held in awe, and they lose their lustre.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads this as a king's loss of enemies' fear, while Manakkudavar has ordinary esteem from others, so the same verse can be statecraft or general ethics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:922":{"book":"kural","num":922,"t":["உண்ணற்க கள்ளை உணில்உண்க சான்றோரான்","எண்ணப் படவேண்டா தார்"],"p":"Let no liquor be drunk; if it is desired, let it be drunk by those who care not for esteem of the great.","r":"Do not drink; if anyone does, let it be those who don't wish to be counted by the worthy.","n":"The permission is a trap. It sounds like a concession but actually states the price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:923":{"book":"kural","num":923,"t":["ஈன்றாள் முகத்தேயும் இன்னாதால் என்மற்றுச்","சான்றோர் முகத்துக் களி"],"p":"Intoxication is painful even in the presence of (one's) mother; what will it not then be in that of the wise ?","r":"Drunken joy is painful even in the sight of the woman who bore you; what then in the sight of the worthy?","n":"The argument runs from the most forgiving audience to the least, so the mother works as a limit case. களி, the word for drunken joy, is otherwise a word of delight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:924":{"book":"kural","num":924,"t":["நாண்என்னும் நல்லாள் புறங்கொடுக்கும் கள்ளென்னும்","பேணாப் பெருங்குற்றத் தார்க்கு"],"p":"The fair maid of modesty will turn her back on those who are guilty of the great and abominable crime of drunkenness.","r":"Shame, the good woman, turns her back on those with the great and repugnant fault called liquor.","n":"புறங்கொடுக்கும் may carry a battlefield sense of turning one's back in flight; that is my suggestion, not a commentator's. Parimelazhagar explains the feminine personification as following a Sanskrit convention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:925":{"book":"kural","num":925,"t":["கையறி யாமை உடைத்தே பொருள்கொடுத்து","மெய்யறி யாமை கொளல்"],"p":"To give money and purchase unconsciousness is the result of one's ignorance of (one's own actions).","r":"To give up wealth and take self-oblivion in return must come from கையறியாமை.","n":"The irony is commercial: one pays good money to buy ignorance.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கையறியாமை is helpless not-knowing what to do, itself the fruit of former deeds.","அவன் பழவினைப் பயனாய செய்வதறியாமையைத் தனக்குக் காரணமாக உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","கையறியாமை is simply not seeing what benefit results.","பயன் அறியாமை யுடைத்து"],["kalaignar","It is indescribable stupidity, with no deed or fruit involved.",""]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:926":{"book":"kural","num":926,"t":["துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நஞ்சுண்பார் கள்ளுண் பவர்"],"p":"They that sleep resemble the deed; (likewise) they that drink are no other than poison-eaters.","r":"Those asleep are no different from the dead, and those who drink are always eating poison.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is an analogy. Sleepers differ from the dead yet lack awareness just as they do; drinkers differ from poison-eaters yet lack awareness permanently. He rejects a rival crosswise pairing.","கள்ளுண்பார் நஞ்சுண்பாரின் வேறாதல் உடையரேனும், எக்காலத்தும் அறிவின்மையான் வேறு எனப்பட்டார்"],["kalaignar","Drinkers and poison-eaters are the same, and therefore the drinker's sleep is the same as death.",""]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:927":{"book":"kural","num":927,"t":["உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கள்ளொற்றிக் கண்சாய் பவர்"],"p":"Those who always intoxicate themselves by a private (indulgence in) drink; will have their secrets detected and laughed at by their fellow-townsmen.","r":"Those who always seek out liquor and sink with drooping eyes are seen through and laughed at by their own town.","n":"ஒற்றி occurs twice: he resorts to drink in hiding, and the town spies out what is within. Parimelazhagar says it is secret drinking and Manakkudavar seeking out where liquor is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:928":{"book":"kural","num":928,"t":["களித்தறியேன் என்பது கைவிடுக நெஞ்சத்து","ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்"],"p":"Let (the drunkard) give up saying \"I have never drunk\"; (for) the moment (he drinks) he will simply betray his former attempt to conceal.","r":"Give up saying you have never been drunk, because what is hidden in the heart grows on that very spot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The speaker is a secret drinker claiming sobriety when not drinking; as soon as he drinks, the hidden fault comes out larger.","மறைந்துண்டு வைத்து யான் கள்ளுண்டறியேன் என்று உண்ணாத பொழுது தம் ஒழுக்கங் கூறுதலையொழிக"],["manakkudavar","The speaker claims drink does not intoxicate him; drink itself makes the tongue slip and reveal the hidden things of the mind.","கள்ளுண்டால் களித்தறியே னென்பதனைக் கைவிடுக"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:929":{"book":"kural","num":929,"t":["களித்தானைக் காரணம் காட்டுதல் கீழ்நீர்க்","குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று"],"p":"Reasoning with a drunkard is like going under water with a torch in search of a drowned man.","r":"To offer reasons to the drunk is like reaching with fire for one who has plunged beneath the water.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துரீஇ is searching with a lamp: as light doesn't enter water, so reason doesn't enter his mind.","நீருள் மூழ்கினான் ஒருவனைப் பிறனொருவன் விளக்கினல் நாடுதலை யொக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","துரீஇ is burning him with fire: a submerged man can't be touched by it.","நீரின் கீழே முழுகினானைத் தீயினாற் சுட்டது போலும்"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:930":{"book":"kural","num":930,"t":["கள்ளுண்ணாப் போழ்திற் களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்","உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு"],"p":"When (a drunkard) who is sober sees one who is not, it looks as if he remembered not the evil effects of his (own) drink.","r":"When sober, and seeing another man drunk, doesn't he reflect on the lapse of his own drinking?","n":"After 929's closed door the chapter leaves one open: self-recognition. Parimelazhagar names the logic as inference: what he sees in the other he could infer about himself. The question form suggests the chance is seldom taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:931":{"book":"kural","num":931,"t":["வேண்டற்க வென்றிடினும் சூதினை வென்றதூஉம்","தூண்டிற்பொன் மீன்விழுங்கி அற்று"],"p":"Though able to win, let not one desire gambling; (for) even what is won is like a fish swallowing the iron in fish-hook.","r":"Don't want gambling even if you can win, for what you win is like the metal of the hook swallowed by a fish.","n":"The verse attacks gambling at its strongest case, a win. Parimelazhagar adds that the winnings are a shackle keeping the gambler from leaving. பொன் here means the hook's metal, not gold, though the sound of 'gold' sharpens the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:932":{"book":"kural","num":932,"t":["ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்க்கும் உண்டாங்கொல்","நன்றெய்தி வாழ்வதோர் ஆறு"],"p":"Is there indeed a means of livelihood that can bestow happiness on gamblers who gain one and lose a hundred ?","r":"Is there any way of living well for gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred?","n":"ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் is sequence as well as ratio. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar hear the hope that follows the first gain, so the loss is driven by the win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:933":{"book":"kural","num":933,"t":["உருளாயம் ஓவாது கூறின் பொருளாயம்","போஒய்ப் புறமே படும்"],"p":"If the king is incessantly addicted to the rolling dice in the hope of gain, his wealth and the resources thereof will take their departure and fall into other's hands.","r":"If one keeps calling the falling of the dice, the gains of wealth will leave and pass elsewhere.","n":"The chiming of உருளாயம் and பொருளாயம் sets one income against another: the income of the dice swallows the income of wealth. Parimelazhagar makes the subject a king and the destination his enemies, which the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:934":{"book":"kural","num":934,"t":["சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும் சூதின்","வறுமை தருவதொன்று இல்"],"p":"There is nothing else that brings (us) poverty like gambling which causes many a misery and destroys (one's) reputation.","r":"There is nothing that gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and destroys distinction.","n":"சிறுமை elsewhere is smallness of character. The commentators read it here as miseries, but both senses are available, since gambling diminishes a person as well as afflicting him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:935":{"book":"kural","num":935,"t":["கவறும் கழகமும் கையும் தருக்கி","இவறியார் இல்லாகி யார்"],"p":"Penniless are those who by reason of their attachment would never forsake gambling, the gambling-place and the handling (of dice).","r":"Those who, in pride, clung to the dice, the gaming hall and the skilled hand have come to nothing.","n":"The past tense makes this an appeal to history. Parimelazhagar names the example: the Pandavas losing their kingdom and going to the forest. The verse names no one, so this epic reference is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:936":{"book":"kural","num":936,"t":["அகடாரார் அல்லல் உழப்பர்சூ தென்னும்","முகடியான் மூடப்பட் டார்"],"p":"Those who are swallowed by the goddess called \"gambling\" will never have their hunger satisfied, but suffer the pangs of hell in the next world.","r":"Those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling will not fill themselves and will suffer distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அகடு is the belly: they go hungry here, and suffer hell in the next world.","இம்மைக்கண் வயிறாரப் பெறார்; மறுமைக்கண் நிரயத் துன்பம் உழப்பர்."],["manakkudavar","அகடு is what lies within, the senses and the mind, which are never satisfied, and the covering hides their good qualities.","தமக்கு உள்ளாகிய இந்திரியங்களும் மனமும் இன்புற்று நிறை யப்பெறார்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:937":{"book":"kural","num":937,"t":["பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்","கழகத்துக் காலை புகின்"],"p":"To waste time at the place of gambling will destroy inherited wealth and goodness of character.","r":"If காலை enters the gaming hall, it will ruin long-held wealth and character.","n":"Whichever reading is taken, time is the stake, not money: ruin comes from what the hours would have been spent on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","காலை means the time set apart for virtue, wealth and pleasure, especially a king's.","அறம் பொருள் இன்பங்கட்கு அடைத்த காலம் அரசனுக்குச் சூதாடு களத்தின்கண் கழியுமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","காலை means the morning hour.","சூது கழகத்தின்கண்ணே காலைப்பொழுது புகுவானாயின்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:938":{"book":"kural","num":938,"t":["பொருள் கெடுத்துப் பொய்மேற் கொளீஇ அருள்கெடுத்து","அல்லல் உழப்பிக்கும் சூது"],"p":"Gambling destroys property, teaches falsehood, puts an end to benevolence, and brings in misery (here and hereafter).","r":"Gambling destroys wealth, makes one take up lying, destroys compassion and drives one through misery.","n":"The list is a causal ladder, and the moral rungs come after the financial one. Parimelazhagar assigns the means: loss robs, the winner's position breeds lies, and the rancour of the game kills compassion. In his reading, then, wins and losses alike do damage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:939":{"book":"kural","num":939,"t":["உடைசெல்வம் ஊண்ஒளி கல்விஎன்று ஐந்தும்","அடையாவாம் ஆயங் கொளின்"],"p":"The habit of gambling prevents the attainment of these five: clothing, wealth, food, fame and learning.","r":"Clothing, wealth, food, lustre and learning, these five don't come near one who takes to gaming.","n":"அடையாவாம் says that they won't arrive, while Pope's 'depart' adds that they were held already. The list runs from bare needs to learning, and puts survival and culture under one head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["soodhu","selvam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:940":{"book":"kural","num":940,"t":["இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல் துன்பம்","உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்"],"p":"As the gambler loves (his vice) the more he loses by it, so does the soul love (the body) the more it suffers through it.","r":"As a gambler loves the game more with every loss, so the living being's love grows with every suffering it goes through.","n":"An unexpected turn to the human condition: the chapter's closing verse uses gambling to diagnose attachment to life itself. Parimelazhagar insists the real target is the gambler, with the soul there to drive the point home, but the verse's grammar makes the soul the thing explained.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul's love is for the body, through which it suffers; as the one quitting gambling is admired, so is the soul that spurns the body.","சூதினை வெறுத்து ஒழிவானை யொக்கும் உடம்பினை வெறுத்தொழியும் உயிர்"],["kalaignar","The love is for life itself, which grows as the body suffers; no renunciation is implied.",""]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:941":{"book":"kural","num":941,"t":["மிகினும் குறையினும் நோய்செய்யும் நூலோர்","வளிமுதலா எண்ணிய மூன்று"],"p":"If (food and work are either) excessive or deficient, the three things enumerated by (medical) writers, flatulence, biliousness, and phlegm, will cause (one) disease.","r":"The three counted by the learned, starting with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short.","n":"Deficiency is as dangerous as excess. The verse defers to the நூலோர் and doesn't claim the theory as its own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What exceeds or falls short is food and activity, relative to one's constitution, and that disturbs the three.","உணவும் செயல்களும் ஒருவன் பகுதிக்கு ஒத்த அளவின் அன்றி"],["manakkudavar","What exceeds or falls short is food, sleep and sexual union.","உணவும் உறக்கமும் இணைவிழைச்சும் தன்னுடம்பின் அளவிற்கு மிகினும் குறையினும்"],["kalaignar","What exceeds or falls short is the three humours themselves.",""]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:942":{"book":"kural","num":942,"t":["மருந்தென வேண்டாவாம் யாக்கைக்கு அருந்தியது","அற்றது போற்றி உணின்"],"p":"No medicine is necessary for him who eats after assuring (himself) that what he has (already) eaten has been digested.","r":"No medicine is called for by the body if one eats only after attending to the digestion of what was eaten.","n":"போற்றி, 'with care', turns digestion into something to watch. Parimelazhagar lists bodily signs of it, such as lightness and clean belching. His remark on உணின் concedes that this is hard to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","marundhu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:943":{"book":"kural","num":943,"t":["அற்றால் அறவறிந்து உண்க அஃதுடம்பு","பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு"],"p":"If (one's food has been) digested let one eat with moderation; (for) that is the way to prolong the life of an embodied soul.","r":"When digestion is complete, eat knowing the measure: that is how one who has gained a body can carry it far.","n":"உடம்பு பெற்றான், 'one who has received a body', treats the body as a gift. Parimelazhagar pushes further: a human body is the only vehicle for this world, the next and liberation, so length of life serves those ends. The verse itself states no such purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:944":{"book":"kural","num":944,"t":["அற்றது அறிந்து கடைப்பிடித்து மாறல்ல","துய்க்க துவரப் பசித்து"],"p":"(First) assure yourself that your food has been digested and never fail to eat, when very hungry, whatever is not disagreeable (to you).","r":"Knowing the food is digested, hold to what is not incompatible and eat it after real hunger.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets out three kinds of incompatibility: with one's constitution, with the season and hour, and between foods. His example is honey and ghee in equal amounts turning to poison, which Manakkudavar shares.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:945":{"book":"kural","num":945,"t":["மாறுபாடு இல்லாத உண்டி மறுத்துண்ணின்","ஊறுபாடு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"There will be no disaster to one's life if on e eats with moderation, food that is not disagreeable.","r":"If one eats with மறுத்து food that has no incompatibility, life takes no injury.","n":"I'm unsure of Manakkudavar's sense. His gloss, with its example of ginger after jackfruit, doesn't obviously fit the verse, and I record it without claiming to understand it fully.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுத்து means refusing the amount the heart wants and stopping at what brings no disease.","தன் உள்ளம் வேண்டிய அளவினான் அன்றிப் பிணிவாரா அளவினால்"],["manakkudavar","He takes it as eating by setting aside the food, with incompatibility illustrated by a compensating food.","சுவையும் வீரியமும் மாறுபாடில்லாத உணவை நீக்கி யுண்ப னாயின்"]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:946":{"book":"kural","num":946,"t":["இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்கண் இன்பம்போல் நிற்கும்","கழிபேர் இரையான்கண் நோய்"],"p":"As pleasure dwells with him who eats moderately, so disease (dwells) with the glutton who eats voraciously.","r":"As joy stays with the one who eats knowing how to stop short, disease stays with the one who eats enormously.","n":"இரையான், 'feeder', is a word for animals' feed. Parimelazhagar notes that this likens the glutton to a beast. The contrast is inbam and noy, not health and disease: moderation yields pleasure, so restraint isn't presented as a sacrifice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:947":{"book":"kural","num":947,"t":["தீயள வன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்","நோயள வின்றிப் படும்"],"p":"He will be afflicted with numberless diseases, who eats immoderately, ignorant (of the rules of health).","r":"If one eats hugely, not discerning the measure of the fire within, disease will arise beyond all measure.","n":"அளவு occurs on both sides: disregard the measure of the fire, and disease loses its measure. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read தீ simply as hunger, while Parimelazhagar reads it as the belly's fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:948":{"book":"kural","num":948,"t":["நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி அதுதணிக்கும்","வாய்நாடி வாய்ப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"Let the physician enquire into the (nature of the) disease, its cause and its method of cure and treat it faithfully according to (medical rule).","r":"Investigate the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so as to get it right.","n":"நாடி recurs three times and sets the order: identify, trace, select, then act. Kalaignar adds that this applies to the diseases of society, which is his extension. Parimelazhagar lists surgery and cautery among the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:949":{"book":"kural","num":949,"t":["உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும்","கற்றான் கருதிச் செயல்"],"p":"The learned (physician) should ascertain the condition of his patient; the nature of his disease, and the season (of the year) and (then) proceed (with his treatment).","r":"The learned one should act having considered the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time.","n":"Learning alone isn't enough: the verse makes the patient's measure come first, so that treatment is individual. Parimelazhagar explicitly wants both the book's way and the physician's own discernment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:950":{"book":"kural","num":950,"t":["உற்றவன் தீர்ப்பான் மருந்துழைச் செல்வானென்று","அப்பால் நாற் கூற்றே மருந்து"],"p":"Medical science consists of four parts, viz., patient, physician, medicine and compounder; and each of these (again) contains four sub-divisions.","r":"The patient, the healer, the medicine and the one who attends: medicine has these four parts.","n":"The verse calls the whole arrangement மருந்து, so the patient is himself part of the medicine, not its recipient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழைச்செல்வான் is the compounder, and each of the four has four sub-qualities of its own.","நான்கு பகுதியையுடைய நான்கு திறத்தது"],["kalaignar","உழைச்செல்வான் is one who stands by to help, and treatment simply has four parts.",""]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:951":{"book":"kural","num":951,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாகச்","செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Consistency (of thought, word and deed) and fear (of sin) are conjointly natural only to the high-born.","r":"Only in those born to a good house do integrity and shame exist together, naturally.","n":"இயல்பாக and ஒருங்கு both limit the claim: it is about these qualities coming naturally and together, which leaves open whether others can acquire them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","naanam","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth decides: others can be taught these qualities but won't keep them for long.","பிறர்க்காயின் கற்பித்த வழியும் நெடிது நில்லா"],["kalaignar","Conduct defines birth: only those with these qualities can be regarded as high-born.",""]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:952":{"book":"kural","num":952,"t":["ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும் இம் மூன்றும்","இழுக்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்"],"p":"The high-born will never deviate from these three; good manners, truthfulness and modesty.","r":"Those of good birth don't slip in these three: conduct, truthfulness and shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps the three onto body, speech and mind, and adds that this comes without learning. Manakkudavar takes நாண் physically, as covering one's nakedness, while Kalaignar has honour instead and again makes these the test of who counts as high-born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","vaaimai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:953":{"book":"kural","num":953,"t":["நகைஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை நான்கும்","வகையென்ப வாய்மைக் குடிக்கு"],"p":"A cheerful countenance, liberality, pleasant words, and an unreviling disposition, these four are said to be the proper qualities of the truly high-born.","r":"A smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning: these four, they say, belong to a truly good lineage.","n":"None of the four is about ancestry, and all are ways of treating someone else. Parimelazhagar specifies the setting: when the destitute come, for everyone scorns those who have nothing. That turns etiquette into a test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","eegai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:954":{"book":"kural","num":954,"t":["அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்"],"p":"Though blessed with immense wealth, the noble will never do anything unbecoming.","r":"Even if they gain crores upon crores, the well-born do nothing that diminishes them.","n":"பெறினும் can mean holding wealth or being offered it. Parimelazhagar and Pope lean toward the first, and Kalaignar toward a bribe, which makes this a claim about being unbuyable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","selvam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:955":{"book":"kural","num":955,"t":["வழங்குவ துள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பழங்குடி","பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று"],"p":"Though their means fall off, those born in ancient families, will not lose their character (for liberality).","r":"Even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family doesn't part from its nature.","n":"The verse measures their poverty by what they can give, not by what they have. Parimelazhagar explains பழங்குடி with the Chera, Chola and Pandya, excellent since creation, which ties this to royal houses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:956":{"book":"kural","num":956,"t":["சலம்பற்றிச் சால்பில செய்யார்மா சற்ற","குலம்பற்றி வாழ்தும் என் பார்"],"p":"Those who seek to preserve the irreproachable honour of their families will not viciously do what is detrimental thereto.","r":"Those who say 'we will live holding to our spotless lineage' will not do unworthy things through deceit.","n":"என்பார் makes lineage a resolve: it applies to those who declare they will live by it. That moves lineage from something one has to something one takes on. Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:957":{"book":"kural","num":957,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்தார் கண்விளங்கும் குற்றம் விசும்பின்","மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து"],"p":"The defects of the noble will be observed as clearly as the dark spots in the moon.","r":"A fault in the well-born stands out high, like the spot on the moon in the sky.","n":"A cost of height: the fault is not larger, but more visible. Each part maps: lineage to sky, virtues to moon, fault to spot. Manakkudavar draws the practical moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:958":{"book":"kural","num":958,"t":["நலத்தின்கண் நாரின்மை தோன்றின் அவனைக்","குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப் படும்"],"p":"If one of a good family betrays want of affection, his descent from it will be called in question.","r":"If want of affection shows in one of a good house, people will have doubts about his lineage.","n":"The chapter's claim protects itself here: when a well-born man is cruel, the doubt falls on his birth and not on the rule that birth gives virtue. Reading conduct as evidence of birth also makes the doubt a slur on his parentage, which is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:959":{"book":"kural","num":959,"t":["நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும் காட்டும்","குலத்தில் பிறந்தார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"As the sprout indicates the nature of the soil, (so) the speech of the noble indicates (that of one's birth).","r":"A sprout shows what lies in the ground; the words from the mouth show the lineage of the one born in it.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the disguise: it looks as if the verse is teaching how to identify lineage, but it is really a command to speak sweetly. Note கால், a sprout: the evidence appears at the earliest moment. Kalaignar's version has the mature crop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:960":{"book":"kural","num":960,"t":["நலம்வேண்டின் நாணுடைமை வேண்டும் குலம் வேண்டின்","வேண்டுக யார்க்கும் பணிவு"],"p":"He who desires a good name must desire modesty; and he who desires (the continuance of) a family greatness must be submissive to all.","r":"If you want goodness, want shame; if you want lineage, want humility toward all.","n":"வேண்டின் treats lineage as something one can want and go after. This is the verse most at odds with 951, since a thing given by birth need not be wanted.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","யார்க்கும் means everyone who is due deference, such as priests, the worthy, ascetics and parents, shown by rising and going to meet them.","பணியப்படுவார் யாவர் மாட்டும் பணிதலை வேண்டுக"],["manakkudavar","யார்க்கும் means all people without limit.","யாவர்மாட்டும் தாழ்ந் தொழுகுதலை விரும்புக"]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:961":{"book":"kural","num":961,"t":["இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்","குன்ற வருப விடல்"],"p":"Actions that would degrade (one's) family should not be done; though they may be so important that not doing them would end in death.","r":"Even if they are so excellent as to be indispensable, give up things that would diminish you.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a deliberate polemic: he says it rejects a Sanskrit rule that one may do degrading things to survive. The verse names no rival.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The acts are ones whose omission would mean death, and they must still be refused.","'இறப்பவரும் வழி இளிவந்தன செய்தாயினும் உய்க' என்னும் வடநூல் முறையை மறுத்து"],["manakkudavar","What is given up is wealth and pleasure that would reduce one's nature, however great the amount.","இது பொருளும் இன்பமும் மிகினும், தன்மை குறைவன் செய்யற்க"],["kalaignar","They are obligatory actions, to be avoided if they lessen one's dignity; death is not raised.",""]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:962":{"book":"kural","num":962,"t":["சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யாரே சீரொடு","பேராண்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to maintain their) honour, will surely do nothing dishonourable, even for the sake of fame.","r":"Those who want renown and great manliness together won't do unworthy things even for renown.","n":"சீர் is used against itself: renown bought with unworthy acts is not renown. பேராண்மை is explained by Parimelazhagar as a firmness that doesn't falter anywhere, and he takes it here as honour, not prowess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:963":{"book":"kural","num":963,"t":["பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல் சிறிய","சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு"],"p":"In great prosperity humility is becoming; dignity, in great adversity.","r":"In abundance you must bow, and in slight and shrunken circumstances you must stand tall.","n":"The posture runs against the circumstances: humility when the world would permit pride, and height when the world would press one down. Humility and honour, often seen as rivals, are the same discipline at different times.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","panivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:964":{"book":"kural","num":964,"t":["தலையின் இழிந்த மயிரனையர் மாந்தர்","நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை"],"p":"They who have fallen from their (high) position are like the hair which has fallen from the head.","r":"People who have fallen from their position are like hair that has fallen from the head.","n":"The verse says மாந்தர், people generally, and Parimelazhagar narrows it to the well-born. Nothing about the hair changes when it falls, only its place, so worth is presented as depending on where it stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:965":{"book":"kural","num":965,"t":["குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர் குன்றுவ","குன்றி அனைய செயின்"],"p":"Even those who are exalted like a hill will be thought low, if they commit deeds that are debasing.","r":"Even those like a hill will sink, if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed.","n":"குன்று works four ways: hill, sinking, lowering, and the tiny red seed குன்றி. The sound itself demonstrates how little separates the great from the small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:966":{"book":"kural","num":966,"t":["புகழ்இன்றால் புத்தேள்நாட்டு உய்யாதால் என்மற்று","இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று நிலை"],"p":"Of what good is it (for the high-born) to go and stand in vain before those who revile him ? it only brings him loss of honour and exclusion from heaven.","r":"It brings no praise and doesn't carry one to the gods' land: why then go and stand behind those who scorn you?","n":"The argument is from utility, not pride: it weighs both possible rewards and finds neither.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Heaven is a real reward that this conduct fails to gain.","ஏனைப் புத்தேளுலகத்துச் செலுத்தாது"],["kalaignar","Heaven is non-existent, and the line asks rhetorically whether it could be obtained this way.",""]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:967":{"book":"kural","num":967,"t":["ஒட் டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின் அந்நிலையே","கெட்டான் எனப்படுதல் நன்று"],"p":"It is better for a man to be said of him that he died in his usual state than that he eked out his life by following those who disgraced him.","r":"Rather than live by following those who don't accept you, it is better to be called ruined in that very condition.","n":"கெட்டான் means ruined and can mean dead. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar take death, while Manakkudavar stays with the word. Parimelazhagar says this replies to someone who would still go for wealth and survival. The verse's சொல் is about what is said of a person, so reputation is the stake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:968":{"book":"kural","num":968,"t":["மருந்தோமற்று ஊன்ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை பெருந்தகைமை","பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து"],"p":"For the high-born to keep their body in life when their honour is gone will certainly not prove a remedy against death.","r":"When greatness comes to be stripped of its dignity, is a life nursing the flesh a medicine?","n":"The commentators complete the question: medicine against dying. Since death will come anyway, living on buys nothing. ஊன் reduces the body to meat, which Parimelazhagar notes is what a person is after honour goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","marundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:969":{"book":"kural","num":969,"t":["மயிர்நீப்பின் வாழாக் கவரிமா அன்னார்","உயிர்நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்"],"p":"Those who give up (their) life when (their) honour is at stake are like the yark which kills itself at the loss of (even one of) its hairs.","r":"Those who are like the yak that won't live once its hair is gone give up their lives when honour's moment comes.","n":"The image is presented as nature, which Parimelazhagar says shows that this is inborn in them. Kalaignar distances himself with என்பார்கள், 'people say'. The verse doesn't say whether the death is by one's own hand; Kalaignar's next gloss, on 970, does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் வரின் means the point at which honour is won by giving up life.","உயிர் நீக்கத்தான் மானம் எய்தும் எல்லை வரின்"],["manakkudavar","மானம் வரின் means when honour is about to be destroyed.","மான முடையார், மானம் அழியவரின் உயிர்விடுவர்"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:970":{"book":"kural","num":970,"t":["இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்","ஒளிதொழுது ஏத்தும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will (always) praise and adore the fame of the honourable who would rather die than suffer indignity.","r":"The world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who won't live when disgrace comes.","n":"The reward is entirely posthumous and in others' mouths, and ஒளி is the fame that outlasts the person. Parimelazhagar adds heaven by citing Purananuru, which reverses 966's point only in appearance, since there heaven was what degrading service failed to earn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:971":{"book":"kural","num":971,"t":["ஒளிஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை இளிஒருவற்கு","அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்"],"p":"One's light is the abundance of one's courage; one's darkness is the desire to live destitute of such (a state of mind.)","r":"One's radiance is the wealth of aspiration, and one's disgrace is saying 'we will live without it'.","n":"ஒளி and இளி close the previous chapter's last verse (970) and open this one, so the two chapters are stitched together by these words. வெறுக்கை means wealth, so aspiration is presented as a holding. Disgrace lies in the stated resolve to live without it, not in any failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:972":{"book":"kural","num":972,"t":["பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் சிறப்பொவ்வா","செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமை யான்"],"p":"All human beings agree as regards their birth but differ as regards their characteristics, because of the different qualities of their actions.","r":"Birth is alike for all living beings, and distinction is unequal because of the difference in the work they do.","n":"The verse says எல்லா உயிர்க்கும், wider than humanity. செய்தொழில் could be occupation or conduct, and the whole verse's social force depends on that word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth is alike only in being embodied through karma; the work that tests greatness itself differs by varna and individual.","தொழிற்பாகுபாடுகள் வருணந்தோறும் யாக்கைதோறும் வேறுபடுதலின்"],["manakkudavar","Greatness simply can't be recognized by lineage.","பெருமையாவது குலத்தினால் அறியப்படா தென்பதூஉம்"],["kalaignar","All are equal by birth, and difference appears only in the skill shown at one's work.",""]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:973":{"book":"kural","num":973,"t":["மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர் கீழிருந்தும்","கீழல்லார் கீழல் லவர்"],"p":"Though (raised) above, the base cannot become great; though (brought) low, the great cannot become base.","r":"Those who aren't high are not high even when placed high, and those who aren't low are not low even when placed low.","n":"A single word, மேல் or கீழ், names both position and worth, and the verse pries them apart. Pope's couplet takes the position as birth, but Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the seat that wealth gives, a high couch against bare ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:974":{"book":"kural","num":974,"t":["ஒருமை மகளிரே போலப் பெருமையும்","தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின் உண்டு"],"p":"Even greatness, like a woman's chastity, belongs only to him who guards himself.","r":"Greatness too, like single-minded women, exists only if one holds on to oneself.","n":"The model of masculine greatness is a woman. Conduct expected of wives becomes the standard for great men, a striking move given chapter 91. Parimelazhagar explains self-holding as restraint of mind, speech and body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:975":{"book":"kural","num":975,"t":["பெருமை யுடையவர் ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றின்","அருமை உடைய செயல்"],"p":"(Though reduced) the great will be able to perform, in the proper way, deeds difficult (for others to do).","r":"Those with greatness will accomplish in the right way what is hard to do.","n":"ஆற்றின் adds a limit: the rare deed must be done in the right way. Parimelazhagar adds 'even when poor', which the verse doesn't say. Kalaignar reverses the direction, so that those who do such things are the ones who become great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:976":{"book":"kural","num":976,"t":["சிறியார் உணர்ச்சியுள் இல்லை பெரியாரைப்","பேணிக் கொள் வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு"],"p":"It is never in the nature of the base to seek the society of the great and partake of their nature.","r":"The aim of cherishing and taking on the great does not exist in the minds of the small.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They don't seek to serve the great and acquire their nature, since those proud of birth, wealth or learning think they already have it.","பெரியாரை வழிபட்டு அவர் இயல்பினையாம் கோடும்"],["manakkudavar","They don't seek to take the great as support, so having great men as allies is itself greatness.","இது பெரியாரைப் பெறுதலும் பெருமையென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:977":{"book":"kural","num":977,"t":["இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்றாம் சிறப்புந்தான்","சீரல் லவர்கண் படின்"],"p":"Even nobility of birth, wealth and learning, if in (the possession of) the base, will (only) produce everincreasing pride.","r":"Even distinction, should it fall to the unworthy, turns entirely into acts of excess.","n":"Distinction is morally neutral and amplifies whoever has it. Manakkudavar takes சிறப்பு as wealth, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the surplus of birth, wealth and learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:978":{"book":"kural","num":978,"t":["பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை சிறுமை","அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து"],"p":"The great will always humble himself; but the mean will exalt himself in self-admiration.","r":"Greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself.","n":"பணியும் and அணியும் rhyme, so what looks like opposite motions sound alike. Parimelazhagar reports another reading in which this is a wonder at the way of the world: the high go low and the low go high.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:979":{"book":"kural","num":979,"t":["பெருமை பெருமிதம் இன்மை சிறுமை","பெருமிதம் ஊர்ந்து விடல்"],"p":"Freedom from conceit is (the nature of true) greatness; (while) obstinacy therein is (that of) meanness.","r":"Greatness is the absence of pride, and smallness is riding on pride and being done with it.","n":"பெருமிதம், 'pride', shares its root with பெருமை, so the verse defines greatness by the absence of what sounds like its own excess. Manakkudavar marks this as where the chapter turns from negative points to defining greatness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:980":{"book":"kural","num":980,"t":["அற்றம் மறைக்கும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","குற்றமே கூறி விடும்"],"p":"The great hide the faults of others; the base only divulge them.","r":"Greatness hides another's weakness, and smallness only tells their faults.","n":"அற்றம் is literally a vulnerable exposure, so this hides a nakedness as much as a fault. Silence, not achievement, finishes the portrait of greatness that opened with high aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:981":{"book":"kural","num":981,"t":["கடன்என்ப நல்லவை எல்லாம் கடன்அறிந்து","சான்றாண்மை மேற்கொள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"It is said that those who are conscious of their duty and behave with a perfect goodness will regard as natural all that is good.","r":"For those who know what is fitting and take on fullness of character, all that is good is said to be கடன்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் means what is natural: all good qualities are inherent, otherwise the word சான்றாண்மை wouldn't apply.","நல்லனவாய குணங்கள் எல்லாம் இயல்பாயிருக்கும்"],["kalaignar","கடன் means duty, and all efforts made to fulfil it count as good duties.",""]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:982":{"book":"kural","num":982,"t":["குணநலம் சான்றோர் நலனே பிறநலம்","எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The only delight of the perfect is that of their goodness; all other (sensual) delights are not to be included among any (true) delights.","r":"The goodness of the worthy is the goodness of character; any other goodness isn't counted among goodnesses at all.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் as bodily good looks, and says it doesn't even appear in the lists of goods such as birth and learning. Kalaignar also has அழகு. The verse's own பிற is open, and a stronger reading dismisses everything other than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:983":{"book":"kural","num":983,"t":["அன்புநாண் ஒப்புரவு கண்ணோட்டம் வாய்மையொடு","ஐந்துசால் ஊன்றிய தூண்"],"p":"Affection, fear (of sin), benevolence, favour and truthfulness; these are the five pillars on which perfect goodness rests.","r":"Love, shame, fellow-feeling, kindly regard and truthfulness: these five are the pillars planted to bear worth.","n":"Four of the five are about how one regards other people. Parimelazhagar stretches two of them: love extends beyond kin, and truth holds everywhere. Their absence makes worth fall, not merely diminish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","naanam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:984":{"book":"kural","num":984,"t":["கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை பிறர்தீமை","சொல்லா நலத்தது சால்பு"],"p":"Penance consists in the goodness that kills not , and perfection in the goodness that tells not others' faults.","r":"Austerity has the goodness of not killing, and worth has the goodness of not telling others' evil.","n":"The verse sets speech at the level of the ascetic's non-violence. Talking of another's fault is thus the moral equivalent of what killing is for a tapas. Kalaignar makes the silence cover harm done by others, which leaves open how it fits with exposing wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:985":{"book":"kural","num":985,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல் அதுசான்றோர்","மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை"],"p":"Stooping (to inferiors) is the strength of those who can accomplish (an undertaking); and that is the weapon with which the great avert their foes.","r":"The strength of those who accomplish is to bow; that is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes around.","n":"படை and மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் use the language of war, and the pun is that a foe (மாற்றார்) gets changed (மாற்றும்), not destroyed. Parimelazhagar makes the bowing concrete and strategic: stooping to gather allies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:986":{"book":"kural","num":986,"t":["சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின் தோல்வி","துலையல்லார் கண்ணும் கொளல்"],"p":"The touch-stone of perfection is to receive a defeat even at the hands of one's inferiors.","r":"If asked what the touchstone of worth is, it is accepting defeat even from those who aren't one's equal.","n":"Parimelazhagar specifies that one accepts it while able to win. Without that, the verse describes mere loss. The defeat then lifts one above the other person, an inversion of the usual sense of victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:987":{"book":"kural","num":987,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்","என்ன பயத்ததோ சால்பு"],"p":"Of what avail is perfect goodness if it cannot do pleasing things even to those who have pained (it) ?","r":"If you don't do what is sweet even to those who have done you harm, what has worth brought?","n":"A demanding test is framed as a question about use: worth is judged by what it produces in the hardest case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:988":{"book":"kural","num":988,"t":["இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும்","திண்மை உண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Poverty is no disgrace to one who abounds in good qualities.","r":"Poverty is no disgrace for one who has the firmness called worth.","n":"திண்மை makes worth a hardness able to bear pressure. The verse doesn't call poverty good, only that it doesn't carry the disgrace it otherwise would.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","selvam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:989":{"book":"kural","num":989,"t":["ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம்பெயரார் சான்றாண்மைக்கு","ஆழி எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"Those who are said to be the shore of the sea of perfection will never change, though ages may change.","r":"Those called the ஆழி of worth won't shift even when the age shifts.","n":"ஊழி and ஆழி chime. The cosmic dissolution is a scale reference for constancy.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆழி means shore: worth is the sea, and they are what holds it in when other seas overflow.","சால்புடைமையாகிய கடற்குக் கரை என்று சொல்லப்படுவார்"],["kalaignar","ஆழி means sea: they stand as the sea that doesn't change when all seas are overturned.",""]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:990":{"book":"kural","num":990,"t":["சான்றவர் சான்றாண்மை குன்றின் இருநிலந்தான்","தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை"],"p":"If there is a defect in the character of the perfect, (even) the great world cannot bear (its) burden.","r":"If the worthy decline in worth, the great earth itself will not bear its load.","n":"Parimelazhagar says both events are impossible, so this is a thought experiment showing their dependence. He also reports a reading in which the worthy are the earth's helpers in bearing. Kalaignar reads பொறை as the world's forbearance: it won't patiently tolerate their decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:991":{"book":"kural","num":991,"t":["எண்பதத்தால் எய்தல் எளிதென்ப யார்மாட்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"If one is easy of access to all, it will be easy for one to obtain the virtue called goodness.","r":"They say the practice called courtesy is easy to reach through being accessible to everyone.","n":"வழக்கு makes courtesy a public custom, not an inner trait. Being available turns out to be the whole method, and யார்மாட்டும் gives it no exception. Parimelazhagar calls it something that wins over the whole world, which adds a strategic note.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:992":{"book":"kural","num":992,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் இவ்விரண்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"Affectionateness and birth in a good family, these two constitute what is called a proper behaviour to all.","r":"Love and being born in a seasoned family: these two are the way called courtesy.","n":"This puts birth among courtesy's sources. Parimelazhagar softens it, saying குடிப்பிறத்தல் here means the conduct of those so born, and neither factor suffices alone. Kalaignar goes further, asking only that one have the marks of such birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:993":{"book":"kural","num":993,"t":["உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால் வெறுத்தக்க","பண்பொத்தல் ஒப்பதாம் ஒப்பு"],"p":"Resemblance of bodies is no resemblance of souls; true resemblance is the resemblance of qualities that attract.","r":"Likeness of limbs isn't likeness among people; true likeness is likeness of settled character.","n":"வெறுத்தக்க usually means hateful, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as செறியத்தக்க, 'to be drawn close', which is what makes the line work. Pope's gloss, 'qualities that attract', follows him. I can't confirm this sense from the verse alone.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is likeness to good people: bodily likeness doesn't amount to that, because the body doesn't last and character does.","செறியத்தகாத உடம்பால் ஒத்தல் ஒருவனுக்கு நன்மக்களோடு ஒப்பாகாமையின்"],["kalaignar","The issue is membership of humankind: those without good character shouldn't be counted as people.",""]],"ch":"k:100"},"k:994":{"book":"kural","num":994,"t":["யனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த பயனுடையார்","பண்புபா ராட்டும் உலகு"],"p":"The world applauds the character of those whose usefulness results from their equity and charity.","r":"The world honours the character of those whose useful lives come from doing good with fairness.","n":"The world honours the பண்பு, not the deeds or their outcome, which makes deeds evidence of character. Parimelazhagar says that it is useful to oneself and to others alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:995":{"book":"kural","num":995,"t":["நகையுள்ளும் இன்னா திகழ்ச்சி பகையுள்ளும்","பண்புள பாடறிவார் மாட்டு"],"p":"Reproach is painful to one even in sport; those (therefore) who know the nature of others exhibit (pleasing) qualities even when they are hated.","r":"Scorn hurts even in jest, so those who know how others feel have courtesy even in enmity.","n":"Parimelazhagar insists that the two clauses are one argument, and criticizes others who read them apart: knowing that scorn hurts is the reason for courtesy. பாடு here is what another person undergoes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:996":{"book":"kural","num":996,"t":["பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம் அதுஇன்றேல்","மண்புக்கு மாய்வது மன்"],"p":"The (way of the) world subsists by contact with the good; if not, it would bury itself in the earth and perish.","r":"The world exists by resting upon those of courtesy; otherwise it would enter the earth and perish.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes உலகம் here the way of the world, not the planet. Kalaignar makes it a should: worldly practices ought to depend on such people. A claim about reality becomes a recommendation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:997":{"book":"kural","num":997,"t":["அரம்போலும் கூர்மைய ரேனும் மரம்போல்வர்","மக்கட்பண்பு இல்லா தவர்"],"p":"He who is destitute of (true) human qualities (only) resembles a tree, though he may possess the sharpness of a file.","r":"Even though sharp as a file, those without human courtesy are like trees.","n":"The chime of அரம் and மரம் has the point in it. Parimelazhagar says the file wears down what it touches without wearing itself, and the tree has only one sense. Intelligence used without courtesy isn't merely inadequate, it is destructive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:998":{"book":"kural","num":998,"t":["நண்பாற்றார் ஆகி நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்","பண்பாற்றார் ஆதல் கடை"],"p":"It is wrong (for the wise) not to exhibit (good) qualities even towards those who bearing no friendship (for them) do only what is hateful.","r":"Failing in courtesy, even toward those who act without friendship and without kindness, is lowness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is reflexive: respond in kind and you become like them. The insult harms the character of the one who returns it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:999":{"book":"kural","num":999,"t":["நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு மாயிரு ஞாலம்","பகலும்பாற் பட்டன்று இருள்"],"p":"To those who cannot rejoice, the wide world is buried darkness even in (broad) day light.","r":"For those who aren't capable of laughing, the great wide world lies in darkness even in the daytime.","n":"Laughter is treated as a skill (வல்லர்), and the lack of it as blindness. Parimelazhagar makes it the joy of mingling, and says such people don't understand the world because they can't mix with it. He reports a variant reading too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1000":{"book":"kural","num":1000,"t":["பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் நன்பால்","கலந்தீமை யால்திரிந் தற்று"],"p":"The great wealth obtained by one who has no goodness will perish like pure milk spoilt by the impurity of the vessel.","r":"Great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel.","n":"Wealth is good milk here, and the fault is the holder's alone. பெற்ற, 'obtained', suggests to Parimelazhagar that he couldn't have created it himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1001":{"book":"kural","num":1001,"t":["வைத்தான்வாய் சான்ற பெரும்பொருள் அஃதுண்ணான்","செத்தான் செயக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"He who does not enjoy the immense riches he has heaped up in his house, is (to be reckoned as) dead, (for) there is nothing achieved (by him).","r":"One who has stored up great wealth to the brim and doesn't eat it, is dead: there is nothing left for him to do.","n":"உண்ணான், 'doesn't eat', treats wealth as food. Parimelazhagar widens it to the five senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The man who does not use it is himself dead, and has nothing further to do with it.","அதனை நுகரானாயின், செத்தான்; அவன் பின்பு செய்யக்கிடந்தது யாது மில்லை."],["parimelazhagar","An alternative he allows: once dead, one has no right over it, so enjoy it as soon as it comes.","வையாது பெற்றபொழுதே நுகர்க"],["kalaignar","It is about a literal death: what good is the wealth to one who dies without enjoying it?",""]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1002":{"book":"kural","num":1002,"t":["பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"He who knows that wealth yields every pleasure and yet is so blind as to lead miserly life will be born a demon.","r":"Through the delusion of clinging without giving, thinking all things come by wealth, comes a மாணாப் பிறப்பு.","n":"The delusion isn't the belief that wealth achieves everything, a point the Kural makes elsewhere: it lies in clinging while holding that belief.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means rebirth as a ghost that hungers amid food, matching those who watched others' hunger amid plenty.","நிறைதலில்லாத பேய்ப்பிறப்பு உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It describes a person: he becomes a low being with no distinction, with no rebirth involved.",""]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1003":{"book":"kural","num":1003,"t":["ஈட்டம் இவறி இசைவேண்டா ஆடவர்","தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"A burden to the earth are men bent on the acquisition of riches and not (true) fame.","r":"The appearance on earth of men who cling to accumulation and don't want renown is a burden to the land.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies the renown with virtue achieved by giving, so the choice isn't between wealth and vanity. Manakkudavar draws the harshest conclusion: it would be better if they hadn't been born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1004":{"book":"kural","num":1004,"t":["எச்சமென்று என்எண்ணுங் கொல்லோ ஒருவரால்","நச்சப் படாஅ தவன்"],"p":"What will the miser who is not liked (by any one) regard as his own (in the world to come) ?","r":"What does one wanted by no one imagine will remain behind him?","n":"The issue is what survives a person, and the question implies nothing does, since fame only rests on givers. Pope's gloss moves this to 'the world to come' while his couplet does not. The Tamil says எச்சம், what is left behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1005":{"book":"kural","num":1005,"t":["கொடுப்பதூஉம் துய்ப்பதூஉம் இல்லார்க்கு அடுக்கிய","கோடியுண் டாயினும் இல்"],"p":"Those who neither give (to others) nor enjoy (their property) are (truly) destitute, though possessing immense riches.","r":"Those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, even with crores upon crores.","n":"An ownership paradox: possession is defined by use. Parimelazhagar says giving is named first because virtue outranks pleasure. Kalaignar fuses the two: those not happy in giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1006":{"book":"kural","num":1006,"t":["ஏதம் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தான்துவ்வான் தக்கார்க்கொன்று","ஈதல் இயல்பிலா தான்"],"p":"He who enjoys not (his riches) nor relieves the wants of the worthy is a disease to his wealth.","r":"One who doesn't use it himself and doesn't naturally give any of it to the deserving is a misfortune with great wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is a disease of the wealth, which could have done both things.","இரண்டும் செய்தற்கு இடனுடைத்தாய செல்வத்திற்கு ஒரு நோய்."],["manakkudavar","The great wealth obtained by such a person is itself faulty.","பெற்ற பெருஞ் செல்வம் குற்றமுடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1007":{"book":"kural","num":1007,"t":["அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான் செல்வம் மிகநலம்","பெற்றாள் தமியள்மூத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who never bestows anything on the destitute is like a woman of beauty growing old without a husband.","r":"The wealth of one who gives nothing to the destitute is like a woman of exceeding beauty growing old alone.","n":"The simile mourns what is wasted, and treats a woman's worth as realized through a husband. Parimelazhagar's gloss deepens it: she is unmarried for want of anyone to give her away, a giver being missing in both halves, and she herself loses something too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1008":{"book":"kural","num":1008,"t":["நச்சப் படாதவன் செல்வம் நடுவூருள்","நச்சு மரம்பழுத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who is disliked (by all) is like the fruit-bearing of the etty tree in the midst of a town.","r":"The wealth of one who isn't wanted is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of town.","n":"நச்சப்படாதவன், 'unwanted', and நச்சு, 'poison', chime, and it is what makes the verse work. Being close by is what makes it worse: Parimelazhagar says he is near the needy and still gives nothing. Manakkudavar extends it, saying any who eat out of desire will die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1009":{"book":"kural","num":1009,"t":["அன்பொரீஇத் தற்செற்று அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய","ஒண்பொருள் கொள்வார் பிறர்"],"p":"Strangers will inherit the riches that have been acquired without regard for friendship, comfort and charity.","r":"Others will take the bright wealth amassed by giving up love, by self-denial and by ignoring virtue.","n":"The list gives three prices paid, and the last word takes away the prize. Parimelazhagar says பிறர் excludes even his own line. Kalaignar makes ignoring virtue into unethical earning, which is a different charge from not giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1010":{"book":"kural","num":1010,"t":["சீருடைச் செல்வர் சிறுதுனி மாரி","வறங்கூர்ந் தனையது உடைத்து"],"p":"The short-lived poverty of those who are noble and rich is like the clouds becoming poor (for a while).","r":"The சிறுதுனி of the renowned rich has the nature of rain-clouds going dry.","n":"As a closing verse it gives the positive pole of the chapter's whole contrast: the wealth that does give.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சிறுதுனி is brief poverty: those who gave everything soon recover, like a cloud. He rejects the other reading as not suited to this chapter.","நிற்கும் காலம் சிறிதாய வறுமை"],["manakkudavar","சிறுதுனி is slight displeasure that stops their giving, and the world suffers as with failed rain.","சீருடைய செல்வர் சிறுதுனியால் ஈயாதொழிதல், மாரி பெய் யாமை மிக்காற் போலும்"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1011":{"book":"kural","num":1011,"t":["கருமத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணுந் திருநுதல்","நல்லவர் நாணுப் பிற"],"p":"True modesty is the fear of (evil) deeds; all other modesty is (simply) the bashfulness of virtuous maids.","r":"Shame is being ashamed through one's actions; anything other is the shame of women of the bright brow.","n":"The verse separates moral shame from bodily modesty and assigns the second to women. Whichever women are meant, the distinction is gendered.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The women are honoured family women, and he rejects explicitly the reading that they are courtesans.","அழகிய நுதலினையுடைய குலமகளிர் நாண்கள்."],["manakkudavar","They are courtesans, good only in their brow, so that any other shame is merely like theirs.","அழகிய நுதலினாலே நல்லாராகிய கணிகையர் நாணத்தோ டொக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1012":{"book":"kural","num":1012,"t":["ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல","நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Food, clothing and the like are common to all men but modesty is peculiar to the good.","r":"Food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame.","n":"The verse seems to separate humans from other life, but the commentators draw the line within humanity. Parimelazhagar contrasts all people with good people, and Manakkudavar contrasts base people with leaders. Parimelazhagar lists the rest of what is shared as sleep, fear and desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1013":{"book":"kural","num":1013,"t":["ஊனைக் குறித்த உயிரெல்லாம் நாண்என்னும்","நன்மை குறித்தது சால்பு"],"p":"As the body is the abode of the spirit, so the excellence of modesty is the abode of perfection.","r":"As all lives are fixed on flesh, worth is fixed on the good called shame.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஊன் is the body: worth can't have effect without shame, as a soul can't without a body. He reports ஊண் as a variant.","உயிர் உடம்பொடு கூடியல்லது பயனெய்தாத வாறுபோலச் சால்பு நாணோடு கூடியல்லது பயன் எய்தாது"],["manakkudavar","The word is food: living things want food above all, and worth wants shame above all.","பலவகை உயிரும் மேற்கூறிய எல்லாவற்றினும் உண்டியைக் கருதிற்று"]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1014":{"book":"kural","num":1014,"t":["அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு அஃதின்றேல்","பிணிஅன்றோ பீடு நடை"],"p":"Is not the modesty ornament of the noble ? Without it, their haughtiness would be a pain (to others).","r":"Isn't shame an ornament to the worthy? Without it, isn't a proud walk a disease?","n":"அணி and பிணி rhyme. Parimelazhagar makes this a disease for those who have to watch it. A walk, in other words, is public property and judged by the watcher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1015":{"book":"kural","num":1015,"t":["பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார் நாணுக்கு","உறைபதி என்னும் உலகு"],"p":"The world regards as the abode of modesty him who fear his own and other's guilt.","r":"The world calls those who feel shame for others' disgrace as for their own the dwelling of shame.","n":"This enlarges shame beyond guilt: one feels it for what one did not do. Moral concern here can't be reduced to self-regard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1016":{"book":"kural","num":1016,"t":["நாண்வேலி கொள்ளாது மன்னோ வியன்ஞாலம்","பேணலர் மேலா யவர்"],"p":"The great make modesty their barrier (of defence) and not the wide world.","r":"The high don't care for the wide world unless they have shame for a fence.","n":"The verse can mean they would take shame rather than the world, or that they won't want the world without it; Parimelazhagar holds the first and reports the second. Pope's couplet follows the second. Kalaignar has shame as the best of protections, with no choice involved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1017":{"book":"kural","num":1017,"t":["நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால்","நாண்துறவார் நாணாள் பவர்"],"p":"The modest would rather lose their life for the sake of modesty than lose modesty for the sake of life.","r":"Those ruled by shame will give up life for shame, and won't give up shame for life.","n":"The syntax folds on itself like the choice it describes. நாணாள்பவர் can be 'those who wield shame', as a commander does, and not those who are ruled by it. Kalaignar glosses the object as மானம், showing how closely the two virtues run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1018":{"book":"kural","num":1018,"t":["பிறர்நாணத் தக்கது தான்நாணா னாயின்","அறம்நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"Virtue is likely to forsake him who shamelessly does what others are ashamed of.","r":"If one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, it's a fault that virtue itself would be ashamed of.","n":"நாண recurs and virtue becomes a person who blushes. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read this as virtue leaving such a man, since virtue doesn't approach the shameless, so shame is a precondition of virtue and not one of its parts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1019":{"book":"kural","num":1019,"t":["குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின் நலஞ்சுடும்","நாணின்மை நின்றக் கடை"],"p":"Want of manners injures one's family; but want of modesty injures one's character.","r":"A lapse in principle burns one's lineage, and lasting shamelessness burns every good thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the ranking out: destroying shame is worse than destroying conduct, which is significant given the rank chapter 14 gives conduct. Fire (சுடும்) is used for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","ozhukkam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1020":{"book":"kural","num":1020,"t":["நாண்அகத் தில்லார் இயக்கம் மரப்பாவை","நாணால் உயிர்மருட்டி அற்று"],"p":"The actions of those who are without modesty at heart are like those of puppet moved by a string.","r":"The movements of those with no shame within are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string.","n":"The pun is on நாண், which means both shame and string. The puppet has the one thing the man lacks, as Parimelazhagar notes, in a neat inversion. It leaves a man without shame to be moved from outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1021":{"book":"kural","num":1021,"t":["கருமம் செயஒருவன் கைதூவேன் என்னும்","பெருமையின் பீடுடையது இல்"],"p":"There is no higher greatness than that of one saying. I will not cease in my effort (to raise my family).","r":"No greatness has more dignity than that of one who says 'I will not stay my hand from the work'.","n":"The verse says only கருமம், and family comes from the chapter's title. Parimelazhagar rejects two rival readings, one's own work and others' work, the second because serving others isn't leadership. Greatness here sits in the declaration (என்னும்), before any result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1022":{"book":"kural","num":1022,"t":["ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும் எனஇரண்டின்","நீள்வினையால் நீளும் குடி"],"p":"One's family is raised by untiring perseverance in both effort and wise contrivances.","r":"By long-continued work in the two things called effort and mature knowledge, the family is extended.","n":"நீள்வினையால் நீளும் is a chime, in which the long labour lengthens the line.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடி is one's own family, and it rises.","ஒருவன் குடி உயரும்."],["kalaignar","குடி is the people attached to him, whose pride rises.",""]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1023":{"book":"kural","num":1023,"t":["குடிசெய்வல் என்னும் ஒருவற்குத் தெய்வம்","மடிதற்றுத் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"The Deity will clothe itself and appear before him who resolves on raising his family.","r":"For one who says 'I will raise my people', தெய்வம் tucks up its garment and goes ahead on its own.","n":"The image of tightened dress is homely and physical: the divine as a worker who gets ready to help.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The deity is said to lead, and leading is an act ascribed to destiny (நியதி).","முன் நடப்பார் செயல் நியதிமேல் ஏற்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","What is called divine action is the power of nature.",""]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1024":{"book":"kural","num":1024,"t":["சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும் தம்குடியைத்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are prompt in their efforts (to better their family) need no deliberation, such efforts will of themselves succee d.","r":"For those who work without delay for their people, the end arrives by itself, without deliberation.","n":"The agent is unnamed. Parimelazhagar supplies the deity of 1023, while Manakkudavar supplies the man's good deeds (நல்வினை), and Kalaignar leaves success to come by itself. Each fills the gap to suit his view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1025":{"book":"kural","num":1025,"t":["குற்றம் இலனாய்க் குடிசெய்து வாழ்வானைச்","சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு"],"p":"People will eagerly seek the friendship of the prosperous soul who has raised his family without foul means.","r":"The world gathers around one who lives faultlessly raising his people, as kin.","n":"The chime of சுற்றம் and சுற்றும் turns the noun into what kin do. Parimelazhagar's motive for this is unsentimental: people come for their own benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1026":{"book":"kural","num":1026,"t":["நல்லாண்மை என்பது ஒருவற்குத் தான்பிறந்த","இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்"],"p":"A man's true manliness consists in making himself the head and benefactor of his family.","r":"What's called good manliness is making oneself capable of ruling the house one was born to.","n":"நல்லாண்மை and இல்லாண்மை chime. Parimelazhagar says the adjective நல் is there to exclude the warrior's manliness. Kalaignar reverses the sense: one who rules well brings pride to his birth-family, so it isn't about ruling the family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1027":{"book":"kural","num":1027,"t":["அமரகத்து வன்கண்ணர் போலத் தமரகத்தும்","ஆற்றுவார் மேற்றே பொறை"],"p":"Like heroes in the battle-field, the burden (of protection etc.) is borne by those who are the most efficient in a family.","r":"As with the stout-hearted in battle, the burden among kin falls on the capable.","n":"The commentators stress that many enter the field and many are born in a family: it isn't that others are forbidden, but that it falls to whoever can carry it. A question of ability, not of seniority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1028":{"book":"kural","num":1028,"t":["குடிசெய்வார்க் கில்லை பருவம் மடிசெய்து","மானங் கருதக் கெடும்"],"p":"As a family suffers by (one's) indolence and false dignity there is to be so season (good or bad) to those who strive to raise their family.","r":"Those who raise their people have no season; if they idle and think about மானம், it will be ruined.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","maanam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் is the pride of asking 'why must I alone suffer while the rest are comfortable?'","'இக்குடியிலுள்ளார் யாவரும் இன்பமுற இக்காலத்துத் துன்பமுறுவேன் யானோ?'"],["kalaignar","It is being shaken by degrading attacks made on oneself.",""]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1029":{"book":"kural","num":1029,"t":["இடும்பைக்கே கொள்கலம் கொல்லோ குடும்பத்தைக்","குற்ற மறைப்பான் உடம்பு"],"p":"Is it only to suffering that his body is exposed who undertakes to preserve his family from evil ?","r":"Is the body of one who covers his household against fault only a vessel for suffering?","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a pointed remark and not self-pity: the man accepts it, since the family's rise is his reward.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறைத்தல் is shielding, as with a shield against spears: he protects them from the three kinds of suffering.","மூவகைத் துன்பமும் உறற்பாலதாய தன் குடியை அவை உறாமற்காக்க முயல்வானது உடம்பு"],["manakkudavar","மறைத்தல் is concealing the failings found among kin.","சுற்றத்தார் மாட்டு உளதாகிய குறையை மறைக்கக் கருதுவான் உடம்பு"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1030":{"book":"kural","num":1030,"t":["இடுக்கண்கால் கொன்றிட வீழும் அடுத்தூன்றும்","நல்லாள் இலாத குடி"],"p":"If there are none to prop up and maintain a family (in distress), it will fall at the stroke of the axe of misfortune.","r":"When trouble cuts it at the foot, a family with no good person to prop it will fall.","n":"A tree image is implied: Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply the axe. They take நல்லாள் as a good man (ஆண்மகன்), while Kalaignar has anyone with the ability. The same word names a personified good woman in 924 and 1040; here it divides as நல் ஆள், 'good person', which doesn't itself specify a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1031":{"book":"kural","num":1031,"t":["சுழன்றும்ஏர்ப் பின்னது உலகம் அதனால்","உழந்தும் உழவே தலை"],"p":"Agriculture, though laborious, is the most excellent (form of labour); for people, though they go about (in search of various employments), have at last to resort to the farmer.","r":"Though it whirls about, the world follows the plough; therefore, though toilsome, farming is foremost.","n":"The verse concedes the toil and doesn't romanticize it (உழந்தும்). Its case is a structural one: all other paths end up at the same place. சுழன்றும் may also suggest the world turning like a wheel behind the plough, and Kalaignar's version keeps this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1032":{"book":"kural","num":1032,"t":["உழுவார் உலகத்தார்க்கு ஆணிஅஃ தாற்றாது","எழுவாரை எல்லாம் பொறுத்து"],"p":"Agriculturists are (as it were) the linch-pin of the world for they support all other workers who cannot till the soil.","r":"Those who plough are the linchpin of the world's people, for they carry all those who can't do it and rise to other things.","n":"Parimelazhagar says உழுவார் extends to those who have others plough for them, citing a parallel, which brings landowners into the honour. The verse doesn't say so. He reports a variant, தொழுவாரே, in which those unable to plough cringe to their patrons, which anticipates 1033.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1033":{"book":"kural","num":1033,"t":["உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்மற் றெல்லாம்","தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல் பவர்"],"p":"They alone live who live by agriculture; all others lead a cringing, dependent life.","r":"Only those who live by eating from their own ploughing truly live; all others bow to eat, and walk behind.","n":"Parimelazhagar's வாழ்வார் are those who belong to themselves (தமக்குரியராய்): the verse is about independence as much as food.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rest bow to other patrons and live on what they are given.","பிறரைத் தொழுது, அவர் சில கொடுப்பத் தம் உயிரோம்பி"],["kalaignar","The rest bow to the farmers themselves.",""]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1034":{"book":"kural","num":1034,"t":["பலகுடை நீழலும் தங்குடைக்கீழ்க் காண்பர்","அலகுடை நீழ லவர்"],"p":"Patriotic farmers desire to bring all other states under the control of their own king.","r":"Those with the shade of grain-ears will see many umbrellas' shade under their own umbrella.","n":"The royal umbrella is set against the grain's shade. Manakkudavar notices a pun: an 'umbrella-less shade'. The verse claims that farming, not armies, underwrites conquest.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The umbrella is that of their king, whose conquests their grain makes possible.","பலவேந்தர் - குடை நிழலதாய மண் முழுதினையும் தம் வேந்தர்குடைக்கீழே காண்பர்."],["kalaignar","Farmers themselves have the strength to bring all governments under them.",""]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1035":{"book":"kural","num":1035,"t":["இரவார் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று ஈவர் கரவாது","கைசெய்தூண் மாலை யவர்"],"p":"Those whose nature is to live by manual labour will never beg but give something to those who beg.","r":"Those whose way is to eat what their hands produce don't ask, and give to those who ask without hiding.","n":"The hand is the verse's key: it labours and it gives, and it never stretches out. Parimelazhagar restricts கைசெய் to ploughing, while Kalaignar makes it any self-earned work. The verse anticipates the chapters on begging, whose word கரவாது it uses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1036":{"book":"kural","num":1036,"t":["உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின் இல்லை விழைவதூஉம்","விட்டேம்என் பார்க்கும் நிலை"],"p":"If the farmer's hands are slackened, even the ascetic state will fail.","r":"If farmers fold their hands, even those who say they have given up desire won't be able to stay in that state.","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', carries a slight edge. Parimelazhagar extends the verse to all of this world's aims, the next world's and liberation. He and another reading differ on whether விழைவது is food or desire in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1037":{"book":"kural","num":1037,"t":["தொடிப்புழுதி கஃசா உணக்கின் பிடித்தெருவும்","வேண்டாது சாலப் படும்"],"p":"If the land is dried so as to reduce one ounce of earth to a quarter, it will grow plentifully even without a handful of manure.","r":"If you dry the soil so that a measure of earth falls to a quarter, it will grow abundantly without even a handful of manure.","n":"A rare place where the Kural gives technical advice, with exact weights. Kalaignar reads the reduction as coming from repeated ploughing, while Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar have sun-drying. The moral of attention before input is left unstated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1038":{"book":"kural","num":1038,"t":["ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல் கட்டபின்","நீரினும் நன்றதன் காப்பு"],"p":"Manuring is better than ploughing; after weeding, watching is better than watering (it).","r":"Manuring is better than ploughing, and after weeding, guarding is better than watering.","n":"A ladder of care in which each stage beats the previous one, and watchfulness comes out above water, the thing plants most obviously need. Parimelazhagar sets out the sequence of five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1039":{"book":"kural","num":1039,"t":["செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின் நிலம்புலந்து","இல்லாளின் ஊடி விடும்"],"p":"If the owner does not (personally) attend to his cultivation, his land will behave like an angry wife and yield him no pleasure.","r":"If the owner stays away and doesn't go, the land will resent and sulk like a wife.","n":"ஊடி is a term from love poetry, used here in farm advice. கிழவன், 'owner', also means husband, a double meaning that makes the comparison work. Parimelazhagar says that the owner must go himself and not send others, which is in some tension with his widening of உழுவார் in 1032.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1040":{"book":"kural","num":1040,"t":["இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரைக் காணின்","நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்"],"p":"The maiden, Earth, will laug h at the sight of those who plead poverty and lead an idle life.","r":"Seeing those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle, the good woman called Land laughs.","n":"The Earth doesn't scold but laughs. Parimelazhagar notes this is the bridge to the next chapter on poverty, and he has a variant இரப்பாரை, 'those who beg', which Manakkudavar uses, pointing to the later chapters too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1041":{"book":"kural","num":1041,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாதது யாதெனின் இன்மையின்","இன்மையே இன்னா தது"],"p":"There is nothing that afflicts (one) like poverty.","r":"If asked what is more painful than poverty, what's more painful than poverty is poverty.","n":"The sentence tries to find a comparison and fails. As Parimelazhagar says, if it has no equal, nothing can exceed it. Tautology is the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1042":{"book":"kural","num":1042,"t":["இன்மை எனவொரு பாவி மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"When cruel poverty comes on, it deprives one of both the present and future (bliss).","r":"The sinner called poverty comes, with this world and the next both lost.","n":"பாவி personifies it as an evil actor, although Manakkudavar reads a poor person who has the sin. The next world is lost, in his reason, because one can't afford charity, so poverty denies access to virtue itself. That cuts against the assumption that merit is equally open to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1043":{"book":"kural","num":1043,"t":["தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும் தொகையாக","நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை"],"p":"Hankering poverty destroys at once the greatness of (one's) ancient descent and (the dignity of one's) speech.","r":"The desire called poverty destroys at once one's ancient descent and தோல்.","n":"The verse identifies poverty with desire, நசை. Parimelazhagar explains that where there's no craving there's no poverty, which is a remarkable reframing of an economic state as a mental one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தோல் means graceful speech, as Tolkappiyam uses it, and he rejects reading it as the body.","பழைய குடிவரவினையும் அதற்கு ஏற்ற சொல்லினையும் ஒருங்கே கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","தோல் means beauty of form.","குடிப்பிறப்பினையும் வடிவழகினையும் ஒருங்கு கெடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","தோல் means fame, and the desire is a greed that poverty causes.",""]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1044":{"book":"kural","num":1044,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த","சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்"],"p":"Even in those of high birth, poverty will produce the fault of uttering mean words.","r":"Even in the well-born, poverty brings the lapse from which degrading words are born.","n":"Parimelazhagar says what the words are: 'you must give to us'. He describes the lapse precisely: at the height of suffering, they forget their birth for a moment. The uncontrolled word is இளிவந்த, degrading, and that is the verdict of chapter 97 on such a person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1045":{"book":"kural","num":1045,"t":["நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத்","துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்"],"p":"The misery of poverty brings in its train many (more) miseries.","r":"Within the suffering called poverty, many sufferings arrive and settle.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists a day's humiliations in order: going to the rich man's door, seeing him, being refused, accepting what he gives, and working out how to use it. One of the most vivid passages in his commentary, it is his own and not the verse's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1046":{"book":"kural","num":1046,"t":["நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The words of the poor are profitless, though they may be sound in thought and clear in expression.","r":"Even if they speak of good matters, fully understood, the words and meaning of the poor go slack.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a social mechanism and not a cognitive one: nobody listens, in case they come to feel obliged to help. So the meaning fails not in the speaker but in what the listeners are afraid of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1047":{"book":"kural","num":1047,"t":["அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு ஈன்றதா யானும்","பிறன்போல நோக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He that is reduced to absolute poverty will be regarded as a stranger even by his own mother.","r":"One in poverty unconnected with virtue is looked at as a stranger, even by the mother who bore him.","n":"The mother is again the limit case, as in 923. Parimelazhagar's reason is unsentimental: she fears that he would be taking and not giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறஞ்சாரா describes the poverty: it doesn't come from virtue nor yield virtue.","காரண காரியங்களுள் ஒன்றானும் இயையாமை."],["kalaignar","It describes the man, who has left the path of virtue because of poverty.",""]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1048":{"book":"kural","num":1048,"t":["இன்றும் வருவது கொல்லோ நெருநலும்","கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு"],"p":"Is the poverty that almost killed me yesterday, to meet me today too ?","r":"Will it come today too, the poverty that as good as killed me yesterday?","n":"The verse switches to first-person speech. Parimelazhagar imagines the speaker: someone who with great difficulty filled his belly yesterday. In போலும் he survived what felt like death, which means he will face it again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1049":{"book":"kural","num":1049,"t":["நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும் நிரப்பினுள்","யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது"],"p":"One may sleep in the midst of fire; but by no means in the midst of poverty.","r":"One can even sleep in the middle of fire, but in the middle of poverty, there is no way of closing the eyes.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains the sleep in fire as a possibility through mantra and medicine, which rationalizes what looks like hyperbole. He hears the same speaker as in 1048.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1050":{"book":"kural","num":1050,"t":["துப்புர வில்லார் துவரத் துறவாமை","உப்பிற்கும் காடிக்கும் கூற்று"],"p":"The destitute poor, who do not renounce their bodies, only consume their neighbour's salt and water.","r":"If those without means don't fully renounce, they are death to salt and to gruel.","n":"The mockery falls on tiny things, salt and sour gruel: the poor person's mere continuing destroys these and nothing more.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To renounce fully is to give up the body itself, for honour's sake; those left by kin have done everything else.","நின்ற தம் உடம்பினையும் துறத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","It is to give up attachment to wealth, which brings pleasure to the poor.","இது துறப்பாராயின், இன்ப முறலா மென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1051":{"book":"kural","num":1051,"t":["இரக்க இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின் கரப்பின்","அவர்பழி தம்பழி அன்று"],"p":"If you meet with those that may be begged of, you may beg; (but) if they withhold (their gift) it is their blame and not yours.","r":"Ask if you find those worth asking; if they withhold, the blame is theirs and not yours.","n":"The chapter starts by moving the moral burden from the asker to the withholder. Parimelazhagar says such givers understand before anything is said, and the blame sticks to them as a stain does on pure white cloth, because they're rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1052":{"book":"kural","num":1052,"t":["இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல் இரந்தவை","துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்"],"p":"Even begging may be pleasant, if what is begged for is obtained without grief (to him that begs).","r":"Even asking is pleasure to a person, if what is asked for comes without suffering.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering is the asker's: the courting, reading the giver's moods and being refused, which is spared if the giver anticipates.","அவையுறாமல் வருதலாவது, அவர் முன்னுணர்ந்து ஈயக்கோடல்."],["kalaignar","The suffering is that of both, giver and receiver.",""]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1053":{"book":"kural","num":1053,"t":["கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று","இரப்புமோ ரேஎர் உடைத்து"],"p":"There is even a beauty in standing before and begging of those who are liberal in their gifts and understand their duty (to beggars).","r":"Even standing and asking before those of unconcealing heart who know கடன் has a certain beauty.","n":"முன்நின்று is significant: for Parimelazhagar merely standing is enough, and it's the absence of spoken words that keeps the grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் is honour, and it is the asker's: he reads 963 to explain why such a giver can sense a poor person's dignity.","கரத்தல் இல்லாத நெஞ்சினையுடைய மானம் அறிவார்"],["manakkudavar","கடன் is social obligation, the duty of helping.","கரப்பிலாத நெஞ்சினை யுடைய ஒப்புரவறிவார்"],["kalaignar","கடன் is the giver's sense of duty.",""]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1054":{"book":"kural","num":1054,"t":["இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும் கரத்தல்","கனவிலும் தேற்றாதார் மாட்டு"],"p":"To beg of such as never think of withholding (their charity) even in their dreams, is in fact the same as giving (it oneself);","r":"Even asking is like giving, from those who don't conceive of concealment even in a dream.","n":"An extraordinary equivalence that erases the difference in rank. Parimelazhagar tempers it: asking brings no fame, but nor does it damage any you already have, so that is all the likeness. The strong statement is the verse's and the caveat is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1055":{"book":"kural","num":1055,"t":["கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால் கண்ணின்று","இரப்பவர் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"As there are in the world those that give without refusing, there are (also) those that prefer to beg by simply standing before them.","r":"It's because there are people on earth who don't conceal that askers take up standing before them.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds the alternative: without such people they would give up their lives, since they can't give up honour. So the existence of generous people is what keeps them alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1056":{"book":"kural","num":1056,"t":["கரப்பிடும்பை யில்லாரைக் காணின் நிரப்பிடும்பை","எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்"],"p":"All the evil of begging will be removed at the sight of those who are far from the evil of refusing.","r":"If one sees those without the affliction of concealment, every affliction of poverty is destroyed at once.","n":"Concealing is called இடும்பை, the same word as poverty, so the verse treats the wealthy miser as a sufferer too. The cure comes merely at the sight (காணின்). Manakkudavar concludes one shouldn't ask from those who are merely rich.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1057":{"book":"kural","num":1057,"t":["இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவாரைக் காணின் மகிழ்ந்துள்ளம்","உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து"],"p":"Beggars rejoice exceedingly when they behold those who bestow (their alms) with kindness and courtesy.","r":"If one sees those who give without scorn and contempt, the heart is delighted and rejoices deep inside.","n":"The joy lies in being given to without being lowered. உள்ளுள் உவப்பது goes deeper than the material relief, and it's the manner of the gift that's being measured.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1058":{"book":"kural","num":1058,"t":["இரப்பாரை இல்லாயின் ஈர்ங்கண்மா ஞாலம்","மரப்பாவை சென்றுவந் தற்று"],"p":"If there were no beggars, (the actions done in) the cool wide world would only resemble the movement of a puppet.","r":"If there were no askers, this wide cool world would be wooden puppets coming and going.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without someone to give to, others would earn no fame or merit, and they might as well be lifeless.","அவர்க்கு ஈதலைச் செய்து புகழும் புண்ணியமும் எய்தாமையின், உயிருடையரல்லார்"],["manakkudavar","Asking and giving are the order of the world, so asking shouldn't be treated as low.","இரத்தலும் ஈதலும் உலகியல்பாதலான், இரத்தல் இழிவென்று கொள்ளப்படா"],["kalaignar","He changes the subject: those who won't let askers near are the puppets.",""]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1059":{"book":"kural","num":1059,"t":["ஈவார்கண் என்னுண்டாம் தோற்றம் இரந்துகோள்","மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை"],"p":"What (praise) would there be to givers (of alms) if there were no beggars to ask for and reveive (them).","r":"What renown could givers have, if there were none who want to ask and take?","n":"The logic has an uncomfortable side: if givers' renown requires askers, a world without poverty would be poorer in virtue. The verse doesn't address this, and it treats askers as a need, not as a problem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1060":{"book":"kural","num":1060,"t":["இரப்பான் வெகுளாமை வேண்டும் நிரப்பிடும்பை","தானேயும் சாலும் கரி"],"p":"He who begs ought not to be angry (at a refusal); for even the misery of (his own) poverty should be a sufficient reason (for so doing).","r":"The one who asks shouldn't be angry; the affliction of his own poverty is proof enough.","n":"His poverty proves that wealth is hard to come by, so the refuser may have a real reason. This turns the sufferer's own case into a reason for patience. It is a counterweight to 1051, which puts blame on those who withhold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1061":{"book":"kural","num":1061,"t":["கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாமை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Not to beg (at all) even from those excellent persons who cheerfully give without refusing, will do immense good.","r":"Not asking, even from those precious as eyes who give gladly without concealing, is worth ten million times more.","n":"The verse picks the best case for asking, then refuses it. Parimelazhagar narrows this: it's about not asking even friends who already know of one's poverty, and he concludes one should try to live by hard effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1062":{"book":"kural","num":1062,"t":["இரந்தும் உயிர்வாழ்தல் வேண்டின் பரந்து","கெடுக உலகியற்றி யான்"],"p":"If the Creator of the world has decreed even begging as a means of livelihood, may he too go abegging and perish.","r":"If even by begging one must live, let the one who made the world wander and perish.","n":"The Kural's sharpest line against any cosmic order. It doesn't assert that a creator exists, since the curse is conditional. Kalaignar makes that explicit: the one 'said to be' the creator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","oozh","inmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The creator decrees each being's livelihood along with its karma at conception; if that includes begging, the evil returns on him and he should suffer the same.","சில உயிர்க்கு இரத்தலையும் ஒரு செய் தொழிலாகக் கற்பித்தானாயின், அத்தீவினையால் தானும் அத்துன்பமுறல் வேண்டும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about those who choose to beg to survive; the conclusion is that death is better than begging.","இஃது இரக்கு மதனின், இறத்தல் அமையு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","He distances himself from the idea: it is whoever is 'said' to have created the world.",""]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1063":{"book":"kural","num":1063,"t":["இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்துதீர் வாமென்னும்","வன்மையின் வன்பாட்ட தில்"],"p":"There is no greater folly than the boldness with which one seeks to remedy the evils of poverty by begging (rather than by working).","r":"Nothing is harder than the hardness that says 'we will end the misery of poverty by begging'.","n":"வன்மை, hardness, is the opposite of what one would expect of an asker's position. Begging isn't weakness here but a kind of obstinate insensitivity. Manakkudavar adds that it doesn't even work, since it won't end poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1064":{"book":"kural","num":1064,"t":["இடமெல்லாம் கொள்ளாத் தகைத்தே இடமில்லாக்","காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு"],"p":"Even the whole world cannot sufficiently praise the dignity that would not beg even in the midst of destitution.","r":"The worth that won't consent to asking even when no means remain is too great for all of space to contain.","n":"இடம் is used twice: as all of space, and as means or room. A man with no room left holds something that no room can hold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1065":{"book":"kural","num":1065,"t":["தெண்ணீர் அடுபுற்கை ஆயினும் தாள்தந்தது","உண்ணலின் ஊங்கினிய தில்"],"p":"Even thin gruel is ambrosia to him who has obtained it by labour.","r":"Though it is gruel thin as clear water, nothing is sweeter than eating what one's own effort brought.","n":"தாள், literally the foot, stands for effort. Sweetness has become a moral quality here and doesn't describe the taste. Parimelazhagar says it's equal to ambrosia, because it's one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1066":{"book":"kural","num":1066,"t":["ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும் நாவிற்கு","இரவின் இளிவந்த தில்"],"p":"There is nothing more disgraceful to one's tongue than to use it in begging water even for a cow.","r":"Even if one asks for water for a cow, nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking.","n":"A limit case with the worthiest cause and the smallest request, and it's still degrading. Parimelazhagar: virtue itself should be done by effort, never by begging. He also notes that நாவிற்கு confines the disgrace to the spoken word, and that this still exceeds every other disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1067":{"book":"kural","num":1067,"t":["இரப்பன் இரப்பாரை எல்லாம் இரப்பின்","கரப்பார் இரவன்மின் என்று"],"p":"I beseech all beggars and say, \"If you need to beg, never beg of those who give unwillingly.\"","r":"I beg of all who beg: if you must beg, don't beg of those who conceal.","n":"The poet himself takes on the posture he has just condemned, begging beggars. The repetition of இரப்பன், இரப்பாரை and இரப்பின் is almost a chant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1068":{"book":"kural","num":1068,"t":["இரவென்னும் ஏமாப்பில் தோணி கரவென்னும்","பார்தாக்கப் பக்கு விடும்"],"p":"The unsafe raft of begging will split when it strikes on the rock of refusal.","r":"The unsafe boat called asking will split apart when it strikes the rock called concealment.","n":"The sea of poverty is supplied by both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar. The verse's point isn't the rock alone: the boat itself is flawed, and this is what ஏமாப்பில் says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1069":{"book":"kural","num":1069,"t":["இரவுள்ள உள்ளம் உருகும் கரவுள்ள","உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"To think of (the evil of) begging is enough to melt one's heart; but to think of refusal is enough to break it.","r":"Thinking of begging, the heart melts; thinking of concealment, even what remains is gone.","n":"The heart has two stages: it melts, and then there's nothing left to melt. Concealment is ranked as the worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The melting heart is that of an onlooker who imagines the cruelty; he notes that all others say it's the asker's.","இதற்குப் பிறரெல்லாம் 'இரக்கின்றவர் உள்ளம் உருகும்' என்று உரைத்தார்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns the asker, who has nothing to gain.","இஃது இரப்பார்க்கு ஆக்கமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1070":{"book":"kural","num":1070,"t":["கரப்பவர்க்கு யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ இரப்பவர்","சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்"],"p":"Saying \"No\" to a beggar takes away his life. (but as that very word will kill the refuser) where then would the latter's life hide itself ?","r":"As the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal, where does the life of the one who conceals hide?","n":"The rhetorical question is a charge: the word that kills the hearer ought to kill the speaker first. Parimelazhagar glosses 'life goes' as being stunned with helplessness, a less literal sense than the verse's own. He reports another reading as a dread-filled man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1071":{"book":"kural","num":1071,"t":["மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர் அவரன்ன","ஒப்பாரி யாங்கண்ட தில்"],"p":"The base resemble men perfectly (as regards form); and we have not seen such (exact) resemblance (among any other species).","r":"The base look just like human beings; we have never seen so close a resemblance.","n":"Bitter praise opens the portrait. The likeness is complete, and that is the whole problem, since there's no outward way of telling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a likeness of body between two different kinds, distinguished only by whether they have good qualities.","மக்கட் சாதிக்கும் கயச்சாதிக்கும் வடிவு ஒத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","It is hypocrisy within one person, who is base while acting good.",""]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1072":{"book":"kural","num":1072,"t":["நன்றறி வாரிற் கயவர் திருவுடையர்","நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்"],"p":"The low enjoy more felicity than those who know what is good; for the former are not troubled with anxiety (as to the good).","r":"The base are more fortunate than those who know what's good, for they have no anguish in their heart.","n":"A real observation under the sarcasm: moral knowledge has a psychological cost. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the good worry that they don't do enough, about obstacles to their good deeds, and about what might result. திரு here is mock praise, as he says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1073":{"book":"kural","num":1073,"t":["தேவர் அனையர் கயவர் அவருந்தாம்","மேவன செய்தொழுக லான்"],"p":"The base resemble the Gods; for the base ac t as they like.","r":"The base are like the gods, for they too act as they please.","n":"Having looked like humans in 1071, they now look like gods. Parimelazhagar says the difference between them goes unstated, since the gods act by understanding and the base by lowness. Kalaignar's gloss says 'the gods of the Puranas', which gives the barb a second target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1074":{"book":"kural","num":1074,"t":["அகப்பட்டி ஆவாரைக் காணின் அவரின்","மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base feels proud when he sees persons whose acts meaner than his own.","r":"Seeing அகப்பட்டி, the base outdo them and swagger with pride.","n":"அகப்பட்டி is hard to fix, and I've left it untranslated. The readings below differ so widely that I can't be confident of either.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are people who behave as they please like strays, on a smaller scale than the base man, and he boasts by exceeding them.","தன்னிற் சுருங்கிய பட்டியாய் ஒழுகுவாரைக் கண்டானாயின்"],["manakkudavar","These are women at home with grace, and the base pride themselves on being more graceful still.","மனையகத்திருந்து பொற்புடையாராகிய பெண்டிரைக் காணின்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1075":{"book":"kural","num":1075,"t":["அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம் எச்சம்","அவாவுண்டேல் உண்டாம் சிறிது"],"p":"(The principle of) behaviour in the mean is chiefly fear; if not, hope of gain, to some extent.","r":"Fear is the conduct of the base, and beyond that there's some, if there's desire.","n":"A theory of external motivation: there's no inner source of good conduct in them, so it can only come from fear of punishment and hope of gain. Parimelazhagar names the king as what they fear, and Kalaignar reverses the order and makes it pretence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1076":{"book":"kural","num":1076,"t":["அறைபறை அன்னர் கயவர்தாம் கேட்ட","மறைபிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்க லான்"],"p":"The base are like a drum that is beaten, for they unburden to others the secrets they have heard.","r":"The base are like a beaten drum, for they carry any secret they hear and proclaim it to others.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the drum only sounds when beaten, a working likeness. உய்த்து, 'carrying', suggests to him the secret is a heavy burden to such a man, so that he has to go and put it down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1077":{"book":"kural","num":1077,"t":["ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார் கயவர் கொடிறுடைக்கும்","கூன்கையர் அல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"The mean will not (even) shake off (what sticks to) their hands (soon after a meal) to any but those who would break their jaws with their clenched fists.","r":"The base won't even shake their wet hands for any but those with bent fists who would break their jaws.","n":"The smallest gift is shaking drops of water off a washed hand. The gift is measured by what it takes to get it. Parimelazhagar sums up: they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1078":{"book":"kural","num":1078,"t":["சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல்","கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்"],"p":"The great bestow (their alms) as soon as they are informed; (but) the mean, like the sugar-cane, only when they are tortured to death.","r":"The worthy are of use at a word, and the base, like sugar-cane, only when crushed.","n":"A sugar-cane is sweet, so the image concedes that the base do have something to give. But it comes out only under pressure. கொல்ல, 'to kill', is unusually strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandraanmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1079":{"book":"kural","num":1079,"t":["உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின் பிறர்மேல்","வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base will bring an evil (accusation) against others, as soon as he sees them (enjoying) good food and clothing.","r":"If they see others clothed and fed, the base are able to find a fault in them.","n":"Ordinary eating and dressing are enough to bring it on; no great success is required.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They invent faults that don't exist, with skill enough to make them believable.","அவர்மாட்டு வடுவில்லையாகவும் உண்டாக்கவல்லனாம்."],["manakkudavar","They search out faults that do exist in such people.","அவர் மாட்டு உள்ள குற்றங்களை ஆராயவல்லாராவர் கயவர்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1080":{"book":"kural","num":1080,"t":["எற்றிற் குரியர் கயவரொன்று உற்றக்கால்","விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து"],"p":"The base will hasten to sell themselves as soon as a calamity has befallen them. For what else are they fitted ?","r":"What are the base fit for? When something befalls them, they are fit to sell themselves, quickly.","n":"The Book of Wealth closes with a person as a thing sold. Parimelazhagar's reading of விரைந்து is grim: they hurry before buyers learn what they are. An answer to the question is the insult: this is the only use they have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","maanam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1081":{"book":"kural","num":1081,"t":["அணங்குகொல் ஆய்மயில் கொல்லோ கனங்குழை","மாதர்கொல் மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Is this jewelled female a celestial, a choice peahen, or a human being ? My mind is perplexed.","r":"The man, on first seeing her, cannot tell whether this woman in heavy earrings is a goddess, a rare peahen or a human being, and his heart reels.","n":"The three guesses aren't mere compliments. Parimelazhagar ties each to evidence: her indescribable form and his distress suggest a goddess, her grace and the grove suggest a peahen, and the way she looks back suggests a woman. Kalaignar's rendering drops the goddess and gives 'beauty that torments me' instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1082":{"book":"kural","num":1082,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல் தாக்கணங்கு","தானைக்கொண் டன்ன துடைத்து"],"p":"This female beauty returning my looks is like a celestial maiden coming with an army to contend against me.","r":"The man says that when she answers his look with her own, it is as if a goddess who strikes on her own had brought an army with her.","n":"The returned look is the blow. Parimelazhagar says he has now settled that she is human, so the goddess of 1081 survives only as a figure, and his pain has grown from her beauty to her meaningful glance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1083":{"book":"kural","num":1083,"t":["பண்டறியேன் கூற்றென் பதனை இனியறிந்தேன்","பெண்டகையால் பேரமர்க் கட்டு"],"p":"I never knew before what is called Yama; I see it now; it is the eyes that carry on a great fight with (the help of) female qualities.","r":"The man says he never knew what Death was, and now he does: it has a woman's grace and large eyes that make war.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the first line as a contrast between hearsay and experience: Death was something known from what the learned say, and only now seen. He adds that the man calls her Death because the suffering outweighs the pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1084":{"book":"kural","num":1084,"t":["கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றத்தால் பெண்டகைப்","பேதைக்கு அமர்த்தன கண்"],"p":"These eyes that seem to kill those who look at them are as it were in hostilities with this feminine simplicity.","r":"The man says the eyes of this artless, womanly girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them, and he sets those eyes against, or alongside, her gentleness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அமர்த்தன means the eyes are at odds with her gentle nature; they are cruel where she is not.","அமர்த்தல்: மாறுபடுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The word means the eyes fit her: they suit the girl in the work they do.","அமர்தல் - மேவல்."],["kalaignar","The eyes are a puzzling contradiction in someone so womanly.",""]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1085":{"book":"kural","num":1085,"t":["கூற்றமோ கண்ணோ பிணையோ மடவரல்","நோக்கமிம் மூன்றும் உடைத்து"],"p":"Is it Yama, (a pair of) eyes or a hind ?- Are not all these three in the looks of this maid ?","r":"The man asks whether it is Death, an eye or a doe, since the young woman's look has the nature of all three.","n":"The list holds opposites: the look kills, it seeks him out, and it startles like a frightened animal. Parimelazhagar sums this up as pleasure and pain given together, which sets up the double look of 1091.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1086":{"book":"kural","num":1086,"t":["கொடும்புருவம் கோடா மறைப்பின் நடுங்கஞர்","செய்யல மன்இவள் கண்"],"p":"Her eyes will cause (me) no trembling sorrow, if they are properly hidden by her cruel arched eye-brows.","r":"The man says that if her curved brows would only stop bending and screen her eyes, those eyes could not cause him this trembling pain.","n":"The conceit turns on the word கோடா, 'unbending'. Parimelazhagar treats the brows as the eyes' close friends, who could restrain them only if they were straight themselves, because anyone who rebukes a friend must be upright. Manakkudavar reads a small scene into it: she has lowered her head and her brows now shade her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1087":{"book":"kural","num":1087,"t":["கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம் மாதர்","படாஅ முலைமேல் துகில்"],"p":"The cloth that covers the firm bosom of this maiden is (like) that which covers the eyes of a rutting elephant.","r":"The man says the cloth over her firm breasts is like the face-cloth over the eyes of a rutting elephant.","n":"A covering usually suggests safety, but this one announces danger. Parimelazhagar makes modesty the reason for the cloth, while the man, seeing strength through it, thinks of what it holds back. Manakkudavar has the man address her directly as மாதரே, while Parimelazhagar has him speak about her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1088":{"book":"kural","num":1088,"t":["ஒண்ணுதற் கோஒ உடைந்ததே ஞாட்பினுள்","நண்ணாரும் உட்குமென் பீடு"],"p":"On her bright brow alone is destroyed even th at power of mine that used to terrify the most fearless foes in the battlefield.","r":"The man laments that his might, feared in battle even by enemies who never met him there, has broken before her bright forehead alone.","n":"The ஓ marks wonder at the mismatch: so great a strength and so small a thing as a forehead. Parimelazhagar is careful to note that this isn't boasting, because the man is grieving for what he has lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1089":{"book":"kural","num":1089,"t":["பிணையேர் மடநோக்கும் நாணும் உடையாட்கு","அணியெவனோ ஏதில தந்து"],"p":"Of what use are other jewels to her who is adorned with modesty, and the meek looks of a hind ?","r":"The man asks what use foreign ornaments are to one who already has a doe's shy look and modesty.","n":"It sounds like a pure compliment, but both older commentators hear a complaint. For Parimelazhagar, whoever adorned her lacks sense, because the jewels burden her and harm him; for Manakkudavar, he praises her while seeming to accuse her of cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1090":{"book":"kural","num":1090,"t":["உண்டார்கண் அல்லது அடுநறாக் காமம்போல்","கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல் இன்று"],"p":"Unlike boiled honey which yields delight only when it is drunk, love gives pleasure even when looked at.","r":"Brewed liquor delights only those who drink it, not those who merely see it as love does; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies an unspoken close: love is better than liquor, yet he hasn't obtained it because he hasn't yet read her intent. He also takes காமம் here to mean the person in whom love is enjoyed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks as he sets out to read her intent.","தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிதல் உற்றான் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman thinks this, pained after seeing the man, which reverses the chapter's point of view.","இது தலைமகள் தலைமகனைக்கண்டுழி வருத்தமுற்றுக் கருதியது."]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1091":{"book":"kural","num":1091,"t":["இருநோக்கு இவளுண்கண் உள்ளது ஒருநோக்கு","நோய்நோக்கொன் றந்நோய் மருந்து"],"p":"There are two looks in the dyed eyes of this (fair one); one causes pain, and the other is the cure thereof.","r":"The man sees two looks in her kohl-lined eyes: one gives him the sickness and the other is its cure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful look discloses desire in her mind and the healing look shows her love; he rejects calling the first a neutral look, since one-sided pain wouldn't be mutual love.","நோய் செய்யும் நோக்கு அவள் மனத்தனாய காமக்குறிப்பினை வெளிப்படுத்துகின்ற நோக்கு."],["manakkudavar","The two looks are successive: the first look that hurt him, then a later one mixed with shyness and concern for his pain.","நோய்நோக்கென்பது முதல் நோக்கின நோக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1092":{"book":"kural","num":1092,"t":["கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம் காமத்தில்","செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது"],"p":"A single stolen glance of her eyes is more than half the pleasure (of sexual embrace).","r":"The man says the brief look her eyes steal is not half of love's fulfilment but more than half.","n":"The arithmetic is odd until you see why it works. Parimelazhagar says the glance guarantees that union will follow, so its value lies in what it promises. Stealing also suits the setting: this is secret love, and the eyes are the first thieves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kalaviyal","kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1093":{"book":"kural","num":1093,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள் அஃதவள்","யாப்பினுள் அட்டிய நீர்"],"p":"She has looked (at men) and stooped (her head); and that (sign) waters as it were (the corn of) our love.","r":"The man says she looked, and having looked lowered her head, and that gesture was water poured into the bond between them.","n":"The verse says only யாப்பு, a binding, and water poured into it. The growing crop of love that makes the image cohere comes from the commentators, and Parimelazhagar notes that the metaphor is only partly stated. Lowering her head is what waters the bond, so modesty here works for desire and not against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1094":{"book":"kural","num":1094,"t":["யான்நோக்கும் காலை நிலன்நோக்கும் நோக்காக்கால்","தான்நோக்கி மெல்ல நகும்"],"p":"When I look, she looks down; when I do not, she looks and smiles gently.","r":"The man observes that when he looks she looks at the ground, and when he doesn't she looks at him and smiles softly.","n":"The whole verse is timing. Their eyes never meet, and that very avoidance tells him she is watching. Parimelazhagar takes மெல்ல நகும் as rejoicing inwardly without showing it, and mentions that others read it as a visible smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1095":{"book":"kural","num":1095,"t":["குறிக்கொண்டு நோக்காமை அல்லால் ஒருகண்","சிறக்கணித்தாள் போல நகும்"],"p":"She not only avoids a direct look at me, but looks as it were with a half-closed eye and smiles.","r":"The man says that she doesn't look at him directly, but smiles as though narrowing one eye.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She narrows one eye as she glances; the 'as though' is there because even this isn't openly done.","சிறக்கணித்தல்: சுருங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","He reads the line as 'at one moment', and the verb as agreeing: she smiles as one who has consented.","ஒருகால் உடம்பட்டாள் போல"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1096":{"book":"kural","num":1096,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்போல் சொலினும் செறாஅர்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though they may speak harshly as if they were strangers, the words of the friendly are soon understood.","r":"The man reflects that the words of those without real anger are quickly seen through, even when they speak like strangers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The harsh speaker is the friend, who is sending him away while really seeking to help; he says this to himself.","தோழி சேண்படுத்தவழி அவள் குறிப்பு அறிந்த தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The case is one where the body shows agreement and speech refuses, which is taken as consent.","உறுப்பினாலிசைவுகாட்டி, உரையினால் மறுப்பினும் உடன்படுதலா மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speaker is the woman herself, hiding love under a stranger's manner.",""]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1097":{"book":"kural","num":1097,"t":["செறாஅச் சிறுசொல்லும் செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்","உறாஅர்போன்று உற்றார் குறிப்பு"],"p":"Little words that are harsh and looks that are hateful are (but) the expressions of lovers who wish to act like strangers.","r":"The man reasons that small harsh words without anger and looks like an enemy's are the signs of those who are close while seeming distant.","n":"1096 and 1097 give him a rule for treating refusal as disguise, and the verses rest it entirely on செறாஅ, the absence of real anger. In my reading that condition is doing all the work, and it is exactly what the man can't verify from outside. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that he needn't fear such signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1098":{"book":"kural","num":1098,"t":["அசையியற்கு உண்டாண்டோ ர் ஏஎர்யான் நோக்கப்","பசையினள் பைய நகும்"],"p":"When I look, the pitying maid looks in return and smiles gently; and that is a comforting sign for me.","r":"The man says that when he looks, she softens and smiles gently, and there is something special about the swaying woman at that moment.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏர் here is the good sign that shows in her smile, a promise that won't fail him after his pleading look.","தோன்றுகின்றதோர் நன்மைக் குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","ஏர் is beauty, the quality that surpasses her form at that moment.","அழகு - தன்வடிவினுள் மிக்க குணம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1099":{"book":"kural","num":1099,"t":["ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்","காதலார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Both the lovers are capable of looking at each other in an ordinary way, as if they were perfect strangers.","r":"Looking at each other with a stranger's common glance is found only in lovers; Parimelazhagar gives this to the friend, who is working out the affair, while the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"A public pretence of indifference becomes the evidence. The couple's disguise gives them away to the one person placed to notice, so this is close observation from outside the romance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1100":{"book":"kural","num":1100,"t":["கண்ணொடு கண்இணை நோக்கொக்கின் வாய்ச்சொற்கள்","என்ன பயனும் இல"],"p":"The words of the mouths are of no use whatever, when there is perfect agreement between the eyes (of lovers).","r":"Once eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use at all; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the claim very concrete: the useless words are the couple's cover stories, hers about guarding the millet field and his about hunting. Out of context it reads as a maxim about wordless love.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend, having heard both, speaks in general terms about what she has noticed.","இருவர் சொல்லும் கேட்டு உலகியல்மேல் வைத்துக் கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The man, about to approach her, speaks to his own heart.","இது சார்தலுறுகின்ற தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1101":{"book":"kural","num":1101,"t":["கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்","ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள"],"p":"The (simultaneous) enjoyment of the five senses of sight, hearing, taste, smell and touch can only be found with bright braceleted (women).","r":"The man says that all five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching, find their objects in this one bright-braceleted woman.","n":"The claim is about concentration. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that pleasures usually taken from different things at different times are here had at once, from one person. He also folds in the erotic techniques named in Sanskrit treatises, which the verse doesn't mention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1102":{"book":"kural","num":1102,"t":["பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிறமன் அணியிழை","தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து"],"p":"The remedy for a disease is always something different (from it); but for the disease caused by this jewelled maid, she is herself the cure.","r":"The man says the cure for a disease is usually something other than its cause, but this jewelled woman is herself the cure for the sickness she brings.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens பிற: ordinary medicine is opposite in nature to the disease's cause. Love therefore breaks a rule of medicine, not only of common sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1103":{"book":"kural","num":1103,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள் துயிலின் இனிதுகொல்","தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு"],"p":"Can the lotus-eyed Vishnu's heaven be indeed as sweet to those who delight to sleep in the delicate arms of their beloved ?","r":"The man asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one can be sweeter than sleeping on the soft arms of the one you love.","n":"The question is left open, and the commentators answer it for him. Parimelazhagar has the man's friend object that one fit for supreme bliss shouldn't sink to this small pleasure, and takes the man's point to be that heaven requires hard austerities which this sleep does not.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The world is Vishnu's, reached by ascetics who renounce the senses; he rejects the Indra reading on the ground that lotus-eyed isn't Indra's name.","தாமரைக்கண்ணான் என்பது அவனுக்குப் பெயரன்மையின், அஃது உரையன்மையறிக."],["manakkudavar","The world is Indra's heaven, and the point is that heaven's pleasure is simply this.","இந்திரனது சுவர்க்கம்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1104":{"book":"kural","num":1104,"t":["நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும்","தீயாண்டுப் பெற்றாள் இவள்?"],"p":"From whence has she got this fire that burns when I withdraw and cools when I approach ?","r":"The man asks where she found this fire that burns when he moves away and cools when he comes near.","n":"Parimelazhagar observes a small sleight: the fire is the man's own desire, which he attributes to her because she is what draws it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1105":{"book":"kural","num":1105,"t":["வேட் ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே","தோட் டார் கதுப்பினாள் தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of her whose locks are adorned with flowers delight me as if they were the very sweets I have desired (to get).","r":"The man says her shoulders, with flowers in her hair, feel like each thing one craves at the moment of craving it.","n":"The point is freshness across repetition. For Parimelazhagar, they have already met several times and she still binds him as if new. Manakkudavar's text differs: where the verse has hair adorned with flowers, he has hair fallen loose on her shoulders during love, which changes the picture from adornment to its undoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1106":{"book":"kural","num":1106,"t":["உறுதோறு உயிர்தளிர்ப்பத் தீண்டலால் பேதைக்கு","அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of this fair one are made of ambrosia, for they revive me with pleasure every time I embrace them.","r":"The man says that since her touch makes his life put out fresh shoots each time they meet, this girl's shoulders must be made of ambrosia.","n":"Ambrosia is inferred from an effect, in the manner of a logical argument. உயிர்தளிர்ப்ப also makes his life a plant, withering between meetings, which Manakkudavar pushes further: without her that life would die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1107":{"book":"kural","num":1107,"t":["தம்மில் இருந்து தமதுபாத்து உண்டற்றால்","அம்மா அரிவை முயக்கு"],"p":"The embraces of a gold-complexioned beautiful female are as pleasant as to dwell in one's own house and live by one's own (earnings) after distributing (a portion of it in charity).","r":"The man says the embrace of this lovely dark-gold woman is like living in your own home and eating your own food after sharing it.","n":"A pleasure is measured by a virtue. The comparison brings the whole ethic of the householder into the bedroom, as a kind of satisfaction that has nothing to hide or to owe.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her friend has urged marriage, and he declines: this secret union already gives him the reward of the householder's life.","'இல்லறஞ்செய்தார் எய்தும் துறக்கத்து இன்பம் எனக்கு இப்புணர்ச்சியே தரும்' என வரைவு உடன்படான் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The woman embraced is already his wife, so the verse describes married pleasure.",""]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1108":{"book":"kural","num":1108,"t":["வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே வளியிடை","போழப் படாஅ முயக்கு"],"p":"To ardent lovers sweet is the embrace that cannot be penetrated even by a breath of breeze.","r":"The man says an embrace so close that not even a breeze can part it is sweet to two who desire each other.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds an evasion in the word இருவர், 'two'. The friend has urged the uninterrupted union of marriage, and he answers that this holds for two people, whereas they are not two. Manakkudavar sees only the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He agrees in form but declines the marriage she urges, because here there aren't two.","'ஈண்டு இருவர் இல்லை இன்மையான், இஃது ஒவ்வாது' என்பது கருத்து."]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1109":{"book":"kural","num":1109,"t":["ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல் இவைகாமம்","கூடியார் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"Love quarrel, reconciliation and intercourse - these are the advantages reaped by those who marry for lust.","r":"The man names quarrelling, making up and uniting as the rewards of those who have come together in love.","n":"The verse puts the quarrel inside love's rewards, and first in the list. That is the seed of the book's final chapter.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a dismissal: quarrels belong to married men who stray to courtesans, and a couple as one as they are wants no such 'reward'.","அப்பயன் இருதலைப் புள்ளிள் ஓருயிராய் உழுவலன்புடைய எமக்கு வேண்டா"],["manakkudavar","This is delight: the man has just ended her sulk and united with her, and celebrates the sequence.","ஊடற் குறிப்புத் தோன்ற நின்ற தலைமகளை ஊடல் தீர்த்துப் புணர்ந்த தலைமகன்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1110":{"book":"kural","num":1110,"t":["அறிதோறு அறியாமை கண்டற்றால் காமம்","செறிதோறும் சேயிழை மாட்டு"],"p":"As (one's) ignorance is discovered the more one learns, so does repeated intercourse with a well-adorned female (only create a desire for more).","r":"The man says that closeness to this red-jewelled woman works like knowledge: each advance shows how much was lacking before.","n":"Parimelazhagar closes the chapter by noting that the woman feels this joy too, but it shows in her only through signs and never in speech. Nine verses of male voice therefore rest on a convention, and he says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1111":{"book":"kural","num":1111,"t":["நன்னீரை வாழி அனிச்சமே நின்னினும்","மென்னீரள் யாம்வீழ் பவள்"],"p":"May you flourish, O Anicham! you have a delicate nature. But my beloved is more delicate than you.","r":"The man addresses the anicham flower, granting it delicacy and telling it that the woman he loves is more delicate still.","n":"வாழி sounds like a blessing, but for Parimelazhagar it is a polite way to end the flower's pride: stop thinking you are the softest. He also says the man has just touched her, so the praise comes from touch and not sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1112":{"book":"kural","num":1112,"t":["மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே இவள்கண்","பலர்காணும் பூவொக்கும் என்று"],"p":"O my soul, fancying that flowers which are seen by many can resemble her eyes, you become confused at the sight of them.","r":"The man scolds his heart for reeling whenever it sees flowers, supposing that her eyes resemble what anyone can look at.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The flowers don't match her eyes, and the heart is rebuked for its false comparison now that he alone has seen them.","அம்மலர்கள் ஒவ்வாமை கண்டு, ஒப்புமை கருதிய நெஞ்சை இகழ்ந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","Her eyes match flowers in colour but not in quality.","கண் பூவினது நிறமொக்குமாயினும்"],["kalaignar","The verse is direct praise: her eyes are indeed a flower that many see and admire.",""]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1113":{"book":"kural","num":1113,"t":["முறிமேனி முத்தம் முறுவல் வெறிநாற்றம்","வேலுண்கண் வேய்த்தோ ளவட்கு"],"p":"The complexion of this bamboo-shouldered one is that of a shoot; her teeth, are pearls; her breath, fragrance; and her dyed eyes, lances.","r":"The man describes her: bamboo shoulders, a body like young shoots, pearl teeth, fragrance, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a witty setting as an alternative: her friend claims not to know whom he means, so this list is a description for identifying her. Everything in it is soft except the eyes, the one weapon among the delights, as in chapter 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1114":{"book":"kural","num":1114,"t":["காணின் குவளை கவிழ்ந்து நிலன்நோக்கும்","மாணிழை கண்ணொவ்வேம் என்று"],"p":"If the blue lotus could see, it would stoop and look at the ground saying, \"I can never resemble the eyes of this excellent jewelled one.\"","r":"The man says that if blue water-lilies could see, they would bow their heads and look at the ground, saying they can't match the eyes of this finely jewelled woman.","n":"The lilies would take the posture of a modest woman, which is the very gesture she makes in 1094. Parimelazhagar draws out the unstated corollary: lacking sight and shame, real lilies stand proudly staring at the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1115":{"book":"kural","num":1115,"t":["அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள் பெய்தாள் நுகப்பிற்கு","நல்ல படாஅ பறை"],"p":"No merry drums will be beaten for the (tender) waist of her who has adorned herself with the anicham without having removed its stem.","r":"The man says that she has worn anicham flowers without removing their stems, so no good drums will sound for her waist.","n":"This is a mock funeral. As Parimelazhagar spells it out, the stems' weight will snap her waist, and then only the drum for the dead will sound. Praise by imagined catastrophe is one of the book's boldest jokes. He glosses கால் as the flower's bud or stalk end, and Manakkudavar as its stems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1116":{"book":"kural","num":1116,"t":["மதியும் மடந்தை முகனும் அறியா","பதியின் கலங்கிய மீன்"],"p":"The stars have become confused in their places not being able to distinguish between the moon and the maid's countenance.","r":"The man says the stars have strayed from their places, unable to tell the moon from the young woman's face.","n":"This gives a fanciful explanation for why stars move at all: they are lost. Manakkudavar says so directly, that the poet presents their motion as confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1117":{"book":"kural","num":1117,"t":["அறுவாய் நிறைந்த அவிர்மதிக்குப் போல","மறுவுண்டோ மாதர் முகத்து"],"p":"Could there be spots in the face of this maid like those in the bright full moon ?","r":"The man asks whether a woman's face has spots, as the bright moon does when it fills out its lost parts.","n":"This undoes the previous verse. Waning and spots should have told the stars the difference, so Parimelazhagar hears mockery of the stars: they had every means of knowing, and still failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1118":{"book":"kural","num":1118,"t":["மாதர் முகம்போல் ஒளிவிட வல்லையேல்","காதலை வாழி மதி"],"p":"If you can indeed shine like the face of women, flourish, O moon, for then would you be worth loving ?","r":"The man tells the moon that if it could shine as her face does, it too could be loved.","n":"வாழி varies in force: Parimelazhagar calls it agreement when spoken to the flower in 1111 and contempt here, because the moon's spots make the condition impossible. Manakkudavar treats it as an empty particle. The tone of the verse depends on that small word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1119":{"book":"kural","num":1119,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் முகமொத்தி யாயின்","பலர்காணத் தோன்றல் மதி"],"p":"O moon, if you wish to resemble the face of her whose eyes are like (these) flowers, do not appear so as to be seen by all.","r":"The man tells the moon that if it wants to resemble the face of the flower-eyed woman, it must not appear where many can see it.","n":"The criterion has moved from brightness to privacy. For Parimelazhagar, what disqualifies the moon is that it shows itself to everyone, while he alone has fully enjoyed her face. Praise here shades into possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1120":{"book":"kural","num":1120,"t":["அனிச்சமும் அன்னத்தின் தூவியும் மாதர்","அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்"],"p":"The anicham and the feathers of the swan are to the feet of females, like the fruit of the (thorny) Nerunji.","r":"The man says anicham flowers and swan's down are to her feet what the nerunji fruit is.","n":"The verse says நெருஞ்சிப் பழம், the fruit, and Kalaignar and Pope turn it into a thorn. Parimelazhagar explains the choice of fruit, since it is hard at the tip.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a covert refusal: her friend has proposed elopement, and feet like these couldn't cross the wilderness.","உடன் போக்கு உரைத்த தோழிக்கு அதனது அருமை கூறி மறுத்தது."],["manakkudavar","It is plain praise: her feet are softer than these.","இஃது அவையிற்றினும் மெல்லியது அடியென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1121":{"book":"kural","num":1121,"t":["பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்றே பணிமொழி","வாலெயிறு ஊறிய நீர்"],"p":"The water which oozes from the white teeth of this soft speeched damsel is like a mixture of milk and honey.","r":"The man says the moisture at the white teeth of this soft-spoken woman is like milk mixed with honey.","n":"Parimelazhagar notices why the comparison is a mixture and not one sweet thing: milk and honey each have a known taste, while their blend has a sweetness you can't name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1122":{"book":"kural","num":1122,"t":["உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்னமற் றன்ன","மடந்தையொடு எம்மிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love between me and this damsel is like the union of body and soul.","r":"The man says the bond between him and this girl is what the bond between body and life is.","n":"நட்பு, the word for friendship, is what the verse uses. Parimelazhagar's reading is that these are words for her: she is privately wondering who he is, whether he loves her and whether they will meet again, and he answers what he reads in her face. He files the verse under fear of parting, and Manakkudavar agrees: I can't leave you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","uyir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1123":{"book":"kural","num":1123,"t":["கருமணியிற் பாவாய்நீ போதாயாம் வீழும்","திருநுதற்கு இல்லை இடம்"],"p":"O you image in the pupil (of my eye)! depart; there is no room for (my) fair-browed beloved.","r":"The man tells the image in the pupil of his eye to leave, because there is no room there for the fair-browed woman he loves.","n":"The conceit depends on the Tamil word for the pupil's reflection, பாவை, a little figure living inside the eye. Parimelazhagar sets this at the moment she is leaving him: since he can't bear not seeing her, she must move in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1124":{"book":"kural","num":1124,"t":["வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ஆயிழை சாதல்","அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து"],"p":"My fair-jewelled one resembles the living soul (when she is in union with me), the dying soul when she leaves me.","r":"The man says the finely jewelled woman is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she goes.","n":"She isn't compared to life and death as things, but to what happens to a life, its staying in the body and its leaving. Parimelazhagar notes that the ideas of 'for me' and 'in union' have to be supplied, since the verse says only 'when she leaves'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1125":{"book":"kural","num":1125,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் மறப்பின் மறப்பறியேன்","ஒள்ளமர்க் கண்ணாள் குணம்"],"p":"If I had forgotten her who has bright battling eyes, I would have remembered (thee); but I never forget her. (Thus says he to her maid).","r":"The man, answering her friend's question whether he remembers them, says he would recall the qualities of the bright-eyed woman if he ever forgot them, but he doesn't know how to forget.","n":"The answer is a logical joke that turns the friend's question over: remembering presupposes forgetting, so no, he never thinks of her. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both mark this as the end of the man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1126":{"book":"kural","num":1126,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார் இமைப்பின் பருகுவரா","நுண்ணியர்எம் காத லவர்"],"p":"My lover would not depart from mine eyes; even if I wink, he would not suffer (from pain); he is so ethereal.","r":"The woman says her lover won't leave her eyes and isn't hurt even if she blinks, so subtle is he.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this a strategic setting: she fears her friend will blame the absent man, and speaks to herself so that the friend will overhear. Her defence of him is that he hasn't gone anywhere. The invisible subtle body sounds like metaphysics, but he explains it as ceaseless thinking grown so ripe that the man is always before her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1127":{"book":"kural","num":1127,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளார் காத லவராகக் கண்ணும்","எழுதேம் கரப்பாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover abides in my eyes, I will not even paint them, for he would (then) have to conceal himself.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her eyes, she doesn't even line them with kohl, knowing he would be hidden.","n":"A real sign of separation, an unadorned face, is given a loving explanation. Manakkudavar adds a second one: she is so busy watching him within that she has no time to dress.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","ninaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1128":{"book":"kural","num":1128,"t":["நெஞ்சத்தார் காத லவராக வெய்துண்டல்","அஞ்சுதும் வேபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover is in my heart, I am afraid of eating (anything) hot, for I know it would pain him.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her heart, she is afraid to eat hot food, knowing he would be burned.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies the occasion: her friend has asked why she isn't eating. Loss of appetite is one of the visible symptoms, and this is a graceful evasion of the question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1129":{"book":"kural","num":1129,"t":["இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல் அனைத்திற்கே","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"I will not wink, knowing that if I did, my lover would hide himself; and for this reason, this town says, he is unloving.","r":"The woman says she won't blink, knowing he would be hidden, and for this much the town calls him loveless.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a quarrel behind 'this town': the friend has criticised the man to help her endure, and she, hurt, refuses to name the friend, calling her the town. Sleeplessness, the very evidence against him, she presents as her own act of care.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1130":{"book":"kural","num":1130,"t":["உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும் இகந்துறைவர்","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"My lover dwells in my heart with perpetual delight; but the town says he is unloving and (therefore) dwells afar.","r":"The woman says he lives joyfully in her heart always, while this town says he is loveless and lives apart.","n":"The last words are identical to 1129's, and the refrain suggests how worn out she is by this argument. Whether her certainty is comfort or denial, the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1131":{"book":"kural","num":1131,"t":["காமம் உழந்து வருந்தினார்க்கு ஏமம்","மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி"],"p":"To those who after enjoyment of sexual pleasure suffer (for want of more), there is no help so efficient as the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard and no strength but the palmyra horse.","n":"ஏமம, 'safeguard', is a surprising word for a public humiliation. The safety he means is pressure: Parimelazhagar explains that the man has given up on the friend's help and intends to get his way as men of old did.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழந்து assumes that he has already enjoyed love and has now lost access to it.","அரியராய மகளிரோடு காமத்தை அனுபவித்துப் பின் அது பெறாது துன்புற்ற ஆடவர்க்கு"],["manakkudavar","The word means only striving and suffering because of love, and says nothing of past union.","காமம் காரணமாக முயன்று வருந்தினார்க்கு"]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1132":{"book":"kural","num":1132,"t":["நோனா உடம்பும் உயிரும் மடலேறும்","நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து"],"p":"Having got rid of shame, the suffering body and soul save themselves on the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says his body and life, unable to bear it any longer, will mount the palmyra horse, once shame has been put aside.","n":"He says body and life will do this, and not 'I', as though his person had dissolved into what still suffers. Parimelazhagar adds that with the threat, the man is really looking for the friend to see his desperation and grant his plea quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1133":{"book":"kural","num":1133,"t":["நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன் இன்றுடையேன்","காமுற்றார் ஏறும் மடல்"],"p":"Modesty and manliness were once my own; now, my own is the palmyra horse that is ridden by the lustful.","r":"The man says he once had shame and sound manliness, and today he has the palmyra horse that lovers ride.","n":"The form is an inventory of property, then and now. Parimelazhagar defines the two lost goods: shame keeps one from base acts, and manliness is not giving way to anything. Notably, it is the man who claims shame here; நாண் in the Kural isn't only a woman's virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1134":{"book":"kural","num":1134,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும் நாணொடு","நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை"],"p":"The raft of modesty and manliness, is, alas, carried-off by the strong current of lust.","r":"The man says that the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a neat debating context: she objects that shame and manliness are rafts against the flood, and so can't be lost. He concedes that they are rafts, and answers that this flood takes the raft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1135":{"book":"kural","num":1135,"t":["தொடலைக் குறுந்தொடி தந்தாள் மடலொடு","மாலை உழக்கும் துயர்"],"p":"Has brought me 'horse of palm,' and pangs of eventide!","r":"The man says that the girl with bangles like a garland has given him the palmyra horse and the anguish of evening.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanatory note gives the point: love exists at all times but flowers at evening, and the very name for her implies a young girl, who only echoes her friend. So the man tells the friend that what the girl gave, the friend can take away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1136":{"book":"kural","num":1136,"t":["மடலூர்தல் யாமத்தும் உள்ளுவேன் மன்ற","படல்ஒல்லா பேதைக்கென் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes will not close in sleep on your mistress's account; even at midnight will I think of mounting the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that because of that simple girl his eyes can't close, so even at midnight he will surely think of mounting the palmyra horse.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that பேதை here refers to her foolishness and not her age. Manakkudavar has the friend teasing that he'll forget by morning, and his answer turns his insomnia into a guarantee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madal","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1137":{"book":"kural","num":1137,"t":["கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும் மடலேறாப்","பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க தில்"],"p":"There is nothing so noble as the womanly nature that would not ride the palmyra horse, though plunged a sea of lust.","r":"Nothing has greater worth than womanhood, which suffers a sea of desire and doesn't mount the palmyra horse; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The praise also rests on a restriction: she may not take the only remedy he has been boasting about. Who says it changes its force, since from him it is an excuse, and from the friend it is a demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers the friend's taunt that men ought to endure better: it is the birth of women that has this restraint, and he lacks it.","'பிறப்பு விசேடத்தால் அவ்வடக்கம் எனக்கு இல்லையாகா நின்றது; நீ அஃது அறிகின்றிலை,' என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man that his beloved suffers just as much, and that women's lot is simply not to ride, to calm his distress.","தலைமகன் ஆற் றாமை நீங்குதற்பொருட்டுத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1138":{"book":"kural","num":1138,"t":["நிறையரியர் மன்அளியர் என்னாது காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"Even the Lust (of women) transgresses its secrecy and appears in public, forgetting that they are too chaste and liberal (to be overcome by it).","r":"Love doesn't stop to think that they are firm in restraint or deserving of pity, but breaks through secrecy and comes out in public; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a hint under the generalisation: she seems to talk about all women, but means her own love has grown, and that the matter must now be disclosed to the family.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","alar","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman speaks, her guarded love having grown; desire isn't afraid of women's firmness, நிறை அரியர்.","'இவர் நிறையால் நாம் மீதூர்தற்கு அரியர்' என்று அஞ்சுதல் செய்யாது"],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to refuse a daytime meeting because of scandal, and the woman lacks restraint.","இஃது அம்பலம் அலரும் ஆகாவென்று தோழி பகற்குறி மறுத்தது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1139":{"book":"kural","num":1139,"t":["அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றேஎன் காமம்","மறுகின் மறுகும் மருண்டு"],"p":"My lust, feeling that it is not known by all, reels confused in the streets (of this town).","r":"The woman says that her love, thinking that not everyone knows, wanders bewildered round the streets.","n":"Desire is made a separate creature, resentful of her former self-control. She can't ride the horse, so, in effect, her love goes out into the street without her. Parimelazhagar assigns the two verbs to two stages of gossip, அம்பல் and அலர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1140":{"book":"kural","num":1140,"t":["யாம்கண்ணின் காண நகுப அறிவில்லார்","யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு"],"p":"Even strangers laugh (at us) so as to be seen by us, for they have not suffered.","r":"The woman says fools laugh within her sight because they have not suffered as she has.","n":"Parimelazhagar names the unnamed fools: it is her friend, who has told her family and now teases her. He also reports, and rejects, a reading in which the man says this to the friend, because a man's speech wouldn't fit here at this point in the chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1141":{"book":"kural","num":1141,"t":["அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும் அதனைப்","பலரறியார் பாக்கியத் தால்"],"p":"My precious life is saved by the raise of rumour, and this, to my good luck no others are aware of.","r":"A precious life holds on because the gossip has risen, and by good fortune the many don't know this; for Parimelazhagar the man speaks of his own life, while Manakkudavar speaks of her life and leaves the speaker unnamed.","n":"The joke rests on the second line: if the gossips knew they were helping, they would stop. Commentators differ on the source of luck. Parimelazhagar says தெய்வத்தால் and Manakkudavar says புண்ணியத்தாலே; one is divine and the other is merit, for a verse whose word is the Sanskrit-derived பாக்கியம்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, unable to reach her, is sustained by talk that makes her seem attainable.","அங்ஙனம் அரியாளை எளியளாக்கி எடுக்கின்றமையின், அஃது அவ்வாருயிர்க்குப் பற்றுக்கோடாக நின்றது"],["manakkudavar","It is her life that the gossip sustains.","அவளது ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்"],["kalaignar","It is the couple's life, which survives on hope that talk will help their love succeed.",""]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1142":{"book":"kural","num":1142,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் அருமை அறியாது","அலரெமக்கு ஈந்ததிவ் வூர்"],"p":"Not knowing the value of her whose eyes are like flowers this town has got up a rumour about me.","r":"The man says this town, not knowing how rare the flower-eyed woman is, has made him a present of gossip.","n":"ஈந்தது is the verb for a generous gift. Parimelazhagar takes it that way, as an ironic thanks. The rarity differs by commentator: for Parimelazhagar, she is hard to reach, and for Manakkudavar, it is her noble birth that the gossips ignore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1143":{"book":"kural","num":1143,"t":["உறாஅதோ ஊரறிந்த கெளவை அதனைப்","பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து"],"p":"Will I not get a rumour that is known to the (whole) town ? For what I have not got is as if I had got it (already).","r":"The man asks whether the gossip the whole town knows isn't his to have, since it feels like having what he hasn't yet obtained.","n":"The gossip isn't true yet, and that is its use: being talked about as a couple gives a foretaste of being one. Manakkudavar gives a more practical reason, in which the man tells the friend that the talk will bring the families round.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1144":{"book":"kural","num":1144,"t":["கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம் அதுவின்றேல்","தவ்வென்னும் தன்மை இழந்து"],"p":"Rumour increases the violence of my passion; without it it would grow weak and waste away.","r":"The man says gossip makes his love flourish, and that without it the love would lose its nature and droop.","n":"The sound carries the argument: கவ்வை, கவ்விது, தவ்வென்னும். Parimelazhagar explains கவ்விது as a coinage from the noun, and தவ் as a word that imitates sagging. The claim is that love depends in part on being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1145":{"book":"kural","num":1145,"t":["களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால் காமம்","வெளிப்படுந் தோறும் இனிது"],"p":"As drinking liquor is delightful (to one) whenever one is in mirth, so is lust delightful to me whenever it is the subject of rumour.","r":"Love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking becomes more desired each time one gets drunk; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says that his own desire is so strong that gossip itself gives him pleasure.","வேட்கை மிகுதியால் அலரும் இன்பஞ் செய்யாநின்றது என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend replies to the man, who has asked whether this gossip is hurting them.","நுமக்குத் துன்பமாயிற்றே இவ்வலரென்று வினாவிய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1146":{"book":"kural","num":1146,"t":["கண்டது மன்னும் ஒருநாள் அலர்மன்னும்","திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண் டற்று"],"p":"It was but a single day that I looked on (my lover); but the rumour thereof has spread like the seizure of the moon by the serpent.","r":"The woman says she saw him on only one day, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon.","n":"An eclipse is an event no one can miss, and a tiny private act is set against it. Parimelazhagar has her speaking to her friend with the man in earshot, so the complaint is really a demand for marriage. Kalaignar pauses to mark the serpent as an imaginary notion, which the commentators before him don't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1147":{"book":"kural","num":1147,"t":["ஊரவர் கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல்","நீராக நீளும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady (of lust) is manured by the talk of women and watered by the (harsh) words of my mother.","r":"The woman says this sickness grows long, with the town's gossip as its manure and her mother's words as its water.","n":"Whatever is meant to shrink the love feeds it, as Parimelazhagar spells out. The one verse in the chapter to name her mother shows where the gossip actually hurts: at home. Parimelazhagar takes ஊரவர் to mean the women of the town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1148":{"book":"kural","num":1148,"t":["நெய்யால் எரிநுதுப்பேம் என்றற்றால் கெளவையால்","காமம் நுதுப்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"To say that one could extinguish passion by rumour is like extinguishing fire with ghee.","r":"The woman says that to think gossip can put out love is like thinking ghee can put out fire.","n":"The grammar frames this as an error in thought, 'to say we will extinguish'. Parimelazhagar places it in the same scene as 1147, so it is also her answer to the friend's advice that she should take the talk seriously and bear up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1149":{"book":"kural","num":1149,"t":["அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ அஞ்சலோம்பு என்றார்","பலர்நாண நீத்தக் கடை"],"p":"When the departure of him who said \"fear not\" has put me to shame before others, why need I be ashamed of scandal.","r":"The woman asks how she can be ashamed of gossip when the man who told her not to fear has left her, in a way that shames everyone who sees it.","n":"This verse is the turn in the chapter, with gossip now no longer a game. The promise 'fear not' is quoted against him. Shame has moved from what she feels about the town to what those watching feel on her behalf, so she has none left to spend on rumour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1150":{"book":"kural","num":1150,"t":["தாம்வேண்டின் நல்குவர் காதலர் யாம்வேண்டும்","கெளவை எடுக்கும்இவ் வூர்"],"p":"The rumour I desire is raised by the town (itself); and my lover would if desired consent (to my following him).","r":"The speaker, whom Parimelazhagar identifies as the friend talking to the woman while the man overhears, says the town is raising the very gossip they want, and the lover will grant it if asked or if he wishes.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தாம் வேண்டின் means 'if we ask', and the friend is steering the man towards elopement, which the gossip now justifies.","இனிக் காதலர்தாமும்யாம் வேண்டியக்கால் அதனை இனிதின் நேர்வர்"],["kalaignar","The words mean 'if the lover also wishes', and his consent depends on his own desire.",""]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1151":{"book":"kural","num":1151,"t":["செல்லாமை உண்டேல் எனக்குரை மற்றுநின்","வல்வரவு வாழ்வார்க் குரை"],"p":"If it is not departure, tell me; but if it is your speedy return, tell it to those who would be alive then.","r":"If you aren't leaving, tell me; if you mean to return quickly, tell that to those who will still be alive by then; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"One of the book's sharpest lines, a refusal to accept comfort. If the friend is the speaker, then Parimelazhagar's explanation for 'me' matters: she says எனக்கு for the woman because the two aren't distinct. He notes that some give the verse to the woman.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend answers the man, who has promised a quick return, and aims to stop his leaving.","'பிரிந்து கடிதின் வருவல்' என்ற தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend, who has told her he will come back soon.","இது கடித்து வருவாரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமையாற் கூறி யது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1152":{"book":"kural","num":1152,"t":["இன்கண் உடைத்தவர் பார்வல் பிரிவஞ்சும்","புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு"],"p":"His very look was once pleasing; but (now) even intercourse is painful through fear of separation.","r":"The woman says that பார்வல் alone once gave her delight, and now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation.","n":"The cruelty is that the most that love can give is spoiled by what it anticipates. Parimelazhagar cites Kalittokai for the telling sign: he now fusses over her more than before, which is how she knows he is leaving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பார்வல் is his look back in courtship, sweet because it promised union.","அவர் நோக்கு மாத்திரமும் புணர்ச்சி குறித்தமையான் நமக்கு இன்ப முடைத்தாயிருக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","It is her own watching for his arrival, a present joy set against the anxiety of being with him.","நங்காதலரை வரவு பார்த்திருக்கு மது"]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1153":{"book":"kural","num":1153,"t":["அரிதரோ தேற்றம் அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்","பிரிவோ ரிடத்துண்மை யான்"],"p":"As even the lover who understands (everything) may at times depart, confidence is hardly possible.","r":"The woman says trust is hard, since parting sometimes happens even with those who understand.","n":"அறிவுடையார் isn't a compliment to his learning. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that he knows his own word and knows she can't bear it, which makes his going worse. The verse shifts from this man's fault to the general difficulty of trusting anyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1154":{"book":"kural","num":1154,"t":["அளித்தஞ்சல் என்றவர் நீப்பின் தெளித்தசொல்","தேறியார்க்கு உண்டோ தவறு"],"p":"If he who bestowed his love and said \"fear not\" should depart, will it be the fault of those who believed in (his) assuring words ?","r":"The woman asks, if the one who was gracious and told her not to fear should leave, whether any fault lies with those who believed his assuring words.","n":"She refers to herself in the third person as தேறியார், 'those who trusted', which both commentators note. This turns the private complaint into a general moral question about a promise and who answers for it. Parimelazhagar names the fault as the mismatch between his word and deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1155":{"book":"kural","num":1155,"t":["ஓம்பின் அமைந்தார் பிரிவோம்பல் மற்றவர்","நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு"],"p":"If you would save (my life), delay the departure of my destined (husband); for if he departs, intercourse will become impossible.","r":"The woman tells her friend that if she wants to protect her, she must prevent the departure of the one who is fit for her, because once he goes, union will be hard.","n":"அமைந்தார் is a quiet word: the one who is fitting. Parimelazhagar adds divine ordering, saying he is the one fit by தெய்வம் to be her lord, and Pope follows with 'destined'. The verse itself doesn't mention destiny. He also explains why union would be hard: her life would have gone with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1156":{"book":"kural","num":1156,"t":["பிரிவுரைக்கும் வன்கண்ணர் ஆயின் அரிதவர்","நல்குவர் என்னும் நசை"],"p":"If he is so cruel as to mention his departure (to me), the hope that he would bestow (his love) must be given up.","r":"The woman says that if he can be so hard as to speak of leaving, the hope that he would come back and be gracious is pointless.","n":"The offence is the announcement, even more than the journey. For Parimelazhagar, one who could plan this while still at her side isn't likely to soften at a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1157":{"book":"kural","num":1157,"t":["துறைவன் துறந்தமை தூற்றாகொல் முன்கை","இறைஇறவா நின்ற வளை"],"p":"Do not the rings that begin to slide down my fingers forebode the separation of my lord ?","r":"The woman asks if the bangles slipping past her wrist don't already announce that the lord of the shore has left her.","n":"தூற்றா is the verb for spreading gossip, so her own body has joined the town. Parimelazhagar sees a rebuke to the friend, who brings the news: 'you too only do what these bangles do', when she should have stopped him. The past tense 'has left' is anticipatory, since the news alone has made her thin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1158":{"book":"kural","num":1158,"t":["இன்னாது இனன்இல்ஊர் வாழ்தல் அதனினும்","இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு"],"p":"Painful is it to live in a friendless town; but far more painful is it to part from one's lover.","r":"The woman says living in a town without one's own people is painful, and being parted from the one who is dear is more painful still.","n":"This sounds like a proverb, but Parimelazhagar says it is aimed. Her friend agreed to his leaving instead of preventing it, and so she speaks as if she has no companion here at all, while seeming only to speak about the way of the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1159":{"book":"kural","num":1159,"t":["தொடிற்சுடின் அல்லது காமநோய் போல","விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ"],"p":"Fire burns when touched; but, like the sickness of love, can it also burn when removed ?","r":"Fire burns only when touched, and can it burn when one moves away, as the sickness of love does? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend, who has said love burns only where it is, like fire, and that she should therefore bear it.","'காமம் தீயே போன்று தான் நின்ற இடத்தைச் சுடுமாகலான் நீ ஆற்றல் வேண்டும்' என்ற தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks, on seeing the woman's distress after the man has left.","தலைமகன் பிரிந்துழித் தலைமகளாற்றாமை கண்டு தோழிகூறியது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1160":{"book":"kural","num":1160,"t":["அரிதாற்றி அல்லல்நோய் நீக்கிப் பிரிவாற்றிப்","பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"As if there were many indeed that can consent to the impossible, kill their pain, endure separation and yet continue to live afterwards.","r":"The woman says that many agree to the impossible, remove their pain, endure the parting, and stay on alive afterwards.","n":"It is all in the tone. Taken flatly, as Pope's couplet takes it, this says many cope; read as Parimelazhagar does, 'many' means no one, and the unstated close is that she too will die. Kalaignar makes the turn explicit by adding, 'but I?'. The three verbs slow down the task of enduring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1161":{"book":"kural","num":1161,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் யானிஃதோ நோயை இறைப்பவர்க்கு","ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்"],"p":"I would hide this pain from others; but it (only) swells like a spring to those who drain it.","r":"The woman says she would hide this pain, but, as water does in a spring when one draws from it, it only rises.","n":"The commentators differ quietly over what the drawing out is. Kalaignar makes concealing itself the action, so hiding is what increases it. For Parimelazhagar, the concealment simply fails, as water wells up for one who draws from a spring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1162":{"book":"kural","num":1162,"t":["கரத்தலும் ஆற்றேன்இந் நோயைநோய் செய்தார்க்கு","உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"I cannot conceal this pain, nor can I relate it without shame to him who has caused it.","r":"The woman says she cannot hide this sickness, and telling the one who caused it also brings her shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a remarkable reason for the shame: if she sends a messenger, it will imply she is still alive. The grief that is meant to prove love would be disproved by being reported. Her friend has offered her two options, and she refuses both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1163":{"book":"kural","num":1163,"t":["காமமும் நாணும் உயிர்காவாத் தூங்கும்என்","நோனா உடம்பின் அகத்து"],"p":"(Both) lust and shame, with my soul for their shoulder pole balance themselves on a body that cannot bear them.","r":"The woman says love and shame hang from the ends of a carrying pole, which is her life, within her body that can't bear it.","n":"The verb தூங்கும் conveys the balance: as Parimelazhagar reads it, neither side outweighs the other, so she can't commit to either, and the pole will snap. Kalaignar shifts the image so that the body is the pole; the verse says the life is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1164":{"book":"kural","num":1164,"t":["காமக் கடல்மன்னும் உண்டே அதுநீந்தும்","ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்"],"p":"There is indeed a flood of lust; but there is no raft of safety to cross it with.","r":"The woman says the sea of love certainly exists, and the safe raft for crossing it certainly doesn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the missing raft as a reproach to the friend, who should have sent word and been the raft. Manakkudavar agrees that she means she has no helper. It is more personal than a cosmic complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1165":{"book":"kural","num":1165,"t":["துப்பின் எவனாவர் மன்கொல் துயர்வரவு","நட்பினுள் ஆற்று பவர்"],"p":"He who can produce sorrow from friendship, what can he not bring forth out of enmity ?","r":"The woman asks what those who can bring such grief in friendship would do in enmity.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a rebuke to the friend, for not sending a messenger when she could have prevented the grief.","தூது விடாமை நோக்கித் தோழியோடு புலந்து சொல்லியது"],["kalaignar","It is about the lover, who gives her the grief of parting even while they are close.",""]],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1166":{"book":"kural","num":1166,"t":["இன்பம் கடல்மற்றுக் காமம் அஃதடுங்கால்","துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது"],"p":"The pleasure of lust is (as great as) the sea; but the pain of lu st is far greater.","r":"The woman says the pleasure of love is a sea, and when love afflicts her, the pain is greater than the sea.","n":"This rejects a consoling arithmetic. The friend has said that those who've had the joy should expect the pain, and the woman's point, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, is that an equal measure could be borne, but this isn't equal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1167":{"book":"kural","num":1167,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்","யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்"],"p":"I have swam across the terrible flood of lust, but have not seen its shore; even at midnight I am alone; still I live.","r":"The woman says she swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore, and even at midnight there is only herself.","n":"யானே உளேன் can mean 'I alone am here', and for Parimelazhagar also 'I am still alive', which he attributes to some evil deed's result. That idea of past deeds is his and isn't in the verse. Pope's prose follows him by adding 'still I live'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1168":{"book":"kural","num":1168,"t":["மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி அளித்திரா","என்னல்லது இல்லை துணை"],"p":"The night which graciously lulls to sleep all living creatures, has me alone for her companion.","r":"The woman says the night, which puts all living beings to sleep, is to be pitied, as it has no companion but her.","n":"Everything turns on அளித்து. It is usually a word of pity or grace, and Parimelazhagar marks it as contempt: the night has foolishly chosen as its companion someone about to die. She isn't lamenting her solitude so much as mocking her only companion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1169":{"book":"kural","num":1169,"t":["> கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய இந்நாள்","நெடிய கழியும் இரா"],"p":"The long nights of these days are far more cruel than the heartless one who is torturing me.","r":"The woman says these long nights passing now are crueller than the cruelty of that cruel man.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the implicit contrast: nights that were short when they were happy have now grown long. The word கொடியார், 'the cruel one', replaces his name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1170":{"book":"kural","num":1170,"t":["உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின் வெள்ளநீர்","நீந்தல மன்னோஎன் கண்"],"p":"Could mine eyes travel like my thoughts to the abode (of my absent lord), they would not swim in this flood of tears.","r":"The woman says that if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does, they wouldn't be swimming in a flood of tears.","n":"For Parimelazhagar, the mind's journey is only thought, while eyes need a body to walk there, so what the heart can do isn't really a substitute for seeing. He says this verse opens the next chapter, and the eyes' complaint begins here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1171":{"book":"kural","num":1171,"t":["கண்தாம் கலுழ்வ தெவன்கொலோ தண்டாநோய்","தாம்காட்ட யாம்கண் டது"],"p":"As this incurable malady has been caused by my eyes which showed (him) to me, why should they now weep for (him).","r":"The woman asks why her eyes weep, when it was they who showed him to her, and so she came to know this unending sickness.","n":"The verse says 'we saw the sickness' with no mention of him, and the object of 'showed' has to be supplied, as Parimelazhagar says. In her account, seeing him and seeing the sickness are the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1172":{"book":"kural","num":1172,"t":["தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய உண்கண் பரிந்துணராப்","பைதல் உழப்பது எவன்?"],"p":"The dyed eyes that (then) looked without foresight, why should they now endure sorrow, without feeling sharply (their own fault).","r":"The woman asks why these kohl-lined eyes, which looked as they did, now suffer in misery without coming to terms with it.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெரிந்துணரா means they looked without considering what would follow, so they should now bear what they caused.","மேல் விளைவதனை ஆராய்ந்தறியாது அன்று காதலரை நோக்கி நின்ற உண்கண்கள்"],["manakkudavar","It means the opposite: they looked after judging him good, and now they no longer recognise that goodness.","முன்பு அவர் நல்லரென்று தெரிந்து உணர்ந்து நோக்கிய உண்கண் கள்"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1173":{"book":"kural","num":1173,"t":["கதுமெனத் தாநோக்கித் தாமே கலுழும்","இதுநகத் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"They themselves looked eagerly (on him) and now they weep. Is not this to be laughed at ?","r":"The woman says it's laughable that her eyes looked at him so quickly on their own and now weep on their own.","n":"The repeated தாம் is the whole charge: they acted of their own accord both times. Manakkudavar pictures real laughter, a distracted woman laughing through her distress and being asked why, which makes this more disturbing than wit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1174":{"book":"kural","num":1174,"t":["பெயலாற்றா நீருலந்த உண்கண் உயலாற்றா","உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து"],"p":"These painted eyes have caused me a lasting mortal disease; and now they can weep no more, the tears having dried up.","r":"The woman says her kohl-lined eyes, having fixed in her an inescapable, fatal sickness, have dried up and can no longer even weep.","n":"Dry eyes come after much weeping. What seems a relief is presented as the eyes' punishment: in Parimelazhagar's words, harm they did her has come back to them by itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1175":{"book":"kural","num":1175,"t":["படலாற்றா பைதல் உழக்கும் கடலாற்றாக்","காமநோய் செய்தஎன் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes have caused me a lust that is greater than the sea and (they themselves) endure the torture of sleeplessness.","r":"The woman says her eyes, which gave her a sickness too large for the sea, now suffer torments and can't sleep.","n":"The commentators use the language of moral consequence with some wit: Parimelazhagar says அத் தீவினையால், 'by that evil deed', and Manakkudavar has harm returning to those who harm others. The verse just shows the sequence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1176":{"book":"kural","num":1176,"t":["ஓஒ இனிதே எமக்கிந்நோய் செய்தகண்","தாஅம் இதற்பட் டது"],"p":"The eyes that have given me this disease have themselves been seized with this (suffering). Oh! I am much delighted.","r":"The woman exclaims how sweet it is that the eyes that gave her this sickness have fallen into it themselves.","n":"Vengeance against one's own organ can only hurt the avenger, and that absurdity is the point. Parimelazhagar's gloss, 'as if my suffering had ended', keeps the distance between the delight and any real relief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1177":{"book":"kural","num":1177,"t":["உழந்துழந் துள்நீர் அறுக விழைந்திழைந்து","வேண்டி அவர்க்கண்ட கண்"],"p":"The eyes that became tender and gazed intently on him, may they suffer so much as to dry up the fountain of their tears.","r":"The woman curses them: may the eyes that longed and melted and begged to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone.","n":"The verse echoes itself: உழந்துழந்து against விழைந்திழைந்து, suffering matching longing in the same rhythm. Pay-back is built into the sound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1178":{"book":"kural","num":1178,"t":["பேணாது பெட்டார் உளர்மன்னோ மற்றவர்க்","காணாது அமைவில கண்"],"p":"He is indeed here who loved me with his lips but not with his heart but mine eyes suffer from not seeing him.","r":"The woman says there is one who desired without cherishing, and her eyes can't be at peace without seeing him.","n":"The syntax allows several readings, and the second one Parimelazhagar gives rests on a different text, கொண்கனை. I'm unsure which is best. The steady part is the last clause: whatever he is, the eyes can't do without him.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says he hasn't really gone away. She replies: yes, someone who loved in words and not at heart is here, but what use is that if the eyes don't see him?","நெஞ்சால் விழையாது வைத்துச் சொல் மாத்திரத்தால் விழைந்தவர் இவ்விடத்தே உளர்"],["manakkudavar","It's a question, with no claim that he's near: does anyone desire what shouldn't be desired? He has gone without wishing to see them, and the eyes still can't settle.","விரும்பத்தகாததனை விரும்புவாரும் உளரோ?"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1179":{"book":"kural","num":1179,"t":["வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா ஆயிடை","ஆரஞர் உற்றன கண்"],"p":"When he is away they do not sleep; when he is present they do not sleep; in either case, mine eyes endure unbearable agony.","r":"The woman says that when he doesn't come her eyes don't sleep, and when he does they still don't sleep, so either way they suffer terribly.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies the two reasons: they stay up watching for him, and when he is there they stay up for fear he'll leave. Kalaignar's gloss reads this more generally, as the lot of all lovers' eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1180":{"book":"kural","num":1180,"t":["மறைபெறல் ஊரார்க்கு அரிதன்றால் எம்போல்","அறைபறை கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"It is not difficult for the people of this place to understand the secret of those whose eyes, like mine, are as it were beaten drums.","r":"The woman says it isn't hard for the town to learn the secret in the hearts of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums.","n":"The eyes' final offence is treason. The friend has asked her to hide his cruelty so the town won't blame him, and she answers that the eyes make that impossible. Parimelazhagar notes the variant reading அறைபறைக் கண்ணார், which removes the poetic strain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","alar","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1181":{"book":"kural","num":1181,"t":["நயந்தவர்க்கு நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன் பசந்தவென்","பண்பியார்க்கு உரைக்கோ பிற"],"p":"I who (then) consented to the absence of my loving lord, to whom can I (now) relate the fact of my having turned sallow.","r":"The woman, to herself, asks to whom she can describe her pallor, since she herself agreed to the absence of the man who loves her.","n":"Consent has closed off complaint. Parimelazhagar reads every word of this as her logic: having agreed, she feels it would be a further wrong even to tell anyone. 1181 is the first verse of the chapter, so the book's most visible symptom starts with her taking responsibility for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1182":{"book":"kural","num":1182,"t":["அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால் இவர்தந்தென்","மேனிமேல் ஊரும் பசப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The woman says the pallor climbs on and rides over her body, proud that he is the one who gave it.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the chariot-and-rider sense of இவர்தல் and adds that this is a claim of right, like a retainer's, which she can't resist because it comes from him. By his reading she is also comforting her friend: it's riding me by right, so don't worry. For Manakkudavar, she is saying that it isn't her doing, so take it up with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1183":{"book":"kural","num":1183,"t":["சாயலும் நாணும் அவர்கொண்டார் கைம்மாறா","நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து"],"p":"He has taken (away) my beauty and modesty, and given me instead disease and sallowness.","r":"The woman says he took away her grace and modesty, and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange.","n":"The language is commercial: கைம்மாறா, 'in exchange'. Parimelazhagar notices the crossed pairing, in which sickness replaces modesty, since the more she tries to suppress it, the more it grows, and pallor replaces grace. He takes சாயல் as bodily beauty, and Manakkudavar as femininity (பெண்மை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1184":{"book":"kural","num":1184,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் உரைப்பது அவர்திறமால்","கள்ளம் பிறவோ பசப்பு"],"p":"I think (of him); and what I speak about is but his excellence; still is there sallowness; and this is","r":"The woman says she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits, and still the pallor comes, a kind of cheating.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a small argument: body follows mind and speech, and both are faithfully his, so the body shouldn't have paled. The verse never accuses her of faithlessness; the pallor is the deceiver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1185":{"book":"kural","num":1185,"t":["உவக்காண்எம் காதலர் செல்வார் இவக்காண்என்","மேனி பசப்பூர் வது"],"p":"Just as my lover departed then, did not sallowness spread here on my person ?","r":"The woman says: look there, her lover is going, and look here, the pallor is spreading over her body.","n":"The verse is built only from the pointing words உவக்காண் and இவக்காண், which turn distance into simultaneity. Parimelazhagar compares his leaving and the pallor's coming to the changing of day and night. He treats it as a memory of a past parting, and Manakkudavar as happening right now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1186":{"book":"kural","num":1186,"t":["விளக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் இருளேபோல் கொண்கன்","முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் பசப்பு"],"p":"Just as darkness waits for the failing light; so does sallowness wait for the laxity of my husband's intercourse.","r":"The woman says pallor watches for her lover's embrace to weaken, as darkness watches for a lamp to falter.","n":"Darkness isn't an enemy that attacks; it is simply what is there when the lamp fails. Read so, pallor is her natural state, and his embrace is what holds it off. Parimelazhagar's conclusion: if it waits for this tiny opening, what won't it do in a real separation?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1187":{"book":"kural","num":1187,"t":["புல்லிக் கிடந்தேன் புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்","அள்ளிக்கொள் வற்றே பசப்பு"],"p":"I who was in close embrace just turned aside and the moment I did so, sallowness came on me like something to be seized on.","r":"The woman says she lay in his embrace and turned slightly, and at that the pallor was on her so thick it could be scooped up.","n":"This is the chapter's most vivid hyperbole, and it is almost comic in its domesticity. Manakkudavar's setting makes it sad: he has left without telling her, and the friend sees the change in her next morning and is told this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1188":{"book":"kural","num":1188,"t":["பசந்தாள் இவள்என்பது அல்லால் இவளைத்","துறந்தார் அவர்என்பார் இல்"],"p":"Besides those who say \"she has turned sallow\" there are none who say \"he has forsaken her\".","r":"The woman says people will say she has grown pale, and no one says he has left her.","n":"A flat remark about how blame is apportioned. The cause is invisible and the symptom is on her skin, so she bears the talk. Parimelazhagar reads the plural 'people' as aimed at the friend, whom she refuses to name in her anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1189":{"book":"kural","num":1189,"t":["பசக்கமன் பட்டாங்கென் மேனி நயப்பித்தார்","நன்னிலையர் ஆவர் எனின்"],"p":"If he is clear of guilt who has conciliated me (to his departure) let my body suffer its due and turn sallow.","r":"The woman says let her body go pale as it will, if the man who persuaded her is in a good state.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்னிலையர் means blameless in the friend's eyes: since he must be held faultless, what does her body matter? This is bitter.","முன் இப்பிரிவின் கொடுமையறியாத என்னை இதற்கு உடம்படுத்திப் பிரிந்தவர் தவறிலராகவே வேண்டுவது"],["manakkudavar","The condition means he should suffer as she does, which is close to a curse.","நம்மைக் காதலித்த வரும் நம்மைப் போலத் துன்பமுறுவராயின்"],["kalaignar","It means if he is well, and so it is a loving sacrifice.",""]],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1190":{"book":"kural","num":1190,"t":["பசப்பெனப் பேர்பெறுதல் நன்றே நயப்பித்தார்","நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்"],"p":"It would be good to be said of me that I have turned sallow, if friends do not reproach with unkindness him who pleased me (then).","r":"The woman says that being called pale is good, if they won't spread talk of how unkind the man who won her has been.","n":"Parimelazhagar pushes பசப்பெனப் பேர் to its limit: not 'she has pallor' but 'she is pallor', a loss of identity she says she will welcome. He also says she speaks in scorn at the friend, who has criticised him to help her. Her good name is what she is ready to give for his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1191":{"book":"kural","num":1191,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே","காமத்துக் காழில் கனி"],"p":"The women who are beloved by those whom they love, have they have not got the stone-less fruit of sexual delight ?","r":"The woman says those who are loved by the ones they love have obtained the stoneless fruit of love.","n":"A fruit without a stone is enjoyment with nothing to spit out. The verse is general and ungendered, while Parimelazhagar makes it 'women loved by husbands', adds that this needs good deeds from before, and supplies the unstated close that she hasn't got this fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1192":{"book":"kural","num":1192,"t":["வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால் வீழ்வார்க்கு","வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி"],"p":"The bestowal of love by the beloved on those who love them is like the rain raining (at the proper season) on those who live by it.","r":"The woman says the grace shown by the beloved to the one who loves them is like the rain to those who live by it.","n":"The grace here is measured and timely, as rain is, and Parimelazhagar takes that to include a man who returns from duty when he should. He brings out the shadow: if the rain fails, those dependent on it die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1193":{"book":"kural","num":1193,"t":["வீழுநர் வீழப் படுவார்க்கு அமையுமே","வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"The pride that says \"we shall live\" suits only those who are loved by their beloved (husbands).","r":"The woman says the pride of saying 'we shall live' suits only those who are loved by those they love.","n":"செருக்கு, 'pride', is usually a vice in the Kural, and here it is a privilege she envies. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree on the corollary: what suits her is death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1194":{"book":"kural","num":1194,"t":["வீழப் படுவார் கெழீஇயிலர் தாம்வீழ்வார்","வீழப் படாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"Even those who are esteemed (by other women) are devoid of excellence, if they are not loved by their beloved.","r":"The woman says that those who are esteemed are still without something, if they aren't loved by the one they love.","n":"Manakkudavar ends with a proverb, கொண்டான் கயிற் கண்டான் காயும்: if the husband is angry at her, all who see her will be too. That gives a social reason why public esteem can't survive this loss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The esteemed one is honoured by chaste women, and கெழீஇயிலர் means she lacks good deeds, that is, she has bad karma.","கெழீஇயின்மை; நல்வினையின்மை"],["manakkudavar","The esteemed one is honoured by the world, and the word means she becomes one without being desired.","விருப்பமில்லா ராவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about the partner who withdraws love, who is then thought to lack the sense of friendship.",""]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1195":{"book":"kural","num":1195,"t":["நாம்காதல் கொண்டார் நமக்கெவன் செய்பவோ","தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை"],"p":"He who is beloved by me, what will he do to me, if I am not beloved by him ?","r":"The woman asks what those she loves can do for her if they themselves don't love her.","n":"It answers a compliment. Parimelazhagar has the friend say that she bore this well because of her love; the reply is that love has brought her only pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1196":{"book":"kural","num":1196,"t":["ஒருதலையான் இன்னாது காமம்காப் போல","இருதலை யானும் இனிது"],"p":"Lust, like the weight of the KAVADI, pains if it lies in one end only but pleases if it is in both.","r":"The woman says love is painful when it is on one side only, and sweet on both sides, like the load of a carrying pole.","n":"It is a physical fact that a load at both ends is easier than half that load at one. The verse therefore doesn't claim only that shared love is pleasant; shared love is also lighter to carry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1197":{"book":"kural","num":1197,"t":["பருவரலும் பைதலும் காணான்கொல் காமன்","ஒருவர்கண் நின்றொழுகு வான்"],"p":"Would not cupid who abides and contends in one party (only) witness the pain and sorrow (in that party)?","r":"The woman asks whether Kaman, who works on one side alone, doesn't see her misery and grief.","n":"A rare appearance of a named love god in the Kural, and the verse makes him a partial combatant. The older commentators take the theology seriously: for Parimelazhagar, a god should know all and be impartial, and for Manakkudavar, he wouldn't hurt her if he saw, being a god. Kalaignar's version has him as simply indifferent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1198":{"book":"kural","num":1198,"t":["வீழ்வாரின் இன்சொல் பெறாஅது உலகத்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"There is no one in the world so hard-hearted as those who can live without receiving (even) a kind word from their beloved.","r":"The woman says that no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without receiving a kind word from their beloved.","n":"வன்கண் is ambiguous between hardness and courage, and Pope chooses 'braver'. The commentators read it as an accusation she makes against herself. Parimelazhagar notes that any word at all from him would count as sweet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1199":{"book":"kural","num":1199,"t":["நசைஇயார் நல்கார் எனினும் அவர்மாட்டு","இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு"],"p":"Though my beloved bestows no love on one, still are his words sweet to my ears.","r":"The woman says that though the man she longs for gives no grace, even இசை from him is sweet to her ears.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இசை means any word at all about him, even that he isn't coming, and she hasn't got even that.","'அவர் வாரார் என்னுஞ் சொல்லாயினும் அமையும்,' என்பதுபட நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The word means his fame, and words that praise him are honey to her.",""]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1200":{"book":"kural","num":1200,"t":["உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய் கடலைச்","செறாஅஅய் வாழிய நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Live, O my soul, would you who relate your great sorrow to strangers, try rather to fill up your own sea (of sorrow).","r":"The woman tells her heart that it wants to tell its pain to one who is not close, and that it may as well try to fill up the sea.","n":"வாழிய, 'may you live', is sarcasm here for Parimelazhagar. The chapter ends with her declining what she spent the chapter wanting, and the sea from 1137 and 1164 comes back as a job to be done with a basket. It is an image of utter futility.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1201":{"book":"kural","num":1201,"t":["உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ் செய்தலால்","கள்ளினும் காமம் இனிது"],"p":"Sexuality is sweeter than liquor, because when remembered, it creates a most rapturous delight.","r":"The man says love is sweeter than liquor, because even thinking of it brings a great joy that doesn't fade.","n":"Parimelazhagar's headnote has him speaking to the friend who has carried his message: his claim is also a report of loneliness and faithfulness. The older comparison with liquor gains a new term here, since liquor needs drinking while love needs only memory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1202":{"book":"kural","num":1202,"t":["எனைத்தொனறு ஏனிதேகாண் காமம்தாம் வீழ்வார்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று ஏல்"],"p":"Even to think of one's beloved gives one no pain. Sexuality, in any degree, is always delightful.","r":"Love is sweet in every way, since thinking of the beloved brings no pain; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says how he has coped: love is sweet in union and in parting alike.","தான் ஆற்றிய வகை கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman rejects the friend's warning that her inability to endure will bring pain.","இது நீ இவ்வாறு ஆற்றாயாகின்றது துன்பம் பயக்குமென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் மறுத்துக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1203":{"book":"kural","num":1203,"t":["நினைப்பவர் போன்று நினையார்கொல் தும்மல்","சினைப்பது போன்று கெடும்"],"p":"I feel as if I am going to sneeze but do not, and (therefore) my beloved is about to think (of me) but does not.","r":"The woman wonders if he seems about to think of her and doesn't, since a sneeze seems to come and then dies away.","n":"She reads her own body for evidence about his mind, using a folk belief that a sneeze means a distant loved one is thinking of you. Manakkudavar notes that she is repeating what ordinary women say, a rare moment of commentary on popular custom. An aborted sneeze makes for deflating, near-comic evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1204":{"book":"kural","num":1204,"t":["யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து எந்நெஞ்சத்து","ஓஒ உளரே அவர்"],"p":"He continues to abide in my soul, do I likewise abide in his ?","r":"The woman asks whether she too has a place in his heart, for, ah, he is always in hers.","n":"The verse uses the same indwelling that she argued for with such confidence in chapter 113, but now with doubt. Parimelazhagar extends the doubt into a tender either-or: is he kept away by unfinished work, or does he not remember her?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1205":{"book":"kural","num":1205,"t":["தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார் நாணார்கொல்","எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்"],"p":"He who has imprisoned me in his soul, is he ashamed to enter incessantly into mine.","r":"The woman asks whether he, who has barred her from his heart, isn't ashamed to keep coming into hers.","n":"Her thoughts of him are treated as his actions, so she can rebuke him for trespassing. கடிகொண்டார் means he has guarded his heart against her; Pope's prose, 'imprisoned me in his soul', gets this backwards, though his couplet has it right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1206":{"book":"kural","num":1206,"t":["மற்றியான் என்னுளேன் மன்னோ அவரொடி யான்","உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்"],"p":"I live by remembering my (former) intercourse with him; if it were not so, how could I live ?","r":"The woman asks what else she could live by, since she lives only by remembering the days she shared with him.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets it as a reply to the friend's advice to forget. He lists the things she lacks that would otherwise sustain her: his messenger, sending her own. Memory is what is left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1207":{"book":"kural","num":1207,"t":["மறப்பின் எவனாவன் மற்கொல் மறப்பறியேன்","உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்"],"p":"I have never forgotten (the pleasure); even to think of it burns my soul; could I live, if I should ever forget it ?","r":"The woman asks what would become of her if she forgot, since she doesn't know forgetting, and even remembering burns.","n":"A precise bind: remembering burns her, and forgetting would kill her, as Parimelazhagar supplies. Compare 1206, where memory is what keeps her alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1208":{"book":"kural","num":1208,"t":["எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார் அனைத்தன்றோ","காதலர் செய்யும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"He will not be angry however much I may think of him; is it not so much the delight my beloved affords me ?","r":"The woman says that however much she thinks of him, he doesn't get angry; that much is the honour her lover does her.","n":"All she can claim is that he doesn't object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar hears the sarcasm directed at her friend's promise that he will come and bring her joy. Manakkudavar defines grace as not getting angry even at a fault, so the 'honour' is very small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1209":{"book":"kural","num":1209,"t":["விளியுமென் இன்னுயிர் வேறல்லம் என்பார்","அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து"],"p":"My precious life is wasting away by thinking too much on the cruelty of him who said we were not different.","r":"The woman says her dear life is ending, as she thinks too much about the lack of grace in one who said they weren't separate.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a key distinction: in her own words, it's not the parting that is killing her, but his lack of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1210":{"book":"kural","num":1210,"t":["விடாஅது சென்றாரைக் கண்ணினால் காணப்","படாஅதி வாழி மதி"],"p":"May you live, O Moon! Do not set, that I mine see him who has departed without quitting my soul.","r":"The woman speaks to the moon, blessing it and asking about seeing with her eyes the man who went away without leaving her.","n":"விடாஅது சென்றாரை, 'who left without leaving', packs the whole chapter's paradox into two words.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She begs the moon not to set, so that their eyes can meet on it while both look at it.","மதி இருவரானும் நோக்கப்படுதலின் இருவர் கண்ணும் அதன்கண்ணே சேர்தல்."],["manakkudavar","She reproaches the moon for not setting: if it set she could sleep and see him in dreams.","பட்டதாயின், என் கண் உறங்கும்; உறங்கினால், அவரைக் காணலாமென் பது கருத்து."],["kalaignar","She asks it not to set, to give her light to seek him out with her eyes.",""]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1211":{"book":"kural","num":1211,"t":["காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவினுக்கு","யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து"],"p":"Where with shall I feast the dream which has brought me my dear one's messenger ?","r":"What feast can be given to the dream that came with a message from the lover? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The Kural's great social duty of hospitality is turned on a dream guest. Parimelazhagar's little note gets the joke exactly: it is a guest for which one can find nothing to serve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman herself saw the dream and says this.","தலைமகன் தூது வரக் கண்டாள் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend says she dreamed that messengers came, in order to comfort the woman.","இது தலைமகளாற்றுதற் பொருட்டுக் காதலர் வாராநின்றாரென்று தூதர் வாக் கனாக் கண்டேனென்று தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1212":{"book":"kural","num":1212,"t":["கயலுண்கண் யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சிற் கலந்தார்க்கு","உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன் மன்"],"p":"If my fish-like painted eyes should, at my begging, close in sleep, I could fully relate my sufferings to my lord.","r":"The woman says that if her carp-like kohl-lined eyes would sleep when she begs them to, she would tell the one who joined with her that she is surviving.","n":"She must beg her eyes for sleep, after cursing them in chapter 118. Parimelazhagar notes her reason for preferring this to a messenger: there are things kept back from messengers, or shortened, which she could tell him in full.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1213":{"book":"kural","num":1213,"t":["நனவினால் நல்கா தவரைக் கனவினால்","காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்"],"p":"My life lasts because in my dream I behold him who does not favour me in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says her life continues because she sees in her dreams the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes it as a reassurance to the friend who worries: 'don't worry, I'm bearing up'. It may comfort the friend, but what it actually says is hardly comforting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1214":{"book":"kural","num":1214,"t":["கனவினான் உண்டாகும் காமம் நனவினான்","நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு"],"p":"There is pleasure in my dream, because in it I seek and obtain him who does not visit me in my wakefulness.","r":"The woman says there is pleasure in dreams, since they seek out and bring her the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"The dream becomes an agent, a go-between travelling to where he is. That is what she wanted from her friend. Manakkudavar has the friend ask what use a dream is without real enjoyment, and the verse is her answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1215":{"book":"kural","num":1215,"t":["நனவினால் கண்டதூஉம் ஆங்கே கனவுந்தான்","கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது"],"p":"I saw him in my waking hours, and then it was pleasant; I see him just now in my dream, and it is (equally) pleasant.","r":"The woman says that what she saw in waking life was sweet only then, and likewise the dream is sweet at the time it is seen.","n":"A strong philosophical move: both are equally over when over. Waking joy has no special standing in memory. Parimelazhagar's gloss is plain: 'the two are equal for me', and what she had before was no more than this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1216":{"book":"kural","num":1216,"t":["நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின் கனவினால்","காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்"],"p":"Were there no such thing as wakefulness, my beloved (who visited me) in my dream would not depart from me.","r":"The woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover in the dream wouldn't leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies a charged word, பாவி, 'sinner', for waking, and reads ஒன்று as pointing up its cruelty. Waking is what barges in and parts them, so the person responsible for leaving is no longer him but waking itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1217":{"book":"kural","num":1217,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார் கனவனால்","என்எம்மைப் பீழிப் பது"],"p":"The cruel one who would not favour me in my wakefulness, what right has he to torture me in my dreams?","r":"The woman asks why the cruel one who gives no grace in waking life torments her in dreams.","n":"A flat reversal of the last six verses, with the comfort now a torment. Parimelazhagar explains: on waking she doesn't find him, and because he vanishes from her shoulder, that too brings pain. He also tests a proverb against her case, that what isn't in waking isn't in dreams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1218":{"book":"kural","num":1218,"t":["துஞ்சுங்கால் தோள்மேலர் ஆகி விழிக்குங்கால்","நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து"],"p":"When I am asleep he rests on my shoulders, (but) when I awake he hastens into my soul.","r":"The woman says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder, and when she wakes he quickly moves into her heart.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's reading she is defending him against her friend: he never leaves her at all. விரைந்து, 'quickly', gives the speed of a fugitive, and Manakkudavar hears the friend's tease that prompted it: where does he go when sleep ends?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1219":{"book":"kural","num":1219,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காரை நோவர் கனவினால்","காதலர்க் காணா தவர்"],"p":"They who have no dear ones to behold in their dreams blame him who visits me not in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says those who haven't seen their lovers in dreams are the ones who blame the man for not being gracious in waking life.","n":"This is an attack by pretending to speak about outsiders, as both commentators note. For Parimelazhagar she is hinting that the friend has no lover, which is a harsh thing to imply, and marks how angry the blaming makes her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1220":{"book":"kural","num":1220,"t":["நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர் கனவினால்","காணார்கொல் இவ்வூ ரவர்"],"p":"The women of this place say he has forsaken me in my wakefulness. I think they have not seen him visit me in my dreams.","r":"The woman says that the people of this town say he has left her in waking life, and asks if they don't see him in dreams.","n":"A surreal question, since no one else could see her dream. Parimelazhagar makes sense of it by way of the unity between her and her friend: if they are truly one, what the woman saw, the friend has seen too, and in not seeing she has become an outsider, 'this town'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1221":{"book":"kural","num":1221,"t":["மாலையோ அல்லை மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும்","வேலைநீ வாழி பொழுது"],"p":"Live, O you evening are you (the former) evening? No, you are the season that slays (married) women.","r":"The woman tells the hour, with a bitter blessing, that it isn't evening at all but வேலை, which eats the life of those once wed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேலை is a time word, the hour of ending, and he mentions but sets aside others who take it as 'you've become a spear'.","மகளிர் உயிரையுண்ணும் இறுதிக்காலமாய் இருந்தாய்."],["manakkudavar","The word is வேல் plus ஐ: you have become a spear that eats life. He adds evening is hot, so it's not really evening.","உயிரை உண்பதாகிய வொரு வேலாயிருந்தாய்"],["kalaignar","Also a spear that drinks life, with the blessing made explicit.",""]],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1222":{"book":"kural","num":1222,"t":["புன்கண்ணை வாழி மருள்மாலை எம்கேள்போல்","வன்கண்ண தோநின் துணை"],"p":"A long life to you, O dark evening! You are sightless. Is your help-mate (also) as hard-hearted as mine.","r":"The woman says to the bewildered evening, may you live, you look sad-eyed; is your partner as hard-eyed as mine?","n":"The tender moment of the chapter: she stops fighting the hour and sees it as a fellow sufferer. Parimelazhagar explains that the pale evening's fading of light is what suggests its grief, and says she has put her own inner state onto it. The word மருள் means both the mixing of day and night and being confused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1223":{"book":"kural","num":1223,"t":["பனிஅரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை துனிஅரும்பித்","துன்பம் வளர வரும்"],"p":"The evening that (once) came in with trembling and dimness (now) brings me an aversion for life and increasing sorrow.","r":"The woman says that evening, which comes with chill and pale, now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses துனி strongly, as disgust with living. Evening used to tremble and pale before her when she was happy; now she trembles before it. Manakkudavar keeps the reversal smaller, with today's evening following yesterday's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1224":{"book":"kural","num":1224,"t":["காதலர் இல்வழி மாலை கொலைக்களத்து","ஏதிலர் போல வரும்"],"p":"In the absence of my lover, evening comes in like slayers on the field of slaughter.","r":"The woman says that when her lover is absent, evening comes like enemies coming to a killing ground.","n":"ஏதிலர் is the word she has been using of the town and of the man's accusers; Parimelazhagar glosses it here as 'those with no grace at all', executioners. That evening was once her friend is part of the pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1225":{"book":"kural","num":1225,"t":["காலைக்குச் செய்தநன்று என்கொல் எவன்கொல்யான்","மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை?"],"p":"What good have I done to morning (and) what evil to evening?","r":"The woman asks what good she has done to morning and what enmity she has shown to evening.","n":"It is framed as a legal puzzle about reward and punishment. Both commentators note a hidden reversal: morning used to be the hated hour, when they feared his leaving, and now it's the shore at the end of the flood of night. Evening made the opposite change.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1226":{"book":"kural","num":1226,"t":["மாலைநோய் செய்தல் மணந்தார் அகலாத","காலை அறிந்த திலேன்"],"p":"Previous to my husband's departure, I know not the painful nature of evening.","r":"The woman says she didn't know that evening brings sickness, while he who wed her had not yet left.","n":"It answers the most painful question: why did you consent? Parimelazhagar spells out that she would have refused had she known this. So 1181's self-accusation gets its answer: she agreed in ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1227":{"book":"kural","num":1227,"t":["காலை அரும்பிப் பகலெல்லாம் போதாகி","மாலை மலரும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady buds forth in the morning, expands all day long and blossoms in the evening.","r":"The woman says this sickness buds in the morning, ripens through the day and opens in the evening.","n":"A flower is made a sickness, in a lovely, cruel inversion. Parimelazhagar's reasons are as vivid as the verse: at morning she is still comforted by a dream, by day memory thins, and at evening she watches creatures and people returning to their mates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1228":{"book":"kural","num":1228,"t":["அழல்போலும் மாலைக்குத் தூதாகி ஆயன்","குழல்போலும் கொல்லும் படை"],"p":"The shepherd's flute now sounds as a fiery forerunner of night, and is become a weapon that slays (me).","r":"The woman says the herdsman's flute is a messenger for the burning evening and a weapon that kills.","n":"The one sound in the chapter, pastoral and sweet in itself. Parimelazhagar reads it as a change from 'pleasant before' to deadly now. Evening has its own envoy and arms, like a king making war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1229":{"book":"kural","num":1229,"t":["பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும் மதிமருண்டு","மாலை படர்தரும் போழ்து"],"p":"When night comes on confusing (everyone's) mind, the (whole) town will lose its sense and be plunged in sorrow.","r":"The woman says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind, the whole town is bewildered and suffers.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is missing in this source, but his note says the unstated thought is 'I shall die', and he treats evening as Death, implying that the town grieves for her. He adds that others read it as her own mind reeling, and with so little of his comment left, I can't say which he preferred in full.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1230":{"book":"kural","num":1230,"t":["பொருள்மாலை யாளரை உள்ளி மருள்மாலை","மாயும்என் மாயா உயிர்"],"p":"My (hitherto) unextinguished life is now lost in this bewildering night at the thought of him who","r":"The woman says that thinking of the one whose nature is wealth, in this confused evening, her life that wouldn't die is dying.","n":"The wordplay does much of the work: பொருள்மாலை, 'wealth-natured', against மருள்மாலை, 'confused evening', and மாயும் against மாயா. For Parimelazhagar, he has broken his word to keep to wealth, so he has lost his nature to it. Manakkudavar says she takes him to have neither love nor virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1231":{"book":"kural","num":1231,"t":["சிறுமை நமக்கொழியச் சேட்சென்றார் உள்ளி","நறுமலர் நாணின கண்"],"p":"While we endure the unbearable sorrow, your eyes weep for him who is gone afar, and shun (the","r":"The friend tells her that as she thinks of the man gone far away, leaving them this misery, her eyes are ashamed before fragrant flowers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He left the pain with them when he went far away, and the friend is the speaker.","இவ்வாற்றாமை நம்கண்ணே நிற்பத் தாம் சேணிடைச் சென்ற காதலரை"],["manakkudavar","He went so that their hardship would end, which gives his journey a very different motive.","நமக்குத் துன்பம் ஒழிய வேண்டி, நெடு நெறிக்கண் சென்றாரை"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1232":{"book":"kural","num":1232,"t":["நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்","பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்"],"p":"The discoloured eyes that shed tears p rofusely seem to betray the unkindness of our beloved.","r":"The friend says that her eyes, pale and running with tears, seem to be telling others of the unkindness of the one they desired.","n":"போலும், 'seem', is doing precise work. Parimelazhagar calls it inference: others deduce what the eyes don't actually say. For the friend, the point is that weeping exposes him, so stop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pasalai","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1233":{"book":"kural","num":1233,"t":["தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்","மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders that swelled on the day of our union (now) seem to announce our separation clearly (to the public).","r":"Her shoulders, which swelled on the day of union, seem to announce loudly that he has left; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"The record needs both moments. Parimelazhagar says those who saw the swelling and now the wasting will quickly work out what happened. It is a before-and-after that is hard to explain away.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend addresses her: your shoulders are doing what they shouldn't.","இன்ப மிகுதியால் பூரித்த நின் தோள்கள்"],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to her friend, who has seen her shoulders waste.","பிரிவிடை யாற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் தனது தோள் வாட்ட முற்றது கண்ட தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1234":{"book":"kural","num":1234,"t":["பணைநீங்கிப் பைந்தொடி சோரும் துணைநீங்கித்","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"In the absence of your consort, your shoulders having lost their former beauty and fulness, your bracelets of pure gold have become loose.","r":"Her shoulders, their old beauty faded with her partner gone, have lost their fullness, and her bright bangles slip; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"தொல்கவின், 'old beauty', matters: for Parimelazhagar she has lost both the beauty he gave and the beauty she had before him. Commentators also differ over the bangle, gold for Parimelazhagar, emerald for Manakkudavar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says these shoulders are betraying his lack of love and his false word.","இவை இங்ஙனம் செயற்பாலவல்ல."],["manakkudavar","The woman tells her friend that her shoulders are now thinning as well as losing their beauty.","தோள் அழகழிதலே யன்றி மெலி வதுஞ் செய்யாநின்றதென்று தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1235":{"book":"kural","num":1235,"t":["கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும் தொடியொடு","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"The (loosened) bracelets, and the shoulders from which the old beauty has faded, relate the cruelty of the pitiless one.","r":"The friend says the shoulders, with bangles lost and old beauty faded, are declaring the cruelty of the cruel one.","n":"The friend now says openly what 1232-1234 only implied, with both commentators attributing it to her. Manakkudavar gives the purpose: she blames him to help the woman bear up. That prepares the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1236":{"book":"kural","num":1236,"t":["தொடியொடு தோள்நெகிழ நோவல் அவரைக்","கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து"],"p":"I am greatly pained to hear you call him a cruel man, just because your shoulders are reduced and your bracelets loosened.","r":"The woman tells her friend that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what hurts is hearing him called cruel.","n":"The friend's strategy has backfired. Parimelazhagar spells out the claim: it's not his absence I can't bear, but what you say. Kalaignar widens the accusers from the friend to anyone who sees her. It's the same defence as in 1190.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1237":{"book":"kural","num":1237,"t":["பாடு பெறுதியோ நெஞ்சே கொடியார்க்கென்","வாடுதோட் பூசல் உரைத்து"],"p":"Can you O my soul! gain glory by relating to the (so-called) cruel one the clamour of my fading shoulders?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it could win any glory by telling the 'cruel' one of the uproar over her wasting shoulders.","n":"A sardonic challenge. For Parimelazhagar, she says கொடியார் only in quotation, as in 'the one they call cruel'. He reads பூசல் as the friend's blame and her own distress, while Manakkudavar reads it as the town's gossip.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","melivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1238":{"book":"kural","num":1238,"t":["முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது","பைந்தொடிப் பேதை நுதல்"],"p":"When I once loosened the arms that were in embrace, the forehead of the gold-braceleted women turned sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when he once loosened the arms that held her, the forehead of the girl with bright bangles turned pale.","n":"The voice changes suddenly: we have been with her, and now he is far away and remembering. The conclusion, as Parimelazhagar gives it: if that much did this, what will this absence do? I must go quickly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He loosened his arms out of tenderness, thinking the tight embrace would hurt her.","'இவட்கு நோம்' என்று கருதி ஒரு ஞான்று யான் நெகிழ்ந்தேனாக"],["manakkudavar","He loosened them because he was intending to leave, and she sensed this.","யான் பிரிவதாக நினைத்து, அவள் முயங்கியகைகளை நீக்கினே னாக"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1239":{"book":"kural","num":1239,"t":["முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழப் பசப்புற்ற","பேதை பெருமழைக் கண்"],"p":"When but a breath of breeze penetrated our embrace, her large cool eyes became sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when a cool breeze slipped between them in their embrace, the girl's large cool eyes grew pale.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the arithmetic: if a breeze could do this, what about mountains, forests and whole countries now between them? Manakkudavar says three verses of the man's speech begin here, which is a different division from Parimelazhagar's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1240":{"book":"kural","num":1240,"t":["கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே","ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு"],"p":"Was it at the sight of what the bright forehead had done that the sallowness of her eyes became sad?","r":"The man asks whether the pallor in her eyes itself grew distressed on seeing what her bright forehead had done.","n":"An odd and very ingenious image, and the commentary has to work hard. For Parimelazhagar, the eye's pallor is ashamed because the forehead paled faster, so her parts are competing to lose their beauty, and he should hurry. I find this plausible but can't be sure the verse means that much.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1241":{"book":"kural","num":1241,"t":["நினைத்தொன்று சொல்லாயோ நெஞ்சே எனைத்தொன்றும்","எவ்வநோய் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து"],"p":"O my soul, will you not think and tell me some medicine be it what it may, that can cure this incurable malady?","r":"The woman asks her heart to think and tell her of some medicine, of whatever kind, for this incurable sickness.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes எனைத்தொன்றும், 'of any kind', very far: even a remedy that means giving up shame, which is dearer than life. The heart is asked because she herself no longer has the power to know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1242":{"book":"kural","num":1242,"t":["காதல் அவரிலர் ஆகநீ நோவது","பேதைமை வாழியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"May you live, O my soul! While he is without love, for you to suffer is (simple) folly.","r":"The woman tells her heart, with a mocking blessing, that it's folly for it to suffer when he has no love.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a surprising purpose in this: it isn't telling the heart to give up, but to stop waiting and go, since going would be wise. On this reading, it's the first move towards a woman going after a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1243":{"book":"kural","num":1243,"t":["இருந்துள்ளி என்பரிதல் நெஞ்சே பரிந்துள்ளல்","பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்"],"p":"O my soul! why remain (here) and suffer thinking (of him)? There are no lewd thoughts (of you) in him who has caused you this disease of sorrow.","r":"The woman asks her heart why it sits and suffers, thinking of him, when the one who caused this sickness has no thought of pity.","n":"இருந்து, 'sitting here', is the barb, because the alternatives are stated in Parimelazhagar's gloss: neither going to him nor dying. Waiting has become the worst option.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1244":{"book":"kural","num":1244,"t":["கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி நெஞ்சே இவையென்னைத்","தின்னும் அவர்க்காணல் உற்று"],"p":"O my soul! take my eyes also with you, (if not), these would eat me up (in their desire) to see him.","r":"The woman tells her heart to take the eyes with it if it goes, since they are eating her alive with longing to see him.","n":"A practical request and one of the book's most physical: the body has parts she wants to send away. By Parimelazhagar's reading, this is a way to mean that she herself will go, since she can't part with her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1245":{"book":"kural","num":1245,"t":["செற்றார் எனக்கை விடல்உண்டோ நெஞ்சேயாம்","உற்றால் உறாஅ தவர்"],"p":"O my soul! can he who loves not though he is beloved, be forsaken saying he hates me (now)?","r":"The woman asks her heart if one can really abandon him as an enemy, simply because he doesn't respond when she draws close.","n":"The commentators shift the emphasis subtly. For Parimelazhagar, she lacks the strength to abandon him; for Manakkudavar, the issue is whether it would be proper. One treats loyalty as weakness, and the other treats it as what's right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1246":{"book":"kural","num":1246,"t":["கலந்துணர்த்தும் காதலர்க் கண்டாற் புலந்துணராய்","பொய்க்காய்வு காய்திஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! when you see the dear one who remove dislike by intercourse, you are displeased and continue to be so. Nay, your displeasure is (simply) false.","r":"The woman tells her heart that when it sees the man who ends quarrels by union, it can't stay angry, and so its present anger is false.","n":"She exposes her own anger as fake, calling it பொய்க்காய்வு. The heart is angry only while he's absent, and is no use when it would count.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1247":{"book":"kural","num":1247,"t":["காமம் விடுஒன்றோ நாண்விடு நன்னெஞ்சே","யானோ பொறேன்இவ் விரண்டு"],"p":"O my good soul, give up either lust or honour, as for me I can endure neither.","r":"The woman tells her good heart to give up either desire or shame, since she can't bear both.","n":"நன்னெஞ்சே, 'good heart', isn't flattery alone. For Parimelazhagar, the heart is good because holding on to both keeps her womanhood intact, but she can't afford that goodness, as her life is at stake. யானோ implies a split: 'you may be able to, I can't.'","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","nenju"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1248":{"book":"kural","num":1248,"t":["பரிந்தவர் நல்காரென்று ஏங்கிப் பிரிந்தவர்","பின்செல்வாய் பேதைஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"You are a fool, O my soul! to go after my departed one, while you mourn that he is not kind enough to favour you.","r":"The woman calls her heart a fool for following the man who has left, while lamenting that he isn't gracious.","n":"Parimelazhagar's surviving note gives the logic: he saw her distress and left anyway, so how would telling him at a distance change anything? This reverses 1242-1244, where she was urging the heart to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1249":{"book":"kural","num":1249,"t":["உள்ளத்தார் காத லவரால் உள்ளிநீ","யாருழைச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! to whom would you repair, while the dear one is within yourself?","r":"The woman asks her heart whom it goes looking for, since her lover is within it.","n":"Parimelazhagar says it's laughable to go looking outside for one who is inside. To me, this resembles the later devotional complaint about seeking God outside, though this resemblance isn't something the verse or its commentators claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1250":{"book":"kural","num":1250,"t":["துன்னாத் துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா","இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்"],"p":"If I retain in my heart him who has left me without befriending me, I shall lose even the (inward) beauty that remains.","r":"The woman says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close, she will lose still more of her கவின்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has lost her outward beauty, and will now lose her inner beauty too, meaning her dignity and restraint.","முன் இழந்த புறக்கவினேயன்றி நின்ற அகக்கவினும் இழப்போம்."],["kalaignar","It is bodily beauty that will fade further as she wastes away.",""]],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1251":{"book":"kural","num":1251,"t":["காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும்","நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு"],"p":"The axe of lust can break the door of chastity which is bolted with the bolt of modesty.","r":"The woman says that the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint, bolted with the bar of shame.","n":"The architecture is exact, as Parimelazhagar explains: restraint is the door that keeps what is inside from being taken, shame is the bolt, since restraint holds as long as shame does. Manakkudavar has அறிவு, 'understanding', as the door, though the verse says நிறை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1252":{"book":"kural","num":1252,"t":["காமம் எனவொன்றோ கண்ணின்றென் நெஞ்சத்தை","யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்"],"p":"Even at midnight is my mind worried by lust, and this one thing, alas! is without mercy.","r":"The woman says that this thing called desire has no pity, for it puts her heart to work even at midnight.","n":"The image is forced labour: everyone else's work stops at midnight, and hers doesn't. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar identify the task as sending the heart to him, which, Parimelazhagar notes, isn't a proper task for a woman, and is why she says 'this thing called desire'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1253":{"book":"kural","num":1253,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் காமத்தை யானோ குறிப்பின்றித்","தும்மல்போல் தோன்றி விடும்"],"p":"I would conceal my lust, but alas, it yields not to my will but breaks out like a sneeze.","r":"The woman says she would hide her desire, but like a sneeze it comes out without her intending it.","n":"The comparison is deliberately undignified. குறிப்பின்றி means without intent, using a word that in chapter 110 named her coded signs, which were deliberate. Now the sign sends itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","nirai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1254":{"book":"kural","num":1254,"t":["நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன் யானோஎன் காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"I say I would be firm, but alas, my malady breaks out from its concealment and appears in public.","r":"The woman says she used to say she had restraint, yet her desire passes beyond concealment and comes out in public.","n":"The tense matters, on Parimelazhagar's gloss: 'until today I thought of myself as restrained'. She has lost not only the restraint but also a belief about who she is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1255":{"book":"kural","num":1255,"t":["செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை காமநோய்","உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று"],"p":"The dignity that would not go after an absent lover is not known to those who are sticken by love.","r":"The woman says the greatness of not going after one who has turned hostile is something those who are sick with love can't know.","n":"Honour isn't rejected, but it's placed out of reach. Parimelazhagar gives the other half: those who don't have the sickness can know it, and he calls them the people of honour (மானம் உடையார்). He also softens going after to continual thought, and softens செற்றார், 'enemy', as one who has made time painful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","perumai","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1256":{"book":"kural","num":1256,"t":["செற்றவர் பின்சேறல் வேண்டி அளித்தரோ","எற்றென்னை உற்ற துயர்"],"p":"The sorrow I have endured by desiring to go after my absent lover, in what way is it excellent?","r":"The woman exclaims, in irony, how fine is the grief that has come to her, making her want to go after one who has turned hostile.","n":"Both older commentators hear sarcasm in அளித்து, 'sweet', as they did in the night verse 1168, meaning the opposite. Her comment on her own condition has become a dry detachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1257":{"book":"kural","num":1257,"t":["நாணென ஒன்றோ அறியலம் காமத்தால்","பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்"],"p":"I know nothing like shame when my beloved does from love (just) what is desired (by me).","r":"The woman says she knows nothing of shame when the man she loves does in desire what she longs for.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The context is that he has returned from courtesans; the shame she failed to feel is shame at touching a chest they have touched.","நாண் - பரத்தையர் தோய்ந்த மார்பைத் தோய்தற்கு நாணுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It's a general statement on women and desire, and doesn't mention any courtesans.","நாணென்பதொன்று அறியார் மகளிர், காமம் காரணமாக"]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1258":{"book":"kural","num":1258,"t":["பன்மாயக் கள்வன் பணிமொழி அன்றோநம்","பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"Are not the enticing words of my trick-abounding roguish lover the weapon that breaks away my feminine firmness?","r":"The woman says that the soft speech of that thief of many deceptions is the weapon that breaks her womanhood.","n":"The chapter's picture of siege ends here, with Parimelazhagar glossing பெண்மை as restraint, a fortress taken by words. She knows them to be deceptive, and that doesn't protect her. பணிமொழி was how he praised her, in 1121.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1259":{"book":"kural","num":1259,"t":["புலப்பல் எனச்சென்றேன் புல்லினேன் நெஞ் சம்","கலத்தல் உறுவது கண்டு"],"p":"I said I would feign dislike and so went (away); (but) I embraced him the moment I say my mind began to unite with him!","r":"The woman says she went away meaning to sulk, and embraced him when she saw her heart beginning to join with him.","n":"She didn't yield to him but to her heart, and embracing is what's left when her instrument defects. Parimelazhagar says it exactly: the heart that is the means of sulking has itself turned into the means of joining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1260":{"book":"kural","num":1260,"t":["நிணந்தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்க்கு உண்டோ","புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"Is it possible for those whose hearts melt like fat in the fire to say they can feign a strong dislike and remain so?","r":"The woman asks whether those whose hearts are like fat thrown in a fire can ever say 'we will join and then hold out in a sulk'.","n":"An extraordinary image of unlovely and total melting: not wax or butter, but animal fat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புணர்ந்து here means simply coming close, or meeting; sulking fails at first encounter.","புணர்தல் - ஈண்டு மிக நணுகுதல்; எதிர்ப்படுதலுமாம்."],["kalaignar","The word means after union; one can't then keep up the quarrel.",""]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1261":{"book":"kural","num":1261,"t":["வாளற்றுப் புற்கென்ற கண்ணும் அவர்சென்ற","நாளொற்றித் தேய்ந்த விரல்"],"p":"My finger has worn away by marking (on the wall) the days he has been absent while my eyes have lost their lustre and begin to fail.","r":"The woman says her eyes have lost their light and her fingers have worn down from marking the days since he left.","n":"Fingers wear down from touching marks on a wall, in Parimelazhagar's and Kalaignar's picture, and from folding to count, in Manakkudavar's. The verse itself just says they marked the days. Either way, this is the book's most concrete image of waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1262":{"book":"kural","num":1262,"t":["இலங்கிழாய் இன்று மறப்பின்என் தோள்மேல்","கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து"],"p":"O you bright-jewelled maid, if I forget (him) today, my shoulders will lose their beauty even in the other life and make my bracelets loose.","r":"The woman tells her bright-jewelled friend that if she forgets him today, her beauty will leave and the bangles will slip from her shoulders thereafter.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","melivu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Today' is the day of her death and மேல் is the next birth: remembering him at the moment of death is how she can reach him in the next life.","இவ்வெல்லைக்கண் நினைந்தால் மறுமைக்கண் அவரை எய்தி அன்புறலாம்;"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about this life: if she forgets, her body will fail, and the address means 'you who have no pain'.","தம்மழகினை நீக்கி வளை முதலான அணிகலங்களையும் கழலவிடும்"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1263":{"book":"kural","num":1263,"t":["உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்","வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்"],"p":"I still live by longing for the arrival of him who has gone out of love for victory and with valour as his guide.","r":"The woman says she still lives by longing for the return of the man who left, longing for victory, with his own resolve as his companion.","n":"The pointed word is துணை, 'companion'. Parimelazhagar spells it out: he has scorned her as a partner and taken resolve instead. There are two longings set side by side, நசைஇ in each line, for victory and for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1264":{"book":"kural","num":1264,"t":["கூடிய காமம் பிரிந்தார் வரவுள்ளிக்","கோடுகொ டேறுமென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My heart is rid of its sorrow and swells with rapture to think of my absent lover returning with his love.","r":"The woman says that thinking of the return of the man who left with the love they shared, her heart கோடுகொடேறும்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The heart puts out branches, swelling and rising as a tree does, with sorrow gone.","என் நெஞ்சு வருத்தமொழிந்து மேன்மேற் பணைத்தெழாநின்றது."],["manakkudavar","The heart climbs a tree to look for him, because from the top one can see someone coming from afar.","என்னெஞ்சம் மரத்தின் மேலேறிப் பாராநின்றது"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1265":{"book":"kural","num":1265,"t":["காண்கமன் கொண்கனைக் கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின்","நீங்கும்என் மென்தோள் பசப்பு"],"p":"May I look on my lover till I am satisfied and thereafter will vanish the sallowness of my slender shoulders.","r":"The woman says may she see her husband till her eyes are full, and after that the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes what this is answering: the friend brings news that he's coming, and asks why she still pales. Her answer is that hearing won't do it, since the eyes must see. The news of the cure isn't the cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","pasalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1266":{"book":"kural","num":1266,"t":["வருகமன் கொண்கன் ஒருநாள் பருகுவன்","பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட"],"p":"May my husband return some day; and then will I enjoy (him) so as to destroy all this agonizing sorrow.","r":"The woman says may her husband come one day, and she'll drink him in so that all this agonising sickness is destroyed.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes பருகுவன் drinking through all five senses, recalling 1101, and says that in that single day she'll destroy not only all past pain but all that might still come. That one day is asked to carry a lot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1267":{"book":"kural","num":1267,"t":["புலப்பேன்கொல் புல்லுவேன் கொல்லோ கலப்பேன்கொல்","கண்அன்ன கேளிர் விரன்"],"p":"On the return of him who is as dear as my eyes, am I displeased or am I to embrace (him); or am I to do both?","r":"The woman asks, should he who is dear as her eyes return, whether she will sulk, embrace him or do both.","n":"The chapter's real interest here is the planning, with him not yet home. Parimelazhagar sets aside an alternative, that the third means to behave as if nothing happened: she wants to do all three, and that's her impatience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1268":{"book":"kural","num":1268,"t":["வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன் மனைகலந்து","மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து"],"p":"Let the king fight and gain (victories); (but) let me be united to my wife and feast the evening.","r":"Let the king fight and win, and let there be a home-joined feast for the evening; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar rejects the woman's reading at length, on the grounds that she wouldn't name the king rather than the husband. But Manakkudavar gives a reason for that: the king's unfinished war is what holds him back. Evening, the woman's executioner, is for the man a feast to come.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, stuck on campaign, says to himself that he wants to join his wife; மனை here stands for the wife.","வேந்தற்கு உற்றுழிப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் வினை முடிவு நீட்டித் துழித் தலைமகளை நினைந்து தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks: let the king win, and I will prepare a feast at home, with மனை meaning the kitchen.","நம் காதலர்க்கு விருந்து செய்வே மாக"],["kalaignar","The man speaks: if the leader wins, he will feast with his wife.",""]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1269":{"book":"kural","num":1269,"t":["ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல் செல்லும்சேண் சென்றார்","வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்கு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"To those who suffer waiting for the day of return of their distant lovers one day is as long as seven days.","r":"One day passes like seven for those who pine with an eye on the day when those far away return; Parimelazhagar gives it to the man, projecting her pain, and the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the general phrasing as proof that it can't be the woman: she would say 'me', so the man is imagining how it is for her. If he's right, it's a rare moment in the book when a man grasps the other side of his absence. He takes 'seven' as meaning many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1270":{"book":"kural","num":1270,"t":["பெறின்என்னாம் பெற்றக்கால் என்னாம் உறினென்னாம்","உள்ளம் உடைந்துக்கக் கால்"],"p":"After (my wife) has died of a broken heart, what good will there be if she is to receive me, has received me, or has even embraced me?","r":"What use is it if she gets, or has got, or even touches, once the heart has broken and spilled? The speaker is the man, of her, for Parimelazhagar, and the woman, of herself, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uyir","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man fears that she may die before he gets back, and it can't be her voice, since then it would be a lament and not impatience.","இது தலைமகள் கூற்றாயவழி இரங்கலாவதல்லது விதுப்பாகாமை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend who says that he will come: what use, if my own heart has already broken?","இது வருவா ரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1271":{"book":"kural","num":1271,"t":["கரப்பினுங் கையிகந் தொல்லாநின் உண்கண்","உரைக்கல் உறுவதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"Though you would conceal (your feelings), your painted eyes would not, for, transgressing (their bounds), they tell (me) something.","r":"The man tells her that though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes have gone beyond her control and have something they want to say.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting the man has returned and is lavishing affection on her, and she takes fright, which makes this a kind of tragic misunderstanding. Kalaignar has the eyes declare love that resists separation. The verse only says there is something.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1272":{"book":"kural","num":1272,"t":["கண்ணிறைந்த காரிகைக் காம்பேர்தோட் பேதைக்குப்","பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது"],"p":"Unusually great is the female simplicity of your maid whose beauty fills my eyes and whose shoulders resemble the bamboo.","r":"The man says this artless girl who fills his eyes, with her bamboo shoulders, has a great measure of womanly nature.","n":"The compliment may be a rebuke. Parimelazhagar glosses நீர்மை as மடமை, 'naivety', and has him complain to her friend that she fears a separation he has no thought of. Kalaignar takes it as simple praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1273":{"book":"kural","num":1273,"t":["மணியில் திகழ்தரு நூல்போல் மடந்தை","அணியில் திகழ்வதொன்று உண்டு"],"p":"There is something that is implied in the beauty of this woman, like the thread that is visible in a garland of gems.","r":"The man says that, like the thread that shows in strung gems, there is something that shows through this young woman's beauty.","n":"The gem image needs transparent stones, and Parimelazhagar says crystal: inside but visible. What is inside isn't compatible with the outer beauty, he says, but appears with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It's an intent that he can't himself identify, and he asks her friend to find out what it is.","மடந்தையது அணியகத்துக் கிடந்து புறத்துப் புலனாகின்றதொரு குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","It's a hidden sorrow, which shows though she conceals it.","இவள் மறைக்கவும் தோற்றுகின்றதொரு துன்பம் உண்டு"]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1274":{"book":"kural","num":1274,"t":["முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்போல் பேதை","நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"There is something in the unmatured smile of this maid like the fragrance that is contained in an unblossomed bud.","r":"The man says there is something within the bud of this girl's smile, like the scent within a closed bud.","n":"It's a companion to 1273, except that here nothing shows: scent inside a bud can't be perceived, so his certainty about it is a matter of faith. Manakkudavar sets it in courtship, with the man telling his own heart that her smile promises joy, and Kalaignar says the smile holds the memory of her lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1275":{"book":"kural","num":1275,"t":["செறிதொடி செய்திறந்த கள்ளம் உறுதுயர்","தீர்க்கும் மருந்தொன்று உடைத்து"],"p":"The well-meant departure of her whose bangles are tight-fitting contains a remedy that can cure my great sorrow.","r":"The stealth with which the tight-bangled woman slipped away holds a medicine for great sorrow; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man tells the friend that she has hidden from him out of a false fear, and the cure is for the friend to explain that he isn't leaving.","என்கண் இல்லாத தொன்றனை உட்கொண்டு அது காரணமாக என்னை மறைத்துப் போன குறிப்பு"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man, in courtship, that the woman's slipping away coyly means that his plea will succeed.","எதிர்ப்பட்ட தலைமகற்கு நின்குறை முடியும்; நீ இவ்விடைச் செல்லென்று தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1276":{"book":"kural","num":1276,"t":["பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல் அரிதாற்றி","அன்பின்மை சூழ்வ துடைத்து"],"p":"The embrace that fills me with comfort and gladness is capable of enduring (my former) sorrow and meditating on his want of love.","r":"The woman tells her friend that his great comfort and eager lovemaking contains in it her having to endure hardship again and to think about his lack of love.","n":"A sad piece of reading. His tenderness is the very thing that gives him away, since she reasons from excess of kindness to a coming parting. Both older commentators give it to her, speaking to the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1277":{"book":"kural","num":1277,"t":["தண்ணந் துறைவன் தணந்தமை நம்மினும்","முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை"],"p":"My bracelets have understood before me the (mental) separation of him who rules the cool seashore.","r":"The woman says her bangles knew before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted from her.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the separation inward: he is still physically with her but has left in his mind. Her body has registered this before her understanding did. So bangles, the book's most public symptom, are here her private source of knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["melivu","pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1278":{"book":"kural","num":1278,"t":["நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்எம் காதலர் யாமும்","எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து"],"p":"It was but yesterday my lover departed (from me); and it is seven days since my complexion turned sallow.","r":"The woman says that her lover left only yesterday, and her body has been pale for seven days.","n":"The sum doesn't add up, on purpose. The pain started before the event, because she read it coming.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He hasn't physically left: 'left yesterday' means he made up his mind then, shown by yesterday's tenderness.","'நெருநற்றுச் செய்த தலையளியாற் பிரிவு துணியப்பட்டது' என்பாள், 'நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்' என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","He has actually left, and she was grieving before he went.","இஃது அவர் பிரிவதன் முன்னும் பிரிவரென் றேங்கி, இன்புற்றில் மென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1279":{"book":"kural","num":1279,"t":["தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி அடிநோக்கி","அஃதாண் டவள்செய் தது"],"p":"She looked at her bracelets, her tender shoulders, and her feet; this was what she did there (significantly).","r":"Parimelazhagar's speaker, the friend, tells the man that she looked at her bangles, looked at her soft shoulders and looked at her feet: this is what she did there.","n":"The whole verse is a silent gesture, and the commentators read it as a sentence: 'these will slip if you leave me; these will thin; so I'll go with you on these'. The verse itself doesn't state the conclusion. This is perhaps the most purely dramatic thing in the Kural, and the friend's 'that's what she did' leaves him to work it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1280":{"book":"kural","num":1280,"t":["பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்தென்ப கண்ணினால்","காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு"],"p":"To express their love-sickness by their eyes and resort to begging bespeaks more than ordinary female excellence.","r":"Those who know say that telling love's sickness with the eyes and begging adds more womanhood to womanhood; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers: he praises her decision to go with him, as a way of telling her friend he won't be leaving at all.","தன் பிரிவின்மைக் குறிப்பினை அறிவுறுப்பான், அவள் பெண்மையினைப் பிறர்மேலிட்டு வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to the man, having seen the woman's response to news of his leaving.","இது பிரிவுணர்த்திய தலைமகள் குறிப்புக்கண்டு தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1281":{"book":"kural","num":1281,"t":["உள்ளக் களித்தலும் காண மகிழ்தலும்","கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற் குண்டு"],"p":"To please by thought and cheer by sight is peculiar, not to liquor but lust.","r":"The woman says that being intoxicated at a thought and delighted at a look belong not to liquor but to love.","n":"Parimelazhagar's setting is pointed: the friend teases her about why she isn't angry at a man planning to leave. So the famous line is an excuse. He also distinguishes the words: களித்தல், with awareness intact, and மகிழ்தல், with awareness lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1282":{"book":"kural","num":1282,"t":["தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும் பனைத் துணையும்","காமம் நிறைய வரின்"],"p":"If women have a lust that exceeds even the measure of the palmyra fruit, they will not desire (to feign) dislike even as much as the millet.","r":"The woman says that when love fills to the size of a palmyra tree, there should be no sulking, not even the size of a millet seed.","n":"The huge and tiny are the proverbial measures. Parimelazhagar says she seems to be giving others good advice, and is in fact describing her own eagerness. Manakkudavar has her say this to her own heart, which is thinking of sulking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1283":{"book":"kural","num":1283,"t":["பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும் கொண்கனைக்","காணா தமையல கண்"],"p":"Though my eyes disregard me and do what is pleasing to my husband, still will they not be satisfied unless they see him.","r":"The woman says that though he disregards her and does only what pleases him, her eyes won't rest without seeing him.","n":"It's a clear admission, with no self-deception. He is at fault, she knows it, and the eyes don't care. Pope's prose wrongly makes the eyes the ones who disregard her; the commentators and his couplet agree it is the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1284":{"book":"kural","num":1284,"t":["ஊடற்கண் சென்றேன்மன் தோழி அதுமறந்து","கூடற்கண் சென்றது என் னெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my friend! I was prepared to feign displeasure but my mind forgetting it was ready to embrace him.","r":"The woman tells her friend that she went there to sulk, but her heart forgot that and went there to unite.","n":"The same verb, சென்றேன் and சென்றது, is used for both: she and her heart set out together and went to different places. Parimelazhagar's word for this is அறைபோக, treason in a military sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1285":{"book":"kural","num":1285,"t":["எழுதுங்கால் கோல்காணாக் கண்ணேபோல் கொண்கன்","பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து"],"p":"Like the eyes which see not the pencil that paints it, I cannot see my husband's fault (just) when I meet him.","r":"The woman says that, as the eye can't see the stick that lines it at the moment of lining, she doesn't see her husband's fault when she sees him.","n":"A wonderfully precise image: the object is at its closest and most intimate exactly when it can't be seen. Parimelazhagar notes that the eye knows the stick well enough at other times, and so she too knows his faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1286":{"book":"kural","num":1286,"t":["காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால்","காணேன் தவறல் லவை"],"p":"When I see my husband, I do not see any faults; but when I do not see him, I do not see anything but faults.","r":"The woman says that when she sees him she doesn't see his faults, and when she doesn't see him she sees nothing but his faults.","n":"A perfectly symmetrical verse about what presence does to judgement. As with the previous verse, this isn't presented as an illusion that she can see through and correct; it's just how it is. Sight is what removes her power to judge him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1287":{"book":"kural","num":1287,"t":["உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய் பவரேபோல்","பொய்த்தல் அறிந்தென் புலந்து"],"p":"Like those who leap into a stream which they know will carry them off, why should a wife feign dislike which she knows cannot hold out long?","r":"The woman asks what use it is to sulk, knowing it will fail, like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off.","n":"Parimelazhagar registers a strain in the syntax, and offers an alternate shape: 'I learned by trying once that sulking fails; I won't do it again'. Pope's couplet is rather different in having a rescue. The swimmer who knows she'll be swept away jumps in anyway, and the verse doesn't deny that this might be the fun of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1288":{"book":"kural","num":1288,"t":["இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும் களித்தார்க்குக்","கள்ளற்றே கள்வநின் மார்பு"],"p":"O you rogue! your breast is to me what liquor is to those who rejoice in it, though it only gives them an unpleasant disgrace.","r":"Thief, your chest is to us as liquor is to those who drink it, though it does them shameful harm; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend tells the man that she has seen the woman's eagerness, and uses 'us' for them both.","தலைமகள் புணர்ச்சி? விதுப்பு அறிந்த தோழி, தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to the man herself, having let go of the wish to sulk.","இது புலவிக்குறிப்பு நீங்கின தலை மகள் தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1289":{"book":"kural","num":1289,"t":["மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம் சிலர்அதன்","செவ்வி தலைப்படு வார்"],"p":"Sexual delight is more delicate than a flower, and few are those who understand its real nature.","r":"Love is softer than a flower, and few attain its moment; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman, to her heart, for Manakkudavar.","n":"The famous line has a practical use. Parimelazhagar lists what makes this moment rare: two people need to share intent, desire, enjoyment and pleasure at one time, in a fit place, moment and setting. He has the man saying this to end her sulk, so the maxim is also a plea.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks during a sulk that won't yield to his pleading, in order to end it.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman, seeing that he desires union, tells her own heart that this isn't the moment to sulk.","இப்பொழுது ஊடுவையாயின், இக்காமஞ் செவ்விதப்பு மென்று புணர்ச்சி வேட்கையால் தலைமகள் நெஞ்சோடு கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1290":{"book":"kural","num":1290,"t":["கண்ணின் துனித்தே கலங்கினாள் புல்லுதல்","என்னினும் தான்விதுப் புற்று"],"p":"She once feigned dislike in her eyes, but the warmth of her embrace exceeded my own.","r":"The man recalls that she sulked with her eyes alone and then, more eager than he for the embrace, was undone.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this an edge: spoken during her present sulk, it means 'the woman who gave up so quickly was not this one'. Manakkudavar has him simply observing her now. A rare moment of him looking back on her eagerness, and one that might flatter him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1291":{"book":"kural","num":1291,"t":["அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல் கண்டும் எவன்நெஞ்சே","நீஎமக்கு ஆகா தது"],"p":"O my soul! although you have seen how his soul stands by him, how is it you do not stand by me?","r":"The woman asks her heart why it isn't hers, when it has seen how his heart belongs to him.","n":"An elegantly sharp opening. Two hearts are compared as servants, and his has a better idea of loyalty. Parimelazhagar adds the sting: even those who can't figure out what to do can copy someone else, and the heart won't do even that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1292":{"book":"kural","num":1292,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்க்கண்ட கண்ணும் அவரைச்","செறாஅரெனச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! although you have known him who does not love me, still do you go to him, saying \"he will not be displeased.\"","r":"The woman says that her heart, even after seeing that he isn't close to her, goes to him as if he weren't hostile.","n":"The heart's reason is the small hope, that he won't be angry. Parimelazhagar says it's relying on old familiarity, which is a thin basis, and calls it the greatest folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1293":{"book":"kural","num":1293,"t":["கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்இல் என்பதோ நெஞ்சேநீ","பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்"],"p":"O my soul! do you follow him at pleasure under the belief that the ruined have no friends?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it follows him as it pleases, on the principle that the ruined have no friends.","n":"A piece of proverbial wisdom about false friends, turned against the heart. By using it she also admits that she is the one ruined. Parimelazhagar glosses கெட்டார் as her loss of honour, and the verse casts her heart as a fair-weather friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1294":{"book":"kural","num":1294,"t":["இனிஅன்ன நின்னொடு சூழ்வார்யார் நெஞ்சே","துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்காண் மற்று"],"p":"O my soul! you would not first seem sulky and then enjoy (him); who then would in future consult you about such things?","r":"The woman tells her heart that it won't first sulk and then enjoy, so no one will plan such things with it any more.","n":"It's almost comic: she drops the heart from her team as an unreliable accomplice. Sulking here is a strategy with a sequence to it, sulk first and enjoy later, and the heart can't wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1295":{"book":"kural","num":1295,"t":["பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின்பிரிவு அஞ்சும்","அறாஅ இடும்பைத்தென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul fears when it is without him; it also fears when it is with him; it is sub ject to incessant sorrow.","r":"The woman says her heart fears not having him and, on having him, fears parting, so it is in endless grief.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting she says this within the hearing of a friend who has come as an envoy, so it also means she will let him in. He observes that there is no third state, which makes this grief complete.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1296":{"book":"kural","num":1296,"t":["தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால் என்னைத்","தினிய இருந்ததென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My mind has been (here) in order to eat me up (as it were) whenever I think of him in my solitude.","r":"The woman says that when she sits alone and thinks of him, her heart is there only to eat her.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the irony: it stays with her only to work on her, not to help her quarrel. Manakkudavar gives this a small domestic turn: she tells the friend, 'you've come now, and so I've escaped it'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1297":{"book":"kural","num":1297,"t":["நாணும் மறந்தேன் அவர்மறக் கல்லாஎன்","மாணா மடநெஞ்சிற் பட்டு"],"p":"I have even forgotten my modesty, having been caught in my foolish mind which is not dignified enough to forget him.","r":"The woman says that, stuck with this worthless, foolish heart that can't forget him, she has even forgotten shame.","n":"There are two forgettings in tension. Parimelazhagar's definition of shame is one of the finest things in his commentary: however long two have been together, to shrink as if seeing the other for the first time. His definition of the heart's foolishness: remembering when he's away, forgetting when he's there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1298":{"book":"kural","num":1298,"t":["எள்ளின் இளிவாம்என்று எண்ணி அவர்திறம்","உள்ளும் உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul which clings to life thinks only of his (own) gain in the belief that it would be disgraceful for it to despise him.","r":"The woman says her heart, thinking it would be disgraceful to despise him, keeps thinking of what pertains to him, out of உயிர்க்காதல்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","uyir","ninaivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்க்காதல் means the heart loves its own life: it won't die, and so it plans reconciliation.","உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு - உயிர்மேல் காதலையுடைய என் நெஞ்சு"],["kalaignar","It's a heart whose love is as dear as life, and it thinks on his greatness.",""]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1299":{"book":"kural","num":1299,"t":["துன்பத்திற்கு யாரே துணையாவார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி"],"p":"Who would help me out of one's distress, when one's own soul refuses help to one?","r":"Who will be a companion in grief, when one's own heart is no companion? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"If it's the man, then he has come to the state she has been in for ten verses. His heart is doing to him what hers did: urging union when he should be standing firm.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks, in a sulk that won't yield, whose heart still wants her.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman replies to the friend who asks who would help.","இது தலைமகள் துணையாவார் யாரென்ற தோழிக்குக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1300":{"book":"kural","num":1300,"t":["தஞ்சம் தமரல்லர் ஏதிலார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி"],"p":"It is hardly possible for strangers to behave like relations, when one's own soul acts like a stranger.","r":"The man, for Parimelazhagar, says that it's no wonder if strangers aren't one's own, when one's own heart isn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a witty aim: 'stranger' here is the sulking woman, who no longer seems his beloved, so if his own heart pains him for her sake, it's a small thing if she, a stranger, sulks. The other commentators don't name a speaker, and read it as a maxim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1301":{"book":"kural","num":1301,"t":["புல்லா திராஅப் புலத்தை அவர் உறும்","அல்லல்நோய் காண்கம் சிறிது"],"p":"Let us witness awhile his keen suffering; just feign dislike and embrace him not.","r":"The friend tells the woman not to embrace him but to sulk, so that they may watch his sharp pain for a little while.","n":"It's mischief with a purpose. Parimelazhagar says the friend has actually come as his envoy, and sides with the woman, teasing, as a way of bringing her round. சிறிது, 'a little', is the word that matters: the quarrel mustn't go on long.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1302":{"book":"kural","num":1302,"t":["உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி அதுசிறிது","மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்"],"p":"A little dislike is like salt in proportion; to prolong it a little is like salt a little too much.","r":"The friend says that sulking is like the right amount of salt, and letting it go on a little too long is like a little too much salt.","n":"The image puts sulking in the kitchen, as an everyday and necessary ingredient without any drama. The second line is exact: just a little too much is enough to spoil it. Kalaignar makes the measure a time span, and Parimelazhagar makes it knowing when desire is about to fade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1303":{"book":"kural","num":1303,"t":["அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்றால் தம்மைப்","புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்"],"p":"For men not to embrace those who have feigned dislike is like torturing those already in agony.","r":"The woman says that not embracing those who have sulked with you is like adding sharp pain to those already in distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She means courtesans: go back to them, she tells the man returning from them, since they'll suffer without you. It's sarcasm.","நும்மைப் பெறாது புலந்தூடியிருக்கின்ற பரத்தையரைப் போய்ப் புலவி நீக்கிப் புல்லீராயின், அவராற்றார்"],["kalaignar","It's a general truth about lovers: leaving a sulking partner unreconciled is cruel.",""]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1304":{"book":"kural","num":1304,"t":["ஊடி யவரை உணராமை வாடிய","வள்ளி முதலரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Not to reconcile those who have feigned dislike is like cutting a faded creeper at its root.","r":"The woman says that not reconciling those who have sulked is like cutting a withered creeper at its root.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She again means the other women, and she adds that she has no right to sulk, since she has their sons to look at; so go to them.","நும்மோடு ஊடிய பரத்தையரை ஊடலுணர்த்திக் கூடாதொழிதல்"],["kalaignar","It's about any partner who stays aloof from a sulking lover.",""]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1305":{"book":"kural","num":1305,"t":["நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு ஏஎர் புலத்தகை","பூஅன்ன கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"An increased shyness in those whose eyes are like flowers is beautiful even to good and virtuous husbands.","r":"The grace of those good in their virtues is the depth of sulking in flower-eyed women; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, reconciled, reflects to himself that even faultless men find this sulking lovely.","தலைமகளைப் புலவி நீக்கிக் கூடிய தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the woman that sulking suits courtesans, and that she shouldn't sulk at those not worth it.","இது பரத்தையரோடு புலந்து கூறிய தலைமகட்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1306":{"book":"kural","num":1306,"t":["துனியும் புலவியும் இல்லாயின் காமம்","கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று"],"p":"Sexual pleasure, without prolonged and short-lived dislike, is like too ripe, and unripe fruit.","r":"Without serious and light quarrels, love is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"There are two kinds of quarrels for two kinds of failure, and the verse uses them in order: no deep quarrel, and love is fruit going off; no light quarrel, and love is unripe. Manakkudavar supplies the tastes, sour and astringent. Pope's couplet reads 'ripened fruit' as a good thing, and the commentators don't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man praises both kinds of quarrel after making up.","இவ்விரண்டும் வேண்டும் என்று வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks, saying that there is some good in the sulking that is then healed.","இஃது உணர்தற்கு நல்லது உளதென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1307":{"book":"kural","num":1307,"t":["ஊடலின் உண்டாங்கோர் துன்பம் புணர்வது","நீடுவ தன்று கொல் என்று"],"p":"The doubt as to whether intercourse would take place soon or not, creates a sorrow (even) in feigned dislike.","r":"The man says there is one pain even within sulking: the worry about whether union will be delayed or not.","n":"This is where the game has its risk. Parimelazhagar takes it as the worry that union will be put off, and Kalaignar as a worry that the time for union will shrink. In either case, the player can't be sure the quarrel will end in time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1308":{"book":"kural","num":1308,"t":["நோதல் எவன்மற்று நொந்தாரென்று அஃதறியும்","காதலர் இல்லா வழி"],"p":"What avails sorrow when I am without a wife who can understand the cause of my sorrow?","r":"What use is it to suffer, when there is no lover who knows that this person is suffering? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar, and the friend, speaking to him, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is himself sulking, because she won't yield to his pleading.","இவள் நம் காதலியல்லள்; அன்மையின், இந்நோவு அறியாள்"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man this when she has come inside, knowing about his sulking.","அதனை யறிந்து அகம்புக்க தோழி அவனுக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1309":{"book":"kural","num":1309,"t":["நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே புலவியும்","வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது"],"p":"Like water in the shade, dislike is delicious only in those who love.","r":"The man says water is sweet in the shade, and sulking too is sweet only with those who love.","n":"It's a reciprocal theory of sulking. A quarrel is a pleasure only inside a shared love, and without it there is only heat. Parimelazhagar applies this pointedly: she doesn't feel this for him, and so her sulk is unpleasant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1310":{"book":"kural","num":1310,"t":["ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு என்நெஞ்சம்","கூடுவேம் என்பது அவா"],"p":"It is nothing but strong desire that makes her mind unite with me who can leave her to her own dislike.","r":"The man, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's wish to join with someone who leaves a sulk to wither is simply craving.","n":"அவா, 'craving', is a strong word to use on one's own heart, with a long negative history in the Kural's ethics. He diagnoses himself exactly as the ascetic chapter might, and it doesn't stop him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1311":{"book":"kural","num":1311,"t":["பெண்ணியலார் எல்லாரும் கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்","நண்ணேன் பரத்தநின் மார்பு"],"p":"You are given to prostitution; all those who are born as womankind enjoy you with their eyes in an ordinary way. I will not embrace you.","r":"The woman calls him a philanderer and refuses his chest, because all women feast on him in common with their eyes.","n":"The offence is only that he was seen, and being seen is a form of being consumed. பரத்த is applied to him in the vocative, and is the verse's own word for his straying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பெண்ணியலார் means courtesans, who, lacking other virtues, have only the nature of women. They look at him as his procession passes.","பரத்தையர்க்குள்ளது பெண் இயற்கை மாத்திரமே என்னுங் கருத்தால், 'பெண் இயலார்' என்றாள்."],["manakkudavar","It simply means all women, who think of his chest as common property.","நின் மார்பைப் பெண்மை யுடையா ரெல்லாரும் தமக்குப் பொதுவாக நினைத்துக்"]],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1312":{"book":"kural","num":1312,"t":["ஊடி இருந்தேமாத் தும்மினார் யாம்தம்மை","நீடுவாழ் கென்பாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"When I continued to be sulky he sneezed and thought I would (then) wish him a long life.","r":"The woman tells her friend that while she was sulking he sneezed, knowing she would then say 'long life' and so break her silence.","n":"A real gem of observed domestic strategy. Parimelazhagar says what makes this a 'fine point': she takes a natural sneeze for a deliberate sign. We can't tell if he meant it, and that is the fun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1313":{"book":"kural","num":1313,"t":["கோட்டுப் பூச் சூடினும் காயும் ஒருத்தியைக்","காட்டிய சூடினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even if I were adorned with a garland of branch-flowers, she would say I did so to show it to another woman.","r":"The man tells her friend that even if he wears a wreath of bent flowers, she is angry and says he wore it to show some other woman.","n":"The word கோட்டுப்பூ is a puzzle. Parimelazhagar offers flowers woven in a curve or flowers from another landscape, Manakkudavar offers side-worn flowers, and Kalaignar flowers from a tree-branch. The offence doesn't depend on the answer, which is the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1314":{"book":"kural","num":1314,"t":["யாரினும் காதலம் என்றேனா ஊடினாள்","யாரினும் யாரினும் என்று"],"p":"When I said I loved her more than any other woman, she said \"more than others, yes, more than others,\" and remained sulky.","r":"The man says he told her he loved more than anyone, and she sulked: 'than whom, than whom?'","n":"A grammatical quarrel. He meant 'we love more than any other couple', and she took it to mean 'more than the other women I love'. The commentators spell this out, and the verse gives only her repeated words, one of the most natural bits of speech in the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1315":{"book":"kural","num":1315,"t":["இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம் என்றேனாக்","கண்நிறை நீர்கொண் டனள்"],"p":"When I said I would never part from her in this life her eyes were filled with tears.","r":"The man says he told her they wouldn't part in this birth, and her eyes filled with tears.","n":"A promise made in the language of rebirth backfires on its own qualifier: 'in this birth' implies 'not in the next'. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar explain it so. Kalaignar has her ask 'is there a next birth, then?', which is a modern touch that isn't in the verse. Rebirth is taken for granted, as material for a joke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","pirivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1316":{"book":"kural","num":1316,"t":["உள்ளினேன் என்றேன்மற் றென்மறந்தீர் என்றென்னைப்","புல்லாள் புலத்தக் கனள்"],"p":"When I said I had remembered her, she said I had forgotten her and relaxing her embrace, began to feign dislike.","r":"The man says he told her he had thought of her, and she said 'why did you forget?', and wouldn't embrace him, and went into a sulk.","n":"A strict logical inference, and Parimelazhagar uses a term from Indian logic for it, அருத்தாபத்தி: remembering presupposes forgetting. She is using the man's own argument from 1125.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1317":{"book":"kural","num":1317,"t":["வழுத்தினாள் தும்மினேன் ஆக அழித்தழுதாள்","யாருள்ளித் தும்மினீர் என்று"],"p":"When I sneezed she blessed me, but at once changed (her mind) and wept, asking, \"At the thought of whom did you sneeze?\"","r":"The man says that when he sneezed she blessed him, and then undid it and wept: 'who were you thinking of, that you sneezed?'","n":"The blessing comes automatically and the suspicion comes a second later, and the verse catches that interval. Parimelazhagar notes that the belief behind this is a women's usage (மகளிர் வழக்கு) and that she's taking it as literal fact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1318":{"book":"kural","num":1318,"t":["தும்முச் செறுப்ப அழுதாள் நுமர்உள்ளல்","எம்மை மறைத்திரோ என்று"],"p":"When I suppressed my sneezing, she wept saying, \"I suppose you (did so) to hide from me your own people's remembrance of you\".","r":"The man says that when he suppressed a sneeze, she wept: 'are you hiding from me that your people are thinking of you?'","n":"It's the next stage of the farce. He has learned from 1317, and that's now the crime. Parimelazhagar sums it up: it's a fault to sneeze and a fault not to. So what is there to do?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1319":{"book":"kural","num":1319,"t":["தன்னை உணர்த்தினும் காயும் பிறர்க்கும்நீர்","இந்நீரர் ஆகுதிர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I try to remove her dislike, she is displeased and says, \"This is the way you behave towards (other women).\"","r":"The man says that even when he tries to appease her, she is angry and says that this is how he behaves with other women too.","n":"The cure has become evidence. Parimelazhagar notes the extra detail: he has even admitted to faults he didn't commit in order to placate her, and that confession is what now counts against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1320":{"book":"kural","num":1320,"t":["நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும் அனைத்துநீர்","யாருள்ளி நோக்கினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I look on her contemplating (her beauty), she is displeased and says, \"With whose thought have you (thus) looked on my person?\"","r":"The man says that even when he just sits and gazes at her in thought, she is angry: 'whom are you thinking of as you look me over?'","n":"Silence and adoration are his last resort, and they fail too. For Parimelazhagar, her logic is very neat: as no one woman could match all her parts, he must be comparing each part with a different woman, so there must be several. So she wants them named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1321":{"book":"kural","num":1321,"t":["இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு ஆயினும் ஊடுதல்","வல்லது அவர்அளிக்கு மாறு"],"p":"Although my husband is free from defects, the way in which he embraces me is such as to make me feign dislike.","r":"The woman says that even though he has no fault, the way he shows his grace has the power to bring on a sulk.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kaamam","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is jealousy: his love is so delightful that others surely enjoy it too, and she can't stand the thought.","யான் எய்தற் பாலதாய இத்தலையளி ஒழிந்தாரும் எய்துவர் எனக்கருதி, அது பொறாமையான் ஊடல்நிகழா நின்றது"],["kalaignar","He reverses the direction: the quarrel, between faultless lovers, builds up their love.",""]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1322":{"book":"kural","num":1322,"t":["ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி நல்லளி","வாடினும் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"His love will increase though it may (at first seem to) fade through the short-lived distress caused by (my) dislike.","r":"The woman says that even if his fine grace droops a little through the small misery sulking brings, it rises to greatness.","n":"She is justifying the risk to a friend who has pointed out she could have his kindness without quarrelling. வாடினும், 'though it droop', uses a word for a plant that comes back stronger. Parimelazhagar keeps the concession small: a quarrel with no real fault passes quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1323":{"book":"kural","num":1323,"t":["புலத்தலின் புத்தேள்நாடு உண்டோ நிலத்தொடு","நீரியைந் தன்னார் அகத்து"],"p":"Is there a celestial land that can please like the feigned dislike of those whose union resembles that of earth and water?","r":"The woman asks if there is any heaven to compare with sulking with one to whom one is joined as water to earth.","n":"Readers of Tamil poetry will recall Kuruntokai 40 here, with its rain and red earth. I offer that as an echo. In the sequence Manakkudavar's text gives, these are the last words of the Kural.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Water takes on the character of the ground it's in, so men take on the nature of the women they touch, which is the reason for her sulking. It's a surprisingly suspicious reading of a famous image.","நீர் தான் நின்ற நிலத்தியல்பிற்றாமாறு போலக் காதலரும் தாம் கூடிய மகளிரியல்பினராகலான்"],["manakkudavar","Earth and water together combine heat and coolness, and in the same way lovers experience joy and pain as one.","நிலத்தொடு நீரியை தலால் அவை வெப்பமுந்தட்பமுங் கூடியிருக்குமாறு போல்"],["kalaignar","புத்தேள்நாடு isn't a divine world but a new one, which removes heaven from the verse.",""]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1324":{"book":"kural","num":1324,"t":["புல்லி விடாஅப் புலவியுள் தோன்றுமென்","உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"In prolonged dislike after an embrace there is a weapon that can break my heart.","r":"Within the sulk that follows an embrace and won't let go arises the weapon that breaks 'my' heart; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Both identify the weapon: his bows and soft words for Parimelazhagar, and soft words (பணிமொழி) for Manakkudavar. Being broken is exactly what she is after, so the battle imagery here is pure pleasure. Compare the same weapon in 1258.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","nirai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend's question about what will end this sulk.","'அப்புலவி இனி யாதான் நீங்கும்?' என்றாட்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","He calls the group at this point the man's speech.","இவையெட்டும் தலைமகன் கூற்று ."]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1325":{"book":"kural","num":1325,"t":["தவறிலர் ஆயினும் தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்","அகறலின் ஆங்கொன் றுடைத்து"],"p":"Though free from defects, men feel pleased when they cannot embrace the delicate shoulders of those whom they love.","r":"The man, delighted after making up, says that though men may be faultless, being kept from the soft arms of those they love has a certain something.","n":"ஆங்கொன்று, 'a certain something', is an honest refusal to name it, and Parimelazhagar takes it that way: the pleasure is beyond words. Even the wrongly accused gets something from this. It matches 1321, in which she sulks at a man with no fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1326":{"book":"kural","num":1326,"t":["உணலினும் உண்டது அறல்இனிது காமம்","புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது"],"p":"To digest what has been eaten is more delightful than to eat more; likewise love is more delightful in dislike than intercourse.","r":"The man says digesting what you have eaten is sweeter than eating, and for love sulking is sweeter than union.","n":"Earthy and unusual, as digestion rarely comes into love poetry. The thought behind it is appetite: as Parimelazhagar says, hunger makes food better, and distance makes union better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1327":{"book":"kural","num":1327,"t":["ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார் அதுமன்னும்","கூடலிற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Those are conquerors whose dislike has been defeated and that is proved by the love (which follows).","r":"The man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses wins, as can be seen when they come together.","n":"A paradox with a precise explanation in Parimelazhagar: the one who gives way first gets the great joy, and only later learns this. He adds the first-person point: I gave way, and so I got this joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1328":{"book":"kural","num":1328,"t":["ஊடிப் பெறுகுவம் கொல்லோ நுதல்வெயர்ப்பக்","கூடலில் தோன்றிய உப்பு"],"p":"Will I enjoy once more through her dislike, the pleasure of that love that makes her forehead perspire?","r":"The man asks whether by sulking they might again win that savour which appeared in union, with her forehead wet with sweat.","n":"உப்பு, 'salt', stands for sweetness here, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as இனிமை, which he explains as all five senses at once, recalling 1101. It's a frankly physical detail near the end of the Kural, and Manakkudavar notes that sulking belongs to both of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1329":{"book":"kural","num":1329,"t":["ஊடுக மன்னோ ஒளியிழை யாமிரப்ப","நீடுக மன்னோ இரா"],"p":"May the bright-jewelled one feign dislike, and may the night be prolonged for me to implore her!","r":"The man wishes that the bright-jewelled woman would keep on sulking, and that the night would last, so that he may go on pleading.","n":"The pleading has become what he wants, and not merely a way to get to what he wants. யாமிரப்ப, 'that we may plead': the man who began the book with his might broken now prays for the chance to beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1330":{"book":"kural","num":1330,"t":["ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம் அதற்கின்பம்","கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Dislike adds delight to love; and a hearty embrace (thereafter) will add delight to dislike.","r":"The man says sulking is love's pleasure, and the pleasure of sulking is being able to come together and embrace.","n":"In the received order, the last verse of the entire work ends on பெறின், 'if one gets to'. So 1,330 verses on virtue, wealth and love close with a chained definition and an open condition. Parimelazhagar explains the condition: knowing how far to go is the hard part, so that the union will still be possible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"t":["அறுசுவை யுண்டி அமர்ந்தில்லாள் ஊட்ட","மறுசிகை நீக்கியுண் டாரும் - வறிஞராய்ச்","சென்றிரப்பர் ஓரிடத்துக் கூழ்எனின், செல்வம்ஒன்று","உண்டாக வைக்கற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"Those who ate erewhile, course after course, food of six flavours, supplied by their complaisant spouse, now roam as paupers and beg a mess of pottage here and there; if so, let wealth be counted as a thing of nought!","r":"Those who once ate six-flavoured food served by a willing wife, waving away a second helping, may go begging for gruel somewhere; so wealth is not a thing to be counted as lasting.","n":"The detail of refusing the second mouthful sets the excess of the past against the single bowl of gruel begged later; the argument rests on a picture, not on a general claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"t":["துகள்தீர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தோன்றியக்கால் தொட்டுப்","பகடு நடந்தகூழ் பல்லாரோ டுண்க","அகடுற யார்மாட்டும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","சகடக்கால் போல வரும்."],"p":"When you own ample wealth acquired by blameless means, with many sharing eat the grain that steers have trodden out! In centre poised prosperity stands with no man, but revolves like the waggon's wheel.","r":"When blameless wealth comes, from that very day share its food with many, because wealth never stays level with anyone but turns like a cart wheel.","n":"துகள்தீர் (blameless) qualifies the wealth to be shared: the verse assumes it was rightly gained. Mudaliar reads the food trodden out by beasts as a hint that others have a share in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"t":["யானை எருத்தம் பொலியக் குடைநிழற்கீழ்ச்","சேனைத் தலைவராய்ச் சென்றோரும் - ஏனை","வினைஉலப்ப வேறாகி வீழ்வர்தாம் கொண்ட","மனையாளை மாற்றார் கொள."],"p":"Those who rode resplendent forth on the neck of an elephant, beneath the state umbrella's shade, as the leaders of the host, when 'other deeds' destroy, shallchange and fall, while foes lead away their wives as captives.","r":"Even kings who rode in state on elephants under the royal umbrella, at the head of armies, fall and are changed when their deeds turn against them, and enemies take the wives they married.","n":"Both commentators take ஏனை வினை as evil deeds that destroy; Pope keeps it as 'other deeds'. The verse itself names only 'the other deeds', so the cause of the fall is left in the vocabulary of karma rather than of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"t":["நின்றன நின்றன நில்லா எனவுணர்ந்து","ஒன்றின ஒன்றின வல்லே செயின்செய்க","சென்றன சென்றன வாழ்நாள் செறுத்துடன்","வந்தது வந்தது கூற்று."],"p":"The things of which you said, ‘they stand, they stand,' stand not; mark this, and perform what befits, yea! what befits, with all your power! Your days are gone, are gone! and death close pressing on is come, is come!","r":"Realise that the things you think will stand do not stand, and do what good you can at once, for your days are passing and Death is coming in anger.","n":"Mudaliar notes that the first two doublings express abundance and the last two continuity: the form of the verse enacts days going and Death arriving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"t":["என்னானும் ஒன்றுதம் கையுறப் பெற்றக்கால்","பின்னாவ தென்று பிடித்திரா - முன்னே","கொடுத்தார் உயப்போவர் கோடில்தீக் கூற்றம்","தொடுத்தாறு செல்லும் சுரம்."],"p":"When you have gained and hold in hand any single thing, retain it not with the thought, ‘This will serve some other day!’ Those who have given betimes shall escape the desert road along which death, an unyielding foe, drags his captives away.","r":"Whatever comes into your hand, do not hold it back thinking it can be given later; those who gave first will escape the waste land through which impartial Death drags his captives.","n":"கோடில் makes Death impartial: it is the one force that does not favour the rich, which is why only what was given away counts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"t":["இழைத்தநாள் எல்லை இகவா பிழைத்தெரிணஇக்","கூற்றம் குதித்துய்ந்தார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஆற்றப்","பெரும்பொருள் வைத்தீர், வழங்குமின், நாளைத்","தழீஇம் தழீஇம் தண்ணம் படும்."],"p":"Man's days pass not their assigned bound. None here on earth have ever escaped death's power, made off and got free. O hoarder of ample wealth, dispense it! On the morn the funeral drum will sound.","r":"The appointed span of days cannot be exceeded and no one here has leapt clear of Death and escaped; you who hold great wealth, give it away, for tomorrow the funeral drum may sound.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the 'appointed day' as the lifespan set by past deeds, and cites Kural 269 for the verb குதித்தல், the very verb the verse uses to deny that anyone escapes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"t":["தோற்றம்சால் ஞாயிறு நாழியா வைகலும்","கூற்றம் அளந்துநும் நாளுண்ணும் - ஆற்ற","அறஞ்செய் தருளுடையீர் ஆகுமின் யாரும்","பிறந்தும் பிறவாதா ரில்."],"p":"Death every day takes that fount of light the sun as a measure, metes out your days, and so devours.Do deeds of virtue full of kindly grace. Though all are born, none are exempt from death.","r":"Using the sun as a measure, Death daily measures out and eats your days, so do much virtue and be compassionate; the closing line is read in more than one way.","n":"The image makes each sunrise a scoop of grain taken from one's store of days. Pope reads the last line as 'none who are born escape death', which is a third reading beside the two commentators'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","arul","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Whoever fails to do this, though born human, is counted among the unborn, that is, with beasts.",""],["shanmuganar","Be compassionate like the great who have reached the state of no further birth.",""]],"ch":"n:1"},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"t":["செல்வர்யாம் என்றுதாம் செல்வுழி எண்ணாத","புல்லறி வாளர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எல்லில்","கருங்கொண்மூ வாய்திறந்த மின்னுப்போல் தோன்றி","மருங்கறக் கெட்டு விடும்."],"p":"The ample wealth of men of mean understanding, who say, ' We're rich,' yet ponder not their path and end, appears, and perishes, and leaves no trace; like the flash, when the black thunder-cloud by night opens its mouth.","r":"The great wealth of the petty-minded, who say 'we are rich' and never think of where they must go, appears like lightning from a dark cloud at night and vanishes without a trace.","n":"Both commentators take 'where they must go' as the next world, so the fault is not having wealth but forgetting the journey it cannot make.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"t":["உண்ணான் ஒளிநிறான் ஓங்கு புகழ்செய்யான்","துன்னருங் கேளிர் துயர்களையான் - கொன்னே","வழங்கான் பொருள்காத் திருப்பானேல், அஆ","இழந்தான்என் றெண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"He eats not, sheds no light of splendour around, performs no deeds that merit lofty praise, soothes no sorrow that choice friends feel, spends nought, but hoards his wealth in vain : “Aha! he's lost it all,' shall men pronounce.","r":"One who neither eats, nor wins honour, nor does deeds of renown, nor relieves the distress of close kin, nor gives, but only guards his wealth in vain, will be thought of with pity as one who has lost it.","n":"The miser is pronounced to have lost his wealth while he still holds it: possession without use is already loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"t":["உடாஅதும் உண்ணாதும் தம்உடம்பு செற்றும்","கெடாஅத நல்லறமும் செய்யார் - கொடாஅது","வைத்தீட்டி னார்இழப்பர், வான்தோய் மலைநாட","உய்த்தீட்டும் தேனீக் கா஢."],"p":"Those who stint in clothes and food, and mortify their bodies, yet do not deeds of deathless virtue, and bestow nothing, hoarding shall suffer loss:— Lord of the cloud-capped hills !--this the hoarding honey-bee attests. ---------","r":"Those who stint on clothing and food, wear down their bodies, do no lasting good and give nothing, will lose what they hoard, as the honey bee's store shows.","n":"Mudaliar spells out the comparison: as someone else takes the honey the bees gathered, thieves and others take the hoard. The verse names only the bee as witness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"t":["நரைவரும் என்றெண்ணி நல்லறி வாளர்","குழவி யிடத்தே துறந்தார் - புரைதீரா","மன்னா இளமை மகிழ்ந்தாரே கோல்ஊன்றி","இன்னாங் கெழுந்திருப் பார்."],"p":"\"Grey eld will come,’—the wise remembering this renounce the world even in tender age; but they who joy in youth, unstable, never free from fault, shall erewhile painfully rise up leaning on a staff.","r":"Knowing that grey hair will come, the wise renounced in childhood itself, while those who delighted in faulty, fleeting youth will later rise painfully leaning on a staff.","n":"குழவியிடத்தே ('in infancy') pushes renunciation as early as language allows; the verse treats the foresight of age, not age itself, as the mark of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"t":["நட்புநார் அற்றன நல்லாரும் அஃகினார்","அற்புத் தளையும் அவிழ்ந்தன - உட்காணாய்","வாழ்தலின் ஊதியம் என்னுண்டாம்? வந்ததே","ஆழ்கலத் தன் ன கலி."],"p":"Served are the ties of friendship; minished are the pleasant ones; love's bonds are loosened too; then look within and say, what profit is there in this joyous life of thine ? The cry comes up as from a sinking ship!","r":"The cords of friendship are cut, those once close have drawn back, the bonds of love have come loose: look within and ask what profit there is in living on, for misery has come as on a sinking ship.","n":"The verse is spoken to an ageing person, perhaps the speaker himself (உட்காணாய், 'look within'). Pope's 'joyous life' is his addition; the Tamil asks only about the gain of living.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","natpu","anbu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","நல்லார் are women, whose affection has waned.",""],["shanmuganar","நல்லார் are the learned and good, who have lessened their closeness.",""]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"t":["சொல்தளர்ந்து கோல்ஊன்றிச் சோர்ந்த நடையினராய்ப்","பல்கழன்று பண்டம் பழிகாறும் - இல்செறிந்து","காம நெறிபடரும் கண்ணினார்க்கு இல்லையே","ஏம நெறிபடரு மாறு."],"p":"Speech falters, they lean on a staff, and walk tottering, their teeth fall out; yet, till the vessel (the body) is scorned by all, they linger in the house, still indulging fond desires; to these no way of safety opens out.","r":"Those whose speech fails, who lean on a staff, walk unsteadily and lose their teeth, yet stay in the house with their eyes on desire until the body is scorned, have no way to the path of safety.","n":"Both commentators take the 'path of safety' as the way to release and இல்செறிந்து as clinging to household life, so the verse faults not old age but desire that outlives the body's capacity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","ilvaazhkkai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"t":["தாழாத் தளராத் தலைநடுங்காத் தண்டுன்றா","வீழா இறக்கும் இவள்மாட்டும் - காழ்இலா","மம்மர்கொள் மாந்தர்க் கணங்காகும் தன்கைக்கோல்","அம்மனைக்கோல் ஆகிய ஞான்று."],"p":"To men that cherish weak desire for her that's doomed to droop and fail, supporting her palsied limbs with a staff, and then to fall and pass away, what anguish comes, when she grasps in her hands the staff her mother held!","r":"When a once-desired woman, bent, frail, head shaking and leaning on a staff, comes to hold the staff her mother held, she becomes a torment to men of unsteady mind who still desire her.","n":"அணங்கு is read by Mudaliar as an enchanting goddess and by Shanmuganar and Pope as pain; the line is hard, and the readings below differ on whose mother is meant.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","அம்மனைக்கோல் is the staff of her own mother.",""],["shanmuganar","Her staff looks to the men like the staff their own mother leaned on, and that pains them.",""]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"t":["எனக்குத்தாய் ஆகியாள் என்னைஈங் கிட்டுத்","தனக்குத்தாய் நாடியே சென்றாள் - தனக்குத்தாய்","ஆகி யவளும் அதுவானால் தாய்த்தாய்க்கொண்டு","ஏகும் அளித்திவ் வுலகு."],"p":"My mother bare me, left me here, and went to seek her mother, who in the selfsame manner has gone in search; and thus in ceaseless round goes on the mother-quest. Such is the grace this world affords !","r":"My mother left me here and went in search of her mother, and that mother did the same; this world goes on, mother seeking mother, a pitiable thing.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the 'seeking' as taking a new birth, so the chain is the cycle of births; Pope and Mudaliar keep it as each generation following the last into death. அளித்து is read as 'pitiable' by Mudaliar's gloss எளிமையுடைத்து; Pope renders it ironically as 'grace'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"t":["வெறியயர் வெங்களத்து வேல்மகன் பாணி","முறியார் நறங்கண்ணி முன்னர்த் தயங்க","மறிகுள குண்டன்ன மன்னா மகிழ்ச்சி","அறிவுடை யாளர்கண் இல்."],"p":"The lamb in the ruddy slaughter-house will crop the fragrant shoots that dangle from the garland in the slayer's hand; such transient gladness of the thoughtless, youthful hour is never found amid the wise.","r":"The fleeting joy of youth, like a sacrificial lamb nibbling the tender leaves of the garland held before it in the slaughter ground, is not found among the wise.","n":"The garland that feeds the lamb is the sign of its slaughter: youthful pleasure is enjoyed in the presence of what ends it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","inbam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"t":["பனிபடு சோலைப் பயன்மர மெல்லாம்","கனியுதிர்ந்து வீழ்ந்தற் றிளமை - நனிபெரிதும்","வேல்கண்ணள் என்றிவளை வெஃகன்மின் மற்றிவளும்","கோல்கண்ண ளாகும் குனிந்து."],"p":"The sweet fruit from every tree that bears in the dewy grove must fall to earth. Thus youth decays. Desire not her whose eyes gleam bright as darts. Full soon she too will walk bent down, with a staff to aid her dim sight.","r":"Youth falls as fruit drops from every tree in a cool grove; do not desire this woman for her spear-like eyes, for she too will stoop and walk with a staff for eyes.","n":"The wordplay turns வேல்கண்ணள் (spear-eyed) into கோல்கண்ணள் (staff-eyed): the same compound form names her beauty and her decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"t":["பருவம் எனைத்துள பல்லின்பால் ஏனை","இருசிகையும் உண்டீரோ என்று - வரிசையால்","உண்ணாட்டம் கொள்ளப் படுதலால் யாக்கைக்கோள்","எண்ணார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"’How old are you ?' 'How last your teeth ?' and, ‘Do you eat two courses yet?' men ask with kindly courtesy. By such close questions urged, the wise will learn to judge the body as a thing of nought.","r":"Because others ask in turn, 'How old are you? How are your teeth? Do you still eat twice?', and so examine one's body, the wise do not count on the body's vigour.","n":"The questions are polite and routine; the verse's point is that such solicitude already treats one as declining. Mudaliar records two readings of இருசிகை, a second mouthful or two meals a day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"t":["மற்றறிவாம் நல்வினை யாம்இளையம் என்னாது","கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்தே கரவா - தறம்செய்ம்மின்","முற்றி யிருந்த கனியொழியத் தீவளியால்","நற்காய் உதிர்தலும் உண்டு."],"p":"Say not, 'in after time we'll learn virtue, we're young;' but while wealth is yours conceal it not; do virtuous deeds. When evil tempests rage, not the ripe fruit alone, but the unripe fruit's fair promise also falls.","r":"Do not say 'we are young, we will learn good deeds later'; while wealth is in hand, do virtue without hiding it, for a harsh wind can drop good unripe fruit while the ripe stays on the tree.","n":"Mudaliar notes the alternative reading of கனி ஒழிய as 'the ripe fruit remaining on the tree', which sharpens the point: death may take the young and leave the old.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","aram","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"t":["ஆட்பார்த் துழலும் அருளில்கூற் றுண்மையால்","தோட்கோப்புக் காலத்தால் கொண்டுய்ம்மின் - பீட்பிதுக்கிப்","பிள்ளையைத் தாய்அலறக் கோடலான் மற்றதன்","கள்ளம் கடைப்பிடித்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Relentless death is roaming round, and eyes his man! 'Tis true. Take up your wallet, scape betimes. He bears away the new-born babe, while the mother sorely laments. It is good to bear in mind his guile. --------","r":"Since merciless Death roams looking for his people, take provisions for the journey in good time; it is well to keep his stealth in mind, for he takes even the unborn child while its mother wails.","n":"Mudaliar reads தோட்கோப்பு, a bundle carried on the shoulder, as the good deeds one needs for the next world; Pope renders it 'wallet'. The verse leaves the provisions as a figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","ilamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The shoulder-bundle is the stock of good deeds needed for the next world.",""]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"t":["மலைமிசைத் தோன்றும் மதியம்போல் யானைத்","தலைமிசைக் கொண்ட குடையர் - நிலமிசைத்","துஞ்சினார் என்றெடுத்துத் தூற்றப்பட் டாரல்லால்","எஞ்சினார் இவ்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"Even kings that rode on elephants beneath the state umbrella's shade, like the moon appearing over some bill, have had their names proclaimed on earth as dead ;— not any in this world have escaped. Time is fleeting,—use it As the measure of your days the shining orb each day unfailing rises; so before your joyous days have passed away, perform ye 'fitting deeds of grace'; for none abide on earth.","r":"Even kings who rode on elephants under umbrellas like the moon rising over a hill have been carried off and spoken of as dead; no one in this world has been left over.","n":"Mudaliar takes தூற்றப்பட்டார் as 'were disparaged by many', while Pope has 'had their names proclaimed' and Shanmuganar 'spoken of lightly'; whether the dead kings are mocked or merely announced is left open by the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"t":["வாழ்நாட் கலகா வயங்கொளி மண்டிலம்","வீழ்நாள் படாஅ தெழுதலால் - வாழ்நாள்","உலவாமுன் ஒப்புர வாற்றுமின் யாரும்","நிலவார் நிலமிசை மேல்."],"p":"","r":"Because the bright sun, as the measure of one's days, rises without missing a day, do ஒப்புரவு before your days run out; no one stays on the earth beyond them.","n":"ஒப்புரவு is the Kural's word for serving the community with one's means; Mudaliar cites Parimelazhagar's definition. He also says some read the last line in strained ways, without giving them. Pope's translation of this quatrain is attached to 21 in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","oppuravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"t":["மன்றம் கறங்க மணப்பறை யாயின","அன்றவர்க் காங்கே பிணப்பறையாய்ப் - பின்றை","ஒலித்தலும் உண்டாமென்று உய்ந்துபோம் ஆறே","வலிக்குமாம் மாண்டார் மனம்."],"p":"The marriage drums that sounded out in the festive hall, there and that very day have served for him as funeral drums! Men of lofty minds will note that thus it haps, and will strive to gain the way to escape.","r":"The drums that sounded through the hall as wedding drums may sound that same day, in that same place, as funeral drums for the bridegroom; seeing this, the minds of the worthy hold fast to the way of escape.","n":"The same instruments serve both rites; the verse makes the reversal one of meaning, not of sound. Shanmuganar names the way of escape as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"t":["சென்றே எறிப ஒருகால் சிறுவரை","நின்றே எறிப பறையினை - நன்றேகாண்","முக்காலைக் கொட்டினுள் மூடித்தீக் கொண்டுஎழுவர்","செத்தாரைச் சாவார் சுமந்து."],"p":"They march and then strike once! A little while they wait, then strike the drum a second time. Behold, how fine! The third stroke sounds. They veil it, take the fire, and go forth :—the dying bear the deed!","r":"The drummers beat once on arrival, wait a little and beat again, and at the third beat the corpse is covered and the fire taken up; the dying carry the dead.","n":"நன்றேகாண் ('fine, see!') is sarcasm for Mudaliar and a question, 'is such a life good?', for Shanmuganar; either way the last line carries the point, that the bearers are already on the same road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"t":["கணம்கொண்டு சுற்றத்தார் கல்லென் றலறப்","பிணம்கொண்டு காட்டுய்ப்பார்க் கண்டும் - மணம் கொண்டீண்டு","உண்டுண்டுண் டென்னும் உணர்வினால் சாற்றுமே","டொண்டொண்டொ டென்னும் பறை."],"p":"To him, who, although he sees them bear the corpse to the burning ground, while friends in troops loudly lament, boldly asserts that wedded life is bliss on earth, the funeral drum speaks out, and mocks his vain utterance.","r":"To the one who sees kin wailing in crowds as a corpse is carried to the burning ground, yet marries and believes there is joy here, the funeral drum answers with its டொண் டொண் டொண்.","n":"The poem sets the man's 'உண்டு உண்டு உண்டு' (there is, there is) against the drum's 'டொண் டொண் டொண்'; Mudaliar calls the drum's reply a poetic fancy. Pope's 'mocks his vain utterance' paraphrases that echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"t":["நார்த்தொடுத் தீர்க்கிலென் நன்றாய்ந் தடக்கிலென்","பார்த்துழிப் பெய்யிலென் பல்லோர் பழிக்கிலென்","தோற்பையுள் நின்று தொழிலறச் செய்தூடும்","கூத்தன் புறப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"When the 'soul', that, taking its stand in this skin clad frame, has fully wrought its works, and partaken of life's experiences, has gone forth, what matters it whether you attach ropes to the body and drag it away, or carefully bury it, or throw it aside in any place you light upon, or if many revile the departed ?","r":"Once the dancer who stayed in this skin bag and did its work has left, what does it matter whether the body is dragged off with a rope, buried with care, thrown anywhere or reviled by many?","n":"The life is a performer and the body a costume; once it has gone, the treatment of the remains has no meaning. Mudaliar lists several readings of ஊட்டும், among them the life enjoying, or being made to enjoy, the fruit of its deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"t":["படுமழை மொக்குளின் பல்காலும் தோன்றிக்","கெடுமிதோர் யாக்கையென் றெண்ணித் - தடுமாற்றம்","தீர்ப்பேம்யாம் என்றுணரும் திண்ணறி வாளரை","நேர்ப்பார்யார் நீணிலத்தின் மேல்."],"p":"'Like a bubble, that in pelting rain appears full oft, and disappears, is this our frame.' So sages have judged, steadfast in wisdom, and have decided to end this dubious strife. On this wide earth who equal these?","r":"Who on this wide earth equals those firm in understanding who see this body as a bubble in pelting rain that forms and bursts again and again, and resolve to end their wandering?","n":"'Again and again' (பல்காலும்) suggests repeated births as well as the bubble's quickness, and Mudaliar takes தடுமாற்றம் as the confusion of births. Shanmuganar reads it as worldly confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"t":["யாக்கையை யாப்புடைத்தாப் பெற்றவர் தாம்பெற்ற","யாக்கையா லாய பயன்கொள்க - யாக்கை","மலையாடு மஞ்சுபோல் தோன்றிமற் றாங்கே","நிலையாது நீத்து விடும்."],"p":"Those who've gained and held fast by this well-knit frame (a human body) should take the gain the body they have gained is intended to yield. Like a cloud that wanders over the hills, the body here appears, and abiding not, departs leaving no trace behind.","r":"Those who have a sturdy body should take the fruit that body can yield, for it appears like mist on a hill and departs there without staying.","n":"The verse does not despise the body here; it treats it as a means whose use must be taken quickly. Shanmuganar names the fruit as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","payan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"t":["புல்நுனிமேல் நீர்போல் நிலையாமை என்றெண்ணி","இன்னினியே செய்க அறவினை - இன்னினியே","நின்றான் இருந்தான் கிடந்தான்தன் கேள்அலறச்","சென்றான் எனப்படுத லால்."],"p":"Considering that all things are transient as the dew-drop on the tip of a blade of grass, now, now at once, do virtuous deeds! \"Even now he stood, he sat, he fell—while his kindred cried aloud he died :' such is man's history!","r":"Knowing that the body lasts like water on the tip of a blade of grass, do virtuous deeds right now, for people say 'he stood, he sat, he lay down, and with his kin wailing he was gone'.","n":"The four verbs compress a death into one breath of speech, the way it will be reported; Mudaliar supplies 'the body' as the subject of நிலையாமை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"t":["கேளாதே வந்து கிளைகளாய் இல்தோன்றி","வாளாதே போவரால் மாந்தர்கள் - வாளாதே","சேக்கை மரன்ஒழியச் சேண்நீங்கு புள்போல","யாக்கை தமர்க்கொழிய நீத்து."],"p":"Unasked men come, appear in the home as kinsmen, and then silently go. As the bird silently deserts the tree where its nest yet remains, and goes far off, so these leave but their body to their friends. -----------","r":"Unasked they come and appear in the home as kin, and wordlessly they go, leaving the body to their own people as a bird flies far off leaving its nesting tree.","n":"The double வாளாதே (silently) frames a life with two unannounced events, arrival and departure; family is a place of passage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"t":["அகத்தாரே வாழ்வார்என் றண்ணாந்து நோக்கிப்","புகத்தாம் பெறாஅர் பறங்கடை பற்றி","மிகத்தாம் வருந்தி இருப்பரே மேலைத்","தவத்தால் தவம்செய்யா தார்."],"p":"‘Yet those within are blest,' so saying, they look up, but obtain no entrance: their place is at the outer gate. There will they suffer much, who thro' lack of former penitence do no penance now. [In a former state penance won for them a human shape. As men they have now failed.]","r":"Those who, on the strength of earlier austerity, do no austerity now will look up at a house and say 'those inside are the ones who live', unable to enter, clinging to the outer door and grieving.","n":"The quatrain implies merit is a stock that is used up: present comfort is paid for by past austerity, and without renewal the next state is the beggar's at the gate. Pope's main text reads the cause as a lack of former penance, but his bracketed note agrees with Shanmuganar that past penance won their present state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","iravu"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","Those who enjoyed the fruit of past virtue and do none in this birth will stand outside in their next birth.",""]],"ch":"n:4"},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"t":["ஆவாம்நாம் ஆக்கம் நசைஇ அறமறந்து","போவாம்நாம் என்னாப் புலைநெஞ்சே - ஓவாது","நின்றுஞற்றி வாழ்தி எனினும்நின் வாழ்நாள்கள்","சென்றன செய்வ துரை."],"p":"Say not, O silly soul, we will live desiring wealth and die forgetting virtue! We'll say that ceaselessly toiling thou shalt live long; but tell me, what wilt thou do when all thy happy days are over?","r":"Base heart, which says 'let us prosper, desiring wealth and forgetting virtue', even if you live on toiling without rest, your days are gone: tell me what you will do.","n":"The address is to one's own heart (புலைநெஞ்சே), and the closing question is left unanswered; the speaker is arguing with himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"t":["வினைப்பயன் வந்தக்கால் வெய்ய உயிரா","மனத்தின் அழியுமாம் பேதை - நினைத்ததனைத்","தொல்லைய தென்றுணர் வாரே தடுமாற்றத்து","எல்லை இகந்தொருவு வார்."],"p":"When the ‘fruit of deeds' is come, the fool sighs heavily, and all his soul dies out; but those who reflect and say, ,'Tis old desert,' will pass beyond the bound of life's perplexity, and escape (by devoting themselves to virtue).","r":"When the fruit of past deeds arrives, the fool sighs hotly and his mind breaks; those who think of what comes as old desert pass beyond the bounds of confusion and are freed.","n":"Accepting suffering as the ripening of one's own past deeds is presented as the way out of the round itself, not only as consolation. Pope's 'by devoting themselves to virtue' is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"t":["அரும்பெறல் யாக்கையைப் பெற்ற பயத்தால்","பெரும்பயனும் ஆற்றவே கொள்க - கரும்பூர்ந்த","சாறுபோல் சாலவும் பின்உதவி மற்றதன்","கோதுபோல் போகும் உடம்பு."],"p":"As the gain from the mortal frame now reached —and which is so hard to reach —with all thy might lay hold of virtue's lasting good. As the juice expressed from the sugar-cane 'twill afterwards be thine aid, when the body goes like refuse flung away.","r":"With the gain of having obtained this rare body, take its great fruit fully; like juice pressed from cane, it serves you afterwards, and the body goes like the crushed refuse.","n":"The body is valued only as the cane is: for what can be pressed out of it. The juice, virtue, outlasts the body and is what helps 'afterwards'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"t":["கரும்பாட்டிக் கட்டி சிறுகாலைக் கொண்டார்","துரும்பெழுந்து வேம்கால் துயராண் டுழவார்","வருந்தி உடம்பின் பயன்கொண்டார் கூற்றம்","வருங்கால் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Those who have pressed the sugar-cane, and early taken the juice, when the refuse heaped up burns, will suffer no grief: those who have toiled and gained the fruit won from embodied existence will feel no pangs when death shall come.","r":"As those who crushed their cane and took the jaggery early do not grieve when the refuse catches fire and burns, those who laboured to take the body's fruit feel no distress when Death comes.","n":"சிறுகாலை (early) carries the argument: the jaggery was made in time. The burning of refuse quietly suggests cremation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"t":["இன்றுகொல் அன்றுகொல் என்றுகொல் என்னாது","பின்றையே நின்றது கூற்றமென் றெண்ணி","ஒருவுமின் தீயவை ஒல்லும் வகையால்","மருவுமின் மாண்டார் அறம்."],"p":"‘This day ?’ That day? What day? O question not the time! Bethink you death stands behind you ever waiting! Put from you every evil thing; and with all your powers embrace the virtue which sages teach.","r":"Without asking whether it is today, that day or some other day, think that Death stands right behind you; leave evil as far as you can and hold to the virtue the worthy practise.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையால் (as far as you can) sets a measure within reach, unlike the absolute demands of renunciation elsewhere in these chapters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"t":["மக்களா லாய பொரும்பயனும் ஆயுங்கால்","எத்துணையும் ஆற்றப் பலவானால் - தொக்க","உடம்பிற்கே ஒப்புராவு செய்தொழுகா தும்பர்க்","கிடந்துண்ணப் பண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"When you examine closely the mighty gains to be acquired by birth in a human shape, if they seem manifold, perform not deeds which suit the body's frame alone, but deeds whose fruit is joy in the world to come!","r":"If the gains that come through human birth are many when examined, one should not live doing service only to the gathered-up body, but should act so as to enjoy them in the world above.","n":"The verse applies ஒப்புரவு, normally service to the community, to the body itself, ironically: serving the body is a misplaced kind of duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"t":["உறக்கும் துணையதோர் ஆலம்வித் தீண்டி","இறப்ப நிழற்பயந் தாஅங்கு - அறப்பயனும்","தான்சிறி தாயினும் தக்கார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","வான்சிறிதாப் போர்த்து விடும்."],"p":"The banyan seed, though it be minute as one might see in dreams, grows to a mighty tree of amplest shade; so gifts from a virtuous hand, received by a worthy hand, though small, will hide the diminish'd heavens.","r":"As a banyan seed of the smallest size grows and casts a vast shade, so the fruit of virtue, though small, when it reaches the hands of the worthy, spreads so that the sky seems small.","n":"The growth depends on the recipient: தக்கார் கைப்பட்டக்கால் (if it falls into worthy hands). Pope reads உறக்கும் துணைய as 'minute as one might see in dreams'; Shanmuganar renders the size differently, but both make the seed tiny.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"t":["வைகலும் வைகல் வரக்கண்டும் அஃதுணரார்","வைகலும் வைகலை வைகுமென் றின்புறுவர்","வைகலும் வைகல்தம் வாழ்நாள்மேல் வைகுதல்","வைகலை வைத்துணரா தார்."],"p":"Daily they see the passing day added to the sum of the days gone by, as a day that is spent from out the store of their days, yet daily, as they see day dawn, they say joyously, 'This day will abide with us till the close of day?’","r":"Seeing day follow day, they do not grasp it, and rejoice each day that the day will pass well; they do not count each passing day as a day taken from their own lives.","n":"The quatrain plays வைகல் (day; passing) through eight forms, so the sound of repetition becomes the experience it describes. Mudaliar reports another reading of வைகுதல் as 'staying', and supplies the unstated conclusion: the wise would act on virtue at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"t":["மான அருங்கலம் நீக்கி இரவென்னும்","ஈன இளிவினால் வாழ்வேன்மன் - ஈனத்தால்","ஊட்டியக் கண்ணும் உறுதிசேர்ந் திவ்வுடம்பு","நீட்டித்து நிற்கும் எனின்."],"p":"Parting with honour's jewel I might still consent to live a suppliant's life of shame, if when maintained by such disgrace, this body could abide in strength and last for length of days. --------------","r":"I would put off the rare jewel of honour and live by the shame of begging, if a body fed by such disgrace would gain strength and last.","n":"The conditional is counterfactual: the body will not last, so honour is never worth selling for it. Mudaliar notes the particle மன் signals exactly this unfulfilled 'I would'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:41":{"book":"naladiyar","num":41,"t":["மாக்கேழ் மடநல்லாய் என்றரற்றும் சான்றவர்","நோக்கார்கொல் நொய்யதோர் புக்கிலை-யாக்கைக்கோர்","ஈச்சிற கன்னதோர் தோல்அறினும் வேண்டுமே","காக்கை கடிவதோர் கோல்."],"p":"‘O gentle maiden, fair and good !' These paragons that thus rave, know they not 'the heavenly home invisible?’ Let a bit of skin be broken slight as an insect's wing, and you need the stick that drives a way the crows !","r":"The 'worthies' who cry 'O fair, lovely girl': do they not see the body's paltry dwelling? If skin the size of a fly's wing is torn from it, a stick is needed to drive away the crows.","n":"Both commentators take புக்கில் as the body, a temporary lodging of the life; Pope renders it 'the heavenly home invisible'. Mudaliar notes that சான்றவர் (worthies) is used ironically for the petty-minded. The crows show how little stands between the admired body and carrion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pullarivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The புக்கில் is the body itself, a lodging where the life stays for a while.",""],["shanmuganar","The admirers do not know that the body is a paltry shelter.",""]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:42":{"book":"naladiyar","num":42,"t":["தோல்போர்வை மேலும் தொளைபலவாய்ப் பொய்ம்மறைக்கும்","மீப்போர்வை மாட்சித் துடம்பானால் - மீப்போர்வை","பொய்ம்மறையாக் காமம் புகலாது மற்றதனைப்","பைம்மறியாப் பார்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"If the body which, with a covering of skin above, possesses many apertures, owes its beauty only to that outward cloak that veils the false (the inner foulness), then is it fitting to say no word of lustful desire which hides the false from itself by the covering veil, but to regard that body as an undeveloped embryo.","r":"If the body, full of openings beneath its skin, is handsome only through an outer covering that hides its falsity, then without speaking desire one should look at it as a bag turned inside out.","n":"The commentators agree on the bag turned inside out; Pope instead renders பைம்மறி as 'an undeveloped embryo'. The verse asks for a change of viewpoint, not a change in the body.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Look at the body as one looks at a bag turned so that the inside faces out.",""],["shanmuganar","Turn the body inside out like a bag and its truth becomes plain.",""]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:43":{"book":"naladiyar","num":43,"t":["தக்கோலம் தின்று தலைநிறையப் பூச் சூடி","பொய்க்கோலம் செய்ய ஒழியுமே - எக்காலும்","உண்டி வினையுள் உறைக்கும் எனப்பெரியோர்","கண்டுகை விட்ட மயல்."],"p":"Will impurity ever cease from the worthless body which the great have abandoned, knowing it to be reeking with odours from processes connected with nutrition, though aromatics be chewed, the head covered with garlands, and the body adornedwith false splendour?","r":"Will chewing fragrant spices, filling the head with flowers and putting on a false finery remove the foulness the great saw and abandoned, which is always strengthened by the work of eating?","n":"Mudaliar takes மயல் as the impurities of a woman's body; Shanmuganar applies it to the body in general. The verse says only that the great saw it and gave it up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","undi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:44":{"book":"naladiyar","num":44,"t":["தெண்ணீர்க் குவளை பொருகயல் வேலென்று","கண்ணில்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவேனோ","உண்ணீர் களைந்தக்கால் நுங்குசூன் றிட்டன்ன","கண்ணீர்மை கண்டொழுகு வேன்."],"p":"Shall I abandon (my ascetic purpose) because blind, low men worry me, saying (that woman's eye is like) the water-lily in the clear stream, or the warring carp, or a javelin? I will pursue my virtuous way as having seen (that) the eye's real nature (is), like (that of) the palm-tree fruit (which is) scooped out, after the water has been drained off!","r":"Shall I give way when blind and petty men trouble me by calling eyes water-lilies in clear water, darting carp or spears? I will go on my way having seen what an eye is: like a palm fruit scooped out once its water is drained.","n":"The speaker is a renunciant guarding his resolve; the verse is personal resistance rather than general teaching. கண்ணில் (eyeless) turns the praisers' subject back on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:45":{"book":"naladiyar","num":45,"t":["முல்லை முகைமுறுவல் முத்தென் றிவைபிதற்றும்","கல்லாப்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவெனோ","எல்லாரும் காணப் புறங்காட் டுதிர்ந்துக்க","பல்லென்பு கண்டொழுகு வேன3"],"p":"Though worthless men untaught should fret my soul and rave of teeth like jasmine buds and pearls, shall I forego my fixed resolve, who have seen in the burning ground those bones—the fallen teeth —strewn round for all to see ?","r":"Shall I give way when untaught petty men chatter that smiles are jasmine buds and pearls? I will go on my way having seen the teeth and bones fallen and scattered in the burning ground for all to see.","n":"A companion to 44 with the same frame; the evidence here is not anatomy but the cremation ground, so impurity and impermanence meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:46":{"book":"naladiyar","num":46,"t":["குடரும் கொழுவும் குருதியும் என்பும்","தொடரும் நரம்பொடு தோலும் - இடையிடையே","வைத்த தடியும் வழும்புமாம் மற்றிவற்றுள்","எத்திறத்தாள் ஈர்ங்கோதை யாள்."],"p":"(The body) is entrails, and marrow, and blood, and bone, and connecting tendons, and skin, and here and there flesh interposed, and fat. In the midst of these, what sort of a being is she who wears the fresh garlands?","r":"Entrails, fat, blood, bone, linking sinews, skin, and flesh and grease set here and there: among these, which part is the woman with the fresh garland?","n":"The question is a search for the person among the parts, and implies she is none of them; the verse uses the list, not argument, to dissolve the beloved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:47":{"book":"naladiyar","num":47,"t":["ஊறி உவர்த்தக்க ஒன்பது வாய்ப்புலனும்","கோதிக் குழம்பலைக்கும் கும்பத்தைப் - பேதை","பெருந்தோளி பெய்வளாய் என்னுமீப் போர்த்த","கருந்தோலால் கண்விளக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"The fool will address the earthen pot (the body), from which defilement oozes, which from nine disgusting outlets scatters pollution, and in which slimy liquids move to and fro—and say, 'O thou of the rounded arms,' ‘O thou with armlets decked, ‘—because it is made bright to his eyes by a covering of black skin.","r":"The fool calls out 'broad-shouldered one, bangle-wearer' to a pot that seeps filth from nine openings and churns with slime, because its covering of dark skin dazzles his eyes.","n":"The terms of address the fool uses are the stock epithets of love poetry, so the verse aims at a literary convention as well as at desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:48":{"book":"naladiyar","num":48,"t":["பண்டம் அறியார் படுசாந்தும் கோதையும்","கண்டுபா ராட்டுவார் கண்டிலர்கொல் - மண்டிப்","பெடைச்சேவல் வன்கழுகு பேர்த்திட்டுக் குத்தும்","முடைச்சாகா டச்சிற் றுழி."],"p":"They know not what the body is; with sandal paste and flowers they make it fine. Have they not seen, I pray, the vultures and their mates in flocks with busy beaks devour the body foul when the chariot-axle is snapt?","r":"Those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing what the body is: have they not seen vultures, male and female, crowd in and tear at the stinking cart once its axle breaks?","n":"Mudaliar reads the axle as the life and the cart as the body. The cart image recurs from 2, where wealth turned like a wheel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:49":{"book":"naladiyar","num":49,"t":["கழிந்தார் இடுதலை கண்டார்நெஞ் சுட்கக்","குழிந்தாழ்ந்த கண்ணவாய்த் தோன்றி - ஒழிந்தாரைப்","போற்றி நெறிநின்மின் இற்றிதன் பண்பென்று","சாற்றுங்கொல் சாலச் சிரித்து."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, at the sight of which the gazer fears, with deep cavernous eyes appear, and grinning say to those who still survive, 'Guard well! In virtue's path stand fast. This is the body's grace and worth.''","r":"The skulls of the dead lying about, with deep sunken eye-holes that frighten those who see them, seem to laugh loudly and tell the living 'guard yourselves and keep to the path: this is what the body comes to'.","n":"The speech is a poetic fancy, as Mudaliar notes (தற்குறிப்பேற்றம்). He names the path as renunciation; Shanmuganar names it as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:50":{"book":"naladiyar","num":50,"t":["உயிர்போயார் வெண்டலை உட்கச் சிரித்துச்","செயிர்தீர்க்கும் செம்மாப் பவரைச் - செயிர்தீர்ந்தார்","கண்டிற் றிதன்வண்ண மென்பதனால் தம்மையோர்","பண்டத்துள் வைப்ப திலர்."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, grinning so as to excite disgust, cure the vain lovers of life of their folly. Those who are cured of this folly, seeing (the skulls in the burning ground), say “such is this body,' and so value themselves as nothing. --------------","r":"The white skulls of the dead, grinning so as to frighten, cure the infatuated of their fault; those so cured, seeing that this is the body's nature, do not count themselves as anything of worth.","n":"Mudaliar takes the infatuated as men besotted with women and 'themselves' as their bodies; Shanmuganar takes it as the proud, cured of arrogance. The verse's own words allow both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:51":{"book":"naladiyar","num":51,"t":["விளக்குப் புகஇருள் மாய்ந்தாங்கு ஒருவன்","தவத்தின்முன் நில்லாதாம் பாவம் - விளக்குநெய்","தேய்விடத்துச் சென்றிருள் பாய்ந்தாங்கு நல்வினை","தீர்விடத்து நிற்குமாம் தீது."],"p":"As when a lamp enters darkness dies, so sin stands not before man's penitence. As, when in a lamp the oil wastes, darkness rushes in, so evil takes its stand where deeds of virtue cease.","r":"As darkness dies when a lamp comes in, sin cannot stand before a person's austerity; and as darkness rushes back when the lamp's oil runs out, evil settles in once good deeds are exhausted.","n":"The second half limits the first: austerity protects only while the merit that feeds it lasts, so the lamp makes merit something burned up, not something owned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Austerity here stands for the whole way of renunciation.",""]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:52":{"book":"naladiyar","num":52,"t":["நிலையாமை நோய்மூப்புச் சாக்காடென் றெண்ணித்","தலையாயார் தங்கருமம் செய்வார் - தொலைவில்லாச்","சத்தமும் சோதிடமும் என்றாங் கிவைபிதற்றும்","பித்தரின் பேதையார் இல்."],"p":"The chief of men reflect on change, disease, old age, and death, and do their needful work. Those who raving teach the endless round of sevenfold science, and the lore of stars, are maddest of the foolish throng.","r":"The best of people reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do the work that is truly theirs; none are more foolish than the madmen who prattle endlessly of சத்தம் and astrology.","n":"The target is not learning as such but study that never ends, pursued in place of what death makes urgent. 'Their own work' (தங்கருமம்) echoes Kural 266, which Mudaliar quotes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thavam","kalvi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","'Their own work' is austerity, and the endless lore is grammar and mathematics or astrology, tool-sciences studied for a lifetime instead of the way of renunciation.",""],["shanmuganar","The wise do the good deeds fitting for them, and the endless lore is Vedic sastra and astrology.",""]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:53":{"book":"naladiyar","num":53,"t":["இல்லம் இளமை எழில்வனப்பு மீக்கூற்றம்","செல்வம் வலிஎன் றிவையெல்லாம் - மெல்ல","நிலையாமை கண்டு நெடியார் துறப்பர்","தலையாயார் தாம்உய்யக் கொண்டு."],"p":"The chief of men in quite thought discern how house, and youth, and beauty's grace, and high estate, and wealth, and strength, all pass away; and thus, to save themselves, prolonging not the time, renounce all these.","r":"Seeing that home, youth, beauty, high repute, wealth and strength all fail to last, the best of people, considering slowly, renounce them without delay so as to save themselves.","n":"மெல்ல and நெடியார் pull two ways: the examination is unhurried, the renouncing is not. Mudaliar keeps both, glossing one as deliberate inquiry and the other as acting without delay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:54":{"book":"naladiyar","num":54,"t":["துன்பம் பலநாள் உழந்தும் ஒருநாளை","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இடைதெரிந் தின்னாமை நோக்கி மனையாறு","அடைவொழிந்தார் ஆன்றமைந் தார்."],"p":"Though wretched men suffer afflictions many a day, yet one day's delight they eagerly desire. The men of calm and full wisdom, in pleasure's core see pain, and quit the pleasant household paths.","r":"Fools, after many days of suffering, still long for a single day's pleasure; the full and settled, discerning pleasure for what it is and seeing its pain, keep off the household way.","n":"The verse names household life (மனையாறு) as what the wise leave, which makes it a direct answer to the Kural's defence of the householder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The wise weigh, impartially, how small household pleasure is.",""],["shanmuganar","The wise see that pleasure comes only now and then, a little at a time, and pain is the greater part.",""]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:55":{"book":"naladiyar","num":55,"t":["கொன்னே கழிந்தன் றிளமையும் இன்னே","பிணியொடு மூப்பும் வருமால் - துணிவொன்றி","என்னொடு சூழா தெழுநெஞ்சே போதியோ","நன்னெறி சேர நமக்கு."],"p":"In vain is my youth spent. Even now disease and old age will come. O soul ! be bold; wrangle no more with me, but rise! Wilt thou not go where both thou and I may gain virtue's path?","r":"The speaker tells his own heart that youth has passed to no purpose and disease and old age are coming now, and asks it to stop going its own way and set out with him on the good path.","n":"The heart is treated as a separate, wayward party to be persuaded, not commanded. Mudaliar takes the good path to be release through renunciation; Shanmuganar says only 'a good way'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:56":{"book":"naladiyar","num":56,"t":["மாண்ட குணத்தொடு மக்கட்பே றில்லெனினும்","பூண்டான் கழித்தற் கருமையால் - பூண்ட","மிடியென்னும் காரணத்தின் மேன்முறைக் கண்ணே","கடியென்றார் கற்றறிந் தார்."],"p":"Though your wife possess no excellence and bear no child, it is hard to get rid of the marriage bond. For this cause since he who weds puts sorrow on, in olden days the learned made marriage a synonym of dread.","r":"Even if a wife lacks good qualities and bears no children, the husband cannot put her away; because marriage brings this hardship, the learned gave it the name கடி.","n":"The argument turns on a pun, since கடி means both marriage and 'shun'. The verse finds no fault in marriage to a good wife; it argues from the worst case that the bond, once made, can't be undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word கடி has both senses, marriage and removal, and the poet invents a reason for the name, a figure Mudaliar calls நிருக்தி.",""]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:57":{"book":"naladiyar","num":57,"t":["ஊக்கித்தாம் கொண்ட விரதங்கள் உள்ளுடையத்","தாக்கருந் துன்பங்கள் தாந்தலை வந்தக்கால்","நீக்கி நிறூஉம் உரவோரே நல்லொழுக்கம்","காக்கும் திருவத் தவர்."],"p":"When troubles arise, hard to resist, to cause them to break the vows which their lofty spirits have pledged, the men of power set griefs aside and firmly fix their souls in right. These are the blessed, guarding decorum's rule.","r":"When unbearable troubles come that would break the vows they took on with zeal, those who push the troubles aside and hold the vows firm are the blessed ones who guard good conduct.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","thitpam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:58":{"book":"naladiyar","num":58,"t":["தம்மை யிகழ்ந்தமை தாம்பொறுப்ப தன்றிமற்று","எம்மை இகழ்ந்த வினைப்பயத்தால் - உம்மை","எரிவாய் நிரயத்து வீழ்வர்கொல் என்று","பரிவதூஉம் சான்றோர் கடன்."],"p":"To bear with those that speak contemptuous words; yea, more, to say, 'Ah, will these sink in the other world to hell, the place of fire, as fruit of their contemptuous words;' and to grieve, is duty of the perfect man.","r":"Bearing contempt is not enough: the worthy also grieve for those who scorned them, fearing that the deed will drop them into the fiery hell.","n":"The pity looks to the offender's future, not to one's own injury, so forbearance becomes a form of compassion. Parimelazhagar cites this quatrain when glossing Kural 157.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","arul","vinai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:59":{"book":"naladiyar","num":59,"t":["மெய்வாய்கண் மூக்குச் செவியெனப் பேர்பெற்ற","ஐவாய வேட்கை அவாவினைக் - கைவாய்","கலங்காமல் காத்துய்க்கும் ஆற்றல் உடையான்","விலங்காது வீடு பெறும்."],"p":"He who undisturbed, in the ordered way of right, has power to guard and guide the desires and lusts that find entrance by the five sense-gates, called 'body, mouth, eye, nose, and ear' - unfailing shall gain 'release.'","r":"One who has the strength to guard and steer, without wavering, the craving that enters through the five gates named body, mouth, eyes, nose and ears will surely reach release.","n":"The senses are called five mouths (ஐவாய) through which craving feeds; the verse asks for them to be steered, not destroyed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:60":{"book":"naladiyar","num":60,"t":["துன்பமே மீதூரக் கண்டும் துறவுள்ளார்","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இசைதொறும் மற்றதன் இன்னாமை நோக்கிப்","பசைதல் பரியாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though wretched men behold afflictions urge and press, renunciation is not in their thoughts; delight they eagerly desire. The great in every joy behold its pain, and seek it not. -----","r":"Fools, though they see suffering mount, have no thought of renouncing and long only for pleasure; the higher ones, whenever pleasure comes, look to the pain in it and do not cling to it.","n":"It reworks 54 almost line for line, but where 54 has the wise leave household life, this has them refuse attachment as each pleasure arrives: a discipline inside experience rather than a change of state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","pattru","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:61":{"book":"naladiyar","num":61,"t":["மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க மதியா","மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க - மதித்தேறி","ஈயும் தலைமேல் இருத்தலால் அஃதறிவார்","காயும் சுதமின்மை நன்று."],"p":"Who pass esteeming us, let them pass on! And those who contemn and trample on us as they pass, let them too pass on! If even a fly (especially unclean) should climb, trampling on their head, it is well that the wise who know its worth, should feel no wrath.","r":"Let those who honour us pass, and let those who trample us pass too; since even a fly can climb onto one's head, those who understand this do well to be free of burning anger.","n":"The fly deflates the insult: being stepped on is something even the smallest creature does, so it carries no weight. The printed second and third lines repeat மதித்- where both commentators read மிதித்- (trample), and the last line's wording looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","porai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:62":{"book":"naladiyar","num":62,"t":["தண்டாச் சிறப்பின்தம் இன்னுயிரைத் தாங்காது","கண்டுழி யெல்லாம் துறப்பவோ - மண்டி","அடிபெயரா தாற்ற இளிவந்த போழ்தின்","முடிகிற்கும் உள்ளத் தவர்."],"p":"Although disgraces throng thickly, and may not be repulsed, will those whose minds are set upon finishing the work begun, renounce sweet life's unfailing worth in their impatience, whenever they see (evils)?","r":"Those whose minds can carry a task through will not throw away their precious life whenever they meet disgrace, even when it presses so hard they cannot move a step.","n":"Placed in a chapter on not being angry, the quatrain can be read as treating death at humiliation as anger turned on oneself. That is an interpretation from its placement; the verse itself argues only from the unfinished task.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thitpam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:63":{"book":"naladiyar","num":63,"t":["காவா தொருவன்தன் வாய்திறந்து சொல்லும்சொல்","ஓவாதே தன்னைச் சுடுதலால் - ஓவாதே","ஆய்ந்தமைந்த கேள்வி அறிவுடையார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","காய்ந்தமைந்த சொல்லார் கறுத்து."],"p":"If a man open his mouth and speak unguarded words, his words will ceaselessly burn his soul. The wise who ceaselessly hear, and ponder well and calmly, even in their wrath, will never give utterance to words of fire.","r":"Unguarded words from an open mouth burn the speaker without end, so the learned, who listen and reflect without end, never speak scorching words even when angry.","n":"The repeated ஓவாதே sets the endless burning of careless speech against endless listening as its cure. The burn falls on the speaker, not the hearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kelvi","vekuli","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:64":{"book":"naladiyar","num":64,"t":["நேர்த்து நிகரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்","வேர்த்து வெகுளார் விழுமியோர் - ஓர்த்ததனை","உள்ளத்தான் உள்ளி உரைத்துராய் ஊர்கேட்பத்","துள்ளித்தூண் முட்டுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"When men who are beneath them confront them, and speak unseemly words, the excellent wax not hot with anger. The base man will brood over it, chafe and rave for all the town to hear, and leap, and dash his head against a post.","r":"The excellent do not grow hot with anger when people beneath them confront them with unfit words; the base brood on it, rant for the town to hear, leap about and butt a pillar.","n":"The comic last line makes anger self-injuring rather than wicked: the head that hits the pillar is the angry man's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:65":{"book":"naladiyar","num":65,"t":["இளையான் அடக்கம் அடக்கம் கிளைபொருள்","இல்லான் கொடையே கொடைப்பயன் - எல்லாம்","ஒறுக்கும் மதுகை உரனுடை யாளன்","பொறுக்கும் பொறையே பொறை."],"p":"The young man's self-restraint is self-restraint. The gift of him who owns no stores of wealth is gift indeed. When wan has means and might to punish every fault, if he forbear, call him the patient man.","r":"Restraint in youth is true restraint, a gift from one without wealth is the true fruit of giving, and the patience of a strong person able to punish everything is true patience.","n":"Each virtue counts only where its opposite would be easy: youth tempts, poverty excuses, power permits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","adakkam","eegai","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:66":{"book":"naladiyar","num":66,"t":["கல்லெறிந் தன்ன கயவர்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்","எல்லாரும் காணப் பொறுத்துய்ப்பர் - ஓல்லை","இடுநீற்றால் பைஅவிந்த நாகம்போல் தத்தம்","குடிமையான் வாதிக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"As stones the base shower down their bitter words: the noble bear, in sight of all, and let them pass, by sense of noble worth constrained : like serpent's crest at once by touch of sacred ash subdued.","r":"The well-born bear the stone-like bitter words of the base in front of everyone and let them pass, held back by their own lineage as a cobra's hood is quelled at once by sacred ash.","n":"The image grants that the noble have venom: their forbearance is power held in, not its absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kudi","kadunjol","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:67":{"book":"naladiyar","num":67,"t":["மாற்றாராய் நின்றுதம் மாறேற்பார்க்கு ஏலாமை","ஆற்றாமை என்னார் அறிவுடையார் - ஆற்றாமை","நேர்த்தின்னா மற்றவர் செய்தக்கால் தாம்அவரைப்","பேர்த்தின்னா செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"When men stand forth as our enemies, and would begin the conflict, to decline the strife is not, in the language of the wise, lack of power. Even when men have confronted and done us intolerable evils, it is good not to do them evil in return.","r":"The wise do not call it weakness to decline a fight with those who come at you as enemies; even when they confront you and do unbearable harm, it is good not to harm them in return.","n":"The first half answers the charge of weakness before the second recommends restraint. With neither commentary aligned, this reading rests on the Tamil and Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","innaa","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:68":{"book":"naladiyar","num":68,"t":["நெடுங்காலம் ஓடினும் நீசர் வெகுளி","கெடுங்காலம் இன்றிப் பரக்கும் - அடுங்காலை","நீர்கொண்ட வெப்பம்போல் தானே தணியுமே","சீர்கொண்ட சான்றோர் சினம்."],"p":"","r":"The anger of the low keeps spreading however long it runs and never dies, while the anger of the worthy cools of itself, like the heat water takes on when boiled.","n":"Water's heat is borrowed from the fire, so the simile can suggest that the worthy's anger comes from outside and leaves them as they were. That is my reading of the image, not the commentators'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:69":{"book":"naladiyar","num":69,"t":["உபகாரம் செய்ததனை ஓராதே தங்கண்","அபகாரம் ஆற்றச் செயினும் - உபகாரம்","தாம்செய்வ தல்லால் தவற்றினால் தீங்கூக்கல்","வான்தோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கில்."],"p":"Though men think not of good received, and do much ill to men of family whose fame has touched the sky, these still do good; nor are they wont provoked by faults, to render evil to the thankless ones.","r":"Even when people forget the help they received and do great harm, those born in families whose fame touches the sky only keep doing good, and do not seek to repay the fault with harm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nanri","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:70":{"book":"naladiyar","num":70,"t":["கூர்த்துநாய் கெளவிக் கொளக்கண்டும் தம்வாயால்","பேர்த்துநாய் கெளவினார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஈர்த்தன்றிக்","கீழ்மக்கள் கீழாய சொல்லியக்கால் சொல்பவோ","மேன்மக்கள் தம்வாயால் மீட்டு."],"p":"Though a dog, in range, should lay hold of them, there are none in the world who, in return, would lay hold of it with their mouth; and thus, when low men speak, not what is fitting, but low words, will high-minded men, in reply, utter such words with their mouth. ----------","r":"No one bitten by a raging dog bites it back; so when the low speak low words, will the high answer in kind with their own mouths?","n":"The analogy works by ranking the insulter with the dog: answering in kind would mean stooping to its level.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","porai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:71":{"book":"naladiyar","num":71,"t":["கோதை யருவிக் குளர்வரை நன்னாட","பேதையொடு யாதும் உரையற்க - பேதை","உரைக்கிற் சிதைந்துரைக்கும் ஒல்லும் வகையான்","வழக்கிக் கழிதலே நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land, where down the cool mountains the streams fall as garlands!— With a fool hold no converse. If you speak with him, in replying he will pervert your words. To slip away from him as best you can is well.","r":"Lord of the good cool hill country where streams fall like garlands: say nothing at all to a fool, who will twist what you say; slipping away as best you can is best.","n":"Forbearance here takes the form of withdrawal rather than endurance: the wise don't stay to bear the fool's talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","sol","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:72":{"book":"naladiyar","num":72,"t":["நேரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால் மற்றது","தாரித் திருத்தல் தகுதிமற் - றோரும்","புகழ்மையாக் கொள்ளாது பொங்குநீர் ஞாலம்","சமழ்மையாக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"When persons not our equals say unfitting things, to bear and to be still is worthy conduct. The world girt by foaming waters regards not a contrary course as praise-worthy, but as discreditable.","r":"When those not one's equals say unfit things, it is fitting to bear them in silence; the world does not count the opposite as praise but as disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar takes the 'world' that judges to be the worthy, not people at large.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pazhi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:73":{"book":"naladiyar","num":73,"t":["காதலார் சொல்லும் கடுஞ்சொல் உவந்துரைக்கும்","ஏதிலார் இன்சொலின் தீதாமோ - போதெலாம்","மாதர்வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஆவ தறிவார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore, where bright insects hum around every flower!—Are severe words from loving lips harder to bear, if men can only rightly estimate their result, than pleasant words that strangers courteously speak?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the teeming sea, where bees hum over every flower: for one who finds people who know what is good for him, are harsh words from those who love worse than pleasant words that strangers speak with a smile?","n":"The condition carries the weight: harsh words are worth more only if one finds friends who know what is good (ஆவது அறிவார்). Mudaliar reads பெறின் as stressing how rare such friends are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kadunjol","inchol","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:74":{"book":"naladiyar","num":74,"t":["அறிவ தறிந்தடங்கி அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சி","உறுவ துலகுவப்பச் செய்து - பெறுவதனால்","இன்புற்று வாழும் இயல்பினார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","துன்புற்று வாழ்தல் அரிது."],"p":"Those who know what should be known and rule themselves thereby; who fear what should be feared ; who use all their faculties to bless the world; and whose nature rejoices in all good gained: are for ever free from woes.","r":"Those who know what must be known, keep restraint, fear what must be feared, do what is due so that the world rejoices, and live content with what they get, are hardly ever in suffering.","n":"Mudaliar glosses அடங்கி as 'being forbearing', which ties this broad portrait of contentment to the chapter's theme.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","adakkam","acham","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:75":{"book":"naladiyar","num":75,"t":["வேற்றுமை யின்றிக் கலந்திருவர் நட்டக்கால்","தேற்றா ஓழுக்கம் ஒருவன்கண் உண்டாயின்","ஆற்றும் துணையும் பொறுக்க பொறானாயின்","தூற்றாதே தூர விடல்."],"p":"When two with strict accord unite in friendship's bond, if one betray the other's confidence by unkind act, this latter should endure as best he may. And if he can't endure, he should not divulge it, but withdraw himself.","r":"When two have joined in friendship without any distinction and one of them shows conduct the other cannot make sense of, the other should bear it as far as he can, and if he can't, should withdraw without spreading it abroad.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","purangooral"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The friend's lapse is an act done unknowingly, or one that can't be told to others.",""],["shanmuganar","The friend is found in bad conduct.",""]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:76":{"book":"naladiyar","num":76,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தான்நோவின் அல்லது - துன்னிக்","கலந்தாரைக் கைவிடுதல் கானக நாட","விலங்கிற்கும் விள்ளல் அரிது."],"p":"but never forsake him. Lord of the woodlands! Separation is hard even to beasts; therefore if friends do things that are unpleasant, think them pleasant. bid yourself cease (from wrath), and blame yourself alone; but forsake not those that have been joined to you in the intimacy of friendship.","r":"Lord of the forest land: even if friends do hurtful things, wish them to pass as if pleasant and blame only yourself, but do not abandon those joined to you closely, for parting is hard even for beasts.","n":"Blaming oneself is left open: Mudaliar records two readings, one moral and one karmic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","anbu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One blames oneself either for the bad deeds of a former birth that caused the hurt, or for befriending without first examining the friend.",""]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:77":{"book":"naladiyar","num":77,"t":["பெரியார் பெருநட்புக் கோடல்தாம் செய்த","அரிய பொறுப்பஎன் றன்றோ - அரியரோ","ஒல்லென் அருவி உயர்வரை நன்னாட","நல்லசெய் வார்க்குத் தமர்."],"p":"Is not the reason why the close friendship of the great is sought, that they will bear even with faults hard to endure? Lord of the good land of high mountains, with resounding waterfalls! — to good people are intimate friends rare?","r":"Is it not because the great bear with faults that are hard to bear that people seek their deep friendship? Lord of the good high hills with roaring falls, are close friends rare for those who do good?","n":"The closing question can be taken two ways: Mudaliar reads it as 'friends are not rare for the good', Shanmuganar as 'the great are not hard to win for those who do good without fault'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:78":{"book":"naladiyar","num":78,"t":["வற்றிமற் றாற்றப் பசிப்பினும் பண்பிலார்க்கு","அற்றம் அறிய உரையற்க - அற்றம்","மறைக்கும் துணையார்க் குரைப்பவே தம்மைத்","துறக்கும் துணிவிலா தார்."],"p":"Though sore wasted with hunger, let not men tell out their destitution to ungracious churls. Those indeed who lack resolution to deny themselves may tell their wants to those who are able and willing to save them from destitution.","r":"Even if wasted and starving, do not tell your need to people without courtesy; those who lack the resolve to give up their life tell it only to those able to cover it.","n":"Both commentators take தம்மைத் துறக்கும் துணிவு as resolve to give up one's life, so the quatrain ranks starving in silence above asking even the right person; Pope reads it as resolve to deny oneself. மறைக்கும் can mean relieving the need or keeping it secret, and Mudaliar allows both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:79":{"book":"naladiyar","num":79,"t":["இன்பம் பயந்தாங் கிழிவு தலைவரினும்","இன்பத்தின் பக்கம் இருந்தைக்க - இன்பம்","ஒழியாமை கண்டாலும் ஓங்கருவி நாட","பழியாகா ஆறே தலை."],"p":"Although shameful things may present themselves. as things that yield pleasure, flee from that pleasure's side! Though thou couldst see pleasures that cease not soon, — Lord of the land of fountains high! — the guiltless way is best.","r":"Lord of the land of high waterfalls: even if disgrace comes along the way pleasure opened, and even if the pleasure looks as if it would never end, the way that brings no blame comes first.","n":"The second concession is the stronger: not only brief pleasures but lasting ones are to be refused if they carry blame. The first two lines are knotted, and Mudaliar resolves இருந்தைக்க as addressed to the listener.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","inbam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:80":{"book":"naladiyar","num":80,"t":["தான்கெடினும் தக்கார்கே டெண்ணற்க தன்உடம்பின்","ஊன்கெடினும் உண்ணார்கைத் துண்ணற்க - வான்கவிந்த","வையக மெல்லாம் பெறினும் உரையற்க","பொய்யோடு இடைமிடைந்த சொல்."],"p":"Though ruin seize you plan not ruin to the just! Though body's flesh should waste, eat not from hands unfit! Though the whole earth o'er-arched by heaven accrue as gain, never speak word with falsehood mixed !","r":"Though ruined, do not plan the ruin of the worthy; though your flesh wastes, do not eat from the hands of those you must not eat from; though you would gain the whole sky-roofed earth, do not speak words mixed with falsehood.","n":"Each refusal is set against the extreme loss that would excuse it, which is how the quatrain belongs to a chapter on forbearance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vaaimai","undi","porai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those whose food must not be eaten are the guru, the deity and the poor; he records others who say lower castes or those who serve without love.",""]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:81":{"book":"naladiyar","num":81,"t":["அச்சம் பெரிதால் அதற்கின்பம் சிற்றளவால்","நிச்சம் நினையுங்கால் கோக்கொலையால் - நிச்சலும்","கும்பிக்கே கூர்த்த வினையால் பிறன்தாரம்","நம்பற்க நாணுடை யார்."],"p":"The fear it brings is great ; its pleasure is brief ; each day if it is divulged death threatens by the king's decree; and ever it is a deed that tends to the pains of hell; O shamefast men, desire not your neighbour's wife.","r":"The fear is great and the pleasure small; thought through, it risks death at the king's hands and is a deed that daily sharpens toward hell, so those with a sense of shame should not set their hopes on another man's wife.","n":"The case is prudential from start to finish, weighing fear against pleasure, but the appeal at the end is to shame (நாணுடையார).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:82":{"book":"naladiyar","num":82,"t":["அறம்புகழ் கேண்மை பெருமைஇந் நான்கும்","பிறன்தாரம் நச்சுவார்ச் சேரா - பிறன்தாரம்","நச்சுவார்ச் சேரும் பகைபழி பாவம்என்று","அச்சத்தோடு இந்நாற் பொருள்."],"p":"Virtue, praise, friendship, greatness, all these four draw not anigh the men who covet their neighbour's wife. Hatred, disgrace and guilt, with fear, these four possessions abide with men who covet their neighbour's wife.","r":"Virtue, renown, friendship and greatness do not come to those who desire another man's wife; enmity, blame, sin and fear do.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","aram","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:83":{"book":"naladiyar","num":83,"t":["புக்க விடத்தச்சம் போதரும் போதச்சம்","துய்க்கு மிடத்தச்சம் தோன்றாமல் காப்பச்சம்","எக்காலும் அச்சம் தருமால் எவன்கொலோ","உட்கான் பிறன்இல் புகல்."],"p":"In entering in there's fear; in home returning fear; during enjoyment is fear; in guarding the secret is fear; it evermore brings fear: why shuns he not with dread the entrance of neighbour's house?","r":"Fear on entering, fear on returning, fear while enjoying, fear in keeping it hidden: it brings fear at every moment, so why does a man enter another's house without dread?","n":"The repetition of அச்சம் enacts the condition it describes. The closing question turns on the same word: the one who lives in fear lacks the one fear that would have kept him out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:84":{"book":"naladiyar","num":84,"t":["காணின் குடிப்பழியாம் கையுறின் கால்குறையும்","ஆணின்மை செய்யுங்கால் அச்சமாம் - நீள்நிரயத்","துன்பம் பயக்குமால் துச்சாரி, நீகண்ட","இன்பம் எனக்கெனைத்தால் கூறு."],"p":"If any one see, disgrace lights on the house; if any hand should seize, leg's maimed; in the doing of the shameful deed is dread; it yields as fruit vast hell's affliction : tell me, O profligate, what measure of delight is thine ?","r":"If seen, the family is disgraced; if caught, a leg is cut off; the unmanly act itself is fear; and it yields long suffering in hell: tell me, profligate, what pleasure did you find?","n":"Mudaliar reads ஆணின்மை as the absence of the qualities that make a man, so the adulterer's daring is renamed unmanliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","acham","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:85":{"book":"naladiyar","num":85,"t":["செம்மையொன் றின்றிச் சிறியா ரினத்தராய்க்","கொம்மை வரிமுலையாள் தோள்மாணஇ - உம்மை","வலியால் பிறர்மனைமேல் சென்றாரே, இம்மை","அலியாகி ஆடிஉண் பார்."],"p":"Those who, in a former state, without any regard to right, becoming associates of the mean, enjoyed the embraces of beautiful women, and by violence approached their neighbour's wife, in this state will become eunuchs, and dancing shall earn their bread.","r":"Those who in a former birth, with no sense of right and in the company of the mean, went by force to other men's wives become eunuchs in this birth and live by dancing.","n":"The punishment mirrors the offence: those who used male force are reborn without it. This is the chapter's one claim about a past birth; the Kural's chapter on another's wife makes none.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:86":{"book":"naladiyar","num":86,"t":["பல்லா ரறியப் பறையறைந்து நாள்கேட்டுக்","கல்யாணம் செய்து கடிபுக்க - மெல்லியல்","காதல் மனையாளும் இல்லாளா என்ஒருவன்","ஏதில் மனையாளை நோக்கு."],"p":"While his loving wife dwells in his home, the tender one whom he espoused, — seeking (out an auspicious) day, and sounding the drum, for many folks to know,—and 'whom he guards as his own, what means a man's glance at another's wife?","r":"When a man has at home a tender, loving wife, married on a day fixed by asking, with drums beaten for many to know, why should he look at a stranger's wife?","n":"The public wedding is the point: a bond announced to many and guarded (கடி) is set against one that has to be hidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:87":{"book":"naladiyar","num":87,"t":["அம்பல் அயல்எடுப்ப அஞ்சித் தமர்பாணஇ","வம்பலன் பெண்மாணஇ மைந்துற்று - நம்பும்","நிலைமைஇல் நெஞ்சத்தான் துப்புரவு பாம்பின்","தலைநக்கி யன்ன துடைத்து."],"p":"The enjoyment of the man of unstable heart, who under the influence of infatuation approaches his neighbour's wife and sets his affections upon her, while neighbours spread abroad his guilt, and kinsmen dread and mourn, is like (that of the person who takes pleasure in) licking a serpent's head.","r":"The pleasure of the unsteady-hearted man who, maddened, unites with another's wife while neighbours gossip and his own people tremble and grieve, is like licking a cobra's head.","n":"The image holds pleasure and lethal danger in a single act, so no separate weighing of costs is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:88":{"book":"naladiyar","num":88,"t":["பரவா, வெளிப்படா, பல்லோர்கண் தங்கா","உரவோர்கண் காமநோய் ஓஓ.","கொடிதே. விரவாருள் நாணுப் படல்அஞ்சி யாதும்","உரையாதுஉள் ஆறி விடும்."],"p":"The disease of lust in men mighty (in wisdom) gains not ascendancy, is not revealed, does not remain fixed on many objects. O! it is a cruel conflict; but fearing to incur shame in the midst of their foes, they say nothing about it, and it is extinguished within them.","r":"In the strong, the sickness of desire does not spread, show itself or settle on many; fearing shame among those outside, they say nothing and it subsides within. Oh, how cruel it is!","n":"The strong are not free of desire, only able to contain it, and the cry 'how cruel' can fall on that containment. Mudaliar reads it so: desire that can't come out and must be suffered inwardly is the cruellest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because it doesn't come out but stays within and subsides only after great suffering, the desire of the wise is called very cruel.",""]],"ch":"n:9"},"n:89":{"book":"naladiyar","num":89,"t":["அம்பும் அழலும் அவிர்கதிர் ஞாயிறும்","வெம்பிச் சுடினும் புறம்சுடும் - வெம்பிக்","கவற்றி மனத்தைச் சுடுதலால், காமம்","அவற்றினும் அஞ்சப் படும்."],"p":"Arrow and fire and sun with glistening rays may rage and burn; but these burn the outer man alone. Lust rages and distracts and burns the mind, and is more to be feared than they.","r":"Arrow, fire and the sun's bright rays, however they blaze, burn only the outside; desire, blazing and troubling the mind, burns the mind itself, so it is to be feared more than they.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:90":{"book":"naladiyar","num":90,"t":["ஊருள் எழுந்த உருகெழு செந்தீக்கு","நீருள் குளித்தும் உயலாகும் - நீருள்","குளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடுமேகுன் றேறி","ஒளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடும்."],"p":"From the ruddy fire that fiercely rises in the village you may scape by bathing in water. — Although you bathe in water, lust will burn; and though you climb the hill and hide you there, still lust will burn! -----------","r":"From a fierce red fire rising in the town one can escape by plunging into water; but desire burns even if one plunges into water, and burns even if one climbs a hill to hide.","n":"Water and the hilltop are the two refuges from fire, and both fail because this fire travels with the one who flees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:91":{"book":"naladiyar","num":91,"t":["இல்லா விடத்தும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","உள்ள விடம்போல் பெரிதுவந்து - மெல்லக்","கொடையொடு பட்ட குணனுடைய மாந்தர்க்கு","அடையாவாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு"],"p":"Even in their adverse hour, up to the measure of their means, as in the prosperous times, with large rejoicing heart to give is their nature still. To such good men heaven's gate is never closed.","r":"Those whose nature is to give gladly, as much as they can, even when they have little, as if they had plenty, will not find the doors of that world shut against them.","n":"Gladness is part of the qualification: the gift is measured against the giver's means and his spirit, not its size.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The doors are those of the other world, which Pope calls heaven.",""],["shanmuganar","The doors are those of release (வீடுபேறு), not of a heaven.",""]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:92":{"book":"naladiyar","num":92,"t":["முன்னரே சாம்நாள் முனிதக்க மூப்புள","பின்னரும் பீடழிக்கும் நோயுள - கொன்னே","பரவன்மின் பற்றன்மின் பாத்துண்மின் யாதும்","கரவன்மின் கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்து."],"p":"Before (you) are death's day and age detestable ; behind is disease that humbles pride. Discursive thoughts indulge not. Cling not to earth. Eat, sharing food; hide not your powers while wealth is in your hand.","r":"Death's day and hateful old age stand right before you, and disease that destroys strength comes after; don't run about in vain, don't clutch, share before you eat, and hide nothing while wealth is in your hand.","n":"The case for giving here is impermanence, not merit or fame: wealth is to be used while the hand still holds it. Mudaliar takes பரவன்மின் as 'don't roam after more wealth'; Pope has 'indulge not discursive thoughts'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:93":{"book":"naladiyar","num":93,"t":["நடுக்குற்றுத் தற்சேர்ந்தார் துன்பம் துடையார்","கொடுத்துத்தான் துய்ப்பினும் ஈண்டுங்கால் ஈண்டும்","மிடுக்குற்றுப் பற்றினும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","விடுக்கும் வினையுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Though dread of want they do not relieve the woes of men who as suppliants draw near! Yet although men enjoy and give, wealth grows in growing time: cling to it, and yet it flies when former deeds that brought wealth have lost their power.","r":"Whether one ignores the misery of those who come trembling or gives and enjoys, wealth gathers when its time to gather comes; and however tightly it is held, it leaves when the good deeds that sent it run out.","n":"The argument removes the miser's motive: giving can't lose wealth that merit holds, and clutching can't keep wealth that merit has stopped holding. Mudaliar notes the variant இடுக்குற்று (clutching tight) for மிடுக்குற்று (with force).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai","oozh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:94":{"book":"naladiyar","num":94,"t":["இம்மி யரிசித் துணையானும் வைகலும்","நும்மில் இயைவ கொடுத்துண்மின் - உம்மைக்","கொடாஅ தவரென்பர் குண்டுநீர் வையத்(து)","அடாஅ அடுப்பி னவர்."],"p":"Daily having given somewhat, though but the fraction of a grain of rice, according to your ability, eat ye (your own food). Those who gave not in that world, men say on this earth girt by deep waters are those on whose hearth nothing is cooked.","r":"Give daily, before eating, from what you can, even a grain of rice's worth; the quatrain backs this with a saying about those who do not give and those whose hearth is never lit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உம்மை means 'in a former birth': those who did not give then are the poor with unlit hearths now.",""],["shanmuganar","உம்மை means 'you': the poor with unlit hearths will scorn you as misers.",""]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:95":{"book":"naladiyar","num":95,"t":["மறுமையும் இம்மையும் நோக்கி ஒருவற்கு","உறுமா றியைவ கொடுத்தல் - வறுமையால்","ஈதல் இசையா தெனினும் இரவாமை","ஈதல் இரட்டி யுறும்."],"p":"Regarding the other world and this world, give to any suppliant, in fitting way, according to your ability. And if on account of poverty giving is not possible, yet refraining from begging is twice as meritorious as giving.","r":"With an eye to the next world and this one, give what you can in a fitting way; and if poverty makes giving impossible, not begging is worth twice as much as giving.","n":"Mudaliar names the two rewards: heavenly joy in the next world and renown in this one. The last line keeps the poor inside the economy of giving: they give by not taking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:96":{"book":"naladiyar","num":96,"t":["நடுவூருள் வேதிகை சுற்றுக்கோட் புக்க","படுபனை யன்னர் பலர்நச்ச வாழ்வார்","குடிகொழுத்தக் கண்ணுங்கொடுத்துண்ணா மாக்கள்","இடுகாட்டுள் ஏற்றைப் பனை."],"p":"Those who live desired of many are as a fertile palm entered in the altar's enclosure in midmost of the town. Those who, even when their house grows great, give not before they eat, are like the sterile palm in the burning ground.","r":"Those who live so that many desire them are like a fruit-bearing palm enclosed by a platform in the middle of town; those who do not give before eating, even when their household thrives, are like a barren male palm in the burning ground.","n":"Both are palms; fruit and place separate them. The giver stands where everyone gathers, the miser among the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:97":{"book":"naladiyar","num":97,"t":["பெயற்பால் மழைபெய்யாக் கண்ணும் உலகம்","செயற்பால செய்யா விடினும் - கயற்புலால்","புன்னை கடியும் பொருகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப.","என்னை உலகுய்யு மாறு."],"p":"Even when the rain rains not in due season, if all the world should fail in virtuous actions that ought to be done, —Lord of the warring sea's cool shore, where acrid fume of fish in Punnai's perfume dies! - how scapes the world?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the pounding sea, where the punnai's scent drives off the smell of fish: if the rain does not fall when it should, and the world does not do what it should, how will the world survive?","n":"Both commentators take the 'world' that must act to be its good or wise people, so their giving is set beside rain as a second support of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","eegai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:98":{"book":"naladiyar","num":98,"t":["ஏற்றகைம் மாற்றாமை என்னானும் தாம்வரையாது","ஆற்றாதார்க் கீவதாம் ஆண்கடன் - ஆற்றின்","மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப மாறீவார்க் கீதல்","பொலிகடன் என்னும் பெயர்த்து."],"p":"Denying to no out-stretched hand, to give to needy men as he hath power, is duty of a man.—Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore !—A gift to those that can return the gift is usury !","r":"Never refusing an outstretched hand and holding nothing back, to give to those who can't repay is a man's duty; lord of the cool shore, giving to those who will give back earns only the name of a profitable loan.","n":"Mudaliar lists several ways to take தாம் வரையாது: excluding no gift, excluding no person, setting no rules, or keeping nothing back for oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:99":{"book":"naladiyar","num":99,"t":["இறப்பச் சிறிதென்னா தில்லென்னா தென்றும்","அறப்பயன் யார்மாட்டும் செய்க - முறைப்புதவின்","ஐயம் புகூஉம் தவசி கடிஞைபோல்","பைய நிறைத்து விடும்."],"p":"Say not ‘ 'tis passing little,' nor ‘ ' tis nought I give;' on all confer thy boon of virtuous charity, Like the dish the mendicant presents from door to door, by frequent doles 'twill be filled full.","r":"Never say 'it is too little' or 'I have nothing'; always do the good of giving, to anyone, for like the ascetic's bowl filled at door after door, it fills slowly.","n":"The bowl belongs to the one who receives, but it stands for what fills in the giver: Mudaliar says the fruit of virtue fills his life, Shanmuganar that good deeds accumulate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:100":{"book":"naladiyar","num":100,"t":["கடிப்பிடு கண்முரசம் காதத்தோர் கேட்பர்","இடித்து முழங்கியதோர் யோசனையோர் கேட்பர்","அடுக்கிய மூவுலகும் கேட்குமே சான்றோர்","கொடுத்தா ரெனப்படும் சொல்."],"p":"The sound of beaten drum a katham off they'll hear; the thunder's voice through a whole yojanai will reach men's ears; the three successive worlds will hear the word that says, ‘Men excellent their gifts have given.' ------------","r":"A drum struck with a stick is heard a காதம் away and rolling thunder a யோசனை, but the word 'they gave', linked with the worthy, is heard through all three stacked worlds.","n":"Mudaliar records a third reading, 'they gave to the worthy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","saandror"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word is 'he gave', spoken by the worthy about a giver.",""],["shanmuganar","The word is praise that the worthy themselves gave.",""]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:101":{"book":"naladiyar","num":101,"t":["பல்லாவுள் உய்த்து விடினும் குழக்கன்று","வல்லதாம் தாய்நாடிக் கோடலைத் - தொல்லைப்","பழவினையும் அன்ன தகைத்தேதற் செய்த","கிழவனை நாடிக் கொளற்கு."],"p":"Although you send forth the tender calf amid many cows, it has unerring skill to seek out its own mother. Deeds of old days have even so the power to search him out to whom their fruit pertains.","r":"A young calf let loose among many cows can still find its own mother, and an old deed has the same skill in seeking out the one who did it.","n":"The image makes the deed active: it searches, as the calf does, rather than waiting to be met. Neither the verse nor Mudaliar says the deed comes from a former birth; Shanmuganar adds that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:102":{"book":"naladiyar","num":102,"t":["உருவும் இளமையும் ஒண்பொருளும் உட்கும்","ஒருவழி நில்லாமை கண்டும் - ஒருவழி","ஒன்றேயும் இல்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை உடம்(பு)இட்டு","நின்றுவீழ்ந் தக்க(து) உடைத்து."],"p":"Beauty and youth, and glittering wealth and reverence abide not in one stay. To him who, though he sees this, does no single virtuous act in this one stage of being, life's joy stands with the body and falls with it.","r":"Beauty, youth, fine wealth and the awe one commands do not stay; one who sees this and still does not one good thing has a life that merely takes a body, stands, and falls.","n":"The failure condemned is not vice but the absence of even one good act; the body is wasted rather than misused. Mudaliar offers two senses of ஒருவழி ஒன்றேயும்: any single good deed, or one deed of the kind that leads to the highest state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:103":{"book":"naladiyar","num":103,"t":["வளம்பட வேண்டாதார் யார்யாரும் இல்லை","அளந்தன போகம் அவர்அவர் ஆற்றால்","விளங்காய் திரட்டினார் இல்லை, களங்கனியைக்","காரெனச் செய்தாரும் இல்."],"p":"Who would not see Prosperity? All seek her gifts; but as men's ways are, so each man's enjoyments are meted out. Who made the Vilam's apple round? Or who gave its dusky hue to the Kalam fruit ?","r":"Everyone wants prosperity, but each person's enjoyments are measured out according to their own course, just as no one rounded the vilam fruit or blackened the kalam berry.","n":"The fruits are chosen because their shape and colour belong to them by nature. The verse says only அவரவர் ஆற்றால்; both commentators take that 'way' to be one's former deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:104":{"book":"naladiyar","num":104,"t":["உறற்பால நீக்கல் உறுவர்க்கும் ஆகா,","பெறற்பா லனையவும் அன்னவாம் மாரி","வறப்பின் தருவாரும் இல்லை, அதனைச்","சிறப்பின் தணிப்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"Not even saints can drive away predestined ills; and all the fated gain must needs accrue. In time of drought who can bestow the rain ? Or who can check its rich abundance when it falls?","r":"Not even the உறுவர் can remove what is bound to befall, and what is bound to be gained is the same; no one can bring rain in a drought or check it when it pours.","n":"Mudaliar records Shanmuganar's kind of reading of the second line as another view, under which the verse is a reversed parallel: rain withheld matches gains withheld, rain unstoppable matches pains unstoppable.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","mazhai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The உறுவர் are sages, and destined pleasures can no more be prevented than destined pains.",""],["shanmuganar","No strong person can stop what fate brings, and no one can obtain by their own effort what they wish to gain.",""]],"ch":"n:11"},"n:105":{"book":"naladiyar","num":105,"t":["தினைத்துணைய ராகித்தந் தேசுள் அடக்கிப்","பனைத்துணையார் வைகலும் பாடழிந்து வாழ்வர்","நினைப்பக் கிடந்த தெவனுண்டாம் மேலை","வினைப்பயன் அல்லால் பிற."],"p":"Those who lose like stately palms, when their greatness is gone, become small asthe millet seed, hiding their glory within, —and so they pass their days. This is the fruit of deeds of former days: when you think of it, what other cause can there be?","r":"Men once as great as palm trees shrink to the size of a millet seed, hide their brightness within and live on diminished day after day; think about it, and what cause is there but the fruit of former deeds?","n":"The verse turns a common sight, the fallen great, into an argument by elimination: no other cause can be thought of. Mudaliar specifies that the deeds are evil ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:106":{"book":"naladiyar","num":106,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் வீயவும்","கல்லாதார் வாழ்வ தறிதிரேல் - கல்லாதார்","சேதனம் என்னுமச் சேறகத் தின்மையால்","கோதென்று கொள்ளாதாம் கூற்று."],"p":"Those that know the fruit of varied and profound learning die off, while the unlearned joyously live on. Would you know the cause ? — The unlearned posses within no 'sap of sapience'; — so death deems then refuse stalks, and takes them not!","r":"If you ask why those who know the fruit of deep learning die while the unlearned live on, it is because the unlearned have none of the sap called sense, so Death passes them over as husks.","n":"The verse is a joke with an edge: a long life for the unlearned is recast as Death's contempt. It doesn't say the learned die because of their learning; it supplies a fanciful cause, which Mudaliar names as a figure of imputed cause (ஏதுத் தற்குறிப்பேற்றவணி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:107":{"book":"naladiyar","num":107,"t":["இடும்பைகூர் நெஞ்சத்தார் எல்லாரும் காண","நெடுங்கடை நின்றுழல்வ தெல்லாம் - அடம்பப்பூ","அன்னம் கிழிக்கும் அலைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","முன்னை வினையாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the sea's cool shore, where amid the wave swans sport, tearing to shreds the Adambu flowers ! When those whose hearts are sore with urgent need standbegging, and wander through the long street, in sight of all, this is the fruit of former deeds.","r":"When people with aching hearts stand at the gates of great houses, begging in sight of everyone, all of that is their former deeds, lord of the cool shore where swans tear the adambu flowers.","n":"The address to the lord of the shore frames the quatrain as words spoken to a chieftain. Both commentators make the deeds evil ones from a former birth; the verse says only முன்னை வினை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:108":{"book":"naladiyar","num":108,"t":["அறியாரும் அல்லர் அறிவ தறிந்தும்","பழியோடு பட்டவை செய்தல் - வளியோடி","நெய்தல் நறவுயிர்க்கும் நீள்கடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","செய்த வினையான் வரும்."],"p":"They are not ignorant; but, though what man should know they know, yet they do deeds that bring guilt to their souls. — Lord of the wide sea's pleasant shore, where breezes breathe the lily's fragrance round ! — This comes from former deeds.","r":"When people who are far from ignorant, who know what ought to be known, still do blameworthy things, that comes from the deeds they have done, lord of the long cool shore where the breeze carries the honey-scent of the neythal.","n":"The quatrain explains a moral puzzle, knowing the good and doing wrong, by pushing the cause back into earlier deeds. It explains the act; it does not excuse it, and pazhi still attaches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:109":{"book":"naladiyar","num":109,"t":["ஈண்டுநீர் வையத்துள் எல்லாரும் எத்துணையும்","வேண்டார்மன் தீய விழைபயன் நல்லவை","வேண்டினும் வேண்டா விடினும் உற்றபால","தீண்டா விடுதல் அரிது."],"p":"On the earth begirt by gathering waters no men desire in anywise evil things, but choice fruit of good things. Yet whether they desire, or abhor, it is hard to shun the touch of what fate assigns.","r":"No one on this sea-girt earth wants evil in the least, and all want good results; but wanted or not, what is bound to come is hard to keep from touching them.","n":"Desire is shown to be idle in both directions: wanting good does not bring it, and not wanting evil does not prevent it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:110":{"book":"naladiyar","num":110,"t":["சிறுகா பெருகா முறைபிறழ்ந்து வாரா","உறுகாலத் தூற்றாகா ஆமிடத்தே யாகும்","சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறியும் அதனால்","இறுகாலத் தென்னை பரிவூ."],"p":"The early fates diminish not, nor do they increase, they come not in order changedhelp in troublous times is none; what haps will happen, there and then; and so, when all things fail, why grieve? -----------","r":"What was set in the early mark does not shrink or grow, does not come out of order, gives no support in hard times, and arrives where it is due; so why grieve when the time of ruin comes?","n":"சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறி is literally 'the mark set in the small time'; both commentators read it as what was fixed in the womb. The chapter ends not with fear but with an argument for calm, since a fixed sum cannot be changed by grieving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:111":{"book":"naladiyar","num":111,"t":["இசையா ஒருபொருள் இல்லென்றல் யார்க்கும்","வசையன்று வையத் தியற்கை - நசையழுங்க","நின்றோடிப் பொய்த்தல் நிரைதொடீஇ. செய்ந்நன்றி","கொன்றாரின் குற்ற முடைத்து."],"p":"O maid with many armlets graced! To answer 'no' disgraces no man, when the boon asked exceeds his means. It is the world's course. But to delay and cheat the soul faint with desire is a sin like his who 'slays' a benefit conferred.","r":"Saying 'I have none' about something one cannot give is no disgrace and is the way of the world; but keeping someone waiting till their hope dies and then failing them is a worse fault than killing a kindness received.","n":"The verse doesn't fault refusal; it faults the false promise that turns refusal into torment. Both commentators read கொன்றாரின் as a comparison, worse than ingratitude, where Pope says only 'like'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","eegai","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:112":{"book":"naladiyar","num":112,"t":["தக்காரும் தக்கவ ரல்லாரும் தந்நீர்மை","எக்காலும் குன்றல் இலராவர் - அக்காரம்","யாவரே தின்னினும் கையாதாம் கைக்குமாம்","தேவரே தின்னினும் வேம்பு."],"p":"Men of worth, and men unworthy too, retain their natures ever unchanged. Whoever they be that eat it, sugar can never grow bitter; and margosa is bitter even when eaten by gods.","r":"The worthy and the unworthy never fall away from their own natures: jaggery is not bitter whoever eats it, and neem is bitter even if gods eat it.","n":"The claim runs both ways: circumstances cannot spoil the good, and they cannot redeem the bad. The gods are named to say that even the highest eater does not change the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:113":{"book":"naladiyar","num":113,"t":["காலாடு போழ்தில் கழிகிளைஞர் வானத்து","மேலாடு மீனின் பலராவர் - ஏலா","இடரொருவர் உற்றக்கால் ஈர்ங்குன்ற நாட","தொடர்புடையேம் என்பார் சிலர்."],"p":"When a man moves prosperonsly on, devoted kinsfolk are countless as shining stars that move in the upper heaven. But when grievous affliction haps,—Lord of the dripping hills !—few claim close alliance with him.","r":"When a man prospers, kin who cling to him are more numerous than the stars that move in the sky; when unbearable trouble strikes, few say 'we are related to him', lord of the cool hills.","n":"Mudaliar notes that கிளைஞர் means, by its root, those who cling like branches, which sharpens the irony of their leaving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:114":{"book":"naladiyar","num":114,"t":["விடுவிலா வையத்து மன்னிய மூன்றில்","நடுவண தெய்த இருதலையும் எய்தும்","நடுவண தெய்தாதான் எய்தும் உலைப்பெய்து","அடுவது போலும் துயர்."],"p":"Of the three things that endure upon this faultless earth, he who gains the midmost gains the two extremes also. He who gains not the midmost gains the cruel smart that the turtle feels when put into the pot to boil.","r":"Of the three that endure in this world, whoever gains the middle one gains both ends as well; whoever does not gains a suffering like being boiled in a furnace.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three; both commentators identify them as virtue, wealth and pleasure, with wealth in the middle. For a work that elsewhere calls wealth unreliable, this is a strong statement of its worth. Pope's turtle in the pot is his own; Mudaliar has a smith's furnace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:115":{"book":"naladiyar","num":115,"t":["நல்லாவின் கன்றாயின் நாகும் விலைபெறூஉம்","கல்லாரே யாயினும் செல்வர்வாய்ச் சொற்செல்லும்","புல்லீரப் போழ்தின் உழவேபோல் மீதாடிச்","செல்லாவாம் நல்கூர்ந்தார் சொல்."],"p":"A young heifer fetches a good price when it is the calf of a good cow; so the words of rich men though unlearned, pass current. Poor men's words, like the plough when moisture is scanty, merely graze the surface and are of no avail.","r":"Even a young calf fetches a price if its mother is a good cow, and the words of the rich are accepted even if they are unlearned; the words of the poor glance off like a plough in barely moist soil.","n":"The calf image says worth is borrowed from the source, not earned; the plough image says the poor man's words do not sink in. The verse reports this as how things are, not as how they should be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:116":{"book":"naladiyar","num":116,"t":["இடம்பட மெய்ஞ்ஞானம் கற்பினும் என்றும்","அடங்காதார் என்றும் அடங்கார் - தடங்கண்ணாய்","உப்போடு நெய்பால் தயிர்காயம் பெய்திடினும்","கைப்பறா பேய்ச்சுரையின் காய்."],"p":"O wide-eyed one! though you cook the wild gourd pouring in salt, ghee, milk, curds, and spices, it never loses its bitterness. So those who never discipline themselves, though they may learn extensive works of true wisdom, never become disciplined.","r":"Those without restraint stay unrestrained even if they study true wisdom widely, as a wild bitter gourd stays bitter though cooked with salt, ghee, milk, curd and asafoetida.","n":"Mudaliar takes அடங்காதார் as those who are by nature not given to restraint, so the point is that learning cannot change a nature, not that learning is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:117":{"book":"naladiyar","num":117,"t":["தம்மை இகழ்வாரைத் தாமவரின் முன்னிகழ்க","என்னை அவரொடு பட்டது - புன்னை","விற்றபூங் கமழ்கானல் வீங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","உற்றபால யார்க்கும் உறும்."],"p":"When men scorn you, before their faces scorn them too ! what has a man to do with them? — Lord of shore where beauteous Punnai-flowers perfume the glades that surround the swelling tide, — what fated comes to all!","r":"Scorn those who scorn you, before they do; what have you to do with them? What is bound to come comes to everyone, lord of the shore fragrant with punnai flowers.","n":"The link between the advice and the last line is not stated. One reading is that since what is fated will come anyway, there is nothing to lose by answering scorn. Mudaliar takes அவரின் முன் either as at the very moment they scorn or to their faces rather than behind their backs, and calls the verse a way to silence groundless slanderers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","oozh","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:118":{"book":"naladiyar","num":118,"t":["ஆவே றுருவின வாயினும் ஆபயந்த","பால்வே றுருவின அல்லவாம் - பால்போல்","ஒருதன்மைத் தாகும் அறநெறி ஆபோல்","உருவு பலகொளல் ஈங்கு."],"p":"Though cows in form are diverse, the milk they yield is not diverse. The way of virtue, like that milk, is one in nature, though the schools that teach it here are like those cows, of many forms.","r":"Cows differ in colour, but the milk they give does not; virtue, like milk, is of one nature, while its ways here take many forms, like the cows.","n":"Mudaliar takes the many ways as the many means of practising one virtue. Pope makes them the schools or sects that teach it, which turns the verse into a statement about religious diversity; the Tamil says only நெறி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:119":{"book":"naladiyar","num":119,"t":["யாஅர் உலகத்தோர் சொல்லில்லார்? தேருங்கால்","யாஅர் உபாயத்தின் வாழாதார்? - யாஅர்","இடையாக இன்னாத தெய்தாதார்? யாஅர்","கடைபோகச் செல்வம்உய்த் தார்?"],"p":"Look well! of whom hath not the world found word to say ? And who have not by prudence prospered in life's way? Ah! who in life's mid course no bitter grief have known ? Ah! who to end of life have kept their wealth their own?","r":"Who in the world has escaped having a word said against them? Who has not lived by stratagem? Who has not met hardship midway? Who has kept their wealth to the end?","n":"The four questions expect the answer 'no one', so the verse states the common lot without saying it. Mudaliar gives two senses of உபாயம்: the four political stratagems, or the ordinary means of livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pazhi","idumbai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:120":{"book":"naladiyar","num":120,"t":["தாஞ்செய் வினையல்லால் தம்மொடு செல்வதுமற்று","யாங்கணும் தேரின் பிறிதில்லை - ஆங்குத்தாம்","போற்றிப் புனைந்த உடம்பும் பயமின்றே","கூற்றும்கொண் டோ டும் பொழுது."],"p":"Save a man's deed: nought goes with him, search where you will. The body which men cherish so, and adorn, is itself profitless indeed when death shall seize and hurry off with it. ------","r":"Search where you will, nothing goes with a person but the deeds they have done; even the body they guarded and adorned is of no use when Death seizes it and runs.","n":"வினை here is not only good deeds: what goes along can be a burden as well as a support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:121":{"book":"naladiyar","num":121,"t":["துக்கத்துள் தூங்கித் துறவின்கட் சேர்கலா","மக்கள் பிணத்த சுடுகாடு - தொக்க","விலங்கிற்கும் புள்ளிற்கும் காடே புலன்கெட்ட","புல்லறி வாளர் வயிறு."],"p":"The burning ground is filled with the corpses of men that will not give themselves up to a self renouncing life, but oscillate amid sorrows; and the maws of perverted foolish men are a mere burning-ground for beasts and birds.","r":"The burning grounds are full of the corpses of people who linger in sorrow and do not turn to renunciation, and the bellies of senseless, petty-minded people are burning grounds for the beasts and birds they eat.","n":"The two halves mirror each other: people end in the burning ground, and the eater's belly becomes a burning ground for animals. The image says that the meat-eater carries a cremation ground inside.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pulaal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sorrow in which people linger is household life itself; Mudaliar also says the verse rejects meat-eating.",""],["shanmuganar","The sorrow is the world's suffering in general, in which people are bewildered.",""]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:122":{"book":"naladiyar","num":122,"t":["இரும்பார்க்குங் காலராய் ஏதிலார்க் காளாய்க்","கரும்பார் கழனியுள் சேர்வர் - சுரும்பார்க்கும்","காட்டுளாய் வாழுஞ் சிவலும் குறும்பூமும்","கூட்டுளாய்க் கொண்டுவைப் பார்."],"p":"Their legs in iron bound, as slaves to alien lords they will till the black and barren soil, who snared and kept in cages partridges and quails, that dwell in wilds where beetles hum amid the flowers.","r":"Those who catch partridges and quails that live in the humming forest and keep them in cages will go, with irons clanking on their legs, as slaves to strangers into the fields.","n":"The punishment mirrors the crime: those who confined free birds are themselves confined and made to serve. Mudaliar reads கரும்பு ஆர் கழனி as sugarcane fields, and as an alternative as barren and fertile lands; Pope follows the barren reading. Shanmuganar places the punishment in the next birth; the verse doesn't say when.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:123":{"book":"naladiyar","num":123,"t":["அக்கேபோல் அங்கை யொழிய விரலழுகித்","துக்கத் தொழுநொய் எழுபவே - அக்கால்","அலவனைக் காதலித்துக் கான்முரித்துத் தின்ற","பழவினை வந்தடைந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Like fire their palms shall glow, their fingers rotaway, who loved in other times on crabs to feed, and broke their joints, what time the guilt of ‘olden deeds' comes home, and leprosy's fierce pangs assail.","r":"When the old deed of loving to eat crabs and breaking their legs comes home, people get the painful skin disease, white like conch beads, their fingers rotting away and only the palms left.","n":"Again the punishment copies the act: the crab's legs were broken, and the eater's fingers rot. Mudaliar and Shanmuganar read அக்கே போல் as like conch beads; Pope has palms that glow like fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:124":{"book":"naladiyar","num":124,"t":["நெருப்பழல் சேர்ந்தக்கால் நெய்போல் வதூஉம்","எரிப்பச்சுட் டெவ்வநோய் ஆக்கும் - பரப்பக்","கொடுவினைய ராகுவர் கோடாரும் கோடிக்","கடுவினைய ராகியார்ச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Even things (soft and soothing) like ghee, when joined with the fierce heat of fire, will blaze and burn, and cause bitter anguish : so even upright men are perverted and give themselves up to deeds of utter evil, when they attach themselves to those whose deeds are evil.","r":"Even ghee, when it meets fire, burns and causes painful wounds; so even upright people turn and do widely cruel deeds when they join those of harsh deeds.","n":"Mudaliar notes the force of the image: ghee is what soothes burns, so the very thing that heals becomes what wounds. The verse makes company stronger than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:125":{"book":"naladiyar","num":125,"t":["பெரியவர் கேண்மை பிறைபோல நாளும்","வரிசை வரிசையா நந்தும் - வரிசையால்","வானூர் மதியம்போல் வைகலும் தேயுமே","தானே சிறியார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Great men's intimate regard will daily grow in order due like the crescent moon. Mean men's alliance like the full moon that rides the sky daily by degrees dwindles away of itself.","r":"The friendship of the great grows day by day in due order like the crescent moon, while the bond with the small wanes by itself day by day like the full moon moving across the sky.","n":"The word தானே says the waning needs no cause: such a bond decays of itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:126":{"book":"naladiyar","num":126,"t":["சான்றோ ரெனமதித்துச் சார்ந்தாய்மன் சார்ந்தாய்க்குச்","சான்றாண்மை சார்ந்தார்கண் இல்லாயின் - சார்ந்தாய்கேள்","சாந்தகத் துண்டென்று செப்புத் திறந்தொருவன்","பாம்பகத்துக் கண்ட துடைத்து."],"p":"Thou didst attach thyself (to unworthy persons), saying, they are men of absolute integrity! If to thee who hast thus attached thyself, integrity in those thine intimates does not appear; hear, O thou who hast so attached thyself, it is as if one opened a casket, thinking it contained an odorous unguent, and saw a snake within.","r":"You attached yourself to someone, thinking him one of the worthy; if the worth is not in him, listen, it is like opening a casket expecting sandal paste and finding a snake inside.","n":"The repeated சார்ந்தாய் ('you who attached yourself') is a reproach: the fault lies partly with the one who did not examine before attaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:127":{"book":"naladiyar","num":127,"t":["யாஅர் ஒருவர் ஒருவர்தம் உள்ளத்தைத்","தேருந் துணைமை யுடையவர் - சாரல்","கனமணி நின்றிமைக்கும் நாடகேள் மக்கள்","மனம்வேறு செய்கையும் வேறு."],"p":"What single man has power to search and clearly know the inmost self of other men? Lord of the land where weighty gems glisten on mountain slope, O hear ! — Men's minds are otherwise, and otherwise their deeds.","r":"Who is there with the power to examine another's mind and know it? Listen, lord of the land where heavy gems glitter on the slopes: people's minds are one thing and their deeds another.","n":"The gap between mind and deed is offered as the reason minds cannot be known: behaviour is no evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kurippu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:128":{"book":"naladiyar","num":128,"t":["உள்ளத்தான் நள்ளா துறுதித் தொழிலராய்க்","கள்ளத்தான் நட்டார் கழிகேண்மை - தெள்ளிப்","புனற்செதும்பு நின்றலைக்கும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனத்துக்கண் மாசாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the flowery hilly land where streams wash out and carry hither and thither (precious things) from the marshy land ! — The effusive friendship of those who do not attach themselves (to us) in heart, but perform certain friendly acts merely to strengthen their own position, and who form friendships guilefully, will issue in disappointment to the minds (of those who are intimate with them).","r":"The lavish friendship of those who do not join you at heart but act with an eye to their own gain, befriending you with guile, turns into a stain on the mind, lord of the flowering hills.","n":"Whose mind is stained is left open. Mudaliar says the friendship itself becomes a fault, Shanmuganar that it grows tainted and perishes, and Pope that it brings disappointment to those deceived.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:129":{"book":"naladiyar","num":129,"t":["ஓக்கிய ஒள்வாள்தன் ஒன்னார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","ஊக்கம் அழிப்பதூஉம் மெய்யாகும் - ஆக்கம்","இருமையுஞ் சென்று சுடுதலால் நல்ல","கருமமே கல்லார்கண் தீர்வு."],"p":"If the glittering swords a man brandishes (be allowed to) fall into the hands of his foes, it will assuredly come about that he will thus destroy also his own power of action. So wealth (bestowed on the foolish) will go and burn up (merit accruing in) both worlds, and therefore the really good thing is to keep clear of the foolish.","r":"If a raised, gleaming sword falls into an enemy's hand it truly destroys one's strength; since ஆக்கம் among the unlearned burns one in both worlds, the good course is to get away from them.","n":"Pope offers a third sense, wealth bestowed on the foolish. On every reading the sword image says that what should protect you turns against you once it is in the wrong hands.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kallaamai","natpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","ஆக்கம் means joining the unlearned, and that association is what burns.",""],["shanmuganar","ஆக்கம் is one's own growth, which friendship with the unlearned burns up in both worlds.",""]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:130":{"book":"naladiyar","num":130,"t":["மனைப்பாசம் கைவிடாய் மக்கட்கென் றேங்கி","எனைத்தூழி வாழ்தியோ, நெஞ்சே - எனைத்தும்","சிறுவரையே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றல்லால்","உறுபயனோ இல்லை உயிர்க்கு."],"p":"O mind! thou leavest not the bonds of home. How many cycles, pray, with thou live yearning still for children? Save the good he has done, however small its measure may be, there is no true result to any living soul.","r":"Heart, you won't let go of attachment to home, and you pine for the sake of children; for how many ages do you think you'll live? Apart from the good one has done, however small, there is no real gain for a living being.","n":"Both commentators read மனைப்பாசம் as attachment to one's wife. The verse doesn't call family evil; it says family is not the gain the soul carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","makkal","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:131":{"book":"naladiyar","num":131,"t":["குஞ்சி யழகும் கொடுந்தானைக் கோட்டழகும்","மஞ்சள் அழகும் அழகல்ல - நெஞ்சத்து","நல்லம்யாம் என்னும் நடுவு நிலைமையால்","கல்வி அழகே அழகு."],"p":"Beauty of locks, beauty of circling garments' folds, beauty of saffron tint: these are not beauty true. Integrity of soul that brings the conscience peace is learning's gift: that only is beauty true!","r":"The beauty of a man's hair, of a garment's curving border, of a saffron wash, is not beauty; the beauty of learning, with the fairness of heart that lets one say 'we are good', is beauty.","n":"Mudaliar notes that some read the three adornments as a man's (hair), both sexes' (garment) and a woman's (saffron), and so take the verse to commend learning for women as well as men; he says others give the verse other meanings too.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi","naduvu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","True beauty is learning held together with conduct that lets one judge fairly that one has not strayed from virtue.",""],["shanmuganar","Learning is true beauty because it produces the impartial quality of being good at heart.",""]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:132":{"book":"naladiyar","num":132,"t":["இம்மை பயக்குமால் ஈயக் குறைவின்றால்","தம்மை விளக்குமால் தாமுளராக் கேடின்றால்","எம்மை யுலகத்தும் யாம்காணேம் கல்விபோல்","மம்மர் அறுக்கும் மருந்து."],"p":"Since in this world it yields fruit; since given it grows not less ; since it makes men illustrious; since it perishes not as long as (its possessors) themselves exist; in any world we see not any medicine that, like learning, removes the delusions of sense.","r":"Learning yields fruit in this life, does not lessen when given, makes its owner shine, and does not perish while they live; in no world do we see a medicine like it for cutting away delusion.","n":"The disagreement is whether learning lasts as long as its owner or outlasts them through fame. Mudaliar notes that learning is called a medicine without delusion being called a disease, an incomplete metaphor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","marundhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Learning does not perish for as long as its owner lives.",""],["shanmuganar","Learning makes its owner live on forever through fame and does not perish.",""]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:133":{"book":"naladiyar","num":133,"t":["களர்நிலத் துப்பிறந்த உப்பினைச் சான்றோர்","விளைநிலத்து நெல்லின் விழுமிதாக் கொள்வர்","கடைநிலத்தோ ராயினும், கற்றறிந் தோரைத்","தலைநிலத்து வைக்கப் படும்."],"p":"The excellent regard the salt produced in brackish ground as choicer than the Nel from fertile soil. It is fitting to place in the first rank the learned wise, though (sprung) from the lowest origin.","r":"The worthy prize salt born in brackish ground above rice grown in fertile fields; so the learned, though of the lowest origin, are to be set in the highest place.","n":"The image works because the place of origin is called low only by habit: salt from barren soil is worth more than grain from good soil. Mudaliar takes கடைநிலத்தோர் as those born in a low caste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:134":{"book":"naladiyar","num":134,"t":["வைப்புழிக் கோட்படா வாய்த்தீயின் கேடில்லை","மிக்க சிறப்பின் அரசர் செறின்வவ்வார்","எச்சம் எனஒருவன் மக்கட்குச் செய்வன","விச்சைமற் றல்ல பிற."],"p":"It cannot be taken from its place of deposit; it does not perish anywhere by fire; if kings of surpassing grandeur are angry they cannot take it away; (and therefore) what any man should provide for his children as a legacy is learning. Other things are not (real wealth).","r":"It cannot be taken from where it is kept, it is not lost when given to a fit person, and kings angered by its eminence cannot seize it; so what a man should leave his children as their inheritance is learning and nothing else.","n":"Each clause is a way ordinary wealth is lost: theft, giving, a king's anger. Both commentators read வாய்த்து ஈயின் as giving to a fit recipient; Pope reads வாய்த் தீயின் and has learning not perish by fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:135":{"book":"naladiyar","num":135,"t":["கல்வி கரையில கற்பவர் நாள்சில","மெல்ல நினைக்கின் பிணிபல - தெள்ளிதன்","ஆராய்ந் தமைவுடைய கற்பவே நீரொழியப்","பாலுண் குருகின் தெரிந்து."],"p":"Learning hath no bounds, the learners' days are few. If you think calmly diseases many wait around! With clear discrimination learn what is meet for you, like the swan that leaving the water drinks the milk.","r":"Learning has no shore, learners have few days, and, thought over calmly, many illnesses; so the wise learn with clear discernment what is fitting, like the bird that drinks the milk and leaves the water.","n":"The argument for selection is arithmetic: boundless material, a short life, and illnesses that shorten it further. Mudaliar names the bird as the swan and notes the belief that it can separate milk from water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","therithal","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:136":{"book":"naladiyar","num":136,"t":["தோணி இயக்குவான் தொல்லை வருணத்துக்","காணிற் கடைப்பட்டான் என்றிகழார் - காணாய்","அவன்துணையா ஆறுபோ யற்றேநூல் கற்ற","மகன்துணையா நல்ல கொளல்."],"p":"As none contemn the ferryman, by old caste rule to lowest rank assigned, but cross the stream by help he lends; so take thou teachings good and wise by help of him who is the learned man.","r":"No one despises the ferryman for being of the lowest of the old castes, but crosses the river with his help; so take what is good from a man who has learned books, whatever his birth.","n":"The argument is from ordinary practice: people already accept help from the low-born when they need it, so they should accept teaching on the same terms. The verse speaks of using the learned man's help, not of honouring him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:137":{"book":"naladiyar","num":137,"t":["தவலருந் தொல்கேள்வித் தன்மை யுடையார்","இகலிலர் எஃகுடையார் தம்முள் குழீஇ","நகலின் இனிதாயின் காண்பாம் அகல்வானத்(து)","உம்ப ருறைவார் பதி."],"p":"We shall see whether any greater bliss is found in the city inhabited by the dwellers in the ample heaven than is felt when men of natures formed by old imperishable lore, from rivalries exempt keen as tempered steel, meet together and laugh.","r":"If the city of those who live in the wide heaven is sweeter than when people of sound and ancient learning, free of rivalry and sharp of mind, gather and laugh together, then we'll go and see it.","n":"The conditional is a polite refusal: Mudaliar reads it as saying that such joy is found on earth and not in heaven, so there is no wish to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:138":{"book":"naladiyar","num":138,"t":["கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மை","நுனியின் கரும்புதின் றற்றே - நுனிநீக்கித்","துரி஢ன்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ பண்பிலா","ஈரமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"diminishes in sweetness. Lord of the cool shore of the resounding sea intimacy with learned people is like eating sugar cane from the (tender, juicy) tip; association with graceless. sapless men is like leaving the (tender) tip and eating it from the (hard, dry) root.","r":"Friendship with the learned is like eating sugarcane from the tip, sweeter as one goes; association with graceless, unfeeling people is like eating it from the root end, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea.","n":"The cane is the same in both cases; only the direction differs. So the verse is about how a bond develops over time, not how it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:139":{"book":"naladiyar","num":139,"t":["கல்லாரே யாயினும் கற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகின்","நல்லறிவு நாளுந் தலைப்படுவர் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்ணிறப் பாதிரிப்பூச் சேர்தலால் புத்தோடு","தண்ணீர்க்குத் தான்பயந் தாங்கு."],"p":"","r":"Even the unlearned, if they keep company with the learned, gain good sense day by day, as a new pot gives its water the scent of the bright, long-prized pathiri flowers placed in it.","n":"Mudaliar explains the image: the new pot is the unlearned person, the flowers the learned, and the scent good sense. The water gains the fragrance without becoming flowers, so the change is real but borrowed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:140":{"book":"naladiyar","num":140,"t":["அலகுசால் கற்பின் அறிவுநூல் கல்லா(து)","உலகநூ லோதுவ தெல்லாம் - கலகல","கூஉந் துணையல்லால் கொண்டு தடுமாற்றம்","போஒம் துணையறிவா ரில்."],"p":"If men leaving works of wisdom, that contain well-weighed instruction, unstudied, devote themselves to the recitation of mere worldly literature, they will acquire a store of empty high-sounding words, but not that wisdom by means of which mental confusion (that treats unreal things as real) is removed. ------------","r":"Of all the many kinds of learning, studying worldly books while leaving the books of wisdom unread amounts only to clamour; there is no one who knows how to make confusion leave by that means.","n":"Mudaliar names the worldly books as logic and grammar, and takes தடுமாற்றம் as the stumbling through births; Pope takes it as the mind's confusion that treats unreal things as real. The verse does not say worldly learning is false, only that it cannot do this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:141":{"book":"naladiyar","num":141,"t":["உடுக்கை உலறி உடம்பழிந்தக் கண்ணும்","குடிப்பிறப் பாளர்தம் கொள்கையில் குன்றார்","இடுக்கண் தலைவந்தக் கண்ணும் அரிமா","கொடிப்புல் கறிக்குமோ மற்று."],"p":"Though their clothes may be old and their body worn with want, men of noble birth diminish nothing of their due observances. Will the lion nibble the creeping grass although sorest need should assail him?","r":"Even when their clothes are in rags and their bodies wasted, the well-born do not fall from their principles; would a lion, however hard pressed, chew creeping grass?","n":"The lion is chosen because grass is not merely beneath it but against its nature, so the verse makes principle a matter of what one is. Mudaliar records a variant reading, வரிமா, which would make the animal a tiger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:142":{"book":"naladiyar","num":142,"t":["சான்றாண்மை சாயல் ஒழுக்கம் இநவ்முன்றும்","வான்றோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கல்லது - வான்றோயும்","மைதவழ் வேற்ப. படாஅ பெருஞ்செல்வம்","எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பிறர்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the hills traversed by clouds that touch the heavens! true excellence, and dignity, and good conduct, — these three things belong to men of race that touches heaven, and not to others, even though they may have acquired great wealth.","r":"Fullness of character, gentleness and good conduct belong only to those born of a family that touches the sky; they do not come to others even when they gain great wealth, lord of the hills where clouds creep.","n":"The point is aimed at wealth: money cannot buy these qualities. Mudaliar glosses சாயல் as mildness, restraint in mind, word and body, and notes that some take it as restraint from blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","saandraanmai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:143":{"book":"naladiyar","num":143,"t":["இருக்கை எழலும் எதிர்செலவும் ஏனை","விடுப்ப ஒழிதலோ டின்ன - குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றா வொழுக்கமாக் கொண்டார் கயவரோடு","ஒன்றா வுணரற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rising from one's seat, going out to meet, seeing a guest off, and the like are what the well-born have taken as unfailing conduct; they are not to be thought of as one with the base.","n":"Only Shanmuganar's commentary is aligned here, and the readings of விடுப்ப ஒழிதல் (as seeing off) and of whom the courtesies are for (elders who visit) are his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:144":{"book":"naladiyar","num":144,"t":["நல்லவை செய்யின் இயல்பாகும் தீயவை","பல்லவர் தூற்றம் பழியாகும் - எல்லாம்","உணரும் குடிப்பிறப்பின் ஊதிய மென்னோ,","புணரும் ஒருவர்க் கெனின்?"],"p":"If men (of noble birth) do good things it is natural to them. As to evil things (to commit these is impossible to them); for this would be guilt which many would bruit abroad. What greater good then can accrue to men than high-birth, if it be their lot, to which the perception of all (these things) belongs!","r":"If the well-born do good, it is called their nature; if they do evil, it becomes blame spread by many; what then does a person gain from the all-knowing high birth that falls to them?","n":"Pope reads the same question the other way, as praise: what greater good can come to anyone than high birth? So the two readings reach opposite conclusions. Mudaliar's commentary isn't aligned for this quatrain, so this can't be recorded as a contest between commentators.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pazhi","payan"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The closing question is ironic: high birth brings no gain of its own, since good is credited to birth and evil is loudly blamed.",""]],"ch":"n:15"},"n:145":{"book":"naladiyar","num":145,"t":["கல்லாமை அச்சம் கயவர் தொழிலச்சம்","சொல்லாமை யுள்ளுமோர் சோர்வச்சம் - எல்லாம்","இரப்பார்க்கொன் றீயாமை அச்சம் மரத்தாரிம்","மாணாக் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Dread of unlearned ignorance; dread of the work that base men do; dread of forgetful slip in words which one must not utter; dread of not giving to those that ask: those who are born of a race not so distinguished by conscientious fears are as trees.","r":"Fear of being unlearned, fear of base work, fear of a slip in speech, and fear of not giving to those who ask: those born of an undistinguished family who lack all these are like wood.","n":"These fears are presented as marks of good birth, a moral sensitivity. The third fear is read two ways: Pope takes it as dread of a careless word, and Shanmuganar as the dismay of being unable to speak in an assembly of the good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kudi","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:146":{"book":"naladiyar","num":146,"t":["இனநன்மை இன்சொல்ஒன் றீதல்மற் றேனை","மனநன்மை என்றிவை யெல்லாம் - கனமணி","முத்தோ டிமைக்கு முழங்குவரித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணே யுள."],"p":"Lord of the roaring salt-sea's cool shore, where gleam rare gems with pearls!Association with the good, pleasant speech, a liberal hand, and purity of mind, —all these are only found among the nobly born.","r":"Good company, sweet speech, giving, and goodness of mind are all found only among the well-born, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where great gems gleam with pearls.","n":"The claim is exclusive: these qualities are found only among the well-born, not merely more often.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inchol","eegai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:147":{"book":"naladiyar","num":147,"t":["செய்கை யழிந்து சிதல்மண்டிற் றாயினும்","பெய்யா ஒருசிறை பேரில் உடைத்தாகும்","எவ்வ முழந்தக் கடைத்துங் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"Though its frame-work has perished, and thronging white ants infest it, in a spacious mansion some room will still afford shelter from the rain. Thus, although want annoys them, the nobly born even yet will do what should be done.","r":"A great house whose woodwork has decayed and which white ants have overrun still keeps one corner where the rain does not fall; so even when hardship presses, the well-born do what is theirs to do.","n":"The image does not claim the house is intact, only that some shelter remains; the well-born, too, can do less but not nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:148":{"book":"naladiyar","num":148,"t":["ஒருபுடை பாம்பு கொளினும் ஒருபுடை","அங்கண்மா ஞாலம் விளக்குறூஉம் - திங்கள்போல்","செல்லாமை செவ்வனேர் நிற்பினும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு","ஒல்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Like the moon which affords light to the fair and spacious earth with one side, while the dragon holds the other, the nobly born do not become remiss in works of seemly benevolence, though poverty (inability) stand fronting them.","r":"Like the moon that lights the wide fair earth with one side while the serpent holds the other, the well-born do not slacken in serving others even when poverty stands squarely before them.","n":"The serpent is the eclipse, as Shanmuganar notes. The last line shares its words, ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார், with Kural 218.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","kudi","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:149":{"book":"naladiyar","num":149,"t":["செல்லா விடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார் செய்வன","செல்லிடத்தும் செய்யார் சிறியவர் - புல்வாய்","பருமம் பொறுப்பினும் பாய்பரி மாபோல்","பொருமுரண் ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"Men of mean descent, even when all goes well with them, will not perform the good deeds that the well-born will do, even when things do not go well with them. Though the antelope should bear a pillion, it rushes not to war like the prancing charger.","r":"What the well-born do even when they cannot, the small will not do even when they can; an antelope may bear a saddle, but it cannot do battle like a leaping horse.","n":"The saddle is outward equipment, so the verse says that giving the small the means does not give them the nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:150":{"book":"naladiyar","num":150,"t":["எற்றொன்றும் இல்லா இடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","அற்றத்தற் சேர்ந்தார்க் கசைவிடத் தூற்றாவர்","அற்றக் கடைத்தும் அகல்யா றகழ்ந்தக்கால்","தெற்றெனத் தெண்ணீர் படும்."],"p":"The nobly born, even when destitute, are props in time of feebleness to the needy ones that draw nigh to them. So, when the wide river is dry, if you dig in its bed, forth with clear waters gush out. -------------","r":"Even when they have nothing at all, the well-born are a prop to those who come to them in need, as a wide river, when dried up, yields clear water at once if you dig its bed.","n":"The river image implies that the means of giving is still there below the surface; the well-born have to be dug for, not asked from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:151":{"book":"naladiyar","num":151,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகனிலாப் பாரிக்கும்","திங்களும் சான்றோரும் ஒப்பர்மன் - திங்கள்","மறுவாற்றும், சான்றோரஃ தாற்றார் தெருமந்து","தேய்வர் ஒருமா சுறின்."],"p":"The moon that diffuses light through heaven's fair realms, and truly worthy men are alike: yet that endures a spot, while the truly worthy endure it. not; — perplexed and sad they pine away if but one stain appear.","r":"The noble resemble the moon that spreads its light across the sky, except that the moon carries its spot while the noble, if one stain falls on them, pine away and waste.","n":"The comparison turns into a contrast: the moon is the lesser because it puts up with its blemish. Sensitivity to stain is made the mark of superiority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","maanam","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because the moon is specified as the moon in the wide sky, the 'noble' of the chapter title are to be taken as the high-born.",""]],"ch":"n:16"},"n:152":{"book":"naladiyar","num":152,"t":["இசையும் எனினும் இசையா தெனினும்","வசைதீர எண்ணுவர் சான்றோர் - விசையின்","நா஢மா உளங்கிழித்த அம்பினின் தீதோ,","அரிமாப் பிழைப்பெய்த கோல்?"],"p":"Whether success attend, or do not attend the work, the excellent will even ponder blameless ends.—Is the shaft that missed the lion worse than the arrow sent forth, that with its impulse pierced the jackal's heart?","r":"Whether the aim succeeds or not, the worthy plan only what carries no blame: is the arrow that missed a lion worse than the one that tore open a jackal's breast?","n":"Two ideas sit together: the aim must be blameless, and it must be high. A noble failure outranks a mean success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","muyarchi","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:153":{"book":"naladiyar","num":153,"t":["நரம்பெழுந்து நல்கூர்ந்தா ராயினும் சான்றோர்","குரம்பெழுந்து குற்றங்கொ ண்டேறார் - உரங்கவறா","உள்ளமெனும் நாரினால் கட்டி உளவரையால்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The excellent though emaciated and poor, do not transgress the limits of virtue and commit evil. With wisdom for the pillar, with perseverance as the band, they bind (the mind); and as long as they live they do what it behoves them to do.","r":"Though poverty leaves them all sinew, the worthy do not cross the bounds into wrongdoing; binding the mind with understanding and resolve, they do what ought to be done as far as their means allow.","n":"The last phrase sets a measure: duty is scaled to what one has, not abandoned. 185 ends with the same words, செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","ozhukkam","ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:154":{"book":"naladiyar","num":154,"t":["செல்வுழிக் கண்ணொருநாள் காணினும் சான்றவர்","தொல்வழிக் கேண்மையிற் றோன்றப் புரிந்தியாப்பர்","நல்வரை நாட. சிலநாள் அடிப்படின்","கல்வரையும் உண்டாம் நெறி."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills ! —If trodden for a few days a path is formed over even the craggy hill; so excellent persons, though they have seen (worthy) men only for one day, as they were travelling, will bind them to their soul, exhibiting all the marks of an ancient friendship.","r":"If the worthy see someone just once on the road, they bind that person to them as if in a friendship of long standing, just as a few days' treading makes a path even over rock.","n":"The image cuts two ways: a path on rock needs repeated treading, yet the worthy form the bond at one meeting. Pope adds that those so bound are themselves worthy; the Tamil does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:155":{"book":"naladiyar","num":155,"t":["புல்லா வெழுத்திற் பொருளில் வறுங்கோட்டி","கல்லா ஒருவன் உரைப்பவும் கண்ணோடி","நல்லார் வருந்தியும் கேட்பரே, மற்றவன்","பல்லாருள் நாணல் பரிந்து."],"p":"Even when one speaks who has an ungrammatical knowledge of the letter, but not of the meaning who is of a low (empty) school, and is unlearned the good with kindly compassion will listen, though it is pain to them, being grieved that he should be put to shame in the presence of many.","r":"When an unlearned man who knows neither grammar nor meaning speaks in an empty gathering, the good listen out of kindly regard, though it pains them, to spare him shame before many.","n":"Mudaliar glosses கண்ணோடி as தாட்சிண்யம், courteous indulgence. The good endure bad speech not for its sake but for the speaker's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kallaamai","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:156":{"book":"naladiyar","num":156,"t":["கடித்துக் கரும்பினைக் கண்தகர நூறி","இடித்துநீர் கொள்ளினும் இன்சுவைத்தே யாகும்","வடுப்பட வைதிறந்தக் கண்ணும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","கூறார்தம் வாயிற் சிதைந்து."],"p":"Although you bite the sugar-cane, crush it till its joints are broken, grind it, and express its juice, it still will be sweet. The highly born, even when men have passed by abusing them so as to wound, never lose their self-respect so as to utter from their mouth (words of abuse).","r":"Sugar-cane bitten, crushed at the joints and pounded for its juice is still sweet; so the well-born, even when abused so as to wound, do not lose themselves and speak in kind.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the close as positive, that they keep speaking sweetly; the Tamil says only that they do not speak as people undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","porai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:157":{"book":"naladiyar","num":157,"t":["கள்ளார், கள் ளுண்ணார், கடிவ கடிந்தெரிணஇ,","எள்ளிப் பிறரை இகழ்ந்துரையார், - தள்ளியும்","வாயில்பொய் கூறார், வடுவறு காட்சியார்","சாயிற் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"They defraud not, drink not palm-wine, shun what is forbidden, never despise and speak contemptuously of their neighbours, nor even forgetfully do they utter anything untrue with their mouth : these men of faultless vision grieve not though they fall into distress.","r":"Those of faultless vision do not steal, do not drink, shun what is forbidden, do not scorn others, and never lie even by a slip; and if they decline in fortune, they do not grieve.","n":"The list of abstentions ends in an inner state: equanimity in decline is presented as the fruit of clean conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalavu","kal","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:158":{"book":"naladiyar","num":158,"t":["பிறர்மறை யின்கண் செவிடாய்த் திறனறிந்து","ஏதிலா ரிற்கண், குருடனாய்த் தீய","புறங்கூற்றின் மூகையாய் நிற்பானேல், யாதும்","அறங்கூற வேண்டா அவற்கு."],"p":"Deaf to others' secrets, blind to his neighbour's wife, dumb to evil backbiting—if, knowing what is befitting, a man thus abides, it is not necessary to teach him any virtuous precepts.","r":"One who is deaf to others' secrets, blind to a neighbour's wife and dumb in evil backbiting, knowing what is fitting, needs no teaching of virtue at all.","n":"Mudaliar reads 'if he stands so' as stressing how rare this is. Virtue here is a matter of restraint of the senses, not of instruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piranil","purangooral","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:159":{"book":"naladiyar","num":159,"t":["பன்னாளும் சென்றக்கால் பண்பிலார் தம்முழை","என்னானும் வேண்டுப என்றிகழ்ப - என்னானும்","வேண்டினும் நன்றுமற் றென்று விமுமியோர்","காண்டொறும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு."],"p":"They who are destitute of kindly courtesy, even after the lapse of many days, will contemn (those that visit them), saying, ‘they will ask something of us'. The excellent will treat them with distinction whenever they see them, saying, ‘if they ask something of us, it is well'.","r":"Those without good character scorn a frequent visitor, thinking he wants something from them; the excellent, thinking it good even if he asks, honour him every time they see him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:160":{"book":"naladiyar","num":160,"t":["உடையார் இவரென் றொருதலையாப் பற்றிக்","கடையாயார் பின்சென்று வாழ்வா - உடைய","பிலந்தலைப் பட்டது போலாதே, நல்ல","குலந்தலைப் பட்ட விடத்து."],"p":"The lowest sort of men say, there are men of property,’— cling to them steadily, follow them, and so flourish. Is it not as when a mine of treasure has been found, when men of good lineage have become our friends. --------------","r":"The base settle on the rich as their one hope and live by trailing after them; yet meeting people of good family is like coming upon a treasure cave.","n":"The contrast is between attaching oneself to money and to lineage. The verse prizes good birth as company; it does not say what the treasure consists of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","selvam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:161":{"book":"naladiyar","num":161,"t":["பொறுப்பரென் றெண்ணிப் புரைதீர்ந்தார் மாட்டும்","வெறுப்பன செய்யாமை வேண்டும் - வெறுத்தபின்","ஆர்க்கும் அருவி யணிமலை நன்னாட","பேர்க்குதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land whose hills resounding water-falls adorn ! — You may not even to faultless men do things that enkindle wrath; for when their wrath is once kindled, it is hard for any one to change their mood.","r":"Do not do what offends even the faultless, counting on them to forgive; once they turn away, no one can undo it.","n":"The warning targets presumption: the very forbearance of the great is what tempts the offence. Pope reads the last line as the great one's mood being hard to change; both commentators read it as the resulting harm being impossible to remove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","porai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:162":{"book":"naladiyar","num":162,"t":["பொன்னே கொடுத்தும் புணர்தற் கா஢யாரைக்","கொன்னே தலைக்கூடப் பெற்றிருந்தும் - அன்னோ","பயனில் பொழுதாக் கழிப்பரே, நல்ல","நயமில் அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Although those whose (pretended) wisdom is without moral excellence have gained access to men to whom approach is difficult even by gifts of gold alas! they pass away (the precious moments) as mere waste time.","r":"People of loveless cleverness who have freely gained the company of the great, whom others cannot reach even by paying gold, alas, let the time pass without profit.","n":"Mudaliar takes நயம் as delight or relish, so the fault is an inability to enjoy and draw on what the great offer, not ill will.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pedhaimai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:163":{"book":"naladiyar","num":163,"t":["அவமதிப்பும் ஆன்ற மதிப்பும் இரண்டும்","மிகைமக்க ளான்மதிக்கற் பால - நயமுணராக்","கையறியா மாக்கள் இழிப்பும் எடுத்தேத்தும்","வையார் வடித்தநூ லார்."],"p":"Both disesteem and thorough esteem are in the class of things that depend on the estimation of the great. Those who have a discriminating knowledge of true science, lay no stress on either the abuse or the fulsome praise of man who comprehend not moral principles, and know not the rules of propriety.","r":"Disesteem and high esteem alike are for the superior to judge; those versed in refined learning take no account of the scorn or the extravagant praise of the base who know neither grace nor propriety.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pugazh","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:164":{"book":"naladiyar","num":164,"t":["விரிநிற நாகம் விடருள தேனும்","உருமின் கடுஞ்சினம் சேணின்றும் உட்கும்","அருமை யுடைய அரண்சேர்ந்தும் உய்யார்","பெருமை யுடையார் செறின்."],"p":"The serpent rich of hue, though it dwell in the mountain-cleft, from far is frighted by the thunderbolt's fierce wrath; so men escape not though hid in strongholds hard to reach, when those great in virtue are wroth.","r":"A brightly hooded snake living in a crevice still dreads the thunder's fury from afar; so those who have angered the great do not escape even inside a fortress hard to reach.","n":"Pope reads them as those great in virtue, closer to Shanmuganar. On Mudaliar's reading the fortress is a defence against royal power; on the others, against a moral force no wall can stop.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran","vekuli"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The 'great' whose anger cannot be escaped are kings.",""],["shanmuganar","The great are elders in general, angered by someone who has wronged them.",""]],"ch":"n:17"},"n:165":{"book":"naladiyar","num":165,"t":["எம்மை யறிந்திலிர் எம்போல்வார் இல்லென்று","தம்மைத்தாம் கொள்வது கோளன்று - தம்மை","அரியரா நோக்கி அறனறியும் சான்றோர்","பெரியராக் கொள்வது கோள்."],"p":"‘You know not all our worth, for equals have we none:’ when self thus estimates itself, this is not ‘esteem’ ! When perfect men, proficients in virtue, regard any as dear, and esteem them as great, this is ‘esteem’ !","r":"Saying 'you do not know us; there is none like us' is not esteem; esteem is when the worthy who know virtue see one as rare and accept one as great.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saandror","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:166":{"book":"naladiyar","num":166,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப. நாணிழல் போல","விளியும் சிறியவர் கேண்மை - விளிவின்றி","அல்கு நிழற்போல் அகன்றகன் றோடுமே","தொல்புக ழாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the great sea’scool margin !—Friendships formed with mean men dwindling die like shadows of the morn; friendship of men of old renown as shadows of the after-day will lengthen out and grow.","r":"The friendship of the small dwindles like the morning shadow, while the bond of those of long renown keeps lengthening like the shadow of the declining day.","n":"Mudaliar offers two timings for the shadows (dawn against noon, or forenoon against afternoon); the point, shrinking against growing, is the same either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:167":{"book":"naladiyar","num":167,"t":["மன்னர் திருவும் மகளிர் எழினலமும்","துன்னியார் துய்ப்பர் தகல்வேண்டா - துன்னிக்","குழைகொண்டு தாழ்ந்த குளிர்மர மெல்லாம்","உழைதங்கண் சென்றார்க் கொருங்கு."],"p":"Those who press their suit, fitness a part, enjoy the wealth of kings, and the charms of maidens fair; so all the cool (shady) trees that droop earthward with thick foliage, give room to those that thronging seek their shelter.","r":"Those who keep close enjoy the wealth of kings and the beauty of women, without needing to be worthy; just as cool trees heavy with leaf give shelter alike to all who come near.","n":"The verse states a fact about proximity rather than merit. Under this chapter's heading it may counsel staying near the great, but neither commentator draws that out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:168":{"book":"naladiyar","num":168,"t":["தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலார் கண்ணும்","பிரியப் பெரும்படர் நோய்செய்யும் - பெரிய","உலவா இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பயார் மாட்டும்","கலவாமை கோடி யுறும்."],"p":"Severance from even those who have no under standing [lit. no understanding that understands (so as) to understand] causes great and spreading sorrow. — Lord of the shore of the great, unfailing, swelling bay !—To be intimate with none is ten million times the best.","r":"Parting even from those who lack discernment brings great, lingering sorrow; so, lord of the long brimming backwaters, not mixing with anyone at all is ten million times better.","n":"This is the renunciant's conclusion drawn from the pain of attachment, and it cuts against the next chapter's praise of good company. Mudaliar spells out the comparison: not joining is better than joining and then parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pattru","thuravu","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:169":{"book":"naladiyar","num":169,"t":["கல்லாது போகிய நாளும் பெரியவர்கண்","செல்லாது வைகிய வைகலும் - ஒல்வ","கொடாஅ தொழிந்த பகலும் உரைப்பின்","படாஅவாம் பண்புடையார் கண்."],"p":"Days gone by without learning, those passed without any intercourse with the great, those spent without giving what is fitting;— in the case of the excellent, if you tell them over, none such occur.","r":"Days passed without learning, without going to the great, without giving what one can: if you count them, none such are found among people of good character.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kalvi","eegai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:170":{"book":"naladiyar","num":170,"t":["பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை ஒன்றிற்","குரியா ருரிமை யடக்கம் - தெரியுங்கால்","செல்வ முடையாருஞ் செல்வரே தற்சேர்ந்தார்","அல்லல் களைப வெனின்."],"p":"The greatness of the great is [the quality of littleness (in their own sight), i. e.] humility; the (real) acquisition of those who have acquired (any). one (science) is modest self-restraint. If you rightly understand things, those possessors of wealth only are really wealthy who relieve the wants of those that approach them (as suppliants). ----------------","r":"The greatness of the great is humility; the fitting quality of one who owns any one excellence is restraint; and, rightly seen, the wealthy are wealthy only if they relieve the distress of those who come to them.","n":"Mudaliar takes ஒன்றிற்கு as any one of learning, wealth or manliness, and records another reading, 'to the Supreme'. Pope narrows it to one science.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","adakkam","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:171":{"book":"naladiyar","num":171,"t":["அறியாப் பருவத் தடங்காரோ டொன்றி","நெறியல்ல செய்தொழுகி யவ்வும் - நெறியறிந்த","நற்சார்வு சாரக் கெடுமே வெயில்முறுகப்","புற்பனிப் பற்றுவிட் டாங்கு."],"p":"In youth unwise, though men consort with haughty ones and walk in lawless ways; yet, when they join with those that know the righteous path, their faults shall melt away as dew from off the grass when sunbeams scorch.","r":"Faults committed in thoughtless youth, keeping company with the unruly and going astray, vanish once one joins good people who know the right way, as dew lets go of the grass when the sun grows hot.","n":"Mudaliar names these faults the faults of association (சகவாச தோஷங்கள்). Company is both the cause of the wrong and its cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kutram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:172":{"book":"naladiyar","num":172,"t":["அறிமின் அறநெறி அஞ்சுமின் கூற்றம்","பொறுமின் பிறர்கடுஞ்சொல் போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம்","வெறுமின் வினைதீயார் கேண்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெறுமின் பெரியார்வாய்ச் சொல்."],"p":"Know virtue's path ! Dread death! Beat others' words severe! Beware ye practise no deceit! Friendship detest with men of evil deeds! Daily get gain of words that fall from greatmen's lips!","r":"Know the path of virtue; dread Death; bear the harsh words of others; guard against deceit; shun friendship with evildoers; and always take in the words of the great.","n":"Mudaliar reads போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம் as preventing deceit from arising in oneself, and allows வாய்ச்சொல் to mean the blessing of the great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kootru","porai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:173":{"book":"naladiyar","num":173,"t":["அடைந்தார்ப் பிரிவும் அரும்பிணியும் கேடும்","உடங்குடம்பு கொண்டார்க் குறலால் - தொடங்கிப்","பிறப்பின்னா தென்றுணரும் பேரறிவி னாரை","உறப்புணர்க அம்மாஎன் நெஞ்சு."],"p":"Severance of close-joined friends and sore disease and death, all these combined hap to those that have assumed a human body: so the truly wise have felt that birth, from the very first, is bitterness. Ah! soul, cleave close to these!","r":"Since parting from those one is close to, grievous illness and ruin come together to all who take a body, O my heart, cling to those of great understanding who see that birth is bitter from its start.","n":"The speaker addresses his own heart. The reason for seeking the wise is not their virtue but their insight into birth as suffering.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inam","nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","கேடு is harm or misfortune in general.",""],["shanmuganar","கேடு is death, completing the list of separation, illness and death.",""]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:174":{"book":"naladiyar","num":174,"t":["இறப்ப நினையுங்கால் இன்னா தெனினும்","பிறப்பினை யாரும் முனியார் - பிறப்பினுள்","பண்பாற்றும் நெஞ்சத் தவர்களோ டெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நண்பாற்றி நட்கப் பெறின்."],"p":"Though when you ponder it, it is surpassing: bitterness, none hate (this mortal) birth, if in (this mortal) birth they ever perform friendly acts to men whose hearts are set on noble excellence, and gain their friendship.","r":"Though birth, when thought through, is extremely bitter, no one would resent it if within it they could keep lasting friendship with people whose hearts practise goodness.","n":"Mudaliar notes that 'if they can get it' (பெறின்) marks how rare such friendship is. The verse redeems birth without denying its pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:175":{"book":"naladiyar","num":175,"t":["ஊரங் கணநீர் உரவுநீர் சேர்ந்தக்கால்","பேரும் பிறிதாகித் தீர்த்தமாம் - ஓருங்","குலமாட்சி இல்லாரும் குன்றுபோல் நிற்பர்","நலமாட்சி நல்லாரைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"When water from the town-sewers has joined the great river its very name is changed, and it becomes a 'sacred stream’. Ponder well! Even those who have no greatness of clan, if they ally themselves with good men of goodly fame, shall stand firm as a hill.","r":"Town drain water that joins a great river loses its name and becomes holy water; consider: even those without lineage stand like a hill by joining the good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:176":{"book":"naladiyar","num":176,"t":["ஒண்கதிர் வாள்மதியும் சேர்தலால் ஓங்கிய","அங்கண் விசும்பின் முயலும் தொழப்படுஉம்","குன்றிய சீர்மைய ராயினும் சீர்பெறுவர்,","குன்றன்னார் கேண்மை கொளின்."],"p":"Because it is in the bright-beamed moon the very hare in heaven's lofty fair expanse is worshipped. Though scant their worthiness, men obtain worth who share friendship with those firm as a hill.","r":"The hare in the bright moon is worshipped along with it in the sky; so those of slight worth gain worth by friendship with men as steady as a hill.","n":"Mudaliar explains the hare as the moon's dark mark: no one removes the spot before worshipping the moon, so the flaw shares the honour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","natpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:177":{"book":"naladiyar","num":177,"t":["பாலோ டளாயநீர் பாலாகு மல்லது","நீராய் நிறம்தெரிந்து தோன்றாதாம் - தேரின்","சிறியார் சிறுமையும் தோன்றாதாம், நல்ல","பெரியார் பெருமையைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Commingled with milk water becomes milk; at least, it shows no more as water by its hue. Look close, the mean men's meanness shows not if they join themselves to greatness of the good and great.","r":"Water mixed with milk becomes milk and no longer shows its own colour; so, on inspection, the smallness of small people does not show when they join the greatness of the good.","n":"The verse says the meanness is hidden, not removed. That is a weaker claim than 171, where faults vanish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:178":{"book":"naladiyar","num":178,"t":["கொல்லை யிரும்புனத்துக் குற்றி யடைந்தபுல்","ஒல்காவே யாகும் உழவ ருழுபடைக்கு","மெல்லியரே யாயினும் நற்சார்வு சார்ந்தார்மேல்","செல்லாவாம் செற்றார் சினம்."],"p":"Grass close around the stump in the field adjoining the house will not be destroyed by the ploughshare of the farmer ; [thus] though men are weak, if they get under safe protection, the wrath of their foes will not reach them.","r":"Grass growing against a stump in a large field is not moved by the farmer's plough; so the anger of enemies cannot reach the weak who have joined good company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:179":{"book":"naladiyar","num":179,"t":["நிலநலத்தால் நந்திய நெல்லேபோல் தத்தம்","குலநலத்தால் ஆகுவர் சான்றோர் - கலநலத்தைத்","தீவளி சென்று சிதைத்தாங்குச் சான்றாண்மை","தீயினம் சேரக் கெடும்."],"p":"As Nel that flourishes through the goodness of the soil, good men become illustrious by the excellence of their respective clanships. Goodness perishes when it comes near evil associates, as the tornado advances and destroys the excellence of the ship.","r":"As paddy thrives by the goodness of its soil, the worthy flourish by the goodness of their own group; and worth is ruined by joining evil company, as a gale wrecks a sound ship.","n":"Pope's 'clanships' leans toward birth. Which reading one takes decides whether this verse agrees or collides with 195.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","saandraanmai","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","குலம் is the company one joins, keeping the verse within the chapter's theme.",""],["shanmuganar","குலம் is the family one is born into; the worthy are formed by their birth.",""]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:180":{"book":"naladiyar","num":180,"t":["மனத்தான் மறுவில ரேனுந்தாம் சேர்ந்த","இனத்தால் இகழப் படுவர் - புனத்து","வெறிகமழ் சந்தனமும் வேங்கையும் வேமே","எறிபுனம் தீப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"Though themselves pure in mind, even good men incur contempt because of their associations. So in the woodland glade when a conflagration takes place (not the worthless brushWood only, but) the fragrant Sandal and Vengai too are consumed. --------------","r":"Though spotless in mind, people are scorned for the company they keep: when a cleared field is set on fire, the fragrant sandal and the vengai burn with the rest.","n":"The verse concerns reputation and fate together: the pure are blamed, and the fire also destroys them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:181":{"book":"naladiyar","num":181,"t":["ஈத லிசையா திளமைசேண் நீங்குதலால்","காத லவரும் கருத்தல்லர் - காதலித்து","ஆதுநா மென்னு மவாவினைக் கைவிட்டுப்","போவதே போலும் பொருள்."],"p":"To give is no longer ours; and youth is gone far off ; our once beloved ones think of us no more! To depart, having abandoned 'desire' that bids us love and hope for future joys, appears the thing that's meet.","r":"We can no longer give, youth has gone far off, and those who once loved us no longer think of us; it seems the right course is to give up the craving that says 'we will love and prosper' and to leave.","n":"The reasons for renouncing here are practical and even rueful, not doctrinal. Mudaliar specifies the departure as going into renunciation, and the loved ones as women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","avaa","inmai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:182":{"book":"naladiyar","num":182,"t":["இற்சார்வின் ஏமாந்தேம் ஈங்கமைந்தேம் என்றெண்ணிப்","பொச்சாந் தொழுகுவர் பேதையார் - அச்சார்வு","நின்றன போன்று நிலையா எனவுணர்ந்தார்","என்றும் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Fools thinking 'we have found joy in the refuge of home,' and 'we are here in perfect repose, forgetful (of the instability of all things), go on their way. Those who have felt that 'refuge' is unstable, while it seems to stand fast, never fix their fond desire (on it).","r":"Fools think 'we found contentment by taking to home life; here we are complete', and go on heedless; those who see that such supports only seem to stand and do not last never grieve.","n":"Mudaliar allows பரிவது to mean either grieving or desiring; Pope takes the second. The verse targets complacency in home life; whether it condemns home life itself depends on the reader.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilvaazhkkai","pochchaappu","pattru"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The supports that fail are the attachments of household life.",""]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:183":{"book":"naladiyar","num":183,"t":["மறுமைக்கு வித்து மயலின்றிச் செய்து","சிறுமைப் படாதேநீர் வாழ்மின் - அறிஞராய்","நின்றுழி நின்றே நிறம்வேறாம் காரணம்","இன்றிப் பலவு முள."],"p":"See that ye sow seed that in other world may germinate; free your lives from earth's bewilder-ment and meanness ; stand in your lot as wise men; the changing hue of things shall without cause fade, and many things be new.","r":"Without confusion sow the seed for the next life, and live wisely without being brought low; for standing just where it stands the body changes its colour without any cause, and many ills arise.","n":"Mudaliar supplies the subject: it is the body whose youth and qualities change unbidden. Pope's version speaks more vaguely of 'the changing hue of things'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:184":{"book":"naladiyar","num":184,"t":["உறைப்பருங் காலத்தும் ஊற்றுநீர்க் கேணி","இறைத்துணினும் ஊராற்றும் என்பர் - கொடைக்கடனும்","சாஅயக் கண்ணும் பெரியார்போல் மற்றையார்","ஆஅயக் கண்ணும் அரிது."],"p":"The well of springing water serves the town that draws and drinks, even in time when rains are scant; so great men in adverse hour dispense due gifts that others give not even in their best estate.","r":"They say a spring-fed well, even in a season of scant rain, serves the town that draws from it; the great keep their duty of giving even when they have declined, as others do not even when they have prospered.","n":"Only the Tamil and Pope are available for this quatrain; neither commentary is aligned to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","perumai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:185":{"book":"naladiyar","num":185,"t":["உறுபுனல் தந்துல கூட்டி அறுமிடத்தும்","கல்லூற் றுழியூறும் ஆறேபோல் - செல்வர்","பலர்க்காற்றிக் கெட்டுலந்தக் கண்ணும் சிலர்காற்றிச்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The river pours forth a mighty stream and feeds the world; and when it is dried up, if men dig in its bed, streams gush out! So good men when rich, give to many; and, when ruined, give still at least to some, and do what should be done.","r":"Like a river that poured out abundant water to feed the world and, when dry, still yields water from springs dug in its bed, the generous who gave to many still give to a few when ruined, and do what ought to be done.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:186":{"book":"naladiyar","num":186,"t":["பெருவரை நாட பெரியோர்கட் டீமை","கருநரைமேற் சூடேபோல் தோன்றும் - கருநரையைக்","கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் சிறியார்மேல்","ஒன்றானும் தோன்றாக் கெடும்."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hills! Any evil in men of moral greatness shows like a brand on a mighty bull; but although mean men do painful deeds, like the slaughter of that same mighty bull, none of these attracts attention.","r":"Lord of the high hills, a fault in the great shows like a brand on a great white bull, while the small may do things as cruel as killing that bull and not one of them is noticed.","n":"This is an observation about visibility, not a defence of the small. The high standard applies because the great are watched.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:187":{"book":"naladiyar","num":187,"t":["இசைந்த சிறுமை இயல்பிலா தார்கண்,","பசைந்த துணையும் பரிவாம் - அசைந்த","நகையேயும் வேண்டாத நல்லறிவி னார்கண்","பகையேயும் பாடு பெறும்."],"p":"In proportion to the degree of one's intimacy with men essentially mean and without good qualities sorrow accrues; but even the hostility of those who do not desire forbidden things even in jest will confer dignity.","r":"Intimacy with the inherently mean brings grief for as long as it lasts, but even enmity with the wise, who would not want harmful things even in jest, brings dignity.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","sirumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:188":{"book":"naladiyar","num":188,"t":["மெல்லிய நல்லாருள் மென்மை அதுவிறந்(து)","ஒன்னாருள் கூற்றுட்கும் உட்குடைமை - எல்லாம்","சலவருட் சாலச் சலவே நலவருள்","நன்மை வரம்பாய் விடல்."],"p":"With gentle fair ones use gentleness surpassing theirs; with foes display a wrath that death's self might dread; with persistent men show a persistency to match ; amongst men of good do good; let the law of life be thus laid down.","r":"Be gentler than the gentle good; toward foes, be a terror that Death itself would fear; toward the false, utterly false; toward the good, goodness itself: let these be one's bounds.","n":"A policy of matching each person in kind. Pope reads சலவர் as the persistent, to be met with persistence; Mudaliar reads them as liars, to be met with lies. Mudaliar also notes that others construe the verse differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Toward liars, one should be wholly a speaker of lies.",""]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:189":{"book":"naladiyar","num":189,"t":["கடுக்கி யொருவன் கடுங்குறளைப் பேசி","மயக்கி விடினும் மனப்பிரிப் பொன்றின்றித்","துளக்க மிலாதவர் தூய மனத்தார்","விளக்கினுள் ஒண்சுடரே போன்று."],"p":"These are the pure in heart who, though any vex, and with use of slanderous words would fain perplex them, with calm unruffled mind abide unwavering, like the bright flame within the lamp.","r":"Though someone hurries in and tries to confuse them with cruel slander, the pure in heart stay unshaken, with no division in their mind, like the bright flame inside a lamp.","n":"The flame is steady because the lamp shields it; the image suggests an inner composure that slander cannot reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","purangooral","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:190":{"book":"naladiyar","num":190,"t":["முற்றுற்றும் துற்றினை நாளும் அறஞ்செய்து","பிற்றுற்றுத் துற்றுவர் சான்றவர் - அத்துற்று","முக்குற்றம் நீக்கி முடியும் அளவெல்லாம்","துக்கத்துள் நீக்கி விடும்."],"p":"The excellent will daily give to the needy in charity their first-served food; they themselves will eat what is served after: such good conduct removes the three faults, and from sorrow sets men free, through all the days, till comes the end. ---------------","r":"The worthy daily give in charity the food served first and eat what is served after; that eating removes the three faults and keeps them from sorrow as long as life lasts.","n":"Shanmuganar names the three faults as desire, anger and delusion. Pope leaves them unnamed, and no Mudaliar gloss is aligned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","undi","saandror","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:191":{"book":"naladiyar","num":191,"t":["கோளாற்றக் கொள்ளாக் குளத்தின்கீழ்ப் பைங்கூழ்போல்","கேளீவ துண்டு கிளைகளோ துஞ்சுப","வாளாடு கூத்தியர் கண்போல் தடுமாறும்","தாளாளர்க் குண்டோ தவறு."],"p":"As for kindred that feed upon what relatives give them they will die off like green grain below a tank, which does not hold a sufficient supply (of water). Is failure possible to men of energy that are quick in movement as the eyes of those who perform the sword-dance ?","r":"Like the green crop below a tank that does not hold back its water, kin live by what their relative gives and rest easy; can failure come to the energetic, who move as quickly as the eyes of women in the sword dance?","n":"Pope reads the first half in reverse: kin who live on handouts die off like crops under a tank that holds too little water. The Tamil supports Shanmuganar's 'rest' (துஞ்சுப) at least as well, so the verse probably praises the energetic as the one who feeds the family.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The other kin live without hardship on what the energetic one gives.",""]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:192":{"book":"naladiyar","num":192,"t":["ஆடுகோ டாகி அதரிடை நின்றதூஉம்","காழ்கொண்ட கண்ணே களிறணைக்கும் கந்தாகும்","வாழ்தலும் அன்ன தகைத்தே ஒருவன்றான்","தாழ்வின்றித் தன்னைச் செயின்."],"p":"What once stood by the wayside, a twig that bent to every touch, when its core is developed within, may become a stake to which they tie an elephant. Life too is thus, if man himself, unfailing make himself !","r":"A twig that swayed by the path, once its core has hardened, becomes a post to tether an elephant; life is like that too, if a person makes himself without slackening.","n":"Shanmuganar reads தாழ்வின்றி as not letting oneself sink; Pope as 'unfailing'. Either way the self is something made over time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:193":{"book":"naladiyar","num":193,"t":["உறுபுலி ஊனிரை யின்றி ஒருநாள்","சிறுதேரை பற்றியும் தின்னும் - அறிவினால்","காற்றொழில் என்று கருதற்க கையினால்","மேற்றொழிலும் ஆங்கே மிகும்."],"p":"The huge tiger, when lacking flesh for food one day, may even seize a little frog and eat it. Think not 'By (all my) knowledge I only gain menial tasks;\" to the skillful hand nobler employments shall even there abound.","r":"A mighty tiger that finds no meat one day will catch and eat even a small frog; so do not think your knowledge fits you only for lowly work: through skill, higher work will come in that very place.","n":"Pope titles it 'Stoop to conquer'. Shanmuganar reads the advice as taking the humble job that is available, since better skilled work may follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:194":{"book":"naladiyar","num":194,"t":["இசையா தெனினும் இயற்றியோ ராற்றால்","அசையாது நிற்பதாம் ஆண்மை - இசையுங்கால்","கண்டால் திரையலைக்கும் கானலந் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பெண்டிரும் வாழாரோ மற்று."],"p":"Manliness is working on, in no wise faltering, remaining stedfast though the matter succeed not. When all is successful,— Lord of the cool lovely shore, in whose groves the waves agitate the scented thorn, — will not even women live and flourish?","r":"Manliness is standing firm in the way one has undertaken even when the thing does not succeed; lord of the cool seashore groves, when things succeed by themselves, don't even women manage to live?","n":"The taunt assumes that women cannot persist through failure. The point is that persistence, not success, is the measure of effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","muyarchi","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:195":{"book":"naladiyar","num":195,"t":["நல்ல குலமென்றும் தீய குலமென்றும்","சொல்லள வல்லால் பொருளில்லை - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்பொரு ளொன்றோ தவம்கல்வி யாள்வினை","என்றிவற்றான் ஆகும் குலம்."],"p":"When men speak of 'good caste' and 'bad caste' it is a mere form of speech, and has no real meaning. Not even by possessions, made splendid by ancient glories, but by self-denial, learning, and energy is Caste determined.","r":"'Good caste' and 'bad caste' are only words with nothing behind them; not long-famous wealth but austerity, learning and effort are what make a lineage.","n":"A strong claim from a Jain text that questions birth rank. Shanmuganar takes ஒண்பொருள் ஒன்றோ as asking whether fine wealth alone suffices; Pope reads it as 'not even by possessions'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","thavam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:196":{"book":"naladiyar","num":196,"t":["ஆற்றும் துணையும் அறிவினை உள்ளடக்கி","ஊக்கம் உரையார் உணர்வுடையார் - ஊக்கம்","உறுப்பினால் ஆராயும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்","குறிப்பின்கீழ்ப் பட்ட துலகு."],"p":"The world is subject to the nod of the brilliant (diplomatists) who search out (and know) men's designs from outward indications (lit. from) their members, i.e. from eye, gesture, tone, expression etc.).","r":"The perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done and do not voice their drive; the world lies under the sign of those bright ones who read others' drive from their features.","n":"Mudaliar takes the first ஊக்கம் as one's own effort and the second as the stirring in others' minds, so the verse pairs concealment with insight. He also records a reading in which the world serves at their nod.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:197":{"book":"naladiyar","num":197,"t":["சிதலை தினப்பட்ட ஆல மரத்தை","மதலையாய் மற்றதன் வீழுன்றி யாங்குக்","குதலைமை தந்தைகண் தோன்றிற்றான் பெற்ற","புதல்வன் மறைப்பக் கெடும்."],"p":"If the banyan's trunk be eaten by the gnawing ant, its 'branch root' bears it up, like a buttress; even so, when decay appears in the sire, the son he has begotten shall hide it, and weakness is no more.","r":"When termites have eaten the banyan, its aerial roots stand as pillars and hold it up; so when weakness shows in a father, the son he begot covers it and it disappears.","n":"Mudaliar notes that மதலை, the prop, also means a son, so the image and the claim share a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:198":{"book":"naladiyar","num":198,"t":["ஈனமாய் இல்லிருந் தின்றி விளியினும்","மானும் தலைவருவ செய்பவோ? - யானை","வரிமுகம் புண்படுக்கும் வள்ளுகிர் நோன்றாள்","அரிமா மதுகை யவர்."],"p":"The lion's pointed claw and mighty foot will wound the spotted face of an elephant; those who have power like his , — though bereft of all, they die in want within their home, — will they do deeds that bring disgrace?","r":"Those with the strength of a lion, whose sharp claws and mighty paws wound an elephant's spotted face, would sooner die in want and low estate at home than do anything that brings disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar reads மானம் here as dishonour (அவமானம்) and maps the lion's claws onto keen intelligence and resourceful effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","inmai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:199":{"book":"naladiyar","num":199,"t":["தீங்கரும் பீன்று திரள்கால் உளையலா஢","தேங்கமழ் நாற்றம் இழந்தாஅங்கு - ஓங்கும்","உயர்குடி யுள்பிறப்பின் என்னாம் பெயர்பொறிக்கும்","பேராண்மை இல்லாக் கடை."],"p":"Like the flowers on a rounded stalk, with hair like filaments that sweet cane bare, when the sweet fragrance they breathed is lost, what gain accrues from birth in a lofty noble house when energy that makes the name noteworthy, is wanting?","r":"As the cane flower on its thick stalk, with its mane of filaments, loses its honeyed scent, what is birth in a lofty family worth without the great manliness that engraves one's name?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:200":{"book":"naladiyar","num":200,"t":["பெருமுத் தரையர் பெரிதுவந் தீயும்","கருனைச்சோ றார்வர் கயவர் - கருனையைப்","பேரும் அறியார் நனிவிரும்பு தாளாண்மை","நீரும் அமிழ்தாய் விடும்."],"p":"The base feed full of rice and savoury food, that men, great lords of the triple lands, with generous gladness give; but water won with willing strenuous toil by those who know, not savoury food by name even, will turn to nectar. -------------","r":"The base fill themselves on rice and fried dishes that the great Mutharaiyar give with delight; but for those who do not even know the names of such dishes, water won by their own eager effort becomes nectar.","n":"Pope makes the Mutharaiyar 'lords of the triple lands', one of the readings Mudaliar reports. The verse brands even the guests of a generous patron as base for living on another's food.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The Mutharaiyar were best taken as a generous patron of the poet's own time.",""]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:201":{"book":"naladiyar","num":201,"t":["வயாவும் வருத்தமும் ஈன்றக்கால் நோவும்","கவாஅன் மகற்கண்டு தாய்மறந் தாஅங்கு","அசாஅத்தான் உற்ற வருத்தம் உசாஅத்தன்","கேளிரைக் காணக் கெடும்."],"p":"As a mother when she sees her son upon her lap forgets the languors, the pains, and the throes of birth; so trouble arising from weakness will die when a man sees his sympathizing kinsmen around him.","r":"As a mother forgets the cravings of pregnancy, the strain of carrying and the pain of birth when she sees her son on her lap, the trouble a person has suffered through weakness vanishes when he sees kin who ask after him.","n":"The kin do nothing but come and ask how he is (உசாவுதல்). The comfort the verse describes lies in being asked after, not in material help.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:202":{"book":"naladiyar","num":202,"t":["அழன்மண்டு போழ்தின் அடைந்தவர்கட் கெல்லாம்","நிழல்மரம்போல் நேரொப்பத் தாங்கிப் - பழுமரம்போல்","பல்லார் பயன்துய்ப்பத் தான்வருந்தி வாழ்வதே","நல்லாண் மகற்குக் கடன்."],"p":"To yield ready protection alike to all, as a tree affords shade to those that seek its shelter when the heat grows fierce; and to live toiling so that many may enjoy the gain, resembling thus a fruit producing tree, is the duty of the manly man.","r":"The duty of a good man is to shelter all who come to him alike, as a shade tree does in fierce heat, and to toil himself so that many enjoy what he yields, as a fruiting tree does.","n":"தான் வருந்தி makes the giver's own hardship part of the duty rather than a cost to avoid. Mudaliar takes பழுமரம் as the banyan and notes that others read it as a ripe fruiting tree, as Shanmuganar and Pope do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","naduvu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:203":{"book":"naladiyar","num":203,"t":["அடுக்கல் மலைநாட. தற்சேர்ந் தவரை","எடுக்கல மென்னார் பெரியோர் - அடுத்தடுத்து","வன்காய் பலபல காய்ப்பினும் இல்லையோ","தன்காய் பொறுக்கலாக் கொம்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where mountains piled on mountains rise! the great demur not to support their kith and kin ;—there is no bough but will support the fruit it bears, though clustered thick great fruits and many cling thereon.","r":"Lord of the land of piled-up hills, the great never say 'we will not support' those who have come to depend on them; is there any branch that cannot bear its own fruit, however many and heavy they hang?","n":"The image makes dependants the supporter's own fruit, so bearing them is part of his nature rather than an act of charity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","perumai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:204":{"book":"naladiyar","num":204,"t":["உலகறியத் தீரக் கலப்பினும் நில்லா","சிலபகலாம் சிற்றினத்தார் கேண்மை - நிலைதிரியா","நிற்கும் பெரியோர் நெறியடைய நின்றனைத்தால்","ஒற்கமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though mingled in a complete intimacy so that all the world knows of it, the friendship of the little will last but little time. Connection with the firm unyielding men endures till the great one's path, who never swerve, is reached.","r":"The friendship of small-natured people does not last beyond a few days, even when they mingle so fully that the world knows it; the bond with those who never falter holds as steadily as the ascetics who keep to their path to the goal.","n":"Both commentators read the comparison as ascetics who never leave the discipline of renunciation, and Mudaliar names the goal as release (வீடு). Pope's translation makes the line say how long the bond lasts, which is less clear in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:205":{"book":"naladiyar","num":205,"t":["இன்னர் இனையர் எமர்பிறர் என்னும்சொல்","என்னும் இலராம் இயல்பினால் - துன்னித்","தொலைமக்கள் துன்பம்தீர்ப் பாரேயார் மாட்டும்","தலைமக்க ளாகற்பா லார்."],"p":"‘Such are they and so many;’ ‘these are ours; those strange :’ those worthy to be classed as chief of men say nothing like this ; so to speak is not their nature ; for they relieve the distress of ALL that troubled come to them!","r":"Those fit to be counted chief among men have no trace of the talk that sorts people as 'of this kind, so many, ours, others', and they draw near and relieve the distress of anyone who is perishing.","n":"In a chapter on embracing kin, this verse removes the boundary of kinship: for the best people, anyone in distress counts as their own. Mudaliar glosses எமர் and பிறர் as those we want and those we don't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","arul","perumai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:206":{"book":"naladiyar","num":206,"t":["பொற்கலத்துப் பெய்த புலியுகிர் வான்புழுக்கல்","அக்காரம் பாலோ டமரார்கைத் துண்டலின்","உப்பிலிப் புற்கை உயிர்போல் கிளைஞர்மாட்டு","எக்காலத் தானு மினிது."],"p":"More sweet than rice, though white as tiger's claw with milk and sugar served on plate of gold, by loveless hands, is any tasteless mess, in any dish, when shared with kindred dear as life.","r":"Saltless millet gruel in any vessel, taken among kin as dear as life, is always sweeter than rice white as a tiger's claw, served with sugar and milk in a golden dish from the hands of those who are not one's own.","n":"The contrast is total on every count, the vessel, the grain, the salt and the giver, and only the giver decides the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:207":{"book":"naladiyar","num":207,"t":["நாள்வாய்ப் பெறினும்தந் நள்ளாதா ரில்லத்து","வேளாண்மை வெங்கருனை வேம்பாகும் - கேளாய்,","அபராணப் போழ்தின் அடகிடுவ ரேனும்","தமராயார் மாட்டே இனிது."],"p":"Most bitter (Margosa) is the bounteous meal of dainty food at early dawn in house of those who love us not. Hear thou! though not till evening given, the mess of herbs when eaten with our own is sweet.","r":"A hot fried feast given as hospitality at the proper hour in the house of those not close to one is bitter as neem; greens served at an untimely afternoon hour are sweet when they come from one's own people.","n":"Even the hour is reversed: the stranger's meal is punctual and the kin's is late, and the kin's is still the sweeter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:208":{"book":"naladiyar","num":208,"t":["முட்டிகை போல முனியாது வைகலும்","கொட்டியுண் பாரும் குறடுபோல் கைவிடுவர்","சுட்டுக்கோல் போல எரியும் புகுவரே","நட்டார் எனப்படு வார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who pound at you every day like a hammer, eating off you, let go like tongs when the fire comes; those rightly called intimates enter the fire with you like the smith's poker.","n":"The three smithy tools sort people by how they behave in the heat. Mudaliar offers two readings of முனியாது, 'without tiring' or 'showing affection', and of கொட்டி, 'striking' or 'pestering'. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those who enter the fire are loving kin.",""],["shanmuganar","Those who enter the fire are true friends.",""]],"ch":"n:21"},"n:209":{"book":"naladiyar","num":209,"t":["நறுமலர்த் தண்கோதாய் நட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்","மறுமையும் செய்வதொன் றுண்டோ - இறுமளவும்","இன்புறுவ இன்புற் றெழீஇ அவரோடு","துன்புறுவ துன்புறாக் கால்."],"p":"O maid adorned with fresh garlands of fragrant flowers! is there one thing that even in other world, friends may perform for friends, if till they die, their joys they share, but shun to share their griefs ?","r":"Maiden with the cool garland of fragrant flowers, if friends share their friends' joys until death but not their sorrows, is there anything they could do for them even in the next world?","n":"The claim is negative: sharing only joys makes no bond at all. Mudaliar again glosses நட்டார் as kin, where Shanmuganar has friends. The verse is addressed to a woman, which Mudaliar notes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:210":{"book":"naladiyar","num":210,"t":["விருப்பிலார் இல்லத்து வேறிருந் துண்ணும்","வெருக்குக்கண் வெங்கருனை வேம்பாம் - விருப்புடைத்","தன்போல்வா ரில்லுள் தயங்குநீர்த் தண்புற்கை","என்போ டியைந்த அமிழ்து."],"p":"The savoury fried curry, (in colour) like a cat's eye, which one eats seated a part in the house of those who are without affection, will be bitter as margosa; but cold gruel (weak and insipid), like clear water, in the house of affectionate equals, is ambrosia that cleaves to the bones. --------------","r":"A hot fried dish the colour of a cat's eye, eaten sitting apart in the house of those without affection, is bitter as neem; watery cold gruel in the house of loving equals is ambrosia that joins with one's bones.","n":"வேறிருந்து, sitting apart, names the slight: the food is served but the guest is kept separate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","undi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:211":{"book":"naladiyar","num":211,"t":["கருத்துணர்ந்து கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மையெஞ் ஞான்றுங்","குருத்தின் கரும்புதின் ற்றறே - குருத்திற்கு","எதிர்செலத்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ, என்றும்","மதுர மிலாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Intimacy with those who understand the real intention (of one's words), and who have acquired wisdom by learning, will at all times be like eating sweet cane from the tender shoots; but attachment to those who have no sweetness of disposition is like eating it in a direction opposite to the tender shoot (it grows harder and less sweet).","r":"Friendship with those who have learned with understanding is always like eating sugarcane from the tip down, sweeter as one goes; the bond with those who lack sweetness is like eating it from the root toward the tip.","n":"The quatrain nearly repeats 138 in the chapter on learning, with a different refrain. Mudaliar explains that eating from the tip makes the cane grow sweeter as one goes, and from the root less sweet.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sweetness the second group lacks is the sweetness of learning.",""],["shanmuganar","The sweetness they lack is a sweet disposition, as Pope also has it.",""]],"ch":"n:22"},"n:212":{"book":"naladiyar","num":212,"t":["இற்பிறப் பெண்ணி இடைதிரியா ரென்பதோர்","நற்புடை கொண்டமை யல்லது - பொற்கேழ்","புனலொழுகப் புள்ளிரியும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனமறியப் பட்டதொன் றன்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of flowery hills, where wild-fowl golden in hue fly, scared by the rush of the waterfall. Regarding the mobility of their birth—these will not swerve:—To say thus is a good ground of confidence—(a good position to take up); but to say ‘their minds are known,' is not any (i. e. real ground).","r":"Lord of the flowering hills, where birds scatter as golden water pours down, one may befriend people on the good ground that, mindful of their family, they will not change midway; apart from that, a person's mind is not something that can be known.","n":"The verse doesn't praise high birth for its own sake. It falls back on lineage because the mind can't be read. Mudaliar draws the rule: befriend those of high family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","aaraaydhal","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:213":{"book":"naladiyar","num":213,"t":["யானை யனைவர் நண்பெரிணஇ நாயனையார்","கேண்மை கெழீஇக் கொளல்வேண்டும், - யானை","அறிந்தறிந்தும் பாகனையே கொல்லும், எறிந்தவேல்","மெய்யதா வால்குழைக்கும் நாய்."],"p":"Forsaking friendship with those who resemble the elephant, embrace and hold fast intimacy with those who are like the dog; for the elephant will slay even its keeper though it has long known him ; but the dog will wag its tail when it has in its body the javelin (hurled at it by its angry master).","r":"Give up the friendship of those who are like elephants and hold close those who are like dogs: the elephant may kill even its keeper after knowing him for years, while the dog wags its tail with its master's spear in its body.","n":"The test is loyalty under injury, not strength or rank: the elephant is the nobler animal and still loses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:214":{"book":"naladiyar","num":214,"t":["பலநாளும் பக்கத்தா ராயினும் நெஞ்சில்","சிலநாளும் ஒட்டாரோ டொட்டார் - பலநாளும்","நீத்தா ரெனக்கை விடலுண்டோ , தந்நெஞ்சத்","தியாத்தாரோ டியாத்த தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though men dwell side by side for many days when their souls cleave not (are not congenial) for even a few days they retain not their friendship. But is it possible to let go attachment's well-knit ties, though those to whom one's soul is knit dwell many days afar?","r":"Those who are not joined in heart for even a few days do not become close, though they stay beside one for many days; and would anyone let go of the bond with those tied to their heart because they have been away for many days?","n":"Nearness and distance are both set aside: the heart's tie alone makes and keeps a friend. Shanmuganar supplies 'the great' as the subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:215":{"book":"naladiyar","num":215,"t":["கோட்டுப்பூப் போல மலர்ந்துபிற் கூம்பாது","வேட்டதே வேட்டதாம் நட்பாட்சி - தோட்ட","கயப்பூப்போல் முன்மலர்ந்து பிற்கூம்பு வாரை","நயப்பாரும் நட்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"The use and wont of friendship is that, what once it has loved it loves always,— like the flower on the tree-branch which having once unfolded afterwards closes not; but who will esteem, or make friends of those who are like flowers on the surface of the excavated tank, which unfold and afterwards close themselves?","r":"The true conduct of friendship is to go on loving what it first loved, like a tree's flower that opens and doesn't close again; no one esteems or befriends those who open first and close later, like the flowers of a dug pond.","n":"Pond flowers such as the lotus close each evening, so the fault condemned is changeability, not coldness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:216":{"book":"naladiyar","num":216,"t":["கடையாயார் நட்பிற் கமுகனையர் ஏனை","இடையாயார் தெங்கி னனையர் - தலையாயார்","எண்ணரும் பெண்ணைபோன் றிட்டஞான் றிட்டதே,","தொன்மை யுடையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"The lowest sort of men in friendship are like the Arecanut tree. The middle sort are like the cocoa-nut tree. Attachments to the chief of men — who are old friends —is like the Palmyra tree of rare worth : what was given that first day was given once for all.","r":"In friendship the lowest are like the areca palm and the middle like the coconut; the bond of the highest, who keep old ties, is like the palmyra of untold worth: what was given on the first day stays given.","n":"Mudaliar explains the trees: the areca yields only with constant tending, the coconut with occasional tending, and the palmyra gives richly after a single watering. On that reading the grades measure how much a friend has to be given before the friendship yields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pazhaimai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:217":{"book":"naladiyar","num":217,"t":["கழுநீருள் காரட கேனும் ஒருவன்","விழுமிதாக் கொள்ளின் அமிழ்தாம் - விழுமிய","குய்த்துவையார் வெண்சோறே யாயினும் மேவாதார்","கைத்துண்டல் காஞ்சிரங் காய்."],"p":"If one receive you courteously, though what he gives is but rank herbs dressed in water in which rice had been washed, it is ambrosia. To eat from the hands of those who love us not, though it be white rice with rich spicy condiments, is nux vomica.","r":"Even dark greens cooked in rice-water are ambrosia if someone gives them with high regard; white rice with fine seasonings and relishes, taken from the hand of those not in accord with one, is the bitter strychnos fruit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","natpu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:218":{"book":"naladiyar","num":218,"t":["நாய்க்கால் சிறுவிரல்போல் நன்கணிய ராயினும்","ஈக்கால் துணையும் உதவாதார் நட்பென்னாம்?","சேய்த்தானும் சென்று கொளல்வேண்டும், செய்விளைக்கும்","வாய்க்கால் அனையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Of what value is the friendship of those who being very near like the little claw on a dog's leg, afford not help as much as a fly's foot? Though you go far to seek it, lay hold of the friendship of those who are like the water channel that causes the crops in the field to flourish.","r":"What use is the friendship of those as close as the little toe on a dog's foot who give not even a fly's foot of help? Go even far to gain the bond of those who are like the channel that makes the field yield.","n":"Nearness is set against usefulness, and the channel image implies that real help often comes from a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","velanmai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:219":{"book":"naladiyar","num":219,"t":["தெளிவிலார் நட்பின் பகைநன்று சாதல்","விளியா அருநோயின் நன்றால் - அளிய","இகழ்தலின் கோறல் இனிதேமற் றில்ல","புகழ்தலின் வைதலே நன்று."],"p":"Better hate than friendship of the ignorant. Better death than disease which comes on yielding to no remedy. Sweeter is killing thin contempt that breaks the spirit. Better abuse than undeserved praise.","r":"Enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding, death better than an incurable disease, killing kinder than contempt that wounds, and abuse better than praise of qualities one doesn't have.","n":"Only the first of the four comparisons concerns friendship; the other three give it company. Mudaliar's gloss of கோறல் is printed as 'சொல்லுதல்' (speaking), probably an error for கொல்லுதல் (killing), which Shanmuganar and Pope have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","pagai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:220":{"book":"naladiyar","num":220,"t":["மாணஇப் பலரோடு பன்னாள் முயங்கிப்","பெரிணஇப் பொருட்டக்கார்க் கோடலே வேண்டும்","பாணஇ உயிர்செகுக்கும் பாம்பொடும் இன்னா","மாணஇஇப் பின்னைப் பிரிவு."],"p":"-----------","r":"Mix with many over many days, find who matches you, and take only those worth valuing; for it is painful to part after becoming close, even with a snake whose quick bite kills.","n":"The argument rests on the cost of parting: because breaking a bond hurts even when the friend was deadly, the testing must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:221":{"book":"naladiyar","num":221,"t":["நல்லா ரெனத்தாம் நனிவிரும்பிக் கொண்டாரை","அல்லா ரெனினும் அடக்கிக் கொளல்வேண்டும்.","நெல்லுக் குமியுண்டு, நீர்க்கு நுரைஉண்டு","புல்லிதழ் பூவிற்கும் உண்டு."],"p":"When those to whom with strong desire we clung as good, prove otherwise, keep the sad secret bid, - cling to them still! The growing grain has husks; the water has its foam: flowers too have scentless outer sheath of leaves.","r":"Those one eagerly took as good should be kept close, with their faults held within, even if they prove otherwise; rice has its husk, water its foam, and flowers their rough outer petals.","n":"அடக்கிக் கொளல் can mean holding the friend close or keeping the fault to oneself. Shanmuganar takes the second, and Pope's version has both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:222":{"book":"naladiyar","num":222,"t":["செறுத்தோ றுடைப்பினும் செம்புனலோ டுடார்,","மறுத்தும் சிறைசெய்வர் நீர்நசைஇ வாழ்நர்","வெறுப்ப வெறுப்பச் செயினும் பொறுப்பரே","தாம்வேண்டிக் கொண்டார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"If though they dam it up, the fresh flood should burst its bonds, men do not feel aggrieved; but straightway imprison it again, for by the precious stream they live: so though their friends again and again do very disagreeable things, men bear with those whose friendship is dear.","r":"Those who live by water don't rage at the fresh flood when it breaks the dam each time they build it, but dam it again; in the same way people bear with those whose bond they sought, even when they do one hateful thing after another.","n":"The friend is needed the way the farmer needs the stream, so here forbearance rests on dependence as much as on goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:223":{"book":"naladiyar","num":223,"t":["இறப்பவே தீய செயினுந்தந் நட்டார்","பொறுத்தல் தகுவதொன் றன்றோ - நிறக்கோங்கு","உருவவண் டார்க்கு முயர்வரை நாட","ஒருவர் பொறையிருவர் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hilly land, where the bees hum through all the flowery Gongu-glade ! — Though friends should work us surpassing ills, the only thing that is meet is forbearance: patience of one is friendship of the twain.","r":"Lord of the high hills where bees hum over the bright kongu flowers, isn't it right to bear with one's friends even when they do great wrong? The patience of one is the friendship of two.","n":"ஒருவர் பொறை இருவர் நட்பு is the maxim: a friendship survives if either party absorbs the injury, so it doesn't need both to be faultless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:224":{"book":"naladiyar","num":224,"t":["மடிதிரை தந்திட்ட வான்கதிர் முத்தம்","கடுவசை நாவாய் கரையலைக்குஞ் சேர்ப்ப","விடுதற் கா஢யா ரியல்பிலரேல் நெஞ்சம்","சுடுதற்கு மூட்டிய தீ."],"p":"Lord of the shore where pearls of purest lustre are thrown up by circling waves, and where swift darting boats are borne through the surf !—when friends who we may not leave have alien hearts, it is as a scorching fire enkindled in the soul.","r":"Lord of the shore where folding waves cast up bright pearls and swift boats rock against the beach, if those one cannot let go lack good nature, they are a fire lit to burn one's heart.","n":"The verse doesn't say to leave them. It names the pain of a bond one cannot leave. Pope's title, about being angry with those we love, is not in the Tamil. Neither commentary is aligned to this quatrain, though Shanmuganar's comment on it appears under 223.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:225":{"book":"naladiyar","num":225,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடற்பால ரல்லாரைப்","பொன்னாகப் போற்றிக் கொளல்வேண்டும் - பொன்னொடு","நல்லிற் சிதைத்ததீ நாடோ றும் நாடித்தம்","இல்லத்தில் ஆக்குத லால்."],"p":"Though those, from whom you may not part, do grievous things, O maid who art as Lakshmi! you must still cherish your chosen friends—fire destroys men's wealth and happy homes, yet is it sought there and kindled every day.","r":"Those one ought not to part from must be cherished like gold even when they do harm, since the fire that once destroyed a good house along with its gold is still sought out and kindled at home every day.","n":"Fire both destroys and is needed daily, so the friend is cherished because life needs them, not because the harm is forgotten. Pope takes பொன்னாக as an address to a maiden 'as Lakshmi'; Shanmuganar reads it as 'like gold'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:226":{"book":"naladiyar","num":226,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடுதற் கா஢யாரைத்","துன்னாத் துறத்தல் தகுவதோ - துன்னருஞ்சீர்","விண்குத்து நீள்வரை வெற்ப. களைபவோ","கண்குத்திற் றென்றுதங் கை."],"p":"When those from whom it is hard to part do evil things should men at once renounce them?—Lord of the lengthening hills that pierce the sky, whence rarest giftsdescend !—Do men cut off their hand because it pricked their eye?","r":"Lord of the sky-piercing hills of rare excellence, is it right to cast off those hard to part from, though they hurt you? Does anyone cut off their own hand because it poked their eye?","n":"The image makes the friend part of one's own body, so cutting them off harms oneself again and undoes nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:227":{"book":"naladiyar","num":227,"t":["இலங்குநீர்த் தண்சேர்ப்ப. இன்னா செயினும்","கலந்து பழிகாணார் சான்றோர் - கலந்தபின்","தீமை எடுத்துரைக்கும் திண்ணறி வில்லாதார்","தாமும் அவரிற் கடை."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the shining sea! The perfect ones when they have contracted an intimacy with any, see no faults in them, even if they do things that cause pain. Those who, being without stable wisdom, take up and tell out men's evil deeds after contracting an intimacy with them, are themselves worse than they.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of shining waters, once the worthy have joined with someone they see no fault in them even when hurt; those without firm understanding who bring out another's wrongs after joining with him are themselves lower than he is.","n":"The verse ranks exposing a friend's wrong as worse than the wrong itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","saandror","purangooral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:228":{"book":"naladiyar","num":228,"t":["ஏதிலார் செய்த திறப்பவே தீதெனினும்","நோதக்க தென்னுண்டாம் நோக்குங்கால் - காதல்","கழுமியார் செய்த கறங்கருவி நாட","விழுமிதாம் நெஞ்சத்துள் நின்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of resounding waterfalls ! Though what those alien to us have done may be surpassingly evil, what is there to be pained at, when you regard it? Things done by affectionate friends, will be excellent when so regarded by the mind.","r":"Lord of the land of roaring falls, what is there to grieve over, on reflection, when strangers do great wrong? And what those bound by love have done becomes good when held within the heart.","n":"The same harm is treated by who did it: from a stranger it is to be shrugged off, from one who loves it is to be turned into something good within.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:229":{"book":"naladiyar","num":229,"t":["தமரென்று தாங்கொள்ளப் பட்டவர் தம்மைத்","தமரன்மை தாமறிந்தா ராயின், - அவரைத்","தமா஢னும் நன்கு மதித்துத் தமரன்மை","தம்முள் அடக்கிக் கொளல்."],"p":"If we perceive those we have accepted as our own to be not really ours, we must pay even more respect to them than to our own, and keep concealed in our own mind the fact that they are not really ours.","r":"If you find that those you took as your own are not, honour them even more than your own and keep the knowledge that they are not yours shut within you.","n":"Pope's title says to love them the more; the verse says மதித்து, to value or honour.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One is to honour them more than one's real friends, with no suggestion of pretence.",""],["shanmuganar","The honour is an outward show while the truth is kept inside.",""]],"ch":"n:23"},"n:230":{"book":"naladiyar","num":230,"t":["குற்றமும் ஏனைக் குணமும் ஒருவனை","நட்டபின் நாடித் திரிவேனேல் - நட்டான்","மறைகாவா விட்டவன் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","அறைகடல்சூழ் வையம் நக."],"p":"--------------","r":"If, after taking someone as my friend, I go about searching out his faults and his other qualities, may I go where the man who did not guard his friend's secret goes, while the world encircled by the roaring sea laughs.","n":"A curse spoken in the first person. Mudaliar names the destination as hell. Examining a friend belongs before the friendship (chapter 22); after it, examining is treated as betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","aaraaydhal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:231":{"book":"naladiyar","num":231,"t":["செறிப்பில் பழங்கூரை சேறணை யாக","இறைத்துநீர் ஏற்றும் கிடப்பர், - கறைக்குன்றம்","பொங்கருவி தாழும் புனல்வரை நன்னாட","தங்கரும் முற்றுந் துணை."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant well-watered mountain land, where boiling waterfalls pour down from the dark hills! (Poor men) linger beneath the old roof that affords no shelter, baling out the water, and making mud embankments, and enduring the down-pour; (even so unreal friends stick by you) till their purpose has been attained.","r":"Lord of the well-watered hills where waterfalls pour from dark peaks, like people who stay under an old leaking roof, making mud dams and bailing out and catching the water, some stay by you only until their own business is done.","n":"The simile is of discomfort put up with for one's own sake: they stay as one stays under a bad roof, because they need it for now. Shanmuganar adds that they pretend to share one's troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:232":{"book":"naladiyar","num":232,"t":["சீரியார் கேண்மை சிறந்த சிறப்பிற்றாய்","மாரிபோல் மாண்ட பயத்ததாம் - மாரி","வறந்தக்கால் போலுமே வாலருவி நாட","சிறந்தக்கால் சீரிலார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of pure (white, forming) waterfalls! The friendship of the virtuous is of exceeding excellence, and yields glorious results — like (seasonable) rain; but the friendship of the vicious, even in the time of its exuberance, is as when the rain fails in the time of drought.","r":"Lord of the land of white waterfalls, the friendship of the excellent is of surpassing worth and fruitful like rain; that of the worthless, even at its height, is like rain that has failed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:233":{"book":"naladiyar","num":233,"t":["நுண்ணுணர்வி னாரொடு கூடி நுகர்வுடைமை","விண்ணுலகே யொக்கும் விழைவிற்றால் - நுண்ணு஡ல்","உணர்வில ராகிய ஊதியம் இல்லார்ப்","புணர்தல் நிரயத்துள் ஒன்று."],"p":"Enjoyment of the society of men of refinement resembles the heavenly world in the pleasure it affords. Closest contact with those destitute of fine perception of the value of learned pursuits —men who gain no wisdom from you, nor you from them—is one of the hells.","r":"Enjoying the company of people of fine understanding is as desirable as the heavenly world; being joined to profitless people without understanding of subtle learning is one of the hells.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","kalvi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:234":{"book":"naladiyar","num":234,"t":["பெருகுவது போலத் தோன்றிவைத் தீப்போல்","ஒருபொழுதுஞ் செல்லாதே நந்தும் - அருகெல்லாம்","சந்தன நீள்சோலைச் சாரன் மலைநாட","பந்தமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where wide groves of Sandal cover the hilly slopes! Friendship with those who feel not its real obligation, like fire in the straw (suddenly) appears, seeming as though it would increase, but never advancing dies out.","r":"Lord of the hills whose slopes are lined with long sandal groves, the bond with those who have no real tie seems to grow, then, like fire in straw, dies without lasting even a moment.","n":"Mudaliar glosses பந்தம் as the bond of love; Shanmuganar as true attachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:235":{"book":"naladiyar","num":235,"t":["செய்யாத செய்துநாம் என்றலும் செய்வதனைச்","செய்யாது தாழ்த்துக்கொண் டோ ட்டலும் - மெய்யாக","இன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி இகழ்ந்தார்க்கும் அந்நிலையே","துன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி தரும்."],"p":"The promising to do what cannot be done, and the putting off and leaving undone through delay things that could be done, will forthwith bring experience of sorrow even to those who have condemned truthfully the pleasant experiences of life (ascetics and saints).","r":"Saying 'we will do it' of what cannot be done, and putting off what can be done, bring suffering at once even to those who have truly scorned the enjoyment of pleasure.","n":"'Even to ascetics' means these faults hurt whatever one's indifference to pleasure. The verse doesn't mention friends; only its place in this chapter suggests these are marks of an unfit friend.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","madi","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:236":{"book":"naladiyar","num":236,"t":["ஒருநீர்ப் பிறந்தொருங்கு நீண்டக் கடைத்தும்","விரிநீர்க் குவளையை ஆம்பலொக் கல்லா","பெருநீரார் கேண்மை கொளினுநீர் அல்லார்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The Ambal (water-lily) does not equalthe expanding Kuvalai though born and growing together with it in the same pool : though they attain to intimacy with those of generous instincts, the deeds of men in whom these instincts are lacking will be diverse.","r":"Though born in the same pool and growing up together, the water-lily never equals the spreading kuvalai; so though they gain the friendship of the large-natured, the deeds of those who lack that nature remain different.","n":"Pope's title speaks of a lotus; the Tamil compares the ஆம்பல் with the குவளை, the blue water-lily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:237":{"book":"naladiyar","num":237,"t":["முற்றற் சிறுமந்தி முற்பட்ட தந்தையை","நெற்றுக்கண் டன்ன விரலான் ஞெமிர்த்திட்டுக்","குற்றிப் பறிக்கும் மலைநாட இன்னாதே","ஒற்றுமை கொள்ளாதார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the hilly land where the immature little monkey, with its finger like a bean-pod, will flip its father when it meets him, and poke him and snatch fruit from him! Afflictive indeed is friendship with the uncongenial.","r":"Lord of the hills where the unripe little monkey, with fingers like dry bean-pods, pokes and prises at the father who meets it to snatch his food, the friendship of those not of one mind with you is painful.","n":"The image is of closeness without regard: the young monkey takes from the one nearest to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:238":{"book":"naladiyar","num":238,"t":["முட்டுற்ற போழ்தின் முடுகியென் னாருயிரை","நட்டா னொருவன்கை நீட்டேனேல் - நட்டான்","கடிமனை கட்டழித்தான் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","நெடுமொழி வையம் நக."],"p":"If I hasten not to put forth my hand and offer my precious life to my friend when in distress, may I depart whither he goes who has violated the sanctity of his friend's wedded wife, while the far-famed world laughs!","r":"If, when my friend is in distress, I do not hurry to put my precious life in his hands, may I go where the man who violated his friend's guarded wife goes, while the world of lasting words laughs.","n":"The chapter on unfit friendship includes this vow of what a fit friend owes. Mudaliar names the destination as hell and gives three readings of நெடுமொழி வையம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saakkaadu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:239":{"book":"naladiyar","num":239,"t":["ஆன்படு நெய்பெய் கலனுள் அதுகளைந்து","வேம்படு நெய்பெய் தனைத்தரோ - தேம்படு","நல்வரை நாட நயமுணர்வார் நண்பெரிணஇப்","புல்லறிவி னாரொடு நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills where honey flows! To forsake the friendship of those who know the right, and cultivate that of shallow pretenders to knowledge, is like emptying out cow's ghee from a vessel and pouring into it margosa oil.","r":"Lord of the good hills where honey flows, leaving the friendship of those who know what is good to befriend the petty-minded is like emptying cow's ghee from a vessel and filling it with neem oil.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:240":{"book":"naladiyar","num":240,"t":["உருவிற் கமைந்தான்கண் ஊராண்மை யின்மை","பருகற் கமைந்தபால் நீரளா யற்றே","தெரிவுடையார் தீயினத்தா ராகுதல் நாகம்","விரிபெடையோ டாடிவிட் டற்று."],"p":"The absence of generosity in those whose exterior is pleasing, is like the mingling of water with the milk provided for food: when men of understanding take to bad company it is like the disporting of a Cobra with a female viper. -------------","r":"One fitted for good form who lacks generosity toward his town is like milk fit to drink mixed with water; the discerning taking up with bad company is like a cobra mating with a female viper.","n":"Mudaliar records the belief that a cobra mating with a viper dies, so the image predicts ruin.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velanmai","inam","arivu","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உருவிற்கு அமைந்தான் is first one with some qualities fit for greatness; Mudaliar gives good looks only as another view.",""],["shanmuganar","It is one handsome in form, as Pope also has it.",""]],"ch":"n:24"},"n:241":{"book":"naladiyar","num":241,"t":["பகைவர் பணிவிடம் நோக்கித் தகவுடையார்","தாமேயும் நாணித் தலைச்செல்லார் காணாய்","இளம்பிறை யாயக்கால் திங்களைச் சேராது","அணங்கருந் துப்பின் அரா."],"p":"Worthy men, when they behold where foes are foiled, themselves too feel sore abashed, and do not hasten on to crush them. Behold, the strong invulnerable dragon draws not near the moon (to swallow it) when it is in its tender crescent days!","r":"When the worthy see their enemies humbled, they themselves feel shame and do not advance on them; see, the snake whose strength nothing can trouble does not seize the moon while it is a young crescent.","n":"The shame belongs to the victor, not the defeated. Shanmuganar names the snake as Rahu, the eclipse serpent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:242":{"book":"naladiyar","num":242,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப நல்கூர்ந்த மக்கட்கு","அணிகல மாவ தடக்கம் - பணிவில்சீர்","மாத்திரை யின்றி நடக்குமேல் வாமுர்","கோத்திரம் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the spreading sea ! To men in poverty a modest self-restraint is the chiefest ornament. If a man live in unbending pride, and in a manner unbefitting his position, his fellow-townsmen will revile his race.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the vast sea, for the poor, restraint is the ornament; if one lives without humility and beyond measure, the town one lives in will speak ill of one's family.","n":"The cost of a poor man's lack of restraint falls on his lineage, not only on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inmai","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:243":{"book":"naladiyar","num":243,"t":["எந்நிலத்து வித்திடினும் காஞ்சிரங்காய் தெங்காகா","தென்னாட் டவருஞ் சுவர்க்கம் புகுதலால்","தன்னாற்றா னாகும் மறுமை வடதிசையும்","கொன்னாளர் சாலப் பலர்."],"p":"Whatever soil you sow it in, the Strychnos nut grows not into a cocoa-palm. Some of the Southern land have entered Paradise! It is man's way of life that decides his future state. Full many from the Northern land are denizens of hell.","r":"In whatever soil it is sown, a strychnos seed will not become a coconut; since even those of the south enter heaven, one's next world comes from oneself, and in the north too there are very many idle wasters.","n":"Pope calls the northerners 'denizens of hell'; கொன்னாளர் means those who spend their days to no purpose, which is milder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ozhukkam","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The south is the Tamil land, and the verse rebuts a northern claim that those born there cannot reach a good state.",""],["shanmuganar","The south is Yama's quarter, the direction of death, rather than a region of people.",""]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:244":{"book":"naladiyar","num":244,"t":["வேம்பின் இலையுள் கனியினும் வாழைதன்","தீஞ்சுவை யாதும் திரியாதாம் ஆங்கே","இனந்தீ தெனினும் இயல்புடையார் கேண்மை","மனந்தீதாம் பக்கம் அரிது."],"p":"Though ripened amid margosa leaves the fruit of the plantain loses no atom of its sweet flavour. Even so the friendship of men of noble mood, although their race be evil, can hardly work ill to the mind.","r":"Though ripened among neem leaves, the plantain keeps its sweet taste unchanged; so, though their company is bad, the friendship of those of good nature rarely turns to a bad mind.","n":"Pope reads இனம் as 'race'; both commentators read it as the company one keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:245":{"book":"naladiyar","num":245,"t":["கடல்சார்ந்தும் இன்னீர் பிறக்கும், மலைசார்ந்தும்","உப்பீண் டுவரி பிறத்தலால் தத்தம்","இனத்தனையர் அல்லர் எறிகடற்றண் சேர்ப்ப","மனத்தனையர் மக்களென் பார்."],"p":"Though close by the sea, sweet waters oft-times spring up there; on the hill-side the waters often gush out all brine! Thus men are not as their race. — Lord of the dashing sea's cool shore! Men are as their minds.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the dashing sea, since sweet water rises even beside the sea and brackish water even on the hills, people are not as their kind but as their minds.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","People are simply not like those of their own group; the mind decides.",""],["shanmuganar","People are judged not only by their group but by their minds as well, so group still counts.",""]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:246":{"book":"naladiyar","num":246,"t":["பராஅரைப் புன்னை படுகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஒராஅலும் ஒட்டலுஞ் செய்பவோ? - நல்ல","ம்ருஉச்செய் தியார்மாட்டும் தங்கு மனத்தார்","விராஅஅய்ச் செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the cool sea-shore, where flourishes the thick-stemmed laurel ! Men whose minds are good (constant), and who adhere to whomsoever they have formed an intimacy with, will not sometimes avoid men, and at other times be intimate. It is good not to have fits of alternating warmth and indifference.","r":"Lord of the cool shore where thick-trunked punnai trees grow, do people of steady mind, who stay with whomever they have formed a good bond, leave and then rejoin? It is good not to mix with people indiscriminately.","n":"Pope takes விராய் as alternating warmth and coldness; both commentators take it as mixing with people without examination.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:247":{"book":"naladiyar","num":247,"t":["உணர உணரும் உணர்வுடை யாரைப்","புணரப் புணருமாம் இன்பம் - புணா஢ன்","தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா தாரைப்","பிரியப் பிரியுமாம் நோய்."],"p":"Join the men who throughly feel true wisdom's inner sense, and forthwith joy joins you. Join yourself to men devoid of the accurate preception of knowledge, and then parting from them is parting from pain.","r":"Join those whose understanding grasps things as they should be grasped, and joy joins you; if you join those without discernment, the pain parts only when you part from them.","n":"The verse is built on repeated words (உணர உணரும், புணரப் புணரும், தெரியத் தெரியும், பிரியப் பிரியும்), a figure Mudaliar names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:248":{"book":"naladiyar","num":248,"t":["நன்னிலைக்கண் தன்னை நிறுப்பானும், தன்னை","நிலைகலக்கிக் கீழிடு வானும் - நிலையினும்","மேன்மே லுயர்த்து நிறுப்பானும், தன்னைத்","தலையாகச் செய்வானும் தான்."],"p":"He that establishes a man in good, and he that disturbs that good position and casts him down, and he that more and more exalts a man and establishes him, and he that makes a man head (among men) is (the MAN) HIMSELF.","r":"The one who sets a person in a good state, the one who unsettles and casts him down, the one who raises him ever higher, and the one who makes him the head of others, is that person himself.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause as one's understanding or lack of it; the verse itself names none. It also names no fate, which sits uneasily with chapter 11 on old deeds, unless those deeds are counted as one's own doing too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:249":{"book":"naladiyar","num":249,"t":["கரும வரிசையால் கல்லாதார் பின்னும்","பெருமை யுடையாரும் சேறல் - அருமரபின்","ஓதம் அரற்றும் ஒலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பேதைமை யன்ற தறிவு."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the sounding sea, where from old time the billows roar!— In the course of their affairs when even great men follow after the unlearned, this is not folly but wisdom.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea, when even the great follow behind the unlearned for the sake of a task, that is not folly but understanding.","n":"The verse allows status to be set aside for a purpose (கரும வரிசையால்); it doesn't say the great should follow the unlearned in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","perumai","kalvi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:250":{"book":"naladiyar","num":250,"t":["கருமமு முட்படாப் போகமும் துவ்வாத்","தருமமும் தக்கார்க்கே செய்யா - ஒருநிலையே","முட்டின்றி மூன்று முடியமேல் அஃதென்ப","பட்டினம் பெற்ற கலம்."],"p":"If a man has wrought all fitting works, enjoyed all seemly pleasures, done deeds of charity to worthy men: if he shall have accomplished all these three unchecked, in this one state, of him men will say: ‘that is a ship that has gained the haren' -------------","r":"If one does one's work, enjoys one's pleasures, and does virtuous giving to the worthy, and all three are completed without hindrance in a single life, people call it a ship that has reached the port.","n":"A full worldly life is praised here, in a work that mostly urges renunciation, through the image of a trading voyage brought home. Pope reads ஒருநிலையே as 'in this one state'; Shanmuganar as 'all together'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","inbam","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:251":{"book":"naladiyar","num":251,"t":["நுண்ணுணர் வின்மை வறுமை, அஃதுடைமை","பண்ணப் பணைத்த பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எண்ணுங்கால்","பெண்ணவாய் ஆணிழந்த பேடி யணியாளோ,","கண்ணவாத் தக்க கலம்."],"p":"The want of refined knowledge is poverty, its possession is very great and abundant wealth. When one considers, will not a sexless creature, more woman than man, adorn herself with the jewels that her eye desires ?","r":"Lack of fine understanding is poverty and having it is great wealth; when one thinks about it, a eunuch who longs to be a woman will still put on the jewels that catch every eye.","n":"The simile is left unexplained in the verse. Shanmuganar draws the conclusion that the wealth of the senseless is like the eunuch's jewels: an adornment on something that lacks the essential thing. Pope's heading puts it as 'mere externals are nothing'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:252":{"book":"naladiyar","num":252,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் பாடழிந்து","அல்ல லுழப்ப தறிதிரேல் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","நாவின் கிழத்தி யுறைதலால் சேராளே","பூவின் கிழத்தி புலந்து."],"p":"Men of vast and varied lore are seen in low estate, and suffer want. Would you know the reason? The anciently renowned ‘Lady of the tongue' abides with them). ‘The Lady of the flowers' is jealous, and draws not near !","r":"If you would know why men of wide learning lose standing and suffer want: the ancient Lady of the Tongue lives with them, so the Lady of the Flower keeps away in a sulk.","n":"A playful explanation (Mudaliar names the figure தற்குறிப்பேற்றம், an imputed cause), not a theology of fortune. Mudaliar offers two reasons for the quarrel: Lakshmi and Sarasvati are mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, or two women do not share one man in peace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:253":{"book":"naladiyar","num":253,"t":["கல்லென்று தந்தை கழற அதனையோர்","சொல்லென்று கொள்ளா திகழ்ந்தவன் - மெல்ல","எழுத்தோலை பல்லார்முன் நீட்ட விளியா","வழுக்கோலைக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"He who, when his father urgently bade him learn, did not take it as a serious matter, but contemned it; when, before many men, some one gently presents a written palm-leaf, will in anger fetch a stick to beat him as guilty of an insult.","r":"One who scorned his father's urging to learn, not taking it as worth heeding, will grab a stick in anger when someone quietly holds out a written leaf to him before many people.","n":"The anger is the unlearned man's shame turned outward: he cannot read the leaf in public. Mudaliar records other readings of the last line, one in which he laughs in contempt, one in which he makes a noise to slip away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:254":{"book":"naladiyar","num":254,"t":["கல்லாது நீண்ட ஒருவன் உலகத்து","நல்லறி வாள ரிடைப்புக்கு - மெல்ல","இருப்பினும் நாயிருந் தற்றே, இராஅது","உரைப்பினும் நாய்குரைத் தற்று."],"p":"When a man who has grown up without learning enters the society of the wise, if he sit still, it is as if a dog sat there; and if he rise to speak, it is as though a dog barked.","r":"When a man who grew up without learning enters among the wise, if he sits quietly it is as if a dog sat there, and if he speaks it is as if a dog barked.","n":"Silence does not save him: Mudaliar allows மெல்ல இருப்பினும் to mean 'even if he sits with restraint', which makes the verse deny the unlearned any good way to be present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","avai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:255":{"book":"naladiyar","num":255,"t":["புல்லாப்புன் கோட்டிப் புலவ ரிடைப்புக்குக்","கல்லாத சொல்லும் கடையெல்லாம் - கற்ற","கடாஅயினும் சான்றவர் சொல்லார் பொருண்மேல்","படாஅ விடுபாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"All the baser sort consorting with scholars of a heterodox and low school, will utter illiterate rubbish; but men replete with learning, though urgently asked, utter not the results of their learning, knowing that (the asker) would fail to apply their minds to the import of what was said.","r":"The low all go among the poets of petty, senseless assemblies and say things they never learned; but the worthy, even when asked, do not speak of what they have learned, knowing it would not be taken in.","n":"Pope calls the assembly 'heterodox'; neither commentator says so. Mudaliar has it as an assembly not joined to understanding, and Shanmuganar's poets are false poets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","saandror","sol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:256":{"book":"naladiyar","num":256,"t":["கற்றறிந்த நாவினார் சொல்லார்தம் சோர்வஞ்சி","மற்றைய ராவார் பகர்வர் பனையின்மேல்","வற்றிய ஓலை கலகலக்கும் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பச்சோலைக் கில்லை யொலி."],"p":"Men of learned tongues are silent, fearing some slip; others (ignorant men) will speak out; on the Palmyra tree the dried-up leaves make a loud rustling noise ; but evermore the green leaf gives forth no sound !","r":"The learned and eloquent hold back, afraid of a lapse, while others talk freely: on the palmyra the dry leaves rattle, but the green leaf never makes a sound.","n":"Mudaliar notes the images answer the statements in reverse order (green leaf to the learned, dry leaves to the rest). Silence here signals life and fullness, noise dryness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:257":{"book":"naladiyar","num":257,"t":["பன்றிக்கூழ்ப் பத்தரில் தேமா வடித்தற்றால்","நன்றறியா மாந்தர்க் கறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","குன்றின்மேற் கொட்டுந் தறிபோல் தலைதகர்ந்து","சென்றிசையா வாகுஞ் செவிக்கு."],"p":"","r":"Telling the way of virtue to people who do not know the good is like pressing sweet mango juice into a pig's trough; like a stake driven on a rock, it splits at the head and does not go into their ears.","n":"Two similes for two failures: the teaching is wasted (the trough) and it cannot penetrate (the stake on rock). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 256.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pedhaimai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:258":{"book":"naladiyar","num":258,"t":["பாலால் கழீஇப் பலநாள் உணக்கினும்","வாலிதாம் பக்கம் இருந்தைக் கிருந்தன்று","கோலால் கடாஅய்க் குறினும் புகலொல்லா","நோலா உடம்பிற் கறிவு."],"p":"Though you wash it with milk for many days and dry it, charcoal on no hypothesis becomes white ! So into the undisciplined body wisdom enters not, though you teach it, driving it in with a stick.","r":"Charcoal washed in milk and dried for many days never turns white, and understanding will not enter a body that has not earned it, though you drive it in with a stick.","n":"நோலா உடம்பு is the hinge. Both commentators take it as a body without merit from good deeds (Mudaliar: from a former birth, so without innate understanding); Pope reads 'undisciplined body', which moves the cause from past births to present discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:259":{"book":"naladiyar","num":259,"t":["பொழிந்தினிது நாறினும் பூமிசைதல் செல்லாது","இழிந்தவை காமுறூஉம் ஈப்போல், - இழிந்தவை","தாங்கலந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கென்னாகும் தக்கார்வாய்த்","தேன்கலந்த தேற்றச்சொல் தேர்வு."],"p":"To those whose minds are full of foul things, — like the fly which goes not to feed on the flower that pours forth sweetness and breathes perfume, but fixes its eager desire on ordure,—What clear comprehension can there be of the lucid words full of honied sweetness that issue from the mouths of the worthy?","r":"Like the fly that will not settle on a flower pouring honey and fragrance but craves filth, those whose minds are set on low things get nothing from the clear, honeyed words of the worthy.","n":"The fault is in desire, not capacity: the fly passes the flower by preference. Mudaliar notes that 'low things' is a polite way of naming filth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:260":{"book":"naladiyar","num":260,"t":["கற்றா ருரைக்கும் கசடறு நுண்கேள்வி","பற்றாது தன்னெஞ் சுதைத்தலால் - மற்றுமோர்","தன்போ லொருவன் முகநோக்கித் தானுமோர்","புன்கோட்டி கொள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"The base man does not apprehend the faultless words of accurate instruction which the learned utter. These pain his mind. He therefore looks in the face of some other one like himself (for encouragement), and convenes a wretched assembly of his own: i.e. He finds one like-minded, and the two set up a sect. -------------","r":"Because his heart kicks away the faultless, subtle teaching of the learned, the base man looks into the face of another like himself and sets up a petty assembly of his own.","n":"The rejection breeds a rival: two of a kind confirm each other. Pope reads it as founding a sect; Mudaliar's கோட்டி is the talk given there, Shanmuganar's a platform speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kelvi","avai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:261":{"book":"naladiyar","num":261,"t":["அருகல தாகிப் பலபழுத்தக் கண்ணும்","பொரிதாள் விளவினை வாவல் குறுகா","பெரிதணிய ராயினும் பீடிலார் செல்வம்","கருதும் கடப்பாட்ட தன்று."],"p":"The bat approaches not the Heronia, with its dry stem, though it be night at hand and bear abundant fruit; so although mean people are very close at hand, their wealth is not a thing that can be counted upon.","r":"Bats do not go to the wood-apple tree with its cracked trunk, though it stands near and bears much fruit; so the wealth of men without worth, however close they live, is not something anyone thinks of counting on.","n":"Nearness is the point: what is close but unapproachable is as good as far. Mudaliar specifies who does not count on it: the poor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:262":{"book":"naladiyar","num":262,"t":["அள்ளிக்கொள் வன்ன குறுமுகிழ வாயினும்","கள்ளிமேல் கைந்நீட்டார் சூடும்பூ அன்மையால்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் கீழ்களை","நள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"Men reach not out their band to the Kalli (Cactus), though it bears delicate round buds by the handful, because these are not flowers they can weave into a garland to crown themselves withal; so wise people from no friendships with the base however great their wealth may be.","r":"No one reaches for the cactus, though its small buds look lovely enough to gather by the handful, because they are not flowers to wear; so the wise do not seek out the base, however rich.","n":"The cactus buds are attractive but unusable; wealth is the attraction, character the use. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:263":{"book":"naladiyar","num":263,"t":["மல்கு திரைய கடற்கோட் டிருப்பினும்,","வல்லூற் றுவரில் கிணற்றின்கண் சென்றுண்பர்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் சேண்சென்றும்","நல்குவார் கட்டே நசை."],"p":"Though men live on the curved shore of the sea with its multitudinous waves, they go and drink at the well, with its perennial fountain of fresh water from the rock; so even if those (who are neighbours) are very wealthy, the desire (of the poor) is towards the liberal, though these may be far to seek.","r":"Though they live on the shore of the wave-filled sea, people go and drink at a well with a strong, sweet spring; so though the rich are near, desire turns to the givers, even far away.","n":"The sea is huge and useless to drink; the small well gives. Size of wealth does not draw anyone, only its being given. Mudaliar's commentary is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:264":{"book":"naladiyar","num":264,"t":["புணர்கடல்சூழ் வையத்துப் புண்ணியமோ வேறே","உணர்வ துடையா ரிருப்ப - உணர்விலா","வட்டும் வழுதுணையும் போல்வாரும் வாழ்வரே","பட்டும் துகிலும் உடுத்து."],"p":"In the world surrounded by the (all-)embracing sea, merit is quite an indifferent matter! Understanding ones are (poor); and even those of no understanding—who are like mere palm tree tufts and brinjals—live prosperously, clad in silks and rich garments.","r":"In this sea-girt world merit is a thing apart: while men of understanding are there, even those with no understanding, like the dice-piece and the brinjal, live clad in silk and fine cloth.","n":"புண்ணியம் (merit) is set apart from understanding: prosperity follows merit, not sense. Mudaliar works out the two images: the dice-piece that returns to the board after being cut down (the shameless who come back to the assembly) and the brinjal that fruits hidden (those who hide their wealth). Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:265":{"book":"naladiyar","num":265,"t":["நல்லார் நயவர் இருப்ப நயமிலாக்","கல்லார்க்கொன் றாகிய காரணம் - தொல்லை","வினைப்பய னல்லது வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன் றில்."],"p":"While pleasant folk and just abide (in poverty), you ponder why men unjust and ignorant have any joy. It is fruit of 'ancient deeds', thou whose long eyes are darts :—to thoughtful mind no other cause occurs.","r":"Long-eyed one, when the good and just remain poor, the only cause you will find on reflection for the unjust and unlearned having wealth is the fruit of old deeds.","n":"The verse answers the scandal of undeserved wealth with former deeds, and so denies that present wealth says anything about present worth. Mudaliar specifies good deeds of a former birth. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:266":{"book":"naladiyar","num":266,"t":["நாறாக் தகடேபோல் நன்மலர்மேற் பொற்பாவாய்","நீறாய் நிலத்து விளியரோ - வேறாய","புன்மக்கள் பக்கம் புகுவாய்நீ பொன்போலும்","நன்மக்கள் பக்கம் துறந்து."],"p":"O golden dame, that sittest like a scentless leaf on a beauteous flower! Die, and fall in ashes to the ground: thou enterest homes of worthless men of perverse mind, forsaking good men pure as gold.","r":"Golden lady seated on the fine flower like its scentless outer petal, may you die and turn to ash on the earth: you leave the good, who are like gold, and go to the side of worthless men.","n":"A curse on Lakshmi herself. Mudaliar explains the simile: as the outer petal lacks the flower's scent, the goddess who lives on the lotus has none of its softness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:267":{"book":"naladiyar","num":267,"t":["நயவார்கண் நல்குரவு நாணின்று கொல்லோ","பயவார்கண் செல்வம் பரம்பப் பயின்கொல்","வியவாய்காண் வேற்கண்ணாய் இவ்விரண்டும் ஆங்கே","நயவாது நிற்கு நிலை."],"p":"Has poverty, that bides with men of righteous souls, no shame? Does wealth to ungenerous men stick like glue?O thou of dart-like eye, with wonder see that thus, no just discernment made, these two abide !","r":"Does poverty feel no shame in staying with the good? Is the wealth of the useless a glue that holds fast? See with wonder, dart-eyed one, how these two stay just where they ought not.","n":"Poverty and wealth are made into agents with manners: one shameless, the other sticky. The verse states the wonder without explaining it; 265 gives the explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:268":{"book":"naladiyar","num":268,"t":["வலவைக ளல்லாதார் காலாறு சென்று","கலவைகள் உண்டு கழிப்பர் - வலவைகள்","காலாறும் செல்லார் கருனையால் துய்ப்பவே","மேலாறு பாய விருந்து."],"p":"Men who are not void of shame will travel forth on foot, and feed on scraps, —so pass their days ; the shameless ones make no journeys on foot, but feed on dainties at home, perspiring over the feast.","r":"Those who are not shameless walk long roads and live on mixed scraps, while the shameless never stir a foot and feast at home on fried dishes as rivers of ghee and milk flow over them.","n":"Shame is what keeps the decent poor; its absence is what lets the others thrive. Mudaliar takes the scraps as begged food and the feasters as eating alone behind closed doors without giving; Shanmuganar names the feasters' fault as dishonesty and deceit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","inmai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:269":{"book":"naladiyar","num":269,"t":["பொன்னிறச் செந்நெல் பொதியொடு பீள்வாட","மின்னொளிர் வானங் கடலுள்ளுங் கான்றுகுக்கும்","வெண்மை யுடையார் விழுச்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால்","வண்மையும் அன்ன தகைத்து."],"p":"While the red paddy's golden germ is parched within the ear and dies, the cloud gleaming with lightnings pours forth its treasures on the sea. When silly men gain ample wealth, even so are their liberal gifts bestowed !","r":"While the golden red paddy withers in the ear, the lightning-lit cloud pours its rain into the sea; the giving of fools who come into great wealth is of the same kind.","n":"The fault is not that fools never give but that they give where it is not needed. Mudaliar's 'also' (உம்மை) implies they otherwise do nothing good with wealth either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:270":{"book":"naladiyar","num":270,"t":["ஓதியும் ஓதார் உணர்விலார் ஓதாதும்","ஓதி யனையார் உணர்வுடையார் - தூய்தாக","நல்கூர்ந்தும் செல்வர் இரவாதார், செல்வரும்","நல்கூர்ந்தார் ஈயா ரெனின்."],"p":"Men void of understanding, though they learn, learn not! Men of understanding, though unlearned, are as men learned! They are rich, though utter paupers, who never beg; the rich are paupers if they bestow nothing ! -------------","r":"Those without understanding remain unlearned though they study, and those with understanding are as the learned though they have not studied; those who never beg are rich though poor, and the rich are poor if they do not give.","n":"Four reversals that make the inner quality, not the outward possession, decide the name. Mudaliar takes தூய்தாக as a pure mind, a condition on the poor who do not beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:271":{"book":"naladiyar","num":271,"t":["நட்டார்க்கும் நள்ளா தவர்க்கும் உளவரையால்","அட்டது பாத்துண்டல் அட்டுண்டல் - ஆட்டது","அடைத்திருந் துண்டொழுகும் ஆவதின் மாக்கட்கு","அடைக்குமாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு."],"p":"To eat your own meal, after sharing what you have cooked, to the extent of your ability, both with those who are friends and those who are not friends, is 'cooking and eating' (= is real house keeping). To the good-for-nothing human beings whose habit of life it is to shut themselves up and eat alone what they have cooked, the door of yonder world will be shut.","r":"To share what is cooked, as far as one has, with friends and with those who are not friends, and then eat, is what cooking and eating means; for the worthless who shut the door and eat inside, the door of the other world is shut.","n":"The punishment mirrors the fault: a shut door for a shut door. Mudaliar takes 'friends' as kin and 'not friends' as guests; Pope has 'friends and foes', which widens the duty further than either commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:272":{"book":"naladiyar","num":272,"t":["எத்துணை யானும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","சிற்றறஞ் செய்தார் தலைப்படுவர் - மற்றைப்","பெருஞ்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால் பின்னறிதும் என்பார்","அழிந்தார் பழிகடலத் துள்."],"p":"Whatever the measure be, those who do even lesser acts of charity to the measure of their power shall attain to excellence. But those who, when they have obtained great wealth. say 'we will be wise (and give) by and by,' are lost in a sea of guilt.","r":"Those who do small acts of virtue, however little, within their means, rise; those who say they will think about it after they gain great wealth are lost in a sea of blame.","n":"The verse reverses the usual excuse: it is the small giver who is great and the one waiting for great wealth who drowns. Mudaliar notes that 'are lost' is past tense for certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:273":{"book":"naladiyar","num":273,"t":["துய்த்துக் கழியான் துறவோர்க்கொன் றீகலான்","வைத்துக் கழியும் மடவோனை - வைத்த","பொருளும் அவனை நகுமே உலகத்து","அருளும் அவனை நகும்."],"p":"The senseless man who spends not time in enjoyment (of his wealth), and who gives nothing to pions devotees, but hoards and dies, —him his hoarded wealth derides; him all that is gracious in the world derides.","r":"The fool who neither enjoys his wealth nor gives anything to renunciants, but hoards it and dies, is laughed at by the very wealth he stored, and by the compassion in the world.","n":"The wealth itself is made to mock him: it outlives him and was never used. The gift named is to renunciants (துறவோர்), a pointer to the Jain setting. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:274":{"book":"naladiyar","num":274,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் துய்த்தலும் தேற்றா இடுக்குடை","உள்ளத்தான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் - இல்லத்து","உருவுடைக் கன்னியரைப் போலப் பருவத்தால்","ஏதிலான் துய்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"The great wealth obtained by the man of straitened soul, who knows not how either to give or to enjoy, shall be enjoyed by a stranger, in due season—like lovely virgins remaining unmarried in the dwelling.","r":"The great wealth of the tight-hearted man who knows neither how to give nor how to enjoy will in due season be enjoyed by a stranger, like a lovely daughter of the house.","n":"The daughter is raised in the house and goes to an outsider when she comes of age; the image makes the loss natural and certain, not a mishap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:275":{"book":"naladiyar","num":275,"t":["எறிநீர்ப் பெருங்கடல் எய்தி யிருந்தும்","அறுநீர்ச் சிறுகிணற் றூறல்பார்த் துண்பர்","மறுமை யறியாதா ராக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை."],"p":"Though they have got the mighty sea with its dashing waves (to drink from), men wait for the stream slowly issuing from the little well, often dry, and drink there; so the exceeding poverty of the virtuous is preferable to the wealth of those who know not of the world to come.","r":"Though they live by the great sea with its dashing waves, people wait at a small, nearly dry well for its trickle and drink; so the deep poverty of the worthy is better than the wealth of those who know nothing of the next world.","n":"The sea is plenty that cannot be used; the small well is little that can. Shanmuganar takes 'knowing the next world' as knowing and doing the good deeds that belong to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:276":{"book":"naladiyar","num":276,"t":["எனதென தென்றிருக்கும் ஏழை பொருளை","எனதென தென்றிருப்பன் யானும் - தனதாயின்","தானும் அதனை வழங்கான் பயன்துவ்வான்","யானும் அதனை அது."],"p":"As to the property which the wretched churl claims saying 'It is mine, it is mine ,' I too chime in with \"It is mine, it is mine;' for if it is his, he himself spends it not, nor enjoys the benefit of it; and I, too, neither spend nor enjoy it!","r":"The wealth the fool keeps calling 'mine, mine', I too call 'mine, mine': if it is his, he neither gives it nor enjoys it, and neither do I.","n":"A joke with a sting: ownership that produces no use is no different from not owning. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 275.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:277":{"book":"naladiyar","num":277,"t":["வழங்காத செல்வரின் நல்கூர்ந்தார் உய்ந்தார்","இழந்தா ரெனப்படுதல் உய்ந்தார் - உழந்ததனைக்","காப்புய்ந்தார் கல்லுதலும் உய்ந்தார்தங் கைந்நோவ","யாப்புயந்தார் உய்ந்த பல."],"p":"The poor have escaped much from which rich men that dispense not suffer. They have escaped the reputation of having lost (their substance). They have escaped the toil of saving it. They have escaped (the labour of) digging (to hide it). They have escaped the ache of hands securing it from powerful plunderers. Many are the (sorrows) they have escaped.","r":"The poor have escaped much that troubles the rich who do not give: being called ruined, the toil of guarding, the digging to bury it, the aching hands that clutch it; many are their escapes.","n":"The list is ironic consolation, not praise of poverty: the comparison is only with the miser. Pope reads the last escape as tying it up against powerful plunderers; Mudaliar as gripping it tight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","ivaral","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:278":{"book":"naladiyar","num":278,"t":["தனதாகத் தான்கொடான் தாயத் தவரும்","தமதாய போழ்தே கொடாஅர் - தனதாக","முன்னே கொடுப்பின் அவர்கடியார் தான்கடியான்","பின்னை அவர்கொடுக்கும் போழ்து."],"p":"While it was his he gave not; and his heirs, how it is theirs, give not. Before, while it was his, if he had given, they would not have reproved (him); and afterwards, if they had given, he would not have reproved them! Men's own avarice is the sole reason for the lack of charity.","r":"While it was his he did not give, and his heirs do not give once it is theirs; had he given first, they would not have stopped him, and when they give later, he cannot stop them.","n":"The argument removes every excuse: nobody prevented either party from giving. Pope's added sentence states the moral, that only one's own avarice stops giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:279":{"book":"naladiyar","num":279,"t":["இரவலர் கன்றாக ஈவார்ஆ வாக","விரகிற் சுரப்பதாம் வண்மை - விரகின்றி","வல்லவர் ஊன்ற வடிஆபோல் வாய்வைத்துக்","கொல்லச் சுரப்பதாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"Liberality is to yield gladly, as a cow does to its calf, the askers being the calf and the givers the cow; the base yield, like a cow milked by force, only when strong men press and torment them.","n":"The same act, giving, is good or base by the way it comes out. Mudaliar records several readings of வாய் வைத்து: by many devices, by reviling, or with instruments. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:280":{"book":"naladiyar","num":280,"t":["ஈட்டலும் துன்பமற் றீட்டிய வொண்பொருளைக்","காத்தலும் ஆங்கே கடுந்துன்பம் - காத்தல்","குறைபடில் துன்பம் கெடில்துன்பம் துன்பக்கு","உறைபதி மற்றைப் பொருள்."],"p":"Gathering it together is trouble, and even so the guarding of resplendent wealth is severe trouble. If the guarded heap diminish, it is trouble. If it perish, it is trouble. Wealth is trouble's very dwelling-place! ---------------","r":"Gaining wealth is suffering, guarding it is bitter suffering, its dwindling is suffering and its loss is suffering: wealth is the very dwelling place of suffering.","n":"The verse speaks of wealth itself, not only the miser's, though the chapter setting is not giving. Every stage, from earning to loss, is suffering, so no stage is safe. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:281":{"book":"naladiyar","num":281,"t":["அத்திட்ட கூறை அரைச்சுற்ற வாழினும்","பத்தெட் டுடைமை பலருள்ளும் பாடெய்தும்","ஒத்த குடிப்பிறந்தக் கண்ணுமொன் றில்லாதார்","செத்த பிணத்தின் கடை."],"p":"Though he wraps a cloth dyed red around his loins, a dozen coins or so, will gain (the wretch) respect among many men! The man devoid of wealth, though born of noble race, is viler (in the world's estimation) than a lifeless corpse !","r":"Even one who lives with a dyed cloth wrapped round his waist gains standing among many if he has ten or eight coins; those who have nothing, though born into a fitting family, are lower than a corpse.","n":"The ochre or patched cloth (Mudaliar gives both) marks someone of no standing; a handful of coins outweighs it, while noble birth without money counts for less than a dead body. The verse reports how the world judges, without endorsing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:282":{"book":"naladiyar","num":282,"t":["நீரினும் நுண்ணிது நெய்யென்பார், நெய்யினும்","யாரும் அறிவர் புகைநுட்பம் - தேரின்","நிரப்பிடும்பை யாளன் புகுமே, புகையும்","புகற்கா஢ய பூழை நுழைந்து."],"p":"Where water cannot enter! the more insinuating ghi glides in; and smoke as a subtler power to penetrate than even ghi. If you look into it, the man debased by poverty will enter haunts where smoke scarce finds a way.","r":"Ghee is said to be finer than water and everyone knows smoke is finer than ghee; but if you consider it, the man in the affliction of want gets in through openings even smoke can hardly pass.","n":"A comic hyperbole of need pressing in anywhere for help. Mudaliar's first reading divides the phrase as இரப்பு இடும்பை, the affliction of begging, and gives நிரப்பு, poverty, as another; Shanmuganar and Pope take poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:283":{"book":"naladiyar","num":283,"t":["கல்லோங் குயர்வரைமேல் காந்தள் மலராக்கால்","செல்லாவாம் செம்பொறி வண்டினம் - கொல்லைக்","கலாஅற் கிளிகடியுங் கானக நாட","இலாஅஅர்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"When on the high hill's crags the Kanthal blooms no more, the crimson-spotted beetle tribe seek not its boughs;—Lord of the hills wherefrom they scare parrots with stones! — the needy have no kin.","r":"Lord of the forest land where they scare parrots from the millet with stones: when the kanthal does not flower on the high rocky hill, the red-spotted bees do not go there; the poor have no kin.","n":"Kin are bees and wealth the flower: they come for what is given, not for the person. The address to a hill chief borrows the frame of the akam poems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:284":{"book":"naladiyar","num":284,"t":["உண்டாய போழ்தின் உடைந்துழிக் காகம்போல்","தொண்டரா யிரவர் தொகுபவே - வண்டாய்த்","திரிதரும் காலத்துத் தீதிலிரோ என்பார்","ஒருவரும் இல்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"When wealth is there, obsequious myriads will assemble, like crows around the fallen corpse. When wealth, as the beetle wheels its flight, is gone, no one in all the world will ask, ‘Is it well with you?'","r":"While there is wealth, a thousand gather as servants, like crows at a carcass; when one wanders like a bee, no one in the world asks 'are you well?'","n":"The crows at the carcass make the flatterers feeders on the rich man, not friends. Both commentators make the poor man the one wandering like a bee for food; Pope makes the wealth the thing that flies off like a beetle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:285":{"book":"naladiyar","num":285,"t":["பிறந்த குலமாயும் பேராண்மை மாயும்","சிறந்ததங் கல்வியும் மாயும் - கறங்கருவி","கன்மேல் க்ழுஉங் கணமலை நன்னாட","இன்மை தழுவப்பட் டார்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land of clustering hills whose crags are washed by sounding waterfalls !—Their race is nought, their manly prowess is nought, their rare learning is nought, when men are held in poverty's embrace.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills where loud waterfalls wash the rocks: for those whom poverty has seized, the family they were born into, great manliness and their excellent learning all perish.","n":"The three goods named are ones normally thought independent of wealth: birth, character and learning. Mudaliar lists the qualities of ஆண்மை as fullness, forbearance, discernment and firmness, or else as valour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","aanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:286":{"book":"naladiyar","num":286,"t":["உள்கூர் பசியால் உழைநசைஇச் சென்றார்கட்கு","உள்ளூ ரிருந்துமோன் றாற்றாதான் - உள்ளூர்","இருந்துயிர் கொன்னே கழியாது தான்போய்","விருந்தினன் ஆதலே நன்று."],"p":"Although he dwells within the village, and sees the poor draw nigh with hungry soul desiring aid, he yields them none : why then in the village does he vainly pass his days? It were better he went to be a guest himself !","r":"One who lives in his own village but can give nothing to those who come to him driven by hunger had better go away and be a guest somewhere than waste his life staying there.","n":"Who is meant decides the tone. On Mudaliar's reading it is advice to a poor man, matching 288; on Shanmuganar's and Pope's it is scorn for a miser, and Shanmuganar lets 'guest' also mean a guest of the other world, that is, death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","virundhu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The man is poor and unable to help, and is advised to go and live as a guest elsewhere.",""],["shanmuganar","The man is a miser who gives nothing, and would do better to leave for another place or the other world.",""]],"ch":"n:29"},"n:287":{"book":"naladiyar","num":287,"t":["நீர்மையே யன்றி நிரம்ப எழுந்ததங்","கூர்மையும் எல்லாம் ஒருங்கிழப்பர் - கூர்மையின்","முல்லை அலைக்கும் எயிற்றாய். நிரப்பென்னும்","அல்லல் அடையப்பட் டார்."],"p":"O thou whose teeth vie in sharpness with jasmine buds! — When sharp distress of poverty assails, men lose all their attributes of goodness at once, with the mind's acuteness gained from amplest stores of wisdom.","r":"Girl whose teeth outdo the sharp jasmine buds: those overtaken by the affliction called want lose at once not only their good nature but their fully risen sharpness of mind and everything else.","n":"The verse plays on கூர்மை: the sharpness lost by the poor is held up against the sharpness of the girl's teeth. Mudaliar notes that நிரப்பு, literally fullness, is used here for its opposite. Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 286.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:288":{"book":"naladiyar","num":288,"t":["இட்டாற்றுப் பட்டொன் றிரந்தவர்க் காற்றாது","முட்டாற்றுப் பட்டும் முயன்றுள்ளூர் வாழ்தலின்","நெட்டாற்றுச் சென்று நிரைமனையில் கைந்நீட்டும்","கெட்டாற்று வாழ்க்கையே நன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rather than live on in one's own village, straitened and blocked and struggling, unable to give to one who asks, it is better to go a long way and live the ruined life of holding out one's hand at row after row of houses.","n":"Begging is ranked above the shame of refusing an asker at home. Mudaliar says it repeats 286 and cites Kural 478 for இட்டு (narrow). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:289":{"book":"naladiyar","num":289,"t":["கடகம் செறிந்ததங் கைகளால் வாங்கி","அடகு பறித்துக்கொண் டட்டுக் - குடைகலனா","உப்பிலி வெந்தைதின் றுள்ளற்று வாழ்பவே,","துப்புரவு சென்றுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"The hands once loaded with golden bracelets now cull the forest-herb and cook the meal; and then eat the mess unseasoned, from the palm-leaf for a dish! Thus sad at heart they live, when fortune is gone and ruin come.","r":"When their means are spent and gone, with the hands that once wore bracelets they pull down branches, pick greens, cook them, and eat the boiled leaves without salt from a folded palm-leaf, living on with broken spirit.","n":"The bracelets on the hands that now strip branches carry the whole fall in one image. Mudaliar cites Kalittokai for குடை as a palm-leaf vessel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","nilaiyaamai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:290":{"book":"naladiyar","num":290,"t":["ஆர்த்த பொறிய அணிகிளர் வண்டினம்","பூத்தொழி கொம்பின்மேல் செல்லாவாம் - நீர்த்தருவி","தாழா உயர்சிறப்பின் தண்குன்ற நன்னாட","வாழாதார்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"The humming spotted beetle tribes all bright in hue gather not on the branch that has ceased to blow. Lord of the good cool hilly land, of high renown, whence bounteous streams flow down unceasingly—the unprosperous have no kin! --------------","r":"Lord of the cool, good hill land whose waterfalls never fail: the bright, humming, spotted bees do not go to the branch that has flowered and finished; those who have lost their prosperity have no kin.","n":"Mudaliar notes it shares the thought of 283. The difference is small: here the branch has flowered and finished, so the stress falls on prosperity once had and lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:291":{"book":"naladiyar","num":291,"t":["திருமதுகை யாகத் திறனிலார் செய்யும்","பெருமிதம் கண்டக் கடைத்தும் - எரிமண்டிக்","கானந் தலைப்பட்ட தீப்போல் கனலுமே,","மான முடையார் மனம்."],"p":"The mind of those possessed of honourable feeling will kindle into flame, like the fire that has caught the forest when the conflagration rages, whenever they behold the haughty acts of those who are destitute of virtuous habits, and to whom their wealth is their only strength.","r":"Whenever they see the arrogance of the worthless, whose only strength is their wealth, the minds of men of honour blaze like fire raging through a forest.","n":"The fire is inward: the verse says the mind burns, not that they act on it. Mudaliar quotes a definition of மானம் as not falling from one's station and not living on when brought low by fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","selvam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:292":{"book":"naladiyar","num":292,"t":["என்பாய் உகினும் இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று","தம்பா டுரைப்பரோ தம்முடையார் - தம்பாடு","உரையாமை முன்னுணரும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்க்கு","உரையாரோ தாமுற்ற நோய்."],"p":"Will (honourable men who are) 'masters of themselves' follow graceless men to tell of their sufferings, tbough fallen away to mere skeletons ? Do they not (rather) tell the pain they have felt to those enlightened souls that understand their sufferings before they speak ?","r":"Will those who own themselves follow the graceless to tell of their plight, even if they waste to bone? Will they not tell their pain to the clear-sighted, who grasp it before it is spoken?","n":"Honour here does not forbid asking; it chooses whom to ask. The right patron reads the need before it is said, so the honourable never have to beg in words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:293":{"book":"naladiyar","num":293,"t":["யாமாயின் எம்மில்லம் காட்டுதும் தாமாயின்","காணவே கற்பழியும் என்பார்போல் - நாணிப்","புறங்கடை வைத்தீவர் சோறும் அதனால்","மறந்திடுக செல்வர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"As for ourselves we would introduce them (these friends) to our household : but as regards them, they are ashamed (of us), as though they said, as soon as they (the ladies) saw them (the friends,) it would be the destruction of their womanly reserve; and so they seat us at the backdoor and give us rice! Therefore let us dismiss all thought of rich men's friendship.","r":"We would bring our guests into our homes, but they, as if their wives' chastity would be lost if we so much as saw them, shame-faced, set rice before us at the back door; so forget the friendship of the rich.","n":"Both commentators take the poor speaker as one who would let his wife serve guests, while the rich hide theirs and feed the poor at the back. Mudaliar notes another reading in which இல்லம் is simply the house and கற்பு is firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","natpu","selvam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:294":{"book":"naladiyar","num":294,"t":["இம்மையும் நன்றாம் இயல்நெறியும் கைவிடாது","உம்மையும் நல்ல பயத்தலால் - செம்மையின்","நானம் கமழும் கதுப்பினாய். நன்றேகாண்","மான முடையார் மதிப்பு."],"p":"O thou whose locks diffuse the odour of pleasant musk! Behold, especially good is (it to cultivate) that which is esteemed by men of honourable mind; for in this world it is (obviously) good : and, since it leads to perseverance in the way of virtue, it will yield good things in yonder world also.","r":"Girl with hair fragrant with musk, see how good it is, the esteem of men of honour: it does good in this life and, never leaving the right way, yields good things in the next.","n":"Honour is justified by its fruit in both worlds, which makes it a part of virtue rather than pride. Mudaliar glosses மதிப்பு as the principle men of honour hold, and notes another reading: being well regarded by others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:295":{"book":"naladiyar","num":295,"t":["பாவமும் ஏனைப் பழியும் படவருவ","சாயினும் சான்றவர் செய்கலார் - சாதல்","ஒருநாள் ஒருபொழுதைத் துன்பம் அவைபோல்","அருநவை ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"The men 'fulfilled of excellence,' though death were the alternative, do not deeds that entail sin and guilt. Death is an affliction for one day, and for a little while. There is nothing that works irreparable ill like those (deeds).","r":"The worthy will not do what brings sin and blame, even if they must die: death is the pain of one day, one moment, and nothing does such lasting harm as those.","n":"The argument is a calculation of duration: death's pain is brief, sin and blame's are not. Mudaliar separates them: sin follows one into later lives, blame stays in this world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:296":{"book":"naladiyar","num":296,"t":["மல்லன்மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்பவ ருள்ளெல்லாம்","செல்வ ரெனினும் கொடாதவர் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","நல்கூர்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பெருமுத் தரையரே,","செல்வரைச் சென்றிரவா தார்."],"p":"Poor are the men that give not, even though deemed wealthiest of all that flourish on the teeming apple earth! They who even when they are poor seek not as suppliants wealthy men are 'Lords of the three mighty lands.'","r":"Among all who live in this rich, wide world, those who do not give are poor even if they are rich; and those who, even in poverty, do not go and beg from the rich are the great Muttaraiyar.","n":"பெருமுத்தரையர் is a name of liberality: Mudaliar takes them as generous patrons of the author's time, also named in an earlier quatrain, and Shanmuganar as a royal line; Pope translates the name as 'lords of the three mighty lands'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:297":{"book":"naladiyar","num":297,"t":["கடையெலாம் காய்பசி அஞ்சுமற் றேனை","இடையெலாம் இன்னாமை அஞ்சும் - புடைபரந்த","விற்புருவ வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய் தலையெல்லாம்","சொற்பழி அஞ்சி விடும்."],"p":"Thou who hast long dart-like eyes with eye-brows extending far, like a bow! the lowest class of men dread burning hunger; the other (or middle class) dread what is unpleasant; all the chief of men fear words that impute crime.","r":"Girl of long dart-like eyes under wide bow-brows: the lowest all fear burning hunger, the middle fear hardship, and the highest fear only the blame in people's words.","n":"A scale of persons by what they fear. Shanmuganar adds what follows from each fear: the lower will do anything, the highest refrain from wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","acham","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:298":{"book":"naladiyar","num":298,"t":["நல்லர் பெரிதளியர் நல்கூர்ந்தார் என்றெள்ளிச்","செல்வர் சிறுநோக்கு நோக்குங்கால் - கொல்லன்","உலையூதும் தீயேபோல் உள்கனலும் கொல்லோ,","தலையாய சான்றோர் மனம்."],"p":"When the very worthy and thoroughly learned see the rich men's glance of disparagement, as they say contemptuously, 'These are good people —persons in greatly reduced circumstances—poor folks,' does not their mind kindle into flame within them, like the fire by the breath of the bellows on the blacksmith's forge?","r":"When the rich look down on them and say with contempt, 'good people, very kind, now poor', the minds of the foremost worthy may burn within like fire blown in a smith's forge.","n":"The insult is disguised as praise. Both commentators take அளியர் as kind or compassionate, and Shanmuganar makes the kindness the cause of the poverty; Pope takes it as pitiable, 'in greatly reduced circumstances'. Mudaliar glosses எற்றி where the verse has எள்ளி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saandror","inmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:299":{"book":"naladiyar","num":299,"t":["நச்சியார்க் கீயாமை நாணன்று நாணாளும்","அச்சத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணன்றாம் - எச்சத்தின்","மெல்லிய ராகித்தம் மேலாயார் செய்தது","சொல்லா திருப்பது நாண்."],"p":"It is no shame (disgrace) not to (be able to give to those who desire it of us. The shrinking on account of fear (felt) day by day is not shame (modesty). But to become reduced in other ways, and not to [dare to] tell what injuries those who love us not have inflicted on us is shame (disgrace, or self-respect).","r":"Not giving to those who come wanting is no shame, and shrinking each day from fear is no shame; the shame, when one is reduced by want, is to leave unspoken what one's superiors did to one.","n":"What the superiors did is left unnamed, so the verse can mean either keeping silent about their slights or failing to acknowledge their help, and the sense of நாண் shifts with it. Mudaliar gives both readings; Pope takes it as not daring to tell their injuries.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam","nanri"],"pos":[["mudaliar","His main reading: what the richer did is the slight they showed, which one does not speak of; he also reports the reading that one fails to proclaim their help.",""],["shanmuganar","The greatest shame is failing to speak gratefully of help one's superiors gave in one's poverty.",""]],"ch":"n:30"},"n:300":{"book":"naladiyar","num":300,"t":["கடமா தொலைச்சிய கானுறை வேங்கை","இடம்வீழ்ந்த துண்ணா திறக்கும் - இடமுடைய","வானகம் கையுறினும் வேண்டார் விழுமியோர்","மானம் மழுங்க வரின்."],"p":"The jungle-haunting tiger that slays the wild cow, refuses to eat and passes by what has fallen (of itself) in its path (i. e. carrion); so the excellent, though the wide realms of heaven were within their reach, would not desire them, if to be obtained (only) by the loss of honour. --------------","r":"The forest tiger that has killed a great beast will starve to death rather than eat it if it falls on its left; so the excellent would not want even wide heaven placed in their hands if it came at the dulling of their honour.","n":"Both commentators take கடமா as the elephant (Mudaliar also gives wild cow) and இடம் வீழ்ந்தது as falling on the left, citing a Purananuru poem on the tiger's custom; Pope reads a wild cow and carrion fallen in its path. Heaven itself, not just wealth, is refused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:301":{"book":"naladiyar","num":301,"t":["நம்மாலே யாவரிந் நல்கூர்ந்தார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","தம்மாலாம் ஆக்கம் இலரென்று - தம்மை","மருண்ட மனத்தார்பின் செல்பவோ தாமும்","தெருண்ட அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Will men themselves possessed of clear discerning knowledge follow after those who have yielded themselves up to mental bewilderment, and who (erroneously) say (of suppliants), ‘These poor folks depend entirely on us, evermore are they without resources in themselves ?'","r":"Would people of clear understanding follow after the self-deluded rich, who tell themselves that the poor live only through them and have no means of their own?","n":"The fault named is the patron's self-importance, not his stinginess. Mudaliar glosses தம்மை மருண்ட as being dazed by one's own estimate of oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:302":{"book":"naladiyar","num":302,"t":["இழித்தக்க செய்தொருவன் ஆர உணலின்","பழித்தக்க செய்யான் பசித்தல் தவறோ?","விழித்திமைக்கு மாத்திரை யன்றோ ஒருவன்","அழித்துப் பிறக்கும் பிறப்பு."],"p":"Is not a man's dying and birth again measured by the twinkling of an eye? Is it then a fault if a man rather choose to suffer hunger, doing no blameworthy actions, than to feed full, doing things that entail disgrace ?","r":"Since dying and being born again takes no longer than opening the eyes and blinking, is it wrong for a man to go hungry without doing anything blameworthy rather than eat his fill by doing what degrades him?","n":"The argument rests on the shortness of a life: a full stomach bought with disgrace feeds a body that will be gone in a blink. Mudaliar gives three ways to take the eye image, one of them sleeping and waking, and cites Kural 339 for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","pazhi","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:303":{"book":"naladiyar","num":303,"t":["இல்லாமை கந்தா இரவு துணிந்தொருவர்","செல்லாரும் அல்லர் சிறுநெறி - புல்லா","அகம்புகுமின் உண்ணுமின் என்பவர்மாட் டல்லான்","முகம்புகுதல் ஆற்றுமோ மேல்?"],"p":"There will never be wanting those who, with their destitution as their support (making it their excuse), will venture upon mendicancy, and tread the way of humiliation; but will the noble-minded man enter (as a suppliant) the presence of any save of those who will embrace him and say, 'Enter my dwelling, eat of my food ?'","r":"Some, leaning on their poverty, will dare to beg and take the mean road, but the worthy go before no one except those who embrace them and say 'come in, eat'.","n":"Mudaliar reads முகம் புகுதல் as putting up with a patron's frown, and Shanmuganar as showing one's own face to a patron. Either way, the only giver the worthy approach is one who receives them as a guest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:304":{"book":"naladiyar","num":304,"t":["திருத்தன்னை நீப்பினும் தெய்வம் செறினும்","உருத்த மனத்தோ டுயர்வுள்ளி னல்லால்","அருத்தம் செறிக்கும் அறிவிலார் பின்சென்று","எருத்திறைஞ்சி நில்லாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though fortune forsake him, and fate frown, the man of lofty soul, dwelling with steadfast mind on things above, disdains to stand with bending neck in the train of the foolish who hoard their wealth.","r":"Though fortune leaves him and the gods turn against him, the man of worth keeps his mind on his own high standing and never stands with bowed neck behind fools who hoard their wealth.","n":"திரு and தெய்வம் are the two supports a person might count on, and the verse takes both away before asking for steadiness. The stance is independence from fortune, not defiance of it. Mudaliar takes உருத்த மனம் as a mind roused with energy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ookkam","oozh","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:305":{"book":"naladiyar","num":305,"t":["கரவாத திண்ணன்பின கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாது வாழ்வதாம் வாழ்க்கை - இரவினை","உள்ளுங்கால் உள்ளம் உருகுமால் என்கொலோ","கொள்ளுங்கால் கொள்வார் குறிப்பு."],"p":"To live asking naught even from those dear as an eye, whose love is sure, and who never refuse, is happy life. Since the mind dissolves in shame at the very thought of beggary,— when men receive alms, what are the receivers' thoughts, I pray ?","r":"Real living is to live without asking even of those dear as one's eyes, whose love is firm and who hold nothing back; the heart melts at the mere thought of begging, so what must the asker feel at the moment of taking?","n":"The last line moves from the giver to what the receiver feels while taking. Mudaliar reads கொள்வார் குறிப்பு as the taker's state of mind; Shanmuganar names it as shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","manam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:306":{"book":"naladiyar","num":306,"t":["இன்னா இயைக இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தானிரப்பத் தீர்வதற் - கென்னைகொல்","காதல் கவற்றும் மனத்தினாற் கண்பாழ்பட்டு","ஏதி லவரை இரவு."],"p":"When a man himself, begs of himself, saying, ‘Let troubles come, let joys depart!' the sting of poverty is extracted; why then, for this purpose, should any one, his mind agitated with desire, and his eye wasted with weeping, beg of strangers ?","r":"When poverty can be ended by asking of oneself, 'let pain come, let pleasures go', why should anyone, with a mind racked by desire and eyes sunk, go begging of strangers?","n":"Mudaliar divides தானிரப்ப two ways: தான் நிரப்ப, filling oneself with contentment, or தான் இரப்ப, begging of oneself. Pope takes the second and Shanmuganar the first. On either reading the cure for poverty is inward: consenting to hardship removes the want that drives begging.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","avaa","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:307":{"book":"naladiyar","num":307,"t":["என்றும் புதியார் பிறப்பினும் இவ்வுலகத்","தென்று மவனே பிறக்கலான் - குன்றின்","பரப்பெலாம் பொன்னொழுகும் பாயருவி நாட","இரப்பாரை எள்ளா மகன்."],"p":"Lord of the land where gold is borne down by rushing waterfalls from every hilly slope! — Though in this world new (kinds of) men are continually born, that very man is never born that does not scorn mendicants.","r":"Though new people are born into this world every day, the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born, lord of the hill country where waterfalls carry gold down every slope.","n":"Mudaliar gives two reasons for the overstatement: such a man reaches the good state and is not born again, or he simply cannot be found. The first plays on பிறக்கலான் ('is not born').","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:308":{"book":"naladiyar","num":308,"t":["புறுத்துத்தன் இன்மை நலிய அகத்துத்தன்","நன்ஞானம் நீக்கி நிறீஇ ஒருவனை","ஈயாய் எனக்கென் றிரப்பானேல் அந்நிலையே","மாயானோ மாற்றி விடின்."],"p":"Want wastes his outward frame; he lays aside wisdom, his inward being's good; and so resolving, begs of some stranger, saying, 'Give to me.’— If then the boon be refused, must he not that very instant die?","r":"With want wasting his body outside and his good sense put away inside, a man goes to another and says 'give to me'; if he is refused, will he not die on the spot?","n":"Two losses come before the refusal: the body already worn down by want, and the understanding set aside so that asking is possible at all. Shanmuganar adds that the death would come from shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:309":{"book":"naladiyar","num":309,"t":["ஒருவ ரொருவரைச் சார்ந்தொழுகல் ஆற்றி","வழிபடுதல் வல்லுத லல்லால் - பரிசழிந்து","செய்யீரோ என்னானும் என்னுஞ்சொற் கின்னாதே","பையத்தான் செல்லும் நெறி?"],"p":"Men form close intimacies with others and live as their dependants, and this is permissible ; but is it more painful quietly to go one's way (to the hermitage) than, lost to all sense of personal dignity, to say, 'Will ye not do aught for me?’","r":"It is proper to attach oneself to another and serve him with respect, but is the slow road of poverty more painful than losing one's dignity and saying 'won't you do something for me?'","n":"Both commentators allow dependence on a patron, with deference, and forbid only the words of asking, so the line is drawn in speech. Mudaliar derives பைய from பை (suffering); Shanmuganar takes it as 'slowly'. Pope's 'to the hermitage' is his own addition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sol","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:310":{"book":"naladiyar","num":310,"t":["பழமைகந் தாகப் பசைந்த வழியே","கிழமைதான் யாதானும் செய்க - கிழமை","பொறார் அவரென்னின் பொத்தித்தம் நெஞ்சத்","தறாஅச் சுடுவதோர் தீ."],"p":"Relying on old friendship, in an affectionate manner, render such assistance as your intimacy Warrants. If he will not endure (the assistance), will it not be a fire heaped up and burning in his bosom? ----------------","r":"Where old acquaintance has grown into affection, one should do whatever help that intimacy calls for; if the claim of intimacy is not borne, it becomes a fire banked in the heart that burns without end.","n":"Whose heart burns is not settled. Shanmuganar says it is the old friend who came to ask and was turned away. Pope reverses the roles: help is offered, and the needy friend who cannot bear to accept it burns. Mudaliar says only that the one asked fails to give the fitting help.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai","natpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:311":{"book":"naladiyar","num":311,"t":["மெய்ஞ்ஞானக் கோட்டி உறழ்வழி விட்டாங்கோர்","அஞ்ஞானந் தந்திட் டதுவாங் கறத்துழாய்க்","கைஞ்ஞானங் கொண்டொழுகுங் காரறி வாளர்முன்","சொன்ஞானஞ் சோர விடல்."],"p":"","r":"Before people of dark understanding, who leave the way of an assembly of true knowledge, put forward some piece of ignorance, spread it everywhere and live by petty learning, let the wise leave their wisdom unspoken.","n":"The advice is silence, not argument. Mudaliar records another reading of கைஞ்ஞானம் as 'skill of the hand', the habit of striking whoever objects, which would anticipate 312 and 315.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:312":{"book":"naladiyar","num":312,"t":["நாப்பாடம் சொல்லி நயமுணர்வார் போல்செறிக்கும்","தீப்புலவர் சேரார் செறிவுடையார் - தீப்புலவன்","கோட்டியுள் குன்றக் குடிப்பழிக்கும் அல்லர்க்கால்","தோட்புடைக் கொள்ளா எழும்."],"p":"Well-disciplined men come not near the heretic (=man of evil learning, the teacher of an evil system), who stores up (in his memory certain formulas, comp. 304), as though he understood their worth, and repeats them by rote (lit. as a tongue-lesson). The heretic, if worsted in the assembly, will abuse the family (of his opponents); or he will spring up and challenge them to fight.","r":"The disciplined keep away from the bad scholar who recites lessons by rote as if he knew their beauty; worsted in the assembly he will abuse their family, or else slap his shoulders and rise to fight.","n":"Pope calls the தீப்புலவன் a heretic, the teacher of an evil system. Mudaliar's gloss is simply one without learning, and Shanmuganar's a false poet. The danger here is social: the loser turns to insult and violence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:313":{"book":"naladiyar","num":313,"t":["சொற்றாற்றுக் கொண்டு சுனைத்தெழுதல் காமுறுவர்,","கற்றாற்றல் வன்மையும் தாம்தேறார் - கற்ற","செலவுரைக்கும ஆறறியார் தோற்ப தறியார்","பலவுரைக்கும் மாந்தர் பலர்."],"p":"Many are the men that utter many things,—who long to raise up (in debate), from mere love of talking and an itching tongue; who do not themselves apprehend the power and might of learning; who know not the way to utter with penetrating force whatthey have learnt; and who know not what defeat means.","r":"Many people talk at length: they itch to rise and debate on the strength of a word, cannot judge the power of learning, do not know how to make what they learned reach the hearer, and do not know when they are beaten.","n":"Mudaliar gives several ways to divide the opening words, one of them 'with words for goads', which Shanmuganar follows. The last failure, not knowing defeat, is what keeps the talk going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","solvanmai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:314":{"book":"naladiyar","num":314,"t":["கற்றதூஉ மின்றிக் கணக்காயர் பாடத்தால்","பெற்றதாம் பேதையோர் சூத்திரம் - மற்றதனை","நல்லா ரிடைப்புக்கு நாணாது சொல்லித்தன்","புல்லறிவு காட்டி விடும்."],"p":"Without any learning of his own, the fool has obtained one formula from overhearing the lessons of a tutor (who was teaching others ), yet unabashed he enters the circle of the good, speaks it out, and makes exhibition of his mean understanding.","r":"With no learning of his own, a fool picks up a single sutra by overhearing a teacher's lesson, then walks unabashed into a gathering of the good, recites it, and shows how petty his understanding is.","n":"Mudaliar specifies that the fool has only the bare text (மூல பாடம்) of the sutra, overheard from lessons given to other students.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","pullarivu","avai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:315":{"book":"naladiyar","num":315,"t":["வென்றிப் பொருட்டால் விலங்கொத்து மெய்கொள்ளார்","கன்றிக் கறுத்தெழுந்து காய்வாரோ - டொன்றி","உரைவித் தகமெழுவார் காண்பவே, கையுள்","சுரைவித்துப் போலுந்தம் பல்."],"p":"They who arise to utter words of wisdom, having associated themselves with the assembly of those who rage and burn and fume with anger, like beasts, for the mere sake of victory, not understanding the truth, shall see their own teeth like pumpkin seeds in their hands!","r":"Those who join people who rage and burn like beasts for the sake of victory and will not accept the truth, and who rise among them to show off their eloquence, will see their own teeth in their hands like gourd seeds.","n":"Mudaliar names the figure: teeth in the hand say, without saying it, that they will be knocked out. The warning falls on the speaker who chooses that company, not on the brawlers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","igal","solvanmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:316":{"book":"naladiyar","num":316,"t":["பாடமே ஓதிப் பயன்தெரிதல் தேற்றாத","முடர் முனிதக்க சொல்லுங்கால் - கேடருஞ்சீர்ச்","சான்றோர் சமழ்த்தனர் நிற்பவே, மற்றவரை","ஈன்றாட் கிறப்பப் பரிந்து."],"p":"When foolish men chant their lesson, not knowing the fruit that lesson yields, but uttering words that gender wrath, the learned ones, whose fame dies not, will stand by ashamed, sorely pitying the mother that bare them.","r":"When fools who only recite the text and cannot make out its meaning say things that provoke anger, the worthy, whose renown does not decay, stand ashamed and grieve deeply for the mother who bore them.","n":"The shame belongs to the listeners, not the fool. Mudaliar quotes the kind of lament the worthy might make, that she bore him for others to laugh at, and names the figure as imputed cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","saandror","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:317":{"book":"naladiyar","num":317,"t":["பெறுவது கொள்பவர் தோள்போல் நெறிப்பட்டுக்","கற்பவர்க் கெல்லாம் எளியநூல் - மற்றம்","முறிபுரை மேனியர் உள்ளம்போன் றியார்க்கும்","அறிதற் கா஢ய பொருள்."],"p":"Like the charms of those who sell their love for what they gain, the sacred texts are easy to those who rightly learn then; but like the minds of these whose forms are soft as tender shoots, the meaning is hard to all.","r":"Books are easy for anyone who learns them in due order, like the shoulders of women who take whatever they are paid, but their meaning is hard for anyone to grasp, like the minds of those same women with skin like tender mango shoots.","n":"One simile does both halves of the work: access to the text is cheap and open, and understanding is closed. Pope's 'sacred texts' narrows நூல், which the commentators leave general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:318":{"book":"naladiyar","num":318,"t":["புத்தகமே சாலத் தொகுத்தும் பொருடெரியார்","உய்த்தக மெல்லா நிறைப்பினும் - மற்றவற்றைப்","போற்றும் புலவரும் வேறே பொருடெரிந்து","தேற்றும் புலவரும் வேறு."],"p":"Although men gather together books in abundance, and, not understanding their contents, fill the whole house with them; the sages who merely take care of books are of one sort, and the sages who understand their contents, and make them clear to others, are of another sort.","r":"Some collect books in great numbers and fill the whole house with them without understanding what they say; the scholars who keep books are one kind, and those who understand them and make others understand are another.","n":"Shanmuganar takes போற்றும் as keeping the books with worship (பூசை போட்டு) and calls the keepers false scholars. The verse itself says only that they are a different kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:319":{"book":"naladiyar","num":319,"t":["பொழிப்பகல நுட்பநூ லெச்சமிந் நான்கின்","கொழித்தகலங் காட்டாதார் சொற்கள் - பழிப்பில்","நிரையாமா சேர்க்கும் நெடுங்குன்ற நாட","உரையாமோ நூலிற்கு நன்கு?"],"p":"Lord of the land of long chains of hills where the wild cattle assemble in herds! Is that a good commentary to a faultless composition, which consists of the words of those who do not sift it thoroughly, in the four divisions of summary, amplification, minute exposition, and supplementary information, and thus exhibit the full import?","r":"Lord of the long hills where herds of wild cattle gather, can the words of those who do not sift a faultless work through the four modes of summary, breadth, subtlety and remainder, and so show its full extent, be a good commentary on it?","n":"A quatrain about commentary, on whose terms its commentators disagree. Mudaliar adds that others define the four modes differently again.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The four are a method of disputation: breadth raises objections, subtlety answers them, and the remainder states the conclusion they establish.",""],["shanmuganar","The remainder is research that brings out what lies hidden and unsaid in the text.",""]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:320":{"book":"naladiyar","num":320,"t":["இற்பிறப் பில்லா ரெனைத்துநூல் கற்பினும்","சொற்பிறரைக் காக்குங் கருவியரோ? - இற்பிறந்த","நல்லறி வாளர் நவின்றநூல் தேற்றாதார்","புல்லறிவு தாமறிவ தில்."],"p":"Will those who are not of high family, however they may study learned works, be fit instruments to guard others from faults of speech? And men of good family who are well learned will not (seem to) be aware of the deficiencies of those who do not comprehend the works they talk of. -------------","r":"However many books they learn, can those not born of a good house be the means of guarding others' words? And the well-born wise take no notice of the petty understanding of those who do not grasp the books they talk of.","n":"Pope offers a third reading of the first half: guarding others from faults of speech. The second half fits Mudaliar's reading best, since the well-born show the restraint the first half denies to the low-born by overlooking a fool's errors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","pullarivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those of low birth lack the restraint needed to protect others' words from scorn.",""],["shanmuganar","Those of low birth cannot protect and care for others by what they say.",""]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:321":{"book":"naladiyar","num":321,"t":["அருளின் அறமுரைக்கும் அன்புடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்","பொருளாகக் கொள்வர் புலவர் - பொருளல்லா","ஏழை அதனை இகழ்ந்துரைக்கும் பாற்கூழை","முழை சுவையுணரா தாங்கு."],"p":"Men of sense receive as a reality the words from the mouth of the loving ones who graciously teach virtue. The (ignorant)wretch, himself unreal, speaks (of virtue) with contempt. Just so the ladle distinguishes not the flavour of the rice boiled in milk.","r":"The learned take as real worth the words of loving people who teach virtue out of compassion; the fool, himself of no worth, mocks them, as a ladle cannot taste the milk-rice it stirs.","n":"The ladle is in the milk-rice all the time and tastes nothing: being near good teaching is not the same as taking it in. Mudaliar takes பொருளல்லா ஏழை as a fool of no account, and Shanmuganar as one who does not grasp the real meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:322":{"book":"naladiyar","num":322,"t":["அவ்வியம் இல்லார் அறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","செவ்விய ரல்லார் செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்","கவ்வித்தோல் தின்னும் குணுங்கர்நாய் பாற்சோற்றின்","செவ்விய கொளல்தேற்றா தாங்கு."],"p":"When men of ungrudging soul declare the way of virtue, those who are not rightly disposed, although they give ear, hear not. Even so the currier's dog seizes on and devours leather, not appreciating the flavour of milk and rice.","r":"When those free of crookedness explain the way of virtue, those not rightly disposed do not hear it even when they lend an ear, just as the tanner's dog that chews leather cannot take the taste of milk-rice.","n":"Mudaliar reads அவ்வியம் as crookedness of mind, or as envy standing for every bad trait. The image draws on a caste occupation: Mudaliar glosses குணுங்கர் as புலையர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kelvi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:323":{"book":"naladiyar","num":323,"t":["இமைக்கும் அளவிற்றம் இன்னுயிர்போம் மார்க்கம்","எனைத்தானும் தாங்கண் டிருந்தும் - தினைத்துணையும்","நன்றி புரிகல்லா நாணின் மடமாக்கள்","பொன்றிலென் பொன்றாக்கா லென்?"],"p":"What matters it whether they live or die—the shameless stupid people who do no good, not even as much as a grain of millet, though they see by every species of example the way in which their life so sweet to them, passes away in the twinkling of an eye?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, those dull and shameless people who see in every way that their dear life may leave in the blink of an eye, and still do not a millet grain's worth of good?","n":"Mudaliar lets நன்றி mean any good deed, or specifically help given to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:324":{"book":"naladiyar","num":324,"t":["உளநாள் சிலவால் உயிர்க்கேமம் இன்றால்,","பலர்மன்னுந் தூற்றும் பழியால், - பலருள்ளும்","கண்டாரோ டெல்லாம் நகாஅ தெவனொருவன்","தண்டித் தனிப்பகை கோள்."],"p":"Since being's days are few, and life no safeguardowns, and guilt by many blamed is rife, mid many men, why, laughing not with all they see, should any sulk apart, and nurse a sullen hate ?","r":"The days left are few, life has no safeguard, and the blame that many spread is everywhere; why then should anyone, among so many, refuse to smile with those he meets and stand apart nursing a private enmity?","n":"The case against sullen enmity rests on shortage of time rather than on virtue: with life brief and unsafe, hostility is a waste. Mudaliar lets ஏமம் mean pleasure as well as protection.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","nilaiyaamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:325":{"book":"naladiyar","num":325,"t":["எய்தி யிருந்த அவைமுன்னர்ச் சென்றெள்ளி","வைதான் ஒருவன் ஒருவனை - வைய","வயப்பட்டான் வாளா இருப்பானேல், வைதான்","வியத்தக்கான் வாழும் எனின்."],"p":"A man has gone before the assembly that had gathered together, and contemning another has reviled him. Now if the reviled one remain silent, the reviler is to be wondered at if he survive; (for abuse is his very life, and the patience of the reviled one has closed his mouth.)","r":"If a man goes before a gathered assembly and reviles another, and the one reviled stays silent, it is a wonder if the reviler survives.","n":"The verse doesn't say how the reviler is undone. Pope supplies a reason, that abuse is the reviler's life and silence stops his mouth. The commentators say only that he will not prosper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kadunjol","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:326":{"book":"naladiyar","num":326,"t":["மூப்புமேல் வாராமை முன்னே அறவினையை","ஊக்கி அதன்கண் முயலாதான் - நூக்கிப்","புறத்திரு போகென்னும் இன்னாச்சொல் இல்லுள்","தொழுத்தையால் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"He who before old age comes, has not undertaken and zealously carried out works of virtue, will be pushed about in the house addressed in harsh language, and bidden to get on one side or to go out, by even the maid-servants.","r":"One who does not eagerly take up virtuous deeds and work at them before old age comes on will be pushed aside in his own house and told 'sit outside, go away' even by the maidservant.","n":"The penalty is social, not karmic: humiliation in old age, inside one's own home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:327":{"book":"naladiyar","num":327,"t":["தாமேயும் இன்புறார், தக்கார்க்கு நன்றாற்றார்","ஏமஞ்சார் நன்னெறியும் சேர்கலார் - தாமயங்கி","ஆக்கத்துள் தூங்கி அவத்தமே வாழ்நாளைப்","போக்குவார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"Men of scanty wisdom are those who do not themselves enjoy any sweets of life, bestow no benefactions on worthy persons, draw not nigh the good path that safeguards (the soul), but infatuated and absorbed in the acquisition of wealth, pass away their days of life in vain.","r":"People of petty understanding neither enjoy their wealth nor do good to the deserving nor join the good path that protects; dazed and sunk in their prosperity, they spend their days for nothing.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause of the daze: not knowing that body and wealth do not last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:328":{"book":"naladiyar","num":328,"t":["சிறுகாலை யேதமக்குச் செல்வுழி வல்சி","இறுகிறுகத் தோட்கோப்புக் கொள்ளார் - இறுகிறுகிப்","பின்னறிவாம் என்றிருக்கும் பேதையார் கைகாட்டும்","பொன்னும் புளிவிளிங்கா யாம்."],"p":"In the very earliest time (in early youth) they take not food for the journey which they must make (into another world), tying tightly the wallet on their shoulders ; but tying tightly (their treasure bags), they say, 'In after days we will learn wisdom;' the gold these idiots will indict with their hands (as legacies, when they are speechless, and in the grasp of death) will be as sour vilam fruit.","r":"Fools who do not tie on tightly, early in life, the provisions for the road they must take, but tighten their grip on their wealth and say 'we will see to it later', will find that the gold they point to with their hands turns into a sour vilam fruit.","n":"The commentators fill in a deathbed scene: the dying man, unable to speak, gestures for gold to give away, and his family takes the gesture to mean a sour vilam fruit. Mudaliar quotes a Civaka Cintamani verse telling this story. The quatrain gives only the gesture and the sour fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","marumai","ivaral","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:329":{"book":"naladiyar","num":329,"t":["வெறுமை யிடத்தும் விழிப்பிணிப் போழ்தும்","மறுமை மனத்தாரே யாகி - மறுமையை","ஐந்தை யனைத்தானும் ஆற்றிய காலத்துச்","சிந்தியார் சிற்றறிவி னார்."],"p":"When poor, or when disease's deadly grasp they feel, to other world alone their minds are given ; but when wealth grows, no thought, small as a grain of mustard seed, give they to other world —these souls unwise!","r":"In poverty and in grave illness the small-minded think of the next world, but once they are able to act they give it not a mustard seed's worth of thought.","n":"The irony is that concern for the next life arrives only when one is least able to act on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","aram","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:330":{"book":"naladiyar","num":330,"t":["என்னேமற் றிவ்வுடம்பு பெற்றும் அறம்நினையார்","கொன்னே கழிப்பர்தம் வாழ்நாளை - அன்னோ","அளவிறந்த காதற்றம் ஆருயி ரன்னார்க்","கொளஇழைக்கும் கூற்றமும் கண்டு."],"p":"Though they have gained a human frame, of virtue think they not! In vain they spent their days. Alas! and yet they see death eagerly hasting to snatch away those infinitely dear, like to their very souls! Why is this so? -------------","r":"Alas, though they have gained this human body they do not think of virtue and waste their days, even while they watch Death preparing to take those they love beyond measure, as dear as their own lives.","n":"Shanmuganar calls the human body hard to obtain; the verse's இவ்வுடம்பு implies as much without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:331":{"book":"naladiyar","num":331,"t":["கொலைஞர் உலையேற்றித் தீமடுப்ப ஆமை","நிலையறியா தந்நீர்ப் படிந்தாடி யற்றே","கொலைவல் பெருங்கூற்றம் கோள்பார்ப்ப ஈண்டை","வலையகத்துச் செம்மாப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"While the turtle's murderers have put it into the pot, and kindled fire beneath, it sports unconscious of its fate: such is their worth who joy entangled in life's not, while death, the mighty murderer, waits to seize them.","r":"As a turtle put in a pot by its killers, with the fire lit beneath, plays in the water without knowing its state, so are those who revel in this world's net while Death, the great killer, waits for the moment to seize them.","n":"The water the turtle enjoys is what will kill it, just as the world's pleasures are the net. Mudaliar notes that மாண்பு ('worth') is used ironically.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:332":{"book":"naladiyar","num":332,"t":["பெருங்கட லாடிய சென்றார் ஒருங்குடன்","ஓசை அவிந்தபின் ஆடுது மென்றற்றால்","இற்செய் குறைவினை நீக்கி அறவினை","மற்றறிவாம் என்றிருப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"They went to bathe in the great sea, but cried 'we will wait till all its roar is hushed, then bathe!' Such is their worth who say, 'we will get rid of all our household toils and cares and then we will practise virtue and be wise.'","r":"Those who say they will see to virtue once the household's unfinished tasks are done are like people who went to bathe in the great sea and said they would bathe when its roar had died down.","n":"Household duties, like the noise of the sea, never stop, so the simile turns postponement into permanent refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","ilvaazhkkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:333":{"book":"naladiyar","num":333,"t":["குலந்தவம் கல்வி குடிநம்முப் பைந்தும்","விலங்காமல் எய்தியக் கண்ணும் - நலஞ்சான்ற","மையறு தொல்சீர் உலகம் அறியாமை","நெய்யிலாப் பாற்சோற்றின் நேர்."],"p":"Though a man be of good caste, have performed deeds of ascetic virtue, acquired learning, is of good family and attained a ripe old age, so that in these five points he is faultless; yet his ignorance of what is thoroughly good, without stain, sanctioned by ancient precedent, and of good renown in the world's ways, will render all his advantages like thin, watery milk with boiled rice.","r":"Though one has all five, high caste, austerity, learning, standing and age, without lack, not knowing the good, stainless and anciently honoured உலகம் makes it all like milk-rice without ghee.","n":"The commentators also divide on குடி: household prosperity for Mudaliar, good family for Shanmuganar. Pope's 'thin, watery milk' departs from the Tamil, which has rice without ghee.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kudi","kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உலகம் means the worlds to be reached after this life, heaven and release, so what the fool lacks is knowledge of what leads there.",""],["shanmuganar","உலகம் means the ways of the world, and what the fool lacks is knowledge of how to live by them.",""]],"ch":"n:34"},"n:334":{"book":"naladiyar","num":334,"t":["கன்னனி நல்ல கடையாய மாக்களின்","சொன்னனி தாமுணரா வாயினும் - இன்னினியே","நிற்றல் இருத்தல் கிடத்தல் இயங்குதலேன்","றுற்றவர்க்குத் தாமுதவ லான்."],"p":"Stones are much better than low men ; since, though those (too) are utterly without apprehension of your words, yet they (stones) are of assistance to those that employ them, inasmuch as they at once stand (where they are fixed), remain (where they are put), lie (where they are laid), and remove (when they are moved).","r":"Stones are far better than base people: though they cannot understand words either, they serve whoever comes to them, at once, for standing, sitting, lying and walking on.","n":"The comparison is not about intelligence, which both lack, but about use: the stone at least gives itself to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","payan","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:335":{"book":"naladiyar","num":335,"t":["பெறுவதொன் றின்றியும் பெற்றானே போலக்","கறுவுகொண் டேலாதார் மாட்டும் - கறுவினால்","கோத்தின்னா கூறி உரையாக்கால் பேதைக்கு","நாத்தின்னும் நல்ல சுனைத்து."],"p":"If the fool, though nothing is to be gained (by such conduct), acting as though he had gained something, waxing wroth against those who disdain (or are unable) to contend with him, cannot say and utter forth in his wrath a string of evil words a grievous itching will gnaw his tongue.","r":"Though nothing is gained by it, the fool, as if he had gained something, grows angry even with those who don't deserve it, and if he cannot string harsh words together and say them, a fierce itch gnaws at his tongue.","n":"The targets are read three ways: Mudaliar's ஏலாதார் are those for whom such anger is unfitting, Shanmuganar's (printed under 334) are superiors who are no match for the fool, and Pope's are those who will not or cannot contend with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vekuli","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:336":{"book":"naladiyar","num":336,"t":["தங்கள் மரபில்லார் பின்சென்று தாமவரை","எங்கண் வணக்குதும் என்பவர் - புன்கேண்மை","நற்றளிர்ப் புன்னை மலருங் கடற்சேர்ப்ப","கற்கிள்ளிக் கையிழந் தற்று."],"p":"Lord of the sea-shore where the laurel with its bright foliage flowers! The poor (pretence of) intimacy enjoyed by those who follow after (wealthy but) utterly uncongenial men, and say 'we will bend them to our will,' is like loosing one's hand while digging into a rock.","r":"Those who follow people without good conduct, saying 'we will make them bow to us', have a poor kind of friendship, lord of the shore where the laurel with fine shoots flowers: it is like losing one's hand pinching a rock.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship and cites a Pazhamozhi line saying no one pinches a rock and keeps their hand. Pope supplies 'wealthy' for the people followed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:337":{"book":"naladiyar","num":337,"t":["ஆகா தெனினும் அகத்துநெய் யுண்டாகின்","போகா தெறும்பு புறஞ்சுற்றும் - யாதும்","கொடாஅ ரெனினும் உடையாரைப் பற்றி","விடாஅர் உலகத் தவர்."],"p":"If there be ghi in the pot, though they cannot get at it, the ants will swarm without ceasing around the outside ; and so the people of the world will cling to and not leave the possessors of wealth, though these give them nothing.","r":"If there is ghee inside a pot, ants keep circling its outside without leaving, though they cannot get at it; so people cling to the wealthy and won't leave them, though they give nothing.","n":"The quatrain describes a habit of the world without blaming it in so many words; being placed in this chapter is what marks the habit as folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pedhaimai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:338":{"book":"naladiyar","num":338,"t":["நல்லவை நாடொறும் எய்தார் அறஞ்செய்யார்","இல்லாதார்க் கியாதொன்றும் ஈகலார் - எல்லாம்","இனியார்தோள் சேரார் இசைபட வாழார்","முனியார்கொல் தாம்வாழும் நாள்."],"p":"No good each day they gain; nor deed of virtue do; nothing to needy men impart; nor know they joy of loved ones' sweet embrace ; devoid of fame they live: do such not loathe the days they live?","r":"They gain no good thing day by day, do no virtue, give nothing to the needy, never hold their beloved in their arms, and do not live with renown: will they not come to loathe the days they live?","n":"The list mixes virtue, giving, married love and fame, so the fool fails at every aim of life, not only at renunciation. Mudaliar allows நல்லவை to be read as நல் அவை, a good assembly, and Shanmuganar takes that reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:339":{"book":"naladiyar","num":339,"t":["விழைந்தொருவர் தம்மை வியப்ப ஒருவர்","விழைந்திலேம் என்றிருக்கும் கேண்மை - தழங்குகுரல்","பாய்திரைசூழ் வையம் பயப்பினும் இன்னாதே","ஆய்நலம் இல்லாதார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Friendship with those who are destitute of exquisite goodness, and who, though you cultivate them with affectionate deference, (morosely) say we entertain affection for none,'—though it should yield as its fruit the earth, which the sea with its noisy chime of leaping billows girds around,—would be only affliction.","r":"When one person admires and seeks another who answers 'we don't want you', that friendship with those lacking discerning goodness is painful, even if it should yield the whole earth girdled by the roaring sea.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship, or a false one. The fool is the one who rejects the regard offered, but the warning is for the one who keeps offering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:340":{"book":"naladiyar","num":340,"t":["கற்றனவும் கண்ணகன்ற சாயலும் இற்பிறப்பும்","பக்கத்தார் பாராட்டப் பாடெய்தும் தானுரைப்பின்","மைத்துனர் பல்கி மருந்தின் தணியாத","பித்தனென் றெள்ளப் படும்."],"p":"The learning a man has acquired, his world - renowned excellence, and his nobility of birth, will gain for him respect when the bystanders celebrate them; but if he himself proclaim them, the young kinsmen crowding around will deride him as one afflicted with a mania not to be mitigated by any medicine. -----------","r":"Learning, wide gentleness and good birth win honour when others praise them; if a man praises them himself, his brothers-in-law multiply and mock him as a madman no medicine can cure.","n":"Mudaliar explains மைத்துனர் (brothers-in-law) as mockers: the joking relationship stands for ridicule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:341":{"book":"naladiyar","num":341,"t":["கப்பி கடவதாக் காலைத்தன் வாய்ப்பெயினும்","குப்பை கிளைப்போவாக் கோழிபோல் - மிக்க","கனம்பொதிந்த நூல்விரித்துக் காட்டினும் கீழ்தன்","மனம்புரிந்த வாறே மிகும்."],"p":"The fowl, though each morning duly you scatter broken grain into its very mouth, will ceaselessly scratch in the refuse-heap; so, though you expound and show the base man works of learning, full of weighty wisdom, he will but the more resolutely go on in the way in which his mind finds delight.","r":"As a hen keeps scratching the rubbish heap though grain is poured into its mouth each morning, so the base man, however you lay open books full of weighty meaning for him, goes further along the way his mind likes.","n":"மிகும் suggests excess, not just persistence: Mudaliar glosses it as going beyond bounds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kalvi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:342":{"book":"naladiyar","num":342,"t":["காழாய கொண்டு கசடற்றார் தஞ்சாரல்","தாழாது போவாம் எனஉரைப்பின் - கீழ்தான்","உறங்குவாம் என்றெழுந்து போமாம், அஃதன்றி","மறங்குமாம் மற்றொன் றுரைத்து."],"p":"If you say to the base man, ‘Let us without delay go to seek refuge with faultless sages possessors of mature wisdom,' he will probably get up and make off, exclaiming, 'Let us go and slumber,!’ or he will perhaps demur, and change the subject.","r":"Say to the base man 'let us go without delay to the faultless who hold firm learning', and he gets up and leaves saying 'let's sleep', or else refuses with some other excuse.","n":"The comedy is in the excuses: sleep first, and failing that, anything else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:343":{"book":"naladiyar","num":343,"t":["பெருநடை தாம்பெறினும் பெற்றி பிழையா","தொருநடைய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - பெருநடை","பெற்றக் கடைத்தும் பிறங்கருவி நன்னாட","வற்றாம் ஒருநடை கீழ்."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of swelling torrents! If thoroughly worthy men gain some added dignity, their nature knows no deviation, but they go on in the same even path of virtue ; and although the base obtain promotion, his conduct too changes not, (promotion betters him not.)","r":"Even if they gain great prosperity, the worthy keep one even course without falling from their nature; when the base gain it, the verse says only that they are 'able in one course', lord of the good land of shining waterfalls.","n":"Pope agrees with Shanmuganar that the base man's conduct does not change. Mudaliar takes பெருநடை as wealth and records that others read it as great distinction.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","With prosperity the base man becomes adept at a conduct different from his earlier one.",""],["shanmuganar","With prosperity the base man keeps to his own low course.",""]],"ch":"n:35"},"n:344":{"book":"naladiyar","num":344,"t":["தினையனைத்தே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றுண்டால்","பனையனைத்தா உள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் - பளையனைத்","தென்றும் செயினும் இலங்கருவி நன்னாட","நன்றில நன்றறியார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of glistening torrents ! The excellent will deem any favour done them, though small as a grain of millet, to be large as a palm tree. You may constantly confer favours huge as a palm tree, but they are not benefits, when conferred on those who are not grateful.","r":"If they have enjoyed a good done to them, though it is only the size of a millet grain, the worthy think of it as a palm tree; but goods the size of a palm, done again and again, are no good at all to those who do not know a good, lord of the land of bright waterfalls.","n":"Mudaliar cites Kural 104 and Parimelazhagar's remark that millet and palm are conventional measures of small and great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:345":{"book":"naladiyar","num":345,"t":["பொற்கலத் தூட்டிப் புறந்தரினும் நாய்பிறர்","எச்சிற் கிமையாது பார்த்திருக்கும் - அச்சீர்","பெருமை யுடைத்தாக் கொளினுங்கீழ் செய்யும்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The dog, though you tend it with care, feeding it from a golden vessel, will watch, eye fixed for others' leavings ; even so, although you receive the base as if they were possessed of greatness, the deeds they do will be otherwise (= their real character will be seen by their conduct).","r":"A dog, though reared on food from a golden bowl, watches unblinking for others' leftovers; so even if you treat the base as great, the deeds they do will be different.","n":"The golden bowl is the honour shown: it changes the setting, not the appetite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:346":{"book":"naladiyar","num":346,"t":["சக்கரச் செல்வம் பெறினும் விழுமியோர்","எக்காலும் சொல்லார் மிகுதிச்சொல் - எக்காலும்","முந்திரிமேற் காணி மிகுவதேல் கீழ்தன்னை","இந்திரனா எண்ணி விடும்."],"p":"Though they obtain imperial wealth, the excellent never utter a vaunting word ; but if at any time the base see some small fraction added to his scanty store, he will deem himself great as Indra.","r":"Even with an emperor's wealth the excellent never speak a boastful word, but if a base man gains a காணி on top of a முந்திரி, he thinks himself Indra.","n":"The two words are tiny fractions. Shanmuganar gives முந்திரி as 1/320 and காணி as 1/80; Mudaliar gives காணி as 1/64 and முந்திரி as a quarter of that, and allows the words to be read in the other order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","perumai","selvam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:347":{"book":"naladiyar","num":347,"t":["மைதீர் பசும்பொன்மேல் மாண்ட மணியழுத்திச்","செய்த தெனினுஞ் செருப்புத்தன் காற்கேயாம்","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலால் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Though made of faultless yellow gold, enwrought with choicest pearls, the slipper serves but for one's foot; though the base be deemed prosperous through the wealth they have gained, their baseness will be seen by their actions.","r":"Though a sandal is made of flawless gold set with fine gems, it is still for the foot; so the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"The sandal's material is the wealth and its place is the nature: costly making does not move it from the foot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:348":{"book":"naladiyar","num":348,"t":["கடுக்கெனச் சொல்வற்றாம், கண்ணோட்டம் இன்றாம்","இடுக்கண் பிறர்மாட் டுவக்கும், - அடுத்தடுத்து","வேகம் உடைத்தாம், விறன்மலை நன்னாட","ஏகுமாம் எள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"The base are quick to speak harshly, have no kindly regard, rejoice in others' troubles, fly into rage again and again, go where they please and scorn everyone, lord of the good land of mighty hills.","n":"A plain list with no image, which Mudaliar classes as straightforward description (தன்மை நவிற்சி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kadunjol","kannottam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:349":{"book":"naladiyar","num":349,"t":["பழைய ரிவரென்று பன்னாட்பின் நிற்பின்","உழையினிய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - விழையாதே","கள்ளுயிர்க்கும் நெய்தற் கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","எள்ளுவர் கீழா யவர்."],"p":"Lord of the resounding sea's cool shore, where the Neythal breathes honied fragrance round! The worthy, if men for many days stand waiting in their train will say 'these are old acquaintance,' and will make the place pleasant to them; the base in such circumstances feel no affection, but simply despise them.","r":"If people stand by them for many days, the worthy grow kind to them, thinking 'these are old friends'; the base feel no warmth and simply scorn them, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where the neydal breathes honey.","n":"Mudaliar takes standing by someone as being subordinate and obedient to them, so the test is how each kind treats long-serving dependants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kayamai","saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:350":{"book":"naladiyar","num":350,"t":["கொய்புல் கொடுத்துக் குறைத்தென்றும் தீற்றினும்","வையம்பூண் கல்லா சிறுகுண்டை - ஐயகேள்,","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலாற் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Hear, O Sir! The little bullock-calves will not learn to bear the yoke and draw the chariot, though you give them cut grass, clip them, and constantly rub them down; so, though they have gained wealth, the base will be seen (to be so) by the deeds they do. -----------------","r":"Hear, sir: small bullocks will never learn to wear the yoke and draw the cart however long you feed them cut grass, and the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"Care cannot supply a capacity that is not there; the bullocks are well fed and still small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:351":{"book":"naladiyar","num":351,"t":["ஆர்த்த அறிவினர் ஆண்டிளைய ராயினும்","காத்தோம்பித் தம்மை அடக்குப - மூத்தொறூஉம்","தீத்தொழிலே கன்றித் திரிதந் தெருவைபோல்","போத்தறார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"The thoroughly wise, though young in years, will guard themselves in stern self-repression. The low unwise as age comes on become mature in evil works, and are like reeds wind-shaken, hollow still!","r":"The fully wise restrain and guard themselves even when young in years, while the petty-minded grow more practised in wrongdoing the older they get and, like the எருவை, never lose their hollowness.","n":"Age is set aside as a cause: wisdom doesn't wait for years, and years don't cure folly. Mudaliar gives two senses for both எருவை and போத்து: a reed that never fills its hollow, or a kite that never sheds its faults. Shanmuganar takes the kite and the faults, and Pope the hollow reed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","adakkam","kayamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:352":{"book":"naladiyar","num":352,"t":["செழும்பெரும் பொய்கையுள் வாழினும் என்றும்","வழும்பறுக்க கில்லாவாம் தேரை - வழும்பில்சீர்","நூல்கற்றக் கண்ணும் நுணுக்கமொன் றில்லாதார்","தேர்கிற்கும் பெற்றி அரிது."],"p":"Though frogs flourish long in a rich large lake, they never rid themselves of their slime; so it is hard for those void of fine perception, even when they have learned pure and excellent text-books, to acquire the gift of clear understanding.","r":"Frogs that live in a rich, large pond never rid themselves of their slime; in the same way, those without fine discernment rarely gain the power to examine what they read, even after studying flawless, excellent books.","n":"What is missing is not learning but நுணுக்கம், a subtlety that study doesn't supply. The pond's abundance matches the excellence of the books: neither reaches what is already in the creature. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 351.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:353":{"book":"naladiyar","num":353,"t":["கணமலை நன்னாட. கண்ணின் றொருவர்","குணனேயுங் கூற்ற கா஢தால், - குணனழுங்கக்","குற்றம் உழைநின்று கூறுஞ் சிறியவர்கட்கு","எற்றா லியன்றதோ நா."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of mountain chains ! It is hard to stand before a man, and publish even his praise. How then can mean men's tongues depreciate a man's good qualities and proclaim his faults while standing before him?.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills: it is hard even to speak of a man's virtues when he isn't present, so what are the tongues of petty people made of, that they stand at his side and proclaim his faults until his virtues are spoiled?","n":"Both commentators read கண் இன்று as 'not before his eyes', and Mudaliar rejects splitting it as கண் நின்று. The contrast is then between a decent reluctance to praise someone even behind his back and the brazenness of faulting him beside him. Pope takes the other split and has the praise spoken to the man's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:354":{"book":"naladiyar","num":354,"t":["கோடேந் தகலல்குல் பெண்டிர்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை","சேடியர் போலச் செயல்தேற்றார் - கூடிப்","புதுப்பெருக்கம் போலத்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை காட்டி","மதித்திறப்பர் மற்றை யவர்."],"p":"Chaste women trick not out their charms of womanhood, as is as the wont of the wanton. Like fountains ever fresh those others make display of every charm, and vaunt their beauty as they pass.","r":"Household women do not know how to dress up their womanliness the way their maids do it for them; the other kind, like a fresh flood, put their charms on show themselves, think highly of themselves, and then pass on.","n":"In a chapter on the base the verse works as a figure, and Mudaliar names it பிறிது மொழிதல்: the wise don't praise themselves, and fools vaunt themselves to extract wealth. The verse itself speaks only of the two kinds of women.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","varaivinmagalir","panivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","இறப்பர் means the courtesans leave a man once they have taken all his wealth; the verse is an allegory of fools who praise themselves to get money.",""],["shanmuganar","இறப்பர் means they overstep all bounds, rating themselves highly; he reads the verse as a contrast between the women themselves.",""]],"ch":"n:36"},"n:355":{"book":"naladiyar","num":355,"t":["தளிர்மேலே நிற்பினும் தட்டாமற் செல்லா","உளிநீரார் மாதோ கயவர் - அளிநீரார்க்","கென்னானும் செய்யார் எனைத்தானும் செய்பவே","இன்னாங்கு செய்வார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"The base are like the chisel, that, though it is over the bud (to be sculptured), moves not without some one to strike it. On those who are courteous these confer no boon whatever. They will do everything for those who use violent means.","r":"The base are like a chisel that won't cut in, even set on a tender shoot, unless someone strikes it: they do nothing at all for the kind, and anything at all for those who treat them harshly.","n":"A tender shoot is the softest thing a chisel could meet, so the image says the base yield to nothing gentle. Mudaliar lets அளிநீரார் mean either the compassionate or those whose state calls for pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:356":{"book":"naladiyar","num":356,"t":["மலைநலம் உள்ளும் குறவன் பயந்த","விளைநிலம் உள்ளும் உழவன் சிறந்தொருவர்","செய்தநன் றுள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் கயந்தன்னை","வைததை உள்ளி விடும்."],"p":"The hill-man thinks upon the beauty of his hills; the farmer thinks upon his fields that have yielded him rich crops; the good think on the boons bestowed by worthy men; the base man's thoughts are fixed on abuse he has received.","r":"The hill-dweller thinks of the wealth of his hills and the farmer of the field that yielded; the worthy think of a good someone did them, while the base dwell on the time someone abused them.","n":"The first two figures set up the rule that each mind returns to what it values; the last two say what the worthy and the base value. Mudaliar notes the verse calls the base man கயம், a neuter noun, to show how low he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:357":{"book":"naladiyar","num":357,"t":["ஒருநன்றி செய்தவர்க் கொன்றி யெழுந்த","பிழைநூறும் சான்றோர் பொறுப்பர் - கயவர்க்கு","எழுநூறு நன்றிசெய் தொன்றுதீ தாயின்","எழுநூறும் தீதாய் விடும்."],"p":"The good attach themselves to those that have done them one act of kindness, and forgive a hundred wrongs that arise. The base receive seven hundred benefits, but one wrong will turn them all to evil.","r":"The worthy bear a hundred faults, coming one after another, from someone who once did them a kindness; for the base, if one thing goes wrong after seven hundred kindnesses, all seven hundred turn into harm.","n":"Mudaliar takes the hundred and the seven hundred as standing for 'many' and 'very many'. The asymmetry is the point: for one kind of person a single kindness outweighs a hundred wrongs, and for the other a single wrong cancels seven hundred kindnesses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:358":{"book":"naladiyar","num":358,"t":["ஏட்டைப் பருவத்தும் இற்பிறந்தார் செய்வன்","மோட்டிடத்தும் செய்யார் முழுமக்கள் - கோட்டை","வயிரஞ் செறிப்பினும் வாட்கண்ணாய் பன்றி","செயிர்வேழ மாகுத லின்று."],"p":"Worthless persons even in high estate do not the deeds that men of noble birth perform even in their poverty. Okeen-eyed maid! Men may enring and arm the boar's tusks, but it will not even so become a warlike elephant.","r":"What those of good family do even when weakened by want, the utterly foolish don't do even at the height of their fortune; bright-eyed one, fit a boar's tusks with diamond rings and it still won't become an angry war elephant.","n":"The verse doesn't name the deeds. Pope supplies liberality, and Mudaliar says only 'good deeds'. The base are called முழுமக்கள், which Mudaliar glosses as fools, so the lack here is of sense as well as of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kayamai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:359":{"book":"naladiyar","num":359,"t":["இன்றாதும் இந்நிலையே ஆதும் இனிச்சிறிது","நின்றாதும் என்று நினைத்திருந் - தொன்றி","உரையின் மகிழ்ந்துதம் உள்ளம்வே றாகி","மரையிலையின் மாய்ந்தார் பலர்."],"p":"‘To-day, at once, a little hence, we shall gain our end!’ So many speak and think, and joyously tell it out among their friends ; but soon their eager minds are changed; and they have perished like a lotus leaf.","r":"Many people thought 'today we'll have it, right now, or in a little while', rejoiced as they said so to others, and then, their hopes overturned, perished like lotus leaves.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they expected, and names no base person. Mudaliar supplies both: they waited to become rich without doing good, and withered like the lotus leaf that flourishes and dies where it stands. Shanmuganar's commentary for this quatrain is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","avaa","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:360":{"book":"naladiyar","num":360,"t":["நீருள் பிறந்து நிறம்பசிய தாயினும்","ஈரங் கிடையகத் தில்லாகும் - ஓரும்","நிறைப்பெருஞ் செல்வத்து நின்றக் கடைத்தும்","அறைப்பெருங்கல் அன்னா ருடைத்து."],"p":"Though born in the water, and its hue appear green, the Netti's pith knows no moisture; so in the world are men of amplest wealth whose hearts are hard as stones upon the rocky mountain fell. -------------","r":"Though it is born in water and is green, the netti reed has no moisture in its pith; in the same way the world holds men who stay hard as great rock slabs even while living in full, great wealth.","n":"The image is exact: the plant lives in water and holds none, as these men live among plenty and give none. Mudaliar's gloss on the rock is that it never softens (இளகுதல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:361":{"book":"naladiyar","num":361,"t":["மழைதிளைக்கு மாடமாய் மாண்பமைந்த காப்பாய்","இழைவிளக்கு நின்றிமைப்பின் என்னாம்? - விழைதக்க","மாண்ட மனையாளை இல்லாதான் இல்லகம்","காண்டற் கா஢யதோர் காடு."],"p":"The mansion meets the clouds. A stately band of warders keep watch around. Gems glisten therein like lamps. What then? Where the owner has not a wife of dainty excellence: the house within is a waste, hard to explore.","r":"What use is a mansion where the clouds play, under fine guard, with jewels shining like lamps, if its owner has no admirable wife? That house is a wilderness too grim to look at.","n":"Pope's 'hard to explore' reads காண்டற்கு அரிய as a trackless waste; Mudaliar reads it as a forest too harsh even to look at. Shanmuganar takes இழைவிளக்கு as a wick lamp that never goes out, where Mudaliar has jewels shining like lamps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:362":{"book":"naladiyar","num":362,"t":["வழக்கெனைத்து மில்லாத வாள்வாய்க் கிடந்தும்","இழுக்கினைத் தாம்பெறுவ ராயின் - இழுக்கெனைத்தும்","செய்குறாப் பாணி சிறிதே அச் சின்மொழியார்","கையுறாப் பாணி பெரிது."],"p":"Though compassed round with a faultless guard of swords, ifonce they gain freedom, in little time they are stained with every fault; and long is the time those soft-voiced once spurn every law of right","r":"Even when women are kept under a faultless guard of swords, the time in which soft-spoken women commit no fault is short and the time in which they don't keep to good conduct is long; the verse's condition on this is disputed.","n":"This is one of the collection's harshest lines on women, and its scope depends on the condition in the second line. Mudaliar cites Kural 57 with it, though 57 praises women's own guard rather than doubting it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","He reads the condition as 'even if they get no chance to stray': strict guard can stop the deed, but most women's minds still go astray.",""],["shanmuganar","He keeps the condition as written: the claim applies if women take the faulty way.",""]],"ch":"n:37"},"n:363":{"book":"naladiyar","num":363,"t":["எறியென் றெதிர்நிற்பாள் கூற்றம் சிறுகாலை","அட்டில் புகாதாள் அரும்பிணி - அட்டதனை","உண்டி யுதவாதாள் இல்வாழ்பேய் இம்மூவர்","கொண்டானைக் கொல்லும் படை."],"p":"Death is the wife that stands and dares her spouse to strike! Disease is she who enters not the kitchen betimes! Demon domestic is she who cooks and gives no alms! These three are swords to slay their lords !","r":"The wife who stands up to her husband saying 'strike me' is Death; the one who doesn't go into the kitchen at dawn is an incurable disease; the one who doesn't serve the food she has cooked is a demon in the house; these three are weapons that kill the man who took them.","n":"Pope's 'gives no alms' makes the third fault a failure of charity. Both commentators make it a failure to serve the husband, and Mudaliar adds that she eats the food herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:364":{"book":"naladiyar","num":364,"t":["கடியெனக் கேட்டுங் கடியான், வெடிபட","ஆர்ப்பது கேட்டும் அதுதெளியான் - பேர்த்துமோர்","இற்கொண் டினித஧ருஉம் ஏமுறுதல் என்பவே","கற்கொண் டெறியுந் தவறு."],"p":"He hears (that marriage is another name for) dread, and yet he dreads it not! He hears the fearful funeral drum, but it gives him no sense ! Again to take a wife and to dwell bewildered amidst domestic enjoyments is a fault that merits stoning; sosay (the wise).","r":"He hears marriage called a dread and doesn't give it up; he hears the funeral drum beat loud enough to split heads and still doesn't see; to take a wife again and live lost in pleasure, they say, is the fault of stoning oneself.","n":"Shanmuganar places the drum at the first wife's funeral, which makes this a verse about remarriage. Mudaliar points back to quatrain 56, where கடி names marriage as something to be dreaded. On either reading the verse goes further than a warning about remarrying: it treats household life itself as what the wise tell one to give up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilvaazhkkai","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:365":{"book":"naladiyar","num":365,"t":["தலையே தவமுயன்று வாழ்தல் ஒருவர்க்","கிடையே இனியார்கண் தங்கல் - கடையே","புணராதென் றெண்ணிப் பொருள்நசையால் தம்மை","உணரார்பின் சென்று நிலை."],"p":"The best thing is a life spent in penitential practices. The middle course is to live with dear ones around. The worst of all is, with the thought that we have not enough, through desire of wealth subserviently to follow those who understand us not.","r":"The best way to live is to strive in austerity; the middle way is to dwell with those one loves; the lowest is to follow, out of desire for wealth, people who don't recognise one's worth, even thinking it won't come.","n":"இனியார் is general, 'the dear ones', and both commentators narrow it to wives. Mudaliar reads புணராது என்று எண்ணி as 'even while thinking it won't be gained', which makes the lowest life futile as well as servile; Pope's 'not enough' reads it as discontent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ilvaazhkkai","avaa","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:366":{"book":"naladiyar","num":366,"t":["கல்லாக் கழிப்பர் தலையாயார் நல்லவை","துவ்வாக் கழிப்பர் இடைகள் கடைகள்","இனிதுண்ணோம் ஆரப் பெறேமியாம் என்னும்","முனிவினாற் கண்பா டிலர்."],"p":"","r":"The best pass their time in learning, the middle in enjoying good things, and the lowest lie sleepless with resentment, saying 'we haven't eaten well, we haven't got enough'.","n":"கல்லா here means 'having learned', not 'not learning'. Mudaliar specifies books of spiritual knowledge and goods gained by right means; the verse says only learning and good things. The lowest are marked by craving rather than by poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:367":{"book":"naladiyar","num":367,"t":["செந்நெல்லா லாய செழுமுளை மற்றுமச்","செந்நெல்லே யாகி விளைதலால் - அந்நெல்","வயனிறையக் காய்க்கும் வளவய லூர","மகனறிவு தந்தை யறிவு."],"p":"","r":"Lord of the rich-fielded town where the paddy fills the fields: since the sprout from red paddy grows again into that same red paddy, a son's understanding is his father's.","n":"The verse states inheritance as a law, the way a seed breeds true. It doesn't say whether the son learns from his father or is born like him. Mudaliar's parallel, 'the son tells the father's worth', reads the son as the sign of his lineage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:368":{"book":"naladiyar","num":368,"t":["உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வரும் சான்றோரும் கெட்டுப்","புடைப்பெண்டிர் மக்களும் கீழும் பெருகிக்","கடைக்கால் தலைக்கண்ண தாகிக் குடைக்கால்போல்","கீழ்மேலாய் நிற்கும் உலகு."],"p":"The wealthy men of great possessions and the perfect have perished, while wantons' sons and base men multiply. The lowest takes chief place, and, like the umbrella's handle, this world is upside down!","r":"The wealthy of long standing and the worthy decline, while concubines' sons and base men flourish; what belongs at the feet comes to the head, and like an umbrella's handle the world stands upside down.","n":"Shanmuganar explains the umbrella: its handle points down when the umbrella is open and up when it is folded, so the same world can flip between orders. The complaint mixes moral and social rank, putting the worthy beside old wealth and the base beside concubines' sons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:369":{"book":"naladiyar","num":369,"t":["இனியார்தம் நெஞ்சத்து நோயுரைப்ப அந்நோய்","தணியாத உள்ளம் உடையார் - மணிவரன்றி","வீழும் அருவி விறன்மலை நன்னாட","வாழ்வின் வரைபாய்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills whence streams descend sweeping along pearls! Better men should jump down a precipice than live with a mind not disposed to assuage the pain of the dear ones who tell them of their heart's pain.","r":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills, whose falls sweep down gems: those whose hearts don't ease the pain their dear ones tell them of would do better to leap from a cliff than to live.","n":"The verse doesn't require them to remove the pain, only to be moved by it; a heart unmoved disqualifies a life. Mudaliar takes இனியார் as friends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arul","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:370":{"book":"naladiyar","num":370,"t":["புதுப்புனலும் பூங்குழையார் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","விதுப்புற நாடின்வே றல்ல - புதுப்புனலும்","மாரி அறவே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","வாரி அறவே அறும்."],"p":"‘The mountain freshed, and the love of those adorned With pendant jewels rare examined calmly prove To differ nought: that fails when rains that feed it fail; And love of these fails too, when income fails !’ ------------","r":"Fresh floodwater and the friendship of women with fine earrings, examined calmly, are no different: the flood ends when the rain ends, and their love ends when the income ends.","n":"Mudaliar records a second reading of விதுப்பு அற, 'without desire', which would make the examiner a man no longer infatuated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:371":{"book":"naladiyar","num":371,"t":["விளக்கொளியும் வேசையர் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","துளக்கற நாடின்வே றல்ல - விளக்கொளியும்","நெய்யற்ற கண்ணே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","கையற்ற கண்ணே அறும்."],"p":"The lamp's light and harlot's love examined well are seen to differ not a whit; the lamp's light goes out when the feeding oil is consumed ! and the harlot's love is spent when the lavish hand has spent its wealth.","r":"The lamp's light and the friendship of harlots, examined steadily, are no different: the light ends when the oil runs out, and their love ends when the hand is empty.","n":"Mudaliar takes கை, the hand, as the money held in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:372":{"book":"naladiyar","num":372,"t":["அங்கோட் டகலல்குல் ஆயிழையாள் நம்மோடு","செங்கோடு பாய்துமே என்றாள்மன் - செங்கோட்டின்","மேற்காணம் இன்மையான் மேவா தொழிந்தாளே","காற்கால்நோய் காட்டிக் கலுழ்ந்து."],"p":"She (of enticing beauty), adorned with choice jewels, said forsooth, 'I will leap with you down the steep precipice;' but on the very brow of the precipice, because I had no money, she, weeping and pointing to her aching feet, withdrew and left me alone!","r":"The woman with fine jewels said she would leap with us from the high peak; but at the peak, since we had no gold, she showed a pain in her feet, wept, and wouldn't come on.","n":"The speaker is the dupe himself, and the promise he recalls is a lovers' death pact. Mudaliar takes the foot pain as a lie and the tears as feigned; the verse says only that she showed the pain and wept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:373":{"book":"naladiyar","num":373,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அமரர் தொழப்படுஞ்","செங்கண்மா லாயினும் ஆகமன்- தங்கைக்","கொடுப்பதொன் றில்லாரைக் கொய்தளி ரன்னார்","விடுப்பர்தங் கையால் தொழுது."],"p":"Though he be Mal, the fiery-eyed, whom in the heaven's fair homes immortals worship, if in hand he bring no gift the women tender as the buds men cull, will straight dismiss him, bowing low with folded hands.","r":"Even if he were the red-eyed Mal whom the gods of the wide sky worship, women tender as plucked shoots would join their hands in worship and send away a man with nothing in his hand to give.","n":"The joke lies in the gesture: hands joined as if in worship are the hands that dismiss him. Mudaliar takes மால் as Tirumal, with Indra as an alternative; Shanmuganar has Indra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:374":{"book":"naladiyar","num":374,"t":["ஆணமில் நெஞ்சத் தணிநீலக் கண்ணார்க்குக்","காணமி லாதார் கடுவனையர் - காணவே","செக்கூர்ந்து கொண்டாரும் செய்த பொருளுடையார்","அக்காரம் அன்னார் அவர்க்கு."],"p":"To the damsels of loveless hearts, whose eyes are as beauteous as blue water-lilies, those who have no wealth are as poison! Even those who have turned the (oil) press, in sight of all men, if they have wealth, are as sugar to them.","r":"To women with loveless hearts and lovely blue-lily eyes, men without money are poison; but even a man seen by all driving an oil press is sugar to them if he has earned wealth.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the oil press as a low trade. The verse says money erases even that rank in their eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:375":{"book":"naladiyar","num":375,"t":["பாம்பிற் கொருதலை காட்டி ஒருதலை","தேம்படு தெண்கயத்து மீன்காட்டும் - ஆங்கு","மலங்கன்ன செய்கை மகளிர்தோள் சேர்வர்","விலங்கன்ன வெள்ளறிவி னார்."],"p":"The silly ones, who are as beasts, seek the embraces of women who are like the eel, which shows one head to the snake, and another head to the fish, in the sweet clear lake : (are of a double and deceitful nature).","r":"Like the eel that shows one end to the snake and the other to the fish in the clear, sweet pond, women of double conduct are embraced by the dull-witted, who are like beasts.","n":"Mudaliar explains the eel's trick as matching each man's nature and showing him love. The verse turns part of the blame on the men, who are the beasts. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 374.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:376":{"book":"naladiyar","num":376,"t":["பொத்தநூற் கல்லும் புணர்பிரியா அன்றிலும்போல்","நித்தலும் நம்மைப் பிரியலம் என்றுரைத்த","பொற்றொடியும் போர்த்தகர்க்கோ டாயினாள் நன்னெஞ்சே","நிற்றியோ போதியோ நீ."],"p":"‘We will never part, like the precious stone strung on its thread, or the Andril,' said the damsel with golden bracelets: she has now become the horn of a fighting ram (she angrily repels me). Dear heart! dost linger still, or wilt thou go?","r":"The woman of golden bangles who said 'we'll never part from you', like a gem strung on its thread and the anril birds that never separate, has turned away like a fighting ram's horn; my good heart, will you stay, or will you go?","n":"The speaker turns from her to his own heart, which is still with her. Both commentators take the last line as 'will you stay with her or come with me?', so the heart is a separate party the man has to win back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","nenju","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:377":{"book":"naladiyar","num":377,"t":["ஆமாபோல் நக்கி அவர்கைப் பொருள்கொண்டு","சேமாபோல் குப்புறூஉஞ் சில்லைக்கண் அன்பினை","ஏமாந் தெமதென் றிருந்தார் பெறுபவே","தாமாம் பலரால் நகை."],"p":"Those who fondly reckon upon the devoted love of the worthless wanton, that, like the wild ox, licks the hand and despoils men of their wealth, and then, like the buffalo, bounds away, shall suffer the deserved ridicule of many.","r":"Those who are fooled into thinking theirs the love of a worthless woman who licks like a wild cow, takes the wealth from their hands, and then lies down like a balking ox, will be laughed at by many.","n":"Mudaliar cites a belief that the wild cow's lick pleases at first and kills later. குப்புறூஉம் is either lying face down, as Mudaliar takes it first and Shanmuganar takes it, or bounding away, as in Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:378":{"book":"naladiyar","num":378,"t":["ஏமாந்த போழ்தின் இனியார்போன் றின்னாராய்த்","தாமார்ந்த போதே தகர்கோடாம் - மானோக்கின்","தந்நெறிப் பெண்டிர் தடமுலை சேராரே,","செந்நெறிச் சேர்துமென் பார்."],"p":"Those whose avowed purpose it is to walk in the way of rectitude seek not the embrace of the fascinating, fawn-eyed damsels, who walk in a way of their own' who are pleasant when gratified with gifts, and when they are filled( =when no more gifts are to be expected) are like the horn of the fighting ram.","r":"Those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace the large breasts of doe-eyed women who go their own way, women who seem kind while a man is infatuated and turn harsh as a ram's horn 'once they are full'; who is full is disputed.","n":"Mudaliar glosses செந்நெறி as the path to liberation, which makes avoiding these women a renunciant's discipline; Shanmuganar has only 'the good way'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","ozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","தாம் ஆர்ந்தபோது refers to the men: when, now poor, they come of their own desire, the women turn harsh.",""],["shanmuganar","It refers to the women: once they are filled with a man's wealth, they turn harsh.",""]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:379":{"book":"naladiyar","num":379,"t":["ஊறுசெய் நெஞ்சந்தம் உள்ளடக்கி ஒண்ணுதலார்","தேறா மொழிந்த மொழிகேட்டுத் - தேறி","எமரென்று கொள்வாரும் கொள்பவே யார்க்கும்","தமரல்லர் தம்உடம்பி னார்."],"p":"Let those accept (wantons' false love), who take them as their own believing the words uttered to inspire belief, by the bright-browed ones, who keep concealed within the cruelty that lurks in their heart. Those whose bodies are their sole wealth belong to none, (i. e. they have no souls to give!).","r":"Some do take bright-browed women as their own, trusting words spoken to be believed by women who hide their harmful intent; but such women belong to no one except their own bodies.","n":"Pope adds 'they have no souls to give'. Mudaliar's gloss is narrower: their attachment reaches only as far as their own bodies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:380":{"book":"naladiyar","num":380,"t":["உள்ளம் ஒருவன் உழையதா ஒண்ணுதலார்","கள்ளத்தாற் செய்யுங் கருத்தெல்லாந் - தெள்ளி","அறிந்த விடத்தும் அறியாராம் பாவம்","செறிந்த உடம்பி னவர்."],"p":"Though the dupes clearly discern and know the guileful intentions entertained by the bright browed ones, even where their minds are (apparently) set upon some persons, they whose bodies are full of sin do not recognise it! ---------","r":"The scheming of bright-browed women whose minds are with another man is clearly known, and still those whose bodies are full of sin don't grasp it; who it is that knows is disputed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The dupes themselves examine and know the guile, yet don't grasp that the women should be shunned.",""],["shanmuganar","The world knows the guile clearly; the sinful fools alone don't know it.",""]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:381":{"book":"naladiyar","num":381,"t":["அரும்பெறற் கற்பின் அயிராணி யன்ன","பெரும்பெயர்ப் பெண்டி ரெனினும் - விரும்பிப்","பெறுநசையால் பின்னிற்பா ரின்மையே பேணும்","நறுநுதலாள் நன்மைத் துணை."],"p":"Though women live famed as Ayirani for rarest gift of chastity, absence of men that stand enamoured of their charms is a help in way of good to those of fragrant brow who guard themselves.","r":"Even among women as famous as Indrani for a chastity hard to attain, the fragrant-browed woman who keeps the quality that leaves no man following her in desire is the good companion.","n":"Pope reads it differently: the absence of admirers is a help to women who guard themselves, as though even the chaste benefit from not being tempted. Both commentators make the absence her own doing. Indrani is the standard of the comparison, so the claim is strong: even such women are measured by this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:382":{"book":"naladiyar","num":382,"t":["குடநீரட் டுண்ணும் இடுக்கட் பொழுதும்","கடனீ ரறவுண்ணும் கேளிர் வரினும்","கடனீர்மை கையாறாக் கொள்ளு மடமொழி","மாதர் மனைமாட்சி யாள்."],"p":"her duties with kindness of speech. When in the straitened time they cook and eat with but one pot of water on the hearth,— if relatives arrive enough to drain the sea, the soft-voiced dame, the glory of her home, well fulfils each seemly duty.","r":"Even in hard times, when the household cooks with a single pot of water, and even if kin arrive enough to drink the sea dry, the soft-spoken woman who keeps her duties as her settled way is the glory of the home.","n":"The two hyperboles push in opposite directions, one pot against a sea, and her duty holds between them. Shanmuganar names the duty as hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vaazhkkaithunai","inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:383":{"book":"naladiyar","num":383,"t":["நாலாறும் ஆறாய் நனிசிறிதாய் எப்புறனும்","மேலாறு மேலுறை சோரினும் - மேலாய","வல்லாளாய் வாழும்ஊர் தற்புகழு மாண்கற்பின்","இல்லாள் அமர்ந்ததே இல்."],"p":"On every side the narrow dwelling lies open, on every part the rain drips down; yet, if the dame has noble gifts, praised by townsfolk for her modest worth, such a housewife's blest abode is indeed a home! The wife : 'placens uxor.' She is sweet to the eye, and adorned in the way that a lover loves; she enforces awe: her virtue shames the village folk; she is submissive; but in fitting place is stern, yet sweetly relents :—such a soft-voiced dame is a wife.","r":"Though the house is open on all four sides, tiny, and dripping with rain from the roof everywhere, where the excellent wife lives, capable in the highest virtues and praised by the town she lives in, that alone is a home.","n":"Mudaliar draws the consequence the verse leaves unsaid, that other houses are forests, which completes the pair with 361.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","karpu","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:384":{"book":"naladiyar","num":384,"t":["கட்கினியாள், காதலன் காதல் வகைபுனைவாள்,","உட்குடையாள், ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள், - உட்கி","இடனறிந் தூடி இனிதின் உணரும்","மடமொழி மாதராள் பெண்."],"p":"","r":"She is a wife who is lovely to the eye, adorns herself as her husband loves, holds him in awe, has the modesty that is shy before the town, and, in awe of him, knows when to sulk and relents sweetly: the soft-spoken woman.","n":"Mudaliar gives two senses of ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள்: one who is shy before the townsfolk, or one whose conduct puts the townsfolk to shame. The translation attached to 383 in Pope's text takes the second. Sulking is counted among a good wife's virtues, provided it is well timed and quickly ended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:385":{"book":"naladiyar","num":385,"t":["எஞ்ஞான்றும் எங்கணவர் எந்தோள்மேற் சேர்ந்தெழினும்","அஞ்ஞான்று கண்டேம்போல் நாணுதுமால் - எஞ்ஞான்றும்","என்னை கெழீஇயினர் கொல்லோ பெருள்நசையால்","பன்மார்பு சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்."],"p":"‘Whenever our spouse takes us in his embrace, we feel a timid shame as if we saw him then for the first time and they (wantons) daily, through desire of gain, submit to the embraces of many! How can this be?'","r":"However often our husband rises from our shoulders, we feel as shy as if we were seeing him for the first time; how, then, do those who embrace many men's chests for gain do it every day?","n":"The wife speaks with wonder rather than scorn: she can't imagine the life she sets against her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","karpu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:386":{"book":"naladiyar","num":386,"t":["உள்ளத் துணர்வுடையான் ஓதிய நூலற்றால்","வள்ளன்மை பூண்டான்கண் ஒண்பொருள் - தெள்ளிய","ஆண்மகன் கையில் அயில்வாள் அனைத்தரோ","நாணுடையாள் பெற்ற நலம்."],"p":"As the scroll that he reads, whose inmost heart well understands it, is goodly wealth with him who is graced by a generous spirit. As a keen weapon in the hand of a clear-souled hero, is the loveliness that a modest woman owns.","r":"Like the learning of one with understanding in his heart, like bright wealth in the hands of a generous man, like a sharp sword in the hand of a clear-minded hero, is the beauty a modest woman has.","n":"The verse gives three similes and no explanation. Mudaliar supplies one: learning and a giver's wealth serve everyone, but a hero's sword destroys whoever stands against it, so a modest woman's beauty serves her husband alone and ruins any other who desires it. Shanmuganar pairs the first two similes as both useful and calls the sword only notable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:387":{"book":"naladiyar","num":387,"t":["கருங்கொள்ளும் செங்கொள்ளும் தூணிப் பதக்கென்று","ஒருங்கொப்பக் கொண்டானாம் ஊரன் - ஒருங்கொவ்வா","நன்னுதலார்த் தோய்ந்த வரைமார்பன் நீராடாது","என்னையும் தோய வரும்."],"p":"'The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, as if they were all the same! He, whose chest is broad as a mountain, having associated with the beautiful browed ones , — inferior to me! — unpurified, seeks my society too!'","r":"The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, all as one; the mountain-chested man who embraced bright-browed women unlike me comes, without bathing, to embrace me too.","n":"The grain proverb is the wife's charge of indiscrimination: he treats the courtesans and his wife as one measure. Mudaliar says the verse shows the wife's own purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:388":{"book":"naladiyar","num":388,"t":["கொடியவை கூறாதி பாண. நீ கூறின்","அடிபைய இட்டொதுங்கிச் சென்று - துடியின்","இடக்கண் அனையம்யாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","வலக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"‘O minstrel, utter not cruel words, or if thou utter them, softly draw back thy step, and go to utter them to those who are like the lute's right side; for we, to the lord of the town, are as its left.'","r":"Minstrel, don't speak cruel words; if you must, step softly aside and go: to the lord of the town we are like the drum's left face, so tell it to those who are like its right face.","n":"The right face of the துடி is struck and the left isn't, as Mudaliar explains: the wife calls herself the side that is no longer played. The minstrel is the husband's go-between.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:389":{"book":"naladiyar","num":389,"t":["சாய்ப்பறிக்க நீர்திகழும் தண்வய லூரன்மீது","ஈப்பறக்க நொந்தேனும் யானேமன் - தீப்பறக்கத்","தாக்கி முலைபொருத தண்சாந் தணியகலம்","நோக்கி யிருந்தேனும் யான்."],"p":"'I am she, forsooth, that felt a pang when a fly alighted on the lord of the town, surrounded by cool rice-fields over which the waters gleam, where they pluck the rich grass! And I am she who have lived to look upon his breast adorned with cool sandal-wood paste, which has been warmly embraced by others.","r":"I am the one who grieved if a fly so much as flew over the lord of the cool town where water gleams once the reeds are pulled; and I am the one who has sat looking at his chest, its cool sandal paste smeared by breasts that struck it till sparks flew.","n":"The repeated 'I am the one' sets two times side by side without comment, and the grief lies in the gap between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:390":{"book":"naladiyar","num":390,"t":["அரும்பவிழ் தாரினான் எம்அருளும் என்று","பெரும்பொய் உரையாதி, பாண - கரும்பின்","கடைக்கண் அனையம்நாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","இடைக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"'Minstrel , utter not a gross falsehood, saying that he who wears a garland of opening buds will favour us!’ ‘To the lord of the town we are as the (tasteless) tip of the sugar-cane ; therefore, tell (such a tale) to them who are like its middle (sweet, juicy) joints.'","r":"Minstrel, don't tell the great lie that the man with the garland of opening buds will be kind to us; to the lord of the town we are like the end joints of the sugar-cane, so tell it to those who are like the middle joints.","n":"Mudaliar gives the situation: the husband, back from a courtesan and refused, sends the minstrel to soothe her, and she mocks the messenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:391":{"book":"naladiyar","num":391,"t":["முயங்காக்காற் பாயும் பசலைமற் றூடி","உயங்காக்கால் உப்பின்றாம் காமம் - வயங்கோதம்","நில்லாத் திரையலைக்கும் நீள்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","புல்லாப் புலப்பதோர் ஆறு."],"p":"'Lord of the cool shore of the deep bay, where the gleaming ocean's restless billows beat! 'If there be no fond embrace, a sickly hue will spread itself (over her face); and, if there be no lovers' quarrels, love will lack its zest. ‘To embrace and disagree is the one way (of love).'","r":"Lord of the cool shore with long backwaters where the bright sea's restless waves beat: without embrace, pallor spreads; without sulking and pining, love has no salt; embracing and then sulking is one way.","n":"Mudaliar takes உப்பு as sweetness and compares the Sanskrit theorists, who call separation the sweeter mode. The verse sets pallor and saltlessness as twin dangers: love needs both union and its interruption.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:392":{"book":"naladiyar","num":392,"t":["தம்மமர் காதலர் தார்சூழ் அணியகலம்","விம்ம முயங்கும் துணையில்லார்க் - கிம்மெனப்","பெய்ய எழிலி முழங்கும் திசையெல்லாம்","நெய்தல் அறைந்தன்ன நீர்த்து."],"p":"‘To those who were wont to strain in close embrace their own beloved whose broad breast was girt with garlands, when the rains patter down, it is as though one beat the funeral drum through all the regions where the muttering of the thunder is heard from out the clouds; since they are deprived of their loved one's society.'","r":"For women without the companion whose garlanded chest they would embrace till it swelled, the clouds thundering in every direction as the rain comes down sound like the beat of a funeral drum.","n":"The verse doesn't say why the rain hurts. Mudaliar does: the man promised to come back by the rainy season, so the rains arriving without him are the sign of a broken word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:393":{"book":"naladiyar","num":393,"t":["கம்மஞ்செய் மாக்கள் கருவி ஒடுக்கிய","மம்மர்கொள் மாலை மலராய்ந்து பூத்தொடுப்பாள்","கைம்மாலை இட்டுக் கலுழ்ந்தாள் துணையில்லார்க்கு","இம்மாலை என்செய்வ தென்று."],"p":"At wildering eventide, when workmen all put by their tools, she culled choice flowers, and wove a gay garland ; — then let it fall from her hands, and wailed, 'What can this wreath avail to me who weep alone?’","r":"In the bewildering evening, when craftsmen have put away their tools, she was choosing flowers and weaving a garland; she threw down the garland in her hand and wept: 'What use is this garland to those without a companion?'","n":"The verse ends on இம்மாலை, which can mean 'this garland' or 'this evening'. The commentators take it as the garland, but the double sense may be intended. Mudaliar has the friend report the scene to the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:394":{"book":"naladiyar","num":394,"t":["செல்சுடர் நோக்கிச் சிதரா஢க்கண் கொண்டநீர்","மெல்விரல் ஊழ்தெறியா விம்மித்தன் - மெல்விரலின்","நாள்வைத்து நங்குற்றம் எண்ணுங்கொல், அந்தோதன்","தோள்வைத் தணைமேல் கிடந்து."],"p":"","r":"Watching the setting sun, flicking away again and again with soft fingers the tears in her red-streaked eyes, sobbing, does she, alas, count the days on her soft fingers and reckon up my faults, lying on the bed with her arm for a pillow?","n":"Mudaliar has the man speak this to his charioteer on the way home, imagining her grief, and cites Kural 1261. 'Our faults' turns her counting of days into a reckoning of his wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:395":{"book":"naladiyar","num":395,"t":["கண்கயல் என்னும் கருத்தினால் காதலி","பின்சென்றது அம்ம சிறுசிரல் - பின்சென்றும்","ஊக்கி யெழுந்தும் எறிகல்லா ஒண்புருவம்","கோட்டிய வில்வாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"‘The little kingfisher seeing the eyes of my beloved (as she was disporting in the tank) and taking them for carp, followed her; but though it followed, and poised itself aloft, it darted not down, recognising her gleaming brow bent above them as a bow!","r":"Taking my beloved's eyes for carp, the little kingfisher followed her; but though it followed and rose eager to strike, it didn't dive, recognising her bright brow as a drawn bow.","n":"Mudaliar calls it மயக்கவணி, the figure of mistaken identity, and has the man say it in wonder to his friend. Pope adds that the man is showing his own timid reverence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:396":{"book":"naladiyar","num":396,"t":["அரக்காம்பல் நாறும்வாய் அம்மருங்கிற் கன்னோ","பரற்கானம் ஆற்றின கொல்லோ - அரக்கார்ந்த","பஞ்சிகொண் டுட்டினும் பையெனப் பையெனவென்று","அஞ்சிப்பின் வாங்கும் அடி."],"p":"'When I applied the (softest) cotton soaked in the red dye to the foot of her whose waist is lovely, and whose mouth breathes the fragrance of the red water-lily, did she not cry \"gently, gently,\" and shrinking draw it back? And oh! has it endured the stony, desert path? '","r":"Feet that drew back in fear, crying 'gently, gently', even when red dye was applied with soft cotton: have they, alas, borne the stony desert path, the feet of my girl with lily-scented lips and lovely waist?","n":"Mudaliar identifies the speaker as the girl's mother, who has learned that her daughter has gone with her lover through the wilderness. The verse sets the softest touch the mother knows against the hardest ground she can imagine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:397":{"book":"naladiyar","num":397,"t":["ஓலைக் கணக்கர் ஒலியடங்கு புன்செக்கர்","மாலைப் பொழுதில் மணந்தார் பிரிவுள்ளி","மாலை பரிந்திட் டழுதாள் வனமுலைமேல்","கோலஞ்செய் சாந்தந் திமிர்ந்து."],"p":"When those who con the palm leaf scrolls had ceased, in eve's din twilight hour, she thought of her absent spouse! and weeping plucked the flowers from out her wreath, and brushed the odorous sandal from her lovely breast!","r":"In the dim red evening, when the palm-leaf accountants fall silent, she thought of the parting of the man who married her, tore off her garland, and wept, rubbing away the sandal paste that adorned her lovely breasts.","n":"Mudaliar records two settings: the friend describing her grief in his absence, or the friend telling the man she won't consent to his going. The second makes the parting still to come. He also reports a view that the silenced voices are schoolchildren reciting, which Shanmuganar adopts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:398":{"book":"naladiyar","num":398,"t":["கடக்கருங் கானத்துக் காளைபின் நாளை","நடக்கவும் வல்லையோ என்றி - சுடர்த்தொடீஇ","பெற்றா னொருவன் பெருங்குதிரை அந்நிலையே","கற்றான் அஃதூரும் ஆறு."],"p":"'Thou hast said, O maiden with burnished brace - lets ! have you strength to walk on the morrow after your beloved? When one (a warrior) has obtained a splendid horse, that very instant he has learned how to ride it! (his enthusiasm teaches him).","r":"You ask, friend of the bright bangles, whether I can walk tomorrow behind my young bull of a man through the forest that is hard to cross; a man who gets a great horse learns to ride it that very moment.","n":"The answer is a proverb: the ability comes with the thing. Mudaliar spells out the hint, that following one's husband is not hard. Pope's 'his enthusiasm teaches him' is his own explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ookkam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:399":{"book":"naladiyar","num":399,"t":["முலைக்கண்ணும் முத்தும் முழுமெய்யும் புல்லும்","இலக்கணம் யாதும் அறியேன் - கலைக்கணம்","வேங்கை ஦வ்ருஉம் நெறிசெலிய போலும்என்","தீம்பாவை செய்த குறி."],"p":"‘My breast, my necklace of pearls, and my whole body she embraced ! I knew not what it portended. It was the sign, it seems, made by my lovely one that she was about to set out on the path where the herds of antelopes flee in fright from the tiger.'","r":"I didn't understand the sign at all when she embraced me so that my nipples, my pearls and my whole body were pressed; it seems the sign my sweet doll made meant she was about to take the path where herds of deer flee from the tiger.","n":"Mudaliar has the mother speak after her daughter's flight. The embrace was a farewell that only the leaving gave meaning to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:400":{"book":"naladiyar","num":400,"t":["கண்மூன் றுடையானும் காக்கையும் பையரவும்","என்னீன்ற யாயும் பிழைத்ததென் - பொன்னீன்ற","கோங்கரும் பன்ன முலையாய் பொருள்வயின்","பாங்கனார் சென்ற நெறி."],"p":"The triple-eyed (Civan), the crow, the hooded snake; the mother that bare me: what have these done amiss ? Maiden whose bosom bears the Gongu buds, all gold! The way my lover went for wealth is my pain.’","r":"The three-eyed one, the crow, the hooded snake, the mother who bore me: what wrong did they do? Friend with breasts like golden kongu buds, it is the road my lover took for wealth.","n":"The verse names four suspects and acquits them without saying why. Both commentators supply the myths: Siva burned Kama and let him live again, the crow raised the koel's chick, the snake swallowed the moon and released it, and the mother let her live, so each lies behind one torment of separation. On that reading she refuses the easy targets and blames the one real cause, the journey for wealth. The verse closes the Naladiyar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"ac:1":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":1,"t":["நன்றி யறிதல் பொறையுடைமை இன்சொல்லோடு","இன்னாத எவ்வுயிர்க்கும் செய்யாமை கல்வியோடு","ஒப்புரவு ஆற்ற அறிதல் அறிவுடைமை","நல்லினத் தாரோடு நட்டல் இவைஎட்டும்","சொல்லிய ஆசார வித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names eight things as the seed from which the conduct called ஆசாரம் grows: gratitude, forbearance, sweet speech, doing nothing painful to any living being, learning, knowing how to do oppuravu fully, wisdom, and friendship with good company.","n":"The book that goes on to list rules of bathing and eating opens by rooting them in inner virtues; வித்து makes the ritual rules the plant and the ethics the seed, so the order of the work is itself a claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","nanri","kollaamai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 1","lb":"AC1","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["நன்றி யறிதல்","knowing a kindness received is a seed of ஆசாரம்",1],["பொறையுடைமை","having forbearance is a seed of ஆசாரம்",1],["இன்சொல்","sweet speech is a seed of ஆசாரம்",1],["இன்னாத எவ்வுயிர்க்கும் செய்யாமை","not doing painful things to any living being is a seed of ஆசாரம்",2],["கல்வி","learning is a seed of ஆசாரம்",2],["ஒப்புரவு ஆற்ற அறிதல்","knowing how to carry out oppuravu, help to the community, in full is a seed of ஆசாரம்",3],["அறிவுடைமை","possessing wisdom is a seed of ஆசாரம்",3],["நல்லினத் தாரோடு நட்டல்","making friends with those of good company is a seed of ஆசாரம்",4]],"cw":"இவைஎட்டும்","ch":"ac:1"},"ac:2":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":2,"t":["பிறப்பு நெடுவாழ்க்கை செல்வம் வனப்பு","நிலக்கிழமை மீக்கூற்றம் கல்விநோ யின்மை","இலக்கணத்தால் இவ்வெட்டும் எய்துப என்றும்","ஒழக்கம் பிழையா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who never fail in conduct gain eight things by right: good birth, long life, wealth, beauty, ownership of land, high renown, learning and freedom from disease.","n":"Birth heads the list of rewards, so good birth is treated as something conduct earns, presumably in a later life, rather than as the ground of conduct; the verse does not say which life the eight arrive in. ஒழக்கம் on line 4 is read as ஒழுக்கம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","pirappu","selvam","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 2","lb":"AC2","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறப்பு","good birth is gained by those who never fail in conduct",1],["நெடுவாழ்க்கை","long life is gained by them",1],["செல்வம்","wealth is gained by them",1],["வனப்பு","beauty is gained by them",1],["நிலக்கிழமை","lordship over land is gained by them",2],["மீக்கூற்றம்","high renown is gained by them",2],["கல்வி","learning is gained by them",2],["நோ யின்மை","freedom from disease is gained by them",2]],"cw":"இவ்வெட்டும்","ch":"ac:1"},"ac:3":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":3,"t":["தக்கிணை வேள்வி தவம்கல்வி இந்நான்கும்","முப்பால் ஒழுக்கினால் காத்துய்க்க - உய்க்காக்கால்","எப்பாலும் ஆகா கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that gifts to priests, sacrifice, penance and learning are to be kept and carried through by the threefold discipline, and that if they are not, they come to nothing on any side and perish.","n":"முப்பால் ஒழுக்கு is not explained in the verse; it can be the discipline of mind, speech and body, or conduct in the three aims of aram, porul and inbam. On either reading the claim is that rite and penance are void without conduct to hold them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","thavam","kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 3","lb":"AC3","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தக்கிணை","the gift to priests perishes unless guarded by threefold conduct",1],["வேள்வி","sacrifice perishes unless guarded by threefold conduct",1],["தவம்","penance perishes unless guarded by threefold conduct",1],["கல்வி","learning perishes unless guarded by threefold conduct",1]],"cw":"இந்நான்கும்","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period on the Vedic acts of fee-giving and sacrifice and how they are kept fruitful"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:4":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":4,"t":["வைகறை யாமம் துயிலெழுந்து தான்செய்யும்","நல்லறமும் ஒண்பொருளும் சிந்தித்து வாய்வதில்","தந்தையும் தாயும் தொழுதெழுக என்பதே","முந்தையோர் கண்ட முறை"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports the order the ancients saw: to wake in the last watch before dawn, think over the good deeds and the honest wealth one will pursue that day, and rise worshipping father and mother.","n":"Aram and porul are planned before rising, and the first act of worship is to the parents, not to a god. வாய்வதில் on line 2 is not clear to me; it may mean 'in what is fitting' or 'as far as it comes to hand'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","vazhipaadu","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 4","lb":"AC4","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"an order-of-the-day rule of the period: the hour of waking and the first act of the day"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:5":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":5,"t":["எச்சிலார் தீண்டார் பசுப்பார்ப்பார் தீத்தேவர்","உச்சந் தலையோடு இவைஎன்ப யாவரும்","திட்பத்தால் தீண்டாப் பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists five things that, it says, no one in a state of எச்சில் touches: the cow, brahmins, fire, the gods and the crown of one's own head.","n":"பார்ப்பார் is a birth-group term; the verse sets brahmins beside the cow, fire and the gods as things an impure hand must not reach, so the rule ranks people by birth as well as regulating purity. The crown of the head is the one item on one's own body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theettu","thooimai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 5","lb":"AC5","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["பசு","the cow is not touched by one in எச்சில்",1],["பார்ப்பார்","brahmins are not touched by one in எச்சில்",1],["தீ","fire is not touched by one in எச்சில்",1],["தேவர்","the gods are not touched by one in எச்சில்",1],["உச்சந் தலை","the crown of the head is not touched by one in எச்சில்",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","caste"],"a pollution rule of the period: what the unclean hand is barred from touching, with brahmins ranked among sacred things"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:6":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":6,"t":["எச்சிலார் நோக்கார் புலைதிங்கள் ஞாயிறுநாய்","தக்கவீழ் மீனோடே இவ்வைந்தும் தெற்றென","நன்கறிவார் நாளும் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists five things those in a state of எச்சில் do not look at: புலை, the moon, the sun, a dog and a falling star, and says those who know this well keep it daily and promptly.","n":"புலை is a caste term here most likely naming the outcaste person, set in a list with a dog and the heavenly bodies as sights barred to the unclean; it could also be read as an impure thing such as flesh, which is why the verse is marked uncertain. தக்கவீழ் மீன் is read as a falling star.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theettu","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 6","lb":"AC6","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["புலை","the outcaste person, or on another reading an impure thing, is not looked at by one in எச்சில்",1],["திங்கள்","the moon is not looked at by one in எச்சில்",1],["ஞாயிறு","the sun is not looked at by one in எச்சில்",1],["நாய்","a dog is not looked at by one in எச்சில்",1],["தக்கவீழ் மீனோடே","a falling star is not looked at by one in எச்சில்",2]],"cw":"இவ்வைந்தும்","nr":[["purity","caste"],"a pollution rule of the period: what is barred from sight while the mouth is unclean, with a birth-group among the barred sights"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:7":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":7,"t":["எச்சில் பலவும் உளமற் றவற்றுள்","இயக்கம் இரண்டும் இணைவிழைச்சு வாயின்","விழைச்சுஇவை எச்சில் இந்நான்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says there are many kinds of எச்சில் and singles out four: the two excretions, sexual union and the mouth's desire, eating.","n":"The count of four is reached by taking இயக்கம் இரண்டும் as two (urine and faeces), so three items are listed here; வாயின் விழைச்சு is read as eating. Eating, the body's most ordinary act, is put in the same class as excretion and intercourse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theettu","thooimai","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 7","lb":"AC7","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["இயக்கம் இரண்டும்","the two excretions are each a kind of எச்சில், counted as two",2],["இணைவிழைச்சு","sexual union is a kind of எச்சில்",2],["வாயின் விழைச்சு","the mouth's desire, eating, is a kind of எச்சில்",2]],"cw":"இந்நான்கு","nr":[["purity"],"a pollution rule of the period: the classes of bodily act that make a person unclean"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:8":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":8,"t":["நால்வகை எச்சிலும் நன்கு கடைப்பிடித்து","ஓதார் உரையார் வளராரே எஞ்ஞான்றும்","மேதைகள் ஆகுறு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says those who would become learned observe the four kinds of எச்சில் carefully and never recite, speak or lie down to sleep while in them.","n":"The reward named is மேதை, learning or brilliance, not merit: keeping the purity rules is tied to the power of recitation. வளரார் is read as 'do not lie down to sleep'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theettu","kalvi","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 8","lb":"AC8","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["ஓதார்","one in எச்சில் does not recite",2],["உரையார்","one in எச்சில் does not speak",2],["வளராரே","one in எச்சில் does not lie down to sleep",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a pollution rule of the period: recitation and speech barred while unclean"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:9":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":9,"t":["நாளந்தி கோல்தின்று கண்கழீஇத் தெய்வத்தைத்","தானறியும் ஆற்றால் தொழுதெழுக அல்கந்தி","நின்று தொழுதல் பழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets the morning rite: at dawn twilight one chews the tooth-stick, washes the eyes and rises after worshipping the deity in the way one knows, and it calls worship standing at evening twilight a fault.","n":"தானறியும் ஆற்றால் leaves the form of worship and the deity to the worshipper; only the hour and the posture are fixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aasaaram","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 9","lb":"AC9","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual","purity"],"an order-of-the-day rule of the period: cleansing and worship at the two twilights"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:10":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":10,"t":["தேவர் வழிபாடு தீக்கனா வாலாமை","உண்டது கான்றல் மயிர்களைதல் ஊண்பொழுது","வைகு துயிலொடு இணைவிழைச்சுக் கீழ்மக்கள்","மெய்யுறல் ஏனை மயலுறல் ஈரைந்தும்","ஐயுறாது ஆடுக நீர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists ten occasions on which, it says, one bathes without hesitation: worship of the gods, a bad dream, uncleanness, vomiting what one has eaten, cutting the hair, mealtime, rising from sleep, sexual union, bodily contact with low people, and the other, falling into confusion.","n":"Ten is reached by taking ஊண்பொழுது and வைகு துயில் as two occasions; ஏனை மயலுறல் is obscure and may mean any other bewilderment. கீழ்மக்கள் can be a moral term (the base), as often in the Kural, but a contact that calls for a bath points to a birth-group, so it is read as a caste term here. வாலாமை, uncleanness, may include menstrual or death pollution; the verse does not say whose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","theettu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 10","lb":"AC10","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["தேவர் வழிபாடு","worship of the gods calls for a bath",1],["தீக்கனா","a bad dream calls for a bath",1],["வாலாமை","a state of uncleanness calls for a bath",1],["உண்டது கான்றல்","vomiting what one has eaten calls for a bath",2],["மயிர்களைதல்","cutting the hair calls for a bath",2],["ஊண்பொழுது","the time of the meal calls for a bath",2],["வைகு துயிலொடு","rising from sleep calls for a bath",3],["இணைவிழைச்சு","sexual union calls for a bath",3],["கீழ்மக்கள் மெய்யுறல்","touching the body of low people calls for a bath",3],["ஏனை மயலுறல்","the other, falling into confusion, calls for a bath",4]],"cw":"ஈரைந்தும்","nr":[["purity","caste"],"a pollution rule of the period: the occasions that require bathing, one of them contact with a ranked group"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:11":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":11,"t":["உடுத்தலால் நீராடார் ஒன்றுடுத்து உண்ணார்","உடுத்தாடை நீருள் பிழியார் விழுத்தக்கார்","ஒன்றுடுத்து என்றும் அவைபுகார் என்பதே","முந்தையோர் கண்ட முறை"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports the ancients' rule that the worthy do not bathe unclothed, do not eat wearing a single cloth, do not wring the cloth they wear inside the water, and never enter an assembly in a single cloth.","n":"உடுத்தலால் is read as உடுத்து அலால், 'except clothed'. The single-cloth rule joins the meal and the assembly, so dress is a matter of both purity and public standing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","thooimai","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 11","lb":"AC11","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["உடுத்தலால் நீராடார்","the worthy do not bathe except clothed",1],["ஒன்றுடுத்து உண்ணார்","the worthy do not eat in a single cloth",1],["நீருள் பிழியார்","the worthy do not wring the worn cloth in the water",2],["அவைபுகார்","the worthy do not enter the assembly in a single cloth",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on dress at bathing, eating and in the assembly"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:12":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":12,"t":["தலையுரைத்த எண்ணெயால் எவ்வுறுப்பும் தீண்டார்","பிறர்உடுத்த மாசுணியும் தீண்டார் செருப்புக்","குறையெனினும் கொள்ளார் இரந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the worthy do not touch any limb with the oil rubbed on the head, do not touch the soiled cloth another has worn, and do not beg for sandals even when they lack them.","n":"The last item moves from pollution to self-respect: a want as small as sandals does not license begging, so the verse joins bodily purity and the refusal to ask.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 12","lb":"AC12","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["தலையுரைத்த எண்ணெயால் எவ்வுறுப்பும் தீண்டார்","oil rubbed on the head is not used to touch another limb",1],["பிறர்உடுத்த மாசுணியும் தீண்டார்","the soiled cloth another wore is not touched",2],["செருப்புக் குறையெனினும் கொள்ளார் இரந்து","sandals are not got by begging even when lacking",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on oil and others' worn cloth as sources of uncleanness"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:13":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":13,"t":["நீருள் நிழல்புரிந்து நோக்கார் நிலம்இராக்","கீறார் இராமரமும் சேரார் இடர்எனினும்","நீர்தொடாது எண்ணெய் உரையார் உரைத்தபின்","நீர்தொடார் நோக்கார் புலை"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists things the worthy avoid: gazing at their reflection in water, scratching the ground at night, going near trees at night, rubbing on oil without first touching water even in distress, and, once oiled, touching water or looking at புலை.","n":"The order of oil and water on lines 3-4 is hard to fix: one reading is that water is touched before oiling and not again after, until the bath. புலை is again a caste term most likely naming the outcaste person, though it could be an impure thing; the bar on seeing it here attaches to the oiled state, not to எச்சில்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","theettu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 13","lb":"AC13","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["நீருள் நிழல்புரிந்து நோக்கார்","the worthy do not gaze at their reflection in water",1],["நிலம்இராக் கீறார்","the worthy do not scratch the ground at night",1],["இராமரமும் சேரார்","the worthy do not go near trees at night",2],["நீர்தொடாது எண்ணெய் உரையார்","the worthy do not rub on oil without touching water, even in distress",3],["உரைத்தபின் நீர்தொடார்","once oiled, the worthy do not touch water",3],["நோக்கார் புலை","once oiled, the worthy do not look at புலை",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","caste"],"a pollution rule of the period on night, oil and water, with a birth-group among the barred sights"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:14":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":14,"t":["நீராடும் போழ்தில் நெறிப்பட்டார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நீந்தார் உமியார் திளையார் விளையாடார்","காய்ந்தது எனினும் தலைஒழிந்து ஆடாரே","ஆய்ந்த அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who keep the path never, while bathing, swim, spit, splash about or play, and do not bathe leaving out the head even when it is dry.","n":"The ritual bath is set against the bath as pleasure: every banned act is play in water. காய்ந்தது எனினும் is read as 'even if the head is dry'; it could also mean 'even if the water is cold'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 14","lb":"AC14","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["நீந்தார்","they do not swim while bathing",2],["உமியார்","they do not spit while bathing",2],["திளையார்","they do not splash about while bathing",2],["விளையாடார்","they do not play while bathing",2],["தலைஒழிந்து ஆடாரே","they do not bathe leaving out the head",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on the manner of the purifying bath"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:15":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":15,"t":["ஐம்பூதம் பார்ப்பார் பசுத்திங்கள் ஞாயிறு","தம்பூதம் எண்ணாது இகழ்வானேல் தம்மெய்க்கண்","ஐம்பூதம் அன்றே கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who scorns the five elements, brahmins, the cow, the moon and the sun, not reckoning them as his own elements, has the five elements of his own body perish at once.","n":"தம்பூதம் makes the outer things kin to the elements of the body, so contempt for them is self-destruction. பார்ப்பார் is a birth-group term, and the verse places brahmins among cosmic and sacred things owed reverence by birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","igal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 15","lb":"AC15","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["ஐம்பூதம்","the five elements are not to be scorned",1],["பார்ப்பார்","brahmins are not to be scorned",1],["பசு","the cow is not to be scorned",1],["திங்கள்","the moon is not to be scorned",1],["ஞாயிறு","the sun is not to be scorned",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a rule of the period that ranks brahmins by birth among things owed reverence"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:16":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":16,"t":["அரசன் உவாத்தியான் தாய்தந்தை தம்முன்","நிகரில் குரவர் இவர்இவரைத்","தேவரைப் போலத் தொழுக என்பதே","யாவரும் கண்ட நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The verse names the king, the teacher, the mother, the father and the elder brother as the peerless elders, and reports as the way all have seen that they are to be worshipped as gods.","n":"The king heads a list otherwise made of household and teacher, so political and family authority are given one kind of reverence; the wife's duty to the husband, which the Kural treats this way, is not among them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","panivu","vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 16","lb":"AC16","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["அரசன்","the king is a peerless elder to be worshipped as a god",1],["உவாத்தியான்","the teacher is a peerless elder to be worshipped as a god",1],["தாய்","the mother is a peerless elder to be worshipped as a god",1],["தந்தை","the father is a peerless elder to be worshipped as a god",1],["தம்முன்","the elder brother is a peerless elder to be worshipped as a god",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:1"},"ac:17":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":17,"t":["குரவர் உரையிகந்து செய்யார் விரதம்","குறையுடையார் தீர மறவார் - நிறையுவா","மேல்கோலும் தின்னார் மரங்குறையார் என்பவே","நல்லறி வாளர் துணிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse gives the settled view of the wise: they do not act against their elders' word, do not forget to complete vows left unfinished, do not chew the tooth-stick on the full-moon day, and do not cut trees.","n":"நிறையுவா மேல் may mean the full-moon day and the days after it, or the full and new moon; and the tree-cutting bar may belong to the same day or stand alone. The list joins obedience, vows and calendar rules as one kind of thing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","aasaaram","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 17","lb":"AC17","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["குரவர் உரையிகந்து செய்யார்","the wise do not act against their elders' word",1],["குறையுடையார் தீர மறவார்","the wise do not forget to complete unfinished vows",2],["மேல்கோலும் தின்னார்","the wise do not chew the tooth-stick on the full-moon day",3],["மரங்குறையார்","the wise do not cut trees",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a calendar and vow rule of the period"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:18":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":18,"t":["நீராடிக் கால்கழுவி வாய்பூசி மண்டலஞ்செய்து","உண்டாரே உண்டார் எனப்படுவர் அல்லாதார்","உண்டார்போல் வாய்பூசிச் செல்வர் அதுவெறுத்துக்","கொண்டார் அரக்கர் குறித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that only those who eat after bathing, washing the feet, rinsing the mouth and drawing the circle on the ground are said to have eaten; the others, rinsing their mouths as if they had eaten, go off, and the rakshasas, resenting this, take their meal as their own.","n":"The sanction is not illness or sin but theft: an unritualed meal is not eaten by the eater at all, because demons claim it. மண்டலஞ்செய்து is read as marking the circle for the leaf.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","thooimai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 18","lb":"AC18","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["நீராடி","bathing before the meal makes it truly eaten",1],["கால்கழுவி","washing the feet before the meal makes it truly eaten",1],["வாய்பூசி","rinsing the mouth before the meal makes it truly eaten",1],["மண்டலஞ்செய்து","drawing the circle before the meal makes it truly eaten",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual","diet"],"a rule of the period on the purifying acts before a meal"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:19":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":19,"t":["காலினீர் நீங்காமை உண்டிடுக பள்ளியுள்","ஈரம் புலராமை ஏறற்க என்பதே","பேரறி வாளர் துணிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports the settled view of the very wise that one eats before the water on the feet has dried, and does not climb into bed before the wetness has dried.","n":"The same wet feet are required for the meal and forbidden for the bed; the rule turns on timing, not on water as such.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","thooimai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 19","lb":"AC19","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["காலினீர் நீங்காமை உண்டிடுக","one eats while the feet are still wet",1],["ஈரம் புலராமை ஏறற்க","one does not climb into bed while still wet",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on wet feet at meal and bed"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:20":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":20,"t":["உண்ணுங்கால் நோக்கும் திசைகிழக்குக் கண்ணமர்ந்து","தூங்கான் துளங்காமை நன்குஇரீஇ யாண்டும்","பிறிதியாதும் நோக்கான் உரையான் தொழுதுகொண்டு","உண்க உகாஅமை நன்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets the posture of the meal: one faces east, sits well, does not doze or sway, looks at nothing else and does not speak, and eats after worship, carefully, without spilling.","n":"Every item governs the eater's body and attention; none governs the food, its amount or its kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","thisai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 20","lb":"AC20","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["நோக்கும் திசைகிழக்கு","the direction faced while eating is east",1],["தூங்கான்","the eater does not doze",2],["துளங்காமை","the eater does not sway",2],["நோக்கான்","the eater looks at nothing else",3],["உரையான்","the eater does not speak",3],["தொழுதுகொண்டு","the eater eats after worship",3],["உகாஅமை","the eater does not spill",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period on direction, posture and silence at the meal"],"ch":"ac:1"},"ac:21":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":21,"t":["விருந்தினர் மூத்தோர் பசுசிறை பிள்ளை","இவர்க்கூண் தொடுத்தல்லால் உண்ணாரே என்றும்","ஒழுக்கம் பிழையா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who never stray from conduct never eat without first serving food to five: guests, elders, the cow, caged creatures and children.","n":"The list joins people and animals in one order of precedence at the meal: the guest stands first, and the cow and the caged creature (சிறை, read as birds or beasts kept in confinement; it could also mean captives) rank with elders and children as dependants of the house. The verse has no count word, so no items are given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","ozhukkam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 21","lb":"AC21","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:2"},"ac:22":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":22,"t":["ஒழிந்த திசையும் வழிமுறையால் நல்ல","முகட்டு வழியூண் புகழ்ந்தார் இகழ்ந்தார்","முகட்டு வழிகட்டில் பாடு"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say that the other directions too are good in their proper order for eating, and that those who approved eating along the line of the roof-ridge disapproved of laying the bed along it.","n":"The verse follows 20, which fixes east for the meal, and allows the remaining directions in sequence. முகட்டு வழி is read as the line of the roof-ridge; it might instead name an upward or northern direction, and the contrast between food and bed turns on that word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","undi","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 22","lb":"AC22","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period on the directions for eating and for the bed"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:23":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":23,"t":["கிடந்துண்ணார் நின்றுண்ணார் வெள்ளிடையும் உண்ணார்","சிறந்து மிகவுண்ணார் கட்டில்மேல் உண்ணார்","இறந்தொன்றும் தின்னற்க நின்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that the worthy do not eat lying down, standing, or in the open, do not eat to excess even when the food is choice, and do not eat on the bed, and it adds that one should not, breaking the rule, eat anything standing.","n":"Excess (மிகவுண்ணார்) is set among rules of posture and place, so moderation is presented as part of a code of bodily decorum rather than as a matter of health. வெள்ளிடை is read as open ground; இறந்து on line 3 is read as transgressing the rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 23","lb":"AC23","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","diet"],"a rule of the period on posture, place and amount at the meal"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:24":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":24,"t":["முன்துவ்வார் முன்னெழார் மிக்குறார் ஊணின்கண்","என்பெறினும் ஆற்றவலம் இரார் தம்மிற்","பெரியார்தம் பாலிருந்தக் கால்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when people sit at a meal with those greater than themselves, they do not eat first, do not rise first, do not press close, and whatever they receive do not sit far to the right.","n":"The greater ones (பெரியார்) are greater than oneself, by age or standing; the verse does not say by birth. மிக்குறார் is read as not crowding them and ஆற்றவலம் இரார் as not taking the right-hand seat, both doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","undi","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 24","lb":"AC24","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of precedence of the period at a shared meal"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:25":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":25,"t":["கைப்பன எல்லாம் கடைதலை தித்திப்ப","மெச்சும் வகையால் ஒழிந்த இடையாகத்","துய்க்க முறைவகையால் ஊண்"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets the order of the meal: the sweet things first, the bitter things last, and the rest in between in the approved way.","n":"The rule governs the food itself, its sequence by taste, where most of the meal rules govern the eater's body; the verse gives no reason, medical or ritual.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 25","lb":"AC25","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"a rule of the period on the order of tastes in a meal"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:26":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":26,"t":["முதியவரைப் பக்கத்து வையார் விதிமுறையால்","உண்பவற்றுள் எல்லாஞ் சிறிய கடைப்பிடித்து","அன்பில் திரியாமை ஆசாரம் நீங்காமை","பண்பினால் நீக்கல் கலம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse seems to say that one does not seat the aged beside oneself at the meal, by rule takes a little of each thing eaten, and, without failing in affection or leaving ஆசாரம், puts away the vessel courteously.","n":"Line 1 is doubtful: பக்கத்து வையார் may bar seating elders at one's side as equals, or may bar leaving them aside. Affection (அன்பு) and ஆசாரம் stand side by side as the two things not to lose, so the rule of the meal is tied to feeling as well as form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","anbu","aasaaram","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 26","lb":"AC26","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual","diet"],"a rule of the period on seating, portions and clearing the vessel"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:27":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":27,"t":["இழியாமை நன்குமிழ்ந்து எச்சில் அறவாய்","அடியோடு நன்கு துடைத்து வடிவுடைத்தா","முக்கால் குடித்துத் துடைத்து முகத்துறுப்பு","ஒத்த வகையால் விரலுறுத்தி வாய்பூசல்","மிக்கவர் கண்ட நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes the mouth-rinsing that the great have seen as the way: spitting out well so no எச்சில் is left and nothing drips, wiping well to the root, sipping three times and wiping, and touching the organs of the face with the fingers in the fitting manner.","n":"This is a step-by-step procedure rather than a list of bars; வாய்பூசல் names the rite and the steps exist to clear எச்சில். அடியோடு is read as the root of the teeth or gums.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","theettu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 27","lb":"AC27","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a purification rule of the period: the manner of rinsing the mouth"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:28":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":28,"t":["இருகையால் தண்ணீர் பருகார் ஒருகையால்","கொள்ளார் கொடாஅர் குரவர்க்கு இருகை","சொறியார் உடம்பு மடுத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that the worthy do not drink water with both hands, do not take from or give to their elders with one hand, and do not scratch their body with both hands.","n":"The hands are counted in each rule: two hands are wrong for drinking and scratching, one hand is wrong for exchange with elders, so the same count is courtesy in one place and indecorum in another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","guru","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 28","lb":"AC28","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period on the use of the hands in drinking, giving and touching the body"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:29":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":29,"t":["அந்திப் பொழுது கிடவார் நடவாரே","உண்ணார் வெகுளார் விளக்கிகழார் முன்னந்தி","அல்குண்டு அடங்கல் வழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that at evening twilight people do not lie down, walk about, eat, grow angry or scorn the lamp, and that the way is to take the night meal and settle to rest.","n":"Anger sits in a list of acts barred by the hour, so a moral fault is treated as untimely rather than wrong in itself. முன்னந்தி on line 2 is unclear: it may mean before the twilight, which would put the meal before dusk, or the twilight itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vekuli","aasaaram","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 29","lb":"AC29","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"an order-of-the-day rule of the period for the evening twilight"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:30":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":30,"t":["கிடக்குங்கால் கைகூப்பித் தெய்வம் தொழுது","வடக்கொடு கோணம் தலைசெய்யார் மீக்கோள்","உடற்கொடுத்துச் சேர்தல் வழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that on lying down one joins the hands and worships the deity, does not lay the head to the north or to a corner, and sleeps with the body covered by an upper cloth.","n":"கோணம் is read as the intermediate directions; with வடக்கு it leaves only east, south and west for the head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thisai","vazhipaadu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 30","lb":"AC30","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period on worship before sleep and the direction of the head"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:31":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":31,"t":["இருதேவர் பார்ப்பார் இடைபோகார் தும்மினும்","மிக்கார் வழுத்தில் தொழுதெழுக ஒப்பார்க்கு","உடன்செல்லல் உள்ளம் உவந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one does not pass between two gods or two பார்ப்பார், that when one sneezes and one's superiors bless one, one rises worshipping them, and that one goes along gladly with one's equals.","n":"பார்ப்பார் is a caste term for brahmins, used here to mark persons who may not be walked between, alongside the gods. இருதேவர் பார்ப்பார் may be two gods and brahmins, or two brahmin 'gods'; either way brahmins are given the deference of sacred things. The verse ranks people three ways in three lines: brahmins, superiors (மிக்கார்) and equals (ஒப்பார்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","pirappu","panivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 31","lb":"AC31","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a rule of the period giving brahmins, by birth-name, the deference due to gods"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:32":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":32,"t":["புற்பைங்கூழ் ஆப்பி சுடலை வழிதீர்த்தம்","தேவ குலம்நிழல் ஆநிலை வெண்பலிஎன்று","ஈரைந்தின் கண்ணும் உமிழ்வோடு இருபுலனும்","சோரார் உணர்வுடை யார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists ten places where those with understanding neither spit nor void the two excretions: grass, green crops, cow-dung, the cremation ground, a path, sacred water, a temple, shade, a cow-pen and the place of the white offering.","n":"The ten mix the sacred (temple, sacred water, the white offering), the useful (crops, shade, path) and the cow's things (dung, pen), so the bar protects both holiness and common use. வெண்பலி is read as the place of a white offering or of ash; it is doubtful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theettu","thooimai","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 32","lb":"AC32","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["புற்","grass is a place where spitting and voiding are barred",1],["பைங்கூழ்","green crops are a place where they are barred",1],["ஆப்பி","cow-dung is a place where they are barred",1],["சுடலை","the cremation ground is a place where they are barred",1],["வழி","a path is a place where they are barred",1],["தீர்த்தம்","sacred water is a place where they are barred",1],["தேவ குலம்","a temple is a place where they are barred",2],["நிழல்","shade is a place where they are barred",2],["ஆநிலை","a cow-pen is a place where they are barred",2],["வெண்பலி","the place of the white offering is a place where they are barred",2]],"cw":"ஈரைந்தின்","nr":[["purity"],"a pollution rule of the period: places barred for spitting and excretion"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:33":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":33,"t":["பகல்தெற்கு நோக்கார் இராவடக்கு நோக்கார்","பகல்பெய்யார் தீயுனுள் நீர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that by day one does not face south and by night does not face north, and that by day one does not pour water into fire.","n":"The verse does not name the act for which the directions are fixed; set between 32 and 34, both on spitting and excretion, it most likely concerns those acts. The water-and-fire rule is a separate bar tied to the same hour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","theettu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 33","lb":"AC33","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on directions by day and night and on quenching fire"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:34":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":34,"t":["பத்துத் திசையும் மனத்தான் மறைத்தபின்","அந்தரத்து அல்லால் உமிழ்வோடு இருபுலனும்","இந்திர தானம் பெறினும் இகழாரே","தந்திரத்து வாழ்துமென் பார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who claim to live by the rule-book, having veiled the ten directions in their mind, spit and void only in a secluded place, and would not slight this even to gain the throne of Indra.","n":"மனத்தான் மறைத்தபின் makes the screening an act of mind as well as of place. அந்தரத்து is read as a secluded spot; it could mean the open air. தந்திரத்து வாழ்துமென் பார் may carry irony toward those who only say they live by the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theettu","thooimai","thisai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 34","lb":"AC34","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a pollution rule of the period on where spitting and excretion are allowed"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:35":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":35,"t":["நடைவரவு நீரகத்து நின்றுவாய் பூசார்","வழிநிலை நீருள்ளும் பூசார் மனத்தால்","வரைந்துகொண் டல்லது பூசார் கலத்தினால்","பெய்பூச்சுச் சீரா தெனின்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when rinsing from a vessel is not possible, people do not rinse the mouth standing in running water or in water lying on the path, nor without first marking off the place in their mind.","n":"The conditional on line 4 makes the vessel the norm and natural water the fallback. நடைவரவு நீர் is read as flowing water and வழிநிலை நீர் as standing water on a road; both readings are tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 35","lb":"AC35","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a purification rule of the period on the water fit for rinsing the mouth"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:36":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":36,"t":["சுடரிடைப் போகார் சுவர்மேல் உமியார்","இடரெனினும் மாசுணி கீழ்தம்மேற் கொள்ளார்","படைவரினும் ஆடை வளியுரைப்பப் போகார்","பலரிடை ஆடை உதிராரே என்றும்","கடனறி காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who know their duty never pass between lamps, never spit on a wall, never take on themselves a soiled cloth worn below even in trouble, never go with their cloth blowing in the wind even if an army comes, and never shake out their cloth among many people.","n":"The conditions rise in pressure (இடரெனினும், படைவரினும்): the rule holds in distress and even under attack. Line 2 is obscure: மாசுணி கீழ் is read as a soiled lower garment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","thooimai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 36","lb":"AC36","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a rule of the period on fire, spitting and the handling of clothes"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:37":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":37,"t":["பிறர் மனை கள்களவு சூது கொலையோடு","அறனறிந்தார் இவ்வைந்து நோக்கார் - திறனிலரென்று","எள்ளப் படுவதூஉம் அன்றி நிரயத்துச்","செல்வழி உய்த்திடுத லால்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who know aram do not even look at five things: another's wife, toddy, theft, gambling and killing, because besides being scorned as worthless they are driven down the road to hell.","n":"The bar is on looking (நோக்கார்), not only on doing. The verse gives two sanctions, one in this world (scorn) and one after it (hell). An ethics verse inside the ritual book, it needs no norm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kal","soodhu","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 37","lb":"AC37","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறர் மனை","another man's wife is not looked at by those who know aram",1],["கள்","toddy is not looked at by them",1],["களவு","theft is not looked at by them",1],["சூது","gambling is not looked at by them",1],["கொலையோடு","killing is not looked at by them",1]],"cw":"இவ்வைந்து","ch":"ac:2"},"ac:38":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":38,"t":["பொய்குறளை வௌவல் அழுக்காறு இவைநான்கும்","ஐயம்தீர் காட்சியார் சிந்தியார் - சிந்திப்பின்","ஐயம் புகுவித்து அருநிரயத் துய்த்திடும்","தெய்வமும் செற்று விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of clear insight do not even think of four things: lying, slander, seizing what is another's and envy, because thinking of them brings suspicion, drives one into hard hell, and makes even the deity destroy one.","n":"The bar moves inward from the eye in 37 to the mind (சிந்தியார்). ஐயம் புகுவித்து is read as bringing one under suspicion; it might instead mean bringing doubt into one's own mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","vaaimai","naragam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 38","lb":"AC38","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்","lying is not thought of by those of clear insight",1],["குறளை","slander is not thought of by them",1],["வௌவல்","seizing another's goods is not thought of by them",1],["அழுக்காறு","envy is not thought of by them",1]],"cw":"இவைநான்கும்","ch":"ac:2"},"ac:39":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":39,"t":["தமக்கென்று உலையேற்றார் தம்பொருட்டூண் கொள்ளார்","அடுக்களை எச்சில் படாஅர் மனைப்பலி","ஊட்டினமை கண்டுண்க ஊண்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the worthy do not set a pot on the fire for themselves alone, do not take food for their own sake, do not let the kitchen be touched by எச்சில், and eat only after seeing the house offering fed.","n":"Sharing and purity are joined in one rule of the hearth: cooking for oneself and defiling the kitchen are barred together, and the offering (மனைப்பலி) must be fed before the household eats.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","eegai","theettu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 39","lb":"AC39","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period on the kitchen and the household offering before the meal"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:40":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":40,"t":["உயர்ந்ததன் மேலிரார் உள்ளழிவு செய்யார்","இறந்தின்னா செய்தக் கடைத்தும் குரவர்","இளங்கிளைஞர் உண்ணு மிடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that where elders and young kin are eating, one does not sit above them and does not wound them inwardly, even if they have done one great harm.","n":"Forbearance is placed at the table and limited to kin: the meal is a time when even a grave wrong is not to be answered. உள்ளழிவு is read as hurt to the feelings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","porai","sutram","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 40","lb":"AC40","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of precedence of the period when kin are eating"],"ch":"ac:2"},"ac:41":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":41,"t":["கண்ணெச்சில் கண்ணூட்டார் காலொடு கால்தேயார்","புண்ணியம் ஆய தலையோடு றுப்புறுத்த","நுண்ணிய நூலறிவி னார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that those with subtle knowledge of the texts do not put the eye's discharge into the eye, do not rub one foot against the other, and do not let other limbs touch the head, which it calls holy.","n":"Line 2 is damaged: தலையோடு றுப்புறுத்த is read as the head with the other limbs pressed to it, a rule that the head, called புண்ணியம், is not touched by lower parts; the first rule, கண்ணெச்சில் கண்ணூட்டார், may instead bar touching the eye with a hand soiled by spittle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","theettu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 41","lb":"AC41","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity"],"a purity rule of the period on how the body's parts may touch, with the head treated as holy"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:42":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":42,"t":["தீண்டாநாள் முந்நாளும் நோக்கார்நீ ராடியபின்","ஈராறு நாளும் இகவற்க என்பதே","பேரறி வாளர் துணிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse gives as the settled view of the greatly wise that a man does not look at his wife on the three days she may not be touched, and that after she has bathed he does not leave her for the twelve days that follow.","n":"தீண்டாநாள் names the menstrual days by the bar on touch; the verse does not say why the twelve days after the bath matter, but setting them right after the bath ties the rule of pollution to the timing of marital union. The closing phrase பேரறி வாளர் துணிவு recurs from verse 19.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theettu","ilvaazhkkai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 42","lb":"AC42","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["gender","purity"],"a menstrual pollution rule of the period: the wife is not touched or looked at for three days, and the husband stays with her for twelve days after her bath"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:43":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":43,"t":["உச்சியம் போழ்தோடு இடையாமம் ஈரந்தி","மிக்க இருதேவர் நாளோ டுவாத்திதிநாள்","அட்டமியும் ஏனைப் பிறந்தநாள் இவ்வனைத்தும்","ஒட்டார் உடனுறைவின் கண்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists the times on which, it reports, husband and wife do not come together: noon, midnight, the two twilights, the days of the two great gods, the uvā tithi days, the eighth day of the moon, and one's birthday.","n":"இருதேவர் நாள் is not explained; it names the festival days of two gods without saying which. டுவாத்திதிநாள் is read as உவா joined to திதிநாள், the new- and full-moon days, and taken as one item; ஏனைப் பிறந்தநாள் is read as the birth-star day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","aasaaram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 43","lb":"AC43","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["உச்சியம் போழ்தோடு","noon is a time when the couple do not come together",1],["இடையாமம்","midnight is such a time",1],["ஈரந்தி","the two twilights are such times",1],["மிக்க இருதேவர் நாளோ","the days of the two great gods are such days",2],["டுவாத்திதிநாள்","the new- and full-moon tithi days are such days",2],["அட்டமியும்","the eighth day of the lunar fortnight is such a day",3],["ஏனைப் பிறந்தநாள்","one's birthday is such a day",3]],"cw":"இவ்வனைத்தும்","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period setting the hours and calendar days on which marital union is barred"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:44":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":44,"t":["நாழி மணைமேல் இரியார் மணைகவிழார்","கோடி கடையுள் விரியார் கடைத்தலை","ஓராது கட்டில் பாடஅர் அறியாதார்","தந்தலைக்கண் நில்லா விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports household rules: one does not set the measuring vessel down on the seat-plank or turn the plank over, does not unfold new cloth in the doorway, does not lie on a cot at the threshold without thought, and does not let strangers stand at one's head.","n":"இரியார் and பாடஅர் are doubtful forms; பாடஅர் is read as படார், 'do not lie down', and the last rule, அறியாதார் தந்தலைக்கண் நில்லா விடல், can also mean keeping unknown people from standing at the head of the house. These look like rules of omen rather than of pollution.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 44","lb":"AC44","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"household omen rules of the period about the seat, new cloth, the threshold and strangers"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:45":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":45,"t":["துடைப்பம் துகள்காடு புல்லிதழ்ச் செத்தல்","கருங்கலம் கட்டில் கிழிந்ததனோடு ஐந்தும்","பரப்பற்க பந்த ரகத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five things that are not to be spread inside the pandal: a broom, sweepings, dried grass and leaves, a black pot, and a torn cot.","n":"துகள்காடு is taken as one thing, a heap of dust and sweepings, to reach the verse's five; split into dust and refuse the list would run to six. The pandal is presumably the booth raised for a wedding or festival, though the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 45","lb":"AC45","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["துடைப்பம்","a broom is not spread inside the pandal",1],["துகள்காடு","sweepings and refuse are not spread there",1],["புல்லிதழ்ச் செத்தல்","dried grass and dead leaves are not spread there",1],["கருங்கலம்","a black pot is not spread there",2],["கட்டில் கிழிந்த","a torn cot is not spread there",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","nr":[["ritual","purity"],"a rule of the period keeping unclean and inauspicious things out of the festive booth"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:46":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":46,"t":["காட்டுக் களைந்து கலம்கழீஇ இல்லத்தை","ஆப்பிநீ ரெங்கும் தெளித்துச் சிறுகாலை","நீர்ச்சால் கரக நிறைய மலரணிந்து","இல்லம் பொலிய அடுப்பினுள் தீப்பெய்க","நல்லது உறல்வேண்டு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who want good to come to them clear away the refuse, wash the vessels, sprinkle cow-dung water all through the house, fill the water-pot and the karakam early in the morning, adorn them with flowers, and light fire in the hearth so that the house shines.","n":"The reason given is the household's fortune, நல்லது உறல், not purity as such: cleaning and lighting the hearth are read as making the house auspicious.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","ilvaazhkkai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 46","lb":"AC46","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","ritual"],"a rule of the period for the morning cleansing of the house with cow-dung water and the first lighting of the hearth"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:47":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":47,"t":["அட்டமியும் ஏனை உவாவும் பதினான்கும்","அப்பூமி காப்பார்க்கு உறுகண்ணும் மிக்க","நிலத்துளக்கு விண்ணதிர்ப்பு வாலாமை பார்ப்பார்","இலங்குநூல் ஓதாத நாள்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists the days on which brahmins do not recite the shining texts: the eighth day, the new and full moon, the fourteenth, a time of affliction to the one who guards the land, a great earthquake, thunder in the sky, and a state of impurity.","n":"பார்ப்பார் names a birth-group whose duty is set here; the verse does not rank them against others but makes Vedic recitation theirs. The king's distress stands in the same list as earthquake and thunder, as a disturbance of the world that halts recitation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","andhanar","kaalam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 47","lb":"AC47","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["அட்டமியும்","the eighth lunar day is a day without recitation",1],["ஏனை உவாவும்","the new and full moon are such days",1],["பதினான்கும்","the fourteenth lunar day is such a day",1],["அப்பூமி காப்பார்க்கு உறுகண்","affliction to the land's guardian halts recitation",2],["மிக்க நிலத்துளக்கு","a great earthquake halts it",2],["விண்ணதிர்ப்பு","thunder in the sky halts it",3],["வாலாமை","impurity halts it",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual","purity","caste"],"a rule of the period on the days a birth-group, the பார்ப்பார், suspend Vedic recitation, impurity among them"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:48":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":48,"t":["கலியாணம் தேவர் பிதிர்விழா, வேள்வியென்ற","ஐவகை நாளும் இகழா தறஞ்செய்க","பெய்க விருந்திற்கும் கூழ்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that on five kinds of day, a wedding, the festivals of the gods and of the ancestors, other festivals and a sacrifice, one does aram without slighting it, and serves food to guests as well.","n":"Line 1 names four occasions; the five of ஐவகை are reached by taking தேவர் and பிதிர் each with விழா and விழா again as festivals in general. விருந்திற்கும் 'for guests too' makes feeding guests the added duty of the day, after aram.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","virundhu","vazhipaadu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 48","lb":"AC48","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கலியாணம்","a wedding day is a day for doing aram",1],["தேவர்","the festival of the gods is such a day",1],["பிதிர்","the festival of the ancestors is such a day",1],["விழா","a festival is such a day",1],["வேள்வி","a sacrifice is such a day",1]],"cw":"ஐவகை","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period for the festival and rite days of the household calendar"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:49":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":49,"t":["உடைநடை சொற்சோர்வு வைதலிந் நான்கும்","நிலைமைக்கும் கல்விக்கும் ஆண்மைக்கும் தத்தம்","குடிமைக்கும் தக்க செயல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that dress, gait, slackness of speech and rebuke, these four, are to be done as befits one's standing, learning, manliness and family.","n":"Even the faults, slackness of speech and abuse, are to be kept in measure with one's place; the rule is not to avoid them but to fit them. Family (குடிமை) closes the four measures, so outward conduct is graded by lineage as well as by learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","sol","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 49","lb":"AC49","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["உடை","dress is to fit one's standing, learning, manliness and family",1],["நடை","gait is to fit them",1],["சொற்சோர்வு","slackness of speech is to fit them",1],["வைதலிந்","rebuke is to fit them",1]],"cw":"நான்கும்","nr":[["caste"],"a rule of the period that grades outward conduct by one's family rank among other measures"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:50":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":50,"t":["பழியார் இழியார் பலருள் உறங்கார்","இசையாத நேர்ந்து கரவார் இசைவின்றி","இல்லாரை எள்ளி இகழ்ந்துரையார் தள்ளியும்","தாங்கருங் கேள்வி யவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of rare learning by listening do not blame others, do not lower themselves, do not sleep among many, do not promise and then hold back, and do not, even by a slip, mock and deride the poor without cause.","n":"இசையாத நேர்ந்து கரவார் is doubtful: it may mean they do not agree to what is unfitting and then hide it, or do not conceal after consenting; தள்ளியும் is read as 'even by a slip'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","inmai","karavu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 50","lb":"AC50","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:51":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":51,"t":["மின்னொளியும் வீழ்மீனும் வேசையர்கள் கோலமும்","நம்மொளி வேண்டுவார் நோக்கார் பகற்கிழவோன்","முன்னொளியும் பின்னொளியும் அற்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who want their own radiance do not look at lightning, a falling star, the finery of harlots, or the sun at its rising and setting.","n":"The reason given is the viewer's own lustre (நம்மொளி), not pollution; the harlots' finery sits among dangerous lights in the sky. The sun's முன்னொளி and பின்னொளி are read as its light at dawn and dusk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","varaivinmagalir","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 51","lb":"AC51","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["மின்னொளியும்","the flash of lightning is not looked at by those who want their radiance",1],["வீழ்மீனும்","a falling star is not looked at",1],["வேசையர்கள் கோலமும்","the finery of harlots is not looked at",1],["பகற்கிழவோன் முன்னொளியும்","the sun's light at rising is not looked at",2],["பின்னொளியும்","the sun's light at setting is not looked at",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","gender"],"a rule of the period on sights to be avoided, with harlots' adornment among them"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:52":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":52,"t":["படிறும் பயனிலவும் பட்டி யுரையும்","வசையும் புறனும் உரையாரே என்றும்","அசையாத உள்ளத் தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of unshaken mind never speak deceit, useless words, loose talk, abuse or backbiting.","n":"The five kinds of speech are tied to one cause, a mind that does not waver (அசையாத உள்ளம்); the verse treats bad speech as the sign of an unsteady mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","purangooral","payan","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 52","lb":"AC52","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["படிறும்","deceit is never spoken by the steady-minded",1],["பயனிலவும்","useless words are never spoken",1],["பட்டி யுரையும்","loose talk is never spoken",1],["வசையும்","abuse is never spoken",2],["புறனும்","backbiting is never spoken",2]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:53":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":53,"t":["தெறியொடு கல்லேறு வீளை விளியே","விகிர்தம் கதம்,கரத்தல் கைபுடை தோன்ற","உறுப்புச் செகுத்தலோடு இன்னவை யெல்லாம்","பயிற்றார் நெறிப்பட் டவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists acts that those who keep the path do not practise: flicking with the fingers, throwing stones, whistling, calling out, grimacing, anger, concealment, clapping, and harming a limb so that it shows.","n":"Bodily habits and two moral faults, anger and concealment, stand in one list of things not to be made a habit (பயிற்றார்). விகிர்தம் is read as grimacing, and தோன்ற உறுப்புச் செகுத்தல் is uncertain: maltreating a limb in view, or a gesture of the limbs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","vekuli","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 53","lb":"AC53","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["தெறியொடு","flicking is not practised by those on the path",1],["கல்லேறு","stone-throwing is not practised",1],["வீளை","whistling is not practised",1],["விளியே","calling out is not practised",1],["விகிர்தம்","grimacing is not practised",2],["கதம்","anger is not practised",2],["கரத்தல்","concealment is not practised",2],["கைபுடை","clapping the hands is not practised",2],["உறுப்புச் செகுத்தலோடு","harming a limb in view is not practised",3]],"cw":"இன்னவை யெல்லாம்","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:54":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":54,"t":["முறுவல் இனிதுரை கால்நீர் இணைபாய்","கிடக்கையோடு இவ்வைந்து மென்ப தலைச்சென்றார்க்கு","ஊணொடு செய்யும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that a smile, a sweet word, water for the feet, a mat and a bed, these five, are the honours paid together with food to one who has come.","n":"Food is assumed, and the five are what goes with it: the welcome begins with the face and ends with a place to sleep.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inchol","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 54","lb":"AC54","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[["முறுவல்","a smile is an honour paid with food to the guest",1],["இனிதுரை","a sweet word is such an honour",1],["கால்நீர்","water for the feet is such an honour",1],["இணைபாய்","a mat is such an honour",1],["கிடக்கையோடு","a bed is such an honour",2]],"cw":"இவ்வைந்து","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:55":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":55,"t":["கறுத்த பகைமுனையும் கள்ளாட்டுக் கண்ணும்","நிறுத்த மனமில்லார் சேரி அகத்தும்","குணநோக்கிக் கொண்டவர் கோள்விட் டுழியும்","நிகரில் அறிவினார் வேண்டார் பலர்தொகு","நீர்க்கரையும் நீடு நிலை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that people of peerless understanding do not wish to stay long at a hostile battlefront, at a drinking bout, in the street of those without a steady mind, where one taken on for his qualities has lost his hold, or at a water's edge where many crowd.","n":"குணநோக்கிக் கொண்டவர் கோள்விட் டுழியும் is unclear; it may be a place where a man chosen for his virtue has been dropped, or where those who chose by virtue have given up their resolve. The rule is about lingering (நீடு நிலை), not entering.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","idam","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 55","lb":"AC55","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:56":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":56,"t":["முளிபுல்லும் கானமும் சேரார்தீக் கூட்டார்","துளிவிழக் கால்பரப்பி ஓடார் தெளிவிலாக்","கானம் தமியர் இயங்கார் துளியஃகி","நல்குரவு ஆற்றப் பெருகினும் செய்யாரே","தொல்வரவின் தீர்ந்த தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that people do not go into dry grass or forest or kindle fire there, do not run with legs spread when rain falls, do not travel alone through a pathless forest, and, even if the rain fails and poverty swells greatly, do not take up work that departs from their ancestral line.","n":"The verse moves from bodily prudence to a rule of hereditary occupation: தொல்வரவின் தீர்ந்த தொழில், work cut off from the old descent, is barred even in famine. That last line is a birth rule of the period, set beside rules of safety as if of one kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 56","lb":"AC56","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a rule of the period binding a person to the occupation of his descent even in poverty"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:57":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":57,"t":["பாழ்மனையும் தேவ குலனும் சுடுகாடும்","ஊரில் வழியெழுந்த ஒற்றை முதுமரனும்","தாமே தமியர் புகாஅர் பகல்வளரார்","நோயின்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who wish to be free of disease do not enter alone a ruined house, a temple, the burning ground or the lone old tree that rises by a village road, and do not sleep by day.","n":"The reason given is health, not pollution: the four places are lonely and feared, and தமியர் 'alone' is the condition of the bar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","idam","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 57","lb":"AC57","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:58":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":58,"t":["எழுச்சிக்கண் பிற்கூவார் தும்மார் வழுக்கியும்","எங்குற்றுச் சேறீரோ என்னாரே முன்புக்கு","எதிர்முகமா நின்றும் உரையார் இருசார்வும்","கொள்வர் குரவர் வலம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that when someone sets out, people do not call after him from behind, do not sneeze, do not even by a slip ask 'where are you going?', do not step in front and speak face to face, and keep their elders on their right on both sides.","n":"The first four rules protect a departure from ill omen; the last, கொள்வர் குரவர் வலம், moves to reverence, keeping the elders on the right. இருசார்வும் may mean going and coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","guru","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 58","lb":"AC58","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"omen rules of the period for a departure, and the rite of keeping elders on the right"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:59":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":59,"t":["உடம்புநன்று என்றுரையார் ஊதார் விளக்கும்","அடுப்பினுள் தீநந்தக் கொள்ளார் அதனைப்","படக்காயார் தம்மேற் குறித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that people do not say 'my body is well', do not blow out a lamp, do not take fire from a hearth so that it goes out, and do not warm themselves at it with the fire turned toward themselves.","n":"Saying the body is well seems to court misfortune; the last clause, படக்காயார் தம்மேற் குறித்து, is obscure and may bar warming oneself directly at the hearth fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 59","lb":"AC59","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"rules of the period about the household fire and about words that invite ill"],"ch":"ac:3"},"ac:60":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":60,"t":["யாதொன்றும் ஏறார் செருப்பு வெயின்மறையார்","ஆன்றவிந்த முத்த விழுமியார் தம்மோடுஅங்கு","ஓராறு செல்லுமிடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when travelling a road together with the full, self-controlled, elder and eminent, one mounts nothing, wears no sandals and does not shade oneself from the sun.","n":"Deference takes the form of matching the elder's hardship on the road: one gives up mount, sandals and shade beside him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","saandror","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 60","lb":"AC60","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:3"},"ac:61":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":61,"t":["வான்முறை யான்வந்த நான்மறை யாளரை","மேன்முறைப் பால்தம் குரவரைப் போலொழுகல்","நூன்முறை யாளர் துணிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports it as the settled view of those versed in the treatises that the knowers of the four Vedas, who came by the order of heaven, are to be treated in the higher way, as one's own elders are.","n":"The verse does not name the brahmin by a birth word; it names him by the four Vedas and by a coming வான்முறையான், 'by the order of heaven', so the rank is grounded in a sacred origin rather than in conduct. குரவர் here takes the sense verse 16 gives it, the five elders worshipped as gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedham","guru","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 61","lb":"AC61","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a rule of the period that ranks the Veda-knowers with one's own elders by a heaven-given order"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:62":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":62,"t":["கால்வாய்த் தொழுவு சமயம் எழுந்திருப்பு","ஆசாரம் என்பர் குரவர்க்கு இவையிவை","சாரத்தால் சொல்லிய மூன்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that the elders call three acts the ஆசாரம் owed to elders, stated in essence: bowing at the feet, attendance at the proper time, and rising when they come.","n":"சமயம் is the doubtful item: it can be waiting on the elder at the fit time, or keeping the elder's own creed; the first fits the bodily acts beside it. The verse says the three were told சாரத்தால், as the gist, so it offers them as a summary of reverence, not a full code.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","aasaaram","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 62","lb":"AC62","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["கால்வாய்த் தொழுவு","bowing at the feet is ஆசாரம் owed to elders",1],["சமயம்","attendance at the proper time is ஆசாரம் owed to elders",1],["எழுந்திருப்பு","rising up at their coming is ஆசாரம் owed to elders",1]],"cw":"மூன்று","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:63":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":63,"t":["துறந்தாரைப் பேணலும் நாணலும்தாம் கற்ற","மறந்தும் குரவர்முன் சொல்லாமை மூன்றும்","திறங்கண்டார் கண்ட நெறி"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things as the path seen by those who saw the right manner: caring for those who have renounced, modesty, and not speaking before elders, even by lapse, of what one has learned.","n":"The third item is a restraint on learning itself: the learned man keeps his learning silent in the presence of elders, so learning is ranked below reverence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","naanam","adakkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 63","lb":"AC63","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["துறந்தாரைப் பேணலும்","caring for those who have renounced belongs to the right path",1],["நாணலும்","modesty belongs to the right path",1],["மறந்தும் குரவர்முன் சொல்லாமை","not voicing one's own learning before elders, even by lapse, belongs to the right path",2]],"cw":"மூன்றும்","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:64":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":64,"t":["பார்ப்பார் தவரே சுமந்தார் பிணிப்பட்டார்","மூத்தார் இளையார் பசுப்பெண்டிர் என்றிவர்கட்டு","ஆற்ற வழிவிலங்கி னாரே பிறப்பிடைப்","போற்றி யெனப்படு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names eight to whom one gives way on the road (brahmins, ascetics, those carrying loads, the sick, the old, the young, cows and women) and says those who step fully aside for them are called praiseworthy among births.","n":"பார்ப்பார் is a birth term and heads the list, followed by the ascetic; the rest are the burdened and the weak, so a rank by birth and a care for the vulnerable are given one gesture. பிறப்பிடைப் போற்றி may mean praised among men, or praised in their births to come; the verse does not settle it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","pirappu","panivu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 64","lb":"AC64","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["பார்ப்பார்","brahmins are to be given way to",1],["தவரே","ascetics are to be given way to",1],["சுமந்தார்","those carrying loads are to be given way to",1],["பிணிப்பட்டார்","the sick are to be given way to",1],["மூத்தார்","the old are to be given way to",2],["இளையார்","the young are to be given way to",2],["பசு","cows are to be given way to",2],["பெண்டிர்","women are to be given way to",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a road rule of the period that puts brahmins first among those one gives way to"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:65":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":65,"t":["ஈன்றாள் மகள்தம் உடன்பிறந்தாள் ஆயினும்","சான்றார் தமித்தா(க) உறையற்க ஐம்புலனும்","தாங்கற்கு அரிதஆக லான்"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that the worthy should not stay alone even with their own mother, daughter or sister, because the five senses are hard to hold back.","n":"The reason given is not the woman's conduct but the man's senses: even சான்றார் are not trusted to restrain the five, so the rule removes the occasion rather than relying on restraint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kaamam","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 65","lb":"AC65","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period separating men from their nearest kinswomen when alone"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:66":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":66,"t":["கடைவிலக்கிற் காயார் கழிகிழமை செய்யார்","கொடையளிக்கண் பொச்சாவார் கோலநேர்செய்யார்","இடையறுத்துப் போகிப் பிறனொருவன் சேரார்","கடைபோக வாழ்துமென் பார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who mean to live on to the end (in a great one's service) are not angered when stopped at the gate, do not take excessive liberties of intimacy, are not careless at the giving of gifts, do not dress as their lord does, and do not cut in between to go and join another.","n":"கடை is the gate of the great house, which sets the verse in attendance on a lord. கோலநேர் செய்யார் is read as not matching the lord's dress; இடையறுத்துப் போகிப் பிறனொருவன் சேரார் can be not breaking off to attach oneself to another patron, or not passing between two people to reach a third. கடைபோக வாழ்தும் is survival as much as virtue.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","adakkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 66","lb":"AC66","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:67":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":67,"t":["தமக்குற்ற கட்டுரையும் தம்மிற் பெரியார்","உரைத்ததற்கு உற்ற உரையுமஃ தன்றிப்","பிறர்க்குற்ற கட்டுரையும் சொல்லற்க சொல்லின்","வடுக்குற்ற மாகி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says one should not speak a pronouncement about oneself, a retort answering what one's betters have said, or a pronouncement about others, and that speaking them turns into a fault that scars.","n":"The three speeches cover self, superior and third party, so the verse closes off every subject on which one might pronounce; வடுக்குற்றம் makes the fault a lasting mark, not a passing lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","adakkam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 67","lb":"AC67","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:68":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":68,"t":["பெரியார் உவப்பனதாம் உவவார் இல்லம்","சிறியாரைக் கொண்டு புகாஅர் அறிவறியாப்","பிள்ளையே ஆயினும் இழித்துரையார் தம்மோடு","அளவளா வில்லா இடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says they do not take pleasure in what the great take pleasure in, do not enter a house bringing lowly men, and do not speak slightingly even of an undiscerning child in a place where they are not on familiar terms.","n":"The child that may not be slighted is most naturally the great house's own child; தம்மோடு அளவளாவில்லா இடத்து limits the rules to a house where one is not intimate, so the verse is about the stranger's caution in another's household.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 68","lb":"AC68","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:69":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":69,"t":["முனியார் துனியார் முகத்தெதிர் நில்லார்","தனிமை இடத்துக்கண் தம்கருமம் சொல்லார்","இனியவை யாமறிதும் என்னார் கசிவின்று","காக்கைவெள் என்னும் எனின்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says they do not show anger or sulk, do not stand facing the great one, do not speak of their own affairs to him in private, and do not say 'we know what is pleasant', even if he says without kindness that the crow is white.","n":"The closing clause gives the measure of the whole rule: the attendant does not contradict the great one even on a plain falsehood. The verse does not call this truth; it calls it conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","adakkam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 69","lb":"AC69","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:70":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":70,"t":["உமிவும் உயர்ந்துழி ஏறலும் பாக்கும்","வகையில் உரையும் வளர்ச்சியும் ஐந்தும்","புணரார் பெரியா ரகத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five things they do not do in the house of the great: spitting, climbing to a high seat, chewing areca, speaking out of order, and lying down.","n":"வளர்ச்சி is read as lying down (வளர், to sleep); a reading 'growing familiar' is possible. The list mixes bodily acts and speech as one class of liberties.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","perumai","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 70","lb":"AC70","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[["உமிவும்","spitting is not done in the house of the great",1],["உயர்ந்துழி ஏறலும்","climbing to a raised place is not done there",1],["பாக்கும்","chewing areca is not done there",1],["வகையில் உரையும்","speech out of its proper order is not done there",2],["வளர்ச்சியும்","lying down is not done there",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:71":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":71,"t":["இறைவர்முன் செல்வமும் கல்வியும் தேசும்","குணனும் குலமுடையார் கூறார் பகைவர்போல்","பாரித்துப் பல்காற் பயின்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of good family do not speak before their lord of their own wealth, learning, lustre and virtue, spreading them out again and again as an enemy would.","n":"பகைவர்போல் gives the reason: display of one's own excellences before the lord reads as a rival's challenge, so the restraint is prudence. குலமுடையார் makes it a mark of good birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","adakkam","kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 71","lb":"AC71","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:72":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":72,"t":["பெரியார் மனையகத்தும் தேவ குலத்தும்","வணங்கார் குரவரையும் கண்டால் அணங்கொடு","நேர்பெரியார் செல்லு மிடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that in the house of the great and in a temple they do not bow even to their own elders when they see them, nor when the great go with the deity.","n":"Reverence is ruled by place: where a greater object of worship is present, bowing to another, even to the elders verse 16 says to worship as gods, is withheld. The third line, அணங்கொடு நேர்பெரியார் செல்லுமிடத்து, is hard; it may mean when the great go in procession with the deity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vazhipaadu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 72","lb":"AC72","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period on where obeisance to elders is withheld"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:73":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":73,"t":["நகையொடு கொட்டாவி காறிப்புத் தும்மல்","இவையும் பெரியார்முன் செய்யாரே செய்யின்","அசையாது நிற்கும் பழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse names laughter, yawning, hawking and sneezing as things they do not do before the great, and says that if done, blame stays fixed.","n":"Involuntary acts of the body are treated as conduct: the verse holds a man to blame for a sneeze as for a laugh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","pazhi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 73","lb":"AC73","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[["நகையொடு","laughter is not done before the great",1],["கொட்டாவி","yawning is not done before the great",1],["காறிப்புத்","hawking is not done before the great",1],["தும்மல்","sneezing is not done before the great",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:74":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":74,"t":["நின்றக்கால் நிற்க அடக்கத்தால் என்றும்","இருந்தக்கால் ஏவாமை ஏகார் பெருந்தக்கார்","சொல்லிற் செவிகொடுத்துக் கேட்டீக மீட்டும்","வினாவற்க சொல்லொழிந்தக் கால்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when the great one stands one stands in restraint, when he sits one does not leave unless sent, one listens with attention when he speaks, and one does not ask again once his word is over.","n":"The rule on questions is the sharpest: the hearer may not reopen what the great one has closed, so listening is complete when he stops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kelvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 74","lb":"AC74","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:75":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":75,"t":["உடுக்கை இகவார் செவிசொறுண்டார் கைம்மேல்","எடுத்துரையார் பெண்டிர்மேல் நோக்கார் செவிச்சொல்லும்","கொள்ளார் பெரியார் அகத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that in the house of the great they do not let their garment slip, do not speak with the hand raised, do not look at the women, and do not take in whispered words.","n":"செவிசொறுண்டார் on line 1 is not a clear word; it may be a scribal form of 'scratching the ear'. The bar on looking at the women of the house sets them inside the lord's domain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 75","lb":"AC75","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period barring the attendant's gaze on the women of a great house"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:76":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":76,"t":["விரைந்துரையார் மேன்மேல் உரையார்பொய் யாய","பரந்துரையார் பாரித்து உரையார் - ஒருங்கெனைத்தும்","சில்லெழுத்தினாலே பொருளடங்கக் காலந்தால்","சொல்லுக செவ்வி அறிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one does not speak hastily, on and on, in false and sprawling ways, or at length, but speaks knowing the right moment, with few syllables that hold the whole sense together.","n":"செவ்வி is the hearer's fit moment, so brevity and timing are one rule. காலந்தால் on line 3 is unclear, perhaps காலத்தால்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","solvanmai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 76","lb":"AC76","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:77":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":77,"t":["தம்மேனி நோக்கார் தலையுளரார் கைந்நொடியார்","எம்மேனி ஆயினும் நோக்கார் தலைமகன்","தம்மேனி அல்லால் பிற"],"p":"","r":"The verse says they do not look at their own body, do not tousle their hair, do not snap their fingers, and look at no body but that of the chief.","n":"The gaze itself is disciplined: the attendant's eyes belong to the chief, as in verse 75 they may not fall on his women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","adakkam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 77","lb":"AC77","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:78":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":78,"t":["பிறரொடு மந்திரம் கொள்ளார் இறைவனைச்","சாரார் செவியோரார் சாரின் பிறிதொன்று","தேர்வார்போல் நிற்க திரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says they do not take counsel with others in the lord's presence, do not go near the lord or listen in, and if near, stand turned away as if attending to something else.","n":"Line 3 gives a posture, not only an abstention: one acts out inattention so as to be seen not to be listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 78","lb":"AC78","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:79":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":79,"t":["துன்பத்துள் துன்புற்று வாழ்தலும் இன்பத்துள்","இன்ப வகையால் ஒழுகலும் - அன்பின்","செறப்பட்டார் இல்லம் புகாமையும் மூன்றும்","திறப்பட்டார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things found in the capable: sharing the sorrow when there is sorrow, conducting oneself in joyful ways when there is joy, and, out of loyalty, not entering the house of those who have fallen under wrath.","n":"The sorrow and joy are most likely the lord's, whose moods the attendant mirrors; செறப்பட்டார் are those who have incurred anger, presumably the lord's, so avoiding their house is loyalty, not unkindness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","anbu","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 79","lb":"AC79","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[["துன்பத்துள் துன்புற்று வாழ்தலும்","sorrowing in a time of sorrow is found in the capable",1],["இன்ப வகையால் ஒழுகலும்","behaving joyfully in a time of joy is found in the capable",2],["செறப்பட்டார் இல்லம் புகாமையும்","not entering the house of those under wrath is found in the capable",3]],"cw":"மூன்றும்","ch":"ac:4"},"ac:80":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":80,"t":["தெறுவந்தும் தங்குரவர் பேருரையார் இல்லத்து","உறுமி நெடிதும் இராஅர் - பெரியாரை","என்று முறைகொண்டு கூறார் புலையரையும்","நன்கறிவார் கூறார் முறை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that even when provoked they do not utter their elders' names, do not stay long in a house clearing the throat, do not address the great by terms of kinship, and that those who know well do not address even the புலையர் by terms of kinship.","n":"புலையர் is a caste term for a birth-group held outcaste; here it plainly names persons. The final rule withholds kin-address from them as from the great, at the opposite end of rank; முறை may also mean addressing them with honour. நன்கறிவார் repeats the close of verse 6.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pirappu","sol","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 80","lb":"AC80","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a rule of the period withholding kinship address from those ranked outcaste"],"ch":"ac:4"},"ac:81":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":81,"t":["புழைக்கடைப் புகார் அரசன் கோட்டி உரிமை","இவற்றுக்கண் செவ்வியார் நோக்காரே அவ்வத்","தொழிற்குரிவர் அல்லா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the upright who do not hold the office concerned do not enter by the back gate and do not look into the king's assembly or the women's quarters of the palace.","n":"The bar is tied to office: அவ்வத் தொழிற்குரிவர் அல்லா தவர் (read as தொழிற்குரியவர்) leaves the same doors open to those whose work lies there, so the rule is about trespass, not about the places being forbidden as such. செவ்வியார் may name the upright or those who watch for an opening; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","adakkam","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 81","lb":"AC81","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[["புழைக்கடைப் புகார்","the back gate is not entered by those whose office it is not",1],["அரசன் கோட்டி","the king's assembly is not looked into by those not called to it",1],["உரிமை","the women's quarters of the palace are not looked into by those not serving there",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:82":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":82,"t":["வண்ண மகளிரி இடத்தொடு தம்மிடம்","ஒள்ளியம் என்பார் இடம்கொள்ளார் தெள்ளி","மிகக்கிழமை உண்டெனினும் வேண்டாவே பெண்டிர்க்கு","உவப்பன வேறாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who count themselves wise do not take a seat near the place of the women, even when they have a very close claim of intimacy, because what pleases women turns out to be other than one supposes.","n":"The reason given is a claim about women's liking, not about the men's desire, so the rule rests on a generalisation about women. Line 1 is hard: மகளிரி is likely மகளிர் miswritten, and whether வண்ண மகளிர் are the king's women (as the neighbouring verses would suggest) or any women of a house is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","arivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 82","lb":"AC82","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period keeping men from sitting near women, justified by a claim about women's changeable liking"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:83":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":83,"t":["நிரல்படச் செல்லார் நிழன்மிதித்து நில்லார்","உரையிடை ஆய்ந்து உரையார் ஊர்முனிவ செய்யார்","அரசர் படையளவுஞ் சொல்லாரே என்றும்","கடைபோக வாழ்துமென் பார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists what those who mean to live long and to the end do not do: walk along in a row, stand treading on a shadow, speak by picking at another's talk in its midst, do what the town resents, or tell even the size of the king's army.","n":"Whose shadow and whose row the verse means is unstated; the last item, the king's army, sets the list at court. The close, கடைபோக வாழ்தும், makes the rules a means of survival rather than a virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","sol","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 83","lb":"AC83","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"king","it":[["நிரல்படச் செல்லார்","walking along in a row is avoided",1],["நிழன்மிதித்து நில்லார்","standing on another's shadow is avoided",1],["உரையிடை ஆய்ந்து உரையார்","speaking into the middle of another's talk to pick at it is avoided",2],["ஊர்முனிவ செய்யார்","doing what the town resents is avoided",2],["அரசர் படையளவுஞ் சொல்லாரே","even the size of the king's army is not told",3]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:84":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":84,"t":["அளையுறை பாம்பும் அரசும் நெருப்பும்","முழைஉறை சீயமும் என்றிவை நான்கும்","இளைய எளிய பயின்றனஎன்று எண்ணி","இகழின் இழுக்கந் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names four things that bring harm if one slights them, thinking them young, weak or familiar: the snake that lives in a hole, the king, fire, and the lion that lives in a cave.","n":"The three reasons for contempt (இளைய, எளிய, பயின்றன) map loosely onto the four things: a young king, a small fire, a snake or lion one has grown used to. The king sits second, among beasts and fire, so his claim to respect is his power to harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pochchaappu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 84","lb":"AC84","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["அளையுறை பாம்பும்","the snake in its hole harms one who slights it as small or familiar",1],["அரசும்","the king harms one who slights him as young or familiar",1],["நெருப்பும்","fire harms one who slights it as small",1],["முழைஉறை சீயமும்","the lion in its cave harms one who slights it as familiar",2]],"cw":"என்றிவை நான்கும்","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:85":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":85,"t":["அறத்தொடு கல்யாணம் ஆள்வினை கூரை","இறப்பப் பெருகியக் கண்ணும் - திறப்பட்டார்","மன்னரின் மேம்பட்ட செய்யற்க செய்யின்","மன்னிய செல்வம் கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that capable men, even when their charity, weddings, enterprise and houses grow very great, do nothing that outdoes the king, and that if they do, their lasting wealth is ruined.","n":"Even அறம் is under the limit: a subject's giving must not outshine the king's. The rule is prudential, with ruin of wealth as its sanction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","alavu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 85","lb":"AC85","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[["அறத்தொடு","charity, however great, is not made to outdo the king's",1],["கல்யாணம்","a wedding is not made grander than the king's",1],["ஆள்வினை","enterprise is not pushed beyond the king's",1],["கூரை","the house is not built above the king's",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:86":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":86,"t":["உண்டது கேளார்; குரவரை மிக்காரைக்","கண்டுழிக் கண்டால் மனந்திரியார்; புல்லரையும்","உண்டது கேளார் விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one does not ask elders and superiors what they have eaten, does not change countenance on meeting them wherever they are met, and does not ask even mean people what they have eaten.","n":"The question 'what did you eat' is barred upward and downward alike: புல்லரையும் extends the courtesy to the lowly. The syntax of lines 1-2 is loose; the reading takes குரவரை மிக்காரை as the object of both clauses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","undi","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 86","lb":"AC86","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["உண்டது கேளார்","asking elders and superiors what they have eaten is avoided",1],["மனந்திரியார்","turning one's mind or face on meeting them is avoided",2],["புல்லரையும் உண்டது கேளார்","even mean people are not asked what they have eaten",2]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:87":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":87,"t":["கிடந்தாரைக் கால்கழுவார் பூப்பெய்யார் சாந்தும்","மறந்தானும் எஞ்ஞான்றும் பூசார் கிடந்தார்கண்","நில்லார்தாம் கட்டின் மிசை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that people do not wash the feet of one lying down, do not put flowers on him, never smear him with sandal paste even by oversight, and do not stand on the cot where someone lies.","n":"Washing the feet, laying flowers and anointing with sandal are acts done to a body laid out in death, so the rule most likely keeps the living sleeper from being treated as a corpse; the verse does not say so. கட்டின் மிசை is read as the cot.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 87","lb":"AC87","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["கால்கழுவார்","the feet of one lying down are not washed",1],["பூப்பெய்யார்","flowers are not put on one lying down",1],["சாந்தும்","sandal paste is never smeared on one lying down, even by oversight",1],["நில்லார்தாம் கட்டின் மிசை","one does not stand on the cot where someone lies",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period barring toward the sleeping the acts of the funeral rite"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:88":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":88,"t":["உதவிப் பயனுரையார் உண்டி பழியார்","அறத்தொடு தான்நோற்ற நோன்பு வியவார்","திறத்துளி வாழ்தும்என் பார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who mean to live in the right way do not speak of the return on help they gave, do not find fault with food, and do not marvel at their own charity and the vows they kept.","n":"All three are faults of the tongue about one's own goods: help given, food set before one, merit earned. Faulting food sits between the two boasts, so ingratitude to the host is grouped with self-praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","panivu","thavam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 88","lb":"AC88","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["உதவிப் பயனுரையார்","the fruit of help one has given is not spoken of",1],["உண்டி பழியார்","food is not found fault with",1],["அறத்தொடு தான்நோற்ற நோன்பு வியவார்","one's own charity and kept vows are not admired aloud",2]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:89":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":89,"t":["எய்யாத வேண்டார் இரங்கார் இகந்ததற்குக்","கைவாரா வந்த இடுக்கண் மனம் அழுங்கார்","மெய்யாய காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of true vision do not want what cannot be had, do not grieve over what has passed, and are not troubled at heart by a hardship that has come beyond their power to prevent.","n":"Each item is scoped: only the unattainable, only the past, only the trouble கைவாரா, beyond one's hand. The verse does not counsel calm before everything, only before what cannot be changed, and calls that calm a matter of vision (மெய்யாய காட்சி) rather than of will.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","avaa","idukkan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 89","lb":"AC89","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["எய்யாத","what cannot be attained is not wanted",1],["இகந்ததற்குக்","what has passed is not grieved over",1],["கைவாரா வந்த இடுக்கண்","a hardship beyond one's control does not distress the mind",2]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:90":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":90,"t":["தலைக்கிட்ட பூமேவார் மோந்தபூச் சூடார்","பசுக்கொடுப்பின் பார்ப்பார்கைக் கொள்ளாரே என்றும்","புலைக்கு எச்சில் நீட்டார் விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that people do not take up flowers already worn on the head or wear flowers already smelled, do not accept a cow from a brahmin's hand when one is given, and never hold out leavings to the புலை.","n":"புலை is a caste term for the outcaste, set here as one to whom even leavings are not held out, so the rule bars a contact through food; it could also be read as an impure thing, hence the doubt. The cow rule is unclear: it may bar taking back a cow from a brahmin, as brahmins receive and do not give.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theettu","andhanar","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 90","lb":"AC90","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["தலைக்கிட்ட பூ","flowers already worn on the head are not taken up",1],["மோந்தபூ","flowers already smelled are not worn",1],["பசுக்கொடுப்பின் பார்ப்பார்கைக் கொள்ளாரே","a cow is not taken from a brahmin's hand when given",2],["புலைக்கு எச்சில் நீட்டார்","leavings are not held out to the outcaste",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","caste"],"rules of the period on used flowers and leavings, with a birth-group barred even from receiving leavings and brahmins set apart as receivers"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:91":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":91,"t":["மோட்டுடைப் போர்வையோடு ஏக்கழுத்துந் தாளிசைப்பும்","காட்டுளே யானும் பழித்தார மாம்தம்மின்","மூத்த உளஆக லான்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that a thick wrap worn over the body, a stiffly raised neck and the noise of the feet are blamed even in the forest, since there are always those older than oneself.","n":"The reason, தம்மின் மூத்த உள, makes the rule hold where no one is watching: even in the forest there are elders, perhaps the old trees and beings of the forest themselves. தாளிசைப்பு is read as the sound of the feet; it may be crossing the legs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","agandhai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 91","lb":"AC91","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["மோட்டுடைப் போர்வை","a thick wrap over the body is blamed",1],["ஏக்கழுத்து","a stiffly raised neck is blamed",1],["தாளிசைப்பும்","noisy footsteps are blamed",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:92":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":92,"t":["தலைஇய நற்கருமஞ் செய்யுங்கால் என்றும்","புலையர்வாய் நாள்கேட்டுச் செய்யார் - தொலைவில்லா","அந்தணர்வாய்ச் சொல்கேட்டுச் செய்க அவர் வாய்ச்சொல்","என்றும் பிழைப்ப தில்லை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when one performs an important auspicious rite one never fixes the day by asking the புலையர், and that one acts on the word of the unfailing அந்தணர், whose word never errs.","n":"புலையர் and அந்தணர் are set as two birth-groups whose word on the day is untrusted and trusted; புலையர் is a caste term for the outcaste and here names persons, not a thing. That the புலையர் could be asked at all implies they were reckoners of days in the period.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","pirappu","kaalam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 92","lb":"AC92","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a rule of the period on who may fix the day of a rite, by birth-group"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:93":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":93,"t":["மன்றத்து நின்று உஞற்றார் மாசுதிமிர்ந் தியங்கார்","என்றும் கடுஞ்சொல் உரையார் இருவராய்","நின்று உழியும் செல்லார் விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that people do not stand and bustle in the public square, do not go about smeared with dirt, never speak harsh words, and do not go to a place where two stand together.","n":"The last item guards a private talk from intrusion; the first keeps one from making a show in the common square. உஞற்றார் is read as 'do not exert or bustle'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","thooimai","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 93","lb":"AC93","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["மன்றத்து நின்று உஞற்றார்","bustling while standing in the public square is avoided",1],["மாசுதிமிர்ந் தியங்கார்","going about smeared with dirt is avoided",1],["கடுஞ்சொல் உரையார்","harsh words are never spoken",2],["இருவராய்","a place where two stand together is not approached",2]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:94":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":94,"t":["கைசுட்டிக் கட்டுரையார் கால்மேல் எழுத்திடார்","மெய்சுட்டி இல்லாரை உள்ளாரோடு ஒப்புரையார்","கையில் குரவர் கொடுப்ப இருந்துஏலார்","ஐயமில் காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of clear vision do not point with the hand while speaking, do not write with the foot, do not compare the poor with the rich by pointing at them, and do not sit while receiving what elders hand them.","n":"The third item makes a comparison of rich and poor a discourtesy to the poor, not only to the rich. கால்மேல் எழுத்திடார் is obscure: writing with the toe, or writing on the leg.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sol","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 94","lb":"AC94","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["கைசுட்டிக் கட்டுரையார்","pointing with the hand while speaking is avoided",1],["கால்மேல் எழுத்திடார்","writing with or on the foot is avoided",1],["இல்லாரை உள்ளாரோடு ஒப்புரையார்","the poor are not compared with the rich by pointing",2],["இருந்துஏலார்","what elders hand over is not received sitting",3]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:95":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":95,"t":["தன்னுடம்பு தாரம் அடைக்கலம் தன்னுயிர்க்குஎன்று","உன்னித்து வைத்த பொருளோ டிவைநான்கும்","பொன்னினைப் போல்போற்றிக் காத்துய்க்க உய்க்காக்கால்","மன்னிய ஏதம் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names four things to be guarded like gold and used with care, one's own body, one's wife, a deposit placed in one's keeping, and wealth set aside for one's own life, and says that failing to do so brings lasting harm.","n":"The wife stands between the body and a deposit held in trust, so she is listed among goods in the man's keeping; the deposit, another's property, is guarded as closely as one's own body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vaazhkkaithunai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 95","lb":"AC95","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தன்னுடம்பு","one's own body is to be guarded like gold",1],["தாரம்","one's wife is to be guarded like gold",1],["அடைக்கலம்","a deposit placed in one's keeping is to be guarded like gold",1],["உன்னித்து வைத்த பொருளோ","wealth set aside for one's own life is to be guarded like gold",2]],"cw":"டிவைநான்கும்","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period that lists the wife among the goods a man guards"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:96":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":96,"t":["நந்தெறும்பு தூக்கணம் புள்காக்கை என்றிவைபோல்","தம்கருமம் நல்ல கடைப்பிடித்துத் தங்கருமம்","அப்பெற்றி யாக முயல்பவர்க்கு ஆசாரம்","எப்பெற்றி யானும் படும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that ஆசாரம் comes in any case to those who, like the ant, the weaver bird and the crow, hold fast to their own good work and strive to keep it in that manner.","n":"The creatures are named without their qualities, so the model is their steady keeping to their own work. நந்தெறும்பு may be one word, 'the good ant', or two, the snail and the ant; it is taken as one item.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","muyarchi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 96","lb":"AC96","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["நந்தெறும்பு","the ant (or the snail and the ant) is a model of holding to one's own work",1],["தூக்கணம் புள்","the weaver bird is a model of holding to one's own work",1],["காக்கை","the crow is a model of holding to one's own work",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:97":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":97,"t":["தொழுதாலும் வாய்புதைத் தானும் அஃதன்றிப்","பெரியார்முன் யாதும் உரையார் பழியவர்","கண்ணுள்ளே நோக்கி யுரை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that before the great one says nothing except with folded hands or a covered mouth, and that speaking while looking into their eyes is a fault.","n":"The last line can also be read as an instruction to speak looking at them; the reading takes பழி as naming the direct gaze a fault. The verse is missing from one of the source texts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 97","lb":"AC97","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:98":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":98,"t":["சூதர் கழகம் அரவம் அறாக்களம்","பேதைகள் அல்லார் புகாஅர் புகுபவேல்","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who are not fools do not enter the gamblers' hall or the ground where the din never stops, and that one who enters them meets many harms.","n":"The danger is in the place, not only the play: entering is the fault, and the noisy ground is ranked with the gaming hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","pedhaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 98","lb":"AC98","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["சூதர் கழகம்","the gamblers' hall is not entered by the wise",1],["அரவம் அறாக்களம்","the ground of unceasing din is not entered by the wise",1]],"cw":"","ch":"ac:5"},"ac:99":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":99,"t":["உரற்களத்தும் அட்டிலும் பெண்டிர்கள் மேலும்","நடுக்கற்ற காட்சியார் நோக்கார் எடுத்திசையார்","இல்லம் புகாஅர் விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those of steady vision do not look at the pounding place, the kitchen or the women there, do not raise their voices, and do not enter the house.","n":"The pounding place and the kitchen are where the women of a house work, so the bar on looking is a bar on looking at women at their work; the verse ends by keeping the visitor out of the house altogether.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","ilvaazhkkai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 99","lb":"AC99","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"all","it":[["உரற்களத்தும்","the pounding place is not looked at",1],["அட்டிலும்","the kitchen is not looked at",1],["பெண்டிர்கள் மேலும்","women are not looked at",1],["எடுத்திசையார்","the voice is not raised",2],["இல்லம் புகாஅர்","the house is not entered",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period keeping a man's eyes off women and their working spaces in another's house"],"ch":"ac:5"},"ac:100":{"book":"acharakkovai","num":100,"t":["அறியாத தேயத்தான் ஆதுலன் மூத்தான்","இளையான் உயிரிழந்தான் அஞ்சினான் உண்டான்","அரசர் தொழில்தலை வைத்தான் மணாளனென்ற","ஒன்பதின்மர் கண்டீர் உரைக்குங்கால் மெய்யான","ஆசாரம் வீடுபெற் றார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names nine who, rightly spoken of, are freed from ஆசாரம்: one from an unknown land, the destitute, the old, the young, one who has lost a life, the frightened, one who has eaten, one who has put the king's work first, and the bridegroom.","n":"The code closes by bounding itself: the stranger who cannot know the local rule, those too weak or too young, those in fear or grief, and those taken up by the king's work or by their own wedding. உயிரிழந்தான் most likely means one bereaved by a death, though it can read as one near death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","inmai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Acharakkovai 100","lb":"AC100","poet":"பெருவாயின் முள்ளியார்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["அறியாத தேயத்தான்","one from an unknown land is freed from ஆசாரம்",1],["ஆதுலன்","the destitute is freed from it",1],["மூத்தான்","the old man is freed from it",1],["இளையான்","the young are freed from it",2],["உயிரிழந்தான்","one who has lost a life, most likely the bereaved, is freed from it",2],["அஞ்சினான்","the frightened is freed from it",2],["உண்டான்","one who has eaten is freed from it",2],["அரசர் தொழில்தலை வைத்தான்","one who has put the king's work first is freed from it",3],["மணாளனென்ற","the bridegroom is freed from it",3]],"cw":"ஒன்பதின்மர்","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period naming who is exempt from the rules of conduct"],"ch":"ac:5"},"el:payiram":{"book":"elathi","num":1,"t":["இல்லறநூல்ஏற்ற துறவறநூல் ஏயுங்கால்","சொல்லறநூல் சோர்வின்றித் தொக்குரைத்து - நல்ல","அணிமேதை யாய்நல்ல வீட்டு நெறியும்","கணிமேதை செய்தான் கலந்து"],"p":"","r":"The payiram says that Kanimethai, an ornament of good learning, gathered and told without lapse the books of virtue, joining the renouncer's code to the householder's, and mixed into the work the good way to release as well.","n":"It names the book's plan rather than its title: two codes, the household and the renunciant, made one, with release added. கணிமேதை is the author's name, and அணிமேதை puns on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","veedu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi payiram","lb":"ELpayiram","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:0":{"book":"elathi","num":2,"t":["அறுநால்வ ராய்புகழ்ச் சேவடி யாற்றப்","பெறுநால்வர் பேணி வழங்கிப் - பெறுநால்","மறைபுரிந்து வாழுமேல் மண்ணொழிந்து விண்ணோர்க்(கு)","இறைபுரிந்து வாழ்தல் இயல்பு"],"p":"","r":"The invocation says that one who tends and gives to the four, holding to the red feet praised by the twenty-four and following the four scriptures, will by nature leave the earth and live as lord over the gods.","n":"அறுநால்வர் (six times four) is most likely the twenty-four Tirthankaras, which fits a Jain author, but the verse names no one. Who the four that are tended and given to are, and which four scriptures (நால் மறை) are meant, the verse does not say; our reading of a Jain fourfold canon and fourfold gift is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","eegai","marumai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 0","lb":"EL0","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:1":{"book":"elathi","num":3,"t":["சென்ற புகழ்செல்வம் மீக்கூற்றம் சேவகம்","நின்ற நிலைகல்வி வள்ளன்மை - என்றும்","வழிவந்தார் பூங்கோதாய் ஆறும் மறையின்","வழிவந்தார் கண்ணே வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names six things (fame that has spread, wealth, eminence, valour in service, steadfast learning and liberality) and says, to a woman with flowered hair, that they are beauty only in those who walk the way of the scripture.","n":"The goods named are worldly ones, but the closing line makes them beautiful only on the virtuous, so the verse ranks conduct over the goods it lists. நின்ற நிலை is taken with கல்வி as one item (steadfast learning); read apart, the list has seven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam","pugazh","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 1","lb":"EL1","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["சென்ற புகழ்","fame that has spread is a beauty in those who walk the scripture's way",1],["செல்வம்","wealth is a beauty in them",1],["மீக்கூற்றம்","eminence, being spoken of highly, is a beauty in them",1],["சேவகம்","valour in service is a beauty in them",1],["நின்ற நிலைகல்வி","learning that stands firm is a beauty in them",2],["வள்ளன்மை","liberality is a beauty in them",2]],"cw":"ஆறும்","ch":"el:1"},"el:2":{"book":"elathi","num":4,"t":["கொலையுரியான் கொல்லான் புலால் மயங்கான் கூர்த்த","அலைபுரியான் வஞ்சியான் யாதும் - நிலைதிரியான்","மண்ணவர்க்கும் அன்றி மதுமலிபூங் கோதாய்","விண்ணவர்க்கும் மேலாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who, though entitled to kill, does not kill, is not deluded by flesh, does no sharp torment, does not deceive and never swerves from his state rises above not only those on earth but those in the sky.","n":"கொலையுரியான் கொல்லான் is taken as one item, one who has the right to kill (a king or a judge) and does not; read as two negatives (does not abet killing, does not kill) the verse has six items, the table's count. உரியான் is a positive form, which is why we read five.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","vanjam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 2","lb":"EL2","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலையுரியான் கொல்லான்","one who has the right to kill yet does not kill rises above earth and sky",1],["புலால் மயங்கான்","one not deluded into eating flesh rises so",1],["கூர்த்த அலைபுரியான்","one who does no sharp torment rises so",1],["வஞ்சியான்","one who does not deceive rises so",2],["நிலைதிரியான்","one who never swerves from his state rises so",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:3":{"book":"elathi","num":5,"t":["தவம் எளிது தானம் அரிதுக் கார்க்கேல்","அவம் அரி(து) ஆதல் எளிதால் - அவமிலா","இன்பம் பிறழின் இயைஎளிது மற்றதன்","துன்பம் துடைத்தல் அரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets easy things against hard ones: penance is easy and giving hard; [avoiding] waste is hard and falling into it easy; when blameless joy slips, joining with [grief] is easy, but wiping away that grief is hard.","n":"Only the first pair is clear: the verse ranks giving as harder than penance. Line 1 ends in the obscure அரிதுக் கார்க்கேல், and the middle pairs are our construal of a damaged line; the six items follow the verse's easy/hard predicates.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 3","lb":"EL3","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["தவம்","penance is easy",1],["தானம்","giving is hard",1],["அவம்","[avoiding] waste is hard",2],["ஆதல்","falling into it is easy",2],["இன்பம் பிறழின்","when blameless joy slips, falling in with its loss is easy",3],["துன்பம் துடைத்தல்","wiping away that grief is hard",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:4":{"book":"elathi","num":6,"t":["இடர்த்தீர்த்தல் எள்ளாமை கீழினஞ்சே ராமை","படர்தீர்த்தல் யார்க்கும் பழிப்பின் - நடைதீர்த்தல்","கண்டவர் காமுறுஞ் சொல்காணின் கல்வியின்கண்","விண்டவர்நூல் வேண்டா விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names six practices (relieving distress, not scorning, not joining the base, relieving grief, giving up conduct blamed by all, and speech that those who see it desire) and says that where these are seen, the books of those who expound learning can be let go as unneeded.","n":"The closing line sets conduct above book-learning: the list does what the books are for. கண்டவர் காமுறுஞ் சொல் is read as the sixth item and காணின் as 'if [these] are seen'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","oppuravu","inam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 4","lb":"EL4","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["இடர்த்தீர்த்தல்","relieving others' distress makes the books of learning unneeded",1],["எள்ளாமை","not scorning others does so",1],["கீழினஞ்சே ராமை","not joining the company of the base does so",1],["படர்தீர்த்தல்","relieving grief does so",2],["யார்க்கும் பழிப்பின் - நடைதீர்த்தல்","giving up conduct that all would blame does so",2],["கண்டவர் காமுறுஞ் சொல்","speech that those who meet it desire does so",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:5":{"book":"elathi","num":7,"t":["தனக்கென்றும் ஓர்பாங்கன் பொய்யான்மெய் யாக்கும்","எனக்கென்(று) இயையான்யா தொன்றும் - புனர்க்கொன்றை","போலும் இழையார்சொல் தேறான் களியானேல்","சாலும் பிறநூலின் சார்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who is always his own companion, does not lie and makes his word true, claims nothing as 'mine', does not trust the words of women adorned like konrai blossoms and is not intoxicated, has what the support of other books would give.","n":"Like verses 4 and 14, the verse ends by setting the books aside, but சாலும் பிறநூலின் சார்பு can mean either that he needs no other book or that he is enough to be one; தனக்கென்றும் ஓர்பாங்கன் may instead mean one who keeps a single friend. The distrust of women's words is a gender rule of the period.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vaaimai","pattru","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 5","lb":"EL5","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["தனக்கென்றும் ஓர்பாங்கன்","one who is ever his own companion has what other books give",1],["பொய்யான்மெய் யாக்கும்","one who does not lie and makes his word true has it",1],["எனக்கென்(று) இயையான்","one who claims nothing as his own has it",2],["இழையார்சொல் தேறான்","one who does not trust the words of adorned women has it",3],["களியானேல்","one who is not intoxicated has it",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period that a man of virtue does not trust women's words"],"ch":"el:1"},"el:6":{"book":"elathi","num":8,"t":["நிறையுடைமை நீர்மை யுடைமை கொடையே","பொறையுடைமை பொய்ம்மை புலாற்கண் - மறையுடைமை","வேயன்ன தோளாய் இவையுடையான் பல்லுயிர்க்கும்","தாயன்னன் என்னத் தகும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, to a woman with bamboo-like shoulders, that one who has self-restraint, a gentle nature, giving, patience, and a door shut to falsehood and to flesh is fit to be called a mother to all living beings.","n":"மறையுடைமை governs both பொய்ம்மை and புலால், so the last two items share one predicate of keeping them out; the six are counted that way. The reward is not heaven but a title: mother to every life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","nirai","porai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 6","lb":"EL6","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["நிறையுடைமை","self-restraint makes one a mother to all lives",1],["நீர்மை யுடைமை","a gentle nature does so",1],["கொடையே","giving does so",1],["பொறையுடைமை","patience does so",2],["பொய்ம்மை","keeping falsehood shut out does so",2],["புலாற்கண் - மறையுடைமை","keeping flesh shut out does so",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:7":{"book":"elathi","num":9,"t":["இன்சொல் அளாவல் இடம்இனிதூண் யாவர்க்கும்","வன்சொல் களைந்து வகுப்பானேல் - மென்சொல்","முருந்தேய்க்கும் முட்போல் எயிற்றினாய் நாளும்","விருந்தேற்பர் விண்ணோர் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a man, putting away harsh words, gives to everyone sweet speech, warm mingling, a place to sit and good food, the gods in the sky will hasten to receive him as their guest every day.","n":"The four gifts are graded from words to food, and the fifth item is a removal (harsh words) that frames them all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inchol","marumai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 7","lb":"EL7","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["இன்சொல்","sweet speech to all wins the gods' welcome",1],["அளாவல்","cordial mingling does so",1],["இடம்","giving a place does so",1],["இனிதூண்","giving good food does so",1],["வன்சொல் களைந்து","putting away harsh words does so",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:8":{"book":"elathi","num":10,"t":["உடன்படான் கொல்லான் உடன்றார்நோய் தீர்ந்து","மடம்படான் மாண்டார்நூல் மாண்ட - இடம்பட","நோக்கும்வாய் நோக்கி நுழைவானேல் மற்றவனை","ஆக்குமவர் ஆக்கும் அணைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who does not consent to killing, does not kill, relieves the sickness of the afflicted, does not fall into folly, and enters the excellent books of the excellent, studying them widely, will be raised by those who raise men, who come close to him.","n":"ஆக்குமவர் ஆக்கும் அணைந்து is obscure: 'those who make [men great]' may be the gods or the virtuous. Consenting to a killing is counted as an act separate from killing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","kalvi","arul","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 8","lb":"EL8","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["உடன்படான்","one who does not consent to killing is raised up",1],["கொல்லான்","one who does not kill is raised up",1],["உடன்றார்நோய் தீர்ந்து","one who relieves the sickness of the afflicted is raised up",1],["மடம்படான்","one who does not fall into folly is raised up",2],["மாண்டார்நூல்","one who enters widely into the books of the excellent is raised up",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:9":{"book":"elathi","num":11,"t":["கற்றாரைக் கற்ற துணையார் எனமதியார்","உற்றாரை அன்னணம் ஓராமல் - அற்றார்கட்(கு)","உண்டி உறையுள் உடுக்கை இவைஈந்தார்","பண்டிதராய் வாழ்வார் பயின்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who, not rating the learned only by their learning nor their kin only by kinship, give food, shelter and clothing to the destitute will live as learned men, practised in it.","n":"The reward is a paradox: giving, not study, makes one a பண்டிதர். Lines 1-2 are hard to construe; we read them as giving without regard to the receiver's learning or kinship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 9","lb":"EL9","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கற்றாரைக் கற்ற துணையார் எனமதியார்","not rating the learned only by their learning makes one a learned man",1],["உற்றாரை அன்னணம் ஓராமல்","not weighing kin by kinship does so",2],["உண்டி","giving food to the destitute does so",3],["உறையுள்","giving them shelter does so",3],["உடுக்கை","giving them clothing does so",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:10":{"book":"elathi","num":12,"t":["செங்கோலான் கீழ்க்குடிகள் செல்வமும் சீரிலா","வெங்கோலான் கீழ்க்குடிகள் வீந்துகவும் - வெங்கோல்","அமைச்சர் தொழிலும் அறியலம்ஒன்(று) ஆற்ற","எனைத்தும் அறியாமை யான்"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets the wealth of subjects under a just sceptre against the ruin of subjects under a cruel and worthless one and the work of a cruel sceptre's ministers, and says that 'we' cannot know these fully for not knowing anything at all.","n":"Lines 3-4 (அறியலம்ஒன்(று) ஆற்ற எனைத்தும் அறியாமை யான்) are obscure; the speaker's disclaimer may be ironic, or the sense may be that a ruler who cannot do even one thing fully cannot know these. The three items are clear, the predicate is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","kudi","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 10","lb":"EL10","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"king","it":[["செங்கோலான் கீழ்க்குடிகள் செல்வமும்","the wealth of subjects under a just sceptre is set out",1],["வெங்கோலான் கீழ்க்குடிகள் வீந்துகவும்","the ruin of subjects under a cruel sceptre is set against it",2],["அமைச்சர் தொழிலும்","the work of a cruel sceptre's ministers is named with it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:11":{"book":"elathi","num":13,"t":["அவாஅறுக்கல் உற்றான் தளரான்அவ் ஐந்தின்","அவாஅறுப்பின் ஆற்ற அமையும் - அவாஅறான்","ஆகும் அவனாயின் ஐங்களிற்றின் ஆட்டுண்டு","போகும் புழையுள் புலந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who has set out to cut desire does not slacken, that cutting the desire for those five fully is enough, and that one who does not cut it is tossed about by the five elephants and goes sullen into the hole.","n":"The verse does not list its five; ஐங்களிறு (five elephants) makes them the senses. புழை, the narrow hole, is most likely rebirth. The three items are the verse's three states of desire, not a counted list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","adakkam","piravi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 11","lb":"EL11","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["அவாஅறுக்கல் உற்றான்","one who sets out to cut desire does not slacken",1],["அவாஅறுப்பின்","cutting desire for the five fully is enough",2],["அவாஅறான்","one who does not cut it is tossed by the five elephants into the hole",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:12":{"book":"elathi","num":14,"t":["கொலைக்களம் வார்குத்துச் சூதாடும் எல்லை","அலைக்களம் போர்யானை ஆக்கும் - நிலைக்களம்","முச்சா ரிகைஒதுங்கும் ஓரிடத்தும் இன்னவை","நச்சாமை நோக்காமை நன்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five places (the killing ground, the ground where men gamble with strips, the place of torment, the ground where war elephants are trained, and the place where the three circlings are made) and says it is good neither to desire them nor to look at them.","n":"Even looking is barred: the rule reaches past doing violence to watching it. வார்குத்து and முச்சாரிகை are rare words; the latter is perhaps the wheeling of horses or elephants in drill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","soodhu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 12","lb":"EL12","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலைக்களம்","it is good not to desire or look at the killing ground",1],["வார்குத்துச் சூதாடும் எல்லை","nor at the place where men gamble",1],["அலைக்களம்","nor at the place of torment",2],["போர்யானை ஆக்கும் - நிலைக்களம்","nor at the ground where war elephants are trained",2],["முச்சா ரிகைஒதுங்கும் ஓரிடத்தும்","nor at the place where the three circlings are made",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:13":{"book":"elathi","num":15,"t":["விளையாமை உண்ணாமை ஆடாமை ஆற்ற","உளையாமை உட்குடைத்தா வென்று - களையாமை","நூற்பட்டார் பூங்கோதாய் நோக்கின் இவையாறும்","பாற்பட்டார் கொண்டொழும் பண்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that not sowing, not eating, not bathing, not fretting greatly, conquering so as to inspire awe, and not plucking out are, on inspection, the six marks kept by those who hold to the books and stand in their portion.","n":"Our division of line 2 (உட்குடைத்தா வென்று, களையாமை) is doubtful. Read as a Jain ascetic's code, not sowing and not weeding would avoid harm to life in the soil, and not bathing is a known renunciant rule, the reverse of the householder's bathing rules elsewhere in this group.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","adakkam","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 13","lb":"EL13","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["விளையாமை","not sowing is a mark of the ascetic",1],["உண்ணாமை","fasting is one",1],["ஆடாமை","not bathing is one",1],["உளையாமை","not fretting greatly is one",2],["உட்குடைத்தா வென்று","conquering so as to inspire awe is one",2],["களையாமை","not plucking or weeding is one",2]],"cw":"இவையாறும்","ch":"el:1"},"el:14":{"book":"elathi","num":16,"t":["பொய்யான் புலாலொடு கட்போக்கித் தீயன","செய்யான் சிறியார் இனஞ்சேரான் - வையான்","கயலியல்உண் கண்ணாய்! கருதுங்கால் என்றும்","அயல அயலவாம் நூல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, to a woman with fish-like eyes, that for one who does not lie, has put away flesh and toddy, does no evil, does not join the base and does not revile, the books are, on reflection, always beside the point.","n":"Flesh and toddy are joined by ஒடு but counted as two, giving six. The predicate (அயல அயலவாம் நூல்) puts the books outside such a man, the third time in the chapter conduct replaces the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pulaal","kal","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 14","lb":"EL14","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யான்","one who does not lie has no need of the books",1],["புலாலொடு","one who has put away flesh has none",1],["கட்போக்கித்","one who has put away toddy has none",1],["தீயன - செய்யான்","one who does no evil has none",1],["சிறியார் இனஞ்சேரான்","one who does not join the base has none",2],["வையான்","one who does not revile has none",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:15":{"book":"elathi","num":17,"t":["கண்போல்வார்க் காயாமை கற்றார் இனஞ்சேர்தல்","பண்போல் கிளவியார்ப் பற்றாமை - பண்போலும்","சொல்லார்க்(கு) அருமறைசோ ராமை சிறிதெனினும்","இல்லார்க்(கு) இடர்தீர்த்தல் நன்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls good five things: not being angry with those dear as one's eyes, joining the company of the learned, not clinging to those whose speech is like music, not letting a precious secret slip to those whose words are melodious, and relieving the distress of the poor even a little.","n":"Items 3 and 4 both guard against sweet speakers: one forbids attachment, the other forbids trusting them with a secret. பண்போல் கிளவியார் may be women, but the word does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","vekuli","eegai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 15","lb":"EL15","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்போல்வார்க் காயாமை","not being angry with those dear as one's eyes is good",1],["கற்றார் இனஞ்சேர்தல்","joining the company of the learned is good",1],["பண்போல் கிளவியார்ப் பற்றாமை","not clinging to those of music-like speech is good",2],["அருமறைசோ ராமை","not letting a precious secret slip to sweet talkers is good",3],["இல்லார்க்(கு) இடர்தீர்த்தல்","relieving the poor's distress, even a little, is good",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:16":{"book":"elathi","num":18,"t":["துறந்தார்கண் துன்னித் துறவார்(கு) இடுதல்","இறந்தார்க்கு இனிய இசைத்தல் - இறந்தார்","மறுதலை சுற்றம் மதித்தோம்பு வானேல்","இறுதலில் வாழ்வே இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a man draws near to renunciants, gives to those who have not renounced, speaks well of the dead, and honours and tends the kin the dead leave behind, his life is sweet and without ruin.","n":"Four items, not six: the verse pairs those who renounced with those who did not, then the dead with their kin. இறந்தார்க்கு இனிய இசைத்தல் may also mean rites for the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","thuravu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 16","lb":"EL16","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["துறந்தார்கண் துன்னித்","drawing near to renunciants makes life sweet",1],["துறவார்(கு) இடுதல்","giving to those who have not renounced does so",1],["இறந்தார்க்கு இனிய இசைத்தல்","speaking well of the dead does so",2],["மறுதலை சுற்றம்","honouring and tending the kin the dead leave does so",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:17":{"book":"elathi","num":19,"t":["குடியோம்பல் வன்கண்மை நூல்வன்மை கூடம்","மடியோம்பும் ஆற்றல் உடைமை - முடியோம்பி","நாற்றம் சுவைகேள்வி நல்லார் இனஞ்சேர்தல்","தேற்றானேல் தேறும் அமைச்சு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names what makes a trusted minister: guarding the people, resolve, strength in learning, secret counsel, the power to keep off sloth, and, guarding the crown and discerning in smell, taste and hearing, keeping the company of the good.","n":"Six nominal items are counted; முடியோம்பி நாற்றம் சுவைகேள்வி is read as a phrase qualifying the last, which is doubtful. கூடம் may be secret counsel or stratagem. The last line (தேற்றானேல் தேறும்) may mean that even untested, such a man is to be trusted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kudi","kalvi","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 17","lb":"EL17","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"king","it":[["குடியோம்பல்","guarding the people marks a trusted minister",1],["வன்கண்மை","resolve does so",1],["நூல்வன்மை","strength in learning does so",1],["கூடம்","secret counsel does so",1],["மடியோம்பும் ஆற்றல் உடைமை","the power to keep off sloth does so",2],["நல்லார் இனஞ்சேர்தல்","keeping the company of the good does so",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:18":{"book":"elathi","num":20,"t":["போகம் பொருள்கேடு மான்வேட்டம் பொல்லாக்கள்","சோகம் படுஞ்சூதே சொல்வன்மை - சோகக்","கடுங்கதத்துத் தண்டம் அடங்காமை காப்பின்","அடுங்கதமில் ஏனை அரசு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a king guards against sensual indulgence, waste of wealth, deer-hunting, evil toddy, gambling that brings grief, harsh force of words, cruel and wrathful punishment, and lack of restraint, other kings will have no wrath that can destroy him.","n":"The first seven items match the old list of a king's seven vices (women, hunting, drink, dice, harsh words, harsh punishment, waste); அடங்காமை closes them as their sum. Eight items against the table's six.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soodhu","kal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 18","lb":"EL18","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"king","it":[["போகம்","guarding against sensual indulgence keeps a king safe from rivals",1],["பொருள்கேடு","guarding against waste of wealth does so",1],["மான்வேட்டம்","guarding against deer-hunting does so",1],["பொல்லாக்கள்","guarding against evil toddy does so",1],["சோகம் படுஞ்சூதே","guarding against gambling that brings grief does so",2],["சொல்வன்மை","guarding against harsh force of words does so",2],["கடுங்கதத்துத் தண்டம்","guarding against cruel, wrathful punishment does so",3],["அடங்காமை","guarding against lack of restraint does so",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:19":{"book":"elathi","num":21,"t":["கொல்லான் கொலைபுரியான் பொய்யான் பிறர்மனைமேல்","செல்லான் சிறியார் இனஞ்சேரான் - சொல்லும்","மறையின் செவியிலன் தீச்சொற்கண் மூங்கை","இறையில் பெரியாற்கு இவை"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as belonging to one greater than a king: not killing, not causing killing, not lying, not going after another's wife, not joining the base, being deaf to secrets told, and dumb to evil speech.","n":"Seven items against the table's six. இறையில் பெரியான் can be 'one greater than a king' or 'the great one without fault'; either way the list is a measure of greatness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai","piranil","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 19","lb":"EL19","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொல்லான்","not killing belongs to one greater than a king",1],["கொலைபுரியான்","not causing killing does",1],["பொய்யான்","not lying does",1],["பிறர்மனைமேல்","not going after another's wife does",1],["சிறியார் இனஞ்சேரான்","not joining the base does",2],["மறையின் செவியிலன்","being deaf to secrets that are told does",3],["தீச்சொற்கண் மூங்கை","being dumb to evil speech does",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:1"},"el:20":{"book":"elathi","num":22,"t":["மின்னேர் இடையார்சொல் தேறான் விழைவோரான்","கொன்னே வெகுளான் கொலைபுரியான் - பொன்னே","உறுப்பறுத் தன்ன கொடையுவப்பான் தன்னின்","வெறுப்பறுத்தான் விண்ணகத்தும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who does not trust the words of women with lightning-slim waists, does not heed desire, does not rage in vain, does not kill, delights in giving even when it is like cutting off a limb, and has cut hatred out of himself has no equal even in heaven.","n":"The giving that cuts like a limb is the hardest gift, which recalls verse 3's ranking of giving above penance. The distrust of women's words repeats verse 5's gender rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vekuli","kollaamai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 20","lb":"EL20","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["மின்னேர் இடையார்சொல் தேறான்","one who does not trust the words of slim-waisted women has no equal in heaven",1],["விழைவோரான்","one who does not heed desire has none",1],["கொன்னே வெகுளான்","one who does not rage in vain has none",2],["கொலைபுரியான்","one who does not kill has none",2],["கொடையுவப்பான்","one who delights in giving even when it cuts like a limb has none",3],["வெறுப்பறுத்தான்","one who has cut out hatred has none",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period that the virtuous man does not trust women's words"],"ch":"el:1"},"el:21":{"book":"elathi","num":23,"t":["இளமை கழியும் பிணிமூப்(பு) இயையும்","வளமை வலிஇவை வாடும் - உளநாளால்","பாடே புரியாது பால்போலும் சொல்லினாய்","வீடே புரிதல் வீதி!"],"p":"","r":"Youth passes, disease and old age arrive, and wealth and strength wither, so the verse, addressing a woman of milk-sweet speech, says that while one's days remain the rule is to seek release rather than worldly toil.","n":"The verse counts five things, not six: two that leave (youth, and with wealth, strength) and two that come (disease, old age), with wealth and strength sharing one verb; பாடு is read as worldly toil, set against வீடு. வீதி in line 4 is taken as விதி, 'the rule'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","veedu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 21","lb":"EL21","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["இளமை","youth passes away",1],["பிணி","disease comes to join one",1],["மூப்(பு)","old age comes to join one",1],["வளமை","wealth withers",2],["வலி","strength withers",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:22":{"book":"elathi","num":24,"t":["வாள்அஞ்சான் வன்கண்மை அஞ்சான் வனப்பஞ்சான்","ஆள்அஞ்சான் ஆம்பொருள் தானஞ்சான் - நாள்எஞ்சாக்","காலன் வரஒழிதல் காணின்வீ டெய்திய","பாவின்நூல் எய்தப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"Death, who does not let one's days run their full count, fears neither sword nor fierce courage, neither beauty nor men nor wealth, and seeing that he comes, the verse says one should take up the book of verse of the one who reached release.","n":"The five things are what a person might use against Death, and each is named only to be refused; the remedy is not a deed but a book, பாவின்நூல், apparently the scripture of the Jina who attained வீடு. ஆம்பொருள் தானஞ்சான் could also be split to include தானம், 'gifts', as a sixth thing Death does not fear; the reading here takes தான் as emphatic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 22","lb":"EL22","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாள்","Death does not fear the sword",1],["வன்கண்மை","Death does not fear fierce courage",1],["வனப்","Death does not fear beauty",1],["ஆள்","Death does not fear men or a following",2],["ஆம்பொருள்","Death does not fear the wealth one has gained",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:23":{"book":"elathi","num":25,"t":["குணநோக்கான் கூழ்நோக்கான் கோலமும் நோக்கான்","மணநோக்கான் மங்கலமும் நோக்கான் - கணநோக்கான்","கால்காப்பு வேண்டான் பெரியார்நூல் காலற்கு","வாய்காப்புக் கோடல் வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"Death regards neither a person's virtue nor his wealth nor his looks, neither a wedding nor an auspicious rite nor the moment, and accepts no guard set at his feet, so the verse calls it beautiful to take the great ones' book as a guard against his mouth.","n":"Six நோக்கான் clauses make the count; கால்காப்பு வேண்டான் is a seventh refusal that turns toward the remedy, a guard (காப்பு) of a different kind, the scripture, closing the mouth that eats. கணம் may be the moment (no right time to die) or a crowd; the reading takes the moment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 23","lb":"EL23","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["குண","Death does not regard a person's virtues",1],["கூழ்","Death does not regard wealth",1],["கோலமும்","Death does not regard appearance",1],["மண","Death does not regard a marriage",2],["மங்கலமும்","Death does not regard an auspicious occasion",2],["கண","Death does not regard the moment",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:24":{"book":"elathi","num":26,"t":["பிணிபுறப்பு மூப்போடு சாக்காடு துன்பம்","தணிவில் நிரப்பிவை தாழா - அணியின்","அரங்கின்மேல் ஆடுநர்போல் ஆகாமல் நன்றாம்","நிரம்புமேல் வீட்டு நெறி"],"p":"","r":"Disease, birth, old age, death, sorrow and unabating want do not hold back, so the verse says the way of release is good if one fulfils it rather than becoming like dancers who play on a decorated stage.","n":"Birth is counted among the evils, not as a beginning: the list is the round of births itself, closed by want. The dancers on the stage are those who play out a life's roles and leave; the image makes worldly life a performance rather than a sin. The count word இவை is fused into நிரப்பிவை, so count_word is left null; the six are counted as written.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","nilaiyaamai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 24","lb":"EL24","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிணி","disease does not hold back",1],["புறப்பு","birth does not hold back",1],["மூப்போடு","old age does not hold back",1],["சாக்காடு","death does not hold back",1],["துன்பம்","sorrow does not hold back",1],["தணிவில் நிரப்","unabating want does not hold back",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:25":{"book":"elathi","num":27,"t":["பாடகஞ் சாராமை பாத்திலார் தாம்விழையும்","நாடகஞ் சாராமை நாடுங்கால் - நாடகம்","சேர்ந்தால் பகைபழி தீச்சொல்லே சாக்காடே","தீர்ந்தால்போல் தீரா வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names not frequenting the anklet-wearers and not frequenting the shows that the shareless desire, for once one takes to such shows, enmity, blame, evil talk and death come and do not leave, though they seem to.","n":"The six things are two abstentions and four consequences: the verse sets the cause beside its fruits. பாடகம், the anklet, stands for those who wear it, most likely dancing women; பாத்திலார், 'those without a share', is obscure and may mean men of no settled portion or the women themselves. தீர்ந்தால்போல் தீரா: the harms seem to end and return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi","pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 25","lb":"EL25","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பாடகஞ் சாராமை","not frequenting the anklet-wearers is named as fitting",1],["நாடகஞ் சாராமை","not frequenting the shows desired by the shareless is named as fitting",2],["பகை","enmity comes to one who takes to the shows",3],["பழி","blame comes to one who takes to the shows",3],["தீச்சொல்லே","evil talk comes to one who takes to the shows",3],["சாக்காடே","death comes to one who takes to the shows",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:26":{"book":"elathi","num":28,"t":["மாண்டமைந்(து)ஆ ராய்ந்த மதிவனப்பே வன்கண்மை","ஆண்டமைந்த கல்வியே சொல்லாற்றல் - பூண்டமைந்த","காலம் அறிதல் கருதுங்கால் தூதுவர்க்கு","ஞாலம் அறிந்த புகழ்"],"p":"","r":"For envoys the verse names six excellences: a beauty of mind matured and tested, courage, learning well mastered, power of speech, knowing the time, and fame the world knows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai","kalvi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 26","lb":"EL26","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"king","it":[["மதிவனப்பே","a matured and tested beauty of mind is an envoy's excellence",1],["வன்கண்மை","courage is an envoy's excellence",1],["கல்வியே","well-mastered learning is an envoy's excellence",2],["சொல்லாற்றல்","power of speech is an envoy's excellence",2],["காலம் அறிதல்","knowing the time is an envoy's excellence",3],["ஞாலம் அறிந்த புகழ்","fame known to the world is an envoy's excellence",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:27":{"book":"elathi","num":29,"t":["அஃகுநீ செய்யல் எனஅறிந்(து) ஆராய்ந்தும்","வெஃகல் வெகுடலே தீக்காட்சி - வெஃகுமான்","கள்ளத்த அல்ல கருதின் இவைமூன்றும்","உள்ளத்த ஆக உரை"],"p":"","r":"Coveting, anger and wrong view, which a person goes on doing even after learning and weighing the warning that they diminish him, are named three faults that belong to the mind.","n":"This verse opens a sequence (27 to 29) that sorts faults by the faculty that commits them: three of the mind here, four of speech in 28, three of the body in 29, ten in all. தீக்காட்சி, 'evil seeing', is wrong view, a doctrinal fault, counted with two passions. கள்ளத்த அல்ல is hard: it may say these are not hidden faults or not deeds of the hand; the reading takes the contrast as mind against outward act.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","vekuli","manam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 27","lb":"EL27","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["வெஃகல்","coveting is a fault of the mind",2],["வெகுடலே","anger is a fault of the mind",2],["தீக்காட்சி","wrong view is a fault of the mind",2]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"el:2"},"el:28":{"book":"elathi","num":30,"t":["மையேர் தடங்கண் மயிலன்னாய் சாயலே","மெய்யே உணர்ந்தார் மிகஉரைப்பர் - பொய்யே","குறளை கடுஞ்சொல் பயனிலசொல் நான்கும்","மறலையின் வாயினவாம் மற்று"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman of kohl-dark wide eyes and a peacock's grace, the verse says those who know the truth hold that lying, slander, harsh words and useless words are four faults that come by the mouth.","n":"The four are the faults of speech in the three-faculty scheme begun in 27. மறலையின் is obscure; it may be 'of the wicked' or a form of மறலி, Death, which would make the mouth Death's own; the reading keeps only that the four belong to the mouth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","vaaimai","purangooral","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 28","lb":"EL28","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யே","lying is a fault of the mouth",2],["குறளை","slander is a fault of the mouth",3],["கடுஞ்சொல்","harsh speech is a fault of the mouth",3],["பயனிலசொல்","useless speech is a fault of the mouth",3]],"cw":"நான்கும்","ch":"el:2"},"el:29":{"book":"elathi","num":31,"t":["நிலையளவின் நின்ற நெடியவர்தாம் நேரா","கொலைகளவு காமத்தீ வாழ்க்கை - அலையளவி","மையெனநீள் கண்ணாய்! மறுதலைய இம்மூன்றும்","மெய்யள வாக விதி"],"p":"","r":"Killing, theft and a life of burning lust, which the great who stand firm in their vows do not consent to, are named three opposed acts whose rule is that they belong to the body.","n":"It completes the ten faults of 27 to 29 with the three of the body. நிலையளவின் நின்ற, 'who stood in the measure of their station', points to those keeping vows, but the counsel itself is general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kalavu","kaamam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 29","lb":"EL29","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலை","killing is a fault of the body",2],["களவு","theft is a fault of the body",2],["காமத்தீ வாழ்க்கை","a life of burning lust is a fault of the body",2]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"el:2"},"el:30":{"book":"elathi","num":32,"t":["மாண்டவர் மாண்ட அறிவினால் மக்களைப்","பூண்டவர்ப் போற்றிப் புரக்குங்கால் - பூண்ட","ஓளரதனே கேத்திரசன் காணீனன் கூடன்","கிரிதன்பென கற்பவன் பேர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names the first six of the kinds of son that the wise recognise when they take up and protect children: the son of one's own wife, the son of the field, the son of an unmarried girl, the hidden son, the bought son and a sixth whose name is garbled.","n":"These are Sanskrit law-book names of legal sonship (ஔரச, க்ஷேத்ரஜ, கானீந, கூட, க்ரீத, and probably பௌநர்ப்பவ, the son of a remarried woman) in Tamil spelling; with 31 they make the twelve. The verse makes a son a status the household can acquire, not only beget. The identifications are from the Sanskrit names, not from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirappu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 30","lb":"EL30","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஓளரதனே","the son of one's own wife is named among recognised sons",3],["கேத்திரசன்","the son of the field, begotten on one's wife by another, is named among them",3],["காணீனன்","the son of an unmarried girl is named among them",3],["கூடன்","the son born in secret is named among them",3],["கிரிதன்","the bought son is named among them",4],["கற்பவன்","a sixth kind, its name corrupt, is named among them",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender","caste"],"a classification of legal sonship of the period by the circumstances of birth: from the wife, from the wife by another man, from an unmarried girl, born in secret, bought"],"ch":"el:2"},"el:31":{"book":"elathi","num":33,"t":["மத்த மயிலன்ன சாயலாய் மன்னிய சீர்த்","தத்தன் சகோடன் கருத்திரமன் புத்திரி","புத்ரனப வித்தனோடு பொய்யில் உருகிருதன்","இத்திறத்த எஞ்சினோர் பேர்"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman with the grace of a maddened peacock, the verse names the remaining six kinds of son: the given son, the son taken with the bride, the made son, the son of an appointed daughter, the cast-off son and a son of a sixth kind.","n":"It completes the list of 30 (தத்தன் dattaka, சகோடன் sahodha, கருத்திரமன் kritrima, புத்திரி புத்ரன் putrika-putra, அபவித்தன் apaviddha, and உருகிருதன், perhaps svayamdatta, the self-given). Line 3 runs புத்ரன் and அபவித்தன் together; பொய்யில் may praise the last or the list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirappu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 31","lb":"EL31","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தத்தன்","the given son is named among the remaining kinds",2],["சகோடன்","the son brought with the bride is named among them",2],["கருத்திரமன்","the made son is named among them",2],["புத்திரி","the son of an appointed daughter is named among them",2],["வித்தனோடு","the cast-off son taken in is named among them",3],["உருகிருதன்","a sixth kind, perhaps the self-given son, is named among them",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender","caste"],"the rest of the period's twelve kinds of legal son, sorted by how the boy came to the house: given, brought by the bride, adopted, through a daughter, found abandoned"],"ch":"el:2"},"el:32":{"book":"elathi","num":34,"t":["உரையான் குலன்குடிமை ஊனம் பிறரை","உரையான் பொருளொடுவாழ்(வு) ஆயு - உரையானாய்ப்","பூவாதி வண்டுதேர்ந்து உண்குழலாய் ஈத்துண்பான்","தேவாதி தேவனாய்த் தேறு"],"p":"","r":"One who does not speak of his own lineage and family standing, does not speak of others' defects, does not speak of his wealth, his way of life or his span of years, and who gives before he eats, is to be known, the verse says to a woman of bee-sought tresses, as god of gods.","n":"Five of the six restraints are silences about oneself (birth, standing, wealth, life, age); the one about others is not to name their defects. The reward is given to the giver, ஈத்துண்பான், so the silences frame the giving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","eegai","purangooral","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 32","lb":"EL32","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["குலன்","not speaking of one's lineage",1],["குடிமை","not speaking of one's family standing",1],["ஊனம் பிறரை","not speaking of others' defects",1],["பொருளொடு","not speaking of one's wealth",2],["வாழ்(வு)","not speaking of one's way of life",2],["ஆயு","not speaking of one's span of years",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:33":{"book":"elathi","num":35,"t":["பொய்யுரையான் வையான் புறங்கூறான் யாவரையும்","மெய்யுரையான் உள்ளனவும் விட்டுரையான் - எய்யுரையான்","கூந்தல் மயிலன்னாய்! குழீஇயவான் விண்ணோர்க்கு","வேந்தனாம் இவ்வுலகம் விட்டு"],"p":"","r":"One who does not lie, does not revile, does not backbite, speaks truth to everyone, speaks openly of what he has and does not speak to wound will, the verse says to a peacock-like woman, leave this world and become king of the assembled gods.","n":"All six are about speech; உள்ளனவும் விட்டுரையான் is read as one who does not hide what he has from those who ask, which joins speech to giving. எய்யுரையான் is uncertain: 'not speaking words that pierce' or 'not mocking'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","purangooral","sol","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 33","lb":"EL33","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யுரையான்","not lying",1],["வையான்","not reviling",1],["புறங்கூறான்","not backbiting",1],["மெய்யுரையான்","speaking truth to all",2],["உள்ளனவும் விட்டுரையான்","speaking openly of what one has",2],["எய்யுரையான்","not speaking to wound",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:34":{"book":"elathi","num":36,"t":["சிதையுரையான் செற்றம் உரைய சீறில்லான்","இயல்புரையான் ஈனம் உரையாள் - நசையவர்க்குக்","கூடுவ(து) ஈவானைக் கொவ்வைபோல் செவ்வாயாய்","நாடுவர் விண்ணோர் நயந்து"],"p":"","r":"One who does not speak destructive words or speak in hatred, has no anger, does not proclaim his own nature, does not speak of baseness, and gives what fits to those who want it is, the verse says to a woman with lips red as the kovvai fruit, sought with love by the gods.","n":"Verses 32 to 34 form a run: restraint of speech joined to giving, rewarded each time in heaven, rising from god of gods to king of the gods to sought by the gods. The fruit is put in the gods' wish, not in his merit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","vekuli","eegai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 34","lb":"EL34","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["சிதையுரையான்","not speaking destructive words",1],["செற்றம் உரைய","not speaking in hatred",1],["சீறில்லான்","having no anger",1],["இயல்புரையான்","not proclaiming one's own nature",2],["ஈனம் உரையாள்","not speaking of baseness",2],["கூடுவ(து) ஈவானைக்","giving what fits to those who want it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:35":{"book":"elathi","num":37,"t":["துறந்தார் துறவாதார் துப்பிலார் தோன்றார்","இறந்தார்ஈ டற்றார் இளையர் - சிறந்தவர்க்கும்","பண்ணாரும் சொல்லாய் பழியில்ஊண் பாற்படுத்தான்","மண்ணாளும் மன்னாய் மற்று"],"p":"","r":"One who gives blameless food as their share to renouncers and to those not renounced, to the powerless, the unseen, the bereaved, the destitute and the young, and to the excellent as well, will, the verse says to a woman of musical speech, rule the earth as king.","n":"The recipients run to eight, not six: the verse adds சிறந்தவர்க்கும், 'to the excellent too', after the needy, so the list closes by widening, not narrowing. தோன்றார் may be those who keep out of sight (the shamed poor), and இறந்தார் those whose support has died; துறவாதார் may be householders in need. பாற்படுத்தான், 'set as a share', makes the gift a portion due, not a favour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","marumai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 35","lb":"EL35","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["துறந்தார்","giving food to renouncers",1],["துறவாதார்","giving food to those who have not renounced",1],["துப்பிலார்","giving food to the powerless",1],["தோன்றார்","giving food to those who do not show themselves",1],["இறந்தார்","giving food to the bereaved",2],["ஈடற்றார்","giving food to the destitute",2],["இளையர்","giving food to the young",2],["சிறந்தவர்க்கும்","giving food to the excellent as well",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:36":{"book":"elathi","num":38,"t":["காலில்லார் கண்ணில்லார் நாவில்லார் யாரையும்","பாலில்லார் பற்றிய நூலில்லார் - சாலவும்","ஆழப் படும்ஊண் அமைத்தார் இமையவரால்","வீழப் படுவார் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"Those who prepare a deeply satisfying meal for the lame, the blind, the mute, those with no kin at all and those with no learning to hold to will swiftly, the verse says, be desired by the gods.","n":"The count is five: three bodily lacks, then a social lack (no kin) and a lack of learning, so the unlearned are set among the disabled as people owed food. சாலவும் ஆழப் படும் is taken with the meal ('filling deeply'); read as 'those deeply sunk in need' it would make a sixth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","arul","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 36","lb":"EL36","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["காலில்லார்","feeding the lame",1],["கண்ணில்லார்","feeding the blind",1],["நாவில்லார்","feeding the mute",1],["யாரையும் பாலில்லார்","feeding those with no kin at all",1],["பற்றிய நூலில்லார்","feeding those with no learning to hold to",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:37":{"book":"elathi","num":39,"t":["அழப்போகான் அஞ்சான் அலறினால் கேளான்","எழப்போகான் ஈடற்றார் என்றும் - தொழப்போகான்","என்னேஇக் காலன்ஈ(டு) ஓரான் தவமுயலான்","கொன்னே யிருத்தல் குறை"],"p":"","r":"Death does not go away when people weep, does not fear, does not listen when they cry out, does not leave for the helpless, does not go away when they worship, and regards no worth, so the verse calls it a failing to sit idle without striving at penance.","n":"Each item is a plea that fails (tears, threats, cries, helplessness, worship), so worship itself is counted among the useless defences; only தவம், practised beforehand, is set against Death. The six are actions Death refuses, not things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thavam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 37","lb":"EL37","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["அழப்போகான்","Death does not leave when one weeps",1],["அஞ்சான்","Death does not fear",1],["அலறினால் கேளான்","Death does not listen to cries",1],["எழப்போகான்","Death does not rise and leave",2],["தொழப்போகான்","Death does not leave when worshipped",2],["ஈ(டு) ஓரான்","Death regards no worth",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:38":{"book":"elathi","num":40,"t":["எழுத்தினால் நீங்கா(து)எண் ணால்ஒழியா(து) ஏத்தி","வழுத்தினால் மாறாது மாண்ட - ஒழுக்கினால்","நேராமை சால உணர்வார் பெருந்தவம்","போகாமை சாலப் புலை"],"p":"","r":"The great penance of those who fully understand that one does not reach it except by noble conduct is something not removed by letters, not ended by numbers, not altered by praise and hymns, and failing to go that way is utter baseness.","n":"The verse sets penance against three learned or ritual substitutes, script, number (counting or astrology) and praise, and makes conduct the only road. புலை here is baseness, not a caste term. The syntax is compressed and the subject of the three negatives may instead be the bond of deeds that penance alone removes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ozhukkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 38","lb":"EL38","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["எழுத்தினால்","it is not removed by letters",1],["எண்ணால்","it is not ended by numbers",1],["வழுத்தினால்","it is not altered by praise and hymns",2],["ஒழுக்கினால்","it is reached only by noble conduct",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:39":{"book":"elathi","num":41,"t":["சாவ(து) எளி(து)அரிது சான்றாண்மை நல்லது","மேவல் (எளிது)அரிது மெய்போற்றல் - ஆவதன்கண்","சேறல் எளிது நிலையரிது தெள்ளியராய்","வேறல் எளி(து)அரிது சொல்"],"p":"","r":"Dying is easy and fullness of character hard; to reach for the good is easy and to guard truth hard; to go toward what will be is easy and to stand firm hard; to win by being clever is easy and the right word hard.","n":"Four easy-and-hard pairs make eight things; each hard thing is the lasting form of its easy partner. Putting death among the easy things reverses the death verses earlier in the chapter. The last pair ranks speech above victory; (எளிது) in line 2 is an editorial restoration.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","vaaimai","sol","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 39","lb":"EL39","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["சாவ(து)","dying is easy",1],["சான்றாண்மை","fullness of character is hard",1],["நல்லது மேவல்","reaching for the good is easy",1],["மெய்போற்றல்","guarding truth is hard",2],["சேறல்","going toward what will be is easy",3],["நிலை","standing firm is hard",3],["வேறல்","winning by cleverness is easy",4],["சொல்","the right word is hard",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:40":{"book":"elathi","num":42,"t":["உலையாமை உற்றதற்(கு) ஓடி உயிரை","அலையாமை ஐயப் படாமை - நிலையாமை","தீர்க்கும்வாய் தேர்ந்து பசியுண்டு நீக்குவான்","நோக்கும்வாய் விண்ணின் உயர்வு"],"p":"","r":"Not wavering, hastening to what has befallen others, not tormenting living beings, not doubting, choosing the way that ends impermanence, and removing hunger where it is found make one whose gaze is on the heights of heaven.","n":"The list joins inner steadiness (not wavering, not doubting), care (running to help, removing hunger), non-harm and the choice of release, so feeding the hungry stands beside the way out of impermanence. The division of line 1 is uncertain: உற்றதற்கு ஓடி may go with உலையாமை ('not shaken by what befalls').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","pasi","kollaamai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 40","lb":"EL40","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["உலையாமை","not wavering",1],["உற்றதற்(கு) ஓடி","hastening to what has befallen",1],["அலையாமை","not tormenting living beings",2],["ஐயப் படாமை","not doubting",2],["நிலையாமை","choosing the way that ends impermanence",2],["பசியுண்டு நீக்குவான்","removing hunger where it is found",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:2"},"el:41":{"book":"elathi","num":43,"t":["குறுகான் சிறியாரைக் கொள்ளான் புலால்பொய்","மறுகான் பிறர்பொருள் வௌவான் - இறுகானாய்","ஈ(டு)அற் றவர்(கு)ஈவா னாயின் நெறிநூல்கள்","பாடிறப்ப பன்னும் இடத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names six marks of a man (he does not draw near the mean, does not take flesh, does not stray into falsehood, does not seize others' goods, is not close-fisted, and gives to those who have no means) and says that, if he is such, the books of the path will recite his worth beyond measure.","n":"The reward is not a rebirth but praise in the texts themselves (நெறிநூல்கள் ... பன்னும்): the giver becomes a subject the books recite. The last two items turn from refusals to giving: not being tight (இறுகான்) and giving to the resourceless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pulaal","vaaimai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 41","lb":"EL41","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["குறுகான் சிறியாரைக்","he does not draw near the mean",1],["கொள்ளான் புலால்","he does not take flesh",1],["பொய் மறுகான்","he does not wander in falsehood",1],["பிறர்பொருள் வௌவான்","he does not seize what belongs to others",2],["இறுகானாய்","he is not close-fisted",2],["ஈ(டு)அற் றவர்(கு)ஈவா னாயின்","he gives to those who have nothing to fall back on",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"the Jain abstention from flesh of the period, counted among a giver's marks"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:42":{"book":"elathi","num":44,"t":["கொல்லான் உடன்படான் கொல்வார் இனஞ்சேரான்","புல்லான் பிறர்பால் புலான்மயங்கல் - செல்லான்","குடிப்படுத்துக் கூழ்ஈந்தான் கொல்யானை ஏறி","அடிப்படுப்பான் மண்ஆண்(டு) அரசு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who does not kill, does not consent to killing, does not join the company of killers, does not cling to what is others', does not stray into the delusion of flesh, and has fed people, settling them in households, will rule the earth as a king, mounted on a killing elephant, bringing foes under his feet.","n":"The non-killer's reward is a killing elephant (கொல்யானை) and enemies trodden under: the verse sees no strain in paying non-violence with conquest. புல்லான் பிறர்பால் may mean not embracing another's wife or not attaching himself to others; குடிப்படுத்து may be 'settling families' or 'establishing his own house'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","eegai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 42","lb":"EL42","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கொல்லான்","he does not kill",1],["உடன்படான்","he does not consent to killing",1],["கொல்வார் இனஞ்சேரான்","he does not join the company of killers",1],["புல்லான் பிறர்பால்","he does not cling to what belongs to others",2],["புலான்மயங்கல் செல்லான்","he does not go into the delusion of flesh",2],["குடிப்படுத்துக் கூழ்ஈந்தான்","he gave food, settling people in households",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"the Jain rule against killing and flesh of the period, extended to consenting and to company"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:43":{"book":"elathi","num":45,"t":["சூ(து)உவவான் பேரான் கலா(வு)உரையான் யார்திறத்தும்","வா(து)உவவான் மாதரார் சொல்தேறான் - காதுதாழ்","வான்மகர வார்குழையாய் மாதவர்க்கு ஊண்ஈந்தான்","தான்மகர வாய்மாடத் தான்"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman with long makara earrings, the verse says that one who does not delight in gambling, does not swerve, does not speak deceit, does not relish disputes with anyone, does not trust women's words, and has given food to great ascetics will dwell in a mansion with makara-mouthed ornament.","n":"The makara of the woman's earring returns as the makara mouths of the reward mansion, a verbal rhyme of the frame. பேரான் (does not move away) and கலா(வு) (theft, or a form of கலாம், strife) are unsure. The distrust of women's words recurs in verses 5 and 20.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","eegai","thavam","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 43","lb":"EL43","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["சூ(து)உவவான்","he does not delight in gambling",1],["பேரான்","he does not swerve from his place",1],["கலா(வு)உரையான்","he does not speak deceit or strife",1],["யார்திறத்தும் வா(து)உவவான்","he does not relish disputing with anyone",1],["மாதரார் சொல்தேறான்","he does not trust the words of women",2],["மாதவர்க்கு ஊண்ஈந்தான்","he has given food to great ascetics",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period that a man of virtue does not trust women's words"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:44":{"book":"elathi","num":46,"t":["பொய்யான்பொய் மேவான் புலால்உண்ணான் யாவரையும்","வையான் வழிசீத்து வால்அடிசில் - நையாதே","ஈத்துண்பான் ஆகும் இருங்கடல்சூழ் மண்ணரசாய்ப்","பாத்துண்பான் ஏத்துண்பான் பாடு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who does not lie, does not take to falsehood, does not eat flesh, does not revile anyone, clears the road, and gives fine white rice without grudging before he eats will become a king of the sea-girt earth whose lot is to share his food and be praised.","n":"The reward repeats the act: one who gave before eating (ஈத்துண்பான்) becomes a king who shares before eating (பாத்துண்பான்), so kingship is the same virtue on a larger table. Clearing the road (வழிசீத்து) is a public work set among private restraints.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vaaimai","pulaal","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 44","lb":"EL44","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பொய்யான்","he does not lie",1],["பொய் மேவான்","he does not take to falsehood",1],["புலால்உண்ணான்","he does not eat flesh",1],["யாவரையும் வையான்","he reviles no one",1],["வழிசீத்து","he clears the road",2],["வால்அடிசில் நையாதே ஈத்துண்பான்","he gives white rice without grudging before he eats",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"the Jain abstention from flesh of the period, set among a giver's marks"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:45":{"book":"elathi","num":47,"t":["இழுக்கான் இயல்நெறி இன்னாத வெஃகான்","வழுக்கான் மனைபொருள் வௌவான் - ஒழுக்கத்தால்","செல்வான் செயிரில்ஊண் ஈவான் அரசாண்டு","வெல்வான் விடுப்பான் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who does not slip from the proper way, does not covet harmful things, does not err, does not seize the house and goods of others, walks by right conduct and gives faultless food will rule, conquer, and quickly let go.","n":"விடுப்பான் விரைந்து closes the reward: on one reading the king soon lets the kingdom go, a Jain turn in which rule is a stage before renunciation; on another he quickly releases those he has conquered. மனைபொருள் may be another's house and goods or another's wife and goods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","eegai","arasu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 45","lb":"EL45","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["இழுக்கான் இயல்நெறி","he does not slip from the proper way",1],["இன்னாத வெஃகான்","he does not covet harmful things",1],["வழுக்கான்","he does not err",2],["மனைபொருள் வௌவான்","he does not seize another's house and goods",2],["ஒழுக்கத்தால் செல்வான்","he goes by right conduct",2],["செயிரில்ஊண் ஈவான்","he gives faultless food",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:46":{"book":"elathi","num":48,"t":["களியான்கள் ளுண்ணான் களிப்பாரைக் காணான்","ஒளியான் விருந்துக்(கு) உலையான் - எளியாரை","எள்ளான்நீத் துண்பானேல் ஏதமில் மண்ணாண்டு","கொள்வான் குடிவாழ்வான் கூர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if one does not revel, does not drink toddy, does not look upon drinkers, neither hides from nor flinches at guests, does not scorn the poor, and eats only after giving, he will rule a land without harm and his family will thrive abundantly.","n":"Three items circle toddy (revelry, drinking, the sight of drinkers) and three circle the table (guests, the poor, eating after giving), so abstention and hospitality are one portrait. ஒளியான் விருந்துக்(கு) உலையான் is taken as one item; split, it gives seven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","virundhu","eegai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 46","lb":"EL46","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["களியான்","he does not revel",1],["கள் ளுண்ணான்","he does not drink toddy",1],["களிப்பாரைக் காணான்","he does not look upon those who revel in drink",1],["ஒளியான் விருந்துக்(கு) உலையான்","he neither hides from guests nor wearies of them",2],["எளியாரை எள்ளான்","he does not scorn the poor",2],["நீத் துண்பானேல்","he eats only after giving",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"the period's rule against toddy, extended to the sight of drinkers"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:47":{"book":"elathi","num":49,"t":["பெரியார்சொல் பேணிப் பிறழாது நின்று","பரியா அடியார்ப் பறியான் - கரியார்சொல்","தேறான் இயையான் தெளிந்தடிசில் ஈத்துண்பான்","மாறான்மண் ஆளுமாம் மற்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who keeps the words of the great and stands without swerving, does not despoil servants who do not desert him, does not trust the words of the black-hearted, does not join them, gives food with a clear mind before he eats, and does not change, will rule the earth.","n":"பரியா அடியார் is unsure: servants who do not run off, or the unpitied poor at one's feet. கரியார் may be the black-hearted or, since கரி is a witness, false witnesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandror","eegai","inam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 47","lb":"EL47","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பெரியார்சொல் பேணிப் பிறழாது நின்று","he keeps the words of the great and stands without swerving",1],["பரியா அடியார்ப் பறியான்","he does not despoil the servants who stay by him",2],["கரியார்சொல் தேறான்","he does not trust the words of the black-hearted",2],["இயையான்","he does not join with them",3],["தெளிந்தடிசில் ஈத்துண்பான்","he gives food with a clear mind before he eats",3],["மாறான்","he does not change",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:48":{"book":"elathi","num":50,"t":["வேற்றரவம் சேரான் விருந்தொழியான் தன்இல்லுள்","சோற்றரவம் சொல்லியுண் பானாயின் - மாற்றரவம்","கேளான் கிளைஓம்பின் கேடில் அரசனாய்","வாளான்மண் ணாண்டு வரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a man does not join in strangers' clamour, does not turn away guests, calls out the sound of the meal in his house before he eats, does not listen to hostile talk, and cherishes his kin, he will come as an unfailing king ruling the earth by the sword.","n":"Three அரவம் words (sound) carry the verse: strangers' noise he avoids, the rice-call he makes, hostile noise he will not hear. Calling out before eating makes the meal public. Five things, not six; கிளைஓம்பின் is counted as the fifth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 48","lb":"EL48","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["வேற்றரவம் சேரான்","he does not join in strangers' clamour",1],["விருந்தொழியான்","he does not turn away guests",1],["சோற்றரவம் சொல்லியுண் பானாயின்","he announces the meal in his house before he eats",2],["மாற்றரவம் கேளான்","he does not listen to hostile talk",2],["கிளைஓம்பின்","he cherishes his kin",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:49":{"book":"elathi","num":51,"t":["யானை குதிரைபொன் கன்னியே ஆணிரையோ(டு)","ஏனை ஒழிந்த இவையெல்லாம் - ஆன்நெய்யால்","எண்ணனாய் மாதவர்க்(கு) ஊண்ஈந்தான் வைசிர","வண்ணனாய் வாழ்வான் வகுத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names elephants, horses, gold, a maiden, herds of cattle and all other gifts besides, and says that one who, with these in mind, gave food with cow's ghee to great ascetics will live as Vaisravana, the lord of wealth.","n":"The relation of the list to the food gift turns on எண்ணனாய்: either the food with ghee is reckoned as worth all these great gifts, or the giver gave all these and food as well. On the first reading the verse ranks a meal for ascetics above the royal gift catalogue. The maiden (கன்னி) stands in a list of goods given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","selvam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 49","lb":"EL49","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["யானை","an elephant is one of the great gifts weighed against the food",1],["குதிரை","a horse is one of the great gifts",1],["பொன்","gold is one of the great gifts",1],["கன்னியே","a maiden given in marriage is one of the great gifts",1],["ஆணிரையோ(டு)","herds of cattle are one of the great gifts",1],["ஏனை ஒழிந்த இவையெல்லாம்","all the remaining gifts are counted with them",2]],"cw":"இவையெல்லாம்","nr":[["gender"],"a period gift-list that counts a maiden given in marriage among goods given away"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:50":{"book":"elathi","num":52,"t":["எள்ளே பருத்தியே எண்ணெய் உடுத்தாடை","வள்ளே துணியே இவற்றோடு - கொள்ளென","அன்புற்(று) அசனம் கொடுத்தான் துணையினோ(டு)","இன்புற்(று) வாழ்வான் இயைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who gave sesame, cotton, oil, cloth to wear, a vessel and cloth, and with them food given with love, saying 'take it', will live happily joined with his companion.","n":"The goods are household stuffs, and the reward is household joy: a life with one's partner. வள் is unsure (a vessel or a measure), and உடுத்தாடை and துணி may both be cloth, one worn and one uncut.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","anbu","vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 50","lb":"EL50","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["எள்ளே","sesame is given with the food",1],["பருத்தியே","cotton is given",1],["எண்ணெய்","oil is given",1],["உடுத்தாடை","cloth to wear is given",1],["வள்ளே","a vessel is given",2],["துணியே","cloth is given",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:51":{"book":"elathi","num":53,"t":["உண்ணீர் வளம்குளம் கூவல் வழிப்புரை","தண்ணீரை அம்பலந்தான் பாற்படுத்தான் -பண்ணீர","பாடலோ(டு) ஆடல் பயின்றுயிர் செல்வானாய்க்","கூடலொ(டு) ஊடலுளான் கூர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who made over to common use a tank rich in drinking water, a well, a wayside shelter, a water-booth and a public hall will live abundantly amid tuneful song and dance, in love's union and its quarrels.","n":"The reward is pleasure, not heaven or kingship: music, dance, and the akam pair of union and sulking (கூடல், ஊடல்). Five works are counted; உண்ணீர் வளம் could be read as a sixth (a source of drinking water) rather than as the tank's quality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","eegai","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 51","lb":"EL51","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["உண்ணீர் வளம்குளம்","a tank rich in drinking water, made over to common use",1],["கூவல்","a well made over to common use",1],["வழிப்புரை","a wayside shelter made over to common use",1],["தண்ணீரை","a water-booth made over to common use",2],["அம்பலந்","a public hall made over to common use",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:52":{"book":"elathi","num":54,"t":["இல்லிழந்தார் கண்ணிழந்தார் ஈண்டியசெல் வம்இழந்தார்","நெல்லிழந்தார் ஆணிரை தான்இழந்தார்க்(கு) -எல்உழந்து","பண்ணியூண் ஈந்தவர் பல்யானை மன்னராய்","எண்ணிஊண் ஆர்வார் இயைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who toil by day to cook and feed people who have lost their house, their eyes, their gathered wealth, their paddy or their herds will become kings with many elephants and eat with considered plenty.","n":"Every recipient is defined by a loss (இழந்தார் five times), so the list is a census of ruin: blindness sits among the losses of house, grain and cattle. Five things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","inmai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 52","lb":"EL52","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["இல்லிழந்தார்","those who have lost their house are fed",1],["கண்ணிழந்தார்","those who have lost their eyes are fed",1],["ஈண்டியசெல் வம்இழந்தார்","those who have lost their gathered wealth are fed",1],["நெல்லிழந்தார்","those who have lost their paddy are fed",2],["ஆணிரை தான்இழந்தார்க்(கு)","those who have lost their herds are fed",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:53":{"book":"elathi","num":55,"t":["கடம்பட்டார் காப்பில்லார் கைத்தில்லார் தங்கான்","முடம்பட்டார் மூத்தார்மூப் பில்லார்க்(கு) - உடம்பட்(டு)","உடையராய் இல்லுள்ஊண் ஈத்துண்பார் மண்மேல்","படையராய் வாழ்வார் பயின்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who, consenting and having the means, feed in their own houses the indebted, the unprotected, those with nothing in hand, the lame, the old and the young will live on earth as masters of armies.","n":"The feeding is in the house (இல்லுள்), not at a public charity. தங்கான் is read as தம் கால் (their own legs) before முடம்பட்டார்; மூப்பில்லார் may be the young or those with no elders to keep them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","ilvaazhkkai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 53","lb":"EL53","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கடம்பட்டார்","the indebted are fed in the house",1],["காப்பில்லார்","those without protection are fed",1],["கைத்தில்லார்","those with nothing in hand are fed",1],["தங்கான் முடம்பட்டார்","those lamed in their legs are fed",1],["மூத்தார்","the old are fed",2],["மூப்பில்லார்க்(கு)","the young, or those without elders, are fed",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:54":{"book":"elathi","num":56,"t":["பார்ப்பார் பசித்தார் தவசிகள் பாலர்கள்","கார்ப்பார் தமையாதும் காப்பிலார் - தூப்பால","நிண்டாரால் எண்ணாது நீத்தவர் மண்ணாண்டு","பண்டாரம் பற்றவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who, without calculating, gave food to brahmins, the hungry, ascetics, children and those who have no one at all to protect them will rule the earth with a full treasury.","n":"பார்ப்பார் is a caste term, here naming brahmins by birth as a class of fit recipients, set first beside the hungry and the unprotected. The end of line 2 and start of line 3 (தூப்பால நிண்டாரால்) are corrupt; a sixth recipient may be hidden there.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","andhanar","thavam","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 54","lb":"EL54","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பார்ப்பார்","brahmins are among those fed",1],["பசித்தார்","the hungry are fed",1],["தவசிகள்","ascetics are fed",1],["பாலர்கள்","children are fed",1],["கார்ப்பார் தமையாதும் காப்பிலார்","those who have no one at all to protect them are fed",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a period list of fit recipients that names brahmins by birth (பார்ப்பார்) first"],"ch":"el:3"},"el:55":{"book":"elathi","num":57,"t":["ஈன்றார்ஈன் கால்தளர்வார் சூலார் குழவிகள்","மான்றார் வளியான் மயங்கினார்க்கு - ஆனார்என்(று)","ஊண்ஈய்த்(து) உறுநோய் களைந்தார் பெருஞ்செல்வம்","காண்ஈய்த்து வாழ்வார் கலந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who, treating them as their own, fed and relieved the grave ills of women who have given birth, those weakened at delivery, the pregnant, infants, the distraught and those stricken by wind-disease will live joined with great wealth.","n":"Four of the six recipients belong to the birth-room (mothers, those weak in labour, the pregnant, infants), so the verse makes childbirth a field of charity. The giver both feeds and cures (ஊண்ஈய்த்து உறுநோய் களைந்தார்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","makkal","noy","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 55","lb":"EL55","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஈன்றார்","women who have given birth are fed and tended",1],["ஈன் கால்தளர்வார்","those weakened at delivery are fed and tended",1],["சூலார்","pregnant women are fed and tended",1],["குழவிகள்","infants are fed and tended",1],["மான்றார்","the distraught are fed and tended",2],["வளியான் மயங்கினார்க்கு","those stricken by wind-disease are fed and tended",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:56":{"book":"elathi","num":58,"t":["தலையாளர் தாப்பாளர் தாழ்ந்தவர் பெண்டீர்","உயை஡ளர் ஊண்ஒன்றும் இல்லார் - கிளைஞராய்","மாவலந்த நோக்கினாய் ஊண்ஈய்ந்தபர் மாக்கடால்சூழ்","நாவலம்தீ(வு) ஆள்வாரே நன்கு"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman with a deer's glance, the verse says that those who, as kin, gave food to those who were heads of houses, the distressed, the fallen, women, the toiling poor and those with no food at all will rule the island of Jambu girt by the great sea.","n":"Line 2's உயை஡ளர் is corrupt (an unassigned code point) and several words (தாப்பாளர், ஊண்ஈய்ந்தபர், மாக்கடால்) look miscopied, so the recipients after the first are guesses. The reward, rule of நாவலம்தீவு, is the widest in the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","sutram","inmai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 56","lb":"EL56","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தலையாளர்","those who were heads of houses are fed",1],["தாப்பாளர்","the distressed are fed",1],["தாழ்ந்தவர்","the fallen are fed",1],["பெண்டீர்","women are fed",1],["உயை஡ளர்","the toiling poor (a corrupt word) are fed",2],["ஊண்ஒன்றும் இல்லார்","those with no food at all are fed",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:57":{"book":"elathi","num":59,"t":["கருஞ்சிரங்கு வெண்தொழுநோய் கல்வளி காயும்","பெருஞ்சிரங்கு பேர்வயிற்றுத் தீயார்க்(கு) - அருஞ்சிரமம்","ஆற்றிஊண் ஈத்(து)அவை தீர்த்தார் அரசராய்ப்","போற்றிஊண் உண்பார் புரந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who took hard pains to feed and cure sufferers of black scab, white leprosy, stone, wind-disease, burning great sores and the fire of a swollen belly will become kings who protect and eat in honour.","n":"Divided into six by reading கல்வளி as stone and wind-disease and காயும் as qualifying the great sores; read கல்வளி as one ailment and the count is five. The giver here is a healer as well as a feeder (அவை தீர்த்தார்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","eegai","marundhu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 57","lb":"EL57","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கருஞ்சிரங்கு","those with black scab are fed and cured",1],["வெண்தொழுநோய்","those with white leprosy are fed and cured",1],["கல்","those with stone are fed and cured",1],["வளி","those with wind-disease are fed and cured",1],["காயும் பெருஞ்சிரங்கு","those with burning great sores are fed and cured",1],["பேர்வயிற்றுத் தீயார்க்(கு)","those with the fire of a swollen belly are fed and cured",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:58":{"book":"elathi","num":60,"t":["காமாடார் காமியார் கல்லார் இனஞ்சேரார்","ஆமாடார் ஆயந்தார் நெறிநின்று -தாமாடா(து)","ஏற்றாரை இன்புற ஈய்ந்தார்முன் இம்மையான்","மாற்றாரை மாற்றிவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who do not sport in lust, do not desire, do not join the unlearned, do not say yes lightly, stand in the way of those who have inquired, and, without boasting, gave to those who asked so that they rejoiced, will in this very life overcome their enemies.","n":"The reward is in this life (இம்மையான்), where most verses of the run look to a later birth. காமாடார் and ஆமாடார் are obscure; the second may be 'do not dance to every yes'. ஆயந்தார் is read as ஆய்ந்தார்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kaamam","inam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 58","lb":"EL58","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["காமாடார்","they do not sport in lust",1],["காமியார்","they do not desire",1],["கல்லார் இனஞ்சேரார்","they do not join the company of the unlearned",1],["ஆமாடார்","they do not assent lightly",2],["ஆயந்தார் நெறிநின்று","they stand in the way of those who have inquired",2],["ஏற்றாரை இன்புற ஈய்ந்தார்","they gave to those who asked so that they rejoiced",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:59":{"book":"elathi","num":61,"t":["வணங்கி வழியொழுகி மாண்டார்சொல் கொண்டு","நுணங்கியநூல் நோக்கி நுழையா - இணங்கிய","பால்நோக்கி வாழ்வான் பழிஇல்லா மன்னனாய்","நூல்நோக்கி வாழ்வான் நுனித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who bows, follows the right way, takes the words of the excellent, studies the subtle books and penetrates them, and lives attending to what is fitting will live as a blameless king, examining the books closely.","n":"The only verse of the run without a gift of food: the six things are a learner's acts, and the reward, a blameless king who still reads (நூல்நோக்கி), returns to the study of line 2. நுழையா is read as the old participle 'having entered'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","saandror","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 59","lb":"EL59","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"king","it":[["வணங்கி","he bows to the worthy",1],["வழியொழுகி","he follows the right way",1],["மாண்டார்சொல் கொண்டு","he takes the words of the excellent",1],["நுணங்கியநூல் நோக்கி","he studies the subtle books",2],["நுழையா","he penetrates them",2],["இணங்கிய பால்நோக்கி","he attends to what is fitting",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:60":{"book":"elathi","num":62,"t":["பெருமை புகழ்அறம் பேணாமை சீற்றம்","அருமைநூல் சால்பில்லார் சாரின் இருமைக்கும்","பாவம் பழிபகை சாக்காடே கேடச்சம்","சாபம்போல் சாருஞ் சலித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if one draws near those without worth, who neglect greatness, fame and virtue, are given to anger and lack the rare books, then sin, blame, enmity, death, ruin and fear will cling to him in both worlds like a curse.","n":"Line 1 may describe the unworthy (neglecting greatness, fame and virtue) or list what is lost by the company. கேடச்சம் joins ruin and fear in one word, so the five items counted here are six evils; 'both worlds' (இருமைக்கும்) makes the cost run past this life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","pazhi","kedu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 60","lb":"EL60","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["பாவம்","sin clings to one who keeps bad company",3],["பழி","blame clings to him",3],["பகை","enmity clings to him",3],["சாக்காடே","death clings to him",3],["கேடச்சம்","ruin and fear cling to him",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:3"},"el:61":{"book":"elathi","num":63,"t":["ஆர்வமே செற்றம் கதமே அறையுங்கால்","ஒர்வமே செய்யும் உலோபமே - சீர்சாலா","மானமே மேய உயிர்க்(கு)ஊனம் என்னுமே","ஊனமே தீர்ந்தவர் ஒத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names desire, hatred, anger, partiality, avarice and pride without worth, and says that those rid of defect declare, by the scripture, that these are defects of the soul.","n":"The six are called ஊனம், a flaw of the உயிர், not wrongs against others; the list holds the passions that Jain teaching treats as stains of the soul (anger, pride, greed) beside desire and hatred. ஒர்வமே is a rare form, read here as ஓர்வம் 'leaning to one side', and மானம் is pride, not the honour the word often means in the Kural.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","avaa","vekuli","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 61","lb":"EL61","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆர்வமே","desire is a defect of the soul",1],["செற்றம்","hatred is a defect of the soul",1],["கதமே","anger is a defect of the soul",1],["ஒர்வமே","partiality, if that is the word, is a defect of the soul",2],["உலோபமே","avarice is a defect of the soul",2],["மானமே","pride that brings no worth is a defect of the soul",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:62":{"book":"elathi","num":64,"t":["கூத்தும் விழவும் மணமும் கொலைக்களமும்","ஆர்த்த முனையுள்ளம் வேறிடத்தும் - ஒத்தும்","ஒழுக்கம் உடையவர் செல்லாரே செல்லின்","இழுக்கம் இழவும் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who keep both scripture and conduct do not go to dances, festivals, weddings, killing grounds, the roaring battlefront or other such places, and that if they go it brings them lapse and loss.","n":"Pleasures and violence are barred together: a wedding sits beside the killing ground. முனையுள்ளம் is probably for முனையுள்ளும் 'within the battlefront', and வேறிடத்தும் 'other places too' leaves the sixth item open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","adakkam","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 62","lb":"EL62","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["கூத்தும்","those of conduct do not go to dance performances",1],["விழவும்","nor to festivals",1],["மணமும்","nor to weddings",1],["கொலைக்களமும்","nor to the killing ground",1],["ஆர்த்த முனையுள்ளம்","nor into the roaring battlefront",2],["வேறிடத்தும்","nor to other such places",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:63":{"book":"elathi","num":65,"t":["ஊணொடு கூறை எழுத்தாணி புத்தகம்","பேணொடும் எண்ணும் எழுத்திலை மாணொடு","கேட்டெழுதி ஓதிவாழ் வார்க்(கு)ஈய்ந்தார் இம்மையான்","வேட்டெழுத வாழ்வார் விரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who gave food, clothing, a stylus, a book and, with care, the leaf for figures and letters to those who live by hearing, writing and reciting will in this very life live amply, writing as they wish.","n":"The gift is the material of study, and the reward named seems to be learning itself within this life (இம்மையான்), not a later heaven. பேணொடும் is read as 'with care', not as a sixth thing, so five things are counted; வேட்டெழுத is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 63","lb":"EL63","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஊணொடு","giving food to students brings ample life here",1],["கூறை","so does giving them clothing",1],["எழுத்தாணி","so does giving them a stylus",1],["புத்தகம்","so does giving them a book",1],["எண்ணும் எழுத்திலை","so does giving the leaf for figures and letters",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:64":{"book":"elathi","num":66,"t":["உயர்ந்தான் தலைவனென்(று) ஒப்புடைத்தான் நோக்கி","உயர்ந்தான்நூல் ஓதி ஒடுங்கி - உயர்ந்தான்","அருந்தவம் ஆற்றச் செயின்வீடாம் என்றார்","பெருந்தவம் செய்தார் பெரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that those who did great penance said release comes if one regards the Exalted one as the lord, recites the Exalted one's book, holds oneself in, and fully performs the Exalted one's hard penance.","n":"உயர்ந்தான் is repeated three times, tying regard, study and penance to one figure. The order (taking him as lord, his scripture, then penance) is close to the Jain sequence of right faith, knowledge and conduct; that is our reading, not the verse's word. ஒப்புடைத்தான் is obscure. The verse does not count; the four items are the steps it names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","iraivan","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 64","lb":"EL64","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["உயர்ந்தான் தலைவனென்(று)","taking the Exalted one as lord is the first step to release",1],["உயர்ந்தான்நூல் ஓதி","reciting his book is the next",2],["ஒடுங்கி","holding oneself in is the next",2],["அருந்தவம் ஆற்றச் செயின்","fully performing his hard penance brings release",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:65":{"book":"elathi","num":67,"t":["காலனார் ஈடறுத்தால் காண்குறின் முற்றுணர்ந்த","பாலனார் நூலமர்ந்து பாராது - வாலிதா","ஊறுபா(டு) இல்லா உயர்தவம் தான்புரியின்","ஏறுமோ மேலுலகம் ஓர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether one who wishes to see Death's strength cut down and who, delighting in the scripture of the all-knowing one and looking at nothing else, performs a pure high penance that harms nothing will not, having understood, rise to the upper world.","n":"The penance must be ஊறுபாடு இல்லா, causing no injury, so non-harm is a condition of the austerity, not a separate virtue. பாலனார் and பாராது are uncertain; the question form (ஏறுமோ) expects 'yes'. The verse does not count; the three items are the conditions it sets.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kootru","kollaamai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 65","lb":"EL65","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["நூலமர்ந்து","delighting in the all-knowing one's scripture is a condition of rising",2],["பாராது","looking at nothing else is a condition",2],["ஊறுபா(டு) இல்லா உயர்தவம்","a high penance that harms nothing is a condition",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:66":{"book":"elathi","num":68,"t":["பொய்தீர் புலவர் பொருள்புரிந்து ஆராய்ந்த","மைதீர் உயர்கதியின் மாண்புரைப்பின் - மைதீர்","சுடரின்று சொல்லின்று மாறின்று சோர்வின்(று)","இடரின்(று) இனிதுயிலும் இன்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when the faultless learned who have searched its meaning describe the greatness of the stainless high state, there is no light, no speech, no opposition, no weariness, no trouble and no sweet sleep there.","n":"The highest state is described only by what it lacks, and the list denies the goods of life (light, speech, sleep) as well as its ills; even rest is not offered as its pleasure. மாறு can be change or opposition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 66","lb":"EL66","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["சுடரின்று","there is no light in the high state",3],["சொல்லின்று","there is no speech there",3],["மாறின்று","there is no opposition or change there",3],["சோர்வின்(று)","there is no weariness there",3],["இடரின்(று)","there is no trouble there",4],["இனிதுயிலும்","there is not even sweet sleep there",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:67":{"book":"elathi","num":69,"t":["கூரம்பு வெம்மணல் ஈர்மணி தூங்கலும்","ஈரும் புகையிரு ளோ(டு)இருள்நூல் - ஆராய்ந்(து)","அழிகதி இம்முறையான் ஆன்றார் அறைந்தார்","இழிகதி இம்முறையான் ஏழு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names sharp arrows, hot sand, hanging gems, the wet, smoke, darkness and the darkness beyond it, and says the great, having examined the scripture, declared these in order to be the seven low states of ruin.","n":"We read the seven as the Jain sevenfold hell, whose names run from gem and gravel through sand, mud and smoke to darkness and great darkness; the verse's words fit that scheme only roughly, and கூரம்பு and ஈர்மணி தூங்கல் are hard to place. The sixth item is copied with its sandhi (புகையிரு ளோ(டு), 'with darkness' after smoke). The verse counts seven (ஏழு), not six, and we follow it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 67","lb":"EL67","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கூரம்பு","the state of sharp arrows is the first of the low states",1],["வெம்மணல்","the state of hot sand is one",1],["ஈர்மணி தூங்கலும்","the state of hanging gems is one",1],["ஈரும்","the wet state is one",2],["புகை","the state of smoke is one",2],["யிரு ளோ(டு)","the state of darkness is one",2],["இருள்","the deeper darkness is the seventh",2]],"cw":"ஏழு","ch":"el:4"},"el:68":{"book":"elathi","num":70,"t":["சாதல் பொருள்கொடுத்தல் இன்சொல் புணர்வுலத்தல்","நோதல் பிரிவில் கவறலே - ஓதலின்","அன்புடையார்க்(கு) உள்ளன ஆறு குணமாக","மென்புடையார் வைத்தார் விரித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the gentle set out six qualities in those who love: dying for the beloved, giving wealth, sweet speech, delight in being together, pain at their pain, and grief at separation.","n":"Love is defined by six acts and feelings, ranging from the gift of life to the grief of parting. புணர்வுலத்தல் is read as புணர்வு உவத்தல் 'rejoicing in union'; as written it could mean wasting away in union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","natpu","inchol","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 68","lb":"EL68","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["சாதல்","dying for the beloved marks one who loves",1],["பொருள்கொடுத்தல்","giving wealth marks one who loves",1],["இன்சொல்","sweet speech marks one who loves",1],["புணர்வுலத்தல்","delight in being together marks one who loves",1],["நோதல்","feeling pain at the other's pain marks one who loves",2],["பிரிவில் கவறலே","grieving at separation marks one who loves",2]],"cw":"ஆறு","ch":"el:4"},"el:69":{"book":"elathi","num":71,"t":["எடுத்தல் முடக்கல் நிமிர்த்தல் நிலையே","படுத்தலோ(டு) ஆடல் பகரின் - அடுத்துயிர்","ஆறு தொழிலென்(று) அறைந்தார் உயர்ந்தவர்","வேறு தொழிலாய் விரித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that the exalted set out lifting, bending, straightening, standing, lying down and moving as the six distinct actions that attend a living being.","n":"The verse makes no moral claim; it classifies the body's movements. Why an ethics work keeps this list is not said; it may serve the Jain care over every act that could harm a creature, but that is a guess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 69","lb":"EL69","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["எடுத்தல்","lifting is one of the six actions of a living being",1],["முடக்கல்","bending is one",1],["நிமிர்த்தல்","straightening is one",1],["நிலையே","standing still is one",1],["படுத்தலோ(டு)","lying down is one",2],["ஆடல்","moving is one",2]],"cw":"ஆறு","ch":"el:4"},"el:70":{"book":"elathi","num":72,"t":["ஐயமே பிச்சை அருந்தவர்க்(கு) ஊண்ஆடை","ஐயமே இன்றி அறிந்(து) ஈந்தான் - வையமும்","வானும் வரிசையால் தானாளும் நாளுமே","ஈனமே இன்றி இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who, knowing and without doubt, gave alms, begged food, food and clothing to those of hard penance will rule earth and sky in due order, every day, sweetly and without lack.","n":"ஐயம் is used twice in two senses, alms in line 1 and doubt in line 2, so the gift must be given without hesitation. The reward is rule over both worlds, set out in order (வரிசையால்). The verse names four gifts, taking ஐயம் and பிச்சை as two kinds of alms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 70","lb":"EL70","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஐயமே","giving alms to ascetics wins rule of earth and sky",1],["பிச்சை","giving them begged food does too",1],["ஊண்","giving them food does too",1],["ஆடை","giving them clothing does too",1]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:71":{"book":"elathi","num":73,"t":["நடப்பார்க்(கு) ஊண் நல்ல பொறைதாங்கி னார்க்(கு)ஊண்","கிடப்பார்க்(கு)ஊண் கேளிர்க்(கு)ஊண் கேடின்று உடல்சார்ந்த","வானகத்தார்க்(கு) ஊணே மறுதலையார்க்கு ஊண்அமைத்தான்","தானகத்தே வாழ்வான் தக"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who arranged food for wayfarers, for those who bore heavy loads, for those lying sick, for kin, for those of the sky and for those of the other side lives fittingly in his own house.","n":"The householder's table is set for six, most of them with no claim of kinship. வானகத்தார் with கேடின்று உடல்சார்ந்த is unclear (the dead, or gods); மறுதலையார் may be enemies or those of another creed, and பொறைதாங்கினார் may be porters or the forbearing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","ilvaazhkkai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 71","lb":"EL71","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நடப்பார்க்(கு)","feeding wayfarers is part of a fitting household life",1],["பொறைதாங்கி னார்க்(கு)","feeding those who bore burdens is part of it",1],["கிடப்பார்க்(கு)","feeding those lying sick is part of it",2],["கேளிர்க்(கு)","feeding kin is part of it",2],["வானகத்தார்க்(கு)","feeding those of the sky is part of it",3],["மறுதலையார்க்கு","feeding those of the other side is part of it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:72":{"book":"elathi","num":74,"t":["உணராமை யால்குற்றம் ஒத்தான் வினையாம்","உணரான் வினைப்பிறப்புச் செய்யும் - உணராத","தொண்டுஇருந் துன்பம் தொடரும் பிறப்பினால்","மண்டிலமும் ஆகும் மதி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that from not understanding comes fault, from fault comes karma, the one without understanding makes the karma that brings birth, and through birth the great suffering follows on, so that it becomes a circle.","n":"The chain closes on itself (மண்டிலம்): suffering in birth feeds the same ignorance. ஒத்தான் and தொண்டு are obscure, and the exact links of the chain are our reading; the six items are the links of the chain as the verse names them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","arivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 72","lb":"EL72","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["உணராமை","not understanding is the start of the chain",1],["குற்றம்","fault comes from not understanding",1],["வினை","karma comes from fault",1],["பிறப்பு","the one without understanding makes karma that brings birth",2],["துன்பம்","great suffering follows through birth",3],["மண்டிலமும்","the whole becomes a circle",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:73":{"book":"elathi","num":75,"t":["மனைவாழ்க்கை மாதவம் என்றிரண்டும் மாண்ட","வினைவாழ்க்கை யாக விழைப - மனைவாழ்க்கை","பற்றுதல் இன்றி விடுதல்முன் சொல்லுமேல்","பற்றுதல் பாத்தில் தவம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that household life and great penance are both desired as noble ways of acting, and, as far as the words allow, that household life is letting go without clinging while penance is a clinging that is undivided.","n":"Both ways are called வினைவாழ்க்கை and neither is ranked above the other; each is defined by its relation to பற்று. The second half is obscure: பாத்தில் may be 'without share' or 'without division', and the reading of it is tentative. The verse counts two (இரண்டும்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thavam","pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 73","lb":"EL73","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["மனைவாழ்க்கை","household life is a noble way of acting, defined by letting go without clinging",1],["மாதவம்","great penance is a noble way of acting, defined by undivided holding",1]],"cw":"என்றிரண்டும்","ch":"el:4"},"el:74":{"book":"elathi","num":76,"t":["இடைவனப்பும், தோள்வனப்பும், ஈடில் வனப்பும்","நடைவனப்பும் நாணின் வனப்பும் - புடைசால்","கழுத்தின் வனப்பும் வனப்பல்ல எண்ணோ(டு)","எழுத்தின் வனப்பே வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the beauty of the waist, of the shoulders, beauty beyond compare, the beauty of gait, of modesty and of the neck are not beauty, and that the beauty of numbers and letters alone is beauty.","n":"The six rejected beauties are the conventional marks of a woman's body, and the list includes modesty, a virtue, among them. Learning is named as எண் and எழுத்து, numbers and letters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 74","lb":"EL74","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["இடைவனப்பும்","the beauty of the waist is not beauty",1],["தோள்வனப்பும்","the beauty of the shoulders is not beauty",1],["ஈடில் வனப்பும்","beauty beyond compare is not beauty",1],["நடைவனப்பும்","the beauty of gait is not beauty",2],["நாணின் வனப்பும்","the beauty of modesty is not beauty",2],["கழுத்தின் வனப்பும்","the beauty of the neck is not beauty",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:75":{"book":"elathi","num":77,"t":["அறுவர்தம் நூலும் அறிந்துணர்வு பற்றி","மறுவரவு மாறான நீக்கி - மறுவரவின்","மாசா ரியனா மறுதலைச்சொல் மாற்றுதலே","ஆசா ரியன(து) அமைவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the fitness of a teacher lies in knowing the books of the six schools, holding to understanding, removing what runs against the doctrine, and refuting the contrary words of a false teacher.","n":"The teacher is defined as a debater: he must know the rival systems in order to answer them. மறுவரவு and மாசாரியன் are hard: the second is read as மாசு ஆரியன், a stained teacher, set against ஆசாரியன். The verse does not count; the four items are the parts of fitness it names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","samayam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 75","lb":"EL75","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["அறுவர்தம் நூலும்","knowing the books of the six schools is part of a teacher's fitness",1],["அறிந்துணர்வு பற்றி","holding to understanding is part of it",1],["மாறான நீக்கி","removing what runs against the doctrine is part of it",2],["மறுதலைச்சொல் மாற்றுதலே","refuting the contrary word of a false teacher is part of it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:76":{"book":"elathi","num":78,"t":["ஒல்லுவ நல்ல உருவவேற் கண்ணினாய்","வல்லுவ நாடி வகையினால் - சொல்லின்","கொடையினார் போகம் சுவர்க்கம் தவத்தால்","அடையாத் தவத்தினால் வீடு"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells a woman with spear-like eyes that, examining what one can do of the good things possible, giving brings enjoyment, penance brings heaven, and the penance that does not cling brings release.","n":"The three are a ladder of means and fruits, and the top rung is a further kind of penance, not knowledge. அடையாத் தவம் is read as penance that holds to nothing; it could be penance that does not stop. The verse names three means and fruits, not six things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","veedu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 76","lb":"EL76","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொடையினார்","those who give gain enjoyment",3],["தவத்தால்","by penance one gains heaven",3],["அடையாத் தவத்தினால்","by penance that does not cling one gains release",4]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:77":{"book":"elathi","num":79,"t":["நாற்கதியும் துன்ப நவைதீர்த்தல் வேண்டுவான்","பாற்கதியின் பாற்பட ஆராய்ந்து நூற்கதியின்","எல்லை உயர்த்தார் தவமுயலின் மூன்(று) ஐந்(து) ஏழ்","வல்லைளீ(டு) ஆகும் வகு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who wishes to end the suffering and harm of the four states of birth and, examining the scripture's path, strives in penance to reach the pure state will gain release quickly, in three, five or seven births.","n":"Release is given a term in births, not promised at once. நாற்கதி is the four states of birth of Jain teaching; வல்லைளீ(டு) is read as வல்லை வீடு 'release quickly', which the sense requires. The items are the three spans of births the verse names, not a list of things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 77","lb":"EL77","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["மூன்(று)","release may come in three births",3],["ஐந்(து)","or in five",3],["ஏழ்","or in seven",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:78":{"book":"elathi","num":80,"t":["தாயிழந்த பிள்ளை தலையிழந்த பெண்டாட்டி","வாயிழந்த வாழ்வினார் வாணிகம் - போயிழந்தார்","கைத்தூண் பொருளிழந்தார் கண்ணிலவர்க்(கு) ஈந்தார்","வைத்து வழங்கிவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who keep and give to the motherless child, the widow, those whose living has lost its means, those ruined in trade, those who lost the wealth that fed them and the blind will live well.","n":"Each recipient is named by what was lost (mother, husband, livelihood, trade, wealth, sight), so the list is of the newly helpless. வைத்து வழங்கி asks that one keep in order to give. வாயிழந்த may also mean the mute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","oppuravu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 78","lb":"EL78","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தாயிழந்த பிள்ளை","giving to the motherless child brings a good life",1],["தலையிழந்த பெண்டாட்டி","so does giving to the widow",1],["வாயிழந்த வாழ்வினார்","so does giving to those whose living lost its means",2],["வாணிகம் - போயிழந்தார்","so does giving to those ruined in trade",2],["கைத்தூண் பொருளிழந்தார்","so does giving to those who lost the wealth that fed them",3],["கண்ணிலவர்க்(கு)","so does giving to the blind",3]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:79":{"book":"elathi","num":81,"t":["சாக்காடு கேடு பகைதுன்பம் இன்பமே","நாக்காடு நாட்டறை போக்குமென - நாக்காட்ட","நட்டார்க்(கு) இயையின் தமக்கியைந்த கூ(று) உடம்","பட்டார்வாய்ப் பட்டது பண்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that death, ruin, enmity, sorrow, joy and banishment from the land all come by the tongue, and, as far as the obscure close allows, that one's character is what passes the lips when speech suits friends.","n":"Joy stands among the outcomes, so the tongue is the source of good as well as ill. நாக்காடு and the last two lines are obscure; நாட்டறை போக்கு is read as exile from one's country.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","kedu","pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 79","lb":"EL79","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"all","it":[["சாக்காடு","death comes by the tongue",1],["கேடு","ruin comes by it",1],["பகை","enmity comes by it",1],["துன்பம்","sorrow comes by it",1],["இன்பமே","joy also comes by it",1],["நாட்டறை போக்கு","banishment from the land comes by it",2]],"cw":"","ch":"el:4"},"el:80":{"book":"elathi","num":82,"t":["புலையாளர் புண்பட்டார் கண்கெட்டார் போக்கில்","நிலையாளர் நீர்மை இழந்தார் - தலையாளர்க்கு","ஊண்கொடுத்(து) ஊற்றாய் உதவினார் மன்னராய்க்","காண்கொடுத்து வாழ்வார் கலந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who gave food to and were a spring of help for outcastes, the wounded, the blind, those unable to move, those who have lost their former state and the eminent will live as kings, giving audience among their people.","n":"புலையாளர் is a caste term for those of outcaste birth; here it heads a list of people to be fed, marking a low station rather than barring contact. போக்கில் நிலையாளர் is read as those who cannot move, and தலையாளர் as the eminent fallen on need; both are doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","oppuravu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Elathi 80","lb":"EL80","poet":"கணிமேதையார்","dm":"householder","it":[["புலையாளர்","feeding outcastes brings the life of a king",1],["புண்பட்டார்","so does feeding the wounded",1],["கண்கெட்டார்","so does feeding the blind",1],["நிலையாளர்","so does feeding those unable to move",2],["நீர்மை இழந்தார்","so does feeding those who lost their former state",2],["தலையாளர்க்கு","so does feeding the eminent in need",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank term of the period names outcastes as a class among the needy; the verse sets no bar on them"],"ch":"el:4"},"iv:0":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":1,"t":["கண்மூன் றுடையான்தாள் சேர்தல் கடிதினிதே","தொல்மாண் துழாய்மாலை யானைத் தொழலினிதே","முந்துறப் பேணி முகநான் குடையானைச்","சென்றமர்ந் தேத்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The invocation names three acts of worship as sweet: reaching quickly the feet of the three-eyed god, bowing to the one who wears the ancient, glorious tulasi garland, and going with first care to praise the four-faced one.","n":"The three gods are not named but are marked by their emblems: the third eye, the tulasi garland and the four faces, so the opening honours Siva, Vishnu and Brahma in turn without ranking one sect over another. The first is 'quickly' sweet (கடிதினிதே), which gives the opening its only note of urgency.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 0","lb":"IV0","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்மூன் றுடையான்தாள் சேர்தல்","reaching the feet of the three-eyed one is quickly sweet",1],["தொல்மாண் துழாய்மாலை யானைத் தொழ","worshipping the one with the ancient glorious tulasi garland is sweet",2],["முகநான் குடையானைச் சென்றமர்ந் தேத்தல்","going with prior care and praising the four-faced one is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:1":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":2,"t":["பிச்சைபுக் காயினுங் கற்றல் மிகஇனிதே","நற்சலையில் கைக்கொடுத்தல் சாலவும் முன்னினிதே","முத்தேர் முறுவலார் சொல்லினி தாங்கினிதே","தெற்றவும் மேலாயார்ச் சேர்வு"],"p":"","r":"Learning even if one must go begging for it is named very sweet, offering a hand in a good assembly sweet, the speech of women whose smile is like pearls sweet, and joining those clearly superior sweet as well.","n":"The first item sets learning above the shame of begging, the one thing the book elsewhere treats as a loss of dignity. நற்சலையில் in line 2 is not a known word; it reads best as நற்சவையில் 'in a good assembly', and the item is held on that reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","iravu","avai","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 1","lb":"IV1","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிச்சைபுக் காயினுங் கற்றல்","learning, even if it means entering on begging, is very sweet",1],["நற்சலையில் கைக்கொடுத்தல்","lending a hand in a good assembly is very sweet, and first",2],["முத்தேர் முறுவலார் சொல்","the words of women with pearl-like smiles are sweet",3],["தெற்றவும் மேலாயார்ச் சேர்வு","keeping company with those who are clearly superior is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:2":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":3,"t":["உடையான் வழக்கினி தொப்ப முடிந்தால்","மனைவாழ்க்கை முன் இனிது மாணாதா மாயின்","நிலையாமை நோக்கி நெடியார் துறத்தல்","தலையாகத் தான்இனிது நன்கு"],"p":"","r":"The just dealing of a man of means is named sweet; household life is foremost sweet when it is carried through in harmony; and if it does not prosper, renouncing without delay in view of impermanence is sweet above all.","n":"The verse ranks the two lives by a condition: the household comes first (முன்) while it goes well, and renunciation becomes the highest sweetness (தலையாக) only when it fails, so renouncing is the remedy for a household that has not prospered, not a higher calling in itself. உடையான் வழக்கு is compressed; it may be the fair conduct of the householder (the 'one who has') or a lawsuit settled fairly, so the first item is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 2","lb":"IV2","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["உடையான் வழக்க","the fair dealing of the man who has property is sweet",1],["மனைவாழ்க்கை","household life is foremost sweet if it is completed in agreement",2],["நிலையாமை நோக்கி நெடியார் துறத்தல்","renouncing without delay, seeing impermanence, is sweet above all when the household has not prospered",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:3":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":4,"t":["ஏவது மாறா இளங்கிளைமை முன்இனிதே","நாளும் நவைபோகான் கற்றல் மிகஇனிதே","ஏருடையான் வேளாண்மை தானினிது ஆங்கினிதே","தேரிற்கோள் நட்புத் திசைக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Young kin who never refuse what they are bidden are named foremost sweet, learning every day without straying into fault very sweet, the farming of a man who owns his plough sweet, and, on reflection, friendship made for a far region sweet too.","n":"The four together are the supports of a house: obedient young kin, daily learning, a plough of one's own and friends abroad. The last line is compressed; தேரின் 'if one considers' and திசைக்கு 'for a region' allow the friendship to be one formed in, or for the sake of, distant parts, so the fourth item is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kalvi","uzhavu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 3","lb":"IV3","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஏவது மாறா இளங்கிளைமை","young kin who do not go against what they are bidden are foremost sweet",1],["நாளும் நவைபோகான் கற்றல்","learning daily, without going into fault, is very sweet",2],["ஏருடையான் வேளாண்மை","farming by one who owns the plough is sweet",3],["கோள் நட்புத் திசைக்கு","friendship taken for a far region, when weighed, is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:4":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":5,"t":["யானை யுடைய படைகாண்டல் முன்இனிதே","ஊனைத்தின் றூனைப் பெருக்காமை முன்இனிதே","கான்யாற் றடைகரை யூர்இனி தாங்கினிதே","மான முடையார் மதிப்பு"],"p":"","r":"Seeing an army that has elephants is named foremost sweet, not swelling one's flesh by eating flesh foremost sweet, a village on the shore of a forest river sweet, and the regard of men who have honour sweet as well.","n":"The list sets abstinence from flesh between a royal spectacle and a pleasant village, as one more sweetness, and gives it the same 'foremost' (முன்) as the elephant army. It mirrors Innaa Narpathu 22 almost item for item.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 4","lb":"IV4","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["யானை யுடைய படைகாண்டல்","seeing an army that has elephants is foremost sweet",1],["ஊனைத்தின் றூனைப் பெருக்காமை","not eating flesh to increase one's own flesh is foremost sweet",2],["கான்யாற் றடைகரை யூர்","a village on the near bank of a forest river is sweet",3],["மான முடையார் மதிப்பு","the esteem of those who have honour is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:5":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":6,"t":["கொல்லாமை முன்இனிது கோல்கோடி மாராயஞ்","செய்யாமை முன்இனிது செங்கோலன் ஆகுதல்","எய்துங் திறத்தால் இனிதென்ப யார்மாட்டும்","பொல்லாங் குரையாமை நன்கு"],"p":"","r":"For a ruler the verse names as foremost sweet not killing and not granting honours by bending the sceptre, names being a just-sceptred king sweet by the means that achieve it, and calls not speaking ill of anyone good.","n":"மாராயம் is the honour a king confers; the second item is about favour bestowed unjustly, not about punishment. The last item, not speaking ill of anyone, is closed with நன்கு 'good' rather than இனிது, and applies beyond the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","sengol","arasu","purangooral"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 5","lb":"IV5","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["கொல்லாமை","not killing is foremost sweet",1],["கோல்கோடி மாராயஞ் செய்யாமை","not conferring honours by bending the sceptre is foremost sweet",1],["செங்கோலன் ஆகுதல்","becoming a king of the straight sceptre, by the means that achieve it, is sweet",2],["யார்மாட்டும் பொல்லாங் குரையாமை","not speaking evil of anyone is good",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:6":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":7,"t":["ஆற்றுந் துணையால் அறஞ்செய்கை முன்இனிதே","பாற்பட்டார் கூறும் பயமொழி மாண்பினிதே","வாய்ப்புடைய ராகி வலவைகள் அல்லாரைக்","காப்படையக் கோடல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Doing dharma to the extent of one's strength is named foremost sweet, the fruitful words spoken by those on the right path sweet, and taking as one's guard men who are capable and not shameless sweet.","n":"வலவை usually names a shameless or wanton person; the third item asks that protectors be chosen both able (வாய்ப்புடையர்) and not of that kind, which fits a householder's or a chief's guard alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","sol","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 6","lb":"IV6","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆற்றுந் துணையால் அறஞ்செய்கை","doing dharma to the measure of one's capacity is foremost sweet",1],["பாற்பட்டார் கூறும் பயமொழி","the fruitful words of those on the right path are sweet in their excellence",2],["வாய்ப்புடைய ராகி வலவைகள் அல்லாரைக் காப்படையக் கோடல்","taking as guards those who are capable and not shameless is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:7":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":8,"t":["அந்தண ரோத்துடைமை ஆற்ற மிகஇனிதே","பந்தம் உடையான் படையாண்மை முன்இனிதே","தந்தையே ஆயினுந் தானடங்கான் ஆகுமேல்","கொண்டடையா னாகல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names brahmins' keeping of the Veda very sweet, the leading of an army by a man with bonds of loyalty foremost sweet, and not attaching oneself even to a father if he will not restrain himself sweet.","n":"The first item ties Vedic recitation to a birth-group as its proper excellence. The third is the hardest claim in the chapter: it puts self-restraint above filial bond, so an unrestrained father is to be kept at a distance. பந்தம் may be the bond of loyalty to the king or the tie of kin among the troops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedham","padai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 7","lb":"IV7","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அந்தண ரோத்துடைமை","brahmins' possession of the Veda is very sweet",1],["பந்தம் உடையான் படையாண்மை","army leadership by one with bonds of loyalty is foremost sweet",2],["தந்தையே ஆயினுந் தானடங்கான் ஆகுமேல் கொண்டடையா னாகல்","not cleaving to one who will not restrain himself, even a father, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a norm of the period assigning Vedic recitation to the brahmin as his proper excellence"],"ch":"iv:1"},"iv:8":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":9,"t":["ஊருங் கலிமா உரனுடைமை முன்இனிதே","தார்புனை மன்னர் தமக்குற்ற வெஞ்சமத்துக்","கார்வரை யானைக் கதங்காண்டல் முன்இனிதே","ஆர்வ முடையவர் ஆற்றவும் நல்லவை","பேதுறார் கேட்டல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The strength of the spirited horse one rides is named foremost sweet, seeing the fury of dark mountain-like elephants in the fierce battle of garlanded kings foremost sweet, and the eager hearing very good things without confusion sweet.","n":"This is a five-line verse; the second item takes two lines. The first two items are a warrior's pleasures and the third turns to listening, so the list moves from the field of battle to the hall of learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kelvi","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 8","lb":"IV8","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["ஊருங் கலிமா உரனுடைமை","the strength of the spirited horse one rides is foremost sweet",1],["தார்புனை மன்னர் தமக்குற்ற வெஞ்சமத்துக் கார்வரை யானைக் கதங்காண்டல்","seeing the wrath of dark mountain-like elephants in the fierce battle of garlanded kings is foremost sweet",2],["ஆர்வ முடையவர் ஆற்றவும் நல்லவை பேதுறார் கேட்டல்","the eager hearing very good things without being confused is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:9":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":10,"t":["தங்க ணமர்புடையார் தாம்வாழ்தல் முன்இனிதே","அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகல்நிலாக் காண்பினிதே","பங்கமில் செய்கைய ராகிப் பரிந்துயார்க்கும்","அன்புடைய ராதல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The well-being of those who love us is named foremost sweet, seeing the wide moonlight in the beautiful sky sweet, and being of faultless deeds and, with tenderness, loving toward everyone sweet.","n":"The list widens love outward: first the circle that loves us, then a sight open to all, then love given to everyone (யார்க்கும்), so the moonlight sits as the image between the private and the universal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sutram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 9","lb":"IV9","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தங்க ணமர்புடையார் தாம்வாழ்தல்","the flourishing of those who hold us dear is foremost sweet",1],["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகல்நிலாக் காண்ப","seeing the wide moonlight of the beautiful sky is sweet",2],["பங்கமில் செய்கைய ராகிப் பரிந்துயார்க்கும் அன்புடைய ராதல்","being of flawless deeds and tenderly loving toward all is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:10":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":11,"t":["கடமுண்டு வாழாமை காண்டல் இனிதே","நிறைமாண்பில் பெண்டிரை நீக்கல் இனிதே","மனமாண்பி லாதவரை யஞ்சி யகறல்","எனைமாண்புந் தான்இனிது நன்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names sweet seeing that one lives without taking on debt, putting away women who lack the excellence of self-restraint, and fearing and keeping away from those of mean mind, which is sweet in every way.","n":"கடம் is debt; the first item is living without borrowing. The second is a gender rule of the period: it treats a woman's நிறை as the condition of keeping her, and says nothing of the man's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","karpu","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 10","lb":"IV10","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கடமுண்டு வாழாமை","seeing that one lives without incurring debt is sweet",1],["நிறைமாண்பில் பெண்டிரை நீக்கல்","the verse names putting away women who lack the excellence of restraint as sweet",2],["மனமாண்பி லாதவரை யஞ்சி யகறல்","fearing and leaving those of mean mind is sweet in every way",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period that women lacking chastity or restraint are to be sent away"],"ch":"iv:1"},"iv:11":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":12,"t":["அதர்சென்று வாழாமை ஆற்ற இனிதே","குதர்சென்று கொள்ளாத கூர்மை இனிதே","உயிர்சென்று தான்படினும் உண்ணார்கைத் துண்ணாப்","பெருமைபோற் பீடுடையது இல்"],"p":"","r":"Not living by going on the road is named very sweet, a keenness that does not go crooked to take sweet, and nothing is as noble as the greatness that will not eat from the hand of the unworthy even if life should go.","n":"The last item is ranked by negation ('there is nothing nobler'), not called sweet, so it caps the list. அதர்சென்று வாழாமை may mean not living as a wayfaring beggar or not living by highway robbery, and உண்ணார் may be those who do not share their food or simply the base; both turn the verse on dignity, but the exact sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","vehkaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 11","lb":"IV11","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அதர்சென்று வாழாமை","not living by going upon the road is very sweet",1],["குதர்சென்று கொள்ளாத கூர்மை","a sharpness that does not go astray to take is sweet",2],["உண்ணார்கைத் துண்ணாப் பெருமை","the greatness of not eating from the hand of the unworthy, even if life departs, is nobler than anything",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:12":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":13,"t":["குழவி பிணியின்றி வாழ்தல் இனிதே","சுழறும் அவையஞ்சான் கல்வி இனிதே","மயரிக ளல்லராய் மாண்புடையார்ச் சேரும்","திருவுந்தீர் வின்றேல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"A child's living without illness is named sweet, the learning of one who does not fear the clamorous assembly sweet, and the wealth that comes to excellent men who are not fools sweet if it does not run out.","n":"Each item carries a condition: the child without illness, learning without fear of the assembly, wealth without exhaustion. Sweetness is in each case the thing kept safe from its typical failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi","avai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 12","lb":"IV12","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["குழவி பிணியின்றி வாழ்தல்","a child living without sickness is sweet",1],["சுழறும் அவையஞ்சான் கல்வி","the learning of one who does not fear the clamouring assembly is sweet",2],["மயரிக ளல்லராய் மாண்புடையார்ச் சேரும் திரு","wealth that comes to excellent men who are not fools is sweet if it does not fail",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:13":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":14,"t":["மான மழிந்தபின் வாழாமை முன்இனிதே","தான மழியாமைத் தானடங்கி வாழ்வினிதே","ஊனமொண் றின்றி உயர்ந்த பொருளுடைமை","மானிடவர்க் கெல்லாம் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Not living on after honour is destroyed is named foremost sweet, living restrained so that one's gifts do not fail sweet, and possessing high wealth without a single flaw sweet for all people.","n":"Verse 27 repeats the first claim in nearly the same words (மானம் படவரின் வாழாமை). தானம் may be gifts or one's standing; either way restraint is what keeps it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 13","lb":"IV13","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மான மழிந்தபின் வாழாமை","not living on once honour is destroyed is foremost sweet",1],["தான மழியாமைத் தானடங்கி வாழ்வ","living self-restrained so that one's giving does not perish is sweet",2],["ஊனமொண் றின்றி உயர்ந்த பொருளுடைமை","having lofty wealth without one flaw is sweet for all people",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:14":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":15,"t":["குழவி தளர்நடை காண்டல் இனிதே","அவர்மழலை கேட்டல் அமிழ்தின் இனிதே","வினையுடையான் வந்தடைந்து வெய்துறும் போழ்து","மனனஞ்சான் ஆகல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Seeing a child's tottering walk is named sweet, hearing its babble sweeter than amrita, and having a mind that does not fear when the fruit of one's deeds arrives and burns sweet.","n":"The third item turns from the nursery to adversity. வினையுடையான் is most likely the bearer of past deeds whose fruit now comes upon him; it could also be an enemy with a task in hand, so the item is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam","oozh","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 14","lb":"IV14","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["குழவி தளர்நடை காண்டல்","seeing a child's faltering steps is sweet",1],["அவர்மழலை கேட்டல்","hearing their babble is sweeter than amrita",2],["மனனஞ்சான் ஆகல்","being undaunted in mind when one's deeds come upon one and burn is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:15":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":16,"t":["பிறன்மனை பின்னோக்காப் பீடினி தாற்ற","வறனுழக்கும் பைங்கூழ்க்கு வான்சோர் வினிதே","மறமன்னர் தங்கடையுள் மாமலைபோல் யானை","மதமுழக்கங் கேட்டல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The nobility of not looking back after another man's wife is named very sweet, rain falling on green crops suffering drought sweet, and hearing the rut-roar of mountain-like elephants at the gates of valiant kings sweet.","n":"பின்னோக்கா is 'not looking after', not even turning the eye to follow her; the restraint is in the glance. Innaa Narpathu 38 names the same act from the other side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","mazhai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 15","lb":"IV15","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறன்மனை பின்னோக்காப் பீட","the dignity of not looking after another's wife is very sweet",1],["வறனுழக்கும் பைங்கூழ்க்கு வான்சோர் வ","rain pouring on green crop that suffers drought is sweet",2],["மறமன்னர் தங்கடையுள் மாமலைபோல் யானை மதமுழக்கங் கேட்டல்","hearing the rut-roar of mountain-like elephants at the gates of valiant kings is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:16":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":17,"t":["சுற்றார்முன் கல்வி உரைத்தல் மிகஇனிதே","மிக்காரைச் சேர்தல் மிகமாண முன்இனிதே","எள்துணை யானும் இரவாது தான்ஈதல்","எத்துணையும் ஆற்ற இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Speaking one's learning before the learned is named very sweet, joining one's betters foremost sweet, and giving of oneself without begging even a sesame's worth sweet in every measure.","n":"The text reads சுற்றார்முன், which as written would be 'before those around one'; கற்றார்முன் 'before the learned' fits the sense and the Kural's phrase, and the reading here follows that, uncertainly. The third item sets giving against asking: one gives without ever asking even a little.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 16","lb":"IV16","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["சுற்றார்முன் கல்வி உரைத்தல்","setting out one's learning before the learned is very sweet",1],["மிக்காரைச் சேர்தல்","joining those greater than oneself is foremost sweet",2],["எள்துணை யானும் இரவாது தான்ஈதல்","giving without begging even a sesame-seed's worth is sweet in every measure",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:17":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":18,"t":["நாட்டார்க்கு நல்ல செயலினி தெத்துணையும்","ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல் அதனின் முன்இனிதே","பற்பல தானியத்தது ஆகிப் பலருடையும்","மெய்த்துணையுஞ் சேரல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Doing good to the people of the land is named sweet in every measure, winning over those who are not one's allies sweeter still, and having many kinds of grain and joining true companions drawn from many sweet.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 nearly quote Kural 679, but read நாட்டார்க்கு 'to the people of the land' where the Kural has நட்டார்க்கு 'to friends'; this may be a misprint, and on the Kural's wording the verse's audience would be anyone, not a ruler.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 17","lb":"IV17","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["நாட்டார்க்கு நல்ல செயல","doing good to the people of the land is sweet in every measure",1],["ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்","winning over those not allied to one is sweeter still",2],["பற்பல தானியத்தது ஆகிப் பலருடையும் மெய்த்துணையுஞ் சேரல்","being stocked with many grains and gathering true allies from many is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:18":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":19,"t":["மன்றின் முதுமக்கள் வாழும் பதிஇனிதே","தந்திரத்தின் வாழும் தவசிகள் மாண்பினிதே","எஞ்சா விழுச்சீர் இருமுது மக்களைக்","கண்டெழுதல் காலை இனிது"],"p":"","r":"A town where the elders of the assembly live is named sweet, the excellence of ascetics who live by their discipline sweet, and rising in the morning to see one's two aged parents of unfailing worth sweet.","n":"Three kinds of elder are honoured in turn: the town's elders, the ascetics, and one's own parents (இருமுது மக்கள்), and the last is placed at the start of the day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","thavam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 18","lb":"IV18","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மன்றின் முதுமக்கள் வாழும் பதி","a town where the elders of the public hall live is sweet",1],["தந்திரத்தின் வாழும் தவசிகள் மாண்ப","the excellence of ascetics living by their discipline is sweet",2],["எஞ்சா விழுச்சீர் இருமுது மக்களைக் கண்டெழுதல் காலை","rising at morning having seen one's two aged parents of unfailing worth is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:19":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":20,"t":["நட்டார்ப் புறங்கூறான் வாழ்தால் நனிஇனிதே","பட்டாங்கு பேணிப் பணிந்தொழுதல் முன்இனிதே","முட்டில் பெரும்பொருள் ஆக்கியக்கால் மற்றது","தக்குழி ஈதல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Living without speaking ill of friends behind their backs is named very sweet, keeping to the truth and conducting oneself humbly foremost sweet, and, once one has made great wealth without lack, giving it where it is fit sweet.","n":"வாழ்தால் in line 1 is a misprint for வாழ்தல். The gift is to go தக்குழி, 'where it is fitting', which makes the recipient's worth, not his need, the test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","vaaimai","panivu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 19","lb":"IV19","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நட்டார்ப் புறங்கூறான் வாழ்தால்","living without backbiting friends is very sweet",1],["பட்டாங்கு பேணிப் பணிந்தொழுதல்","keeping to the truth and conducting oneself humbly is foremost sweet",2],["தக்குழி ஈதல்","giving great wealth, once made, where it is fitting is sweet",4]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:20":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":21,"t":["சலவாரைச் சாரா விடுதல் இனிதே","புலவர்தம் வாய்மொழி போற்றல் இனிதே","மலர்தலை ஞாலத்து மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்","தகுதியால் வாழ்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"Letting go of the fickle without approaching them is named sweet, cherishing the true words of the learned sweet, and living fittingly toward all the lives of the wide world sweet.","n":"The chapter closes on the widest scope in the book so far: right conduct toward every living being of the world (மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kelvi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 20","lb":"IV20","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["சலவாரைச் சாரா விடுதல்","leaving the fickle without drawing near them is sweet",1],["புலவர்தம் வாய்மொழி போற்றல்","cherishing the true words of the learned is sweet",2],["மலர்தலை ஞாலத்து மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம் தகுதியால் வாழ்தல்","living fittingly toward all the lives of the wide world is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:1"},"iv:21":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":22,"t":["பிறன்கைப் பொருள்வௌவான் வாழ்தல் இனிதே","அறம்புரிந் தல்லவை நீக்கல் இனிதே","மறந்தேயும் மாணா மயரிகள் சேராத்","திறந்தெரிந்து வாழ்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things sweet: living without seizing wealth held in another's hand, doing dharma and putting away what is not dharma, and living with the discernment never to join unworthy, deluded men, even by forgetting.","n":"The third item asks for more than avoidance: மறந்தேயும் ('even by forgetting') makes keeping out of bad company a matter of trained discernment (திறம்), not of a single decision.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","aram","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 21","lb":"IV21","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறன்கைப் பொருள்வௌவான் வாழ்தல்","living without seizing wealth that is in another's hand is sweet",1],["அறம்புரிந் தல்லவை நீக்கல்","doing dharma and putting away what is not dharma is sweet",2],["மறந்தேயும் மாணா மயரிகள் சேராத் திறந்தெரிந்து வாழ்தல்","living with the discernment never to join unworthy, deluded men, even through forgetfulness, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:22":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":23,"t":["வருவா யறிந்து வழங்கல் இனிதே","ஒருவர்பங் காகாத ஊக்கம் இனிதே","பெருவகைத் தாயினும் பெட்டவை செய்யார்","திரிபின்றி வாழ்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things sweet: giving with knowledge of one's income, a zeal that does not make one another's dependant, and living without swerving as one who does not do what he craves even when it would delight him greatly.","n":"Line 2 reads பங்கு ('share') where tamilvu reads பாங்கு ('side'); on either reading the zeal is one that does not hang on someone else. All three items keep a person inside his own measure: of income, of independence, of desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","alavu","ookkam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 22","lb":"IV22","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வருவா யறிந்து வழங்கல்","giving while knowing one's income is sweet",1],["ஒருவர்பங் காகாத ஊக்கம்","a zeal that does not become someone else's portion or dependant is sweet",2],["பெருவகைத் தாயினும் பெட்டவை செய்யார் திரிபின்றி வாழ்தல்","living without deviating, not doing what one craves even when it would give great delight, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:23":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":24,"t":["காவோ டறக்குளம் தொட்டல் மிகஇனிதே","ஆவோடு பொன்னீதல் அந்தணர்க்கு முன்இனிதே","பாவமும் அஞ்சாராய்ப் பற்றுந் தொழில்மொழிச்","சூதரைச் சோர்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as very sweet digging a charity tank together with a grove, as foremost sweet giving gold together with cows to brahmins, and as sweet leaving the சூதர் who, not fearing even sin, speak the language of a grasping trade.","n":"சூதர் may be gamblers (சூது) or men who deceive with speech; பற்றுந் தொழில்மொழி ('speech of a grasping trade') fits either, and the line does not settle which. The verse grades its items (மிக, முன்), and the gift to brahmins is the one called foremost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","eegai","andhanar","soodhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 23","lb":"IV23","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["காவோ டறக்குளம் தொட்டல்","digging a public charity tank along with a grove is very sweet",1],["ஆவோடு பொன்னீதல் அந்தணர்க்கு","giving gold together with cows to brahmins is foremost sweet",2],["பாவமும் அஞ்சாராய்ப் பற்றுந் தொழில்மொழிச் சூதரைச் சோர்தல்","leaving the சூதர், who do not fear sin and whose talk serves a grasping trade, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a gift norm of the period that names brahmins as the foremost recipients of cows and gold"],"ch":"iv:2"},"iv:24":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":25,"t":["வெல்வது வேண்டி வெகுளாதா னோன்பினிதே","ஒல்லுந் துணையும்ஒன்று உய்ப்பான் பொறை இனிதே","இல்லாது காமுற் றிரங்கி இடர்ப்படார்","செய்வது செய்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things sweet: the austerity of one who wants to win and yet does not rage, the forbearance of one who carries a thing through as far as his strength allows, and doing what is to be done without craving what one lacks and grieving in distress.","n":"Calling the first item நோன்பு, a word for austerity, makes holding back anger in a contest a kind of penance. ஒன்று உய்ப்பான் in line 2 is compressed; 'one who sees a single thing through' is a reading, not a certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thavam","porai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 24","lb":"IV24","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வெல்வது வேண்டி வெகுளாதா னோன்ப","the austerity of one who, though he wants to win, does not rage is sweet",1],["ஒல்லுந் துணையும்ஒன்று உய்ப்பான் பொறை","the forbearance of one who carries a thing through as far as he is able is sweet",2],["இல்லாது காமுற் றிரங்கி இடர்ப்படார் செய்வது செய்தல்","doing what is to be done, without longing for what one lacks and suffering in grief, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:25":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":26,"t":["ஐவாய வேட்கை யவாவடக்கல் முன்இனிதே","கைவாய்ப் பொருள்பெறினுங் கல்லார்கண் தீர்வினிதே","நில்லாத காட்சி நிறையில் மனிதரைப்","புல்லா விடுதல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet curbing the craving of the five senses, as sweet keeping away from the unlearned even when wealth would come to hand, and as sweet leaving without embracing men of unsteady views and no self-containment.","n":"The first item turns inward and the other two outward: the senses are a gate to be guarded and so is one's company, and the second item sets a price on the guarding, wealth given up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","avaa","kallaamai","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 25","lb":"IV25","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஐவாய வேட்கை யவாவடக்கல்","curbing the craving that comes through the five senses is foremost sweet",1],["கைவாய்ப் பொருள்பெறினுங் கல்லார்கண் தீர்வ","keeping away from the unlearned, even if wealth would come into one's hand, is sweet",2],["நில்லாத காட்சி நிறையில் மனிதரைப் புல்லா விடுதல்","leaving, without embracing, men whose views do not hold and who have no self-containment is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:26":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":27,"t":["நச்சித்தற் சென்றார் நசைகொல்லா மாண்பினிதே","உட்கில் வழிவாழா ஊக்கம் மிகஇனிதே","எத்திறத் தானும் இயைவ கரவாத","பற்றினின் பாங்கினியது இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as sweet the worth of not killing the hope of those who come to one in desire, as very sweet the spirit that will not live where it is held in no regard, and as sweetest of all a bond that in no way hides what it can give.","n":"The first and last items are about the giver and frame the middle one about honour, so the giver's open hand and the self-respect that will not cringe are made one temper. கரவாத names the Kural's fault of concealment (கரவு) by its absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","karavu","maanam","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 26","lb":"IV26","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நச்சித்தற் சென்றார் நசைகொல்லா","not killing the hope of those who came to one in desire is sweet",1],["உட்கில் வழிவாழா ஊக்கம்","the spirit that will not live where it is not held in regard is very sweet",2],["எத்திறத் தானும் இயைவ கரவாத பற்றின","nothing is sweeter than a bond that does not hide, in any way, what it is able to give",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:27":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":28,"t":["தானங் கொடுப்பான் தகையாண்மைமுன் இனிதே","மானம் படவரின் வாழாமை முன்இனிதே","ஊனங்கொண் டாடார் உறுதி உடையவை","கோள்முறையாற் கோடல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet the noble bearing of one who gives gifts and not living on when dishonour comes, and as sweet taking what has lasting worth by the proper manner of receiving, as those do who do not make much of what is lacking.","n":"ஊனங் கொண்டாடார் is hard: 'those who do not celebrate a defect' is the plainest sense, but the line could also mean those who do not dwell on their losses. கோள்முறையாற் கோடல் uses the grammarians' word for a pupil's receiving of instruction (கோடல்), so the third item may be about receiving teaching; this is a reading, not a certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maanam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 27","lb":"IV27","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தானங் கொடுப்பான் தகையாண்மை","the noble bearing of one who gives gifts is foremost sweet",1],["மானம் படவரின் வாழாமை","not living on when dishonour comes is foremost sweet",2],["ஊனங்கொண் டாடார் உறுதி உடையவை கோள்முறையாற் கோடல்","taking what has lasting worth by the right manner of receiving, without making much of defects, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:28":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":29,"t":["ஆற்றாமை யாற்றென் றலையாமை முன்இனிதே","கூற்றம் வரவுண்மை சிந்தித்து வாழ்வனிதே","ஆக்க மழியினும் அல்லவை கூறாத","தேர்ச்சியின் தேர்வினியது இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet not going about crying to others to relieve one's distress, as sweet living mindful that Death's coming is real, and as sweetest of all the judgment of one who speaks no wrong even when his fortune is falling.","n":"All three items face loss: distress one could beg relief from, death, and ruin of fortune. The closing item makes integrity of speech under ruin the sweetest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kootru","naduvu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 28","lb":"IV28","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆற்றாமை யாற்றென் றலையாமை","not going about asking others to relieve one's distress is foremost sweet",1],["கூற்றம் வரவுண்மை சிந்தித்து வாழ்வ","living with the thought that Death's coming is real is sweet",2],["ஆக்க மழியினும் அல்லவை கூறாத தேர்ச்சி","nothing is sweeter than the discernment that speaks no wrong even when one's fortune is being destroyed",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:29":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":30,"t":["கயவரைக் கைகழிந்து வாழ்தல் இனிதே","உயர்வுள்ளி ஊக்கம் பிறத்தல் இனிதே","எளியர் இவரென் றிகழ்ந்துரையா ராகி","ஒளிபட வாழ்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things sweet: living with the base put out of one's hands, zeal born of thinking of rising high, and living in brightness without speaking in scorn of people as lowly.","n":"The aspiration in the middle is fenced on both sides: one leaves the base but does not scorn the lowly. Line 1 is nearly the opening line of Thirikadugam 77.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","ookkam","panivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 29","lb":"IV29","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கயவரைக் கைகழிந்து வாழ்தல்","living with the base let go of is sweet",1],["உயர்வுள்ளி ஊக்கம் பிறத்தல்","zeal arising from thinking of rising high is sweet",2],["எளியர் இவரென் றிகழ்ந்துரையா ராகி ஒளிபட வாழ்தல்","living in brightness, without speaking scornfully of anyone as lowly, is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:30":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":31,"t":["நன்றிப் பயன்தூக்கி வாழ்தல் நனிஇனிதே","மன்றக் கொடும்பா டுரையாத மாண்பினிதே","அன்றறிவார் யாரென் றடைக்கலம் வௌவாத","நன்றியின் நன்கினியது இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as very sweet living with the worth of a kindness weighed, as sweet the excellence of saying nothing crooked in the assembly, and as finest of all the goodness that does not seize a thing given in trust on the thought 'who knew of it then?'","n":"நன்றிப் பயன்தூக்கி most likely means weighing a kindness received, as gratitude does; it could be read as weighing the return before doing good, but the verse's other items, both about honesty owed to others, favour gratitude. The third item names the temptation precisely: the deposit has no witness left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","naduvu","kalavu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 30","lb":"IV30","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நன்றிப் பயன்தூக்கி வாழ்தல்","living with the worth of a kindness weighed is very sweet",1],["மன்றக் கொடும்பா டுரையாத மாண்ப","the excellence of speaking nothing crooked in the assembly is sweet",2],["அன்றறிவார் யாரென் றடைக்கலம் வௌவாத நன்றி","nothing is finer than the goodness that does not seize a deposit, thinking no one knew of it at the time",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:31":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":32,"t":["அடைந்தார் துயர்கூரா ஆற்றல் இனிதே","கடன்கொண்டுஞ் செய்வன செய்தல் இனிதே","சிறந்தமைந்த கேள்விய ராயினும் ஆராய்ந்து","அறிந்துரைத்தல் ஆற்ற இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names three things sweet: the strength that does not let the sorrow of those who came for refuge grow, doing what one must do even by borrowing, and, however excellent one's learning by listening, speaking only after examining and knowing.","n":"செய்வன ('what is to be done') is not specified; with the first item about refugees it most naturally covers duties of help, so the second item would accept debt as the price of duty. The third denies that learning excuses one from examining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","kelvi","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 31","lb":"IV31","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அடைந்தார் துயர்கூரா ஆற்றல்","the strength that does not let the sorrow of those who came for refuge grow is sweet",1],["கடன்கொண்டுஞ் செய்வன செய்தல்","doing what is to be done even by taking a loan is sweet",2],["சிறந்தமைந்த கேள்விய ராயினும் ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்துரைத்தல்","speaking after examining and knowing, even for those whose learning by listening is excellent, is very sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:32":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":33,"t":["சுற்றறிந்தார் கூறுங் கருமப் பொருள்இனிதே","பற்றமையா வேந்தன்கீழ் வாழாமை முன்இனிதே","தெற்றென இன்றித் தெளிந்தாரைத் தீங்கூக்காப்","பத்திமையிற் பாங்கினியது இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as sweet the counsel on a task given by those who know all its surroundings, as foremost sweet not living under a king who has no care, and as finest of all the loyalty that does not plot harm against those who trusted one.","n":"பற்றமையா வேந்தன் may be a king who has no regard for his people or for his servants; either way the servant is free, even advised, to leave. Line 3 (தெற்றென இன்றித் தெளிந்தாரை) is compressed: 'those who trusted without asking for certainty' is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","mannar","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 32","lb":"IV32","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["சுற்றறிந்தார் கூறுங் கருமப் பொருள்","the substance of a task as told by those who know everything around it is sweet",1],["பற்றமையா வேந்தன்கீழ் வாழாமை","not living under a king who lacks care is foremost sweet",2],["தெற்றென இன்றித் தெளிந்தாரைத் தீங்கூக்காப் பத்திமை","nothing is finer than a loyalty that does not urge harm on those who trusted one",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:33":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":34,"t":["ஊர்முனியா செய்தொழுகும் ஊக்கம் மிகஇனிதே","தானே மடிந்திராத் தாளாண்மை முன்இனிதே","வாய்மயங்கு மண்டமருள் மாறாத மாமன்னர்","தானை தடுத்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as very sweet the zeal of conducting oneself in ways the town does not resent, as foremost sweet the effort that never sits idle by itself, and as sweet checking the army of great kings who do not give way in the crowded battle.","n":"The list moves from the town to the self to the battlefield, so the same exertion is praised as civic conduct, as industry and as a warrior's stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","madi","aalvinai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 33","lb":"IV33","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஊர்முனியா செய்தொழுகும் ஊக்கம்","the zeal of acting in ways the town does not resent is very sweet",1],["தானே மடிந்திராத் தாளாண்மை","the effort that does not sit idle of its own accord is foremost sweet",2],["வாய்மயங்கு மண்டமருள் மாறாத மாமன்னர் தானை தடுத்தல்","checking, in the thick of battle, the army of great kings who do not turn back is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:34":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":35,"t":["எல்லிப் பொழுது வழங்காமை முன்இனிதே","சொல்லுங்கால் சோர்வின்றிச் சொல்லுதல் மாண்பினிதே","புல்லிக் கொளினும் பொருளல்லார் தங்கேண்மை","கொள்ளர் விடுதல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet not going about at night, as sweet speaking without a slip whenever one speaks, and as sweet leaving, not taking up, the friendship of worthless people even when they embrace one.","n":"வழங்காமை could also mean not giving at night, but with எல்லிப் பொழுது 'not moving about after dark' is the likelier sense. The third item names the hard case: the worthless friend who is affectionate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sol","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 34","lb":"IV34","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["எல்லிப் பொழுது வழங்காமை","not going about at night is foremost sweet",1],["சொல்லுங்கால் சோர்வின்றிச் சொல்லுதல்","speaking without a slip when one speaks is sweet",2],["புல்லிக் கொளினும் பொருளல்லார் தங்கேண்மை கொள்ளர் விடுதல்","leaving, without taking, the friendship of worthless people even if they embrace one is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:35":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":36,"t":["ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரிதல் முன்இனிதே","முற்றான தெரிந்து முறைசெய்தல் முன்இனிதே","பற்றினலாய்ப் பல்லுயிர்க்கும் பாத்தூற்றுப் பாங்கறிதல்","வெற்வேறில் வேந்தர்க்கு இனிது"],"p":"","r":"For kings with victorious spears the verse names as sweet learning the facts by spying through a spy, doing justice after examining everything fully, and knowing how to share out, with attachment, to all living beings.","n":"This is the one verse of the chapter that names its audience (வேந்தர்க்கு). Line 3 is obscure: பற்றினலாய் and பாத்தூற்று are not clear, and 'sharing out to all beings' rests on பாத்து ('dividing'); line 4 (வெற்வேறில்) is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["otru","sengol","vendhan","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 35","lb":"IV35","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரிதல்","for a king, learning the facts by spying through a spy is foremost sweet",1],["முற்றான தெரிந்து முறைசெய்தல்","for a king, doing justice after examining everything fully is foremost sweet",2],["பல்லுயிர்க்கும் பாத்தூற்றுப் பாங்கறிதல்","for a king, knowing the way of sharing out to all living beings is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:36":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":37,"t":["அவ்வித் தழுக்கா றுரையாமை முன்இனிதே","செவ்வியனாய்ச் செற்றுச் சினங்கடிந்து வாழ்வினிதே","கவ்வித்தாங் கொண்டுதாங் கண்டது காமுற்று","வவ்வார் விடுதல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet not speaking out of twisted envy, as sweet living upright after crushing and driving out anger, and as sweet letting go, without snatching, of whatever one sees and desires.","n":"The first item faults envy voiced (உரையாமை), not envy felt; the list runs from tongue to temper to hand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","vekuli","vehkaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 36","lb":"IV36","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அவ்வித் தழுக்கா றுரையாமை","not speaking out of twisted envy is foremost sweet",1],["செவ்வியனாய்ச் செற்றுச் சினங்கடிந்து வாழ்வ","living upright, having crushed and driven out anger, is sweet",2],["கவ்வித்தாங் கொண்டுதாங் கண்டது காமுற்று வவ்வார் விடுதல்","letting go, not snatching and grabbing, what one sees and desires is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:37":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":38,"t":["இளமையை மூப்பென் றுணர்தல் இனிதே","கிளைஞர்மாட் டச்சின்மை கேட்டல் இனிதே","தடமென் பணைத்தோள் தளிரிய லாரை","விடமென் றுணர்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as sweet knowing youth to be old age, hearing that one's kin are free of fear, and knowing as poison women with broad, soft, bamboo-like shoulders and the grace of tender shoots.","n":"The first and third items are renunciant insights that see through youth and through desire; the second, about kin, belongs to the householder and sits oddly between them. The third speaks of women only as objects of a man's desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","thuravu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 37","lb":"IV37","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["இளமையை மூப்பென் றுணர்தல்","knowing youth to be old age is sweet",1],["கிளைஞர்மாட் டச்சின்மை கேட்டல்","hearing that one's kin are free of fear is sweet",2],["தடமென் பணைத்தோள் தளிரிய லாரை விடமென் றுணர்தல்","the verse names as sweet a man's knowing as poison women with broad soft shoulders and tender grace",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a renunciant view of the period that names women's bodies as poison to the man who desires them"],"ch":"iv:2"},"iv:38":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":39,"t":["சிற்றா ளுடையான் படைக்கல மாண்பினிதே","நட்டா ருடையான் பகையாண்மை முன்இனிதே","எத்துணையும் ஆற்ற இனிதென்ப பால்படுங்","சுற்றா உடையான் விருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as sweet the excellence of weapons for one who has few men and as foremost sweet the warlike daring of one who has friends, and reports that the feast for guests of one whose kin stand by him is held sweet beyond all measure.","n":"Each item is conditional on what one has: few men, then weapons; friends, then enmity can be taken on; kin, then the feast. The last item is reported with என்ப ('they say') and given the strongest praise, so the household's hospitality is ranked above the fighter's strengths.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","natpu","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 38","lb":"IV38","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"king","it":[["சிற்றா ளுடையான் படைக்கல மாண்ப","for one with few men, the excellence of weapons is sweet",1],["நட்டா ருடையான் பகையாண்மை","for one who has friends, the daring to take on enemies is foremost sweet",2],["பால்படுங் சுற்றா உடையான் விருந்து","the feast for guests of one whose kin are on his side is said to be sweet in every measure",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:39":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":40,"t":["பிச்சைபுக் குண்பான் பிளிறாமை முன்இனிதே","துச்சி லிருந்து துயர்கூரா மாண்பினிதே","உற்றபே ராசை கருதி அறனொரூஉம்","ஒற்கம் இலாமை இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as foremost sweet that one who eats by going for alms does not clamour, as sweet the dignity of not grieving while living in a borrowed lodging, and as sweet having no want that, from great greed, abandons dharma.","n":"துச்சில் is the Kural's word (340) for the body as the soul's borrowed lodging; the second item can be read literally, of a poor man in borrowed rooms, or with that image. The third item says poverty (ஒற்கம்) is not itself the fault; the fault is the want that greed turns into abandoning dharma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thuravu","avaa","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 39","lb":"IV39","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["பிச்சைபுக் குண்பான் பிளிறாமை","that one who lives by going for alms does not clamour is foremost sweet",1],["துச்சி லிருந்து துயர்கூரா மாண்ப","the dignity of not sinking into grief while living in a borrowed lodging is sweet",2],["உற்றபே ராசை கருதி அறனொரூஉம் ஒற்கம் இலாமை","being free of the want that, driven by great greed, abandons dharma is sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"iv:40":{"book":"iniyavai_narpathu","num":41,"t":["பத்துக் கொடுத்தும் பதியிருந்து வாழ்வினிதே","வித்துற்குற் றுண்ணா விழுப்பம் மிகஇனிதே","பற்பல நாளும் பழுதின்றிப் பாங்குடைய","கற்றலிற் காழினியது இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as sweet living on in one's own town even at a tenfold cost, as very sweet the excellence of not pounding and eating the seed-grain, and as sweetest of all the firm core of learning, faultlessly and over many days, what has worth.","n":"பத்துக் கொடுத்தும் is unclear: 'even paying tenfold' or 'even giving up a tenth'. The first two items are about holding on in hard times, to one's place and to next year's seed, and the book's last item adds learning as the third thing one does not consume.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inmai","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Iniyavai Narpathu 40","lb":"IV40","poet":"பூதஞ்சேந்தனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பத்துக் கொடுத்தும் பதியிருந்து வாழ்வ","living on in one's own town even at the cost of paying ten is sweet",1],["வித்துற்குற் றுண்ணா விழுப்பம்","the excellence of not pounding and eating the seed-grain is very sweet",2],["பற்பல நாளும் பழுதின்றிப் பாங்குடைய கற்றலிற் காழ","nothing is sweeter than the firm core of learning what has worth, faultlessly, over many days",3]],"cw":"","ch":"iv:2"},"in:0":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":1,"t":["முக்கட் பகவ னடிதொழா தார்க்கின்னா","பொற்பனை வெள்ளையை யுள்ளா தொழுகின்னா","சக்கரத் தானை மறப்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","சத்தியான் றாடொழா தார்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The invocation counts four things as painful, all failures of worship: not worshipping the feet of the three-eyed Lord, living without thinking of the white god of the golden palmyra, forgetting the one who bears the discus, and not worshipping the feet of the one who bears the spear.","n":"The four gods are named by emblems only: three eyes (Siva), the golden palmyra banner and white colour (most likely Balarama), the discus (Vishnu) and the சத்தி spear (most likely Murugan). The dative தார்க்கு puts the pain on the one who neglects worship, so the book opens by counting a lack in the person, as its verses go on to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 0","lb":"IN0","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["முக்கட் பகவ னடிதொழா தார்க்","not worshipping the feet of the three-eyed Lord is painful to the one who neglects it",1],["பொற்பனை வெள்ளையை யுள்ளா தொழுக","living without keeping in mind the white god of the golden palmyra is painful",2],["சக்கரத் தானை மறப்","forgetting the god who bears the discus is painful",3],["சத்தியான் றாடொழா தார்க்கு","not worshipping the feet of the spear-bearer is painful to the one who neglects it",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:1":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":2,"t":["பந்தமில் லாத மனையின் வனப்பின்னா","தந்தையில் லாத புதல்வ னழகின்னா","அந்தண ரில்லிருந் தூணின்னா வாங்கின்னா","மந்திரம் வாயா விடின்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the beauty of a house that has no tie of kin, the good looks of a son who has no father, eating while staying in the house of brahmins, and a mantra that does not take effect.","n":"The first two items are beauty without the bond that should frame it (a house without kin, a son without a father). The third is unclear: the verse does not say whose pain it is, the outsider eating in a brahmin house or the brahmins whose house is used, and Project Madurai's variant ஊணின்னாது would make it 'not painful'. மந்திரம் may be a ritual formula or a counsel; வாயா விடின் says only that it does not come off.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","andhanar","mandhiram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 1","lb":"IN1","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"householder","it":[["பந்தமில் லாத மனையின் வனப்","beauty in a house with no tie of kin is painful",1],["தந்தையில் லாத புதல்வ னழக","the good looks of a son who has no father are painful",2],["அந்தண ரில்லிருந் தூண","eating while lodging in the house of brahmins is counted painful, for an unstated party",3],["மந்திரம் வாயா","a mantra that does not take effect is painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a rule of the period about eating in a brahmin's house and about the efficacy of mantra, stated without saying who is barred"],"ch":"in:1"},"in:2":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":3,"t":["பார்ப்பாரிற் கோழியு நாயும் புகலின்னா","ஆர்த்த மனைவி யடங்காமை நன்கின்னா","பாத்தில் புநடைவை யுடையின்னா வாங்கின்னா","காப்பாற்றா வேந்த னுலகு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a fowl and a dog entering a brahmin's house, the unruliness of a wedded wife, wearing a garment that lacks its proper பாத்து, and the land of a king who does not protect it.","n":"The first item is a pollution and birth rule: பார்ப்பார் names brahmins as a birth group, and the fowl and the dog are counted as defiling their house. The second makes a wife's lack of restraint (அடங்காமை) a pain for the house, a gender norm of the period. The third line is corrupt (புநடைவை, likely for புடைவை), and பாத்து may mean a border or one's own share, so the item cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","andhanar","vaazhkkaithunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 2","lb":"IN2","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"householder","it":[["பார்ப்பாரிற் கோழியு நாயும் புக","a fowl and a dog entering a brahmin's house is counted painful",1],["ஆர்த்த மனைவி யடங்காமை","the unruliness of the wife one has bound in marriage is very painful",2],["பாத்தில் புநடைவை யுடை","wearing a garment without its பாத்து is painful (sense uncertain)",3],["காப்பாற்றா வேந்த னுலகு","the land of a king who does not protect it is painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["purity","caste","gender"],"a pollution rule of the period (animals defiling a brahmin household) beside a norm of the wife's restraint"],"ch":"in:1"},"in:3":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":4,"t":["கொடுங்கோல் மறமன்னர் கீழ்வாழ்த லின்னா","நெடுநீர் புணையின்றி நீந்துத லின்னா","கடுமொழி யாளர் தொடர்பின்னா வின்னா","தடுமாறி வாழ்த லுயிர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: living under fierce kings of bent sceptre, swimming long water without a float, association with men of harsh speech, and living in wavering confusion.","n":"The second item is the image for the rest: under a cruel king, among harsh speakers or in an unsettled mind, one is in deep water with nothing to hold. The last item names its sufferer, உயிர்க்கு, so wavering is a pain to life itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kadunjol","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 3","lb":"IN3","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["கொடுங்கோல் மறமன்னர் கீழ்வாழ்த","living as a subject under fierce kings of bent sceptre is painful",1],["நெடுநீர் புணையின்றி நீந்துத","swimming deep water without a float is painful",2],["கடுமொழி யாளர் தொடர்ப","close association with men of harsh speech is painful",3],["தடுமாறி வாழ்த","living in wavering confusion is painful to one's very life",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:4":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":5,"t":["எருதி லுழவர்க்குப் போகீர மின்னா","கருவிகண் மாறிப் புறங்கொடுத்த லின்னா","திருவுடை யாரைச் செறலின்னா வின்னா","பெருவலியார்க் கின்னா செயல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: for ploughmen without oxen, the wet of the soil passing unused; turning one's back in battle with weapons lost; provoking those who have fortune; and doing harm to the very strong.","n":"The first item is a matter of timing: the soil's moisture (ஈரம்) goes by while the ox-less farmer cannot plough. The last two are prudential: the pain is the one the provoker brings on himself. கருவிகண் in line 2 may be கருவிகள் misprinted, or 'where weapons are exchanged'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","vali","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 4","lb":"IN4","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["எருதி லுழவர்க்குப் போகீர","for ploughmen without oxen, the soil's moisture passing unused is painful",1],["கருவிகண் மாறிப் புறங்கொடுத்த","turning one's back in battle with weapons changed or lost is painful",2],["திருவுடை யாரைச் செற","provoking those who have fortune is painful",3],["பெருவலியார்க் கின்னா செயல்","doing harm to the very strong is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:5":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":6,"t":["சிறையில் கரும்பினைக் காத்தோம்ப லின்னா","உறைசேர் பழங்கூரை சேர்ந்தொழுக லின்னா","முறையின்றி யாளு மரசின்னா வின்னா","மறையின்றிச் செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: guarding sugarcane that has no fence, living under an old roof that lets the rain drip through, a kingship that rules without justice, and a deed done without secrecy.","n":"The two images of unprotected keeping (cane without a fence, a house without a sound roof) come before the two cases they illuminate: rule without முறை and action without மறை. The pairing of முறை and மறை in lines 3 and 4 is the verse's own chime.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sengol","aran","vinai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 5","lb":"IN5","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["சிறையில் கரும்பினைக் காத்தோம்ப","guarding sugarcane that has no fence is painful",1],["உறைசேர் பழங்கூரை சேர்ந்தொழுக","living under an old roof through which the rain drips is painful",2],["முறையின்றி யாளு மரச","a kingship that rules without justice is painful",3],["மறையின்றிச் செய்யும் வினை","a deed carried out without secrecy is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:6":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":7,"t":["அறமனத்தார் கூறுங் கடுமொழியு மின்னா","மறமனத்தார் ஞாட்பின் மடிந்தொழுக லின்னா","இடும்பை யுடையார் கொடையின்னா வின்னா","கொடும்பா டுடையார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: harsh words even from those of virtuous mind, men of martial mind slackening in battle, the giving of those who are themselves in distress, and the words of the crooked.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are set against each other, அறமனத்தார் and மறமனத்தார்: each failure is the opposite of the person's own nature. The third item counts a gift as a pain when the giver is in trouble, which the verse leaves without reason: the pain may be the giver's, or the gift's poor fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kadunjol","eegai","madi","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 6","lb":"IN6","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["அறமனத்தார் கூறுங் கடுமொழி","harsh words even from the virtuous-minded are painful",1],["மறமனத்தார் ஞாட்பின் மடிந்தொழுக","men of martial mind slackening in battle is painful",2],["இடும்பை யுடையார் கொடை","giving by those who are in distress is painful",3],["கொடும்பா டுடையார்வாய்ச் சொல்","the words from the mouths of crooked men are painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:7":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":8,"t":["ஆற்ற லிலாதான் பிடித்த படையின்னா","நாற்ற மிலாத மலரி னழகின்னா","தேற்ற மிலாதான் றுணிவின்னா வாங்கின்னா","மாற்ற மறியா னுரை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a weapon held by one without strength, the beauty of a flower without scent, the boldness of one without clear judgement, and the speech of one who does not know how to reply.","n":"Each item is a thing cut off from what makes it work: the weapon from strength, the flower from scent, daring from conviction (தேற்றம்), speech from the power to answer (மாற்றம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","thelivu","solvanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 7","lb":"IN7","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆற்ற லிலாதான் பிடித்த படை","a weapon in the grip of one without strength is painful",1],["நாற்ற மிலாத மலரி னழக","the beauty of a flower that has no scent is painful",2],["தேற்ற மிலாதான் றுணிவ","the daring of one who lacks clear conviction is painful",3],["மாற்ற மறியா னுரை","the speech of one who cannot give a reply is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:8":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":9,"t":["பகல்போலு நெஞ்சத்தார் பண்பின்மை யின்னா","நகையாய நண்பினார் நாரின்மை யின்னா","இகலி னெழுந்தவ ரோட்டின்னா வின்னா","நயமின் மனத்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the lack of good nature in those whose hearts seem as clear as day, the lack of real affection in friends made in laughter, the flight of those who rose up in hostility, and the friendship of the ungracious-minded.","n":"Three of the four items are about friendship and its counterfeits. பகல் போலும் நெஞ்சத்தார் can mean hearts open as daylight or hearts even as the balance (பகல், the middle); either way the pain is a fair appearance with no character behind it. நார் is the fibre of affection that binds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 8","lb":"IN8","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பகல்போலு நெஞ்சத்தார் பண்பின்மை","the want of good nature in those whose hearts appear as clear as day is painful",1],["நகையாய நண்பினார் நாரின்மை","the want of binding affection in friends made in laughter is painful",2],["இகலி னெழுந்தவ ரோட்ட","the running away of those who rose up in hostility is painful",3],["நயமின் மனத்தவர் நட்பு","friendship with the ungracious-minded is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:9":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":10,"t":["கள்ளில்லா மூதூர் களிகட்கு நன்கின்னா","வள்ளல்க ளின்மை பரிசிலர்க்கு முன்னின்னா","வண்மை யிலாளர் வனப்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","பண்ணில் புரவி பரிப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: an old town without toddy to the drinkers, the absence of generous patrons to those who seek gifts (named first among pains), the beauty of the ungenerous, and the gait of a horse without harness.","n":"The first two items name their sufferers in the dative (களிகட்கு, பரிசிலர்க்கு), and set the drinker's want of toddy beside the bard's want of a patron, perhaps with irony. முன் gives the bard's lack the first rank; the third item then turns to the ungenerous rich, whose looks are no comfort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","kal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 9","lb":"IN9","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளில்லா மூதூர்","an old town without toddy is very painful to drinkers",1],["வள்ளல்க ளின்மை","the absence of generous patrons is painful to gift-seekers before all else",2],["வண்மை யிலாளர் வனப்","the beauty of those without generosity is painful",3],["பண்ணில் புரவி பரிப்பு","the running of a horse that is not harnessed is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:10":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":11,"t":["பொருளணர்வா ரில்வழிப் பாட்டுரைத்த லின்னா","இருள்கூர் சிறுநெறி தாந்தனிப்போக் கின்னா","அருளில்லார் தங்கட் செலவின்னா வின்னா","பொருளில்லார் வண்மை புரிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: reciting a song where there is no one who grasps its meaning, going alone down a narrow path dense with darkness, going to those who have no grace, and attempting liberality when one has no wealth.","n":"பொருள் closes the first and last lines: a song without hearers who understand its meaning, generosity without the means. The verse thus sets the wasted gift of words beside the impossible gift of goods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","arul","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 10","lb":"IN10","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பொருளணர்வா ரில்வழிப் பாட்டுரைத்த","reciting a song where no one grasps its meaning is painful",1],["இருள்கூர் சிறுநெறி தாந்தனிப்போக்","going alone down a narrow path thick with darkness is painful",2],["அருளில்லார் தங்கட் செலவ","going to those who have no grace is painful",3],["பொருளில்லார் வண்மை புரிவு","attempting liberality when one has no wealth is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:11":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":12,"t":["உடம்பா டில்லாத மனைவிதோ ளின்னா","இடனில் சிறியரோ டியர்த்தநண் பின்னா","இடங்கழி யாளர் தொடர்பின்னா வின்னா","கடனுடையார் காணப் புகல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the shoulders of a wife who is not of one mind with her husband, friendship bound with petty men who have no room in them, association with the intemperate, and coming where one's creditors can see one.","n":"The first item places the pain in the embrace itself (தோள்): physical closeness without agreement (உடம்பாடு). The last is shame rather than danger: the debtor's pain is being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","natpu","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 11","lb":"IN11","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["உடம்பா டில்லாத மனைவிதோ ள","the embrace of a wife who is not of one mind is painful",1],["இடனில் சிறியரோ டியர்த்தநண்","friendship tied with petty men who have no room in them is painful",2],["இடங்கழி யாளர் தொடர்ப","association with the intemperate is painful",3],["கடனுடையார் காணப் புகல்","entering where one's creditors will see one is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:12":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":13,"t":["தலைதண்ட மாகச் சுரம்போத லின்னா","வலைசுமந் துண்பான் பெருமித மின்னா","புலையுள்ளி வாழ்த லுயிர்க்கின்னா வின்னா","முலையிள்ளாள் பெண்மை விழைவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: crossing the wasteland at the risk of one's head, the pride of one who lives by carrying a net, living with one's mind set on புலை, which is painful to life, and the desire for womanhood of one who has no breasts.","n":"The second item denies pride to a man who eats by carrying the net, an occupation tied to birth groups in this period, so it ranks people by work. புலை in line 3 may be flesh (as in புலால்) or baseness, or the outcaste; the verse does not fix it. The fourth item is a gender norm whose subject is unclear: a breastless woman longing to be counted a woman, or a man's desire for her; இள்ளாள் is likely a misprint for இல்லாள்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","agandhai","pirappu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 12","lb":"IN12","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["தலைதண்ட மாகச் சுரம்போத","crossing the wasteland with one's head as the forfeit is painful",1],["வலைசுமந் துண்பான் பெருமித","the pride of one who lives by carrying a net is counted painful",2],["புலையுள்ளி வாழ்த","living with the mind set on புலை is painful to life",3],["முலையிள்ளாள் பெண்மை விழைவு","the longing for womanhood of one without breasts is counted painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","gender"],"a period ranking that denies pride to a netting occupation, and a norm that ties womanhood to the body"],"ch":"in:1"},"in:13":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":14,"t":["மணியிலாக் குஞ்சரம் வேந் தூர்த லின்னா","துணிவில்லார் சொல்லுந் தறுகண்மை யின்னா","பணியாத மன்னர்ப் பணிவின்னா வின்னா","பிணியன்னார் வாழு மனை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a king riding an elephant without a bell, boldness spoken by those without courage, submission to kings who will not bend, and a house where people like a disease live.","n":"The bell on the war elephant announces the king; without it his progress is unheralded or unsafe. The third item is the courtier's pain: bowing to kings who give no regard in return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","anjaamai","panivu","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 13","lb":"IN13","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"king","it":[["மணியிலாக் குஞ்சரம் வேந் தூர்த","a king riding an elephant without a bell is painful",1],["துணிவில்லார் சொல்லுந் தறுகண்மை","boasts of valour spoken by those without courage are painful",2],["பணியாத மன்னர்ப் பணிவ","submitting to kings who will not bend is painful",3],["பிணியன்னார் வாழு மனை","a house where people like a disease live is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:14":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":15,"t":["வணரொலி யைம்பாலார் வஞ்சித்த லின்னா","துணர்தூங்கு மாவின் படுபழ மின்னா","புணர்பாவை யன்னார் பிரிவின்னா வின்னா","உணர்வா ருணராக் கடை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: being deceived by women with curling five-plaited hair, the fallen fruit of a mango hung with clusters, parting from one who is like a joined doll, and the moment when those who should understand do not understand.","n":"Three of the items come from love: the deceit of women (a gendered claim of the period), the parting from a beloved, and the failure of understanding between those who should know each other's minds. The fallen fruit sits among them as an image of sweetness spoiled by its fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 14","lb":"IN14","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["வணரொலி யைம்பாலார் வஞ்சித்த","being deceived by women with curling five-plaited hair is painful",1],["துணர்தூங்கு மாவின் படுபழ","the fallen fruit of a mango hung with clusters is painful",2],["புணர்பாவை யன்னார் பிரிவ","parting from one who is like a joined doll is painful",3],["உணர்வா ருணராக் கடை","the moment when those who should understand fail to understand is painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period claim that women deceive, stated as one of the pains of love"],"ch":"in:1"},"in:15":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":16,"t":["புல்லார் புரவி மணியின்றி யூர்வின்னா","கல்லா ருரைக்குங் கருமப் பொருளின்னா","இல்லாதார் நல்ல விருப்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","பல்லாரு ணாணப் படல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: riding a poor horse without a bell, the purpose of an undertaking explained by the unlearned, the fine wishes of those who have nothing, and being shamed among many people.","n":"The second item is counsel on an undertaking (கருமப் பொருள்) when the counsellor is unlearned; Iniyavai Narpathu 32 uses the same phrase for counsel given by those who know. புல்லார் புரவி may be a horse of the base or a grass-fed nag.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","inmai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 15","lb":"IN15","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["புல்லார் புரவி மணியின்றி யூர்வ","riding a poor horse without a bell is painful",1],["கல்லா ருரைக்குங் கருமப் பொருள","the purpose of an undertaking as the unlearned explain it is painful",2],["இல்லாதார் நல்ல விருப்ப","the good wishes of those who have nothing are painful",3],["பல்லாரு ணாணப் படல்","being put to shame among many is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:16":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":17,"t":["உண்ணாது வைக்கும் பெரும்பொருள் வைப்பின்னா","நண்ணாப் பகைவர் புணர்ச்சி நனியின்னா","கண்ணி லொருவன் வனப்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","எண்ணிலான் செய்யுங் கணக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: storing great wealth without enjoying it, union with enemies who should not be approached, the good looks of a man without eyes, and accounts kept by one who does not know numbers.","n":"The last two items are paired: a handsome man without eyes and an innumerate man doing accounts are both the same lack of sight, one bodily and one learned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai","kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 16","lb":"IN16","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["உண்ணாது வைக்கும் பெரும்பொருள் வைப்ப","storing great wealth without enjoying it is painful",1],["நண்ணாப் பகைவர் புணர்ச்சி","union with enemies who should not be approached is very painful",2],["கண்ணி லொருவன் வனப்ப","the good looks of a man without eyes are painful",3],["எண்ணிலான் செய்யுங் கணக்கு","accounts kept by one who does not know numbers are painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:17":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":18,"t":["ஆன்றவித்த சான்றோருட் பேதை புகலின்னா","மான்றிருண்ட போழ்தின் வழங்கல் பெரிதின்னா","நோன்றவிந்து வாழாதார் நோன்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","ஈன்றாளை யோம்பா விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a fool entering among the worthy who are full and self-subdued, going about when it has grown dark and dim (very painful), the penance of those who do not live subdued by endurance, and abandoning the mother who bore one without caring for her.","n":"ஆன்றவித்த and நோன்றவிந்து echo each other: the worthy are those who have subdued themselves, and a penance without that subduing is a pain. The last item is the one duty in the verse; the rest are misplacements.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","saandror","thavam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 17","lb":"IN17","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆன்றவித்த சான்றோருட் பேதை புக","a fool entering among the full and self-subdued worthy is painful",1],["மான்றிருண்ட போழ்தின் வழங்கல்","going about when it has grown dark is very painful",2],["நோன்றவிந்து வாழாதார் நோன்ப","the penance of those who do not live subdued by endurance is painful",3],["ஈன்றாளை யோம்பா விடல்","abandoning the mother who bore one without caring for her is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:18":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":19,"t":["உரனுடையா னுள்ள மடிந்திருந்த லின்னா","மறனுடை யாளுடையான் மார்பார்த்த லின்னா","சுரமரிய கானஞ் செலவின்னா வின்னா","மனவறி யாளர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the mind of a strong man lying idle, facing the chest of one who has valiant warriors, going through the hard forest of the wasteland, and association with the poor in mind.","n":"The first item imagines strength (உரன்) present and the mind (உள்ளம்) asleep, a strong man whose power goes unused. மனவறியாளர் are those empty of mind; the variant அகம்வறியாளர் says the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","ookkam","maram","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 18","lb":"IN18","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["உரனுடையா னுள்ள மடிந்திருந்த","the mind of a strong man lying idle is painful",1],["மறனுடை யாளுடையான் மார்பார்த்த","facing the chest of one who commands valiant warriors is painful",2],["சுரமரிய கானஞ் செலவ","going through the hard forest of the wasteland is painful",3],["மனவறி யாளர் தொடர்பு","association with the empty-minded is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:19":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":20,"t":["குலத்துப் பிறந்தவன் கல்லாமை யின்னா","நிலத்திட்ட நல்வித்து நாறாமை யின்னா","நலத்தகையார் நாணாமை யின்னாவாங் கின்னா","கலத்தல் குலமில் வழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: lack of learning in one born to a good family, good seed sown in the ground failing to sprout, women of good quality lacking modesty, and union where there is no family standing.","n":"குலம் frames the verse: the first and last items are about it. The good seed that does not sprout is the image of the first item, birth without learning. கலத்தல் குலமில் வழி makes union (perhaps marriage, perhaps any mixing) outside family rank a pain, a birth norm. நலத்தகையார் are most likely women, and their lack of நாண் is a gender norm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kallaamai","naanam","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 19","lb":"IN19","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["குலத்துப் பிறந்தவன் கல்லாமை","lack of learning in one born to a good family is painful",1],["நிலத்திட்ட நல்வித்து நாறாமை","good seed sown in the ground failing to sprout is painful",2],["நலத்தகையார் நாணாமை","women of good quality lacking modesty is counted painful",3],["கலத்தல் குலமில் வழி","union where there is no family standing is counted painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","gender"],"a period norm against union outside family rank, beside a norm of women's modesty"],"ch":"in:1"},"in:20":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":21,"t":["மாரிநாட் கூவுங் குயிலின் குரலின்னா","வீர மிலாளர் கடுமொழிக் கூற்றின்னா","மாரி வளம்பொய்ப்பி னூர்க்கின்னா வாங்கின்னா","மூரி யெருத்தா லுழவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the koel's voice calling in the rainy season, harsh words spoken by those without valour, the failure of the rain's plenty to a town, and ploughing with a sluggish ox.","n":"மாரி opens lines 1 and 3: the koel's song out of season and the rain that fails in season. The verse moves from the unseasonable to the town's want and ends in the field, with the ox.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","uzhavu","kadunjol","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 20","lb":"IN20","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["மாரிநாட் கூவுங் குயிலின் குர","the koel's voice calling in the rainy season is painful",1],["வீர மிலாளர் கடுமொழிக் கூற்","harsh speech from those without valour is painful",2],["மாரி வளம்பொய்ப்பி","the failure of the rain's plenty is painful to a town",3],["மூரி யெருத்தா லுழவு","ploughing with a sluggish ox is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:1"},"in:21":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":22,"t":["ஈத்த வகையா னுவவாதார்க் கீப்பின்னா","பாத்துண லில்லா ருழைச்சென் றுணலின்னா","மூத்த விடத்துப் பிணியின்னா வாங்கின்னா","ஒத்திலாப் பார்ப்பா னுரை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: giving to people who are not content with the way they were given to, going to eat with those who do not share their food, sickness that comes in old age, and the word of a brahmin who lacks Vedic recitation.","n":"The first two items look at the shared meal from both ends, the gift wasted on the ungrateful and the guest at a house that does not share. The fourth is a birth-rank norm: the brahmin is measured by his ஓத்து (printed ஒத்து), and his speech without it is counted a pain, not an authority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","noy","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 21","lb":"IN21","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஈத்த வகையா னுவவாதார்க் கீப்","giving to those who are not pleased with the manner of the gift is painful",1],["பாத்துண லில்லா ருழைச்சென் றுண","going to eat at the house of those who do not share their food is painful",2],["மூத்த விடத்துப் பிணி","sickness when one has grown old is painful",3],["ஒத்திலாப் பார்ப்பா னுரை","the word of a brahmin without Vedic recitation is painful",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank norm of the period: a brahmin's word is valued by his Vedic recitation and without it is counted a pain"],"ch":"in:2"},"in:22":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":23,"t":["யானையின் மன்னரைக் காண்ட னனியின்னா","ஊனைத்தின் றூனைப் பெருக்குதல் முன்னின்னா","தேனெய் புளிப்பிற் சுவையின்னா வாங்கின்னா","கான்யா றிடையிட்ட வூர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: seeing kings who have no elephants, eating flesh to swell one's own flesh (ranked painful before all), the taste of honey turned sour, and a town cut off by a forest stream.","n":"Each line carries its own intensifier, and முன் 'first, before all' puts the flesh-eater at the head. The other three are things that have lost what makes them work: a king without his elephant force, sweetness gone sour, a town without a road; flesh-eating is filed among them as a corruption of the body's food.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","mannar","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 22","lb":"IN22","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["யானையின் மன்னரைக் காண்ட","seeing kings without elephants is very painful",1],["ஊனைத்தின் றூனைப் பெருக்குதல்","eating flesh to make one's own flesh grow is painful before all else",2],["தேனெய் புளிப்பிற் சுவை","the taste of honey once it has soured is painful",3],["கான்யா றிடையிட்ட வூர்","a town with a forest river between it and the way is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:23":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":24,"t":["சிறையில்லாத மூதூரின் வாயில்காப் பின்னா","துறையிருந் தாடை கழுவுத லின்னா","அறைபறை யன்னவர் சொல்லின்னா வின்னா","நிறையில்லான் கொண்ட தவம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: keeping the gate of an old town that has no wall, washing clothes while sitting at the bathing ford, the talk of people who are like a beaten drum, and penance taken up by one without self-restraint.","n":"All four fail for want of the thing that should hold them in: a wall behind the gate, clean water at the ford, a closed mouth, restraint under the penance. அறைபறை அன்னவர் is the Kural's own image for the base who broadcast what they hear (1076).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","kayamai","thavam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 23","lb":"IN23","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["சிறையில்லாத மூதூரின் வாயில்காப்","guarding the gate of an old town without a wall is painful",1],["துறையிருந் தாடை கழுவுத","sitting at the ford and washing clothes there is painful",2],["அறைபறை யன்னவர் சொல்","the talk of those who are like a beaten drum is painful",3],["நிறையில்லான் கொண்ட தவம்","penance undertaken by one without self-restraint is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:24":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":25,"t":["ஏமமில் மூதூ ரிருத்தன் மிகவின்னா","தீமை யுடையா ரயிலிருந்த னன்கின்னா","காமமுதிரி னுயிர்க்கின்னா வாங்கின்னா","யாமென் பவரொடு நட்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: living in an old town without protection, sitting beside people of evil ways, desire grown ripe, which is painful to life itself, and friendship with those who say 'we'.","n":"யாம் என்பவர் 'those who say we' is read here as the self-asserting, who make every bond about themselves; the line could also mean those who merely claim to be one's own. The third item is not desire as such but desire that has ripened (முதிரின்), with harm to உயிர் named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","inam","kaamam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 24","lb":"IN24","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["ஏமமில் மூதூ ரிருத்தன்","living in an old town without protection is very painful",1],["தீமை யுடையா ரயிலிருந்த","sitting beside people of evil ways is painful",2],["காமமுதிரி","desire, once it has grown ripe, is painful to life",3],["யாமென் பவரொடு நட்பு","friendship with those who keep saying 'we' is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:25":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":26,"t":["நட்டா ரிடுக்கண்கள் காண்டல் நனியின்னா","ஒட்டார் பெருமிதங் காண்டல் பெரிதின்னா","கட்டில்லா மூதூ ருறையின்னா வாங்கின்னா","நட்ட கவற்றினாற் சூது"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: seeing the troubles of friends, seeing the pride of enemies, dwelling in an old town without order, and gambling with dice one has made one's own.","n":"The first two lines are a pair, the friend brought low and the enemy raised high, both pains of watching. நட்ட கவறு is unclear: from நள் it is dice one has grown attached to, matching நட்டார் 'friends' in line 1; from நடு it would be set or planted dice. The reading of the fourth item depends on which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["natpu","pagai","aran","soodhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 25","lb":"IN25","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["நட்டா ரிடுக்கண்கள் காண்டல்","seeing one's friends in trouble is very painful",1],["ஒட்டார் பெருமிதங் காண்டல்","seeing the pride of one's enemies is greatly painful",2],["கட்டில்லா மூதூ ருறை","dwelling in an old town without bonds of order is painful",3],["நட்ட கவற்றினாற் சூது","gambling with dice one has become attached to (or set dice) is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:26":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":27,"t":["பெரியாரோ டியாத்த தொடர்விடுத லின்னா","அரியவை செய்து மெனவுரைத்த லின்னா","பரியார்க்குத் தாமுற்ற கூற்றின்னா வின்னா","பெரியோர்க்குத் தீய செயல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: letting go of a bond tied with the great, saying 'we will do even hard things', telling one's own suffering to those who do not care, and doing evil to the great.","n":"The great open and close the list: first the bond with them let go, last harm done to them, with two faults of speech (the boast, the complaint to the uncaring) between.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","natpu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 26","lb":"IN26","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பெரியாரோ டியாத்த தொடர்விடுத","letting go of a bond tied with the great is painful",1],["அரியவை செய்து மெனவுரைத்த","saying 'we will do even what is hard' is painful",2],["பரியார்க்குத் தாமுற்ற கூற்","telling one's suffering to those who do not feel for one is painful",3],["பெரியோர்க்குத் தீய செயல்","doing evil to the great is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:27":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":28,"t":["பெருமை யுடையாரைப் பீடழித்த லின்னா","கிழமை யுடையார்க் களைந்திடுத லின்னா","வளமை யிலாளர் வனப்பின்னா வின்னா","இளமையுண் மூப்புப் புகல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: destroying the standing of the eminent, casting off those with a close claim on one, beauty in those without means, and falling into old age within youth.","n":"The second line is read with the variant கிழமை யுடையாரை, 'casting off those who have a claim'; as printed (யுடையார்க்கு) the verb is harder to place. இளமையுள் மூப்புப் புகல் may be ageing early or living as the old do while still young.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["perumai","kizhamai","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 27","lb":"IN27","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருமை யுடையாரைப் பீடழித்த","destroying the standing of those who have greatness is painful",1],["கிழமை யுடையார்க் களைந்திடுத","casting off those who have a close claim is painful",2],["வளமை யிலாளர் வனப்","beauty in those who lack means is painful",3],["இளமையுண் மூப்புப் புகல்","entering old age within youth is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:28":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":29,"t":["கல்லாதா னூருங் கலிமாப் பரிப்பின்னா","வல்லாதான் சொல்லு முரையின் பயனின்னா","இல்லார்வாய்ச் சொல்லி னயமின்னா வாங்கின்னா","கல்லாதான் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: an unlearned man riding a prancing horse, the worth of words spoken by an incapable man, the grace of speech in the mouths of the poor, and an unlearned man holding an assembly.","n":"The unlearned man frames the verse, on a horse at the start and before an assembly at the end, two kinds of display he cannot carry. Between them speech loses its weight twice, once for want of skill and once for want of wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","sol","inmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 28","lb":"IN28","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["கல்லாதா னூருங் கலிமாப் பரிப்","the gallop of a spirited horse ridden by an unlearned man is painful",1],["வல்லாதான் சொல்லு முரையின் பய","the yield of words spoken by one without capacity is painful",2],["இல்லார்வாய்ச் சொல்லி னய","the grace of speech in the mouths of the poor is painful",3],["கல்லாதான் கோட்டி கொளல்","an unlearned man holding an assembly is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:29":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":30,"t":["குறியறியான் மாநாக மாட்டுவித்த லின்னா","தறியறியா னீரின் பாய்ந்தாட லின்னா","அறிவறியா மக்கட் பெறலின்னா வின்னா","செறிவிலான் கேட்ட மறை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: making a great cobra dance without knowing its signs, jumping into water to play without knowing where the stakes stand, begetting children without sense, and a secret heard by one who cannot keep it.","n":"Every item is handling something dangerous without the knowledge it needs; the line on children puts them in that series, so a child without sense is counted with a mishandled snake and a leaked secret.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu","makkal","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 29","lb":"IN29","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["குறியறியான் மாநாக மாட்டுவித்த","one who does not know the signs making a great snake dance is painful",1],["தறியறியா னீரின் பாய்ந்தாட","jumping into water to play without knowing the stakes beneath is painful",2],["அறிவறியா மக்கட் பெற","getting children who do not come to understanding is painful",3],["செறிவிலான் கேட்ட மறை","a secret heard by one who cannot hold it close is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:30":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":31,"t":["நெடுமர நீள்கோட் டுயர்பாய்த லின்னா","கடுஞ்சின வேழத் தெதிர்சேற லின்னா","ஒடுங்கி யரவுறையு மில்லின்னா வின்னா","கடும்புலி வாழு மதர்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: leaping high from the long branch of a tall tree, going against an elephant in fierce rage, a house where a snake lives hidden, and a path where a fierce tiger lives.","n":"This is the one verse of the chapter with no moral item: four plain bodily dangers. It shows the scale the other verses measure faults against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 30","lb":"IN30","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["நெடுமர நீள்கோட் டுயர்பாய்த","leaping high from the long branch of a tall tree is painful",1],["கடுஞ்சின வேழத் தெதிர்சேற","going to face an elephant in fierce rage is painful",2],["ஒடுங்கி யரவுறையு மில்","a house in which a snake lives hidden is painful",3],["கடும்புலி வாழு மதர்","a path where a fierce tiger lives is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:31":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":32,"t":["பண்ணமையா யாழின்கீழப் பாடல் பெரிதின்னா","எண்ணறியா மாந்தர் ஒழுக்குநாட் கூற்றின்னா","மண்ணின் முழவி னொலியின்னா வாங்கின்னா","தண்மை யிலாளர் பகை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: singing to a lute not set to its mode, men who cannot reckon telling the days for conduct, the sound of a drum without its clay, and the enmity of those without coolness of temper.","n":"Three items are instruments out of true (an untuned lute, a reckoning without number, a drum without its paste) and the fourth is a person out of true; Kural 573 makes the same move from an unfitted melody to an eye without kindness. The second line is read as an unlettered man fixing auspicious days; the sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kannottam","pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 31","lb":"IN31","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பண்ணமையா யாழின்கீழப் பாடல்","singing to a lute not set to its mode is greatly painful",1],["எண்ணறியா மாந்தர் ஒழுக்குநாட் கூற்","men who do not know reckoning declaring the days for conduct is painful",2],["மண்ணின் முழவி னொலி","the sound of a drum without its clay paste is painful",3],["தண்மை யிலாளர் பகை","the enmity of those who lack coolness of temper is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:32":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":33,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் போற்றா தொழுகுத னன்கின்னா","முன்னை யுரையார் புறமொழிக் கூற்றின்னா","நன்மை யிலாளர் தொடர்பின்னா வாங்கின்னா","தொன்மை யுடையார் கெடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: going about without guarding oneself, the backbiting of those who will not speak to one's face, association with those who lack goodness, and the fall of those of ancient standing.","n":"முன்னை உரையார் names the backbiter by what he fails to do, speak in front, so the fault is set against open speech rather than against silence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","inam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 32","lb":"IN32","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்னைத்தான் போற்றா தொழுகுத","going about without guarding oneself is very painful",1],["முன்னை யுரையார் புறமொழிக் கூற்","the behind-the-back talk of those who will not speak to one's face is painful",2],["நன்மை யிலாளர் தொடர்","association with those without goodness is painful",3],["தொன்மை யுடையார் கெடல்","the ruin of those of ancient standing is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:33":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":34,"t":["கள்ளுண்பான் கூறுங் கருமப் பொருளின்னா","முள்ளுடைக் காட்டி னடத்த னனியின்னா","வெள்ளம் படுமாக் கொலையின்னா வாங்கின்னா","கள்ள மனத்தார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the matter of business a drunkard speaks of, walking in a thorny forest, killing a beast caught in a flood, and association with those of deceitful mind.","n":"The third item faults killing an animal at the moment it is helpless in the flood; the pain lies in the unequal kill, not in hunting as such.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","kolai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 33","lb":"IN33","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளுண்பான் கூறுங் கருமப் பொரு","the business matter a drunkard speaks of is painful",1],["முள்ளுடைக் காட்டி னடத்த","walking through a thorny forest is very painful",2],["வெள்ளம் படுமாக் கொலை","killing a beast caught in a flood is painful",3],["கள்ள மனத்தார் தொடர்பு","association with those of deceitful mind is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:34":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":35,"t":["ஒழுக்க மிலாளார்க் குறவுரைத்த லின்னா","விழுத்தகு நூலும் விழையாதார்க் கின்னா","இழித்த தொழிலவர் நட்பின்னா வின்னா","கழிப்புவாய் மண்டிலங் கொட்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: speaking of kinship to those without good conduct, even an excellent book to those who do not want it, friendship with people of despised occupation, and a whirling circuit whose sense is obscure.","n":"இழித்த தொழிலவர் ranks people by occupation, and in this period occupation and birth-group largely coincide; the verse does not name the trade, so the line may rank by birth or by base work. The first line has a variant (குறைவுரைத்தல், telling one's wants), and the fourth line (கழிப்புவாய் மண்டிலங் கொட்பு) cannot be fixed: it may be circling at a place of exorcism or at the mouth of a pit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["ozhukkam","kalvi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 34","lb":"IN34","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒழுக்க மிலாளார்க் குறவுரைத்த","speaking of kinship (or, by a variant, of one's wants) to those without good conduct is painful",1],["விழுத்தகு நூலும் விழையாதார்க்","even an excellent book is painful to those who do not desire it",2],["இழித்த தொழிலவர் நட்","friendship with people of a despised occupation is painful",3],["கழிப்புவாய் மண்டிலங் கொட்பு","whirling round in a circle at the கழிப்பு place is painful; the sense is unclear",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a rank norm of the period: friendship with people of a despised occupation is counted a pain"],"ch":"in:2"},"in:35":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":36,"t":["எழிலி யுறைநீங்கி னீண்டையார்க் கின்னா","குழலி னினிய மரத் தோசைநன் கின்னா","குழவிக ளுற்ற பிணியின்னா வின்னா","அழகுடையான் பேதை யெனல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the cloud ceasing its rain for those who live here, a wooden sound sweeter than the flute, illness that befalls small children, and a handsome man who is a fool.","n":"The second line is obscure: as printed it is the sound of wood that should be sweeter than the flute, perhaps a wooden instrument gone harsh; the variant குழலினிய does not settle it. The fourth line counts beauty as a pain when sense is missing, as 27 does when wealth is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","makkal","noy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 35","lb":"IN35","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["எழிலி யுறைநீங்கி","the rain cloud withholding its rain is painful to those who live here",1],["குழலி னினிய மரத் தோசை","a wooden sound that should be sweeter than the flute is very painful; the sense is unclear",2],["குழவிக ளுற்ற பிணி","illness that comes upon small children is painful",3],["அழகுடையான் பேதை யெனல்","a man of beauty being a fool is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:36":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":37,"t":["பொருளிலான் வேளாண்மை காமுறுத லின்னா","நெடுமாட நீணகர்க் கைத்தின்மை யின்னா","வருமனை பார்த்திருந் தூணின்னா வின்னா","கெடுமிடங் கைவிடுவார் நட்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a man without wealth longing to be liberal, want of money in a city of tall mansions, eating by watching for the houses one may go to, and the friendship of those who desert one at the time of ruin.","n":"The four make a sequence of a falling householder: the wish to give without means, poverty set against a rich city, living off others' doors, and last the friends who leave.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","eegai","natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 36","lb":"IN36","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"householder","it":[["பொருளிலான் வேளாண்மை காமுறுத","a man without wealth desiring to be liberal is painful",1],["நெடுமாட நீணகர்க் கைத்தின்மை","lacking money in a long city of tall mansions is painful",2],["வருமனை பார்த்திருந் தூ","eating by waiting on the houses one may come to is painful",3],["கெடுமிடங் கைவிடுவார் நட்பு","the friendship of those who abandon one at the time of ruin is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:37":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":38,"t":["நறிய மலர்பெரிது நாறாமை யின்னா","துறையறியா னீரிழிந்து போகுத லின்னா","அறியாண் வினாப்படுத லின்னாவாங் கின்னா","சிறியார்மேற் செற்றங் கொளல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: a fragrant flower that gives little scent, a man who does not know the ford going down into the water, an ignorant man being questioned, and holding rancour against the small.","n":"Three items expose a lack (scent, knowledge of the ford, an answer); the fourth turns from lack to cruelty, rancour aimed at those weaker than oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","arivu","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 37","lb":"IN37","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["நறிய மலர்பெரிது நாறாமை","a fragrant flower not giving its full scent is painful",1],["துறையறியா னீரிழிந்து போகுத","one who does not know the ford going down into the water is painful",2],["அறியாண் வினாப்படுத","an ignorant man being put to questions is painful",3],["சிறியார்மேற் செற்றங் கொளல்","holding rancour against the small is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:38":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":39,"t":["பிறர்மனையாள் பின்னோர்க்கும் பேதைமை யின்னா","மறமிலா மன்னர் செருப்புகுத லின்னா","வெறும்புறம் வெம்புரவி யேற்றின்னா வின்னா","திறனிலான் செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: the folly of going after another man's wife, kings without valour entering battle, riding a fierce horse bareback, and work done by one without skill.","n":"The list makes desire for another's wife one of four ventures undertaken without the strength or skill they need, and it uses the Kural's own word for it, பேதைமை (141).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pedhaimai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 38","lb":"IN38","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறர்மனையாள் பின்னோர்க்கும் பேதைமை","the folly of going after another man's wife is painful",1],["மறமிலா மன்னர் செருப்புகுத","kings without valour entering battle is painful",2],["வெறும்புறம் வெம்புரவி யேற்","mounting a fierce horse on its bare back is painful",3],["திறனிலான் செய்யும் வினை","work done by one without skill is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:39":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":40,"t":["கொடுக்கும் பொருளில்லான் வள்ளன்மை யின்னா","கடித்தமைந்த பாக்கினுட் கற்படுத லின்னா","கொடுத்த விடாமை கவிக்கின்னா வின்னா","மடுத்துழிப் பாடா விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: liberality in one who has nothing to give, a stone in the areca nut one has bitten, a poet not being sent off by the one who gave to him, and a poet not singing where he has been received.","n":"The last two lines look at patronage from both sides: the patron who holds the poet back (கொடுத்த விடாமை, tamilvu கொடுத்து விடாமை) and the poet who does not sing when he is taken in. The reading of the third item turns on that printed form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","parisil","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 39","lb":"IN39","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["கொடுக்கும் பொருளில்லான் வள்ளன்மை","liberality in one with nothing to give is painful",1],["கடித்தமைந்த பாக்கினுட் கற்படுத","a stone turning up in the areca nut one has bitten is painful",2],["கொடுத்த விடாமை","for a poet, not being let go by the one who gives is painful",3],["மடுத்துழிப் பாடா விடல்","a poet failing to sing where he has been received is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"in:40":{"book":"innaa_narpathu","num":41,"t":["அடக்க முடையவன் மீளிமை யின்னா","துடக்க மிலாதவன் றற்செருக் கின்னா","அடைக்கலம் வவ்வுத லின்னாவாங் கின்னா","அடக்க வடங்காதார் சொல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse counts four painful things: fierce daring in a man of restraint, self-conceit in one without standing, seizing what was left in trust, and the speech of those who will not be checked when checked.","n":"அடக்கம் opens and closes the list: in item 1 the restrained man's martial boldness (மீளிமை) is a pain, perhaps because it is out of his nature or because it is terrible when roused; in item 4 the unrestrainable talker. துடக்கம் in item 2 is read as standing or footing; it may also mean a tie.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","adaikkalam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Innaa Narpathu 40","lb":"IN40","poet":"கபிலர்","dm":"all","it":[["அடக்க முடையவன் மீளிமை","fierce daring in one who has restraint is painful",1],["துடக்க மிலாதவன் றற்செருக்","self-conceit in one without standing is painful",2],["அடைக்கலம் வவ்வுத","seizing a deposit left in one's trust is painful",3],["அடக்க வடங்காதார் சொல்","the speech of those who do not subside when checked is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"in:2"},"mk:1":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":1,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","ஓதலிற் சிறந்தன்று ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை"],"p":"","r":"The opening saying ranks the possession of right conduct above recitation of learning.","n":"Line 1 (ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம், 'for all people of the sea-roaring world') is the frame repeated at the head of every ten, so the book's first ranking is addressed to everyone, not to one birth or order. ஓதல் is recitation, learning by repeating a text, which is what conduct is set against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 1","lb":"MK1","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை","having right conduct excels study by recitation",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:2":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":2,"t":["காதலிற் சிறந்தன்று கண்ணஞ்சப் படுதல்"],"p":"","r":"Being held in awe by others' eyes is ranked above being loved.","n":"கண்ணஞ்சப் படுதல் is literally 'being feared by the eye': most plainly, being looked on with respectful fear. It could also mean being shrunk from in sight, which would make the saying harsher. The saying names no ruler; the frame addresses everyone, so the awe here is a standing a person holds, not only a king's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","anbu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 2","lb":"MK2","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணஞ்சப் படுதல்","being regarded with awe excels being held in affection",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:3":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":3,"t":["மேதையிற் சிறந்தன்று கற்றது மறவாமை"],"p":"","r":"Not forgetting what one has learnt is ranked above native brilliance.","n":"மேதை is quickness of mind one has without effort; the saying puts a discipline (keeping what was learnt) above a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 3","lb":"MK3","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றது மறவாமை","retaining what one has learnt excels sharp native intelligence",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:4":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":4,"t":["வண்மையிற் சிறந்தன்று வாய்மை உடைமை"],"p":"","r":"Truthfulness is ranked above liberality in giving.","n":"வண்மை, open-handedness, is the virtue the bardic poems praise most in a patron; the saying sets a virtue of speech above it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 4","lb":"MK4","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாய்மை உடைமை","possessing truthfulness excels generosity",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:5":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":5,"t":["இளமையிற் சிறந்தன்று மெய்பிணி யின்மை"],"p":"","r":"Freedom from bodily disease is ranked above youth.","n":"The saying separates two goods usually counted together: youth without health is not the better thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","ilamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 5","lb":"MK5","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மெய்பிணி யின்மை","being free of bodily disease excels being young",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:6":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":6,"t":["நலனுடை மையின் நாணுச் சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"A sense of shame is ranked above the possession of beauty or good qualities.","n":"நலனுடைமை can be bodily beauty or excellence in general; either way an outward or possessed good is set below an inner restraint. The saying names no gender.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 6","lb":"MK6","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நாணுச்","a sense of shame excels having beauty or good qualities",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:7":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":7,"t":["குலனுடை மையின் கற்புச் சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"The saying ranks chastity above the possession of a good lineage.","n":"The saying does not name a woman, but கற்பு in this literature is chiefly the wife's virtue, so the ranking reports a norm for women. It also demotes birth: lineage is outranked by conduct, as conduct outranks study in saying 1.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 7","lb":"MK7","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கற்புச்","chastity excels being of good lineage",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a norm of the period ranking a woman's chastity above her family's standing"],"ch":"mk:1"},"mk:8":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":8,"t":["கற்றலின் கற்றாரை வழிபடுதல் சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Serving and honouring the learned is ranked above learning itself.","n":"வழிபடுதல் is the word for worship and service; the learned are given the reverence due to a deity or teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 8","lb":"MK8","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றாரை வழிபடுதல்","revering and attending on the learned excels one's own learning",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:9":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":9,"t":["செற்றாரைச் செறுத்தலின் கற்செய்கை சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"The saying ranks கற்செய்கை above subduing those who hate one; the ranked thing is most plausibly the practice of learning, against revenge on enemies.","n":"கற்செய்கை is unclear as written: கல் may be learning (கல்-, to learn) or stone, giving 'deeds of firmness'. Either way something built in oneself is set above striking down a foe; the second reading of the word cannot be fixed from the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 9","lb":"MK9","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்செய்கை","the practice the saying calls கற்செய்கை excels crushing one's enemies",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:10":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":10,"t":["முற்பெரு கலின்பின் சிறுகாமை சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Not dwindling afterwards is ranked above swelling greatly at the start.","n":"The saying names no subject (wealth, friendship, fortune); it is a rule about any course, valuing what holds over what begins large.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 10","lb":"MK10","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சிறுகாமை","not diminishing later excels growing large at first",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:1"},"mk:11":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":11,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","பேரில் பிறந்தமை ஈரத்தின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Birth into a great house is said to be recognised by the kindness, the moistness of heart, a person shows.","n":"Line 1 is the frame that heads every ten; the saying is line 2. ஈரம், literally moisture, is the softness of heart that the next line ties to giving, so the two lines make a chain. பேரில் (பேர் இல், a great house) ranks families, but the test it names is conduct, not descent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","kudi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 11","lb":"MK11","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பேரில் பிறந்தமை","birth in a great house is known by one's kindness",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a claim of the period that a great house of birth shows itself in the conduct of those born to it"],"ch":"mk:2"},"mk:12":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":12,"t":["ஈரம் உடைமை ஈகையின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Possessing kindness is said to be recognised by one's giving.","n":"The inner quality is read only through the outward act: a feeling that does not give is not counted as kindness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 12","lb":"MK12","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஈரம் உடைமை","the possession of kindness is known by giving",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:13":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":13,"t":["சேரா நல்நட்(பு) உதவியின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"A good friendship that does not fail is said to be recognised by the help given in it.","n":"Our text reads சேரா, 'not joined'; tamilvu reads சோரா, 'not slackening', which gives the plain sense followed here. With சேரா the line would speak of the good friendship of those not joined by kin, which is possible but strained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["natpu","velanmai","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 13","lb":"MK13","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சேரா நல்நட்(பு)","an unfailing good friendship is known by the help it gives",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:14":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":14,"t":["கற்றது உடைமை காட்சியின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Having learned is said to be recognised by one's power of seeing, the discernment learning gives.","n":"காட்சி can be insight or judgment, and less likely outward bearing; on either reading learning is proved by what the learner perceives, not by what he recites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 14","lb":"MK14","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றது உடைமை","having learned is known by one's discernment",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:15":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":15,"t":["ஏற்ற முடைமை எதிர்கொளின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Eminence is said to be recognised by the way one goes forward to meet and receive others, or by how one meets an encounter.","n":"எதிர்கொளின் (tamilvu எதிர்கோளின்) is either the courteous going out to receive a guest or superior, or the facing of an opponent or trial. The first fits the ten's run of social virtues; the line does not decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["perumai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 15","lb":"MK15","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஏற்ற முடைமை","eminence is known by one's going forward to receive, or by one's meeting of an encounter",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:16":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":16,"t":["சிற்றில் பிறந்தமை பெருமிதத்தின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Birth in a small house is said to be recognised by swagger and pride.","n":"This is the mirror of line 11: the great house shows in kindness, the small house in பெருமிதம். சிற்றில் may mean a mean or poor family rather than a birth-group; either way the line reads a family's rank from a fault of conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","agandhai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 16","lb":"MK16","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சிற்றில் பிறந்தமை","birth in a small house is known by arrogance",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a claim of the period that low birth shows in arrogant conduct"],"ch":"mk:2"},"mk:17":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":17,"t":["குத்திரம் செய்தலின் கள்வனாதல் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Being a thief is said to be recognised from the doing of trickery.","n":"குத்திரம் is fraud or a crooked trick, not theft itself: the line calls a man a thief from the deceit in his acts before any goods are taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 17","lb":"MK17","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்வனாதல்","being a thief is known by working deceit",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:18":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":18,"t":["சொற்சோர்வு உடைமையின் எச்சோர்வும் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"From a lapse in speech, every other lapse a person has is said to be recognised.","n":"Here the order of the ten turns: what is known (எச்சோர்வும், every lapse) comes after its sign. The item is the thing known, read with the sign சொற்சோர்வு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","pochchaappu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 18","lb":"MK18","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["எச்சோர்வும்","every lapse is known from a slip in speech",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:19":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":19,"t":["அறிவுசோர்வு உடைமையின் பிறிதுசோர்வும் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"From a lapse in understanding, the other lapses too are said to be recognised.","n":"Built on the same frame as line 18, it moves the telling lapse from the tongue to the mind; the two lines together make speech and understanding the two places where all failing shows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pochchaappu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 19","lb":"MK19","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறிதுசோர்வும்","other lapses are known from a lapse of understanding",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:20":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":20,"t":["சீருடை யாண்மை செய்கையின் அறிப"],"p":"","r":"Excellent manly ability is said to be recognised by what one actually does.","n":"The ten closes on deeds: after birth, kindness, friendship and learning, the last test is performance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 20","lb":"MK20","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சீருடை யாண்மை","excellent manly ability is known by deeds",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:2"},"mk:21":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":21,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","யாப்பி லோரை இயல்குணம் பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not fault those who have no binding discipline for the nature they were born with.","n":"யாப்பு is literally a binding or tie; here most likely the binding of training or restraint, so the native temper of the untrained is excused as nature, not chosen fault. It could also mean those with no tie of kin or allegiance. Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten and is printed with this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","panpu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 21","lb":"MK21","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["யாப்பி லோரை இயல்குணம்","the inborn disposition of those who lack restraint is not held against them",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:22":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":22,"t":["மீப்பி லோரை மீக்குணம் பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not fault those of no eminence for lacking the qualities of the eminent.","n":"The line mirrors 21: a lack (மீப்பு இல்லோர்) and a quality (மீக்குணம்). மீக்குணம் can be the higher virtues one should not demand of the lowly, or an overbearing, upward-reaching temper one should not grudge them; a third reading takes மீப்பு இல்லோர் as those with no one above them, whose haughtiness is not blamed. The sense cannot be fixed from the line alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","perumai","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 22","lb":"MK22","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மீப்பி லோரை மீக்குணம்","those without high standing are not blamed with respect to lofty qualities",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:23":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":23,"t":["பெருமை உடையதன் அருமை பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not complain that a great thing is hard to come by.","n":"23 and 24 are a chiasm: பெருமை and அருமை change places, so greatness and rarity are made to entail each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 23","lb":"MK23","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருமை உடையதன் அருமை","the difficulty of what has greatness is not a ground of blame",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:24":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":24,"t":["அருமை யுடையதன் பெருமை பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not grudge the high esteem in which a rare thing is held.","n":"Read with 23: the price of a rare thing and the difficulty of a great one are both accepted as following from what the thing is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 24","lb":"MK24","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["அருமை யுடையதன் பெருமை","the greatness ascribed to what is rare is not blamed",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:25":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":25,"t":["நிறையச் செய்யாக் குறைவினை பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not blame a deed that falls short because it could not be carried through in full.","n":"குறைவினை can be a work left short or a small task; நிறையச் செய்யா says only that it was not done fully, not why. The line excuses the shortfall, not idleness, on the most natural reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 25","lb":"MK25","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நிறையச் செய்யாக் குறைவினை","a deficient work left uncompleted is not censured",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:26":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":26,"t":["முறையில் அரசர்நாட் டிருந்து பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"Those who live in the country of a king who keeps no justice do not openly censure him.","n":"இருந்து is a participle attached to the predicate, so the most natural reading is prudence: one who lives there does not speak against the king. Another reading is that people are not blamed for staying on in such a land. Either way the line counsels the subject, not the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","pazhi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 26","lb":"MK26","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["முறையில் அரசர்நாட் டிருந்து","dwelling in an unjust king's land, one does not voice blame",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:27":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":27,"t":["செய்தக்க நற்கேளிர் செய்யாமை பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not blame good kinsfolk for failing to do what they ought to have done.","n":"The excuse is for good kin (நற்கேளிர்) and for omission (செய்யாமை), not for harm done; the adjective limits the forbearance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","porai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 27","lb":"MK27","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["செய்தக்க நற்கேளிர் செய்யாமை","the omission of what good kin should have done is not held against them",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:28":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":28,"t":["அறியாத் தேசத்து ஆசாரம் பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not find fault with the customary conduct of a country they do not know.","n":"ஆசாரம் is the same word the Acharakkovai uses for its whole code of conduct; here it is tied to a தேசம் and so allowed to differ from place to place. அறியா may mean a land one does not know or whose ways one does not understand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","pazhi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 28","lb":"MK28","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["அறியாத் தேசத்து ஆசாரம்","the rule-bound conduct of an unfamiliar land is not condemned",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:29":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":29,"t":["வறியோன் வள்ளியன் அன்மை பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"People of sense do not blame a poor man for not being open-handed.","n":"The line sets the limit of others' judgment, not the poor man's own duty; it says nothing on whether he should still try to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","eegai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 29","lb":"MK29","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வறியோன் வள்ளியன் அன்மை","a poor man's failure to be generous is not a fault charged to him",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:30":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":30,"t":["சிறியோர் ஒழுக்கம் சிறந்தோரும் பழியார்"],"p":"","r":"Even the most excellent do not blame the conduct of small people.","n":"The உம் in சிறந்தோரும் marks the eminent as the ones most entitled to blame, and so the ones whose restraint matters. சிறியோர் may be the young or the mean in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","porai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 30","lb":"MK30","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சிறியோர் ஒழுக்கம்","the conduct of the small is not censured even by those who excel",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:3"},"mk:31":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":31,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","பழியோர் செல்வம் வறுமையில் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"The wealth of those who carry blame is reckoned no better than poverty.","n":"Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten, so the saying of 31 is line 2 alone. துவ்வாது is read through the ten as 'does not rise above'; the verb's plain sense is to eat or enjoy, so the wealth that is 'no better than poverty' is also wealth that cannot really be enjoyed. Here the comparison word is வறுமையில், with -இல், where the other nine have -இன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pazhi","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 31","lb":"MK31","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பழியோர் செல்வம்","the wealth of the blameworthy is no better than want",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:32":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":32,"t":["கழிதறு கண்மை பேடியின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Reckless daring that goes beyond measure is reckoned no better than the state of a பேடி.","n":"The printed கழிதறு கண்மை is கழி + தறுகண்மை split by the line's spacing: not daring itself but daring in excess. பேடி is a gender word for one neither man nor woman, used in the period as the type of the man without courage; the saying turns that type against the over-bold, so the two extremes meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 32","lb":"MK32","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கழிதறு கண்மை","excessive daring is no better than being a பேடி",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:33":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":33,"t":["நாணில் வாழ்க்கை பசித்தலின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"A life without shame is reckoned no better than going hungry.","n":"The measure is bodily want: whatever such a life has to eat, it is counted as a hungry one, which is the force of துவ்வாது (it does not eat or enjoy).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 33","lb":"MK33","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நாணில் வாழ்க்கை","a shameless life is no better than hunger",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:34":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":34,"t":["பேணில் ஈகை மாற்றலின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Giving done without care for the receiver is reckoned no better than refusing.","n":"பேணில் is read as பேணு + இல், giving without cherishing or honouring the one who receives; the manner of the gift cancels the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 34","lb":"MK34","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பேணில் ஈகை","a gift given without regard is no better than a refusal",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:35":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":35,"t":["செய்யாமை மேற்கோள் சிதடியின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Taking a task upon oneself and not doing it is reckoned no better than being a dullard.","n":"செய்யாமை மேற்கோள் is read as an undertaking (மேற்கோள்) marked by not-doing; it could also be read as taking up the vow of not acting, which would make it idleness rather than a broken undertaking. சிதடி means a fool and also one who is blind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 35","lb":"MK35","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["செய்யாமை மேற்கோள்","an undertaking left undone is no better than stupidity",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:36":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":36,"t":["பொய்வே ளாண்மை புலைமையின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"False benevolence, help that is only a show, is reckoned no better than baseness.","n":"பொய்வே ளாண்மை is பொய் + வேளாண்மை split by the spacing. The measure புலைமை is formed from புலை, a birth-group term of the period that ranks people as low; here it names baseness as a quality, and the saying states no rule about persons, but the word's caste sense lies under the comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velanmai","vaaimai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 36","lb":"MK36","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்வே ளாண்மை","pretended helpfulness is no better than baseness",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:37":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":37,"t":["கொண்டுகண் மாறல் கொடுமையின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Turning one's regard away from those one has taken from is reckoned no better than cruelty.","n":"கொண்டு may mean having received a benefit or having taken someone as one's own (a friend, a dependant); either way கண்மாறல், turning the eye away, names the breach of a bond already accepted, which is ingratitude rather than mere unkindness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","kannottam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 37","lb":"MK37","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொண்டுகண் மாறல்","to receive and then withdraw one's regard is no better than cruelty",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:38":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":38,"t":["அறிவிலி துணைப்பாடு தனிமையின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"The company and support of a fool is reckoned no better than being alone.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","thunai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 38","lb":"MK38","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["அறிவிலி துணைப்பாடு","a fool's support is no better than solitude",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:39":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":39,"t":["இழிவுடை மூப்புக் கதத்தின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Old age that has fallen into disgrace is reckoned no better than anger, on the most direct reading of கதம்.","n":"கதம் most often means anger or heat of temper, and the comparison of a disgraced old age to anger is hard to see; the word may carry another sense here (a sting, a hurt), so the measure of this saying cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 39","lb":"MK39","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இழிவுடை மூப்பு","a degraded old age is no better than கதம்",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:40":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":40,"t":["தானோர் இன்புறல் தனிமையின் துவ்வாது"],"p":"","r":"Enjoying pleasure by oneself alone is reckoned no better than solitude.","n":"The same measure, தனிமை, closes 38: unshared joy is counted as the very loneliness the solitary man suffers, so pleasure kept to oneself is no pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 40","lb":"MK40","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["தானோர் இன்புறல்","enjoying pleasure alone is no better than loneliness",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:4"},"mk:41":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":41,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","நீரறிந்து ஒழுகாதான் தாரம் அல்லன்"],"p":"","r":"One who does not conduct himself with knowledge of the other's nature is said not to be a spouse.","n":"தாரம் ordinarily names a wife, yet the participle and predicate are masculine (ஒழுகாதான், அல்லன்). Either the line, as written, applies the word for a spouse to the husband who does not know his wife's nature, or the masculine endings stand for an older feminine reading (the woman who does not act knowing her husband's nature is no wife). The masculine reading is followed here because it is what the text has. நீர், 'nature', names whose nature only by context. Line 1 is the frame line of the ten.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 41","lb":"MK41","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நீரறிந்து ஒழுகாதான்","one who acts without knowing the other's nature is not a true spouse",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period on what makes a spouse: conduct fitted to the partner's nature; if the line is read as feminine it is a rule for a wife's conduct"],"ch":"mk:5"},"mk:42":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":42,"t":["தார(ம்)மா ணாதது வாழ்க்கை யன்று"],"p":"","r":"A household life whose wife is not excellent is said not to be a household life at all.","n":"The saying puts the whole worth of வாழ்க்கை, the life of the house, on the wife's மாண்பு; it says nothing of the husband here, though the line before it, read as written, set a condition on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 42","lb":"MK42","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தார(ம்)மா ணாதது","a household whose wife lacks excellence is no real household life",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a norm of the period that measures the household by the wife's excellence"],"ch":"mk:5"},"mk:43":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":43,"t":["ஈரலில் லாதது கிளைநட் பன்று"],"p":"","r":"Kin-friendship that has no moistness of heart is said not to be kin-friendship.","n":"கிளைநட்பு joins the kin-group (கிளை) and friendship (நட்பு): the saying holds that relation by blood does not by itself make the bond; ஈரம், wetness, the tenderness of heart, does. ஈரலில் is taken as ஈரம் இல் with an unusual spelling; ஈரல் by itself would be the liver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","natpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 43","lb":"MK43","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஈரலில் லாதது","a tie with kin that lacks tenderness is not truly a bond of kin",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:44":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":44,"t":["சோராக் கையன் சொன்மலை யல்லன்"],"p":"","r":"A man whose hand never loosens to give is said not to be one whose name stands high like a mountain.","n":"சோராக் கை is a hand that does not slacken or let go, taken here as the closed hand of the miser, since the ten names defective things. சொன்மலை is read as சொல் + மலை, 'a mountain of word', i.e. great renown; it could also mean one whose word stands firm like a mountain, which would make the saying about the miser's promises. Either way the closed hand forfeits a standing of the word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","karavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 44","lb":"MK44","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சோராக் கையன்","the tight-fisted man is not one whose renown is great",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:45":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":45,"t":["நேரா நெஞ்சத்தோன் நட்டோன் அல்லன்"],"p":"","r":"One whose heart does not agree with one is said not to be a friend.","n":"நேர்தல் is to agree, consent, come straight; நேரா நெஞ்சம் is a heart that has not come into accord, whatever the outward dealings. The verb returns in 46 and, as நேர்லா, in 49, so three sayings in the ten turn on consent or undertaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 45","lb":"MK45","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நேரா நெஞ்சத்தோன்","one whose heart does not consent to the bond is no friend",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:46":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":46,"t":["நேராமற் கற்றது கல்வி யன்று"],"p":"","r":"What is learnt without submitting or consenting to the learning is said not to be learning.","n":"நேராமல் may mean without the heart's assent, without submitting to the teacher, or without the formal undertaking (or fee) by which a pupil is accepted. All three make learning depend on a relation entered into, not on the content gained; the line does not fix which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 46","lb":"MK46","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நேராமற் கற்றது","learning taken in without assent or submission is not real learning",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:47":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":47,"t":["வாழாமல் வருந்தியது வருத்தம் அன்று"],"p":"","r":"Strain endured without coming to live well by it is said not to count as strain.","n":"வருத்தம் here is taken as the labour of effort, measured by whether one comes to live (வாழ்தல்) by it. A second reading is open: grief suffered by one who has not lived rightly is not true suffering, i.e. it is deserved. The line does not decide between them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","vinai","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 47","lb":"MK47","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாழாமல் வருந்தியது","toil that does not bring one to thrive is not reckoned as real effort",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:48":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":48,"t":["அறத்தாற்றின் ஈயாத(து) ஈனை யன்று"],"p":"","r":"What is not given along the way of aram is said not to be giving.","n":"ஈனை is read as giving (ஈதல், ஈகை). The test is not the size of the gift but the road it travels, அறத்தாறு; the saying does not say what that road requires. The bracketed (து) is an editorial supply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 48","lb":"MK48","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["அறத்தாற்றின் ஈயாத(து)","a gift not made along the path of dharma is not true giving",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:49":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":49,"t":["திறத்தாற்றின் நேர்லா ததுநோன் பன்று"],"p":"","r":"What is not undertaken in the proper manner is said not to be a vow of penance.","n":"நோன்பு is a vow or observance of restraint. The line is built as a twin of 48: அறத்தாற்றின் / திறத்தாற்றின், giving judged by aram's road, penance by its own proper road (திறம்). நேர்லா is read as a form of நேர்தல், to undertake, so the penance is defined by the manner in which it is entered on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thitpam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 49","lb":"MK49","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["திறத்தாற்றின் நேர்லா","an observance not taken up in its proper way is no true penance",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:50":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":50,"t":["மறுபிறப் பறியா ததுமூப் பன்று"],"p":"","r":"Age that does not know of the birth to come is said not to be true old age.","n":"மூப்பு is ordinarily just age; the saying makes it a state reached by knowing, not by years. மறுபிறப்பு assumes rebirth as a thing to be known. The ten ends where 39 in the previous ten also judged old age, there by its disgrace, here by its ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 50","lb":"MK50","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மறுபிறப் பறியா","an old age that has no awareness of rebirth is not real elderhood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:5"},"mk:51":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":51,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","மக்கட் பேற்றின் பெறும்பே(று) இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"Of all gains a person can have, none is said to exceed the gain of children.","n":"Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten, so the saying of 51 is line 2 alone. The line is unconditional: it says children, not good or wise children, which is where it parts from Kural 61 and Innaa Narpathu 29.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 51","lb":"MK51","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மக்கட் பேற்றின்","the getting of children is the greatest of gains",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:52":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":52,"t":["ஒப்புரவு அறிதலின் தகுவரவு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No acquisition is said to be more fitting than knowing one's duty of ஒப்புரவு to the world.","n":"The saying ranks the knowing (அறிதல்), not only the doing, and calls it a தகுவரவு, a fitting thing to come by: the duty is counted as a gain to the one who knows it, not a cost. வரவு as 'gain, what comes in' is our reading; it can also mean simply an arrival or attainment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 52","lb":"MK52","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒப்புரவு அறிதலின்","knowing the duty of ஒப்புரவு is the most fitting thing to come by",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:53":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":53,"t":["வாய்ப்புடை விழைச்சின் நல்விழைச்சு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No desire is said to be better than a desire that can fittingly be fulfilled.","n":"வாய்ப்புடை can mean 'having the chance of coming to pass' or 'fitting, proper'; on the first the line praises realistic desire, on the second lawful desire. 53 and 54 are a matched pair, the same word with and without its negative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 53","lb":"MK53","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாய்ப்புடை விழைச்சின்","a desire that can come to pass is the best of desires",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:54":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":54,"t":["வாயா விழைச்சின் தீவிழைச்சு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No desire is said to be worse than a desire that cannot come to pass.","n":"The fault named is not the object of the desire but its futility: வாயா, 'that does not come about', makes wanting the unattainable the worst kind of wanting. If வாயா is read as 'unfitting', the line instead condemns improper desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 54","lb":"MK54","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாயா விழைச்சின்","a desire that cannot be fulfilled is the worst of desires",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:55":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":55,"t":["இயைவது கரத்தலின் கொடுமை இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No cruelty is said to exceed hiding from others what one is able to give.","n":"Refusal is classed as கொடுமை, cruelty, an active harm, not merely as a want of generosity; and the measure is இயைவது, what one can manage, so the poor man who cannot give is not touched by it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 55","lb":"MK55","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இயைவது கரத்தலின்","concealing what one could give is the greatest cruelty",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:56":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":56,"t":["உணர்விலன் ஆதலின் சாக்காடு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No death is said to be worse than being a person without understanding.","n":"Bodily death is outranked by a living state: the senseless man is already dead in the sense that matters. உணர்வு may be taken narrowly as moral awareness or broadly as understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","saakkaadu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 56","lb":"MK56","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உணர்விலன் ஆதலின்","being without understanding is the worst death",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:57":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":57,"t":["நசையில் பெரியதோர் நல்குரவு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No poverty is said to be greater than desire.","n":"Poverty is moved from what one has to what one wants: a man of means who still craves is, on this saying, the poorest of all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 57","lb":"MK57","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நசையில்","desire is the greatest poverty",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:58":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":58,"t":["இசையின் பெரியதோர் எச்ச மில்லை"],"p":"","r":"No legacy is said to be greater than renown.","n":"எச்சம் is what a person leaves behind, and the word also means offspring; read against 51, the ten gives children the first place among gains in life and fame the first place among what outlasts it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 58","lb":"MK58","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இசையின்","renown is the greatest thing one leaves behind",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:59":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":59,"t":["இரத்தலின் ஊஉங்கு இளிவரவு இல்லை"],"p":"","r":"No disgrace is said to go beyond that of begging.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 59","lb":"MK59","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இரத்தலின்","asking of others is the deepest disgrace",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:60":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":60,"t":["இரப்போர்க்கு ஈதலின் எய்தும் சிறப்பில்லை"],"p":"","r":"No distinction a person can reach is said to exceed that of giving to those who ask.","n":"The ten closes on the counterpart of 59: the same act of asking is the asker's worst disgrace and the giver's highest distinction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 60","lb":"MK60","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இரப்போர்க்கு ஈதலின்","giving to those who ask is the highest distinction one attains",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:6"},"mk:61":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":61,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","பேரறிவி னோன்இனிது வாழா மைபொய்"],"p":"","r":"That a person of great understanding should fail to live well is called false.","n":"Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten, so the saying of 61 is line 2 alone. Every saying of this ten has the shape 'for such a person, such a thing is a lie': a state or wish is denied to a type of person, and the ten works by naming what the person lacks or has. Here the denial runs the other way from the rest: great understanding rules out a bad life, where the other nine name a lack that rules out a good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 61","lb":"MK61","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பேரறிவி னோன்இனிது வாழா மை","a man of great understanding failing to live well is a falsehood, a thing that does not happen",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:62":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":62,"t":["பெருஞ்சீர் ஒன்றன் வெகுளியின் மைபொய்"],"p":"","r":"On the most direct reading of a doubtful line, that one of great eminence should be without anger is called false.","n":"ஒன்றன் makes no clear sense as printed; the ten's pattern wants a person-noun (one of great eminence, perhaps பெருஞ்சீரோன் with தன்), and the reading assumes that. Read so, the line does not praise anger: it says the eminent are never in fact free of it, which may be a warning about the powerful rather than a licence. If ஒன்றன் is kept as 'of one thing', the sense cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 62","lb":"MK62","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருஞ்சீர் ஒன்றன் வெகுளியின் மை","a man of great eminence being without anger is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:63":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":63,"t":["கள்ளுண் போன்சோர்வு இன்மை பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That a drinker of toddy should be free of lapses is called false.","n":"சோர்வு is a slip or a letting go, of attention, of speech or of a secret; the line does not say drink makes a man wicked, only that it makes him unreliable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 63","lb":"MK63","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளுண் போன்சோர்வு இன்மை","a toddy-drinker being free of lapses is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:64":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":64,"t":["காலம்அறி யாதோன் கையுறல் பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who does not know the right time should get what he aims at into his hand is called false.","n":"கையுறல் is 'coming into the hand'; the failure the line names is not of effort or strength but of timing alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 64","lb":"MK64","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["காலம்அறி யாதோன் கையுறல்","success coming into the hand of one who does not know the time is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:65":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":65,"t":["மேல்வரவு அறியாதோன் தற்காத் தல்பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who does not know what is coming should be able to guard himself is called false.","n":"மேல்வரவு is literally 'what comes upon one' or 'what comes later'; the line makes knowing it the condition of any self-protection, so guarding is first a matter of foresight, not of defences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 65","lb":"MK65","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மேல்வரவு அறியாதோன் தற்காத் தல்","self-protection by one who does not know what is coming is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:66":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":66,"t":["உறுவினை காய்வோன் உயர்வுவேண் டல்பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who chafes against the work or trouble that falls to him should seek to rise is called false.","n":"உறுவினை can be the task in hand or what one's past deeds bring upon one; காய்வோன் is one who resents or burns against it. On either reading the line ties rising to accepting what falls to one, but whether that is the labour of the work or the burden of fate the words do not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","perumai","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 66","lb":"MK66","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உறுவினை காய்வோன் உயர்வுவேண் டல்","one who resents what falls to him wanting to rise is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:67":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":67,"t":["சிறுமைநோ னாதோன் பெருமைவேண் டல்பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who will not bear lowliness should seek greatness is called false.","n":"நோனாதோன் is one who does not endure: the line makes the patient bearing of small estate the way to great estate, not its opposite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 67","lb":"MK67","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சிறுமைநோ னாதோன் பெருமைவேண் டல்","one who cannot bear lowliness wanting greatness is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:68":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":68,"t":["பெருமைநோ னாதோன் சிறுமைவேண் டல்பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who will not bear greatness should seek lowliness is called false, the exact mirror of 67.","n":"The line swaps the two words of 67, and its sense is less plain. It may mean that one who cannot carry high station has no real choice of humility, since only the great can stoop by choice; or that one who cannot bear another's greatness wishes him low, and that wish is vain. The first keeps the ten's pattern of a wish denied by a lack; the second reads சிறுமை as the other's fall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 68","lb":"MK68","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருமைநோ னாதோன் சிறுமைவேண் டல்","one who cannot bear greatness choosing lowliness is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:69":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":69,"t":["பொருள்நசை வேட்கையோன் முறைசெயல் பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one with a craving for wealth should do justice is called false.","n":"முறைசெயல் is the doing of justice, the king's or the judge's work; பொருள்நசை வேட்கை doubles the word for desire (நசை, வேட்கை), so the fault named is not having wealth but hunger for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","naduvu","avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 69","lb":"MK69","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"king","it":[["பொருள்நசை வேட்கையோன் முறைசெயல்","one who hungers for wealth doing justice is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:70":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":70,"t":["வாலியன் அல்லாதோன் தவம்செய் தல்பொய்"],"p":"","r":"That one who is not pure should perform penance is called false.","n":"வாலியன் is the clean or pure one; the line does not say whether the purity is of mind or of body, but nothing in it points to a ritual rule, and in a ten about inner lacks it reads most naturally as purity of mind. The penance of the impure is not called useless but false, a show.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 70","lb":"MK70","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["வாலியன் அல்லாதோன் தவம்செய் தல்","penance done by one who is not pure is a falsehood",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:7"},"mk:71":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":71,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","புகழ்வெய் யோர்க்குப் புத்தேள்நா(டு) எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who long for fame, the world of the gods is easily reached.","n":"Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten, so the saying of 71 is line 2 alone. The ten's pattern is a dative of the one who desires (வெய்யோர்க்கு) and a thing that comes easily (எளிது) to that desire; here the desire is a good one and its yield is heaven, so the ten opens on a reward before it turns to the harms a desire brings on itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 71","lb":"MK71","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["புத்தேள்நா(டு)","the land of the gods comes easily to those who desire fame",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:72":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":72,"t":["உறழ்வெய் யோருக்கு உறுசெரு எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who love to contend, a fierce battle comes easily.","n":"உறழ் is to vie or oppose. எளிது can be read two ways here: the contentious man finds battle easy to face, or battle easily falls to his lot. On either reading the battle is the yield of the appetite for rivalry, not a choice made on its merits. The line is not addressed to the king or soldier as such: it names a temper anyone may have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 72","lb":"MK72","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உறுசெரு","a hard battle is readily come by for the lover of contention",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:73":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":73,"t":["ஈரம்வெய் யோர்க்கு நசைகொடை எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who love kindness, giving what is asked for comes easily.","n":"ஈரம் is moistness of heart, the same word the second ten uses for the kindness that giving reveals (12). நசைகொடை is read as a gift answering desire (நசை), the asker's wish; the line places the ease of giving in the giver's disposition, not in the size of his means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 73","lb":"MK73","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நசைகொடை","giving to the wish of the asker comes easily to those who love kindness",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:74":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":74,"t":["குறளைவெய் யோர்க்கு மறைவிரி எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who love tale-bearing, spreading abroad what was secret comes easily.","n":"குறளை is malicious talk carried behind a person's back. மறைவிரி is a compound, the opening out of what was hidden; the line makes the betrayal of confidences the easy consequence of a taste for gossip, so the one who enjoys slander is no safe keeper of secrets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 74","lb":"MK74","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மறைவிரி","laying open a secret comes easily to those who relish tale-bearing",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:75":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":75,"t":["துன்பம்வெய் யோர்க்கு இன்பம் எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who willingly take on hardship, pleasure comes easily.","n":"Read with 76, which reverses its two words: the pair is a chiasmus in which each object of desire turns into its opposite. வெய்யோர் with துன்பம் is odd if taken as 'love suffering'; the sense is those who choose or embrace hardship, toil or austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","inbam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 75","lb":"MK75","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இன்பம்","pleasure is easily had by those who accept hardship",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:76":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":76,"t":["இன்பம்வெய் யோர்க்குத் துன்பம் எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who crave pleasure, pain comes easily.","n":"The mirror of 75. The line blames the wanting (வெய்யோர்), not pleasure itself: pain is what the craving draws on, not something hidden in every pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","idumbai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 76","lb":"MK76","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["துன்பம்","pain readily befalls those who crave pleasure",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:77":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":77,"t":["உண்டிவெய் யோர்க்குப் உறுபிணி எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who love food, serious disease comes easily.","n":"உண்டிவெய்யோர் is the lover of food, the glutton; the line does not say how much is too much, only that the appetite itself brings disease near. The printed யோர்க்குப் before a vowel is irregular (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 77","lb":"MK77","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உறுபிணி","grave disease easily befalls those who love eating",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:78":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":78,"t":["பெண்டிர்வெய் யோர்க்குப் படுபழி எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For men who lust after women, grievous blame comes easily.","n":"பெண்டிர் is plural and unqualified: the line does not say 'another's wife', so the blame is laid on a roving appetite for women in general rather than on one forbidden act. The audience is male; the line makes no rule for women's conduct, so it carries no norm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 78","lb":"MK78","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["படுபழி","deep disgrace easily falls on men who hanker after women",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:79":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":79,"t":["பாரம்வெய் யோர்க்குப் பாத்தூண் எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who are willing to bear a burden, eating after sharing one's food comes easily.","n":"பாத்தூண் is food eaten after it has been divided and shared, the Kural's word for the householder's habit of feeding others. பாரம் is most plainly a load or charge; the reading taken is that one who willingly shoulders responsibility (for kin, guests, dependants) finds sharing food no hardship. The sense of பாரம் here is not certain, and the line could also be read as a load taken on in giving, so the verse is marked uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 79","lb":"MK79","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பாத்தூண்","sharing out one's food before eating comes easily to those who take up a load",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:80":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":80,"t":["சார்பு இலோர்க்கு உறுகொலை எளிது"],"p":"","r":"For those who have no one to lean on, violent killing comes easily.","n":"The closing line breaks the ten's pattern: not a desirer (வெய்யோர்) but one who lacks (இலோர்). சார்பு is support, refuge or protection. Two readings are open: those without protectors are easily killed, or those with no ties to restrain them kill easily. The first fits the dative 'to whom it is easy' as an outcome that befalls, like 76-78, and is taken here; the second cannot be ruled out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 80","lb":"MK80","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உறுகொலை","violent death comes easily where a person has no support",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:8"},"mk:81":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":81,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","முறையில் அரச(ன்) நாடு நல்கூர்ந் தன்று"],"p":"","r":"The land of a king who rules without justice is said to be impoverished.","n":"Line 1 is the frame shared by every ten. Here நல்கூர்தல் is literal as well as figurative: an unjust reign makes the country itself poor, not only the king's name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","naadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 81","lb":"MK81","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"king","it":[["முறையில் அரச(ன்) நாடு","the country of a king without justice is poor",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:82":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":82,"t":["மிகமூத்தோள் காமம் நல்கூர்ந் தன்று"],"p":"","r":"The desire of a woman who is very old is reckoned a poor, wretched thing.","n":"மிகமூத்தோள் is feminine: the saying names a woman, where the next old-age line (86) uses the masculine முதிர்வுடையோன். The judgement is of fitness to age, a norm of the period about women's bodies, and the line gives no reason beyond the frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 82","lb":"MK82","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["மிகமூத்தோள் காமம்","the sexual desire of a very old woman is poor",1]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period judgement that an old woman's desire is unbecoming"],"ch":"mk:9"},"mk:83":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":83,"t":["செற்றுடன் உறவோனைச் சேர்தல்நல் கூர்ந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Joining with one who is close to you yet bears you enmity is reckoned a poor thing to do.","n":"Our text has உறவோனை, one related to you; tamilvu has உறைவோனை, one who lives with you. Either way the person is near and hostile, so the sense holds: alliance with an enemy within gains nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["utpagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 83","lb":"MK83","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["செற்றுடன் உறவோனைச் சேர்தல்","joining with a hostile intimate is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:84":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":84,"t":["பிணிகிடந்தோன் பெற்ற இன்பம்நல் கூர்ந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"The pleasure that comes to a man lying sick is reckoned poor.","n":"கிடந்தோன், the one laid down by disease: the pleasure is real but cannot be enjoyed, so it counts for little.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 84","lb":"MK84","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிணிகிடந்தோன் பெற்ற இன்பம்","pleasure got by one bedridden with illness is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:85":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":85,"t":["தன்போற் றாவழிப் புலவிநல் கூர்ந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Sulking where one is not cherished is reckoned poor.","n":"Read as தன் போற்றாவழி, where the other does not cherish one; the printed spacing (தன்போற் றாவழி) splits போற்றா. Sulking is a lover's play that works only when the other cares enough to appease it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 85","lb":"MK85","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தன்போற் றாவழிப் புலவி","sulking where one is not cherished is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:86":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":86,"t":["முதிர்வுடை யோன்மேனி அணிநல் கூர்ந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Ornament on the body of one who is very old is reckoned poor.","n":"முதிர்வுடையோன் is masculine, so this old-age line, unlike 82, is about a man; adornment cannot restore what age has taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 86","lb":"MK86","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["முதிர்வுடை யோன்மேனி அணி","adornment on an aged man's body is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:87":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":87,"t":["சொற்சொல் லாவழிச் சொலவுநல் கூர்ந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Speaking where one's word does not carry is reckoned poor.","n":"Our text reads சொற்சொல்லாவழி, 'where a word is not spoken', which gives little sense beside சொலவு; tamilvu's சொல் செல்லாவழி, 'where a word does not go', is read here. On our text the line might instead say that talk is poor where nothing worth saying is said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["sol","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 87","lb":"MK87","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சொற்சொல் லாவழிச் சொலவு","speech where words have no effect is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:88":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":88,"t":["அகம்வறி யோன்நண்ணல் நல்கூர்ந் தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Drawing near to one who is empty within is reckoned poor.","n":"அகம் வறியோன் may be one empty of heart (without love or understanding) or one whose house is bare; the first fits a ten on worthless acts better, but the second, approaching a poor man to ask, cannot be excluded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 88","lb":"MK88","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["அகம்வறி யோன்நண்ணல்","approaching one hollow within is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:89":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":89,"t":["உட்(கு)இல் வழிச்சினம் நல்கூர்ந் தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Anger where it inspires no awe is reckoned poor.","n":"உட்கு is the dread a person commands; anger in one who has none is empty show, measured by its effect rather than judged as a vice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 89","lb":"MK89","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உட்(கு)இல் வழிச்சினம்","anger where there is no awe behind it is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:90":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":90,"t":["நட்(பு)இல் வழிச்சேறல் நல்கூர்ந் தன்று"],"p":"","r":"Going to a place where there is no friendship is reckoned poor.","n":"சேறல், going or visiting: the visitor where no friendship waits gets nothing and loses standing, so the ten closes on a social act as it opened on a political one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 90","lb":"MK90","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["நட்(பு)இல் வழிச்சேறல்","going where there is no friendship is poor",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:9"},"mk:91":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":91,"t":["ஆர்கலி யுலகத்து மக்கட் கெல்லாம்","ஓங்கல் வேண்டுவோன் உயர்மொழி தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants to rise high is said not to give up lofty speech.","n":"Line 1 is the frame of the whole ten, so the saying of 91 is line 2 alone. உயர்மொழி may be one's own elevated, worthy speech or the high words of the great that one heeds; the line does not say whose words they are. தண்டான் is read through the ten as 'does not leave off, does not slacken in'; each line pairs a goal with the practice its seeker never drops.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 91","lb":"MK91","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["உயர்மொழி","lofty speech is not given up by one who wants to rise",2]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:92":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":92,"t":["வீங்கல் வேண்டுவோன் பல்புகழ் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants to grow great is said not to leave off winning wide renown.","n":"வீங்கல் is swelling or growing large, a bodily verb used of fortune; பல்புகழ், 'many-fold fame', suggests renown gathered from many deeds rather than one, so the line asks for a steady run of creditable acts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 92","lb":"MK92","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["பல்புகழ்","the many kinds of renown are not let go by one who wants to swell in greatness",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:93":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":93,"t":["கற்றல் வேண்டுவோன் வழிபாடு தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants to learn is said not to slacken in reverent service.","n":"வழிபாடு is worship and devoted service; the line does not name the teacher, but in a saying about learning the one served is most naturally the teacher or the learned. It makes reverence a means of learning, not a reward for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vazhipaadu","guru","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 93","lb":"MK93","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வழிபாடு","reverent attendance is never dropped by one who wants to learn",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:94":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":94,"t":["நிற்றல் வேண்டுவோன் தவஞ்செயல் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants to stand firm is said not to leave off doing penance.","n":"நிற்றல், 'standing', is left without an object: it may be steadfastness in one's path, lasting in the world, or remaining in a higher state. தவம் is the renouncer's discipline, so the line is read as addressed to him, though the frame addresses everyone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 94","lb":"MK94","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["தவஞ்செயல்","doing penance is not given up by one who wants to stand firm",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:95":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":95,"t":["வாழ்க்கை வேண்டுவோன் சூழ்ச்சி தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants a good life is said not to leave off careful deliberation.","n":"வாழ்க்கை can be living in general or the life of the household; சூழ்ச்சி is the weighing of means that the Kural assigns to ministers, here required of anyone who wants to make a life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","aaraaydhal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 95","lb":"MK95","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["சூழ்ச்சி","deliberation is never abandoned by one who wants to live well",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:96":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":96,"t":["மிகுதி வேண்டுவோன் வருத்தம் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants abundance is said not to shrink from toil.","n":"மிகுதி is surplus, more than enough; வருத்தம் is pain of effort, the same word the Kural uses for the body's strain in labour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","selvam","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 96","lb":"MK96","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["வருத்தம்","hard toil is not shunned by one who wants plenty",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:97":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":97,"t":["இன்பம் வேண்டுவோன் துன்பம் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants pleasure is said not to shun the pain that comes with it.","n":"Read with the ten's predicate, the line makes pain the price a seeker of pleasure keeps paying; 98 reverses the pair, so the two lines together say each brings the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 97","lb":"MK97","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["துன்பம்","pain is not shunned by one who wants pleasure",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:98":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":98,"t":["துன்பம் வேண்டுவோன் இன்பம் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants pain, the line says in reverse of 97, does not hold back from pleasure.","n":"Taken literally the saying is a paradox; on the reading taken here it names the unchecked pleasure-seeker by where he ends, since pleasure pursued without stop brings pain. A reading in which the one who seeks hardship (as in penance) still does not refuse all pleasure is possible, but the mirror of 97 favours the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","idumbai","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 98","lb":"MK98","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["இன்பம்","pleasure is not held back from by one who, in effect, wants pain",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:99":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":99,"t":["ஏமம் வேண்டுவோன் முறைசெயல் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants security is said not to leave off acting by just order.","n":"ஏமம் is safety or protection; முறை is the word for justice done by a ruler, so the seeker of security is most plausibly the king, though the line does not name him and a householder's fair dealing is a possible wider reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 99","lb":"MK99","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"king","it":[["முறைசெயல்","acting justly is not given up by one who wants safety",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"mk:100":{"book":"muthumozhikkanchi","num":100,"t":["காமம் வேண்டுவோன் குறிப்புச்சொல் தண்டான்"],"p":"","r":"Someone who wants love is said not to give up speaking by hints.","n":"குறிப்புச்சொல் is words that suggest rather than state; the line makes indirection the lover's constant practice, the last of the ten and the only one in the akam field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Muthumozhikkanchi 100","lb":"MK100","poet":"மதுரைக் கூடலூர் கிழார்","dm":"all","it":[["குறிப்புச்சொல்","speech by hint is not given up by one who wants love",1]],"cw":"","ch":"mk:10"},"nm:0":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":1,"t":["மதிமன்னு மாயவன் வாள்முகம் ஒக்கும்","கதிர்சேர்ந்த ஞாயிறு சக்கரம் ஒக்கும்","முதுநீர்ப் பழனத்துத் தாமரைத் தாளின்","எதிர்மலர் மற்றவன் கண்ஒக்கும் பூவைப்","புதுமலர் ஒக்கும் நிறம்"],"p":"","r":"The invocation sets four likenesses between the world and Mayavan: the moon is like his bright face, the rayed sun like his discus, the lotus flower opening on its stalk in the old-watered field like his eye, and his colour like the fresh bloom of the kaya flower.","n":"The first three likenesses run from the world to the god (the moon resembles his face), the fourth runs back (his colour resembles the flower), so the sky, the field and the bloom are all read as copies of him. The items here count the god's four features, since the comparison is made for them; மதிமன்னு may also be taken as 'in whom wisdom abides', which would leave the moon unnamed and the face without its likeness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 0","lb":"NM0","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மாயவன் வாள்முகம்","his bright face is what the moon resembles",1],["சக்கரம்","his discus is what the rayed sun resembles",2],["மற்றவன் கண்","his eye is what the lotus opening on its stalk resembles",4],["நிறம்","his colour resembles the fresh kaya flower",5]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:0b":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":2,"t":["படியை மடியகத் திட்டான் அடியினால்","முக்காற் கடந்தான் முழுநிலம் - அக்காலத்து","ஆப்பனி தாங்கிய குன்றெடுத்தான் சோவின்","அருமை யழித்த மகன்"],"p":"","r":"The second invocation names four deeds of the son: he put the earth inside his belly, crossed the whole earth with his foot in three steps, lifted the hill that sheltered the cows from the cold rain, and destroyed the impregnability of Sho.","n":"It calls the god only மகன், 'the son', and lets the deeds name him: swallowing the earth, the three strides, the lifted hill (Govardhana by tradition) and the fall of சோ (by tradition Bana's fortress). The verse has no count word; the four deeds are four relative clauses closing on the one noun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 0b","lb":"NM0b","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["படியை மடியகத் திட்டான்","he took the earth into his belly",1],["முக்காற் கடந்தான் முழுநிலம்","he crossed the whole earth with his foot in three steps",2],["ஆப்பனி தாங்கிய குன்றெடுத்தான்","he lifted the hill that kept the cold rain off the cows",3],["சோவின் அருமை யழித்த","he destroyed the impregnability of Sho",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:1":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":3,"t":["எள்ளற்க என்றும் எளியரென்று என்பெறினும்","கொள்ளற்க கொள்ளார்கைம் மேற்பட - உள்சுடினும்","சீறற்க சிற்றிற் பிறந்தாரைக் கூறற்க","கூறல் லவற்றை விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse gives four prohibitions: never despise people as lowly; whatever one would gain, never take anything such that the hand of those one should not take from is above one's own; however it burns within, never rage at those born in small houses; and never hastily say what should not be said.","n":"The four gems here are four negative commands, each verb fronted (எள்ளற்க, கொள்ளற்க, சீறற்க, கூறற்க), so the form turns from naming things to naming restraints. The second and third turn on the giver's or the other's standing: the giver's hand placed above the receiver's, and the small house one is born in. சிற்றில் may mean a poor house as much as a low one; the verse restrains contempt for such people rather than ranking them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maanam","sol","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 1","lb":"NM1","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["எளியரென்று","despising people because they are lowly is forbidden at all times",1],["கொள்ளார்கைம் மேற்பட","taking, at any gain, so that the hand of those one should not receive from is above one's own is forbidden",2],["சிற்றிற் பிறந்தாரை","raging at those born in small houses is forbidden, however it burns within",3],["கூறல் லவற்றை","hastily saying what should not be said is forbidden",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:2":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":4,"t":["பறைபட வாழா அசுணமா உள்ளங்","குறைபட வாழார் உரவோர் - நிறைவனத்து","நெற்பட்ட கண்ணே வெதிர்சாம் தனக்கொவ்வாச்","சொற்பட வாழாதாஞ் சால்பு"],"p":"","r":"The asunam beast does not live once the drum is beaten, the strong do not live once their spirit is lessened, the bamboo in the dense forest dies once it puts out grain, and nobility does not live once a word unworthy of it is spoken.","n":"Two of the deaths come from outside (the drum, a word) and one from the thing's own fruit (the bamboo dies of seeding), so the verse does not say whether nobility is killed by another's slander or by its own unworthy speech: சொற்பட allows both. The fourth item makes nobility a living thing that can die while its bearer lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saandraanmai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 2","lb":"NM2","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அசுணமா","the asunam does not live once the drum sounds",1],["உரவோர்","the strong do not live once their heart is lessened",2],["வெதிர்","the bamboo dies once grain forms on it",3],["சால்பு","nobility does not live once a word unfit for it is spoken",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:3":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":5,"t":["மண்ணி யறிப மணிநலம் பண்ணமைத்து","ஏறிய பின்னறிப மாநலம் மாசறச்","சுட்டறிப பொன்னின் நலங்காண்பார் கெட்டறிப","கேளிரான் ஆய பயன்"],"p":"","r":"One knows a gem's worth by washing it, a horse's worth after harnessing and mounting it, gold's worth by burning it free of dross, and the use of one's kin by being ruined.","n":"Each worth is known only by an ordeal applied to it, and the last ordeal falls on the tester, not the tested: one learns what kin are worth by suffering ruin oneself. The verb அறிப repeats four times, so the verse is a list of tests rather than of goods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","sutram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 3","lb":"NM3","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மணிநலம்","a gem's worth is known by washing it",1],["மாநலம்","a horse's worth is known after it is harnessed and ridden",2],["பொன்னின் நலங்","gold's worth is known by burning it clean of dross",3],["கேளிரான் ஆய பயன்","the use one has of kin is known by being ruined",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:4":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":6,"t":["கள்ளி வயிற்றின் அகில்பிறக்கும் மான்வயிற்றின்","ஒள்ளரி தாரம் பிறக்கும் பெருங்கடலுள்","பல்விலைய முத்தம் பிறக்கும் அறிவார்யார்","நல்லாள் பிறக்குங் குடி"],"p":"","r":"Fragrant agil is born in the belly of the kalli, bright orpiment in the belly of the deer, costly pearls in the great sea, and no one knows in what family a good woman is born.","n":"The first three are precious things born of mean or unlikely sources (a thorny spurge, an animal's belly, salt water), so the closing question is not idle: a good woman's worth cannot be read off her family. The verse does not rank families; it denies that family predicts the person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 4","lb":"NM4","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அகில்","agil is born in the belly of the kalli",1],["ஒள்ளரி தாரம்","bright orpiment is born in the deer's belly",2],["பல்விலைய முத்தம்","costly pearls are born in the great sea",3],["நல்லாள்","no one knows the family a good woman is born in",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:5":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":7,"t":["கல்லிற் பிறக்குங் கதிர்மணி காதலி","சொல்லிற் பிறக்கும் உயர்மதம் - மெல்லென்று","அருளிற் பிறக்கும் அறநெறி எல்லாம்","பொருளிற் பிறந்து விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The bright gem is born of stone, high delight is born of the beloved's word, the way of virtue is born of gentle compassion, and everything is born of wealth.","n":"The last line widens suddenly: after three particular births it makes wealth the source of all things, which sits beside, and does not cancel, the line that gives virtue its own source in அருள். உயர்மதம் is elation, not pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 5","lb":"NM5","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கதிர்மணி","the bright gem is born of stone",1],["உயர்மதம்","high delight is born of the beloved's word",2],["அறநெறி","the way of virtue is born of gentle compassion",3],["எல்லாம்","everything is born of wealth",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:6":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":8,"t":["திருவொக்கும் தீதில் ஒழுக்கம் பெரிய","அறனொக்கும் ஆற்றின் ஒழுகல் பிறனைக்","கொலையொக்கும் கொண்டுகண் மாறல் புலையொக்கும்","போற்றாதார் மன்னர்ச் செலவு"],"p":"","r":"Faultless conduct is like fortune, living by the right path is like great virtue, taking someone up and then turning one's face from him is like killing him, and going before those who do not esteem one is like degradation.","n":"Two goods are made equal to greater goods, two wrongs to greater wrongs. புலை in the last item is the word for the degraded or outcaste condition, used here as a measure of shame, not as a rule about persons. The text reads மன்னர் ('kings'), so the last item may be going to kings who do not esteem one; tamilvu has முன்னர், 'before', which gives the general sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["ozhukkam","kannottam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 6","lb":"NM6","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தீதில் ஒழுக்கம்","faultless conduct is like fortune itself",1],["ஆற்றின் ஒழுகல்","living by the right path is like great virtue",2],["கொண்டுகண் மாறல்","taking someone up and then withdrawing one's regard is like killing him",3],["போற்றாதார் மன்னர்ச் செலவு","going before those who do not esteem one is like degradation",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:7":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":9,"t":["கள்வமென் பார்க்குந் துயிலில்லை காதலிமாட்டு","உள்ளம்வைப் பார்க்குந் துயிலில்லை ஒண்பொருள்","செய்வமென் பார்ககுந் துயிலில்லை அப்பொருள்","காப்பார்க்கும் இல்லை துயில்"],"p":"","r":"There is no sleep for those who plan to steal, none for those who set their heart on a beloved woman, none for those who plan to make bright wealth, and none either for those who guard that wealth.","n":"The thief and the lover lead to the two kinds of rich man, so earning and guarding wealth are ranked with stealing and longing as kinds of sleeplessness. The last item is set apart with உம் ('the guardians too'): having wealth gives no more rest than wanting it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kalavu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 7","lb":"NM7","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்வமென் பார்க்குந்","those who plan to steal get no sleep",1],["காதலிமாட்டு உள்ளம்வைப் பார்க்குந்","those whose heart is set on a beloved get no sleep",1],["ஒண்பொருள் செய்வமென் பார்ககுந்","those who plan to make bright wealth get no sleep",2],["அப்பொருள் காப்பார்க்கும்","those who guard that wealth get no sleep either",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:8":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":10,"t":["கற்றார்முன் தோன்றா கழிவிரக்கங் காதலித்தொன்று","உற்றார்முன் தோன்றா உறாமுதல் - தெற்றென","அல்ல புரிந்தார்க்கு அறந்தோன்றா எல்லாம்","வெகுண்டார்முன் தோன்றா கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"Regret over what is past does not show before the learned, failure does not show before those who have set their love on a thing and pursued it, virtue does not show clearly to those who do what is wrong, and before those who rage everything fails to show and is ruined.","n":"Each item is something that does not appear before a kind of person, but the sense turns: the first two are evils kept away by a virtue, the last two are goods hidden by a vice. உறாமுதல் is rare and may mean 'not attaining' or 'the source of loss'; the reading of the second item rests on that guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vekuli","aram","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 8","lb":"NM8","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கழிவிரக்கங்","regret for what is past does not appear before the learned",1],["உறாமுதல்","failure does not appear before those who love a thing and pursue it",2],["அறந்தோன்றா","virtue does not show clearly to those who do wrong",3],["எல்லாம்","everything vanishes and is ruined before the angry",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:9":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":11,"t":["நிலத்துக்கு அணியென்ப நெல்லுங் கரும்பும்","குளத்துக்கு அணியென்ப தாமரை பெண்மை","நலத்துக்கு அணியென்ப நாணம் தனக்கணியாம்","தான்செல் உலகத் தறம்"],"p":"","r":"They say paddy and sugarcane are the ornament of the land, the lotus the ornament of a pond, modesty the ornament of a woman's beauty, and one's own ornament is virtue for the world one goes to.","n":"The last item turns the ornament inward and forward: the first three adorn something seen, the fourth adorns the self and is worn into the next world. The third item states a gendered norm: modesty is named the ornament of women's beauty, not of the worthy in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aram","marumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 9","lb":"NM9","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நெல்லுங் கரும்பும்","paddy and sugarcane are the ornament of the land",1],["தாமரை","the lotus is the ornament of a pond",2],["நாணம்","modesty is the ornament of a woman's beauty",3],["தான்செல் உலகத் தறம்","virtue for the world one goes to is one's own ornament",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a gender norm of the period: modesty is assigned to women as the ornament of their beauty"],"ch":"nm:1"},"nm:10":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":12,"t":["கந்திற் பிணிப்பர் களிற்றைக் கதந்தவிர","மந்திரத் தாற்பிணிப்பர் மாநாகம் - கொந்தி","இரும்பிற் பிணிப்பர் கயத்தைச்சான் றோரை","நயத்திற் பிணித்து விடல்"],"p":"","r":"They bind a bull elephant to a post to stop its rage, bind the great serpent with a mantra, bind the base with iron after goading them, and the worthy are bound by courtesy.","n":"All four are 'binding' (பிணி), so courtesy is placed among restraints: the worthy are not free of control but are controlled by the gentlest means. The base sit between the beasts and the worthy and need iron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","nayan","oruththal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 10","lb":"NM10","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["களிற்றை","the bull elephant is bound to a post to stop its rage",1],["மாநாகம்","the great serpent is bound by a mantra",2],["கயத்தை","the base are bound with iron after being goaded",3],["சான் றோரை","the worthy are bound by courtesy",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:11":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":13,"t":["கன்றாமை வேண்டுங் கடிய பிறர்செய்த","நன்றியை நன்றாக் கொளல்வேண்டும் - என்றும்","விடல்வேண்டும் தங்கண் வெகுளி அடல்வேண்டும்","ஆக்கும் சிதைக்கும் வினை"],"p":"","r":"One must not be embittered by the harsh things others do, must hold as good the good others did, must always let go of one's own anger, and must destroy the deed that wrecks one's prosperity.","n":"The first two items are a pair about others' acts (their harm not resented, their help remembered), the last two about one's own (one's anger, one's ruinous deed). The fourth item uses அடல், 'killing', for a deed, so the restraint asked is violent toward oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai","vekuli","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 11","lb":"NM11","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கடிய","the harsh things others did must not embitter one",1],["பிறர்செய்த நன்றியை","the good others did must be held as good",1],["தங்கண் வெகுளி","one's own anger must always be let go",3],["ஆக்கும் சிதைக்கும் வினை","the deed that wrecks one's prosperity must be destroyed",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:12":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":14,"t":["பல்லினான் நோய்செய்யும் பாம்பெலாம் கொல்களிறு","கோட்டால் நோய்செய்யும் குறித்தாரை ஊடி","முகத்தான் நோய்செய்வர் மகளிர் முனிவர்","தவத்தால் தருகுவர் நோய்"],"p":"","r":"All snakes harm with their teeth, the killing elephant with its tusk, women harm with their face when sulking at those they love, and sages give harm by their penance.","n":"Each harms with its own proper organ or power, and the sage's power is the penance itself: the verse sets the ascetic's curse beside the snake's bite as a natural weapon. Women's harm is the sulk at a lover, a small pain placed between two deadly ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","oodal","noy","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 12","lb":"NM12","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பாம்பெலாம்","snakes harm with their teeth",1],["கொல்களிறு","the killing elephant harms with its tusk",1],["மகளிர்","women harm with their face when they sulk at those they love",3],["முனிவர்","sages give harm through their penance",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:13":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":15,"t":["பறைநன்று பண்ணமையா யாழின் நிறைநின்ற","பெண்நன்று பீடிலா மாந்தரின் - பண்அழிந்து","ஆர்தலின்நன்று பசித்தல் பசைந்தாரின்","தீர்தலின் தீப்புகுதல் நன்று"],"p":"","r":"A drum is better than a lute out of tune, a woman firm in self-containment is better than men without worth, going hungry is better than eating with one's dignity undone, and entering fire is better than parting from those one is bound to.","n":"Each item prefers a plain or painful thing to a fine thing gone wrong: the crude drum to the spoiled lute, hunger to dishonoured food, fire to separation. The second item sets a woman's நிறை above worthless men, so worth outranks sex here. The last makes parting worse than burning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","nirai","pasi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 13","lb":"NM13","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பறை","a drum is better than an untuned lute",1],["நிறைநின்ற பெண்","a woman firm in self-containment is better than men without worth",1],["பசித்தல்","hunger is better than eating with dignity undone",3],["தீப்புகுதல்","entering fire is better than parting from those one is bound to",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:14":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":16,"t":["வளப்பாத்தி யுள்வளரும் வண்மை கிளைக்குழாம்","இன்சொற் குழியுள் இனிதெழூஉம் வன்சொல்","கரவெழூஉங் கண்ணில் குழியுள் இரவெழூஉம்","இன்மைக் குழியுள் விரைந்து"],"p":"","r":"Liberality grows in the bed of plenty, the throng of kin rises sweetly in the pit of sweet speech, concealment rises in the graceless pit of harsh speech, and begging rises quickly in the pit of poverty.","n":"Every item is a plant in its bed or pit, so dispositions are crops of conditions: plenty grows giving, poverty grows begging. Two are good crops and two bad, and the begging grows 'quickly'. The division of lines 2 and 3 (where வன்சொல் belongs) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","inchol","karavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 14","lb":"NM14","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வண்மை","liberality grows in the bed of plenty",1],["கிளைக்குழாம்","the throng of kin rises sweetly in the pit of sweet speech",1],["கரவெழூஉங்","concealment rises in the graceless pit of harsh speech",3],["இரவெழூஉம்","begging rises quickly in the pit of poverty",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:15":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":17,"t":["இன்னாமை வேண்டின் இரவெழுக இந்நிலத்து","மன்னுதல் வேண்டின் இசைநடுக - தன்னொடு","செல்வது வேண்டின் அறஞ்செய்க வெல்வது","வேண்டின் வெகுளி விடல்"],"p":"","r":"If one wants pain, rise to beg; if one wants to last in this world, plant fame; if one wants what goes along with oneself, do virtue; if one wants to win, give up anger.","n":"The first item is irony in the form of the other three: begging is offered as the sure means to the wish for pain. The pattern 'if you want X, do Y' makes each virtue an instrument, and the last one inverts expectation: one wins by giving up anger, not by using it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","aram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 15","lb":"NM15","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இன்னாமை","the one who wants pain should rise to beg",1],["மன்னுதல்","the one who wants to last in this world should plant fame",2],["தன்னொடு செல்வது","the one who wants what goes along with him should do virtue",2],["வெல்வது","the one who wants to win should give up anger",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:16":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":18,"t":["கடற்குட்டம் போழ்வர் கலவர் படைக்குட்டம்","பாய்மா உடையான் உடைக்கிற்குந் - தோமில்","தவக்குட்டம் தன்னுடையான் நீந்தும் அவைக்குட்டம்","கற்றான் கடந்து விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Sailors split the deep of the sea, the man with a leaping horse can break the deep of an army, the man who holds himself swims the faultless deep of penance, and the learned man crosses the deep of the assembly.","n":"குட்டம் ('depth') is repeated for sea, army, penance and assembly, so the assembly is made as dangerous as the sea or a battle line, and each is crossed only with its own means. தன்னுடையான் is the self-possessed: the means for penance is mastery of oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","thavam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 16","lb":"NM16","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கலவர்","sailors split the deep of the sea",1],["பாய்மா உடையான்","the man with a leaping horse can break through the deep of an army",2],["தன்னுடையான்","the self-possessed swims the faultless deep of penance",3],["கற்றான்","the learned man crosses the deep of the assembly",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:17":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":19,"t":["பொய்த்தல் இறுவாய நட்புகள் மெய்த்தாக","மூத்தல் இறுவாய்த்து இளைநலம் தூக்கில்","மிகுதி இறுவாய செல்வங்கள் தத்தம்","தகுதி இறுவாய்த்து உயிர்"],"p":"","r":"Friendships end in falsehood, youth's beauty truly ends in old age, wealth, weighed up, ends in excess, and life ends at its own allotted measure.","n":"Each thing carries its own end (இறுவாய்): friendship is undone by its own falsehood, wealth by its own excess, so the first three ends are made from within. Only life's end is a fixed measure, தகுதி, not a fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","natpu","ilamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 17","lb":"NM17","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நட்புகள்","friendships end in falsehood",1],["இளைநலம்","youth's beauty ends in old age",2],["செல்வங்கள்","wealth ends in excess",3],["உயிர்","life ends at its own allotted measure",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:18":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":20,"t":["மனைக்காக்கம் மாண்ட மகளிர் ஒருவன்","வினைக்காக்கம் செவ்விய னாதல் - சினச்செவ்வேல்","நாட்டாக்கம் நல்லனிவ் வேந்தென்றல் கேட்டாக்கம்","கேளிர் ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"","r":"Excellent women are the prosperity of a house, being upright is the prosperity of a man's work, people saying 'this king of the angry red spear is good' is the prosperity of the land, and casting off kin is the prosperity of ruin.","n":"The last item is an ironic turn on the predicate: after three things that build, casting off kin 'builds' ruin. The third makes the land's prosperity a matter of the king's name in people's mouths, not of his wealth. The same last line recurs at verse 85.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","sengol","sutram","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 18","lb":"NM18","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["மாண்ட மகளிர்","excellent women are the prosperity of a house",1],["செவ்விய னாதல்","being upright is the prosperity of a man's work",2],["நல்லனிவ் வேந்தென்றல்","people saying the king is good is the prosperity of the land",3],["கேளிர் ஒரீஇ விடல்","casting off one's kin brings ruin as its gain",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:19":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":21,"t":["பெற்றான் அதிர்ப்பிற் பிணையன்னாள் தானதிர்க்கும்","கற்றான் அதிர்ப்பின் பொருளதிர்க்கும் - பற்றிய","மண்ணதிர்ப்பின் மன்னவன் கோலதிர்க்கும் பண்ணதிர்ப்பின்","பாடல் அதிர்ந்து விடும்"],"p":"","r":"If the husband shakes, the doe-like wife trembles; if the learned man shakes, the meaning shakes; if the land he holds is shaken, the king's sceptre shakes; and if the tune shakes, the song shakes too.","n":"Each pair is a dependence: the wife on the husband, the sense on the learned man, the sceptre on the land, the song on the tune. The third reverses the usual order: the king's rule depends on the land, not the land on the king. அதிர் may be 'tremble' or 'roar'; on the second the first item would say that a husband's anger makes his wife tremble.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kol","kalvi","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 19","lb":"NM19","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிணையன்னாள்","the doe-like wife trembles when her husband shakes",1],["பொருளதிர்க்கும்","the meaning shakes when the learned man shakes",2],["மன்னவன் கோலதிர்க்கும்","the king's sceptre shakes when the land he holds is shaken",3],["பாடல்","the song shakes when its tune shakes",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:20":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":22,"t":["மனைக்குப்பாழ் வாள்நுதல் இன்மை தான்செல்லும்","திசைக்குப்பாழ் நட்டோ ரை இன்மை இருந்த","அவைக்குப்பாழ் மூத்தோரை இன்மை தனக்குப்பாழ்","கற்றறிவு இல்லா உடம்பு"],"p":"","r":"Lack of a bright-browed wife is waste to a house, lack of friends is waste to the region one travels to, lack of elders is waste to the assembly one sits in, and a body without learned understanding is waste to oneself.","n":"Three items are absences in a place (house, foreign region, assembly); the fourth puts the absence inside the person, so the unlearned body is itself a ruined house. பாழ் is waste ground, not merely loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","vaazhkkaithunai","natpu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 20","lb":"NM20","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாள்நுதல் இன்மை","lack of a bright-browed wife lays a house waste",1],["நட்டோ ரை இன்மை","lack of friends lays waste the region one goes to",2],["மூத்தோரை இன்மை","lack of elders lays waste the assembly one sits in",3],["கற்றறிவு இல்லா உடம்பு","a body without learned understanding is waste to oneself",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:1"},"nm:21":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":23,"t":["மொய்சிதைக்கும் ஒற்றுமை இன்மை ஒருவனைப்","பொய்சிதைக்கும் பொன்போலும் மேனியைப் - பெய்த","கலஞ்சிதைக்கும் பாலின் சுவையைக் குலஞ்சிதைக்குங்","கூடார்கண் கூடி விடின்"],"p":"","r":"Disunity ruins strength, falsehood ruins a man's gold-like body, the vessel it is poured into spoils the taste of milk, and joining with those one should not join ruins a lineage.","n":"Each gem has the same verb, சிதைக்கும், so the verse is a list of what ruins what; the milk spoiled by its vessel is the image that explains the last gem, bad company spoiling a family. ஒருவனை on line 1 can go with the disunity (it ruins a man's strength) or with the falsehood of line 2; the reading takes the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inam","vaaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 21","lb":"NM21","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒற்றுமை இன்மை","lack of unity ruins strength",1],["பொய்","falsehood ruins a man's gold-like body",2],["பெய்த கலஞ்","the vessel milk is poured into spoils its taste",2],["கூடார்கண் கூடி","joining with those one should not join ruins one's lineage",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:22":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":24,"t":["புகழ்செய்யும் பொய்யா விளக்கம் இகந்தொருவர்ப்","பேணாது செய்வது பேதைமை - காணாக்","குருடனாச் செய்வது மம்மர் இருள்தீர்ந்த","கண்ணாரச் செய்வது கற்பு"],"p":"","r":"Unfailing clarity is what brings renown, folly is what makes one overstep and act without regard for others, bewilderment is what makes one a blind man who sees nothing, and learning is what gives eyes cleared of darkness.","n":"கற்பு here is learning, not chastity: it is set against மம்மர், the blindness of bewilderment, and gives eyes free of darkness. The four make two pairs, a gain against a fault and a blindness against a sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pedhaimai","mayal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 22","lb":"NM22","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யா விளக்கம்","unfailing clarity brings renown",1],["பேதைமை","folly makes one transgress and neglect others",2],["மம்மர்","bewilderment makes one a blind man who cannot see",3],["கற்பு","learning gives eyes from which darkness has gone",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:23":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":25,"t":["மலைப்பினும் வாரணந் தாங்கும் அலைப்பினும்","அன்னேயென் றோடுங் குழவி சிலைப்பினும்","நட்டார் நடுங்கும் வினைசெய்யார் ஒட்டார்","உடனுறையும் காலமும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"The elephant bears its load even when it struggles, the child that is beaten still runs to its mother crying 'mother', friends even when angry do nothing that makes one tremble, and there is no time when the hostile live together with one.","n":"Three bonds hold under strain (each with பினும், 'even if') and the fourth, the enemy, never binds at all. மலைப்பினும் வாரணம் தாங்கும் is taken as the elephant bearing its rider though it rages; it may mean a protector who stands firm though attacked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 23","lb":"NM23","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாரணந்","the elephant still bears its burden even when it struggles",1],["குழவி","the child, though beaten, runs to its mother crying 'mother'",2],["நட்டார்","friends, though angered, do nothing that makes one tremble",3],["ஒட்டார்","the hostile never have a time of dwelling together with one",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:24":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":26,"t":["நகைநலம் நட்டார்கண் நந்துஞ் சிறந்த","அவைநலம் அன்பின் விளங்கும் விசைமாண்ட","தேர்நலம் பாகனாற் பாடெய்தும் ஊர்நலம்","உள்ளானால் உள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"The good of laughter grows among friends, the good of a fine assembly shines through good will, the good of a swift chariot is won through its driver, and the good of a town is weighed by the one who dwells in it.","n":"Each நலம் depends on someone other than the thing itself: friends, kindness, a driver, an inhabitant. உள்ளான் may be the town's leading man rather than any resident.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","avai","anbu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 24","lb":"NM24","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நகைநலம்","the good of laughter flourishes among friends",1],["அவைநலம்","the good of an assembly shines through love",2],["தேர்நலம்","the worth of a swift chariot gains distinction through its driver",3],["ஊர்நலம்","the good of a town is judged by the one within it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:25":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":27,"t":["அஞ்சாமை அஞ்சுதி ஒன்றின் தனக்கொத்த","எஞ்சாமை எஞ்சல் அளவெல்லாம் - நெஞ்சறியக்","கோடாமை கோடி பொருள்பெறினும் நாடாதி","நட்டார்கண் விட்ட வினை"],"p":"","r":"The verse, in the second person, names four things: if one fears anything, to fear fearlessness; not to fall short of what befits one, to the full of one's means; not to swerve from what the heart knows even for a crore of wealth; and not to pry into a task left with friends.","n":"Each of the first three plays a negative noun against its own verb (அஞ்சாமை அஞ்சுதி, எஞ்சாமை எஞ்சல், கோடாமை கோடி); the second is the hardest to construe and may mean not leaving undone what one can do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","naduvu","natpu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 25","lb":"NM25","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அஞ்சாமை","fearlessness is the thing to fear, if one fears at all",1],["எஞ்சாமை","not falling short of what suits one is to be kept to the full",2],["கோடாமை","not swerving against one's conscience holds even for a crore",3],["நட்டார்கண் விட்ட வினை","a task entrusted to friends is not to be scrutinised",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:26":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":28,"t":["அலைப்பான் பிறவுயிரை யாக்கலும் குற்றம்","விலைப்பாலிற் கொண்டூன் மிசைதலும் குற்றம்","சொலற்பால அல்லாத சொல்லுதலும் குற்றம்","கொலைப்பாலுங் குற்றமே யாம்"],"p":"","r":"Rearing other living beings to torment them is a fault, buying flesh at a price and eating it is a fault, saying what should not be said is a fault, and killing is a fault too.","n":"The buyer's eating and the killer's act are counted as two separate faults, and a fault of speech sits between them; அலைப்பான் may mean rearing beings for slaughter rather than for torment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","kutram","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 26","lb":"NM26","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அலைப்பான் பிறவுயிரை யாக்கலும்","rearing other beings in order to torment them is a fault",1],["விலைப்பாலிற் கொண்டூன் மிசைதலும்","buying flesh at a price and eating it is a fault",2],["சொலற்பால அல்லாத சொல்லுதலும்","saying what should not be said is a fault",3],["கொலைப்பாலுங்","killing too is a fault",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:27":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":29,"t":["கோல்நோக்கி வாழும் குடியெல்லாம் தாய்முலைப்","பால்நோக்கி வாழும் குழவிகள் - வானத்","துளிநோக்கி வாழும் உலகம் உலகின்","விளிநோக்கி இன்புறூஉங் கூற்று"],"p":"","r":"All the people live looking to the sceptre, infants live looking to their mother's breast milk, the world lives looking to the rain from the sky, and Death rejoices looking to the world's dying.","n":"Three dependencies on a giver are closed by a fourth that inverts them: Death too 'lives looking', but to the world's end, and is glad of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kudi","mazhai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 27","lb":"NM27","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["குடியெல்லாம்","all the people live by looking to the king's sceptre",1],["குழவிகள்","infants live by looking to the mother's milk",2],["உலகம்","the world lives by looking to rain from the sky",3],["கூற்று","Death rejoices looking to the world's dying",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:28":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":30,"t":["கற்பக் கழிமடம் அஃகும் மடம் அஃகப்","புற்கந்தீர்ந்து இவ்வுலகின் கோளுணரும் கோளுணர்ந்தால்","தத்துவ மான நெறிபடரும் அந்நெறி","இப்பால் உலகின் இசைநிறீஇ - உப்பால்","உயர்ந்த உலகம் புகும்"],"p":"","r":"As one learns, deep ignorance lessens; as it lessens, freed of meanness, one grasps the nature of this world; grasping it, one walks the path of truth; and that path sets fame in this world and enters the high world beyond.","n":"The four gems are links of one chain, each clause's result the next clause's condition, so learning leads by stages to the other world. The verse runs to five lines where the rest have four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","madam","pugazh","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 28","lb":"NM28","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்பக்","learning lessens deep ignorance",1],["மடம் அஃகப்","when ignorance lessens one is freed of meanness and grasps the world's nature",1],["கோளுணர்ந்தால்","grasping that nature, one walks the path of truth",2],["அந்நெறி","that path sets fame here and enters the higher world",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:29":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":31,"t":["குழித்துழி நிற்பது நீர்தன்னைப் பல்லோர்","பழித்துழி நிற்பது பாவம் - அழித்துச்","செறிவுழி நிற்பது காமம் தனக்கொன்று","உறுவுழி நிற்பது அறிவு"],"p":"","r":"Water stays where there is a hollow, sin stays where many cast blame, desire stays where it crowds in destroying, and understanding stays where something befalls oneself.","n":"Each thing is placed by where it 'stands' (நிற்பது, -உழி), so the last makes understanding something that shows itself in one's own trouble. அழித்துச் செறிவுழி is hard; it may mean where desire, having broken restraint, presses close.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kaamam","pazhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 29","lb":"NM29","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நீர்","water settles where there is a hollow",1],["பாவம்","sin stays where many cast blame",2],["காமம்","desire stays where it presses in, destroying",3],["அறிவு","understanding stands firm where trouble befalls oneself",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:30":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":32,"t":["திருவின் திறலுடையது இல்லை ஒருவற்குக்","கற்றலின் வாய்த்த பிறஇல்லை - எற்றுள்ளும்","இன்மையின் இன்னாதது இல்லைஇல் என்னாத","வன்கையின் வன்பாட்டது இல்"],"p":"","r":"Nothing is mightier than fortune, nothing suits a man better than learning, of all things nothing is more painful than poverty, and nothing is firmer than the strong hand that never says 'there is none'.","n":"Four superlatives: the pain of want (line 3) is answered by the giving hand (line 4). வன்பாட்டது, read as 'firmer', may instead mean harder in labour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kalvi","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 30","lb":"NM30","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["திருவின்","nothing is mightier than fortune",1],["கற்றலின்","nothing suits a man better than learning",2],["இன்மையின்","nothing is more painful than poverty",3],["இல் என்னாத வன்கையின்","nothing is firmer than the hand that never says 'there is none'",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:31":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":33,"t":["புகைவித்தாப் பொங்கழல் தோன்றும் சிறந்த","நகைவித்தாத் தோன்றும் உவகை - பகையொருவன்","முன்னம்வித் தாக முளைக்கும் முளைத்தபின்","இன்னாவித் தாகி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Blazing fire appears with smoke as its seed, joy appears with laughter as its seed, enmity sprouts with a man's own former intent as its seed, and once sprouted it becomes the seed of suffering.","n":"The seed image runs through all four, and the last turns the third forward: enmity is both a sprout of one's own intent and the seed of further pain. முன்னம் may also mean a prior deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","innaa","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 31","lb":"NM31","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொங்கழல்","blazing fire appears from smoke as seed",1],["உவகை","joy appears from fine laughter as seed",2],["பகை","enmity sprouts from a man's prior intent as seed",2],["முளைத்தபின்","once sprouted, enmity becomes the seed of suffering",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:32":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":34,"t":["பிணியன்னர் பின்நோக்காப் பெண்டிர் உலகிற்கு","அணியன்னர் அன்புடை மாக்கள் - பிணிபயிரின்","புல்லன்னர் புல்லறிவின் ஆடவர் கல்லன்னர்","வல்லென்ற நெஞ்சத் தவர்"],"p":"","r":"Women who do not look ahead are like a disease, loving people are like an ornament to the world, men of mean understanding are like weeds in a crop, and the hard-hearted are like stone.","n":"The only item that names a sex judges women, and பின்நோக்கா ('not looking behind') is unclear: heedless of consequence, or not looking after the household. The other three are men or people at large.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pullarivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 32","lb":"NM32","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பின்நோக்காப் பெண்டிர்","women who do not look ahead are like a disease",1],["அன்புடை மாக்கள்","loving people are like an ornament to the world",2],["புல்லறிவின் ஆடவர்","men of mean understanding are like weeds among crops",3],["வல்லென்ற நெஞ்சத் தவர்","the hard-hearted are like stone",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a judgment of the period on women's conduct, likening heedless women to disease"],"ch":"nm:2"},"nm:33":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":35,"t":["அந்தணரின் நல்ல பிறப்பில்லை என்செயினும்","தாயின் சிறந்த தமரில்லை யாதும்","வளமையோ டொக்கும் வனப்பில்லை எண்ணின்","இளமையோ டொப்பதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"","r":"There is no birth better than the brahmin's, whatever one does; no kin more excellent than the mother; no beauty equal to prosperity; and nothing to compare with youth.","n":"அந்தணர் here ranks a birth-group above all others, a caste ranking stated as fact. என்செயினும் ('whatever one does') can close line 1, making the brahmin's birth best whatever he does, or open line 2, making the mother the best kin whatever she does; the caste claim is much stronger on the first reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","andhanar","ilamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 33","lb":"NM33","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அந்தணரின்","no birth is better than the brahmin's",1],["தாயின்","no kin is more excellent than the mother",2],["வளமையோ","no beauty equals prosperity",3],["இளமையோ","nothing compares with youth",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank of the period: the brahmin's birth ranked above all others"],"ch":"nm:2"},"nm:34":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":36,"t":["இரும்பின் இரும்பிடை போழ்ப பெருஞ்சிறப்பின்","நீருண்டார் நீரான்வாய் பூசுப - தேரின்","அரிய அரியவற்றாற் கொள்ப பெரிய","பெரியரான் எய்தப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"Iron is split by iron, those who have drunk water rinse the mouth with water, rare things are won, on examination, by rare means, and great things are reached through great men.","n":"Two physical cases of like working on like (iron on iron, water on water) prove the two moral ones; the mouth-rinsing is cited as a familiar act, not laid down as a rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saandror","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 34","lb":"NM34","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இரும்பின்","iron is split by iron",1],["நீருண்டார்","those who drink water cleanse their mouths with water",2],["அரிய","rare things are obtained by rare means",3],["பெரிய","great things are reached through the great",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:35":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":37,"t":["மறக்களி மன்னர்முன் தோன்றும் சிறந்த","அறக்களி இல்லாதார்க்கு ஈயமுன் தோன்றும்","வியக்களி நல்கூர்ந்தார் மேற்றாம் கயக்களி","ஊரில் பிளிற்றி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The frenzy of valour shows before kings, the joy of virtue shows when one gives to those who have nothing, the joy of conceit falls upon the poor, and the joy of the base blares through the town.","n":"Four kinds of களி (elation) are told apart by where they show. வியக்களி நல்கூர்ந்தார் மேற்றாம் may mean a poor man's empty pride or the boast of the rich made over the poor.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","kayamai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 35","lb":"NM35","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மறக்களி","the elation of valour shows before kings",1],["அறக்களி","the elation of virtue shows in giving to those who lack",2],["வியக்களி","the elation of conceit falls upon the poor",3],["கயக்களி","the elation of the base is trumpeted through the town",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:36":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":38,"t":["மையால் தளிர்க்கும் மலர்க்கண்கள் மாலிருள்","நெய்யால் தளிர்க்கும் நிமிர்சுடர் - பெய்ய","முழங்கத் தளிர்க்குங் குருகிலை நட்டார்","வழங்கத் தளிர்க்குமாம் மேல்"],"p":"","r":"Flower-like eyes brighten with collyrium, an upright flame in the dark night grows with ghee, the kuruku leaf sprouts when rain pours and thunder roars, and friends flourish the more when one gives.","n":"The verb தளிர்க்கும் ('puts out shoots') makes all four things grow from what they are fed. Whether the friends flourish when one gives to them, or one flourishes when friends give, the line allows both; குருகிலை is an uncertain plant name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 36","lb":"NM36","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மலர்க்கண்கள்","flower-like eyes brighten with collyrium",1],["நிமிர்சுடர்","an upright flame grows with ghee in the night",2],["குருகிலை","the kuruku leaf sprouts with pouring, thundering rain",3],["நட்டார்","friends flourish the more when there is giving",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:37":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":39,"t":["நகைஇனிது நட்டார் நடுவண் பொருளின்","தொகைஇனிது தொட்டு வழங்கின் - வகையுடைப்","பெண்இனிது பேணி வழிபடின் பண்இனிது","பாடல் உணர்வார் அகத்து"],"p":"","r":"Laughter is sweet among friends, a store of wealth is sweet if one gives it out with one's own hand, a woman of good kind is sweet if she tends and serves, and music is sweet in the hearts of those who understand song.","n":"Each sweet thing is sweet only under a condition (among friends, if given, if she serves, to the knowing), so the verse ranks the condition above the thing. The woman's sweetness is made to depend on her service.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","natpu","eegai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 37","lb":"NM37","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நகை","laughter is sweet in the midst of friends",1],["பொருளின் தொகை","a store of wealth is sweet if given out by one's own hand",1],["வகையுடைப் பெண்","a woman of good kind is sweet if she tends and serves",2],["பண்","music is sweet in the hearts of those who understand song",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm making a woman's worth depend on her tending and service"],"ch":"nm:2"},"nm:38":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":40,"t":["கரப்பவர்க்குச் செல்சார் கவிழ்தலெஞ் ஞான்றும்","இரப்பவர்க்குச் செல்சாரொன் றீவார் பரப்பமைந்த","தானைக்குச் செல்சார் தறுகண்மை ஊனுண்டல்","செய்யாமை செல்சா ருயிர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"For those who hide what they have, the end they come to is always downfall; for those who beg, the refuge is those who give; for a wide army, the support is valour; and for living beings, the refuge is others' not eating flesh.","n":"செல்சார் ('the place one goes to') is a refuge in three items and an ironic destination in the first: the hoarder's only refuge is ruin. The last item gives the creatures' side of abstaining from flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","eegai","pulaal","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 38","lb":"NM38","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கவிழ்தல","downfall is the end that comes to those who hide their wealth",1],["றீவார்","givers are the refuge of those who beg",2],["தறுகண்மை","valour is the support of a wide army",3],["ஊனுண்டல் செய்யாமை","not eating flesh is the refuge of living beings",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:39":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":41,"t":["கண்டதே செய்பவாங் கம்மியர் உண்டெனக்","கேட்டதே செய்ப புலனாள்வார் - வேட்ட","இனியவே செய்ப அமைந்தார் முனியாதார்","முன்னிய செய்யுந் திரு"],"p":"","r":"Craftsmen make what they have seen, the learned do what they have heard to be so, those close to one do the sweet things one desires, and fortune does what the unresentful intend.","n":"The first three are sources of action (sight, hearing, another's wish); the fourth gives fortune itself to the patient. புலனாள்வார் is read as the learned, and அமைந்தார் as intimates; both words allow other senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","natpu","selvam","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 39","lb":"NM39","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கம்மியர்","craftsmen make what they have seen",1],["புலனாள்வார்","the learned do what they have heard to be so",2],["அமைந்தார்","those close to one do the pleasing things one wants",3],["திரு","fortune does what the unresentful intend",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:40":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":42,"t":["திருவுந் திணைவகையான் நில்லாப் பெருவலிக்","கூற்றமுங் கூறுவ செய்துண்ணாது - ஆற்ற","மறைக்க மறையாதாங் காமம் முறையும்","இறைவகையான் நின்று விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Fortune does not abide by rank of birth, mighty Death does not take its food by doing what it is told, desire does not stay hidden however hard one hides it, and justice stands as the king is.","n":"Three things no human order controls are set beside a fourth that depends wholly on one man, the king. திணைவகையான் may mean by class of birth or by kind of person.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","kaamam","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 40","lb":"NM40","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["திருவுந்","fortune does not stay according to rank or kind",1],["கூற்றமுங்","Death does not act at anyone's bidding",2],["காமம்","desire cannot be hidden however hard it is hidden",3],["முறையும்","justice stands according to the king",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:2"},"nm:41":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":43,"t":["பிறக்குங்கால் பேரெனவும் பேரா இறக்குங்கால்","நில்லெனவும் நில்லா உயிரெனைத்தும் - நல்லாள்","உடன்படின் தானே பெருகும் கெடும்பொழுதில்","கண்டனவும் காணாக் கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"All lives, at birth, do not leave though told to go and, at death, do not stay though told to stay; wealth swells of itself when the good lady consents, and when ruin comes even what was in plain sight vanishes.","n":"The first two gems are one sentence about உயிரெனைத்தும், split by birth and death; the third gives no subject for what grows, and நல்லாள் is most likely Fortune (Tiru), so the swelling is of wealth. The four together set coming and going, gain and loss, beyond anyone's command.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","uyir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 41","lb":"NM41","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறக்குங்கால்","at birth a life does not depart though told to go",1],["இறக்குங்கால்","at death a life does not stay though told to stay",1],["நல்லாள்","when the good lady (Fortune) consents, it grows of itself",2],["கண்டனவும்","in the time of ruin even what was seen vanishes from sight",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:42":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":44,"t":["போரின்றி வாடும் பொருநர்சீர் கீழ்வீழ்ந்த","வேரின்றி வாடும் மரமெல்லாம் - நீர்பாய்","மடையின்றி நீள்நெய்தல் வாடும் படையின்றி","மன்னர்சீர் வாடி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The glory of fighters withers without war, every tree withers without roots that go down, the tall neytal withers without a sluice to bring water, and the glory of kings withers away without an army.","n":"Two natural images sit between two martial ones, so the army is set down as the root and the water-channel of a king's glory rather than its ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 42","lb":"NM42","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"king","it":[["பொருநர்சீர்","the glory of fighting men withers when there is no war",1],["மரமெல்லாம்","every tree withers without roots that strike down",2],["நீள்நெய்தல்","the tall neytal withers without a sluice through which water flows",3],["மன்னர்சீர்","the glory of kings withers without an army",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:43":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":45,"t":["ஏதிலா ரென்பார் இயல்பில்லார் யார்யார்க்கும்","காதலா ரென்பார் தகவுடையார் - மேதக்க","தந்தை யெனப்படுவான் தன்னுவாத்தி தாயென்பாள்","முந்துதான் செய்த வினை"],"p":"","r":"Those called strangers are those without good nature, those called dear to everyone are the worthy, the excellent one called father is one's teacher, and the one called mother is the deed one did before.","n":"Each gem redefines a kinship word: stranger and beloved by character, father as the teacher, and mother as one's own former deed, the source from which one's present life is born. The last turns the household's closest word into a word for karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","guru","sutram","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 43","lb":"NM43","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஏதிலா ரென்பார்","those called strangers are the people without good nature",1],["காதலா ரென்பார்","those called beloved of all are the worthy",2],["மேதக்க தந்தை","the excellent father is one's own teacher",2],["தாயென்பாள்","the mother is the deed one did earlier",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:44":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":46,"t":["பொறிகெடும் நாணற்ற போழ்தே நெறிபட்ட","ஐவரால் தானே வினைகெடும் - பொய்யா","நலம்கெடும் நீரற்ற பைங்கூழ் நலம்மாறின்","நண்பினார் நண்பு கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"Fortune perishes the moment shame is lost, action is ruined of itself by the five once they have taken their course, the green crop's unfailing goodness perishes without water, and the friendship of friends perishes when the good they gain changes.","n":"ஐவர் 'the five' is read here as the five senses running their own way; the line gives no noun, and பொறி on line 1 can mean the senses as well as fortune. The fourth gem treats friendship's end on lost advantage as a natural fact, like a crop without water, rather than as a vice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","adakkam","natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 44","lb":"NM44","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொறி","fortune is lost the moment shame is lost",1],["வினை","action is ruined by the five once they run their own course",2],["பைங்கூழ்","the unfailing goodness of green crop perishes without water",3],["நண்பினார் நண்பு","friends' friendship perishes when the benefit changes",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:45":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":47,"t":["நன்றிசாம் நன்றறியா தார்முன்னர்ச் சென்ற","விருந்தும் விருப்பிலார் முன்சாம் - அரும்புணர்ப்பின்","பாடல்சாம் பண்ணறியா தார்முன்னர் ஊடல்சாம்","ஊடல் உணரா ரகத்து"],"p":"","r":"A kindness dies before those who do not know kindness, a guest who has come dies before those without welcome, a song of rare composition dies before those who do not know melody, and a lover's sulk dies in the heart of one who does not understand sulking.","n":"All four are offerings that live only in a receiver able to take them: gratitude, hospitality, art and love fail not through the giver but through the one before whom they are set.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","virundhu","oodal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 45","lb":"NM45","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நன்றி","a good deed dies before those who do not know good",1],["விருந்தும்","a guest who arrives dies before those who have no desire to receive him",2],["பாடல்","a song of rare composition dies before those who do not know melody",3],["ஊடல்","a sulk dies in the heart of one who does not understand sulking",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:46":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":48,"t":["நாற்ற முரைக்கும் மலருண்மை கூறிய","மாற்ற முரைக்கும் வினைநலந் தூக்கின்","அகம்பொதிந்த தீமை மனமுரைக்கும் முன்னம்","முகம்போல முன்னுரைப்ப தில்"],"p":"","r":"Scent tells that a flower is there, spoken words tell, when weighed, the quality of a person's doing, the mind tells the evil packed within, and nothing tells the intention beforehand as the face does.","n":"The four are signs and what they disclose; the last is a ranking, since nothing discloses earlier (முன்) than the face, and the face outruns even the mind of line 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 46","lb":"NM46","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மலருண்மை","the presence of a flower is told by its scent",1],["வினைநலந்","the worth of one's doing is told by one's words when they are weighed",2],["அகம்பொதிந்த தீமை","evil packed inside is told by the mind",3],["முன்னம்","the intention is told first by the face, and nothing tells it sooner",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:47":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":49,"t":["மழையின்றி மாநிலத்தார்க் கில்லை மழையும்","தவமிலார் இல்வழி இல்லை தவமும்","அரச னிலாவழி இல்லை அரசனும்","இல்வாழ்வார் இல்வழி யில்"],"p":"","r":"The people of the great earth have nothing without rain, there is no rain where there are no ascetics, no penance where there is no king, and no king where there are no householders.","n":"The gems form a chain, each resting on the next, so the whole order of world, rain, penance and kingship rests at last on the householder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","thavam","arasu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 47","lb":"NM47","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மாநிலத்தார்க்","the people of the earth have nothing without rain",1],["மழையும்","rain does not fall where there are no ascetics",1],["தவமும்","penance does not exist where there is no king",2],["அரசனும்","a king does not exist where there are no householders",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:48":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":50,"t":["போதினான் நந்தும் புனைதண்தார் மற்றதன்","தாதினான் நந்துஞ் கரும்பெல்லாந் - தீதில்","வினையினான் நந்துவர் மக்களுந் தத்தம்","நனையினான் நந்தும் நறா"],"p":"","r":"A cool woven garland flourishes by its flowers, the bees all flourish by pollen, people flourish by faultless deeds, and honey flourishes by its own buds.","n":"கரும்பு is read as the bee, which suits தாது (pollen), not as sugarcane; நறா may be honey or toddy. Three things of the flower frame one human one, so a person's deeds are to him what blossom is to garland and honey.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 48","lb":"NM48","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["புனைதண்தார்","a cool woven garland thrives by its blossoms",1],["கரும்பெல்லாந்","the bees all thrive by pollen",2],["மக்களுந்","people thrive by faultless deeds",3],["நறா","honey thrives by its own buds",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:49":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":51,"t":["சிறந்தார்க் கரிய செறுதலெஞ் ஞான்றும்","பிறந்தார்க் கரிய துணைதுறந்து வாழ்தல்","வரைந்தார்க் கரிய வகுத்தூண் இரந்தார்க்கொன்","றில்லென்றல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"","r":"Anger is at all times hard for the eminent, living apart from one's companions is hard for those born into a family, sharing out food is hard for those who hold back, and saying 'there is nothing' to those who beg is hard for anyone.","n":"Three gems tie a difficulty to a kind of person; the fourth widens it to everyone, so refusing a beggar is set as harder than the others. வரைந்தார் is read as those who set limits on giving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","vekuli","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 49","lb":"NM49","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["செறுதலெஞ்","hostility is always hard for the eminent",1],["துணைதுறந்து வாழ்தல்","living after leaving one's companions is hard for those born into kin",2],["வகுத்தூண்","sharing out food is hard for those who hold back",3],["இரந்தார்க்கொன்","saying 'nothing' to those who beg is hard for anyone",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:50":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":52,"t":["இரைசுடும் இன்புறா யாக்கையுட் பட்டால்","உரைசுடும் ஒண்மை யிலாரை - வரைகொள்ளா","முன்னை ஒருவன் வினைசுடும் வேந்தனையுந்","தன்னடைந்த சேனை சுடும்"],"p":"","r":"Food burns when it enters a body that takes no pleasure in it, speech burns those without brightness of mind, a man's former deed, which knows no bound, burns him, and even a king is burned by the army that has joined him.","n":"Each thing meant to sustain or serve turns on its holder: food, words, one's own past acts and one's own army. The last carries the theme to the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","undi","padai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 50","lb":"NM50","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இரை","food burns a body that takes no pleasure in it",1],["உரை","speech burns those without brightness of mind",2],["முன்னை ஒருவன் வினை","a man's former deed burns him without limit",3],["தன்னடைந்த சேனை","the army that has joined him burns even a king",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:51":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":53,"t":["எள்ளற் பொருள திகழ்தல் ஒருவனை","உள்ளற் பொருள துறுதிச்சொல் - உள்ளறிந்து","சேர்தற் பொருள தறநெறி பன்னூலும்","தேர்தற் பொருள பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Scorning a person is itself a thing to be scorned, words of counsel are things to be pondered, the path of dharma is a thing to join after knowing it within, and the meanings of the many books are things to be examined.","n":"Each gem gives the right act toward a thing (to deride, to ponder, to join, to sift), and the first turns back on the scorner: contempt is matter for contempt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","aram","kalvi","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 51","lb":"NM51","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["திகழ்தல்","scorning a man is a thing for derision",1],["துறுதிச்சொல்","words of counsel are things to be pondered",2],["தறநெறி","the path of dharma is to be joined after knowing it within",3],["பன்னூலும்","the meaning of the many books is to be examined",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:52":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":54,"t":["யாறுள் அடங்கும் குளமுள வீறுசால்","மன்னர் விழையுங் குடியுள - தொன்மரபின்","வேதம் உறுவன பாட்டுள வேளாண்மை","வேள்வியோ டொப்ப உள"],"p":"","r":"There are tanks that hold rivers within them, there are subjects whom glorious kings desire, there are songs that reach the ancient Veda, and there are acts of beneficence equal to sacrifice.","n":"Each gem says a lesser thing can equal a greater: a tank a river, a subject the king's desire, a song the Veda, giving a sacrifice. வேளாண்மை is read as beneficence, as in the Kural, though it can also mean husbandry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velanmai","vedham","kudi","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 52","lb":"NM52","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["யாறுள் அடங்கும் குளமுள","there are tanks that contain rivers",1],["மன்னர் விழையுங் குடியுள","there are subjects that glorious kings desire",2],["வேதம் உறுவன பாட்டுள","there are songs that attain to the ancient Veda",3],["வேளாண்மை","there is beneficence equal to sacrifice",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:53":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":55,"t":["எருதுடையான் வேளாளன்; ஏலாதான் பார்ப்பான்;","ஒருதொடையான் வெல்வது கோழி - உருபோடு","அறிவுடையாள் இல்வாழ்க்கைப் பெண்ணென்ப சேனைச்","செறிவுடையான் சேனா பதி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the vellalan is the one who owns oxen, the brahmin the one who does not accept, the cock the one that wins with a single strike, the woman of household life one who has wisdom with her beauty, and the commander the one who holds his army close.","n":"The verse counts five, not four. வேளாளன் and பார்ப்பான் are birth-group names, and the verse defines each by a mark (oxen; not accepting), which can be read as naming what each group should have or as limiting the name to those who have it. ஏலாதான் is read as one who does not accept gifts; it may mean one who does not take up some work.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","vaazhkkaithunai","padai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 53","lb":"NM53","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வேளாளன்","the vellalan is the man who owns oxen",1],["பார்ப்பான்","the brahmin is one who does not accept",1],["கோழி","the cock is what wins with one strike",2],["இல்வாழ்க்கைப் பெண்","the woman of household life is one with wisdom as well as beauty",3],["சேனா பதி","the commander is the one who holds his army close",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","gender"],"defines birth-group names (vellalan, brahmin) and the wife by the marks the period expected of them"],"ch":"nm:3"},"nm:54":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":56,"t":["யானை யுடையார் கதன்உவப்பர் மன்னர்","கடும்பரிமாக் காதலித் தூர்வர் - கொடுங்குழை","நல்லாரை நல்லவர் நாணுவப்பர் அல்லாரை","அல்லார் உவப்பது கேடு"],"p":"","r":"Kings who own elephants delight in their fury and love to ride swift horses, good men delight in the modesty of good women with curved earrings, and the bad delighting in the bad is ruin.","n":"Three delights are named as fitting to their owners, and the fourth turns the pattern: when like delights in like among the bad, the delight is ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","naanam","arasu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 54","lb":"NM54","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"king","it":[["யானை யுடையார் கதன்","kings who own elephants delight in their fury",1],["கடும்பரிமாக்","kings love to ride swift horses",2],["நல்லாரை","good men delight in the modesty of good women",3],["அல்லாரை","the bad delighting in the bad is ruin",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:55":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":57,"t":["கண்ணிற் சிறந்த உறுப்பில்லை கொண்டானின்","துன்னிய கேளிர் பிறரில்லை மக்களின்","ஒண்மையவாய்ச் சான்ற பொருளில்லை ஈன்றாளோ","டெண்ணக் கடவுளு மில்"],"p":"","r":"No organ excels the eye, no kin is closer than the husband, no wealth is brighter or fuller than children, and no god is to be counted with the mother who bore one.","n":"Four superlatives each name the first of its kind; the second speaks from a wife's place (கொண்டான், the man who took her) and the fourth sets the mother above any god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","sutram","kan","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 55","lb":"NM55","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணிற்","no bodily organ is better than the eye",1],["கொண்டானின்","no kin is closer than the husband",1],["மக்களின்","no wealth is brighter or fuller than children",2],["ஈன்றாளோ","no god can be counted alongside the mother who bore one",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"ranks the husband as a wife's closest kin, a household norm of the period"],"ch":"nm:3"},"nm:56":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":58,"t":["கற்றன்னர் கற்றாரைக் காதலர் கண்ணோடார்","செற்றன்னர் செற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தவர் - தெற்றென","உற்ற துரையாதார் உள்கரந்து பாம்புறையும்","புற்றன்னர் புல்லறிவி னார்"],"p":"","r":"Those who love the learned are like the learned, those without kindly regard and those who join enemies are like enemies, and the small-minded who do not plainly say what has befallen them are like an anthill where a snake lives hidden.","n":"The grouping of lines 1 and 2 is unclear: கண்ணோடார் may stand alone or with செற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தவர். The verse judges people by what they cling to and by what they conceal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","kalvi","kannottam","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 56","lb":"NM56","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றாரைக் காதலர்","those who love the learned are like the learned",1],["கண்ணோடார்","those without kindly regard are like enemies",1],["செற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தவர்","those who join enemies are like enemies",2],["புல்லறிவி னார்","the small-minded who hide what befell them are like an anthill hiding a snake",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:57":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":59,"t":["மாண்டவர் மாண்ட வினைபெறுப வேண்டாதார்","வேண்டா வினையும் பெறுபவே - யாண்டும்","பிறப்பார் பிறப்பார் அறனின் புறுவர்","துறப்பார் துறக்கத் தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The excellent gain excellent deeds, those who are not wanted gain unwanted deeds too, those who delight in dharma are born again everywhere, and those who renounce belong to heaven.","n":"Line 3 is hard to divide: பிறப்பார் is doubled, and அறனின் புறுவர் can be read as those who rejoice in dharma. On the reading taken here it sets household dharma, which leads to rebirth, against renunciation, which leads to heaven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 57","lb":"NM57","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மாண்டவர்","the excellent gain excellent deeds",1],["வேண்டாதார்","the unwanted gain unwanted deeds as well",1],["பிறப்பார்","those who delight in dharma are born again",3],["துறப்பார்","those who renounce are of heaven",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:58":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":60,"t":["என்றும் உளவாகும் நாளும் இருசுடரும்","என்றும் பிணியும் தொழிலொக்கும் - என்றும்","கொடுப்பாரும் கொள்வாரும் அன்னர் பிறப்பாரும்","சாவாரும் என்றும் உளர்"],"p":"","r":"The days and the two lights exist always, disease and labour are always alike, givers and takers are always the same, and those born and those who die always exist.","n":"Where most verses on this theme stress that the person passes, this one stresses that the pattern stays: sun and moon, sickness and work, giving and taking, birth and death are permanent features of the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","piravi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 58","lb":"NM58","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நாளும் இருசுடரும்","the days and the two lights always exist",1],["பிணியும்","disease and labour are always alike present",2],["கொடுப்பாரும் கொள்வாரும்","givers and takers are always the same",3],["பிறப்பாரும்","those born and those dying always exist",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:59":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":61,"t":["இனிதுண்பா னென்பான் உயிர்கொல்லா துண்பான்","முனிதக்கா னென்பான் முகன்ஒழிந்து வாழ்வான்","தனிய னெனப்படுவான் செய்தநன் றில்லான்","இனிய னெனப்படுவான் யார்யார்க்கே யானும்","முனியா ஒழுக்கத் தவன்"],"p":"","r":"The one who eats sweetly is the one who eats without killing life, the one fit to be shunned is the one who lives with a turned-away face, the one called lonely is he who has done no good, and the one called pleasant is he whose conduct no one resents.","n":"Each gem redefines a common word by conduct: sweet eating by not killing, loneliness by having done no good. The verse runs to five lines where the others have four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","ozhukkam","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 59","lb":"NM59","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இனிதுண்பா னென்பான்","the one who eats sweetly is he who eats without killing",1],["முனிதக்கா னென்பான்","the one fit to be resented is he who lives without a kind face",2],["தனிய னெனப்படுவான்","the lonely one is he who has done no good",3],["இனிய னெனப்படுவான்","the pleasant one is he whose conduct no one resents",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"names eating without killing as the sweet way of eating, a diet rule of non-killing"],"ch":"nm:3"},"nm:60":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":62,"t":["ஈத்துண்பா னென்பான் இசைநடுவான் மற்றவன்","கைத்துண்பான் காங்கி யெனப்படுவான் தெற்ற","நகையாகும் நண்ணார்முன் சேறல் பகையாகும்","பாடறியா தானை இரவு"],"p":"","r":"The one who eats after giving is the one who plants fame, the one who eats from another's hand is called a kaangi, going before those who are not one's own becomes plainly laughable, and begging from one who does not know one's plight becomes enmity.","n":"காங்கி is a rare word; it plainly names the dependant who lives from another's hand. The first two gems set giver against taker, the last two count the costs of asking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","iravu","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 60","lb":"NM60","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஈத்துண்பா னென்பான்","the one who eats after giving plants fame",1],["கைத்துண்பான்","the one who eats from another's hand is called a kaangi",2],["நண்ணார்முன் சேறல்","going before those not close to one becomes a laughing-stock",3],["பாடறியா தானை இரவு","begging from one who does not know one's plight turns to enmity",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:3"},"nm:61":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":63,"t":["நெய்விதிர்ப்ப நந்தும் நெருப்பழல் சேர்ந்து","வழுத்த வரங்கொடுப்பர் நாகர் - தொழுத்திறந்து","கன்றூட்ட நந்துங் கறவை கலம்பரப்பி","நன்றூட்ட நந்தும் விருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names four things that thrive by being given to: fire when ghee is sprinkled on it, the gods who grant boons when approached and praised, the milch-cow when the stall is opened for her calf to suckle, and hospitality when vessels are laid out and guests are fed well.","n":"The verb நந்தும் (thrives) is repeated for fire, cow and guest-feeding, so the verse makes a single law of increase by outflow: the cow thrives by letting the calf drink, the household's hospitality by feeding. நாகர் is read here as the celestials; the verse does not say more about them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","eegai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 61","lb":"NM61","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நெருப்பழல்","the fire thrives when ghee is sprinkled on it",1],["நாகர்","the gods grant boons when approached and praised",2],["கறவை","the milch-cow thrives when the stall is opened and her calf is let to suckle",3],["விருந்து","hospitality thrives when vessels are spread and guests are fed well",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:62":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":64,"t":["பழியின்மை மக்களால் காண்க வொருவன்","கெழியின்மை கேட்டா லறிக பொருளின்","நிகழ்ச்சியா னாக்க மறிக புகழ்ச்சியால்","போற்றாதார் போற்றப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"A man's freedom from blame is to be seen in his children, his want of true friends is learned in his ruin, his prosperity is known by how his wealth moves, and by praise even those who did not esteem him are brought to esteem him.","n":"கேட்டால் can be 'by ruin' (கேடு) or 'by hearing'; the reading takes ruin, which makes adversity the test of friendship. The last item breaks the 'know X by Y' pattern: புகழ்ச்சி may be one's fame or flattery offered to others, and the line does not settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pazhi","natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 62","lb":"NM62","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பழியின்மை","a man's blamelessness is seen through his children",1],["கெழியின்மை","his lack of true friends is learned when ruin comes",2],["பொருளின்","his prosperity is known by the movement of his wealth",2],["புகழ்ச்சியால்","by praise even those who did not esteem him come to esteem him",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:63":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":65,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளும் காண்புழிக் காதற்றாம் பெண்ணின்","உருவின்றி மாண்ட வுளவா - மொருவழி","நாட்டுள்ளும் நல்ல பதியுள பாட்டுள்ளும்","பாடெய்தும் பாட லுள"],"p":"","r":"Within each kind some excel: among eyes some are dear to the one who looks, among women some are excellent without beauty of form, within a country there are good towns, and among songs there are songs that win distinction.","n":"The four gems share one form (within X there are Y that excel), so worth is placed inside a class rather than given to the class. The first line is compressed; காதற்றாம் is taken as 'dear', but the clause could be read otherwise. The second item separates a woman's worth from her looks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 63","lb":"NM63","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணுள்ளும்","among eyes, some are dear to the one who looks at them",1],["பெண்ணின்","among women, some are excellent though without beauty of form",1],["நல்ல பதி","within a country there are towns that are good",3],["பாட்டுள்ளும்","among songs there are some that attain distinction",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:64":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":66,"t":["திரியழல் காணில் தொழுப விறகின்","எறியழல் காணின் இகழ்ப - ஒருகுடியில்","கல்லாது மூத்தானைக் கைவிட்டுக் கற்றான்","இளமைபா ராட்டும் உலகு"],"p":"","r":"People worship fire seen as the flame of a wick and scorn it seen as the blaze of firewood; likewise, in one family, the world passes over the elder who grew old without learning and praises the youth of the one who learned.","n":"The first two gems are one fire valued by its setting; the last two turn that image on a household, so age, like the substance of fire, does not decide worth. The ranking is inside ஒருகுடி, one family, where seniority would normally rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 64","lb":"NM64","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["திரியழல்","fire as the flame of a wick is worshipped",1],["எறியழல்","fire as a blaze of firewood is scorned",2],["கல்லாது மூத்தானைக்","the elder who grew old without learning is passed over",3],["இளமை","the youth of the one who learned is praised",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:65":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":67,"t":["கைத்துடையான் காமுற்ற துண்டாகும் வித்தின்","முளைக்குழாம் நீருண்டேல் உண்டாம் திருக்குழாம்","ஒண்செய்யாள் பார்த்துறின் உண்டாகும் மற்றவள்","துன்புறுவாள் ஆகின் கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"What the man with wealth desires comes to be, a cluster of sprouts comes from seed if there is water, a host of fortune comes if the bright red lady looks on a man, and if she is grieved it perishes.","n":"ஒண்செய்யாள் is the goddess of fortune. The verse runs from wealth's power to its dependence: the rich get what they want, but the wealth itself waits on her glance, as sprouts wait on water. The fourth gem is the third reversed; one could count three things and a consequence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","oozh","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 65","lb":"NM65","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கைத்துடையான் காமுற்ற","what the man with wealth desires comes to be",1],["முளைக்குழாம்","a cluster of sprouts comes from seed if there is water",2],["திருக்குழாம்","a host of fortune comes if the bright lady looks on one",2],["மற்றவள்","if she is grieved, the fortune perishes",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:66":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":68,"t":["ஊனுண் டுழுவை நிறம்பெறூஉம் நீர்நிலத்துப்","புல்லினான் இன்புறூங்உங் காலேயம் - நெல்லின்","அரிசியான் இன்புறூங்உங் கீழெல்லாந் தத்தம்","வரிசையான் இன்புறூஉம் மேல்"],"p":"","r":"The tiger gains its sheen by eating flesh, cattle delight in the grass of wet land, all the low delight in rice from paddy, and the high delight in their own proper standing.","n":"கீழ் and மேல் rank people, but the verse ranks them by what satisfies them, not by a birth rule; it states no rule of who may eat or be fed, so it carries no norm. It sets the low with the tiger and the cow as creatures pleased by food, and the high apart, pleased by வரிசை (their rank and its due).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","undi","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 66","lb":"NM66","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["டுழுவை","the tiger gains its colour by eating flesh",1],["காலேயம்","cattle delight in the grass of wet land",2],["கீழெல்லாந்","all the low delight in rice",3],["மேல்","the high delight in their own due standing",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:67":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":69,"t":["பின்னவாம் பின்னதிர்க்குஞ் செய்வினை என்பெறினும்","முன்னவாம் முன்னம் அறிந்தார்கட்கு - என்னும்","அவாவாம் அடைந்தார்கட்கு உள்ளம் தவாவாம்","அவாவிலார் செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"","r":"Deeds that will recoil show it only afterward, whatever they bring, but to those who knew in advance they show it beforehand; for those who have gained things the heart is ever craving; and the deeds of those without craving do not fail.","n":"The syntax of lines 1 and 2 is dense, with பின்னவாம் and முன்னவாம் played against each other; the reading is one defensible parse. The verse pairs foresight with freedom from craving as the two things that make a deed safe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 67","lb":"NM67","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பின்னதிர்க்குஞ் செய்வினை","deeds that will recoil show their result only afterward",1],["முன்னம் அறிந்தார்கட்கு","to those who knew beforehand, such deeds show themselves in advance",2],["அடைந்தார்கட்கு உள்ளம்","the heart of those who have obtained is ever craving",3],["அவாவிலார் செய்யும் வினை","the deeds of those without craving do not fail",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:68":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":70,"t":["கைத்தில்லார் நல்லவர் கைத்துண்டாய்க் காப்பாரின்","வைத்தாரின் நல்லர் வறியவர் - பைத்தெழுந்து","வைதாரின் நல்லர் பொறுப்பவர் செய்தாரின்","நல்லர் சிதையா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"Those without wealth are better than those who have it and only guard it, the poor are better than those who have stored it up, those who bear are better than those who swell up and revile, and those who do not break are better than those who did.","n":"Each gem is a ranking (X is better than Y), so the verse praises poverty only against hoarding, not in itself. பைத்தெழுந்து pictures the reviler rising like a cobra's spread hood. The last line is obscure: சிதையாதவர் may be those who do not ruin others or those who do not falter, and செய்தார் those who did harm or did a deed and spoiled it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","porai","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 68","lb":"NM68","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கைத்தில்லார்","those without wealth are better than those who have it and only guard it",1],["வறியவர்","the poor are better than those who have laid wealth by",2],["பொறுப்பவர்","those who bear are better than those who rise up and revile",3],["சிதையா தவர்","those who do not break are better than those who did",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:69":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":71,"t":["மகனுரைக்கும் தந்தை நலத்தை ஒருவன்","முகனுரைக்கும் உள்நின்ற வேட்கை - அகல்நீர்ப்","புலத்தியல்பு புக்கான் உரைக்கும் நிலத்தியல்பு","வானம் உரைத்து விடும்"],"p":"","r":"A son tells of his father's goodness, a man's face tells the desire that stands within him, one who has entered a wide well-watered field tells its nature, and the sky tells the nature of the land.","n":"Every gem is a sign that discloses something hidden, so the verse is about inference: the son is evidence of the father as rain is of the land. The third item may contrast with the fourth: one learns a field by going into it, a land by its rain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","makkal","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 69","lb":"NM69","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தந்தை நலத்தை","a father's goodness is told by his son",1],["உள்நின்ற வேட்கை","the desire within a man is told by his face",2],["புலத்தியல்பு","the nature of a watered field is told by one who has entered it",3],["நிலத்தியல்பு","the nature of the land is told by the sky",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:70":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":72,"t":["பதிநன்று பல்லார் உறையின் ஒருவன்","மதிநன்று மாசறக் கற்பின் - நுதிமருப்பின்","ஏற்றான்வீ றெய்தும் இனநிரை தான்கொடுக்குஞ்","சோற்றான்வீ றெய்தும் குடி"],"p":"","r":"A town is good if many live in it, a man's mind is good if he learns without fault, the sharp-horned bull gains glory by the herd of its own kind, and a family gains glory by the food it gives.","n":"Two gems say what makes a thing good (நன்று) and two what gives it glory (வீறு); in each the worth comes from what surrounds or flows from it, except the mind, which is made good from within by learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 70","lb":"NM70","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பதி","a town is good if many live in it",1],["மதி","a man's mind is good if he learns without fault",2],["ஏற்றான்","the sharp-horned bull gains glory by its herd",3],["குடி","a family gains glory by the food it gives",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:71":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":73,"t":["ஊர்ந்தான் வகைய கலினமா நேர்ந்தொருவன்","ஆற்றல் வகைய வறஞ்செய்கை தொட்ட","குளத்தனைய தூம்பின் அகலங்கள் தத்தம்","வளத்தனைய வாழ்வார் வழக்கு"],"p":"","r":"A bridled horse is as its rider makes it, a man's doing of virtue is according to his strength, the width of the sluices is according to the tank that was dug, and the ways of those who live are according to their means.","n":"Every gem says 'according to' (வகைய, அனைய), so virtue is measured like a horse, a sluice and a household budget. The verse does not excuse doing little; it sizes the duty to the capacity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aram","alavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 71","lb":"NM71","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கலினமா","a bridled horse is according to its rider",1],["வறஞ்செய்கை","the doing of virtue is according to one's strength",2],["தூம்பின் அகலங்கள்","the width of the sluices is according to the dug tank",3],["வாழ்வார் வழக்கு","the ways of those who live are according to their means",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:72":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":74,"t":["ஊழியும் யாண்டெண்ணி யாத்தன யாமமும்","நாழிகை யானே நடந்தன - தாழியாத்","தெற்றென்றார் கண்ணே தெளிந்தனர் வெட்கென்றார்","வெஞ்சொலா லின்புறு வார்"],"p":"","r":"Aeons pass reckoned by years and the night watches by naazhigai; clear understanding is found in those who are clear without delay; and the harsh delight in harsh words.","n":"The first two gems say great spans are made of small units. தாழியா and தெற்றென்றார் are hard; the reading takes them as 'without delaying' and 'the clear-minded', which makes the third gem a claim that clarity shows itself at once. The last gem explains harsh speech by the speaker's nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","thelivu","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 72","lb":"NM72","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஊழியும்","aeons pass by the count of years",1],["யாமமும்","the night watches pass by the naazhigai",1],["தெற்றென்றார்","clear understanding rests with those who are clear without delay",3],["வெட்கென்றார்","the harsh delight in harsh words",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:73":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":75,"t":["கற்றான் தளரின் எழுந்திருக்கும் கல்லாத","பேதையான் வீழ்வானேல் கால்முரியும் எல்லாம்","ஒருமைத்தான் செய்த கருவி தெரிவெண்ணின்","பொய்யாவித் தாகி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"If the learned man stumbles he rises again, if the unlearned fool falls his leg breaks, and, considered well, any means made with single-minded care becomes a seed that does not fail.","n":"The verse counts three things, not the book's usual four: the learned man's fall, the fool's fall, and the single-minded means. The third gem gives the reason for the first two: learning is a means made with care, and so it holds when one slips.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pedhaimai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 73","lb":"NM73","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றான்","the learned man rises again if he stumbles",1],["பேதையான்","the unlearned fool breaks his leg if he falls",2],["ஒருமைத்தான் செய்த கருவி","a means made with single-minded care becomes an unfailing seed",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:74":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":76,"t":["தேவ ரன்னர் புலவரும் தேவர்","தமரனையர் ஓரூர் உறைவார் - தமருள்ளும்","பெற்றன்னர் பேணி வழிபடுவார் கற்றன்னர்","கற்றாரைக் காத லவர்"],"p":"","r":"Poets are like gods, those who live in one town are like kin, among kin those who cherish and serve one are like parents, and those who love the learned are like the learned.","n":"Each gem lifts one group to the rank of another (gods, kin, parents, the learned), so nearness and devotion confer standing. The last gem lets the unlearned share the rank of learning through love of the learned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sutram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 74","lb":"NM74","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["புலவரும்","poets are like gods",1],["ஓரூர் உறைவார்","those who live in one town are like kin",2],["பேணி வழிபடுவார்","among kin, those who cherish and serve one are like parents",3],["கற்றாரைக் காத லவர்","those who love the learned are like the learned",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:75":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":77,"t":["தூர்ந்தொழியும் பொய்பிறந்த போழ்தே மருத்துவன்","சொல்கென்ற போழ்தே பிணியுரைக்கும் - நல்லார்","விடுகென்ற போழ்தே விடுக உரியான்","தருகெனின் தாயம் வகுத்து"],"p":"","r":"Something is choked off the moment a lie is born, a physician names the disease the moment he is asked to speak, good people should be let go the moment they ask to leave, and the rightful heir, when he asks, should be given his share by division.","n":"Every gem turns on போழ்தே (at that very moment): each act is due at once. The first line does not name what is filled in and lost when a lie is born (trust, merit or friendship); the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","noy","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 75","lb":"NM75","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்","the moment a lie is born, something is choked off and lost",1],["மருத்துவன்","a physician names the disease as soon as he is asked",1],["நல்லார்","good people should be let go as soon as they ask to leave",2],["உரியான்","the rightful heir should be given his divided share when he asks",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:76":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":78,"t":["நாக்கி னறிப இனியதை மூக்கினான்","மோந்தறிப எல்லா மலர்களும் நோக்குள்ளும்","கண்ணினால் காண்ப அணியவற்றைத் தொக்கிருந்து","எண்ணினான் எண்ணப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"By the tongue one knows the sweet, by smelling with the nose one knows all flowers, with the eyes one sees ornaments, and what is to be reasoned is reasoned by thought, sitting together.","n":"Three senses are each matched to their own objects, and then thought (எண்ணம்) is given as a fourth faculty with its own object. தொக்கிருந்து (gathering together) makes reasoning a shared act, as in counsel, where the senses work alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","aaraaydhal","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 76","lb":"NM76","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நாக்கி","the tongue knows what is sweet",1],["மூக்கினான்","the nose knows all flowers by smell",1],["கண்ணினால்","the eyes see ornaments",3],["எண்ணினான்","what is to be reasoned is reasoned by thought, together",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:77":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":79,"t":["சாவாத எல்லை பிறந்த உயிரெல்லாம்","தாவாத இல்லை வலிகளும் - மூவா","இளமை இசைந்தாரும் இல்லை வளமையிற்","கேடுஇன்றிச் சென்றாரும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"There is no bound within which born lives do not die, no strengths that do not decline, no one who has gained unaging youth, and no one who has passed through prosperity without ruin.","n":"All four gems are negations (இல்லை, இல்), so the verse is a list of things that do not exist. It draws no counsel from impermanence; it only states it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 77","lb":"NM77","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறந்த உயிரெல்லாம்","there is no bound within which born lives escape death",1],["வலிகளும்","there are no strengths that do not decline",2],["இளமை இசைந்தாரும்","no one has gained unaging youth",3],["கேடுஇன்றிச் சென்றாரும்","no one has passed through prosperity without ruin",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:78":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":80,"t":["சொல்லான் அறிப ஒருவனை மெல்லென்ற","நீரான் அறிப மடுவினை - யார்கண்ணும்","ஒப்புரவினான் அறிப சான்றாண்மை மெய்க்கண்","மகிழான் அறிப நறா"],"p":"","r":"A man is known by his speech, a pool by its soft water, nobility by helpfulness toward anyone, and toddy by the joy it brings to the body.","n":"Each thing is known by a sign that comes out of it. Toddy closes the list without blame: the verse uses it as a plain case of a thing known by its effect, set level with nobility. யார்கண்ணும் (toward anyone at all) is what makes oppuravu a test of worth rather than favour to kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","oppuravu","sol","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 78","lb":"NM78","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒருவனை","a man is known by his speech",1],["மடுவினை","a pool is known by its soft water",2],["சான்றாண்மை","nobility is known by helpfulness toward anyone",3],["நறா","toddy is known by the joy in the body",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:79":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":81,"t":["நாவன்றோ நட்பறுக்கும் தேற்றமில் பேதை","விடுமன்றோ வீங்கிப் பிணிப்பின் அவாஅப்","படுமன்றோ பன்னூல் வலையிற் கெடுமன்றோ","மாறுள் நிறுக்கும் துணிபு"],"p":"","r":"Is it not the tongue that cuts friendship, does not the undiscerning fool let go even when tightly bound, is not craving caught in the net of many books, and does not a resolve set amid enmity come to ruin?","n":"All four gems are rhetorical questions with அன்றோ. The second and fourth lines are hard: வீங்கிப் பிணிப்பின் is read as 'though bound tight', and மாறுள் நிறுக்கும் துணிபு as a resolve weighed or set among foes. The third gem makes learning a net that catches craving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","avaa","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 79","lb":"NM79","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நா","the tongue cuts friendship",1],["தேற்றமில் பேதை","the undiscerning fool lets go though tightly bound",1],["அவாஅப்","craving is caught in the net of many books",2],["துணிபு","a resolve set amid enmity comes to ruin",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:80":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":82,"t":["கொடுப்பின் அசனங் கொடுக்க விடுப்பின்","உயிரிடை ஈட்டை விடுக்க எடுப்பிற்","கிளையுட் கழிந்தார் எடுக்க கெடுப்பின்","வெகுளி கெடுத்து விடல்"],"p":"","r":"If one gives, one should give food; if one lets go, one should let go of the hoard while life remains; if one lifts, one should lift the kin who have fallen; and if one destroys, one should destroy anger.","n":"The verse is built on four conditionals (கொடுப்பின், விடுப்பின், எடுப்பின், கெடுப்பின்), each naming the best object of a verb. உயிரிடை ஈட்டை is unclear: it may be the hoard one holds while alive, or gain got from living beings; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","sutram","vekuli","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 80","lb":"NM80","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அசனங்","if one gives, food is what to give",1],["உயிரிடை ஈட்டை","if one lets go, the hoard held while alive is what to let go",2],["கிளையுட் கழிந்தார்","if one lifts, the fallen among one's kin are whom to lift",3],["வெகுளி","if one destroys, anger is what to destroy",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:4"},"nm:81":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":83,"t":["நலனும் இளமையும் நல்குரவின் கீழ்ச்சாம்","குலனும் குடிமையும் கல்லாமைக் கீழ்ச்சாம்","வளமில் குளத்தின்கீழ் நெற்சாம் பரமல்லாப்","பண்டத்தின் கீழ்ச்சாம் பகடு"],"p":"","r":"Beauty and youth are said to die beneath poverty, lineage and family standing beneath want of learning, paddy beneath a tank that has no water, and a draught ox beneath a load beyond its strength.","n":"Every item repeats கீழ்ச்சாம், 'dies beneath': each good thing is killed by what should be under it and is missing or too heavy. Lineage, in the Kural's terms a matter of birth, is set among things that need a support from below to live.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","inmai","kudi","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 81","lb":"NM81","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நலனும் இளமையும்","beauty and youth die under poverty",1],["குலனும் குடிமையும்","lineage and family standing die under want of learning",2],["நெற்","paddy dies below a tank with no water in it",3],["பகடு","a draught ox dies under a load beyond its strength",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:82":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":84,"t":["நல்லார்க்கும் தம்மூரென் றூரில்லை நன்னெறிச்","செல்வார்க்கும் தம்மூரென் றூரில்லை - அல்லாக்","கடைகட்கும் தம்மூரென் றூரில்லை தங்கைத்","துடையார்க்கும் எவ்வூரு மூர்"],"p":"","r":"The good, those who walk the good path, and the base outcasts all have no town they call their own, while for those with something in hand every town is their town.","n":"The refrain 'no town is their own' must mean opposite things: the good and the renouncer belong everywhere, the base are kept nowhere. The last line turns it again: for the man with means every town is his, so the belonging the first two earn by virtue the rich buy. கடைகள் (the lowest) is a word of contempt; whether it ranks people by birth or by conduct the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","saandror","thuravu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 82","lb":"NM82","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நல்லார்க்கும்","the good have no single town of their own",1],["நன்னெறிச் செல்வார்க்கும்","those who go the good path have no town of their own",1],["அல்லாக் கடைகட்கும்","the worthless lowest have no town of their own",2],["தங்கைத் துடையார்க்கும்","for those who have wealth in hand every town is their town",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:83":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":85,"t":["கல்லா ஒருவர்க்குத் தம்வாயிற் சொற்கூற்றம்","மெல்லிலை வாழைக்குத் தானீன்ற காய்கூற்றம்","அல்லவை செய்வார்க்கு அறங்கூற்றம் கூற்றமே","இல்லத்துத் தீங்கொழுகு வாள்"],"p":"","r":"For the unlearned man his own speech is death, for the soft-leaved plantain the fruit it bears is death, for those who do wrong dharma is death, and death too is the woman who lives wickedly in the house.","n":"Each death comes from inside the one it kills: one's own words, one's own fruit, the dharma one breaks, one's own wife. The plantain, which dies after fruiting, is the image that holds the four together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kallaamai","aram","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 83","lb":"NM83","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தம்வாயிற் சொற்","his own words are death to the unlearned man",1],["தானீன்ற காய்","the fruit it bears is death to the plantain",2],["அறங்","dharma is death to those who do wrong",3],["இல்லத்துத் தீங்கொழுகு வாள்","the wife who acts wickedly in the house is death",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:84":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":86,"t":["நீரான்வீ றெய்தும் விளைநிலம் நீர்வழங்கும்","பண்டத்தாற் பாடெய்தும் பட்டினம் - கொண்டாளும்","நாட்டான்வீ றெய்துவர் மன்னவர் கூத்தொருவன்","ஆடலாற் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"","r":"Cultivated land gains its glory by water, a port town its greatness by the goods it trades, kings their glory by the land they rule, and a dancer his name by his dancing.","n":"The king stands in a row with a field, a port and a dancer: each is great by the one thing it works on, so the king's glory is borrowed from the land he holds, not his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","naadu","pugazh","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 84","lb":"NM84","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["விளைநிலம்","cultivated land gains glory by water",1],["பட்டினம்","a port town gains greatness by the goods it trades",2],["மன்னவர்","kings gain glory by the land they rule",3],["கூத்தொருவன்","a dancer gains repute by his dance",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:85":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":87,"t":["ஒன்றூக்கல் பெண்டிர் தொழில்நலம் என்றும்","நன்றூக்கல் அந்தணர் உள்ளம் பிறனாளும்","நாடூக்கல் மன்னர் தொழில்நலம் கேடூக்கல்","கேளிர் ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names striving after one alone as the excellence of women's work, striving always after the good as the mind of the அந்தணர், striving for the land another rules as the excellence of kings' work, and leaving one's kin as striving after one's own ruin.","n":"All four turn on ஊக்கல், striving, so the verse assigns each station its proper object of effort; the fourth breaks the pattern by naming a striving that ruins. ஒன்றூக்கல் for women is most likely single devotion to the husband. The அந்தணர் are given a mind rather than a rite, which leaves open whether the word names a birth-group or the virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","andhanar","mannar","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 85","lb":"NM85","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஒன்றூக்கல்","striving after one alone is the excellence of women's work",1],["நன்றூக்கல்","always striving after good is the mind of the அந்தணர்",2],["நாடூக்கல்","striving for land another rules is the excellence of kings' work",3],["கேளிர் ஒரீஇ விடல்","abandoning one's kin is striving after ruin",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm assigning women the single-minded devotion that is their proper work, beside the work proper to andhanar and kings"],"ch":"nm:5"},"nm:86":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":88,"t":["கள்ளாமை வேண்டும் கடிய வருதலால்","தள்ளாமை வேண்டும் தகுதி யுடையன","நள்ளாமை வேண்டும் சிறியரோடு யார்மாட்டும்","கொள்ளாமை வேண்டும் பகை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says one must not steal, since harsh things follow; must not cast off what is worthy; must not befriend the small; and must not take up enmity with anyone.","n":"Four negatives in -ஆமை, each with வேண்டும். Only the first gives a reason (கடிய வருதலால்). யார்மாட்டும் can close the third line (befriend the small with no one) or open the fourth (take enmity with no one); the sense survives either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","inam","pagai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 86","lb":"NM86","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளாமை","not stealing is required, because cruel consequences come",1],["தள்ளாமை","not rejecting what is worthy is required",2],["நள்ளாமை","not befriending small-minded men is required",3],["கொள்ளாமை","not taking up enmity is required",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:87":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":89,"t":["பெருக்குக நட்டாரை நன்றின்பா லுய்த்துத்","தருக்குக வொட்டாரைக் கால மறிந்தாங்கு","அருக்குக யார்மாட்டும் உண்டி சுருக்குக","செல்லா இடத்துச் சினம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse bids one increase friends by leading them to the good, stand proud against enemies when the time is known, eat sparingly wherever one is, and curb anger where it cannot take effect.","n":"The four imperatives rhyme (பெருக்குக, தருக்குக, அருக்குக, சுருக்குக): two increase, two decrease. Friends grow by what one does for them, not by numbers. The anger rule is limited to where anger 'does not go', leaving open anger where it can prevail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","undi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 87","lb":"NM87","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நட்டாரை","friends are to be increased by leading them to the good",1],["வொட்டாரை","towards enemies one is to be proud, knowing the right time",2],["உண்டி","food is to be kept scant wherever one is",3],["செல்லா இடத்துச் சினம்","anger where it cannot avail is to be curbed",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:88":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":90,"t":["மடிமை கெடுவார்கண் நிற்கும் கொடுமைதான்","பேணாமை செய்வார்கண் நிற்குமாம் பேணிய","நாணின் வரைநிற்பர் நற்பெண்டிர் நட்டமைந்த","தூணின்கண் நிற்கும் களிறு"],"p":"","r":"Sloth stays with those bound for ruin, cruelty stays with those who neglect care, good women stay within the bound of the shame they cherish, and the elephant stays at the post planted for it.","n":"The verb நிற்கும், 'stays', joins a vice that settles in a person with a restraint that holds one. Good women's shame is set beside the elephant's post: the bound is chosen and cherished (பேணிய), yet it is a tether.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","naanam","karpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 88","lb":"NM88","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மடிமை","sloth stays with those who are to perish",1],["கொடுமை","cruelty stays with those who neglect to care",1],["நற்பெண்டிர்","good women stay within the bound of their cherished shame",3],["களிறு","the elephant stays at the post planted for it",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm that good women keep within the limit set by நாண், compared to a tethered elephant"],"ch":"nm:5"},"nm:89":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":91,"t":["மறையறிய அந்தண் புலவர் முறையொடு","வென்றி அறிப அரசர்கள் - என்றும்","வணங்கல் அணிகலம் சான்றோர்க்கு அஃதன்றி","அணங்கல் வணங்கின்று பெண்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says learned அந்தணர் know the Veda, kings know victory joined with just rule, bowing is always the ornament of the worthy, and the last line sets a woman's humility apart, on one reading as her not being a torment.","n":"Each station has its proper excellence and the worthy have humility for theirs. The last line (அஃதன்றி அணங்கல் வணங்கின்று பெண்) is obscure: it may say a woman's bowing is not being an அணங்கு to her husband, or that a bowing woman is no அணங்கு. அந்தண் புலவர் may also be 'gracious learned men'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","saandror","andhanar","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 89","lb":"NM89","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அந்தண் புலவர்","learned அந்தணர் know the Veda",1],["அரசர்கள்","kings know victory together with just rule",2],["வணங்கல்","bowing is at all times the ornament of the worthy",3],["பெண்","for a woman, beyond that, humility lies in not afflicting",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm setting a separate humility for women beside the Veda of the andhanar and the victory of kings"],"ch":"nm:5"},"nm:90":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":92,"t":["பட்டாங்கே பட்டொழுகும் பண்புடையாள் காப்பினும்","பெட்டாங் கொழுகும் பிணையிலி - முட்டினும்","சென்றாங்கே சென்றொழுகும் காமம் கரப்பினும்","கொன்றான்மேல் நிற்குங் கொலை"],"p":"","r":"A woman of good character keeps to the way she was set in, one without a bond goes as she pleases though guarded, desire goes where it went though checked, and a killing, though hidden, rests on the killer.","n":"The common claim is that inner nature outruns outward control: guarding, blocking and hiding all fail. Desire and murder are the general cases that make the point about women more than a point about women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaamam","kolai","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 90","lb":"NM90","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பண்புடையாள்","a woman of good character keeps to the way set for her",1],["பிணையிலி","a woman with no bond goes as she pleases even when guarded",2],["காமம்","desire goes on where it was going even when obstructed",3],["கொலை","a killing, though concealed, rests on the killer",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period view of women's conduct as fixed by character, against which guarding them is useless"],"ch":"nm:5"},"nm:91":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":93,"t":["வன்கண் பெருகின் வலிபெருகும் பான்மொழியார்","இன்கண் பெருகின் இனம்பெருகும் சீர்சான்ற","மென்கண் பெருகின் அறம்பெருகும் வன்கண்","கயம்பெருகின் பாவம் பெரிது"],"p":"","r":"Where hardness of eye grows strength grows, where the sweet looks of soft-spoken women grow kin grow, where the gentle eye of the worthy grows dharma grows, and where the hard eye of the base grows sin is great.","n":"The same வன்கண் opens and closes the verse: in the first line it is daring that brings strength, in the last the hard eye of the base that brings sin. The eye is the measure throughout: hard, sweet, gentle, hard again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kayamai","aram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 91","lb":"NM91","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வன்கண்","where hardness of eye grows, strength grows",1],["பான்மொழியார் இன்கண்","where the sweet looks of women of milky speech grow, kin grow",1],["சீர்சான்ற மென்கண்","where the gentle eye of the worthy grows, dharma grows",2],["வன்கண் கயம்","where the hard eye of the base grows, sin is great",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:92":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":94,"t":["இளமைப் பருவத்துக் கல்லாமை குற்றம்","வளமிலாப் போழ்தத்து வள்ளன்மை குற்றம்","கிளைஞரில் போழ்திற் சினம்குற்றம் குற்றம்","தமரல்லார் கையகத் தூண்"],"p":"","r":"Not learning in youth, being liberal when one has no means, anger when one has no kin, and eating from the hand of those not one's own are each named a fault.","n":"Every fault is a virtue or need out of its time or place: learning missed in its season, giving without the means for it, anger without kin to stand behind it, food taken from strangers. The last is about dependence, not purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","kallaamai","eegai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 92","lb":"NM92","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இளமைப் பருவத்துக் கல்லாமை","not learning in youth is a fault",1],["வளமிலாப் போழ்தத்து வள்ளன்மை","liberality at a time without means is a fault",2],["கிளைஞரில் போழ்திற் சினம்","anger when one has no kin is a fault",3],["தமரல்லார் கையகத் தூண்","eating from the hand of those not one's own is a fault",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:93":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":95,"t":["எல்லா விடத்தும் கொலைதீது மக்களைக்","கல்லா வளர விடல்தீது - நல்லார்","நலந்தீது நாணற்று நிற்பின் குலந்தீது","கொள்கை யழிந்தக் கடை"],"p":"","r":"Killing is evil in every place, letting one's children grow up unlearned is evil, the beauty of good women is evil if they stand without shame, and lineage is evil when its principle is gone.","n":"Only killing is evil without condition (எல்லா விடத்தும்); the other three are goods that turn evil when something is lost. A family's standing is made to depend on its கொள்கை, its held principle, not on birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kalvi","naanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 93","lb":"NM93","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலை","killing is evil everywhere",1],["மக்களைக் கல்லா வளர விடல்","letting one's children grow up unlearned is evil",1],["நல்லார் நலந்","the beauty of good women is evil if they lose shame",2],["குலந்","lineage is evil when its principle is destroyed",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm making women's beauty a fault when it is not held in by நாண்"],"ch":"nm:5"},"nm:94":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":96,"t":["ஆசாரம் என்பது கல்வி அறஞ்சேர்ந்து","போகம் உடைமை பொருளாட்சி யார்கண்ணும்","கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மை முறைமை தெரிந்தாள்வான்","உண்ணாட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse defines ஆசாரம் as learning, the rule of wealth as having enjoyment joined with dharma, justice as showing favour to no one, and says the king who rules with discernment does not lack inner searching.","n":"A run of definitions (X என்பது Y). Calling ஆசாரம் simply learning is a strong claim beside the ritual sense of the word in the Acharakkovai. The division of the last two lines is not certain: உண்ணாட்டம் இன்மையும் இல் may mean the discerning ruler never fails to look inward, or that he lacks no inner inquiry.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","kalvi","sengol","kannottam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 94","lb":"NM94","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"king","it":[["ஆசாரம்","right conduct is learning",1],["பொருளாட்சி","command of wealth is having enjoyment joined with dharma",2],["முறைமை","justice is showing favour to no one",3],["தெரிந்தாள்வான்","the ruler who rules with discernment does not lack inner inquiry",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:95":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":97,"t":["கள்ளின் இடும்பை களியறியும் நீர்இடும்பை","புள்ளினுள் ஓங்கல் அறியும் நிரப்பிடும்பை","பல்பெண்டிர் ஆள னறியும் கரப்பிடும்பை","கள்வன் அறிந்து விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The drunkard knows the misery of toddy, the highest of birds knows the misery of thirst, the man of many wives knows the misery of want, and the thief knows the misery of concealment.","n":"Each misery is known only from inside: the one who suffers it is the only witness. புள்ளினுள் ஓங்கல் is most likely the bird said to drink only rain; the verse does not name it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kal","kalavu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 95","lb":"NM95","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளின் இடும்பை","the misery of toddy is known by the drunkard",1],["நீர்இடும்பை","the misery of thirst is known by the highest among birds",1],["நிரப்பிடும்பை","the misery of want is known by the man with many wives",2],["கரப்பிடும்பை","the misery of concealment is known by the thief",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:96":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":98,"t":["வடுச்சொல் நயமில்லார் வாய்த்தோன்றும் கற்றார்வாய்ச்","சாயிறுந் தோன்றா கரப்புச்சொல் - தீய","பரப்புச்சொல் சான்றோர்வாய்த் தோன்றா கரப்புச்சொல்","கீழ்கள்வாய்த் தோன்றி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Wounding words appear in the mouths of the graceless, deceitful words do not appear in the mouths of the learned even when they die, evil talebearing does not appear in the mouths of the worthy, and deceitful words appear in the mouths of the base.","n":"Two pairs of mouths: graceless and base speak, learned and worthy do not. கரப்புச்சொல் is repeated, once withheld and once spoken, which makes the verse a mirror. சாயிறுந் is probably a corruption of சாயினும், 'even if they die'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kadunjol","vanjam","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 96","lb":"NM96","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வடுச்சொல்","scarring words appear in the mouths of the graceless",1],["கரப்புச்சொல்","deceitful words do not appear in the mouths of the learned even if they die",2],["தீய பரப்புச்சொல்","evil talebearing does not appear in the mouths of the worthy",2],["கரப்புச்சொல்","deceitful words appear in the mouths of the base",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:97":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":99,"t":["வாலிழையார் முன்னர் வனப்பிலார் பாடிலர்","சாலும் அவைப்படின் கல்லாதான் பாடிலன்","கற்றான் ஒருவனும் பாடிலனே கல்லாதார்","பேதையார் முன்னர்ப் படின்"],"p":"","r":"Those without beauty have no standing before bejewelled women, the unlearned man has none if he enters a fit assembly, and even a learned man has none if he falls among the unlearned and foolish.","n":"Three items, not four: the verse names three who lose standing (பாடிலர், பாடிலன், பாடிலனே), and splitting கல்லாதார் பேதையார் into two audiences would not be defensible. The turn is the third: learning, which saves the second case, does not save one in the wrong audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi","kallaamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 97","lb":"NM97","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வனப்பிலார்","those without beauty have no standing before adorned women",1],["கல்லாதான்","the unlearned man has no standing in a fit assembly",2],["கற்றான் ஒருவனும்","even a learned man has no standing before the unlearned and foolish",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:98":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":100,"t":["மாசுபடினும் மணிதன்சீர் குன்றாதாம்","பூசிக் கொளினும் இரும்பின்கண் மாசொட்டும்","பாசத்துள் இட்டு விளக்கினும் கீழ்தன்னை","மாசுடைமை காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"A gem does not lose its worth though it is soiled, dirt sticks to iron though it is smeared and polished, and the base, though bound and scoured, show their own dirt.","n":"Three items: gem, iron and the base. The washing is the point: the gem needs none, the iron does not hold it, and the base, even scrubbed while held fast (பாசத்துள் இட்டு), show their nature again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 98","lb":"NM98","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மணி","a gem does not lose its worth though soiled",1],["இரும்பின்கண்","dirt clings to iron though it is coated",2],["கீழ்","the base, though bound and polished, show their own dirt",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:99":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":101,"t":["எண்ணொக்கும் சான்றோர் மரீஇயாரின் தீராமை","புண்ணெக்கும் போற்றார் உடனுறைவு - பண்ணிய","யாழொக்கும் நட்டார் கழறும்சொல் பாழொக்கும்","பண்புடையாள் இல்லா மனை"],"p":"","r":"Not parting from the worthy one keeps company with is like the eye, living with those who do not care for one is like a wound, the rebuke of friends is like the lute, and a house without a woman of good character is like a ruin.","n":"Four likenesses in ஒக்கும். The first word எண்ணொக்கும் is doubtful: as written it could mean 'like sesame' or 'like reckoning', and a reading 'like the eye' assumes கண். A friend's rebuke heard as lute music is the turn of the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saandror","idithal","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 99","lb":"NM99","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["சான்றோர் மரீஇயாரின் தீராமை","not leaving the worthy one is close to is like the eye, if the doubtful first word is read so",1],["போற்றார் உடனுறைவு","living with those who do not care for one is like a wound",2],["நட்டார் கழறும்சொல்","the rebuking words of friends are like the lute",3],["பண்புடையாள் இல்லா மனை","a house without a woman of good nature is like a ruin",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:100":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":102,"t":["ஏரி சிறிதாயின் நீரூரும் இல்லத்து","வாரி சிறிதாயின் பெண்ணூரும் மேலைத்","தவஞ்சிறி தாயின் வினையூரும் ஊரும்","உரன்சிறி தாயின் பகை"],"p":"","r":"If the tank is small the water overflows, if the household's income is small the wife takes over, if earlier penance was small deeds overrun one, and if strength is small the enemy overruns.","n":"ஊரும் carries the verse: water spills over, and the others ride over the one who is too small to hold them. பெண்ணூரும் most likely means the wife overrides the husband when he brings in too little; it is a claim about the household, not a rule. மேலைத் தவம் is penance done before, perhaps in earlier lives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","vali","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 100","lb":"NM100","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஏரி","when a tank is small its water overflows",1],["இல்லத்து வாரி","when the household's income is small the wife takes over",1],["மேலைத் தவஞ்","when earlier penance was small, karma overruns",2],["உரன்","when strength is small, enmity overruns",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:101":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":103,"t":["வைததனால் ஆகும் வசையே வணக்கமது","செய்ததனால் ஆகும் செழுங்கிளை செய்த","பொருளினால் ஆகுமாம் போகம் நெகிழ்ந்த","அருளினால் ஆகும் அறம்"],"p":"","r":"Reviling yields reproach, doing obeisance yields thriving kin, wealth one has made yields enjoyment, and grace that has softened yields dharma.","n":"A four-step table of causes, each with ஆகும். Enjoyment is tied to wealth and dharma to grace, not to wealth: the verse keeps the two apart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 101","lb":"NM101","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வைததனால்","reviling yields reproach",1],["வணக்கமது செய்ததனால்","doing obeisance yields thriving kin",1],["செய்த பொருளினால்","wealth one has made yields enjoyment",2],["நெகிழ்ந்த அருளினால்","softened grace yields dharma",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:102":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":104,"t":["ஒருவ னறிவானும் எல்லாம் யாதொன்றும்","ஒருவ னறியா தவனும் ஒருவன்","குணன் அடங்கக் குற்றமு ளானும் ஒருவன்","கணன்அடங்கக் கற்றானும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"There is no one who knows everything, no one who knows nothing at all, no one whose faults hide every virtue, and no one who has learned all the arts.","n":"The single closing இல் negates all four, so the verse is four 'there is none'. It denies both extremes of knowledge and both extremes of character at once. கணன் (the full count of the arts or treatises) is a rare word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","kutram","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 102","lb":"NM102","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒருவ னறிவானும் எல்லாம்","there is no one who knows everything",1],["யாதொன்றும் ஒருவ னறியா தவனும்","there is no one who knows nothing at all",1],["குணன் அடங்கக் குற்றமு ளானும்","there is no one whose faults cover all his virtues",3],["கணன்அடங்கக் கற்றானும்","there is no one who has learned the whole range of learning",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:103":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":105,"t":["மனைக்கு விளக்கம் மடவார் மடவார்","தமக்குத் தகைசால் புதல்வர் மனக்கினிய","காதற் புதல்வர்க்குக் கல்வியே கல்விக்கும்","ஓதிற் புகழ்சால் உணர்வு"],"p":"","r":"The light of a house is its wife, the light of wives is worthy sons, the light of beloved sons is learning, and the light of learning is understanding that brings fame.","n":"A chain: each light is lit by the next, so the house finally shines by understanding. The wife is the first link but not the last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 103","lb":"NM103","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["மடவார்","the wife is the light of the house",1],["தகைசால் புதல்வர்","worthy sons are the light of wives",2],["கல்வியே","learning is the light of beloved sons",3],["புகழ்சால் உணர்வு","understanding full of fame is the light of learning",4]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"nm:104":{"book":"nanmanikkadigai","num":106,"t":["இன்சொலான் ஆகும் கிழமை இனிப்பிலா","வன்சொலான் ஆகும் வசைமனம் - மென்சொலின்","நாவினால் ஆகும் அருள்மனம் அம்மனத்தான்","வீவிலா வீடாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Sweet speech makes close kinship, harsh speech without sweetness makes a reviling mind, a tongue of gentle speech makes a mind of grace, and with that mind one reaches the release that does not perish.","n":"A chain from the tongue to release: speech shapes the mind, and the mind gains வீடு. Harsh speech yields a fault in the speaker's own mind (வசைமனம்), not only others' anger. The verse is nearly the same as Sirupanchamoolam 89.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","kadunjol","arul","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nanmanikkadigai 104","lb":"NM104","poet":"விளம்பிநாகனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இன்சொலான்","sweet speech makes close kinship",1],["வன்சொலான்","harsh speech makes a reviling mind",2],["மென்சொலின் நாவினால்","a tongue of gentle speech makes a mind of grace",2],["அம்மனத்தான்","the one with that mind gains unperishing release",3]],"cw":"","ch":"nm:5"},"pz:payiram":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":1,"t":["பிண்டியின் நீழல் பெருமான் அடிவணங்கிப்","பண்டைப் பழமொழி நானூறும் - கொண்டினிதா","முன்றுறை மன்னவன் நான்கடியும் செய்தமைத்தான்","இன்றுறை வெண்பா இவை"],"p":"","r":"The prefatory verse says that the king of Munrurai, bowing at the feet of the Lord who sits in the shade of the pindi tree, made all four hundred old proverbs into four-line venpas and set them out sweetly.","n":"The pindi (asoka) tree is the tree under which the Jain Arhat is shown seated, so the bow places the work in a Jain frame without naming the faith. The proverbs are called பண்டை, already old: the poet claims to have set them in verse, not to have made them, and names himself only by his seat, முன்றுறை மன்னவன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhamozhi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru payiram","lb":"PZpayiram","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"pz:1"},"pz:0":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":2,"t":["அரிதவித்து ஆசின்று உணர்ந்தவன் பாதம்","விரிகடல் சூழ்ந்த வியன்கண்மா ஞாலத்து","உரியதனிற் கண்டுணர்ந்தார் ஓக்கமே போலப்","'பெரியதன் ஆவி பெரிது.'"],"p":"","r":"The invocation sets the feet of the one who quelled the enemy within and knew without flaw beside the rising of those in the wide sea-girt world who have seen and known them as their due, and clinches it with the proverb that the steam of a great thing is great.","n":"The sentence has no main verb: the feet stand at the head and the worshippers' rise is compared to something left unsaid, so the sense is carried by the proverb, that what issues from a great thing is itself great, here the standing that comes to those who know the great one's feet. அரி may be the senses' enemies or delusion; ஆசின்று உணர்ந்தவன் reads as one who knows without fault, an epithet of the omniscient one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","perumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 0","lb":"PZ0","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரியதன் ஆவி பெரிது","the steam (or breath) of a great thing is great","those who know the great one's feet rise high in turn","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the greatness that passes from the worshipped one to those who know his feet"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:1":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":3,"t":["ஆற்றும் இளமைக்கண் கற்கலான் மூப்பின்கண்","போற்றும் எனவும் புணருமோ - ஆற்றச்","'சுரம்போக்கி உல்குகொண்டார் இல்லையே யில்லை","மரம்போக்கிக் கூலிகொண் டார்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether one who would not learn in the youth that is fit for it can expect to gain learning in old age, and answers with the proverb that no one ever took toll after the wilderness road was passed, nor wages after the tree was cleared.","n":"Both halves of the proverb are about payment that can only be taken while the work is still to be done: once the road is crossed or the tree is moved, the claim is gone. Youth is cast as that moment for learning. மரம்போக்கி may be the felling of a tree or the plying of a boat (மரம், a raft) across water; either way the wage falls due before the task ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ilamai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 1","lb":"PZ1","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சுரம்போக்கி உல்குகொண்டார் இல்லையே யில்லை மரம்போக்கிக் கூலிகொண் டார்","there is no one who took toll after the desert road was crossed, none who took wages after the tree was cleared","learning not gained in youth cannot be claimed in old age","general","","no story; the proverb is the instance, a payment that lapses once the work is past"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:2":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":4,"t":["சொற்றொறும் சோர்வு படுதலால் சோர்வின்றிக்","கற்றொறும் கல்லாதேன் என்று வழியிரங்கி","உற்றொன்று சிந்தித்து உழன்றொன்று அறியுமேல்","'கற்றொறுந்தான் கல்லாத வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Because a man lapses at every word he speaks, the one who, without flagging, laments at each stage of study that he has not yet learnt, ponders one thing and labours until he knows it, knows the truth of the saying that the more one learns the more one finds oneself unlearned.","n":"The verse makes the sense of one's own ignorance a working method, not a mood: the lament drives the next round of pondering and labour (உற்றொன்று சிந்தித்து உழன்றொன்று அறியுமேல்). The first line gives the reason as the lapses that each spoken word exposes. The grammar of lines 1 to 3 is loose and the subject is supplied.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai","arivu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 2","lb":"PZ2","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கற்றொறுந்தான் கல்லாத வாறு","how, with each learning, one is the more unlearned","true study shows the learner how much remains unlearnt","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the earnest learner who measures himself after each lesson"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:3":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":5,"t":["விளக்கு விலைகொடுத்துக் கோடல் விளக்குத்","துளக்கமின் றென்றனைத்தும் தூக்கி விளக்கு","மருள்படுவ தாயின் மலைநாட! என்னை","'பொருள்கொடுத்துக் கொள்ளார் இருள்'"],"p":"","r":"A lamp is bought for a price only after everything is weighed to be sure it will not flicker, and if the lamp itself should spread confusion, lord of the hill country, what use is it, for no one pays money to buy darkness.","n":"The verse never names learning: the lamp stands for it only through the heading, and the point is that knowledge bought at a cost is worth having only if it clears confusion (மருள்). One may also read it as advice to test a teacher or a text before taking it, as one tests a lamp before paying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","mayal","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 3","lb":"PZ3","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பொருள்கொடுத்துக் கொள்ளார் இருள்","no one gives wealth to buy darkness","learning that leaves one confused is worth nothing for its cost","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the purchase of a lamp that gives no light"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:4":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":6,"t":["ஆற்றவும் கற்றவும் அறிவுடையார்; அஃதுடையார்","நாற்றிசையும் செல்லாத நாடில்லை - அந்நாடு","வேற்றுநா டாகா; தமவேயாம் ஆயினால்","'ஆற்றுணா வேண்டுவ தில்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have learnt deeply are the wise, and there is no land in the four directions they do not go to; those lands are not foreign to them but their own, so, as the proverb says, they need no food for the road.","n":"The proverb turns belonging into provision: the learned carry nothing because every land feeds them as its own. Line 1 as printed (ஆற்றவும் கற்றவும்) is awkward; the reading takes it as 'those who have learnt much'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 4","lb":"PZ4","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆற்றுணா வேண்டுவ தில்","there is no need of food for the journey","the learned are at home everywhere and need nothing carried","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the learned traveller"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:5":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":7,"t":["உணற்கினிய இன்னீர் பிறிதுழி இல்லென்றும்","கிணற்றுகத்துத் தேரைபோல் ஆகார் - கணக்கினை","முற்றப் பகலும் முனியா(து) இனிதோதிக்","'கற்றலின் கேட்டலே நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The wise do not become like the frog in the well that thinks there is no sweet water to drink anywhere else, and even beside reciting a treatise sweetly all day without tiring, the proverb holds that hearing is better than learning.","n":"The frog image gives the reason for the ranking: one's own text, however diligently recited, is one well, and hearing from others is what shows there is water elsewhere. So the verse ranks listening above solitary study, not above learning as such. கணக்கு is the treatise or text conned by rote.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 5","lb":"PZ5","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கற்றலின் கேட்டலே நன்று","hearing is better than learning","listening to others frees the learner from the narrowness of his own text","exemplum","கிணற்றுகத்துத் தேரை","the frog in the well takes its well for the only sweet water there is"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:6":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":8,"t":["உரைமுடிவு காணான் இளமையோன் என்ற","நரைமுது மக்கள் உவப்ப - நரைமுடித்துச்","சொல்லால் முறைசெய்தான் சோழன் 'குலவிச்சை","கல்லாமல் பாகம் படும்'"],"p":"","r":"When grey-haired elders said a young man could not see a case through to its end, the Chola tied on grey hair, judged the case by his words to their satisfaction, and so proved the saying that a family's craft is half known without being learnt.","n":"The proverb credits the king's skill to birth, not study: his line's craft of judging comes to him half-made. That sits oddly under a heading on learning, and against the counted works that rank learning above lineage. The disguise answers the elders on their own terms, appearance, while the judgement answers them on substance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kudi","ilamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 6","lb":"PZ6","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குலவிச்சை கல்லாமல் பாகம் படும்","a family's craft is half-known without being learnt","inherited capacity: the king judges well though young","named","சோழன்","a young Chola king, doubted by grey elders, put on grey hair and judged their case to their satisfaction"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:7":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":9,"t":["புலமிக் கவரைப் புலமை தெரிதல்","புலமிக் கவர்க்கே புலனாம் - நலமிக்க","பூம்புனல் ஊர! பொதுமக்கட் காகாதே","'பாம்பறியும் பாம்பின கால்'"],"p":"","r":"Only the deeply learned can discern the scholarship of the deeply learned, lord of the town of flowering waters; it is not open to common people, for a snake knows the feet of a snake.","n":"The proverb rests on the belief that a snake's legs are hidden from all but other snakes: learning is not merely hard for outsiders to judge, it is invisible to them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 7","lb":"PZ7","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பாம்பறியும் பாம்பின கால்","a snake knows a snake's feet","only peers in learning can judge learning","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the learned judging the learned"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:8":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":10,"t":["நல்லார் நலத்தை உணரின் அவரினும்","நல்லார் உணர்ப பிறருணரார் - நல்ல","மயிலாடு மாமலை வெற்ப! மற்றென்றும்","'அயிலாலே போழ்ப அயில்'"],"p":"","r":"If the goodness of the good is to be known, those better than they will know it and others will not, lord of the great hill where peacocks dance, for spear is split only by spear.","n":"The verse is about goodness of character, not learning, though it stands under the learning heading; it pairs with 7 but asks more, since the knower must be better (அவரினும் நல்லார), not merely equal. அயில் is a spear point or iron: a hard thing is cut only by what is as hard or harder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 8","lb":"PZ8","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அயிலாலே போழ்ப அயில்","they split iron with iron","only those better than the good can know their goodness","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the judging of goodness"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:9":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":11,"t":["சுற்றறிந்தார் கண்ட அடக்கம் அறியாதார்","பொச்சாந்து தம்மைப் புகழ்ந்துரைப்பார் - தெற்ற","அறைகல் அருவி அணிமலை நாட!","'நிறைகுடம் நீர்தளும்பல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know the restraint that the widely learned have found praise themselves in their heedlessness, lord of the fair hill country where the falls strike the rock, for a full pot does not slop its water.","n":"Self-praise is traced to two lacks at once: not knowing the learned man's restraint, and பொச்சாப்பு, forgetful heedlessness. The proverb implies the half-filled pot without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kalvi","pochchaappu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 9","lb":"PZ9","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நிறைகுடம் நீர்தளும்பல் இல்","a full pot does not slop its water","the learned are restrained, the half-learned boast","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the quiet of the truly learned against the boasting of the shallow"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:10":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":12,"t":["விதிப்பட்ட நூலுணர்ந்து வேற்றுமை இல்லார்","கதிப்பவர் நூலினைக் கையிகந்தா ராகிப்","பதிப்பட வாழ்வார் பழியாய செய்தல்","'மதிப்புறத்துப் பட்ட மறு'"],"p":"","r":"When those who have learnt the prescribed texts and live conspicuously in their town go beyond the texts of those who teach and do blameworthy things, it is a spot fallen on the face of the moon.","n":"The fault is no larger in the learned, but more visible, because they are seen across the town as the moon is seen across the sky. Lines 1 and 2 are hard: வேற்றுமை இல்லார் may mean those of steady conduct or those without distinction of rank, and கதிப்பவர் may be those who expound or the revered; the reading takes the general sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pazhi","ozhukkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 10","lb":"PZ10","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மதிப்புறத்துப் பட்ட மறு","a blemish fallen on the surface of the moon","the faults of the learned are seen by all","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the misdeed of a learned and well-known man"],"ch":"pz:1"},"pz:11":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":13,"t":["சுற்றானும் சுற்றார்வாய்க் கேட்டானும் இல்லாதார்","தெற்ற உணரார் பொருள்களை - எற்றேல்","அறிவில்லான் மெய்தலைப் பாடு பிறிதில்லை","'நாவற் கீழ்ப் பெற்ற கனி'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have neither learnt for themselves nor heard from the mouths of the learned do not understand things clearly, and if such a man ever lights on the truth it is no more his doing than a fruit picked up under the naaval tree.","n":"The verse names two routes to knowledge, one's own study and listening to the learned, and denies the man who has taken neither; the proverb then grants that he may be right on occasion and takes the credit from him, since the fruit fell and was not earned. As printed, line 1 reads சுற்றானும் சுற்றார்வாய், which makes poor sense under the heading; the reading assumes கற்றானும் கற்றார்வாய்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","kelvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 11","lb":"PZ11","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாவற் கீழ்ப் பெற்ற கனி","a fruit got under the naaval (jamun) tree","an unlearned man's occasional true judgement is luck, not knowledge","general","","no story; the proverb stands for any windfall that drops to whoever happens to stand below"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:12":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":14,"t":["கல்லாதான் கண்ட கழிநுட்பம் கற்றார்முன்","சொல்லுங்கால் சோர்வு படுதலால் - நல்லாய்!","'வினாமுந் துறாத உரையில்லை; இல்லை","கனாமுந் துறாத வினை'"],"p":"","r":"When an unlearned man voices before the learned some very fine point he has hit upon, it falters, because, as the saying goes, no answer comes without a question going before it, and no deed without a dream going before it.","n":"Only the first half of the paired proverb does the work: among the learned every statement is met by questions, and the unlearned man's insight, never tested by questioning, gives way under them. The second half (the dream before the deed) rides along as the proverb's usual partner. The verse is addressed to a woman (நல்லாய்!); the address is a frame, not the counsel's audience. Its first line is shared, nearly word for word, with verse 13.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 12","lb":"PZ12","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வினாமுந் துறாத உரையில்லை; இல்லை கனாமுந் துறாத வினை","there is no answer that a question does not precede, and no deed that a dream does not precede","the unlearned man's insight collapses when the learned question it","general","","no story; the proverb states a general order of things, question before answer and omen before act"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:13":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":15,"t":["கல்லாதான் கண்ட கழிநுட்பம் காட்டரிதால்","நல்லேம்யாம் என்றொருவன் நன்கு மதித்தலென்","சொல்லால் வணக்கி வெகுண்(டு)அரு கிற்பார்க்கும்","'சொல்லாக்கால் சொல்லுவ தில்'"],"p":"","r":"Since the very fine point an unlearned man has found is hard for him to show, there is no sense in his rating himself highly as one of the good, for even those able to bend others by speech have said nothing if they do not speak.","n":"Read with 12, the verse closes the unlearned man's last exit: spoken before the learned his insight falters (12), and kept unspoken it does not exist for anyone (13). Line 3 is obscure: the printed வெகுண்(டு)அரு கிற்பார்க்கும் carries an editor's bracket and its sense, perhaps those who can overawe or come at others in anger, is not certain; the reading takes the line only as 'even the powerful in speech'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","sol","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 13","lb":"PZ13","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சொல்லாக்கால் சொல்லுவ தில்","if one does not speak, nothing is said","insight that cannot be put into words earns no standing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the able speaker who stays silent, and through him to the unlearned man whose insight cannot be shown"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:14":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":16,"t":["கல்வியான் ஆய சுழிநுட்பம் கல்லார்முன்","சொல்லிய நல்லவும் தீயவாம் - எல்லாம்","இவர்வரை நாட! 'தமரையில் லார்க்கு","நகரமும் காடுபோன் றாங்கு'"],"p":"","r":"The deep subtleties that learning yields, spoken before the unlearned, turn every good thing said into a bad one, lord of the land of high hills, just as for those who have no kin even a city is like a forest.","n":"The image makes the audience a kind of kin: the learned man among the unlearned is as friendless as a stranger in a crowded city, and what is good in his words finds no one to receive it, so it does harm instead. The text has சுழிநுட்பம் here where 12 and 13 have கழிநுட்பம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 14","lb":"PZ14","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தமரையில் லார்க்கு நகரமும் காடுபோன் றாங்கு","to those without their own people even a city is like a forest","learned speech among the unlearned is not merely wasted but turned to harm","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the learned speaker who finds no hearers of his own kind"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:15":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":17,"t":["கல்லா தவரிடைக் கட்டுரையின் மிக்கதோர்","பொல்லாத தில்லை ஒருவற்கு - நல்லாய்!","'இழுக்கத்தின் மிக்க இழிவில்லை இல்லை","ஒழுக்கத்தின் மிக்க உயர்வு'"],"p":"","r":"For a man nothing is worse than making a fine, well-wrought speech among the unlearned, since there is no lowness greater than a lapse of conduct and no height greater than conduct.","n":"The verse classes the choice of audience as conduct: holding forth among the unlearned is an இழுக்கம், a lapse that lowers the speaker, not merely a waste of eloquence. The proverb's ozhukkam and izhukkam pair is the pair of the Kural's chapter on conduct, here used as a known saying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","ozhukkam","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 15","lb":"PZ15","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இழுக்கத்தின் மிக்க இழிவில்லை இல்லை ஒழுக்கத்தின் மிக்க உயர்வு","no disgrace exceeds a lapse, no elevation exceeds right conduct","speaking learnedly among the unlearned is a lapse that lowers the speaker","general","","no story; the proverb is a general maxim on conduct"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:16":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":18,"t":["கற்றாற்று வாரைக் கறுப்பித்துக் கல்லாதார்","சொற்றாற்றுக் கொண்டு சுனைத்தெழுதல் - எற்றெனின்","தானும் நடவான் 'முடவன் பிடிப்பூணி","யானையோ டாடல் உறவு'"],"p":"","r":"For the unlearned to provoke those who live by learning, take up a contest of words and rise itching for a fight is like a lame man who cannot even walk wanting to sport with an elephant.","n":"The proverb's point is the gap between the wish and the means: the lame man cannot walk, yet wants a game with the strongest of beasts. பிடிப்பூணி is obscure; it may tie the elephant to a she-elephant (பிடி) or to a fetter, and the reading leaves it as 'an elephant' only. சொற்றாற்று and சுனைத்து are also uncommon; the reading takes them as a fight in words and an itch to rise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","vekuli","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 16","lb":"PZ16","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முடவன் பிடிப்பூணி யானையோ டாடல் உறவு","a lame man's wish to play with an elephant","the unlearned man's challenge to the learned is a desire far beyond his strength","general","","no story; the lame man of the proverb is a type, applied to the unlearned who pick a quarrel with the learned"],"ch":"pz:2"},"pz:17":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":19,"t":["கேட்பாரை நாடிக் கிளக்கப் படும்பொருட்கண்","வேட்கை அறிந்துரைப்பார் வித்தகர் - வேட்கையால்","வண்டு வழிபடரும் வாட்கண்ணாய்! 'தோற்பன","கொண்டு புகாஅர் அவை'"],"p":"","r":"The skilled speak only after seeking out their hearers and learning what they want in the matter to be spoken, as bees go where their desire leads, for no one carries into an assembly the things that will lose there.","n":"The verse makes the hearers' desire (வேட்கை), not the truth of the matter, the speaker's first measure; the bee image in line 3 repeats வேட்கை so that the speaker's skill is to follow desire as the bee follows scent. The address to a woman (வாட்கண்ணாய்!) is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 17","lb":"PZ17","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தோற்பன கொண்டு புகாஅர் அவை","people do not enter an assembly carrying things that will be defeated","speech that does not meet the hearers' desire is a losing case brought into the assembly","general","","no story; the proverb states what anyone entering a contest of an assembly avoids"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:18":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":20,"t":["ஒருவர் உரைப்ப உரைத்தால் அதுகொண்டு","இருவரா வாரும் எதிர்மொழியல் பாலா","பெருவரை நாட! சிறிதேனும் 'இன்னாது","இருவர் உடனாடல் நாய்'"],"p":"","r":"When one person has spoken his piece, it is not fitting to take it up and answer back so that the two become opponents, for, lord of the great-hilled land, it goes ill even a little when two dogs play together.","n":"The proverb's point is that dogs' play turns into a fight; a counter-reply in assembly is play of the same kind. Lines 1-2 are compressed and could also be read as forbidding a second man to join in answering one speaker; the reading takes இருவரா as the exchange becoming a two-sided quarrel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","igal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 18","lb":"PZ18","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இன்னாது இருவர் உடனாடல் நாய்","it is unpleasant when two dogs play together","answering back a speaker in assembly turns discussion into a quarrel","general","","no story; two dogs at play, which soon fall to snarling"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:19":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":21,"t":["துன்னி இருவர் தொடங்கிய மாற்றத்தில்","பின்னை உரைக்கப் படற்பாலான் - முன்னி","மொழிந்தால் மொழியறியான் கூறல் 'முழந்தாள்","கிழிந்தானை மூக்குப் பொதிவு'"],"p":"","r":"In an exchange two men have closely begun, if the one whose turn is to speak afterwards pushes forward and speaks first, his is the talk of one who does not know speech, like wrapping the nose of a man whose knee is torn.","n":"The proverb mocks a remedy put on the wrong place: speaking out of turn answers what has not yet been raised and misses the wound. பின்னை உரைக்கப் படற்பாலான் may instead mean the one who is to be addressed later; on either reading the fault is pre-empting the order of the exchange.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 19","lb":"PZ19","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முழந்தாள் கிழிந்தானை மூக்குப் பொதிவு","bandaging the nose of a man whose knee is torn","speaking before one's turn in a dispute misses the point at issue","general","","no story; a misplaced dressing for an injury elsewhere"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:20":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":22,"t":["கல்லாதும் கேளாதும் கற்றாரவை நடுவண்","சொல்லாடு வாரையும் அஞ்சற்பாற்(று) - எல்லருவி","பாய்வரை நாட! 'பரிசழிந் தாரோடு","தேவரும் ஆற்றல் இலர்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who, having neither learnt nor listened, bandy words in the midst of an assembly of the learned are themselves to be feared, lord of the hills where bright cascades leap, for even the gods have no power against those who have lost all decency.","n":"The fear runs the unexpected way: not the unlearned fearing the learned, but the learned well advised to fear the unlearned talker, because shamelessness (பரிசழிந்தார்) cannot be answered by argument. The verse joins the two routes of 11, study and listening, as what these talkers lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","avai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 20","lb":"PZ20","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பரிசழிந் தாரோடு தேவரும் ஆற்றல் இலர்","even the gods are powerless against those who have lost their decency","the learned cannot prevail over shameless unlearned talkers and should avoid them","general","","no story; the proverb sets the shameless beyond even divine power"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:21":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":23,"t":["அகலம் உடைய அறிவடையார் நாப்பண்","புகலறியார் புக்கவர் தாமே - இகலினால்","வீண்சேர்ந்த புன்சொல் விளம்பல் அதுவன்றோ","'பாண்சேரி பற்கிளக்கு மாறு"],"p":"","r":"Those who, not knowing how to enter, have entered among men of broad understanding and then out of contentiousness utter empty, mean words are doing just what the saying of the bards' quarter and its talk of teeth describes.","n":"The proverb's sense is not clear from the words: பாண்சேரி பற்கிளக்குமாறு is 'the way the bards' quarter tells of teeth', perhaps boasting of what the poor have chewed, or teeth to be lost in a quarrel; the reading leaves it open. The verse's own counsel, that entering without knowing how leads to vain contention, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","igal","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 21","lb":"PZ21","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பாண்சேரி பற்கிளக்கு மாறு","the way the bards' quarter speaks of teeth","the empty contentious talk of one who has pushed in among the wise","general","","an allusion to a saying about the bards' street and teeth whose point the verse does not spell out"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:22":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":24,"t":["மானமும் நாணும் அறியார் மதிமயங்கி","ஞானம் அறிவார் இடைப்புக்குத் தாமிருந்து","ஞானம் வினாஅய் உரைத்தல் 'நகையாகும்","யானைப்பல் காண்பான் புகல்'"],"p":"","r":"For those who know neither honour nor shame, their minds confused, to enter among men who know wisdom, sit there and speak questioning that wisdom is laughable, like the venture of one who goes in to see an elephant's tusk.","n":"The verse puts the fault in character before learning: it is the lack of மானம் and நாண் that lets the man sit among the wise and question them. The proverb may hold a pun, since நகை is both laughter and teeth: one who would see an elephant's tusk must get close to a dangerous thing, and the attempt is absurd.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam","gnaanam","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 22","lb":"PZ22","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நகையாகும் யானைப்பல் காண்பான் புகல்","laughable is the going-in of one who wants to see an elephant's tusk","the shameless man who questions the wise makes himself ridiculous","general","","no story; a man pushing close to an elephant to inspect its tusk"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:23":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":25,"t":["அல்லவையுள் தோன்றி அலவலைத்து வாழ்பவர்","நல்லவையுள் புக்கிருந்து நாவடங்கக் கல்வி","அளவிறந்து மிக்கார் அறிவெள்ளிக் கூறல்","'மிளகுளு வுண்பான் புகல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who live chattering in bad assemblies, when they enter a good assembly and speak scorning the knowledge of men whose learning has passed all measure and keeps their tongues in check, are like a man eager to eat weevil-eaten pepper.","n":"The learned are silent because of their learning (நாவடங்கக் கல்வி அளவிறந்து மிக்கார்), and the chatterer reads that silence as emptiness and mocks it. The proverb (மிளகுளு, worm-eaten pepper) seems to mean relishing what has no savour left, but its point is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","adakkam","pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 23","lb":"PZ23","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மிளகுளு வுண்பான் புகல்","the eagerness of one who eats worm-eaten pepper","the chatterer's mockery of the restrained learned is a taste for what is worthless","general","","no story; a man relishing spoiled pepper"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:24":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":26,"t":["நல்லவை கண்டக்கால் நாச்சுருட்டி நன்றுணராப்","புல்லவையுள் தம்மைப் புகழ்ந்துரைத்தல் - புல்லார்","புடைத்தறுகண் அஞ்சுவான் 'இல்லுள்வில் லேற்றி","இடைக்கலத்து எய்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"To roll up one's tongue on seeing a good assembly and then praise oneself in a mean assembly that does not understand the good is the way of a man who, afraid of the fierce flank of his enemies, strings his bow inside his house and shoots at the pot in the middle of it.","n":"The verse pairs two faults as one: silence from fear before the good and self-praise before the mean are the same cowardice, brave only where nothing answers back. The proverb's bow drawn at home against a household pot is the clearest image in the chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","anjaamai","agandhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 24","lb":"PZ24","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லுள்வில் லேற்றி இடைக்கலத்து எய்து விடல்","stringing a bow inside the house and shooting at the pot in its middle","silent among the good and boastful among the mean is cowardice that performs where it is safe","exemplum","","a man who fears to face the enemy's fierce side shows his archery at home on a pot"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:25":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":27,"t":["நடலை இலராகி நன்றுணரார் ஆய","முடலை முழுமக்கள் மொய்கொள் அவையுள்","உடலா ஒருவற்கு உறுதி உரைத்தல்","'கடலுளால் மாவடித் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"To speak sound counsel to someone in a crowded assembly of thoroughly dull men who, untroubled, do not grasp the good, is like sifting flour in the sea.","n":"நடலை இலராகி (being without trouble or pretence) and உடலா ஒருவற்கு (to one who does not dispute, or does not agree) are both doubtful; the reading takes the first as the fools' complacency and the second as the hearer counselled. The proverb's loss is total: the flour is not spoiled but simply gone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","pedhaimai","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 25","lb":"PZ25","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடலுளால் மாவடித் தற்று","like sifting flour in the sea","counsel spoken among dull men is wholly lost","general","","no story; flour strained into the sea is lost"],"ch":"pz:3"},"pz:26":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":28,"t":["அறிவினால் மாட்சியொன்(று) இல்லா ஒருவன்","பிறிதினால் மாண்டது எவனாம் - பொறியின்","மணிபொன்னும் சாந்தமும் மாலையும் இன்ன","'அணியெல்லாம் ஆடையின் பின்'"],"p":"","r":"A man who has no excellence through understanding cannot be made excellent by anything else, since jewels of gem and gold, sandal paste, garlands and every such ornament come only after the cloth.","n":"The proverb does not dismiss ornaments; it orders them. Understanding is the cloth, the thing whose lack is nakedness, and the other goods are adornments that only make sense on a clothed body. பொறியின் in line 2 is read as 'finely wrought', qualifying the gem and gold jewels.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 26","lb":"PZ26","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அணியெல்லாம் ஆடையின் பின்","all ornaments come after the garment","other excellences adorn only a man who first has understanding","general","","no story; the proverb states the order of dressing, cloth first and jewels after"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:27":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":29,"t":["ஆயிரவ ரானும் அறிவிலார் தொக்கக்கால்","மாயிரு ஞாலத்து மாண்பொருவன் போல்கலார்","பாயிருள் நீக்கும் 'மதியம்போல் பன்மீனும்","காய்கலா வாகும் நிலா'"],"p":"","r":"Even if a thousand men without understanding gather, they do not equal one excellent man in the wide world, just as the many stars cannot give the moonlight that the moon gives as it clears the spreading dark.","n":"The argument is against number: excellence is a difference of kind, so adding fools does not approach one wise man. The quotation marks open at மதியம்போல், so the simile is printed as part of the proverb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pedhaimai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 27","lb":"PZ27","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மதியம்போல் பன்மீனும் காய்கலா வாகும் நிலா","the many stars cannot shed moonlight as the moon does","one man of understanding outweighs any number of the senseless","general","","no story; the proverb sets the single moon against the crowd of stars"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:28":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":30,"t":["நற்கறிவு இல்லாரை நாட்டவும் மாட்டாதே","சொற்குறி கொண்டு துடிபண் உறுத்துவபோல்","வெற்பறைமேல் தாழும் இலங்கருவி நன்னாட!","'கற்றறிவு போகா கடை'"],"p":"","r":"Those who lack good sense cannot be set firm in anything, unlike a drum that is brought to its beat by a spoken cue, lord of the good land where shining falls drop over the rock slopes, as the saying is, 'where learned knowledge has not gone in'.","n":"The sense is hard to fix. Line 2 is taken as the drummer who strikes the துடி to word-signals, the responsive instrument against which the senseless are measured; the proverb's கடை is taken as the conditional 'when ... has not', so that instruction fails where learning has not first entered. Other divisions of line 2 are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pedhaimai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 28","lb":"PZ28","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கற்றறிவு போகா கடை","when learned knowledge has not gone in","the senseless cannot be set right where no learning has entered","general","","no story; the proverb names the condition under which a person cannot be steadied"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:29":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":31,"t":["ஆணம் உடைய அறிவினார் தந்நலம்","மானும் அறிவி னவரைத் தலைப்படுத்தல்","மானமர் கண்ணாய்! மறங்கெழு மாமன்னர்","'யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"For men of firm understanding, winning over those whose understanding matches their own worth is, lady with a deer's eyes, like the valiant great kings' binding an elephant by means of an elephant.","n":"The proverb pictures the capture of a wild elephant with a trained one: the wise are drawn in only by someone of their own kind. ஆணம் in line 1 is taken as firmness or support; it can also mean affection, which would make the bond warmer. The address to a woman is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","soozhchi","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 29","lb":"PZ29","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று","it is like binding an elephant with an elephant","the wise are won over only through those of equal understanding","general","மாமன்னர்","kings capture a wild elephant by using a tame one"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:30":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":32,"t":["தெரிவுடையா ரோடு தெரிந்துணர்ந்து நின்றார்","பரியார் இடைப்புகார் பண்பறிவார் மன்ற","விரியா இமிழ்திரை வீங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'அரிவாரைக் காட்டார் நரி'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have taken stock and understood alongside the discerning do not push in among people who have no regard for them, for they surely know others' natures, lord of the shore of loud waves, just as the jackal does not show itself to the reapers.","n":"The understanding in question is social: knowing who will not value one and keeping away. The jackal is not cowardly in the image but shrewd; it knows the reapers with their sickles will strike. பரியார் is read as 'those without regard'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","therithal","inam","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 30","lb":"PZ30","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அரிவாரைக் காட்டார் நரி","jackals do not show themselves to the reapers","the discerning keep out of company that does not value them","general","","no story; the jackal in the harvest field keeps out of the reapers' sight"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:31":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":33,"t":["பொற்பவும் பொல்லா தனவும் புனைந்திருந்தார்","சொற்பெய்து உணர்த்துதல் வேண்டுமோ? - விற்கீழ்","அரிபாய் பரந்தகன்ற கண்ணாய்! - 'அறியும்","பெரிதாள் பவனே பெரிது'"],"p":"","r":"Need anyone put into words, for those who have themselves arranged both the fitting and the unfitting, what these are, lady of wide red-streaked eyes beneath bow brows, since he who manages much is the one who knows much?","n":"Read here as a rhetorical question: one who has handled both good and bad affairs already knows them and needs no instruction. புனைந்திருந்தார் could also mean 'those adorned with' fair and foul qualities, which would turn the verse toward a person whose character speaks for itself; the proverb fits the first reading better.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 31","lb":"PZ31","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறியும் பெரிதாள் பவனே பெரிது","the one who handles much knows much","the experienced need no telling what is fit and unfit","general","","no story; the proverb credits knowledge to the one with wide charge of affairs"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:32":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":34,"t":["பரந்த திறலாரைப் பாசிமேல் இட்டுக்","கரந்து மறைக்கலும் ஆமோ? - நிரந்தெழுந்து","வேயின் திரண்டதோள் வேற்கண்ணாய் 'விண்ணியங்கும்","ஞாயிற்றைக் கைம்மறைப்பார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Can men of widespread strength be hidden by covering them with moss, lady with bamboo-smooth shoulders and spear-like eyes, when no one can hide with his hand the sun that moves in the sky?","n":"The verse is about திறல், ability or power, rather than understanding as such; it fits its heading only if the editor took ability as the outward show of understanding. The moss is a thin cover, as the hand is against the sun: the attempt to suppress is mocked as much as the greatness is praised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vali","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 32","lb":"PZ32","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["விண்ணியங்கும் ஞாயிற்றைக் கைம்மறைப்பார் இல்","there is no one who can hide with the hand the sun moving through the sky","real ability cannot be concealed by those who would suppress it","general","","no story; the proverb mocks a hand raised to cover the sun"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:33":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":35,"t":["அருவிலை மாண்கலனும் ஆன்ற பொருளும்","திருவுடைய ராயின் திரிந்தும் - வருமால்","பெருவரை நாட! பிரிவின் றதனால்","'திருவினும் திட்பம் பெறும்'"],"p":"","r":"Fine jewels beyond price and plentiful wealth come back even after they have strayed, if their owners have fortune, lord of the great hills, but since understanding never parts from one at all, firmness, as the saying goes, gains more than fortune.","n":"The verse concedes more than most praise of learning does: lost riches really can return. Its point is that their return depends on fortune, while understanding needs no return because it never leaves. The subject of பிரிவின்று is unstated and is supplied from the heading as understanding; திட்பம் is taken as the steadiness of that possession.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thitpam","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 33","lb":"PZ33","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["திருவினும் திட்பம் பெறும்","firmness gains more than fortune","understanding, which never leaves, is worth more than wealth that returns only by fortune","general","","no story; the proverb ranks steadiness above luck"],"ch":"pz:4"},"pz:34":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":36,"t":["விழுத்தொடையர் ஆகி விளங்கித்தொல் வந்தார்","ஒழுக்குடையர் ஆகி ஒழுகல் -பழத்தெங்கு","செய்த்தலை வீழும் புனலூர! அஃதன்றோ","'நெய்த்தலைப்பால் உக்கு விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"For those of excellent descent who come shining from an old line to live as men of conduct is, the verse asks the lord of the watered town where ripe coconuts drop into the fields, like rich milk with ghee on it poured out.","n":"The verse treats old lineage as a good in its own right and good conduct as what is added on top of it, abundance upon abundance, as the ripe coconut falling into the paddy field doubles a fertile land. The proverb is hard: நெய்த்தலைப்பால் உக்கு விடல் could also be read as a waste (spilling the cream-topped milk), but that sits ill with the praise in lines 1-2; the reading takes it as richness overflowing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 34","lb":"PZ34","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நெய்த்தலைப்பால் உக்கு விடல்","milk with ghee on its top, poured out","good conduct in the well-born adds excellence to an excellence already given by birth","general","","no story; the proverb stands for richness added to richness"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:35":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":37,"t":["கள்ளி அகிலும் கருங்காக்கைச் சொல்லும்போல்","எள்ளற்க யார்வாயின் நல்லுரையைத் தெள்ளிதின்","ஆர்க்கும் அருவி மலைநாட! 'நாய்கொண்டால்","பார்ப்பாரும் தின்பர் உடும்பு'"],"p":"","r":"As fragrant agil comes from the kalli and an omen from the black crow's call, one should not scorn a good word from whatever mouth it comes, says the verse to the lord of the hill country of loud clear falls, for if a dog has caught a monitor lizard even brahmins eat it.","n":"The two opening images are low sources of something prized: a scented wood from a thorny spurge and a meaningful sign from a despised bird. The proverb takes the extreme case: பார்ப்பார், the brahmins, a birth-group named here for its strict abstention from flesh, will eat what a dog (itself an impure catcher) has brought in, if it is good. The caste term is not used to rank people but to set the hardest test of a rule against a low source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 35","lb":"PZ35","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்கொண்டால் பார்ப்பாரும் தின்பர் உடும்பு","if a dog catches a monitor lizard, even brahmins eat it","a good word is to be taken whatever the lowness of the mouth that speaks it","general","","no story; a type case in which the lowness of the bringer does not stop even the strictest eater"],"nr":[["diet","caste","purity"],"the proverb presumes the period's norm that brahmins abstain from flesh and shun what a dog has touched, and makes its breaking the measure of a good thing's pull"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:36":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":38,"t":["தந்நடை நோக்கார் தமர்வந்த வாறறியார்","செந்நடை சேராச் சிறியார்போல் ஆகாது","நின்னடை யானே நடஅத்தா! 'நின்னடை","நின்னின்(று) அறிகிற்பார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells its hearer, addressed as அத்தா, not to become like the small men who do not watch their own conduct, do not know the way their kin came and never join the straight path, but to walk by his own conduct, since no one knows that conduct apart from him.","n":"The proverb can be taken two ways: that only the man himself knows his own conduct, so he must be its keeper, or that others know a man's conduct only from the man himself, so he is its evidence. Both make conduct a thing the self answers for, against the small men who look neither at themselves nor at their forebears.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","sirumai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 36","lb":"PZ36","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நின்னடை நின்னின்(று) அறிகிற்பார் இல்","there is no one who can know your walk apart from you","a man must keep his own conduct because it is known only through him","general","","no story; the proverb is said to the hearer himself"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:37":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":39,"t":["நீர்த்தன்று ஒருவர் நெறியன்றிக் கொண்டக்கால்","பேர்த்துத் தெருட்டல் பெரியார்க்கும் ஆகாதே","கூர்த்தநுண் கேள்வி அறிவுடையார்க்(கு) ஆயினும்","'ஓர்த்தது இசைக்கும் பறை'"],"p":"","r":"When someone takes up a course that is not fit and off the path, turning him back and setting him right is beyond even the great, even those of keen and subtle learning and wisdom, for a drum sounds what has been set in it.","n":"The verse puts the limit not in the adviser but in the advised: learning and hearing (கூர்த்தநுண் கேள்வி) do not reach one who has chosen. The proverb's ஓர்த்தது is what has been resolved or intended; the drum gives back only that, as a fixed man gives back only his resolve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kelvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 37","lb":"PZ37","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஓர்த்தது இசைக்கும் பறை","a drum sounds what it has been set to","a man set on a wrong course cannot be turned back even by the wisest","general","","no story; the drum stands for the man fixed on his own purpose"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:38":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":40,"t":["தங்குற்றம் நீக்கல ராகிப் பிறர்குற்றம்","எங்கேனும் தீர்த்தற்கு இடைப்புகுதல் - எங்கும்","வியனுலகில் 'வெள்ளாடு தன்வளி தீராது","அயல்வளி தீர்த்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"To push in anywhere to remove others' faults without removing one's own is, in all the wide world, the goat that clears another's wind and not its own.","n":"The verse's words தங்குற்றம் நீக்கல and பிறர்குற்றம் are the Kural's own pair (436), turned from a condition of the king's justice into a mockery of any meddler. The proverb's goat is a curer that cannot cure itself; வளி is the wind-disorder of the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 38","lb":"PZ38","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வெள்ளாடு தன்வளி தீராது அயல்வளி தீர்த்து விடல்","the goat does not clear its own wind but clears its neighbour's","one who corrects others' faults while keeping his own is absurd","general","","no story; the goat is any healer of others who leaves his own sickness"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:39":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":41,"t":["கெடுவல் எனப்பட்ட கண்ணும் தனக்கோர்","வடுவல்ல செய்தலே வேண்டும் - நெடுவரை","முற்றுநீர் ஆழி வரையகத்(து) ஈண்டிய","'கல்தேயும் தேயாது சொல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even when one is told 'I will be ruined', one must do only what brings no scar on oneself, for in the land ringed by the full sea and its long hills, a stone wears away but a word does not.","n":"கெடுவல் is the man's own ruin, which the verse accepts as the lesser loss; the scar (வடு) is the reproach a deed leaves, and the proverb's word is what is said of the doer, which outlives the hills.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","ozhukkam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 39","lb":"PZ39","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கல்தேயும் தேயாது சொல்","stone wears away, a word does not","one should choose ruin over a shameful deed, since the report of it lasts","general","","no story; the proverb sets the lasting word against the wearing stone"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:40":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":42,"t":["பொருந்தாப் பழியென்னும் பொல்லாப் பிணிக்கு","மருந்தாகி நிற்பதாம் மாட்சி - மருந்தின்","தணியாது விட்டக்கால் தண்கடல் சேர்ப்ப!","'பிணியீ டழித்து விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"For the evil disease called unfitting reproach, the medicine is excellence of character, and if it is not cured by medicine, says the verse to the lord of the cool sea shore, the disease destroys one's strength.","n":"பொருந்தாப் பழி is most likely blame that does not fit the man, an undeserved reproach, though it may be reproach that should never attach to him; either way the verse does not tell him to ignore it but to answer it with மாட்சி, as a disease must be treated or it wastes the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","marundhu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 40","lb":"PZ40","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பிணியீ டழித்து விடும்","a disease destroys one's strength","a reproach must be answered by worth of conduct or it ruins the man","general","","no story; untreated illness stands for untreated reproach"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:41":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":43,"t":["உரிஞ்சி நடப்பாரை உள்ளடி நோவா","நெருஞ்சியும் செய்வதொன் றில்லை - செருந்தி","இருங்கழித் தாழும் எறிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'பெரும்பழியும் பேணாதார்க்(கு) இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even the nerunji thorn can do nothing to hurt the inner soles of those who walk with scraping feet, says the verse to the lord of the cool shore of beating waves where the serunthi bends over the wide backwater, and even great reproach is nothing to those who do not care for it.","n":"The verse is not praise of indifference: the hardened sole stands for the shameless, whom blame cannot reach as it reaches the man who cares. Under the heading ஒழுக்கம் it describes a failure, not a strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 41","lb":"PZ41","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரும்பழியும் பேணாதார்க்(கு) இல்","even great reproach does not exist for those who do not mind it","the shameless are untouched by reproach as calloused feet by thorns","general","","no story; the proverb names a type, the man who does not heed blame"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:42":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":44,"t":["ஆவிற்கு அரும்பனி தாங்கிய மாலையும்","கோவிற்குக் கோவலன் என்றுலகம் கூறுமால்","தேவர்க்கு மக்கட்(கு) எனல்வேண்டா 'தீங்குரைக்கும்","நாவிற்கு நல்குரவு இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The world calls even Mal, the lord who bore the hard cold rain for the cows, a mere cowherd, and there is no need to ask whether it is gods or men: the tongue that speaks evil never runs short.","n":"The parse takes மாலையும் as மால் with உம் (even Mal) rather than மாலை, evening, and கோவிற்கு as 'to the lord'. On that reading the example is a god whose saving act is turned into a slight on his rank; the point is that slander spares no one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","sol","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 42","lb":"PZ42","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீங்குரைக்கும் நாவிற்கு நல்குரவு இல்","the tongue that speaks evil knows no poverty","the evil-speaking tongue finds something to belittle even in a god's saving deed","named","அரும்பனி தாங்கிய மாலையும்","Mal, having sheltered the cows from hard cold rain, is still called by the world a cowherd"],"ch":"pz:5"},"pz:43":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":45,"t":["பூவுட்கும் கண்ணாய்! பொறுப்பர் எனக்கருதி","யாவர்க்கே யாயினும் இன்னா செயல்வேண்டா","தேவர்க்கும் கைகூடத் திண்ணன்பி னார்க்கேயும்","'நோவச்செய் நோயின்மை இல்'"],"p":"","r":"One should not hurt anyone at all on the thought that they will bear it, since even for the gods and for those of steadfast love there is no escaping pain for one who makes others suffer.","n":"The verse closes the excuse that the victim's forbearance licenses the harm: the other's patience does not cancel the rebound on the one who hurts. Line 3 is hard to construe: தேவர்க்கும் and திண்ணன்பினார்க்கேயும் may name those to whom the proverb applies (no one, not even gods, is exempt) or those one must not hurt even when they would forgive; the reading takes the first, and கைகூட is left loosely rendered. The address பூவுட்கும் கண்ணாய் (you whose eyes flowers fear) is a frame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["innaa","porai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 43","lb":"PZ43","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நோவச்செய் நோயின்மை இல்","for one who makes others ache there is no freedom from ache","hurting someone because they will forgive still brings pain back on the one who hurts","general","","no story; the proverb states the rebound of pain as a rule that binds even gods and the steadfast"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:44":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":46,"t":["வினைப்பயன் ஒன்றின்றி வேற்றுமை கொண்டு","நினைத்துப் பிறர்பனிப்ப செய்யாமை வேண்டும்","புனம்பொன் அவிர்சுணங்கி பூங்கொம்பர் அன்னாய்!","'தனக்கின்னா இன்ன பிறர்க்கு'"],"p":"","r":"With no gain to be had from the act and only a sense of the other as different from oneself, one must not deliberately do what makes others tremble, for what is painful to oneself is painful to others.","n":"வேற்றுமை கொண்டு names the cause the proverb answers: treating the other as unlike oneself is what lets one hurt them, and the proverb denies the difference by making one's own pain the measure of theirs. வினைப்பயன் ஒன்றின்றி marks the case as gratuitous harm, with nothing even gained by it. The address to a woman likened to a flowering branch is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","arul","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 44","lb":"PZ44","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தனக்கின்னா இன்ன பிறர்க்கு","what is painful to oneself is painful to others","gratuitous harm rests on a false sense that others feel differently from oneself","general","","no story; the proverb is the self-measure applied to anyone who would harm another for no purpose"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:45":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":47,"t":["ஆற்றார் இவரென்(று) அடைந்த தமரையும்","தோற்றத்தாம் எள்ளி நலியற்க - போற்றான்","'கடையடைத்து வைத்துப் புடைத்தக்கால் நாயும்","உடையானைக் கவ்வி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"One should not, judging by appearances that they are powerless, scorn and torment even one's own people who have come to depend on one, for a dog shut in and beaten by a master who does not care for it will bite him.","n":"The verse names the dependants (அடைந்த தமர்) as the victims and the master's belief in their weakness as the error; the proverb shows the weakness is only apparent, and the detail of the shut door (கடையடைத்து) makes cornering the cause of the bite. The proverb's opening quotation mark falls at line 3 and there is no closing mark; போற்றான் at the end of line 2 belongs to the image (the uncaring owner) though it stands outside the marks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","sutram","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 45","lb":"PZ45","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடையடைத்து வைத்துப் புடைத்தக்கால் நாயும் உடையானைக் கவ்வி விடும்","if one shuts a door on it and beats it, even a dog will bite its owner","dependants scorned as powerless will turn on the one who torments them","exemplum","","a master who does not care for his dog shuts it in, beats it, and is bitten"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:46":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":48,"t":["நெடியது காண்கிலாய் நீயொளியை நெஞ்சே!","கொடியது கூறினாய் மன்ற - அடியுளே","'முற்பகல் கண்டான் பிறன்கேடு தன்கேடு","பிற்பகல் கண்டு விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells his heart that it cannot see what lies far ahead and has surely spoken a cruel thing, for he who looks on another's ruin in the forenoon sees his own ruin in the afternoon.","n":"The verse is addressed to one's own heart, so the cruelty rebuked is an intention or a wish voiced inwardly, not yet a deed; the proverb's கண்டான் (saw) also stops short of doing, and fits one who looks on or wishes for another's ruin. நீயொளியை in line 1 and அடியுளே in line 2 are obscure; the reading takes அடியுளே loosely as 'at the root' (in the end) and leaves நீயொளியை unrendered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 46","lb":"PZ46","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முற்பகல் கண்டான் பிறன்கேடு தன்கேடு பிற்பகல் கண்டு விடும்","one who saw another's ruin in the forenoon will see his own ruin in the afternoon","even a cruel thought toward another returns as one's own ruin, and soon","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the speaker's own heart, which has voiced a cruel wish"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:47":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":49,"t":["தோற்றத்தால் பொல்லார் துணையில்லார் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","மாற்றத்தால் செற்றார் எனவலியார் ஆட்டியக்கால்","'ஆற்றாது அவரழுத கண்ணீர் அவையவர்க்குக்","கூற்றமாய் வீழ்ந்து விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"When the strong torment those who are ill-favoured in looks, those without support, the poor, and those they count enemies over a word, the tears the victims weep, unable to bear it, fall on the strong as Death.","n":"The verse names four kinds of the defenceless, one of them (மாற்றத்தால் செற்றார், those taken as foes for a retort) defenceless only because the strong have chosen to treat a word as enmity. The tears are made the agent of the strong man's death, with no king or god named as the one who punishes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vali","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 47","lb":"PZ47","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆற்றாது அவரழுத கண்ணீர் அவையவர்க்குக் கூற்றமாய் வீழ்ந்து விடும்","the tears they wept, unable to bear it, fall upon them as Death","oppressing the defenceless brings death on the oppressor through his victims' tears","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the strong who torment four kinds of the weak"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:48":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":50,"t":["மிக்குடையர் ஆகி மிகமதிக்கப் பட்டாரை","ஒற்கப் படமுயறும் என்றல் இழுக்காகும்","நற்கெளி தாகி விடினும் நளிர்வரைமேல்","'கற்கிள்ளிக் கையுய்ந்தார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"To say one will try to bring down those who have much and are held in high esteem is a fault, even if it looks easy, for no one ever pinched a stone off a cold mountain and came away with his hand unhurt.","n":"நற்கெளி தாகி விடினும் concedes that the attack may look easy, and the proverb explains why the look deceives: the mountain does nothing, the hand is hurt by its own act against something harder than itself. The target is the great in means and repute, not necessarily the virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 48","lb":"PZ48","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கற்கிள்ளிக் கையுய்ந்தார் இல்","no one has pinched at stone and kept the hand whole","attacking the established great harms the attacker, however easy it seems","general","","no story; the proverb's image of pinching rock on a cold hill is applied to anyone who sets out to bring the great low"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:49":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":51,"t":["நீர்த்தகவு இல்லார் நிரம்பாமைத் தந்நலியின்","கூர்த்தவரைத் தாம்நலிதல் கோளன்றால் - சான்றவர்க்குப்","பார்த்தோடிச் சென்று கதம்பட்டு 'நாய்கவ்வின்","பேர்த்துநாய் கவ்வினார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"If people lacking worth, out of their own incompleteness, torment the worthy, it is not the way of the worthy to torment them back in anger, for when a dog runs up angered and bites, no one has ever bitten the dog back.","n":"The ground of forbearance is rank, not compassion: the offender is likened to a dog, and retaliating would mean doing what a dog does. கோள் names non-retaliation as the settled stand of the worthy. Line 2's கூர்த்தவரை may mean the worthy are 'keen' or that they 'grow sharp' (angered); the reading takes the second, with the worthy as subject of நலிதல்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 49","lb":"PZ49","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்கவ்வின் பேர்த்துநாய் கவ்வினார் இல்","if a dog bites, no one bites the dog back","the worthy do not answer the harm of the base in kind","general","","no story; the dog that runs up and bites stands for the unworthy who torment the worthy"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:50":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":52,"t":["காழார மார்ப! கசடறக் கைகாவாக்","கீழாயோர் செய்த பிழைப்பினை மேலாயோர்","உள்ளத்துக் கொண்டுநேர்ந்(து) ஊக்கல் 'குறுநரிக்கு","நல்லாநா ராயம் கொளல்'"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a man with a garlanded chest that for the superior to take to heart the wrongs done by the low, who do not keep their hands free of stain, and to rise against them in return is like taking up a fine great weapon against a little jackal.","n":"Where 49 says retaliation is beneath the worthy, this verse says it is waste: the great means of the superior are spent on a target too small to deserve them. The proverb's நல்லா நாராயம் is obscure; it may name a fine iron arrow (நாராசம்) or a mighty weapon, and the reading keeps only the sense of a great weapon used on a small creature. The address காழார மார்ப suggests a man of rank, perhaps a king, though the counsel is general.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","kayamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 50","lb":"PZ50","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குறுநரிக்கு நல்லாநா ராயம் கொளல்","taking up a fine great weapon for a little jackal","answering the faults of the low wastes the strength of the great on what is unworthy of it","general","","no story; the image of a great weapon spent on a small jackal is applied to the superior who avenges the faults of the low"],"ch":"pz:6"},"pz:51":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":53,"t":["இறப்பச் சிறியவர் இன்னா செயினும்","பிறப்பினால் மாண்டார் வெகுளார் - திறத்துள்ளி","'நல்ல விறகின் அடினும் நனிவெந்நீர்","இல்லம் சுடுகலா வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Even when the very small do them harm, those made excellent by birth weigh the matter and do not grow angry, for water heated however hot with good firewood never sets the house on fire.","n":"The proverb grants that the great are heated, as water is by good fuel, and denies only that the heat does harm: water hot enough to scald still cannot burn the house that holds it. The verse grounds this restraint in birth (பிறப்பினால் மாண்டார்), not in learning or effort, and the counsel turns on திறத்துள்ளி, weighing the nature of the offender.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","porai","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 51","lb":"PZ51","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நல்ல விறகின் அடினும் நனிவெந்நீர் இல்லம் சுடுகலா வாறு","though heated with good firewood, very hot water cannot burn down the house","the well-born may be heated by the harm of small men but their heat does no damage","general","","no story; the proverb is the household image of boiling water that scalds but cannot set its house alight"],"nr":[["caste"],"a lineage claim of the period: freedom from anger is credited to those excellent by birth, set against the very small (இறப்பச் சிறியவர்)"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:52":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":54,"t":["ஆறாச் சினத்தன் அறிவிலன் மற்றவனை","மாறி ஒழுகல் தலையென்ப - ஏறி","வளியால் திரையுலாம் வாங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'தெளியானைத் தேறல் அரிது'"],"p":"","r":"A man whose anger never cools has no sense, and the wise say the best course is to keep out of his way, for it is hard to trust one who never becomes clear.","n":"The counsel is not to bear the angry man but to step aside from him (மாறி ஒழுகல்), and the proverb gives the reason: one who never settles into clearness (தெளியான்) cannot be relied on. The address to the lord of the wave-beaten shore (சேர்ப்ப) is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thelivu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 52","lb":"PZ52","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தெளியானைத் தேறல் அரிது","it is hard to trust one who is not clear-minded","avoid the man of unquenchable anger, since he cannot be trusted","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the type of the man whose anger never subsides"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:53":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":55,"t":["உற்றதற் கெல்லாம் உரஞ்செய்ய வேண்டுமோ?","கற்றறிந்தார் தம்மை வெகுளாமைக் காப்பமையும்","நெற்செய்யப் புல்தேய்ந்தாற் போல 'நெடும்பகை","தற்செய்யத் தானே கெடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether one must show force at everything that befalls one, and says that for the learned it is enough to guard themselves from anger, since as grass wears away when paddy is grown, a long enmity perishes of itself when one does one's own work.","n":"The image makes the feud a weed that dies not by being pulled but by being crowded out by the crop: tending one's own field is the whole remedy. தற்செய்ய could also mean 'when one does good to oneself'; either way the action is on one's own side, not against the enemy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai","porai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 53","lb":"PZ53","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நெடும்பகை தற்செய்யத் தானே கெடும்","a long enmity perishes of itself when one does one's own part","the learned need not retaliate, since a feud starves when one simply tends one's own good","general","","no story; the proverb is set beside the farmer's sight of grass dying out as the paddy grows"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:54":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":56,"t":["எய்தா நகைச்சொல் எடுத்துரைக்கப் பட்டவர்","வைதாராக் கொண்டு விடுவர்மன் - அஃதால்","புனற்பொய்கை ஊர! 'விளக்கெலி கொண்டு","தனக்குநோய் செய்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those at whom an unfit jest is aimed take it as abuse, so the jester is like the rat that carries off the lamp and brings harm on itself.","n":"The verse comes at anger from the other side: the counsel is not to restrain one's own anger but not to rouse another's with a mocking word, which the hearer takes as reviling (வைதாரா). The rat that drags the lamp's wick sets fire to itself or its nest; the harm returns to the one who started it. Its place under வெகுளாமை rests on that reversal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","vekuli","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 54","lb":"PZ54","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["விளக்கெலி கொண்டு தனக்குநோய் செய்து விடல்","a rat taking the lamp and causing harm to itself","the one who makes an unfit jest draws the hearer's anger on himself","exemplum","","a rat that seizes a burning lamp and is hurt by it"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:55":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":57,"t":["தெரியா தவர்தம் திறனில்சொல் கேட்டால்","பரியாதார் போல இருக்க - பரிவில்லா","வம்பலர் வாயை அவிப்பான் புகுவரே","'அம்பலம் தாழ்க்கூட்டு வார்'"],"p":"","r":"When one hears the senseless words of those who do not understand, one should sit as if untouched, for those who try to stop the mouths of heartless strangers are like men who would lock up the public hall.","n":"The reason given for silence is futility, not virtue: the hall (அம்பலம்) belongs to everyone and has no door to bolt, and the talk of strangers (வம்பலர்) is as open. பரியாதார் போல, 'as if not grieved', admits that the words do hurt and asks only that the hurt not be shown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kallaamai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 55","lb":"PZ55","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அம்பலம் தாழ்க்கூட்டு வார்","those who would bar the public hall with a bolt","trying to silence the idle talk of strangers is futile, so one should sit unmoved","general","","no story; the proverb names the absurd effort of locking a hall that stands open to all"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:56":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":58,"t":["கையார உண்டமையால் காய்வார் பொருட்டாகப்","பொய்யாகத் தம்மை பொருளல்லார் கூறுபவேல்","மையார உண்டகண் மாணிழாய்! என்பரிவ","'செய்யாத செய்தா வெனில்?'"],"p":"","r":"If worthless men, having eaten their fill at the hands of one's enemies, speak lies about one for their sake, there is no cause for grief, the verse tells a woman, since what is charged is a thing one never did.","n":"The slanderers are hirelings: கையார உண்டமையால் makes their lies a return for food given by those who bear a grudge (காய்வார்). The comfort rests on innocence alone; a true charge is left outside the counsel. The quoted words read as the end of a question (what grief is there, if it is said you did what you did not?), so the proverb is only a clause, and its sense depends on the words before it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","purangooral","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 56","lb":"PZ56","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["செய்யாத செய்தா வெனில்","if it is said one did what one did not do","a false charge spread by hired slanderers is no cause for grief or anger","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anyone falsely accused by men paid in food"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:57":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":59,"t":["ஆய்ந்த அறிவினர் அல்லாதார் புல்லுரைக்குக்","காய்ந்தெதிர் சொல்லுபவோ கற்றறிந்தார்? தீந்தேன்","முசுக்குத்தி நக்கு மலைநாட! தம்மைப்","'பசுக்குத்தின் குத்துவார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The learned do not answer in heat the petty talk of those without examined knowledge, for no one gores back a cow that gores him.","n":"The proverb ranks the unlearned speaker with a beast: the cow's butting is in its nature and returning it would make a man its equal. The image is a cow, not a hostile animal, so the fool's offence is treated as artless rather than wicked. The hill-country frame (honey licked by the monkey) is an address to the lord of the hills.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kalvi","porai","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 57","lb":"PZ57","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பசுக்குத்தின் குத்துவார் இல்","if a cow gores, there are none who gore it back","the learned do not retort to the empty words of the unlearned","general","","no story; the proverb rests on the plain fact that no one butts back a cow"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:58":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":60,"t":["நோவ உரைத்தாரைத் தாம்பொறுக்க லாகாதார்","நாவின் ஒருவரை வைதால் வயவுரை","பூவிற் பொலிந்தகன்ற கண்ணாய்! அதுவன்றோ","'தீயில்லை ஊட்டும் திறம்'"],"p":"","r":"When those who cannot bear hurtful words revile in return with their tongue, the verse tells a woman, that abuse is the way of setting fire to one's own house.","n":"The proverb is best read as தீ இல்லை ஊட்டும் திறம், 'the way of feeding fire to the house', so the retort burns the one who makes it. Line 2's வயவுரை is unclear; the reading takes it as the reviling word returned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kadunjol","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 58","lb":"PZ58","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீயில்லை ஊட்டும் திறம்","the manner of setting fire to a house","answering abuse with abuse destroys the one who answers","general","","no story; the proverb names arson against one's own dwelling"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:59":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":61,"t":["சுறுத்தாற்றித் தம்மைக் கடியசெய் தாரைப்","பொறுத்தாற்றிச் சேறல் புகழால் - ஒறுத்தாற்றின்","வானோங்கு மால்வரை வெற்ப! பயனின்றே","'தானோன் றிடவரும் சால்பு'"],"p":"","r":"To bear with and go on past those who provoked one and did one harsh things is the way to renown, while punishing them brings no gain, since nobility comes only when one endures.","n":"The verse sets two fruits apart: renown (புகழ்) from enduring, and nothing (பயனின்றே) from punishing, though it assumes the power to punish is there (ஒறுத்தாற்றின்). The proverb then names the deeper fruit, சால்பு, as something that arrives (வரும்) of itself to one who endures. சுறுத்தாற்றி in line 1 is a rare form; the reading takes it as 'provoking'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","saandraanmai","pugazh","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 59","lb":"PZ59","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தானோன் றிடவரும் சால்பு","nobility comes when one oneself endures","forbearing toward those who harmed one, not punishing them, yields renown and nobility","general","","no story; the proverb is a maxim about how worth is gained"],"ch":"pz:7"},"pz:60":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":62,"t":["அறிவன்று அழகன்று அறிவதூஉம் அன்று","சிறியர் எனப்பாடும் செய்யும் - எறிதிரை","சென்றுலாம் சேர்ப்ப! 'குழுவத்தார் மேயிருந்த","என்றூடு அறுப்பினும் மன்று'"],"p":"","r":"To treat the great as small is neither wisdom nor grace nor even sound knowing, and it brings harm on the one who does it; a hall where the assembly once sat stays a hall even if it is cut into.","n":"The verse denies the slight three goods in a row (அறிவு, அழகு, அறிவது) before naming its cost (பாடு). The proverb is hard: on the reading taken here, மன்று is the assembly's hall, and its standing survives damage, so the great keep their greatness even when diminished or slighted. Line 4's என்றூடு is obscure, and the proverb could also mean that a quarrel (ஊடு) broken off still leaves the assembly a place of judgement.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 60","lb":"PZ60","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குழுவத்தார் மேயிருந்த என்றூடு அறுப்பினும் மன்று","the place where the assembly sat is still the assembly hall even if it is cut through","belittling the great does not make them small and only harms the one who belittles","general","","no story; the proverb's hall stands for anyone whose standing does not end when it is damaged or slighted"],"ch":"pz:8"},"pz:61":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":63,"t":["ஆமாலோ என்று பெரியாரை முன்னின்று","தாமாச் சிறியார் தறுகண்மை செய்தொழுகல்","போமாறு அறியாப் புலன்மயங்கி ஊர்புக்குச்","சாமாகண் காணாத வாறு.'"],"p":"","r":"For small men to stand before the great, say a scoffing 'is that so?' and keep behaving with insolent boldness of their own accord is like a man with his senses confused, not knowing his way, who walks into a town and does not see the place where he will die.","n":"ஆமாலோ is the small man's mock assent, the tone of the insolence rather than an argument. The verse makes the offender blind rather than wicked: his fault is that he cannot see where he is going. Only the closing quotation mark is printed, so the proverb's start is not fixed; it is taken here as lines 3 and 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai","saakkaadu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 61","lb":"PZ61","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["போமாறு அறியாப் புலன்மயங்கி ஊர்புக்குச் சாமாகண் காணாத வாறு","like one who, not knowing the road and with senses dazed, enters a town and does not see where he will die","the insolence of the small toward the great is a blind walk to their own death","general","","no story; a dazed traveller who walks into the place of his death without seeing it"],"ch":"pz:8"},"pz:62":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":64,"t":["எல்லாத் திறத்தும் இறப்பப் பெரியாரைக்","கல்லாத் துணையார்தாம் கைப்பித்தல் - சொல்வின்","நிறைந்தார் வளையினாய்! அஃதால் 'எருக்கு","மறைந்துயானை பாய்ச்சி விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"For people of no learning to provoke the great, who excel in every respect, is, the verse tells a woman with full bangles, like hiding behind an erukku bush and driving an elephant to charge.","n":"கைப்பித்தல், to make bitter, names the offence as needling rather than open attack, and the proverb answers it: the provoker thinks himself covered, but the erukku is a thin shrub no elephant notices. The verse sets the unlearned against the great, so the greatness here is chiefly of learning (எல்லாத் திறத்தும்). சொல்வின் in line 2 is read as சொல்லின்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kallaamai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 62","lb":"PZ62","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எருக்கு மறைந்துயானை பாய்ச்சி விடல்","hiding behind an erukku shrub and setting an elephant charging","the unlearned who needle the great trust a cover that cannot save them","general","","no story; one who goads an elephant from behind a flimsy shrub that will not hide him"],"ch":"pz:8"},"pz:63":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":65,"t":["முன்னும் ஒருகால் பிழைப்பானை ஆற்றவும்","பின்னும் பிழைப்பப் பொறுப்பவோ - இன்னிசை","யாழின் வண்டார்க்கும் புனலூர! 'ஈனுமோ","வாழை இருகால் குலை'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks, of the lord of a watered town where bees hum like a sweet-toned yaazh, whether anyone will bear with a man who has erred once before and errs again, greatly; does a plantain bear a bunch twice?","n":"The question form expects 'no': forbearance is spent on the first offence. The proverb is apt because the plantain bears its one bunch and is cut down, so the second offence finds nothing left to give. The subject of பொறுப்பவோ is unstated; under the heading it is the great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 63","lb":"PZ63","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஈனுமோ வாழை இருகால் குலை","does the plantain put out a bunch twice?","the great forgive a fault once, not a repeated one","general","","no story; the plantain that fruits once and is spent"],"ch":"pz:8"},"pz:64":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":66,"t":["நெடுங்காலம் வந்தார் நெறியின்மை கண்டு","நடுங்கிப் பெரிதும் நலிவார் பெரியர்","அடும்பார் அணிகானற் சேர்ப்ப! 'கெடுமே","கொடும்பாடு உடையான் குடி'"],"p":"","r":"When the great see that those who have long been with them have left the right way, they tremble and are greatly pained, says the verse to the lord of a shore grove decked with adumbu creepers; the house of one who acts crookedly will perish.","n":"நெடுங்காலம் வந்தார் makes the offenders long-standing dependents or associates, not strangers, and the great respond with dread and grief rather than anger; the ruin in the proverb follows the crooked man's own conduct. நலிவார் can also be transitive, 'they will greatly afflict them', which would make the great the agents of the ruin; the reading cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pazhaimai","kedu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 64","lb":"PZ64","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கெடுமே கொடும்பாடு உடையான் குடி","the house of one given to crooked conduct will surely perish","long association with the great does not save one who strays from their way","general","","no story; the proverb names a type, the man of crooked ways and his family line"],"ch":"pz:8"},"pz:65":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":67,"t":["செய்த கருமம் சிறிதானும் கைகூடா","மெய்யா உணரவும் தாம்படார் - எய்த","நலத்தகத் தம்மைப் புகழ்தல் 'புலத்தகத்துப்","புள்ளரைக் கால் விற்பேம் எனல்'"],"p":"","r":"When the work they undertook has not come off even a little and they cannot yet know its outcome truly, people who praise themselves as having reached full success are like those who say they will sell at a fraction the birds still in the field.","n":"The fault is timing as much as vanity: the praise runs ahead of a deed not yet accomplished, so it is a claim on a result that may never come, as birds in the field are not yet anyone's to sell. Line 2 (மெய்யா உணரவும் தாம்படார்) is hard to construe; it is read here as 'they have not come to know it truly', i.e. the outcome is still unknown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","agandhai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 65","lb":"PZ65","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலத்தகத்துப் புள்ளரைக் கால் விற்பேம் எனல்","saying 'we will sell, at an eighth of the price, the birds that are still out in the field'","self-praise before the work is done is a sale of what one does not yet have","general","","no story; the proverb is the boast of a seller who prices birds he has not caught"],"ch":"pz:9"},"pz:66":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":68,"t":["தமரேயும் தம்மைப் புகழ்ந்துரைக்கும் போழ்தில்","அமரா(து) அதனை அகற்றலே வேண்டும்","அமையாகும் வெற்ப! 'அணியாரே தம்மைத்","தமவேனும் கொள்ளாக் கலம்'"],"p":"","r":"Even when it is one's own people who praise one, one should not settle into it but push it away, the verse tells the man of the bamboo hills, for those who wear ornaments do not put on jewels that do not fit them even if the jewels are their own.","n":"The proverb turns on தமவேனும்: the jewels are one's own as the praisers are one's own kin (தமர்), and ownership does not make either fit. Praise is treated as something worn, to be refused when it does not sit on the wearer's measure. The address வெற்ப is the akam vocative for a hill chief, used here without any love situation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","sutram","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 66","lb":"PZ66","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அணியாரே தம்மைத் தமவேனும் கொள்ளாக் கலம்","those who adorn themselves do not wear ornaments that will not fit them, even when the ornaments are their own","praise from one's own kin is to be refused when it does not fit","general","","no story; the proverb is the ordinary practice of anyone dressing in jewels"],"ch":"pz:9"},"pz:67":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":69,"t":["தாயானும் தந்தையா லானும் மிகவின்றி","வாயின்மீக் கூறும் அவர்களை ஏத்துதல்","நோயின்(று) எனினும் 'அடுப்பின் கடைமுடங்கும்","நாயைப் புலியாம் எனல்'"],"p":"","r":"To extol those who have no eminence from mother or father yet speak themselves up with the mouth, though it does no harm, is like calling the dog that curls up at the edge of the hearth a tiger.","n":"The verse blames the praiser, not only the boaster: the one who echoes a hollow self-report names a hearth dog a tiger. It concedes that the flattery is harmless (நோயின்று), so the fault is falsity and absurdity, not injury. மிகவின்றி with தாயானும் தந்தையாலானும் is read as 'without distinction through either parent'; it could also mean 'not much esteemed even by their own mother and father', which makes the boasters people even their parents do not rate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","sol","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 67","lb":"PZ67","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அடுப்பின் கடைமுடங்கும் நாயைப் புலியாம் எனல்","saying of the dog that lies curled at the hearth's edge that it is a tiger","praising self-proclaimed men of no standing gives a great name to a small thing","general","","no story; the proverb names a timid house dog given a tiger's name"],"ch":"pz:9"},"pz:68":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":70,"t":["பல்கிளையுள் பார்த்துறான் ஆகி ஒருவனை","நல்குரவால் வேறாக நன்குணரான் - சொல்லின்","உரையுள் வளவியசொல் சொல்லா ததுபோல்","'நிரையுள்ளே இன்னா வரைவு'"],"p":"","r":"A man among his many kin who does not attend to one of them, does not know him well, and keeps him apart because he is poor, like one who in speaking holds back the generous word from him, commits the painful wrong of drawing a line within a single row.","n":"The proverb pictures a row (நிரை), most naturally a line of kin seated to eat or gathered together, within which one is marked off; the wrong is the partiality inside a body that should be one. The verse fits its heading on praise through line 3: the poor kinsman is denied the good word (வளவியசொல்) the others get. பார்த்துறான் in line 1 is obscure; it is read as 'does not look to him', and the syntax of lines 1-3 is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","inmai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 68","lb":"PZ68","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நிரையுள்ளே இன்னா வரைவு","a distinction drawn within the row is painful","singling out a poor kinsman for lesser regard among kin is a wrong","general","","no story; the proverb names any line of people in which one is set apart"],"ch":"pz:9"},"pz:69":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":71,"t":["நீறூர்ந்தும் ஒட்டா நிகரில் மணியேபோல்","வேறாகத் தோன்றும் விளக்கம் உடைத்தாகித்","'தாறாப் படினும் தலைமகன் தன்னொளி","நூறா யிரவர்க்கு நேர்'"],"p":"","r":"Like a peerless gem that will not let the ash heaped over it cling and shows apart in its own brightness, the leading man, even when he is brought low, has a light of his own that matches a hundred thousand men.","n":"The gem image says two things at once: the covering does not stick, and the shine is the gem's own, not lent by its setting. தாறாப் படினும் is obscure; it is read here as 'even if he falls into a reduced state', which the ash image supports, but the word cannot be fixed. The quotation marks take in the whole of lines 3-4, so the counsel and the proverb are one sentence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandror","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 69","lb":"PZ69","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தாறாப் படினும் தலைமகன் தன்னொளி நூறா யிரவர்க்கு நேர்","even when he is brought down, a leader's own light is equal to a hundred thousand","the worth of the noble shows through any covering of low fortune","general","","no story; the proverb itself names the type, the foremost man whose worth outweighs a crowd"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:70":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":72,"t":["ஒற்கத்தாம் உற்ற இடத்தும் உயர்ந்தவர்","நிற்பவே நின்ற நிலையின்மேல் - வற்பத்தால்","தன்மேல் நலியும் 'பசிபெரி தாயினும்","புன்மேயா தாகும் புலி'"],"p":"","r":"Even when they have fallen into want, the exalted keep to the station they stood in, as a tiger, though famine-hunger torments it greatly, will not graze on grass.","n":"The tiger does not refuse food; it refuses food beneath its kind. So the verse's point is not abstinence but keeping one's station: poverty may take the meal, it does not make the high stoop to what is low.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","maanam","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 70","lb":"PZ70","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பசிபெரி தாயினும் புன்மேயா தாகும் புலி","however great its hunger, the tiger does not graze on grass","the exalted hold to their standing in poverty","general","","no story; the proverb is the tiger's nature taken as an instance"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:71":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":73,"t":["மாடம் அழிந்தக்கால் மற்றும் எடுப்பதோர்","கூடம் மரத்திற்குத் துப்பாகும் - அஃதேபோல்","பீடிலாக் கண்ணும் பெரியோர் பெருந்தகையர்","'ஈடில் லதற்கில்லை பாடு'"],"p":"","r":"As, when a mansion falls, its hall timber serves to raise another, so the great remain of great worth even when they have lost their standing, for what has no substance has no worth.","n":"The image is of salvage: the house is gone but its timber is still good building stuff, so ruin strips the great of position, not of use. The proverb is read as ஈடு 'substance, strength' and பாடு 'worth, standing'; பாடு can also mean suffering or loss, which would give 'what has no substance has nothing to lose', and the verse's sense depends on the choice. கூடம் மரத்திற்குத் துப்பாகும் is compressed and the line's construction is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandror","perumai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 71","lb":"PZ71","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஈடில் லதற்கில்லை பாடு","to that which has no substance there is no worth","the great keep their worth when their status is gone, because it lay in their substance","general","","no story; the proverb states a rule of worth, read against the timber of a fallen mansion"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:72":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":74,"t":["இணரோங்கி வந்தாரை என்னுற்றக் கண்ணும்","உணர்பவர் அஃதே உணர்ப - உணர்வார்க்கு","அணிமலை நாட! 'அளறாடிக் கண்ணும்","மணிமணி யாகி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Whatever befalls those who rose high, the discerning recognise them as what they are, lord of the lovely hills, for to those who know, a gem stays a gem though it has rolled in the mire.","n":"The weight falls on the knower, not the gem: உணர்பவர், உணர்வார்க்கு. Worth survives the mire only for an eye that can see it, so the verse is as much about discernment as about the noble. இணரோங்கி, read here as 'risen high, flourishing', is uncertain; it may refer to lineage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandror","arivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 72","lb":"PZ72","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அளறாடிக் கண்ணும் மணிமணி யாகி விடும்","even when it has wallowed in mud, a gem turns out a gem","the discerning see the noble's worth through any misfortune","general","","no story; the proverb is the gem in the mud"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:73":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":75,"t":["கற்றதொன் றின்றி விடினும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","மற்றொன றறிவாரின் மாணமிக நல்லால்","பொற்ப உரைப்பான் புகவேண்டா 'கொற்சேரித்","துன்னூசி விற்பவர் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if they have learnt nothing, the well-born are far better than those who know other things, and there is no need to go and speak finely to them, for nobody sells sewing needles in the smiths' street.","n":"The verse grants the well-born a knowledge that does not come by learning: to instruct them is to bring needles to the street where needles are made. It ranks lineage above schooling. குடிப்பிறந்தார் is a word of lineage rank, not of a named birth-group. Line 2 is read as மற்றொன்று அறிவாரின் 'than those who know something else'; as printed the line lacks a pulli and the comparison could also be 'they know another thing (of their own)'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 73","lb":"PZ73","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொற்சேரித் துன்னூசி விற்பவர் இல்","there is no one who sells sewing needles in the smiths' street","the well-born need no instruction, having what it would teach","general","","no story; the proverb is the trader who would bring wares to the place that makes them"],"nr":[["caste"],"a claim of the period that high lineage carries worth and knowledge without learning"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:74":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":76,"t":["முல்லைக்குத் தேரும் மயிலுக்குப் போர்வையும்","தொல்லை அளித்தாரைக் கேட்டறிதும் - சொல்லின்","நெறிமடற் பூந்தாழை நீடுநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'அறிமடமும் சான்றோர்க்(கு) அணி'"],"p":"","r":"We have heard tell of those of old who gave a chariot to a jasmine creeper and a mantle to a peacock, lord of the shore of long waters where the pandanus flowers, and so, by that account, even foolishness done knowingly is an ornament to the worthy.","n":"The verse does not deny that the gifts were foolish: a creeper cannot ride and a peacock is not cold. It calls the folly an ornament because it was knowing (அறி-மடம்), the act of a giver who saw the absurdity and gave anyway out of pity. The verse names neither giver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","saandror","arul","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 74","lb":"PZ74","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறிமடமும் சான்றோர்க்(கு) அணி","even knowing foolishness is an ornament to the worthy","the givers' excessive gifts were knowing folly and so adorned them","named","தொல்லை அளித்தாரை","givers of old who gave a chariot to a jasmine creeper and a mantle to a peacock"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:75":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":77,"t":["பல்லார் அவைநடுவண் பாற்பட்ட சான்றவர்","சொல்லார் ஒருவரையும் உள்ளூன்றப் பல்லா","நிரைப்புறங் காத்த நெடியோனே யாயினும்","'உரைத்தால் உரைபெறுதல் உண்டு'"],"p":"","r":"In the midst of an assembly of many, the worthy who keep to the right part will not say to anyone a word that lodges deep within, for, even if it were the tall one who guarded the herds of cattle, one who speaks will be spoken back to.","n":"The restraint is argued from consequence, not from kindness: a wounding word in company draws a reply, and no rank, not even a god's, exempts the speaker. நிரைப்புறங் காத்த நெடியோன் points to Mal as the herdsman; the verse does not say which retort he received, and the proverb may simply set the highest case.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avai","kadunjol","saandror","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 75","lb":"PZ75","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உரைத்தால் உரைபெறுதல் உண்டு","if one speaks, one gets speech in return","a wounding word in an assembly brings a retort on any speaker, however great","named","நெடியோனே","even the tall one who guarded the herds of cattle would be answered if he spoke so"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:76":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":78,"t":["எனக்குத் தகவன்றால் என்பதே நோக்கித்","தனக்குத் கரியாவான் தானாய்த் - தவற்றை","நினைத்துத்தன் கைகுறைத்தான் தென்னவனும் 'காணார்","எனச்செய்தார் மாணா வினை'"],"p":"","r":"Looking only to the thought 'this is not fitting for me', being himself his own witness, the southern king thought on his fault and cut off his own hand, while it is those who think 'no one will see' who do unworthy deeds.","n":"The proverb is a foil: it names the ignoble motive, secrecy, that the king's act refutes. He was unseen, there was no accuser, and he still judged and punished himself, so the standard is internal (எனக்குத் தகவன்று), not the fear of being seen. The counsel is general, the example a king's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","kutram","manam","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 76","lb":"PZ76","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["காணார் எனச்செய்தார் மாணா வினை","those who did unworthy deeds did them thinking 'they will not see'","the worthy judge their own faults though no one has seen them","named","தென்னவனும்","the Pandya king, judging his own fault with himself as witness, cut off his own hand"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:77":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":79,"t":["தீப்பால் வினையினைத் தீரவும் அஞ்சாராய்க்","காப்பாரே போன்றுரைத்த பொய்குறளை -ஏய்ப்பார்முன்","சொல்லோ டொருப்படார் சோர்வின்றி மாறுபவே","'வில்லோடு காக்கையே போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"Before deceivers who have no fear at all of evil deeds and who speak lies and slander as if they were guarding one's interest, the worthy do not join in their words but turn away without fail, like a crow from a bow.","n":"The slanderer's cover is concern (காப்பாரே போன்று): he backbites as if protecting the listener. The crow does not argue with the bow; it is gone at the sight of it. So the worthy do not refute such talk, they withdraw from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","saandror","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 77","lb":"PZ77","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வில்லோடு காக்கையே போன்று","like the crow with the bow","the worthy at once withdraw from slanderers who pose as protectors","general","","no story; the proverb is the crow's flight at the sight of a bow"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:78":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":80,"t":["மடங்கிப் பசிப்பினும் மாண்புடை யாளர்","தொடங்கிப் பிறருடைமை மேவார் - குடம்பை","மடலொடு புட்கலாம் மால்கடற் சேர்ப்ப!","'கடலொடு காட்டொட்டல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even when hunger bends them double, the worthy do not set about desiring what belongs to others, lord of the great sea's shore where birds may nest in the palmyra fronds, for the sea and the forest never meet.","n":"The proverb makes the separation a fact of nature, not an effort: the worthy and another's goods are as far apart as sea and forest. The shore with birds nesting in the palm fronds may hint that each kind has its own place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","saandror","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 78","lb":"PZ78","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடலொடு காட்டொட்டல் இல்","there is no joining of the sea with the forest","the worthy do not covet others' goods even in hunger","general","","no story; the proverb is the fixed separation of sea and forest"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:79":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":81,"t":["நிரைதொடி தாங்கிய நீள்தோள்மாற்(கு) ஏயும்","உரையொழியா வாகும் உயர்ந்தோர்கண் குற்றம்","மரையா கன்றூட்டும் மலைநாட! 'மாயா","நரையான் புறத்திட்ட சூடு'"],"p":"","r":"Faults in the exalted never pass out of talk, as the talk that clings to long-armed Mal who held women of rowed bangles never ends, lord of the hills where the wild cow suckles its calf, like the brand burnt on the back of a grey bull, which never fades.","n":"The brand fades from no hide, but it shows most on a pale one: the grey bull, like the exalted, carries the mark visibly and for good. The Mal clause is hard: நிரைதொடி தாங்கிய is read as his holding (embracing) bangled women, the herding-god's dalliance, an old story that is never let drop; the episode cannot be fixed from the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 79","lb":"PZ79","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மாயா நரையான் புறத்திட்ட சூடு","the brand set on the back of a grey bull, which does not fade","a fault in the exalted is spoken of forever","named","நீள்தோள்மாற்(கு)","the talk about long-armed Mal and the bangled women he held is never dropped"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:80":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":82,"t":["கன்றி முதிர்ந்த கழியப்பன் னாள்செயினும்","ஒன்றும் சிறியார்கண் என்றானும் - தோன்றாதாம்","ஒன்றாய் விடினும் உயர்ந்தார்ப் படுங்குற்றம்","'குன்றின்மேல் இட்ட விளக்கு'"],"p":"","r":"Though the small commit for many days faults grown hard and ripe, nothing at all ever shows in them, but a fault that befalls the exalted, even a single one, is a lamp set on a hill.","n":"The verse is a contrast of visibility, not of guilt: the small man's many matured faults pass unseen, the great man's one is seen from everywhere. It does not say the small are better off morally, only that height exposes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 80","lb":"PZ80","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குன்றின்மேல் இட்ட விளக்கு","a lamp placed on a hill","a single fault of the exalted is seen by all, while the many faults of the small go unseen","general","","no story; the proverb is the lamp on the hilltop, seen from all around"],"ch":"pz:10"},"pz:81":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":83,"t":["ஈட்டிய ஒண்பொருள் இன்றெனினும் ஒப்புரவு","ஆற்றும் மனைப்பிறந்த சான்றவன் - ஆற்றவும்","போற்றப் படாதாகிப் புல்லின்றி மேயினும்","'ஏற்றுக்கன்(று) ஏறாய் விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The worthy man born into a house that practises ஒப்புரவு goes on doing it even when he has no earned wealth, and though no one tends him and he grazes where there is no grass, he grows into what his house is, as a bull's calf becomes a bull.","n":"The proverb puts the weight on birth over nurture: the calf is neither cared for nor fed and still becomes a bull. The verse does not say the man earns again; it says the habit of the house survives the loss of the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","kudi","saandror","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 81","lb":"PZ81","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஏற்றுக்கன்(று) ஏறாய் விடும்","a bull's calf turns into a bull","the habit of helping others, inherited from the house, outlasts poverty and neglect","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the man of a giving house who is poor and untended"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:82":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":84,"t":["அடர்ந்து வறியராய் ஆற்றாத போழ்தும்","இடங்கண்(டு) அறிவாம்என்(று) எண்ணி இராஅர்","மடங்கொண்ட சாயல் மயிலன்னாய்! 'சான்றோர்","கடங்கொண்டும் செய்வார் கடன்'"],"p":"","r":"Even when they are crushed into want and cannot manage, the worthy do not sit back thinking they will see to it when a chance comes; they do what is due even if they must borrow to do it.","n":"The proverb turns on கடன், which is both debt and duty: the worthy take on a debt (கடங்கொண்டும்) to pay a duty (கடன்). Line 2 names the excuse they refuse, putting off the act until a better time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","saandror","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 82","lb":"PZ82","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சான்றோர் கடங்கொண்டும் செய்வார் கடன்","the worthy will do their duty even by taking a loan","want is no ground for postponing what one owes others","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the worthy man in want"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:83":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":85,"t":["மொய்கொண் டெழுந்த அமரகத்து மாற்றார்வாய்ப்","பொய்கொண் டறைபோய்த் திரிபவர்க்(கு) என்கொலோ?","மையுண்(டு) அமர்ந்தகண் மாணிழாய்! 'சான்றவர்","கையுண்டும் கூறுவர் மெய்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks what can be said for those who, in a crowded battle, carry the enemy's lies in their mouths and go over in treachery, and answers that the worthy speak the truth even when they have eaten from another's hand.","n":"கையுண்டும் most plausibly means having eaten at someone's hand, that is, being beholden to him, so the worthy are not bought by what they have received; it could also be read as having taken something into the hand, a bribe. Lines 1 and 2 are compressed and the sense of அறைபோய் as going over in treachery is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","saandror","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 83","lb":"PZ83","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சான்றவர் கையுண்டும் கூறுவர் மெய்","the worthy speak the truth even after eating from someone's hand","being beholden does not bend the worthy man's truth, as it bends the traitor's","general","","no story; the proverb is set against the traitor in battle who repeats the enemy's lie"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:84":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":86,"t":["ஆண்டீண்டு எனவொன்றோ வேண்டா அடைந்தாரை","மாண்டிலா ரென்றே மறைப்பக் கிடந்ததோ?","பூண்தாங்கு இளமுலை பொற்றொடி! 'பூண்ட","பறையறையார் போயினார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says there is no question of 'there' or 'here': those who have taken in people who came to them do not then cast them off as worthless, any more than anyone who has strapped on a drum has gone away without beating it.","n":"The argument rests on a commitment once taken up: the drum, once worn, is beaten. Lines 1 and 2 are a rhetorical question whose subject is unstated, and ஆண்டீண்டு may mean 'in this place or that' or 'this world or the next'; the reading takes மாண்டிலார் as 'people without worth', the excuse for disowning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 84","lb":"PZ84","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பூண்ட பறையறையார் போயினார் இல்","no one who put on a drum has gone off without beating it","a protection once given is not withdrawn by calling the protected unworthy","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who has taken in those who sought him"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:85":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":87,"t":["பரியப் படுபவர் பண்பிலார் ஏனும்","திரியப் பெறுபவோ சான்றோர் - விரிதிரைப்","பாரெறியும் முந்நீர்த் துறைவ! 'கடனன்றோ","ஊரறிய நட்டார்க்கு உணா'"],"p":"","r":"The worthy do not turn against those they have cherished even if those people lack good qualities, for feeding the friends one made in the sight of the whole village is a duty.","n":"The proverb makes the bond public: the friendship was made where the village could see (ஊரறிய), so dropping it would break a pledge others witnessed, not only a private feeling. The verse grants the friends' faults outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saandror","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 85","lb":"PZ85","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடனன்றோ ஊரறிய நட்டார்க்கு உணா","is it not a duty to feed those one befriended before the village","a friendship made openly binds even when the friend proves unworthy","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the worthy man whose friends prove of poor character"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:86":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":88,"t":["தெற்றப் பகைவர் இடர்ப்பாடு கண்டக்கால்","மற்றுங்கண் ஓடுவர் மேன்மக்கள் - தெற்ற","நவைக்கப் படுந்தன்மைத் தாயினும் 'சான்றோர்","அவைப்படின் சாவாது பாம்பு'"],"p":"","r":"When the better sort see a plain enemy in distress they still show him kindly regard, even if he is of a kind that does harm, for a snake brought into the assembly of the worthy does not die.","n":"The case is the hardest one: the enemy is open (தெற்றப் பகைவர்) and still dangerous (நவைக்கப் படுந்தன்மைத்து), and the moment is his weakness, when killing him would be easy. The snake is the image for such a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","pagai","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 86","lb":"PZ86","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சான்றோர் அவைப்படின் சாவாது பாம்பு","a snake that comes before the assembly of the worthy does not die","the worthy spare and pity even a harmful enemy in distress","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the dangerous enemy who falls into the good man's power"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:87":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":89,"t":["இறப்ப எமக்கீ(து) இழிவரலென்(று) எண்ணார்","பிறப்பிற் சிறியாரைச் சென்று - பிறப்பினால்","சாலவும் மிக்கவர் சார்ந்தடைந்து வாழ்பவே","'தால அடைக்கலமே போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says those far above others by birth, not thinking it too great a lowering, go to those small by birth and live in reliance on them, like a தால shelter.","n":"பிறப்பிற் சிறியார் and பிறப்பினால் மிக்கவர் rank people by birth; the verse takes the ranking as given and praises the high-born for setting it aside when they need shelter. The proverb is obscure: தாலம் may be the palmyra, a plate, or the earth, and I cannot fix what a தால அடைக்கலம் was. Whether the verse praises humility or reports necessity is read from the heading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","panivu","adaikkalam","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 87","lb":"PZ87","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தால அடைக்கலமே போன்று","like a தால refuge (sense not fixed)","the high-born are not shamed by depending on those below them by birth","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the high-born who take refuge with the low-born"],"nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank of the period: people ranked as great or small by birth, taken as given"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:88":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":90,"t":["பெரிய குடிப்பிறத் தாரும் தமக்குச்","சிறியார் இனமாய் ஒழுகுதல் - எறியிலை","வேலொடு நேரொக்கும் கண்ணாய்! அஃதன்றோ","'பூவோடு நாரியைக்கு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when those born in great families conduct themselves as kin with those lesser than they, it is the way the string is joined to the flower.","n":"The image leaves the gain mostly with the lesser party, the string that is carried with the flower, though the garland needs both. The phrase சிறியார் இனமாய் ஒழுகுதல் could also be read the other way, the lesser keeping company as kin of the great; the reading follows the heading. குடி here ranks families by birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inam","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 88","lb":"PZ88","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பூவோடு நாரியைக்கு மாறு","the way string is joined with flowers","the great raise the lesser by joining them, as a flower lends itself to its string","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to great families who take lesser people into their company"],"nr":[["caste"],"family rank of the period: great and small houses ranked by birth, taken as given"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:89":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":91,"t":["சிறியவர் எய்திய செல்வத்தின் நாணப்","பெரியவர் நல்குரவு நன்றே -தெரியின்","மதுமயங்கு பூங்கோதை மாணிழாய்! 'மோரின்","முதுநெய் தீதாகலோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The poverty of the great is better than the wealth the small have come by, which is a thing to be ashamed of, for old ghee never becomes worse than buttermilk.","n":"The proverb concedes that the ghee is old, that is, that the great have declined, and holds that what they are still ranks above the fresh but lesser thing. நாண attaches shame to the small men's wealth, not to the poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","saandror","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 89","lb":"PZ89","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மோரின் முதுநெய் தீதாகலோ இல்","old ghee is never worse than buttermilk","the declined worth of the great still outranks the wealth of the small","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the great man fallen poor beside the small man grown rich"],"ch":"pz:11"},"pz:90":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":92,"t":["மிக்குப் பெருகி மிகுபுனல் பாய்ந்தாலும்","உப்பொழிதல் செல்லா ஒலிகடல்போல் - மிக்க","இனநலம் நன்குடைய ராயினும் 'என்றும்","மனநலம் ஆகாவாம் கீழ்'"],"p":"","r":"The roaring sea does not lose its salt though great floods of fresh water pour into it, and in the same way the base never come to goodness of mind, however good the company they keep.","n":"Lines 3-4 turn the wording of a familiar couplet inside out: இனநலம் நன்குடைய ராயினும் ... மனநலம் ஆகா, good company in full, and still no good mind. The image is chosen against the idea that water takes on the nature of what it meets: the sea takes in the river and stays salt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","inam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 90","lb":"PZ90","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["என்றும் மனநலம் ஆகாவாம் கீழ்","the base never become good of mind","good company cannot reform the base","general","","no story; the proverb states the nature of the base as a type, matched by the salt sea"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:91":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":93,"t":["தக்காரோ(டு) ஒன்றித் தமராய் ஒழுகினார்","மிக்காரால் என்று சிறியாரைத் தாம்சேறார்","கொக்கார் வளவய லூரா! 'தினலாமோ","அக்காரம் சேர்ந்த மணல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have joined the worthy and live as their own people do not go over to the small, even when the small are said to be superior, for, as the verse asks the lord of the fertile town of cranes, can anyone eat sand that has jaggery mixed into it?","n":"The proverb grants the small some sweetness (அக்காரம்) and still calls them sand: a little attraction does not make the mixture fit to take in. மிக்காரால் என்று is compressed; it may mean 'thinking them great' or 'because others say they are great'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 91","lb":"PZ91","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தினலாமோ அக்காரம் சேர்ந்த மணல்","can sand mixed with jaggery be eaten?","those settled among the worthy do not take up with the small, whatever is said for them","general","","no story; the proverb is an image of something sweetened but still inedible"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:92":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":94,"t":["தந்தொழில் ஆற்றும் தகைமையார் செய்வன","வெந்தொழிலர் ஆய வெகுளிகட்குக் கூடுமோ?","மைந்(து)இறை கொண்ட மலைமார்ப! 'ஆகுமோ","நந்துழுத எல்லாம் கணக்கு?'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether the angry men of cruel work can do what those whose character is to do their own proper work do, and answers with the proverb that not everything ploughed counts as a harvest.","n":"நந்துழுத can be divided நம் உழுத, 'what we ploughed', or நந்து உழுத, 'what the snail ploughed'; on the second, the snail's trail looks like a furrow and is not one, which suits the contrast between the base and the worthy better. Either way the claim is that the look of work is not its yield.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","vekuli","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 92","lb":"PZ92","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆகுமோ நந்துழுத எல்லாம் கணக்கு","can all that is (snail-)ploughed be counted as ploughing?","the cruel and angry cannot do what those of proper character do","general","","no story; the proverb is a ploughing image whose sense depends on how நந்துழுத is divided"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:93":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":95,"t":["பூத்தாலும் காயா மரமுள மூத்தாலும்","நன்கறியார் தாமும் நனியுளர் - பாத்தி","விதைத்தாலும் நாறாத வித்துள பேதைக்கு","'உரைத்தாலும் தோன்றா(து) உணர்வு'"],"p":"","r":"There are trees that flower and bear no fruit, people who grow old and still do not understand well, and seeds that do not sprout though sown in a prepared bed, and so for a fool understanding does not appear however much he is told.","n":"The verse stacks three failures of growth, flower without fruit, age without understanding, seed without sprout, so that telling the fool is the fourth sowing that comes to nothing. The human case sits in the middle of the plant images: old age is treated as a season that may pass without yield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 93","lb":"PZ93","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உரைத்தாலும் தோன்றா(து) உணர்வு","though told, understanding does not appear","instruction cannot produce understanding in a fool","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the fool as a type"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:94":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":96,"t":["ஓர்த்த கருத்தும் உலகும் உணராத","மூர்க்கர்க்கு யாதும் மொழியற்க! - மூர்க்கன்தான்","கொண்டதே கொண்டு விடானாகும் 'ஆகாதே","உண்டது நீலம் பிறிது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says not to speak anything to the stubborn who grasp neither reasoned thought nor the ways of the world, since such a man keeps hold of what he has taken up and will not let it go, as cloth that has drunk indigo will not take another colour.","n":"The image puts the fault in a first impression that has set: the மூர்க்கன் is not empty but already dyed, so new words have nothing to take on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","pedhaimai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 94","lb":"PZ94","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆகாதே உண்டது நீலம் பிறிது","what has drunk indigo does not become anything else","it is useless to counsel the obstinate, who hold to what they first took up","general","","no story; the proverb is a dyeing image applied to the obstinate as a type"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:95":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":97,"t":["தெற்ற ஒருவரைத் தீதுரை கண்டக்கால்","இற்றே அவரைத் தெளியற்க - மற்றவர்","யாவரே யாயினும் நன்கொழுகார் 'கைக்குமே","தேவரே தின்னினும் வேம்பு'"],"p":"","r":"Once you have plainly seen a man speak evil, the verse says, do not trust him from then on, for such people behave badly whoever they are, as neem is bitter even if the gods eat it.","n":"The proverb places the bitterness in the neem, not the eater: even the highest company does not sweeten it. So the one evil word seen is taken as evidence of a nature, not of a lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kayamai","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 95","lb":"PZ95","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கைக்குமே தேவரே தின்னினும் வேம்பு","neem is bitter even if the gods themselves eat it","one seen speaking evil should not be trusted again, whatever his standing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the evil-speaker as a type"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:96":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":98,"t":["காடுறை வாழ்க்கைக் கருவினை மாக்களை","நாடுறைய நல்கினும் நன்கொழுகார் - நாடொறும்","கையுள தாகி விடினும் 'குறும்பூழ்க்குச்","செய்யுள(து) ஆகும் மனம்'"],"p":"","r":"Men of the forest life, of cruel deeds, do not behave well even if they are granted a place to live in the settled country, as a quail kept in the hand every day still has its mind on the field.","n":"செய் in the proverb is the field the quail came from: captivity and daily handling do not move its mind. The verse ranks a people by its way of life (காடுறை வாழ்க்கை) and its work (கருவினை), and denies that settlement changes them; whether it means forest peoples as a group or only those of cruel deeds among them, the Tamil does not settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 96","lb":"PZ96","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குறும்பூழ்க்குச் செய்யுள(து) ஆகும் மனம்","the quail's mind stays on the field","granting settled land does not change those of forest life and cruel deeds","general","","no story; the proverb is a captive-bird image applied to the forest men as a type"],"nr":[["caste"],"a ranking of the period: forest dwellers of cruel work are held unable to be reformed by settlement"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:97":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":99,"t":["கருந்தொழிலர் ஆய கடையாயர் தம்மேல்","பெரும்பழி யேறுவ பேணார் - இரும்புன்னை","புன்புலால் தீர்க்கும் துறைவ! மற்(று) 'அஞ்சாதே","தின்பது அழுவதன் கண்'"],"p":"","r":"The lowest, of black deeds, do not care about the great blame that falls on them, lord of the shore where the great punnai drives off the stink of fish, just as some will eat without fear in a house of weeping.","n":"The proverb's eater at a house of mourning is shameless rather than cruel: he does what the occasion forbids because the opinion of others has no hold on him. The shore frame sets the punnai's scent against the fish smell, a cleansing the base never receive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","pazhi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 97","lb":"PZ97","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அஞ்சாதே தின்பது அழுவதன் கண்","eating without fear where there is weeping","the base are untouched by the blame they incur","general","","no story; the proverb names the shameless act of eating at a house of mourning"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:98":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":100,"t":["மிக்க பழிபெரிதும் செய்தக்கால் மீட்டதற்குத்","தக்கது அறியார் தலைசிறத்தல் - எக்கர்","அடும்(பு)அலரும் சேரப்ப! 'அகலுள்நீ ராலே","துடும்பல் எறிந்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"When men have done a very great wrong and do not know the fitting way to make it good, their trying to stand out is, as the verse tells the lord of the shore where the adumbu flowers on the sand, like throwing water from a small dish at a great blaze.","n":"The sense turns on துடும்பல் and on what தலைசிறத்தல் does here; the reading takes the proverb as a remedy too small for the harm. அகலுள் நீர் may also be the water in a lamp-bowl. Both the image and the counsel are offered tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","kayamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 98","lb":"PZ98","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அகலுள்நீ ராலே துடும்பல் எறிந்து விடல்","throwing (at) a துடும்பல் with the water in a small bowl","a great wrong is not undone by small or showy amends","general","","no story; the proverb is an image of an inadequate remedy, as far as its rare word allows"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:99":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":101,"t":["மாணாப் பகைவரை மாறொறுக் கல்லாதார்","பேணா துரைக்கும் உரைகேட்டு வந்ததுபோல்","ஊணார்ந்(து) உதவுவதொன்று இல்லெனினும் 'கள்ளினைக்","காணாக் களிக்கும் களி'"],"p":"","r":"Those who could not repay their unworthy enemies take delight, as if a victory had come to them, on hearing the words others speak without regard, and though this gives them nothing to eat or drink, theirs is the drunkenness of one who has not even seen the toddy.","n":"The syntax of lines 1-2 is loose; the reading takes the base man's joy to come from others' slander of his enemy, an empty gain he could not win himself. The proverb mocks intoxication without the drink.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","pagai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 99","lb":"PZ99","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கள்ளினைக் காணாக் களிக்கும் களி","the elation of one drunk without seeing the toddy","the base rejoice in what does them no good and is not their own doing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the base man's borrowed triumph"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:100":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":102,"t":["உழந்ததூஉம் பேணாது ஒறுத்தமை கண்டும்","விழைந்தார்போல் தீயவை பின்னரும் செய்தல்","தழங்கண் முழவிரங்கும் தண்கடற் சேர்ப்ப!","'முழங்குறைப்ப சாண்நீளு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To go on doing evil afterwards as if one wanted to, though one has seen that one was punished with no regard to one's labours, is, lord of the cool sea shore where the broad-faced drum sounds, like a thing that grows a span for every cubit cut from it.","n":"முழம் குறைப்ப சாண் நீளும்: cut a cubit and it grows back a span. Punishment trims the base man's wrongdoing but does not stop its growth. The verse does not name who is punished; the heading makes it the base.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kutram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 100","lb":"PZ100","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முழங்குறைப்ப சாண்நீளு மாறு","as a span grows back when a cubit is cut","punishment does not cure the base of wrongdoing","general","","no story; the proverb is an image of regrowth after cutting"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:101":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":103,"t":["அல்லவை செய்ப அலப்பின் அலவாக்கால்","செல்வ(து) அறிகலர் ஆகிச்சி தைத்தெழுப","கல்லாக் கயவர் இயல்போல் 'நரியிற்(கு) ஊண்","நல்யாண்டும் தீயாண்டும் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The unlearned base do wrong if they are harassed, and if they are not, not knowing where to go, they spoil things and rise up, as for the jackal there is no good year or bad year for food.","n":"The reading takes அலப்பின் as 'if pressed' and அலவாக்கால் as 'if not pressed': the base do harm in either case, as the jackal feeds on carrion in famine and plenty alike. The division of lines 1-2 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kallaamai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 101","lb":"PZ101","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நரியிற்(கு) ஊண் நல்யாண்டும் தீயாண்டும் இல்","for the jackal food is no different in a good year or a bad one","the base do wrong whether they are pressed or left alone","general","","no story; the proverb is a jackal image applied to the base"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:102":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":104,"t":["கூரறிவி னார்வாய்க் குணமுடைச்சொல் கொள்ளாது","காரறிவு கந்தாக் கடியன செய்வாரைப்","பேரறியார் ஆயின பேதைகள் யாருளரோ?","'ஊரறியா மூரியோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether there are any fools whose names go unknown among those who refuse the good words from the mouths of the keen-minded and, leaning on their dark understanding, do harsh things, and answers that there is no bull the village does not know.","n":"The proverb's மூரி is the rogue bull that every villager has learnt to step around: the fool's harsh deeds make him notorious, so his folly is public, not private.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","pullarivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 102","lb":"PZ102","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஊரறியா மூரியோ இல்","there is no bull the village does not know","the fool who rejects wise counsel and acts harshly becomes known to all","general","","no story; the proverb is a village image of the notorious rogue"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:103":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":105,"t":["நிரந்து வழிவந்த நீசருள் எல்லாம்","பரந்தொருவர் நாடுங்கால் பண்புடையார் தோன்றார்","மரம்பயில் சோலை மலைநாட! என்றும்","'குரங்கினுள் நன்முகத்த இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Among all the low who come down in a continuous line, search as one may, those of good character do not appear, lord of the mountain land of groves thick with trees, for among monkeys there is none with a fine face.","n":"நிரந்து வழிவந்த நீசர் is a claim about descent: character is said to run in a line, and a whole line is denied any good member. நீசர் here ranks people by birth; the proverb makes the line a species.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kudi","pirappu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 103","lb":"PZ103","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குரங்கினுள் நன்முகத்த இல்","among monkeys none has a handsome face","good character is not found among those of base descent","general","","no story; the proverb makes the base line a kind, like monkeys"],"nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank claim of the period: those descended from a base line are held to have no one of good character"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:104":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":106,"t":["ஊழாயி னாரைக் களைந்திட்(டு) உதவாத","கீழாயி னாரைப் பெருக்குதல் - யாழ்போலும்","தீஞ்சொல் மழலையாய்! தேனார் 'பலாக்குறைத்துக்","காஞ்சிரை நட்டு விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"To clear out those who are fit and raise up the base who are of no use is, the verse tells a woman of sweet lisping speech like the yaazh, like cutting down the honeyed jackfruit and planting the bitter kanjirai.","n":"ஊழாயினார் most likely means those who are fit or of the right order; the act described (களைந்திட்டு ... பெருக்குதல்) is one of patronage, removing some and promoting others. The address to a woman is a frame and not the counsel's audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","therithal","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 104","lb":"PZ104","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பலாக்குறைத்துக் காஞ்சிரை நட்டு விடல்","cutting down the jackfruit and planting the kanjirai","displacing the fit to promote the base destroys what was useful","general","","no story; the proverb is an orchard image of exchanging a sweet tree for a bitter one"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:105":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":107,"t":["பெரியார்க்குச் செய்யும் சிறப்பினைப் பேணிச்","சிறியார்க்குச் செய்து விடுதல் - பொறிவண்டு","பூமேல் இசைமுரலும் ஊர! அதுவன்றோ","'நாய்மேல் தவிசிடு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To take the honour due to the great and give it instead to the small is, lord of the town where spotted bees hum on the flowers, like spreading a seat of honour for a dog.","n":"The fault judged is the giver's: the verse does not say what the dog does with the seat, only that laying it out is absurd and takes from the great what was theirs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 105","lb":"PZ105","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்மேல் தவிசிடு மாறு","like spreading a seat on a dog","honour meant for the great is wasted and misplaced on the small","general","","no story; the proverb is an image of misplaced honour"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:106":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":108,"t":["பேதுறவு தீரப் பெருக்கத் தலையளித்து","ஆசறு செய்யாராய் ஆற்றப் பெருகினும்","மாசற மாண்ட மனமுடையர் ஆகாத","'கூதறைகள் ஆகார் குடி.'"],"p":"","r":"Even if one shows them great favour so that their distress is ended, and they prosper greatly without fault, the worthless do not become men of good family with minds made spotless.","n":"The patron's favour is given in full (பெருக்கத் தலையளித்து) and their prosperity is granted as faultless, so the failure lies only in what they are. குடி here is the standing of a good house, which the verse holds cannot be conferred. The closing quotation marks enclose the clause of the main sentence; the proverb may have been marked editorially.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kudi","selvam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 106","lb":"PZ106","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூதறைகள் ஆகார் குடி","the worthless do not become (a good) family","favour and prosperity do not raise the base into good family","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the base as a type"],"nr":[["caste"],"a rank claim of the period: the base cannot become of good family through favour or wealth"],"ch":"pz:12"},"pz:107":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":109,"t":["நெறியால் உணராது நீர்மையும் இன்றிச்","சிறியார் எளியரால் என்று - பெரியாரைத்","தங்கள்நேர் வைத்துத் தகவல்ல கூறுதல்","'திங்களை நாய்குரைத் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"Petty men who neither perceive by the right way nor have any good nature, thinking the great easy to approach, set them level with themselves and speak unfitting words, and the verse calls this a dog barking at the moon.","n":"The proverb works in two directions: the barking is the base man's insolence, and the moon, untouched and out of reach, is the great man's standing, so the insult is shown to be both noisy and harmless. எளியர் is what the petty think of the great, not what the great are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 107","lb":"PZ107","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["திங்களை நாய்குரைத் தற்று","it is like a dog barking at the moon","the petty man's slighting of the great is empty noise that does not reach them","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the petty man who speaks slightingly of the great as his equals"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:108":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":110,"t":["மறுமையொன் றுண்டோ மனப்பட்ட எல்லாம்","பெறுமாறு செய்ம்மின் என்பாரே- நறுநெய்யுள்","கட்டி அடையைக் களைவித்துக் 'கண்சொரீஇ","இட்டிகை தீற்று பவர்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who say 'is there any next world? get for yourselves all that the mind desires' are, the verse says, like people who have the cake of jaggery soaked in fragrant ghee thrown away and then, eyes streaming, feed on brick.","n":"The image turns the materialist's offer upside down: he promises the fill of every desire, and the proverb says that what he really hands over is brick in place of a sweet, eaten in tears. The quotation marks open at கண்சொரீஇ, though the thrown-away ghee cake (lines 2-3) belongs to the same image. Whose eyes stream, the speaker's followers' or the speakers' own, the words leave open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","kayamai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 108","lb":"PZ108","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கண்சொரீஇ இட்டிகை தீற்று பவர்","those who, eyes streaming, feed (someone) brick","denying the next world for present desire gives up a real good for a hard and painful one","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who deny the next world and urge the fill of present desire"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:109":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":111,"t":["கண்ணில கயவர் கருத்துணர்ந்து கைமிக","நண்ணி யவர்க்கு நலனுடைய செய்பவேல்","எண்ணி இடர்வரும் என்னார் 'புலிமுகத்து","உண்ணி பறித்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who, having read the mind of graceless base men, go close to them and do them good things without reckoning that trouble will come of it are, the verse says, plucking a tick from a tiger's face.","n":"The good deed is real and the tick a real relief to the tiger, which is the point: the danger lies in the nature of the one helped, not in the help. கண்ணில names the lack of kindly regard (கண்ணோட்டம்) that makes them repay with harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kannottam","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 109","lb":"PZ109","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலிமுகத்து உண்ணி பறித்து விடல்","plucking off the tick on a tiger's face","kindness done to the graceless base puts the doer within reach of their harm","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anyone who does favours for the graceless base"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:110":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":112,"t":["திருந்தாய்நீ ஆர்வத்தைத் தீமை உடையார்","வருந்தினார் என்றே வயப்படுவ துண்டோ?","அரிந்தரிகால் பெய்தமையக் கூட்டியக் கண்ணும்","'பொருந்தாமண் ஆகா சுவர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells the listener to correct his eagerness, asking whether the wicked ever come under control just because they have suffered, for even when chopped straw is worked into it unfit clay will not become a wall.","n":"The straw stands for the hardship or correction mixed into the base man's life; the clay, not the binder, decides whether a wall stands. ஆர்வம் is the hope of reforming them, and the opening imperative turns the verse on the would-be reformer rather than the wicked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 110","lb":"PZ110","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பொருந்தாமண் ஆகா சுவர்","clay that does not bind will not make a wall","hardship does not make the wicked tractable, because the fault is in their nature","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the hope that suffering will reform the wicked"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:111":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":113,"t":["குலத்துச் சிறியார் கலாந்தணிப்பான் புக்கு","விலக்குவார் மேலும் எழுதல் - நிலத்து","நிலையழுங்க வேண்டிப் புடைத்தக்கால் 'வெண்மாத்","தலைக்கீழாக் காதி விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"Men small by lineage, going in to settle a quarrel, rise up even against those who would part the fighters, and the verse likens this to white flour that, beaten to settle it firm on the ground, turns over and spills.","n":"குலத்துச் சிறியார் is a birth term: it ranks people by the family they come from and lays the quarrelsome temper on that birth, though it could also mean those who are the mean ones of a family. The proverb is obscure; வெண்மா may be white flour or a pale animal, and காதி விடல் is taken here as spilling. The sense that the attempt to calm only overturns things is fairly secure; the image's detail is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kudi","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 111","lb":"PZ111","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வெண்மாத் தலைக்கீழாக் காதி விடல்","the white flour turning upside down and spilling","the base sent to calm a quarrel only overturn it further","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the low-born peacemaker who turns on the peacemakers"],"nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank view of the period: quarrelsomeness is attributed to those of small lineage"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:112":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":114,"t":["சொல்எதிர்ந்து தம்மை வழிபட்டு ஒழுகலராய்க்","கல்எறிந்தாற் போலத் கலாந்தலைக் கொள்வாரை","இல்இருந்(து) ஆற்ற முனிவித்தல் 'உள்ளிருந்து","அச்சாணி தாம்கழிக்கு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To keep in one's house, and to anger greatly, those who contradict what they are told, will not live in obedience, and take up a quarrel like a thrown stone is, the verse says, to pull out the axle-pin oneself while sitting inside the cart.","n":"இல்இருந்து and உள்ளிருந்து answer each other: the defiant are inside the house as the rider is inside the cart, so the ruin is self-made and falls on the one who provokes them. The verse does not forbid keeping such men; it is the provoking (முனிவித்தல்) of them at close quarters that the proverb condemns.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kayamai","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 112","lb":"PZ112","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உள்ளிருந்து அச்சாணி தாம்கழிக்கு மாறு","like pulling out the axle-pin oneself from inside","provoking the quarrelsome among one's own household undoes oneself","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who provokes the defiant within his own house"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:113":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":115,"t":["நாணார் பரியார் நயனில செய்தொழுகும்","பேணா அறிவிலா மக்களைப் பேணி","ஒழுக்கி அவரோடு உடனுறை செய்தல்","'புழுப்பெய்து புண்பொதியு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To look after senseless people who know no shame, feel for no one, do worthless things and cherish nothing, to guide them and live together with them is, the verse says, like putting worms into a wound and binding it up.","n":"The image makes the harm one's own care: the bandage that should heal the wound seals in what eats it. பேணி and ஒழுக்கி say the companion is not only tolerated but fostered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kayamai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 113","lb":"PZ113","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புழுப்பெய்து புண்பொதியு மாறு","like putting in worms and binding up a wound","fostering and living with the shameless lets the harm grow under one's own care","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who shelters and lives with the shameless"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:114":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":116,"t":["பொல்லாத சொல்லி மறைத்தொழுகும் பேதைதன்","சொல்லாலே தன்னைத் துயர்ப்படுக்கும் - நல்லாய்!","மணலுள் முழுகி மறைந்து கிடக்கும்","'நுணலும்தன் வாயால் கெடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The fool who speaks evil words and goes about hiding brings sorrow on himself by his own speech, the verse tells a good lady, as even the frog sunk and hidden in the sand perishes by its own mouth.","n":"The frog's croak betrays its hiding place, so the proverb joins the two things the verse says of the fool: he hides, and he speaks, and the second undoes the first. The address நல்லாய்! is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","pedhaimai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 114","lb":"PZ114","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நுணலும்தன் வாயால் கெடும்","even a frog perishes by its own mouth","the fool's own speech undoes the concealment he relies on","general","","no story; the frog in the sand, betrayed by its croak, stands for the hidden man betrayed by his words"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:115":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":117,"t":["தாக்குற்ற போழ்தில் தமரேபோல் நன்குரைத்துப்","போக்குற்ற போழ்தில் புறனழீஇ மேன்மைக்கண்","நோக்கற் றவரைப் பழித்தலென்? என்னானும்","'மூக்கற்ற தற்கில் பழி'"],"p":"","r":"Why blame those who speak well like kin when they meet a man and slander him behind his back when he has gone, having no eye for anything higher, since in any case for one whose nose is already cut off there is no further disgrace.","n":"The verse does not excuse the backbiter; it says blame cannot touch him, because a man already disfigured has nothing left to lose by reproach. புறனழீஇ is the Kural's own word for backbiting (182).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","kayamai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 115","lb":"PZ115","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மூக்கற்ற தற்கில் பழி","for one who has lost his nose there is no disgrace","reproach is wasted on the backbiter, who is already beyond shame","general","","no story; the noseless man, past disfigurement, is applied to the backbiter past shame"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:116":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":118,"t":["கோவாத சொல்லும் குணனிலா மாக்களை","நாவா லடக்கல் அரிதாகும் - நாவாய்","களிக்கள்போல் தூங்கும் கடல்சேர்ப்ப! 'வாங்கி","வளிதோட் கிடுவாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"It is hard to restrain with the tongue the qualityless brutes who speak unstrung words, lord of the shore where ships sway like drunkards, for no one catches the wind and puts it into a leather bag.","n":"மாக்கள், not மக்கள், puts such men among beasts. The proverb gives the reason talk cannot curb them: their words are wind, which has no body to be held. The swaying ships of the address echo the unsteady talkers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 116","lb":"PZ116","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வாங்கி வளிதோட் கிடுவாரோ இல்","no one catches the wind and puts it in a skin bag","speech cannot restrain those whose speech has no substance","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the attempt to silence loose talkers with words"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:117":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":119,"t":["தெரியாதார் சொல்லும் திறனின்மை தீதாப்","பரியார் பயனின்மை செய்து - பெரியார்சொல்","கொள்ளாது தாம்தம்மைக் காவா தவர் 'பிறரைக்","கள்ளராச் செய்குறு வார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who do not mind that the ignorant speak and that incapacity is bad, who do useless things, will not take the words of the great and do not guard themselves, are, the verse says, the ones who make others into thieves.","n":"The proverb shifts the blame for a theft onto the careless owner: the man who leaves himself unguarded has made the thief. Line 1 is loosely joined; தெரியாதார் சொல்லும் திறனின்மை may be the ignorant man's talk and his want of skill taken together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","thunai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 117","lb":"PZ117","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பிறரைக் கள்ளராச் செய்குறு வார்","they make others into thieves","the man who will not take counsel and guard himself invites the harm others do him","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who will not heed the great or guard himself"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:118":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":120,"t":["செய்த கொடுமை உடையான் அதன்பயம்","எய்த உரையான் இடரினால் - எய்தி","மரிசாதி யாயிருந்த 'மன்றஞ்சு வார்க்குப்","பரிகாரம் யாதொன்றும் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"One who has done a cruelty and will not speak so that its consequence may come to him sits wasting in distress, and the verse closes that for those who fear the public hall there is no remedy at all.","n":"The proverb seems to say that the wrongdoer who will not face the assembly (மன்று) where wrongs are judged cuts himself off from the only remedy; dread of being judged keeps the cruelty unhealed. மரிசாதி is obscure, and the reading of lines 1-3 is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 118","lb":"PZ118","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மன்றஞ்சு வார்க்குப் பரிகாரம் யாதொன்றும் இல்","for those who fear the assembly there is no remedy whatever","the wrongdoer who shuns judgement has no way to make amends","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the wrongdoer who will not bring his deed before the hall"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:119":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":121,"t":["முதுமக்கள் அன்றி முனிதக்கா ராய","பொதுமக்கள் பொல்லா ஒழுக்கம் - அதுமன்னும்","குன்றத்து வீழும் கொடியருவி நன்னாட!","'மன்றத்து மையல்சேர்ந் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"The wicked conduct of the common run of people, who unlike the elders deserve scorn, is, lord of the good land where streams fall from the hill, like madness settled in the village hall.","n":"The village hall (மன்றம்) is the most public place there is, so the proverb seems to say the base man's misconduct is open, settled and seen by all, not hidden. மன்னும் may mean 'lasting' or be an expletive; the contrast with முதுமக்கள் is not developed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 119","lb":"PZ119","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மன்றத்து மையல்சேர்ந் தற்று","it is like madness settled in the common hall","the misconduct of the base is a standing public disorder","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the misconduct of common, scornworthy people"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:120":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":122,"t":["தருக்கி ஒழுகித் தகவல்ல செய்தும்","பெருக்க மதித்தபின் பேணாத செய்தும்","கரப்புடை உள்ளம் கனற்று பவரே","'செருப்பிடைப் பட்ட பரல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who behave arrogantly and do unfitting things, who do uncaring things after being highly esteemed, and who vex others with a concealing heart are, the verse says, the gravel caught in a sandal.","n":"The gravel is small, inside what one wears and walks on, and hurts at every step; the image fits men who are close, honoured and hidden in their hearts, not open enemies. பெருக்க மதித்தபின் makes the injury follow on the honour done them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kayamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 120","lb":"PZ120","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["செருப்பிடைப் பட்ட பரல்","gravel caught within a sandal","the hidden ill will of those one has honoured galls at every step","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to arrogant, ungrateful and concealing men kept close"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:121":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":123,"t":["உறுமக்க ளாக ஒருவரை நாட்டிப்","பெறுமாற்றம் இன்றிப் பெயர்த்தே யொழிதல்","சிறுமைக்கு அமைந்ததோர் செய்கை அதுவே","'குறுமக்கள் காவு நடல்'"],"p":"","r":"To set someone up as one's close man and then remove him without any gain to him is a deed fitted to smallness, and the verse likens it to little children planting a grove.","n":"Children plant twigs in play and pull them up the same day, so the proverb names the fault as whim: installing and removing without reason. பெறுமாற்றம் இன்றி can mean without his gaining anything or without any cause found in him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","therithal","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 121","lb":"PZ121","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குறுமக்கள் காவு நடல்","little children planting a grove","casting off without cause one raised to intimacy is childish smallness","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who raises a man to intimacy and drops him without cause"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:122":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":124,"t":["உரைசான்ற சான்றோர் ஒடுங்கி உறைய","நிறையுளர் அல்லார் நிமிர்ந்து பெருகல்","வரைதாழ் இலங்கருவி வெற்ப! அதுவே","'சுரையாழ அம்மி மிதப்பு'"],"p":"","r":"When the worthy, full in speech, live withdrawn and those without self-restraint stand tall and flourish, lord of the hill where bright falls come down, that is the gourd sinking and the grinding stone floating.","n":"The proverb is an impossibility, so the verse calls the state of things against nature, not merely unjust. The measure of rank is conduct and speech (உரைசான்ற, நிறையுளர் அல்லார்), not birth or wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","kayamai","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 122","lb":"PZ122","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சுரையாழ அம்மி மிதப்பு","the gourd sinking and the grinding stone floating","the rise of the unworthy over the worthy is an inversion of nature","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a time when the worthy sink and the unrestrained rise"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:123":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":125,"t":["தேர்ந்துகண் ஓடாது தீவினையும் அஞ்சலராய்ச்","சேர்ந்தாரை யெல்லாம் சிறிதுரைத்துத் - தீர்ந்த","விரகர்கட் கெல்லாம் வெறுப்பனவே செய்யும்","'நரகர்கட்கு இல்லையோ நஞ்சு?'"],"p":"","r":"Those who, without discernment or kindly regard and without fear of evil deeds, speak slightingly to all who come to them and do what all the skilled and accomplished hate are answered by the question whether there is no poison for the people of hell.","n":"The proverb makes such men hell's own people; whether it means that poison is their due or that they are the poison hell holds, the words do not settle. தீர்ந்த விரகர் are those finished in skill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kayamai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 123","lb":"PZ123","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நரகர்கட்கு இல்லையோ நஞ்சு?","is there no poison for the dwellers in hell?","men without grace or fear of evil are already of hell and merit its poison","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to graceless men who fear no evil and slight everyone"],"ch":"pz:13"},"pz:124":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":126,"t":["ஒட்டிய காதல் 'உமையாள் ஒரு பாலக்","கட்டங்கம் வெல்கொடி கொண்டானும் கொண்டானே'","விட்டாங்கு அகலா முழுமெய்யும் கொள்பவே","நட்டாரை ஒட்டி யுழி"],"p":"","r":"Those who join with friends take them, once united, as a whole body that never parts from them, for even he who bears the khatvanga and the conquering banner took Uma, joined to him in clinging love, on one side of himself.","n":"The argument is a fortiori: the god took his consort as one half of his body, and friends take each other whole (முழுமெய்யும்), so friendship is pictured as a closer union than the divine pair. Unusually the quoted proverb opens the verse instead of closing it, and lines 3-4 draw the counsel from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 124","lb":"PZ124","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உமையாள் ஒரு பாலக் கட்டங்கம் வெல்கொடி கொண்டானும் கொண்டானே","even he of the khatvanga and the victorious banner took Uma to one side of himself","friends united take each other wholly, more than the god's half","named","கட்டங்கம் வெல்கொடி கொண்டானும்","the god who bears the khatvanga and the victorious banner took Uma as one side of his body"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:125":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":127,"t":["புரையின்றி நட்டார்க்கு நட்டார் உரைத்த","உரையும் பொருள்முடிவும் ஒன்றால் - உரைபிறிது","கொண்டெடுத்துக் கூறல் கொடுங்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'ஒன்றேற்று வெண்படைக்கோள் ஒன்று'"],"p":"","r":"Between those who have befriended without flaw, the words one friend speaks to the other and the meaning those words reach are one, the verse tells the lord of the cool shore of winding backwaters, and to take the words up in another sense and repeat them is, as the proverb has it, to accept one thing and take up the white blade as another.","n":"The fault is put on the hearer, not the speaker: a friend's words are to be taken at the meaning he intends, and re-telling them in a twisted sense is a betrayal. The proverb's sense is not secure; ஒன்றேற்று வெண்படைக்கோள் ஒன்று is read here as 'accepting is one act, seizing the bright weapon another', a picture of seeming agreement turned into attack.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sol","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 125","lb":"PZ125","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒன்றேற்று வெண்படைக்கோள் ஒன்று","taking up one thing and the seizing of the white weapon is another","to twist a true friend's words when repeating them is to turn agreement into attack","general","","no story; the proverb sets an act of acceptance against the drawing of a weapon"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:126":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":128,"t":["விலங்கேயும் தம்மோ(டு) உடனுறைதல் மேவும்","கலந்தாரைக் கைவிடுதல் ஒல்லா - இலங்கருவி","தாஅய் இழியும் மணலைநாட! 'இன்னாதே","பேஎயோ டானும் பிரிவு'"],"p":"","r":"Even beasts desire to dwell with those who live with them, and abandoning those one has mingled with is not fitting, the verse tells the lord of the land where bright cascades spread down over the sand, for parting is painful even from a demon.","n":"The proverb makes the pain of parting independent of the friend's worth: if separation from a பேய் hurts, the hurt is in the bond itself, not in the goodness of the one left. Lines 1-2 are close in wording to Naladiyar 76 (கலந்தாரைக் கைவிடுதல், விலங்கு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 126","lb":"PZ126","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இன்னாதே பேஎயோ டானும் பிரிவு","parting is painful even from a demon","a bond once formed should not be broken, since parting hurts whatever the companion","general","","no story; the proverb names the least lovable companion"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:127":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":129,"t":["இனியாரை உற்ற இடர்தீர் உபாயம்","முனியார் செயினும் மொழியால் முடியா","துனியால் திரையுலாம் நூங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'பனியால் குளநிறைதல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The means of removing the distress that has come upon dear ones is not accomplished by words, even when it is offered without grudging, the verse tells the lord of the deep-watered shore where waves roll restlessly, for a tank is never filled by dew.","n":"Kind words are not denied value, only sufficiency: dew is real water and still fills no tank. முனியார் செயினும் is read as 'even if done without vexation'; it may instead refer to the dear ones themselves, and துனியால் in line 3 is an odd opening for the address.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 127","lb":"PZ127","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பனியால் குளநிறைதல் இல்","a tank does not fill with dew","a friend's distress is removed by deeds, not by speech","general","","no story; the proverb is the farmer's knowledge that dew cannot fill a reservoir"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:128":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":130,"t":["தாம்நட்(டு) ஒழுகுதற்குத் தக்கார் எனல்வேண்டா","யார்நட்பே யாயினும் நட்புக் கொளல்வேண்டும்","கானாட்டு நாறும் கதுப்பினாய்! 'தீற்றாதோ","நாய்நட்டால் நல்ல முயல்?'"],"p":"","r":"There is no need to insist that only the worthy are fit to keep friendship with, the verse tells a woman whose hair smells of the forest, for one should take friendship whoever's it is: will not a dog, once befriended, feed one a good hare?","n":"The counsel is pragmatic and runs against the usual demand to test friends first: even a low creature, made a friend, brings in game. The heading (the nature of friendship) fits, but the verse values friendship by its yield rather than by the friend's character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","payan","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 128","lb":"PZ128","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீற்றாதோ நாய்நட்டால் நல்ல முயல்?","if one makes friends with a dog, will it not feed one a good hare?","friendship with anyone, even the lowly, can bring profit","general","","no story; the proverb is the hunting dog that brings its master game"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:129":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":131,"t":["தீர்ந்தேம் எனக்கருதித் தேற்றா(து) ஒழுகித்தாம்","ஊர்ந்த பரிவும் இலராகிச் - சேர்ந்தார்","பழமை கந்தாகப் பரியார் புதுமை","'முழநட்பிற் சாணுட்கு நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"Some, thinking 'we have reached the end of it', go on without giving assurance and lose even the care they once showed, and friends who lean on old acquaintance as on a post pay no heed to this new coldness; so a span of respect is better than a cubit of friendship.","n":"The proverb measures regard (உட்கு, awe or respect) against intimacy and ranks a little of the first above much of the second, a warning that old familiarity without care decays. The syntax of lines 1-3 is loose and who 'leans on old acquaintance' (the neglectful or the neglected) is not fixed; the reading here is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pazhaimai","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 129","lb":"PZ129","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முழநட்பிற் சாணுட்கு நன்று","a span of respect is better than a cubit of friendship","friendship that trades on old acquaintance and drops its care is worth less than a little respect","general","","no story; the proverb weighs two measures of length against each other"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:130":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":132,"t":["கொழித்துக் கொளப்பட்ட நண்பின் அவரைப்","பழித்துப் பலர்நடுவண் சொல்லாடார் - என்கொல்?","விழித்தலரும் நெய்தல் துறைவா! 'உரையார்","இழித்தக்க காணிற் கனா'"],"p":"","r":"Those whose friendship they took after sifting them like grain, they do not blame by speaking of them among many, and why so, the verse asks the lord of the ford where the neythal opens like waking eyes: people do not tell their dream when they have seen something shameful in it.","n":"The proverb makes a chosen friend's fault like a shameful dream: it is one's own, seen privately, and telling it exposes the teller. The sifting (கொழித்து) comes first; the silence is owed because the choice was one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","purangooral","aaraaydhal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 130","lb":"PZ130","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உரையார் இழித்தக்க காணிற் கனா","if they see something base in a dream, they do not tell it","a friend chosen with care is not blamed in public","general","","no story; the proverb is the ordinary reticence about a shameful dream"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:131":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":133,"t":["நண்பொன்றித் தம்மாலே நாட்டப்பட்ட டார்களைக்","கண்கண்ட குற்றம் உளவெனினும் காய்ந்தீயார்","பண்கொண்ட தீஞ்சொல் பணைத்தோளாய்! 'யாருளரோ","தங்கன்று சாக்கறப் பார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those whom they themselves have set firmly in friendship they do not burn against, even when faults are seen with their own eyes, the verse tells a woman with bamboo-like shoulders and speech sweet as melody, for who is there who would watch their own calf die?","n":"Two things press the duty: the friend was established by oneself (தம்மாலே நாட்டப்பட்டார்), and the faults are seen, not reported (கண்கண்ட). The calf makes the friend one's own young, whose loss one bears oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","kutram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 131","lb":"PZ131","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["யாருளரோ தங்கன்று சாக்கறப் பார்","who is there who would look on while their own calf dies?","one does not turn in anger on a friend one has made, even for faults one has seen","general","","no story; the proverb is the herder's care for his own calf"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:132":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":134,"t":["தம்தீமை இல்லாதார் நட்டவர் தீமையையும்","எம்தீமை என்றே உணர்பதாம் - அந்தண்","பொருதிரை வந்துலாம் பொங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'ஒருவர் பொறைஇருவர் நட்பு'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have no fault of their own take even the faults of the friends they have made as 'our fault', the verse tells the lord of the shore where battering waves roll over swelling water, for the forbearance of one is the friendship of two.","n":"It is the faultless friend who owns the fault (எம்தீமை), so the forbearance is not a trade between two sinners but a taking-on by the one who need not. The same proverb closes Naladiyar 223.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 132","lb":"PZ132","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒருவர் பொறைஇருவர் நட்பு","the bearing of one is the friendship of two","a friendship survives when one partner takes the other's fault as his own","general","","no story; the proverb is a maxim about how a bond of two is kept"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:133":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":135,"t":["தெற்றப் பரிந்தொருவர் தீர்ப்பனப் பட்டார்க்(கு)","உற்ற குறையை உரைப்பதாம் - தெற்ற","அறையார் அணிவளையாய்! தீர்தல் உறுவார்","'மறையார் மருத்துவர்க்கு நோய்'"],"p":"","r":"It is fitting to tell the want that has come upon one to someone who, moved by clear care, will remove it, the verse tells a woman of fine bangles, for those who wish to be cured do not hide their disease from the physician.","n":"The counsel is to the sufferer, and it carries a condition: disclosure is owed to the one who will remedy (தீர்ப்பனப் பட்டார்), as a sick man tells the healer, not to everyone. அறையார் is unclear; the address is read as praise of the woman's bangles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","noy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 133","lb":"PZ133","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மறையார் மருத்துவர்க்கு நோய்","they do not hide their illness from the physician","one's trouble should be told openly to a friend able and willing to remove it","general","","no story; the proverb is the patient who tells the healer everything"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:134":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":136,"t":["முட்டின்(று) ஒருவர் உடைய பொழுதின்கண்","அட்டிற்றுத் தின்பவர் ஆயிரவர் ஆபவே","கட்டலர்தார் மார்ப! கலியூழிக் காலத்துக்","'கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"While a man has no want, those who eat what is cooked in his house become a thousand, the verse tells a man whose breast bears a garland of opened blossoms, but in the age of Kali the ruined have no friends.","n":"The verse sets the loss of friends in an age (கலியூழிக் காலத்து), not only in a man's fortune, so the desertion is a mark of the times. It describes false friends under a heading on the nature of friendship, the negative case that proves the rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 134","lb":"PZ134","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டாரோ இல்","for those who are ruined there are no friends","the many who feed at a prosperous house are not friends, as ruin shows","general","","no story; the proverb is the common experience of the ruined man left alone"],"ch":"pz:14"},"pz:135":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":137,"t":["கண்ணுள் மணியேபோல் காதலாய் நட்டாரும்","எண்ணும் துணையில் பிறராகி நிற்பராய்","எண்ணிஉயிர் கொள்வான் வேண்டித் திரியினும்","'உண்ணும் துணைக்காக்கும் கூற்று'"],"p":"","r":"Even those who befriended one with love as dear as the pupil of the eye may, when it comes to the reckoning, stand as strangers and go about scheming to take one's life, yet Death itself keeps a man until his allotted eating is done.","n":"The proverb consoles rather than warns: the false friend's plot cannot cut a life short, because the term is Death's and not the plotter's. Death is pictured as an eater who also guards its food until the time of eating (உண்ணும் துணை), so the very power one fears is what protects one from the friend. எண்ணும் துணையில் in line 2 may also mean 'in the time it takes to think', making the friend's turn sudden.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","kootru","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 135","lb":"PZ135","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உண்ணும் துணைக்காக்கும் கூற்று","Death guards a man for as long as he is to eat","a treacherous friend's plotting cannot take a life before its fated term","general","","no story; Death keeps every life until its appointed portion is consumed"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:136":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":138,"t":["எயப்புழி வைப்பாம் எனப்போற்றப் பட்டவர்","உற்றுழி ஒன்றுக்கு உதவலர் பைந்தொடீஇ!","அச்சிடை இட்டுத் திரியின் அதுவன்றோ","'மச்சேற்றி ஏணி களைவு.'"],"p":"","r":"Those one cherished as a store laid by for the day of weakness do not help with a single thing when trouble comes, the verse tells a woman with fresh bangles, and if they go about after setting one in the midst of danger, that is taking away the ladder after making one climb to the loft.","n":"The fault grows across the verse: first the reserve friend merely fails to help, then he is the one who has put the man in peril and leaves him there, as the ladder is taken from one already raised. எயப்புழி stands for எய்ப்புழி, the time of exhaustion or want. அச்சிடை இட்டு is compressed; the reading takes அச்சு as அச்சம், danger, though it could be the axle or a frame, which would leave the image of being stranded unchanged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","vanjam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 136","lb":"PZ136","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மச்சேற்றி ஏணி களைவு","removing the ladder after making someone climb into the loft","the reserve friend who leads one into danger and then withdraws his help","general","","no story; a man is helped up to a loft and the ladder is taken away"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:137":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":139,"t":["பாப்புக் கொடியாற்குப் பால்மேனி யான்போலத்","தாக்கி அமருள் தலைப் பெய்யார் - போக்கி","வழியராய் நட்டார்க்கு மாதவம் செய்வாரே","'கழிவிழாத் தோளேற்று வார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who, like the milk-white one with the man of the snake banner, do not join the battle and strike, but go off on the road and perform great penance for their friends, are those who lift a child onto their shoulders when the festival is over.","n":"The verse scorns piety put in the place of a friend's duty: the penance may be done in the friend's name, but help that arrives after the need is past is the shoulder offered when the procession has gone. It names its figures only by epithet, the white-bodied one and the bearer of the snake flag.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 137","lb":"PZ137","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கழிவிழாத் தோளேற்று வார்","those who lift someone on their shoulders after the festival is over","a friend who withdraws to pious works when his friend needs him in battle gives help too late to be any","named","பால்மேனி யான்","the milk-white one did not fight in the battle for his friend of the snake banner but went away on the road doing penance"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:138":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":140,"t":["இடையீ(டு) உடையார் இவர்அவரோ(டு) என்று","தலையாயர் ஆராய்ந்தும் காணார் - கடையாயர்","முன்னின்று கூறும் குறளை தெரிதலால்","'பின்னின்னா பேதையார் நட்பு.'"],"p":"","r":"The highest kind of men, even when they look into it, do not find that this friend has a rift with that one, but the lowest, because they take in the tale-bearing spoken to their face, show that the friendship of fools is painful afterwards.","n":"The verse ranks friends by how they receive slander: the best do not credit a quarrel even when they search for one, the lowest believe what is told them in person. The pain of the fool's friendship comes later, when the slander has done its work. தெரிதலால் is read as 'heeding'; it could mean that the low are known by the slander they speak openly, which would make them the tale-bearers rather than the hearers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","purangooral","pedhaimai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 138","lb":"PZ138","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பின்னின்னா பேதையார் நட்பு","the friendship of fools turns painful afterwards","friends who believe slander against one break the bond and bring later pain","general","","no story; the fool's friendship, sweet at first, pains in the end"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:139":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":141,"t":["தாமகத்தால் நட்டுத் தமரென்று ஒழுகியக்கால்","நாணகத்துத் தாமின்றி நன்றொழுகார் ஆயினென்","மான்மானும் கண்ணாய்! மறந்தும் பரியலரா","'கானகத்து உக்க நிலா'"],"p":"","r":"When one has befriended people from the heart and treated them as one's own, what of it if they, having no shame within, do not behave well, the verse asks a deer-eyed woman: they will not care for one even by mistake, and the love is moonlight shed in the forest.","n":"The proverb makes the loss one of waste rather than injury: moonlight falling in an empty forest harms no one and delights no one. The subject of மறந்தும் பரியலரா is not stated; it may be the shameless friends, who never care, or the wise, who do not grieve over such friends even in passing. On either reading the heart's friendship given to the shameless is spent for nothing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","naanam","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 139","lb":"PZ139","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கானகத்து உக்க நிலா","moonlight that falls in the forest","love given to shameless friends who do not return it is wasted","general","","no story; moonlight spilt where no one sees it"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:140":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":142,"t":["கண்டறிவார் போலார் கெழீஇயின்மை செய்வாரைப்","பண்டறிவார் போலாது தாமும் அவரேபோல்","விண்டொரீஇ மாற்றி விடுதல் அதுவன்றோ","'விண்டற்கு விண்டல் மருந்து'"],"p":"","r":"Toward those who act without fellowship, unlike people who have seen and known one, the right course is to behave not as one who knew them of old but as they do, breaking off, drawing away and casting them out, for the remedy for one who breaks is to break.","n":"The verse answers the claim of old acquaintance directly: பண்டறிவார் போலாது, 'not like one who knew them before', sets aside the indulgence long friendship usually earns. The proverb's மருந்து treats the break as a cure, not a revenge. கண்டறிவார் போலார் is compressed and may describe them as acting like strangers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pazhaimai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 140","lb":"PZ140","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["விண்டற்கு விண்டல் மருந்து","for one who has broken away, breaking away is the medicine","a friend who turns unfriendly is to be answered by ending the friendship, not by old indulgence","general","","no story; a cure matched to the ailment"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:141":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":143,"t":["பெரியநட் டார்க்கும் பகைவர்க்கும் சென்று","திரிவின்றித் தீர்ந்தார்போல் சொல்லி அவருள்","ஒருவரோ டொன்றி ஒருப்படா தாரே","'இருதலைக் கொள்ளியென் பார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who go both to their great friends and to the enemies, speak to each as if settled without wavering, and yet join firmly with neither are the ones people call a brand burning at both ends.","n":"The fault is not open betrayal but refusal to belong: the double speaker seems resolved to each side, and the brand that burns at both ends cannot be held by either party. திரிவின்றித் தீர்ந்தார்போல், 'as if settled without change', marks the show of firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 141","lb":"PZ141","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இருதலைக் கொள்ளியென் பார்","they are called a firebrand alight at both ends","one who courts both friends and their enemies without committing to either","general","","no story; a stick burning at both ends that no one can grasp"],"ch":"pz:15"},"pz:142":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":144,"t":["பேருலையுள் பெய்த அரிசியை வெந்தமை","ஓர்மூழை யாலே உணர்ந்தாங்கு - யார்கண்ணும்","கண்டதனால் காண்டலே வேண்டுமாம் யார்கண்ணும்","'கண்டது காரணம்ஆ மாறு.'"],"p":"","r":"As one ladleful taken from a great pot shows that all the rice in it is cooked, so in anyone what has been seen should be enough to see the rest, for what is seen becomes the ground of knowing.","n":"The verse argues from a sample: one part of a person, like one ladle of rice, stands for the whole. யார்கண்ணும் is said twice, so the rule is pressed on every person without exception.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","therithal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 142","lb":"PZ142","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கண்டது காரணம்ஆ மாறு","the way what is seen becomes the reason","a little seen of a person is sufficient ground for judging the whole of them","general","","no story; the cook's test of one ladle is the instance"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:143":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":145,"t":["யாம்தீய செய்த மலைமறைந்த(து) என்றெண்ணித்","தாம்தீயார் தம்தீமை தேற்றாராய் - ஆம்பல்","மணவில் கமழும் மலிதிரைச் சேர்ப்ப!","'கணையினுந் கூரியவாம் கண்'"],"p":"","r":"The wicked, thinking the evil they did is hidden as behind a mountain, do not grasp that their evil is plain, for, the poet tells the lord of the shore, eyes are sharper than arrows.","n":"The mountain image (மலைமறைந்தது) is the wrongdoer's own belief; the proverb answers it with others' eyes, which pierce what he thinks is screened. The address to the lord of the shore (சேர்ப்ப!) is a frame and carries no akam situation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","vanjam","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 143","lb":"PZ143","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கணையினுந் கூரியவாம் கண்","eyes are sharper than arrows","evil deeds believed hidden are seen by others' eyes","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the type of the wrongdoer who thinks himself unseen"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:144":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":146,"t":["வெள்ளம் வருங்கால் ஈரம்பட்(டு) அஃதேபோல்","கள்ளம் உடையாருக் கண்டே அறியலாம்","ஒள்அமர் கண்ணாய்! ஒளிப்பினும் 'உள்ளம்","படர்ந்ததே கூறும் முகம்'"],"p":"","r":"As dampness spreads before a flood arrives, those who carry deceit can be known on sight, the poet tells a bright-eyed woman, for even when they hide it the face tells what the heart has gone towards.","n":"The flood image makes the face's sign a forerunner: the wetness comes before the water, as the face shows the intent before the act. The claim is specific to கள்ளம், deceit that is being hidden (ஒளிப்பினும்), not to feeling in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","vanjam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 144","lb":"PZ144","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உள்ளம் படர்ந்ததே கூறும் முகம்","the face tells just what the heart has spread towards","deceit can be read in the face even when it is concealed","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the deceitful who try to conceal their purpose"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:145":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":147,"t":["நோக்கி அறிகல்லாத் தம்உறுப்புக் கண்ணாடி","நோக்கி அறிய அதுவேபோல் - நோக்கி","முகனறிவார் முன்னம் அறிய அதுவே","'மகனறிவு தந்தை அறிவு'"],"p":"","r":"As one sees in a mirror the parts of one's own body that one cannot see by looking, so for those who can read faces the face itself is enough to know another's intention, as the son's understanding shows the father's.","n":"முன்னம், the inward intention, is the old term for what a sign reveals. The proverb's fit is not plain: it may mean that the outward (the son, the face) shows the inward (the father, the mind), as the reading takes it, or that understanding is inherited, which would bear on the observer's skill rather than on the face.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 145","lb":"PZ145","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மகனறிவு தந்தை அறிவு","the son's understanding is the father's understanding","what cannot be seen directly is known through the thing that reflects it, as the mind through the face","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the reader of faces"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:146":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":148,"t":["ஓரும் ஒருவர் ஒருவர்தம் உள்ளத்தைத்","தேரும் திறமரிதால் தேமொழி! - யாரும்","குலக்குல வண்ணத்த ராகுப ஆங்கே","'புலப்பல வண்ணத்த புள்'"],"p":"","r":"It is hard for one person to discern another's mind, the poet tells a sweet-spoken woman, but everyone takes the colours of their own kin, as birds take many colours according to their lands.","n":"The first two lines grant that minds are hard to read; the proverb offers lineage (குலம்) as the way round the difficulty, so character is made to follow birth-group as plumage follows habitat. குலம் here is kin or lineage, a birth-group, and the verse uses it to rank natures.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kudi","pirappu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 146","lb":"PZ146","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலப்பல வண்ணத்த புள்","birds are of many colours according to their lands","a person's hidden nature can be judged by the colour of their kin","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to people of every kin"],"nr":[["caste"],"a claim of the period that a person's nature follows the kin-group of birth"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:147":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":149,"t":["காப்பான் மடமகள் காப்பான்கைப் பட்டிருந்தும்","மேய்ப்பாட்ட தென்றுண்ணா ளாயினாள் - தீப்புகைபோல்","மஞ்சாடு வெற்ப! 'மறைப்பினும் ஆகாதே","தஞ்சாதி மிக்கு விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"A keeper's young daughter, though she had come into the hands of a keeper, refused to eat, saying the food was of the pasture, and the poet tells the lord of the misty hill that one's own birth-group comes out strongly however it is hidden.","n":"The episode is obscure: காப்பான் occurs twice and may name a herdsman and a protector or king, and மேய்ப்பாட்டது is a rare form; the words allow that a girl of herding birth, placed in a higher house, betrayed her birth by a habit about food. சாதி in the proverb is a birth-group term, and the verse uses it to say birth cannot be concealed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","kudi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 147","lb":"PZ147","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மறைப்பினும் ஆகாதே தஞ்சாதி மிக்கு விடும்","even if hidden it will not do: one's own birth-group shows out strongly","the marks of birth show through any concealment","exemplum","காப்பான் மடமகள்","a keeper's young daughter, though in a keeper's hands, would not eat food she called of the pasture"],"nr":[["caste"],"a claim of the period that the nature of one's birth-group (சாதி) cannot be hidden"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:148":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":150,"t":["முயலலோ வேண்டா முனிவரை யானும்","இயல்பினார் என்பது இனத்தால் அறிக","கயலியலும் கண்ணாய்! கரியரோ வேண்டா","'அயலறியா அட்(டு)ஊணோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"No effort of probing is needed: whether even sages are of good nature is to be known by the company they keep, the poet tells a carp-eyed woman, and no witnesses are needed, for there is no cooked meal the neighbours do not know of.","n":"The test is extended even to முனிவர், the renouncers whose inner state might seem beyond judging. கரியர் are witnesses: the verse sets aside the formal proof of testimony for the evidence of company and common knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kurippu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 148","lb":"PZ148","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அயலறியா அட்(டு)ஊணோ இல்","there is no cooked food that the neighbourhood does not know","a person's nature is known from their company without inquiry or witnesses","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anyone, sages included, whose nature is in question"],"ch":"pz:16"},"pz:149":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":151,"t":["எமக்குத் துணையாவார் வேண்டுமென் றெண்ணித்","தமக்குத் துணையாவார்த் தாந்தெரிதல் வேண்டா","பிறர்க்குப் பிறர்செய்வ துண்டோ மற்றில்லை","'தமக்கு மருத்துவர் தாம்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says one need not go looking for helpers in the thought that one needs them, since no one does for another what that other must do, and closes that each is his own physician.","n":"Line 3 is a rhetorical question answered at once (உண்டோ மற்றில்லை), so the denial is absolute: the verse does not say helpers are bad, only that the striver's own task cannot be carried by them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","muyarchi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 149","lb":"PZ149","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தமக்கு மருத்துவர் தாம்","each person is his own physician","one's own effort, not a sought-out helper, cures one's own need","general","","no story; the physician image is applied to anyone who waits on helpers"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:150":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":152,"t":["கற்றதொன்(று) இன்றி விடினும் கருமத்தை","அற்ற முடிப்போன் அறிவுடையான் - உற்றியம்பும்","நீத்தநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப! 'இளையோனே ஆயினும்","மூத்தோனே ஆடு மகன்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls wise the man who carries a task through to its end even if he has learned nothing, and closes that the man who acts, though young, is the elder.","n":"The verse detaches அறிவு from கல்வி: wisdom is shown in finishing the work (அற்ற முடிப்போன்), and the proverb moves seniority likewise from years to deeds. The address to the lord of the shore (நீத்தநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப) is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 150","lb":"PZ150","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இளையோனே ஆயினும் மூத்தோனே ஆடு மகன்","the man who does the work is the elder even if he is young","rank in wisdom goes to the one who completes the task, not to learning or age","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a type of person"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:151":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":153,"t":["வேளாண்மை செய்து விருந்தோம்பி வெஞ்சமத்து","வாளாண்மை யாலும் வலியராய்த் தாளாண்மை","தாழ்க்கு மடிகோள் இலராய் 'வருந்தாதார்","வாழ்க்கை திருந்துதல் இன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse names the strivers as those who do வேளாண்மை, tend guests, are strong with the sword in fierce battle and have none of the sloth that drags down effort, and closes that the life of those who do not toil never comes right.","n":"வேளாண்மை beside விருந்தோம்பி is most likely beneficence to guests rather than farming, as in the old pairing of the two words; the verse then joins household giving and battle valour under one demand for toil (தாளாண்மை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","madi","virundhu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 151","lb":"PZ151","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வருந்தாதார் வாழ்க்கை திருந்துதல் இன்று","the life of those who do not labour does not become well ordered","household giving and valour alike need unremitting effort","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a type of person"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:152":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":154,"t":["ஒன்றால் சிறிதால் உதவுவதொன்(று) இல்லையால்","என்றாங்(கு) இருப்பின் இழுக்கம் பெரிதாகும்","அன்றைப் பகலேயும் வாழ்கலார் நின்றது","'சென்றது பேரா தவர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who sits idle reasoning that a single small effort helps nothing incurs a great fault, and that those who do not stir cannot get through even that very day.","n":"The quotation marks enclose only சென்றது பேரா தவர், which is not a whole saying; the proverb probably includes அன்றைப் பகலேயும் வாழ்கலார் and perhaps நின்றது. நின்றது சென்றது may mean 'what remains and what has gone' or 'whether it stays or goes'; the reading takes பேராதவர் as those who do not move.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","madi","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 152","lb":"PZ152","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சென்றது பேரா தவர்","those who do not stir (from what has gone)","neglecting small efforts as useless leaves one unable to live even a day","general","","no story; applied to the idler who scorns small effort"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:153":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":155,"t":["இனியாரும் இல்லாதார் எம்மிற் பிறர்யார்","தனியேம்யாம் என்றொருவர் தாமடியல் வேண்டா","முனிவில ராகி முயல்க 'முனிவில்லார்","முன்னிய(து) எய்தாமை இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says no one should sink into sloth saying 'I have no one dear, who else is mine, I am alone', but should strive without weariness, and closes that those without weariness never fail to reach what they set out for.","n":"முனிவு here is weariness or vexation with the task; the verse turns the lack of kin, usually a reason for pity, into a mere excuse for sloth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","madi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 153","lb":"PZ153","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முனிவில்லார் முன்னிய(து) எய்தாமை இல்","for those without weariness there is no failing to reach what they intended","the friendless man's own unwearied effort is enough to reach his aim","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a type of person"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:154":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":156,"t":["தற்றூக்கித் தன்துணையும் தூக்கிப் பயன்தூக்கி","மற்றவை கொள்வ மதிவல்லார் - அற்றன்றி","'யாதானும் ஒன்றுகொண்டு யாதானும் செய்தக்கால்","யாதானும் ஆகி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the clever take up a matter after weighing themselves, weighing their helpers and weighing the gain, and that otherwise, if one does anything with anything, it turns into anything at all.","n":"The repeated யாதானும் makes the proverb enact its point: random means and random action give a random result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vali","payan","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 154","lb":"PZ154","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["யாதானும் ஒன்றுகொண்டு யாதானும் செய்தக்கால் யாதானும் ஆகி விடும்","if you take up anything and do anything, it becomes anything","action without weighing self, helpers and gain has no determinate outcome","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a type of person"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:155":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":157,"t":["வீங்குதோள் செம்பியன் சீற்றம் விறல்விசும்பில்","தூங்கும் எயிலும் தொலைத்தலால் - ஆங்கு","முடியும் திறத்தால் முயல்க தாம் 'கூரம்பு","அடியிழுப்பின் இல்லை அரண்'"],"p":"","r":"Since the wrath of the broad-shouldered Chola destroyed even the fort that hung in the sky, the verse bids one strive by means that will succeed, and closes that when a sharp arrow is drawn to its base no fortress holds.","n":"The heroic memory is used for method, not for praise: what brought the sky-fort down was force applied by the right means (முடியும் திறத்தால்), and the arrow drawn full to the head is that means in small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aran","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 155","lb":"PZ155","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூரம்பு அடியிழுப்பின் இல்லை அரண்","if the sharp arrow is drawn back to its base there is no defence against it","effort applied by an effective method overcomes any obstacle","named","செம்பியன்","the Chola whose wrath destroyed the fort hanging in the sky"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:156":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":158,"t":["எங்கண் ஒன்றில்லை எமரில்லை என்றொருவர்","தங்கண் அழிவதாம் செய்யற்க - எங்காணும்","நன்கு திரண்டு 'பெரியவாம் ஆற்றவும்","முன்கை நெடியார்க்குத் தோள்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says no one should bring about his own ruin by saying 'there is nothing in me, I have no kin', and closes that for those whose forearms are long the shoulders are everywhere well rounded and very great.","n":"The proverb puts strength in one's own body: long arms go with great shoulders, so reach and power belong to the person, not to his kin. எங்காணும் நன்கு திரண்டு is taken with the shoulders; the link of the proverb to effort is implied, not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aanmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 156","lb":"PZ156","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரியவாம் ஆற்றவும் முன்கை நெடியார்க்குத் தோள்","those with long forearms have very great shoulders","one's own capacity, not wealth or kin, is the ground of strength","general","","no story; the bodily sign of the strong man is applied to anyone who pleads lack of kin"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:157":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":159,"t":["நிலத்தின் மிகையாம் பெருஞ்செல்வம் வேண்டி","நலத்தகு வேந்தருள் நல்லாரைச் சார்ந்து","நிலத்து நிலைகொள்ளாக் காலரே காணின்","'உலக்கைமேல் காக்கை' என்பார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who, seeking great wealth beyond their land, attach themselves to the good among worthy kings but find no firm footing on the ground, are called by those who see them 'a crow on a pestle'.","n":"A crow perched on a pestle in use must fly up at every stroke; the verse's நிலத்து நிலைகொள்ளாக் காலர் may mean the courtiers' own unsteadiness or the instability of any footing gained through a king's favour. Its place under முயற்சி suggests the contrast with wealth won by one's own effort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 157","lb":"PZ157","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உலக்கைமேல் காக்கை","a crow on a pestle","wealth sought through attachment to kings gives no stable place","general","","no story; applied to the hanger-on at court"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:158":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":160,"t":["தலைக்கொண்ட தங்கருமம் தாமடி கொண்டு","கடைப்பிடி யில்லாதார்பால் வைத்துக் - கடைப்பிடி","மிக்கோடி விட்டுத் திரியின் அது 'பெரிது","உக்கோடிக் காட்டி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if men take up their own task as foremost, then out of sloth put it in the hands of those with no firm grip, and themselves drop their grip and wander off, the task will show itself greatly spilled and run to waste.","n":"கடைப்பிடி is said twice, of the deputies who lack it and of the master who lets it go; the failure is double, in the choice of hands and in one's own slackening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","thitpam","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 158","lb":"PZ158","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரிது உக்கோடிக் காட்டி விடும்","it will show itself greatly spilled and running away","a task handed to the irresolute through sloth comes to ruin","general","","no story; applied to the master who delegates and wanders"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:159":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":161,"t":["தம்மால் முடிவதனைத் தாமாற்றிச் செய்கல்லார்","பின்னை ஒருவரால் செய்வித்தும் என்றிருத்தல்","சென்னீர் அருவி மலைநாட! 'பாய்பவோ","வெந்நீரும் அடாதார் தீ"],"p":"","r":"The verse says it is no use for those who will not do with their own effort what they can do to wait, thinking they will have it done later by another, for, lord of the hill land of red-water falls, would those who will not even heat water leap into fire?","n":"The proverb reads most naturally of the hoped-for helper: one who will not do even a small easy service for you will not do a hard one. It could also be read of the idler himself, who will not do the great thing if he will not do the small.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","madi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 159","lb":"PZ159","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பாய்பவோ வெந்நீரும் அடாதார் தீ","would those who will not even heat water leap into fire?","others will not do the hard part of one's task when they will not do the easy part","general","","no story; applied to the man who waits for another to do his work"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:160":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":162,"t":["முழுதுடன் முன்னே வகுத்தவன் என்று","தொழுதிருந்தக் கண்ணே ஒழியுமோ அல்லல்","'இழுகினான் ஆகாப்ப தில்லையே முன்னம்","எழுதினான் ஓலை பழுது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether suffering ends by sitting with folded hands, saying 'He apportioned everything beforehand', and closes that when the leaf written earlier is faulty there is nothing the one who smears it over cannot make.","n":"The writer of the leaf is the one who fixed fate (முழுதுடன் முன்னே வகுத்தவன்). இழுகினான் is taken as one who smears out and rewrites the palm leaf, so effort can correct a bad fate; if it is read as 'the slack man', the proverb instead says the leaf does not fail the idler, with a reversed sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 160","lb":"PZ160","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இழுகினான் ஆகாப்ப தில்லையே முன்னம் எழுதினான் ஓலை பழுது","if the leaf written before is faulty, nothing is beyond the one who smears it over","effort can rewrite what fate wrote, so passive worship of the fixer of fate ends no trouble","general","","no story; the scribe's correction of a palm leaf is applied to fate"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:161":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":163,"t":["முடிந்தற்(கு) இல்லை முயற்சி முடியாது","ஒடிந்ததற்(கு) இல்லை பெருக்கம் - வடிந்தற","வல்லதற்(கு) இல்லை வருத்தம் 'உலகினுள்","இல்லதற்(கு) இல்லை பெயர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says there is no effort for what is done, no increase for what broke off unfinished, no hardship for one who is thoroughly capable, and closes that in the world there is no name for what does not exist.","n":"The four clauses share the frame X-க்கு இல்லை Y, so the proverb reads as the last of a series: what was never done earns no name. வடிந்தற வல்லதற்கு is of doubtful sense; taken here as one refined to full capacity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 161","lb":"PZ161","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உலகினுள் இல்லதற்(கு) இல்லை பெயர்","in the world there is no name for what does not exist","an undone or half-done work gets no renown","general","","no story; the general truth closes a series of four"],"ch":"pz:17"},"pz:162":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":164,"t":["செந்நீரார் போன்று சிதைய மதிப்பார்க்கும்","பொய்ந்நீரார் போன்று பொருளை முடிப்பார்க்கும்","அந்நீர் அவரவர்க்குத் தக்காங்(கு) ஒழுகுபவே","'வெந்நீரின் தண்ணீர் தெளித்து'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that toward those who look upright but mean to wreck the matter, and toward those who look false yet bring the matter to completion, the capable act in whatever way suits each, as one sprinkles cold water into hot.","n":"Lines 1-2 can be construed two ways: either the two kinds of person are judged by deeds rather than looks, or the doer himself puts on an upright face and a false one by turns to suit each. The proverb fits both: hot water is tempered, not thrown out. The point either way is that the manner is fitted to the person, not fixed in advance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 162","lb":"PZ162","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வெந்நீரின் தண்ணீர் தெளித்து","sprinkling cold water into hot water","conduct toward others in a task is measured to each person's real nature, as heat is tempered","general","","no story; the proverb is the everyday act of tempering hot water with cold"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:163":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":165,"t":["தாமாற்ற கில்லாதார் தாஞ்சாரப் பட்டாரைத்","தீமாற்றத் தாலே பகைப்படுத்திட்(டு) - ஏமாப்ப","முன்ஓட்டுக் கொண்டு முரண்அஞ்சிப் போவாரே","'உண்ஒட்(டு) அகல்உடைப் பார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who cannot carry the matter through themselves, after stirring the people they had attached themselves to into enmity by evil talk, run off first for their own safety, fearing the clash: they are breakers of the shard-bowl they ate from.","n":"The fault is double: they start the quarrel with words and leave the fighting to others. The proverb's bowl is a humble broken shard, which sharpens the ingratitude: they wreck even the poorest thing that fed them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","pagai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 163","lb":"PZ163","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உண்ஒட்(டு) அகல்உடைப் பார்","they smash the potsherd dish they ate from","embroiling one's own patrons in a quarrel and deserting them is ruin of one's own support","general","","no story; the proverb is the ingrate who breaks the very vessel that fed him"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:164":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":166,"t":["புரையக் கலந்தவர் கண்ணும் கருமம்","உரையின் வழுவா துவப்பவே கொள்க","வரையக நாட! 'விரைவிற் கருமம்","சிதையும் இடராய் விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Even with those one is closely joined to, the verse tells the lord of the hill country, a task should be taken up so that they are pleased and without slipping from what was said, for a task pressed in haste breaks down and turns into trouble.","n":"Closeness is the case where one might think haste and bluntness safe; the verse says intimates too must be brought along gently and held to the agreed word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","soozhchi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 164","lb":"PZ164","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["விரைவிற் கருமம் சிதையும் இடராய் விடும்","work done in haste falls apart and becomes a misfortune","even among intimates a task must be carried through patiently and by agreement","general","","no story; the proverb states the general rule that haste ruins work"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:165":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":167,"t":["நிலைஇய பண்பிலார் நேரல்லர் என்றொன்(று)","உளைய உரையார் உறுதியே கொள்க","வளையொலி ஐம்பாலாய்! வாங்கி இருந்து","'தொளையெண்ணார் அப்பந்தின் பார்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells a woman with tinkling bangles and braided hair that one should not say to the point of hurting 'they have no steady character, they are not straight', but take only the benefit, for those who take the appam and eat it do not count its holes.","n":"It does not deny the flaw; it says the flaw is not the eater's business once the cake is in hand. The counsel is about getting the work out of imperfect people, not about trusting them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","vinai","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 165","lb":"PZ165","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தொளையெண்ணார் அப்பந்தின் பார்","those who eat the appam do not count its holes","one takes the use of a flawed helper without voicing his flaws","general","","no story; the proverb is the eater who enjoys a holed cake without tallying the holes"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:166":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":168,"t":["அன்பின் நெகிழ வழிபட்டுக் கொள்ளாது","நின்ற பொழுதின் முடிவித்துக் கொள்வது","கன்றுவிட்(டு) ஆக்கறக்கும் போழ்தில் கறவானாய்","'அம்புவிட்(டு) ஆக்கறக்கும் ஆறு'"],"p":"","r":"To have a thing finished on the spot instead of winning it by serving the other with love until he softens is like one who will not milk when the calf has been let to the cow, and is the way of milking a cow by loosing an arrow at it.","n":"Milk comes when the calf is let in; the verse makes affection the let-down that force cannot produce. The arrow image also implies that force kills the source it wants to use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vinai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 166","lb":"PZ166","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அம்புவிட்(டு) ஆக்கறக்கும் ஆறு","the way of milking a cow by shooting an arrow","getting a task done by compulsion instead of patient affection destroys what it seeks","general","","no story; the proverb is the absurd milker who wounds the cow instead of letting the calf in"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:167":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":169,"t":["மடியை வியங்கொள்ளின் மற்றைக் கருமம்","முடியாத வாறே முயலும் - கொடியன்னாய்!","பாரித் தவனை நலிந்து தொழில்கோடல்","'மூரி உழுது விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells a creeper-like woman that if one employs a slothful man he strives only in such a way that the task is never finished, and that forcing work out of one who has gone slack is ploughing with a lazy old bullock.","n":"பாரித்தவன் is read here as the man gone slack and heavy; it may instead mean one who made large promises, in which case the verse joins sloth to boasting. மூரி is the old or idle bullock that lies down in the furrow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 167","lb":"PZ167","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மூரி உழுது விடல்","ploughing with a lazy old bullock and leaving off","a slothful agent cannot be driven to finish a task","general","","no story; the proverb is the farmer whose idle bullock leaves the field unploughed"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:168":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":170,"t":["ஆணியாக் கொண்ட கருமம் பதிற்றாண்டும்","பாணித்தே செய்ய வியங்கொள்ளின் - காணி","பயவாமல் செய்வாரார் 'தஞ்சாகா டேனும்","உயவாமல் சேறலோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"If one sets a man to do, dragging it out over ten years, a task one holds as central, who will do it without drawing some small share for himself, since not even one's own cart goes without grease?","n":"காணி is a tiny fraction; the verse expects the agent's cut as the cost of the work, not as theft. ஆணி (linchpin) and the greased cart make one image: the central task and the vehicle both need lubricating. பாணித்தல் may also mean the employer's own delay.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 168","lb":"PZ168","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தஞ்சாகா டேனும் உயவாமல் சேறலோ இல்","there is no going, even for one's own cart, without greasing it","an agent kept long at a task must be allowed his small share","general","","no story; the proverb is the cart axle that must be greased to move"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:169":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":171,"t":["விட்டுக் கருமம் செயவைத்த பின்னரும்","முட்டா தவரை வியங்கொளல் வேண்டுமால்","தொட்டக்கால் மாழ்கும் தளிர்மேலே நிற்பினும்","'தட்டாமல் செல்லாது உளி'"],"p":"","r":"Even after a task has been handed over to be done, one must keep directing those who would otherwise stall, for a chisel does not go in without being struck, even when it stands on a tender shoot that wilts at a touch.","n":"விட்டு, 'having let go', concedes the handing over and still demands direction after it. The tender shoot makes the point that even the easiest task does not move itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","muyarchi","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 169","lb":"PZ169","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தட்டாமல் செல்லாது உளி","the chisel does not go in unless it is struck","entrusted work still needs the employer's continued prompting","general","","no story; the proverb is the carver's chisel that needs the mallet even on soft wood"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:170":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":172,"t":["காட்டிக் கருமம் கயவர்மேல் வைத்தவர்","ஆக்குவர் ஆற்ற எமக்கென்றே அமர்ந்திருத்தல்","மாப்புரை நோக்கின் மயிலன்னாய்! 'பூசையைக்","காப்பிடுதல் புன்மீன் தலை'"],"p":"","r":"For those who have shown the task to the base, laid it on them and then sat at ease thinking 'they will do it well for us', the verse tells a peacock-like woman with a doe's glance, it is setting a cat to guard the heads of small fish.","n":"Two faults are joined: the wrong agent, and the employer's idleness after appointing him. The cat is not lazy; it is the wrong nature for the trust.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 170","lb":"PZ170","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பூசையைக் காப்பிடுதல் புன்மீன் தலை","setting the cat to guard the fish heads","trusting the base with a task and not watching it hands the goods to the one who will take them","general","","no story; the proverb is the guard whose appetite is the very danger"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:171":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":173,"t":["தெற்ற அறிவுடையார்க்(கு) அல்லால் திறனிலா","முற்றலை நாடிக் கருமம் செயவையார்","கற்றொன் றறிந்து கசடற்ற காலையும்","'மற்றதென் பாற்றேம்பல் நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The discerning do not seek out an incapable man to entrust a task to, but only those of clear knowledge; even when such a man has learnt, come to know something and been rid of faults, it is better to go without than to give it to him.","n":"முற்றல் is hard: it may be the old, the 'ripe' in years, so that seniority is the false ground being refused; or simply a stiff, unfit man. The proverb's words are also hard to divide (மற்று அதன்பால் தேம்பல், 'to pine for want of it'); the reading takes it as 'better to go without'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 171","lb":"PZ171","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மற்றதென் பாற்றேம்பல் நன்று","it is better to waste away for lack of it","a task should wait rather than go to one without practical capacity, whatever his learning","general","","no story; the proverb prefers want to an unfit remedy"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:172":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":174,"t":["உற்றான் உறாஅன் எனல்வேண்டா ஒண்பொருளைக்","கற்றானை நோக்கியே கைவிடுக்க - கற்றான்","கிழவனுரை கேட்கும் கேளான் எனினும்","'இழவன்று எருதுண்ட உப்பு'"],"p":"","r":"There is no need to ask whether a man is kin or not: valuable matter should be let go into the hands of the learned man alone, for the learned man heeds his master's word, and even if he does not, salt eaten by the ox is no loss.","n":"The concession is the striking part: even if the learned agent disobeys, what he consumes is like the salt fed to the working ox, an outlay that returns as work. Kinship is set aside as a test altogether.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vinai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 172","lb":"PZ172","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இழவன்று எருதுண்ட உப்பு","salt eaten by the bullock is no loss","what is spent on a learned agent is not wasted even if he does not always obey","general","","no story; the proverb is the farmer's salt that keeps his working bullock strong"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:173":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":175,"t":["கட்டுடைத் தாகக் கருமம் செயவைப்பின்","பட்டுண்டாங்(கு) ஓடும் பரியாரை வையற்க","தொட்டாரை ஒட்டாப் பொருளில்லை 'இல்லையே","அட்டாரை ஒட்டாக் கலம்'"],"p":"","r":"When a task is set to be done under safeguards, one should not put on it careless men who make off once they have had their fill, since there is no wealth that does not cling to those who touch it, as there is no pot that does not cling to those who cook in it.","n":"The verse treats the handler's taking as a law of nature, like the cook's tasting; the choice it leaves is only of handlers who care for the work. கட்டுடைத்தாக can also mean 'with binding authority', so the safeguard may be the master's own control.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 173","lb":"PZ173","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லையே அட்டாரை ஒட்டாக் கலம்","there is no cooking pot that does not stick to those who cook in it","whoever handles wealth keeps some of it, so careless handlers must not be chosen","general","","no story; the proverb is the cook to whose hands the pot's contents always cling"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:174":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":176,"t":["நாட்டிக் கொளப்பட்டார் நன்மை இலராயின்","காட்டிக் களைதும் எனவேண்டா - ஓட்டி","இடம்பட்ட கண்ணாய்! 'இறக்கும்மை யாட்டை","உடம்படுத்து வௌவுண்டார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"If those who were set up in office prove to have no goodness, the verse tells a wide-eyed woman, there is no need to say 'we will expose them and remove them': drive them off, for no one was ever robbed by agreeing to part with a black goat that was going to die.","n":"The proverb is hard: இறக்கும் மையாட்டை 'the dark goat that is about to die' and உடம்படுத்து 'having consented' are read here as letting go what is lost anyway at no cost. ஓட்டி may belong to the counsel ('drive them off') or to the address. The counsel is quiet dismissal over public exposure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","therithal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 174","lb":"PZ174","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இறக்கும்மை யாட்டை உடம்படுத்து வௌவுண்டார் இல்","no one was ever robbed in giving up a goat that was going to die anyway","a worthless appointee is best let go quietly, since keeping or shaming him gains nothing","general","","no story; the proverb is the owner who loses nothing by parting with a dying goat"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:175":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":177,"t":["அகந்தூய்மை இல்லாரை ஆற்றப் பெருக்கி","இகந்துழி விட்டிருப்பின் அஃதால் - இகந்து","நினைந்து தெரியானாய் 'நீள்கயத்துள் ஆமை","நனைந்துவா என்று விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"If one greatly raises up men with no inner purity and leaves them in some far place out of reach, that, done without thinking and discerning, is letting a tortoise go into a long pond with the words 'get wet and come back'.","n":"அகந்தூய்மை here is honesty of mind in an agent, not ritual purity. The tortoise is at home in the pond; sending it there and asking it to return is asking it to act against its nature with no one watching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 175","lb":"PZ175","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீள்கயத்துள் ஆமை நனைந்துவா என்று விடல்","letting a tortoise into a long pond and saying 'get wet and come back'","an untrustworthy agent raised high and sent beyond oversight will not return what was entrusted","general","","no story; the proverb is the owner who frees a tortoise in its own element and expects it back"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:176":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":178,"t":["உழையிருந்து நுண்ணிய கூறிக் கருமம்","புரையிருந்த வாறறியான் புக்கான் விளிதல்","நிரையிருந்து மாண்ட 'அரங்கினுள் வட்டு","கரையிருந் தார்க்கெளிய போர்'"],"p":"","r":"When one stands nearby saying subtle things about a task, the man who has gone into it without knowing its hidden state perishes, for on the well-made board laid out in rows the war of the dice is easy for those who sit at its edge.","n":"The verse holds two people: the bystander whose advice is cheap because he risks nothing, and the doer who enters without knowing the inside of the work. புரை may be the hidden flaw or the inner chamber; either way it is what the onlooker does not see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 176","lb":"PZ176","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அரங்கினுள் வட்டு கரையிருந் தார்க்கெளிய போர்","the battle of dice on the board is easy for those sitting at the edge","advice from outside a task is easy, and the one who acts on it without inner knowledge pays","general","","no story; the proverb is the onlooker at a dice game who finds every move easy"],"ch":"pz:18"},"pz:177":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":179,"t":["சுற்றத்தார் நட்டார் எனச்சென்று ஒருவரை","அற்றத்தால் தேறார் அறிவுடையார் - கொற்றப்புள்","ஊர்ந்துலகம் தாவின அண்ணலே யாயினும்","'சீர்ந்தது செய்யாதார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The wise do not, going on the ground that someone is kin or friend, trust him with their weak point, for even the lord who rides the victorious bird and strode across the world did what served his ends.","n":"அற்றம் is the exposed or unguarded point, the thing one must hide; the verse refuses kinship and friendship as grounds for disclosing it. The proof is drawn from a god: if even he acted for his own advantage, no lesser bond is a guarantee. The sense of சீர்ந்தது is not certain; 'what suited him' is a reading from context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marai","thelivu","sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 177","lb":"PZ177","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சீர்ந்தது செய்யாதார் இல்","there is no one who does not do what suits him","since everyone, even a god, acts for advantage, kinship or friendship is no ground for revealing one's weak point","named","கொற்றப்புள் ஊர்ந்துலகம் தாவின அண்ணலே","the lord who rides the victorious bird and strode over the world, who even so did what served his purpose"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:178":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":180,"t":["வெள்ளமாண் பெல்லாம் உடைய தமரிருப்ப","உள்ளமாண் பில்லா ஒருவரைத் தெள்ளி","மறைக்கண் பிரித்தவரை மாற்றா தொழிதல்","'பறைக்கண் கடிப்பிடு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"When one's own people, rich in every excellence, are at hand, to pick out a man with no excellence of mind, take him aside into one's secret counsel and not put him away is like setting the drumstick to the drum's face.","n":"The fault lies with the one who chose the confidant: passing over worthy kin for a small-minded outsider is itself the beating of the drum, so the leak is the chooser's own act. The syntax of மாற்றா தொழிதல் (not removing him) is loose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marai","therithal","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 178","lb":"PZ178","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பறைக்கண் கடிப்பிடு மாறு","it is the way of putting the stick to the drum's face","confiding a secret to a man of mean mind is preparing its public announcement","general","","no story; the proverb is the act of readying a drum to be sounded"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:179":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":181,"t":["அன்பறிந்த பின்அல்லால் யார்யார்க்கும் தம்மறையே","முன்பிறர்க்(கு) ஓடி மொழியற்க - தின்குறுவான்","கொல்வாங்குக் கொன்றபின் அல்லது 'உயக்கொண்டு","புல்வாய் வழிப்படுவார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"One should not run ahead to tell one's secret to anyone at all before knowing their love, for no one follows the track of an antelope to carry it off safe, only to kill it as the man who means to eat it kills.","n":"The image makes the over-eager listener a hunter on the trail: the one who pursues another's secret pursues it to use it. The construction of lines 2-4 (தின்குறுவான் கொல்வாங்குக் கொன்றபின் அல்லது) is compressed and the reading of the image is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marai","anbu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 179","lb":"PZ179","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உயக்கொண்டு புல்வாய் வழிப்படுவார் இல்","no one follows an antelope's path to take it away alive","one who has not shown love and seeks a secret seeks it to do harm, so it is not to be told before love is known","general","","no story; the hunter tracks the antelope only to kill and eat it"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:180":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":182,"t":["நயவர நட்டொழுகு வாரும்தாம் கேட்ட(து)","உயவா(து) ஒழிவார் ஒருவரும் இல்லை","புயலமை கூந்தல் பொலந்தொடி! சான்றோர்","'கயவர்க்(கு) உரையார் மறை'"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman with cloud-like hair and golden bangles, the verse says that even among those who keep friendship pleasantly there is no one who does not pass on what he has heard, and so the worthy do not tell their secret to the base.","n":"The first half claims more than the proverb: it is not only the base but anyone, even a pleasant friend, who carries on what he hears. The proverb then narrows the rule to the base, so the verse moves from a general distrust to a practical line drawn at the mean. உயவாது shares the root of the Kural's உய்த்து (carrying a secret on).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marai","kayamai","natpu","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 180","lb":"PZ180","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கயவர்க்(கு) உரையார் மறை","they do not tell a secret to the base","since what is heard is always passed on, the worthy keep their secrets from the base","general","","no story; the proverb states the practice of the worthy (சான்றோர்)"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:181":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":183,"t":["பெருமலை நாட! பிறர்அறிய லாகா","அருமறையை ஆன்றோரே காப்பர் - அருமறையை","நெஞ்சிற் சிறியார்க்கு உரைத்தல் 'பனையின்மேல்","பஞ்சிவைத்(து) எஃகிவிட் டற்று'"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the lord of the great mountain land, the verse says that only the noble keep the precious secret that others must not know, and that telling it to the small of heart is like setting cotton on top of a palmyra and carding it loose.","n":"Cotton fluffed on a palm-top is scattered by the wind beyond any gathering back; the image stresses that the loss cannot be undone and spreads in every direction. Keeping a secret is named as a mark of the ஆன்றோர், not merely a prudence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marai","saandror","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 181","lb":"PZ181","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பனையின்மேல் பஞ்சிவைத்(து) எஃகிவிட் டற்று","it is like placing cotton on a palmyra and carding it","a secret told to the small-hearted is scattered past recovery","general","","no story; cotton carded at a height is blown abroad"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:182":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":184,"t":["விளிந்தாரே போலப் பிறராகி நிற்கும்","முளிந்தாரைத் தஞ்சம் மொழியலோ வேண்டா","அளிந்தார்கண் ஆயினும் 'ஆராயா னாகித்","தெளிந்தான் விளிந்து விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"There is no need to speak one's confidences to the hostile who stand apart as strangers, like the dead, and even with the affectionate, one who trusts without examining perishes.","n":"The verse moves from the obvious case, the estranged, to the hard one, the loving, and makes examination necessary even there. தஞ்சம் here is what one leans on, the matter one confides; முளிந்தார் (the withered, dried up) is a rare figure for those whose regard has died.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","aaraaydhal","marai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 182","lb":"PZ182","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆராயா னாகித் தெளிந்தான் விளிந்து விடும்","one who trusts without having examined will perish","even those who show affection must be examined before one confides in them","general","","no story; the proverb states the fate of trust without testing"],"ch":"pz:19"},"pz:183":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":185,"t":["ஆஅம் எனக்கெளி(து) என்றுலகம் ஆண்டவன்","மேஎம் துணையறியான் மிக்குநீர் பெய்திழந்தான்","தோஒம் உடைய தொடங்குவார்க்(கு) 'இல்லையே","தாஅம் தரவாரா நோய்'"],"p":"","r":"The one who ruled the world, thinking the gift easy for him, poured the gift-water lavishly without knowing the measure of the one who had come to him, and lost it all, for those who begin a thing with a flaw in it there is no affliction that they have not brought on themselves.","n":"The heavy lengthenings (ஆஅம், மேஎம், தோஒம், தாஅம்) give the verse its sound. The ruler pours water (நீர் பெய்து) as a giver seals a gift; his fault is not giving but giving without weighing the recipient, so the verse fits its heading as trust given without examination.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","eegai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 183","lb":"PZ183","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லையே தாஅம் தரவாரா நோய்","there is no affliction that does not come of one's own giving","the gift-giver who does not weigh the asker brings his ruin on himself","named","என்றுலகம் ஆண்டவன்","a ruler of the world, sure the gift was easy for him, poured the gift-water without knowing who had come and lost his world"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:184":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":186,"t":["நற்பால சுற்றாரும் நாடாது சொல்லுவர்","இற்பாலர் அல்லார் இயல்பின்மை நோவதென்?","கற்பால் கலங்கருவி நாட! 'மற் றியரானும்","சொற்சோரா தாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even kin of good quality speak without weighing their words, so there is no point in grieving at the want of good nature in those not of good family, lord of the land where cascades stir on the rock, for there is no one at all who never slips in speech.","n":"The argument runs from the greater to the less: if the well-born slip, the lapse of the low-born is no wonder. The verse assumes that good family (இற்பாலர்) yields good nature, and then uses that assumption to excuse rather than to condemn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kutram","kudi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 184","lb":"PZ184","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மற் றியரானும் சொற்சோரா தாரோ இல்","there is no one whoever who does not falter in speech","one should not grieve over another's careless words, since even the good let words slip","general","","no story; a general claim about every speaker"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:185":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":187,"t":["பூந்தண் புனற்புகார்ப் பூமிகுறி காண்டற்கு","வேந்தன் வினாயினான் மாந்தரைச் - சான்றவன்","கொண்டதனை நாணி மறைத்தலால் தன் 'கண்ணிற்","கண்டதூஉம் எண்ணிச் சொலல்'"],"p":"","r":"When the king asked men to show the boundary marks of the land of Pukar of cool flowering waters, the worthy man, ashamed of what he had come to hold, kept it hidden, and so even what one has seen with one's own eyes is to be weighed before it is spoken.","n":"The episode is compressed and its point is unclear: கொண்டதனை may be what the worthy man had taken or accepted, or what he had grasped of the matter. On either reading the proverb's lesson is that eyewitness alone does not license speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","therithal","naanam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 185","lb":"PZ185","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கண்ணிற் கண்டதூஉம் எண்ணிச் சொலல்","even what is seen with the eyes should be spoken after thought","testimony, even of what one saw, needs weighing before it is given","exemplum","வேந்தன்","a king of Pukar asked men to show a land's boundary marks and a worthy man, from shame, concealed what he knew"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:186":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":188,"t":["ஒருவன் உணராது உடன்றெழுந்த போருள்","இருவ ரிடைநட்பான் புக்கால் - பெரிய","வெறுப்பினால் போர்த்துச் செறுப்பின் 'தலையுள்","குறுக்கண்ணி யாகி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"If, in a quarrel that has flared between two who rose in anger without understanding, someone enters between them out of friendship, the two in their great hatred turn on him and he is caught in the knot at the head of the fight.","n":"The mediator is not blamed for malice but for misjudging the moment: the two have not understood (உணராது), and a friend's entry turns both angers on him. குறுக்கண்ணி is a rare word; it is taken as a cross-knot or tie in which the go-between is caught.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 186","lb":"PZ186","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தலையுள் குறுக்கண்ணி யாகி விடும்","he becomes the cross-knot at the head","stepping between two who quarrel without understanding draws the anger of both","general","","no story; the go-between in a quarrel"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:187":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":189,"t":["எனைப்பலவே யாயினும் சேய்த்தாற் பெறலின்","தினைத்துணையே யானும் அணிக்கோடல் நன்றே","இனக்கலை தேன்கிழிக்கும் மேகல்சூழ் வெற்ப!","'பனைப்பதித்(து) உண்ணார் பழம்'"],"p":"","r":"Better to take what is close at hand though it be only a millet-grain's worth than to get however much from far away, lord of the cloud-ringed hills where herds of stags tear the honeycombs, for no one eats the palmyra's fruit by planting the palm.","n":"The palmyra takes many years to bear, so the planter does not live to eat the fruit; the proverb weighs gain by its distance in time as well as space.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["payan","kaalam","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 187","lb":"PZ187","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பனைப்பதித்(து) உண்ணார் பழம்","people do not eat the palmyra's fruit by planting it","a small gain near at hand is to be preferred to a large one far off","general","","no story; the slow-bearing palmyra"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:188":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":190,"t":["மனங்கொண்டக் கண்ணும் மருவில செய்யார்","கனங்கொண்(டு) உரைத்தவை காக்கவே வேண்டும்","சனங்கள் உவப்பன செய்யாவும் செய்க","'இனங்கழு வேற்றினார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that even when they have set their heart on it people do not do what is unfitting, that what has been spoken with weight must be kept, and that one should do even what one would not do if it pleases one's people, for no one ever impaled his own kin.","n":"The proverb names the king's harshest punishment, impalement (கழுவேற்றல்), and says it is never turned on one's own people; the counsel to please the people even at some cost to oneself follows from that bond. Lines 1-3 are three loosely joined counsels, and how they lead to the proverb is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","sutram","vaaimai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 188","lb":"PZ188","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இனங்கழு வேற்றினார் இல்","no one has ever impaled his own kin","one bears with and pleases one's own people rather than treat them as enemies","general","","no story; the punishment of impalement, never inflicted on one's own"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:189":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":191,"t":["கடுப்பத் தலைக்கீறிக் காலும் இழந்து","நடைத்தாரா என்பதூஉம் பட்டு - முடத்தோடு","பேர்பிறி தாகப் பெறுதலால் 'போகாரே","நீர்குறி தாகப் புகல்'"],"p":"","r":"A man whose head was painfully split, who lost the use of his legs as well, who was dubbed 'the walking duck' and with his lameness got another name, shows why people do not go into water taking it for shallow.","n":"The cost the verse dwells on is not the injury alone but the new name it brings, a lasting mockery. நடைத்தாரா is taken as 'walking duck', a nickname for a waddling lame man; the episode behind it is not told.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","aaraaydhal","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 189","lb":"PZ189","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["போகாரே நீர்குறி தாகப் புகல்","people do not go in, taking the water to be shallow","one should not enter on anything before testing its depth","exemplum","","a man who plunged into water thinking it shallow split his head, was lamed and was nicknamed the walking duck"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:190":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":192,"t":["சிறியதாய கூழ்பெற்றுச் செல்வரைச் சேர்ந்தார்","பெரிதாய கூழும் பெறுவர் - அரிதாம்","'இடத்துள் ஒருவன் இருப்புழிப் பெற்றால்","கிடப்புழியும் பெற்று விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have attached themselves to the wealthy, getting at first a little gruel, will get a large portion too, for if a man gets a place to sit in a crowded space, he will get a place to lie down as well.","n":"The verse is a counsel of patient entry, not of greed: the small first share is the foothold. அரிதாம் இடத்துள் makes the space hard to get, so the first seat is what counts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","muyarchi","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 190","lb":"PZ190","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இடத்துள் ஒருவன் இருப்புழிப் பெற்றால் கிடப்புழியும் பெற்று விடும்","one who gets room to sit will get room to lie down too","a small first share from the wealthy grows into a large one","general","","no story; a seat in a crowded place grows into a bed"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:191":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":193,"t":["புன்சொல்லும் நன்சொல்லும் பொய்யின்(று) உணர்கிற்பார்","வன்சொல் வழியராய் வாழ்தலும் உண்டாமோ?","புன்சொல் இடர்ப்படுப்ப தல்லால் ஒருவனை","'இன்சொல் இடர்ப்படுப்ப தில்'"],"p":"","r":"Can those who truly discern mean speech from good speech live by the way of harsh speech, the verse asks, since mean speech brings a man into trouble, while sweet speech never puts anyone in danger.","n":"The argument is from the speaker's safety, not the hearer's pleasure: knowing the two kinds of speech is enough to choose the sweet, because only the mean one rebounds on the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","kadunjol","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 191","lb":"PZ191","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இன்சொல் இடர்ப்படுப்ப தில்","sweet speech never brings one into trouble","one who can tell good speech from mean will not choose harsh words","general","","no story; a claim about speech"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:192":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":194,"t":["மெய்ந்நீர ராகி விரியப் புகுவார்க்கும்","பொய்ந்நீர ராகிப் பொருளை முடிப்பார்க்கும்","எந்நீர ராயினும் ஆகு அவரவர்","'தந்நீர ராதல் தலை'"],"p":"","r":"Whether men enter openly being of true nature or bring their ends about being of false nature, let them be whatever they are; for each, to be true to his own nature is foremost.","n":"The verse seems to set truth and falsehood side by side without choosing between them and to prize only constancy of nature; this may be a counsel to the observer to judge each man by his settled nature rather than his professions. The sense of விரியப் புகுவார் is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu","vaaimai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 192","lb":"PZ192","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தந்நீர ராதல் தலை","being of one's own nature is foremost","whatever a man is, true or false, his constancy to his own nature is what counts","general","","no story; a maxim on keeping one's own nature"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:193":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":195,"t":["யாவரே யாயினும் இழந்த பொருளுடையார்","தேவரே யாயினும் தீங்கோர்ப்பர் - பாவை","படத்தோன்று நல்லாய்! 'நெடுவேல் கெடுத்தான்","குடத்துளும் நாடி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Whoever they may be, those who have lost their property will suspect harm even of the gods, lady who appears like a painted doll, for one who has lost a long spear will search for it even inside a pot.","n":"The proverb is comic: a long spear cannot be in a pot, yet loss drives the search everywhere. Under its heading the verse reads as a warning that a loser's suspicions are no proof against those he suspects.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","selvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 193","lb":"PZ193","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நெடுவேல் கெடுத்தான் குடத்துளும் நாடி விடும்","one who has lost a long spear searches even in a pot","loss makes a man suspect everyone, even the gods","general","","no story; the absurd search for a lost spear"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:194":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":196,"t":["துயிலும் பொழுதத்(து) உடைஊண்மேற் கொண்டு","வெயில்விரி போழ்தின் வெளிப்பட்டா ராகி","அயில்போலுங் கண்ணாய்! அடைந்தார்போல் காட்டி","'மயில்போலும் கள்வர் உடைத்து'"],"p":"","r":"Taking clothes and food in the hours of sleep, coming into view when the sunlight spreads, and showing themselves as though they were attached to one, lady of spear-like eyes, there are thieves like peacocks.","n":"The peacock is a figure of a fair outside; the thieves are fair-seeming dependants who rob by night and appear devoted by day, so the danger is inside the household's own circle. The image is not explained beyond the comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kalavu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 194","lb":"PZ194","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மயில்போலும் கள்வர் உடைத்து","the world has thieves who are like peacocks","those who seem close may be the ones who rob","general","","no story; dependants who steal by night and appear loyal by day"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:195":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":197,"t":["செல்லற்க சேர்ந்தார் புலம்புறச் செல்லாது","நில்லற்க நீத்தார் நெறியொரீப் - பல்காலும்","நாடுக தான்கண்ட நுட்பத்தைக் கேளாதே","'ஓடுக ஊரோடு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says not to go away so that those attached to one are left desolate, not to stay on departing from the path of those who renounced, and to examine again and again the fine point one has seen for oneself, without hearing others, and to run along with the town's way.","n":"The verse joins private, repeated scrutiny with outward conformity: examine for oneself, but act with the town. நீத்தார் may be those who have renounced or those who have left one, and line 2 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arivu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 195","lb":"PZ195","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஓடுக ஊரோடு மாறு","run in step with the town's way","having examined for oneself, one acts in step with the community","general","","no story; a maxim of going with the community"],"ch":"pz:20"},"pz:196":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":198,"t":["தெருளா தொழுகும் திறனிலா தாரைப்","பொருளால் அறுத்தல் பொருளே - பொருள்கொடுப்பின்","பாணித்து நிற்கிற்பார் யாருளரோ? 'வேற்குத்தின்","காணியின் குத்தே வலிது'"],"p":"","r":"Those who act without understanding and have no merit are best cut off with wealth, for who will hold out once wealth is given; a gift's thrust is stronger than a spear's.","n":"The counsel sets giving against force as ways of dealing with the unreasonable, and ranks giving higher: the proverb's spear and காணி stand for the two. காணி can name a holding of land or a small fraction of money; either way the gift is the sharper weapon. The verse plays on பொருள் as both wealth and the fitting course (பொருளால் அறுத்தல் பொருளே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pedhaimai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 196","lb":"PZ196","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வேற்குத்தின் காணியின் குத்தே வலிது","the thrust of a காணி is stronger than the thrust of a spear","the senseless and unworthy are more surely ended by a payment than by force","general","","no story; the proverb opposes the weapon and the gift as two ways to overcome"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:197":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":199,"t":["ஒல்லாத இன்றி உடையார் கருமங்கள்","நல்லவாய் நாடி நடக்குமாம் - இல்லார்க்கு","இடரா வியலும் இலங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'கடலுள்ளும் காண்பவே நன்கு'"],"p":"","r":"For those who have wealth nothing is out of reach and their affairs go well as they intend, while for those who have none the same affairs turn to trouble; the rich see clearly even in the sea.","n":"The address to the lord of the shining shore (இலங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!) is a frame borrowed from the neytal poems. The proverb's sense is not fixed: it may mean that the rich find their way even in a place as trackless as the sea, or that what is in the deep is plain to them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 197","lb":"PZ197","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடலுள்ளும் காண்பவே நன்கு","even within the sea they see well","wealth makes every undertaking go well, where want turns it to hardship","general","","no story; the proverb states the reach of those who have means"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:198":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":200,"t":["அருமை யுடைய பொருளுடையார் தங்கண்","கருமம் உடையாரை நாடார் - எருமைமேல்","நாரை துயில்வதியும் ஊர! 'குளந்தொட்டுத்","தேரை வழிச்சென்றார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who hold wealth that is hard to come by do not go looking for those who have business with them, since no one who dug a tank went off in search of frogs.","n":"The tank draws frogs of itself, so the seekers come to wealth and not wealth to them; the verse states this as a law of nature, without praise or blame. The stork asleep on the buffalo's back in the address (எருமைமேல் நாரை துயில்வதியும் ஊர!) is a marutam frame of ease.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 198","lb":"PZ198","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குளந்தொட்டுத் தேரை வழிச்சென்றார் இல்","no one who dug a tank ever went in pursuit of the frogs","the wealthy need not seek those who need them; the needy come of themselves","general","","no story; the tank and its frogs are the instance"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:199":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":201,"t":["அருளுடை யாருமற் றல்லா தவரும்","பொருளுடை யாரைப் புகழாதார் இல்லை","பொருபடைக் கண்ணாய்! அதுவே 'திருவுடையார்","பண்டம் இருவர் கொளல்'"],"p":"","r":"No one, the compassionate or anyone else, fails to praise those who have wealth, and this is the saying that the goods of the fortunate are taken by two.","n":"The point is that even those with அருள், who should be moved by need and not by wealth, join the praise. The proverb's 'two' most likely answers the verse's two kinds of people (the compassionate and the rest), both of whom take from the rich and so praise them; that reading is ours. The address பொருபடைக் கண்ணாய்! is a frame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pugazh","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 199","lb":"PZ199","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["திருவுடையார் பண்டம் இருவர் கொளல்","the goods of the fortunate are taken by two","praise of the rich is universal because all, good and not, share in their goods","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the rich and those who live by them"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:200":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":202,"t":["உடையதனைக் காப்பான் உடையான் அதுவே","உடையானைக் காப்பதூஉம் ஆகும் - அடையின்","'புதற்குப் புலியும் வலியே புலிக்குப்","புதலும் வலியாய் விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The owner guards what he owns, and what he owns in turn guards him, as the thicket is strength to the tiger and the tiger strength to the thicket.","n":"The proverb makes wealth and owner each the other's defence: the tiger keeps men from cutting the thicket and the thicket hides the tiger. அடையின் ('if they join') makes the protection depend on the two staying together. The verse thus treats guarding wealth as a gain, not a burden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","thunai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 200","lb":"PZ200","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புதற்குப் புலியும் வலியே புலிக்குப் புதலும் வலியாய் விடும்","the tiger is a strength to the thicket, and the thicket becomes a strength to the tiger","guarded wealth protects the one who guards it","general","","no story; the tiger and the thicket are the instance of mutual defence"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:201":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":203,"t":["வருவாய் சிறிதெனினும் வைகலும் ஈண்டின்","பெருவாய்த்தாய் நிற்கும் பெரிதும் - ஒருவாறு","ஒளியீண்டி நின்றால் உலகம் விளக்கும்","'துளியீண்டில் வெள்ளம் தரும்'"],"p":"","r":"Though one's income be small, if it is gathered every day it grows very great, as gathered light lights the world and gathered drops make a flood.","n":"Two images carry the claim before the proverb: light gathered (ஒளியீண்டி) and drops gathered (துளியீண்டில்), both repeating the verb ஈண்டு of line 1, so accumulation, not the size of the income, is the whole argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 201","lb":"PZ201","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["துளியீண்டில் வெள்ளம் தரும்","drops, if they gather, give a flood","a small income gathered daily becomes great wealth","general","","no story; the gathered drops are the instance"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:202":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":204,"t":["உள்ளூர் அவரால் உணர்ந்தாம் முதலெனினும்","எள்ளாமை வேண்டும் இலங்கிழாய்! - தள்ளாது","அழுங்கல் முதுபதி 'அங்காடி மேயும்","பழங்கன்று ஏறாதலும் உண்டு'"],"p":"","r":"Even if the people of one's own town have let us know someone's small beginnings, he should not be despised, for in the old bustling town the calf grazing in the market may also grow into a bull.","n":"முதல் can be the man's origin or his starting capital; under the heading பொருள் the second is likely, but the verse's argument holds for either. The reason given is time: what is small now grows, so contempt based on knowing someone's past misjudges his future. The address இலங்கிழாய்! is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 202","lb":"PZ202","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அங்காடி மேயும் பழங்கன்று ஏறாதலும் உண்டு","the old calf that grazes in the market may also become a bull","a man of known small beginnings may rise, so he is not to be scorned","general","","no story; the market calf is the instance"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:203":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":205,"t":["களமர் பலரானும் கள்ளம் படினும்","வளமிக்கார் செல்வம் வருந்தா - விளைநெல்","அரிநீர் அணைதிறக்கும் ஊர! 'அறுமோ","நரிநக்கிற்(று) என்று கடல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if many field labourers steal, the wealth of the very rich does not suffer, for the sea does not run dry because a jackal licked it.","n":"The verse reports the indifference of great wealth to petty loss without judging the thieving labourers (களமர்). The address pictures a town where the dam is opened to drain the water for reaping the ripe paddy, a frame of abundance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 203","lb":"PZ203","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறுமோ நரிநக்கிற்(று) என்று கடல்","will the sea run dry because a jackal licked it","small thefts do not diminish great wealth","general","","no story; the jackal at the sea is the instance"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:204":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":206,"t":["நாடறியப் பட்ட பெருஞ்செல்வர் நல்கூர்ந்து","வாடிய காலத்தும் வட்குபவோ! - வாடி","வலித்துத் திரங்கிக் கிடந்தே விடினும்","'புலித்தலையை நாய்மோத்தல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Will the great rich whom the land knows cower even when they have become poor and withered; though they lie shrunk and shrivelled, no dog sniffs at a tiger's head.","n":"The verse says the standing of those once known as great outlasts their wealth: the mean (the dog) do not dare to approach even the fallen great (the tiger). வட்குபவோ asks whether they will be abashed and expects no. The counsel sits under பொருள் but is about honour in loss of wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","perumai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 204","lb":"PZ204","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலித்தலையை நாய்மோத்தல் இல்","a dog does not sniff at a tiger's head","the fallen great are not insulted by the mean, nor do they cower","general","","no story; the tiger and the dog are the instance"],"ch":"pz:21"},"pz:205":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":207,"t":["தந்தம் பொருளும் தமர்கண் வளமையும்","முந்துற நாடிப் புறந்தரல் ஓம்புக","அந்தண் அருவி மலைநாட! சேணோக்கி","'நந்துநீர் கொண்டதே போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse urges looking ahead to one's own wealth and to the prosperity of one's kin and taking care to sustain them, and proves it with the proverb of the snail that took in water having looked to the distance.","n":"The care is double: one's own goods and the வளமை of one's kin are both to be looked to முந்துற, beforehand. The proverb is read here as the snail that keeps water in its shell against the dry time; சேணோக்கி may also mean looking far off in space, and நந்து can mean 'increase', so the image is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sutram","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 205","lb":"PZ205","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நந்துநீர் கொண்டதே போன்று","like the snail that took in water","providing in advance for one's own wealth and one's kin's","general","","no story; the proverb's snail, having looked ahead, holds water for the time to come"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:206":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":208,"t":["மறந்தானும் தாமுடைய தாம்போற்றின் அல்லால்","சிறந்தார் தமரென்று தேற்றார்கை வையார்","கறங்குநீர்க் காலலைக்கும் கானலஞ் சேர்ப்ப!","'இறந்தது பேர்தறிவார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Unless one guards one's own goods without ever forgetting them, the wise do not put them in the hands of those they have not tested, on the ground that they are eminent or are kin, for no one knows how to bring back what is gone.","n":"சிறந்தார் தமர் என்று names the two pretexts for trust, standing and kinship, and the verse sets both aside in favour of testing (தேற்றார், those not made sure of). The proverb makes the loss final rather than merely likely.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","therithal","thelivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 206","lb":"PZ206","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இறந்தது பேர்தறிவார் இல்","there is no one who knows how to bring back what has passed away","kinship and standing are no ground for entrusting wealth without testing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to wealth handed to untested kin and lost"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:207":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":209,"t":["அமையா இடத்தோர் அரும்பொருள் வைத்தால்","இமையாது காப்பினும் ஆகா - இமையோரும்","அக்காலத்(து) ஓம்பி அமிழ்துகோட் பட்டமையால்","'நற்காப்பின் தீச்சிறையே நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"Precious wealth kept in an unfit place is not saved even by guarding it without blinking, since even the unblinking gods, guarding their nectar at that time, had it carried off; so a hard enclosure is better than good watching.","n":"The verse puns on இமையாது, 'without blinking', and இமையோர், the gods who never blink: the most vigilant guards there are still lost the nectar. The proverb ranks the place over the watcher. The verse names neither the thief nor the episode.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 207","lb":"PZ207","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நற்காப்பின் தீச்சிறையே நன்று","a harsh enclosure is better than a good guard","a secure place keeps wealth where vigilance alone does not","named","இமையோரும்","the gods guarded the nectar and still had it taken from them"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:208":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":210,"t":["ஊக்கி உழந்தொருவர் ஈட்டிய ஒண்பொருளை","நோக்குமின் என்றிகழ்ந்து நொள்வியார் கைவிடுதல்","போக்கில்நீர் தூஉம் பொருகழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'காக்கையைக் காப்பிட்ட சோறு'"],"p":"","r":"Handing over the bright wealth one earned by striving and toil to worthless men with a careless 'look after it' is like rice put in a crow's keeping.","n":"இகழ்ந்து marks the fault as the owner's contempt for his own labour, not only the keeper's baseness; ஊக்கி உழந்து sets the effort of earning against the ease of the handing over. நொள்வியார் is a rare form; the proverb makes the sense, unfit keepers, clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pochchaappu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 208","lb":"PZ208","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["காக்கையைக் காப்பிட்ட சோறு","rice put in the crow's keeping","wealth entrusted to the base is consumed by the very keeper","general","","no story; the keeper set over the rice is the one who eats it"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:209":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":211,"t":["தொடிமுன்கை நல்லாய்அத் தொக்க பொருளைக்","குடிமகன் அல்லான்கை வைத்தல் - கடிநெய்தல்","வேரி கமழும் விரிதிரைத் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'மூரியைத் தீற்றிய புல்'"],"p":"","r":"Placing gathered wealth in the hands of a man who is not of a good family is, the verse tells a woman, grass fed to a sluggish ox.","n":"குடிமகன் அல்லான் makes birth the test of a keeper, where 208 made worth the test. மூரி is read as an ox that will not work: the grass is spent and nothing comes of it, so the loss is waste, not theft. The address to a woman is the verse's frame, not its audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kudi","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 209","lb":"PZ209","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மூரியைத் தீற்றிய புல்","grass fed to a sluggish ox","wealth entrusted to a man of no family is spent without return","general","","no story; the feed is spent on a beast that gives no work"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:210":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":212,"t":["முன்னை யுடையது காவாது இகந்திருந்து","பின்னையஃ தாராய்ந்து கொள்குறுதல் - இன்னியல்","மைத்தடங்கண் மாதராய்! அஃதாதல் 'வெண்ணெய்மேல்","வைத்து மயில்கொள்ளு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Neglecting what one had at first and then later trying to search it out and get it back is, the verse says, the way of catching a peacock by putting butter on its head.","n":"The proverb is of a roundabout trick that cannot work: the bird is gone before the butter melts. The verse does not say recovery is impossible, as 206 does, but that the late effort is absurd beside the early guarding it replaces.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","selvam","madi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 210","lb":"PZ210","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வெண்ணெய்மேல் வைத்து மயில்கொள்ளு மாறு","the way of catching a peacock by setting butter on it","recovering neglected wealth afterwards is a futile roundabout effort","general","","no story; the proverb is a fool's method of catching a bird"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:211":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":213,"t":["கைவிட்ட ஒண்பொருள் கைவரவு இல்லென்பார்","மெய்ப்பட்ட வாறே உணர்ந்தாரால் - மெய்யா","மடம்பட்ட மானோக்கின் மாமயில் அன்னாய்!","'கடம்பெற்றான் பெற்றான் குடம்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who say that bright wealth once let out of hand does not come back are those who have learned it as it truly happened, for, as the proverb says, he who got the loan got the pot.","n":"மெய்ப்பட்ட வாறே உணர்ந்தார் grounds the saying in experience, not precept. The proverb is obscure: கடம் may be a debt or a large pot, and the line seems to say that whoever has received the thing holds it. The reading here takes கடம் as the loan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 211","lb":"PZ211","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடம்பெற்றான் பெற்றான் குடம்","he who got the loan (or the jar) got the pot","wealth let go belongs in effect to whoever now holds it","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to wealth that has passed into another's hand"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:212":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":214,"t":["கடங்கொண்ட ஒண்பொருளைக் கைவிட் டிருப்பார்","இடங்கொண்டு தம்மினே - என்றால் தொடங்கிப்","பகைமேற்கொண் டார்போலக் 'கொண்டார் வெகுடல்","நகைமேலும் கைப்பாய் விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"When those who have lent out bright wealth and let it lie then take their moment and say 'give it to us', the borrowers turn on them as if they had taken up a feud, and the anger of those who took turns even past good cheer into bitterness.","n":"The fault lies on both sides: the lender let the loan lie (கைவிட்டிருப்பார), and the borrower answers a just claim with enmity. நகைமேலும் suggests the friendly ease that made the loan possible is what turns bitter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vekuli","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 212","lb":"PZ212","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொண்டார் வெகுடல் நகைமேலும் கைப்பாய் விடும்","the anger of those who took turns bitter even over laughter","asking back a loan left long unclaimed turns the borrower into an enemy","general","","no story; the borrower asked to repay grows angry"],"ch":"pz:22"},"pz:213":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":215,"t":["அல்லது செல்வார் அரும்பொருள் ஆக்கத்தை","நல்லது செல்வார் நயப்பவோ? - ஒல்லொலிநீர்","பாய்வதே போலும் துறைவ! கேள் 'தீயன","ஆவதே போன்று கெடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether those who walk the good way would covet the rare wealth gained by those who walk the wrong way, and answers with the proverb that evil things perish even as they seem to prosper.","n":"The address to the lord of the shore, where the loud water seems to rush in, mirrors the proverb: the wave's advance looks like gain and is followed by its ebb. The ill-gotten fortune is not denied; it is said to be the look of growth that precedes ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naduvu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 213","lb":"PZ213","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீயன ஆவதே போன்று கெடும்","evil things perish in the very act of seeming to thrive","the good do not desire wealth won by wrong means, since its rise is the shape of its fall","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the type of man who grows rich by wrong ways"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:214":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":216,"t":["தொன்மையின் மாண்ட துணிவொன்றும் இல்லாதார்","நன்மையின் மாண்ட பொருள்பெறுதல் - இன்னொலிநீர்","கல்மேல் இலங்கு மலைநாட! 'மாக்காய்த்துத்","தன்மேல் குணில்கொள்ளு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when men who lack any resolve proven over time come into wealth of real worth, it is like the mango tree that bears fruit and so draws the throwing-stick upon itself.","n":"The wealth itself is not blamed: it is called fine (நன்மையின் மாண்ட). The fault is in the holder, who has no tested firmness to guard it, so his gain becomes the reason he is struck. தொன்மையின் மாண்ட துணிவு may mean resolve made excellent by long use or by ancestry; the line does not settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","thitpam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 214","lb":"PZ214","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மாக்காய்த்துத் தன்மேல் குணில்கொள்ளு மாறு","the mango that fruits takes the cudgel upon itself","wealth in weak hands draws attack on its owner","general","","no story; a fruiting mango is pelted by those who want its fruit"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:215":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":217,"t":["பெற்றாலும் செல்வம் பிறர்க்கீயார் தாந்துவ்வார்","சுற்றாரும் பற்றி இறுகுபவால் - கற்றா","வரம்பிடைப் பூமேயும் வண்புணல் ஊர!","'மரங்குறைப்ப மண்ணா மயிர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says those who, even having wealth, neither give to others nor enjoy it themselves, and whose kin also cling and grow tight, are named by the proverb of unwashed hair beside the lopping of trees.","n":"Line 2 may say that the miser's kin, holding on to him, become tight-fisted in turn, or that they cling to the hoard. The proverb மரங்குறைப்ப மண்ணா மயிர் is obscure: one reading is that they labour at felling trees while their own hair stays unwashed, toil without any enjoyment, which fits தாந்துவ்வார் (they do not enjoy). The image is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 215","lb":"PZ215","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மரங்குறைப்ப மண்ணா மயிர்","they lop trees, and their hair stays unwashed","wealth neither given nor enjoyed is labour without fruit, and the habit spreads to the kin","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the hoarder who neither gives nor enjoys"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:216":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":218,"t":["வழங்கலும் துய்த்தலும் தேற்றாதான் பெற்ற","முழங்கு முரசுடைச் செல்வம் - தழங்கருவி","வேய்முற்றி முத்துதிரும் வெற்ப! அதுவன்றோ","'நாய்பெற்ற தெங்கம் பழம்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the drum-proclaimed wealth gained by a man who knows neither how to give nor how to enjoy is a dog's coconut.","n":"A dog may own a coconut but cannot break it open or eat it: the proverb is about possession without access. The booming drum (முழங்கு முரசு) marks royal or lordly wealth, so the verse aims at a ruler or great man, not a small householder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 216","lb":"PZ216","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்பெற்ற தெங்கம் பழம்","a coconut that a dog has got","great wealth held by one who cannot give or enjoy it is possessed and useless","general","","no story; a dog guarding a coconut it cannot open"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:217":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":219,"t":["முழவொலி முந்நீர் முழுதுடன் ஆண்டார்","விழவூரில் கூத்தேபோல் வீழ்ந்தவிதல் கண்டும்","இழவென்று ஒருபொருள் ஈயாதான் செல்வம்","'அழகொடு கண்ணின் இழவு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the wealth of one who, though he has seen rulers of the whole sea-girt earth fall and vanish like a dance in a festival town, still gives nothing because giving feels like loss, is the loss of the eye together with its beauty.","n":"The miser calls giving இழவு, loss; the proverb turns his word back on him, since his hoard is the real இழவு, and a double one, as losing an eye destroys sight and looks at once. The kings who vanished like a festival dance make the argument from impermanence: wealth will go in any case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 217","lb":"PZ217","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அழகொடு கண்ணின் இழவு","losing the eye together with its beauty","the hoard of one who will not give is a double loss, of use and of honour","general","","no story; the loss of an eye, which costs both use and looks"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:218":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":220,"t":["நாவின் இரந்தார் குறையறிந்து தாமுடைய","மாவினை மாணப் பொதிகிற்பார் - தீவினை","அஞ்சிலென் அஞ்சா விடிலென் 'குருட்டுக்கண்","துஞ்சிலென் துஞ்சாக்கால் என்?'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks what it matters whether those who, knowing the want of those who beg aloud, wrap up their own goods tightly, fear evil deeds or not, and answers with the proverb: what matter whether a blind eye sleeps or wakes?","n":"The target is the miser who claims piety: his fear of தீவினை is like the blindness of an eye, the same whether shut or open, because it changes nothing he does. மாவினை may be his goods or provisions (மா, grain or flour) and பொதிகிற்பார் those who bundle them away; the exact object is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","vinai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 218","lb":"PZ218","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குருட்டுக்கண் துஞ்சிலென் துஞ்சாக்கால் என்","what if a blind eye sleeps, what if it does not","fear of sin that does not move a man to give is no different from having none","general","","no story; a blind eye open or closed sees alike"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:219":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":221,"t":["படரும் பிறப்பிற்கொன்(று) ஈயார் பொருளைத்","தொடருந்தம் பற்றினால் வைத்திறப் பாரே","அடரும் பொழுதின்கண் இட்டுக் 'குடரொழிய","மீவேலி போக்கு பவர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who give nothing toward the birth that follows and, by the attachment that pursues them, keep their wealth and die, are those who in the pressing hour leave the guts behind and send off the outer fence.","n":"The proverb குடரொழிய மீவேலி போக்குபவர் is obscure. On one reading it sets the inner thing (the gut, what life depends on) against the outer fence, so the hoarder guards the outside and loses the inside: his wealth stays, his next birth goes unprovided. படரும் பிறப்பு, the birth that spreads onward, makes giving a provision for the coming life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","marumai","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 219","lb":"PZ219","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குடரொழிய மீவேலி போக்கு பவர்","those who, the gut being lost, send off the upper fence","keeping wealth till death and giving nothing for the next birth protects the outer while the inner is lost","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the hoarder who dies with his wealth"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:220":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":222,"t":["விரும்பி அடைந்தார்க்கும் சுற்றத் தவர்க்கும்","வருந்தும் பசிகளையார் வம்பர்க்(கு) உதவல்","இரும்பணைவில் வென்ற புருவத்தாய்! 'ஆற்றக்","கரும்பனை அன்ன துடைத்து'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that failing to relieve the gnawing hunger of those who come with love and of one's own kin, while helping strangers, is wholly like the black palmyra.","n":"வம்பர் may be strangers or idle, worthless men; either way they are set against kin and those who came in love. The proverb's image is only கரும்பனை, the dark palmyra, and the verse does not say how it applies; on one reading the tall palm gives nothing to what stands at its foot. The opening quotation mark falls before ஆற்ற, so the proverb includes that word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 220","lb":"PZ220","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆற்றக் கரும்பனை அன்ன துடைத்து","it is wholly of the nature of the black palmyra","giving that passes over hungry kin to reach strangers is misdirected","general","","no story; the palmyra stands for help that does not reach those nearest"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:221":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":223,"t":["வழங்கார் வலியலார் வாய்ச்சொல்லும் பொல்லார்","உழந்தொருவர்க்(கு) உற்றால் உதவலும் இல்லார்","இழந்ததில் செல்வம் பெறுதலும் இன்னார்","'பழஞ்செய்போர் பின்று விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes men who do not give, have no strength, are harsh of speech and do not help one who has toiled when trouble comes, says that gaining wealth from them is no better than losing it, and calls leaving them leaving behind the stack of an old field.","n":"Line 3 is hard: இழந்ததில் செல்வம் பெறுதலும் இன்னார் may say that what one gets from such men is as good as lost, or that they are men from whom no wealth comes. The proverb then counsels quitting them, as one leaves an old field's threshed stack with nothing lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","kadunjol","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 221","lb":"PZ221","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பழஞ்செய்போர் பின்று விடல்","leaving behind the straw stack of an old field","to leave the ungiving is to lose nothing","general","","no story; abandoning a stack already threshed, from which nothing more comes"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:222":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":224,"t":["ஒற்கப்பட் டாற்றார் உணர உரைத்தபின்","நற்செய்கை செய்வார்போல் காட்டி நசையழுங்க","வற்கென்ற செய்கை அதுவால் அவ்வாயுறைப்","புற்கழுத்தில் யாத்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when the destitute, unable to bear it, have spoken so that their need is understood, to make a show of doing good and then dash their hope by a hard act is to tie the mouthful of grass to a beast's neck and let it go.","n":"The beast with grass tied at its neck has its food beside its mouth and cannot eat it: the image is of a gift shown and withheld, worse than a refusal. நசையழுங்க, hope crushed, is the same phrase Naladiyar 111 uses of one who keeps the asker waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","vanjam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 222","lb":"PZ222","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அவ்வாயுறைப் புற்கழுத்தில் யாத்து விடல்","tying that mouthful of grass on the neck and letting it go","a feigned kindness that kills the asker's hope torments rather than helps","general","","no story; a beast turned loose with its grass bound to its neck"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:223":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":225,"t":["அடையப் பயின்றார்சொல் ஆற்றுவராக் கேட்டால்","உடையதொன் றில்லாமை யொட்டின் - படைபெற்று","அடைய அமர்த்தகண் பைந்தொடி! அஃதால்","'இடையன் எறிந்த மரம்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if one who could help, asked by those long close to him, pleads that he has nothing, it is the tree a herdsman has cut.","n":"The refusal is aimed at old intimates (அடையப் பயின்றார்), not strangers. The herdsman's tree is hacked for leaves to feed his flock and left standing but maimed; on that reading the bond is not cut through but spoiled. ஆற்றுவரா may also read as those askers who could not bear it; the proverb's application is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karavu","natpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 223","lb":"PZ223","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இடையன் எறிந்த மரம்","a tree hacked by a herdsman","pleading poverty to old friends maims the bond without ending it","general","","no story; a tree lopped by a herdsman for his flock"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:224":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":226,"t":["மரம்போல் வலிய மனத்தாரை முன்னின்று","இரந்தார் பெறுவதொன் றில்லை - குரங்கூசல்","வள்ளியி னாடு மலைநாட! அஃதன்றோ","'பள்ளியுள் ஐயம் புகல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who stand and beg before men whose minds are hard as wood get nothing, as one gets nothing by seeking alms inside a monastery.","n":"The பள்ளி is a house of renouncers, who themselves live on alms: to beg there is to beg from beggars. The proverb makes the hard-hearted rich man equal in yield to one who owns nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","iravu","ivaral"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 224","lb":"PZ224","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பள்ளியுள் ஐயம் புகல்","entering a monastery to ask for alms","asking of the hard-hearted rich yields as little as asking of those who own nothing","general","","no story; begging alms where the dwellers themselves beg"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:225":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":227,"t":["இசைவ கொடுப்பதூஉம் இல்லென் பதூஉம்","வசையன்று வையத்(து) இயற்கை - அஃதன்றிப்","பசைகொண் டவன்நிற்கப் பாத்துண்ணான் ஆயின்","'நசைகொன்றான் செல்லுலகம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that giving what one can and saying 'I have none' are both no disgrace but the way of the world; but if, while one who has come in hope stands waiting, a man does not share and eat, there is no world for him who killed that hope.","n":"Its first two lines are almost word for word the opening of Naladiyar 111; the two verses part only in the proverb. The fault is not refusal but keeping the asker standing (நிற்ப) and then not sharing. செல்லுலகம் இல் denies him a world to go to after death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 225","lb":"PZ225","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நசைகொன்றான் செல்லுலகம் இல்","there is no world to go to for one who killed hope","an honest refusal is no fault, but to hold the asker in hope and then deny him forfeits the next world","general","","no story; the proverb names the hope-killer as a type"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:226":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":228,"t":["தமராலும் தம்மாலும் உற்றால்ஒன்(று) ஆற்றி","நிகராகிச் சென்றாகும் அல்லர் - இவர்திரை","நீத்தநீர்த் தண்சேர்ப்ப! செய்தது 'உவவாதார்க்(கு)","ஈத்ததை எல்லாம் இழவு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says such men will not, when need comes through their kin or themselves, do anything and stand as equals, and that all that is given to those who do not rejoice in what was done for them is lost.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are elliptic: they seem to describe men who neither help nor return help in trouble, as if to say what kind of recipient wastes a gift. The proverb judges the gift by the receiver's gladness, not by any return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 226","lb":"PZ226","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உவவாதார்க்(கு) ஈத்ததை எல்லாம் இழவு","all that is given to those who do not rejoice is loss","a gift to one who takes no joy in kindness is wasted","general","","no story; the ungrateful recipient as a type"],"ch":"pz:23"},"pz:227":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":229,"t":["எவ்வம் துணையாய்ப் பொருள்முடிக்கும் தாளாண்மை","தெய்வம் முடிப்புழி என்செய்யும்? - மொய்கொண்டு","பூப்புக்கு வண்டார்க்கும் ஊர! 'குறும்பியங்கும்","கோப்புக் குழிச்செய்வ(து) இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks what diligent effort, which reaches its goal with hardship as its companion, can do once the deity has settled the outcome, addressing the lord of a town where bees swarm and hum at the blossoms, and closes with a proverb that digging a set pit is of no use where the kurumbu moves.","n":"The question is rhetorical and grants effort its full credit first (எவ்வம் துணையாய்ப் பொருள்முடிக்கும்) before denying it any force against தெய்வம். The proverb's terms are hard: குறும்பு may be a marauding band or a wild creature and கோப்புக் குழி a pit laid out as a trap, so the sense would be that a prepared trap is idle where the quarry goes its own way; this is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 227","lb":"PZ227","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குறும்பியங்கும் கோப்புக் குழிச்செய்வ(து) இல்","where the kurumbu moves about, there is no making of a laid-out pit","effort, however hard, cannot prevail where the deity has already settled the end","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anyone whose effort meets an outcome the deity has fixed"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:228":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":230,"t":["சுட்டிச் சொலப்படும் பேரறிவி னார்கண்ணும்","பட்ட விருத்தம் பலவானால் - பட்ட","பொறியின் வகைய கருமம் அதனால்","'அறிவினை ஊழே அடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Even in men of great understanding, pointed out by name, when many contrary turns befall them, their undertakings go after the kind of the fortune that has fallen to them, and so, the proverb says, fate defeats understanding.","n":"The claim is about outcomes (கருமம்), not about the wise man's judgment going bad: his knowledge is intact and still loses. பொறி here is the mark of fortune, the word the Kural uses in 618.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 228","lb":"PZ228","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறிவினை ஊழே அடும்","fate overcomes understanding","understanding, however great, does not secure the outcome of a man's works","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to renowned wise men whose works miscarry"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:229":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":231,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகனிலாப் பாரிக்கும்","திங்களும் தீங்குறுதல் காண்டுமால் - பொங்கி","அறைப்பாய் அருவி அணிமலை நாட!","'உறற்பால யார்க்கும் உறும்'"],"p":"","r":"Even the moon, which spreads its broad light across the beautiful sky, is seen to come to harm, the verse tells the lord of the beautiful hill country where falls leap onto the rocks, and so what is bound to befall befalls everyone.","n":"The moon is the instance, not an ornament: if the brightest and highest thing suffers its waning or its seizure, no rank exempts a person. தீங்குறுதல் does not say which harm, waning or eclipse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 229","lb":"PZ229","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உறற்பால யார்க்கும் உறும்","what is due to befall befalls anyone","no height or brightness exempts anyone from what is bound to befall","general","","no story; the moon's harm is the instance, and the proverb is applied to everyone"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:230":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":232,"t":["கழுமலத்தில் யாத்த களிறும் கருவூர்","விழுமியோன் மேற்சென் றதனால் - விழுமிய","வேண்டினும் வேண்டா விடினும் 'உறற்பால","தீண்டா விடுதல் அரிது'"],"p":"","r":"Because the elephant tied up at Kazhumalam went to the excellent man of Karuvur, the verse says, whether one wants excellent things or does not want them, it is hard for what is bound to befall to pass without touching one.","n":"The proverb line is almost word for word the last line of Naladiyar 109 (உற்றபால there, உறற்பால here), so the verse proves its point with a known saying; but where the Naladiyar speaks of unwanted evil, the elephant story turns it to unsought good. The episode is only alluded to: the verse names two places and an elephant that went to a man, and does not say that he was made king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 230","lb":"PZ230","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உறற்பால தீண்டா விடுதல் அரிது","what is bound to befall can hardly be kept from touching one","by tradition the elephant sent out to choose a king, which chose a man of Karuvur; the verse names only the elephant, the two places and the man","named","கருவூர் விழுமியோன்","an elephant tied at Kazhumalam went to an excellent man of Karuvur"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:231":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":233,"t":["ஆஅய் வளர்ந்த அணிநெடும் பெண்ணையை","ஏஎய் இரவெல்லாம் காத்தாலும் - வாஅய்ப்","படற்பாலார் கண்ணே படுமே 'பொறியும்","தொடற்பாலார் கண்ணே தொடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Though one guard all night, shouting, the tall fine palmyra that has grown up, its fruit falls only to those it is to fall to, and fortune too, the proverb says, touches only those it is to touch.","n":"The image sets the watchman who stays up crying out against the one the fruit happens to drop to, so guarding is shown not to confer the gain. The lengthened forms (ஆஅய், ஏஎய், வாஅய்) are metrical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 231","lb":"PZ231","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பொறியும் தொடற்பாலார் கண்ணே தொடும்","fortune too touches those it is due to touch","gain goes to the one fated for it, not to the one who guards it","exemplum","","a man guards a tall palmyra all night and its fruit still falls to another"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:232":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":234,"t":["முற்பெரிய நல்வினை முட்டின்றிச் செய்யாதார்","பிற்பெரிய செல்வம் பெறலாமோ? - வைப்போடு","இகலிப் பொருள்செய்ய எண்ணியக்கால் என்னாம்?","'முதல்இலார்க்(கு) ஊதியம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Can those who did not do great good deeds earlier, without fail, get great wealth later, and what comes of setting out to make wealth by vying with those who have stores, the verse asks, since for those with no capital there is no profit.","n":"The proverb is a merchant's rule, and the verse makes earlier good deeds the capital, so present striving is trade without principal. வைப்போடு இகலி is unclear: it may be rivalry with those who hold stored wealth, or striving against what is laid up by fate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 232","lb":"PZ232","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முதல்இலார்க்(கு) ஊதியம் இல்","there is no profit for those without capital","without the capital of earlier good deeds, effort for wealth yields nothing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who seek great wealth without earlier good deeds"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:233":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":235,"t":["பன்னாளும் நின்ற இடத்தும் கணிவேங்கை","நன்னாளே நாடி மலர்தலால் - மன்னர்","உவப்ப வழிபட் டொழுகினும் செல்வம்","'தொகற்பால போழ்தே தொகும்'"],"p":"","r":"Because the kani-vengai, though it has stood in its place for many days, blossoms only on the good day it seeks out, so wealth, even for those who serve kings so as to please them, gathers only at the time it is due to gather.","n":"கணிவேங்கை is the vengai whose flowering marks the auspicious time, so the tree is itself a reckoner of days; the courtier's service is likened to its long standing, which does not make it bloom early.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 233","lb":"PZ233","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தொகற்பால போழ்தே தொகும்","what is due to gather gathers at its time","service to kings does not hasten wealth before its due time","general","","no story; the vengai's timed flowering is the instance, applied to those who serve kings"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:234":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":236,"t":["குரைத்துக் கொளப்பட்டார் கோளிழுக்குப் பட்டுப்","புரைத்தெழுந்து போகினும் போவர் - அரக்கில்லுள்","பொய்யற்ற ஐவரும் போயினார் 'இல்லையே","உய்வதற்(கு) உய்யா இடம்'"],"p":"","r":"Those seized amid clamour, who have suffered the fault of capture, may yet break out and get away, the verse says, for the five who were free of falsehood escaped from the lac house, and there is no place without escape for those who are to escape.","n":"The first line is obscure: குரைத்துக் கொளப்பட்டார் may be men denounced and taken, and கோளிழுக்கு the disgrace of capture. பொய்யற்ற gives the five a moral quality, but the proverb rests the escape on what is due, not on their truth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 234","lb":"PZ234","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லையே உய்வதற்(கு) உய்யா இடம்","for one who is to escape there is no place he cannot escape from","the five of the lac house are, by tradition, the Pandavas of the Bharata story; the verse does not name them","named","பொய்யற்ற ஐவரும்","the five free of falsehood got out of the lac house"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:235":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":237,"t":["இதுமன்னும் தீதென்று இசைந்ததூஉம் ஆவார்க்கு","அதுமன்னும் நல்லதே யாகும் - மதுமன்னும்","வீநாறு கானல் விரிதிரை தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'தீநாள் திருவுடையார்க்(கு) இல்'"],"p":"","r":"For those who are to prosper, even what was agreed to be surely bad turns out surely good, the verse tells the lord of the cool shore where flowers rich with honey give scent by the spreading waves, since for those who have fortune there is no evil day.","n":"The proverb answers the reckoning of days: the bad day itself loses its force for one with திரு. ஆவார்க்கு, 'for those who will be', makes the good turn depend on the person's lot, not on the thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 235","lb":"PZ235","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீநாள் திருவுடையார்க்(கு) இல்","there is no evil day for those who have fortune","for the fortunate even what all judged bad turns out good","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those whose lot is to prosper"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:236":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":238,"t":["ஆற்றுந் தகைய அரசடைந்தார்க் காயினும்","வீற்று வழியல்லால் வேண்டினும் கைகூடா","தேற்றார் சிறியர் எனல்வேண்டா 'நோற்றார்க்குச்","சோற்றுள்ளும் வீழும் கறி'"],"p":"","r":"Even for those who have come to a king able to give, things do not come to hand, though wanted, except by the way of their lot, so one need not call them dull or small, since for those who did penance curry falls even into their rice.","n":"The verse defends the unrewarded petitioner against contempt: his failure is his lot, not his want of sense. வீற்று வழி is not clear; it may be 'a separate way', the path one's own fortune opens. The proverb pictures gain falling of itself into the plate of one whose penance earned it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","thavam","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 236","lb":"PZ236","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நோற்றார்க்குச் சோற்றுள்ளும் வீழும் கறி","for those who did penance the curry falls even into the rice","what a king gives reaches those whose earlier penance earned it, and the rest are not to be scorned","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who gain at court by the merit of earlier penance"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:237":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":239,"t":["ஆகும் சமயத்தார்க்கு ஆள்வினையும் வேண்டாவாம்","போகும் பொறியார் புரிவும் பயனின்றே","ஏகல் மலைநாட! என்செய்தாங்கு என்பெறினும்","'ஆகாதார்க்(கு) ஆகுவது இல்'"],"p":"","r":"For those whose time is to prosper even effort is not needed, and for those whose fortune is to leave, striving is fruitless, the verse tells the lord of the rocky hill country, so whatever one does and whatever one gets, for those not due to prosper nothing prospers.","n":"The verse names ஆள்வினை and denies it on both sides: needless for the fortunate, useless for the unfortunate. ஆகும் சமயத்தார் makes the favourable lot a matter of timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 237","lb":"PZ237","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆகாதார்க்(கு) ஆகுவது இல்","for those not due to prosper nothing comes to good","effort neither helps the fortunate nor saves the unfortunate","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those whose lot is against them"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:238":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":240,"t":["பண்டுருத்துச் செய்த பழவினை வந்தெம்மை","இன்றொறுக் கின்ற தெனவறியார் - துன்புறுக்கும்","மேவலரை கோவதென்? மின்னேர் மருங்குலாய்!","'ஏவலாள் ஊருஞ் சுடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Not knowing that the old deed done in anger long ago has come and is punishing them now, why do people rage at the foes who make them suffer, the verse asks a woman with a lightning waist, since the servant who is sent also burns a town.","n":"The proverb makes the enemy a mere agent: a town burned on a king's order is burned by his servant, and anger at the servant mistakes the hand for the cause. பண்டுருத்துச் செய்த puts anger at the origin of the deed, so the verse answers anger with anger's own past fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 238","lb":"PZ238","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஏவலாள் ஊருஞ் சுடும்","a man under orders burns even a town","anger at those who harm one is misplaced, since they carry out one's own old deed","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the foe who brings suffering as the agent of one's past deed"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:239":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":241,"t":["சுடப்பட்டு உயிருய்ந்த சோழன் மகனும்","பிடர்த்தலைப் பேரானைப் பெற்றுக் - கடைக்கால்","செயிரறு செங்கோல் செலீஇயனான் 'இல்லை","உயிருடையார் எய்தா வினை.'"],"p":"","r":"The Chola's son, who came through alive after being burned, got the great elephant's nape and at last made his flawless sceptre run, and so, the proverb says, there is no deed that those who keep their life cannot attain.","n":"The proverb fits the heading obliquely: it is less about fate's fixity than about survival keeping every outcome open, though the verse's sequence (burned, saved, mounted, enthroned) implies a lot working itself out. பிடர்த்தலைப் பேரானைப் பெற்று may mean he was carried on the state elephant's neck as the chosen king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 239","lb":"PZ239","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லை உயிருடையார் எய்தா வினை","there is no deed beyond those who keep their life","identified by tradition as Karikalan and the burning of his prison; the verse names only the Chola's son","named","சோழன் மகனும்","a Chola prince survived burning, mounted the great elephant and in the end ruled with a just sceptre"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:240":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":242,"t":["நனியஞ்சத் தக்க அவைவந்தால் தங்கண்","துனியஞ்சார் செய்வ(து) உணர்வார் - பனியஞ்சி","வேழம் பிடிதழூஉம் வேய்சூழ் மலைநாட!","'ஊழம்பு வீழா நிலத்து'"],"p":"","r":"When things greatly to be feared come, those who know what is to be done do not fear their own distress, the verse tells the lord of the bamboo-ringed hill where the bull elephant, fearing the cold, embraces its mate, and the proverb sets this on ground where fate's arrow does not fall.","n":"The closing proverb is elliptical: it may say the knowing stand as if on ground where fate's arrow cannot fall, or, read as an implied question, that there is no such ground and so fear is idle. The elephant sheltering its mate from the cold may hint that the wise meet danger by acting, not by fearing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 240","lb":"PZ240","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஊழம்பு வீழா நிலத்து","on ground where fate's arrow does not fall","those who know their course are not shaken by the fear of what fate brings","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who know what to do when danger comes"],"ch":"pz:24"},"pz:241":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":243,"t":["எங்கண் இனையர் எனக்கருதின் ஏதமால்","தங்கண்ணர் ஆயினும் தகவில கண்டக்கால்","வன்கண்ண னாகி ஒறுக்க 'ஒறுக்கல்லா","மென்கண்ணன் ஆளான் அரசு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that to think 'these are my own people' is a danger: even if they are one's own, a king who sees unfitting deeds must turn hard-eyed and punish, for a soft-eyed king who cannot punish will not rule.","n":"தங்கண்ணர் puts kin and favourites inside the reach of punishment, and the proverb's மென்கண்ணன் turns the Kural's praised kindly eye (கண்ணோட்டம்) into a fault once it stops the rod.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","oruththal","kannottam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 241","lb":"PZ241","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒறுக்கல்லா மென்கண்ணன் ஆளான் அரசு","the soft-eyed man who cannot punish does not rule a kingdom","a king must punish wrong even in his own people","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king who spares his own people"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:242":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":244,"t":["சால மறைத்தோம்பிச் சான்றவர் கைகரப்பக்","காலை கழிந்ததன் பின்றையும் - மேலைக்","கறவைக்கன் றூர்ந்தானைத் தந்தையும் ஊர்ந்தான்","'முறைமைக்கு மூப்பிளமை இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse recalls that though the worthy hid and shielded the deed for long, even after the time had passed the father drove his chariot over the son who had driven over the calf of a milch cow, and proves by it that justice knows neither old nor young.","n":"Lines 1-2 (கைகரப்ப, காலை கழிந்ததன் பின்றையும்) seem to say the elders concealed the son's deed and time went by, so the point is that neither concealment nor delay saved him; the exact sense of கைகரப்ப is not certain. The verse names neither the king nor the son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","oruththal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 242","lb":"PZ242","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முறைமைக்கு மூப்பிளமை இல்","in justice there is no old age or youth","a king's justice does not spare the young, even his own son","named","தந்தை","a father, the king, drove his chariot over his own son, who had run over a cow's calf, though the worthy had hidden the deed"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:243":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":245,"t":["முறைதெரிந்து செல்வர்க்கும் நல்கூர்ந் தவர்க்கும்","இறைதிரியான் நேரொக்க வேண்டும் - முறைதிரிந்து","நேரொழுகா னாயின் அதுவாம் 'ஒருபக்கம்","நீரொழுகிப் பாலொழுகு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says a king who knows justice must, without wavering, deal evenly with the rich and the poor, and that if he swerves and does not deal evenly it is like water flowing on one side and milk on the other.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","naduvu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 243","lb":"PZ243","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒருபக்கம் நீரொழுகிப் பாலொழுகு மாறு","like water running on one side and milk running on the other","unequal justice gives the rich milk and the poor water from one source","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king who judges rich and poor unequally"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:244":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":246,"t":["பொருத்தம் அழியாத பூந்தண்டார் மன்னர்","அருத்தம் அடிநிழ லாரை - வருத்தாது","கொண்டாரும் போலாதே கோடல் அதுவன்றோ","'வண்டூதா துண்டு விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that for kings of unfading fitness and cool flower garlands, taking the wealth of those who live in the shade of their feet without hurting them, as if not taking at all, is the right taking, like the bee that eats the pollen and leaves.","n":"The word division of the proverb (வண்டூதா துண்டு) is unsure; the image is in any case of the bee that feeds on a flower without harming it. அருத்தம் is the Sanskrit artha, wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kudi","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 244","lb":"PZ244","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வண்டூதா துண்டு விடல்","as a bee eats from a flower and leaves it","a king should take revenue so lightly that the subjects feel no loss","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's taking of his subjects' wealth"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:245":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":247,"t":["பாற்பட்டு வாழ்ப எனினும் குடிகள்மேல்","மேற்பட்ட கூட்டு மிகநிற்றல் வேண்டாவாம்","கோல்தலையே யாயினும் கொண்டீக காணுங்கால்","'பாலதலை பாலூறல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say that even when the subjects live in prosperity, the king should not keep his levy over them excessively high, but take within the measure of his rule, since, as the proverb has it, milk does not keep welling beyond its measure.","n":"Lines 3-4 are obscure: கோல்தலையே யாயினும் கொண்டீக may mean 'take and give, even at the sceptre's point' and பாலதலை is unclear, so both the instruction of line 3 and the proverb's image are my best guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kudi","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 245","lb":"PZ245","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பாலதலை பாலூறல் இல்","roughly, milk does not go on welling beyond (its) head; the image is unclear","prosperity does not license excess taxation","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king who presses levy on prosperous subjects"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:246":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":248,"t":["அடைய அடைந்தாரை அல்லவை செய்து","கொடைவேந்தன் கோல்கொடியன் ஆகிக் குடிகள்மேல்","கூட்டிறப்பக் கொண்டு தலையளிப்பின் அஃதன்றோ","'சூட்டறுத்து வாயில் இடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when a giving king harms those who came to him for refuge, turns crooked of sceptre, takes levy from his subjects beyond measure and then shows them favour, it is like cutting off a creature's crest and putting it in its own mouth.","n":"The irony is in கொடைவேந்தன்: the king's generosity is paid for by the people he gives to, so his gift feeds them their own flesh. சூட்டு is most likely the cock's comb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","eegai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 246","lb":"PZ246","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சூட்டறுத்து வாயில் இடல்","cutting off the crest and putting it in the mouth","favour funded by extortion only returns the subjects' own loss to them","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king whose largesse comes from over-taxing his people"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:247":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":249,"t":["வெண்குடைக்கீழ் வாழும் குடிகட்கு வேந்தனும்","செங்கோலன் அல்லாக்கால் செய்வதென் - பொங்கு","படுதிரைச் சேர்ப்ப மற் 'றில்லையே யானை","தொடுவுண்ணின் மூடுங் கலம்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks, addressing the lord of the shore of swelling waves, what the subjects under the white umbrella can do if the king himself is not just, and answers that there is nothing: if the elephant itself takes the food and eats, there is no vessel that can cover it.","n":"The white umbrella is the sign of protection, so the verse's point is that the protector turned taker leaves no higher recourse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kudi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 247","lb":"PZ247","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["றில்லையே யானை தொடுவுண்ணின் மூடுங் கலம்","if the elephant takes and eats, no vessel can cover the food","against an unjust king the subjects have no protection","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to subjects under an unjust king"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:248":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":250,"t":["ஒளியாரை மீதூர்ந்து ஒழுகுவது அல்லால்","களியானை மன்னர்க்கோ கைகடத்தல் ஏதம்","துளியுண் பறவைபோல் செவ்வன்நோற் பாரும்","'எளியாரை எள்ளாதார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say that for kings with rutting elephants, letting matters slip out of hand rather than keeping the upper hand over others is a danger, since even those who do penance rightly, like the bird that lives on raindrops, scorn the weak.","n":"ஒளியாரை and கைகடத்தல் can be read more than one way (those who lack lustre or those who hide; to lose control or to overstep), so the counsel may be either 'keep the upper hand' or 'do not overstep except over the weak'. The rain-drinking bird is the cataka, an image of strict austerity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 248","lb":"PZ248","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எளியாரை எள்ளாதார் இல்","there is no one who does not scorn the weak","a king who appears weak will be scorned by all, even ascetics","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king who lets his power slip"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:249":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":251,"t":["மறுமனத்தன் அல்லாத மாநலத்த வேந்தன்","உறுமனத்த னாகி ஒழுகின் - செறுமனத்தார்","பாயிரம் கூறிப் படைதொக்கால் என்செய்ப?","'ஆயிரம் காக்கைக்கோர் கல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a king of great goodness, not divided in mind, acts with a firm mind, then what can hostile men do even if they gather an army with loud proclamations, for one stone is enough for a thousand crows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","manam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 249","lb":"PZ249","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆயிரம் காக்கைக்கோர் கல்","one stone for a thousand crows","a single resolute king scatters a numerous but weak enemy","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a resolute king facing a gathered enemy"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:250":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":252,"t":["அங்கோல் அவிர்தொடி! ஆழியான ஆயினும்","செங்கோலன் அல்லாக்கால் சேர்ந்தாரும் எள்ளுவரால்","வெங்கோன்மை வேந்தர்கள் வேண்டும் சிறிதெனினும்","'தண்கோல் எடுக்குமாம் மெய்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells a woman of bright bangles that even a king who bears the wheel of rule is scorned by his own allies if he is not just, and yet that kings need some harshness, however little, since even a cool stick, they say, makes the body rise.","n":"The verse holds two things together: injustice earns contempt, and a measure of வெங்கோன்மை is still needed. The proverb's sense (that a light rod still raises welts, or still makes one stand up) is not certain. ஆழியான may be the wheel-bearing emperor or Vishnu.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","veruvu","oruththal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 250","lb":"PZ250","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தண்கோல் எடுக்குமாம் மெய்","even a cool stick, it is said, raises the body","a king needs a little severity even within just rule","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the measure of severity a king must keep"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:251":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":253,"t":["மன்னவன் ஆணைக்கீழ் மற்றையார் மீக்கூற்றம்","என்ன வகையால் செயப் பெறுப? - புன்னைப்","பரப்புநீர் தாவும் படுகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'மரத்தின்கீழ் ஆகா மரம்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks the lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea, where the water leaps over the punnai, how others could gain the upper word under a king's command, since a tree does not grow under a tree.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 251","lb":"PZ251","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மரத்தின்கீழ் ஆகா மரம்","a tree does not grow under a tree","no second authority can rise under a king's rule","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to rivals for power under a king"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:252":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":254,"t":["வழிபட் டவரை வலியராச் செய்தார்","அழிப்பினும் ஆக்கினும் ஆகும் - விழுத்தக்க","பையமர் மாலைப் பணைத்தோளாய்! பாத்தறிவென்","மெல்லக் 'கவுட்கொண்ட நீர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say, to a woman of bamboo shoulders, that those who made strong the men who served them can destroy or raise them as they will, like water held in the cheek, which one may slowly swallow or spit out.","n":"The proverb is only கவுட்கொண்ட நீர், and பாத்தறிவென் மெல்ல is unclear; my reading takes the water in the mouth as wholly at the holder's disposal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vali","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 252","lb":"PZ252","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கவுட்கொண்ட நீர்","water taken into the cheek","those a ruler has raised remain his to make or unmake","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to followers a ruler has raised"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:253":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":255,"t":["தலைமை கருதும் தகையாரை வேந்தன்","நிலைமையால் நேர்செய் திருத்தல் - மலைமிசைக்","காம்பனுக்கும் மென்தோளாய்! அஃதன்றோ 'ஓரறையுள்","பாம்போ(டு) உடனுறையும் ஆறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells a woman of soft shoulders that for a king to keep men who aspire to leadership on an equal footing with himself is like living with a snake in one room.","n":"நிலைமையால் நேர்செய்து may mean raising them to equal rank or keeping them level with one another; either way the danger is ambitious men kept close and strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 253","lb":"PZ253","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஓரறையுள் பாம்போ(டு) உடனுறையும் ஆறு","the way of living with a snake in a single room","rivals given equal standing are a constant danger at close quarters","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king who raises would-be leaders to his own level"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:254":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":256,"t":["கூற்றம் உயிர்கொள்ளும் போழ்து குறிப்பறிந்து","மாற்றம் உடையாரை ஆராயா(து) - ஆற்றவும்","முல்லை புரையும் முறுவலாய்! 'செய்வதென்","வல்லை அரசாட் கொளின்?'"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say, to a woman whose smile is like jasmine, that as Death when it takes a life does not stop to examine those who have pleas, so there is nothing one can do when the king suddenly seizes one.","n":"It is unclear whether the verse warns the subject that royal power is as deaf as Death or reproaches a king who seizes without inquiry; வல்லை (quickly) and அரசாட் கொளின் allow both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kootru","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 254","lb":"PZ254","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["செய்வதென் வல்லை அரசாட் கொளின்","what can one do if the king swiftly seizes (one)","against the king's sudden seizure there is no plea, as against Death","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a man caught by royal power"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:255":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":257,"t":["உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வத்து உயர்ந்த பெருமை","அடக்கமில் உள்ளத்தன் ஆகி - நடக்கையின்","ஒளளியன் அல்லான்மேல் வைத்தல் 'குரங்கின்கைக்","கொள்ளி கொடுத்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that to place the high dignity of great wealth on a man of unrestrained mind who is not upright in conduct is to put a firebrand in a monkey's hand.","n":"ஒளளியன் is probably a slip for ஒள்ளியன், so 'not upright (or not wise) in conduct'. The proverb's monkey will not keep the fire still, so the damage spreads beyond the man himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","adakkam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 255","lb":"PZ255","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குரங்கின்கைக் கொள்ளி கொடுத்து விடல்","giving a firebrand into a monkey's hand","power given to the unrestrained spreads ruin","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to giving high office to the unrestrained"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:256":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":258,"t":["எல்லையொன்(று) இன்றியே இன்னாசெய் தாரையும்","ஒல்லை வெகுளார் உலகாள்வதும் என்பவர்","சொல்லின் வளாஅய்த்தம் தாள்நிழல் கொள்பவே","'கொல்லையுள் கூழ்மரமே போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who do not quickly grow angry even at those who did them boundless harm are the ones said to rule the world, and people, drawn in by their words, take shelter at their feet like a food-bearing tree in a field.","n":"ஒல்லை வெகுளார் is 'not angry quickly', not 'never angry'; the forbearance is of speed. The shelter image, கூழ்மரம், is my reading of a tree whose yield feeds those near it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 256","lb":"PZ256","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொல்லையுள் கூழ்மரமே போன்று","like a food-bearing tree in a field","slowness to anger draws people to a ruler's shelter","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the forbearing ruler around whom people gather"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:257":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":259,"t":["போலந்தார் இராமன் துணையாகத் தான்போந்து","இலங்கைக் கிழவற்(கு) இளையான் - இலங்கைக்கே","போந்திறை யாயதூஉம் பெற்றான் 'பெரியாரைச்","சார்ந்து கெழீஇயிலார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse recalls that the younger brother of Lanka's lord came over himself as ally to Rama of the garland and gained even the kingship of Lanka, and proves by it that none who joined the great and held close to them failed.","n":"The epic case cuts against kin loyalty: the reward comes from leaving his brother for the great one, and the verse passes no judgment on that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","perumai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 257","lb":"PZ257","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரியாரைச் சார்ந்து கெழீஇயிலார் இல்","there are none who joined the great and held close to them who did not thrive","joining and keeping close to the great brings gain, even a kingdom","named","இலங்கைக் கிழவற்(கு) இளையான்","the younger brother of Lanka's lord went over to Rama as his ally and was made king of Lanka"],"ch":"pz:25"},"pz:258":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":260,"t":["கல்வி அகலமும் கட்டுரை வாய்பாடும்","கொல்சின வேந்தன் அவைகாட்டும் - மல்கித்","தலைப்பாய் இழிதரு தண்புனல் 'நீத்தம்","மலைப்பெயல் காட்டும் துணை'"],"p":"","r":"The council of the king of killing anger shows a man's breadth of learning and the skill of his set speech, just as the cool flood that pours down in full at the river's head shows how much rain fell on the mountain.","n":"The argument runs from effect to hidden cause: the rain on the mountain is not seen, only the flood below, and the learning stored in a man is not seen until the king's assembly draws it out. துணை here is a measure, how much, not a companion. The king is கொல்சின, of killing anger, so the assembly that tests is also a dangerous one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","avai","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 258","lb":"PZ258","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீத்தம் மலைப்பெயல் காட்டும் துணை","the flood shows the measure of the rain on the mountain","a counsellor's learning and eloquence are known by what he brings out in the king's council","general","","no story; the river in spate is the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:259":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":261,"t":["செயிரறு செங்கோல் சினவேந்தன் தீமை","பயிரறு பக்கத்தார் கொள்வர் - துகிர்புரையும்","செவ்வாய் முறுவலநற் சின்மொழியாய்! 'செய்தானை","ஒவ்வாத பாவையோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Addressed to a woman with coral-red lips and few sweet words, the verse says that the faults of an angry king whose sceptre is otherwise blameless are laid on the attendants beside him, since no doll is unlike the one who made it.","n":"The counsellors are cast as the maker and the king as the doll, so the king's misdeeds are read as the work of those around him; கொள்வர் may mean they take on the blame or that they take on the king's evil ways. பயிரறு, qualifying பக்கத்தார், is obscure: it may be 'whose calling is cut off' or a corrupt word, and the reading does not rest on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","pazhi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 259","lb":"PZ259","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["செய்தானை ஒவ்வாத பாவையோ இல்","there is no doll that does not resemble the one who made it","a king's faults are charged to the counsellors who shaped him","general","","no story; the doll and its maker are the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:260":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":262,"t":["சுற்றார் பலரைத்தன் கண்ணாக இல்லாதான்","உற்றிடர்ப் பட்ட பொழுதின்கண் தேற்றம்","மரையா துணைபயிரும் மாமலை நாட!","'சுரையாழ் நரம்பறுத் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"Addressed to the lord of the great hills where the marai deer calls its mate, the verse says that for one who has not made many of those around him his eyes, there is no steadying when distress comes, like a gourd-lute whose strings are cut.","n":"The lute is still there when the strings are cut, but it can give no sound: the man without counsellors keeps his state but has no response when trouble strikes. The address image, a deer calling its companion, sets beside the counsel a creature that has a mate to call on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 260","lb":"PZ260","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சுரையாழ் நரம்பறுத் தற்று","it is like cutting the strings of a gourd lute","a ruler without many counsellors as his eyes is helpless in a crisis","general","","no story; the stringless lute is the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:261":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":263,"t":["நல்லவும் தீயவும் நாடிப் பிறருரைக்கும்","நல்ல பிறவும் உணர்வாரைக் கட்டுரையின்","வல்லிதின் நாடி வலிப்பதே 'புல்லத்தைப்","புல்லம் புறம்புல்லு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that to seek out by close discussion those who weigh good and ill, grasp what others say and know other good things besides, and then hold them firmly, is as a bull rubs against a bull's back.","n":"The proverb pictures like supporting like: two bulls lean and rub together, each the other's prop. The chooser is not named, but the heading and the testing by கட்டுரை, set speech, point to a king choosing counsellors. வலிப்பது may mean binding them fast or making oneself strong by them; the proverb fits either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","therithal","thunai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 261","lb":"PZ261","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புல்லத்தைப் புல்லம் புறம்புல்லு மாறு","the way a bull embraces the back of a bull","testing the discerning by discussion and holding them close makes them a mutual support","general","","no story; two bulls rubbing together are the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:262":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":264,"t":["மனத்தினும் வாயினும் மெய்யினும் செய்கை","அனைத்தினும் ஆன்றவிந்தா ராகி - நினைத்திருந்து","ஒன்றும் பரியலராய் 'ஓம்புவார் இல்லெனில்","சென்று படுமாம் உயிர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse describes guardians who are full and subdued in mind, speech, body and every deed, who keep watch in thought and never waver, and says that if there are none such to guard him, life goes and perishes.","n":"The three channels of mind, speech and body, a frame of the Jain and wider ethical codes for restraint, are here demanded of the king's guardians rather than of the ascetic. ஒன்றும் பரியலராய் may mean they are moved by nothing, or that they spare no effort; both make them steadfast. Whose life is at stake is left open; the heading makes it the king's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","adakkam","thunai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 262","lb":"PZ262","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஓம்புவார் இல்லெனில் சென்று படுமாம் உயிர்","if there are none to guard it, life goes and dies","a king without restrained, watchful counsellors is lost","general","","no story; an unguarded life is the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:263":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":265,"t":["செயல்வேண்டா நல்லன செய்விக்கும் தீய","செயல்வேண்டி நிற்பின் விலக்கும் இகல்வேந்தன்","தன்னை நலிந்து தனக்குறுதி கூறலால்","'முன்னின்னா மூத்தார்வாய்ச் சொல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that good counsellors make the warring king do the good he does not want to do and stop him when he stands bent on evil, telling him what is for his good even by afflicting him, and so the words of elders are painful at first.","n":"முன் இன்னா, painful first, implies the sweetness after: the elders' counsel is bitter in the hearing and good in its outcome. The counsel works both ways, pushing to unwanted good as well as holding back from evil, and தன்னை நலிந்து admits that the minister's duty is to hurt the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","idithal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 263","lb":"PZ263","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முன்னின்னா மூத்தார்வாய்ச் சொல்","the word from the mouth of elders is painful at first","a good minister's counsel pains the king before it profits him","general","","no story; the elders' bitter counsel is the instance"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:264":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":266,"t":["செறிவுடைத் தார்வேந்தன் செவ்வியல பெற்றால்","அறிவுடையார் அவ்வியமும் செய்வர் - வறிதுரைத்துப்","பிள்ளை களைமருட்டும் தாயர்போல் அம்புலிமேல்","'ஒள்ளியகாட் டாளர்க்(கு) அரிது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when the wise find the garlanded king in a favourable mood they will even use guile, as mothers soothe their children with idle talk of the moon, and closes with the proverb that it is hard for those who show bright things.","n":"The counsellor is likened to a mother who points to the moon to quiet a child, steering by a pleasing show rather than by plain speech; அவ்வியம், usually crookedness or envy, is here a tactful deceit. The proverb as quoted is hard to construe: it may mean that what bright show achieves is hard for others to achieve, or that the moon shown is hard to give. செவ்வியல பெற்றால் may instead be 'if they get the right moments'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kaalam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 264","lb":"PZ264","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒள்ளியகாட் டாளர்க்(கு) அரிது","it is hard for those who show bright things","the wise may steer a king by artful indirection when his mood allows","exemplum","தாயர்","mothers beguile their children with empty talk, pointing to the moon"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:265":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":267,"t":["தீயன வல்ல செயினும் திறல்வேந்தன்","காய்வன சிந்தியார் சுற்றறிந்தார் - பாயும்","'புலிமுன்னர் புல்வாய்க்குப் போக்கில் அதுவே","வளிமுன்னர் வைப்பாரம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who know the court's ways do not think of anything that would anger the mighty king even when what he does is not evil, for there is no escape for the deer before the leaping tiger, and likewise no load of straw before the wind.","n":"The proverb is doubled, tiger before deer and wind before straw, both images of a force nothing stands against. தீயன அல்ல செயினும் most likely means even when the king does no wrong, so the caution is not a response to his faults but a standing rule; it could also be read as 'even if he does things other than evil'. சிந்தியார், they do not even think it, puts the restraint in the mind as well as the mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vekuli","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 265","lb":"PZ265","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலிமுன்னர் புல்வாய்க்குப் போக்கில் அதுவே வளிமுன்னர் வைப்பாரம் இல்","the deer has no escape before the tiger; just so, a load of straw has none before the wind","no servant survives the anger of a strong king, so the wise never provoke it","general","","no story; the deer before the tiger and straw before the wind are the instances"],"ch":"pz:26"},"pz:266":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":268,"t":["கொடித்திண்டேர் மன்னரால் கூட்டுண்டு வாழ்வார்","எடுத்துமேற் கொண்டவர் ஏய வினையை","மடித்தொழிதல் என்னுண்டாம்? மாணிழாய்! 'கள்ளைக்","குடித்துக் குழைவாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman of fine jewels, the verse asks what place there is for shrinking back from a task the bannered-chariot kings have assigned, for those who live by sharing those kings' food, since no one drinks the toddy and then goes limp.","n":"கூட்டுண்டு, eating together with or from the king, makes the service a debt already taken. குழை (to soften, droop) is read here as losing nerve; the proverb may also mean that one who has drunk cannot then pretend to be sober, and the sense is not fully fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","madi","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 266","lb":"PZ266","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கள்ளைக் குடித்துக் குழைவாரோ இல்","there is no one who drinks toddy and then wilts","one who lives on the king's food cannot slacken on the king's work","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:267":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":269,"t":["வெற்றிவேல் வேந்தன் வியங்கொண்டால் யாமொன்றும்","பெற்றிலேம் என்பது பேதைமையே - மற்றதனை","எவ்வம் இலராகிச் செய்க அதுவன்றோ","'செய்கென்றான் உண்கென்னு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"When the king of the victorious spear gives a command, to say 'we have received nothing' is folly; one should do the work without grievance, for it is the one who says 'do' who says 'eat'.","n":"The verse answers the servant who bargains before working; reward is placed after the work and in the same mouth as the command.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","pedhaimai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 267","lb":"PZ267","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["செய்கென்றான் உண்கென்னு மாறு","he who said 'do it' is the one who says 'eat'","the servant should do the king's work first and trust the reward to follow from the one who commanded it","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:268":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":270,"t":["எமரிது செய்க எமக்கென்று வேந்தன்","தமரைத் தலைவைத்த காலைத் - தமரவற்கு","வேலின்வா யாயினும் வீழார் மறுத்துரைப்பின்","'ஆல்என்னிற் பூல்என்னு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"When the king puts his own men at the head, saying 'let our men do this for us', they do not fall back even at the point of the spear; to answer him back is the way of saying 'pool' when he says 'aal'.","n":"தமர் are the king's own people, and his putting them first is the honour that binds them. பூல் is taken as a plant name chosen for its jingle with ஆல்; the proverb mocks mere contradiction rather than reasoned dissent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","thitpam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 268","lb":"PZ268","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆல்என்னிற் பூல்என்னு மாறு","when one says 'banyan', the other says 'pool'","to contradict the king's commission is perverse contrariness in his trusted men","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:269":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":271,"t":["விடலமை செய்து வெருண்டகன்று நில்லாது","உடலரு மன்னர் உவப்ப ஒழுகின்","மடலணி பெண்ணை மலிதிரை சேர்ப்ப!","'கடல்படா வெல்லாம் படும்'"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the lord of the shore where palmyras with fronds stand by the full waves, the verse says that if one does not act to break away or stand off in fear, but conducts oneself so that kings hard to oppose are pleased, everything that the sea does not yield will come.","n":"The address to a lord of the seashore makes the proverb pointed: even to one whose wealth comes from the sea, the king's favour yields what the sea does not. விடலமை in line 1 is read as 'acting so as to leave'; the word is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 269","lb":"PZ269","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடல்படா வெல்லாம் படும்","all that is not got from the sea will be got","pleasing the king without flinching brings every gain","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:270":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":272,"t":["உவப்ப உடன்படுத்தற் கேய கருமம்","அவற்றவற் றாந்துணைய வாகிப் பயத்தால்","வினைமுதிரின் செய்தான்மேல் ஏறும் பனைமுதிரின்","தாய்தாள்மேல் வீழ்ந்து விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Tasks fit to win the king's glad consent, each taken up in its proper measure, when they ripen in their fruit come back upon the one who did them, as a palm fruit when ripe falls at the foot of its parent tree.","n":"The proverb has no opening quotation mark in our text; the span is taken from the closing mark and the sense. Whether செய்தான்மேல் ஏறும் means the reward or the responsibility comes to the doer is not settled by the verse; the palm image favours a return to the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","mannar","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 270","lb":"PZ270","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பனைமுதிரின் தாய்தாள்மேல் வீழ்ந்து விடும்","when the palm fruit ripens it falls at the foot of the tree that bore it","the fruit of work done for the king returns to its doer","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:271":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":273,"t":["செருக்கெழு மன்னர் திறலுடையார் சேர்ந்தார்","ஒருத்தரை அஞ்சி உலைதலும் உண்டோ?","உருத்த சுணங்கின் ஒளியிழாய்! 'கூரிது","எருத்து வலியநன் கொம்பு'"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman whose bright skin bears the spreading sunangu marks, the verse asks whether those who have joined mighty, proud kings will tremble in fear of any one man, for the fine horn of a strong-necked bull is sharp.","n":"The horn's sharpness is put down to the neck behind it, so the servant's courage is borrowed from the king's strength rather than his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","anjaamai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 271","lb":"PZ271","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூரிது எருத்து வலியநன் கொம்பு","the good horn of a bull with a strong neck is sharp","a servant backed by a strong king need fear no single opponent","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:272":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":274,"t":["வேந்தன் மதித்துவப்பப் பட்டாரைக் கொண்டேனை","மாந்தரும் ஆங்கே மதித்துணர்வர் - ஆய்ந்த","நலமென் கதுப்பினாய்! நாடின 'நெய்பெய்த","கலனேநெய் பெய்து விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Whoever the king esteems and is pleased with, other people too esteem in the same way; addressing a woman of soft, well-tended hair, the verse says that, as is known, the vessel into which ghee was poured is the one that pours ghee.","n":"கொண்டேனை is read as கொண்டு with ஏனை மாந்தரும், 'other people too'. The verse states a fact of reputation, not a merit: esteem flows where the king has poured it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 272","lb":"PZ272","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நெய்பெய்த கலனேநெய் பெய்து விடும்","only the vessel that has been filled with ghee pours ghee","honour conferred by the king is passed on by others to the one he favoured","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:273":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":275,"t":["ஆண்டகை மன்னரைத் சார்ந்தார்தாம் அல்லுறினும்","ஆண்டொன்று வேண்டுதும் என்பது உரையற்க","பூண்தாங்கு மார்ப! பொருள்தக்கார் 'வேண்டாமை","வேண்டிய தெல்லாம் தரும்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have joined manly kings should not, even when they fall into hardship, say 'we want something here'; addressing a man whose chest bears ornaments, the verse says that for the worthy, not wanting gives all that is wanted.","n":"வேண்டாமை is the word of the renouncer's ethics, here put to use at court: the paradox is that freedom from wanting is the surest way to be given. அல்லுறினும் is read as 'even if they suffer distress'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 273","lb":"PZ273","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வேண்டாமை வேண்டிய தெல்லாம் தரும்","not wanting brings all that one wants","the worthy servant who asks nothing of the king even in want receives everything","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:274":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":276,"t":["காவலனை ஆக வழிபட்டார் மற்றவன்","ஏவல் வினைசெய் திருந்தார்க்(கு) உதவடுத்தல்","ஆவணைய நின்றதன் கன்று 'முலையிருப்பத்","தாயணல் தான்சுவைத் தற்று'"],"p":"","r":"For those who have served the guardian king fully and stay doing the work he commands, to seek help from elsewhere is like a calf standing by its cow and sucking her throat while the udder is there.","n":"உதவு அடுத்தல் is read as approaching others for help; the line could also mean that others' help to such servants is superfluous. Either way the king is the udder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","pedhaimai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 274","lb":"PZ274","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முலையிருப்பத் தாயணல் தான்சுவைத் தற்று","it is like sucking the mother's throat while the udder is there","the king's servant who seeks support elsewhere turns from the true source to a dry one","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:275":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":277,"t":["சிறப்புடை மன்னவரைச் செவ்வியின் நோக்கித்","திறத்தின் உரைப்பாரைக்கொன் (று) ஆகாத தில்லை","விறற்புகழ் மன்னர்க்(கு) உயிரன்ன ரேனும்","'புறத்தமைச்சின் நன்றகத்துக் கூன்'"],"p":"","r":"There is nothing that cannot be achieved by those who watch the right moment with distinguished kings and speak with skill; even those who are like life itself to victorious kings fare worse outside, for a hunchback inside is better than a minister outside.","n":"The kūn, a hunchbacked palace attendant, has the inner rooms and so the king's moments; the minister, however high, does not. The verse ranks nearness at the right time above office. Line 2 உரைப்பாரைக்கொன் (று) is read as உரைப்பார்க்கு ஒன்று.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kaalam","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 275","lb":"PZ275","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புறத்தமைச்சின் நன்றகத்துக் கூன்","a hunchback within is better than a minister without","access and timing in speaking to the king outweigh rank and even dearness","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:276":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":278,"t":["இடுகுடைத்தேர் மன்னர் எமக்கமையும் என்று","கடிதவர்தாம் காதலிப்பத் தாம்காதல் கொண்டு","மூடிய எனைத்தும் உணரா முயறல்","'கடிய கனைத்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"To think 'the kings of the parasol chariots will suit us', and, when they love one fiercely, to return the love and strive on without perceiving anything that lies hidden, is like clearing one's throat at a dangerous moment.","n":"The proverb is short and its image cannot be fixed: கடிய may be 'dangerous' or 'quickly', and கனைத்தல் a cough or a neigh. The counsel is clearer than the proof: royal love is not a ground for unguarded action.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","therithal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 276","lb":"PZ276","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடிய கனைத்து விடல்","giving a cough where it is dangerous to make a sound","the favourite who trusts the king's love and does not read what it hides betrays himself","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:277":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":279,"t":["சீர்த்தகு மன்னர் சிறந்தனைத்தும் கெட்டாலும்","நேர்த்துரைத்(து) எள்ளார் நிலைநோக்கிச் - சீர்த்த","கிளையின்றிப் போஒய்த் தனித்தாயக் கண்ணும்","'இளைதென்று பாம்பிகழ்வார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if a worthy king has lost all his greatness, those who weigh his station do not face him and mock him, even when he has gone without his great kin and stands alone, for no one slights a snake because it is small.","n":"நிலைநோக்கி, 'looking to his standing', makes the restraint a judgement of what he still is and may again be; இளைது can mean small or young, and the snake keeps its venom either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","igal","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 277","lb":"PZ277","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இளைதென்று பாம்பிகழ்வார் இல்","no one belittles a snake for being young","a fallen and friendless king keeps his power to strike and must not be scorned","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:278":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":280,"t":["செருக்குடைய மன்னர் இடைப்புக்(கு) அவருள்","ஒருத்தற்(கு) உதவாத சொல்லின் தனக்குத்","திருத்தலும் ஆகாது தீதரம் அதுவே","'எருத்திடை வைக்கோல் தினல்'"],"p":"","r":"To enter among proud kings and speak words that help neither of them is something one cannot afterwards set right for oneself, and that is the bringing of harm: it is eating straw between bulls.","n":"ஒருத்தற்கு உதவாத சொல் is read as words of no use to either party; it may instead mean words that fail the one king one serves. தீதரம் is unusual and is read as a form of தீது, harm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","sol","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 278","lb":"PZ278","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எருத்திடை வைக்கோல் தினல்","eating straw between two bulls","one who meddles between rival kings is crushed between them","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:279":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":281,"t":["பன்னாள் தொழில்செய்து உடைய கவர்ந்துண்டார்","இன்னாமை செய்யாமை வேண்டி இறைவர்க்குப்","பொன்யாத்துக் கொண்டு புகுதல் 'குவளையைத்","தன்னாரால் யாத்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"For those who served many days and secretly took and ate what was the king's to come before him with gold tied up as an offering, hoping he will do them no harm, is tying a water lily with its own fibre.","n":"The image works because the lily is bound by what grew from it: the gift that is meant to win the king over was the king's own. The verse does not say whether the trick succeeds, only what it is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kalavu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 279","lb":"PZ279","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குவளையைத் தன்னாரால் யாத்து விடல்","tying the blue water lily with its own stalk-fibre","to placate the king with gold taken from him is to give him back what was his","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:280":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":282,"t":["மெய்ம்மையே நின்று மிகநோக்கப் பட்டவர்","கைம்மேலே நின்று கறுப்பன செய்தொழுகிப்","பொய்ம்மேலே கொண்டவ் விறைவற்கொன் றார் 'குறைப்பர்","தம்மேலே வீழப் பனை.'"],"p":"","r":"Those who were highly regarded for standing in truth, if they go on doing dark deeds under the king's very hand and take up falsehood, setting themselves against him, fell the palm so that it falls on themselves.","n":"The servant's standing was built on truth (மெய்ம்மையே நின்று), so his falsehood fells the very trunk that held him up. Our text has a full stop inside the closing quotation mark.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vaaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 280","lb":"PZ280","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குறைப்பர் தம்மேலே வீழப் பனை","they cut down the palm so that it falls on them","the trusted servant who turns false brings his own ruin","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:281":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":283,"t":["வெஞ்சின மன்னவன் வேண்டாதவே செயினும்","நெஞ்சத்துட் கொள்வ சிறிதும் செயல்வேண்டா","என்செய்து அகப்பட்டக் கண்ணும் 'எழுப்புபவோ","துஞ்சு புலியைத் துயில்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if the fiercely angry king does what one does not want, one should not act in the least on what one holds in the heart, even when one has been caught for whatever one did, for no one wakes a sleeping tiger from its sleep.","n":"The resentment is granted as real (வேண்டாதவே செயினும்); the counsel is to keep it inside, since the king's quiet anger is the sleeping tiger. Line 3 என்செய்து அகப்பட்டக் கண்ணும் is read as 'whatever one is caught for'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","porai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 281","lb":"PZ281","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எழுப்புபவோ துஞ்சு புலியைத் துயில்","would anyone rouse a sleeping tiger from its sleep","a servant wronged by the king should not stir the king's anger by acting on his grievance","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:282":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":284,"t":["தாமேயும் தம்மைப் புறந்தர வாற்றாதார்","வாமான்றோ மன்னரைக் காய்வது எவன்கொலோ?","ஆமா உகளும் அணிவரை வெற்ப! கேள்","'ஏமரார் கோங்கு ஏறினார்'"],"p":"","r":"Why should those who cannot even protect themselves by themselves be angry with kings of leaping horses? Addressing the lord of a beautiful hill where wild cattle leap, the verse says that those who have climbed the kongu tree have no safety.","n":"Why the kongu gives no safety (brittle wood or thorny trunk) the verse does not say. வாமான்றோ is read as வாம் மான் தேர், the king of leaping horses and chariots.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vekuli","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 282","lb":"PZ282","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஏமரார் கோங்கு ஏறினார்","those who have climbed the kongu tree find no safety","the weak who turn against the king have no refuge","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:283":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":285,"t":["உறாஅ வகையது செய்தாரை வேந்தன்","பொறாஅன் போலப் பொறுத்தால் - பொறாஅமை","மேன்மேலும் செய்து விடுதல் அதுவன்றோ","'கூன்மேல் எழுந்த குரு'"],"p":"","r":"When the king, though seeming unable to bear it, bears with those who have done what does not suit him, to go on doing more and more of what cannot be borne is a boil risen upon a hump.","n":"The verse turns on பொறாஅன் போலப் பொறுத்தல்: the king's patience is shown with displeasure, and the servant who reads only the patience compounds the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","porai","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 283","lb":"PZ283","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூன்மேல் எழுந்த குரு","a boil that has risen on a hump","to presume on the king's forbearance heaps a second affliction on the first","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:284":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":286,"t":["பொருளல்லார் கூறிய பொய்க்குறளை வேந்தன்","தெருளும் திறந்தெரிதல் அல்லால் - வெருளஎழுந்து","ஆடு பவரோடே ஆடார் உணர்வுடையார்","'ஆடுபணைப் பொய்க்காலே போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"Beyond working out how the king may see clearly through the false slander spoken by worthless men, the wise do not dance along with those who rise up in alarm and dance, like the stilts of a dancer to the drum.","n":"பொய்க்கால் is read as the stilts of a stilt-dancer, which move only as the dancer moves them; the proverb mocks those who are carried by another's alarm. The pun on பொய் (false) links the stilts to the false slander.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","purangooral","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 284","lb":"PZ284","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆடுபணைப் பொய்க்காலே போன்று","like the false legs of a dancer to the drum","the wise courtier does not move with the agitation that slander raises but helps the king discern","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the king's servant as a type"],"ch":"pz:27"},"pz:285":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":287,"t":["வன்சார்(பு) உடையர் எனினும் வலிபெய்து","தஞ்சார்(பு) இலாதாரைத் தேசூன்றல் ஆகுமோ","மஞ்சுசூழ் சோலை மலைநாட! யார்க்கானும்","'அஞ்சுவார்க் கில்லை அரண்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether anyone, by pouring in strength, can make those who lack a footing of their own stand in glory, even when they have strong backers, and answers that for those who fear there is no fortress, whoever they are.","n":"The verse sets two supports against each other: வன்சார்பு, the strong backing of allies, and தஞ்சார்பு, a footing of one's own; the first cannot make up for the lack of the second. This reading takes both phrases of the same people; if தஞ்சார்பு இலாதாரை is instead the attacker's victims (those without allies of their own), the verse asks whether the strong may lord it over the friendless, and the proverb fits less well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","aran","vali","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 285","lb":"PZ285","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அஞ்சுவார்க் கில்லை அரண்","there is no fortress for those who fear","allies and strength poured in from outside cannot save a man without courage of his own","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anyone whose own nerve fails, whatever backing he has"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:286":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":288,"t":["எதிர்த்த பகையை இளைதாய போழ்தே","கதித்துக் களையின் முதிராதே தீர்த்து","நனிநயப்பச் செய்தவர் நண்பெல்லாந் தீரத்","'தனிமரம் காடாதல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that an enemy who has risen against one, if rooted out swiftly while still young, does not grow mature, and that once all the friendships of those who had courted him are cut away he is left alone, for a lone tree never becomes a forest.","n":"Two moves are joined: striking early (இளைதாய போழ்தே) and stripping the enemy of his allies (நண்பெல்லாந் தீர). The proverb proves the second: an enemy isolated cannot spread. The syntax of lines 2 and 3 is loose; தீர்த்து and தீர are read as the removal of the enemy and of his friends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaalam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 286","lb":"PZ286","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தனிமரம் காடாதல் இல்","a single tree never becomes a forest","an enemy isolated from his friends while still weak cannot grow into a threat","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to an enemy cut off from his allies"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:287":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":289,"t":["முன்னலிந்து ஆற்ற முரண்கொண்டு எழுந்தோரைப்","பின்னலிதும் என்றிருத்தல் பேதைமையே - பின்சென்று","காம்பன்ன தோளி! கடிதிற் 'கடித்தோடும்","பாம்பின்பல் கொள்வாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls it folly to think of harming later those who have already harmed one greatly and risen in hostility, for, it tells a woman whose shoulders are like bamboo, no one ever takes the fangs of a snake that bites swiftly and runs.","n":"The counsel is against putting off retaliation, not against retaliation: the snake that has bitten and fled cannot be caught afterwards, so the harm stands unanswered. The address to a woman (காம்பன்ன தோளி) frames a political counsel as talk to a beloved, as the hill and shore addresses do elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaalam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 287","lb":"PZ287","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடித்தோடும் பாம்பின்பல் கொள்வாரோ இல்","no one takes the tooth of a snake that has bitten and run","one who has been harmed must answer at once, since the chance passes with the attacker","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to an enemy who strikes and withdraws"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:288":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":290,"t":["நிரம்ப நிரையத்தைக் கண்டதும் நிரையம்","வரம்பில் பெரியானும் புக்கான் - இரங்கார்","கொடியார மார்ப! 'குடிகெட வந்தால்","அடிகெட மன்றி விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse appears to say that even the boundless great one entered hell after seeing it in full, that the pitiless do not relent, and closes with the proverb that when one comes to ruin the community, he is to be put away root and all.","n":"The first two lines are hard to construe: who the வரம்பில் பெரியான் is and why he entered hell the verse does not say, and the link from that episode to the proverb is not plain. The proverb's own sense, that a destroyer of the kudi must be removed down to the foot, fits the heading on dealing with enemies; மன்றி may be a corruption.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kudi","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 288","lb":"PZ288","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குடிகெட வந்தால் அடிகெட மன்றி விடல்","when one comes to ruin the people, cast him off till his foot is gone","one who would ruin the community is to be removed entirely, without pity","named","வரம்பில் பெரியானும்","a boundless great one entered hell after seeing it in full"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:289":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":291,"t":["தமர்அல் லவரைத் தலையளித்தக் கண்ணும்","அமராக் குறிப்பவர்க்(கு) ஆகாதே தோன்றும்","சுவர்நிலம் செய்தமையைக் கூட்டியக் கண்ணும்","உவர்நிலம் உட்கொதிக்கு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that even when one shows favour to those who are not one's own, to men whose intent is not to be reconciled it plainly does not take, just as saline ground boils from within even when one mixes into it what makes good ground for a wall.","n":"The fault is placed in the other's குறிப்பு, his settled intent, not in the favour shown: kindness cannot change a mind made up against one. The soil image makes the hostility a property of the ground itself, which treatment does not reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","utpagai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 289","lb":"PZ289","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உவர்நிலம் உட்கொதிக்கு மாறு","the way saline ground boils within","favours to a settled enemy do not win him over","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those whose intent is fixed against reconciliation"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:290":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":292,"t":["முகம்புறத்துக் கண்டால் பொறுக்கலா தாரை","அகம்புகுதும் என்றிரக்கும் ஆசை இருங்கடத்துத்","தக்க நெறியிடைப் பின்னும் செலப்பெறார்","'ஒக்கலை வேண்டி அழல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the wish of one who begs to be let into the house of those who cannot bear to see his face even outside is like a child crying to be carried on the hip by people who, on a great wilderness road, would not let him walk behind them on the proper path.","n":"The proverb is a picture of misplaced asking: to want closeness (to be taken in, to be carried) from those who refuse even distance (to be seen, to be followed). The verse is about the asker's want of self-respect as much as the host's enmity; its link to the heading is through the hostile host.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 290","lb":"PZ290","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒக்கலை வேண்டி அழல்","crying for the hip, as a child wails to be carried","seeking intimacy from those who cannot bear one's face is a child's futile wailing","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to one who seeks entry where he is hated"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:291":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":293,"t":["ஆற்றப் பெரியார் பகைவேண்டிக் கொள்ளற்க","போற்றாது கொண்டரக்கன் போரில் அகப்பட்டான்","நோற்ற பெருமை உடையாரும் 'கூற்றம்","புறங்கொம்மை கொட்டினார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says one should not seek enmity with the very great, for the demon who took it on without care was caught in battle, and even those who have the greatness of penance have never clapped Death on the back.","n":"The example is a warning against one's own strength: the அரக்கன் is cast as a figure great in penance who still fell, so even power won by austerity does not make the great safe to offend. The proverb turns the point to death, which no penance mocks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pagai","thavam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 291","lb":"PZ291","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூற்றம் புறங்கொம்மை கொட்டினார் இல்","no one ever clapped Death on the back","even those mighty by penance must not provoke the great, since none escapes the death that follows","named","கொண்டரக்கன்","a demon who took on enmity with the great without heed was caught in war"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:292":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":294,"t":["பெரியாரைச் சார்ந்தார்மேல் பேதைமை கந்தாச்","சிறியார் முரண்கொண்டு ஒழுகல் - வெறியொலி","கோநாய் இனம்வெரூஉம் வெற்ப! புலம்புகின்","'தீநாய் எழுப்புமாம் எண்கு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the small, taking folly as their prop, behaving with hostility toward those who have attached themselves to the great, are, lord of the hill where the packs of wild dogs fear the noise, like the bear that by crying out rouses the fierce dog.","n":"The target here is not the great but their dependents (பெரியாரைச் சார்ந்தார்), and the danger lies in waking the protector. எண்கு is the bear; புலம்புகின் is read as its crying out, though it may mean when it is alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","pedhaimai","perumai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 292","lb":"PZ292","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தீநாய் எழுப்புமாம் எண்கு","the bear rouses the fierce dog","attacking those under a great man's protection wakes the great man against oneself","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the small who provoke the dependents of the great"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:293":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":295,"t":["இகலின் வலியாரை எள்ளி எளியார்","இகலின் எதிர்நிற்றல் ஏதம் - அகலப்போய்","என்செய்தே யாயினும் உய்ந்தீக 'சாவாதான்","முன்கை வளையும் தொடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says it is a danger for the weak to scorn those strong in strife and stand against them, and bids them go far off and survive by whatever means, for the man who does not die will yet touch the bangle on the forearm.","n":"The counsel puts survival above standing: என்செய்தே யாயினும், by doing anything at all. The proverb's bangle is most plausibly the joys of later life (the embrace of a bangled arm), held out to the one who lives, so retreat is presented as a gain deferred, not a disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","igal","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 293","lb":"PZ293","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சாவாதான் முன்கை வளையும் தொடும்","the one who does not die will touch the bangle on the forearm","the weak should escape a strong enemy by any means, since the living may yet prosper","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the weak man who flees a stronger foe"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:294":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":296,"t":["வென்றடு நிற்பாறை வெப்பித் தவர்காய்வ(து)","ஒன்றொடு நின்று சிறியார் பலசெய்தல்","குன்றொடு தேன்கலாம் வெற்ப! அதுபெரிதும்","'நன்றொடு வந்ததொன் றன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the small, standing with a single resource, doing many things to inflame those who stand victorious, is, lord of the hill where the bees war with the mountain, a thing that by no means comes out well.","n":"The image is of the bees that sting the cliff on which they hang their hives: an attack that cannot hurt its target. ஒன்றொடு நின்று is read as the small man's single strength against the victor's many; line 1 is likely corrupt (நிற்பாறை for நிற்பாரை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","pagai","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 294","lb":"PZ294","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நன்றொடு வந்ததொன் றன்று","it is not a thing that came with good","provoking the proven strong ends in harm to the provoker","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the small who provoke the victorious"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:295":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":297,"t":["உரைத்தவர் நாவோ பருந்தெறியா தென்று","சிலைத்தெழுந்து செம்மாப் பவரே - மலைத்தால்","இழைத்த திகவா தவரைக் கனற்றிப்","'பலிப்புறத் துண்பார் உணா'"],"p":"","r":"The verse seems to say that those who rise roaring and swagger, asking whether a kite will not peck at the tongue of whoever spoke against them, if they fight and enrage men who do not swerve from what they have resolved, become the food of those who eat behind the offering.","n":"Those who eat behind the bali offering are most plausibly the crows, so the swaggerers end as carrion. The quoted taunt in line 1 and the phrase இழைத்த திகவாதவர் (those who do not exceed what they set) are both hard; the reading follows the most likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","saakkaadu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 295","lb":"PZ295","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பலிப்புறத் துண்பார் உணா","the food of those who eat outside the offering","those who swagger against firm men end up dead","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to boasters who provoke the steadfast"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:296":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":298,"t":["தழங்குரல் வானத்துத் தண்பெயல் பெற்றால்","கிழங்குடைய வெல்லாம் முளைக்குமோர் ஆற்றால்","விழைந்தவரை வேர்சுற்றக் கொண்டொழுகல் வேண்டா","'பழம்பகை நட்பதால் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that as everything with a tuber sprouts in one way when it gets the cool rain of the thundering sky, one should not keep those one has come to favour coiled about one's roots, for old enmity never becomes friendship.","n":"The tuber image explains why old enmity does not end: it lies dormant underground and sprouts as soon as its season comes. விழைந்தவரை is read as former enemies one has come to like; வேர்சுற்ற puts them at the root, the place where a sprouting tuber does most harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 296","lb":"PZ296","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பழம்பகை நட்பதால் இல்","there is no old enmity that becomes friendship","a former enemy kept close will turn when his chance comes","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to reconciled enemies kept close"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:297":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":299,"t":["வெள்ளம் பகையெனினும் வேறிடத்தார் செய்வதென்?","கள்ளம் உடைத்தாகிச் சார்ந்த கழிநட்பு","புள்ளொலிப் பொய்கைப் புனலூர! அஃதன்றோ","'அள்ளில்லத்(து) உண்ட தனிசு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks what those who live elsewhere can do, even if a flood is the enemy, and says that a false, excessive friendship that has come close with deceit is like a debt eaten in one's own house.","n":"The contrast is distance: an enemy as great as a flood harms those far off little, while the deceitful friend at hand consumes from within, as a debt run up in the house eats the household. அள்ளில்லம் is obscure; it is read as the near house, one's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 297","lb":"PZ297","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அள்ளில்லத்(து) உண்ட தனிசு","the debt eaten in the near house","a false friend close at hand is worse than a great enemy far off","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the deceitful friend at one's side"],"ch":"pz:28"},"pz:298":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":300,"t":["இம்மைப் பழியும் மறுமைக்குப் பாவமும்","தம்மைப் பரியார் தமரா அடைந்தாரின்","செம்மைப் பகைகொண்டு சேராதார் தீயரோ?","'மைம்மைப்பின் நன்று குருடு.'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether those who take up open enmity and keep apart are worse than those who, caring nothing for a man, attach themselves to him as his own and bring him blame in this life and sin for the next, and answers that blindness is better than dim sight.","n":"செம்மைப் பகை, 'straight enmity', is set against the false kinsman: the proverb ranks a known lack (blindness) above a sight that seems to see and misleads (மைம்மை, a clouded eye). The harm of the false friend is counted in two worlds, blame here and sin hereafter, which the open enemy cannot cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai","pazhi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 298","lb":"PZ298","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மைம்மைப்பின் நன்று குருடு","blindness is better than clouded sight","an open enemy is less harmful than one who poses as one's own","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the false intimate set against the open enemy"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:299":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":301,"t":["பொருந்தா தவரைப் பொருதட்டக் கண்ணும்","இருந்தமையா ராகி இறப்ப வெகுடல்","விரிந்தருவி வீழ்தரும் வெற்ப! அதுவே","'அரிந்தரிகால் நீர்படுக்கு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To go on raging beyond measure, unsettled, even after one has fought and killed those who would not agree, is like flooding the stubble with water after the crop has been reaped.","n":"The proverb's point is waste: water given to a reaped field feeds nothing. The verse does not blame the killing; it blames the anger that outlives its object. The address வெற்ப!, 'man of the hills', is the poet's frame, not the audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 299","lb":"PZ299","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அரிந்தரிகால் நீர்படுக்கு மாறு","watering the stubble after reaping","rage after the enemy is dead is labour without fruit","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to anger that continues after the enemy is destroyed"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:300":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":302,"t":["வன்பாட் டவர்பகை கொள்ளினும் மேலாயோர்","புன்பாட் டவர்பகை கோடல் பயனின்றே","கண்பாட்ட பூங்காவிக் கானலம் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'வெண்பாட்டம் வெள்ளம் தரும்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if the great take on the enmity of men of hard strength, to take on the enmity of men of low standing brings them nothing, for even a light shower can bring a flood.","n":"The proverb seems to give the reason: a small shower can still swell to a flood, so the petty enemy can do great harm while the great man gains nothing by fighting him. வெண்பாட்டம் is taken here as a light or rainless-looking shower; if it means something else the link between counsel and proverb is less sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 300","lb":"PZ300","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வெண்பாட்டம் வெள்ளம் தரும்","a light shower brings a flood","enmity with the low profits the great nothing and may still harm them","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the great who quarrel with the petty"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:301":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":303,"t":["வாள்திற லானை வளைத்தார்கள் அஞ்ஞான்று","வீட்டிய சென்றார் விளங்கொளி - காட்டப்","பொறுவரு தன்மைகண்(டு) அஃதொழிந்தார் அஃதால்","'உருவு திருவூட்டு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"When men surrounded a man of sword-might in his day and came to kill him, he showed a shining radiance, and seeing a nature they could not bear they gave up the attempt; so form brings fortune.","n":"The verse tells an episode without naming its hero: a man hemmed in by would-be killers who is saved by the radiance he shows. Who he is cannot be fixed from the words. உருவு திருவூட்டும், 'form feeds fortune', makes bearing or presence itself a defence against enemies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vali","perumai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 301","lb":"PZ301","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உருவு திருவூட்டு மாறு","form feeds fortune","an awe-inspiring presence turns enemies away","exemplum","வாள்திற லானை","a man of sword-might surrounded by killers showed a shining radiance and they gave up"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:302":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":304,"t":["வலியாரைக் கண்டக்கால் வாய்வாளா ராகி","மெலியாரை மீதூரும் மேன்மை யுடைமை","புலிகலாம் கொள்யானைப் பூங்குன்ற நாட!","'வலியலாந் தாக்கு வலிது'"],"p":"","r":"To keep one's mouth shut on seeing the strong and to override the weak is the way of superiority, since against the strengthless an attack is strong.","n":"The proverb is hard: வலியலாந் தாக்கு may be 'the attack on those without strength' or a word-play on வலி. Read with its heading the counsel is strategic, not moral, but மேன்மை யுடைமை may also be ironic, mocking a 'superiority' that is quiet before the strong and loud before the weak. The address to the man of the hills where the elephant fights the tiger can support either reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vali","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 302","lb":"PZ302","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வலியலாந் தாக்கு வலிது","the blow against the strengthless is strong","strength is best spent on the weak and withheld from the strong","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to choosing whom to oppose"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:303":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":305,"t":["ஒன்னார் அடநின்ற போழ்தின் ஒருமகன்","தன்னை எனைத்தும் வியவற்க - துன்னினார்","நன்மை யிலராய் விடினும் நனிபலராம்","'பன்மையிற் பாடுடைய தில்'"],"p":"","r":"When enemies stand ready to kill, a single man should not trust in himself at all, for those who gather to him, even if they have no merit, are many, and nothing has more power than numbers.","n":"The verse grants the allies may be worthless (நன்மை யிலராய்) and still prefers them: in battle number outweighs quality. வியவற்க is a warning against self-admiration as much as self-reliance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","thunai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 303","lb":"PZ303","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பன்மையிற் பாடுடைய தில்","nothing has more might than numbers","in war many allies, even poor ones, outweigh one man's own prowess","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the lone man facing enemies"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:304":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":306,"t":["தன்னலி கிற்பான் தலைவரின் தானவற்குப்","பின்னலி வானைப் பெறல்வேண்டும் - என்னதூஉம்","வாய்முன்ன தாக வலிப்பினும் 'போகாதே","நாய்ப்பின்ன தாகத் தகர்'"],"p":"","r":"If one able to crush you comes against you, you must find someone who will harass him from behind, for however hard a ram presses forward with its head, it will not advance with a dog at its back.","n":"The counsel is to open a second front: the enemy is stopped not by meeting him but by threatening his rear. The proverb's quotation mark opens mid-line 3, so போகாதே belongs to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","soozhchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 304","lb":"PZ304","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["போகாதே நாய்ப்பின்ன தாகத் தகர்","a ram will not go forward with a dog behind it","a strong enemy is checked by setting another on his rear","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a stronger enemy's advance"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:305":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":307,"t":["யானும்மற்(று) இவ்விருந்த எம்முனும் ஆயக்கால்","வீரம் செயக்கிடந்த(து) இல்லென்று - கூடப்","படைமாறு கொள்ளப் பகைதூண்டல் அஃதே","'இடைநாயிற்(று) என்பிடு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"To stir up enmity so that allies take arms against each other, by making one say 'with me and this elder brother of mine here, there is nothing left for valour to do', is to throw a bone between dogs.","n":"The quoted boast is the tool of division: flattering one partner that he and his brother are enough sets them quarrelling over the glory. Who speaks the line, the plotter or the flattered ally, is not certain from the syntax; the proverb fixes the sense as sowing discord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 305","lb":"PZ305","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இடைநாயிற்(று) என்பிடு மாறு","throwing a bone in among dogs","enemies who stand together are broken by giving them something to fight over","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to dividing allied enemies"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:306":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":308,"t":["இயற்பகை வெல்குறுவான் ஏமாப்ப முன்னே","அயற்பகை தூண்டி விடுத்தோர் - நயத்தால்","கறுவழங்கிக் கைக்கெளிதாய்ச் செய்க அதுவே","'சிறுகுரங்கின் கையால் துழா'"],"p":"","r":"One who would defeat his natural enemy should, for his own safety, first incite an outside enemy against him and, by kindness, kindle that one's anger and make him easy to his hand; that is stirring with a small monkey's hand.","n":"இயற்பகை is the enemy by nature or position, அயற்பகை the stranger who is his enemy. The proverb is the image of using another creature's hand to stir what one will not touch oneself: the outside power takes the burn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 306","lb":"PZ306","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சிறுகுரங்கின் கையால் துழா","stir with a small monkey's hand","fight a natural enemy through another's hand, kept friendly by favour","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to using a third power against one's enemy"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:307":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":309,"t":["மாற்றத்தை மாற்றம் உடைத்தலால் மற்றவர்க்(கு)","ஆற்றும் பகையால் அவர்களைய - வேண்டுமே","வேற்றுமை யார்க்குமுண் டாகலான் 'ஆற்றுவான்","நூற்றுவரைக் கொன்று விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Since one word breaks another, one should remove enemies through an enmity worked against them, for division is found in everyone, and one man able to act will kill the hundred.","n":"The verse moves from speech (மாற்றத்தை மாற்றம் உடைத்தல்) to division to the proverb: because every group has its fault lines, one capable man who works them outmatches a host. நூற்றுவர், 'the hundred', may point to the hundred brothers of the epic, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 307","lb":"PZ307","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஆற்றுவான் நூற்றுவரைக் கொன்று விடும்","the able one will kill the hundred","division within a host lets one able man overcome it","named","நூற்றுவரை","a capable man destroyed a hundred"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:308":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":310,"t":["தெள்ளி யுணரும் திறனுடையார் தம்பகைக்","குள்வாழ் பகையைப் பெறுதல் உறுதியே","கள்ளினால் கள்ளறுத்தல் காண்டும் அதுவன்றோ","'முள்ளினால் முட்களையு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"For those able to discern, it is a sure gain to win over the enmity that lives inside their enemy, as we see liquor cured by liquor; is that not removing thorn with thorn?","n":"The verse gives two proverbs, one in the counsel (கள்ளினால் கள்ளறுத்தல்) and one quoted. Both make the same point: the cure of a thing is its like, so the enemy's own inner enemy is the best weapon against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","soozhchi","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 308","lb":"PZ308","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முள்ளினால் முட்களையு மாறு","removing thorns with a thorn","an enemy is undone by the enmity within his own camp","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to using the enemy's inner foe"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:309":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":311,"t":["நலிந்தொருவர் நாளும் அடுபாக்குப் புக்கால்","மெலிந்தொருவர் வீழாமைகண்டு - மலிந்தடைதல்","பூப்பிழைத்து வண்டு புடையாடும் கண்ணினாய்!","'ஏப்பிழைத்துக் காக்கொள்ளு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"When someone has gone in day after day to crush and kill another, and sees that the weakened one does not fall, to come to him in fullness of friendship is like taking to one's guard when the arrow has missed.","n":"The line is hard: மலிந்தடைதல் is taken as 'approaching warmly', and காக்கொள்ளும் as 'taking guard' after a missed shot. On this reading the failed oppressor should turn friend before the survivor turns on him. If கா is read otherwise, the proverb's image changes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 309","lb":"PZ309","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஏப்பிழைத்துக் காக்கொள்ளு மாறு","when the arrow misses, take guard","when an attack fails, make peace before the enemy recovers","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to an attacker whose victim survives"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:310":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":312,"t":["மறையா(து) இனிதுரைத்தல் மாண்பொருள் ஈதல்","அறையான் அகப்படுத்துக் கோடல் - முறையால்","நடுவணாச் சென்றவரை நன்கெறிதல் அல்லால்","'ஒடியெறியத் தீரா பகை'"],"p":"","r":"Except by speaking sweetly and openly, by giving fine wealth, by drawing men over through division, and by striking well and in due order those who have come into the middle, enmity is not ended by snapping a stick and throwing it.","n":"The verse names four means against an enemy, sweet words, gifts, division and force, in that order, with force last and 'in due order' (முறையால்). The proverb dismisses the rash gesture: an enmity does not end with a show of breaking. அறையான் is taken as 'by splitting'; it might mean 'by speech'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","inchol","eegai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 310","lb":"PZ310","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒடியெறியத் தீரா பகை","enmity does not end by breaking and throwing","enmity ends by conciliation, gifts, division and measured force, not by a gesture","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the rash way of ending enmity"],"ch":"pz:29"},"pz:311":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":313,"t":["தூக்கி அவர்வெலினும் தாம்வெலினும் வெஞ்சமத்துள்","தாக்கி எதிர்ப்படுவர் தக்கவர் அஃதன்றிக்","காப்பின் அகத்திருந்து காய்வார் மிகவுரைத்தல்","'யாப்பினுள் அட்டிய நீர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the worthy, having weighed the matter, close with the enemy in fierce battle whether the foe wins or they do, while those who sit within the guarded fort and rage with many words are compared to water poured into a யாப்பு.","n":"தூக்கி, 'having weighed', comes before the indifference to outcome: the weighing does not decide whether to fight but is the mark of the worthy who fight anyway. யாப்பு is taken as a binding or enclosure that does not hold water, so the talk runs off; the exact object is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","sol","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 311","lb":"PZ311","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["யாப்பினுள் அட்டிய நீர்","water poured into a binding (an enclosure that does not hold it)","big talk from safety is wasted where the worthy go out and fight","general","","no story; talk of rage from behind the walls runs away like water poured into what cannot hold it"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:312":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":314,"t":["உற்றால் இறைவற்(கு) உடம்பு கொடுக்கிற்பான்","மற்றவற்(கு) ஒன்னாரோ(டு) ஒன்றுமோ - தெற்ற","முரண்கொண்டு மாறாய உண்ணுமோ 'உண்ணா","இரண்டேறு ஒருதுறையுள் நீர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether one who would give his body for his king when need comes could ever join that king's enemies, and answers with the proverb that two bulls in open enmity do not drink at one ford.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","mannar","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 312","lb":"PZ312","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உண்ணா இரண்டேறு ஒருதுறையுள் நீர்","two bulls do not drink water at one ford","the loyal soldier and his king's enemy cannot meet on common ground","general","","no story; two bulls set against each other will not share a watering place"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:313":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":315,"t":["ஆற்ற வினைசெய்தார் நிற்பப் பலவுரைத்து","ஆற்றாதார் வேந்தனை நோவது சேற்றுள்","வழாஅமைக் காத்தோம்பி 'வாங்கும் எருதாங்கு","எழாஅமைச் சாக்கா டெழல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when those who did great deeds stand silent and those who did nothing talk at length and blame the king, it is like the ox that, guarded from slipping, pulls the load out of the mud, while the dead weight rises where it does not.","n":"The proverb's syntax is hard: வாங்கும் எருது (the ox that pulls) stands against சாக்காடு, read here as the useless, dead-weight beast, with எழாஅமை and எழல் set against each other. The general sense, that the doer is silent and the idle make the noise, is clear from lines 1-2; the image is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","sol","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 313","lb":"PZ313","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வாங்கும் எருதாங்கு எழாஅமைச் சாக்கா டெழல்","where the pulling ox does not rise up, the dead weight rises","the silent doer against the complaining idler who blames the king","general","","no story; an ox drawing a cart from the mud and a useless beast set side by side"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:314":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":316,"t":["தாரேற்ற நீண்மார்பின் தம்இறைவன் நோக்கியக்கால்","போரேற்றும் என்பார் பொதுவாக்கல் வேண்டுமோ?","யார்மேற்றாக் கொள்ளினும் கொண்டீக காணுங்கால்","'ஊர்மேற்ற தாம்அமணர்க்(கு) ஓடு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks whether those who say 'we will take the battle' when their garlanded king looks at them need make the charge a common one, and says that whoever it falls to should take it up and give it, as the town is charged with the begging bowl of the Jain monks.","n":"அமணர் are Jain ascetics, and the proverb assumes that the whole town feeds them, each house in turn. The application is unclear: it may mean that a battle pledged in the king's sight is everyone's charge, as the monks' bowl is the town's, or that no one may push it onto the common stock. The reading follows the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","mannar","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 314","lb":"PZ314","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஊர்மேற்ற தாம்அமணர்க்(கு) ஓடு","the Jain monks' bowl is the charge of the town","a pledge made before the king is a duty for whoever it falls on","general","","no story; the alms-bowl of the Jain monks, which the townspeople fill"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:315":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":317,"t":["செம்மாந்து செல்லும் செறுநரை அட்டவர்","தம்மேற் புகழ்பிறர் பாராட்டத் - தம்மேற்றாம்","வீரஞ்சொல் லாமையே வீழ்க களிப்பினும்","'சோரம் பொதியாத வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who killed the strutting foe should, while others praise them, never speak of their own valour even in elation, closing with the proverb of theft that is not bundled up.","n":"The praise is to come from பிறர், others; the warrior's own word about himself is what is to fall. சோரம் பொதியாதவாறு is read as stolen goods that cannot be wrapped and hidden, so the deed shows itself; it could also mean that self-praise exposes a man as a theft does. Either way the claim is that valour needs no telling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 315","lb":"PZ315","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சோரம் பொதியாத வாறு","as a theft is not wrapped up","the warrior's deed speaks without his boasting","general","","no story; something stolen that cannot be bundled out of sight"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:316":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":318,"t":["உரைத்தாரை மீதுரா மீக்கூற்றம் பல்லி","நெரித்த சினைபோலும் நீளிரும் புன்னைப்","பொரிப்பூ இதழுறைக்கும் பொங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'நரிக்கூஉக் கடற்கெய்தா வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Addressed to the lord of the surging shore where the long dark punnai drops puffed flowers like crushed lizard eggs, the verse says the taunting talk of those who spoke does not overwhelm the great, as a jackal's howl does not reach the sea.","n":"Line 1 is compressed: உரைத்தாரை மீதூரா மீக்கூற்றம் is read as the loud claims of talkers that do not rise above their object. The shore address is the akam style used for a counsel of war.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sol","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 316","lb":"PZ316","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நரிக்கூஉக் கடற்கெய்தா வாறு","as a jackal's howl does not reach the sea","the noise of small men does not touch the great","general","","no story; a jackal howling at the sea"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:317":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":319,"t":["அமர்நின்ற போழ்தின்கண் ஆற்றுவா ரேனும்","நிகரின்றி மேல்விடுதல் ஏதம் - நிகரின்றி","வில்லொடுநே ரொத்த புருவத்தாய்! அஃதன்றோ","'கல்லொடு கையெறியு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Addressed to a woman with bow-like brows, the verse says that even when men are able, to rush upon the enemy in battle without being their match is harm, like striking the hand against a stone.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","padai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 317","lb":"PZ317","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கல்லொடு கையெறியு மாறு","like striking the hand on a stone","attacking an unequal foe hurts the attacker","general","","no story; a hand struck against a stone"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:318":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":320,"t":["வரைபுரை வேழத்த வன்பகையென் றஞ்சா","உரைநடை மன்னருள் புக்காங்(கு) அவையுள்","நிரையுரைத்துப் போகாதொன் றாற்றத் துணிக","'திரையவித்து ஆடார் கடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who has entered the assembly of kings should not take fright because the strong enemy has mountain-like elephants, nor talk in turn and leave, but resolve to do one thing, for no one stills the waves before bathing in the sea.","n":"உரைநடை மன்னருள் is read as the council where kings talk; நிரையுரைத்து is speaking one after another. The proverb removes the excuse of waiting for the danger to pass.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 318","lb":"PZ318","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["திரையவித்து ஆடார் கடல்","no one bathes in the sea after stilling its waves","act despite the strong enemy instead of waiting for safety","general","","no story; bathers do not wait for the sea to fall calm"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:319":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":321,"t":["காத்தாற்று நிற்பாரைக் கண்டால் எதிருரையார்","பார்த்தாற்றா தாரைப் பரியாது மீதூர்தல்","யாத்ததே சில்லார் படையாண்மை 'நாவிதன்வாள்","சேப்பிலைக்குக் கூர்த்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that petty men's soldiering is to say nothing against those who stand guard and are able, and to ride pitilessly over those who cannot resist, like a barber's razor sharpened only to cut a colocasia leaf.","n":"சில்லார், 'the few' or 'the small', names a kind of man, not a number. சேப்பிலை is read as the soft leaf of the சேம்பு plant: the edge is real but spent on what offers no resistance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","sirumai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 319","lb":"PZ319","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாவிதன்வாள் சேப்பிலைக்குக் கூர்த்து விடல்","sharpening a barber's razor for a colocasia leaf","the courage of the petty is spent only on the weak","general","","no story; a razor honed and then used on a soft leaf"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:320":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":322,"t":["இஞ்சி அடைத்துவைத்(து) ஏமாந்(து) இருப்பினும்","அஞ்சி அகப்படுவார் ஆற்றாதார் - அஞ்சி","இருள்புக் கிருப்பினும் மெய்யே வெரூஉம்புள்","'இருளின் இருந்தும் வெளி'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the incapable, though they shut themselves behind a fortress wall and feel secure, are caught through their fear, as a frightened bird is startled in its very body though it hides in the dark, being in the open though in darkness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","aran","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 320","lb":"PZ320","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இருளின் இருந்தும் வெளி","in the open though in darkness","walls do not hide or save the fearful","general","","no story; a timid bird that shows itself by its own start even in the dark"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:321":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":323,"t":["உருத்தெழு ஞாட்பினுள் ஒன்னார் தொலையச்","செருக்கினால் செய்கல்லார் செய்வாரே போலத்","தருக்கினால் தம் இறைவன் கூழுண் பவரே","'கருக்கினால் கூறைகொள் வார்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who cannot with spirit make the enemy perish in the raging battle, yet with arrogance eat their king's food as if they were doers, are those who take cloth with a கருக்கு.","n":"The proverb is obscure: கருக்கு may be a jagged edge (the serrated stalk of the palmyra) or the dusk. Read as getting cloth by tearing at it or taking it in the dark, the image is of gain by false or rough means; the counsel, eating the king's food without doing his work, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","mannar","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 321","lb":"PZ321","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கருக்கினால் கூறைகொள் வார்","those who get cloth with a கருக்கு","drawing the king's pay on a show of deeds not done","general","","no story; a man who gets cloth by an improper means"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:322":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":324,"t":["அமர்விலங்கி ஆற்ற அறியவும் பட்டார்","எமர்மேலை இன்னரால் யார்க்குரைத்தும் என்று","தமர்மறையால் கூழுண்டு சேறல் அதுவே","'மகன்மறையாத் தாய்வாழு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who turned aside from battle and were well known to have done so, saying 'our people were such men before; to whom shall we tell it', and live on food under cover of their kin's name, are like a mother who lives hidden behind her son.","n":"எமர் மேலை இன்னரால் is read as an appeal to the ancestors' deeds; தமர் மறையால் is shelter behind one's kin. The proverb inverts the proper order: the mother should be the son's cover, not the son the mother's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 322","lb":"PZ322","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மகன்மறையாத் தாய்வாழு மாறு","as a mother lives with her son as her cover","the coward living on his family's earned name","general","","no story; a mother sheltering behind her son"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:323":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":325,"t":["உறுகண் பலவும் உணராமை கந்தாத்","தறுகண்மை ஆகாதாம் பேதை- தறுகண்","பொறிப்பட்ட வாறல்லால் பூணாதென் றெண்ணி","'அறிவச்சம் ஆற்றப் பெரிது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the fool's daring, propped on his failure to perceive the many dangers, is not true daring, and that daring holds only as it is ingrained, closing with the proverb that the fear that comes of knowing is very great.","n":"பொறிப்பட்டவாறு can be 'as it is stamped in' (by nature or by fate); the verse seems to say courage cannot be put on by ignorance. The proverb then admits that knowledge brings fear, so true courage is the knowing man's, holding against a fear he feels.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","acham","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 323","lb":"PZ323","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறிவச்சம் ஆற்றப் பெரிது","the fear of knowing is very great","the courage of ignorance is not courage","general","","no story; the proverb states the fear that understanding brings"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:324":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":326,"t":["தன்னின் வலியானைத் தானுடையன் அல்லாதான்","என்ன குறையன் இளையரால்? மன்னும்","புலியிற் பெருந்திறல வாயினும் 'பூசை","எலியில் வழிப்பெறா பால்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks what want of young warriors one has who has no one stronger than himself to face, and answers that cats, though mightier than tigers, get no milk where there are no rats.","n":"தன்னின் வலியானைத் தானுடையன் அல்லாதான் is read as the king with no stronger foe; he feels no need of warriors, so their worth goes unrewarded. புலியிற் பெருந்திறல is the warriors' mightiness, said of the cats with irony.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vali","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 324","lb":"PZ324","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பூசை எலியில் வழிப்பெறா பால்","cats get no milk where there are no rats","warriors are valued only while there is an enemy","general","","no story; household cats are fed while they have rats to catch"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:325":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":327,"t":["கொடையும் ஒழுக்கமும் கோளுள் ளுணர்வும்","உடையர் எனப்பட்டு ஒழுகிப் பகைவர்","உடையமேற் செல்கிற்கும் ஊற்றம் இலாதார்","'படையின் படைத்தகைமை நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says of those who are reputed to have generosity, conduct and grasping intelligence but lack the strength to march on the enemy's possessions, that the bearing of an army is better than an army.","n":"The proverb may be praise or irony. Read with lines 1-3, such men serve only as the look of a force, its படைத்தகைமை, not its striking power; it is uncertain whether that is meant as their use or their shame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","ookkam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 325","lb":"PZ325","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["படையின் படைத்தகைமை நன்று","an army's bearing is better than the army","men of good name but no drive to attack serve only as display","general","","no story; the proverb weighs an army's appearance against its strength"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:326":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":328,"t":["இருகயல் உண்கண் இளையவளை வேந்தன்","தருகென்றாற் றன்னையரும் நேரார் - செருவறைந்து","பாழித்தோள் வட்டித்தார் காண்பாம் இனிதல்லால்","'வாழைக்காய் உப்புறைத்தல் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if the king demands the young girl with eyes like two carp, even her brothers will not consent, and there is nothing but the sweet sight of them swinging their mighty shoulders as they declare battle, for no one salts a raw plantain.","n":"This is the Sangam theme of a girl refused to a king, and war that follows, told here as a certainty with a kitchen proverb. தன்னையர் are the girl's elder brothers, not her father. The proverb is read as 'there is no other treatment for it'; the exact sense of salting a plantain is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["magatpaal","maram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 326","lb":"PZ326","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வாழைக்காய் உப்புறைத்தல் இல்","no one rubs salt into a raw plantain","war is the only answer when a king demands a daughter of a proud house","general","","no story; a kitchen practice that is not done"],"ch":"pz:30"},"pz:327":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":329,"t":["நாணின்றி ஆகாது பெண்மை நயமிகு","ஊணின்றி ஆகாது உயிர்வாழ்க்கை - பேணுங்கால்","கைத்தின்றி ஆகா கருமங்கள் காரிகையாய்!","'வித்தின்றிச் சம்பிரதம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, to a woman, that womanhood of great charm is not without modesty, the life of the body is not without food, and the household's undertakings cannot be carried out without money, and clinches it with the proverb that there is no conjuring trick without a seed.","n":"The first two clauses are given as plain preconditions so that the third, money for the work of the house, takes their certainty. சம்பிரதம் is a juggler's or conjuror's feat, most likely the trick of making a plant spring up, which still needs a real seed: even the marvel has a material cause. The line on modesty is a statement of the period about what makes a woman, not a counsel the reading endorses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","ilvaazhkkai","naanam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 327","lb":"PZ327","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வித்தின்றிச் சம்பிரதம் இல்","there is no conjuring feat without a seed","the work of the household needs money as its seed","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to any undertaking that seems to come from nothing"],"nr":[["gender"],"a norm of the period that a woman's womanhood requires modesty"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:328":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":330,"t":["உரிமைதனில் தம்மோடு உழந்தமை கண்டு","பிரிவின்றிப் போற்றப் படுவார் - திரிவின்றித்","தாம்பெற் றதனால் உவவார் 'பெரிதகழின்","பாம்புகாண் பாரும் உடைத்து'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that wives who have shared the toil of the household with their husbands are, once this is seen, to be cherished without parting, and that men who are not content with what they themselves have obtained and keep reaching for more are like those who dig too deep and come upon a snake.","n":"உரிமை is the wife's right place in the house, and உழந்தமை is labour shared there, so her claim to be kept is earned by toil. Who 'does not rejoice in what they got' is not stated; read with the proverb it is most likely the husband who looks past the wife he has, and over-digging finds harm, but it could be read of anyone discontented with his lot.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 328","lb":"PZ328","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெரிதகழின் பாம்புகாண் பாரும் உடைத்து","if one digs too deep, there are some who see a snake","discontent with one's own lot and partner turns up harm","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the man who seeks beyond what he has"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:329":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":331,"t":["அகத்தால் அழிவு பெரிதாயக் கண்ணும்","புறத்தால் பொலிவுறல் வேண்டும் - எனைத்தும்","படுக்கை இலராயக் கண்ணும் 'உடுத்தாரை","உண்டி வினவுவார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that even when the ruin within the house is great one should keep a bright face outside, and even when one has no bed at all, since nobody asks those who are well dressed whether they have eaten.","n":"The proverb is an observation about how the world reads people, not a moral claim: the clothed are presumed fed. The counsel is the household's dignity before others, and it accepts concealment of want as part of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 329","lb":"PZ329","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உடுத்தாரை உண்டி வினவுவார் இல்","no one asks those who are clothed about their food","keeping one's outward state hides an inner want","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the impoverished house that keeps up its outer show"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:330":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":332,"t":["சொல்லாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறியும் பண்பில்தம்","இல்லாளே வந்த விரும்தோம்பிச் - செல்வத்து","இடரின்றி ஏமாந் திருந்தாரே 'நாளும்","கடலுள் துலாம்பண்ணி னார்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those whose wife, by nature reading the unspoken sign, tends the guests who come and who live secure in their wealth without trouble have, as it were, set up a water-lift in the sea every day.","n":"The wife acts on what the husband has not said (சொல்லாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறியும்), so the hospitality is hers. துலாம் here is the counterpoised pole that lifts water from a well; set up in the sea it never runs dry. Line 2 reads விரும்தோம்பி, a slip for விருந்தோம்பி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vaazhkkaithunai","kurippu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 330","lb":"PZ330","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடலுள் துலாம்பண்ணி னார்","they have set up a water-lift in the sea","such a house draws from a store that does not fail","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the house whose wife tends guests"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:331":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":333,"t":["எந்நெறி யானும் இறைவன்தன் மக்களைச்","செந்நெறிமேல் நிற்பச் செயல்வேண்டும் - அந்நெறி","மான்சேர்ந்த நோக்கினாய்! - ஆங்க 'அணங்காகும்","தான்செய்த பாவை தனக்கு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, to a deer-eyed woman, that by whatever means the head of the house must make his children stand on the right path, for the doll one has made oneself becomes a thing that afflicts its maker.","n":"இறைவன் here is the master of the house, the father, not a god or a king. அணங்கு can be a goddess or an afflicting spirit; with the warning of the first lines it is most likely the latter, so the child one formed carelessly becomes one's own torment, but the proverb could also say that what one shapes well becomes one's object of worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ozhukkam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 331","lb":"PZ331","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அணங்காகும் தான்செய்த பாவை தனக்கு","the doll one made becomes an afflicting spirit to oneself","a father answers for the children he shapes","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the parent and the child he forms"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:332":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":334,"t":["ஒக்கும் வகையான் உடன்பொருள் சூதின்கண்","பக்கத் தொருவன் ஒருவன்பாற் பட்டிருக்கும்","மிக்க சிறப்பின ராயினும் 'தாயார்க்கு","மக்களுள் பக்கமோ வேறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that at dice, though the wealth staked on both sides is equal, an onlooker at the side leans to one player, and that so even among children who are all of great worth a mother has one she favours, asking whether mothers' sides among their children are any different.","n":"The proverb is a question; read with the dice image it grants that partiality is natural even where merit is equal, rather than condemning it. The first line's ஒக்கும் வகையான் உடன்பொருள் is compressed and the grammar of line 2 is loose, so the dice scene is reconstructed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","soodhu","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 332","lb":"PZ332","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தாயார்க்கு மக்களுள் பக்கமோ வேறு","is a mother's leaning among her children anything other?","favour among equals is natural, even to a mother","exemplum","","the bystander at a game of dice who takes one player's side though the stakes are equal"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:333":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":335,"t":["தொடித்தோள் மடவார் மருமந்தன் ஆகம்","மடுத்(து)அவர் மார்பில் மகிழ்நன் மடுப்ப","நெறியல்ல சொல்லல்நீ பாண! - 'அறிதுயில்","யார்க்கும் எழுப்பல் அரிது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse has a wife tell the bard that while her husband presses his body to the breasts of bangled young women he should not speak what is not the way, since no one can wake a man who only feigns sleep.","n":"This is an akam situation set under the household heading: the bard comes to plead for the husband who keeps courtesans. The proverb of feigned sleep is most likely said of the husband, who strays knowingly and cannot be reformed by words, though it may also be her refusal to be taken in by the bard's excuses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 333","lb":"PZ333","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறிதுயில் யார்க்கும் எழுப்பல் அரிது","one who feigns sleep is hard for anyone to wake","a man who strays by choice will not be turned by pleading","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the husband who goes to courtesans knowing what he does"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:334":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":336,"t":["விழும்இழை நல்லார் வெருள்பிணைபோல் நோக்கம்","கெழுமிய நாணை மறைக்கும் - தொழுதையுள்","மாலையும் மாலை மறுக்குறுத்தாள் அஃதால்","'சால்பினைச் சால்பறுக்கு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the startled-doe gaze of finely jewelled women hides the modesty that lives in it, as a garland spoils another garland, and closes that this is how excellence cuts excellence.","n":"The sense is that one beauty, the frightened glance, covers another, the modesty, so that a grace is lost to sight by a grace; but line 3 (தொழுதையுள் மாலையும் மாலை மறுக்குறுத்தாள்) is obscure, and both who 'spoiled the garland' and what the garland stands for are guesses. The counsel has little to do with the household heading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 334","lb":"PZ334","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சால்பினைச் சால்பறுக்கு மாறு","the way excellence cuts excellence","one grace may conceal another","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a woman's glance that hides her modesty"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:335":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":337,"t":["தூய்மை மனத்தவர் தோழர் மனையகத்தும்","தாமே தமியர் புகல்வேண்டா - தீமையான்","ஊர்மிகின் இல்லை கரியோ ஒலித்துடன்","'நீர்மிகின் இல்லை சிறை'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that even men of pure mind should not enter alone even the house of a friend, because when the town is loud with talk of wrong no witness can stand, as no dam holds when the water rises.","n":"The innocence of the man is granted: the point is that the town's clamour is a flood, and a pure mind is no defence against it. கரி is a witness; the line says that once rumour rises there is none who can testify to the truth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","alar","natpu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 335","lb":"PZ335","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீர்மிகின் இல்லை சிறை","when the water rises there is no dam","avoid the occasion, for a rumour once loosed cannot be checked","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to rumour overwhelming the innocent"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:336":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":338,"t":["நிறையான் மிகுநல்லா நேரிழை யாரைச்","சிறையான் அகப்படுத்தல் ஆகா - அறையோ","வருந்த வலிதினின் யாப்பினும் 'நாய்வால்","திருந்துதல் என்றுமோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that women of fine jewels who excel by their own restraint cannot be held by confinement, and that one without it, though bound by force until she suffers, is like a dog's tail, which never comes straight.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 and the proverb face two ways: the woman with நிறை needs no prison, and the one without is not changed by it. The verse keeps the assumption that women's conduct is a matter for guarding and says only that force cannot do it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 336","lb":"PZ336","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்வால் திருந்துதல் என்றுமோ இல்","a dog's tail never becomes straight","confinement neither makes nor mends a woman's restraint","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the woman without restraint whom force cannot amend"],"nr":[["gender"],"a norm of the period on guarding women's chastity by confinement"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:337":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":339,"t":["நல்கூர்ந் தவர்க்கு நனிபெரியர் ஆயினார்","செல்விருந் தாகிச் செலவேண்டா - ஓல்வது","இறந்தவர் செய்யும் வருத்தம் 'குருவி","குறங்கறுப்பச் சோரும் குடர்.'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who have grown very great should not go as passing guests to the poor, for the strain of those who give beyond their means is like a sparrow cutting its own thigh, whose entrails faint.","n":"The duty is laid on the guest, not the host: the poor man will feed a great guest beyond his means, so the great should spare him. குறங்கு is the thigh; the sparrow that cuts its own flesh to feed others (perhaps its young) has so little that it collapses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inmai","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 337","lb":"PZ337","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குருவி குறங்கறுப்பச் சோரும் குடர்","when the sparrow cuts its thigh, its entrails faint","a great guest ruins the poor host who must receive him","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the poor host who gives more than he has"],"ch":"pz:31"},"pz:338":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":340,"t":["உடுக்கை மருந்துறையுள் உண்டியோ(டு) இன்ன","கொடுத்துக் குறைதீர்த்தல் ஆற்றி - விடுத்தின்சொல்","ஈயாமை என்ப 'எருமை எறிந்தொருவர்","காயக்கு லோபிக்கும் ஆறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that to give clothing, medicine, shelter, food and the like and relieve a man's want, and then to send him off without a sweet word, is like killing a buffalo for a feast and being stingy over the spice.","n":"The costly things are all given; what is withheld is the one thing that costs nothing, the word at parting (விடுத்து). The proverb's காயம் is the seasoning, cheap beside the slaughtered buffalo, so the meanness is measured by how little was saved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 338","lb":"PZ338","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எருமை எறிந்தொருவர் காயக்கு லோபிக்கும் ஆறு","a man who has slaughtered a buffalo grudges the spice","a large gift spoiled by withholding a sweet word when the receiver is sent off","general","","no story; the proverb's feast-giver kills the buffalo and then stints on the seasoning"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:339":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":341,"t":["தத்தமக்குக் கொண்ட குறியே தவமல்ல","செத்துக சாந்து படுக்கமனன் - ஒத்துச்","சகத்தனாய் நின்றொழுகும் சால்பு தவமே","'நுகத்துப் பகலாணி போன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that the marks each sect takes for itself are not penance, and that penance is the worth of one who keeps an even mind whether he is hacked or smeared with sandal paste and so lives on in the world, like the middle pin of a yoke.","n":"The middle pin (பகலாணி) holds the yoke balanced between the two oxen, so the image is of a mind that leans to neither the wound nor the perfume. The verse sits under இல்வாழ்க்கை but its counsel is about penance; the heading does not fit it. செத்துக and சகத்தனாய் are odd forms, and the sense of line 2 is read from the pairing of cutting and sandal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vesham","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 339","lb":"PZ339","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நுகத்துப் பகலாணி போன்று","like the middle pin of a yoke","true penance as an equal mind toward harm and honour, not the sect's outward mark","general","","no story; the yoke-pin stays at the centre whichever way the oxen pull"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:340":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":342,"t":["உள்ள தொருவர் ஒருவர்கை வைத்தக்கால்","கொள்ளும் பொழுதே கொடுக்கதாம் - கொள்ளார்","நிலைப்பொருள் என்றதனை நீட்டித்தல் வேண்டா","'புலைப்பொருள் தங்கா வெளி'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when someone has placed a thing in one's hand, one should hand it back at the moment he comes to take it, and not put him off thinking that he will not claim it and it has become one's settled wealth, for base wealth does not stay hidden.","n":"The fault named is delay, not denial: the keeper need only stall (நீட்டித்தல்) for the deposit to begin turning into his own. புலை here qualifies wealth, the ill-kept thing, and is not a caste word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","kalavu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 340","lb":"PZ340","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["புலைப்பொருள் தங்கா வெளி","base wealth does not stay; it comes out into the open","returning a deposit promptly when it is asked for","general","","no story; wrongly held wealth neither lasts nor stays concealed"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:341":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":343,"t":["நன்றே ஒருவர்த் துணையுடைமைப் பாப்பிடுக்கண்","நண்டேயும் பார்ப்பான்கண் தீர்த்தலான் - விண்டோ யும்","குன்றகல் நன்னாட! கூறுங்கால் 'இல்லையே","ஒன்றுக்(கு) உதவாத ஒன்று'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells the man of the good land whose hills touch the sky that it is good to have a companion, since even a crab freed a brahmin from the danger of a snake, and that there is nothing which is of no use to something.","n":"The point turns on நண்டேயும், 'even a crab': the companion need not be great to save one. பார்ப்பான் names the traveller in the tale and carries no rule. The heading இல்வாழ்க்கை fits only loosely.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 341","lb":"PZ341","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இல்லையே ஒன்றுக்(கு) உதவாத ஒன்று","there is nothing that is of no use to something","having a companion, however small, against sudden danger","exemplum","பார்ப்பான்","a crab kept as a companion saved a brahmin from a snake"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:342":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":344,"t":["விடலரிய துப்புடைய வேட்கையை நீக்கிப்","படர்வரிய நன்னெறிக்கண் நின்றார் - இடருடைத்தாப்","பெற்ற விடக்கு நுகர்தல் 'கடல்நீந்திக்","கற்றடியுள் ஆழ்ந்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that for those who have put away strong desire that is hard to let go and stood on the good path that is hard to walk, to eat flesh got through another's suffering is to swim across the sea and then drown in the water of a calf's hoofprint.","n":"The sea is the craving already crossed; the hoofprint is a small appetite left over, so the ruin is the more absurd for the size of what was overcome. கற்றடி is read as the calf's hoofprint (கன்று அடி). The heading இல்வாழ்க்கை does not fit a verse addressed to renouncers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","thuravu","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 342","lb":"PZ342","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடல்நீந்திக் கற்றடியுள் ஆழ்ந்து விடல்","having swum the sea, to sink in a calf's hoofprint","the renouncer who has overcome great desire and then eats flesh","general","","no story; a swimmer crosses the ocean and drowns in a puddle"],"nr":[["diet"],"the abstention from flesh of the period, made a condition of the renouncer's path"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:343":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":345,"t":["செறலிற் கொலைபுரிந்து சேண்உவப்பார் ஆகி","அறிவின் அருள்புரிந்து செல்லார் - பிறிதின்","உயிர்செகுத்(து) ஊன்துய்த்(து) ஒழுகுதல் ஓம்பார்","'தயிர்சிதைத்து மற்றொன்(று) அடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who kill in rage and long rejoice in it, and do not walk in grace through understanding, do not refrain from killing other lives and living on their flesh, and that this is like spoiling the curd to cook something else.","n":"The order runs from rage to killing to eating: flesh-eating is the last step of a habit that began without அருள். The proverb is read as giving up the good thing at hand (the curd, grace) to make some other dish; the image is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai","arul","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 343","lb":"PZ343","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தயிர்சிதைத்து மற்றொன்(று) அடல்","to spoil the curd and cook something else","those without grace who kill and eat flesh","general","","no story; the cook ruins the curd to make another dish"],"nr":[["diet"],"the rule against killing and flesh of the period, grounded in grace"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:344":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":346,"t":["நன்கொன்(று) அறிபவர் நாழி கொடுப்பவர்க்(கு)","என்றும் உறுதியே சூழ்க எறிதிரை","சென்றுலாம் சேர்ப்ப! அதுபோல 'நீர்போயும்","ஒன்றிண்டாம் வாணிகம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells the lord of the shore where waves roll that those who know a thing well should always plan the good of the one who gives them a measure of grain, and closes with the proverb that there is no trade in which one becomes two even by going over the sea.","n":"The giver of a நாழி is the one who feeds the speaker, a patron or master. The proverb can be read two ways: that no trade, however far, doubles the capital as serving one's provider does, or that one gains nothing by seeking a richer master abroad. ஒன்றிண்டாம் is read as ஒன்றிரண்டாம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 344","lb":"PZ344","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீர்போயும் ஒன்றிண்டாம் வாணிகம் இல்","there is no trade in which one becomes two, even by crossing the water","steady care for the welfare of the one who provides","general","","no story; overseas trade does not double what is sent"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:345":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":347,"t":["தமனென்று இருநாழி ஈந்தவன் அல்லால்","நமனென்று காயினும் தான்காயான மன்னே!","அவனிவன் என்றுரைத்து எள்ளிமற் 'றியாரே","நமநெய்யை நக்கு பவர்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who gave two measures to a man as his own does not flare up even when that man rages at him like Yama, and asks who else but our own, scorning us as 'that one' and 'this one', would lick our ghee.","n":"The giver bears the kinsman's anger because the gift made him one of his own; only kin take such liberty and still eat from the house. The line division and the subject of காயினும் are uncertain, and line 2 is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","porai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 345","lb":"PZ345","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["றியாரே நமநெய்யை நக்கு பவர்","who else would lick our ghee?","bearing the rudeness of kin one has fed","general","","no story; only one's own people take freely of the household's ghee"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:346":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":348,"t":["நாடி நமரென்று நன்கு புரந்தாரைக்","கேடு பிறரோடு சூழ்தல் - கிளர்மணி","நீடுகல் வெற்ப! நினைப்பின்றித் 'தாமிருந்த","கோடு குறைத்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse tells the lord of the high hills of bright gems that to plot ruin with others against those who sought one out and cared for one well as their own is thoughtlessly to cut the branch on which one sits.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","vanjam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 346","lb":"PZ346","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தாமிருந்த கோடு குறைத்து விடல்","to cut down the branch one was sitting on","plotting with others against one's benefactor","general","","no story; the sitter saws through his own perch"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:347":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":349,"t":["பண்டின ரென்று தமரையும் தம்மையும்","கொண்ட வகையால் குறைதீர நோக்கியக்கால்","விண்டவரோ(டு) ஒன்றிப் புறனுரைப்பின் அஃதாலவ்","'உண்டஇல் தீயிடு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that when someone has looked after a man and his kin as old friends and relieved their want, to join with those who have broken with him and speak against him behind his back is like setting fire to the house where one has eaten.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","purangooral","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 347","lb":"PZ347","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உண்டஇல் தீயிடு மாறு","like setting fire to the house where one ate","backbiting a benefactor in league with his enemies","general","","no story; the guest burns his host's house"],"ch":"pz:32"},"pz:348":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":350,"t":["தமக்குற்ற தேயாகத் தம்மடைந்தார்க்(கு) உற்ற(து)","எமக்குற்ற தென்றுணரா விட்டாக்கால் என்னாம்","இமைத்தருவி பொன்வரன்றும் ஈர்ங்குன்ற நாட!","'உமிக்குற்று கைவருந்து மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"If a man does not feel that what befalls those who depend on him has befallen himself, as though it were his own, what is the use of him, for that is like pounding husk and getting only sore hands.","n":"The verse doubles the identification: the dependants' trouble must be taken as one's own (தமக்குற்றதேயாக) and spoken of as 'ours' (எமக்குற்றது). Kinship without that feeling is not merely cold but wasted effort, which the proverb makes concrete. The hill-country address is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 348","lb":"PZ348","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உமிக்குற்று கைவருந்து மாறு","like pounding chaff and only hurting one's hands","keeping dependants without sharing their trouble as one's own is fruitless toil","general","","no story; the proverb names labour that yields nothing"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:349":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":351,"t":["சேர்ந்தார் ஒருவரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகப் பட்டவர்","தீர்ந்தாராக் கொண்டு தெளியினும் - தேர்ந்தவர்க்குச்","செல்லாமைக் காணாக்கால் செல்லும்வாய் என்னுண்டாம்","'எல்லாம்பொய் அட்டூணே வாய்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if a man on whom others have come to depend trusts them as wholly his own, unless he sees that those he has chosen do not go in want, what way have they to go on, for all else is false and only cooked food is true.","n":"The syntax of lines 1 to 3 is compressed and the sense of தீர்ந்தாரா and செல்லாமை is not certain; on the reading taken here, the patron's trust in his dependants' loyalty counts for nothing unless he feeds them, which is what the proverb's அட்டூண் (food that has been cooked) clinches.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","thelivu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 349","lb":"PZ349","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எல்லாம்பொய் அட்டூணே வாய்","everything is false; only food that has been cooked is true","a bond with dependants is proved by providing for them, not by trusting them","general","","no story; the proverb sets the meal actually served against every other profession"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:350":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":352,"t":["அல்லல் ஒருவர் அடைந்தக்கால் மற்றவர்க்கு","நல்ல கிளைகள் எனப்படுவார் - நல்ல","வினைமரபின் மற்றதனை நீக்கும் அதுவே","'மனைமர மாய மருந்து'"],"p":"","r":"When someone falls into distress, those called his good kin are the ones who remove it by the way of good deeds, and that is the medicine that has become a tree in one's own house.","n":"The verse defines good kin by their act (வினைமரபின் ... நீக்கும்), not by birth; the proverb's tree growing in the house yard is a remedy that needs no search, so kin are help near at hand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 350","lb":"PZ350","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மனைமர மாய மருந்து","the medicine that has grown as a tree of the house","good kin who remove one's distress are a remedy close at hand","general","","no story; the proverb names a remedy growing at home"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:351":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":353,"t":["மெய்யா உணரின் பிறர்பிறர்க்குச் செய்வதென்?","மையார் இருங்கூந்தல் பைந்தொடி! எக்காலும்","செய்யார் எனினும் தமர்செய்வர் 'பெய்யுமாம்","பெய்யா தெனினும் மழை'"],"p":"","r":"Taken truly, what do outsiders ever do for one another; but one's own people, even if they seem never to do anything, will do it, as rain, they say, will rain even when it has held off.","n":"The contrast is between பிறர் (others) and தமர் (one's own). The proverb concedes the delay: kin, like rain, may fail for a season, but their help is certain in the end. The address to a woman is a frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 351","lb":"PZ351","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெய்யுமாம் பெய்யா தெனினும் மழை","rain, they say, will rain even if it has not rained","kin help in the end even when they seem to fail","general","","no story; the proverb rests on the certainty of rain after a dry spell"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:352":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":354,"t":["முன்னின்னார் ஆயினும் மூடும் இடர்வந்தால்","பின்னின்னார் ஆகிப் பிரியார் ஒருகுடியார்","பொன்னாச் செயினும் புகாஅர் புனலூர!","'துன்னினார் அல்லார் பிறர்'"],"p":"","r":"Those of one family, even if they were hostile before, stand behind a man and do not leave him when overwhelming trouble comes, while others will not come in even if he treats them as gold, for outsiders are not those who draw close.","n":"முன்னின்னார் (unpleasant before) and பின்னின்னார் (standing behind afterwards) are a pun on the same syllables; the loyalty is owed to shared family (ஒருகுடியார்), not to past kindness, and the outsider's failure is set against favours given him (பொன்னாச் செயினும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kudi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 352","lb":"PZ352","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["துன்னினார் அல்லார் பிறர்","others are not the ones who draw near","in trouble one's family closes in and strangers stay away","general","","no story; the proverb states the outsider's distance"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:353":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":355,"t":["உளைய உரைத்து விடினும் உறுதி","கிளைகள்வாய்க் கேட்பதே நன்றாம் - விளைவயலுள்","பூமிதித்துப் புட்கலாம் பொய்கைப் புனலூர!","'தாய்மிதித்து ஆகா முடம்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if they speak so that it hurts, it is good to hear counsel for one's good from the mouths of kin, for a mother's treading does not make her young lame.","n":"The address in line 3 (birds treading on flowers in the ripe fields) prepares the proverb's image of a mother bird treading her chicks. The hurt of kin's words is real (உளைய) but cannot cripple, because of who gives it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idithal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 353","lb":"PZ353","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தாய்மிதித்து ஆகா முடம்","a mother's treading does not make one lame","hurtful counsel from kin is to be heeded because it does no lasting harm","general","","no story; the proverb draws on a mother hen or bird treading on her young"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:354":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":356,"t":["தன்னை மதித்துத் தமரென்று கொண்டக்கால்","என்ன படினும் அவர்செய்வ செய்வதே","இன்னொலி வெற்ப! இடரென்னை 'துன்னூசி","போம்வழி போகும் இழை'"],"p":"","r":"When someone, valuing you, has taken you as his own, then whatever befalls, you do what he does, and what trouble is there in that, for the thread goes where the needle goes.","n":"Who values whom in line 1 is not fixed; the reading here takes the subject of கொண்டக்கால் as the one who accepts and the addressee as the accepted kinsman who then follows him. The address in line 3 is to a man of the hills (இன்னொலி வெற்ப).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 354","lb":"PZ354","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["துன்னூசி போம்வழி போகும் இழை","the thread goes the way the sewing needle goes","one taken as kin follows his kinsman's course whatever it costs","general","","no story; the proverb is the needle and thread"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:355":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":357,"t":["கருவினுட் கொண்டு கலந்தாரும் தம்முள்","ஒருவழி நீடும் உறைதலோ துன்பம்","பொருகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப! பூந்தா மரைமேல்","'திருவோடும் இன்னாது துச்சு'"],"p":"","r":"Even for those who mingled together in the womb, living long in one place together is painful, for sharing a dwelling is unpleasant even with Tiru who sits on the lotus.","n":"The heading praises kin, and this verse turns: brothers born of one womb (கருவினுட் கொண்டு கலந்தாரும்) should not stay long under one roof. துச்சு is taken as a shared or borrowed dwelling; the proverb's goddess of fortune makes the point that even the best companion palls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","ilvaazhkkai","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 355","lb":"PZ355","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["திருவோடும் இன்னாது துச்சு","sharing a dwelling is unpleasant even with the goddess of fortune","even brothers should not live together long","general","","no story; the proverb names Tiru on the lotus as the most welcome of housemates"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:356":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":358,"t":["பாரதத் துள்ளும் பணையம்தம் தாயமா","ஈரைம் பதின்மரும் போரெதிர்ந்(து) ஐவரோடு","ஏதில ராகி இடைவிண்டார் ஆதலால்","'காதலோ(டு) ஆடார் கவறு'"],"p":"","r":"In the Bharata too, the twice fifty, staking their inheritance, faced the five in war, became strangers to them and broke apart; therefore one does not play dice with those one loves.","n":"The verse reads the epic as a case about kin: the dice turned brothers' sons into ஏதிலர் (strangers). It does not name Dharma's loss but makes the stake their own share (தம் தாயமா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","soodhu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 356","lb":"PZ356","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["காதலோ(டு) ஆடார் கவறு","people do not play at dice with those they love","gaming among kin destroys kinship","named","ஈரைம் பதின்மரும்","the hundred staked their inheritance at dice against the five, went to war with them and became strangers"],"ch":"pz:33"},"pz:357":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":359,"t":["சிறத்த நுகர்ந்தொழுகும் செல்வம் உடையார்","அறஞ்செய்து அருளுடையர் ஆதல் - பிறங்கல்","அமையொடு வேய்கலாம் வெற்ப! அதுவே","'சுமையொடு மேல்வைப்ப மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"For those whose wealth lets them live enjoying the best, doing aram and becoming gracious is, the verse tells the lord of the hills where bamboo clashes with bamboo, only like setting something more on top of a load already carried.","n":"The proverb is read for its ease: the rich already bear wealth, and adding giving to it costs them little. It could also be read as an added weight that the rich should take on gladly; the image does not settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 357","lb":"PZ357","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சுமையொடு மேல்வைப்ப மாறு","it is the way of putting more on top along with the load","aram and grace come easily to those who already have wealth","general","","no story; the proverb's porter takes a little more on a load already borne"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:358":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":360,"t":["வைத்ததனை வைப்பென்று உணரற்க தாமதனைத்","துய்த்து வழங்கி இருபாலும் - அத்தகத்","தக்குழி நோக்கி அறஞ்செய்யின் அஃதன்றோ","'எய்ப்பினில் வைப்பென் பது'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says not to take stored wealth for a reserve, but to enjoy it, give it out on both sides and do aram looking to the fit occasion, and calls that the true reserve for the time of need.","n":"The proverb's phrase எய்ப்பினில் வைப்பு, the store kept for the time of exhaustion, is taken from the hoard and given to aram. இருபாலும் 'on both sides' may be oneself and others, or this world and the next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","selvam","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 358","lb":"PZ358","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["எய்ப்பினில் வைப்பென் பது","what is called the store for the time of exhaustion","aram done at the fit place, not the hoard, is the reserve for need","general","","no story; the proverb's saving for hard times is redefined"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:359":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":361,"t":["மல்லற் பெருஞ்செல்வம் மாண்டவர் பெற்றக்கால்","செல்வுழியும் ஏமாப்பச் செய்வதாம் - மெல்லியல்","சென்றொசிந் தொல்கு நுசுப்பினாய்! - 'பைங்கரும்பு","மென்றிருந்து பாகு செயல்'"],"p":"","r":"When the worthy come by abundant great wealth, the verse tells a slender-waisted woman, they make it a protection even for where they go after death, like one who chews the green cane and also boils it into jaggery.","n":"The proverb is read as using the cane both ways, tasting some now and turning the rest into a sweet that keeps, so wealth serves both the present and the hereafter. It might instead set chewing against boiling as waste against keeping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 359","lb":"PZ359","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பைங்கரும்பு மென்றிருந்து பாகு செயல்","chewing the green sugarcane and making jaggery of it","the worthy use great wealth so that it also protects them in the world to come","general","","no story; the proverb's cane is both eaten and turned into a sweet that lasts"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:360":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":362,"t":["ஈனுலகத் தாயின் இசைபெறூஉம் அஃதிறந்","தேனுலகத் தாயின் இனிததூஉம் - தானொருவன்","நாள்வாயும் நல்லறம் செய்வார்க்கு இரண்டுலகும்","'வேள்வாய்க் கவட்டை நெறி'"],"p":"","r":"Aram wins fame for one still in this world and is sweetness for one who has passed to the other, so for one who does good aram every day, both worlds are like a forked road whose two branches both go well.","n":"ஈனுலகு and தேனுலகு pair this world and the sweet world above. வேள்வாய்க் கவட்டை is obscure; it is read as the fork where a road parts, both ways being good.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","pugazh","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 360","lb":"PZ360","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வேள்வாய்க் கவட்டை நெறி","the path at the fork","daily aram serves in both worlds","general","","no story; the proverb's forked road leads well either way"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:361":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":363,"t":["மாய்வதன் முன்னே வகைப்பட்ட நல்வினையை","ஆய்வின்றிச் செய்யாதார் பின்னே வழிநினைந்து","நோய்காண் பொழுதின் அறஞ்செய்வார்க் காணாமை","'நாய்காணின் கற்காணா வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"Those who did not readily do the many kinds of good deeds before death, but think of the way later and turn to aram when they see illness, find nothing in it, as a man who sees a dog finds no stone to hand.","n":"ஆய்வின்றி is read as 'without hanging back'; it could also mean 'without discernment'. The proverb says the means is missing at the moment of need, not only that time is short.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","noy","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 361","lb":"PZ361","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நாய்காணின் கற்காணா வாறு","the way one does not see a stone when one sees a dog","aram put off until illness comes fails","general","","no story; the proverb's man finds no stone when the dog comes"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:362":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":364,"t":["தக்கமில் செய்கைப் பொருள்பெற்றால் அப்பொருள்","தொக்க வகையும் முதலும் அதுவானால்","மிக்க வகையால் அறஞ்செய்க எனவெகுடல்","'அக்காரம் பால்செருக்கும் ஆறு'"],"p":"","r":"When one gets wealth by blameless work and the gathered kinds and the capital are all of that, to be angered when told to do aram generously with it is like spurning milk sweetened with sugar.","n":"Line 1's தக்கமில் and line 2's தொக்க வகையும் முதலும் are hard; the sense rests on the proverb. செருக்கும் is read as refusing in pride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 362","lb":"PZ362","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அக்காரம் பால்செருக்கும் ஆறு","the way one spurns milk with sugar","resenting the call to give from honestly earned wealth refuses one's own good","general","","no story; the proverb's man spurns sweetened milk"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:363":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":365,"t":["உலப்பில் உலகத்து உறுதியை நோக்கிக்","குலைத்தடக்கி நல்லறம் கொள்ளார்க் கொளுத்தல்","மலைத்தழு(து) 'உண்ணாக் குழவியைத் தாயார்","அலைத்துப்பால் பெய்து விடல்'"],"p":"","r":"Making those who will not take up good aram take it, checking them with an eye to the lasting good in the endless world, is like mothers who shake the struggling, crying baby that will not feed and pour milk into it.","n":"The verse approves compulsion for the other's good: the unwilling are made to take aram as an infant is fed against its will. குலைத்தடக்கி is read as shaking and holding down. The quotation mark falls after மலைத்தழு(து), so the crying is outside the proverb as printed though it belongs to its image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 363","lb":"PZ363","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உண்ணாக் குழவியைத் தாயார் அலைத்துப்பால் பெய்து விடல்","mothers shaking a baby that will not feed and pouring milk into it","the unwilling should be made to take aram for their lasting good","general","","no story; the proverb's mothers force-feed a refusing infant"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:364":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":366,"t":["அறஞ்செய் பவருக்கும் அறிவுழி நோக்கித்","திறந்தெரிந்து செய்தக்கால் செல்வுழி நன்றாம்","புறஞ்செய்யச் செல்வம் பெருகும் 'அறஞ்செய்ய","அல்லவை நீங்கி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"If even those who do aram look to where it is rightly placed and give with discernment of the ways, it is good for them where they go, their wealth grows as they give it out, and when aram is done, what is not aram departs.","n":"புறஞ்செய்ய is read as giving outward to others. The proverb's அல்லவை, the things that are not good, leave when aram is done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","aaraaydhal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 364","lb":"PZ364","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அறஞ்செய்ய அல்லவை நீங்கி விடும்","when aram is done, the things that are not good leave","aram given with discernment brings good hereafter and removes evil","general","","no story; a general saying"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:365":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":367,"t":["தோற்றம் அரிதாய மக்கட் பிறப்பினால்","ஆற்றும் துணையும் அறஞ்செய்க! - மாற்றின்றி","'அஞ்சும் பிணிமூப்(பு) அருங்கூற்(று) உடனியைந்து","துஞ்ச வருமே துயக்கு'"],"p":"","r":"Since human birth is hard to come by, the verse urges aram as far as one can, for without fail dreaded disease, old age and hard Death come together, and the stupor comes that puts one to sleep.","n":"The whole of lines 3 and 4 is set as the proverb. துயக்கு and துஞ்ச make death a sleep of bewilderment rather than an event.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 365","lb":"PZ365","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["அஞ்சும் பிணிமூப்(பு) அருங்கூற்(று) உடனியைந்து துஞ்ச வருமே துயக்கு","dreaded disease, old age and hard Death join, and stupor comes to put one to sleep","the rare human birth should be used for aram before death","general","","no story; the proverb names the three that come together at life's end"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:366":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":368,"t":["பட்ட வகையால் பலரும் வருந்தாமல்","கட்டுடைத் தாகக் கருதிய நல்லறம்","முட்டுடைத் தாகி 'இடைதவிர்ந்து வீழ்தலின்","நட்டறான் ஆதலே நன்று'"],"p":"","r":"If a good aram, planned to be firmly bound so that many are not burdened by how it falls out, meets obstacles and breaks off midway, it would have been better to be one who never planted it.","n":"நட்டறான் is read as 'one who has not planted'; it could also be read as one who plants and does not cut it off, which would make the proverb a call to carry the aram through. Either way the half-done aram is the fault.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","soozhchi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 366","lb":"PZ366","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இடைதவிர்ந்து வீழ்தலின் நட்டறான் ஆதலே நன்று","rather than fall abandoned midway, better to be one who did not plant","an aram begun must be carried through, or it were better not begun","general","","no story; the proverb's planting abandoned midway"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:367":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":369,"t":["பலநாளும் ஆற்றார் எனினும் அறத்தைச்","சிலநாள் சிறந்தவற்றாற் செய்க - கலைதாங்கி","நைவது போலும் நுசுப்பினாய்! 'நல்லறம்","செய்வது செய்யாது கேள்'"],"p":"","r":"Even if one cannot do aram on many days, the verse tells a woman whose waist seems to wilt under her girdle to do it on some days with the best things, for good aram does what nothing else does.","n":"The proverb is read as 'hear: good aram does what (nothing else) does'; it could also mean that aram's doing is never undone. The counsel trades frequency for the quality of what is given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 367","lb":"PZ367","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நல்லறம் செய்வது செய்யாது கேள்","hear: good aram does what (others) do not","aram done even on a few days, with the best, is worth doing","general","","no story; a general saying addressed to the listener"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:368":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":370,"t":["நோக்கி யிருந்தார் இமைக்கும் அளவின்கண்","நோக்கப் படினும் உணங்கலைப் புட்கவரும்","போற்றிப் புறந்தந்(து) அகப்பட்ட ஒண்பொருட்கும்","'காப்பாரில் பார்ப்பார் மிகும்.'"],"p":"","r":"Birds snatch drying grain in the blink of an eye even while those watching are watching, and for bright wealth won by tending and guarding too, those who eye it are more than those who keep it.","n":"பார்ப்பார் here means those who look out for it, the covetous, set against காப்பார், keepers; it is not the birth-group word. The verse does not name aram: under this heading the lesson is that wealth will not stay kept, so it is better given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 368","lb":"PZ368","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["காப்பாரில் பார்ப்பார் மிகும்","those who watch for it outnumber those who guard","guarded wealth is still lost to the many who covet it","general","","no story; the proverb's birds that take grain from under the watcher's eye"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:369":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":371,"t":["இன்றி யமையா இருமுது மக்களைப்","பொன்றினமை கண்டும் பொருள்பொருளாக் கொள்பவோ","ஒன்றும் வகையான் அறம்செய்க 'ஊர்ந்துருளின்","குன்று வழியடுப்ப தில்'"],"p":"","r":"Having seen the two aged parents one cannot do without perish, will anyone still hold wealth to be the true thing? The verse urges aram in a fitting way, for once a thing rolls, no hill stands in the path to stop it.","n":"இருமுது மக்கள் is read as the two aged parents. The proverb is read as life rolling toward death with nothing to check it; ஊர்ந்துருளின் and குன்று வழியடுப்பது leave the image unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","selvam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 369","lb":"PZ369","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஊர்ந்துருளின் குன்று வழியடுப்ப தில்","when it rolls, no hill blocks the way","death comes unchecked, so wealth should go to aram","general","","no story; the proverb's rolling thing that nothing stops"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:370":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":372,"t":["அற்றாக நோக்கி அறத்திற்கு அருளுடைமை","முற்ற அறிந்தார் முதலறிந்தார் - தெற்ற","முதல்விட் டஃதொழிந்தோர் ஓம்பா ஒழுக்கம்","'முயல்விட்டுக் காக்கை தினல்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who know fully that grace is what aram rests on know its root; those who plainly leave that root and abandon it keep a conduct that guards nothing, like letting the hare go to eat a crow.","n":"முதல், the root or capital, makes அருள் the source of aram; the proverb ranks the forsaken thing above what is taken, the hare above the crow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 370","lb":"PZ370","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முயல்விட்டுக் காக்கை தினல்","letting go the hare and eating the crow","aram without grace gives up the root for a lesser thing","general","","no story; the proverb's hunter drops the better prey for the worse"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:371":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":373,"t":["இம்மைத் தவமும் அறமும் எனஇரண்டும்","தம்மை யுடையார் அவற்றைச் சலமொழுகல்","இம்மைப் பழியேயும் அன்றி மறுமையும்","'தம்மைத்தாம் ஆர்க்குங் கயிறு.'"],"p":"","r":"Those who have both penance and aram in this life, if they practise them falsely, earn blame not only in this life but in the next: it is a rope with which they bind themselves.","n":"சலமொழுகல், practising with deceit, is the fault; the image turns the means of merit into the means of the deceiver's own bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thavam","aram","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 371","lb":"PZ371","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தம்மைத்தாம் ஆர்க்குங் கயிறு","the rope with which one binds oneself","false penance and aram bind the false one in both worlds","general","","no story; the proverb's self-binding rope"],"ch":"pz:34"},"pz:372":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":374,"t":["சிறிய பொருள்கொடுத்துச் செய்த வினையால்","பெரிய பொருள்கருது வாரே - விரிபூ","விராஅம் புனலூர! வேண்(டு)'அயிரை விட்டு","வராஅல் வாங்கு பவர்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who give a small thing and by that act look to gain a large one are, the verse tells the lord of the river town where spread flowers mingle in the water, like anglers who throw away the small ayirai fish to haul in the varal.","n":"The verse does not call such giving wrong in so many words; the angler's image makes it plain that the gift is bait and the giving a kind of trade. Its place under the heading ஈகை implies the censure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 372","lb":"PZ372","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வேண்(டு)அயிரை விட்டு வராஅல் வாங்கு பவர்","they are those who let go the ayirai they wanted and pull in the varal","giving a little in order to get much back is fishing, not giving","general","","no story; the angler who sacrifices a small fish as bait to land a bigger one"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:373":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":375,"t":["கரப்புடையார் வைத்த கடையும் உதவா","துரப்புடைய மன்னர்க்கே துப்புரவ தல்லால்","நிரப்பிடும்பை மிக்கார்க்கு உதவஒன் றீதல்","'சுரத்திடைப் பெய்த பெயல்'"],"p":"","r":"What the concealers have laid up helps no one in the end except as a provision for kings who drive them out and seize it, while giving even one thing to help those crushed by want is rain falling in the dry wilderness.","n":"The verse sets two ends of wealth side by side: the hoard feeds the seizing king, the small gift feeds the destitute. துரப்புடைய may mean kings given to driving people out or to pursuing; either way the hoard passes to royal power.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karavu","eegai","inmai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 373","lb":"PZ373","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["சுரத்திடைப் பெய்த பெயல்","rain poured in the middle of the dry wilderness","a small gift to those in deep want is worth more than any hoard","general","","no story; rain in the wasteland stands for a gift where need is greatest"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:374":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":376,"t":["பல்லாண்டும் ஈண்டிப் பழுதாய்க் கிடந்தது","வல்லான் தெரிந்து வழங்குங்கால் - வல்லே","வளநெடிது கொண்ட(து) அறாஅது அறுமோ","'குளநெடிது கொண்டது நீர்?'"],"p":"","r":"Wealth that lay gathered over many years and idle, once a capable man gives it out with discernment, does not quickly run dry, any more than the water a tank has long held runs out.","n":"The giver must be வல்லான் and must give தெரிந்து, with discernment: the claim that giving does not exhaust wealth is made for discerning giving, not for any scattering. வல்லே (quickly) qualifies the running out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 374","lb":"PZ374","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குளநெடிது கொண்டது நீர்","water that a tank has held a long time","discerning giving from long-gathered wealth does not soon exhaust it","general","","no story; a long-filled tank stands for long-gathered wealth that giving will not soon drain"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:375":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":377,"t":["நினைத்த(து) இதுவென்றந் நீர்மையே நோக்கி","மனத்தது அறிந்தீவார் மாண்டார் - புனத்த","குடிஞை இரட்டும் குளிர்வரை நாட!","'கடிஞையில் கல்லிடுவார் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"The excellent givers are those who see what a suppliant has in mind, judging from his very manner, and give that; the verse tells the lord of cool hills where the owl hoots in the millet field that no one drops a stone into a begging bowl.","n":"The proverb is read as: what goes into the bowl must be what the bowl is for, so the gift must answer the asker's need, and the best giver reads that need before it is spoken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 375","lb":"PZ375","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கடிஞையில் கல்லிடுவார் இல்","there is no one who puts a stone into a begging bowl","a gift should fit what the asker needs, which the best giver reads unasked","general","","no story; the alms bowl takes food, not stones"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:376":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":378,"t":["கூஉய் கொடுப்பதொன் றில்லெனினும் சார்ந்தார்க்குத்","தூஉய்ப் பயின்றாரோ துன்பம் துடைக்கிற்பார்","வாய்ப்புத்தான் 'வாடியக் கண்ணும் பெருங்குதிரை","யாப்புள்வே றாகி விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"Even when they have nothing left to call people in and give, those long used to giving freely are the ones who can wipe away the distress of those who depend on them, as a great horse, even wasted, shows its difference once it is in harness.","n":"தூஉய்ப் பயின்றார் is read as those practised in scattering (giving lavishly); it could also mean those practised in purity. வாய்ப்புத்தான் before the quotation is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 376","lb":"PZ376","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["வாடியக் கண்ணும் பெருங்குதிரை யாப்புள்வே றாகி விடும்","even when it has wasted, a great horse becomes distinct in its harness","the habitual giver keeps relieving his dependents even when his means are gone","general","","no story; the thoroughbred, though thin, shows its breed once harnessed"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:377":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":379,"t":["அடுத்தொன்(று) இரந்தார்க்கொன்று ஈந்தாரைக் கொண்டார்","படுத்தேழை யாமென்று போகினும் போக","அடுத்தேறல் ஐம்பாலாய்! யாவர்க்கே யாயினும்","'கொடுத்தேழை யாயினர் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Let those who came to one who gave a thing to each who asked go off saying they have made him poor; the verse tells a woman with five-plaited hair that, for anyone at all, there is no one who has become poor by giving.","n":"கொண்டார் is read as the takers who leave thinking they have stripped the giver; it could also be his own people who complain that he has ruined them. அடுத்தேறல் in the address is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 377","lb":"PZ377","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொடுத்தேழை யாயினர் இல்","there are none who became poor by giving","giving does not impoverish the giver, whatever onlookers say","general","","no story; the proverb denies that giving impoverishes"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:378":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":380,"t":["இரப்பவர்க்(கு) ஈயக் குறைபடும் என்றெண்ணிக்","கரப்பவர் கண்டறியார் கொல்லோ - பரப்பிற்","துறைக்தோணி நின்றுலாம் தூங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","'இறைத்தோறும் ஊறும் கிணறு'"],"p":"","r":"Have those who conceal their wealth, thinking it will dwindle if they give to askers, never seen for themselves, the verse asks the lord of the shore where a boat rides at the landing on the swelling sea, that a well springs fresh each time it is drawn?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 378","lb":"PZ378","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இறைத்தோறும் ஊறும் கிணறு","the well springs up each time it is drawn","giving does not diminish wealth but renews it","general","","no story; a well refills as water is drawn from it"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:379":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":381,"t":["இரவலர் தம்வரிசை என்பார் மடவார்","கரவலராய்க் கைவண்ணம் பூண்ட - புரவலர்","சீர்வரைய ஆகுமாம் செய்கை சிறந்தனைத்தும்","'நீர்வரைய வாநீர் மலர்'"],"p":"","r":"Only the foolish say a gift goes by the rank of the suppliants; the deeds of patrons who, concealing nothing, have worn generosity as their ornament are all great in the measure of their own worth, as a water-flower rises to the measure of the water.","n":"The verse moves the measure of a gift from the receiver to the giver: வரிசை (rank, due measure) belongs to the patron's சீர், not to the suppliant's standing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 379","lb":"PZ379","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீர்வரைய வாநீர் மலர்","water flowers are bounded by the water","a gift's greatness follows the patron's worth, not the suppliant's rank","general","","no story; a water flower's height follows the depth of water"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:380":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":382,"t":["தொடுத்த பெரும்புலவன் சொற்குறை தீர","அடுத்தர என்றாற்று வாழியரோ என்றான்","தொடுத்தின்னார் என்னலோ வேண்டா 'கொடுப்பவர்","தாமறிவார் தஞ்சீர் அளவு'"],"p":"","r":"When a great poet had composed his song, a patron, to make good the want spoken in his words, said 'give to him as he asks; may you live long'; there is no need to spell out such men by name, for givers themselves know the measure of their own worth.","n":"The first two lines seem to tell a particular episode of a patron answering a poet, but neither is named; அடுத்தர and தொடுத்தின்னார் என்னலோ வேண்டா are hard to construe, so the episode is reported only as far as the words allow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 380","lb":"PZ380","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொடுப்பவர் தாமறிவார் தஞ்சீர் அளவு","givers themselves know the measure of their own worth","a true giver gives by the measure of his own worth without needing to be told","exemplum","பெரும்புலவன்","a patron answers a great poet's composition by bidding that he be given what he lacks and blessing him"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:381":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":383,"t":["மாரியொன் றின்றி வறந்திருந்த காலத்தும்","பாரி மடமகள் பாண்மகற்கு - நீருலையுள்","பொன்தந்து கொண்டு புகாவாக நல்கினாள்","'ஒன்றுறா முன்றிலோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Even in a time of drought without rain, Pari's young daughter gave a bard gold, putting it in the pot of water on the fire to serve as his food; there is no doorstep to which nothing at all comes.","n":"The proverb can be read two ways: every house, even in drought, has something to give, or every house will in time receive something. The episode of gold put in the cooking pot in place of rice is the verse's own; it does not say the daughter was poor, only that the rain had failed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 381","lb":"PZ381","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["ஒன்றுறா முன்றிலோ இல்","there is no front yard where nothing comes","giving goes on in every house, even when the means of giving fail","named","பாரி மடமகள்","in a rainless season Pari's young daughter put gold in the pot of water and gave it to a bard as his food"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:382":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":384,"t":["ஏற்றார்கட் கெல்லாம் இசைநிற்பத் தாமுடைய","மாற்றார் கொடுத்திருப்ப வள்ளன்மை - மாற்றாரை","மண்ணசுற்றிக் கொள்நிற்கும் ஆற்றலார்க்(கு) என்னரிதாம்","'பெண்பெற்றான் அஞ்சான் இழவு'"],"p":"","r":"For the strong, whose generosity makes even their foes hand over what they own so that fame stays with all who receive, and who can encircle and take their foes' land, what is hard to give? The man who has a daughter does not fear loss.","n":"The proverb's father has already reckoned with giving his daughter away, so loss holds no fear for him; the verse applies this to the conquering king, for whom wealth given is wealth that can be won again. மண்ணசுற்றிக் looks corrupt, and the first two lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 382","lb":"PZ382","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"king","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பெண்பெற்றான் அஞ்சான் இழவு","he who has begotten a girl does not fear loss","a king strong enough to take what he gives does not fear the cost of giving","general","","no story; the father of a daughter is resigned to giving her away"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:383":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":385,"t":["பயன்நோக்கா(து) ஆற்றவும் பாத்தறிவொன் றின்றி","இசைநோக்கி ஈகின்றார் ஈகை - வயமாப்போல்","ஆலித்துப் பாயும் அலைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப!","'கூலிக்குச் செய்துண்ணு மாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The giving of those who give lavishly without regard to return and without discernment, looking only to fame, is, the verse tells the lord of the cool shore where the sea leaps roaring like strong horses, the way of working for wages and eating.","n":"பயன்நோக்காது seems to clear these givers of seeking return, but the proverb turns it back: fame is their wage, so giving aimed at fame is still paid work. பாத்தறிவு, the sense that divides the deserving from the rest, is what they lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 383","lb":"PZ383","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூலிக்குச் செய்துண்ணு மாறு","the way of working for hire and eating","giving for fame is wages, not giving","general","","no story; the hired labourer eats by his wage"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:384":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":386,"t":["மறாஅ தவனும் பலரொன் றிரந்தால்","பெறாஅஅன் பேதுறுதல் எண்ணிப் - பொறாஅன்","கரந்துள்ள தூஉம் மறைக்கும் அதனால்","'இரந்தூட்குப் பன்மையோ தீது'"],"p":"","r":"Even a man who never refuses, when many ask him for the same one thing, thinks of the distress of those who will go without and, unable to bear it, hides even what he has; so for living by begging, numbers are bad.","n":"The giver's concealment here comes from pity, not meanness: he hides the one thing because he cannot bear to disappoint the rest. The proverb's counsel falls on the askers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 384","lb":"PZ384","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இரந்தூட்குப் பன்மையோ தீது","for eating by begging, many together is bad","suppliants who come in numbers make even a generous giver withhold","general","","no story; a crowd of askers defeats the one they ask"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:385":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":387,"t":["தோற்றம் பெரிய நசையினார் அந்நசை","ஆற்றா தவரை அடைந்தொழுகல் - ஆற்றுள்","கயற்புரை உண்கண் கனங்குழாய்! அஃதால்","'உயவுநெய் யுட்குளிக்கும் ஆறு'"],"p":"","r":"People of great show and great desire who attach themselves to those who cannot satisfy that desire are, the verse tells a woman with heavy earrings and kohl-lined eyes like the river's kayal, going the way of axle grease that soaks in and is gone.","n":"The image of axle grease that sinks into the wheel and is lost is read as the suppliant's hope and effort spent for nothing; தோற்றம் பெரிய may describe either the seekers' show or the patrons'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","avaa","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 385","lb":"PZ385","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["உயவுநெய் யுட்குளிக்கும் ஆறு","the way axle grease sinks in","seeking from a patron who cannot give wastes the seeker","general","","no story; grease rubbed on an axle soaks away"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:386":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":388,"t":["காப்பிகந்(து) ஓடிக் கழிபெருஞ் செல்வத்தைக்","கோப்பெரியான் கொள்ளக் கொடுத்திராதென் செய்வர்?","நீத்தப் பெரியார்க்கே யாயினும் 'மிக்கவை","மேவிற் பரிகாரம் இல்'"],"p":"","r":"When vast wealth slips its guard and runs off and a great king seizes it, what can those who never gave it away do? Even for great renouncers, when overwhelming things befall, there is no remedy.","n":"The proverb is read as saying that excess, whether of wealth or of misfortune, admits no cure, so wealth should be given before the king takes it; நீத்தப் பெரியார் widens the point to renouncers, which fits the heading only loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","arasu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 386","lb":"PZ386","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["மிக்கவை மேவிற் பரிகாரம் இல்","when excessive things arrive there is no remedy","wealth not given in time is lost past remedy","general","","no story; the hoard swept away to a seizing king"],"ch":"pz:35"},"pz:387":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":389,"t":["எண்ணக் குறைபடாச் செல்வமும் இற்பிறப்பும்","மன்னர் உடைய உடைமையும் - மன்னரால்","இன்னர் எனல்வேண்டா இம்மைக்கும் உம்மைக்கும்","'தம்மை உடைமை தலை.'"],"p":"","r":"Wealth that does not run short however it is counted, good birth and the possessions that kings hold are set aside, and so is being named by kings as men of worth: for this life and the next, the verse says, owning oneself comes first.","n":"தம்மை உடைமை, 'having oneself', is read here as self-mastery, the possession that the external goods of wealth, lineage and royal favour cannot replace; the verse ranks it above even a king's acknowledgement, the honour a court poet might value most.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","selvam","pirappu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 387","lb":"PZ387","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தம்மை உடைமை தலை","possessing oneself is the chief thing","self-mastery outranks wealth, birth and royal esteem in this world and the next","general","","no story; the proverb is set against a list of outer goods: uncounted wealth, birth, royal property and royal recognition"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:388":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":390,"t":["அடங்கி அகப்பட ஐந்தினைக் காத்துத்","தொடங்கிய மூன்றினால் மாண்டீண்டு - உடம்பொழியச்","செல்லும்வாய்க்(கு) ஏமம் சிறுகாலைச் செய்யாரே","'கொல்லிமேல் கொட்டு வைத்தார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who do not, early in life, guard the five so that they are held in check, grow excellent here by the three they have taken up, and so make a safeguard for the road taken when the body falls away, are said by the proverb to have set the drum upon the kolli.","n":"The five are most naturally the senses; the three begun may be thought, word and deed, or in a Jain frame right faith, knowledge and conduct; the verse does not say. கொல்லி is obscure: if it stands for கொள்ளி, the funeral firebrand, the proverb mocks those who beat the drum only when the pyre is lit, which fits the verse's stress on சிறுகாலை, but this is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","marumai","ilamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 388","lb":"PZ388","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கொல்லிமேல் கொட்டு வைத்தார்","they set the drum on the kolli","the safeguard for the next world must be made early, by restraint of the senses","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who leave provision for the afterlife until too late"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:389":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":391,"t":["நட்டாரை யாக்கிப் பகைதணித்து வையெயிற்றுப்","பட்டார் துடியிடை யார்ப்படர்ந்(து) - ஒட்டித்","தொடங்கினார் இல்லத்த தன்பின் 'துறவா","உடம்பினான் என்ன பயன்?'"],"p":"","r":"Once a man has made friends, quieted his enmities, gone after and joined the sharp-toothed women with waists like the drum, and set up a household, the proverb asks what use after that is a body that does not renounce.","n":"The household is not condemned: friendship, peace and marriage are listed as tasks completed, and the question falls only on what comes after them. Renunciation is cast as the use of the body, so the body that stays in the house past its work is called useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilvaazhkkai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 389","lb":"PZ389","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["துறவா உடம்பினான் என்ன பயன்","what is the use of a body that does not renounce?","after the household is complete, renunciation is the body's remaining use","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the householder who has done everything the house asks"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:390":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":392,"t":["இல்வாழ்க்கை யானும் இலதானும் மேற்கொள்ளார்","நல்வாழ்க்கை போக நடுவுனின்(று) - எல்லாம்","ஒருதலையாச் சென்று துணியா தவரே","'இருதலையும் காக்கழித் தார்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who take up neither the householder's life nor the life without a house, and stand in the middle while the good life passes, never resolving to go wholly to one side, are said by the proverb to have lost both ends of the carrying pole.","n":"The verse does not rank the two lives; it condemns only the failure to choose. இருதலையும் கா கழித்தார் pictures the carrier's pole with a load at each end: the undecided drop both, so the image makes indecision itself a loss, not a neutral pause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 390","lb":"PZ390","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["இருதலையும் காக்கழித் தார்","they have lost both ends of the carrying pole","one must commit wholly to one of the two lives, or lose both","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who hover between household and renunciation"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:391":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":393,"t":["வளமையும் தேசும் வலியும் வனப்பும்","இளமையும் இற்பிறப்பும் எல்லாம் - உளவாய்","மதித்தஞ்சி மாறுமஃ தின்மையால் 'கூற்றம்","குதித்துய்ந்து அறிவாரோ இல்'"],"p":"","r":"Wealth, lustre, strength, beauty, youth and good birth may all be present, yet because Death is not turned aside by honouring or fearing it, the proverb asks who knows of anyone who leapt over Death and survived.","n":"The construal of மதித்தஞ்சி மாறும் அஃதின்மையால் is not secure: it may mean that Death cannot be made to turn back by esteem or fear, or that the six goods give no way of escape for all that people value them. Either way the six goods are listed only to be cancelled at one stroke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","ilamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 391","lb":"PZ391","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["கூற்றம் குதித்துய்ந்து அறிவாரோ இல்","no one knows of having leapt over Death and lived","no gift of fortune or birth exempts one from Death","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who trust in wealth, youth, beauty, strength and birth"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:392":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":394,"t":["கொண்டொழுகும் மூன்றற்(கு) உதவாப் பசித்தோற்றம்","பண்டொழுகி வந்த வளமைத்(து)அங்(கு) - உண்டது","கும்பியினும் திச்சென்(று) அறிதலால் 'தன்னாசை","அம்பாயுள் புக்கு விடும்'"],"p":"","r":"The hunger that besets the body, of no help to the three aims one keeps, is as old and as fertile as anything that has come down from of old; since what is eaten is known to go into the belly and turn foul, the proverb says one's own desire becomes an arrow, enters within and destroys.","n":"The three that one keeps (கொண்டொழுகும் மூன்று) are probably aram, porul and inbam, which hunger serves none of. திச்சென்று is obscure and the reading 'turns foul' is a guess from context. அம்பாயுள் is divided here as அம்பு ஆய் உள், desire turning into an arrow against oneself; other divisions are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","avaa","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 392","lb":"PZ392","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["தன்னாசை அம்பாயுள் புக்கு விடும்","one's own desire becomes an arrow, enters within, and finishes one","desire for food harms the one who feeds it","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the craving of the belly for food that only turns to waste"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:393":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":395,"t":["செல்வத் துணையும்தம் வாழ்நாள் துணையும்தாம்","தெள்ளி உணரார் சிறிதினால் செம்மாந்து","பள்ளிப்பால் வாழார் பதிமகிழ்ந்து வாழ்வாரே","'முள்ளித்தேன் உண்ணு மவர்'"],"p":"","r":"Those who do not clearly weigh how far their wealth and their span of life will go with them, who swell with pride at a little and live delighting in the town instead of living in the hermitage, are said by the proverb to be eating the honey of the thorn bush.","n":"பள்ளி is the dwelling of renouncers, in a Jain text likely the monks' retreat, set against பதி, the town. The proverb's honey is sweet but gathered among thorns, so the pleasure of town life is not denied, only priced.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aayul","thuravu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 393","lb":"PZ393","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["முள்ளித்தேன் உண்ணு மவர்","those who eat the honey of the thorny mulli plant","pleasures taken without counting their end are sweetness bought with wounds","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the proud town-dweller who has not reckoned how long wealth and life will last"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:394":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":396,"t":["வன்னெஞ்சி னார்பின் வழிநினைந்து செல்குவை","என்னெஞ்சே! இன்றிழிவை யாயினாய் - சென்னெஞ்சே!","இல்சுட்டி நீயும் இனிதுரைத்துச் சாவாதே","'பல்கட்டப் பெண்டீர் மகார்'"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells his heart that it goes after the hard-hearted, longing for their way, and has today been brought low, and bids it go and not die, as others do, pointing to the house and speaking fondly of it, for wives and children are many bonds.","n":"The verse is an address to one's own heart, so the counsel is self-persuasion. வன்னெஞ்சினார் may be the hard-hearted world the heart longs after, or it may praise the renouncers as hard against attachment; the sense of the first two lines turns on this. கட்டம் may be a bond or a hardship; both suit the proverb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","nenju","makkal","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 394","lb":"PZ394","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"householder","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பல்கட்டப் பெண்டீர் மகார்","wives and children are many bonds","attachment to wife and children holds the heart back from release","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to the heart that clings to home as death nears"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:395":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":397,"t":["சிறந்ததம் மக்களும் செய்பொருளும் நீக்கித்","துறந்தார் தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல்? -கறங்கருவி","ஏனல்வாய் வீழும் மலைநாட? அஃதன்றோ","'யானைபோய் வால்போகா வாறு'"],"p":"","r":"The verse asks the lord of the hill country, where the roaring cascade falls on the millet field, what further tie is left for those who have renounced after giving up even their dear children and the wealth they earned, and says that clinging still would be like the elephant passing through while its tail does not.","n":"The proverb inverts the scale: the elephant (children and wealth) has gone through the gap, and it is the tail, the smallest remnant of attachment, that sticks. The verse thus says the hardest part of renunciation is not the great surrender but the trifle kept after it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 395","lb":"PZ395","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["யானைபோய் வால்போகா வாறு","the way the elephant goes through but its tail does not","a small attachment kept after a great renunciation defeats it","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to a renouncer who keeps some small tie after leaving children and wealth"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:396":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":398,"t":["எனைப்பல் பிறப்பினும் ஈண்டித்தாம் கொண்ட","வினைப்பயன் மெய்யுறுதல் அஞ்சி - எனைத்தும்","கழிப்புழி ஆற்றாமை காண்டும் அதுவே","'குழிப்புழி ஆற்றா குழிக்கு'"],"p":"","r":"Though people fear that the fruit of deeds gathered over many births will come home to the body, the verse says we see them unable to do anything at all at the place where it must be discharged, and closes with a proverb about what a pit cannot do for a pit.","n":"கழிப்புழி, 'where it is to be worked off', seems to be the present life in which the deed's fruit falls due. The proverb குழிப்புழி ஆற்றா குழிக்கு is hard to construe; it may say that what fills one hole cannot fill another, or that a pit cannot be filled from within itself. The application is therefore uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 396","lb":"PZ396","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["குழிப்புழி ஆற்றா குழிக்கு","at the pit's place, they are of no avail for the pit (sense unclear)","fear of deeds' fruit is useless without acting when the fruit comes due","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who fear karmic fruit yet do nothing when it must be worked off"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:397":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":399,"t":["திரியும் இடிஞ்சிலும் நெய்யும் சார்வாக","எரியும் சுடரே ரனைத்தாய்த் - தெரியுங்கால்","சார்வற ஓடிப் பிறப்பறுக்கும் அஃதேபோல்","'நீரற நீர்ச்சார் வறும்'"],"p":"","r":"A flame burns with the wick, the lamp-bowl and the ghee as its supports, and when one looks closely, so the self that runs free of its supports cuts off birth; the proverb says that when the water is gone, what depends on water is gone too.","n":"The lamp is an image of dependence: the flame is real but lives only on what holds it, so release is put as the end of supports, not the destruction of the self. The verse names no subject for ஓடிப் பிறப்பறுக்கும்; the self or the knower is supplied. சுடரே ரனைத்தாய் is oddly divided in the text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 397","lb":"PZ397","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["நீரற நீர்ச்சார் வறும்","when the water dries up, what relies on water dries up too","cutting the supports of attachment ends rebirth","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to birth, which ceases when its supports are removed"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:398":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":400,"t":["ஓதநீர் வேலி உலகத்தார் அந்நெறி","காதலர் என்பது அறிந்தலால் - யாதொன்றும்","கானக நாட! பயிலார் 'பயின்றது","வானகம் ஆகி விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Knowing that the people of the sea-girdled world are lovers of whatever path they follow, the verse tells the lord of the forest country that people practise nothing else, and closes with the proverb that what is practised becomes heaven.","n":"The first lines are hard to construe: அந்நெறி காதலர் may mean that people love the path they are used to, so that habit rules them, or that they love 'that path' of release. The proverb then says either that habit makes its own heaven or that steady practice of the good wins heaven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 398","lb":"PZ398","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பயின்றது வானகம் ஆகி விடும்","what one has practised becomes heaven","practice persisted in becomes the next world one reaches","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to people's attachment to the path they follow"],"ch":"pz:36"},"pz:399":{"book":"pazhamozhi_nanooru","num":401,"t":["பரந்தவர் கொள்கைமேல் பல்லாறும் ஓடார்","நிரம்பிய காட்சி நினைந்தறிந்து கொள்க","வரம்பில் பெருமை தருமே 'பரம்பூரி","என்றும் பதக்கே வரும்'"],"p":"","r":"The verse says not to run along the many paths of the widespread doctrines but to think on, understand and take up the complete vision, which gives greatness without limit, and closes with a proverb that the paramburi always comes to a pathakku.","n":"நிரம்பிய காட்சி, 'the full seeing', in a Jain text may name right faith or right vision, the first of the three jewels, against the doctrines of other schools. பரம்பூரி and பதக்கு (a grain measure) make an obscure proverb: it may say that whatever is filled heaped comes in the end to one fixed measure, so that the many ways yield one result, but this is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","samayam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pazhamozhi Nanooru 399","lb":"PZ399","poet":"முன்றுறை அரையனார்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","pz":["பரம்பூரி என்றும் பதக்கே வரும்","the paramburi always comes to a pathakku (sense unclear)","one complete vision is worth more than many scattered creeds","general","","no story; the proverb is applied to those who run after many doctrines instead of one complete vision"],"ch":"pz:36"},"sp:0":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":1,"t":["முழுதுணர்ந்து மூன்றொழித்து மூவாதான் பாதம்","பழுதின்றி ஆற்றப் பணிந்து - முழுதேத்தி","நண்பாய ஞாலத்து மாந்தர்க்(கு) உறுதியா","வெண்பா உரைப்பன் சில"],"p":"","r":"The poet bows without fault to the feet of the one who knew everything, put away the three and does not age, praises him fully, and says he will tell some venbas of firm use to the people of the world.","n":"The one praised is named only by three attributes (all-knowing, rid of the three, ageless) and by no deity's name; the verse does not say what the three are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 0","lb":"SP0","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:1":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":2,"t":["ஒத்த ஒழுக்கம் கொலைபோய் புலால்களவோ(டு)","ஒத்த இவையல வோர்நாலிட்(டு) - ஒத்த","உறுபஞ்ச மூலந்தீர் மாரிபோல் கூறீர்","சிறுபஞ்ச மூலஞ் சிறந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls on its hearers to tell the Sirupanchamoolam as a rain that ends a great famine, setting out fitting conduct and setting aside the four that are not to be done: killing, lying, meat and theft.","n":"The verse puns on பஞ்சம் (famine) inside the title's பஞ்ச மூலம். The four are read from கொலைபோய் புலால்களவோ(டு) with போய் taken as பொய் (a lie); ஓர்நால் counts these four, and ஒத்த ஒழுக்கம் is the frame they serve, not a fifth item. Lines 1-2 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kollaamai","pulaal","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 1","lb":"SP1","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலை","killing is one of the four the book sets aside",1],["போய்","lying is one of the four the book sets aside",1],["புலால்","eating meat is one of the four the book sets aside",1],["களவோ","theft is one of the four the book sets aside",1]],"cw":"வோர்நாலிட்(டு)","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:2":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":3,"t":["பொருளுடையான் கண்ணதே போகம் அறனும்","அருளுடையான் கண்ணதே ஆகும் - அருளுடையான்","செய்யான் பழிபாவம் சேரான் புறமொழியும்","உய்யான் பிறர்செவிக்கு உய்த்து"],"p":"","r":"Enjoyment lies with the man who has wealth and dharma with the man who has grace, and the man of grace does no blameworthy deed, does not come near sin and does not carry backbiting to others' ears.","n":"Divided into five: two possessions, then three refusals of the gracious man. Wealth gets one clause and grace the rest of the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam","purangooral"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 2","lb":"SP2","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["போகம்","enjoyment belongs to the man who has wealth",1],["அறனும்","dharma belongs to the man who has grace",1],["பழி","the man of grace does no deed that earns blame",3],["பாவம்","the man of grace does not come by sin",3],["புறமொழியும்","the man of grace does not carry backbiting into others' ears",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:3":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":4,"t":["கற்புடைய பெண்ணமிர்து கற்றடங்கி னான்அமிர்து","நற்புடைய நாடமிர்து நாட்டுக்கு - நற்புடைய","மேகமே சேர்கொடி வேந்தமிர்து சேவகனும்","ஆகவே செய்யின் அமிர்து"],"p":"","r":"A chaste woman, a man who has learned and is restrained, a land of good surroundings, a king whose banner reaches the clouds and who is good to his land, and a soldier who does his work as it should be done are each named amrita.","n":"Only the soldier is amrita on a condition (ஆகவே செய்யின், if he does it rightly). Line 3's மேகமே may instead name the rain cloud as a separate amrita for the land, which would make six things; we read it as the height of the king's banner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amudham","karpu","adakkam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 3","lb":"SP3","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["கற்புடைய பெண்","a chaste woman is amrita",1],["கற்றடங்கி னான்","a man who has learned and become restrained is amrita",1],["நற்புடைய நாட","a land with good surroundings is amrita",2],["மேகமே சேர்கொடி வேந்","for the land, a good king whose banner touches the clouds is amrita",3],["சேவகனும்","a soldier is amrita if he does his service as it should be done",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:4":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":5,"t":["கல்லாதான் தான்காணும் நுட்பமும் காதிரண்டும்","இல்லாதாள் ஏக்கழுத்தஞ் செய்தலும் - இல்லாதான்","ஒல்லாப் பொருளில்லார்க்கு ஈந்தளியான் என்றலும்","நல்லவர்கள் கேட்பின் நகை"],"p":"","r":"The subtlety an unlearned man thinks he has found, a proud carriage of the neck by a woman who lacks both ears, and a man with nothing saying he gives unattainable wealth to the needy and is generous are, the verse says, laughter to the good when they hear of them.","n":"Three things, not five; all three are claims that outrun what the person has, so the laughter is at pretension, not at lack. காதிரண்டும் இல்லாதாள் may mean a woman without ears or without ear ornaments.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","igal","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 4","lb":"SP4","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கல்லாதான் தான்காணும் நுட்பமும்","the fine point an unlearned man thinks he sees is laughable to the good",1],["காதிரண்டும் இல்லாதாள் ஏக்கழுத்தஞ் செய்தலும்","a woman lacking both ears holding her neck proudly is laughable",1],["இல்லாதான் ஒல்லாப் பொருளில்லார்க்கு ஈந்தளியான் என்றலும்","a man with nothing claiming to give unattainable wealth to the needy is laughable",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:5":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":6,"t":["உடம்பொழிய வேண்டின் உயர்தவம் ஆற்றீண்டு","இடம்பொழிய வேண்டுமேல் ஈகை - மடம்பொழிய","வேண்டின் அறிமடம் வேண்டேல் பிறர்மனை","யீண்டின் இயையும் திரு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who would be rid of the body should practise high penance, one who would end want here should give, one who would be rid of folly should learn, and one who does not desire another man's wife will gain fortune.","n":"Read as four counsels with fortune (திரு) as the closing reward, so four items. இடம்பொழிய and மடம்பொழிய are hard to divide, and அறிமடம் may be one word (feigned ignorance) rather than அறி plus மடம்; the third item is our best construal. Ending the body means not being born again.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","piranil","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 5","lb":"SP5","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["உயர்தவம்","high penance is the means for one who would be rid of the body",1],["ஈகை","giving is the means for one who would end want here",2],["அறி","learning is the means for one who would be rid of folly",3],["பிறர்மனை","not desiring another man's wife brings fortune",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:6":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":7,"t":["படைதனக்கு யானை வனப்பாகும் பெண்ணின்","இடைதனக்கு நுண்மை வனப்பாம் - நடைதனக்குக்","கோடா மொழிவனப்புக் கோற்(கு)அதுவே சேவகர்க்கு","வாடாதா வன்கண் வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"Elephants are the beauty of an army, slenderness the beauty of a woman's waist, unbending speech the beauty of conduct, the same unbending the beauty of the sceptre, and unwavering courage the beauty of soldiers.","n":"கோடா (not bending) is used once and carried by அதுவே to the sceptre, so truthful speech and just rule are made one quality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai","sengol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 6","lb":"SP6","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["யானை","elephants are the beauty of an army",1],["நுண்மை","slenderness is the beauty of a woman's waist",2],["கோடா மொழி","speech that does not bend is the beauty of conduct",3],["அதுவே","not bending is likewise the beauty of the king's sceptre",3],["வாடாதா வன்கண்","courage that does not wilt is the beauty of soldiers",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:7":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":8,"t":["பற்றினான் பற்றற்றான் நூல்தவசி எப்பொருளும்","முற்றினான் ஆகும் முதல்வன்நூல் - பற்றினால்","பாத்துண்பான் பார்ப்பான் பழியுணர்வான் சான்றவன்","காத்துண்பான் காணான் பிணி"],"p":"","r":"The verse defines five titles by conduct: the ascetic is one who holds to the book of the one free of attachment, the leader is one who has mastered every matter, the பார்ப்பான் is one who shares his food out of devotion, the worthy man is one who is aware of blame, and one who eats with care sees no disease.","n":"பார்ப்பான் is a word for the brahmin by birth; here the verse defines him by an act, sharing food, not by birth. Lines 1-2 are missing from the site's text, and பற்றினான் may also be read with the brahmin (holding to the primal one's book).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","andhanar","saandror","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 7","lb":"SP7","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["தவசி","the ascetic is one who holds to the book of the one free of attachment",1],["முதல்வன்","the leader is one who has mastered every matter",2],["பார்ப்பான்","the brahmin is one who shares his food out of devotion before eating",3],["சான்றவன்","the worthy man is one who is aware of what brings blame",3],["காத்துண்பான்","one who guards his eating does not meet disease",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a varna term defined by conduct: the verse names who counts as a பார்ப்பான் by sharing food"],"ch":"sp:1"},"sp:8":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":9,"t":["கண்வனப்புக் கண்ணோட்டம் கால்வனப்புச் செல்லாமை","எண்வனப்பு இத்துணையாம் என்றுரைத்தல் - பண்வனப்புக்","கேட்டார்நன்(று) என்றல் கிளர்வேந்தன் தன்நாடு","வாட்டான் நஞ்றென்றல் வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"Kindly regard is the beauty of the eye, not going where one should not the beauty of the feet, saying exactly how much the beauty of reckoning, the hearers' saying 'good' the beauty of music, and the people's saying it is good that he does not oppress his land the beauty of a great king.","n":"The last two beauties are both a judgment by others (கேட்டார் நன்று என்றல், நன்று என்றல்): the song and the king are beautiful by what listeners and subjects say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","azhagu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 8","lb":"SP8","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணோட்டம்","kindly regard is the beauty of the eye",1],["செல்லாமை","not going where one should not is the beauty of the feet",1],["இத்துணையாம் என்றுரைத்தல்","stating exactly how much is the beauty of reckoning",2],["கேட்டார்நன்(று) என்றல்","the hearers' saying 'good' is the beauty of music",3],["தன்நாடு வாட்டான் நஞ்றென்றல்","the people's praise that he does not oppress his land is the beauty of a king",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:9":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":10,"t":["கொன்றுண்பான் நாச்சாம் கொடுங்கரிபோ வான்நாச்சாம்","நன்றுணர்வார் முன்கல்லான் நாவுஞ்சாம் - ஒன்றானும்","கண்டுழி நாச்சாம் கடவான் குடிப்பிறந்தான்","உண்டுழி நாச்சாம் உணர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The tongue dies, the verse says, in one who kills and eats, in one who goes as a false witness, in the unlearned before those who know the good, in a debtor when he sees even once, and in the well-born when he has eaten and comes to know of it.","n":"The tongue's death is losing the power to speak for shame. கடவான் is read as the debtor (one who owes) facing his creditor, and the last item as the well-born man struck dumb on learning what or from whom he has eaten; both construals are ours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kallaamai","maanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 9","lb":"SP9","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கொன்றுண்பான்","the tongue of one who kills and eats dies",1],["கொடுங்கரிபோ வான்","the tongue of one who goes as a false witness dies",1],["நன்றுணர்வார் முன்கல்லான்","the tongue of the unlearned dies before those who know the good",2],["கடவான்","the tongue of a debtor dies when he sees even once",3],["குடிப்பிறந்தான்","the tongue of the well-born dies when he has eaten and comes to know it",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:10":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":11,"t":["சிலம்பிக்குத் தன்சினை கூற்றம்நீள் கோடு","விலங்கிற்குக் கூற்றம் மயிர்தான் - வலம்படா","மாவிற்குக் கூற்றமாம் ஞெண்டிற்குத் தன்பார்ப்பு","நாவிற்கு நன்றல் வசை"],"p":"","r":"Its own eggs are death to the spider, its long horns death to the beast, its hair death to the animal that cannot turn, its own young death to the crab, and words of abuse death to the tongue.","n":"Each creature is killed by something of its own; the list sets abuse beside them, so the tongue's ruin comes from itself, not from the one abused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kadunjol","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 10","lb":"SP10","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்சினை","its own eggs are death to the spider",1],["நீள்கோடு","its long horns are death to the beast",1],["மயிர்","its hair is death to the animal that cannot turn",2],["தன்பார்ப்பு","its own young are death to the crab",3],["நன்றல் வசை","abuse that is not good is death to the tongue",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:11":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":12,"t":["நாணிலான் சால்பும் நடையிலான் நன்னோன்பும்","ஊணிலான் செய்யும் உதாரதையும் - ஏணிலான்","சேவகமும் செந்தமிழ் தேற்றான் சுவிச்செயலும்","நாவகமே நாடின் நகை"],"p":"","r":"The worth of a man without shame, the vow of a man without conduct, the generosity of a man without food, the soldiering of a man without strength and the verse-making of one who does not know good Tamil are laughable, the verse says, when weighed by speech.","n":"Each item is a virtue claimed by one who lacks its ground. The fifth turns the book's ridicule on poets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","ozhukkam","igal","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 11","lb":"SP11","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நாணிலான் சால்பும்","the worth claimed by a man without shame is laughable",1],["நடையிலான் நன்னோன்பும்","the vow of a man without conduct is laughable",1],["ஊணிலான் செய்யும் உதாரதையும்","generosity done by a man who has no food is laughable",2],["ஏணிலான் சேவகமும்","soldiering by a man without strength is laughable",2],["செந்தமிழ் தேற்றான் சுவிச்செயலும்","verse-making by one who does not know good Tamil is laughable",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:12":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":13,"t":["கோறலும் நஞ்சூனைத் துய்த்தல் கொடுநஞ்சு","வேறலும் நஞ்சுமா(று) அல்லானைத் - தேறினான்","நீடாங்கு செய்தலும் நஞ்சாம் இளங்கிளியை","நாடாதே தீதுரையும் நஞ்சு"],"p":"","r":"Killing is poison, enjoying meat is a cruel poison, overcoming one who is no match is poison, harming at length one who trusted is poison, and speaking ill of a young girl without inquiry is poison.","n":"Only eating meat gets the stronger கொடுநஞ்சு, so the verse ranks eating above killing. நஞ்சூனைத் fuses நஞ்சு and ஊனை. மா(று) அல்லானை is read as 'one who is no match'; நீடாங்கு செய்தல் and இளங்கிளியை (a young parrot, or a girl addressed as one) are doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","vanjam","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 12","lb":"SP12","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கோறலும்","killing is poison",1],["துய்த்தல்","enjoying meat is a cruel poison",1],["வேறலும்","overcoming one who is no match is poison",2],["தேறினான் நீடாங்கு செய்தலும்","harming at length one who trusted is poison",2],["இளங்கிளியை நாடாதே தீதுரையும்","speaking ill of a young girl without inquiry is poison",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:13":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":14,"t":["இடரின்னா நட்டார்கண் ஈயாமை இன்னா","தொடர்பின்னா நள்ளார்கண் தூயார்ப் - படர்பின்னா","கண்டல் அவர்பூங் கதுப்பினாய் இன்னாதே","கொண்ட விரதம் குறைவு"],"p":"","r":"Distress among friends is painful, not giving is painful, ties with those who are not friends are painful, the suffering of the pure is painful, and, the poet tells a woman, a vow taken and left short is painful.","n":"கண்டல் அவர் in line 3 is hard to place; read with the fourth item it makes it 'seeing the suffering of the pure'. The address பூங்கதுப்பினாய் (you of the flowered hair) is the frame, not an item. Three things are fused with இன்னா in the text, so their `thing` carries it as written.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["innaa","natpu","eegai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 13","lb":"SP13","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["இடரின்னா","distress that befalls friends is painful",1],["ஈயாமை","not giving is painful",1],["தொடர்பின்னா","ties with those who are not friends are painful",2],["தூயார்ப் படர்பின்னா","the suffering of the pure is painful to see",2],["கொண்ட விரதம் குறைவு","a vow taken and left short is painful",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:14":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":15,"t":["கொண்டான் வழியொழுகல் பெண்மகன் தந்தைக்குத்","தண்டான் வழியொழுகல் தன்கிளையஃது - அண்டாதே","வேல்வழி வெம்முனை வீடாது மன்னாடு","கோல்வழி வாழ்தல் குணம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as the virtue of each: for a woman, walking the way of the man who married her; for a son, following his father's way without fail; for kin, the same; for soldiers, not leaving the hot front of spears; and for a king's land, living by his sceptre.","n":"Every item is a following: wife after husband, son after father, soldier at the front, people under the sceptre, so the verse makes one virtue of keeping to one's place in an order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ozhukkam","maram","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 14","lb":"SP14","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["கொண்டான் வழியொழுகல்","for a woman, walking the way of her husband is virtue",1],["தந்தைக்குத் தண்டான் வழியொழுகல்","for a son, following his father's way without fail is virtue",1],["தன்கிளையஃது","for kin, the same following is virtue",2],["வேல்வழி வெம்முனை வீடாது","for soldiers, not leaving the hot front of spears is virtue",3],["கோல்வழி வாழ்தல்","for the king's land, living by his sceptre is virtue",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of women's conduct of the period: the wife is to walk her husband's way"],"ch":"sp:1"},"sp:15":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":16,"t":["பிழைத்தல் பொறுத்தல் பெருமை சிறுமை","இழைத்ததீங்(கு) எண்ணி யிருத்தல் - இழைத்த","பகைகெட வாழ்வதும் பல்பொருளார் நல்லார்","நகைகெட வாழ்வதும் நன்று"],"p":"","r":"Bearing with a wrong is greatness, sitting brooding on harm done is smallness, and it is good to live so that the enmity one made comes to an end and so that the wealthy and the good have no cause to laugh.","n":"Four items, not five: two set greatness against smallness, two name a good way of living. பல்பொருளார் நல்லார் may be read as the subjects (the wealthy and good, living so none laugh at them) rather than the ones who laugh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai","sirumai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 15","lb":"SP15","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பிழைத்தல் பொறுத்தல்","bearing with a wrong is greatness",1],["இழைத்ததீங்(கு) எண்ணி யிருத்தல்","brooding on harm done is smallness",2],["பகைகெட வாழ்வதும்","living so that enmity one caused ends is good",3],["நகைகெட வாழ்வதும்","living so that the wealthy and good have no cause to laugh is good",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:16":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":17,"t":["கதநன்று சான்றாண்மை தீது கழிய","மதநன்று மாண்பில்லார் முன்னர் - விதநன்றால்","கோய்வாயிற் கீழுயிர்க்(கு)ஈ துற்றுக் குரைத்தெழுந்த","நாய்வாயுள் நல்ல தசை"],"p":"","r":"Before people without worth, anger is good, nobility bad and arrogance good, the verse says, for goodness shown there is like good meat in the mouth of a dog that bit and rose barking.","n":"Three items, the inversion of the house virtues made explicit (கதம் and மதம் are fused with நன்று in the text); the closing image says kindness spent on the base is wasted like meat in a dog's mouth. Line 3 (கோய்வாயிற் கீழுயிர்க்(கு)ஈ துற்று) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","saandraanmai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 16","lb":"SP16","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கதநன்று","anger is good before people without worth",1],["சான்றாண்மை","nobility is bad before people without worth",1],["மதநன்று","arrogance is good before people without worth",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:17":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":18,"t":["நட்டாரை யாக்கிப் பகைபணித்து வைஎயிற்றுப்","பட்டார் அகல்அல்கு லார்படிந்து - ஓட்டித்","தொடங்கினார் இல்லகத்து அன்பில் துறவா","உடம்பினான் ஆய பயன்"],"p":"","r":"Making friends, humbling enemies, lying with broad-hipped women of sharp teeth, and keeping love for those within one's house are named the fruit gained by a body that has not renounced.","n":"Four fruits, all of the unrenounced body (துறவா உடம்பு), so the verse grants the householder's life its gains while marking them as the lesser body's. ஓட்டித் தொடங்கினார் is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","natpu","pagai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 17","lb":"SP17","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["நட்டாரை யாக்கி","making friends is a fruit of the unrenounced body",1],["பகைபணித்து","humbling enemies is a fruit of the unrenounced body",1],["வைஎயிற்றுப் பட்டார் அகல்அல்கு லார்படிந்து","lying with broad-hipped women of sharp teeth is a fruit of the unrenounced body",1],["இல்லகத்து அன்பில்","love within one's house is a fruit of the unrenounced body",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:18":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":19,"t":["பொய்யாமை பொன்பெறினும் கள்ளாமை மெல்லியலார்","வையாமை வார்குழலார் நச்சினும் - நையாமை","ஓர்த்துடம்பு பேருமென்று ஊனவாய் உண்ணானேல்","பேர்த்துடம்பு கோடல் அரிது"],"p":"","r":"One who does not lie though he would gain gold, does not steal, does not turn to soft women though long-haired women want him, does no harm, and, knowing the body will leave, does not eat flesh out of craving, will hardly take a body again.","n":"The reward is not this world's but release from rebirth (பேர்த்துடம்பு கோடல் அரிது). Each abstention is set against its strongest temptation (gold, women who desire him). வையாமை and நையாமை are uncertain: we read 'not setting on' and 'not afflicting'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","kalavu","pulaal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 18","lb":"SP18","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யாமை","not lying even for gold leads away from rebirth",1],["கள்ளாமை","not stealing leads away from rebirth",1],["மெல்லியலார் வையாமை","not turning to women though they desire him leads away from rebirth",1],["நையாமை","doing no harm leads away from rebirth",2],["ஊனவாய் உண்ணானேல்","not eating flesh out of craving leads away from rebirth",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:19":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":20,"t":["தேவறே கற்றவர் கல்லாதார் தேருங்கால்","பூதரே முன்பொருள் செய்யாதார் - ஆதரே","துன்பம் இலேம்பண்டு யாமே வனப்புடையேம்","என்பார் இருகால் எருது"],"p":"","r":"Those who have learned are gods and the unlearned, on examination, ghouls; those who did not earn wealth early are wretches; and those who say they had no sorrow before and were the handsome ones are two-legged oxen.","n":"Four items. The scale runs from god to ghoul to beast, and the last item mocks those who live on their past.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai","selvam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 19","lb":"SP19","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றவர்","those who have learned are gods",1],["கல்லாதார்","the unlearned are ghouls",1],["முன்பொருள் செய்யாதார்","those who did not earn wealth early are wretches",2],["துன்பம் இலேம்பண்டு யாமே வனப்புடையேம் என்பார்","those who boast of past ease and beauty are two-legged oxen",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:20":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":21,"t":["கள்ளான்கு தென்றும் கழுமான் கரியாரை","நள்ளான் உயிரிங்க நாவாடான் - நள்ளானாய்","ஊன்மறுத்துக் கொள்ளானேல் ஊனுடம்(பு) எஞ்ஞான்றும்","தான்மறுத்துக் கொள்ளான் தளர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"One who does not steal, never gives himself to gambling, does not befriend the black-hearted, does not move his tongue to take life, and refuses meat will never, worn out, take on a body of flesh again.","n":"The line division கள்ளான்கு தென்றும் is read as கள்ளான் சூது என்றும். The closing pun sets the refused flesh (ஊன்) against the flesh-body (ஊனுடம்பு): refusing the one, he is not given the other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","soodhu","pulaal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 20","lb":"SP20","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கள்ளான்","one who does not steal escapes rebirth",1],["கழுமான்","one who never gives himself to gambling escapes rebirth",1],["கரியாரை நள்ளான்","one who does not befriend the black-hearted escapes rebirth",1],["உயிரிங்க நாவாடான்","one who does not use his tongue to take life escapes rebirth",2],["ஊன்மறுத்துக் கொள்ளானேல்","one who refuses meat escapes rebirth",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:payiram":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":22,"t":["மல்லிவர் மாக்காயன் மாணாக்கன் மாநிலத்துப்","பல்லவர் நோய்நீக்கும் பாங்கினால் - கல்லா","மறுபஞ்சம் தீர்மழைக்கை மாக்காரி யாசான்","சிறுபஞ்ச மூலம்செய் தான்"],"p":"","r":"The payiram says that Makkariyasan, pupil of Makkayan, whose hand is like rain ending the other famine of not learning, made the Sirupanchamoolam to remove the ills of many in the world.","n":"It joins the title's two senses: the five roots as medicine (நோய்நீக்கும்) and பஞ்சம் as famine, here the famine of the unlearned (கல்லா மறுபஞ்சம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","mazhai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam payiram","lb":"SPpayiram","dm":"all","it":[],"cw":"","ch":"sp:1"},"sp:21":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":23,"t":["பூவாது காய்க்கும் மரமுள நன்றறிவார்","மூவாது மூத்தவர் நூல்வல்லார் - தாவா","விதையாமை நாறுவ வித்துள மேதைக்கு","உரையாமை செல்லும் உணர்வு"],"p":"","r":"There are trees that fruit without flowering, and those who know the good and are skilled in books are elders without having aged; there are seeds that sprout without being sown, and to the intelligent understanding reaches without being told.","n":"Two nature images each answered by a kind of person: the tree that fruits unflowered is the young man who is already an elder in knowledge, the seed that sprouts unsown is the quick mind that needs no telling. Divided into five, taking நன்றறிவார் and நூல்வல்லார் as two descriptions; they could be one. Verse 22 is its exact negative.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 21","lb":"SP21","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பூவாது காய்க்கும் மரமுள","there are trees that bear fruit without first flowering",1],["நன்றறிவார்","those who know the good are elders without having aged",1],["நூல்வல்லார்","those skilled in books are likewise elders without age",2],["விதையாமை நாறுவ வித்து","there are seeds that sprout without being sown",3],["மேதைக்கு","to the intelligent, understanding goes home without being spoken",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:22":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":24,"t":["பூத்தாலும் காயா மரமுள நன்றறிவார்","மூவாது மூவர்நூல் தேற்றதார் - பாத்திப்","புதைத்தாலும் நாறாத வித்துள பேதைக்கு","உரைத்தாலும் செல்லா(து) உணர்வு"],"p":"","r":"There are trees that flower yet do not fruit, and men who, though grown old, do not grasp the books; there are seeds that do not sprout even when buried in a bed, and to the fool understanding does not reach even when told.","n":"The mirror of verse 21, image for image: flowering without fruit is age without learning, the buried seed is the fool who is told and still does not take it. Line 2 as printed (மூவாது மூவர்நூல் தேற்றதார்) does not construe cleanly; the reading takes it as the aged who have not understood the books, as the parallel with 21 requires. Four things, not five.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 22","lb":"SP22","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பூத்தாலும் காயா மரமுள","there are trees that flower and still do not fruit",1],["நூல் தேற்றதார்","there are men who do not grasp the books though they have aged",2],["நாறாத வித்து","there are seeds that do not sprout even when buried in a prepared bed",3],["பேதைக்கு","to the fool, understanding does not reach even when spoken",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:23":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":25,"t":["வடி(வு)இளமை வாய்த்த வனப்பு வணங்காக்","குடிகுலமென்(று) ஐந்தும் குறித்து - முடியாத்","துளங்கா நிலைகாணார் தொக்(கு)ஈர் பசுவால்","இளங்கால் துறவா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"Those who rely on five things (bodily form, youth, the beauty that has come to them, a family that bows to no one, and lineage) and do not renounce while young never see the unshaken state that has no end.","n":"Two of the five are pride of birth (குடி, குலம்), set beside the body's goods as things that hold a man back. The deadline is youth itself (இளங்கால்): renunciation put off past youth is the failure. Line 3's தொக்(கு)ஈர் பசுவால் is obscure; it may speak of being dragged by bound attachment, and the reading leaves it out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilamai","nilaiyaamai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 23","lb":"SP23","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["வடி","bodily form is one of the five things relied on by those who do not renounce young",1],["இளமை","youth is one of them",1],["வாய்த்த வனப்பு","the beauty one happens to have is one of them",1],["வணங்காக் குடி","a family that bows to none is one of them",1],["குலமென்(று)","lineage is one of them",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:24":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":26,"t":["கள்ளுண்டல் காணில் கணவன் பிரிந்துறைதல்","வெள்(கு)இல ளாய்ப்பிறர் இல்சேறல் - உள்ளிப்","பிறர்கருமம் ஆராய்தல் தீப்பெண் கிளைமைத்","துறமதுதீப் பெண்ணின் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names as the doings of a bad wife drinking toddy, living apart from her husband, going without shame into other people's houses, prying with intent into others' affairs, and keeping close ties with bad women.","n":"All five faults lie outside the kitchen and the husband's presence: drink, absence, other houses, other people's business, bad company. காணில் in line 1 is hard to place (if one looks, or when he is seen). Line 3-4's கிளைமைத் துறமது is doubtful; it may instead mean cutting off her own kin, and the fifth item is held loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","karpu","kal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 24","lb":"SP24","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["கள்ளுண்டல்","drinking toddy is a bad wife's doing",1],["கணவன் பிரிந்துறைதல்","living apart from her husband is a bad wife's doing",1],["பிறர் இல்சேறல்","going shamelessly into others' houses is a bad wife's doing",2],["பிறர்கருமம் ஆராய்தல்","prying with intent into others' affairs is a bad wife's doing",3],["தீப்பெண் கிளைமை","close ties with bad women are a bad wife's doing",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period for a wife's conduct, listing what marks a bad wife"],"ch":"sp:2"},"sp:25":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":27,"t":["பெருங்குணத்தார்ச் சேர்மின் பிறன்பொருள்வெள வன்மின்","கருங்குணத்தார் கேண்மை கழிமின் - ஒருங்குணர்ந்து","தீச்சொல்லே காமின் வருங்காலன் திண்ணிதே","வாய்ச்சொல்லே யன்று வழக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse urges joining those of great character, not seizing another's wealth, giving up the friendship of the dark-natured and guarding against evil speech, for Death's coming is certain and the reckoning is not mere talk.","n":"Four commands and then their ground: the coming of காலன் is certain, and the last line (வழக்கு is not mere mouth-talk) says the judgment that follows is real. The list's force comes from the fifth item, which is not a command at all but the reason for the other four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","vehkaamai","kootru","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 25","lb":"SP25","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருங்குணத்தார்","those of great character are to be joined",1],["பிறன்பொருள்","another's wealth is not to be seized",1],["கருங்குணத்தார் கேண்மை","the friendship of the dark-natured is to be left",2],["தீச்சொல்லே","evil speech is to be guarded against",3],["வருங்காலன்","Death's coming is certain, and the reckoning is not mere words",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:26":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":28,"t":["வான்குருவிக் கூ(டு)அரக்கு வால்உலண்டு கோல்தகுதல்","தேன்புரிந்து யார்க்கும் செயலாகா - தாம்புரீஇ","வல்லவர் வாய்ப்பன என்னார் ஒரோஒருவர்க்(கு)","ஒல்காதோர் ஒன்று படும்"],"p":"","r":"The sky-bird's nest, lac, the white silkworm's work, the fit of a stick and honey are things no one can make at will; the skilled should not say they achieve whatever they set out to, for to each single being some one thing comes without fail.","n":"The five are works of creatures (the weaver bird's nest, the lac insect, the silkworm, perhaps the termite or bee, the honeybee) that no human craftsman can copy, set against the boast of the skilled. கோல்தகுதல் is obscure and the fourth item is a guess. The point is a limit on human skill: every being has its own one gift.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","agandhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 26","lb":"SP26","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வான்குருவிக் கூ","the nest of the sky-bird cannot be made by anyone at will",1],["அரக்கு","lac cannot be made by anyone at will",1],["வால்உலண்டு","the white silkworm's work cannot be made by anyone at will",1],["கோல்தகுதல்","the fitting of a stick (sense obscure) cannot be done by anyone at will",1],["தேன்","honey cannot be made by anyone at will",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:27":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":29,"t":["அறன்நாட்டான் நன்னெறிக்கண் நிற்க அடங்காப்","புறன்நாட்டான் புன்னெறிப் போகாது - புறன்நட்டான்","கண்டெடுத்துக் கள்களவு சூது கருத்தினால்","பண்டெடுத்துக் காட்டும் பயின்று"],"p":"","r":"The man set in dharma stands on the good path, the unrestrained outsider does not leave the low path, and the one who befriends outsiders picks up toddy, theft and gambling and, practised in them, shows them as of old.","n":"The verse turns on நாட்டான்/நட்டான், the one established and the one who befriends: vices are learned through company. The construal of lines 2-4 is uncertain; the division takes two persons and three vices.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","kal","kalavu","soodhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 27","lb":"SP27","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["அறன்நாட்டான்","the man established in dharma stands on the good path",1],["புறன்நாட்டான்","the unrestrained outsider keeps to the low path",2],["கள்","toddy is what the friend of outsiders picks up and displays",3],["களவு","theft is what he picks up and displays",3],["சூது","gambling is what he picks up and displays",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:28":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":30,"t":["ஆண்ஆக்கம் வேண்டாதான் ஆசான் அவற்(கு)இயைந்த","மாணாக்கன் அன்பான் வழிபடுவான் - மாணாக்கன்","கற்பனைத்தும் மூன்றுங் கடிந்தான் கடியாதான்","நிற்பனைத்தும் நெஞ்சிற்கோர் நோய்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher is one who seeks no gain from men, the pupil who suits him serves him with love, the pupil who learns everything has cast off the three, and for one who has not cast them off all that he holds remains a disease of the heart.","n":"Divided into four: teacher, loving pupil, the pupil who has cast off the three, and the one who has not. The three are not named; in a Jain work they are most likely desire, anger and delusion, the three the invocation says the Lord destroyed (மூன்றொழித்து). Learning without that riddance is called a sickness, not a lesser good. ஆண்ஆக்கம் may mean worldly gain or a man's advancement; the reading takes gain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 28","lb":"SP28","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆசான்","the teacher is one who seeks no gain",1],["மாணாக்கன்","the pupil fit for him serves him with love",2],["மூன்றுங் கடிந்தான்","the pupil who learns all has cast off the three",3],["கடியாதான்","for one who has not cast them off, all he holds is a disease of the heart",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:29":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":31,"t":["நெய்தல் முகிழ்த்துணை யாம்குடுமி நேர்மயிரும்","உய்தல் ஒருதிங்கள் நாளாகும் - செய்தல்","துணங்குநூல் ஓதுதல் கேட்டல்மா ணாக்கர்","வணங்கி வலங்கொண்டு வந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets out a student's observances: hair kept as a tuft like a closing neytal bud with the rest trimmed, a term of a month, and the student's work of reciting the text and listening, coming to the teacher with a bow and circling him to the right.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to construe: உய்தல் ஒருதிங்கள் நாளாகும் may give the interval for trimming the hair or a period of study. துணங்கு in line 3 is obscure. The second half is clearer: recitation, listening, bowing and circumambulation of the teacher.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kelvi","guru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 29","lb":"SP29","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["குடுமி","the student's hair is a tuft like a neytal bud",1],["ஓதுதல்","reciting the text is the student's work",3],["கேட்டல்","listening is the student's work",3],["வணங்கி","the students come bowing",4],["வலங்கொண்டு","the students circle the teacher to the right",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["ritual"],"a rule of the period for a student's tonsure and his ritual approach to the teacher"],"ch":"sp:2"},"sp:30":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":32,"t":["ஒருவன் அறிவானும் எல்லாம் யாதொன்றும்","ஒருவன் அறியா தவனும் ஒருவன்","குணன்அடங்கக் குற்றமில் லானாம் ஒருவன்","கணன்அடங்கக் கற்றானும் இல்"],"p":"","r":"There is no one who knows everything, no one who knows nothing at all, no one wholly without fault with every virtue held in him, and no one who has learned the whole body of the treatises.","n":"Four negations, each closed by the single இல் at the end. The second is the surprise: as no one is all-knowing, no one is wholly ignorant either, so the verse limits contempt as much as pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 30","lb":"SP30","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["அறிவானும்","there is no one who knows everything",1],["அறியா தவனும்","there is no one who knows nothing at all",2],["குற்றமில் லானாம்","there is no one wholly faultless with all virtues in him",3],["கற்றானும்","there is no one who has learned the whole body of treatises",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:31":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":33,"t":["உயிர்நோய்செய் யாமை உறுநோய் மறத்தல்","செயீர்நோய் பிறன்கண்செய் யாமை - செயிர்நோய்","விழைவு வெகுளி இவைவிடுவா னாயின்","இழிவன்(று) இனிது தவம்"],"p":"","r":"If a man does no harm to living beings, forgets the pain that befalls him, does no hurtful wrong to another, and gives up desire and anger, the diseases that are faults, then penance is not lowly but sweet.","n":"Penance is defined wholly by conduct, with no rite or austerity named. மறத்தல் (forgetting) the pain that comes goes further than enduring it. The conclusion answers a doubt the verse assumes: that penance is a lowering of oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kollaamai","porai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 31","lb":"SP31","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["உயிர்நோய்செய் யாமை","not harming living beings makes penance sweet",1],["உறுநோய் மறத்தல்","forgetting the pain that befalls one makes penance sweet",1],["பிறன்கண்செய் யாமை","not doing hurtful wrong to another makes penance sweet",2],["விழைவு","giving up desire makes penance sweet",3],["வெகுளி","giving up anger makes penance sweet",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:32":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":34,"t":["வேட்பவன் பார்ப்பான் விளங்கிழைக்குக் கற்புடைமை","கேட்பவன் கேடில் பெரும்புலவன் - பாட்டவன்","சிந்தையான் ஆகும் சிறந்தது உலகினுள்","தந்தையான் ஆகும் நலம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that he who performs sacrifice is the brahmin, chastity is the excellence of a bright-jewelled woman, he who listens is the flawless great scholar, a poet's excellence comes from his mind, and in the world one's good comes through the father.","n":"Each line names a person by the act that makes him what he is called. பார்ப்பான் is a birth term, but the verse ties it to the act of sacrifice (வேட்பவன்), not to birth; it reports that norm, it does not argue it. The last item gives the father, not one's own deeds, as the source of standing in the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","karpu","kelvi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 32","lb":"SP32","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வேட்பவன்","he who performs the sacrifice is the brahmin",1],["கற்புடைமை","chastity is the excellence of a woman",1],["கேட்பவன்","he who listens is the flawless great scholar",2],["பாட்டவன்","a poet's excellence comes from his mind",2],["தந்தையான்","in the world one's good comes through the father",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste","gender"],"the period's definition of the brahmin by the sacrifice and of a woman's excellence by chastity"],"ch":"sp:2"},"sp:33":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":35,"t":["வைப்பானே வள்ளல் வழங்குவான் வாணிகன்","உய்ப்பானே ஆசான் உயர்கதிக்கு - உய்ப்பான்","உடம்பினார் வேலி ஒருப்படுத்(து)ஊன் ஆரத்","தொடங்கானேல் சேறல் துணிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that only he who lays up is the liberal man and he who hands out is a merchant, that only he who leads to the higher state is a teacher, and that the one so led, keeping the body's hedge and never beginning to eat flesh, is sure to go there.","n":"The first line is a paradox whose sense is uncertain: the 'laying up' is perhaps the store of merit made by giving, and the 'handing out' giving for return, so that the merchant gives and the liberal man saves. The closing rule makes abstaining from flesh the test of whether one has been led upward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","guru","pulaal","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 33","lb":"SP33","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வள்ளல்","only he who lays up is the liberal man",1],["வாணிகன்","he who hands out is a merchant",1],["ஆசான்","only he who leads to the higher state is a teacher",2],["உடம்பினார் வேலி","the one led keeps the body's hedge closed",3],["ஊன்","if he never begins to eat flesh, his going is certain",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:34":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":36,"t":["வைததனால் ஆகும் வசைவணக்கம் நன்றாகச்","செய்ததனால் ஆகும் செழுங்குலமுன் - செய்த","பொருளினால் ஆகுமாம் போகம் நெகிழ்ந்த","அருளினால் ஆகும் அறம்"],"p":"","r":"Blame comes of reviling, homage comes of doing good, a thriving family comes of what was done before, enjoyment comes of wealth, and dharma comes of grace that has melted.","n":"Five effects, each with its cause. The division of lines 1-3 (வசை / வணக்கம் / செழுங்குலம்) is uncertain; செழுங்குலம் may instead be the fruit of homage. The last two repeat verse 2's pairing of wealth with enjoyment and grace with dharma, and add that the grace must be நெகிழ்ந்த, softened.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","vinai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 34","lb":"SP34","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வசை","blame comes from reviling others",1],["வணக்கம்","homage comes from doing good",1],["செழுங்குலமுன்","a thriving family comes from deeds done before",2],["போகம்","enjoyment comes from wealth",3],["அறம்","dharma comes from grace that has melted",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:35":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":37,"t":["இல்இயலார் நல்லறமும் எனைத் துறவறமும்","நல்லியலின் நாடி உரைக்குங்கால் - நல்லியல்","தானத்தால் போதும் தவத்தால் சுவர்க்கமாம்","ஞானத்தால் வீடாக நாட்டு"],"p":"","r":"Surveying the good dharma of householders and the dharma of renunciation, the verse says that giving brings enjoyment, penance brings heaven, and knowledge brings release.","n":"Three ranked paths with three ranked fruits, and heaven is only the middle one: release belongs to knowledge, not to penance. Only three things, not five. போதும் in line 3 is read as போகம், enjoyment; as printed it does not construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","gnaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 35","lb":"SP35","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["தானத்தால்","giving brings enjoyment",3],["தவத்தால்","penance brings heaven",3],["ஞானத்தால்","knowledge brings release",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:36":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":38,"t":["மயிர்வனப்பும் கண்கவரும் மார்பின் வனப்பும்","உகிர்வனப்பும் காதின் வனப்பும் - செயிர்தீர்ந்த","பல்லின் வனப்பும் வனப்பல்ல நூற்கியைந்த","சொல்லின் வனப்பே வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The beauty of the hair, of the eye-catching chest, of the nails, of the ears and of faultless teeth is not beauty; the beauty of speech fitted to learning is beauty.","n":"Five bodily beauties are denied and a sixth affirmed, so the verse counts six; the last undoes the first five. The true beauty is not learning as such but speech that fits the books (நூற்கியைந்த சொல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","sol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 36","lb":"SP36","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["மயிர்வனப்பும்","the beauty of the hair is not beauty",1],["மார்பின் வனப்பும்","the beauty of an eye-catching chest is not beauty",1],["உகிர்வனப்பும்","the beauty of the nails is not beauty",2],["காதின் வனப்பும்","the beauty of the ears is not beauty",2],["பல்லின் வனப்பும்","the beauty of faultless teeth is not beauty",3],["சொல்லின் வனப்பே","the beauty of speech fitted to learning is the true beauty",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:37":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":39,"t":["தொழீஇ அடஉண்ணார் தோழரில் துஞ்சார்","வழீஇய பிறர்பொருளை வௌவார் - கெழீஇக்","கலந்தபின் கீழ்காணார் காணாய் மடவாய்","புலந்தபின் போற்றார் புலை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, addressing a young woman, that the worthy do not eat food got by bowing, do not sleep in a friend's house, do not seize another's property that has gone astray, do not look down on one after joining with him, and do not take up baseness after a quarrel.","n":"The subject is unstated, and taken as the worthy. புலை in line 4 is read as baseness of conduct, not as the caste term. தொழீஇ அட may also mean food cooked by one made to bow, that is, by a servant forced to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","natpu","vehkaamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 37","lb":"SP37","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["தொழீஇ அட","the worthy do not eat food got by bowing",1],["தோழரில்","the worthy do not sleep in a friend's house",1],["பிறர்பொருளை","the worthy do not seize another's property that has gone astray",2],["கீழ்","the worthy do not look down on one after joining with him",3],["புலை","the worthy do not take up baseness after a quarrel",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:38":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":40,"t":["பொய்யாமை நன்று பொருள்நன்று உயிர்நோவக்","கொல்லாமை நன்று கொழிக்குங்கால் - பல்லார்முன்","பேணாமை பேணும் தகைய சிறிதெனினும்","மாணாமை மாண்டார் மனம்"],"p":"","r":"Not lying is good, wealth is good, not killing so that a life suffers is good, not exalting oneself before many is, when weighed, worth esteem, and the minds of the excellent shun unworthiness however small.","n":"Wealth stands between the two vows as a plain good, with no ranking among the three. Lines 3-4 are hard to divide; the reading takes பேணாமை as not making much of oneself and மாணாமை as unworthiness that the excellent mind will not admit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","kollaamai","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 38","lb":"SP38","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யாமை","not lying is good",1],["பொருள்","wealth is good",1],["கொல்லாமை","not killing so that a life suffers is good",2],["பேணாமை","not exalting oneself before many is worth esteem",3],["மாணாமை","even small unworthiness is shunned by the minds of the excellent",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:39":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":41,"t":["பண்டாரம் பல்கணக்குக் கண்காணி பாத்தில்லார்","உண்டார் அடிசிலே தோழரில் - கொண்டாரா","யாக்கைக்குத் தக்க அறிவில்லார்க் காப்படுப்பின்","காக்கைக்குக் காப்படுத்த சோறு"],"p":"","r":"To put a treasury, many accounts, oversight, a kitchen of food and a household in the charge of men without understanding to match their size is like setting a crow to guard cooked rice.","n":"The five are offices of trust in a great house; பாத்தில்லார் உண்டார் and தோழரில் are hard to construe and the last two items are held loosely. The measure of the appointee is understanding fit to his body: a big man with a small mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 39","lb":"SP39","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["பண்டாரம்","a treasury given to the witless is like rice guarded by a crow",1],["பல்கணக்கு","the many accounts given to them are the same",1],["கண்காணி","oversight given to them is the same",1],["அடிசிலே","the kitchen's food given to them is the same",2],["தோழரில்","the household given to them is the same",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:40":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":42,"t":["உடையிட்டார் புல்மேய்வார் ஓடுநீர் புக்கார்","படையிட்டார் பற்றேதும் இன்றி - நடையிட்டார்","இவ்வகை ஐவரையும் என்றும் அணுகாரே","செவ்வகைச் சேவகர் சென்று"],"p":"","r":"Those who have dropped their clothing, those who chew grass, those who have entered running water, those who have laid down their weapons and those who have fled with nothing left: these five, the verse says, true soldiers never go near.","n":"All five are signs of a beaten or helpless enemy: stripped, grass in the mouth in surrender, taking to the river, weapon down, in flight. The restraint is made the mark of the true (செவ்வகை) soldier, not a mercy he may choose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 40","lb":"SP40","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["உடையிட்டார்","those who have dropped their clothing are never approached by true soldiers",1],["புல்மேய்வார்","those who chew grass are never approached",1],["ஓடுநீர் புக்கார்","those who have entered running water are never approached",1],["படையிட்டார்","those who have laid down their weapons are never approached",2],["நடையிட்டார்","those who have fled with nothing are never approached",2]],"cw":"ஐவரையும்","ch":"sp:2"},"sp:41":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":43,"t":["பூவாதான் பூப்புப் புறங்கொடுத் தாள்இலிங்கி","ஓவாதான் கோலம் ஒருபொழுதும் - காவாதாள்","யார்யார் பிறர்மனையாள் உள்ளிட்டில் ஐவரையும்","சாரார் பகைபோல் சலித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five women whom men who know keep away from, recoiling as from an enemy: a girl not yet come of age, a woman past her menses, a woman bearing the sign of renunciation, a woman who at no time guards herself, and any other man's wife.","n":"The list widens the Kural's single prohibition (another's wife) into a set of women barred by age, bodily state, vow and conduct. இலிங்கி is a woman who bears an ascetic's sign; ஓவாதான் கோலம் on line 2 may describe her garb ('the dress of one who never leaves it'), and the division of line 2 is our construal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","karpu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 41","lb":"SP41","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["பூவாதான்","a girl who has not yet come of age is one of the five men avoid",1],["பூப்புப் புறங்கொடுத் தாள்","a woman whose menses have ended is one of the five men avoid",1],["இலிங்கி","a woman bearing the sign of renunciation, in its unbroken garb, is one of the five men avoid",1],["காவாதாள்","a woman who at no time guards herself is one of the five men avoid",2],["பிறர்மனையாள்","whoever is another man's wife is one of the five men avoid",3]],"cw":"ஐவரையும்","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period on which women a man may approach, set by age, menses, vow and marriage"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:42":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":44,"t":["வருவாய்க்குத் தக்க வழக்கறிந்து சுற்றம்","வெகுவாமை வீழ்ந்துவிருந் தோம்பித் - திருவாக்கும்","தெய்வதையும் எஞ்ஞான்றும் தேற்ற வழிபாடு","செய்வதே பெண்டிர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse says a woman's excellence lies in knowing the household's ways in proportion to its income, not angering kin, eagerly tending guests, and always worshipping, with a clear mind, the one who brings her fortune and the gods.","n":"திருவாக்கும் is read here as the one who makes her fortune, most likely the husband, so worship is owed both to him and to the deity; if it qualifies தெய்வதை instead, the list has four items. Income comes first, as in the Kural's definition of the wife.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","virundhu","sutram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 42","lb":"SP42","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["வருவாய்க்குத் தக்க வழக்கறிந்து","knowing the household's ways in proportion to its income is a wife's excellence",1],["வெகுவாமை","not angering the kin is a wife's excellence",2],["வீழ்ந்துவிருந் தோம்பித்","tending guests with eagerness is a wife's excellence",2],["திருவாக்கும்","worshipping the one who brings her fortune is a wife's excellence",2],["தெய்வதையும்","worshipping the deity always, with a clear mind, is a wife's excellence",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm defining a wife's excellence by household management, hospitality and worship"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:43":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":45,"t":["நாள்கூட்டம் மூர்த்தம் அவற்றோடு நன்றாம்அக்","கோள்கூட்டம் யோகம் குணன்உணர்ந்து - தோள்கூட்டல்","உற்றானும் அல்லானும் ஐந்தும் உணர்வானால்","பெற்றால்நாள் கொள்க பெரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if one finds a man who knows the five (the agreement of birth-stars, the auspicious hour, the agreement of planets, the yoga and the qualities) and whether the couple are fit to be joined, one should have him fix the wedding day.","n":"Astrology is given here as a learned craft with five parts, and the counsel is to seek out its expert. தோள்கூட்டல் உற்றானும் அல்லானும் ('one fit and not fit for the joining of shoulders') is read as the reckoning of the couple's match; it could also mean the bridegroom and others.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 43","lb":"SP43","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ritual-conduct","it":[["நாள்கூட்டம்","the agreement of the couple's birth-stars is one of the five the reckoner must know",1],["மூர்த்தம்","the auspicious hour is one of the five",1],["கோள்கூட்டம்","the agreement of the planets is one of the five",2],["யோகம்","the yoga of the day is one of the five",2],["குணன்","the qualities are one of the five",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","nr":[["ritual"],"a ritual rule of the period: the wedding day is fixed by astrological reckoning"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:44":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":46,"t":["பேண்அடக்கம் பேணாப் பெருந்தகைமை பீடுடைமை","நாண்ஒடுக்கம் என்றைந்தும் நண்ணின்றாப் - பூண்ஒடுக்கம்","பொன்வரைக்கோங் கேர்முலைப் பூந்திருவே யாயினும்","தன்வரைத் தாழ்த்தல் அரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five qualities (guarded restraint, a greatness that does not covet, dignity, modesty and reserve) and says a woman without them, even if she were Lakshmi herself with jewelled breasts like kongu buds on a golden hill, is hard to bring within one's bounds.","n":"நாண்ஒடுக்கம் is split into two to reach the count; read as one, there are four. The verse ranks the five above beauty at its divine limit, but its measure is whether the husband can hold her, not her own good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","adakkam","karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 44","lb":"SP44","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["பேண்அடக்கம்","guarded restraint is one of the five without which a woman cannot be held",1],["பேணாப் பெருந்தகைமை","a greatness that does not covet is one of the five",1],["பீடுடைமை","dignity is one of the five",1],["நாண்","modesty is one of the five",2],["ஒடுக்கம்","reserve is one of the five",2]],"cw":"என்றைந்தும்","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm on the qualities that keep a wife within her husband's bounds"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:45":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":47,"t":["வார்சான்ற கூந்தல் வரம்புயர வைகலும்","நீர்சான் றுயரவே நெல்லுயரும் - சீர்சான்ற","தாவாக் குடியுயரத் தாங்கருஞ்சீர்க் கோவுயரும்","ஓவா(து) உரைக்கும் உலகு"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a long-haired woman, the verse traces a chain the world repeats without end: as the ridge of the field rises the water rises, as the water rises the paddy rises, as the paddy rises the undiminished people rise, and as the people rise the king rises.","n":"Each item is the cause of the next, so the list is a ladder, not a set: the king's height is the last effect of a field bank. ஓவா(து) உரைக்கும் உலகு gives it as a common saying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","uzhavu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 45","lb":"SP45","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["வரம்பு","the ridge of the field rising lifts the water",1],["நீர்","the water rising lifts the paddy",2],["நெல்","the paddy rising lifts the people",2],["தாவாக் குடி","the undiminished people rising lifts the king",3],["கோ","the king rises last, as the effect of all the rest",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:46":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":48,"t":["அழியாமை எத்துவமும் சார்ந்தாரை ஆக்கல்","பழியாமை பாத்தல்யார் மாட்டும் - ஒளியாமை","கன்றுசா வப்பால் கறவாமை செய்யாமை","மன்றுசார் வாசு மனை"],"p":"","r":"The verse names the conduct of a good house: wasting nothing, raising up those who depend on it, blaming no one, sharing with everyone without hiding, and not milking so that the calf dies.","n":"The calf whose milk is taken is set in the same list as dependants and guests, so the house's care reaches its animals. The closing மன்றுசார் வாசு மனை is obscure; we take it as praise of such a house before the common ground, and எத்துவமும் as 'anything at all'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","kollaamai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 46","lb":"SP46","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["அழியாமை","wasting nothing at all marks a good house",1],["சார்ந்தாரை ஆக்கல்","raising up those who depend on it marks a good house",1],["பழியாமை","blaming no one marks a good house",2],["பாத்தல்யார் மாட்டும்","sharing with everyone, without hiding anything, marks a good house",2],["கன்றுசா வப்பால் கறவாமை","not milking the cow so that its calf dies marks a good house",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:47":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":49,"t":["நசைகொல்லார் நச்சியார்க்(கு) என்றும் கிளைஞர்","மிசைகொல்லார் வேளாண்மை கொல்லார் - இசைகொல்லார்","பொன்பெறும் பூஞ்சுணங்கின் மென்முலையாய் நன்குணர்ந்தார்","என்பெறினும் கொல்லார் இயைந்து"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a woman, the verse says those who understand well never kill the hope of those who long for them, the meal of their kin, their own generosity or their fame, and will not consent to kill whatever they might gain by it.","n":"The verse plays on கொல்: hope, a meal, generosity and fame are 'killed' by neglect, and the last 'killing' is the literal one, refused at any gain. The fifth killing has no object of its own in the Tamil, so we give four items and read the close as the frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","eegai","pugazh","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 47","lb":"SP47","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நசை","those who know do not kill the hope of those who long for them",1],["மிசை","they do not kill the meal of their kin",2],["வேளாண்மை","they do not kill their own generosity",2],["இசை","they do not kill their fame",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:48":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":50,"t":["நீண்டநீர் காடு களர்நிவந்து விண்தோயும்","மாண்ட மலைமக்கள் உள்ளிட்டு - மாண்டவர்","ஆய்ந்தன ஐந்தும் அரணா உடையானை","வேந்தனா நாட்டல் விதி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the wise have examined five things as a fortress (deep water, forest, saline waste, a lofty hill reaching the sky, and the people) and that the rule is to establish as king the one who has them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","arasu","kudi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 48","lb":"SP48","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["நீண்டநீர்","deep water is one of the five defences",1],["காடு","forest is one of the five defences",1],["களர்","saline waste land is one of the five defences",1],["மாண்ட மலை","a lofty hill reaching the sky is one of the five defences",2],["மக்கள்","the people are one of the five defences",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:49":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":51,"t":["பொச்சாப்புக் கேடு பொருட்செருக்குத் தான்கேடு","முற்றாமை கேடு முரண்கேடு - தெற்றத்","தொழில்மகன் தன்னோடு மாறாயின் என்றும்","உழுமகற்குக் கேடென்(று) உரை"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five ruins for the ploughman: negligence, pride in wealth, leaving work unfinished, quarrelling, and falling out with the skilled worker.","n":"The last two ruins are social: a farmer is ruined by quarrels and by losing the craftsman he depends on, not only by his own sloth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","pochchaappu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 49","lb":"SP49","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["பொச்சாப்புக்","negligence ruins the ploughman",1],["பொருட்செருக்குத்","pride in wealth ruins the ploughman",1],["முற்றாமை","leaving work unfinished ruins the ploughman",2],["முரண்","quarrelling ruins the ploughman",2],["தொழில்மகன்","falling out with the skilled worker ruins the ploughman",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:50":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":52,"t":["கொல்லாமை நன்று கொலைதீ(து) எழுத்தினைக்","கல்லாமை தீது கதந்தீது - நல்லார்","மொழியாமை முன்னே முழுதுங் கிளைஞர்","பழியாமை பல்லார் பதி"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets not killing as good and killing, not learning letters, and anger as bad, and calls a place where kin are wholly free of blame, without the good having to speak first, a town where many may live.","n":"The first four items are verdicts of good and bad in the same breath, so illiteracy and anger are weighed on the scale of killing. The last two lines are hard to divide; we read them as the place where such conduct prevails.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","kolai","kallaamai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 50","lb":"SP50","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கொல்லாமை","not killing is good",1],["கொலை","killing is bad",1],["கல்லாமை","not learning letters is bad",2],["கதந்","anger is bad",2],["பழியாமை","where kin are wholly free of blame, without the good having to speak first, is a town for many",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:51":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":53,"t":["உண்ணாமை நன்றவா நீக்கி விருந்துகண்மா(று)","எண்ணாமை நன்றிகழல் தீதெளியார் - எண்ணின்","அரியரா வார்பிறரிற் செல்லாரே உண்ணார்","பெரியரா வார்பிறர் கைத்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says fasting with desire put away is good, hosting guests without reckoning a return is good, and scorning the weak is bad, and that those who do not go to others' houses are rare and those who do not eat from others' hands are great.","n":"The list joins the ascetic's fast and the householder's guest, then turns both into a pride of self-reliance: not entering others' houses, not eating from their hands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","virundhu","maanam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 51","lb":"SP51","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["உண்ணாமை","fasting is good when desire has been put away",1],["விருந்துகண்மா(று)","hosting guests without reckoning a return is good",1],["தீதெளியார்","scorning the weak is bad",2],["பிறரிற் செல்லாரே","those who do not go to others' houses are rare",3],["பிறர் கைத்து","those who do not eat from others' hands become great",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:52":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":54,"t":["மக்கட் பெறுதல் மடனுடைமை மாதுடைமை","ஒக்க உடனுறைதல் ஊண்அமைவு - தொக்க","அலவலை அல்லாமை பெண்மகளிர்க்(கு) ஐந்து","தலைமகனைத் தாழ்க்கு மருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names the medicines by which women bring the husband to yield: bearing children, innocence, beauty, living with him in accord, restraint in food, and not being restless in speech.","n":"The verse says ஐந்து but the Tamil yields six phrases; we give six, though மடனுடைமை மாதுடைமை may have been counted as one. The medicine works on the husband: the wife's virtues are named as her means of holding him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal","marundhu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 52","lb":"SP52","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["மக்கட் பெறுதல்","bearing children brings the husband to yield",1],["மடனுடைமை","innocence brings the husband to yield",1],["மாதுடைமை","beauty brings the husband to yield",1],["ஒக்க உடனுறைதல்","living with him in accord brings him to yield",2],["ஊண்அமைவு","restraint in food brings him to yield",2],["அலவலை அல்லாமை","not being restless in speech brings him to yield",3]],"cw":"ஐந்து","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm naming a wife's qualities as means of holding her husband"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:53":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":55,"t":["கொண்டான் கொழுநன் உடன்பிறந்தான் தன்மாமன்","வண்டார்பூந் தொங்கல் மகன்தந்தை - வண்தாராய்!","யாப்பார்பூங் கோதை அணிஇழையை நன்கியையக்","காப்பார் கருது மிடத்து"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a garlanded man, the verse names the five who guard a well-adorned woman, when one considers it: the husband who took her, her brother, her maternal uncle, her son and her father.","n":"கொண்டான் கொழுநன் is read as one person, the husband who took her, which gives five; read as two it gives six. Every guardian is a man of her family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","sutram","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 53","lb":"SP53","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["கொண்டான் கொழுநன்","the husband who took her is one of her five guardians",1],["உடன்பிறந்தான்","her brother is one of her guardians",1],["தன்மாமன்","her maternal uncle is one of her guardians",1],["மகன்","her son is one of her guardians",2],["தந்தை","her father is one of her guardians",2]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm placing a woman under the guard of the men of her family"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:54":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":56,"t":["ஆம்பல்வாய் கண்மனம் வார்புருவம் என்றைந்தும்","தாம்பல்வா யோடி நிறைகாத்தல் - ஓம்பார்","நெடுங்கழைநீண் மூங்கில் எனஇகழ்ந்தார் ஆட்டும்","கொடுங்குழை போலக் கொளின்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that if a woman lets her lily-red mouth, eyes, mind and long brows run many ways and does not guard her restraint, she is held like the bent-earringed dancer whom scorners make to perform on a tall bamboo pole.","n":"The count word says five but four parts are named (mouth, eyes, mind, brows); a fifth may be lost or ஆம்பல் may have been counted apart. The guard here is her own நிறை, not a man's watch. The pole dancer of the close is our reading of an obscure image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","karpu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 54","lb":"SP54","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஆம்பல்வாய்","a lily-red mouth that runs many ways loses a woman her restraint",1],["கண்","eyes that run many ways",1],["மனம்","a mind that runs many ways",1],["வார்புருவம்","long brows that play many ways",1]],"cw":"என்றைந்தும்","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm on a woman's guarding of her own glance, speech and mind"],"ch":"sp:3"},"sp:55":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":57,"t":["பொன்பெறுங் கற்றான் பொருள்பெறும் நற்கவி","என்பெறும் வாதி இசைபெறும் - முன்பெறக்","கல்லார்கற் றாரினத்தார் அல்லார் பெறுபவே","நல்லார் இனத்து நகை"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the learned man wins gold, the good poet wealth and the debater fame, while the unlearned and those outside the company of the learned win only laughter among the good.","n":"The rewards are graded by the kind of learning: gold, wealth, fame. என்பெறும் வாதி may be a question ('what does the debater gain? fame').","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai","inam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 55","lb":"SP55","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றான்","the learned man wins gold",1],["நற்கவி","the good poet wins wealth",1],["வாதி","the debater wins fame",2],["கல்லார்","the unlearned win laughter among the good",3],["கற் றாரினத்தார் அல்லார்","those not of the learned's company win laughter among the good",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:56":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":58,"t":["நல்ல வெளிப்படுத்துத் தீய மறந்தொழிந்து","ஒல்லை உயிர்க்(கு)ஊற்றம் கோலாகி - ஒல்லுமெனின்","மாயம் பிறர்பொருட்கண் மாற்றுக மானத்தான்","ஆயின் அழிதல் அறிவு"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls it wisdom to bring the good to light, forget the bad, be quickly a staff of strength to living beings, turn deceit away from others' property, and, for a man of honour, to perish.","n":"The list ends on a hard turn: after four acts of care and honesty, the fifth wise act is to die, and only for the man of honour (மானத்தான் ஆயின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","maanam","arul","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 56","lb":"SP56","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நல்ல வெளிப்படுத்துத்","bringing the good to light is wisdom",1],["தீய மறந்தொழிந்து","forgetting the bad is wisdom",1],["உயிர்க்(கு)ஊற்றம் கோலாகி","being quickly a staff of strength to living beings is wisdom",2],["மாயம் பிறர்பொருட்கண்","turning deceit away from others' property is wisdom",3],["அழிதல்","for a man of honour, perishing is wisdom",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:57":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":59,"t":["தன்நிலையும் தாழாத் தொழில்நிலையும் துப்பெதிர்ந்தார்","இல்நிலையும் ஈடில் இயல்நிலையும் - துன்னி","அளந்தறிந்து செய்வான் அரசமைச்சன் யாதும்","பிளந்தறியும் பேராற்ற லான்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the king's minister, with his great power to split anything open and know it, acts only after closely measuring his own position, the state of the work that must not fail, the state of the houses of those who oppose him in strength, and the matchless order of things.","n":"The Tamil names four நிலை; the minister's own power in the last line may be the fifth root. We give four items.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","aaraaydhal","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 57","lb":"SP57","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["தன்நிலையும்","his own position is measured before he acts",1],["தாழாத் தொழில்நிலையும்","the state of the work that must not fail is measured",1],["இல்நிலையும்","the state of the houses of those who oppose him in strength is measured",2],["ஈடில் இயல்நிலையும்","the matchless order of things is measured",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:58":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":60,"t":["பொருள்போகம் அஞ்சாமை பொன்றுங்கால் போந்த","அருள்போகா ஆரறம்என்(று) ஐந்தும் - இருள்தீரக்","கூறப் படுங்குணத்தான் கூர்வேல்வல் வேந்தனால்","தேறப் படுங்குணத்தி னான்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five qualities (wealth, enjoyment, fearlessness, a grace that does not leave even at the hour of death, and rare dharma) and says the man who has them is the one a king of sharp spear can trust.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arul","anjaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 58","lb":"SP58","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"king","it":[["பொருள்","wealth is a quality of the man the king trusts",1],["போகம்","enjoyment is a quality of the man the king trusts",1],["அஞ்சாமை","fearlessness is a quality of the man the king trusts",1],["அருள்","a grace that does not leave even at the hour of death is a quality of the trusted man",2],["ஆரறம்","rare dharma is a quality of the trusted man",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:59":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":61,"t":["நன்புலத்து வையடக்கி நாளும்மா டோ போற்றிப்","புன்கலத்தைச் செய்(து) எருப் போற்றியபின் - இன்புலத்தின்","பண்கலப்பை என்றிலை பாற்படுப் பான்உழவோன்","நுண்கலப்பை நூலோது வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the farmer, in a good field, stores straw, tends the cattle daily, readies the small tools and tends the manure before he sets the fitted plough to the soil, and those who recite the texts hold a finer plough.","n":"The last line turns the farmer's labour into an image for study: the scholar's plough is the subtle one. Line 1 (மா டோ) and line 3 (என்றிலை) are doubtful, so the division is ours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 59","lb":"SP59","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["வையடக்கி","storing the straw is part of the farmer's care",1],["மா டோ","tending the cattle daily is part of his care",1],["புன்கலத்தை","readying the small tools is part of his care",2],["எருப்","tending the manure is part of his care",2],["பண்கலப்பை","the fitted plough is set to the field last",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:60":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":62,"t":["ஏலாமை நன்றீதல் தீதுபண்(பு) இல்லார்க்குச்","சாலாமை நன்றுநூல் சாயினும் - சாலாமை","நன்று தவநனி செய்தல்தீ(து) என்பாரை","இன்றுகா(று) யாம்கண் டிலம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five claims and says it has never yet seen anyone who makes them: that not receiving is good, that giving is bad, that falling short is good for those without character, that falling short is good even when learning fails, and that doing much penance is bad.","n":"The division turns on sandhi (நன்றீதல் may be நன்று ஈதல்) and on how far என்பாரை reaches. The first claim sits oddly among absurdities, since refusing gifts is praised elsewhere; it may be read as 'refusing to give', or the closing may govern only the last claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","kalvi","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 60","lb":"SP60","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["ஏலாமை","no one is seen saying that not receiving is good",1],["நன்றீதல்","no one is seen saying that giving is bad",1],["சாலாமை","no one is seen saying that falling short is good for those without character",2],["நூல் சாயினும்","no one is seen saying that falling short is good even when learning fails",2],["தவநனி செய்தல்","no one is seen saying that doing much penance is bad",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:3"},"sp:61":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":63,"t":["அரம்போல் கிளையடங்காய் பெண்வியக்கத் தொண்டு","மரம்போல் மகன்மாறாய் நின்று - கரம்போலக்","கள்ளநோய் காணும் அயல்ஐந்தும் ஆகுமேல்","உள்ளநோய் வேண்டா உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five who live close to a man (kin who wear him down like a file and will not be checked, a wife who makes herself a wonder to others, a servant who stands unmoving like a tree, a son who turns against him, and a neighbour who watches like a hidden disease) and says that if these five are his, a life needs no other disease.","n":"The predicate makes the household itself the sickness: every one of the five is someone a man cannot easily leave. Line 1 is hard (அடங்காய், பெண்வியக்க), தொண்டு is read as the servant whose simile மரம்போல் runs onto line 2, and கரம் may mean poison rather than hand, so the first three portraits are readings, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","noy","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 61","lb":"SP61","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["அரம்போல் கிளை","kin who wear one down like a file and will not be held in check are a disease",1],["பெண்வியக்க","a wife who makes herself a thing for others to wonder at is a disease",1],["தொண்டு","a servant who stands unmoving like a tree is a disease",1],["மகன்மாறாய்","a son who stands against his father is a disease",2],["கள்ளநோய் காணும் அயல்","a neighbour who watches one like a hidden disease is a disease",3]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:62":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":64,"t":["நீர்அறம் நன்று நிழல்நன்று தன்இல்லுள்","பார்அறம் நன்றுபாத்(து) உண்பானேல் - பேரறம்","நன்று தளிசாலை நாட்டல் பெரும்போகம்","ஒன்றுமாம் சால வுடன்"],"p":"","r":"The verse calls good the gift of water, the gift of shade, and charity within one's own house when the householder eats only after sharing, calls the founding of a temple and a hall great virtue, and says that together these bring great enjoyment.","n":"The list climbs from a drink of water to a founded building, and நன்று gives way to பேரறம் at the fourth item, so the verse ranks as it counts. Only the third item carries a condition (பாத்து உண்பானேல்): charity at home counts if the giver eats after sharing. பார்அறம் is not certain in sense. The fruit named is பெரும்போகம், enjoyment, not release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","oppuravu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 62","lb":"SP62","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["நீர்அறம்","the charity of giving water is good",1],["நிழல்","giving shade is good",1],["தன்இல்லுள் பார்அறம்","charity within one's own house is good, if the giver eats only after sharing",1],["தளி","founding a temple is great virtue",3],["சாலை","founding a hall for travellers is great virtue",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:63":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":65,"t":["பிடிப்பிச்சை பின்னிறை ஐயங்கூழ் கூற்றோ(டு)","எடுத்திரந்த உப்(பு)இத் துணையோ(டு) - அடுத்துச்","சிறுபயம் என்னார் சிதவலிப்(பு) ஈவார்","பெறுபயன்பின் சாலப் பெரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists small gifts (a handful of alms, a little given afterwards, alms of gruel, salt that the giver had himself begged and brought, and a torn rag) and says that those who give them without calling them of small use gain a very great fruit hereafter.","n":"The gifts are a poor man's: salt he begged for himself (எடுத்திரந்த உப்பு) and a rag. The condition is the giver's attitude (சிறுபயம் என்னார்), not the size of the gift. பின்னிறை and சிதவலிப்பு are uncertain words, so the boundaries of the second and fifth items are a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 63","lb":"SP63","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["பிடிப்பிச்சை","a handful of alms, given without calling it small, bears great fruit",1],["பின்னிறை","a small measure given afterwards bears great fruit",1],["ஐயங்கூழ்","gruel given as alms bears great fruit",1],["எடுத்திரந்த உப்(பு)","salt that the giver begged and brought bears great fruit when given",2],["சிதவலிப்(பு)","a torn rag given bears great fruit",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:64":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":66,"t":["வெந்தீங்காண் வெண்ணெய் மெழுகுநீர் சேர்மண்உப்(பு)","அந்த மகன்சார்ந்த தந்தையென்(று) - ஐந்தினுள்","ஒன்றுபோல் உள்நெகிழ்ந்து ஈயின் சிறிதெனினும்","குன்றுபோற் கூடும் பயன்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five things that melt (butter that sees the hot fire, wax, earth that meets water, salt, and a father whose son has come to him) and says that one who gives with a heart melted like any one of them gathers fruit like a hill, however small the gift.","n":"The five counted things are images, not gifts: they measure the giver's heart. Four are matter softened by heat or water; the fifth, the father at his son's approach, is the one human case and is set last, so the list ends where giving's feeling is most familiar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","manam","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 64","lb":"SP64","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["வெந்தீங்காண் வெண்ணெய்","butter that sees the hot fire melts, and the giver's heart should melt like it",1],["மெழுகு","wax melts, and the giver's heart should melt like it",1],["நீர் சேர்மண்","earth that meets water softens, and the giver's heart should soften like it",1],["உப்(பு)","salt dissolves, and the giver's heart should dissolve like it",1],["மகன்சார்ந்த தந்தை","a father softens when his son comes close, and the giver's heart should soften like his",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தினுள்","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:65":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":67,"t":["குளங்கொட்டுக் கோடு பதித்துவழி சீத்து","உளந்தொட்(டு) உழுவயல் ஆக்கி - வளந்தொட்டுப்","பாகு படுங்கிணற்றோ(டு) என்றிவ்ஐம் பாற்படுப்பான்","ஏகும் சுவர்க்கத்(து) இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that one who carries out five works (digging a tank, setting its bank, clearing a road, breaking ground into a ploughed field, and digging a well whose water is shared) goes sweetly to heaven.","n":"All five are works on land and water that others use after the doer. The reward is சுவர்க்கம், heaven, not release; the Jain works keep these merits in the round of births. உளந்தொட்டு is read as breaking hard ground for the plough. The count word is fused with என்று as written (என்றிவ்ஐம் பாற்படுப்பான்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","uzhavu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 65","lb":"SP65","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["குளங்கொட்டு","digging a tank leads to heaven",1],["கோடு பதித்து","setting a tank's bank leads to heaven",1],["வழி சீத்து","clearing a road leads to heaven",1],["உழுவயல் ஆக்கி","turning ground into ploughed field leads to heaven",2],["பாகு படுங்கிணற்றோ(டு)","digging a well whose water is shared out leads to heaven",3]],"cw":"என்றிவ்ஐம்","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:66":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":68,"t":["போர்த்தும் உரிந்திட்டும் பூசியும் நீட்டியும்","ஓர்த்(து) பால்மறைத்(து) உண்பான்மேய் - ஓர்த்த","அறமாம்மேல் சொல்பொறுக்க அன்றேல் கலிக்கண்","துறவறம்பொய் இல்லறமே வாய்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names outward forms of ascetics (wrapping the body, stripping it bare, smearing it, growing the hair long, and eating hidden and guarded) and says that if these are a virtue truly thought out one should bear what is said, but otherwise, in the Kali age, renunciation is false and household life alone is true.","n":"The five forms answer to the mendicant orders of the period (clothed, naked, smeared, long-haired), though no sect is named. The close is a strong judgement for a Jain work: in கலி the household is the true path and renunciation a lie, unless the renunciant's virtue is truly considered. Line 2 (ஓர்த்து பால்மறைத்து உண்பான்மேய்) is obscure, so the fifth item is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vesham","koodaozhukkam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 66","lb":"SP66","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["போர்த்தும்","wrapping the body is one of the outward forms of renunciation",1],["உரிந்திட்டும்","stripping the body bare is one of the outward forms of renunciation",1],["பூசியும்","smearing the body is one of the outward forms of renunciation",1],["நீட்டியும்","letting the hair grow long is one of the outward forms of renunciation",1],["பால்மறைத்(து) உண்பான்","eating hidden and guarded is one of the outward forms of renunciation",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:67":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":69,"t":["தான்பிறந்த இல்நினைந்து தன்னைக் கடைப்பிடித்துத்","தான்பிற ரால்கருதற் பாடுணர்ந்து - தான்பிறரால்","சாவ எனவாழான் சான்றேரால் பல்யாண்டும்","வாழ்க எனவாழ்தல் நன்று"],"p":"","r":"The verse says it is good to live remembering the house one was born into, holding firmly to oneself, knowing how others regard one, not living so that others say 'let him die', but so that the worthy say for many years 'may he live'.","n":"The five run from the inside out: one's birth-house, oneself, others' regard, others' curse, the worthy's blessing. Two opposite speeches by others (சாவ, வாழ்க) close the verse, so a life is judged by what is said of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pugazh","adakkam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 67","lb":"SP67","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["தான்பிறந்த இல்நினைந்து","remembering the house one was born into is part of living well",1],["தன்னைக் கடைப்பிடித்துத்","holding firmly to oneself is part of living well",1],["பிற ரால்கருதற் பாடுணர்ந்து","knowing how one is regarded by others is part of living well",2],["சாவ எனவாழான்","not living so that others wish one dead is part of living well",3],["வாழ்க எனவாழ்தல்","living so that the worthy wish one long years is good",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:68":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":70,"t":["நெடுக்கல் குறுக்கல் துறைநீர்நீ டாடல்","வடுத்தீர் பகல்வாய் உறையே - வடுத்தீரா","ஆகும்அந் நான்கொழிந்(து) ஐந்தடக்கு வானாநின்","வேகும்பம் வேண்டான் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names four practices (drawing out and cutting short, long bathing in the waters of a ghat, and staying in by day) that do not remove the blemish, and says that one who leaves these four and restrains the five senses, wanting nothing, is released.","n":"The verse has two counts, நான்கு and ஐந்து: four rejected practices and the restraint of the five senses that it approves; they are given as five items, the last of a different kind. What is drawn out and cut short (food, hair, the body) is not said, and வேகும்பம் on line 4 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thavam","thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 68","lb":"SP68","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["நெடுக்கல்","drawing out (a practice) does not remove the blemish",1],["குறுக்கல்","cutting short (a practice) does not remove the blemish",1],["துறைநீர்நீ டாடல்","long bathing in the ghat's waters does not remove the blemish",1],["பகல்வாய் உறையே","staying in by day does not remove the blemish",2],["ஐந்தடக்கு","restraining the five senses, wanting nothing, brings release",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:69":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":71,"t":["கொன்றான் கொலையை உடன்பட்டான் கோடாது","கொன்றதனைக் கொண்டான் கொழுக்குங்கால் - கொன்றதனை","அட்டான் இடவுண்டான் ஐவரினும் ஆகுமெனக்","கட்டெறிந்த பாவம் கருது"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five who share in a killing (the one who killed, the one who agreed to it, the one who took the killed thing without hesitation when it was fat, the one who cooked it and the one who ate it when served) and says the sin should be reckoned as bound on all five.","n":"The chain runs from the act to the meal, and the eater, last, is the one least likely to think himself guilty; the verse closes the gap the eater relies on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 69","lb":"SP69","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கொன்றான்","the one who killed bears the sin",1],["கொலையை உடன்பட்டான்","the one who agreed to the killing bears the sin",1],["கொன்றதனைக் கொண்டான்","the one who took the killed creature bears the sin",2],["அட்டான்","the one who cooked it bears the sin",3],["இடவுண்டான்","the one who ate it when served bears the sin",3]],"cw":"ஐவரினும்","nr":[["diet"],"the Jain rule against flesh-eating of the period, stated as a share in the sin of killing"],"ch":"sp:4"},"sp:70":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":72,"t":["சிறைக்கிடந்தார் செந்தார்க்கு நோற்பார் பலநாள்","உறைக்கிடந்தார் ஒன்றிடையிட்(டு) உண்பார் - பிறைக்கிடந்து","முற்றனைத்தும் உண்ணா தவர்க்கீந்தார் மன்னவராய்க்","கற்றனைத்தும் வாழ்வார் கலந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five kinds of people (those lying in prison, those who fast for the ... (perhaps the dead), those who have lain many days in ..., those who eat only every other day, and those who eat nothing from the crescent to the full moon) and says that those who gave to them will live as kings in all fullness.","n":"Four of the five are fasters by vow; the prisoner, first, goes hungry by force, so the list joins chosen and imposed hunger. செந்தார்க்கு and உறைக்கிடந்தார் are uncertain (செந்தார் may stand for செத்தார், the dead).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","pasi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 70","lb":"SP70","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["சிறைக்கிடந்தார்","giving to those lying in prison brings kingship",1],["செந்தார்க்கு நோற்பார்","giving to those who fast for (perhaps) the dead brings kingship",1],["உறைக்கிடந்தார்","giving to those who have lain confined many days brings kingship",2],["ஒன்றிடையிட்(டு) உண்பார்","giving to those who eat on alternate days brings kingship",2],["முற்றனைத்தும் உண்ணா தவர்","giving to those who fast from the crescent to the full moon brings kingship",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:71":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":73,"t":["ஈன்றெடுத்தல் சூல்புறஞ் செய்தல் குழவியை","ஏன்றெடுத்தல் சூலேற்ற கன்னியை - ஆன்ற","அழிந்தானை இல்வைத்தல் பேரறமா ஆற்ற","மொழிந்தார் முதுநூலார் முன்பு"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists five acts that, it says, the authors of the old books declared great virtue: delivering a child, caring for a pregnant woman, taking up an infant, sheltering in one's house an unmarried girl who has conceived, and sheltering a man who is ruined.","n":"The verse appeals to authority (முதுநூலார்) rather than to a fruit. The unmarried pregnant girl is counted among those to be taken in, not cast out; the verse names her condition plainly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","oppuravu","arul","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 71","lb":"SP71","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஈன்றெடுத்தல்","delivering a child is great virtue",1],["சூல்புறஞ் செய்தல்","caring for a pregnant woman is great virtue",1],["குழவியை","taking up an infant is great virtue",1],["சூலேற்ற கன்னியை","sheltering an unmarried girl who has conceived is great virtue",2],["அழிந்தானை","sheltering a ruined man in one's house is great virtue",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period view of the unmarried girl who has conceived, whom the verse counts it great virtue to shelter"],"ch":"sp:4"},"sp:72":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":74,"t":["வலியிழந்தார் மூத்தார் வடக்கிருந்தார் நோயால்","நலி(பு)அழிந்தார் நாட்(டு)அறைபோய் நைந்தார் - மெலிவொழிய","இன்னவராம் என்னாராய் ஈந்த ஒருதுற்று","மன்னவராய்ச் செய்யும் மதித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that a single mouthful given, without asking who they are, to those who have lost their strength, the old, those who sit facing north to die, those wasted by disease, and those ruined by losing their land, makes the giver a king.","n":"இன்னவராம் என்னாராய், 'not asking who they are', makes the gift blind to the recipient's worth, in a book that elsewhere rewards gifts to ascetics. வடக்கிருந்தார், those who sit facing north to die, stand here among the needy rather than the heroic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vadakkiruththal","noy","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 72","lb":"SP72","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["வலியிழந்தார்","a mouthful given to those who have lost their strength makes the giver a king",1],["மூத்தார்","a mouthful given to the old makes the giver a king",1],["வடக்கிருந்தார்","a mouthful given to those who sit facing north to die makes the giver a king",1],["நலி(பு)அழிந்தார்","a mouthful given to those wasted by disease makes the giver a king",2],["நாட்(டு)அறைபோய் நைந்தார்","a mouthful given to those ruined by the loss of their land makes the giver a king",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:73":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":75,"t":["கலங்காமைக் காத்தல் கருப்பஞ் சிதைந்தால்","இலங்காமை பேரறத்தால் ஈற்றம் - விலங்காமைக்","கோடல் குழவி மருந்து வெருட்டாமை","நாடின் அறம்பெருமை நாட்டு"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists acts of care around birth (guarding the pregnant woman from distress, not neglecting her when the embryo is harmed, help at the delivery, taking up the infant without shrinking from it, medicine for the child, and not frightening it) and says that, examined, the greatness of virtue lies in these.","n":"The verse has no count word. It is divided here into six on the six negated or verbal nouns, but விலங்காமைக் கோடல் may join குழவி, and இலங்காமை after கருப்பஞ் சிதைந்தால் is unclear, so five is as defensible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","makkal","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 73","lb":"SP73","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["கலங்காமைக் காத்தல்","guarding the pregnant woman from distress is great virtue",1],["கருப்பஞ் சிதைந்தால்","not neglecting her when the embryo is harmed is great virtue",1],["ஈற்றம்","help at the delivery is great virtue",2],["விலங்காமைக்","taking up the newborn without shrinking from it is great virtue",2],["மருந்து","medicine for the infant is great virtue",3],["வெருட்டாமை","not frightening the infant is great virtue",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:74":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":76,"t":["சூலாமை சூலிற் படுந்துன்பம் ஈன்றபின்","ஏலாமை ஏற்றால் வளர்ப்பருமை - சால்பவை","வல்லாமை வாய்ப்ப அறிபவர் உண்ணாமை","கொல்லாமை நன்றால் கொழித்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets out the pains of bringing a life into being (failing to conceive, the suffering of pregnancy, being unable to nurse after the birth, the hardship of rearing once begun, and the incapacity that follows) and says that those who know these well find, on sifting, that not eating and not killing are good.","n":"The five pains are given as items; the predicate the verse draws from them is உண்ணாமை and கொல்லாமை. உண்ணாமை is read as not eating flesh, beside not killing, but it may mean fasting. ஏலாமை and சால்பவை வல்லாமை are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","makkal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 74","lb":"SP74","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["சூலாமை","failing to conceive is one of the pains the wise weigh",1],["சூலிற் படுந்துன்பம்","the suffering of pregnancy is one of the pains the wise weigh",1],["ஏலாமை","being unable (to nurse) after the birth is one of the pains the wise weigh",2],["வளர்ப்பருமை","the hardship of rearing once undertaken is one of the pains the wise weigh",2],["வல்லாமை","the incapacity that follows is one of the pains the wise weigh",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["diet"],"the Jain abstention from eating flesh and killing of the period, argued from the pains of bearing life"],"ch":"sp:4"},"sp:75":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":77,"t":["சிக்கர் சிதடர் சிதலைபோல் வாயுடையார்","துக்கர் துருநாமர் தூக்குங்கால் - தொக்க","வருநோய்கள் முன்னாளில் தீர்த்தாரே இந்நாள்","ஒருநோயும் இன்றிவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who in former days cured the gathered diseases of five kinds of sufferer (சிக்கர், the blind or dull, those whose mouths are eaten as by termites, the afflicted, and those with piles) now live free of any disease.","n":"முன்னாளில் and இந்நாள் are read as an earlier birth and this one: present health is the fruit of past healing. The disease words (சிக்கர், சிதடர், துக்கர், துருநாமர்) are rare and their senses are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 75","lb":"SP75","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["சிக்கர்","curing the சிக்கர் earns a life without disease",1],["சிதடர்","curing the blind or dull earns a life without disease",1],["சிதலைபோல் வாயுடையார்","curing those whose mouths are eaten as by termites earns a life without disease",1],["துக்கர்","curing the afflicted earns a life without disease",2],["துருநாமர்","curing those with piles earns a life without disease",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:76":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":78,"t":["பக்கம் படாமை ஒருவர்க்குப் பாடேற்றல்","தக்கம் படாமை தவமல்லாத் - தக்கார்","இழியினர்க்கே யானும் பசித்தார்க்(கு)ஊண் ஈதல்","கழிசினங் காத்தல் கடன்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five duties: not taking sides, taking on another's suffering, not being swayed, giving food to the hungry even if they are the low who are not worthy ascetics, and holding back excessive anger.","n":"The fourth item ranks recipients (தவமல்லாத் தக்கார், இழியினர்) and then sets the ranking aside: the hungry are fed even if low. இழியினர் here ranks people; it may be birth-rank or low conduct. தக்கம் படாமை and பாடேற்றல் are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","naduvu","pasi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 76","lb":"SP76","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பக்கம் படாமை","not taking sides is a duty",1],["ஒருவர்க்குப் பாடேற்றல்","taking on suffering for another is a duty",1],["தக்கம் படாமை","not being swayed is a duty",2],["பசித்தார்க்(கு)ஊண் ஈதல்","giving food to the hungry, even the low, is a duty",3],["கழிசினங் காத்தல்","holding back excessive anger is a duty",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["caste"],"a ranking of recipients by worth and lowness (இழியினர்) of the period, which the verse sets aside for the hungry"],"ch":"sp:4"},"sp:77":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":79,"t":["புண்பட்டார் போற்றுவார் இல்லாதவர் போகுயிரார்","கண்கெட்டார் காலிரண்டும் இல்லாதார் -கண்கண்பட்(டு)","ஆழ்ந்து நெகிழ்ந்தவர்க்கு ஈந்தார் கடைபோக","வாழ்ந்து கழிவார் மகிழ்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those who gave to the wounded, to those with no one to care for them, to those whose life is ebbing, to those who have lost their eyes and to those without both legs, people sunk and broken, will live gladly to the end.","n":"Line 3 (ஆழ்ந்து நெகிழ்ந்தவர்க்கு) sums the five as those sunk and broken; it is read as a summary, not a sixth item.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 77","lb":"SP77","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["புண்பட்டார்","giving to the wounded brings a glad life to the end",1],["போற்றுவார் இல்லாதவர்","giving to those with no one to care for them brings a glad life to the end",1],["போகுயிரார்","giving to those whose life is ebbing brings a glad life to the end",1],["கண்கெட்டார்","giving to those who have lost their eyes brings a glad life to the end",1],["காலிரண்டும் இல்லாதார்","giving to those without both legs brings a glad life to the end",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:78":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":80,"t":["பஞ்சப் பொழுதகத்தே பாத்துண்பான் காவாதான்","அஞ்சா(து) உடைபடையுள் போந்தெறிவான் - எஞ்சாதே","உண்பதுமுன் ஈவான் குழவி பலிகொடுப்பான்","எண்பதின் மேலும்வாழ் வாண்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five who live beyond eighty: one who shares his food in a time of famine, one who does not hoard, one who goes fearlessly into a breaking army and strikes, one who gives before he eats and holds nothing back, and one who makes offerings for children.","n":"The list joins the householder's sharing with the soldier's courage under one reward, long life. காவாதான் may belong with the next clause (one who, not guarding himself, enters the army), and குழவி பலிகொடுப்பான் may be one who feeds children or one who offers for them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aayul","anjaamai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 78","lb":"SP78","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["பஞ்சப் பொழுதகத்தே பாத்துண்பான்","one who shares his food in famine lives beyond eighty",1],["காவாதான்","one who does not hoard lives beyond eighty",1],["உடைபடையுள் போந்தெறிவான்","one who goes fearlessly into a breaking army and strikes lives beyond eighty",2],["உண்பதுமுன் ஈவான்","one who gives before he eats lives beyond eighty",3],["குழவி பலிகொடுப்பான்","one who makes offerings for children lives beyond eighty",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:79":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":81,"t":["வரைவில்லாப் பெண்வையார் மன்னைப்புற்(று) ஏறார்","புரையில்லார் நள்ளார்போர் வேந்தன் - வரைபோல்","கடுங்களிறு விட்டுழிச் செல்லார் வழங்கார்","கொடும்புலி கொட்கும் வழி"],"p":"","r":"The verse lists what the wise (implied) shun: they do not keep a woman without bounds, do not climb an anthill, do not befriend the worthless or a warring king, do not go where a fierce elephant like a hill is let loose, and do not travel the road where a cruel tiger prowls.","n":"The list sets people (a woman without bounds, the worthless, the warring king) among natural dangers (anthill, elephant, tiger), so the human ones are read as hazards of the same kind. வையார் may mean 'do not keep' or 'do not revile', and மன்னைப்புற்று is unclear. The verse has no count word; six are given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mannar","inam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 79","lb":"SP79","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வரைவில்லாப் பெண்","a woman without bounds is shunned",1],["மன்னைப்புற்(று)","climbing an anthill is shunned",1],["புரையில்லார்","friendship with the worthless is shunned",2],["போர் வேந்தன்","friendship with a warring king is shunned",2],["கடுங்களிறு","the place where a fierce elephant is let loose is shunned",3],["கொடும்புலி","the road where a cruel tiger prowls is shunned",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period view that sets the woman 'without bounds' among dangers to be shunned"],"ch":"sp:4"},"sp:80":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":82,"t":["தக்கார் வழிகெடாது ஆகுந் தகாதவர்","உக்க வழியராய் ஒல்குவர் - தக்க","இனத்தினான் ஆகும் பழியும் புகழும்","மனத்தினான் ஆகும் மதி"],"p":"","r":"The verse says the way of the worthy does not perish while the unworthy sink on a fallen way; blame and fame come from one's company, but understanding comes from one's own mind.","n":"The last two lines divide what company governs (reputation) from what it does not (மதி, understanding). The count of five is a reading of a verse without a count word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 80","lb":"SP80","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["தக்கார் வழி","the way of the worthy does not perish",1],["தகாதவர்","the unworthy sink on a fallen way",1],["பழியும்","blame comes from one's company",3],["புகழும்","fame comes from one's company",3],["மதி","understanding comes from one's own mind",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:4"},"sp:81":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":83,"t":["கழிந்தவை தானிரங்கான் கைலாரா நச்சான்","இழிந்தவை இன்புறான் இல்லார் - மொழிந்தவை","மென்மொழியால் உள்நெகிழ்ந்து ஈவானேல் விண்ணோரால்","இன்மொழியால் ஏத்தப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five marks of a man (he does not grieve over what has passed, does not crave what is out of his hand, takes no pleasure in base things, speaks softly, and gives what the poor ask with a heart that melts) and says such a man is praised by the gods in sweet words.","n":"The last two marks belong together: the soft word and the melting heart are the manner of the gift, so the list ends in giving done well, not giving alone. கைலாரா is hard to construe; read here as what has not come into the hand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inchol","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 81","lb":"SP81","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["கழிந்தவை தானிரங்கான்","one who does not grieve over what has passed is praised by the gods",1],["கைலாரா நச்சான்","one who does not crave what is not in his hand is praised",1],["இழிந்தவை இன்புறான்","one who takes no delight in base things is praised",2],["மென்மொழியால்","one who answers the poor in soft words is praised",3],["உள்நெகிழ்ந்து ஈவானேல்","one who gives with a heart that melts within is praised",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:82":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":84,"t":["காடுபோல் கட்கினிய இல்லம் பிறர்பொருள்","ஓடுபோல் தாரம் பிறந்ததாய் - ஊடுபோய்க்","கோத்தின்னா சொல்லானாய்க் கொல்லானேல் பல்லவர்","ஒத்தினால் என்ன குறை"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five marks of a man (he sees his house, lovely to the eye, as a wilderness, others' wealth as a potsherd, others' wives as the mother who bore him, he does not go between people stringing harsh words, and he does not kill) and asks what he would lack even without the recitation of the many scriptures.","n":"The five line up with the Jain lay vows: non-attachment, not stealing, not desiring another's wife, no slander or untruth, not killing. The closing question sets this conduct above scriptural recitation; பல்லவர் ஒத்தினால் is read as ஓத்து, scripture, which the spelling leaves open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piranil","kalavu","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 82","lb":"SP82","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["காடுபோல் கட்கினிய இல்லம்","a house sweet to the eye is to be seen as a wilderness",1],["பிறர்பொருள்","others' wealth is to be seen as a potsherd",1],["தாரம்","another's wife is to be seen as one's own mother",2],["கோத்தின்னா சொல்லானாய்","one does not go between people stringing harsh words together",3],["கொல்லானேல்","one does not kill",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:83":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":85,"t":["தோல்கன்று காட்டிக் கறவார் கறந்தபால்","பாற்பட்டார் உண்ணார் பழிபாவம் - பாற்பட்டார்","ஏற்றவாது இன்புற்று வாழ்வன வீடழியக்","கூற்றுவப்பச் செய்யார் கொணர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says that those on the side of virtue do not milk a cow by showing it a stuffed calf-skin, do not drink milk so drawn, do not do what brings blame and sin, and do not seize creatures living content in what they have, ruining their homes to Death's delight.","n":"The first two items are one practice seen from milker and drinker: the deceit worked on the cow whose calf is dead taints the milk too. The list runs from that small cruelty to the killing of creatures, so the trick on the cow is placed on the same line as killing. The construal of கறந்தபால் with பாற்பட்டார் is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arul","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 83","lb":"SP83","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["தோல்கன்று காட்டிக் கறவார்","the good do not milk a cow by showing it a calf's skin",1],["கறந்தபால்","the good do not drink milk drawn that way",1],["பழி","the good do not do what brings blame",2],["பாவம்","the good do not do what brings sin",2],["வாழ்வன வீடழியக்","the good do not seize contented creatures and destroy their homes for Death's pleasure",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:84":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":86,"t":["நகையொரு மந்திரம் நட்டார்க்கு வாரம்","பகையொரு பாட்டுரையென்(று) ஐந்தும் - தொகையொடு","மூத்தோர் இருந்துழி வேண்டார் முதுநூலுள்","யாத்தார் அறிந்தவர் ஆய்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five things not wanted where elders are seated (laughter, private counsel, partiality to friends, enmity, and song or idle talk) and says the ancient treatises set this down after those who knew had examined it.","n":"It cites old treatises as its authority, an appeal the verses around it rarely make. நட்டார்க்கு வாரம் is read as favouring one's friends, a fault of judgment in an assembly rather than of manners.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","avai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 84","lb":"SP84","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நகை","laughter is not wanted where elders sit",1],["மந்திரம்","private consultation is not wanted where elders sit",1],["நட்டார்க்கு வாரம்","partiality to friends is not wanted where elders sit",1],["பகை","enmity is not wanted where elders sit",2],["பாட்டுரை","song or idle talk is not wanted where elders sit",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:85":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":87,"t":["சத்தமெய்ஞ் ஞானம் தருக்கம் சமயமே","வித்தகர் கண்டவீ(டு) உள்ளிட்டாங்(கு) - அத்தகத்து","அந்தஇவ் ஐந்தும் அறிவான் தலையாய","சிந்திப்பிற் சிட்டன் சிறந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five branches of knowledge (grammar of sound, true knowledge, logic, the religious systems, and release as the wise have seen it) and says one who knows all five is, on reflection, a learned man of the first rank.","n":"சத்தமெய்ஞ் ஞானம் is divided as சத்தம் and மெய்ஞ்ஞானம். The verse ranks learning, and ranks it by subject: release closes the list as the highest thing to be known. It is the first of a pair; verse 86 names the middle rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","gnaanam","samayam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 85","lb":"SP85","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["சத்த","knowing the science of sound and grammar marks the first-rank learned man",1],["மெய்ஞ் ஞானம்","knowing true knowledge marks him",1],["தருக்கம்","knowing logic marks him",1],["சமயமே","knowing the religious systems marks him",1],["வித்தகர் கண்டவீ(டு)","knowing release as the wise have seen it marks him",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:86":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":88,"t":["கண்ணுங்கால் கண்ணும் கணிதமே யாழினோடு","எண்ணுங்கால் சாந்தே இலைநறுக்கிட்(டு) - எண்ணுதல்","இட்டஇவ் ஐந்தும் அறிவான் இடையாய","சிட்டனென்(று) எண்ணப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five arts (one read as the art of the eye, arithmetic, the lute, the making of unguents, and cutting and arranging leaves) and says one who knows these five is reckoned a learned man of the middle rank.","n":"Set against verse 85, the pair grades learning: doctrine and release make the first rank, crafts and accomplishments the middle. The first item, கண்ணும், is unclear; it may name an art of the eye such as painting or the reading of signs, or be only 'also'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 86","lb":"SP86","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணும்","an art of the eye, if that is the sense, marks the middle-rank learned man",1],["கணிதமே","arithmetic marks him",1],["யாழினோடு","the lute marks him",1],["சாந்தே","the making of unguents marks him",2],["இலைநறுக்கிட்(டு)","cutting and arranging leaves marks him",2]],"cw":"ஐந்தும்","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:87":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":89,"t":["நாணிலன் நாய்நன்கு நள்ளாதான் நாய்பெரியார்ப்","பேணிலன் நாய்பிறர் சேவகன்நாய் - ஏணில்","பொருந்திய பூண்முலையார் சேரிகைத்(து) இல்லான்","பருத்தி பகர்வழி நாய்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five men who are dogs: the shameless, the one who does not befriend well, the one who does not honour the great, the one in another's service, and the one with nothing in his hand who goes to the quarter of the jewelled courtesans.","n":"பருத்தி பகர்வழி, the lane where cotton is sold, is hard to fit; it may mark where the penniless man loiters or compare him to a dog in that lane. பிறர் சேவகன் may mean a servant or a soldier of another man. Verse 88 answers this list item by item.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","natpu","varaivinmagalir","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 87","lb":"SP87","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நாணிலன்","the shameless man is a dog",1],["நன்கு நள்ளாதான்","one who does not form good friendships is a dog",1],["பெரியார்ப்","one who does not honour the great is a dog",1],["பிறர் சேவகன்","one who is another's servant is a dog",2],["பூண்முலையார் சேரிகைத்(து) இல்லான்","one with nothing in hand who goes to the courtesans' quarter is a dog",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:88":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":90,"t":["நாண்எளிது பெண்மை நகையெளிது நட்டானேல்","ஏண்எளிது சேவக னேல்பெரியார் - பேண்எளிது","கொம்பு மறைக்கும் இடையாய் அளியன்மீ(து)","அம்பு பறத்தல் அரிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse says, to a woman addressed, that modesty comes easily to womanhood, laughter to a friend, steadfastness to a soldier, and reverence to the great, while arrows seldom fly at a man of compassion.","n":"It reverses verse 87 point by point: shame, friendship, the சேவகன் and honour of the great return, now as what comes easily. The last item turns from easy to rare, which is the list's twist. ஏண் is read as steadfastness and அளியன் as the compassionate man; both are readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","natpu","arul","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 88","lb":"SP88","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["நாண்","modesty is easy to womanhood",1],["நகை","laughter is easy if one is a friend",1],["ஏண்","steadfastness is easy if one is a soldier",2],["பேண்","reverence is easy toward the great",2],["அம்பு பறத்தல்","for an arrow to fly at a compassionate man is rare",4]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period claim about women: modesty treated as natural to womanhood"],"ch":"sp:5"},"sp:89":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":91,"t":["இன்சொலான் ஆகும் கிளமை இயல்பில்லா","வன்சொல்லின் ஆகும் பகைமைமன் - மென்சொல்லின்","ஓய்வில்லா ஆரருளாம் அவ்வருள் நன்மனத்தான்","வீவில்லா விடாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse traces a chain: sweet speech makes kin, harsh speech without good nature makes enemies, gentle speech brings unceasing grace, and that grace, in a good mind, becomes release that does not perish.","n":"The things are steps, not peers: speech leads to grace, grace to release, so the list is a ladder from manners to salvation; the Tamil gives four steps, not five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","kadunjol","arul","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 89","lb":"SP89","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["இன்சொலான்","sweet speech produces kinship",1],["வன்சொல்லின்","harsh speech without good nature produces enmity",2],["மென்சொல்லின்","gentle speech produces unceasing grace",2],["நன்மனத்தான்","grace in a good mind becomes undying release",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:90":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":92,"t":["தக்க(து) இளையான் தவம்செல்வன் ஊண்மறுத்தல்","தக்கது கற்புடை யாள்வனப்புத் - தக்க(து)","தழல்தண்என் தோளான் அறிவிலன் ஆயின்","நிழற்கண் முயிறாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says penance befits a young man, refusing food befits a rich man, and beauty befits a chaste woman, but a man with shoulders strong and fair who lacks understanding becomes like a red ant in the shade.","n":"Each virtue is fitting where it costs most: penance while youth pulls the other way, fasting when food is at hand. The fourth item turns from fitting to unfitting. தழல் may belong with a third தக்கது, and the image of the ant in the shade is read as a nuisance where one rests; the count of four is the Tamil's, not the title's five.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ilamai","karpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 90","lb":"SP90","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["இளையான் தவம்","penance is fitting in a young man",1],["செல்வன் ஊண்மறுத்தல்","refusing food is fitting in a rich man",1],["கற்புடை யாள்வனப்பு","beauty is fitting in a chaste woman",2],["தழல்தண்என் தோளான் அறிவிலன்","a strong-shouldered man without understanding is like a red ant in the shade",3]],"cw":"","nr":[["gender"],"a period norm: a woman's beauty counted fitting only with chastity"],"ch":"sp:5"},"sp:91":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":93,"t":["பொய்யால் சுவர்க்கம்வா யால்நிர யம்பொருள்","மையார் மடந்தையால் வாழ்வினிது - மெய்யென்றால்","மைத்தக நீண்ட மலர்க்கண்ணாய் தீதன்றால்","எத்தவ மானும் படல்"],"p":"","r":"The verse sets out claims that heaven comes by falsehood and hell by truth, and that wealth and a dark-eyed maiden make life sweet, and tells the woman addressed that if this were true, it would be no harm to take up any penance at all.","n":"Read as a reductio: the inverted claims about heaven and hell are absurd, so the conclusion drawn from them is mocked. The spacing of line 1 (வா யால்நிர யம்) and the sense of the close are unsure, and the verse may be read otherwise. It gives four claims, not five, divided at each instrument (falsehood, truth, wealth, the maiden).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","naragam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 91","lb":"SP91","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்யால் சுவர்க்கம்","heaven is said to come by falsehood",1],["யால்நிர யம்","hell is said to come by truth",1],["பொருள்","wealth is said to make life sweet",1],["மையார் மடந்தையால்","a dark-eyed maiden is said to make life sweet",2]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:92":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":94,"t":["புல்லறத்தின் நன்று மனைவாழ்க்கை போற்றுடைத்தே","நல்லறத்தா ரோடு நடக்கலாம் - நல்லறத்தார்க்(கு)","அட்டிட்டுண் டாற்றவாழ்ந் தார்களே இம்மையில்","அட்டிட்டுண் டாற்றவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse says household life is better than a mean dharma and worth guarding, since one can walk with the truly virtuous, and that those who cooked, served and then ate, feeding the virtuous, will live in this life too cooking, serving and eating in plenty.","n":"It does not set household above renunciation as such, only above புல்லறம், a poor or false dharma. The repeated அட்டிட்டுண்டு makes the reward the same act again: feeding now brings the means to feed later. The verse argues rather than counts; the three items are its three claims.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 92","lb":"SP92","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"householder","it":[["மனைவாழ்க்கை","household life is better than a mean dharma",1],["நல்லறத்தா ரோடு நடக்கலாம்","in it one can walk with the virtuous",2],["அட்டிட்டுண் டாற்றவாழ்ந் தார்களே","those who cooked, served and ate will live so again",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:93":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":95,"t":["ஈவது நன்றுதீ(து) ஈயாமை நல்லவர்","மேவது நன்றுமே வாதாரோ(டு) - ஓவாது","கேட்டுத் தலைநிற்க கேடில் உயர்கதிக்கே","ஓட்டுத் தவநிற்கும் ஊர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse says giving is good and withholding evil, joining the good is good and joining those who are not is evil, and that one who keeps hearing and stands firm has a penance that carries him, riding, to the flawless high state.","n":"The first four items are two pairs of good and evil; the fifth is an instruction whose fruit is the high state. Hearing and holding firm are themselves called a penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inam","kelvi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 93","lb":"SP93","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["ஈவது","giving is good",1],["ஈயாமை","not giving is evil",1],["நல்லவர்","joining the good is good",1],["மே வாதாரோ(டு)","joining those who are not good is evil",2],["கேட்டுத் தலைநிற்க","hearing without ceasing and standing firm is a penance that leads to the high state",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:94":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":96,"t":["உண்இடத்தும் ஒன்னார் மெலிவிடத்தும் மந்திரங்கொண்(டு)","எண்இடத்தும் செல்லாமை தான்தலையே - எண்ணி","உரைப்பூசல் கோறல் உயர்தவமேல் கங்கைக்","கரைப்பூசை போற்றக் கடை"],"p":"","r":"The verse ranks conduct: not going where others are eating, where enemies are brought low, or where counsel is taken, is foremost; ending quarrels of words after reflection is high penance; and, beside these, the worship on the bank of the Ganga is lowest.","n":"The three ranks (தலை, உயர், கடை) put restraint first and pilgrimage worship last, so a celebrated rite is counted but placed beneath manners of speech and presence. உரைப்பூசல் கோறல் may instead mean wordy quarrels and killing; கோறல் usually means killing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","theerththam","adakkam","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 94","lb":"SP94","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["உண்இடத்தும்","not going where others eat is foremost",1],["ஒன்னார் மெலிவிடத்தும்","not going where enemies are brought low is foremost",1],["மந்திரங்கொண்(டு)","not going where counsel is taken is foremost",1],["உரைப்பூசல் கோறல்","ending quarrels of words is high penance",3],["கரைப்பூசை","worship on the Ganga's bank is lowest",4]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:95":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":97,"t":["பத்தினி சேவகன் பொத்தில் கடுந்தவசி","பொத்தில் பொருள்திறத்துச் செவ்வியான் - பொத்தின்று","வைத்தார் அதுவழக்குஞ் சான்றவர் தம்செம்மை","செத்தால் அறிக சிறந்து"],"p":"","r":"The verse names five (the chaste wife, the soldier, the flawless ascetic of severe penance, the man upright in dealings with wealth, and the worthy who keep a trust without flaw and judge cases) and says their rightness should be known when they have died.","n":"The test is deferred to death: none of these virtues can be certified while the life can still fail. The list spans house, war, renunciation, trade and judgment, so the claim covers every station.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","thavam","naduvu","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 95","lb":"SP95","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["பத்தினி","the chaste wife's rightness is known after death",1],["சேவகன்","the soldier's rightness is known after death",1],["பொத்தில் கடுந்தவசி","the flawless ascetic's rightness is known after death",1],["பொருள்திறத்துச் செவ்வியான்","the man upright in wealth's dealings is known after death",2],["அதுவழக்குஞ் சான்றவர்","the worthy who keep a trust and judge are known after death",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"sp:96":{"book":"sirupanchamoolam","num":98,"t":["வழிப்படரல் வாயல் வருந்தாமை வாய்மை","குறிப்படரல் தீக்சொற்க ளோடு - மொழிப்பட்ட","காய்ந்து விடுதல் களைந்(து)உயக் கற்றவர்","ஆய்ந்து விடுதல் அறம்"],"p":"","r":"The verse names conduct it calls aram (following the right path, not grieving, truthfulness, attending to intent, and turning in disgust from words joined with evil speech) and says the learned who would be saved examine and let the rest go.","n":"Several words are unclear: வாயல் may be a misprint, and வழிப்படரல் and குறிப்படரல் could mean pursuing a path or a sign in either a good or bad sense. The division into five is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vaaimai","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanchamoolam 96","lb":"SP96","poet":"காரியாசான்","dm":"all","it":[["வழிப்படரல்","following the right path is aram",1],["வருந்தாமை","not grieving is aram",1],["வாய்மை","truthfulness is aram",1],["குறிப்படரல்","attending to intent is aram",2],["காய்ந்து விடுதல்","turning in disgust from words joined with evil speech is aram",3]],"cw":"","ch":"sp:5"},"tk:0":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":1,"t":["கண்ணகல் ஞாலம் அளந்துதூஉம் காமருசீர்த்","தண்ணறும் பூங்குருந்தம் பாய்த்ததூஉம் - நண்ணிய","மாயச் சகடம் உதைத்தூஉம் இம்மூன்றும்","பூவைப்பூ வண்ணன் அடி"],"p":"","r":"The invocation counts three deeds of the dark one coloured like the kaya flower (measuring the wide earth, felling the fragrant kurundham tree, kicking the magic cart that came at him) and names the three together as his feet.","n":"All three deeds are deeds of the foot (a stride, a leap at the tree, a kick), so the predicate 'these three are his feet' is exact: the book's counting form is turned on the god before it is turned on conduct, and the feet are worshipped as the acts they did.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 0","lb":"TK0","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஞாலம் அளந்துதூஉம்","his feet are the stride that measured the wide earth",1],["பூங்குருந்தம் பாய்த்ததூஉம்","his feet are the leap that brought down the cool flowering kurundham",2],["சகடம் உதைத்தூஉம்","his feet are the kick that broke the magic cart that drew near",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:1":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":2,"t":["அருந்ததிக் கற்பினார் தோளும் திருந்திய","தொல்குடியின் மாண்டார் தொடர்ச்சியும் - சொல்லின்","அரில்அகற்றும் கேள்வியார் நட்பும் இம்மூன்றும்","திரிகடுகம் போலு மருந்து"],"p":"","r":"The shoulders of a wife chaste as Arundhati, unbroken company with the excellent of an old well-ordered family, and the friendship of men whose learning by listening clears away the tangle of words are three things that act as a medicine like the three pungent drugs.","n":"The predicate gives the book its name: the three are not called good but curative, which implies a sickness they treat. The second and third items are both kinds of company, so two of the three remedies are chosen associates and only one is the house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","karpu","natpu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 1","lb":"TK1","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["அருந்ததிக் கற்பினார் தோளும்","the embrace of a wife as chaste as Arundhati is a remedy",1],["தொல்குடியின் மாண்டார் தொடர்ச்சியும்","lasting association with the worthy of an old, ordered family is a remedy",2],["கேள்வியார் நட்பும்","friendship with men whose heard learning untangles confused speech is a remedy",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:2":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":3,"t":["தன்குணம் குன்றாத் தகைமையும் தாவில்சீர்","இன்குணத்தார் ஏவினசெய்தலும் - நன்குணர்வின்","நான்மறை யாளர் வழிச்செலவும் இம்மூன்றும்","மென்முறை யாளர் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Keeping one's own good nature from declining, doing what is asked by people of sweet nature and flawless worth, and walking the way of the knowers of the four Vedas are named the work of the gentle.","n":"The Veda-knowers enter as guides to a path, qualified by நன்குணர்வின் (of good understanding); the verse sets them beside one's own character and the bidding of the good, so the gentle person's conduct is drawn from three sources, only one of them Vedic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","ozhukkam","andhanar","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 2","lb":"TK2","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்குணம் குன்றாத் தகைமையும்","not letting one's own character decline is the work of the gentle",1],["இன்குணத்தார் ஏவினசெய்தலும்","doing what the flawless and sweet-natured ask is the work of the gentle",2],["நான்மறை யாளர் வழிச்செலவும்","following the way of the knowers of the four Vedas is the work of the gentle",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:3":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":4,"t":["கல்லார்க்கு இனனாய் ஒழுகலும் காழ்கொண்ட","இல்லாளைக் கோலால் புடைத்தலும் - இல்லம்","சிறியாரைக் கொண்டு புகலுமிம் மூன்றும்","அறியாமை யால்வரும் கேடு"],"p":"","r":"Living as one of the kin of the unlearned, beating one's wife with a stick, and entering a house in the company of petty men are named three ruins that come from ignorance.","n":"காழ்கொண்ட qualifies the wife and is ambiguous: 'firm, of settled worth' would make the item 'beating a steadfast wife', while 'harbouring rancour' would make it 'beating a wife who has turned bitter'; either way the beating is counted as ignorance, but the scope of the condemnation turns on the word. The count word is run into the verb (புகலுமிம் மூன்றும்), so count_word records only மூன்றும் and the third thing is cut before the fused verb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kallaamai","kedu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 3","lb":"TK3","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கல்லார்க்கு இனனாய் ஒழுகலும்","keeping company as kin with the unlearned is a ruin born of ignorance",1],["இல்லாளைக் கோலால் புடைத்தலும்","beating one's (steadfast or embittered) wife with a stick is a ruin born of ignorance",2],["சிறியாரைக் கொண்டு","entering a house with petty men as one's company is a ruin born of ignorance",3]],"cw":"மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a marital norm of the period: the verse counts a husband's beating of his wife among the ruins of ignorance"],"ch":"tk:1"},"tk:4":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":5,"t":["பகைமுன்னர் வாழ்க்கை செயலும் தொகைநின்ற","பெற்றத்துள் கோலின்றிச் சேறலும் - முற்றன்னைக்","காய்வானைக் கைவாங்கிக் கோடலும் இம்மூன்றும்","சாவ வுறுவான் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Setting up one's life in front of one's enemies, walking without a stick into a gathered herd of cattle, and taking by the hand a man who was earlier enraged against oneself are named the doings of one about to die.","n":"The third item rests on dividing முற்றன்னை as முன் + தன்னை, 'oneself, earlier'; read as முற்று + அன்னை it would be 'one who rages at his aged mother', which makes a different item (taking up with a matricidal-tempered man). The first reading keeps all three items about exposure to a known danger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kedu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 4","lb":"TK4","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பகைமுன்னர் வாழ்க்கை செயலும்","making one's living in the face of enemies is the conduct of one bound for death",1],["பெற்றத்துள் கோலின்றிச் சேறலும்","entering a herd of cattle without a stick is the conduct of one bound for death",2],["காய்வானைக் கைவாங்கிக் கோடலும்","taking into one's hand a man who once raged against one is the conduct of one bound for death",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:5":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":6,"t":["வழங்காத் துறையிழிந்து நீர்ப்போக்கும் ஒப்ப","விழைவிலாப் பெண்டீர்த்தோள் சேர்வும் - உழந்து","விருந்தினனாய் வேற்றூர் புகலும் இம்மூன்றும்","அருந்துயரம் காட்டு நெறி"],"p":"","r":"Going down into the water at a ford no one uses, joining the shoulders of women who have no desire for one, and arriving worn out as a guest in a strange town are named roads that lead to hard suffering.","n":"ஒப்ப joins the first two items: union with an unwilling woman is likened to entering water at an unused ford, a danger whose depth is unknown. The third item looks at hospitality from the guest's side, as a hazard of dependence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kaamam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 5","lb":"TK5","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வழங்காத் துறையிழிந்து நீர்ப்போக்கும்","crossing water by an unused ford leads to suffering",1],["விழைவிலாப் பெண்டீர்த்தோள் சேர்வும்","embracing women who do not desire one leads to suffering",2],["விருந்தினனாய் வேற்றூர் புகலும்","entering another town, worn, as a guest leads to suffering",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:6":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":7,"t":["பிறர்தன்னைப் பேணுங்கால் நாணலும் பேணார்","திறன்வேறு கூறில் பொறையும் - அறவினையைக்","காராண்மை போல ஒழுகலும் இம்மூன்றும்","ஊராண்மை யென்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Being abashed when others honour one, bearing it patiently when those who do not honour one speak ill of one's ability, and doing deeds of dharma in the manner of the rain-cloud's giving are named the pride called ஊராண்மை.","n":"The closing word செருக்கு (pride) is paradoxical after three acts of self-effacement: the verse claims that true pride is modesty under praise, patience under scorn and giving without return. காராண்மை is read as the cloud's liberality, which asks nothing back; it might also mean the farmer's labour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","porai","oppuravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 6","lb":"TK6","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிறர்தன்னைப் பேணுங்கால் நாணலும்","shrinking with modesty when honoured is the pride of ஊராண்மை",1],["திறன்வேறு கூறில் பொறையும்","patience when the disrespectful run down one's ability is the pride of ஊராண்மை",2],["காராண்மை போல ஒழுகலும்","practising dharma as the cloud gives rain is the pride of ஊராண்மை",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:7":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":8,"t":["வாளைமீன் உள்ளல் தலைப்படலும் ஆளல்லான்","செல்வக் குடியுட் பிறத்தலும் - பல்அவையுள்","அஞ்சுவான் கற்ற அருநூலும் இம்மூன்றும்","துஞ்சூமன் கண்ட கனா"],"p":"","r":"A vaalai fish caught in the trap, a man of no capacity born into a wealthy family, and hard learning acquired by one who is afraid in assemblies are named a dream seen by a sleeping mute.","n":"The image is exact: a mute's dream is real to him and can never be told, so each item is a good possessed that cannot be brought out. உள்ளல் is taken as a fish trap; if it is a pool or a place, the first item changes from 'caught' to 'stranded'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["payan","kalvi","avai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 7","lb":"TK7","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாளைமீன் உள்ளல் தலைப்படலும்","the vaalai fish's getting into the trap is a mute's dream",1],["செல்வக் குடியுட் பிறத்தலும்","an incapable man's birth into a wealthy house is a mute's dream",2],["அஞ்சுவான் கற்ற அருநூலும்","hard learning gained by one who fears assemblies is a mute's dream",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:8":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":9,"t":["தொல்லவையுள் தோன்றும் குடிமையும் தொக்கிருந்த","நல்லவையுள் மேம்பட்ட கல்வியும் - வெல்சமத்து","வேந்துவப்ப வட்டார்த்த வென்றியும் இம்மூன்றும்","தாந்தம்மைக் கூறாப் பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Lineage that shows itself in an old assembly, learning that stands out in a gathered good assembly, and a victory in battle that delights the king are named three things that need not speak of themselves.","n":"Each item carries its own witness (the old assembly, the good assembly, the pleased king), so the claim is that real worth is proclaimed by a setting, not by its owner. வட்டார்த்த is obscure; it seems to describe the clamour of the battle where the victory was won.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","avai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 8","lb":"TK8","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தொல்லவையுள் தோன்றும் குடிமையும்","good birth that shows in an ancient assembly does not need to proclaim itself",1],["நல்லவையுள் மேம்பட்ட கல்வியும்","learning that excels in a good assembly does not need to proclaim itself",2],["வட்டார்த்த வென்றியும்","victory in battle that gladdens the king does not need to proclaim itself",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:9":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":10,"t":["பெருமை யுடையா ரினத்தின் அகறல்","உரிமையில் பெண்டிரைக் காமுற்று வாழ்தல்","விழுமிய வல்ல துணிதல் இம்மூன்றும்","முழுமக்கள் காத லவை"],"p":"","r":"Moving away from the company of the great, living in desire for women one has no right to, and resolving on things that are not excellent are named what utter fools love.","n":"The predicate is காதல், love: the verse says these are not the fool's mistakes but his preferences, which places folly in what is wanted, not in what is misjudged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","piranil","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 9","lb":"TK9","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெருமை யுடையா ரினத்தின் அகறல்","leaving the company of the great is what fools love",1],["உரிமையில் பெண்டிரைக் காமுற்று வாழ்தல்","living in lust for women not one's own is what fools love",2],["விழுமிய வல்ல துணிதல்","resolving on what is not worthy is what fools love",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:10":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":11,"t":["கணக்காயர் இல்லாத ஊரும் பிணக்கறுக்கும்","மூத்தோரை இல்லா அவைக்களனும் - பாத்துண்ணும்","தன்மையி லாளர் அயலிருப்பும் இம்முன்றும்","நன்மை பயத்தல் இல"],"p":"","r":"A town with no teachers, an assembly with no elders to settle quarrels, and living beside people who do not share their food are named three things that yield no good.","n":"All three are about a place lacking a kind of person, so the verse measures a settlement by who lives in it: the teacher, the arbiter, the sharer. The third makes a neighbour's stinginess a loss to those beside him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","eegai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 10","lb":"TK10","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கணக்காயர் இல்லாத ஊரும்","a town without teachers yields no good",1],["மூத்தோரை இல்லா அவைக்களனும்","an assembly without elders who end disputes yields no good",2],["தன்மையி லாளர் அயலிருப்பும்","living next to those who do not share their food yields no good",3]],"cw":"இம்முன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:11":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":12,"t":["விளியாதான் கூத்தாட்டுக் காண்டலும் வீழக்","களியாதான் காவா துரையும் - தெளியாதான்","கூரையுள் பல்காலும் சேறலும் இம்மூன்றும்","ஊரெல்லாம் நோவ துடைத்து"],"p":"","r":"Watching the dance of one who has not invited one, the unguarded talk of a man who is not drunk enough to fall, and going again and again into the house of one who does not trust one are named three things the whole town finds offensive.","n":"The second item excuses nothing by drink: it is the sober man's careless speech that is blamed. Each of the three words விளியாதான், களியாதான், தெளியாதான் is a negative participle and the readings ('one who has not called', 'one not drunk', 'one not assured') are inferred; ஊரெல்லாம் நோவது உடைத்து may also mean that these bring pain on the doer from the whole town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","kutram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 11","lb":"TK11","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கூத்தாட்டுக் காண்டலும்","going uninvited to watch someone's dance is offensive to the town",1],["காவா துரையும்","a sober man's unguarded talk is offensive to the town",2],["கூரையுள் பல்காலும் சேறலும்","repeatedly entering the house of one who does not trust one is offensive to the town",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:12":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":13,"t":["தாளாளன் என்பான் கடன்படா வாழ்பவன்","வேளாளன் என்பான் விருந்திருக்க உண்ணாதான்","கோளாளன் என்பான் மறவாதான் இம்மூவர்","கேளாக வாழ்தல் இனிது"],"p":"","r":"The verse defines three titles by conduct (the man of effort is one who lives without debt, the vellalan is one who does not eat while a guest is waiting, the man of principle is one who does not forget) and says it is sweet to live with these three as kin.","n":"The form changes here: instead of three things it gives three definitions, each 'X என்பான்' fixing a name by an act. வேளாளன், a name that can denote an agrarian community, is defined by hospitality alone, not by birth or land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","muyarchi","nanri","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 12","lb":"TK12","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["தாளாளன் என்பான் கடன்படா வாழ்பவன்","the man of effort, who lives without debt, is good to have as kin",1],["வேளாளன் என்பான் விருந்திருக்க உண்ணாதான்","the vellalan, who will not eat while a guest waits, is good to have as kin",2],["கோளாளன் என்பான் மறவாதான்","the man of principle, who does not forget, is good to have as kin",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:13":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":14,"t":["சீலம் அறிவான் இளங்கிளை சாலக்","குடியோம்பல் வல்லான் அரசன் - வடுவின்றி","மாண்ட குணத்தான் தவசியென்ற இம்மூவர்","யாண்டும் பெறற்கரி யார்"],"p":"","r":"A young member of a family who knows right conduct, a king able to care well for his people, and an ascetic of excellent character without a blemish are named three people hard to find anywhere.","n":"Each figure is rare because his virtue runs against his station: conduct is not expected of the young, care for subjects is hard for one who rules them, and a flawless life is what the ascetic's vow claims but seldom keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arasu","thavam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 13","lb":"TK13","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["சீலம் அறிவான் இளங்கிளை","a young kinsman who knows right conduct is rare",1],["குடியோம்பல் வல்லான் அரசன்","a king able to protect his people is rare",2],["மாண்ட குணத்தான் தவசி","an ascetic of spotless excellence is rare",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:14":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":15,"t":["இழுக்கல் இயல்பிற்று இளமை பழித்தவை","சொல்லுதல் வற்றாகும் பேதைமை - யாண்டும்","செறுவொடு நிற்கும் சிறுமைஇம் மூன்றும்","குறுகார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"","r":"Youth, whose nature is to slip; folly, which is fixed in speaking words of blame; and meanness, which always stands with hostility, are three things the wise do not go near.","n":"Each item is a state given its defining trait (slipping, blaming, hostility); youth is named as a thing to keep away from, not a time to be used, which makes it a temperament rather than an age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilamai","pedhaimai","sirumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 14","lb":"TK14","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இழுக்கல் இயல்பிற்று இளமை","youth, prone to slip, is shunned by the wise",1],["சொல்லுதல் வற்றாகும் பேதைமை","folly that keeps speaking reproachful words is shunned by the wise",2],["செறுவொடு நிற்கும் சிறுமை","meanness bound up with hostility is shunned by the wise",3]],"cw":"இம் மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:15":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":16,"t":["பொய்வழங்கி வாழும் பொறியறையும் கைதிரிந்து","தாழ்விடத்து நேர்கருதும் தட்டையும் - ஊழினால்","ஒட்டி வினைநலம் பார்ப்பானும் இம்மூவர்","நட்கப் படாஅ தவர்"],"p":"","r":"The luckless wretch who lives by dealing in lies, the hollow man who claims equality when one's fortune has turned and one is low, and the one who attaches himself as fortune runs and looks only to the profit of the business are named three not to be befriended.","n":"பொறியறை and தட்டை are terms of contempt whose exact sense is unsure ('one bereft of fortune', 'a husk'); ஊழினால் ஒட்டி is read as attaching oneself according to another's fortune. Read so, all three fail a friend at the turn of fortune.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vaaimai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 15","lb":"TK15","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பொய்வழங்கி வாழும் பொறியறையும்","the wretch who lives by lies is not to be befriended",1],["நேர்கருதும் தட்டையும்","the hollow man who claims equality when one has fallen is not to be befriended",2],["வினைநலம் பார்ப்பானும்","the one who clings by fortune and watches for gain is not to be befriended",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:16":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":17,"t":["மண்ணின்மேல் வான்புகழ் நட்டானும் மாசில்சீர்ப்","பெண்ணினுள் கற்புடையாள் பெற்றானும் - உண்ணுநீர்","கூவல் குறைவின்றித் தொட்டானும் இம்மூவர்","சாவா உடம்பெய்தி னார்"],"p":"","r":"One who has planted lofty fame on the earth, one who has won a chaste wife from among women of spotless worth, and one who has dug a well of drinking water without stint are named three who have gained a body that does not die.","n":"The 'undying body' is fame's body, but the three routes are unlike: a public reputation, a private marriage and a public work for water. Winning a chaste wife is counted as an achievement equal to the other two.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","karpu","oppuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 16","lb":"TK16","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["வான்புகழ் நட்டானும்","one who has planted high renown has an undying body",1],["கற்புடையாள் பெற்றானும்","one who has gained a chaste wife has an undying body",2],["குறைவின்றித் தொட்டானும்","one who has dug a drinking well without stint has an undying body",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:17":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":18,"t":["மூப்பின்கண் நன்மைக்கு அகன்றானும் கற்புடையாள்","பூப்பின்கண் சாராத தலைமகனும் - வாய்ப்பகையுள்","சொல்வென்றி வேண்டும் இலிங்கியும் இம்மூவர்","கல்விப் புணைகைவிட் டார்"],"p":"","r":"One who in old age has turned away from what is good, the husband who does not go to his chaste wife at her season, and the mendicant bearing a sect's sign who wants to win by words in disputes are named three who have let go the raft of learning.","n":"The three let go of learning at the three stages where it should bind most: the old man at life's end, the householder in his duty to his wife, and the renouncer, whose learning turns into a weapon of debate. இலிங்கி names one who bears a sectarian mark, not necessarily a Saiva ascetic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 17","lb":"TK17","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நன்மைக்கு அகன்றானும்","the old man who turns from the good has abandoned learning's raft",1],["பூப்பின்கண் சாராத தலைமகனும்","the husband who does not come to his chaste wife at her season has abandoned learning's raft",2],["சொல்வென்றி வேண்டும் இலிங்கியும்","the sign-bearing mendicant who seeks victory in wordy disputes has abandoned learning's raft",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a marital rule of the period about the wife's season (பூப்பு): the husband is held bound to go to her then"],"ch":"tk:1"},"tk:18":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":19,"t":["ஒருதலையான் வந்துறூஉ மூப்பும் புணர்ந்தார்க்கு","இருதலையு மின்னாப் பிரிவும் - உருவினை","உள்ளுருக்கித் தின்னும் பெரும்பிணியும் இம்மூன்றும்","கள்வரி னஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"","r":"Old age, which comes surely from one side, separation, painful at both ends to those who were united, and great disease that melts the body within and eats it are named three things to be feared more than thieves.","n":"The comparison with thieves makes all three robbers: of youth, of a companion, of the body. Separation of lovers, an akam theme, is counted among the bodily dreads, and ஒருதலையான் and இருதலை play on 'one side' and 'both sides'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pirivu","noy","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 18","lb":"TK18","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வந்துறூஉ மூப்பும்","old age, which comes inevitably, is to be feared more than thieves",1],["இருதலையு மின்னாப் பிரிவும்","separation, painful to both the parted, is to be feared more than thieves",2],["தின்னும் பெரும்பிணியும்","great disease that melts and consumes the body is to be feared more than thieves",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:19":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":20,"t":["கொல்யானைக் கோடும் குணமிலியும் எல்லிற்","பிறன்கடை நின்றொழுகு வானும் - மறந்தெரியாது","ஆடும்பாம் பாட்டும் அறிவிலியும் இம்மூவர்","நாடுங்கால் தூங்கு பவர்"],"p":"","r":"The witless man who runs at a killing elephant, the man who loiters at another's door by night, and the senseless one who plays with a dancing snake not knowing its fury are named, on examination, three who hang by a thread.","n":"யானைக்கோடும் is read as யானைக்கு ஓடும், 'runs at the elephant', not as 'the elephant's tusk'. The middle item (by night at another man's door) is placed between two acts of reckless courting of animal danger, so the adulterer's risk is treated as physical. தூங்குபவர் may mean those as good as hanged or those who sleep heedless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","piranil","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 19","lb":"TK19","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொல்யானைக் கோடும் குணமிலியும்","the fool who runs at a killing elephant is in mortal danger",1],["பிறன்கடை நின்றொழுகு வானும்","the one who hangs about another's door by night is in mortal danger",2],["ஆடும்பாம் பாட்டும் அறிவிலியும்","the fool who makes a cobra dance without knowing its fury is in mortal danger",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:20":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":21,"t":["ஆசை பிறன்கண் படுதலும் பாசம்","பசிப்ப மடியைக் கொளலும் - கதித்தொருவன்","கல்லானென்று எள்ளப் படுதலும் இம்மூன்றும்","எல்லார்க்கு மின்னா தன"],"p":"","r":"One's hope coming to rest on another, falling into sloth while one's dependants go hungry, and being scorned in anger by someone as unlearned are named three things painful to everyone.","n":"பாசம் here means those bound to one (kin, dependants), so the second item is not sloth in general but sloth at the moment others need one's work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","madi","kallaamai","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 20","lb":"TK20","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆசை பிறன்கண் படுதலும்","having one's hopes depend on another is painful",1],["மடியைக் கொளலும்","taking to sloth while one's dependants starve is painful",2],["கல்லானென்று எள்ளப் படுதலும்","being scorned as unlearned is painful",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:1"},"tk:21":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":22,"t":["வருவாயுள் கால்வழங்கி வாழ்தல் செருவாய்ப்பச்","செய்தவை நாடாச் சிறப்புடைமை - எய்தப்","பலநாடி நல்லவை கற்றல் இம்மூன்றும்","நலமாட்சி நல்லவர் கோள்"],"p":"","r":"Living by giving away a quarter of what comes in, the distinction of not raking over what was done when strife arose, and learning the good things after weighing many are named the excellence that good men hold to.","n":"The giving is fixed as a share, கால் (a quarter) of income, not left to feeling. செருவாய்ப்பச் செய்தவை most likely means what others did to one when a quarrel arose, so the second item is forgiveness; the verse does not say whose deeds they were.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","porai","kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 21","lb":"TK21","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["வருவாயுள் கால்வழங்கி வாழ்தல்","living by giving a quarter of one's income is part of the excellence good men hold",1],["செய்தவை நாடாச் சிறப்புடைமை","the distinction of not searching out what was done in a quarrel is part of that excellence",2],["பலநாடி நல்லவை கற்றல்","learning the good after examining many things is part of that excellence",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:22":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":23,"t":["பற்றென்னும் பாசத் தளையும் பலவழியும்","பற்றறா தோடும் அவாத்தேரும் - தெற்றெனப்","பொய்த்துரை யென்னும் பகையிருளும் இம்மூன்றும்","வித்தற வீடும் பிறப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The fetter called attachment, the chariot of craving that runs down many roads with its clinging never cut, and the hostile darkness called false speech are named three things with whose seed destroyed birth itself perishes.","n":"Each fault is given an image (a rope, a runaway chariot, a darkness), and the third is a fault of speech set among two inner cravings as a cause of rebirth. The other witness reads புகையிருள் (smoky darkness) for பகையிருள், which changes the image but not the claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piravi","pattru","avaa","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 22","lb":"TK22","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["பற்றென்னும் பாசத் தளையும்","the fetter of attachment is a seed of birth whose destruction ends birth",1],["பற்றறா தோடும் அவாத்தேரும்","the chariot of craving that runs many ways unchecked is such a seed",2],["பொய்த்துரை யென்னும் பகையிருளும்","the hostile darkness of false speech is such a seed",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:23":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":24,"t":["தானம் கொடுக்கும் தகைமையும் மானத்தார்","குற்றம் கடிந்த ஒழுக்கமும் - தெற்றெனப்","பல்பொருள் நீங்கிய சிந்தையும் இம்மூன்றும்","நல்வினை யார்க்கும் கயிறு"],"p":"","r":"The worth of giving gifts, the conduct of men of honour that has driven out faults, and a mind that has left many possessions behind are named a rope that binds good deeds for anyone.","n":"கயிறு, a rope, makes the three things the means by which merit is held or drawn up; the third item is a renouncer's detachment, set beside a householder's giving as the same kind of rope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ozhukkam","thuravu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 23","lb":"TK23","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தானம் கொடுக்கும் தகைமையும்","the worth of giving gifts is a rope to good deeds",1],["குற்றம் கடிந்த ஒழுக்கமும்","the fault-free conduct of men of honour is such a rope",2],["பல்பொருள் நீங்கிய சிந்தையும்","a mind that has let go of many possessions is such a rope",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:24":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":25,"t":["காண்டகு மென்றோள் கணிகைவா யின்சொல்லும்","தூண்டிலின் உட்பொதிந்த தேரையும் - மாண்டசீர்க்","காழ்த்த பகைவர் வணக்கமும் இம்மூன்றும்","ஆழ்ச்சிப் படுக்கும் அளறு"],"p":"","r":"The sweet words from the mouth of a courtesan with lovely soft shoulders, the frog hidden as bait inside a fishhook, and the bowing of hardened enemies are named a mire that sinks those who step in.","n":"All three are pleasing surfaces over a harm: speech, bait and homage. The predicate அளறு (mire) adds that the harm is not a blow but a slow sinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","varaivinmagalir","pagai","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 24","lb":"TK24","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["காண்டகு மென்றோள் கணிகைவா யின்சொல்லும்","a courtesan's sweet speech is a mire that sinks",1],["தூண்டிலின் உட்பொதிந்த தேரையும்","the frog wrapped round a hook as bait is such a mire",2],["காழ்த்த பகைவர் வணக்கமும்","the bowing of hardened enemies is such a mire",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:25":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":26,"t":["செருக்கினால் வாழும் சிறியவனும் பைத்தகன்ற","அல்குல் விலைபகரும் ஆய்தொடியும் - நல்லவர்க்கு","வைத்த அறப்புறம் கொன்றானும் இம்மூவர்","கைத்துண்ணார் கற்றறிந் தார்"],"p":"","r":"The verse reports that those who have learnt and understood do not eat from the hand of three: the petty man who lives by arrogance, the bangled woman who sells her body for a price, and the man who destroyed an endowment set apart for the good.","n":"The grounds given are moral, not ritual: all three live by a wrong (pride, sale of the body, theft of a charity). அறப்புறம் is property dedicated to charity, so the third man has robbed the good themselves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","sirumai","varaivinmagalir","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 25","lb":"TK25","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["செருக்கினால் வாழும் சிறியவனும்","the learned do not eat from the hand of the petty man who lives by arrogance",1],["அல்குல் விலைபகரும் ஆய்தொடியும்","they do not eat from the hand of the woman who sells her body",2],["வைத்த அறப்புறம் கொன்றானும்","they do not eat from the hand of one who destroyed a charitable endowment",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["purity","gender"],"a commensality rule of the period: whose food the learned refuse, a courtesan among them"],"ch":"tk:2"},"tk:26":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":27,"t":["ஒல்வ தறியும் விருந்தினனும் ஆருயிரைக்","கொல்வ திடைநீக்கி வாழ்வானும் - வல்லிதின்","சீலமினி துடைய ஆசானும் இம்மூவர்","ஞாலம் எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The guest who knows what his host can afford, the man who lives having put away the killing of precious life, and the teacher richly possessed of sweet good conduct are named the ones who are called the world.","n":"The guest is judged here, not the host: the courtesy asked of him is to know the measure of the house. ஞாலம் எனப்படுவார் means that such people are what 'the world' properly means, as உலகம் often means the worthy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kollaamai","guru","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 26","lb":"TK26","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஒல்வ தறியும் விருந்தினனும்","the guest who knows the host's capacity is called the world",1],["கொல்வ திடைநீக்கி வாழ்வானும்","the man who lives refraining from killing is called the world",2],["சீலமினி துடைய ஆசானும்","the teacher rich in good conduct is called the world",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:27":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":28,"t":["உண்பொழுது நீராடி உண்டலும் என்பெறினும்","பால்பற்றிச் சொல்லா விடுதலும் - தோல்வற்றிச்","சாயினுஞ் சான்றாண்மை குன்றாமை இம்மூன்றும்","தூஉய மென்பார் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Bathing before eating at mealtime, not speaking with partiality whatever one may gain, and not letting one's nobility fail even if the skin dries and one dies are named the work of those who call themselves pure.","n":"The list joins a rule of bodily purity to two inner ones without ranking them; the predicate (தூஉயம் என்பார், those who say 'we are pure') makes all three the test of a claim to purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","naduvu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 27","lb":"TK27","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["உண்பொழுது நீராடி உண்டலும்","bathing before eating is the work of those who claim purity",1],["பால்பற்றிச் சொல்லா விடுதலும்","refusing to speak partially, whatever the gain, is such work",2],["சாயினுஞ் சான்றாண்மை குன்றாமை","keeping one's nobility even unto wasting and death is such work",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["purity"],"a purity rule of the period: bathing before the meal"],"ch":"tk:2"},"tk:28":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":29,"t":["வெல்வது வேண்டி வெகுண்டுரைக்கு நோன்பிலியும்","இல்லது காமுற்று இருப்பானும் - கல்வி","செவிக்குற்றம் பார்த்திருப்பானும் இம்மூன்றும்","உமிக்குத்திக் கைவருந்துவார்"],"p":"","r":"The man without austerity who speaks in anger wanting to win, the man who sits craving what he does not have, and the man who listens to learning only to watch for faults are named three who pound husk and tire their hands.","n":"The image of pounding husk (உமி) is labour with no grain in it: each of the three spends effort where nothing can come of it. நோன்பிலி names anger to win as a lack of austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","avaa","kelvi","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 28","lb":"TK28","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வெல்வது வேண்டி வெகுண்டுரைக்கு நோன்பிலியும்","the unrestrained man who rages to win labours for nothing",1],["இல்லது காமுற்று இருப்பானும்","the man who sits craving what he lacks labours for nothing",2],["செவிக்குற்றம் பார்த்திருப்பானும்","the listener who only watches for faults in what is taught labours for nothing",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:29":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":30,"t":["பெண்விழைந்து பின்செலினும் தன்செலவிற் குன்றாமை","கண்விழைந்து கையுறினும் காதல் பொருட்கின்மை","மண்விழுந்து வாழ்நாள் மதியாமை இம்மூன்றும்","நுண்விழைந்த நூலவர் நோக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Not falling from one's own course even when a woman desires one and follows, having no longing for a thing even when the eye craves it and it comes to hand, and not prizing a span of life that ends in falling to the earth are named the view of the learned who sought out subtle things.","n":"Each item is a restraint under the strongest temptation (the woman follows, the thing is already in hand). மண்விழுந்து வாழ்நாள் மதியாமை is hard: it may mean not counting on a life that falls to earth, or not reckoning days spent sunk in the world; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vehkaamai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 29","lb":"TK29","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெண்விழைந்து பின்செலினும் தன்செலவிற் குன்றாமை","not straying from one's course though a woman pursues one is the learned view",1],["கண்விழைந்து கையுறினும் காதல் பொருட்கின்மை","having no desire for a thing even when it comes to hand is the learned view",2],["மண்விழுந்து வாழ்நாள் மதியாமை","not prizing a life that ends in the earth is the learned view",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:30":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":31,"t":["தன்நச்சிச் சென்றாரை எள்ளா வொருவனும்","மன்னிய செல்வத்துப் பொச்சாப்பு நீத்தானும்","என்றும் அழுக்காறி கந்தானும் இம்மூவர்","நின்ற புகழுடை யார்"],"p":"","r":"One who does not scorn those who come to him in need, one who has put away heedlessness in settled wealth, and one who has always kept clear of envy are named three whose fame endures.","n":"The second item puts the danger of heedlessness in wealth that seems secure (மன்னிய செல்வம்), the moment one stops guarding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai","pochchaappu","azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 30","lb":"TK30","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்நச்சிச் சென்றாரை எள்ளா வொருவனும்","one who does not scorn those who come seeking him has lasting fame",1],["மன்னிய செல்வத்துப் பொச்சாப்பு நீத்தானும்","one who is not heedless in settled wealth has lasting fame",2],["என்றும் அழுக்காறி கந்தானும்","one who has always kept free of envy has lasting fame",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:31":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":32,"t":["பல்லவையுள் நல்லவை கற்றலும் பாத்துண்டாம்","இல்லற முட்டாது இயற்றலும் - வல்லிதின்","தாளி னொருபொரு ளாக்கலும் இம்மூன்றும்","கேள்வியுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"","r":"Learning the good among many things, carrying on without break the household life of sharing one's food, and making wealth firmly by one's own effort are named the head of all that is taught.","n":"The three are learning, giving and earning, and the third supplies the second: the household shares what its own labour (தாள்) made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ilvaazhkkai","eegai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 31","lb":"TK31","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["பல்லவையுள் நல்லவை கற்றலும்","learning the good among many is the chief of all teaching",1],["இல்லற முட்டாது இயற்றலும்","keeping household life of sharing unbroken is the chief of all teaching",2],["தாளி னொருபொரு ளாக்கலும்","making wealth by one's own effort is the chief of all teaching",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:32":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":33,"t":["நுண்மொழி நோக்கிப் பொருள்கொளலும் நூற்குஏலா","வெண்மொழி வேண்டினும் சொல்லாமை - நன்மொழியைச்","சிற்றினம் அல்லார்கண் சொல்லலும் இம்மூன்றும்","கற்றறிந்தார் பூண்ட கடன்"],"p":"","r":"Grasping the meaning by attending to subtle words, not speaking empty words unfit for learning even when asked, and speaking good words only to those who are not base are named the debt the learned have taken on.","n":"One duty of hearing and two of speaking; கடன் (debt) makes them obligations that come with learning, not graces.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sol","avai","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 32","lb":"TK32","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நுண்மொழி நோக்கிப் பொருள்கொளலும்","taking the meaning from subtle words is a duty of the learned",1],["வெண்மொழி வேண்டினும் சொல்லாமை","refusing to speak idle words even when asked is such a duty",2],["சிற்றினம் அல்லார்கண் சொல்லலும்","speaking good words only among those who are not base is such a duty",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:33":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":34,"t":["கோலஞ்சி வாழும் குடியும் குடிதழீஇ","ஆலம்வீழ் போலும் அமைச்சனும் - வேலின்","கடைமணிபோல் திண்ணியான் காப்பும் இம்மூன்றும்","படைவேந்தன் மற்று விடல்"],"p":"","r":"Subjects who live in awe of the sceptre, a minister who holds the people close like the aerial roots of a banyan, and the protection of a man firm as the bead at a spear's butt are named three that a king with an army does not let go.","n":"The closing words படைவேந்தன் மற்று விடல் are obscure: they may mean the king must not let these go, or that a king without them is to be left; the reading takes the first. The banyan's aerial roots both hold the tree up and spread it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","amaichu","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 33","lb":"TK33","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"king","it":[["கோலஞ்சி வாழும் குடியும்","subjects who live in awe of the sceptre are what a king keeps",1],["ஆலம்வீழ் போலும் அமைச்சனும்","a minister who holds the people like banyan roots is what a king keeps",2],["கடைமணிபோல் திண்ணியான் காப்பும்","the guard of a man firm as a spear's butt-bead is what a king keeps",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:34":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":35,"t":["மூன்று கடன்கழித்த பார்ப்பானும் ஓர்ந்து","முறைநிலை கோடா அரசும் - சிறைநின்று","அலவலை இல்லாக் குடியும் இம்மூவர்","உலகம் எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"","r":"The brahmin who has discharged the three debts, the king who weighs matters and does not bend justice, and the people who keep within bounds without turbulence are named the ones who are called the world.","n":"பார்ப்பான் is a birth-group term here, defined by a ritual duty; the verse does not name the three debts, which tradition identifies as owed to sages, gods and ancestors. The verse sets priest, king and people side by side as the order that makes a 'world'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","sengol","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 34","lb":"TK34","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மூன்று கடன்கழித்த பார்ப்பானும்","the brahmin who has paid his three debts is called the world",1],["முறைநிலை கோடா அரசும்","the king who does not bend justice is called the world",2],["அலவலை இல்லாக் குடியும்","a people that keeps within bounds without disorder is called the world",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["caste","ritual"],"a varna norm of the period: the brahmin is defined by his three ritual debts"],"ch":"tk:2"},"tk:35":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":36,"t":["முந்நீர்த் திரையின் எழுந்தியங்கா மேதையும்","நுண்ணூற் பெருங்கேள்வி நூற்கரை கண்டானும்","மைந்நீர்மை இன்றி மயலுறுப்பான் இம்மூவர்","மெய்ந்நீர்மை மேல் நிற்பவர்"],"p":"","r":"A mind of genius that does not rise and toss like the waves of the sea, one who with great hearing of subtle texts has seen the far shore of learning, and one who without darkness of nature cuts away delusion are named three who stand on true nature.","n":"மயலுறுப்பான் is read as மயல் அறுப்பான் (who cuts away delusion); taken as மயல் உறுப்பான் it would say the opposite. The first item may also mean a genius that rises above the sea's waves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi","meyyunarvu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 35","lb":"TK35","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["முந்நீர்த் திரையின் எழுந்தியங்கா மேதையும்","a steady genius that does not toss like waves stands on truth",1],["நுண்ணூற் பெருங்கேள்வி நூற்கரை கண்டானும்","one who has reached the far shore of subtle texts stands on truth",2],["மைந்நீர்மை இன்றி மயலுறுப்பான்","one without dark nature who removes delusion stands on truth",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:36":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":37,"t":["ஊனுண் டுயிர்கட் கருளுடையேம் என்பானும்","தானுடன்பா டின்றி வினையாக்கும் மென்பானும்","காமுறு வேள்வியில் கொல்வானும் இம்மூவர்","தாமறிவர் தாங்கண்ட வாறு"],"p":"","r":"One who eats flesh and says he has compassion for living beings, one who says the killing is done without his own consent, and one who kills in a sacrifice he desires are named three who know only what they themselves have seen fit to see.","n":"All three are self-justifications for taking life: a claim of compassion, a denial of complicity (the buyer who says someone else killed), and the merit of sacrifice. The closing line is ironic: their knowledge is only their own way of seeing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai","arul","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 36","lb":"TK36","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஊனுண் டுயிர்கட் கருளுடையேம் என்பானும்","the flesh-eater who claims compassion deceives himself",1],["தானுடன்பா டின்றி வினையாக்கும் மென்பானும்","one who says the killing happens without his consent deceives himself",2],["காமுறு வேள்வியில் கொல்வானும்","one who kills in a sacrifice he desires deceives himself",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["diet","ritual"],"the period's dispute over flesh-eating and animal sacrifice, stated as the verse's judgment against both"],"ch":"tk:2"},"tk:37":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":38,"t":["குறளையுள் நட்பளவு தோன்றும் உறலினிய","சால்பினில் தோன்றும் குடிமையும் - பால்போலும்","தூய்மையுள் தோன்றும் பிரமாணம் இம்மூன்றும்","வாய்மை யடையார் வழக்கு"],"p":"","r":"The measure of friendship shows in backbiting, lineage shows in a fullness of character pleasant to meet, and authority shows in a purity like milk; these three are named the way of those who hold to truth.","n":"Each item is a sign by which something hidden is known. பிரமாணம் (measure, authority) and the predicate வாய்மை யடையார் வழக்கு are uncertain; அடையார் could also be negative ('those who have not attained truth'), which would reverse the predicate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","purangooral","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 37","lb":"TK37","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["குறளையுள் நட்பளவு","the extent of a friendship is known by backbiting",1],["சால்பினில் தோன்றும் குடிமையும்","lineage is known by pleasing fullness of character",2],["தூய்மையுள் தோன்றும் பிரமாணம்","authority is known by milk-like purity",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:38":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":39,"t":["தன்னை வியந்து தருக்கலும் தாழ்வின்றிக்","கொன்னே வெகுளி பெருக்கலும் - முன்னிய","பல்பொருள் வெஃகும் சிறுமையும் இம்மூன்றும்","செல்வம் உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"","r":"Admiring oneself and swelling with pride, swelling anger in vain without humility, and the meanness of coveting the many things one has set one's mind on are named an army that breaks wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vekuli","vehkaamai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 38","lb":"TK38","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்னை வியந்து தருக்கலும்","self-admiring pride is an army that breaks wealth",1],["கொன்னே வெகுளி பெருக்கலும்","swelling anger for nothing is such an army",2],["பல்பொருள் வெஃகும் சிறுமையும்","the meanness of coveting many things is such an army",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:39":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":40,"t":["புலைமயக்கம் வேண்டிப் பொருட்பெண்டிர்த் தோய்தல்","கலமயக்கம் கள்ளுண்டு வாழ்தல் - சொலைமுனிந்து","பொய்ம்மயக்கம் சூதின் கண்தாங்கால் இம்மூன்றும்","நன்மையி லாளர் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Seeking the base stupor of embracing women bought with wealth, living drunk on toddy from the cup, and, scorning honest speech, bearing the stupor of lies at the dice are named the work of men without goodness.","n":"Each item carries மயக்கம் (stupor), so sex for pay, drink and dice are made three kinds of intoxication. புலை here means the vile or fleshly, not a person; it is not used as a caste term.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","soodhu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 39","lb":"TK39","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["புலைமயக்கம் வேண்டிப் பொருட்பெண்டிர்த் தோய்தல்","embracing women bought with wealth is the work of men without goodness",1],["கலமயக்கம் கள்ளுண்டு வாழ்தல்","living drunk on toddy is such work",2],["பொய்ம்மயக்கம் சூதின் கண்தாங்கால்","holding to the lies of gambling is such work",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:2"},"tk:40":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":41,"t":["வெகுளி நுணுக்கும் விறலு மகளிர்கட்கு","ஒத்த லொழுக்கம் உடைமையும் - பாத்துண்ணும்","நல்லறி வாண்மை தலைப்படலும் இம்மூன்றும்","தொல்லறி வாளர் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"The strength that wears anger thin, having conduct befitting toward women, and attaining the good wisdom of eating after sharing are named the work of those of ancient wisdom.","n":"மகளிர்கட்கு ஒத்த லொழுக்கம் is read as a man's fitting conduct toward women (his wife); it could also be read as conduct that suits women, a rule for them. Anger is measured by quantity (நுணுக்கும், thinning), the counterpart of verse 38's swelling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","eegai","ilvaazhkkai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 40","lb":"TK40","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["வெகுளி நுணுக்கும் விறலு","the strength that wears down anger is the work of the wise",1],["ஒத்த லொழுக்கம் உடைமையும்","having fitting conduct toward women is such work",2],["நல்லறி வாண்மை தலைப்படலும்","attaining the wisdom of sharing one's food is such work",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a marital norm of the period on conduct toward women; the phrase allows more than one reading"],"ch":"tk:2"},"tk:41":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":42,"t":["அலந்தார்க்கொன்று ஈந்த புகழும் துளங்கினும்","தன்குடிமை குன்றாத் தகைமையும் - அன்போடி","நாள்நாளும் நட்டார்ப் பெருக்கலும் இம்மூன்றும்","கேள்வியுள் எல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"","r":"Renown from giving something to those in distress, the worth that keeps one's family standing from sinking even when shaken, and adding to one's friends day by day with affection are named the three chief of all that is heard.","n":"The predicate கேள்வியுள் எல்லாந் தலை ranks three practices above everything learnt by hearing, so the verse measures instruction by what it should make a person do; the second item concedes that the family may be shaken (துளங்கினும்) and asks only that its worth not fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kudi","natpu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 41","lb":"TK41","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அலந்தார்க்கொன்று ஈந்த புகழும்","renown won by giving to the distressed is chief among things heard",1],["தன்குடிமை குன்றாத் தகைமையும்","the worth that keeps one's family standing from declining, even in upheaval, is chief among things heard",2],["நாள்நாளும் நட்டார்ப் பெருக்கலும்","increasing one's friends day after day, with affection, is chief among things heard",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:42":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":43,"t":["கழகத்தால் வந்த பொருள்கா முறாமை","பழகினும் பார்ப்பாரைத் தீப்போல் - ஒழுகல்","உழவின்கண் காமுற்று வாழ்தல் இம்மூன்றும்","அழகென்ப வேளாண் குடிக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Not coveting wealth that comes from the gaming hall, behaving toward brahmins as toward fire even when familiar with them, and living with a love of farming are said to be the beauty of the vellala family.","n":"பார்ப்பார் is a birth-group term and the rule ranks it: the cultivating family is to keep the distance and caution one keeps with fire however close the acquaintance. The verse defines a family's beauty by occupation and by its bearing toward another birth-group, beside a refusal of gambling gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","uzhavu","soodhu","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 42","lb":"TK42","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கழகத்தால் வந்த பொருள்கா முறாமை","not desiring wealth won at the gaming hall is the vellala family's beauty",1],["பழகினும் பார்ப்பாரைத் தீப்போல் - ஒழுகல்","treating brahmins with the caution due to fire, even when familiar, is reported as the vellala family's beauty",2],["உழவின்கண் காமுற்று வாழ்தல்","living with a love of farming is the vellala family's beauty",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank rule of the period: the conduct a cultivating family is to keep toward brahmins"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:43":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":44,"t":["வாயின் அடங்குதல் துப்புரவாம் மாசற்ற","செய்கை அடங்குதல் திப்பியமாம் - பொய்யின்றி","நெஞ்சம் அடங்குதல் வீடாகும் இம்மூன்றும்","வஞ்சத்தின் தீர்ந்த பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Restraint of the mouth is purity, restraint of deed without stain is the divine state, and restraint of the heart without falsehood is release; these three are things free of deceit.","n":"The three restraints rise in order, mouth, deed, heart, and their fruits rise with them, from purity to the heavenly (திப்பியம்) to release (வீடு), so the verse is a ladder rather than a list of equals. மாசற்ற may also be read with துப்புரவாம் (stainless purity) rather than with செய்கை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","veedu","thooimai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 43","lb":"TK43","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாயின் அடங்குதல்","restraint of the mouth is purity",1],["செய்கை அடங்குதல்","restraint of action, without stain, is the divine state",2],["நெஞ்சம் அடங்குதல்","restraint of the heart, without falsehood, is release",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:44":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":45,"t":["விருந்தின்றி உண்ட பகலும் திருந்திழையார்","புல்லப்புடை பெயராக் கங்குலும் - இல்லார்க்கொன்று","ஈயா தொழிந்தகன்ற காலையும் இம்மூன்றும்","நோயே உரனுடை யார்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"A day on which one ate without a guest, a night on which one did not turn to embrace one's well-adorned wife, and a morning on which one went off without giving to the needy are a sickness to the strong-minded.","n":"The three items run through the whole day, day, night and morning (பகல், கங்குல், காலை), so the householder's day is judged complete only when guest, wife and the poor have each had their share; the sickness falls on உரனுடையார், the strong, as if neglect were felt only by those fit to do these duties.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","ilvaazhkkai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 44","lb":"TK44","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["விருந்தின்றி உண்ட பகலும்","a day on which one ate with no guest is a sickness to the strong",1],["புல்லப்புடை பெயராக் கங்குலும்","a night on which one did not turn to embrace one's well-adorned wife is a sickness to the strong",2],["ஈயா தொழிந்தகன்ற காலையும்","a morning on which one left without giving something to those who have nothing is a sickness to the strong",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:45":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":46,"t":["ஆற்றானை ஆற்றென் றலைப்பானும் அன்பின்றி","ஏற்றார்க்கு இயைவ கரப்பானும் - கூற்றம்","வரவுண்மை சிந்தியா தானுமிம் மூவர்","நிரயத்துச் சென்றுவீழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The one who torments a man who cannot do a thing by telling him to do it, the one who without love hides from those who ask what he could give, and the one who does not think on the certainty of Death's coming are three who fall into hell.","n":"The third man has done nothing to anyone: forgetting death is set beside cruelty and concealment as an offence of equal weight, so heedlessness itself earns hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","karavu","kootru","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 45","lb":"TK45","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஆற்றானை ஆற்றென் றலைப்பானும்","one who harasses a person unable to act by ordering him to act falls into hell",1],["ஏற்றார்க்கு இயைவ கரப்பானும்","one who, without love, hides from askers what he could give falls into hell",2],["வரவுண்மை சிந்தியா","one who does not reflect that Death's coming is certain falls into hell",3]],"cw":"மூவர்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:46":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":47,"t":["கால்தூய்மை யில்லாக் கலிமாவும் காழ்கடிந்து","மேல்தூய்மை யில்லாத வெங்களிறும் - சீறிக்","கறுவி வெகுண்டுரைப்பான் பள்ளிஇம் மூன்றும்","குறுகார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"","r":"A spirited horse whose feet are not sound, a fierce elephant with no clean seat above, and the place of one who snarls, nurses rancour and speaks in rage are three things the wise do not go near.","n":"கால்தூய்மை and மேல்தூய்மை are obscure: they may mean a horse not sure of foot and an elephant with no trained rider on its back, or untamed beasts generally. பள்ளி may be the angry man's dwelling or his company. The verse sets an angry man among dangerous animals.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 46","lb":"TK46","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கால்தூய்மை யில்லாக் கலிமாவும்","a spirited horse without soundness of foot is not approached by the wise",1],["மேல்தூய்மை யில்லாத வெங்களிறும்","a fierce elephant without cleanness above is not approached by the wise",2],["வெகுண்டுரைப்பான் பள்ளி","the place of one who rages and speaks in anger is not approached by the wise",3]],"cw":"இம் மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:47":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":48,"t":["சில்சொற் பெருந்தோள் மகளிரும் பல்வகையும்","தாளினால் தந்த விழுநிதியும் - நாடோ றும்","நாத்தளிர்ப்ப ஆக்கிய உண்டியும் இம்மூன்றும்","காப்பிகழ் ஆகாப் பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Women of few words and broad shoulders, the choice wealth one has brought in by many kinds of effort, and food prepared so that it makes the tongue flourish are three things whose guarding must not be slighted.","n":"Women are counted among the goods of the house with earned treasure and good food, all three needing watch; the verse reports the custodial view of women of its period. காப்பிகழ் ஆகா may also be read as 'not to be scorned', which would make the line a praise of the three rather than a rule of keeping them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam","undi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 47","lb":"TK47","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["சில்சொற் பெருந்தோள் மகளிரும்","women of few words and broad shoulders are, the verse says, not to be left unguarded",1],["தாளினால் தந்த விழுநிதியும்","choice wealth earned by one's own effort is not to be left unguarded",2],["நாத்தளிர்ப்ப ஆக்கிய உண்டியும்","food prepared to delight the tongue is not to be left unguarded",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a household norm of the period that counts women among the goods a man must guard"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:48":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":49,"t":["வைதலை இன்சொல்லாக் கொள்வானும் நெய்பெய்த","சோறென்று கூழை மதிப்பானும் - ஊறிய","கைப்பதனைக் கட்டியேன்று உண்பானும் இம்மூவர்","மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The one who takes abuse as sweet speech, the one who values gruel as rice poured with ghee, and the one who eats seeping bitterness accepting it as sugar are three who live having seen the true thing.","n":"Each item takes a bad thing as its opposite, in speech, in food and in taste, and the verse calls this not endurance but sight of the true (மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டு): the bitter is not borne as bitter, it is no longer judged so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","samanilai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 48","lb":"TK48","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வைதலை இன்சொல்லாக் கொள்வானும்","one who takes abuse as sweet words lives having seen the truth",1],["சோறென்று கூழை மதிப்பானும்","one who esteems gruel as ghee-rice lives having seen the truth",2],["கைப்பதனைக் கட்டியேன்று உண்பானும்","one who eats bitterness accepting it as sugar lives having seen the truth",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:49":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":50,"t":["ஏவாது மாற்றும் இளங்கிளையும் காவாது","வைதெள்ளிச் சொல்லும் தலைமகனும் - பொய்தெள்ளி","அம்மனை தேய்க்கும் மனையாளும் இம்மூவர்","இம்மைக்கு உறுதியில் லார்"],"p":"","r":"Young kin who set aside what they are bidden, a head of the house who keeps no guard and speaks in abuse and scorn, and a wife who wears that house down through falsehood are three who are no security for this life.","n":"ஏவாது மாற்றும் may mean 'refuses without being asked' or 'sets aside the bidding'; பொய்தெள்ளி ('sifting falsehood') is unclear. The verse spreads the failure of a house over its junior, its head and its wife, each by a fault of speech or obedience.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","ilvaazhkkai","kadunjol","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 49","lb":"TK49","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஏவாது மாற்றும் இளங்கிளையும்","young kin who set aside what they are bidden are no security for this life",1],["வைதெள்ளிச் சொல்லும் தலைமகனும்","a head of house who, unguarding, speaks in abuse and contempt is no security for this life",2],["அம்மனை தேய்க்கும் மனையாளும்","a wife who wears down that house through falsehood is, the verse says, no security for this life",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a judgment of the period on a wife's conduct in the house, set beside those on kin and husband"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:50":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":51,"t":["கொள்பொருள் வெஃகிக் குடியலைக்கும் வேந்தனும்","உள்பொருள் சொல்லாச் சலமொழி மாந்தரும்","இல்லிருந்து எல்லை கடப்பாளும் இம்மூவர்","வல்லே மழையறுக்கும் கோள்"],"p":"","r":"A king who covets goods to seize and harasses his people, men of deceitful speech who do not say what is within, and a woman who, living in the house, crosses its bounds are three ill planets that swiftly cut off the rain.","n":"கோள் is a malign planet: the verse gives three human faults the astrological power to stop rain, ranging from the throne through common men to a wife, so the cosmic sanction the Kural gives to the king is spread over the whole society.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","mazhai","vaaimai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 50","lb":"TK50","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொள்பொருள் வெஃகிக் குடியலைக்கும் வேந்தனும்","a king who covets plunder and harasses his subjects swiftly cuts off the rain",1],["உள்பொருள் சொல்லாச் சலமொழி மாந்தரும்","men of deceitful speech who hide the truth swiftly cut off the rain",2],["இல்லிருந்து எல்லை கடப்பாளும்","a woman who, being of the house, crosses its bounds is said to cut off the rain",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period binding a wife's conduct to the household's bounds, and her transgression to drought"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:51":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":52,"t":["தூர்ந்துஒழுகிக் கண்ணும் துணைகள் துணைகளே","சார்ந்தொழுகிக் கண்ணும் சலவர் - சலவரே","ஈர்ந்தகல் இன்னாக் கயவர் இவர்மூவர்","தேர்ந்தக்கால் தோன்றும் பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Allies remain allies even when they keep their distance, deceivers remain deceivers even when they keep close, and the base are hurtful like a split stone; these three, when examined, show what they are.","n":"தூர்ந்துஒழுகி is obscure: read with தூரா in verse 56 it means 'when the way is closed', so the ally stays an ally even when estranged. ஈர்ந்தகல் may be a sawn stone that cannot rejoin, an image for the base. The point is constancy of nature under changed conduct.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","kayamai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 51","lb":"TK51","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["துணைகள்","true allies remain allies even when estranged",1],["சலவர்","deceivers remain deceivers even when they keep close",2],["கயவர்","the base are hurtful, like a split stone",3]],"cw":"இவர்மூவர்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:52":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":53,"t":["கண்ணுக்கு அணிகலம் கண்ணோட்டம் காமுற்ற","பெண்ணுக் கணிகலம் நாணுடைமை - நண்ணும்","மறுமைக் கணிகலம் கல்வி இம்மூன்றும்","குறியுடையார் கண்ணே யுள"],"p":"","r":"Kindly regard is the ornament of the eye, modesty the ornament of a woman in love, and learning the ornament for the life to come; these three are found only in those who bear the mark of worth.","n":"Each item has its own wearer (the eye, a woman, the next life), and the closing line gathers them: the three are not acquired apart but are found together in the குறியுடையார். The modesty item assigns naanam to women specifically.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","naanam","kalvi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 52","lb":"TK52","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கண்ணோட்டம்","kindly regard is the ornament of the eye",1],["நாணுடைமை","modesty is, the verse says, the ornament of a woman in love",2],["கல்வி","learning is the ornament for the next world",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a gender norm of the period: modesty assigned as the woman's ornament"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:53":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":54,"t":["குருடன் மனையாள் அழகும் இருள்தீரக்","கற்றறிவு இல்லான் கதழ்ந்துரையும் - பற்றிய","பண்ணின் தெரியாதான் யாழ்கேட்பும் இம்மூன்றும்","எண்ணின் தெரியாப் பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"The beauty of a blind man's wife, the eager talk of a man without learning that clears darkness, and listening to the lute by one who cannot tell its modes are three things that come to nothing when weighed.","n":"Two items are waste on the receiver's side (beauty unseen, music unheard), and the middle one, the unlearned man's eager speech, is put among them: his talk is like a gift with no one able to take it, or a skill with nothing behind it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","payan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 53","lb":"TK53","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["குருடன் மனையாள் அழகும்","the beauty of a blind man's wife comes to nothing",1],["கற்றறிவு இல்லான் கதழ்ந்துரையும்","the hasty speech of a man without learned understanding comes to nothing",2],["பண்ணின் தெரியாதான் யாழ்கேட்பும்","lute-listening by one who cannot tell the modes comes to nothing",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:54":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":55,"t":["தன்பயம் தூக்காரைச் சார்தலும் தாம்பயவா","நல்பயம் காய்வின்கண் கூறலும் - பின்பயவாக்","குற்றம் பிறர்மேல் உரைத்தலும் இம்மூன்றும்","தெற்றெனவு இல்லார் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Attaching oneself to those who do not weigh one's good, speaking of benefits one will not yield oneself in a moment of anger, and charging others with faults when the charge bears no later fruit are the doings of those without clear sense.","n":"The lines are compressed and each item admits more than one parse (தன்பயம் may be one's own good or the other's; காய்வின்கண் may be 'in anger' or 'toward the angry'). The common thread is action without regard to its result, which the closing word தெற்றெனவு (clarity) names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","thelivu","purangooral"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 54","lb":"TK54","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தன்பயம் தூக்காரைச் சார்தலும்","attaching oneself to those who do not weigh one's good is the work of the unclear",1],["நல்பயம் காய்வின்கண் கூறலும்","speaking of good benefits in anger is the work of the unclear",2],["குற்றம் பிறர்மேல் உரைத்தலும்","charging others with faults that bring no later good is the work of the unclear",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:55":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":56,"t":["அருமறை காவாத நட்பும் பெருமையை","வேண்டாது விட்டுஒழிந்த பெண்பாலும் யாண்டானும்","செற்றம்கொண் டாடும் சிறுதொழும்பும் இம்மூவர்","ஒற்றாள் எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"","r":"A friendship that does not keep a weighty secret, a woman who has abandoned the wish for honour, and a petty servant who nurses rancour wherever he is are three who are called spies.","n":"ஒற்றாள் here is not the king's agent but the leak inside one's own circle: friend, woman and servant become informers against the one they are close to. The woman item reports a judgment of the period on women's honour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 55","lb":"TK55","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அருமறை காவாத நட்பும்","a friendship that does not keep a deep secret is a spy",1],["வேண்டாது விட்டுஒழிந்த பெண்பாலும்","a woman who has given up the wish for honour is, the verse says, a spy",2],["செற்றம்கொண் டாடும் சிறுதொழும்பும்","a petty servant who acts out of rancour is a spy",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a judgment of the period that counts a woman without regard for honour among informers"],"ch":"tk:3"},"tk:56":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":57,"t":["முந்தை எழுத்தின் வரவுணர்ந்து பிற்பாடு","தந்தையும் தாயும் வழிபட்டு - வந்த","ஒழுக்கம் பெருநெறி சேர்தல் இம்மூன்றும்","விழுப்ப நெறிதூரா வாறு"],"p":"","r":"First learning the letters as they come, then serving father and mother, and joining the great road of conduct handed down are the three ways by which the path of excellence is not blocked.","n":"முந்தை and பிற்பாடு make this a sequence of life, not a list: letters first, then the parents, then inherited conduct; and the predicate is negative, these keep the road of விழுப்பம் from being choked (தூரா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ozhukkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 56","lb":"TK56","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["முந்தை எழுத்தின் வரவுணர்ந்து","learning the letters first keeps the path of excellence open",1],["தந்தையும் தாயும் வழிபட்டு","serving father and mother afterward keeps the path of excellence open",2],["ஒழுக்கம் பெருநெறி சேர்தல்","joining the great road of inherited conduct keeps the path of excellence open",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:57":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":58,"t":["கொட்டி அளந்தமையாப் பாடலும் தட்டித்துப்","பிச்சைபுக் குண்பான் பிளிற்றலும் - துச்சிருந்தான்","ஆளும் கலங்கா முறுதலும் இம்மூன்றும்","கேள்வியுள் இன்னா தன"],"p":"","r":"Song not measured to the beat, the clamour of one who enters to beg and eat, and a lodger's craving for the vessels his host uses are three things painful to hear.","n":"தட்டித்து and துச்சிருந்தான் ஆளும் கலம் are hard: the second item may be a beggar knocking and shouting, the third a guest in borrowed quarters who covets the master's vessels. The frame கேள்வியுள் echoes verse 41, which names what is best to hear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["innaa","iravu","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 57","lb":"TK57","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொட்டி அளந்தமையாப் பாடலும்","song not measured to the beat is painful to hear",1],["பிச்சைபுக் குண்பான் பிளிற்றலும்","the shouting of one who enters to beg his food is painful to hear",2],["ஆளும் கலங்கா முறுதலும்","a lodger's coveting the vessels his host uses is painful to hear",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:58":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":59,"t":["பழமையை நோக்கி அளித்தல் கிழமையால்","கேளிர் உவப்பத் தழுவுதல் - கேளிராய்த்","துன்னிய சொல்லால் இனம்திரட்டல் இம்மூன்றும்","மன்னற்கு இளையான் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Giving with regard to old ties, embracing kin by right so that they rejoice, and gathering one's people with close words are the work of the king's younger.","n":"The kin-work is assigned not to the king but to the one younger than him: holding the royal kin together is a delegated office. இளையான் may be the younger brother or any junior in the royal line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pazhaimai","kizhamai","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 58","lb":"TK58","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"king","it":[["பழமையை நோக்கி அளித்தல்","giving with regard to long acquaintance is the king's younger's work",1],["கேளிர் உவப்பத் தழுவுதல்","embracing kin by right so that they rejoice is the king's younger's work",2],["துன்னிய சொல்லால் இனம்திரட்டல்","gathering his people with close, kinly words is the king's younger's work",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:59":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":60,"t":["கிளைஞர்க்கு உதவாதவன் செல்வமும் பைங்கூழ்","விளைவின்கண் போற்றான் உழவும் - இளையனாய்க்","கள்ளுண்டு வாழ்வான் குடிமையும் இம்மூன்றும்","உள்ளன போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"","r":"The wealth of one who does not help his kin, the farming of one who does not tend the green crop at ripening, and the family standing of one who lives drinking toddy while young perish while seeming to exist.","n":"உள்ளன போலக் கெடும் says the ruin is hidden: each thing still appears to be there while it is already lost, so the verse warns against judging by possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","sutram","uzhavu","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 59","lb":"TK59","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["கிளைஞர்க்கு உதவாதவன் செல்வமும்","the wealth of one who does not help his kin perishes while seeming to exist",1],["விளைவின்கண் போற்றான் உழவும்","the farming of one who does not guard the crop at ripening perishes while seeming to exist",2],["கள்ளுண்டு வாழ்வான் குடிமையும்","the family standing of one who drinks toddy from youth perishes while seeming to exist",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:60":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":61,"t":["பேஎய்ப் பிறப்பிற் பெரும்பசியும் பாஅய்","விலங்கின் பிறப்பின் வெருவும் - புலம்தெரியா","மக்கள் பிறப்பின் நிரப்பிடும்பை இம்மூன்றும்","துக்கப் பிறப்பாய் விடும்"],"p":"","r":"Great hunger in the birth of a ghost, fear in the birth of a beast, and the misery of want in the birth of humans who cannot discern are three that make birth a birth of sorrow.","n":"Each kind of birth is given its own sorrow, so no birth escapes: the human's is not hunger or fear but want (நிரப்பிடும்பை), and புலம்தெரியா makes it the lot of humans who cannot see clearly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pasi","veruvu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 60","lb":"TK60","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பேஎய்ப் பிறப்பிற் பெரும்பசியும்","great hunger in a ghost's birth makes birth sorrow",1],["விலங்கின் பிறப்பின் வெருவும்","fear in an animal's birth makes birth sorrow",2],["மக்கள் பிறப்பின் நிரப்பிடும்பை","the misery of want in human birth makes birth sorrow",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:3"},"tk:61":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":62,"t":["ஐயறிவும் தம்மை அடைய ஒழுகுதல்","எய்துவது எய்தாமை முற்காத்தல் - வைகலும்","மாறேற்கும் மன்னர் நிலைஅறிதல் இம்மூவர்","சீரேற்ற பேரமைச்சர் கோள்"],"p":"","r":"Conducting oneself so that the fivefold knowledge comes within reach, guarding in advance so that what threatens to arrive does not arrive, and knowing day by day the state of the kings who stand opposed are named the settled practice of great ministers of renown.","n":"ஐயறிவும் is the hard word: it may be the knowledge of the five senses held in order, or fivefold learning a minister must have; either way the first item is a discipline of the self and the other two face outward, to the coming danger and the hostile king. The count word is the personal இம்மூவர் though the three things are practices, not persons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu","otru","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 61","lb":"TK61","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"king","it":[["ஐயறிவும் தம்மை அடைய ஒழுகுதல்","conducting oneself so that the fivefold knowledge is within one's grasp is a great minister's practice",1],["எய்துவது எய்தாமை முற்காத்தல்","guarding beforehand so that the threatened harm never arrives is a great minister's practice",2],["மாறேற்கும் மன்னர் நிலைஅறிதல்","knowing daily how the opposing kings stand is a great minister's practice",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:62":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":63,"t":["நன்றிப் பயன்தூக்கா நாணிலியும் சான்றோர்முன்","மன்றில் கொடும்பா டுரைப்பானும் - நன்றின்றி","வைத்த அடைக்கலங் கொள்வானும் இம்மூவர்","எச்சம் இழந்துவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The shameless man who does not weigh the worth of a kindness done him, the man who speaks crookedly in the public assembly before the worthy, and the man who wrongly keeps a deposit placed with him are named three who live on with their line of descendants lost.","n":"எச்சம் இழந்து names the punishment in the next generation, not in the offender's own life: all three faults are breaches of trust (a kindness, a public judgment, a deposit), and the verse makes the loss of heirs their common wage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","naduvu","adaikkalam","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 62","lb":"TK62","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நன்றிப் பயன்தூக்கா நாணிலியும்","the shameless man who does not weigh a kindness's worth lives with his line cut off",1],["மன்றில் கொடும்பா டுரைப்பானும்","the man who speaks crookedly in the assembly before the worthy lives with his line cut off",2],["வைத்த அடைக்கலங் கொள்வானும்","the man who wrongfully takes a deposit entrusted to him lives with his line cut off",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:63":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":64,"t":["நோவஞ்சா தாரொடு நட்பும் விருந்தஞ்சும்","ஈர்வளையை இல்லத் திருத்தலும் - சீர்பயவாத்","தன்மையி லாளர் அயலிருப்பும் இம்மூன்றும்","நன்மை பயத்தல் இல"],"p":"","r":"Friendship with those who do not shrink from causing pain, keeping in the house a bangled wife who dreads guests, and living beside people whose nature yields no worth are named three things that bring no good.","n":"All three are about who lives near one: a friend, a wife, a neighbour. The wife's fault is not unchastity but fear of guests, so the house fails at the outward duty the next verse assigns her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","virundhu","vaazhkkaithunai","inam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 63","lb":"TK63","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நோவஞ்சா தாரொடு நட்பும்","friendship with those unafraid to hurt brings no good",1],["விருந்தஞ்சும் ஈர்வளையை இல்லத் திருத்தலும்","keeping at home a bangled wife who shrinks from guests brings no good",1],["தன்மையி லாளர் அயலிருப்பும்","living next to people of no worthy nature brings no good",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:64":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":65,"t":["நல்விருந் தோம்பலின் நட்டாளாம் வைகலும்","இல்புறஞ் செய்தலின் ஈன்றதாய் - தொல்குடியின்","மக்கள் பெறலின் மனைக்கிழத்தி இம்மூன்றும்","கற்புடையாள் பூண்ட கடன்"],"p":"","r":"By tending good guests she is a friend, by daily caring for the household she is the mother who bore it, and by bearing children of an old family she is the mistress of the house: these three are named the debts a chaste wife has taken on.","n":"Each duty earns a role (friend, mother, mistress), so the wife is three persons to the house; the closing word கடன் makes them owed, and கற்புடையாள் makes chastity the frame within which all three are counted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai","virundhu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 64","lb":"TK64","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["நல்விருந் தோம்பலின் நட்டாளாம்","by tending good guests the chaste wife acts as a friend, and this is her debt",1],["இல்புறஞ் செய்தலின் ஈன்றதாய்","by caring for the household daily she acts as the mother who bore it, and this is her debt",2],["மக்கள் பெறலின் மனைக்கிழத்தி","by bearing children of an old family she is mistress of the house, and this is her debt",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period for a wife's duties: hospitality, care of the house and bearing children are her debts as a chaste woman"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:65":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":66,"t":["அச்சம் அலைகடலின் தோன்றலும் ஆர்வுற்ற","விட்டகல கில்லாத வேட்கையும் - கட்டிய","மெய்ந்நிலை காணா வெகுளியும் இம்மூன்றும்","தம்நெய்யில் தாம்பொரியு மாறு"],"p":"","r":"Fear rising like the wave-tossed sea, craving that cannot let go of what it has longed for, and anger that cannot see the true state of what is bound up with it are said to be the way one fries in one's own ghee.","n":"The image is self-consumption: the fat that cooks one is one's own, so the three passions need no outside enemy. Fear stands where a quick reader expects confusion beside desire and anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","avaa","vekuli","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 65","lb":"TK65","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அச்சம் அலைகடலின் தோன்றலும்","fear surging like the restless sea makes one fry in one's own ghee",1],["விட்டகல கில்லாத வேட்கையும்","craving that will not release what it has fastened on makes one fry in one's own ghee",2],["மெய்ந்நிலை காணா வெகுளியும்","anger blind to the true state of things makes one fry in one's own ghee",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:66":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":67,"t":["கொழுநனை இல்லாள் கறையும் வழிநிற்கும்","சிற்றாளில் லாதான்கைம் மோதிரமும் - பற்றிய","கோல்கோடி வாழும் அரசனும் இம்மூன்றும்","சால்போடு பட்டது இல"],"p":"","r":"The adornment of a woman who has no husband, the ring on the hand of a man who has no servant to walk behind him, and a king who lives having bent the sceptre he holds are named three things not joined with fitness.","n":"கறை is uncertain: read as dark adornment, the first two items are both ornaments worn without the support that makes them fitting (a husband, a servant), and the king's bent sceptre becomes the same kind of empty sign; read as 'stain', the first item would be a widow's blame, and the parallel weakens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kaimmai","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 66","lb":"TK66","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொழுநனை இல்லாள் கறையும்","the adornment of a husbandless woman is not fitting",1],["சிற்றாளில் லாதான்கைம் மோதிரமும்","a ring on the hand of a man with no attendant is not fitting",2],["கோல்கோடி வாழும் அரசனும்","a king who lives with his sceptre bent is not fitting",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a rule of the period on a woman without a husband: her adornment is counted unfitting"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:67":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":68,"t":["எதிர்நிற்கும் பெண்ணும் இயல்பில் தொழும்பும்","செயிர்நிற்கும் சுற்றமும் ஆகி - மயிர்நரைப்ப","முந்தை பழவினையாய்த் தின்னும் இவைமூன்றும்","நொந்தார் செயக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"","r":"A wife who stands against her husband, servants without proper nature, and kin who stand in fault are said to devour a man, as his hair greys, like the fruit of his old deeds, and there is nothing the sufferer can do about them.","n":"முந்தை பழவினையாய் makes the three household troubles the shape that past karma takes, which is why the verse ends that the sufferer has no remedy: they are borne, not fixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","sutram","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 67","lb":"TK67","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["எதிர்நிற்கும் பெண்ணும்","a wife who stands against her husband devours him like old karma",1],["இயல்பில் தொழும்பும்","servants of no proper nature devour him like old karma",1],["செயிர்நிற்கும் சுற்றமும்","kin who persist in wrong devour him like old karma",2]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:68":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":69,"t":["இல்லார்க்கொன் றீயும் உடைமையும் இவ்வுலகில்","நில்லாமை யுள்ளும் நெறிப்பாடும் - எவ்வுயிர்க்கும்","துன்புறுவ செய்யாத தூய்மையும் இம்மூன்றும்","நன்றறியும் மாந்தர்க்கு உள"],"p":"","r":"Having wealth that gives something to those who have nothing, the right way of one who considers the impermanence of this world, and the purity that does nothing painful to any creature are named three things found in people who know the good.","n":"தூய்மை is defined here by an act toward others, not by bathing or diet: purity is harming no living thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","thooimai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 68","lb":"TK68","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இல்லார்க்கொன் றீயும் உடைமையும்","possessions that give to those who lack are found in those who know the good",1],["நில்லாமை யுள்ளும் நெறிப்பாடும்","a right path shaped by reflecting on impermanence is found in those who know the good",2],["துன்புறுவ செய்யாத தூய்மையும்","the purity of doing no hurt to any creature is found in those who know the good",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:69":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":70,"t":["அருந்தொழில் ஆற்றும் பகடும் திருந்திய","மெய்ந்நிறைந்து நீடிருந்த கன்னியும் - நொந்து","நெறிமாறி வந்த விருந்தும் இம்மூன்றும்","பெறுமா றரிய பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"An ox that does hard work, a maiden of well-formed full body long kept at home, and a guest who arrives in distress having strayed from the road are named three things hard to come by.","n":"The guest is counted as a prize, beside a draught ox and a marriageable girl, not as a burden; the lost and suffering guest is the most valued because he most needs the house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","uzhavu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 69","lb":"TK69","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["அருந்தொழில் ஆற்றும் பகடும்","an ox that does arduous work is hard to obtain",1],["மெய்ந்நிறைந்து நீடிருந்த கன்னியும்","a maiden of full, well-formed body long kept at home is hard to obtain",2],["நெறிமாறி வந்த விருந்தும்","a guest who comes weary after losing the road is hard to obtain",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a period valuation of a young woman's body and seclusion, counted among prized possessions with the ox"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:70":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":71,"t":["காவோ டறக்குளம் தொட்டானும் நாவினால்","வேதம் கரைகண்ட பார்ப்பானும் - தீதிகந்து","ஒல்வதுபாத் துண்ணும் ஒருவனும் இம்மூவர்","செல்வர் எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"","r":"One who dug a tank for charity with a grove beside it, the brahmin who has reached the far shore of the Veda by recitation, and one who, avoiding evil, eats after sharing what he can are the three called rich.","n":"Wealth is redefined as what one has given or mastered: a public work, learning held on the tongue, and food shared. பார்ப்பான் names a birth group, but the verse praises his learning and states no rule of rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","oppuravu","eegai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 70","lb":"TK70","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["காவோ டறக்குளம் தொட்டானும்","the man who dug a charitable tank with a grove is called rich",1],["வேதம் கரைகண்ட பார்ப்பானும்","the brahmin who has mastered the Veda by recitation is called rich",2],["ஒல்வதுபாத் துண்ணும் ஒருவனும்","the man who eats after sharing what he can, shunning wrong, is called rich",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:71":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":72,"t":["உடுத்தாடை இல்லாதார் நீராட்டும் பெண்டிர்","தொடுத்தாண் டவைப்போர் புகலும் - கொடுத்தளிக்கும்","ஆண்மை யுடையவர் நல்குரவும் இம்மூன்றும்","காண அரியவென் கண்"],"p":"","r":"The bathing of those who have no cloth to change into, women who take up and enter the disputation of the assembly, and the poverty of men who have the manliness to give and protect are named three things the poet's eye cannot bear to see.","n":"The first person என் கண் is rare in the book. The line division is doubtful: பெண்டிர் may close the first item (women bathing without a change of cloth) rather than open the second; on the reading taken here, women in the assembly's contest are counted as an unbearable sight. காண அரிய could also mean simply 'rarely seen'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","avai","eegai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 71","lb":"TK71","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["உடுத்தாடை இல்லாதார் நீராட்டும்","bathing by those who have no garment to change into is painful to see",1],["பெண்டிர் தொடுத்தாண் டவைப்போர் புகலும்","women joining the contest of the assembly is painful to see",1],["ஆண்மை யுடையவர் நல்குரவும்","poverty in men whose manliness gives and protects is painful to see",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["gender"],"a period judgment that women entering the assembly's disputation is an unfitting sight"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:72":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":73,"t":["நிறைநெஞ் சுடையானை நல்குர வஞ்சும்","அறனை நினைப்பானை அல்பொருள் அஞ்சும்","மறவனை எவ்வுயிரும் அஞ்சும் இம்மூன்றும்","திறவதில் தீர்ந்த பொருள்"],"p":"","r":"Poverty fears the man of steady heart, ill-gotten wealth fears the man who keeps dharma in mind, and every creature fears the warrior: these three are named matters settled by strength.","n":"The grammar is reversed from the expected: the man does not avoid poverty or wrong wealth, they avoid him, so his inner strength acts on things outside him the way the warrior's acts on creatures.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","aram","maram","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 72","lb":"TK72","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நிறைநெஞ் சுடையானை நல்குர வஞ்சும்","poverty shrinks from the man of steady heart",1],["அறனை நினைப்பானை அல்பொருள் அஞ்சும்","wrongful wealth shrinks from the man who thinks of dharma",2],["மறவனை எவ்வுயிரும் அஞ்சும்","every creature fears the warrior",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:73":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":74,"t":["இரந்துகொண் டொண்பொருள் செய்வல்என் பானும்","பரந்தொழுகும் பெண்பாலைப் பாசமென் பானும்","விரிகட லூடுசெல் வானும் இம்மூவர்","அரிய துணிந்துவாழ் வார்"],"p":"","r":"The man who says he will make bright wealth by begging, the man who calls a woman who goes about with many his bond of love, and the man who sails across the wide sea are named three who live daring what is hard.","n":"The sea voyage is a real risk set beside two risks of judgment, so begging for wealth and trusting a wanton woman are made as perilous as open water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","muyarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 73","lb":"TK73","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இரந்துகொண் டொண்பொருள் செய்வல்என் பானும்","the man who means to build wealth by begging dares the hard",1],["பரந்தொழுகும் பெண்பாலைப் பாசமென் பானும்","the man who calls a woman of roving conduct his love-bond dares the hard",2],["விரிகட லூடுசெல் வானும்","the man who goes through the wide sea dares the hard",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a period judgment on a woman's conduct: a woman who goes about widely is no fit bond"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:74":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":75,"t":["கொலைநின்று தின்றொழுகு வானும் பெரியவர்","புல்லுங்கால் தான்புல்லும் பேதையும் - இல்லெனக்கொன்று","ஈகென் பவனை நகுவானும் இம்மூவர்","யாதும் கடைப்பிடியா தார்"],"p":"","r":"The man who lives by killing and eating, the fool who presumes to embrace the great when they embrace, and the man who laughs at one who says 'I have nothing, give me something' are named three who hold fast to nothing.","n":"கடைப்பிடியாதார் is the common fault: none keeps a rule. The second item is unsure: it may be the fool who takes the familiarity of the great as licence to be familiar himself, or one who clings only when the great cling to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","pedhaimai","iravu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 74","lb":"TK74","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொலைநின்று தின்றொழுகு வானும்","the man who lives by killing and eating keeps no rule",1],["புல்லுங்கால் தான்புல்லும் பேதையும்","the fool who presumes on the great when they embrace him keeps no rule",2],["ஈகென் பவனை நகுவானும்","the man who mocks one who asks for a gift keeps no rule",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:75":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":76,"t":["வள்ளன்மை பூண்டான்கண் செல்வமும் உள்ளத்து","உணர்வுடையான் ஓதிய நூலும் - புணர்வின்கண்","தக்க தறியும் தலைமகனும் இம்மூவர்","பொத்தின்றிக் காழ்த்த மரம்"],"p":"","r":"Wealth in the hands of one bound to generosity, the books learned by one with understanding in his heart, and a leader who knows what is fitting in his alliances are named three trees whose wood has hardened without a hollow.","n":"காழ்த்த மரம் is heartwood, prized for being solid through: the image values soundness within, not fruit given out. புணர்வு may be alliance, counsel or marriage, and the leader is left open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi","arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 75","lb":"TK75","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வள்ளன்மை பூண்டான்கண் செல்வமும்","wealth held by one committed to generosity is solid heartwood",1],["உணர்வுடையான் ஓதிய நூலும்","the learning of one with inner understanding is solid heartwood",2],["தக்க தறியும் தலைமகனும்","the leader who knows what is fit in union is solid heartwood",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:76":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":77,"t":["மாரிநாள் வந்த வருந்தும் மனம்பிறிதாய்க்","காரியத்திற் குன்றாக் கணிகையும் - வீரியத்து","மாற்றம் மறுத்துரைக்கும் சேவகனும் இம்மூவர்","போற்றற்கு அரியார் புரிந்து"],"p":"","r":"A guest who comes on a rainy day, a courtesan whose heart is elsewhere yet who never falls short in her business, and a servant who in his valour answers back against a command are named three who are hard to tend even when one tries.","n":"Our text reads வருந்தும், which would make the first item 'the one who suffers on a rainy day'; the count and the sense want விருந்தும், the guest. The three are hard to keep for different reasons: the guest's need, the courtesan's divided heart, the servant's pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","varaivinmagalir","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 76","lb":"TK76","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["மாரிநாள் வந்த வருந்தும்","a guest arriving on a rainy day is hard to tend",1],["காரியத்திற் குன்றாக் கணிகையும்","a courtesan whose heart is elsewhere yet keeps her trade is hard to keep",2],["மாற்றம் மறுத்துரைக்கும் சேவகனும்","a servant who defiantly contradicts orders in his valour is hard to keep",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a period view of the courtesan as a woman whose divided heart makes her hard to keep"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:77":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":78,"t":["கயவரைக் கையிகந்து வாழ்தல் நயவரை","நள்ளிருளும் கைவிடா நட்டொழுகல் - தெள்ளி","வடுவான வாராமல் காத்தல் இம்மூன்றும்","குடிமா சிலார்க்கே உள"],"p":"","r":"Living out of reach of the base, keeping friendship with the good without forsaking them even in the dead of night, and carefully guarding that nothing scarring comes are named three things found only in those whose family is spotless.","n":"The closing ஏ (உள்ளார்க்கே) restricts the three to the spotless family: the verse reads conduct as the sign of lineage rather than lineage as a licence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","natpu","kayamai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 77","lb":"TK77","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கயவரைக் கையிகந்து வாழ்தல்","living beyond reach of the base is found only in a spotless family",1],["நள்ளிருளும் கைவிடா நட்டொழுகல்","holding to friendship with the good even at midnight is found only in a spotless family",2],["வடுவான வாராமல் காத்தல்","guarding so that no scar of blame comes is found only in a spotless family",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:78":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":79,"t":["தூய்மை உடைமை துணிவாம் தொழிலகற்றும்","வாய்மை உடைமை வனப்பாகும் - தீமை","மனத்தினும் வாயினும் சொல்லாமை மூன்றும்","தவத்தில் தருக்கினார் கோள்"],"p":"","r":"Having purity is boldness, having truthfulness that drives off evil deeds is beauty, and not uttering evil in mind or speech: these three are named the practice of those who have grown strong in penance.","n":"The first two items carry their own predicates (purity is boldness, truth is beauty) inside the count; துணிவாம் தொழிலகற்றும் could also be read together as 'truth that removes rash deeds', which would shift the first item's predicate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thooimai","vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 78","lb":"TK78","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"ascetic","it":[["தூய்மை உடைமை","possessing purity, which is boldness, is the ascetic's practice",1],["வாய்மை உடைமை","possessing truthfulness that drives off evil deeds, which is beauty, is the ascetic's practice",2],["தீமை மனத்தினும் வாயினும் சொல்லாமை","not voicing evil in mind or in speech is the ascetic's practice",2]],"cw":"மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:79":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":80,"t":["பழிஅஞ்சான் வாழும் பசுவும் அழிவினால்","கொண்ட அருந்தவம் விட்டானும் - கொண்டிருந்து","இல்லஞ்சி வாழும் எருதும் இவர்மூவர்","நெல்லுண்டல் நெஞ்சிற்கோர் நோய்"],"p":"","r":"The man who lives without fearing blame, who is a cow; the man who through decline gave up the hard penance he took on; and the man who, having married, lives in fear of his wife, who is an ox: that these three eat rice is a sickness to the heart.","n":"இவர்மூவர் is a personal form, so the cow and the ox are men called beasts; the predicate is that their being fed at all grieves the onlooker. The first item could instead be the cow of a blameless-fearless man, which the count word argues against.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","thavam","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 79","lb":"TK79","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பழிஅஞ்சான் வாழும் பசுவும்","the man who lives unafraid of blame, a mere cow, grieves the heart by eating rice",1],["கொண்ட அருந்தவம் விட்டானும்","the man who abandoned the hard penance he had taken up grieves the heart by eating rice",2],["இல்லஞ்சி வாழும் எருதும்","the married man who lives in fear of his wife, an ox, grieves the heart by eating rice",3]],"cw":"இவர்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a period judgment on a husband who lives in fear of his wife"],"ch":"tk:4"},"tk:80":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":81,"t":["முறைசெய்யான் பெற்ற தலைமையும் நெஞ்சில்","நிறையிலான் கொண்ட தவமும் - நிறைஒழுக்கம்","தேற்றாதான் பெற்றவனப்பும் இவைமூன்றும்","தூற்றின்கண் தூவிய வித்து"],"p":"","r":"Leadership gained by one who does not rule justly, penance taken up by one without restraint of heart, and beauty gained by one who does not make his conduct perfect are named seed scattered into a thicket.","n":"Each item is a gain (rank, penance, beauty) that comes to someone who lacks its inner condition, so the waste lies in the receiver, not in the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nirai","sengol","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 80","lb":"TK80","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["முறைசெய்யான் பெற்ற தலைமையும்","headship won by one who does not do justice is seed thrown into a thicket",1],["நிறையிலான் கொண்ட தவமும்","penance taken up by one without inner restraint is seed thrown into a thicket",2],["தேற்றாதான் பெற்றவனப்பும்","beauty in one who does not perfect his conduct is seed thrown into a thicket",3]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"tk:4"},"tk:81":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":82,"t":["தோள்வழங்கி வாழும் துறைபோல் கணிகையும்","நாள்கழகம் பார்க்கும் நயமிலாச் சூதனும்","வாசிகொண் டொண்பொருள் செய்வானும் இம்மூவர்","ஆசைக் கடலுள் ஆழ்வார்"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan who lives by offering her shoulders like a public ford, the gambler without decency who looks to the gaming hall every day, and the man who makes bright wealth by taking a premium on money are named three who sink in the sea of craving.","n":"The three are not the customers of vice but those who live by it: the seller of the body, the professional gambler and the money-dealer. வாசி is read here as the premium or discount taken in exchanging or lending money; the word allows other trades, so the third figure is the least certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","varaivinmagalir","soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 81","lb":"TK81","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["தோள்வழங்கி வாழும் துறைபோல் கணிகையும்","the courtesan who lives by giving her shoulders to all comers, like a ford anyone may use, sinks in craving",1],["நாள்கழகம் பார்க்கும் நயமிலாச் சூதனும்","the graceless gambler who looks to the gaming hall daily sinks in craving",2],["வாசிகொண் டொண்பொருள் செய்வானும்","the man who makes fine wealth by taking a premium on money sinks in craving",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:82":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":83,"t":["சான்றாருள் சான்றான் எனப்படுதல் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","தோய்ந்தாருள் தோய்ந்தான் எனப்படுதல் - பாய்ந்தெழுந்து","கொள்ளாருள் கொள்ளாத கூறாமை இம்மூன்றும்","நல்லாள் வழங்கும் நெறி"],"p":"","r":"Being called worthy among the worthy at all times, being called close among those who are close, and not saying, even when roused to spring up, what those who will not take it would not accept are named three as the way a good person offers.","n":"The first two are standings given by peers, the third a restraint one keeps oneself, so the verse makes reputation and silence one path. நல்லாள் can be a good person or a good woman; nothing else in the verse points to a woman, so it is read generally.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","natpu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 82","lb":"TK82","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["சான்றாருள் சான்றான் எனப்படுதல்","to be called worthy by the worthy themselves belongs to the good person's path",1],["தோய்ந்தாருள் தோய்ந்தான் எனப்படுதல்","to be called intimate by those one is intimate with belongs to that path",2],["கொள்ளாருள் கொள்ளாத கூறாமை","not speaking, even when provoked, what the unreceptive will not receive belongs to that path",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:83":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":84,"t":["உப்பின் பெருங்குப்பை நீர்படியின் இல்ஆகும்","நட்பின் கொழுமுளை பொய்வழங்கின் இல்ஆகும்","செப்பம் உடையார் மழையனையர் இம்மூன்றும்","செப்பநெறி தூரா வாறு"],"p":"","r":"A great heap of salt is gone if water soaks it, the rich shoot of friendship is gone if a lie is dealt, and the upright are like rain; these three are named a way the path of rectitude is kept from being choked.","n":"Two items are images of dissolution and the third an image of giving, and the closing predicate (செப்பநெறி தூரா ஆறு) has to cover both; it is read as lessons that keep the straight path open, which is a guess at how the list holds together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","natpu","vaaimai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 83","lb":"TK83","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["உப்பின் பெருங்குப்பை","a great heap of salt vanishes once water soaks into it",1],["நட்பின் கொழுமுளை","the thriving shoot of friendship vanishes once falsehood is dealt in it",2],["செப்பம் உடையார்","the upright are like rain, giving to all",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:84":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":85,"t":["வாய்நன்கு அமையாக் குளனும் வயிறாரத்","தாய்முலை உண்ணாக் குழவியும் சேய்மரபில்","கல்விமாண் பில்லாத மாந்தரும் இம்மூவர்","நல்குரவு சேரப்பட் டார்"],"p":"","r":"A tank whose inlet is not well made, an infant that has not drunk its fill at its mother's breast, and people of a long line who lack the excellence of learning are named three joined to poverty.","n":"All three fail to take in what should fill them: water, milk, learning. சேய்மரபில் is read as 'in a long or distant line'; it could instead mean those of a far-off or lesser descent, which would change who the third item faults.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","inmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 84","lb":"TK84","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["வாய்நன்கு அமையாக் குளனும்","a tank whose mouth is badly made is poor",1],["தாய்முலை உண்ணாக் குழவியும்","an infant not fed full at its mother's breast is poor",2],["கல்விமாண் பில்லாத மாந்தரும்","people of a long line who lack excellence in learning are poor",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:85":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":86,"t":["எள்ளப் படுமரபிற்று ஆகலும் உள்பொருளைக்","கேட்டு மறவாத கூர்மையும் முட்டின்றி","உள்பொருள் சொல்லும் உணர்ச்சியும் இம்மூன்றும்","உள்ளிய ஒற்றாள் குணம்"],"p":"","r":"Being of a kind that people scorn, a sharpness that does not forget a secret once heard, and the perception that reports the hidden matter without fail are named the three qualities of a spy worth considering.","n":"The first quality is not a virtue but a cover: to belong to a despised kind is to be overlooked, and the verse counts it among qualities on the same footing as memory and judgment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 85","lb":"TK85","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"king","it":[["எள்ளப் படுமரபிற்று ஆகலும்","being of a kind people look down on is a spy's quality",1],["கேட்டு மறவாத கூர்மையும்","a sharpness that never forgets the secret it has heard is a spy's quality",2],["உள்பொருள் சொல்லும் உணர்ச்சியும்","the discernment to report the hidden matter without gaps is a spy's quality",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:86":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":87,"t":["அன்புப் பெருந்தளை யாப்பு நெகிழ்ந்தொழிதல்","கற்புப் பெரும்புணை காதலின் கைவிடுதல்","நட்பின் நயநீர்மை நீங்கல் இவைமூன்றும்","குற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை"],"p":"","r":"The loosening and ending of the great tie of love, letting go of the great raft of chastity out of desire, and the loss of friendship's gracious nature are named three as an enmity that brings fault.","n":"The three are bonds (a tie, a raft, a nature) and their breaking is called an enmity, as if the broken bond turns into an enemy. Whose chastity is let go is not said: it can be a wife's lapse or, as verse 97 suggests, a husband's casting off a chaste wife for another desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","karpu","natpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 86","lb":"TK86","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அன்புப் பெருந்தளை யாப்பு நெகிழ்ந்தொழிதல்","the great bond of love coming loose and ending is an enmity that brings fault",1],["கற்புப் பெரும்புணை காதலின் கைவிடுதல்","letting the great raft of chastity go out of desire is an enmity that brings fault",2],["நட்பின் நயநீர்மை நீங்கல்","friendship losing its gracious quality is an enmity that brings fault",3]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:87":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":88,"t":["கொல்வது தானஞ்சான் வேண்டலும் கல்விக்கு","அகன்ற இனம்புகு வானும் இருந்து","விழுநிதி குன்றுவிப் பானும் இம்மூவர்","முழுமக்க ளாகற்பா லார்"],"p":"","r":"One who, without fear, wants to kill, one who goes into a company far from learning, and one who sits idle and lets great wealth dwindle are named three fit to be utter fools.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kolai","inam","madi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 87","lb":"TK87","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொல்வது தானஞ்சான் வேண்டலும்","the man who, not afraid of it, wants to kill is an utter fool",1],["அகன்ற இனம்புகு வானும்","the man who enters a company remote from learning is an utter fool",2],["விழுநிதி குன்றுவிப் பானும்","the man who sits still and lets a great store of wealth shrink is an utter fool",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:88":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":89,"t":["பிணிதன்னைத் தின்னுங்கால் தான்வருந்து மாறும்","தணிவில் பெருங்கூற் றுயிருண்ணு மாறும்","பிணைசெல்வ மாண்பின்று இயங்கலிவை மூன்றும்","புணையின் நிலைகலக்கும் ஆறு"],"p":"","r":"The way one suffers when disease eats at him, the way great Death, never abating, consumes life, and wealth tied to one moving on without worth are named three ways that unsettle the standing of the raft.","n":"The raft (புணை) is most likely the body or the life it carries. The third item is the hardest: பிணைசெல்வம் may be wealth bound to one, or pledged wealth, and மாண்பின்று இயங்கல் may mean it passes away without dignity; the reading given is one of several the words allow. The count word is fused by sandhi with the third item (இயங்கலிவை மூன்றும்), so only மூன்றும் is given as written.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","noy","kootru","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 88","lb":"TK88","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பிணிதன்னைத் தின்னுங்கால் தான்வருந்து மாறும்","the suffering when disease devours one shakes the raft",1],["தணிவில் பெருங்கூற் றுயிருண்ணு மாறும்","unrelenting Death eating up life shakes the raft",2],["பிணைசெல்வ மாண்பின்று இயங்கல","bound wealth moving on without worth shakes the raft",3]],"cw":"மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:89":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":90,"t":["அருளினை நெஞ்சத் தடைகொடா தானும்","பொருளினைத் துவ்வான் புதைத்துவைப் பானும்","இறந்து இன்னா சொல்லகிற் பானுமிம் மூவர்","பிறந்தும் பிறந்திலா தார்"],"p":"","r":"One who gives grace no room in his heart, one who buries wealth without enjoying it, and one who oversteps and speaks hurtful words are named three who, though born, are as if never born.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","ivaral","kadunjol","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 89","lb":"TK89","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அருளினை நெஞ்சத் தடைகொடா தானும்","the man who gives grace no shelter in his heart is unborn though born",1],["பொருளினைத் துவ்வான் புதைத்துவைப் பானும்","the man who buries his wealth and does not enjoy it is unborn though born",2],["இறந்து இன்னா சொல்லகிற்","the man who exceeds measure and can speak painful words is unborn though born",3]],"cw":"மூவர்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:90":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":91,"t":["ஈதற்குச் செய்க பொருளை அறநெறி","சேர்தற்குச் செய்க பெருநூலை - யாதும்","அருள்புரிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லைஇம் மூன்றும்","இருள்உலகம் சேராத ஆறு"],"p":"","r":"The verse names earning wealth for the sake of giving, learning the great books so as to join the path of dharma, and speaking words always with grace as three ways not to reach the world of darkness.","n":"This is one of the few verses in the book in the imperative (செய்க, சொல்லுக); each item gives a purpose to a means, wealth for giving and books for dharma, so earning and study are judged by their end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi","arul","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 90","lb":"TK90","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["ஈதற்குச் செய்க பொருளை","making wealth for the sake of giving keeps one from the dark world",1],["சேர்தற்குச் செய்க பெருநூலை","studying the great books to join dharma's path keeps one from the dark world",2],["அருள்புரிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை","speaking every word with grace keeps one from the dark world",3]],"cw":"இம் மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:91":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":92,"t":["பெறுதிகண் பொச்சாந்து உரைத்தல் உயிரை","இறுதிக்கண் யாமிழந்தேம் என்றல் - மறுவந்து","தன்னுடம்பு கன்றுங்கால் நாணுதல் இம்மூன்றும்","மன்னா உடம்பின் குறி"],"p":"","r":"Speaking heedlessly at the moment of gain, saying at the end 'we have lost our life', and feeling shame when a blemish comes and one's own body withers are named three signs of the body that does not last.","n":"The predicate can mean signs that the body is passing (marks of approaching death) or the marks of one who lives as if the body were not passing; the first item, heedless speech at a gain, fits the second sense better, the second and third the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 91","lb":"TK91","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["பெறுதிகண் பொச்சாந்து உரைத்தல்","speaking forgetfully at a time of gain is a sign of the passing body",1],["இறுதிக்கண் யாமிழந்தேம் என்றல்","saying at the end that one has lost one's life is a sign of the passing body",2],["தன்னுடம்பு கன்றுங்கால் நாணுதல்","shame when a blemish appears and one's body withers is a sign of the passing body",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:92":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":93,"t":["விழுத்திணைத் தோன்றா தவனும் எழுத்தினை","ஒன்றும் உணராத ஏழையும் - என்றும்","இறந்துரை காமுறு வானும் இம்மூவர்","பிறந்தும் பிறவா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"One not born in an excellent family line, the wretch who understands not a single letter, and one who always delights in excessive speech are named three who, though born, are as if never born.","n":"விழுத்திணை is read as a high birth-group or lineage (திணை as kind or clan), which makes the first item a rule about birth ranked with two faults of learning and speech. If திணை is read as conduct, the item would fault character instead; the birth reading is the plainer one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","kallaamai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 92","lb":"TK92","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["விழுத்திணைத் தோன்றா தவனும்","the man not born into an excellent line is as if unborn",1],["ஒன்றும் உணராத ஏழையும்","the poor wretch who knows not one letter is as if unborn",2],["இறந்துரை காமுறு வானும்","the man who loves speaking beyond measure is as if unborn",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["caste"],"a birth-rank judgment of the period: not being born in an excellent line is counted with illiteracy as making a life void"],"ch":"tk:5"},"tk:93":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":94,"t":["இருளாய்க் கழியும் உலகமும் யாதும்","தெரியா துரைக்கும் வெகுள்வும் - பொருள்அல்ல","காதல் படுக்கும் விழைவும் இவைமூன்றும்","பேதைமை வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"","r":"A world that passes away in darkness, anger that speaks without knowing anything, and desire that sets love on things of no worth are named three that belong to a creature living in folly.","n":"The three are the familiar triad of delusion, anger and desire, each given by what it does: delusion as a world gone dark, anger as speech without knowledge, desire as love for what is not wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vekuli","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 93","lb":"TK93","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["இருளாய்க் கழியும் உலகமும்","a life passing in darkness belongs to the fool",1],["தெரியா துரைக்கும் வெகுள்வும்","anger that speaks knowing nothing belongs to the fool",2],["காதல் படுக்கும் விழைவும்","desire that fixes love on things of no worth belongs to the fool",3]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:94":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":95,"t":["நண்பில்லார் மாட்டு நசைக்கிழமை செய்வானும்","பெண்பாலைக் காப்பிகழும் பேதையும் - பண்பில்","இழுக்கான சொல்லாடு வானும் இம்மூவர்","ஒழுக்கம் கடைப்பிடியா தார்"],"p":"","r":"One who seeks intimacy of desire with those who bear no friendship, the fool who neglects guarding his woman, and one who speaks words that slip from good character are named three who do not hold to right conduct.","n":"பெண்பாலைக் காப்பிகழும் is read as a man neglecting to guard the woman of his house; the verse counts this as his own lapse of conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","natpu","sol","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 94","lb":"TK94","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["நண்பில்லார் மாட்டு நசைக்கிழமை செய்வானும்","the man who courts intimacy with the unfriendly does not keep conduct",1],["பெண்பாலைக் காப்பிகழும் பேதையும்","the fool who neglects guarding his woman does not keep conduct",2],["இழுக்கான சொல்லாடு வானும்","the man who speaks words that fall from good character does not keep conduct",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a gender norm of the period: a man's guarding of the woman of his house is treated as part of his conduct"],"ch":"tk:5"},"tk:95":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":96,"t":["அறிவுஅழுங்கத் தின்னும் பசிநோயும் மாந்தர்","செறிவழங்கத் தோன்றும் விழைவும் - செறுநரின்","வெவ்வுரை நோனா வெகுள்வும் இவைமூன்றும்","நல்வினை நீக்கும் படை"],"p":"","r":"The disease of hunger that eats until wisdom fails, desire that arises so that self-restraint fails, and anger that cannot bear the hot words of enemies are named three as an army that drives out good deeds.","n":"Hunger, a bodily need, stands in one army with two passions. Line 2 is read as செறிவு அழுங்க, restraint perishing, which the printed text's செறிவழங்க only half shows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasi","avaa","vekuli","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 95","lb":"TK95","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["அறிவுஅழுங்கத் தின்னும் பசிநோயும்","the hunger-sickness that eats until wisdom fails drives out good deeds",1],["செறிவழங்கத் தோன்றும் விழைவும்","desire that rises to undo restraint drives out good deeds",2],["வெவ்வுரை நோனா வெகுள்வும்","anger unable to bear enemies' harsh words drives out good deeds",3]],"cw":"இவைமூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:96":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":97,"t":["கொண்டான் குறிப்பறிவான் பெண்டாட்டி கொண்டன","செய்வகை செய்வான் தவசி கொடிதொரீஇ","நல்லவை செய்வான் அரசன் இவர்மூவர்","பெய்எனப் பெய்யும் மழை"],"p":"","r":"The wife who knows her husband's mind, the ascetic who carries out what he has undertaken, and the king who shuns cruelty and does good are named three at whose word 'rain' the rain falls.","n":"The wife's power is defined by her husband's intention, the ascetic's by his vows, the king's by his justice: each keeps the line of a station. Line 1 prints குறிப்பறிவான், a masculine form, for the wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","karpu","thavam","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 96","lb":"TK96","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கொண்டான் குறிப்பறிவான் பெண்டாட்டி","the wife who knows her husband's wish commands the rain",1],["செய்வகை செய்வான் தவசி","the ascetic who performs what he has taken on commands the rain",2],["நல்லவை செய்வான் அரசன்","the king who turns from cruelty and does good commands the rain",3]],"cw":"இவர்மூவர்","nr":[["gender"],"a gender norm of the period: a wife's merit is defined by knowing and following her husband's mind"],"ch":"tk:5"},"tk:97":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":98,"t":["ஐங்குரவர் ஆணை மறுத்தலும் ஆர்வுற்ற","எஞ்சாத நட்பினுள் பொய்வழக்கும் - நெஞ்சமர்ந்த","கற்புடை யாளைத் துறத்தலும் இம்மூன்றும்","நற்புடையி லாளர் தொழில்"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the command of the five elders, false dealing within a whole and eager friendship, and abandoning the chaste wife settled in one's heart are named three as the acts of those without goodness.","n":"நற்புடையிலாளர் is an unusual form, read as 'those who lack goodness'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","natpu","karpu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 97","lb":"TK97","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"householder","it":[["ஐங்குரவர் ஆணை மறுத்தலும்","refusing the five elders' command is the act of the graceless",1],["எஞ்சாத நட்பினுள் பொய்வழக்கும்","dealing falsely in a full friendship is the act of the graceless",2],["கற்புடை யாளைத் துறத்தலும்","forsaking a chaste wife dear to one's heart is the act of the graceless",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:98":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":99,"t":["செந்தீ முதல்வர் அறம்நினைந்து வாழ்தலும்","வெஞ்சின வேந்தன் முறைநெறியில் சேர்தலும்","பெண்பால் கொழுநன் வழிச்செலவும் இம்மூன்றும்","திங்கள்மும் மாரிக்கு வித்து"],"p":"","r":"The fire-priests living with dharma in mind, the fiercely angry king keeping to the path of just rule, and the wife going the way of her husband are named three as the seed of the month's three rains.","n":"செந்தீ முதல்வர், those foremost at the red fire, names the fire-tending priests by their rite; the verse sets their dharma first and makes a priestly order, a royal order and a wife's obedience joint causes of the rains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","andhanar","sengol","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 98","lb":"TK98","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["செந்தீ முதல்வர் அறம்நினைந்து வாழ்தலும்","the fire-priests living mindful of dharma are a seed of the rains",1],["வெஞ்சின வேந்தன் முறைநெறியில் சேர்தலும்","the fierce king keeping to just rule is a seed of the rains",2],["பெண்பால் கொழுநன் வழிச்செலவும்","the wife going her husband's way is a seed of the rains",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","nr":[["caste","gender","ritual"],"norms of the period: the fire-priests' dharma and the wife's following of her husband are stated as causes of rain"],"ch":"tk:5"},"tk:99":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":100,"t":["கற்றாரைக் கைவிட்டு வாழ்தலும் காமுற்ற","பெட்டாங்கு செய்தொழுகும் பேதையும் - முட்டின்றி","அல்லவை செய்யும் அலவலையும் இம்மூவர்","நல்லுலகம் சேரா தவர்"],"p":"","r":"Living after forsaking the learned, the fool who acts on whatever he desires, and the babbler who does wrong unchecked are named three who will not reach the good world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","saandror","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 99","lb":"TK99","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"all","it":[["கற்றாரைக் கைவிட்டு வாழ்தலும்","living with the learned cast off bars one from the good world",1],["பெட்டாங்கு செய்தொழுகும் பேதையும்","the fool who does as he pleases does not reach the good world",2],["அல்லவை செய்யும் அலவலையும்","the babbler who does wrong without restraint does not reach the good world",3]],"cw":"இம்மூவர்","ch":"tk:5"},"tk:100":{"book":"thirikadugam","num":101,"t":["பத்திமை சான்ற படையும் பலர்தொகினும்","எத்துணையும் அஞ்சா எயிலரணும் - வைத்துஅமைந்த","எண்ணின் உலவா விழுநிதியும் இம்மூன்றும்","மண்ணாகும் மேந்தர்க்கு உறுப்பு"],"p":"","r":"An army full of loyalty, a fortress that fears nothing however many gather against it, and great treasure laid up beyond counting are named three as the limbs of kings who rule the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","padai","aran","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirikadugam 100","lb":"TK100","poet":"நல்லாதனார்","dm":"king","it":[["பத்திமை சான்ற படையும்","an army full of loyalty is a limb of kingship",1],["எத்துணையும் அஞ்சா எயிலரணும்","a walled fort that fears no numbers is a limb of kingship",2],["எண்ணின் உலவா விழுநிதியும்","a stored treasure that counting cannot exhaust is a limb of kingship",3]],"cw":"இம்மூன்றும்","ch":"tk:5"}},"links":[{"a":"k:1","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"The opening verse sets the world under a First One without reservation. In 1062, Valluvar curses whoever ordered the world so that people must beg to live. Read together, they show the book does not treat the world's source as beyond moral judgement.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Here learning has no fruit without bowing at the feet. In 400, learning itself is the one imperishable wealth, with no such condition.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"The same word, ஐந்தவித்தான், describes the Lord here and a human renunciant in 25. Sharing the epithet suggests that the divine model and the human achievement are not wholly separate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Here only those at the Lord's feet cross the sea of births. In 38, on the commentators' reading, a daily good deed blocks the road of births, and God is not mentioned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1031","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1031, the whole world follows the plough. This verse adds what the praise of agriculture leaves out: the plough itself follows the rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"Here giving and austerity cannot last without rain. In 34, virtue is simply a spotless mind and everything else is noise. If virtue is only inward, the drought in 19 shouldn't threaten it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"Hunger is here the world's worst consequence of drought, a natural evil. In 225, relieving hunger outranks the ascetic's power to endure it, which turns this natural fact into a moral priority.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Here the texts' settled view exalts renouncers above all. In 49, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the thing called virtue is household life, and renunciation is good only if it is blameless. The book states both verdicts without reconciling them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:975","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness by rare, difficult acts. Chapter 3 says this of renouncers, while 975 in the chapter on greatness says it of the great in general, so the test is not limited to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"Here the sage's unstoppable anger shows his stature. In 301, real restraint means holding anger back exactly where it can hurt. By the standard of 301, the anger praised in 29 is what should be held back.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:30","b":"k:134","type":"agrees","note":"Both make conduct the test of priestly status: 30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion, and 134 says a பார்ப்பான் is lost if conduct fails, even though forgotten scripture can be relearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"Here outward virtue is noise if the mind is stained. In 280, a shaven head and matted hair are unnecessary if one avoids what the world condemns. Note that 280 keeps the world's blame as the test, so the inward standard of 34 isn't all that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press virtue into the present against the delay of 'later'. 36 promises a companion, and 335 frightens with the hiccup that stops the tongue. Each uses a different motive for the same urgency.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"On the commentators' reading, 37 treats the difference between rider and bearer as virtue made visible. In 972, birth is the same for all beings, and distinction comes from what one does, which still leaves open whether the palanquin bearer's lot is deserved.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"In 23 those who weigh both paths and take up virtue here stand highest in the world. In 46 the householder has nothing to gain by leaving. Parimelazhagar resolves this by limiting 46 to the forest stage, but the verse doesn't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Sharing food is a household duty here, and in 322 it becomes the chief of all that the authors have listed. What began as one household rule becomes the book's summary of virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:234","type":"develops","note":"Here a well-lived earthly life earns a place among the gods. In 234 earthly renown goes further, so that the gods' world ceases to praise its own sages. Both measure human achievement against heaven and rate it highly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:55","type":"tension","note":"Verse 9 calls a head that does not bow to the Lord's feet useless. Here a woman who does not worship a god is the one whom the rain obeys. Parimelazhagar's added 'other gods' softens the contrast, but the Tamil does not contain it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"In 18, even the gods' worship depends on rain. Here rain answers to a woman who bypasses the gods. Together the two verses invert the expected order of gods, rain and humans.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:51","b":"k:478","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The wife lives within her husband's income, and 478 applies the same rule to the king's treasury: a small inflow is no ruin if outflow doesn't widen. The Kural's household economics and statecraft share one rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"Here நிறை is the guard that outdoes all confinement. In 1251, speaking as a woman in love, desire's axe breaks the door of நிறை barred with shame. The book's ethics section treats inner guard as sufficient, and its love section knows it isn't.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Rain is the world's amrita in 11, and in 64 a child's messy gruel beats amrita. The same word is the top of two different scales: what sustains all life, and what is sweet to one parent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"Parimelazhagar's two comments pull apart. On 63 a son's good deeds reach his parents. On 377 he says the fruit of one soul's deed never goes to another (ch038). The verses themselves don't settle how merit is shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"The father's debt and the son's debt form an exchange: he gives the learning that seats the son at the front, and the son gives back a sentence of praise. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 70 makes the link explicit by calling the father 'the one who made him learned'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both say that a living body can be dead in effect, and Manakkudavar on 9 also says such bodies lack life. The criterion is worship in chapter 1 and love in chapter 8, so the two chapters compete for what makes a person alive.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1251","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a bolted door. Love has no bolt here, and in 1251 desire breaks the door of நிறை that shame has barred. The image passes from the ethics of love to its erotic form, with the same result: it won't stay shut in.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"This strengthens Kalaignar's reading of 76. In 773, the highest courage is fierce, and its sharp edge is kindness to those in need, so the book elsewhere does join courage to kindness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"Amrita is beaten twice in these household chapters: a child's stirred gruel is sweeter than it, and even it isn't worth having while a guest waits. In both, food's worth is decided by who else is at the meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:81","b":"k:212","type":"agrees","note":"Both end in the same phrase, வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு: 81 says the home exists for this, and 212 says wealth earned by effort does. Two different goods are given one purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:1005","type":"develops","note":"Here refusal of guests is poverty amid plenty. In 1005, someone with crores is destitute if he neither gives nor enjoys. The later verse widens the charge by also treating failure to enjoy one's wealth as a failure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:1111","type":"same_image","note":"The anicham measures the guest here and the beloved in 1111, and both are more delicate than it. The book's most tender image for a lover was first used for a stranger at the door.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:92","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the pair அகனமர்ந்து and முகனமர்ந்து. In 84 the goddess's glad heart answers the host's glad face. In 92 the glad face outranks even the giver's glad heart, so the same pair yields a different ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:292","type":"tension","note":"Sweet words here must be free of deceit. In 292, even a falsehood counts as truth if it yields blameless good. What 91 excludes by definition, 292 may allow when the outcome justifies it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"Here a pleasant word beats a gladly given gift. In 225, relieving hunger is a power above the ascetic's. A hungry person's view of 92 might differ, which suggests the ranking in 92 assumes someone who isn't in need.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar limits humility in 95 to those who deserve deference. In 125, bowing is good for all and most of all for the rich, which speaks to who should bow rather than to whom, and doesn't contain his limit.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"97 requires speech to be useful and gentle, and 200 says to speak only what is useful. The later chapter keeps the usefulness test and drops the sweetness, which shows the two tests are independent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:316","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 99 argues from how sweet words feel to oneself. 316 makes the same move in general: don't do to others what you know to be painful. The same reciprocity works for speech and for deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:107","type":"same_image","note":"Both use seven births. In 62 a child's character shields the parent, and in 107 memory of a helper persists. Parimelazhagar on 107 refers back to his explanation at 62, so he treats the phrase as one idea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"On Kalaignar's reading of 105, both make the recipient the measure of the gift: the guest's worth in 87 and the helped person's character in 105. Parimelazhagar's different reading of 105 separates them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Help without weighing the return is vaster than the sea, and in 221 giving outside the destitute is a kind of expected repayment. The two define real generosity by lack of calculation, from the receiver's and the giver's sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"The same millet and palm. Here others' small kindnesses are enlarged, and in 433 one's own small faults are. The same mental act, turned in opposite directions, gives gratitude and conscience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"112 promises that a fair person's wealth will last, and 117 allows that such a person may fall into low estate. The chapter keeps both. The second stops the first from being a simple reward promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"Gain got by leaving what is fair should be given up, and in 657 even extreme poverty is better for the worthy than wealth got through blame. The later verse states the cost openly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, both rest on past deeds, and each draws a response. 379 asks why one grieves at the bad after accepting the good, and 115 asks that one's heart not bend. The first is about feeling and the second about justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:282","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar takes 116 to mean that thinking equals doing, and 282 says outright that even thinking of stealing another's goods is wrong. This suggests that the Kural's standard really does reach into thought.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"The five senses are imagined as animals in both cases. In 24 they're elephants controlled by a goad, and in 126 they're a tortoise's limbs drawn in. One is control by force and the other is retreat to safety.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:124","type":"tension","note":"The renouncer quenches the five in 25. In 124, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the householder is restrained while enjoying the senses and is also called greater than a mountain. They are two different pictures of what restraint is.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"Both say that no gain surpasses this, in the same formula (ஊங்கு), but 32 says it of virtue and 122 of restraint. The superlatives compete, as also in 61 on children, which suggests that the formula praises and doesn't actually rank.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:130","type":"same_image","note":"In both அறம் acts like a person, and Parimelazhagar calls it a deity in both. In 77 it burns the loveless like the sun, and here it waits for the restrained like a petitioner. It's the same power with opposite manners.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:129","type":"develops","note":"Harsh speech in 100 is merely a stupid choice of raw fruit. In 129 it leaves a scar that never heals. The first chapter on speech treats the speaker's loss, and this one treats the listener's lasting injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:128","type":"tension","note":"In 109 one good act erases even murderous harm. In 128 one bad word turns all good to nothing. The ledgers are asymmetric, though the verses keep different books: the first is the recipient's memory, and the second, in Parimelazhagar's reading, is the speaker's merit.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:140","type":"tension","note":"The two ask the same question, what learning is worth, and answer differently. For 2, learning lacks fruit without worship, and for 140 it isn't understanding without fitting conduct in the world. One looks up and the other looks out.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:327","type":"develops","note":"131 places conduct above life in general. 327 gives a specific case: don't take another's life, even at the cost of your own. It shows what 'guarding it above life' can literally mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate social rank from worth. 133 says this of conduct and birth, and 973 goes further with the high who aren't high and the low who aren't low. Read together, they show a repeated Kural challenge to status by birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie understanding to how the world lives. Verse 426 calls it அறிவு to live as the world lives, which is a striking statement beside Parimelazhagar's narrowing of 'world' to the noble in 140.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:144","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar uses Indra to illustrate both verses: 25 for the failure that shows the ascetic's power, and 144 for greatness lost through another's wife. This is one story serving two chapters in the commentary, though neither verse tells it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:143","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both call a living person effectively dead. In 80 it's the lack of love, and in 143, as both early commentators say, it's the loss of all three aims. A person can stop being alive in more than one way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:150","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These are the two 'at least this' verses: however much else is left unguarded, guard the tongue; however wrong the rest, don't desire another's wife. They show what the book treats as the last line of moral defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:773","type":"qualifies","note":"Both define பேராண்மை, the same word. In 773 it is ferocity in battle whose edge is kindness, and here it is not looking at another's wife. The book redefines the warrior's virtue as self-mastery.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:160","type":"agrees","note":"Both set a kind of endurance above the ascetic's. 48 says the householder endures more because he also bears others' pain, and 160 says that bearing harsh words comes first. Each reassigns the word நோன்பு or நோன்மை to a life in society.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:154","type":"parallel_domain","note":"நிறை appears in 57 as a woman's inner guard, and here it's what anyone stands to lose without forbearance. The same inner discipline is asked of women as chastity and of people in general as character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:153","type":"same_image","note":"Both call refusal of a guest a kind of poverty. 89 says so directly, and 153 uses it as an analogy for something else, which shows that this was a settled saying that could be drawn on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"Both say that forgetting is what's best. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 108 it's narrowly about one who has also done good. Verse 152 removes that limit, and gives forgetting a rank above forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"157 asks for not retaliating, and 314 goes further: punish the wrongdoer by doing him good so that he's ashamed. What starts as restraint becomes a strategy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"Two verses, for different reasons, place the ascetic's hunger second. In 160 it's after bearing insults, and in 225 it's after relieving others' hunger. The book keeps finding what's greater than fasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"The same goddess. She settles gladly in the home of the glad-faced host in 84, and here she hands the envious to her sister. Fortune is given a personality that responds to moral character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:166","type":"develops","note":"This is Parimelazhagar's own cross-reference. He cites 166 to read 135's simile: as the envious man's kin suffer, so do a man's kin when he lacks conduct. The commentary reads one verse by way of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same logic is used in two domains: the passion is its own executioner. Envy needs no enemies, and in 305 anger left unguarded kills its owner. The book treats inner states as weapons pointed inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:170","type":"tension","note":"169 says that the crooked prosper and the upright fall, and that this should be pondered. Then 170 says that it never happens. The two stand side by side, which suggests that 170 is a moral claim and that 169 knows what experience shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:375","type":"agrees","note":"Both admit that outcomes don't track merit. 375 says so within the chapter on fate, and 169 only asks for thought, though Parimelazhagar explains it through past deeds. The tension in chapter 38 is here in an earlier and quieter form.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:173","type":"develops","note":"In 39, joy comes only from virtue. Here that claim is put to work: the 'small joy' of taking others' goods is set against a greater one, and on Parimelazhagar's reading, the greater is the same joy of virtue as 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:176","type":"tension","note":"In 46 there's nothing to gain by leaving household life. Parimelazhagar on 176 calls household life a path toward renunciation, so leaving is the natural end. This commentator's way of ordering the stages conflicts with the plain force of 46.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:178","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise that wealth lasts on a moral condition: fairness in 112 and not coveting in 178. The second is the first's inward side, since 171 treats coveting as itself a lapse from fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:179","type":"same_image","note":"Virtue in 130 and fortune in 179 both seek out the right person, and they look for the right moment to do so. Parimelazhagar uses செவ்வி in both notes. Moral power is pictured as a courtier waiting for an opening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:363","type":"agrees","note":"Not wanting yields victory here, and in 363 it's the choicest wealth in this world and the next. Chapter 18 is about not wanting others' goods, and 363 extends this to not wanting at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:185","type":"agrees","note":"34 says that a clean mind is all of virtue and the rest is noise. 185 supplies the test: if you can't tell whether the mind behind fine moral talk is clean, listen to how the person speaks of others.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"100 calls harsh speech a foolish choice when sweet words are available. 184 ranks it above backbiting. The two together suggest a scale of speech: sweet, then harsh and open, then hidden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:181","type":"same_image","note":"These are built on the same frame: though one does no virtue and does wrong, at least this one thing. The verses name different things, chastity toward others' wives and not backbiting, so the 'last virtue' isn't a single item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is the model of forbearance in 151, holding up those who dig it. Here it has to talk itself into bearing the backbiter. This suggests that the backbiter does what digging doesn't: he tests even the earth's patience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"Both pick out the false smile. In 182 it's the worst part of backbiting, and in 824 the sweet-smiling face over a bitter heart is to be feared. The moral judgment in chapter 19 becomes a practical warning in the book on friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:436","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These share the word காண்கிற்பின் and the same question about faults. In 190 any person who sees his own faults is safe from harm. In 436 a king who has removed his own and then looks at others' has no fault left. The same self-scrutiny serves an ordinary person and a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:100","type":"tension","note":"100 calls harsh speech a raw fruit taken over a ripe one, yet on Kalaignar's reading 197 tolerates unsweet speech before useless speech. Sweetness is not the highest test of a word.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:291","type":"tension","note":"291 defines truthfulness as speech without harm; 200 demands use. Harmless idle talk passes the one test and fails the other, so the two chapters set different standards for speech.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"645 echoes the same imperative and raises the standard: useful is not enough if some other word could defeat it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:1071","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny full humanity by appearance alone: chaff has the look of grain, and the base look like people. For the Kural, being human is something a person can fail at.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both use 'even by forgetting', 199 for the speech of the clear-sighted and 204 for plotting harm. The first describes an achieved nature, and the second commands what such a nature would be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:1075","type":"tension","note":"201 makes fear the mark of the excellent; 1075 makes fear the conduct of the base. They can be reconciled only by asking what is feared, which suggests that fear of the deed and fear of the penalty are morally different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"203 only asks one to refrain from harming an enemy. 314 goes further and makes kindness to the wrongdoer the punishment itself. Prudent restraint becomes active shaming.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:657","type":"qualifies","note":"205 argues that wrongdoing won't cure poverty. 657 removes that premise: even when wealth does come through blame, the poverty of the worthy is better. Moral refusal doesn't depend on the prediction in 205.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:316","type":"qualifies","note":"They give the same rule different grounds. 206 rests on what will come back to you, 316 on knowing from your own experience what hurts. One argues from consequence and the other from fellow-feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Two pursuers that can't be evaded: the deed follows from behind (பின் சென்று) and fate arrives first (முந்துறும்). Together they close off escape in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on தன்னைத்தான், making care for the self the argument for restraint: from evil deeds here, from anger in 305.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"210 says the upright man's ruin is absent or rare. 169 admits that this ruin does occur and is something to ponder. The Kural keeps the moral promise and the troubling fact side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:226","type":"tension","note":"211 insists that obligation looks for no return. 226 commends feeding the destitute as the place to store one's wealth, and Parimelazhagar says it comes back to help the giver. One motive disclaims reward and the other promises it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:216","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"An exact inversion: the same tree in mid-town, same ripening. In 216 it bears useful fruit; in 1008 it is a poison tree. The chapters on benevolence and on useless wealth share one picture, and only the owner's character changes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:80","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the definition of being alive from body to relationship: 80 to love, 214 to knowing what is due. The body without these is bone and skin, or a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"219 says the good man's poverty is being unable to give. 230 pushes this further: being unable to give makes even death sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"220 only says that ruin from generosity is worth buying. 235 takes up the same word and explains how there can be a ruin that is a gain, and says that only the skilled achieve it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:221","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 212, wealth is for helping those who deserve it. 221 says that true giving goes to those who lack. The worthy recipient and the needy recipient are different tests, and these chapters leave both standing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1051","type":"tension","note":"222 calls taking bad even if it were a path to heaven. 1051 permits begging from the right people and puts any blame on the one who refuses. The chapters on giving and begging judge the same act from different standpoints.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"229 names something worse than begging; 1066 says that nothing disgraces the tongue more than begging, even for a cow's water. Their standards differ: the misery of a life against the shame of an act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:225","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"225 places enduring hunger below removing it. 261 defines austerity as endurance plus not harming living things, so the austerity that 225 ranks low is not the fuller ideal of chapter 27.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1001","type":"develops","note":"228 asks whether hoarders know the joy they forfeit. 1001 gives the outcome: he dies without eating what he amassed, and nothing remains to be done with it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:331","type":"tension","note":"233 names renown as the one thing in the world that doesn't die. 331 calls taking what doesn't last for what lasts the lowest kind of knowledge. Chapter 34 never excepts fame, so fame's permanence is exactly what the later chapter puts in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"222 calls giving good even with no reward in any world. 231 calls giving, with renown, the sole profit of a life. The same act is valued for itself in one and as a gain in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"Both rest on எச்சம், what is left behind. 114 reads a person's worth from what remains after them, and 238 makes renown the remainder that everyone should leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is morally responsive in both: it yields less under a body without fame, and laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. Land is more than a backdrop to human conduct in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"The same reclassification of the living as dead, with different tests: renown in 240, knowing what is due in 214. The chapters on benevolence and fame are linked as cause and result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:241","b":"k:411","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துட் செல்வம், crowns different things: அருள் here and hearing in 411. Both can't be 'the' wealth of wealths unless the phrase is a rhetorical form awarded in context, a caution against reading any single chapter as the Kural's ultimate ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:751","type":"develops","note":"247 mentions as an aside that this world is not for the penniless. 751 makes that its full subject: nothing but wealth makes nobodies into somebodies. The section on virtue quietly grants the premise of the section on wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"248 says those without அருள் are finished and can't become anything. 250, which Parimelazhagar calls the means of arousing அருள், assumes that the person going against the weak can still acquire it. The verdict is absolute, and the very next step offers a way out.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:34","type":"agrees","note":"34 says stainlessness of mind is the whole of virtue and all else is show. 249 makes the same point about compassion: an outwardly right act without it does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:318","type":"agrees","note":"Both derive compassion from one's own experience: 250 remembers fear before the strong, and 318 asks how one who knows pain in his own life could inflict it. The Kural's ethics of harm consistently starts from self-knowledge and not from rules.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"253 uses the weapon-holder as the model of a mind closed to good. 774 celebrates a warrior who plucks a spear from his own body and laughs. The Kural honours in the section on polity the same figure that it distrusts in the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:329","type":"develops","note":"329 condemns killers as base. 256 extends the guilt to the customer who keeps them in business, so the customer can't look down on the butcher.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:88","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both move the worth of sacrifice elsewhere: 88 calls hospitality the வேள்வி that misers miss, and 259 ranks not killing above a thousand sacrifices. The Kural repeatedly tests ritual by how others are treated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"Nearly the same ending: all living beings worship. In 260 the object is one who doesn't kill and doesn't eat flesh; in 268 it is one who has mastered, or on another reading lost, his own self. One route looks outward and the other inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"225 puts the strength of enduring hunger behind that of removing it, which favours the giver. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar draw from 263 that தவம் exceeds giving. Whatever the verses themselves imply, the commentary tradition faces a real conflict here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:203","type":"tension","note":"203 calls refraining from harm even to enemies the height of understanding, and 261 defines austerity by harmlessness. Yet 264 advertises the power to crush foes. Parimelazhagar's 'enemies of virtue' and his comment that ascetics don't think of it are attempts to close this gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a virtuous householder could gain by leaving for the other path. 266 says that those not doing தவம் are doing futile things. The chapters on householding and austerity each claim the highest place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:370","type":"tension","note":"265 recommends austerity because it gets whatever one wants. 370 says the unchanging state comes from removing desire. The means is sold by appeal to the very thing the end abolishes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:268","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 268, its subject has lost the ego, which is exactly the cutting of 'I' and 'mine' in 346. Parimelazhagar's 'possessing oneself' would make the link weaker.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"Two different conquests of death: by the power of austerity in 269, and by the vow of not killing in 326, where death's advance doesn't reach. The Kural offers an ethical route alongside the ascetic one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"270 explains poverty as too little austerity, which is something people could change. 377 says enjoyment is only as the apportioner apportioned. The same fact gets a moral cause and a fatal one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:293","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one's own heart the witness: 272 says that an outward show can't help when the heart knows the fault, and 293 says the heart will burn the one who lies against what it knows. Self-knowledge is the Kural's court of last resort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:278","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"278's மனத்தது மாசாக is the exact opposite of 34's மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன். 34 states the principle that all else is show, and 278 gives the social picture of the show in practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:827","type":"same_image","note":"827 warns that a bowing enemy's words are like a bow's bend, which signals harm. 279 says the lute's curve is straight and the arrow's straightness is cruel. So the curve is a warning in one verse and no evidence at all in the other, which supports 279's point that shape can't be read directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:88","type":"same_image","note":"The same utterance appears in both: in 88, the miser who fed no guests later says பற்றற்றேம் as a lament for lost support, and in 275 the hypocrite says it as a boast of non-attachment. Both are words that the speaker's life makes hollow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the offence to the planning mind: 204 says that merely plotting ruin sets virtue plotting in return, and 282 says that merely thinking of theft is wrong. Parimelazhagar reads the standard in 282 as one for renunciants, while 204 has no such limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:479","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe prosperity that seems to exist and then vanishes: stolen gain 'as if growing' in 283, and in 479 the life of one who doesn't live by measure, which seems to exist and is gone. Both also turn on அளவு, so here the moral and the economic meanings of 'measure' meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says that what is taken with tears goes with tears. Together they make the same bet about ill-gotten gain, but 283 stresses its illusion of growth and 659 stresses its justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:300","b":"k:323","type":"tension","note":"300 finds nothing better than truthfulness, while 323 puts not killing first and truthfulness right behind it. The Kural declares its rankings chapter by chapter, and here it comes close to correcting itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"Almost the same phrase is applied to opposite things: பொய்யா விளக்கு is truthfulness here, and 753 calls wealth the பொய்யா விளக்கம் that cuts through darkness. The section on wealth deliberately takes over the language of the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"297 prizes unbroken not-lying, while 292 admits falsehood under a condition. They are consistent only given 291's redefinition of truth by effect, which shows how much that definition is carrying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"225 favours giving over austerity, the commentators on 263 favour austerity over giving, and 295 now sets truth above both. The chapters rank a shifting set of virtues rather than a fixed ladder.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:298","b":"k:278","type":"agrees","note":"278 shows the bathers with impure minds, and 298 states the principle that this water only reaches the body. Read together, the chapter on hypocrisy provides the case for this general claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"Both are about the moment of having power over someone weaker. 250 offers a memory that stops you: your own fear before the strong. 301 says that only at this moment does restraint count as virtue at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:305","b":"k:264","type":"tension","note":"264 credits austerity with the power to crush foes. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 305, the ascetic who uses this power in a curse loses his austerity and returns to the misery of births. The power is real, but using it destroys its source.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:786","type":"same_image","note":"Both distinguish the laughter of the face from that of the heart. 786 says that friendship that smiles only on the face isn't friendship, and 304 says anger kills both, so a smile alone isn't what is at stake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for restraint under extreme provocation. 157 goes further and grounds it in pity for the wrongdoer's coming fall, whereas 308 offers no reason, only the admission that it is hard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:265","type":"agrees","note":"The same promise, that what one wants arrives as one wants it, is made for austerity in 265 and for non-anger in 309. On this comparison, dropping anger can do the work of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"Both use ஒறுத்தல், punish. 314 says to punish wrongdoers by shaming them with kindness; 550 says that the king punishing the worst of them with death is simply weeding a crop. The word keeps its form while the ethics of person and state divide.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:315","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"Both test அறிவு by conduct toward others: 203 calls not harming enemies its peak, and 315 says it comes to nothing without guarding others from pain. Understanding here is measured morally rather than by what one knows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"207 says that the enmity of deeds can't be escaped. 319 adds speed: by the same afternoon. One closes off every direction and the other closes off time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"330 reports that the diseased and destitute are paying for their killings. 1062 rages that if people must beg to live, the world's maker should perish. One explains misery as deserved, and the other refuses to accept it at all.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"320 says that all pain rests on those who caused pain, looking forward. 330 turns this around and reads a past killing from present suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"327 forbids taking life even to save one's own. 550 likens the king's execution of murderers to weeding a crop. Here the Kural's most absolute rule meets its statecraft, and it does not reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"259 explicitly mentions sacrifice but only of oblations, and sets not killing above it. 328 doesn't mention sacrifice, but the commentators find animal sacrifice there. That shows how a commentary tradition can place a controversy in the text.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says that birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work done. 329 applies this to killing: base by occupation. The commentators' emphasis on 'by work' follows the same principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"333 says wealth is by nature unstaying; 753 calls it the unfailing lamp that drives out darkness wherever one intends. The chapter on impermanence and the chapter on acquiring wealth describe the same thing in opposite terms.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the owner of unstable wealth where to put it. 226 names the place: the hunger of the destitute. 333 explains why it must be done immediately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:257","type":"same_image","note":"Both end உணர்வார்ப் பெறின், 'if those who perceive it could be found'. Flesh as a wound, and a day as a blade: in each, the ordinary hides violence, and the verse doubts that many will see it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says not to put off virtue until later, since it is a companion that doesn't die when you die. 335 supplies the concrete deadline: before the hiccup stops the tongue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:340","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"340 asks gently whether the lodger has no home of its own. 345 draws the conclusion for one who wants to leave for good: even the lodging is too much, let alone other ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:344","type":"tension","note":"341 says each thing given up removes that thing's pain, a piecemeal gain. 344 says keeping even one thing brings it all back. Parimelazhagar accepts both, one as a gain and the other as a risk, but the practical advice differs.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:1101","type":"tension","note":"343 demands that the domain of the five senses be crushed. 1101 finds all five senses' delights gathered in the beloved. The third book takes what renunciation would destroy and fulfils it in one person.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"இன்மை, having nothing, is natural and right for austerity in 344. In 1041, poverty, the same word, is what nothing else can match for misery. The word changes value completely with who has chosen it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:50","type":"qualifies","note":"50 places the good householder among the gods, and 346 and 348 put full renouncers beyond them and everyone else in the net. Parimelazhagar's ladder (fame and heaven for householders, release for renouncers) is how the commentary fits these together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:7","type":"develops","note":"7 says that only those who join the feet of the incomparable one can be rid of grief. 350 repeats the structure with attachment. Parimelazhagar himself notes that the generic word is kept to match the invocation chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:423","type":"same_image","note":"Their second lines are identical. 423 applies it to what we are told: whoever says it, find the truth in it. 355 applies it to what things are: whatever their appearance, find the truth in them. The same habit of mind is both critical thinking and metaphysics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"343 demands crushing the domain of the five senses. 354, on Kalaignar's reading, says that this gets you nothing without true perception. The disciplined renouncer of chapter 35 isn't the goal in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:242","type":"qualifies","note":"For Parimelazhagar, the schools conflict on everything in 242 and yet converge on அருள். On 353, he says that their conflict leaves doubt only experience can settle. Together they give his view of religious pluralism: agreement in ethics, and no resolution by argument in metaphysics.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"356 makes learning (கற்று) the route to seeing the true thing. 373 says however much one studies, one's own understanding prevails. Chapter 38 thus undercuts the very means this one recommends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as conduct free of envy, craving, anger and harsh words. 360 lists desire, anger and confusion, whose ending ends pain. Anger and desire appear in both lists, and the shift to confusion in 360 marks the shift from conduct to knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:352","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe those of மாசறு காட்சி who are free of மருள். 352 says what it does for them; 199 says what it looks like from outside: they don't utter a meaningless word even by accident.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:362","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"Both solve the same problem by the same paradox: you can't leave desire by force, so desire is used against itself. 350 holds to the one without holding, and 362 wants to be without wanting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:363","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"விழுச்செல்வம் names not wanting here and learning in 400. Taken with 241 and 411, the Kural has awarded the title of true wealth to at least four things, and that suggests the phrase is a rhetorical form and not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says inner purity comes through truth. 364 gives the missing link: purity is the absence of craving, and wanting truth brings it. On Parimelazhagar's view, the meaning of truth has shifted from speech to the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:361","b":"k:351","type":"qualifies","note":"351 traces birth to confusion and 361 traces it to craving, and both claim to be the root. Parimelazhagar makes them a sequence, with ignorance first and craving following, but the verses each say it singly.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:201","type":"agrees","note":"Virtue as a kind of fear: in 201 the excellent fear evil deeds, and in 366 what is to be feared is craving, which is behind those deeds. It is the same moral fear moved one step closer to its source.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:320","type":"tension","note":"320 says all pain rests on those who caused pain, and 368 says sorrow comes from craving and doesn't come to those without it. Two single-source explanations of suffering, one ethical and one psychological, sit a few chapters apart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"Just ten verses apart, this one ends the section on renunciation by promising a nature that can't be shifted, once craving goes. 380 asks what is stronger than fate, which gets there first. The Kural moves from the highest claim for human release straight to the strongest claim against human power.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:226","type":"same_image","note":"அற்றார் are the destitute in 226, to be fed, and the freed in 365, to be revered. The same word for lacking marks both the most pitiable person and the most complete one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"Here effort is fate's product. In 620, effort puts fate behind you. The tension is sharper than one between effort and fate as rivals, because 371 removes effort as an independent force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:396","type":"tension","note":"Here one's own understanding prevails over study. In 396, knowledge flows in proportion to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:400","type":"qualifies","note":"374 separates wealth and wisdom as different fates, and 400 goes further by calling learning the only true wealth. Read together, they redefine wealth.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:38","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading both rest on deeds and births: 379 accepts the fruit of past deeds, and 38 stops future births.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"The king must never lack அஞ்சாமை, yet 428 calls not fearing what should be feared folly. Read together, kingly fearlessness can't mean fearing nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:672","type":"qualifies","note":"தூங்காமை is a permanent royal quality here. 672 uses the same word to divide tasks: some should be slept on and some should not. The trait becomes a judgment about each case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 fixes the order, deliberate and then resolve, which supports Manakkudavar's reading of துணிவு here and sits uneasily with Parimelazhagar's action without regard to anything.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:565","type":"agrees","note":"The negative of the same pair. Being hard to see and harsh of look turns great wealth into what a demon has found: 386 gives the praise, 565 the horror.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same verb, வைக்கப்படும், raises a just ruler to இறை and a rightly living householder among the gods. Both are placed there by how they live, one in public and one at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"389 asks the king to bear bitter words; 448 says what follows if no one speaks them: he falls with no enemy needed. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 389 uses the same idea of இடிக்கும் துணை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:140","type":"develops","note":"391 demands standing by what is learned; 140 gives the failure's verdict: those who have learned much but can't act in accord with the world are unlearned and ignorant. Conduct can cancel the very category of learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn eyes into sores, but by different tests: lack of learning here and lack of கண்ணோட்டம், kindly regard, in 575. One verse grounds sight in knowledge and the other in compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:398","b":"k:409","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, the two make one argument across chapters: learning travels with the soul through births, while caste rank ends with the body. That is why 409 can rank learning over birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:396","b":"k:416","type":"tension","note":"396 makes understanding strictly proportional to study. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 416, even a small hearing gives full greatness. Reading gains by degree and listening at once.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"404 denies that native brightness counts without learning; 373 says a person's own understanding prevails over any amount of study. On one reading, the Kural both dismisses innate understanding and calls it unbeatable.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat wealth as worse for being held by the wrong person, but 1000 faults character and 408 faults learning. 1000's spoilt milk also shows the damage to the good thing, not only to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"409 uses high and low birth as a working scale, even to overturn it. 972 denies the scale at the root: birth is the same for all beings, and only deeds differ. The two arguments against birth rank are different in kind.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:414","type":"qualifies","note":"After a chapter that places the unlearned among beasts, 414 opens a door: though you haven't studied, listen. The next chapter quietly withdraws the finality of this one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:411","b":"k:241","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துள் செல்வம், is given to the wealth of the ear here and to the wealth of grace in 241. 400 adds learning. The Kural's chapters each crown their own wealth, which raises the question of how they rank.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:414","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the leaning staff, but 414 has listening as the staff and 415 the speaker's conduct. Read together, the staff only holds if the one speaking has conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"978 says greatness bows while smallness vaunts. 419 gives that a source: the bowing mouth comes from listening, so 978's humility is partly a product of hearing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:355","type":"develops","note":"Nearly the same second line. 355 tests a thing regardless of how it appears; 423 tests it regardless of who says it. Together they strip away both of the surfaces that mislead: appearance and authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:415","type":"tension","note":"423 sets aside the speaker when judging what is said. 415 rests the value of words on the speaker's conduct, and Parimelazhagar says those lacking it shouldn't be heard. Who says it either doesn't matter or matters most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"In the same chapter, 423 demands testing every claim for yourself and 426 says live as the world lives. The tension is softened if உலகம் means the worthy, as Manakkudavar has it, but the verse doesn't say so.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 says fate arrives first even when one devises another way; 429 says foresight spares one from being shaken. Manakkudavar faces this directly, allowing that early guarding softens even what comes from past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:374","type":"tension","note":"374 separates being wealthy from being wise. In Parimelazhagar's gloss on 430, all wealth is made and kept by understanding, while his gloss on 374 draws the conclusion that wealth doesn't need wisdom as its cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on அற்றம். Understanding is the inner fort against it, and one's own fault is the enemy that brings it. So the attacker and the defence are both within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Millet and palmyra both times: 104 inflates a small favour received, and 433 a small fault committed. The same discipline of scale runs in both directions, gratitude for others and severity with oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:436","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same shape and the same காண்கிற்பின். 190 asks anyone to see their own faults as they see others', and 436 makes the order a condition for a ruler who judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:384","type":"qualifies","note":"384 requires மானம் of a king; 432 forbids மானம் that has passed beyond dignity. The same word marks a virtue and a fault, and Parimelazhagar explicitly separates them in both places.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:431","type":"tension","note":"431 praises those without desire, and 440 tells one how to enjoy desire safely. Parimelazhagar resolves this by making the fault excess in 431, but the verses themselves pull apart. Removing and hiding are different programmes.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"444 ranks support as the chief of strengths. 471 lists it as one of four to weigh before acting, alongside the task and the opponent. The superlative here gets absorbed into a calculation there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:448","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"434 calls one's own fault the only enemy that brings ruin. 448 shows the mechanism: without those who rebuke, no outside enemy is needed. Self-correction turns out to be something a person needs other people for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:454","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"373 says a person's own understanding prevails over all study; 454 says understanding only seems to be one's own and lies in one's company. These are two different accounts of where knowledge comes from, within or among others, and they don't fit together easily.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 makes a spotless mind the whole of virtue. 455 makes that purity lean on purity of company. If so, the root of virtue in 34 has a root of its own outside the person.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:114","type":"agrees","note":"114 says worthiness is seen by one's எச்சம், and 456 says purity of mind produces a good one. The same disputed word closes the loop between what is inward and what is left behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says the knowing fear what should be feared. 451 names one such object, a surprising one: not a danger, but a social circle. It only makes sense given 452's claim that company changes understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:449","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முதல், capital, from the merchant's world. 449 applies it to allies and 463 to risk: a king's ledger has two different kinds of principal that he can't afford to lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids what the worthy condemn even if one's mother is starving, the most extreme form of Manakkudavar's reading here, that being able to succeed doesn't excuse scorned means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says to avoid beginning what isn't clear. 469 adds that even with the deed and intent clear, the person is another thing to know. Clarity about the act is not clarity about the recipient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:220","type":"tension","note":"220 says if helpfulness brings ruin, it is worth buying even by selling oneself. 480 says helpfulness that ignores one's means quickly destroys wealth. Aram and Porul give opposite counsel on the same act, and 480 uses the same word, ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:595","type":"tension","note":"595 says a person's height rises with their spirit, as a lotus stem with the water. 473 says many have been broken by exactly that spirit, ஊக்கம், when it wasn't measured. One counts spirit as the measure and the other demands a measure for spirit.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:283","type":"same_image","note":"Prosperity that perishes while appearing to exist or grow. 283 blames fraud and 479 blames not knowing the measure, so honest wealth and stolen wealth can fail in the same deceptive way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:385","type":"develops","note":"385 ends the royal wealth cycle in distribution. 477 sets the limit on that distribution. Parimelazhagar explicitly connects them, sending the reader back to 385 for the way of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"376 says what isn't yours won't stay however you guard it. 482 offers timing as the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. One denies that wealth can be held by care, the other names the means of holding it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because evil is born of it. 487 keeps anger alive in secret until it can be used. The Aram book treats anger as something to give up, while the Porul book treats it as something to store.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:828","type":"develops","note":"488 advises bowing to an enemy until he can be struck. 828 warns that a weapon is hidden in an enemy's folded hands. The Kural gives the technique and the defence against it, from either side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"471 says act after weighing strengths. Parimelazhagar's 485 says that even with greater strength, wait for the time. So a favourable reckoning from 471 still isn't enough.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:481","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The crow beats the owl by day and the crocodile wins in water. Time and place are two axes of the same argument: strength isn't an attribute of a creature but of a creature in a circumstance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:471","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 471 says to act only if your side is stronger. 493 says the weak can win by choosing the ground. If so, the calculation of 471 must already have place in it, or 471's rule is too strict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:444","type":"tension","note":"444 calls greater people as support the chief of all strengths; 497 says no support is needed but fearlessness, if the thinking and place are right. Five chapters later, the book seems to qualify its own superlative.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:500","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"A great elephant is undone in both: by a tiger's spirit in 599 and by a jackal in the mud here. One verse credits the attacker's ஊக்கம் and the other only the ground, so size loses for two quite different reasons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 ranks the learned low-born over the unlearned high-born. 502 makes good birth the first condition for trust. The two can be squared, but the book's position on birth clearly depends on what is being asked.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:349","type":"tension","note":"349 has rebirth cut only when பற்று is gone. On Parimelazhagar's reading, 506 won't trust anyone without பற்று. The same absence is liberation in Aram and a security risk in Porul.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:433","type":"develops","note":"433 treats one's own millet-sized fault as a palm. 504 takes others on balance, by what predominates. Read together, the Kural sets a different standard for judging oneself and judging others.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says standing differs because of deeds, and 505 makes deeds the touchstone. Together they offer a test of worth that doesn't refer to birth, and this is at odds with 502, three verses earlier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791 says of friendship what 510 says of ministers: examine first, because afterwards there is no way out. Both make trust something with a fixed before and after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says doubting one you have trusted brings endless trouble. 520 says inspect him every day. Parimelazhagar sees this and calls for private inspection, but the problem of watching without doubting remains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:501","type":"qualifies","note":"501 builds an elaborate test and, in Parimelazhagar's words, passing shows the person won't change. 514 says that many change anyway, through the work itself. The most careful selection can't test for what office will do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:512","b":"k:385","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The king creates, gathers, guards and apportions in 385; the officer enlarges, increases and watches in 512. Parimelazhagar connects them. The servant's job is a copy of the master's, without the giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:507","type":"qualifies","note":"507 condemns trusting on fondness alone. 513 puts love first among four qualities, so the fault in 507 lies in the 'alone', not in the love.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:523","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both show wealth as a full tank. In 215 it is a public pond serving all, and in 523 it has no banks. The first image is about access, the second about containment: wealth has to be held to be shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"212 says all wealth earned exists to serve the worthy. 524 says its fruit is being surrounded by kin. Both state the purpose of wealth, with different beneficiaries, and neither mentions the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"521 declares that kin stay in adversity. 796 calls adversity a measuring rod for kin, which assumes some won't stay. A generalisation about kin in 53 becomes a test of them in 80.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:305","type":"tension","note":"305 says anger kills the one who has it. In Parimelazhagar's argument for 531, anger at least sometimes reaches the enemy, while elated forgetting only kills oneself. The Aram book's worst self-destroyer gets demoted.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:408","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reasoning on 408, poverty doesn't ruin the good. 532 makes chronic poverty the very image of what destroys understanding. Either understanding survives poverty, or it doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:421","type":"develops","note":"421 builds an inner fort of understanding. 534 shows an outer fort falling to inner fear. Together they say that a fortress is finally a state of mind, which is unusual in a Porul section that counts forts as a limb of the state.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"'Nothing is hard' appears with different conditions: 462 counsel, 472 knowing strength, 483 means and time, and here attention. The refrain's repetition suggests that no single one suffices, and each chapter has claimed the last word.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:540","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same structure: what one has in mind is attained, if. 666's condition is firmness and 540's is continued remembering. They might be one quality seen from the will and from attention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:327","type":"tension","note":"327 says don't take another's life even if it costs your own. 550 says the king's killing of the cruel is like weeding. The book's sharpest split between Aram and Porul: an absolute rule meets the role of king. Parimelazhagar resolves it through caste duty, which the verses don't mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:578","type":"tension","note":"541 makes justice not showing கண்ணோட்டம் to anyone. 578 gives the world to those able to show it without harming the work. So the king both must and must not, and 578's condition, the work unharmed, is the hinge.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:528","type":"tension","note":"528 tells a king not to look at all alike but by rank; 541 says to show no favour to anyone. Household management and the court of justice have different requirements, though they are only two chapters apart.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The chapter on rain calls it the world's nectar because it keeps the world going. 542 gives the sceptre that same place for human society, which is why the failure of one can later cause the other in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion toward all life, and not by birth. If that is carried here, 543's அந்தணர் நூல் need not be the Veda, and this gives Kalaignar's reading a basis inside the Kural.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:761","type":"tension","note":"761 calls a fearless army the chief of a king's assets. 546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that gives victory. Porul itself has two answers to what wins wars, one in its justice chapter and one in its army chapter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that wins. 552 collapses the difference: a sceptre that asks is a spear. The same pair, வேல் and கோல், marks the line between justice and its corruption.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"550 has a king kill the cruel as weeds. The very next verse calls an oppressive king crueller than killers. My reading: read together, the two raise the question 550 leaves open, of who weeds the weeder, and the chapter's answer is tears, rain and time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says what was taken with tears departs with tears. 555 makes the same moral arithmetic political. 659 is a personal warning about money, and here the crying of a whole people is the weapon against a throne.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:558","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty except poverty itself. 558 names something more painful: wealth under a tyrant. The later chapter's superlative doesn't hold in this one's political conditions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:545","type":"agrees","note":"A mirror: just rule brings rain and harvest in 545, and bent rule stops the rain in 559. Kalaignar rationalises both, a comparison in one and reservoirs in the other, because the older reading asks a modern reader to believe in moral weather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:543","type":"develops","note":"543 makes the sceptre the origin of books and virtue, and 560 shows the effect of its absence: the books are forgotten. Parimelazhagar's chain then connects these to rain through sacrifice, which ties 11, 543 and 559 into one circle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:567","type":"qualifies","note":"549 clears the king: punishing crime is no blemish but his work. 567 marks where that clearance ends, since punishment past the limit becomes a file on his own strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"386 promises praise to the king who is easy to see and not harsh of speech. 565 and 566 cost out the opposite, turning two virtues into two ways of losing wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:382","type":"qualifies","note":"382 lists fearlessness as a king's nature. 569 suggests that it also has a material condition: without prepared defences, even a king comes to fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:151","type":"same_image","note":"The earth in 151 patiently bears even those who dig it, the model of forbearance. In 570 that same earth finds one burden too heavy, which gives the hyperbole its force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:541","type":"tension","note":"541 defines justice partly as not extending கண்ணோட்டம் (ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது). This chapter says the world depends on it. 578 is the hinge, and Parimelazhagar cites 541 there: the same grace is the condition of the world and a corruption of justice, depending on where it falls.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:580","type":"tension","note":"578 forbids kindness that damages the work, and 580 praises kindness that swallows poison. Parimelazhagar resolves this by confining forbearance to wrongs against oneself, but the verses themselves don't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:570","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent chapters use the same phrase, நிலக்குப் பொறை, for two different faults: the alliance of harshness with ignorance, and the mere lack of kindly regard. The second is far milder and yet receives the same verdict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:393","type":"same_image","note":"Both call failed eyes wounds. In 393 the eye is failed by lack of learning, and in 575 by lack of kindly regard. Taken together, an eye must both know and regard, and neither alone saves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:576","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"If Manakkudavar's reading of 576 is right, both verses use the wooden doll for a person hollow inside: 1020 lacks shame and 576 lacks regard. On Parimelazhagar's reading the image is a rooted tree, and this parallel fades.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"807 says those of long affection don't give it up even when ruined by friends. 580 gives the same stance its most extreme image. If Parimelazhagar is right that the poisoner is an associate, the poison verse belongs to the same ethic as the chapter on old friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:392","type":"same_image","note":"Both name two things as a person's eyes. 392 gives every living person numbers and letters. 581 gives a ruler spies and the treatise. Each makes sight depend on something outside the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"In adjacent chapters a ruler's eye is first the grace that spares and then the instrument that watches everyone, kin included (584). The Kural does not reconcile the two eyes, and 578 is the nearest it comes to a rule between them.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:274","type":"tension","note":"274 condemns hiding behind ascetic practice as a hunter hides in a bush to trap birds. 586 prescribes exactly that kind of cover for the state's spy, on Manakkudavar's reading at least. The Virtue book's hypocrisy becomes the Wealth book's technique.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"423 tells the individual to find the truth whoever is speaking. 589 turns the same refusal to trust a source into a state procedure, with independence and counting as its method.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"Both turn on the same word. In 594 wealth seeks out அசைவிலா energy. In 371 அசைவின்மை is itself produced by a fate of gain. The causal arrow reverses, so the question becomes whether steadfastness is one's own at all.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"The elephant carries opposite morals three verses apart: under arrows it models unbreakable resolve, and before a tiger it is bulk without resolve. Parimelazhagar accepts this by locating the difference in the tiger's energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"Both speak of the pride of giving (செருக்கு) and both root it in exertion. 613 names the vocabulary of labour and liberality that 598 only implies, so generosity is shown to have an economic foundation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"The Kural keeps naming the one true wealth: learning in 400, what is heard in 411, grace in 241, and energy here. Each dismisses ordinary wealth, but they cannot all be the sole true possession. Each is best read as a chapter's rhetorical peak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Tree-people appear for two different lacks: kindly regard in 576 and energy in 600, and again for human quality in 997. The tree image stands for whatever a chapter takes to be essential to being human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"371 says sloth (மடி) arises from a fate of loss. This chapter treats the same மடி as a thing one can depose at will. The tension is direct, and neither chapter acknowledges the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1023","type":"develops","note":"The same goal, raising one's family, and the same word used differently. 602 asks one to drive out மடி, and in 1023 the deity tightens its மடி (waist-cloth) and comes ahead of the one who resolves on this. That verse's தான்முந்துறும் also echoes fate's in 380.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both invoke Vishnu's realm as the highest measure. 610 offers it to the sleepless ruler, and 605 has just listed sleep as a boat to ruin. 1103 asks whether that realm is even as sweet as sleep on a beloved's arms. The Porul and Kamam books rank sleep and sovereignty oppositely.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:784","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke when one strays, and uses the same word இடி. If Parimelazhagar is right that the scolding in 607 comes from friends, the verse shows what happens when that duty is carried out and fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 asks what could be stronger than fate, which arrives ahead of every stratagem. 620 answers that those who keep toiling see its back. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by saying fate blocks only a few attempts, which the two verses themselves don't say.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:619","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 limits what even hoarders of millions can enjoy to what has been apportioned. 619 guarantees effort a wage whatever divine order decides. One sets a ceiling on enjoyment and the other a floor on reward, and they may coexist only just.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"The same sword in the same hand. In 614 it stands for generosity without labour and in 727 for learning without courage in an assembly. Both say an asset is void without the disposition to use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:595","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வெள்ளத் தனைய, 'as much as the flood'. In 595 the flood lifts a stalk, in a figure for the rise of energy. In 622 the flood is suffering itself, dissolved by a thought. In both the mind is what settles the scale.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:626","b":"k:593","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the cry over lost wealth. 593 says energy is the real asset, so the loss is small. 626 says the one who never clutched has no such cry. One rests on what you still have, and the other on how you first held it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:628","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin இன்பம் விழையான், 'not desiring pleasure'. In 615 this produces a pillar for kin through labour, and in 628 it produces a person pain can't reach. The same renunciation of pleasure serves worldly work and inner calm alike, without any talk of leaving the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for one response to good and bad, but they give different grounds: 379 reasons from fate that it's inconsistent to accept one and grieve the other, and 629 reasons from the training of desire, without mentioning fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:373","type":"qualifies","note":"373 sets one's own understanding above study, and 636 asks for both at once: native acuity with treatises. Taken together, the opposition in 373 is not the Kural's last word on learning and nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"140 calls the learned ignorant if they can't move in step with the world; 637 turns this into an instruction for statecraft. The Virtue book's moral rule becomes a professional one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"889 says an inner enemy holds ruin even when small as a sesame fragment; 639 gives the same inner enemy a face at the king's side and a scale, seventy crore outer foes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:32","type":"tension","note":"32 says nothing brings more gain than virtue, and 644 uses the same ஊங்கு formula to put apt speech above both virtue and gain. This is either high rhetoric or a real rearrangement. Parimelazhagar makes apt speech a cause of virtue to avoid the clash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:127","type":"agrees","note":"127, in the Virtue book, says guard the tongue or suffer from its errors, and the fear is personal shame. 642 makes the same demand for a public reason, since gain and ruin follow. The same discipline serves different goals.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"424 defines wisdom by the same two-part structure: making your speech enter easily and finding the fine meaning in others' words. 646 assigns it to excellence in office, so wisdom and diplomacy are shown to share a form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"69 gives a mother's greatest joy as hearing her son called சான்றோன், greater than at the birth. 656 uses the same two words, ஈன்றாள் and சான்றோர், and places that standing above her hunger. Read together, the son's choice could be seen as what she would have wanted, though neither verse says so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing hurts like poverty. 657 chooses extreme poverty over tainted wealth. The chapter on poverty doesn't withdraw this, so the choice is made in full knowledge of what the poverty will be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both state a return of harm: 319 in morning and evening, 659 in the taking and the leaving. 659 restricts it to wealth and adds the positive half, that clean gains come back.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"381 makes six assets (army, people, wealth, ministers, friends, fortress) the mark of a lion among kings. Parimelazhagar's 'others' in 661 are exactly army, fort and friendship, which he says don't count as firmness. The list of assets is empty without the mind that uses them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:540","type":"agrees","note":"Two versions of the same promise: obtaining what one thinks of is easy if one keeps thinking of it (540), and if one is firm (666). The first asks for attention to be sustained, and the second for action to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:628","type":"tension","note":"628 prevents pain by not desiring pleasure. 669 accepts pain for the sake of the pleasure that results. One asks you to stop wanting joy and the other to work for it. They may differ in scope, since 628 speaks of how one meets pain and 669 of which tasks to take up, but neither verse says so.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:671","b":"k:467","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate thinking from having decided. 467 condemns thinking after deciding as a disgrace, and 671 condemns delay after deciding as a fault, the one seeing it as an intellectual lapse and the other a practical one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:383","type":"qualifies","note":"383 asks a ruler for தூங்காமை, sleeplessness, without condition. 672 divides this: some things should indeed be slept on. Parimelazhagar makes this connection himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:546","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 673 ranks force first where one is stronger. 546 says it isn't the spear that gives victory but the king's straight sceptre. The Kural's strategy and its ethics may be speaking here to different questions. Manakkudavar's reading of 673 removes this tension.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:674","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn about what enmity becomes if left to grow: 879 says fell the thorn tree young, and 674 says the left-over ember flares. 879 addresses timing, and 674 completeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"874 says the world rests in one who can turn enmity into friendship, as a mark of character. 679 turns this into a question of scheduling: it comes before duties to friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"969 praises those who give up life at the loss of honour, like the yak with its hair. 680 describes the weak who bow and take the terms to keep their people steady. Parimelazhagar sees this: the honourable would not choose it, so the verse only reports it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:586","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two agents of the king, both ready for death, with opposite methods: the spy says nothing whatever is done to him (586), and the envoy says everything whatever may come (690). They share an unflinching eye: compare 585's கண்ணஞ்சாது with 686's கண் அஞ்சான்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:667","type":"tension","note":"667 says never to scorn anyone for their form. 684 makes form (உரு) one of three requirements of the envoy. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that foreign courts respect the look of a man. The Kural is apparently distinguishing what people should do from what they in fact do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:803","type":"tension","note":"Both use கெழுதகைமை, the privilege of old intimacy. 803 asks what good long friendship is unless such liberties are accepted as if your own. 700 says to take such liberties with a king brings ruin. Friendship and power apply opposite rules to the same bond.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:638","type":"tension","note":"638 obliges the man at the king's side to tell him what is good, even if the king destroys wise counsel. 696 and 697 advise saying what is not disliked, in the way that he wants. The same courtier is told to be fearless and to be careful. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by reading 'wanted' as also greatly beneficial.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:582","type":"tension","note":"582 makes it a king's work to know everything happening to everyone, swiftly and always. 695 tells those near him not even to listen in on his secrets. Knowledge flows one way, and the Kural presents this imbalance as prudence, not as a problem.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"388 says a king who rules justly and protects is held to be a god among people: the title is earned. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 698, the divinity belongs to any king, young or kin, and the courtier mustn't ask. Other readings of ஒளி remove the conflict.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:563","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapter 57 tells the king not to be dreaded, and this chapter tells the courtier how to live with one who is. Both are realistic, and the Kural addresses each party without assuming the other has listened.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:699","b":"k:654","type":"agrees","note":"Almost the same phrase, துளக்கற்ற காட்சி and நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி. In 654 unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading, even in trouble. In 699 it won't presume on favour. Steadiness shows in both failure and success.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:709","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapters 71 and 110 share the title குறிப்பறிதல். 709 has the eye say what kings won't, and 1100 says that when lovers' eyes agree, words are useless. A single theory of the speaking eye serves both state intelligence and love, and neither treats speech as the deeper channel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:585","type":"tension","note":"706 and 710 say the face and eyes can't but show what is within. 585 asks the spy to stand under suspicious eyes and let nothing spill. If the face always reveals, the spy's art should be impossible, and if the spy's art is possible, this chapter overclaims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Both end with தான்முந்துறும், 'it gets there first'. In 380, fate gets ahead of any plan. In 707, the face gets ahead of any words. 1023 uses it again of a deity. The repeated phrase pictures a force that outruns human intention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:577","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural keeps asking what eyes are for. 577 answers kindly regard, 393 learning, and 705 reading signs. Each makes the physical eye worthless by that chapter's standard, so 'real sight' is defined differently each time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:702","b":"k:698","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, the courtier in 698 must treat the king as a god. 702 then gives that same comparison to the man who can read minds, so the servant who can read the god is set beside him.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:718","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Two poured liquids in three verses: water on a growing bed and nectar in a yard. Nectar is much more precious than water and yet the outcome is reversed. What decides the value of speech is the ground that takes it, not the substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:401","type":"develops","note":"Both use the phrase கோட்டி கொளல், holding forth in company. 401 faults the speaker for lacking learning, and 720 faults the hearers for lacking fitness. Together they give both ways a speech can fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:723","type":"tension","note":"The adjacent chapter says fearless speech in assembly is rarer than courage in battle. 715 says that the best of all good things is holding back among elders. The chapters are a pair and Parimelazhagar reconciles them with precision: only speaking first is restrained.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:780","type":"tension","note":"780 calls a death that brings tears to one's ruler's eyes worth begging for, in the chapter on martial pride. 723 calls those who die among enemies easy to find. The Kural holds both, and each chapter's supreme value is deflated by the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:410","type":"tension","note":"410 makes the unlearned like beasts beside the learned. 729 places a kind of learned person below the unlearned. Learning that seemed to separate humans from animals is here worth less than nothing if the learned can't speak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"650 uses the image of a scentless flower for those who can't expound their learning. 728 and 730 give the assembly as the place of the failure, and fear as its cause, adding a diagnosis to the image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:619","type":"tension","note":"739 says a land that makes you labour for riches is no land. 619 says bodily labour pays its wage even where the divine does not help. The ideal of the place and the ethic of the person pull in opposite directions.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and crops gather in the just king's land. 732 doesn't mention a king, but Parimelazhagar reads the absence of calamity through the king's virtue, which turns the land's fertility into evidence about its ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same closing move in two chapters: a land with every excellence is useless without accord with its king, and a fort with every excellence is useless without people who can act. Material conditions are never allowed to be sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:775","type":"tension","note":"741 allows that a ruler may fear and protect himself behind walls. 775 counts even a blink before a spear as flight. The chapter on the state and the chapter on the warrior hold different standards for fear, and both are the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"747's fort is hard to seize from outside and vulnerable to those within. 421 calls understanding an inner fort (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்) that enemies cannot destroy from within, the very weakness Parimelazhagar finds in 747.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Fort without capable actors and army without leaders end their chapters with the same negative verdict, இல்லது and இல், making human capacity the condition of every material strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:766","type":"qualifies","note":"750 says a fort fails without capable people. In 766 four virtues are the army's ஏமம, and Parimelazhagar concludes that such men need no other fort. Read together, the people are the real wall.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Both make exclusive claims with the same structure. 400 says learning is true wealth and all others are not. 751 says nothing but wealth is anything. The Kural lets each chapter speak in absolutes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:299","type":"tension","note":"Both use பொய்யா விளக்கு. In 299 the unfailing lamp of the wise is truthfulness and all other lamps are no lamps. Here the same phrase names money. Whether this is a deliberate reversal can't be proved, but the two verses compete for one title.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"660 likens wealth made by deceit to water stored in an unfired pot, and Parimelazhagar brings that very image into his comment on 755 to explain why such wealth destroys its maker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"241 ranks grace far above material wealth, which even the base have. 757 makes material wealth the nurse that raises grace. The ranking stands but the independence doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:678","type":"same_image","note":"Two elephant images for getting a task done without personally bearing the risk. In 678 one elephant is used to catch another, and in 758 the owner watches elephants fight. Both separate the one who directs from the danger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:39","type":"tension","note":"39 says only what flows from virtue is pleasure, so virtue is the source. 760 makes rightly made wealth the ground from which virtue and pleasure both come easily. The order of the three aims differs by book.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same formula for two wealths: an army is தலை among a king's riches and listening is தலை among a person's. The phrase shows that 'chief of wealth' is relative to whose life is being described.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:768","type":"tension","note":"763 mocks the sea-roar of an army without heroes. 768 allows that an army without fight can still gain stature through its show. Noise is nothing in one and display is something in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:765","b":"k:269","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two ways of confronting Death personified. In 269 ascetic power may leap past கூற்றம், and in 765 an army stands against it together. The renouncer escapes and the soldier faces, and only one of them needs others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:148","type":"tension","note":"Both use பேராண்மை. Here the word is first attached to fierceness in battle. In 148 the great manhood is not looking at another's wife. The Kural contests what manhood is, and 773's own turn to helping the fallen foe is partway to 148.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:773","type":"tension","note":"Two claims about the sharpest edge (எஃகு). In 759 wealth is the sharpest steel for cutting an enemy's pride. In 773 the edge of valour is helping that enemy when he falls. Cutting and sparing are both called sharpness.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:596","type":"parallel_domain","note":"596 says aim high, since even a failure is as good as not failing. 772 gives the warrior's version: the spear that missed an elephant is better than the arrow that hit a hare. Aim outranks outcome in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:776","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Both judge a life day by day and ask for no wasted days. In 38 the good deed makes a day count, and it blocks future births. In 776 the wound makes a day count. Two complete moral accountings use one form.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:780","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1066 says nothing shames the tongue more than begging, even water for a cow. 780 says this death is worth begging for, with the same root (இர-). Only something beyond the Kural's worst disgrace could justify the word.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:231","type":"parallel_domain","note":"231 says the only profit for a life is to give and live with fame (இசை). 777 wants the same இசை, and pays for it with life itself. Giving and dying are two routes to one reward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"448 says a king with no one to rebuke him falls even without enemies. 784 makes the same office of rebuke the very purpose of friendship. What a counsellor does for a ruler, a friend does for anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:785","type":"tension","note":"783 says friendship grows with practice and 785 says practice isn't needed. Parimelazhagar reconciles them by ranking three causes of friendship and placing shared feeling first, but the verses themselves sit in plain tension.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:801","type":"tension","note":"785 makes acquaintance unnecessary, while a whole chapter on old friendship opens at 801. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 801 eases this by saying that பழைமை is not elapsed time but a certain kind of friendship.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:1110","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both liken a relationship to learning with a 'the more, the more' (-தொறும்). In 783 friendship deepens like study's delight, and in 1110 love with the beloved is like finding one's ignorance with each new thing learned. Learning gives the Kural its model of an inexhaustible bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"791 demands inquiry before a friendship begins. 796 says ruin is what truly measures friends, and Manakkudavar adds it is hard to know them otherwise. So the test that matters most can't be run in advance, and this undercuts the chapter's own program.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"791 says those devoted to friendship cannot leave a friend once made. 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship pure gain. Parimelazhagar's 800 offers a way out: leaving is for what one took on without knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"793 and 794 screen friends by family. 972 says birth is equal for all living beings and only deeds make the difference. The chapter on choosing friends keeps a criterion that the chapter on greatness dissolves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Salt in both, and both about intimacy that hurts a little. In 802 the worthy are salt to friendship's liberties, and in 1302 lovers' sulking is salt that spoils when overdone. 1302 supplies the measure 802 leaves out.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"797 says dropping a fool's friendship is gain. 805 says when an old friend hurts you, consider it folly and go on. The same பேதைமை is a reason for leaving in one chapter and an excuse for staying in the next, and the only difference is how old the bond is.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:807","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"800 says pay if you must to escape a bad friend. 807 says love does not end even when an old friend ruins you. Between them lies a question the Kural doesn't settle: at what point does a harmful friend stop being an old friend and become one you never should have made?","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:818","type":"tension","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke, openly and in advance. 818 says that with a certain kind of friend, say nothing and let it fade. Whether one rebukes turns out to depend on whether the friend is still worth the rebuke.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:817","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter. 817 raises that to a ten-crore disadvantage against enemies. A verse about purpose becomes a verdict on a whole class of companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the women who desire wealth rather than the man bring ruin through sweet words. 813 applies the same logic to friends who calculate, and the comparison runs the other way: chapter 92's accusation is made first of men's friendships.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart as crystal shows what is beside it. 824 fears those whose face shows the reverse. Chapter 71 rests on the reliability of signs and chapter 83 on their failure, and Parimelazhagar's note on 824 calls the face the instrument of reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:826","type":"tension","note":"824 fears hostility that does not show. 826 says an enemy's fine words are soon seen through. The chapter holds two views on whether concealment can succeed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is kind, so judge by deed. 827 says the bent bow means harm. The two verses reach for the same geometry to make the same point, that shape doesn't reveal intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:828","b":"k:71","type":"tension","note":"71 says love can't be locked in, and that tears publish it. 828 says an enemy's tears may hide a weapon. The evidence that the Book of Virtue trusts absolutely is, in the Book of Wealth, a cover.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 says all virtue comes to a stainless mind and the rest is noise. 829 teaches outward friendship with inward death. Parimelazhagar accepts the conflict openly and resolves it as a rule for enemies only, which is a substantive claim about how the books of the Kural relate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:830","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship of the face alone is not friendship. 830 tells the reader to use just that kind on a suspect enemy (முகம் நட்டு). What is a failure in one chapter becomes a technique in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A precise inversion using the same ஒருமை and எழுமை. In 398 learning gained in one birth protects for seven. In 835 folly done in one birth drowns for seven. One life's leverage on all the lives to come works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:823","type":"develops","note":"823 says study doesn't reform the hostile. 834 turns the same point on the student and teacher himself: he is the worst fool. Learning fails as moral change in both, first in the other and then in oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:839","b":"k:782","type":"develops","note":"782 says a fool's friendship wanes like the moon. Parimelazhagar uses that waning to explain 839's joke: because it has been shrinking every day, by the time of parting nothing is left to hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"Two reports of what the world does. 752 says everyone despises those with nothing. 841 says the world doesn't count lack of wealth as lack. Both can't be plain observation, so at least one of them is the Kural speaking for how the world ought to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"Same doubled form (இன்மையுள் இன்மை, இன்மையின் இன்மை). 1041 says the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty. 841 gives the title to lack of understanding. The chapter on poverty and this one rank the worst lack differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"850 makes denying the world's consensus demonic. 423 says wisdom is finding the truth in anything, whoever says it. Parimelazhagar's doctrinal list sharpens this, and Kalaignar's 'evidence' moves 850 closer to 423.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:788","type":"same_image","note":"Both are about the garment and the shame of exposure. In 788 a friend's help comes as fast as the hand to a slipping cloth. In 846 the cloth over the body is exposed as a fraud if faults stay bare. One makes the reflex an ideal and the other mocks it as a substitute.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:314","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both tell the injured not to return harm, but their reasons differ. In 314 the wrongdoer is shamed by kindness. In 852, on Parimelazhagar's reading, one's own position is improved. The same act is justified in moral terms in one book and in terms of advantage in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"829 tells one to answer hidden contempt with hidden death. 852 says not to return harm even to one who plans division. They are three chapters apart in the same stretch on friends and foes, and the Kural offers both as sound counsel.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:771","type":"tension","note":"Two kinds of invincibility, each put as 'don't attempt it'. 771's warrior warns that those who stood before his lord now stand as stones. 855's person is unbeatable because he leans away. One's strength is in striking and the other's in declining.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter (நகுதல்). 860 makes நகல் the very source of good ways, which Parimelazhagar glosses as friendship. The same root gets very different valuations in the chapter on what friendship is for and the chapter on hostility.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"The same two words, வலியார் and மெலியார், with opposite advice. 250 says that when going against the weak, remember how you stand before the strong, which is a brake. 861 tells a ruler to go against the weak with appetite. The Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth are at their furthest apart here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"Two adjacent chapter openings. 861 tells one to keep enmity with the weak. 871 says enmity shouldn't be desired even in jest. Parimelazhagar's way through is that 871 is about unworthy enmity (மாணாத பகை), but the verses don't draw this distinction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same phrase, கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும். 794 says to pay for the friendship of a person of honour. 867 says to pay for the enmity of a fool. The Kural prices enemies and friends in the same way, and both are investments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"874 says the world rests on the one who turns enemies into friends. 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, give only your face and drop even that later. If this is the same event seen from both sides, then each party in 874's triumph has reason to be following 830.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"852 and its chapter say it is best not to return harm and to lean away from hostility. 879 says to kill the threat while it is young. One counsels letting enmity die by itself and the other warns that it won't, and both claim the outcome is self-preservation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:763","type":"same_image","note":"In 763 the rats die when the cobra merely breathes (உயிர்ப்ப). In 880 those who don't break a foe's pride don't survive his breath (on Manakkudavar's reading). The word for breathing is the unit of how little it takes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:1200","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both warn against telling pain to those not attuned to it. In 877 it is a matter of strategy and self-respect, and in 1200 the lover scolds her own heart for telling sorrow to one who doesn't care. The advice carries across books, and it is much sadder in the second one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:881","type":"qualifies","note":"In 735 the land should be free from உட்பகை, which Parimelazhagar glosses as robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. In 881 the same commentator takes the word as kin. The term shrinks from a realm's internal troubles to a family's, and it hurts more in the smaller frame.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a tiny seed against a huge effect. In 104 a millet-seed favour counts as large as a palmyra to those who understand it, and in 889 a sesame-seed enmity holds complete ruin. Small things turn out to be large in the Kural in both moral directions.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:781","type":"qualifies","note":"781 says no guard is as hard to get past as friendship. 891 says not slighting the able outranks every safeguard. Parimelazhagar lists friendship among those it outranks, so a long section that praised friendship and forts closes by ranking something above them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"Death personified, twice. In 765 an army stands united when Death comes raging. In 894 the fool waves Death over with his own hand. The difference between courage and folly is not whether one faces Death, but whether one sent the invitation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both rank fire below something else: in 129 the burn from fire heals and the burn of the tongue doesn't, and in 896 one may survive the fire but not offence given to the great. Fire is the Kural's standard for what is bad but survivable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:29","type":"develops","note":"29 calls the great those who have climbed a hill of virtue, whose anger can't be withstood even for a moment. 898 calls them hill-like, and 896-897 in Parimelazhagar's reading have the same instant destruction. Chapter 90 develops what chapter 3 states about the renunciant, in the political book.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:899","b":"k:25","type":"develops","note":"25 calls Indra sufficient witness to the power of the one who controls the senses. Parimelazhagar makes 899's வேந்தன் Indra as well. The Kural itself only names Indra in 25, so the connection depends on his gloss.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:899","type":"tension","note":"864 says one who doesn't let go of anger is easy prey for anyone. 899 makes the anger of the exalted able to bring down a king. Anger is weakness or supreme power depending on who has it, and 306 warns that it burns the one who has it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both set a wife's or beloved's shoulders against heaven. 906 says that fearing those shoulders leaves no worth even in a godlike life, and 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep on them. Fear and love differ, but the books value the same intimacy in opposite ways.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"Both redefine manliness away from dominance over others. 148 gives பேராண்மை to the man who doesn't look at another's wife, and 907 finds the opposite failure in a man who takes a woman's orders: in each case ஆண்மை is measured by self-command.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"428 calls fearing what should be feared the act of the wise. 905 shows the matching error: the fear here lands on doing good, which ought not be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:1107","type":"qualifies","note":"909 says the man who serves a woman's will loses pleasure with everything else. 1107 compares a beloved's embrace to eating one's own share in one's own home, which suggests that the pleasure 909 says is lost belongs to someone still his own master.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:91","type":"qualifies","note":"91 defines இன்சொல் as speech soaked in love and free of guile. 911 shows what is left when love is removed: the same sweetness becomes the instrument of harm. So the Kural's praise of sweet speech was never about sweetness alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"241 ranks the wealth of grace above material wealth, which even the பூரியார் have. 914 applies the same contrast to choice of company, and 919's பூரியர் are the ones who sink, so these verses share a vocabulary of grace against base wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:916","b":"k:149","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie a man's நலம் to whose shoulders he doesn't touch, using the same verb தோய். 149 says it of another's wife and 916 of women who sell themselves, so the two forbidden categories are treated in one idiom of self-possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Fortune as a goddess who leaves or stays: 617 places her at the feet of the unidle man, and 920 says the ones she has dropped can be known by their company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:1201","type":"tension","note":"926 says that drinkers are eating poison. 1201 says love is sweeter than liquor because it delights even in memory, taking liquor's delight as the benchmark. The book of love borrows, without judging, the pleasure that this chapter condemns.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare sleep to death. 339 makes this a serious claim about mortality, with waking as birth, and 926 only uses it as a pointer to lost awareness. The commonplace serves metaphysics in one and a warning in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:923","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both place a mother and the worthy in a single scene. In 69 her joy peaks when her son is called சான்றோன்; in 923 his drunkenness pains her and is unthinkable before those same people. Read together, 923 is 69 undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:929","b":"k:930","type":"qualifies","note":"Other people's reasons can't reach a drunk (929), but his own sober reflection might (930). The chapter separates argument from self-recognition as means of change.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"Under Parimelazhagar's reading of 940 the two are one argument: the soul clings to the body through suffering as a gambler clings through loss, and 345 says that for those ending birth even the body is one attachment too many. 940 is the disease and 345 the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முகடி, the goddess of misfortune. 617 lodges her in idleness and 936 equates her with gambling. Parimelazhagar's remark on 936 that her name is too ill-omened to say makes gambling her acceptable alias.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:932","type":"develops","note":"The hook's bait in 931 becomes the mechanism in 932: the one small win pulls the hundred losses after it. Parimelazhagar explicitly ties them together with அத்தூண்டிற் பொன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:968","type":"tension","note":"943 teaches how to carry the body far. 968 asks whether keeping the flesh alive is 'medicine' when dignity has been destroyed. The health chapter treats long life as the aim, and the honour chapter, two away, makes it conditional.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:345","type":"tension","note":"The verses value the same body differently. 943's man has received a body and should carry it far, and 345's renouncer finds it one attachment too many.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:471","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A physician and a ruler think in the same form: 949 weighs patient, illness and time before acting, and 471 weighs the deed, oneself, the opponent and allies. Both end in the same verb of action (செயல்), and both reject a universal prescription.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:227","type":"same_image","note":"Both use fire for the belly. 947's fire is a measure to observe, and 227's is the disease of hunger that won't touch one who shares food. One is the eater's problem and the other's cure is ethical, which widens what 'not falling ill' means.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:950","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"950 divides medicine into four roles. 1102 says that other illnesses have other remedies, but the bejewelled woman is herself the remedy for the illness she causes, which collapses the roles of 950 into one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:951","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"951 says that integrity and shame come naturally only to the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all beings and distinction arises from deeds. This is the Kural's sharpest internal disagreement on lineage, and Kalaignar's reversal of 951 can be read as an attempt to harmonize it with 972.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:957","b":"k:1117","type":"same_image","note":"957 makes the moon's spot the emblem of a visible fault. 1117 asks whether the woman's face, like the moon, carries a spot, and implies it doesn't. The same mark serves moral exposure in one book and the lover's denial of imperfection in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:959","b":"k:91","type":"develops","note":"In 91 sweet speech comes from a love-soaked and guileless mind. 959 uses speech as a sign of what lies underneath, as the sprout shows the soil. Together they make speech a surface that discloses the source.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says that giving before a poor man can utter 'I have nothing' is found only in the well-born. 955 takes the giver's own means shrinking, and holds his nature constant. Giving is the chapter's real measure of lineage in both.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"133 says conduct itself is lineage and lapse is low birth. 958 is less radical, since lapse leads people to doubt a man's birth, which keeps birth as the underlying fact while 133 dissolves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:131","type":"develops","note":"131 says that conduct must be guarded above life. 969 and 970 turn that ranking into an actual death, and show what the abstract comparison costs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"961 gives up even what is indispensable if it would lower one. 1028 warns that a man who is idle and stands on honour will destroy the family he should be building. Honour is the highest thing in one chapter and a hazard in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Hair appears at both ends of a value scale in the same chapter. Fallen hair is the worthless thing that a degraded person becomes, and the yak's one lost hair is what can't be survived. The first image is about how others see you, the second about how you see yourself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:780","type":"agrees","note":"Both look at a death through those left behind. 780's soldier dies so that his patron's eyes fill and such death is worth begging for, and 970's man of honour is worshipped by the world. Honour and the warrior ethic share one structure.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says bowing is good for all and an added wealth for the rich. 963 keeps the second half and reverses the first for the poor: they are told to stand tall, not to bow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:56","type":"develops","note":"56 gives the wife's self-guarding, தற்காத்து, as what makes a woman. 974 makes the same self-holding the condition of greatness in anyone. A gendered virtue is universalized, and the phrasing reaches back across the book of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"409 uses மேல் and கீழ் of birth, and ranks the unlearned high-born below the learned low-born. 973 uses the same words of position and wealth. Between them, the Kural detaches worth from both axes of hierarchy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:26","type":"agrees","note":"26, in the chapter on renunciants, defines the great as those who do what is hard. 975 repeats that in the book of wealth, with no renunciation. Parimelazhagar himself cites 26 at 971, so this standard crosses from ascetic to householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls cherishing the great and making them one's own the rarest of rare things, using the same பேணி. 976 explains why it is rare: the small don't even conceive of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:595","type":"agrees","note":"595 says a person's height is as great as their will, as a lotus stem rises with the water. 971 calls that will one's wealth and radiance. Both make aspiration the measure of a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end up in the same place: 980 ends the chapter on greatness with covering faults, and 984 makes not telling others' faults the defining quality of the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"Chapters 97 and 99 give different answers to humiliation. 967 prefers being called dead to living behind those who scorn you, and 986 makes accepting defeat even from lesser people the touchstone of worth. The two make most sense as distinct ideals, honour and fullness of character, and not as one code.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"988 says poverty brings no disgrace to one with worth. 1044 says poverty produces degraded speech even in the well-born. Whether character can hold against poverty is answered one way here and the other in the chapter on poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"151 holds up the earth, bearing those who dig it, as the model of forbearance for people. 990 reverses the dependence: the earth's own bearing relies on the worthy. The model has become the dependent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"Two images of bearing a load form a stack: five qualities are pillars carrying worth, and worth carries what the earth bears. The chapter's architecture rests the world on small daily dispositions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:225","type":"same_image","note":"The formula ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் recurs. In 225 this strength is enduring hunger, ranked below relieving it, and in 985 it is bowing. The Kural keeps redefining strength as some form of restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:314","type":"agrees","note":"314 says one punishes those who harm by doing them good until they are ashamed, and 987 makes that same return a test of whether worth has any use. 314 gives it a purpose, and 987 makes it a measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:984","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"261 defines austerity as enduring pain and not hurting living things. 984 picks out not killing as its core, in order to place not speaking of others' faults at the same rank for worth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"The same three words meet. 871 calls enmity the thing that has no பண்பு, not to be wanted even in jest. 995 says that those who know others' feelings have பண்பு even within enmity. Enmity is by definition devoid of courtesy, and yet courtesy can survive inside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:993","b":"k:1071","type":"develops","note":"993 says that likeness of limbs isn't human likeness, and 1071 gives the unsettling result: the base look exactly like people. The chapter on baseness starts where this verse ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:997","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both call a person a tree for a moral lack: 997 for lacking human courtesy, 576 for having eyes without kindly regard. Each states a difference between being human and being merely alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:996","b":"k:990","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end by making the world depend on a moral elite: if the worthy decline the earth can't carry its load (990), and without the courteous the world sinks into it (996). Two images of ground failing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both judge wealth by where it lands. In 216 wealth in the hands of the kind is a fruit tree in the village, and in 1000 wealth in the discourteous spoils in its vessel. The next chapter's 1008 completes the set with a poisonous tree.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:992","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"972 denies that birth settles distinction, and 992, two chapters later, places birth among courtesy's two sources. Parimelazhagar's gloss, which turns birth into conduct, can be seen as a way of closing the gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:386","type":"parallel_domain","note":"386 says that the land extols a king easy to see and not harsh in speech. 991 gives ordinary people the same recipe. Accessibility is a virtue at every scale in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:786","type":"develops","note":"786 distinguishes the laugh of the face from the laugh of the heart in friendship. Parimelazhagar takes 999's laughter as inner joy in others' company, so a failure of that capacity darkens the world and not merely a friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1008","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"An exact counter-image. 216: wealth in the hands of the kind is a useful tree fruiting in the village. 1008: wealth in the hands of the unwanted is a poison tree fruiting mid-town. Same setting, same act of fruiting, and the holder's character decides whether it feeds or kills.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"214 places one who doesn't do what's fitting 'among the dead', and 1001 calls one who won't use his wealth 'dead'. Both use death as a civil status, assigned for failure to circulate wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says that duty seeks no return, as with rain. 1010 extends the identification of generous wealth with rain: even their failures are like clouds running dry. The comparison is a consistent one in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:954","type":"same_image","note":"அடுக்கிய கோடி recurs. For the well-born in 954, stacked crores cannot buy a lowering act. For the miser in 1005, they amount to nothing. The same sum measures character in one and its absence in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1003","b":"k:570","type":"qualifies","note":"570 says that a harsh sceptre over the unlearned is the only burden on the earth. 1003 uses the same phrase for hoarders. The same claim to being earth's one burden is made against a tyrant and against a hoarder.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:233","type":"agrees","note":"233 says nothing is imperishable but high fame, and 1004 asks what the unwanted hoarder imagines will remain. Parimelazhagar's note supplies the link: fame rests only on the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"1017 says the shame-bearing would give up life before shame. 1251 says that love's axe breaks the door barred by shame. The same supreme barrier falls in the book of love, and the whole book of love depends on that collapse.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"A nearly identical ranking is made for two virtues. 969 gives up life for மானம் and 1017 for நாண். Kalaignar's gloss of 1017 even uses மானம். The verses suggest honour and shame are the outward and inward faces of a single conviction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"Shame is ornament in both. 1014 calls it the ornament of the worthy, and 1089 asks what other ornaments a woman with shame could need. The same figure crosses gender and books.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"315 asks what good knowledge is if one doesn't treat another's pain as one's own. 1015 makes the same move for disgrace: treating another's blame as one's own. Both make moral identification the test of the higher quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"Both first state what is common to all life, and then find சிறப்பு. For 972 that is deeds, and for 1012 it is shame. Read together, the Kural's account of human distinction becomes specific.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:73","type":"same_image","note":"The soul's hold on the body is the simile for virtue's bond in both: in 73 love's tie is like that of life to bone, and in 1013 worth holds on to shame in the same way.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1020","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Wood is used twice for those without inner quality. The courtesy chapter's man is a tree in 997, and the shameless man here a wooden puppet. The puppet is worse, since it has been shaped to look alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"Personified moral forces flee from the faulty. In 924, Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and in 1018 Virtue herself blushes and departs from one who doesn't blush. Shame is first the one who flees and then the condition for virtue's staying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"The same words appear with opposite force. In 380 fate comes first (தான்முந் துறும்), defeating whatever one devises (சூழினும்). In 1023 the divine comes first (தான்முந் துறும்) for the one who resolves, and 1024 adds that the end comes without deliberating (சூழாமல்). Priority over human planning works against effort in one and for it in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:619","type":"develops","note":"619 says that effort pays even when the divine doesn't help. 1023 takes up the other case: effort draws the divine to help. Together they cover both options and in each effort wins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1024","b":"k:467","type":"tension","note":"467 says deliberate and then resolve, and that deliberating afterwards is a fault. 1024 says that for those who work without delay for their people, the outcome comes without deliberating. The two can be reconciled only by treating family work as a special case.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:1025","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the same play on சுற்றம் and சுற்றுதல். In 525 giving and sweet speech surround one with layers of kin, and 1025 extends this until the whole world gathers around as kin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"148's பேராண்மை is not looking at another's wife, and 1026's நல்லாண்மை is ruling one's house. The Kural keeps coining compound forms of manliness that exclude combat, as Parimelazhagar notes here.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1028","b":"k:481","type":"qualifies","note":"481 says that a king needs the right time, like a crow beating an owl by day. Parimelazhagar explicitly cites 481 at 1028 to mark the exception: one who raises his family needs no season. Timing is wisdom for conquest but an excuse for duty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"Under Manakkudavar's reading of 1029 the two use the same verb, மறைத்தல்: greatness hides others' weakness, and so does the family's support. He bears the cost in his body.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:627","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a place for suffering to land: a target in 627 and a vessel in 1029. For 627 this insight is what frees one from distress, and for 1029 the suffering is chosen on others' behalf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"The same earth responds in two ways. In 151 it supports even those who dig into it, the model of patience. In 1040 it laughs at those who won't dig. Being dug is what it tolerates and even wants, so the virtue chapter's image becomes a joke on the idle.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"'X என்னும் நல்லாள்' is a shared formula. Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and Land the good woman laughs at the idle. Both personified women judge men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1330","type":"same_image","note":"ஊடல் is love's sweet sulk in 1330, delightful when embrace follows. 1039 borrows the word to warn an absent owner: the land, like a spouse, wants presence, and here the sulk costs him the harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1036","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of renouncers, and 1036 sets the farmer a level below, as the support of renouncing itself. The chain of dependence runs lower than the book of virtue showed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by one's own effort is the sweetest thing. 1033 makes that a social claim: only those who eat from their own ploughing truly live. The chapters on begging supply this chapter's value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"1051 tells one to ask of those worth asking and makes refusal their fault, not yours. 1035 presents a person who never needs the permission, and who is on the giving end of that same act.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1046","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"423 says the mark of understanding is seeing the truth whoever says it. 1046 reports that a poor man's meaning falls flat however true it is. One gives the ideal, and the other the way the world listens.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1044","b":"k:959","type":"tension","note":"959 says that speech reveals lineage as a sprout reveals soil. 1044 shows the signal failing: poverty brings degraded speech out of the well-born. Speech is a sign of poverty as well as lineage, so the test is less reliable than chapter 96 claims.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:368","type":"agrees","note":"368 says there is no suffering for those without desire. 1043 makes poverty itself into desire, which Parimelazhagar glosses as: without craving there's no poverty. An ascetic insight enters the chapter on economic misery, while the rest of the chapter denies it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1050","b":"k:378","type":"develops","note":"378 says that the destitute would renounce if what they must suffer would pass. 1050, with the same துப்புரவு இல்லார், tells them to do it. The verse of fate explains why it doesn't happen, while this verse only mocks them for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"247 keeps two forms of lack apart: no grace loses the next world, no wealth loses this one. 1042 collapses them: poverty takes both worlds. Grace alone turns out not to secure the next world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"'X என ஒரு பாவி' appears in both. Envy is the sinner in 168 and poverty in 1042, with both sending one down. Parimelazhagar links them himself. The state of being poor gets the same grammar as a vice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:923","type":"same_image","note":"The mother marks a moral limit twice. Her pain at drunkenness (923) still has love in it, because she's the most forgiving audience, and poverty does what drink couldn't by making her look at her son as a stranger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1061","type":"tension","note":"Consecutive chapters open in direct opposition. 1051: ask if you find those worth asking. 1061: not asking, even of those who give gladly and are as dear as eyes, is ten million times better. Parimelazhagar's solution makes 1051 apply only at the edge of death; the verses themselves don't state this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"The wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) marks a lack of real life in both. In 1020 it's a person without shame, and in 1058 a world without askers. Each chapter finds what makes movement into life, one in an inner quality and one in a social transaction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1059","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"232 says that all that speakers praise rests on those who give to askers. 1059 makes the dependence explicit: the fame needs the asker as well. The asker turns out to be necessary for a fame that seemed the giver's alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"Asking from the right person is like giving (1054), yet asking even water for a cow is the most degrading thing the tongue can do (1066). The chapters give opposite values to one act, and the difference of giver doesn't fully explain it, since 1061 refuses even the best givers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:963","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar himself cites 963 here: the poor must stand tall, and asking without speaking lets them do so. The honour chapter's demand shapes how begging can be done at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1062","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 says that even those with crores enjoy only as the apportioner apportioned. 1062 curses that very apportioner, if the portion includes begging. The chapter on fate accepts the apportioning, and this chapter refuses it, and Parimelazhagar's gloss makes the two nearly the same figure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1065","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Gruel is sweeter than ambrosia twice. 64: when one's child's little hand has stirred it. 1065, in Parimelazhagar's reading: when one's own effort earned it. The two supply the Kural's two sources of value in a poor meal, love and independence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"A flimsy vessel breaks up in both. 1134's raft of shame and good manliness is swept away by the flood of love. 1068's boat of asking is split by the rock of concealment. Each shows a failed crossing, but the vessel in 1134 is what one is proud of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:971","type":"agrees","note":"971 calls one's radiance the wealth of aspiration, and 1064 gives its most extreme form. Without means to live, the refusal itself is too large for the world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1080","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Two responses to the same crisis mark the moral range of the Book of Wealth. When honour or distress comes, those like the yak give up life (969), and the base sell theirs for a price, in haste (1080). The book ends on the second.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"A scale of giving. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1051 the worthiest give before anything is said, in 1078 the worthy give when told, and the base give when crushed. 1077 adds that the base give nothing to any who can't hurt them.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1076","b":"k:587","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A spy in 587 hears the hidden and knows it without doubt, and in 1076 the base also hear it, but proclaim it. Trading in secrets is a skill or a vice according to whether one can hold them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:368","type":"qualifies","note":"368 says there's no suffering for those without desire, the ascetic's freedom from anguish. 1072's base have that freedom, by not knowing what is good. The verse exposes a counterfeit of equanimity: peace of mind can't itself be the measure.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1018","type":"tension","note":"1018 sees shame as the condition of virtue, with virtue itself ashamed to approach the shameless. 1075 is a kind of counter-case: the base still have conduct, with fear and desire in place of shame. Their conduct looks alike and differs in its source.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:1073","type":"develops","note":"The same satirical move rises in rank: they're like men, and then they're like gods. What makes them like the gods, acting as they please, is what makes them not like men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"The warrior of 774 pulls a spear from his own body and laughs. The warrior of 1088 has that same reputation and is broken by a forehead. The Kural's courage has no defence against this enemy.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 calls modesty the ornament of the great, and without it their proud bearing is a disease. 1089 uses the same idea for a girl's beauty, so the ethical chapter's metaphor returns as a lover's literal claim that jewels are redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1281","type":"same_image","note":"Both set love above liquor for delighting through sight. 1090 says it at the first encounter and 1281 says it deep in the relationship, so the comparison holds from the start of love through its maturity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:709","type":"qualifies","note":"Both chapters are named குறிப்பறிதல. In 709, a skilled reader of eyes can tell enmity from friendship in a court. 1097 warns that a lover's eyes can put on enmity while friendly, so the statecraft skill has to work harder here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"In 1091 one look wounds and another heals. In 1102 the woman herself is both the disease and its medicine. The paradox grows from a glance to the whole person once they have united.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1101","b":"k:343","type":"tension","note":"343 tells the renunciant to destroy the pull of the five senses and give up all desire at once. 1101 celebrates the same five senses meeting their fill in a single person. The Kural gives each its place in different books without reconciling them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1104","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"In 1104 he marvels at a fire that burns at a distance and cools up close. In 1159, a voice in separation says ordinary fire can't burn at a distance at all. The same paradox goes from a delighted question to a complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:1065","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by your own labour is the sweetest food, for the sake of self-respect. 1107 borrows that dignity of one's own table to describe an embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists the quarrel as love's reward, and the Kural's last verse, 1330, ends the whole work on the same idea. Parimelazhagar's scornful reading of 1109 is hard to square with the fact that 1330 and the final chapters celebrate the sequence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says knowledge wells up in proportion to study, like water in a sand well. 1110 takes the same unlimited knowledge as the measure of desire, except that in love more of it reveals a lack and not a supply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"957 uses the moon's spot to say the flaws of the high-born show as conspicuously as a mark on the moon. 1117 uses the same spot in praise of an unblemished face. In one, a mark reveals a failing; in the other, its absence is the point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1114","b":"k:1094","type":"same_image","note":"The lily in 1114 would bow and look at the ground, as the woman herself does in 1094. The lily is imagined acting out the shame of being outdone, in a gesture she makes for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"Both disqualify a comparison because it is பலர் காணும், seen by many. What starts as a heart scolded about flowers becomes an instruction to the moon, showing how his praise rests on exclusivity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1122","b":"k:338","type":"tension","note":"Both use the same phrase for the friendship of body and life. In 338 it is like a bird flying from an abandoned shell, an image of how easily it ends. In 1122 it is the lover's image for an unbreakable bond. The thing he swears by is what the book on renunciation calls fragile, and in 1124 he seems to know it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1125","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"He says 'I don't know forgetting' with a clever smile. In 1207 the woman uses the same words, மறப்பறியேன், and adds that even remembering burns her. The same claim of constant memory is banter for one and torment for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Here the town's talk is a misunderstanding she can rebut. In 1147, the talk has become manure for the love itself. Her stance towards gossip shifts from defence to using it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1017","type":"tension","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and never give up shame for life. In 1132, the lover sets shame aside to save his body and life. It reverses the ethic exactly, in a book of its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"For the man, love's flood carries off the raft he had. For the woman in 1164, there is a sea of love and no raft at all. Her situation is worse than his: she has neither the raft nor his palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1137","b":"k:54","type":"qualifies","note":"54 asks what has greater worth than a woman, provided she has the firmness of chastity, using the same words, பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க. 1137 gives the worth a specific content: enduring a sea of desire without a public remedy. The praise comes from a man who is announcing that he won't do the same.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:183","type":"tension","note":"183 says that it is better to die than to live by backbiting, because it is a false kind of life. In 1141 backbiting is what keeps someone else alive. The same act has a very different moral look from the side of its target.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a sober drunkard, seeing another drunk, doesn't recognise his own degradation. 1145 takes the same compulsion, wanting more with each intoxication, and uses it with delight. The book that condemns drink reuses its logic to describe love without condemning it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1093","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093, the water poured into love's plot is her lowered glance. In 1147 the water is her mother's harsh words. What nourishes love moves from the most private gesture to the most hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 warns that trusting without examination and doubting after trust both bring endless grief, for those choosing men to work with. 1153 is that same predicament without any such procedure: there is no way to test the one she has already trusted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1149","type":"same_image","note":"Both quote 'fear not' against him. In 1149 his failure removes her shame before the town. In 1154 it raises the question of fault. One promise is doing a lot of work in her argument.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1179","type":"develops","note":"Fear of parting spoils union in 1152. By 1179 it is routine: her eyes can't sleep when he is away or when he is home. Anticipation has become a permanent condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:396","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water that rises as you draw it. In 396, the well is knowledge, and more study brings more. In 1161, the well is pain, and every attempt to bail it out adds more. The image of an inexhaustible resource is the same, and it is a blessing in one and a curse in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1132","type":"tension","note":"The man sets shame aside to mount the palmyra horse (1132). The woman carries shame and love in perfect balance and can drop neither (1163). The book states this asymmetry directly: he can choose and she can only bear the weight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"The man's flood simply takes his raft. The same phrase, காமக் கடும்புனல், in the woman's verse has her still swimming at midnight with no shore. The image remains and the endurance lengthens.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1076","type":"same_image","note":"1076 calls base men a beaten drum because they carry secrets to others. 1180 uses the same அறைபறை for her own eyes. An image of contempt for informers becomes an image of her helplessness: her body is what informs on her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 says harm done to others in the morning comes back to you by itself in the afternoon. Manakkudavar's gloss on 1175 uses this same moral logic, with the eyes as offenders. A serious doctrine is applied, playfully, to a woman's own eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 wishes the eyes could travel to him as the heart does. Next, 1171 begins the attack on them. Parimelazhagar explicitly calls 1170 a preparation for this chapter, so what looks like a change of subject is a single thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1130","type":"qualifies","note":"1130 insists he lives in her heart, so he has not gone anywhere. In 1184 her mind and words are full of him, but her skin has its own view. Her body undercuts what chapter 113 argues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:1133","type":"same_image","note":"Both are inventories. The man in 1133 once had shame and manliness, and now has the palmyra horse, an instrument he can use. The woman in 1183 had grace and shame, and has been left sickness and pallor. Only his new possession is a tool.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1149","type":"develops","note":"In 1149 he has left her and the talk is about her; she can't be ashamed. In 1188 she states the injustice directly: the talk notices the symptom and not who caused it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1192","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"11 says that the rain, sustaining the world, is to be known as ambrosia. 1192 uses the whole opening doctrine for one person's survival. The same dependence that the Kural gives to all life at its start reappears as a lover's condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:1163","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the carrying pole (கா). In 1163, love and shame hang at the ends of her life, and the pole is breaking. In 1196, love should hang on both people. The first is a struggle within one self, and the second is a fault between two.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1198","b":"k:1156","type":"develops","note":"வன்கண் is the same word in both. In 1156 he is hard because he could speak of leaving. In 1198 she calls herself hard for going on living without a word from him. The accusation has moved from him to her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"1202 says thinking of the beloved brings no pain, and 1207 says even thinking burns. If Parimelazhagar is right that a man speaks 1202 and a woman 1207, then the chapter makes a pointed contrast between their experience of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1209","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 is the man's oath: the bond is like body and life. 1209 quotes the same promise, வேறல்லம், as the thing killing her. In 1124 he had said her going was death to him, and now she is the one dying.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1119","type":"tension","note":"In 1119, the man tells the moon not to appear, as no one else should have her beauty. In 1210, the woman begs the moon to stay, since it's the only thing she may share with him. His moon is a rival to exclusive possession, and hers is the only link she has.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says love beats liquor because sight alone is enough. 1201 says memory alone is enough. As he moves away, the comparison holds, and needs less and less of her presence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:81","type":"parallel_domain","note":"81 says the whole point of keeping a home is to tend guests. 1211 applies that duty to a dream, which can be neither fed nor refused. A woman with an empty house is still measuring her situation in terms of the householder's virtue.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1162","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1162, sending a message shames her because it implies she's still alive. In 1212, telling him she has survived is just what she wants, provided it's in a dream. The dream permits what her waking shame forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:339","type":"parallel_domain","note":"339 says death is like sleep and birth is like waking from it, as an image of impermanence. 1216 wishes waking away altogether, for the sake of staying with her lover. If the two were read together, her wish would come close to a wish not to be reborn, though nothing in the verse says this.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1220","b":"k:1130","type":"agrees","note":"Both end by accusing this town of not understanding. In 1130 he dwells in her heart, while in 1220 he comes in her dreams. The defence moves on as her grounds grow thinner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:759","type":"tension","note":"759 tells the reader bluntly to make wealth, for nothing cuts an enemy's pride so sharply. 1230 gives the cost of that advice from where it falls: a man whose nature has become wealth, and a woman who is dying of it. The separate books don't reconcile the claims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1227","b":"k:1135","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man complains of evening's anguish, and Parimelazhagar says love flowers at evening. 1227 makes that a full image in the woman's voice. Evening afflicts both, so longing's timetable isn't gendered, though only his has the palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:1152","type":"develops","note":"1152 is the moment when even union was spoiled by the fear of parting, and on the commentators' reading of 1225 it was morning that brought this fear. Now that the feared thing has happened, morning is innocent. Fear has its own hours.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1231","b":"k:1114","type":"tension","note":"In 1114, the man says the lily would bow to the ground for shame before her eyes. In 1231, the friend says her eyes are ashamed before flowers. The same flower and the same shame have changed sides, measuring what absence has done.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1239","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"1108 calls sweet the embrace that even a breeze can't split, using the verb போழ. In 1239 a breeze does split it, with the same verb போழ, and the result is the first sign of her pallor. The ideal of union and its failure are one word apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1238","b":"k:1187","type":"same_image","note":"The same incident in two voices: in 1187 she turned slightly in his arms and pallor came, and in 1238 he loosened his arms and her forehead paled. In her telling it happens to her, and in his it is what he did.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1163","type":"develops","note":"In 1163, love and shame hang in balance on the pole of her life, and she describes the strain. In 1247 she gives an ultimatum to drop one. Nothing has changed except that she can no longer carry them, and the next chapter tells us which goes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1249","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"In 1130 she uses 'he lives within me' to refute the town's gossip. In 1249 she uses the same fact against her own heart, to stop it from leaving. The argument that once consoled her now traps her.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1177","type":"develops","note":"In 1177 she curses her eyes to weep themselves dry. In 1244 they are eating her, and she wants them gone. The eyes haven't stopped, and her conflict with them has moved from revenge to getting rid of them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:966","type":"tension","note":"966 asks what could be gained, neither fame nor heaven, by going after those who despise you. 1255 is a direct reply from inside love: people with this sickness can't know that kind of honour. The ethics chapter on honour isn't refuted, but declared unavailable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1203","type":"same_image","note":"The sneeze appears in two contrary ways: in 1203 it threatens and doesn't come, which she reads as him not thinking of her. In 1253 it comes uncontrollably, like her desire. So his love fails to arrive and hers can't be stopped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1138","type":"agrees","note":"The same phrase, மறையிறந்து மன்று படும், in both. 1138 presents it as a general truth about women's love, with Parimelazhagar seeing her own case in it. In 1254 she says it in the first person, having once thought herself an exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1259","b":"k:1284","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the same story in the first person: she went to sulk, and her heart went to join him. Whichever is read first, the second confirms that this is a repeating pattern, and that she knows it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1270","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to bring promises of quick return to those who will still be alive. 1270, in Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man finally arriving at that thought himself, from the other end of the journey and perhaps too late. The warning was given at the start of the separation, and he feels it only at the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:591","type":"tension","note":"591 says ஊக்கம், inner resolve, is the only true possession. In 1263, the woman names a very similar quality in உள்ளம் as his companion in leaving her. The virtue the Book of Wealth values most is what she has been left for.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1268","b":"k:1224","type":"tension","note":"If Parimelazhagar's speaker is correct, evening is a coming feast for the man in 1268, and for the woman in 1224 it comes like a killer to the execution ground. They are thinking about the same hour, far apart, and with opposite expectations.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1267 is a plan: sulk, embrace or both. 1259 records how such a plan works out: she goes to sulk, and her heart gives way. The planner in 1267 hasn't learned anything from the experience, or she has and enjoys planning anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1120","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 1120 is the man refusing to elope because her feet can't bear even swan's down. In 1279 she looks at those same feet, her way of saying she will walk. The compliment had been an excuse, and her glance answers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:706","type":"same_image","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it, in the chapter on reading signs in court. 1273 uses crystal the same way, with a thread inside it. Courtier and lover both depend on the transparency of the surface.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:1152","type":"agrees","note":"In 1152 the fear of parting spoils union, and Parimelazhagar cites a poem where unusual fussing is the warning sign. 1276 makes that the very argument: too much tenderness is proof of a departure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1277","b":"k:1157","type":"same_image","note":"Both have bangles announcing the departure of the lord of the shore. In 1157 they announce it to others, and in 1277 they tell her, before she herself knows. The sign has moved inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"104 says that those who know value a favour the size of a millet seed as if it were a palmyra. 1282 uses the same pair for love and sulking. The scale of ethical gratitude is used for measuring love's excess, and this time the small thing is to be removed altogether.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1178","type":"agrees","note":"Both state that he doesn't truly cherish her (பேணாது) and that the eyes can't rest without seeing him (காணாது அமை-). In 1178 this is a complaint on her eyes' folly, and in 1283 it's her reason for not quarrelling. The same fact has moved from grief to an explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1127","type":"same_image","note":"In 1127 she won't line her eyes because he's within them and would be hidden. In 1285 the stick is what the eye can't see. In the absence, kohl was an obstacle. In his presence, he himself is like the stick, too close to be seen.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:90","type":"parallel_domain","note":"90 says that a guest wilts at a cold look as an anicham flower wilts when smelled. 1289 makes love softer still than a flower. In both, what is finest about a relationship fails at a slight change in the other person's face.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1259","type":"same_image","note":"1259 tells it in her voice: I went to sulk, my heart gave way. 1290 tells it in his: she sulked with her eyes and came faster than I did. Each tells the other's defeat as a private pleasure, though for her it's defeat, and for him it's a fond memory.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:796","type":"parallel_domain","note":"796 says ruin has one benefit: it's a measuring rod for friends. 1293 applies this proverb to one's own heart, which she treats as a friend who proves false. The inner dialogue has absorbed a whole social ethic of friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1244","type":"same_image","note":"In 1244 her eyes eat her alive with longing, and in 1296 her heart does. The same verb is used for what are, in effect, her own organs, and these chapters keep treating her as a person being consumed by her parts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"1247 demands that the heart give up either love or shame. 1297 tells us what happened: shame got forgotten. The chapter between them, on breaking restraint, is how.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"ஏதிலர், the 'stranger', has been her word for him in the town's mouth, in 1129 and 1130. In 1300 the same word is used for a stranger, and now the one who isn't one's own is one's own heart. By the end, the outsider is inside.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1328","type":"same_image","note":"உப்பு is the word in both. In 1302 the salt is the sulk, measured carefully. In 1328 the salt is the sweetness of the union that follows, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. The cook's image runs through both chapters, with the same word for the seasoning and the taste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1191","type":"tension","note":"1191 calls requited love the stoneless fruit, the ideal of enjoyment with nothing in the way. 1306 says love without quarrels is fruit gone bad or not yet ripe. So the obstacle that 1191 wishes away, 1306 says is what makes the fruit ripe.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:366","type":"parallel_domain","note":"366 warns the renunciant to fear craving (அவா), because it deceives. In 1310, the sulking man names the same craving as what drives his heart to her. He uses the moral vocabulary correctly, with no intention of following it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1203","type":"tension","note":"The same folk belief at two points in the story. In 1203 she longs for a sneeze as proof he's thinking of her. In 1317 he sneezes in front of her, and the same belief proves someone else is thinking of him. A sign that means what one fears can't be falsified.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1125","type":"develops","note":"In 1125, the man is clever at the friend's expense: I would remember her if I forgot. 1316 shows the woman turning this logic on him: you say you remembered, so you forgot. His own wit has become the trap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1262","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of 1262, she keeps him in mind at death so as to reach him in the next birth. In 1315, 'this birth' is what makes her cry. Rebirth, for the renunciant a fate to escape, is in this book something lovers want to share.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"In 1113, he went over her body part by part, comparing it to shoots, pearls and spears, and she was pleased to be praised. In 1320 she treats that very habit of comparing part by part as evidence that he has others in mind. The praise of chapter 112 is the root of the suspicion.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1323","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"1103 asks whether the Lotus-eyed one's world is sweeter than sleeping on the arms of one's beloved. 1323 repeats the challenge to heaven with a stranger choice: it's the quarrel that outdoes heaven now. Between these two verses, love has come to value its own frictions most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088, a warrior laments his strength being broken by her forehead, and takes it as defeat. In 1327, the man has worked out that losing is the way to win. The book's military imagery, which begins with injury, ends in a pleasantly ruined scoreboard.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1169","type":"tension","note":"In 1169, the woman in separation says the long nights are crueller than the cruel man himself. In 1329, the man in reunion prays for night to lengthen. The same long night is torment or wish, depending on whether the other person is there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:942","type":"parallel_domain","note":"942 says the body needs no medicine if one eats only after the last meal has digested. 1326 takes the same physician's advice and makes it a rule of love: the pause between meals is what keeps appetite and health.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1258","type":"agrees","note":"Both say soft words are the weapon that breaks her defences. In 1258 she says it with bitterness and calls him a thief, and in 1324 she says it with something like eagerness. The weapon hasn't changed, and what she wants from it has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1","b":"k:543","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 1 says the world has ஆதி பகவன் as its first (முதற்றே உலகு), as letters have அ. Verse 543 uses the same word for the king's sceptre: it stands as the ஆதி of the antaṇar's books and of aram (அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய் நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as their cause (காரணம்). The pairing shows the book giving the world one 'first' in its opening and giving aram itself a second, political one in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 2: what has learning yielded, if one does not bow at the good feet (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்)? Verse 315: does understanding achieve anything (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own? Both are rhetorical questions that judge knowledge by a test outside knowledge, worship in one and compassion in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 asks what use learning is (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயன் என்கொல்) if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge. Verse 356 states the fruit positively: those who learn here and see the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not returning. Parimelazhagar reads verse 2's fruit as ending rebirth too, so 356 spells out what verse 2 leaves as a question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:3","b":"k:6","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 3 and 6 end on the same words, நீடுவாழ்வார், 'will live long'. Verse 3 gives this to those who reach the feet (மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்); verse 6 gives it to those who stand in the path of conduct free of falsehood (பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க நெறி நின்றார்) of the one who quenched the five. The repeated ending puts devotion and conduct side by side as routes to the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:350","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 describes the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no affliction. Verse 350 turns the same description into an instruction: hold to the hold of the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை), in order to let attachment go (பற்று விடற்கு). Parimelazhagar notes that பற்றற்றான் is chosen to match the invocation chapter; 350 supplies the mechanism verse 4 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:362","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the word வேண்டாமை but use it differently. In verse 4 it is paired with வேண்டுதல் as something the Lord lacks, which Parimelazhagar glosses as aversion (வெறுத்தல்). In 362 வேண்டாமை is desirelessness, the thing to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்) so that birthlessness comes. A reader should not carry one sense into the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 4 says there is no இடும்பை at any time for those who reach the feet of the one free of liking and aversion. Verse 628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure and calls இடும்பை natural (இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்) does not come to pain. The Book 2 verse keeps the link between absent desire and absent suffering but drops the devotional route, and it concedes that the affliction itself still arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Verse 5 says both kinds of deed, which belong to darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்), do not attach to those who dwell on the Lord's praise; Parimelazhagar explains that good deeds too are a cause of birth. Verse 38 says that doing good (நன்றாற்றின்) with no day lost is the stone that blocks the road of one's days, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as ending rebirth. On those readings one verse counts good deeds among what binds and the other makes them what frees.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:352","type":"develops","note":"Verse 5 ties the two kinds of deed to இருள், which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss as delusion. Verse 352 says that for those of stainless sight who have left confusion (மருள்நீங்கி மாசறு காட்சியவர்க்கு), darkness goes and joy comes (இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும்). The later verse gives knowledge as what removes the darkness that verse 5 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:388","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 5 speaks of the praise of இறைவன், the chapter's word for the Lord. Verse 388 says a king who does justice and protects is set down as இறை for the people (மக்கட்கு இறையென்று வைக்கப்படும்). The shared word shows that the title used for the deity in chapter 1 is one a human ruler earns by conduct in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"Verse 6 promises long life to those who stand in the path of the one who quenched the five. Verse 126 gives the human practice and its reward: if one can draw in the five like a tortoise in one birth (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), it is a protection for seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Verse 126 says அடக்கல், holding in, where verse 6 says அவித்தான், quenched, so the follower's task is stated more modestly than the exemplar's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:622","type":"tension","note":"Verse 7 says it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety (மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the one without equal. Verse 622 says a flood of affliction perishes when a person of understanding thinks in his own mind (அறிவுடையான் உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்). The conflict holds if மனக்கவலை is ordinary mental distress, as Manakkudavar's gloss allows; Parimelazhagar narrows it to the sufferings of rebirth, which 622 does not address.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:1072","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 7 makes freedom from mental anxiety rare, reserved for those who reach the Lord's feet. Verse 1072 says, with irony, that the base are better off than those who know the good, because they have no distress in their hearts (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). Read beside verse 7 it marks that mere absence of worry is not the state the invocation is praising, since the base have it too.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"Verse 8 calls the deity அறவாழி அந்தணன், joining அந்தணன் to aram. Verse 30 defines the word: those called அந்தணர் are the people of aram (அறவோர்), because they act with kindness toward every living being (செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுகலான்). Verse 30 gives the reason for the pairing of title and virtue that verse 8 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:989","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 uses ஆழி for the deity (அறவாழி), read as 'sea of aram' by Parimelazhagar, who also records the reading 'wheel of aram'. Verse 989 calls the worthy சான்றாண்மைக்கு ஆழி and says they do not shift even if the age shifts; there Parimelazhagar reads ஆழி as the shore of the sea of worth. The same word is attached to a virtue in both verses, to a god in one and to people in the other, and its sense is disputed in both.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 says the other seas are hard to swim (பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the antaṇan who is a sea of aram; Parimelazhagar takes the other seas to be wealth and pleasure and the feet to be the raft. In 1164 the woman says the sea of kāmam is there and the safe raft to swim it is not (காமக் கடல் ... அதுநீந்தும் ஏமப் புணை ... இல்). Book 3 shows from inside one of the seas that Parimelazhagar finds in verse 8, with the raft named and missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 9 treats a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறி) as the type of uselessness, while verse 6 praises the one who quenched the five of the sense-gates (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்). Parimelazhagar reads what is quenched in verse 6 as the five desires that travel by the senses, not the senses. Taken together the two verses of the chapter support that reading: a dead sense is a defect, and what is praised is something else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is without worth, like a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே). Verse 418 says ears not pierced by listening are of a deaf kind even though they hear (கேட்பினும் கேளாத் தகையவே). Both measure a part of the body by one proper use and call it dead without that use; the use is worship in one and learning by ear in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 compares the unbowing head to an organ that is present and does not sense. Verse 574 asks what eyes without kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து எவன் செய்யும்). The figure of the organ that is there and useless is applied in Book 2 to a ruler's compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:8","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 8 and 10 both speak of swimming a sea with the Lord's feet as the condition. Verse 10 names the sea as birth (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல்); verse 8 says only 'the other seas' (பிறவாழி), which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth and pleasure. On his reading the chapter has two different seas; without it, verse 8 can look like a first statement of verse 10.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:349","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 10 makes reaching the Lord's feet the condition for crossing birth: those who do not reach them do not swim it (நீந்தார் இறைவனடி சேராதார்). Verse 349 gives a condition with no deity in it: the moment attachment is cut, that cuts birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). The next verse, 350, joins the two by telling one to hold to the one without attachment in order to drop attachment, so 349 shows what reaching the feet has to accomplish.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of birth and others do not. In 1167 the woman says she swims the fierce flood of kāmam and sees no shore (நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்), alone even at midnight. The swimmer who cannot cross, stated as doctrine in verse 10, is spoken in the first person in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields wealth and asks what greater gain a living being has. Verse 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are to be thought about (நினைக்கப்படும்). Parimelazhagar says such outcomes cannot come from crookedness or uprightness and sends the wise to look for a cause in past deeds; either way 169 limits how far verse 31 can be read as a promise visible in one life. His gloss on 31 itself takes செல்வம் as heavenly goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:754","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields distinction and yields wealth too (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Verse 754 uses the same construction with the terms turned round: wealth that came by known means and without wrong yields aram and yields pleasure too (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). Book 1 has virtue producing wealth and Book 2 has rightly earned wealth producing virtue, in matching words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"Verse 32 says no ruin (கேடு) surpasses forgetting aram. Verse 117 gives the other side: the world will not count as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays fair and rests in the good. Together they define ruin by one's relation to virtue and not by one's circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:532","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 32 names forgetting (மறத்தல்) of aram as the greatest ruin. In the chapter on heedlessness, 532 says forgetfulness kills renown (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை) as unrelieved poverty kills understanding. The idea that the damage comes through lapse of attention, without any act of wrongdoing, is carried from ethics into statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"Verse 33 asks for aram everywhere and without a break (ஓவாதே ... எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, then not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 297, that it is better to hold one virtue unfailingly than to take on many and fail in some, this is advice to narrow what verse 33 tells one to spread as widely as possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:477","type":"develops","note":"Verse 33 limits its command with ஒல்லும் வகையான், 'in the way that is possible'. Parimelazhagar explains this for the householder as acting in proportion to one's means, and 477 says so directly: give knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து ஈக), for that is the way to guard wealth while giving. Verse 477 gives the practical content of the qualifier in verse 33. This edition prints அறவறிந்து in 477; the commentary reads அளவு அறிந்து.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:673","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 33 says to do the work of aram by whatever means one can (ஒல்லும் வகையான்), without ceasing, wherever it can go (செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 673, on how to carry out a task, uses the same words: where it is possible the deed is good, and where it is not, act looking for a way that goes (ஒல்லும்வாய் ... செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்). The rule for virtue and the rule for state action are built from the same vocabulary of the possible. This edition prints the first word of 673 as ஙல்லும், a typo for ஒல்லும், as its own commentary shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"Verse 34 sets inner cleanness against outward show. Verse 298 makes the same division and adds how the inner kind is known: outer purity is had by water, and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). It gives a test for the state that verse 34 only asserts. This edition prints புறள்தூய்மை for புறந்தூய்மை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:161","type":"develops","note":"Verse 35 lists envy (அழுக்காறு) first among what aram must avoid. Verse 161 turns that item into a positive rule: take as your way of conduct (ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க) a nature that has no envy in the heart. The chapter on envy opens by treating as a discipline what verse 35 states as an exclusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 35 defines aram by the absence of four faults. Verse 431 defines a sound prosperity the same way for a ruler: the increase of those without arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்) has dignity. Anger is on both lists; Parimelazhagar glosses சிறுமை in 431 as lust, which would bring the two lists closer still.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:233","type":"tension","note":"Verse 36 calls aram a companion that does not perish (பொன்றாத் துணை). Verse 233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands unperishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்), using the same word. Read literally the second excludes the first. Parimelazhagar's reading of 36, that the merit goes with the soul into another body, puts it outside 'the world' of 233; Kalaignar reads 36 itself as lasting fame, which removes the conflict another way.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 36 says aram done now stays as a companion at death. Verse 398 says the learning one has acquired in one birth is a protection through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both speak of something gained in this life that is not lost with the body; Book 2 says of learning what Book 1 says of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:270","type":"agrees","note":"On the reading of Parimelazhagar and Pope, verse 37 explains the difference between palanquin rider and bearer by aram done before. Verse 270 reasons the same way about a whole society: the reason the have-nots are many is that those who do tavam are few and those who do not are many. Both read present inequality as the result of past practice; Parimelazhagar adds that wealth and want in 270 also mean understanding and its lack.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"Verse 37 says there is no need to state the way of aram: look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (பொறுத்தானொடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). Parimelazhagar and Pope take this to mean that the fruit of virtue can be seen in who rides and who carries. Verse 973 says those who are not high are not high even when seated high (மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர்), and Parimelazhagar reads the seating there as wealth and poverty. On those readings one verse lets position show merit and the other denies that position shows anything about the person. The Tamil of 37 is elliptical and does not itself say which of the two is the virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"Verse 38 says daily good is a stone that shuts the road of one's வாழ்நாள்; Parimelazhagar takes வாழ்நாள் as the days of living in a body, so that shutting the road means no more births. Verse 326 says Death does not advance on the வாழ்நாள் of one who keeps to not killing. On Parimelazhagar's reading the same word names something virtue ends in 38 and something virtue protects in 326. On Kalaignar's reading of 38, where the stone smooths life's road, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:334","type":"develops","note":"Verse 38 treats each day as something that can fall away unused or be filled with good. Verse 334 says what a day is: it shows itself as one thing called a day, and it is a blade sawing through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. Verse 334 gives the reason for the daily urgency of verse 38.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:156","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes by aram is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and the rest lies outside and has no renown either. Verse 156 allows that those who retaliate do get joy, for one day (ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம்), while those who forbear have renown for as long as things last (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்; until the world perishes, in Parimelazhagar's gloss). It keeps verse 39's pairing of joy and renown but concedes a short pleasure outside virtue, so the 'only' of 39 reads as a judgment of worth and not a denial that such pleasure is felt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 40 names blame (பழி) as the thing to escape. Verse 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one gives up what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). Both make avoidance of the blameworthy the working test, verse 280 applying it to the ascetic in place of outward marks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 40 gives a two-sided rule: what one should do is aram, and what one should escape is blame (செயற்பாலது ... அறனே, உயற்பாலது ... பழி). Verse 466 gives the same two sides to a ruler as consequences: one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and ruined also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Book 2 restates the pair as policy and makes the omission as costly as the wrong act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"Verse 40 says blame is what a person should escape. Verse 656 tests that rule at its hardest: even if one must see the hunger of the mother who bore one, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை). It shows that the avoidance of blame in verse 40 is meant to hold against a competing duty, and it names whose blame counts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:132","type":"same_image","note":"242 ends பல்லாற்றால் தேரினும் அஃதே துணை: however many ways you examine, அருள் alone is the companion. 132 ends with the same words, தேரினும் அஃதே துணை, said of ஒழுக்கம் (conduct). The identical closing with its exclusive ஏ is given to two different things; read strictly the two claims compete, read as emphasis each chapter is crowning its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:324","type":"develops","note":"242 tells one to search by a good path and hold to அருள் (நல்லாற்றான் நாடி அருளாள்க). 324 asks what the good path is (நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின்) and answers: the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 324 gives content to the நல்லாறு that 242 leaves open, and ties அருள் to not killing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:5","type":"same_image","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined with அருள் (அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்) do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 5 says the two kinds of deed joined with darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்) do not join (சேரா) those devoted to the Lord's praise. Both build on சேர், what one is joined to: in 5 darkness is a quality of deeds (Parimelazhagar: மயக்கம்), in 243 it is a place one enters, and joining something else keeps it away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"243: no entry into the dark painful world for hearts with அருள். 121: restraint leads among the immortals, lack of restraint drives into thick darkness (அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்). Both use darkness as the destination of a failed virtue, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a hell; 243 states only the exemption, 121 states both directions.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"244 pairs மன்னுயிர் and தன்னுயிர்: care for other lives frees one's own life from fear. 268 uses the same pair the other way round: one who has fully gained his own life (தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை) is worshipped by all other lives (ஏனைய மன்னுயிரெல்லாம் தொழும்). Together they show the relation running in both directions, from care of others to one's own safety and from self-mastery to others' reverence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"244: one who tends living beings and holds to அருள் (மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வாற்கு) has no deed his own life need fear (தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்). 326: on the days of one who lives by not killing, Death that eats life does not advance (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று). Both say that sparing other lives secures one's own, once in terms of deeds and once in terms of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"244 promises that the one who protects lives has nothing his own life need fear. 327 uses the same word, தன்னுயிர், to command not taking another's life even if one's own life goes (தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க). 327 shows that the safety promised in 244 is not the condition for the rule: the rule holds even where one's own life is lost.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"245 calls the wide earth where the wind moves a witness (ஞாலம் கரி) that those who hold to அருள் have no distress. 25 calls Indra witness enough (இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி) to the power of one who quenched the five senses. Both close an unprovable claim about a virtue's power by naming a கரி, a witness; 245 appeals to the whole visible world, 25 to one figure from story.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"245 says flatly that those who hold to அருள் have no distress (அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை), and Parimelazhagar takes this to cover this life. 169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடும்) are to be pondered, which concedes that the upright do meet ruin. The conflict holds if அல்லல் includes worldly ruin and if 169 is read as admitting such cases occur; Parimelazhagar softens 169 with 'if they occur' (உளவாயின்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"246 says those who leave அருள் and go on doing wrong have, they say, parted from பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்). 32 says there is no ruin greater than forgetting virtue (அதனை மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு). Both treat wrongdoing as a kind of forgetting; Parimelazhagar reads the பொருள் of 246 as the lasting good, i.e. virtue, which brings the two closer, while Kalaignar reads it as being without wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"246 describes the cruel as people who have forgotten (பொச்சாந்தார்); Parimelazhagar says what they forgot is their own suffering. 250 prescribes an act of memory: when going against the weaker, think of yourself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Within the chapter, 250 supplies the remedy for the forgetting that 246 diagnoses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:248","type":"qualifies","note":"247 puts wealth and அருள் in parallel (பொருளில்லார்க்கு இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு). 248 then breaks the parallel: those without wealth may flower one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished and can hardly become anything again (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). The simile of 247 holds for what each lack costs, not for whether the loss can be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:134","type":"parallel_domain","note":"248: loss of wealth can be reversed, loss of அருள் cannot (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). 134: even if forgotten, the recited text can be learned again (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்), but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines (பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்). Both set a recoverable possession against an unrecoverable loss of character, in the domains of wealth and of learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:355","type":"same_image","note":"249 compares virtue done by one without அருள் to an unclear person seeing the true thing (தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்று). 355 defines understanding by the same phrase: seeing the true thing in whatever thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). 355 shows why the comparison in 249 bites: seeing the true thing is what clarity consists in, so without clarity it does not happen, or happens only once by chance as Parimelazhagar reads it (ஒருகால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the general rule: what one has oneself felt to be painful one must not do to another (இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை ... பிறன்கண் செயல்). 250 applies the same use of one's own experience to a difference in strength: recall your own position before the stronger when you stand over the weaker. 250 is the rule of 316 turned into a specific exercise of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:254","type":"develops","note":"321 asks what virtuous action is and answers not killing (அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை); killing brings all other deeds. 254 uses the same question form for அருள்: what is அருள் and what is not comes down to not killing and killing (யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்). Read together, not killing is the content of both அருள் and virtuous action, which joins the chapter on அருள் to the chapter on not killing. (The first letter of 254 is missing in this edition: ருளல்லது for அருளல்லது.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"321 makes not killing the answer to what virtuous action is. 297 says that if one keeps up not lying, it is good not to do other virtues (பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). Each verse lets one virtue stand for the whole, and they pick different ones; the conflict holds if both are read as claims of sufficiency and not as chapter emphasis. 323 settles the order inside this chapter by putting not lying second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:322","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"322 puts feeding and sustaining lives at the head of the virtues. 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove hunger (அப்பசியை மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்); Parimelazhagar takes the first group to be ascetics. 225 supplies a ranking that fits 322: not harming is completed by actively keeping others alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:323","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"323 ranks not lying behind not killing. 292 says even falsehood stands in the place of truth if it yields good free of fault (புரைதீர்ந்த நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்), and Parimelazhagar's example is a false word that saves a life from death. 292 shows the ranking of 323 at work inside the chapter on truth: where truthful speech and a life pull apart, the life decides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:324","b":"k:291","type":"same_image","note":"324: if asked what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 291: if asked what is called truth, it is speech with no harm at all in it (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). The two definitions share their wording, எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும், and both define a positive term by the complete absence of harm, in action and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"325 says that among all who renounced fearing their condition (நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள்), the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief. 261 defines the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை). 261 states the two parts of the ascetic's practice; 325 says which part decides rank among ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"325 ranks the renouncer who keeps to not killing first. 984 says austerity lies in the goodness of not killing (கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை), and sets it beside worth, which lies in not speaking others' faults. The same claim about renouncers appears in the third part of the book of wealth, used there as the measure for a layman's virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327: even at the cost of your own life, do not do the deed that takes a life. 656: even if you must see your mother hungry, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினும் செய்யற்க). Both have the form 'even at this extreme cost, செய்யற்க', and both name a வினை as what is forbidden; the first is addressed to the virtuous life, the second to a minister's conduct of affairs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327 puts another's life above one's own. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால் நாண்துறவார்). In both, one's own life is not the highest value; what outranks it is another creature's life in 327 and one's own sense of shame in 1017.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"328 refuses gain that comes by killing, however great. 311 says that even if one could get wealth that brings distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்), not doing hurt to others is the settled stand of the stainless. The two adjacent chapters make the same refusal for the lesser harm and the greatest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:657","type":"parallel_domain","note":"328: for the worthy (சான்றோர்க்கு), gain got by killing is the lowest (கடை). 657: the utter poverty of the worthy is above gain got with blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவே தலை). Both judge ஆக்கம் by its source from the standpoint of the சான்றோர், with கடை in one and தலை in the other; 328 belongs to the book of virtue and 657 to the conduct of state business.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:551","type":"develops","note":"329 treats killers as the settled example of baseness. 551 takes that example as a measure and says a king who takes up harassing his people and does wrong is crueller than those who have taken up killing (கொலைமேற்கொண்டாரிற் கொடிதே). The book of statecraft uses the low point fixed in 329 to rank a bad ruler below it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:37","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 infers a past deed from a present condition: the wretched are those who took life. 37 says there is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (சிவிகை பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). On Pope's and Parimelazhagar's reading both argue from visible fortune back to deeds, 37 for good fortune and 330 for bad; Kalaignar reads 37 differently, as about how the virtuous bear life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:270","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 explains diseased and failing lives by past killing. 270 explains why the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who do not are many (நோற்பார் சிலர் பலர் நோலாதவர்). Both give a cause in conduct for an unequal condition seen in the world, 330 for individuals and 270 for the proportion in a population.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:326","type":"develops","note":"330 says those with afflicted bodies and a failing, evil life (செயிர் உடம்பின் செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கையவர்) are, they say, people who separated life from body. 326 says Death does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. The two verses are the chapter's pair of consequences, both stated in terms of body and lifespan: the non-killer's days are spared and the killer's body and living are spoiled. Parimelazhagar places the killing of 330 in a former birth; the Tamil does not say which birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:59","type":"same_image","note":"381 calls the king who has all six limbs (படை, குடி, கூழ், அமைச்சு, நட்பு, அரண்) 'அரசருள் ஏறு', the male beast among kings; 59 says a man whose home lacks a wife who seeks good name has no 'ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை' before those who scorn him. The same figure of the proud male animal is made to depend on what a man has behind him: a kingdom's six supports in one case, a household's good name in the other. (Parimelazhagar takes ஏறு in 59 as a lion; the word itself only says 'male'.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:740","type":"qualifies","note":"381 measures a king by possession (ஆறும் உடையான்), and குடி, which Parimelazhagar takes as the land, is one of the six. 740 says a land that has every excellence is still without use (பயமின்றே) if it lacks accord with its ruler (வேந்தமைவு இல்லாத நாடு). Read together, having the limb is not enough: the limb and the king must fit each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"381 counts the fort (அரண்) among the six things whose possession makes the leading king. 750 says a fort with every excellence (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்) is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The later verse adds the condition that 381's bare 'உடையான்' leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"382 lists fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive (அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவு ஊக்கம்) as a king's nature. 863 describes the man who is easy prey for an enemy: he fears, does not know, does not fit with others, does not give (அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்). Three of the four kingly qualities reappear negated, so the weak enemy of chapter 87 is drawn as the reverse of the king of chapter 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:605","type":"agrees","note":"383 gives three things that stay with a ruler, beginning with தூங்காமை. 605 gives four that are the pleasure-boat of those bound for ruin: delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep (நெடுநீர் மறவி மடி துயில்). The two lists are near-opposites, and 605 covers both senses the commentators give தூங்காமை in 383 (not delaying, not being slothful).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:668","type":"develops","note":"383 names promptness and resolve (தூங்காமை, துணிவுடைமை) as two separate standing qualities, with learning between them. 668 turns them into one instruction for a single task: once a deed has been seen clearly and decided (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), do it without wavering and with delay driven off (தூக்கம் கடிந்து செயல்). The trait list of the king becomes a sequence: decide, then act at once.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:766","type":"parallel_domain","note":"384 pairs மறன் and மானம் in the king. 766 opens its list of the four safeguards of an army with the same pair (மறம் மானம்), followed by keeping to the proven path and trustworthiness. The same two qualities are required at the top and in the ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"384 asks for honour that does not fall short of valour. 773 says fierceness (தறுகண்) is called great manliness, but helping the foe when something befalls him (ஒன்று உற்றக்கால் ஊராண்மை) is its edge (எஃகு). Parimelazhagar explains 384's phrase with examples of not striking the unequal or the fleeing, which is the restraint 773 states directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:377","type":"same_image","note":"385 ends the king's four skills with வகுத்தல், apportioning what has been guarded. 377 uses the same verb for what lies outside human skill: even those who have piled up crores can enjoy them only in the way the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகை), which Parimelazhagar takes as fate. The pairing sets the king's apportioning inside a larger one he does not control.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:756","type":"develops","note":"385 says the king must create ways for wealth to come (இயற்றல்) without naming them. 756 names three that count as the king's wealth: what falls in unowned, customs dues, and what is taken from defeated enemies (உறுபொருள், உல்கு பொருள், ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருள்). Parimelazhagar's note on 385 gives a similar list of sources.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்), his land is spoken of above others. 566 uses the same word for the reverse case: if he is கடுஞ்சொல்லன் and without kindly regard, his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. One trait is given both its reward and its penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"92 ranks the two: being pleasant-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (ஈதலின் நன்றே). 387 does not rank them but joins them, giving 'with' pleasant words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து), and adds protection. The king's version needs both, where the householder's verse lets speech stand above the gift.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:525","type":"parallel_domain","note":"387 says that for one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து அளிக்க), the world answers to his word. 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layers of kin. The same two means work at two scales: they hold a family around a man and the world around a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:648","type":"agrees","note":"387's result clause is that the world becomes as the king says and intends (தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து); Manakkudavar reads this as the world lying under his command. 648 says the world quickly takes up the work it is told (விரைந்து தொழில் கேட்கும் ஞாலம்) when it finds those who can speak in order and sweetly. Both make pleasant speech the cause of the world's compliance; 387 adds giving and protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:547","type":"develops","note":"388: the king who does முறை and protects is counted as இறை. 547 uses the same two words in a loop: the இறை guards the whole earth, and முறை guards him if he carries it out without fail (அவனை முறை காக்கும்). Justice is what earns the title in 388 and what keeps its holder safe in 547.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"388 has the just king set apart as இறை; 702 says one who knows without doubt what is in another's mind should be regarded as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடு ஒப்பக் கொளல்). Parimelazhagar glosses both with the same phrase, 'though a man by birth'. The book grants this standing for a function performed, justice in one case and insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:160","type":"parallel_domain","note":"389 praises a king who endures (பொறுக்கும்) bitter words. 160 says those who fast in penance are great, but rank after those who endure the harsh words others speak (இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பார்). Bearing hard speech is rated as the higher discipline for the ascetic and as the quality that holds a kingdom for the king.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"389 says the world rests under the parasol of a king who can bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his support people who rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை ஆள்வாரை). Parimelazhagar reads 389's bitter words as those of exactly such rebuking companions, so 447 gives the institution and 389 the temperament it needs. Manakkudavar reads 389 differently, as hearing of others' offences and bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"389 states the king's side: he should bear speech that is bitter to hear. 638 states the minister's side: even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to say what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). The duty to speak does not wait on the king having 389's quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:556","type":"same_image","note":"390 calls the one who has giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people 'வேந்தர்க்கு ஒளி', a light to kings. 556 uses the same word: kings endure through just rule, and without it 'மன்னர்க்கு ஒளி' does not endure. 390 makes செங்கோல் one of four sources of that light; 556 makes it the one without which the light goes out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"390 lists அளி, kindly care, among the four things a king must have. 557 says what its absence is like: as lack of rain is to the earth, so is the king's lack of அளி to the living (துளியின்மை ... வேந்தன் அளியின்மை). An item in a list becomes, in the chapter on the crooked sceptre, something people cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:561","type":"develops","note":"541 gives the steps of justice: examine, favour no one, deliberate, act (ஓர்ந்து ... தேர்ந்து செய்வஃதே முறை). 561 defines the king by the same sequence and adds two things: the punishment must match the offence (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது) and aim at the wrong not recurring (தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்). Chapter 57 thus supplies the measure and purpose that 541 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"541 makes the refusal of கண்ணோட்டம் part of the definition of முறை. 577 says those without கண்ணோட்டம் have no eyes, and those who have eyes are never without it. If 577 is read without exception, the judge of 541 is 'eyeless' in the moment of judging; the conflict disappears only if 577 is limited to dealings outside judgment, as 578 suggests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:557","type":"same_image","note":"542: the whole world lives looking to the sky, and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (வானோக்கி ... கோல் நோக்கி). 557 states the same comparison from the side of failure: what lack of rain is to the earth, the king's lack of kindly care is to living beings. The pair shows the rain comparison is about dependence, so that a failed king is a drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"542 compares subjects' dependence on the sceptre to the world's dependence on rain. In 1192 the woman says the tenderness the loved one gives to the one who loves is like the sky yielding to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால்). The same figure of living by the rain is used for a people before their king and a lover before the beloved, and the word அளி serves for both the king's care (557, 390) and the lover's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"543 makes the sceptre the precondition of scripture and virtue. 19 says that giving and austerity will not last in the wide world if the sky does not give (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும் தங்கா ... வானம் வழங்காதெனின்). Rain in Book 1 and just rule in Book 2 hold the same position: neither is itself virtue, but virtue does not continue without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:387","type":"agrees","note":"544 says the world holds to the king who embraces his people. Parimelazhagar explains the embrace as speaking pleasantly and giving what is needed in hardship, which is what 387 names: to one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க), this world answers. 387 says how the embrace is done; 544 adds that it goes together with the sceptre.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:1025","type":"parallel_domain","note":"544: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும்) of the king who embraces his people (குடிதழீஇ) while wielding the sceptre. 1025: the world gathers round as kin (சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு) the man who raises his family and lives without fault. In both the world's attachment is the return for care of one's own குடி, the word meaning the king's subjects in 544 and a man's family in 1025.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 ties rain to the king's right rule. 55 ties it to a wife's fidelity: she who rises worshipping her husband says 'rain' and it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). In the household and in the kingdom the book makes rain answer to a person keeping to their proper role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 says harvest (விளையுள்) comes with just rule. 239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). Both let the moral state of people show in the fertility of the land; 239 does it for any person, 545 for the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"546's condition is that the கோல் does not bend (கோடாது). 118 praises not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like the கோல், here the balance beam, that first levels itself and then weighs. The same word and the same verb serve for the king's sceptre and the scales, so the straightness asked of the ruler is the impartiality asked of the worthy in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"547: the king guards the earth, and justice guards the king (இறை காக்கும் ... முறை காக்கும்). 57: what can the guard of confinement do? The guard that women keep by their own steadfastness is the chief guard (சிறை காக்கும் காப்பு ... நிறை காக்கும் காப்பே தலை). Both verses, in the same rhyming pattern, say that real protection comes from the guarded person's own conduct and not from outside force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"547 says justice protects the king if he does it without fail. 448 calls the king who has no one to rebuke him 'ஏமரா மன்னன்', a king without a guard, and says he perishes even with no one to ruin him. Together they name what guards a ruler: his own just practice and the people who correct him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"548: a king who is not easy of access (எண்பதத்தான் in the negative, as Parimelazhagar construes it) and does not examine and do justice sinks and perishes. 386: a king easy to see (காட்சிக்கு எளியன்) and not harsh in speech has his land praised. Accessibility is stated as a virtue in chapter 39 and its lack as a cause of ruin in chapter 55.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:448","type":"agrees","note":"548 says the unjust, unapproachable king perishes of himself (தானே கெடும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even without enemies'. 448 says the king with no one to rebuke him perishes though no one sets out to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe a ruler's fall with no outside agent, caused by shutting out complaint in one case and criticism in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"548 and 553 share most of a line: a king who does not examine and do justice (ஓரா / நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்). In 548 the king himself perishes; in 553 he loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The pairing shows the same neglect ruining the ruler and the country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:314","type":"qualifies","note":"314 tells a person how to punish those who hurt him: shame them by doing them good and let it go (அவர் நாண நன்னயம் செய்து விடல்). 549 says that for a king, guarding the people and removing offences (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). The words 'no stain' answer the doubt that punishing goes against virtue, which Parimelazhagar spells out; 549 limits 314's rule to one's own injuries and exempts the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:436","type":"qualifies","note":"549 makes removing offences the king's work. 436 sets an order for it: if he first removes his own fault and then looks at others' (தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றம் காண்கிற்பின்), what fault can attach to a ruler? The right to punish in 549 is, by 436, clear of blame only for a king who has corrected himself first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:1038","type":"same_image","note":"550 compares the king's execution of the cruel to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). 1038, on farming itself, says that after weeding (கட்டபின்) guarding the crop is better than watering it. In the farmer's own chapter weeding is one step and guarding counts for more, which fits 549's order of guarding the people first and punishing second.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"483 asks whether anything called அருவினை exists for one who acts with கருவி and knowing காலம். 631 uses the same three words but keeps அருவினை as a separate item the minister must be good at. The pair shows where the minister's list comes from: tools and timing are what make a hard task doable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"631 defines the minister as one excellent in கருவி, காலம், செய்கை and the அருவினை itself. 675 turns nearly the same list into a rule for anyone acting: பொருள், கருவி, காலம், வினை, இடன், 'ஐந்தும் இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்'. Read together, the minister's defining competence is the checklist of 675, with resources and place added there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:632","b":"k:382","type":"parallel_domain","note":"632 lists the minister's qualities: வன்கண், குடிகாத்தல், கற்றறிதல், ஆள்வினை. 382 lists the king's: அஞ்சாமை, ஈகை, அறிவு, ஊக்கம் (383 adds கல்வி and துணிவுடைமை). Firmness, knowledge and drive appear on both lists, so the minister is described on the king's pattern; Manakkudavar even glosses வன்கண் as அஞ்சாமை. Giving is on the king's list only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:187","type":"parallel_domain","note":"187 says those who cannot make friends with pleasant talk 'கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்', split even their own kin, by divisive speech. 633 uses the same verb, பிரித்தல், for a minister's skill, which Parimelazhagar restricts to detaching an enemy's allies. The same act is a fault at home and a craft in statecraft, and what differs is whose bond is being broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:530","type":"develops","note":"633 names rejoining those who parted as a minister's skill. 530 says how the king should handle such a return: one who left (உழைப்பிரிந்து) and came back for a reason is to be taken in after thought, 'எண்ணிக் கொளல்'. The pair shows that பொருத்தல் is not unconditional welcome; 530 supplies the caution.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:679","type":"develops","note":"633 says a minister must be able to divide (பிரித்தல்), keep his own side (பேணிக் கொளல்) and rejoin those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 679 ranks one such act: 'ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்', bringing the unattached over, is more urgent than doing good to friends. 679 gives a priority among the alliance skills that 633 only lists.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 tells the one acting that nothing is out of reach if he examines with a chosen circle and thinks it over himself: 'தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு'. 634 describes the minister with the same two verbs, தெரிதல் and தேர்ந்து செயல். The pairing shows the two sides of one arrangement: 462 is the ruler's need for such company, 634 is what that company must be able to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:671","type":"develops","note":"634 asks of a minister தெரிதல், தேர்ந்து செயல் and 'ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல்', speaking so that the matter is settled. 671 says 'சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல்': deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and letting that resolve linger is a fault. Parimelazhagar glosses ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் as speaking so that துணிவு arises, which makes the minister's decisive word the point where 671's deliberation closes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:441","type":"develops","note":"441 tells the king to choose the friendship of those who are 'அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார்', 'திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து'. 635 describes the counsel-companion in the same words: 'அறனறிந்து ... திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை'. 441 is the instruction to seek such a person and 635 is the portrait of him, with settled speech (ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்) added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:644","type":"develops","note":"635 asks for one who knows அறன், has full and settled speech, and knows திறன். 644 joins the same three terms in one rule: 'திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை', since no அறன் or பொருள் is beyond that. The next chapter thus takes the speech item of 635 and makes fitted speech itself the place where knowing virtue and knowing method show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:503","type":"qualifies","note":"636 implies that nothing withstands the person who has native subtlety and learning. 503 says that even in those who have studied hard texts and are free of fault (அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார்), close examination rarely finds no blankness: 'இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு'. The praise in 636 concerns outwitting opposing schemes; 503 limits it, since the learned still have gaps.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"426 defines அறிவு as living with the world in the way the world lives: 'உலகத்தோடு அவ்வது உறைவது'. 637 gives the working form of this for one who already knows the method: 'உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்'. Together they treat attention to how the world actually goes as part of knowledge and not a concession against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"638 makes it the duty (கடன்) of the one at the king's side to say what is sound (உறுதி) even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself. 448 gives the stake: a king without those who rebuke, 'இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்', perishes even with no one to ruin him. 448 shows why 638 does not release the minister when the king will not listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:696","type":"qualifies","note":"638 obliges the minister to speak sound counsel regardless of the king's reception. 696 governs the manner: knowing the king's mood and considering the time, say what is needed without what is disliked, so that it is wanted ('வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்'). The duty of 638 stands, and 696 conditions how and when it is discharged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:849","type":"tension","note":"849 says 'காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்': one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up not seeing himself, while the other keeps to what he saw. 638 requires exactly this of a minister when the king 'அறிகொன்று அறியான்'. The conflict holds if 849 is read as general advice not to instruct the conceited; Parimelazhagar reads it as a report that the instructor is reviled, which would leave 638 as a duty carried at that cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:817","type":"same_image","note":"639 and 817 use the same construction and the same kind of figure: 'மந்திரியின் ... எழுபது கோடி உறும்' and 'நட்பின் பகைவரால் பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்'. In both an inflated count of open enemies is set against one false intimate, a plotting minister in 639 and a friend good only for laughter in 817. Parimelazhagar reads உறும் in 639 as 'that many enemies will come', and notes others read it as 'that many times better', which is how 817 is read.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"639 says seventy crore enemies are better than a minister at one's side who plots harm ('பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி'). 882 gives the general rule: do not fear enemies like swords, fear the bond of enemies who are like kin ('கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு'). Parimelazhagar explains 639 on the same ground, that open enemies can be guarded against and the one inside cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:664","type":"agrees","note":"640 says those without திறப்பாடு leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order ('முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்'). 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as said is hard ('சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்'). Both locate failure in the gap between a correct plan and its execution; Parimelazhagar glosses the 'saying' of 664 as stating in order how the task will be done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"640 says sound planning comes to nothing in those who lack the capacity to carry it through. 728 says those who have learned many things are of no use ('பயமிலரே') if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. The same pattern appears in two domains: knowledge or planning is counted worthless without the ability that delivers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:600","type":"same_image","note":"971 says 'ஒளி ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை' and 600 says 'உரம் ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை': the same phrase, abundance of spirit, is called a person's light in one and a person's strength in the other. Each then states the lack: living without it is disgrace (இளி) in 971, and those without it are trees in 600. The repetition shows the chapter on greatness resting on the earlier chapter on ஊக்கம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:972","b":"k:133","type":"agrees","note":"133 says having conduct is what good family is ('ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை') and a lapse becomes low birth. 972 says birth is equal and distinction follows from deeds. Both move rank from descent to what a person does, though 133 keeps the vocabulary of birth and Parimelazhagar reads it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account ('பொருளல்லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்'). 973 says the not-high do not become high by being placed high, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as a seat given by wealth. On that reading 973 limits 751: wealth gets a person regarded, and it does not make them great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"57 says confinement does little and the guard a woman keeps by her own steadfastness is the chief guard ('நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை'). 974 takes this as its comparison: like single-minded women, greatness exists only 'தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின்', if one keeps hold of oneself. 974 carries 57's claim about self-guarding into the Porul chapter on greatness, so that greatness, like கற்பு, cannot be kept for someone from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"974 says greatness exists only as long as one keeps oneself. 965 shows the loss: even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. Together they present greatness as a condition that continues only through conduct and can be forfeited by a small act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:468","type":"develops","note":"975 says the great carry out hard deeds 'ஆற்றின்', in the right way. 468 states the other side with the same word: effort not made in the right way ('ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்') collapses even if many stand guard over it. The pair shows that ஆற்றின் in 975 carries weight: hard deeds done by the wrong route fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:451","type":"agrees","note":"451 says greatness fears small company while smallness takes it as kin ('சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்'). 976 says the small have no thought of attaching themselves to the great. Both describe the same sorting from two ends: the small seek their own kind and do not look upward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:838","type":"parallel_domain","note":"977 says even distinction (சிறப்பு), if it falls to the unworthy, results in acts of excess ('இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்று'). 838 says a fool getting something in hand is like a man already deranged getting drunk. Both say a good thing makes its holder worse when the holder is unfit; 838 says it of possessions and a fool, 977 of distinction and the small. Manakkudavar reads the சிறப்பு of 977 as wealth, which brings the two closer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without பண்பு is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel. 977 says distinction landing on the unworthy produces excess. The logic is the same, that the recipient's quality decides what a gift becomes, but 1000 has the wealth spoiled and useless while 977 has the distinction turn into overbearing action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"125 says bowing is good for everyone and for the wealthy it is a further wealth. 978 says greatness bows and smallness decks itself out in self-admiration. Both attach humility to those who have most reason for pride; Parimelazhagar glosses the பணிதல் of 125 as being without பெருமிதம், the word 979 uses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:439","type":"develops","note":"439 commands 'வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை', never admire yourself, among faults a ruler must avoid. 978 uses the same verb to define smallness: 'அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து'. What 439 forbids as a fault, 978 treats as the mark by which the small are known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness bows always ('பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை'). 963 says bowing is needed in abundance and standing tall is needed in reduced circumstances ('சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு'). Taken plainly, 963 limits the என்றும் of 978; Parimelazhagar reads என்றும் in 978 as 'even when distinction has come', which makes the two verses say the same thing about prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:431","type":"same_image","note":"979 defines greatness as the absence of பெருமிதம் and smallness as riding it to the end. 431 uses the same word with approval: the prosperity of those free of செருக்கு, anger and சிறுமை is 'பெருமித நீர்த்து', which Parimelazhagar glosses as having the quality of eminence. The word means conceit in 979 and dignity in 431, and both verses agree that the person without pride is the one who has real stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:844","type":"parallel_domain","note":"844 defines வெண்மை, poor understanding, as the pride that says 'we have brightness' ('ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு'). 979 defines smallness as being carried away by பெருமிதம். In both a defect is identified with the claim to its opposite: the foolish are those sure of their intelligence, and the small are those sure of their greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:846","type":"same_image","note":"846 and 980 share the words அற்றம், மறை and குற்றம். In 846 covering one's own nakedness is itself poor understanding while one's faults stay uncovered, which Parimelazhagar takes as faults not removed. In 980 greatness covers the அற்றம் of others. The image of covering what is exposed is used for self-deception in one verse and for consideration toward others in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:1079","type":"agrees","note":"1079 says the base, on merely seeing others clothed and fed, are able to find a blemish in them ('வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்'). 980 says smallness tells only faults ('குற்றமே கூறி விடும்'). 1079 adds a cause the Tamil of 980 leaves out: Parimelazhagar reads the fault-finding of the base as coming from being unable to bear others' prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:15","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1091 says the same eyes hold the look that makes him ill and the look that cures it. 15 says rain is both what ruins ('கெடுப்பதூஉம்') and what, as support to the ruined, lifts them again ('எடுப்பதூஉம்'). In both, one source is credited with the harm and with its remedy, once in love and once in the world's subsistence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says brewed liquor gladdens only those who drink it and does not, like love, gladden those who merely see ('கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல்'). 1092 puts a measure on that: the small look her eyes steal ('கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்') is not an even half of love but more ('செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது'). Together they make sight alone a large share of love's pleasure; Parimelazhagar takes the 'half' in 1092 to be half of physical union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1326","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1092 ranks a brief stolen look as more than half of love. 1326 ranks sulking above union ('புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது'), as digesting is sweeter than eating. Both verses place the greater part of love's pleasure in something beside the embrace, one before it and one around it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1093","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093 her look followed by a lowered head is 'water she poured' ('அட்டிய நீர்') into the 'யாப்பு', which Parimelazhagar reads as the crop of love binding the two. 718 says speaking before those able to understand is like pouring water into the bed of something already growing ('வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந்தற்று'). Both use watering for a sign or word given to someone already receptive: the gesture or speech does not start the growth, it feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1096","b":"k:826","type":"same_image","note":"1096: though they speak like those unattached ('உறாஅதவர்போல் சொலினும்'), the words of those without anger are quickly understood ('செறாஅர்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). 826 has the same frame reversed: though they say good things like friends ('நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும்'), the words of the unreconciled are quickly understood ('ஒட்டார்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). The shared wording shows one claim applied in love and in statecraft: a spoken mask, harsh or kind, does not long hide the disposition behind it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1097 lists small harsh words spoken without anger and looks like an enemy's ('செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்') as the signs of those who are close while seeming distant. 824 warns of the opposite pairing: a sweet smile on the face with bitterness inside ('முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா'). In both the surface is the reverse of the heart; the lover reads the mismatch with relief, the Book of Wealth reads it with fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1098","b":"k:1274","type":"agrees","note":"In 1098, when he looks she softens and smiles gently ('பசையினள் பைய நகும்'), and he says there is a certain grace in her then ('உண்டு ஆண்டு ஓர் ஏஎர்'). 1274 says there is something inside the bud of her smile, like scent inside a closed bud ('நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன்று உண்டு'). Both treat her slight smile as holding an unspoken meaning, and both leave that meaning as an unnamed 'something'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1099","b":"k:528","type":"same_image","note":"1099 says looking at each other with the common look, like strangers ('ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்'), is found in lovers. 528 uses the same word for a king: he should not look on all in common ('பொதுநோக்கான்') but by rank, and many will live looking to that. In 528 the undifferentiated look is a failure to distinguish; in 1099 it is put on deliberately by the two people who distinguish each other most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1100: when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words are of no use at all ('வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல'). 705, in the Book of Wealth's chapter of the same name, asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign ('என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்'). Both hold that the eye is the organ for knowing another's mind; 705 says so for court service and 1100 for lovers, where it makes speech redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1100 says that once the eyes agree, words from the mouth serve no purpose. 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading ('கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு') adds womanhood to womanhood. 1280 moves from the fact that eyes can replace speech to praise of the woman who chooses to speak that way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to announce his quick return to those who will be alive then ('வாழ்வார்க்கு உரை'), implying she will not be. 1160 says many consent to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads this as her ironic reply to the friend, meaning she is not one of them. The chapter opens and closes on the same word, 'வாழ்வார்', for the survivors she sets herself apart from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1151 the speaker (the friend for Parimelazhagar, the woman for Manakkudavar) says a promise of quick return is only for those who will survive. In 1263 the woman, after he has gone, says she is still alive through wanting his return ('வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்'). The later speech shows the claim of 1151 as said in the moment: the hope of return that was dismissed there is what keeps her alive here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1295","type":"agrees","note":"1152: once even his look was pleasant, but now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation ('பிரிவஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு'). 1295: her heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting ('பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்'), so its misery never ends. Both say that the prospect of parting takes away the pleasure of being together; 1295 states it as the heart's standing condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:514","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1153: trust is hard ('அரிதரோ தேற்றம்'), because parting occurs at some point even with those who understand ('அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்'). 514: even when people have been trusted after every kind of test ('எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்'), many turn different through the nature of the work. Both say that knowing a person's qualities does not secure their future conduct, one about a lover and one about a king's appointee.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1209","type":"develops","note":"1154 holds up his words 'fear not' and says the fault, if he leaves, is not the believer's. 1209 holds up another of his sayings, 'we are not separate' ('வேறல்லம் என்பார்'), and says her life is ending from thinking on his lack of grace. 1154 is the argument made before the parting; 1209 is the same contrast of word and conduct felt after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1155","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 asks him to say he is not going, and leaves talk of return to those who will survive. 1155 turns to the friend: if you would protect, prevent his parting ('பிரிவோம்பல்'), since once he goes union is hard ('நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு'). Both reject return as a remedy and allow only not-leaving; Parimelazhagar supplies that what is to be protected in 1155 is her life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1156","b":"k:1222","type":"same_image","note":"1156 names him 'வன்கண்ணர்' for announcing his departure. In 1222 she asks the evening, which looks as wretched as she is, whether its partner too is hard like hers ('எம்கேள்போல் வன்கண்ணதோ'). The epithet given conditionally in 1156 has become her settled description of him once he is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1157","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"In 1157 the slipping bangles broadcast ('தூற்றா') his leaving. In 1235 the shoulders that have lost bangles and their old beauty tell of the cruel one's cruelty ('கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்'). Both make her thinning body a speaker that reports on him without her consent; 1235 adds a judgment of him to the bare news of 1157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1158","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1158: living in a town without one's own people is painful, and parting from one who is dear is more painful still ('அதனினும் இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு'). 839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting brings no pain at all ('பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்று இல்'). Read together, both verses use the pain of parting as the measure of what a bond was worth: none for a fool's friendship, more than exile among strangers for a lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1160","b":"k:1198","type":"agrees","note":"1160 says many agree to the hard thing, put away their pain, endure the parting and live on ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads it as irony against the friend's advice. 1198 says outright that none in the world are harder than those who live on ('வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்') without a kind word from the beloved. Both treat surviving separation as something that needs explaining, not as the expected course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 11 gives rain the name அமிழ்தம் because the world's life continues through it. In 1106 the lover says her shoulders must be made of amrita (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்) because his life puts out new shoots at each touch (உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). Both verses award the word for the same reason, that the thing keeps life going, once for the whole world and once in a lover's speech about himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:734","type":"develops","note":"Verse 13 says that if the sky proves false (விண் பொய்ப்பின்), hunger stays and torments the world (உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி), sea-girt though it is. Verse 734 defines a land as one that severe hunger (உறுபசி), unending disease and destructive enemies do not reach. Read together, the first mark of a proper country in the Book of Wealth rests on something no ruler supplies, the rain of chapter 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 says ploughing stops when the rain's bounty declines. Verse 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say they have given up desire cannot hold their state. Verse 14 names one cause that would fold those hands, so the failure of rain reaches as far as the renouncer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:15","b":"k:1102","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 15 says it is rain that ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and rain again that stands by the ruined and lifts them (எடுப்பதூஉம்); Parimelazhagar explains that it ruins by not falling. In 1102 the lover says that for other diseases the medicine is something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). The pattern of one thing being both the harm and the only remedy appears for rain and the world and, in a lover's speech, for the beloved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:16","b":"k:557","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 16 says that unless drops fall from the sky (விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்) not even the tip of green grass can be seen. Verse 557 says that what lack of drops (துளியின்மை) is to the earth, the king's lack of tender regard (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him. Verse 16 supplies the picture that 557's comparison assumes: without the king's அளி, the people are the bare ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 211 asks what the world does for the rain-cloud in return (மாரிமாட்டு என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு) and makes it the model of duty that wants no repayment. Verse 17 says even the long sea loses its nature if the cloud, having taken from it (தடிந்து, as Parimelazhagar reads it), does not give back. On that reading the cloud is not a pure giver: it returns what it drew from the sea, and the sea suffers if it withholds. Manakkudavar takes தடிந்து as lightning flashing, and on his reading the qualification falls away.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 17 pictures the cloud not giving (எழிலி தான் நல்காதாகி விடின்) and the sea itself shrinking as a result. Verse 1010 says the brief want of the renowned rich has the nature of the rain-cloud gone dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). Both use the withholding cloud; 1010 turns it into a figure for a giver's temporary incapacity, with the suggestion that it is as general a loss and as passing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 18 says that if the sky dries up (வானம் வறக்குமேல்), festival and worship for the gods will not go on here (சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது). Verse 560 says that if the guardian does not guard (காவலன் காவான் எனின்), the cows' yield shrinks and those of the six duties forget their book (நூல் மறப்பர்). Both say religious practice has a material precondition that can fail; chapter 2 names the rain and chapter 56 names the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 19 says giving and austerity (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும்) will not stay in the world if the sky does not give. Verse 559 says that if the king acts with justice bent (முறைகோடி), the sky will not pour in season (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). Chapter 2 makes virtue depend on rain; 559 makes the rain depend in turn on a human virtue, the ruler's justice, so the dependence runs both ways.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 19 says austerity (தவம்) cannot last without rain. Verse 1036 gives the step in between: if the ploughmen's hands fold, those who say they have left desire have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's practice rests on the farmer's food, and the farmer's on the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 20 ends the chapter by saying nothing in the world, for anyone, holds without the sky. Verse 55 says that for the wife who rises worshipping her husband, it rains when she says 'rain' (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Three chapters after rain is made the condition of everything, the rain is said to answer to one person's steadfastness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:542","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 20 says no one's world can do without water (நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகு), and that ஒழுக்கு cannot exist without the sky. Parimelazhagar takes ஒழுக்கு as the water's unbroken flow; Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take it as ordered conduct. Verse 542 states the first half as a given (வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம்) and sets the people's dependence on the sceptre beside it. On either reading of ஒழுக்கு, 542 treats the dependence 20 argues for as the standard case of something one cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:48","type":"tension","note":"Verse 21 says the settled view of the texts (பனுவல் துணிவு) sets the greatness of those who have renounced within their discipline (ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை) highest among excellent things. Verse 48 says the household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). If 48 is read as ranking the householder above the ascetic, as its plain wording allows, the two chapters give the first place to different lives; if 48 is read as praising only the householder's endurance, they do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:533","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 21 rests its claim on பனுவல் துணிவு, the settled conclusion of the texts. Verse 533 uses the same appeal: that the heedless have no fame is the conclusion of authors of every school (எப்பால் நூலோர்க்கும் துணிவு). These are two places where the Kural, which usually argues from observation, cites the agreement of the books as its ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:22","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 22 says that to state the measure of the greatness of renouncers (துறந்தார்) is like counting the dead of the world (இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண்டற்று). Verse 310 rhymes the same two words: those who exceed in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). In 22 the dead supply only a number too large to count; in 310 the same pairing sets renouncers and the dead as opposite ends, and extends the renouncer's standing to anyone who gives up anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Verse 23 says the greatness of those who have discerned the two (இருமை வகைதெரிந்து) and taken on அறம் here stands highest in the world; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read the two as birth and release and the அறம் as renunciation. Verse 49 says that what is called அறம் is household life (அறன் எனப்பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை). On the commentators' reading of 23 the two verses attach the word அறம், and the highest standing, to different lives. Kalaignar reads 23 as choosing good over bad in general, and on that reading there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 24 calls the one who guards the five senses a seed (வித்து) for the ground called வரன், which Parimelazhagar takes as release. Verse 361 calls craving the seed (வித்து) that bears unceasing birth for all beings. The book uses the seed for both outcomes: restraint of the senses is sown toward release, and craving is what sprouts as rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:664","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 26 divides people by whether they can do what is hard to do. Verse 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as one has said is hard (அரியவாம் சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்). The chapter on firmness in action applies the renouncer's standard to statecraft and names what is hard in a deed: carrying it out as announced.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:354","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 27 says the world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five (ஐந்தின் வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு): taste, light, touch, sound and smell. Verse 354 says that even having gained the fivefold awareness (ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும்) is of no benefit to those without perception of the real (மெய்யுணர்வு). Verse 354 sets a limit on 27: command of the five is not the end, and without true perception it yields nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 27 lists the five sense objects (சுவை ஒளி ஊறு ஓசை நாற்றம்) and says the world belongs to the one who discerns them. In 1101 the lover lists the same five as acts, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்), and says they are all found in this one woman (ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள). The same inventory serves the renouncer as a field to be understood and the lover as a field of enjoyment; the second is a speech in the moment and not a counter-teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:28","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 28 says the greatness of people whose word is full (நிறைமொழி மாந்தர்) is shown on earth by their மறைமொழி; Parimelazhagar explains நிறைமொழி as speech that produces its result whether spoken in grace or in anger. Verse 55 says that when the wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Both give a person's word power over the world as the proof of their standing, for the renouncer through austerity and for the wife through steadfastness at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:310","type":"tension","note":"Verse 29 speaks of the anger (வெகுளி) of those who stand on the hill of good qualities; on Parimelazhagar's reading they are sages whose anger, though it lasts a moment, cannot be warded off by its target. Verse 310 says those who have renounced anger are the equals of renouncers (சினத்தைத் துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை), which makes freedom from anger the renouncer's mark. On Parimelazhagar's reading the book's model renouncer still gets angry, and dangerously. Kalaignar reads 29 as saying their anger does not stay even a moment, and on that reading the two verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"41 makes the householder the support of the three, read by Parimelazhagar as the other orders, ascetics included. 263 asks, with கொல், whether 'the others' (மற்றையவர்கள்) have forgotten தவம் because they want to provide for the renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி); Parimelazhagar glosses 'the others' as householders. The Thavam chapter thus looks at the same arrangement from the ascetic side: the householder's support of renouncers is given as the reason he does no austerity himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:1036","type":"parallel_domain","note":"41 calls the householder the துணை who stands on the good path (நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற) for 'the three' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்), whom Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take as the other three orders of life (Kalaignar instead reads parents, spouse and children). 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say 'we have let go of desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) have no standing (நிலை). On the older commentators' reading both verses make the renouncer's state depend on someone who stays in the world and produces: the householder in Book 1, the farmer in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"41 names the householder the support of 'the three of proper nature' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்); 42 repeats the frame இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை and adds three more: துறந்தார், துவ்வாதவர், இறந்தார். Parimelazhagar takes these as the abandoned, the poor and those who died with no one of their own, so the pair moves from the recognised orders to people with no claim on anyone. Pope and Kalaignar read துறந்தார் as ascetics, which would make 42 overlap 41 rather than extend it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"42 lists துவ்வாதவர், those who have nothing to eat or enjoy, among the people the householder supports. 226 says that ending the destroying hunger of those without means (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where a person who has gained wealth stores it (பொருள் வைப்புழி). 226 turns the duty stated in 42 into a claim about what wealth is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"43 lists five quarters to be tended (ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல்): ancestors, gods, guests (விருந்து), kin and oneself, and calls this the chief thing (தலை). 81 says that all of staying, guarding one's goods and living at home (இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வதெல்லாம்) is for the sake of tending guests and doing them good (விருந்தோம்பி வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு). One of the five items of 43 is taken up, with the same verb ஓம்பு, and made the purpose of the whole household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:524","type":"parallel_domain","note":"43 puts kin (ஒக்கல்) among the five the householder must tend. 524, in the chapters on kingship, says that living so as to be surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்) is the fruit of having gained wealth. The household duty of Book 1 reappears in Book 2 as the point of a ruler's prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:112","type":"parallel_domain","note":"44 promises an unfailing line (வழி) to the life that fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food; Parimelazhagar reads the fear of blame as applying to how the wealth is earned. 112 says the prosperity of the person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). In both, wealth held without wrong is what lasts into the next generation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"44 says a life that fears blame and has பாத்தூண், food shared out, never sees its line fail (வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்). 227 uses the same compound: the one habituated to shared food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை) is hardly touched by the disease called hunger. Both attach a protective result to the same practice, one for the descendants and one for the sharer himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"44 names பாத்தூண், shared food, as the mark of the householder's life. In 1107 the lover says the embrace of the woman is like staying in one's own home and eating one's own after sharing it (தம்மில் இருந்து தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The Book of Love takes the householder's sharing meal as a standard of pleasure, where 44 gives it as a duty with a reward; the comparison is the speaker's, not a teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:45","b":"k:75","type":"develops","note":"45 says that if household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), that is its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்). 75 says the distinction reached by those who have had joy in this world is, 'they say', the result of a way of life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes this as household life lived with love, rewarded with heaven. 75 spells out the பயன் that 45 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:348","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one who conducts household life in the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the outside way as renunciation. 348 says those who have renounced completely (தீரத் துறந்தார்) have reached the head, and the rest (மற்றையவர்) are confused and caught in the net. If புறத்தாறு is renunciation and the question in 46 is rhetorical, 46 denies the gain that 348 reserves for renouncers alone; if புறத்தாறு means only ways outside virtue, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:47","b":"k:266","type":"tension","note":"47 says the one who lives household life according to its nature is first among all who strive (முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை). 266 says those who do தவம் are doing their own work and the rest (மற்றல்லார்), caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார்). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 47, where the strivers are those working to leave the senses, the householder is ranked above ascetics, while 266 counts everyone but the ascetic as wasting effort. Manakkudavar reads முயல்வார் as those who strive for wealth, and on that reading the two do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"48 says a household life that keeps others on their path (ஆற்றின் ஒழுக்கி) and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove that hunger (மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்). Both rank the one who provides above the one who abstains, and both do it by granting the ascetic's own virtue, endurance, and awarding more of it to the provider.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:44","type":"qualifies","note":"44 opens with பழியஞ்சி: the household life that fears blame and shares its food keeps its line. 49 calls household life virtue itself, then adds that it is good if there is nothing others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). On Manakkudavar's reading, where அஃதும் still refers to household life, 49 makes absence of blame the condition on the chapter's whole praise of the householder, which 44 had already built into its description.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"49 says what is called virtue is household life, and 'that too' (அஃதும்) is good if it is not something others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). Parimelazhagar takes அஃதும் as renunciation and the blame as false conduct in ascetics (Manakkudavar keeps it on household life). 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one drops what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on false ascetics works out the condition 49 states in passing: the renouncer's standing rests on being free of blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:413","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 places the person who lives rightly on earth (வையத்துள்) among the gods of the sky. 413 says those who have listening as food for the ear are, though on earth (நிலத்து), equal to the gods who are fed by oblations. Both verses set 'on earth' against the gods and say a human excellence closes the gap, right living in one case and learning by listening in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:52","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"52 says that if excellence of the home (மனைமாட்சி) is not in the wife, the life is nothing, whatever excellence it has (எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்). 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has (எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும்), it is as nothing in those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The two share the phrase and the argument: the outward assets of a home or a fortress count for nothing without the quality of the person inside who runs it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:53","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth (இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால்) and what is there if she does not (உள்ளதென் ... மாணாக் கடை). 430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). The same two-sided formula, one possession that makes all others count or not count, is applied to a wife's character in the home and to understanding in a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:54","b":"k:988","type":"same_image","note":"54 asks what is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு (கற்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). 988 says poverty is no disgrace to a person, if he has the firmness called சால்பு (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). The second line is the same but for one word, so the book describes a woman's fidelity and a worthy person's character with the same term, திண்மை, a firmness that outweighs outward circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:55","b":"k:2","type":"tension","note":"55 praises a wife who does not worship a god (தெய்வம் தொழாஅள்) and worships her husband, and credits her with power over the rain. 2 asks what use learning is if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்), with the same verb in the same negative form. Read literally, 55 commends for the wife the not-worshipping that the first chapter treats as making a person's attainments useless. Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 'other gods' (பிற தெய்வம்) and making the husband her god; the tension depends on taking தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் at face value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"56 defines the woman as one who guards herself (தற்காத்து), cares for her husband and guards the good name (சொற்காத்து) without flagging. 57 asks what guarding by confinement (சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) can do, and says the guard women keep by their own நிறை is the chief one. 57 explains why 56 makes her the subject of the verb 'guard': the guarding that counts cannot be done by anyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:59","type":"develops","note":"56 includes guarding the well-founded word, the couple's good name (தகைசான்ற சொற்காத்து), in its definition of the wife. 59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல் இலோர்க்கு) cannot walk with a proud gait before those who deride them. Parimelazhagar connects them directly, saying 59 states the fault that follows when that good name is not kept: the husband's standing in public depends on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:58","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"58 says women who 'obtain the one who obtained them' (பெற்றாற் பெறின்) gain great distinction in the world where the gods live (புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு); Parimelazhagar supplies 'honour' and reads it as wives who revere their husbands. 50 says the man who lives on earth as he should is placed among the gods of the sky. The two adjacent chapters end on the same reward, one for the householder and one for the wife, each for fulfilling the household role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:237","type":"develops","note":"59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல்) have no proud gait before those who deride them (இகழ்வார்முன்). 237 asks why those who cannot live with renown (புகழ்பட வாழாதார்) resent those who deride them (இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்) and not themselves. Both set புகழ் against இகழ்வார்: 59 says that without renown one has no answer to scorners, and 237 adds that the scorn is then one's own doing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:1014","type":"same_image","note":"59 says that without a home that seeks renown there is no walking with a bull's (Parimelazhagar: lion's) proud gait, ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை. 1014 says shame is an ornament to the worthy, and without it their பீடு நடை is a disease. The proud walk appears twice as something that must be earned by an inner or domestic quality, the household's good name in one verse and a sense of shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:60","b":"k:61","type":"develops","note":"60 says the excellence of the home is called its blessing (மங்கலம்) and the gaining of good children (நன்மக்கட் பேறு) its fine ornament. 61, opening the next chapter, says that of all things to be gained we know none like the gaining of children who know what should be known (அறிவறிந்த மக்கட்பேறு). 61 picks up the word மக்கட்பேறு from 60 and specifies what makes the children 'good': their understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:162","type":"tension","note":"Verse 61 says that among all things gained (பெறுமவற்றுள்) 'we' know of nothing to set beside knowing children. Verse 162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை) being free of envy. The two top rankings conflict only if each superlative is read literally as a ranking across all gains; read as each chapter's idiom of praise they do not.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:300","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses rank their subject first by the author's own witness in the first person plural: 61 has யாமறிவது இல்லை ('we know of none') for knowing children, 300 has யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை ('among what we have seen as true there is none') for truthfulness. The pairing shows the same rare device, the poet's 'we', used to vouch for a household good and for a virtue of speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:44","type":"develops","note":"Verse 44 says that if a household life fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food, its line (வழி) never fails. Verse 62 says that if one gets children in whom blame does not grow (பழிபிறங்கா), no evil touches one for seven births. In 44 the parent's freedom from blame secures the descendants; in 62 the descendants' freedom from blame secures the parent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 says evils will not touch a person in all seven births (எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா) if they get children of character who bring no blame. Verse 398 says the learning gained in one birth is a protection in seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both name a good of this life whose protection outlasts it by seven births: in one case one's children, in the other one's own learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 has blameless children keep evil away through seven births (எழுபிறப்பும்). Verse 835 has the fool, in one birth's doing (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire (அளறு) he sinks in for seven (எழுமையும்). The same seven-birth reckoning is applied in the opposite direction, once to a safeguard and once to a ruin made by oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:114","type":"develops","note":"Verse 63 says people call their children their wealth (தம்பொருள் என்ப தம் மக்கள்), and ties that wealth to deeds (வினையான் வரும்). Verse 114 says whether people are worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம், what they leave behind, which Parimelazhagar takes as their offspring and Kalaignar as the name they leave. On Parimelazhagar's reading the pair makes children both a person's property and the evidence by which that person is judged.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:400","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 63 reports the saying that one's children are one's wealth (தம்பொருள்). Verse 400 says learning is the imperishable wealth (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்) and that other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல). Both move the word for property from goods to something else, but 400 denies the title to everything other than learning, while 63 only reports what people say.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:1103","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 64 sets a child's messy gruel above amrita. In 1103 the lover asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one (தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு) can be sweeter (இனிதுகொல்) than sleeping on the soft arms of the one he loves; Parimelazhagar has him say this to a companion who reproved him. The same move, a heavenly standard of sweetness outdone by an intimate bodily one, appears in the parent's voice in Book 1 and in a lover's speech in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:65","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 65 names two sense pleasures a child gives: touching the child's body is joy to the body (உடற்கின்பம்) and hearing its words is joy to the ear (இன்பம் செவிக்கு). In 1101 the lover says the five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும்), all have their objects in one woman. Both list pleasures sense by sense and locate them in a single loved person, a child in one and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:66","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 66 says only those who have not heard their own children's prattle call the flute (குழல்) and the lute sweet. In 1228 the woman left alone says the herdsman's flute (ஆயன் குழல்) is now a messenger of the burning evening and a weapon that kills; Parimelazhagar notes that it had been sweet to her before. In both the flute stands for accepted sweetness, and in both that sweetness is made to depend on the hearer's situation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:715","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 67 makes it the father's gift that the son sits ahead in the assembly (அவையத்து முந்தி இருப்ப). Verse 715 says the best of good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared root முந்து shows the limit: standing first in the assembly is the goal, but being first to speak before one's seniors is what the book warns against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:722","type":"develops","note":"Verse 67 says a father's good to his son is to make him sit foremost in the assembly; Parimelazhagar takes this as making him learned beyond the learned there. Verse 722 says those called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்) are those who can make what they learned go home before the learned. 722 supplies what the front seat of 67 requires in practice: learning that can be delivered in front of that assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:68","b":"k:399","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 68 says one's children's understanding (அறிவுடைமை) is sweet to all beings on the great earth (மன்னுயிர்க்கெல்லாம் இனிது); Parimelazhagar reads தம்மின் as 'more than to oneself', while Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as children who surpass their parents. Verse 399 says the learned love learning more on seeing that what delights them delights the world (தாமின்புறுவது உலகின்புறக் கண்டு). Both treat knowledge as a pleasure that is not private: the parent's pride in 68 and the scholar's delight in 399 are each measured against the world's share in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:69","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 69 has the mother's joy grow when she hears others call her son worthy (சான்றோன் எனக் கேட்ட தாய்). Verse 1047 says one in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). In both the mother's regard follows the son's standing, rising with his name in 69 and withdrawn in 1047.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:70","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"Verse 70 has onlookers explain a good son by asking what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான்). Verse 265 says தவம் is striven for here because whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). The onlookers' question in 70 assumes the doctrine 265 states, and it applies that doctrine to a son.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1180","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 71 says tears raise a public noise (பூசல் தரும்) about the love inside. In 1180 the woman says it is not hard for the town to get at the secret (மறை) of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the eyes make public what the heart holds, and both describe this as sound: a clamour in 71, a drum in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:220","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 72 says those who have love belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). Verse 220 says that if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth buying even by selling oneself (விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது). Both carry giving past one's goods to one's own person, 72 as a description of love and 220 as the price of duty to the community.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"Verse 72 says the loveless belong to themselves in everything (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others (பிறர்க்கு) even in their bones. Verse 1009 says wealth piled up by putting love aside (அன்பொரீஇ), denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). 1009 gives the outcome of the stance in 72: what the loveless keep for themselves ends with பிறர் anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"Verse 73 says the life's connection with the bone (என்போடு) goes with love. Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter's last verse gives the negative of 73: take away the love and what remains of the union is the bone alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 73 treats the joining of life and body (என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) as something fitted to love. Verse 338 says the friendship between body and life (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு) is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone. The pairing sets a limit on 73: the bond that exists for love's sake is, in the chapter on impermanence, one the life will abandon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:1122","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 73 says the tie of the life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is, they say, a matter of being joined with love (அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes embodiment as the result of a course meant for joining with love. In 1122 the lover says the bond between him and the woman is what the bond between body and life is (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்ன). 73 explains the union of body and life by love, and the lover in 1122 measures his love by that union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:757","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 74 says love gives birth (ஈனும்) to eagerness, and that gives birth to friendship, an unsought distinction (நாடாச் சிறப்பு). Verse 757 calls அருள் the child born of love (அன்பீன் குழவி), raised by the foster-mother wealth. Both use the figure of birth for what comes out of அன்பு, and together they give love two offspring, friendship in the home and grace toward all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:785","type":"develops","note":"Verse 74 derives friendship (நண்பு) from love by way of eagerness (ஆர்வம்) and calls it a distinction that is not sought (நாடா). Verse 785 says meeting and long acquaintance are not needed, because feeling itself (உணர்ச்சிதான்) gives the claim of friendship. The friendship chapter of Book 2 keeps the account given in Book 1: friendship comes from an inner disposition and not from arrangement or contact.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:75","b":"k:39","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes through virtue is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and everything else lies outside it. Verse 75 traces the joy and distinction people reach in the world to a life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு). Set side by side, 75 adds love to the source that 39 confines to அறம், which agrees with verse 45's coupling of the two.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:862","type":"develops","note":"Verse 76 says the ignorant hold that love supports only virtue, while it is the help (துணை) for மறம் as well; Kalaignar reads மறம் as valour, Parimelazhagar as the hostility that love removes. Verse 862 asks how a man without love (அன்பிலன்), without allies and without strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. On Kalaignar's reading 862 is the case in point: in Book 2 love is listed first among the things a fighter needs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:80","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 compares the loveless to a thing without bones (என்பிலது) dried by the sun; Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as a worm. Verse 80 says the body of those without love is bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter uses bone twice for the loveless in opposite ways, once as what they lack and once as all they are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 says அறம் scorches what has no love as the sun scorches what has no bones (வெயில்போலக் காயுமே). Verse 204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both அறம் is an agent that acts against a person, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in both places as அறக்கடவுள், the god of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:576","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 78 likens the loveless life to a withered tree on hard ground. Verse 576 says those who have eyes and no kindly regard are like trees fixed in the soil (மண்ணோடியைந்த மரத்தனையர்). In both a person without the inward quality, அன்பு in one and கண்ணோட்டம் in the other, is reduced to a tree, alive in form and without response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 78 says a life with no love within is like a dried tree putting out shoots on hard ground (வற்றல் மரம் தளிர்த்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar takes as something that does not happen. In 1106 the lover says her touch makes his life sprout each time they meet (உறுதோறு உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). The verb தளிர் carries both: life sprouts where love is, and 78 uses the same sprouting to mark what a loveless life cannot do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:407","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 says the outer limbs are of no use without the inner limb, love. Verse 407 says the fine looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both set a complete exterior against a missing interior, love in Book 1 and understanding in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 asks what all the outer parts can do (புறத்துறுப்பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும்) for those who lack love, the inner part (அகத்துறுப்பு). Verse 574 asks what eyes do (எவன் செய்யும்), beyond seeming to be in the face, when they have no கண்ணோட்டம். The same question in the same words is put to the whole body in 79 and to one organ in 574: a part is judged by the disposition it serves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது). Verse 255 says உயிர்நிலை lies in not eating flesh (உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை); Parimelazhagar reads this as life's staying in the body resting on that virtue. The rare word உயிர்நிலை is used in both to make a moral quality, love or not eating flesh, the condition of being alive at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:1020","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 80 says that for those without love the body is only bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). Verse 1020 says the movements of those with no shame within (நாண் அகத்தில்லார்) are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string (மரப்பாவை ... உயிர்மருட்டியற்று). Both describe a moving body that lacks an inward quality as something without life in it, a frame of bone in one and a puppet in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:82","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"82 says eating alone while the guest is outside (விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல்) is not to be desired even if the food were the medicine against death. 229 says eating by oneself alone (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) to fill up one's store is more bitter than begging. Both judge the solitary meal itself, one by setting it against the best imaginable food and the other against the worst condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"83 says the life of one who tends the arriving guest every day (வைகலும் ஓம்புவான்) does not suffer and go to ruin (பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று). 227 says the disease called hunger rarely touches one accustomed to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both promise that habitual sharing of food protects the sharer from want, once for the host of guests and once for the giver to the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:480","type":"qualifies","note":"83 says daily hospitality never brings a household to ruin (பாழ்படுதல் இன்று), which Parimelazhagar takes to mean the host's goods are not used up. 480 says helpfulness that does not weigh the extent of what one has (உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புரவாண்மை) quickly ends one's wealth (வல்லைக் கெடும்). The Book of Wealth thus attaches a condition of measure to the kind of promise 83 makes without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"84 has the goddess of fortune (செய்யாள்) living in the home of the glad-faced host. 617 says she of the lotus (தாமரையினாள்) lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்), and her dark sister in sloth. The same figure is given two different dwellings, generosity in the Book of Virtue and exertion in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:545","type":"parallel_domain","note":"85 implies that the field of the host who feeds guests first yields without sowing. 545 says rain and harvest gather together (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as he should. In both, right conduct is said to make the land fruitful, at the scale of one household's field and at the scale of a kingdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:1039","type":"tension","note":"85 asks whether one who feeds guests and eats what is left (விருந்தோம்பி மிச்சில் மிசைவான்) need even sow his field (வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ); Parimelazhagar supplies that it will bear by itself. 1039 says that if the owner stays away and does not go to it (செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின்), the land sulks like a wife. The conflict holds only if 85 is taken literally, as Parimelazhagar does, and not as a figure of praise; Kalaignar reads 85 quite differently, as asking whether such a host would hold back even his seed-grain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:86","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"86 says one who tends the departing guest and watches for the next (வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்) will be a good guest to those in the sky (வானத்தவர்க்கு). 50 says one who lives on earth as one should (வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன்) will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வான்உறையும் தெய்வத்துள்). 86 names one specific practice that earns what 50 promises the householder in general, and turns it into a reciprocity: the host becomes the guest. (This edition prints நல்வருந்து in 86 and உநற்யும் in 50; both are typos.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:221","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 87 scales the fruit of hospitality to the guest's worth (விருந்தின் துணைத்துணை). 221 says giving to those who have nothing (வறியார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈவதே ஈகை) is the only real giving, and all the rest is like a loan expecting return. One verse values the gift by the receiver's merit and the other by the receiver's need; the conflict depends on reading துணை in 87 as merit and on treating feeding a guest and giving as the same kind of act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:259","type":"same_image","note":"87 calls hospitality a வேள்வி (sacrifice) whose fruit has no fixed measure; Parimelazhagar notes it is one of the five great sacrifices. 259 says not taking a life and eating it is better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices (அவிசொரிந்து ஆயிரம் வேட்டலின்). In both verses the ritual word is used to rank an ethical practice: one is named a sacrifice, the other is set above a thousand of them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"88 says those who do not take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods painfully (பரிந்தோம்பி) and end by saying 'we have no support' (பற்றற்றேம்). 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who store what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) do not know the joy of giving. Both describe the same sequence of hoarding and then loss, and both set it against a use of wealth the hoarder never tried.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"88 pictures people guarding their goods with pain (பரிந்தோம்பி) and still losing them. 376 says what is not allotted to one will not stay even if guarded with pain (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if poured away (சொரியினும் போகா தம). The fate chapter gives a general ground for 88's observation: anxious guarding does not decide whether wealth stays, so spending it on guests risks nothing that guarding would have saved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:841","type":"develops","note":"89 says poverty in the midst of wealth is the folly (மடமை) of not caring for guests. 841 says lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and that the world does not count other lack as lack. 841 states as a general principle what 89 shows in one case: the real want is a failure of understanding, whatever one owns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:93","type":"develops","note":"90 gives the negative case: a face that turns (முகந்திரிந்து) and looks makes the guest wilt. 93 gives the positive rule in nearly the same terms: with a glad face, looking sweetly (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து இனிது நோக்கி), then sweet words from the heart. The last verse of the hospitality chapter and the third of the next share the face-and-look sequence; Parimelazhagar on 90 also lists the glad face, the sweet word and the good deed as successive stages of welcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:707","type":"develops","note":"90 says a guest wilts when looked at with an altered face (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). 707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 707 explains why 90's guest is hurt before a word is spoken: the face has already reported the host's mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:358","type":"same_image","note":"91 says sweet words are the speech of those who have seen the செம்பொருள் (செம்பொருள் கண்டார் வாய்ச்சொல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses here as those who know virtue. 358 defines understanding as seeing the செம்பொருள் so that the folly of birth departs (செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). The same phrase links ordinary kind speech to the insight the renunciation chapters describe, though the commentator gives the word a more modest sense in 91.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"92 says being sweet-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே). 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) is above those who do austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). Twice the book ranks a quality of speech above material giving, once for kindness and once for truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"93 says virtue lies in sweet words that come from within (அகத்தானாம் இன்சொலினதே அறம்), after a glad face and a kind look. 34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); Parimelazhagar counts speech and outward guise among that rest. 93's word அகத்தானாம் is what keeps sweet speech on the right side of 34's line: it counts as virtue because it issues from the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"93 requires the glad face and the sweet word to come from within (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து ... அகத்தானாம்). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is (முகநக ... நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). The same முகம்/அகம் pairing sets the test of genuineness for kind speech and for friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:824","type":"qualifies","note":"93 places virtue in a glad face followed by sweet words from within (அகத்தானாம்). 824 warns that deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை) are to be feared. 824 describes the counterfeit of 93's first half, so the outward signs 93 begins with cannot by themselves be taken as proof of the inner part.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"94 asserts that sweet speakers do not fall into want, without saying how. 525 says one who can give and speak sweetly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layer on layer of kin (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). 525 supplies a worldly mechanism for 94's promise: sweet speech gathers people around one, and a person so surrounded is not left destitute.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"94 says want that brings suffering (துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை) does not come to those whose sweet speech gives joy to everyone. 566 says that if a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. The two verses state the same link between speech and prosperity from opposite sides, for anyone in the Book of Virtue and for a king in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"95 makes humility (பணிவு) the true ornament (அணி). 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம்) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). The same two roots are set against each other in 978: the small person's self-adornment is the false ornament, and the bowing 95 praises is what the great actually wear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and other things are not (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1014 asks whether shame is not the ornament of the worthy (அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு), and 575 names kindly regard as the eye's ornament. The exclusive wording of 95 is therefore the chapter's emphasis: other chapters award the same title to other inner qualities, while agreeing with 95 (as Parimelazhagar reads it) that bodily ornaments are not meant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, the last being painful speech (இன்னாச்சொல்). 96 says virtue grows and its opposite wears away when one speaks good things sweetly (அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும்). 35 only requires avoiding harsh words; 96 adds that the positive practice of sweet, well-chosen speech actively increases virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:128","type":"agrees","note":"96 says that if one seeks out good things and says them sweetly (நல்லவை நாடி இனிய சொலின்), what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows. 128 says that if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்), what was good turns out not good. Together they give speech power over one's stock of virtue in both directions: good words build it and a single bad word undoes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:194","type":"same_image","note":"97 says speech that yields benefit and does not part from pleasantness (பயன்ஈன்று பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்) produces நயன் and brings forth good (நன்றி). 194 says speech with no benefit and no pleasantness (பயன்சாராப் பண்பில்சொல்) is without நயன் and removes one from good (நன்மையின் நீக்கும்). The four terms பயன், பண்பு, நயன் and நன்மை recur in the negative, so 194 is 97 inverted; Parimelazhagar glosses பண்பு as sweetness and நயன் as justice in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:197","type":"qualifies","note":"97 asks for speech that has both benefit (பயன்) and pleasantness (பண்பு), and credits it with producing நயன். 197 says the worthy may say what lacks நயன் if they must, but should not say what lacks பயன் (நயனில சொல்லினும் சொல்லுக ... பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 197 thus ranks the qualities 97 holds together: use is the one that cannot be given up. Parimelazhagar takes நயன் in 197 as justice; Subramuniyaswami takes it as pleasantness, which makes the limit on chapter 10 more direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:291","type":"parallel_domain","note":"98 says sweet speech that is clear of smallness (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய இன்சொல்) gives joy in the next life and this one; Parimelazhagar explains சிறுமை as causing pain to others by what the words mean. 291 defines truth as speech containing no harm at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). Sweetness and truthfulness are both defined by the same test, the absence of harm to others, and not by sound or by literal accuracy. (This edition prints சிறுமையுவு in 98, a typo for சிறுமையுள்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:911","type":"qualifies","note":"98 says sweet speech free of smallness gives joy (இன்சொல் ... இன்பம் தரும்) here and hereafter. 911 says the sweet speech of women who desire wealth and not the man brings harm (இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்). The two predicates answer each other, and the condition in 98 (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய) marks the difference: what 98 promises holds only for sweet words that are not serving a mean purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:318","type":"parallel_domain","note":"99 asks why one who sees how sweetly sweet words yield (இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான்) would use harsh words (எவன்கொலோ வன்சொல் வழங்குவது); Parimelazhagar takes it as one who has felt the pleasure of others' kind words to himself. 318 asks why one who knows what is painful to his own life does painful things to other lives (என்கொலோ மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்). The two rhetorical questions have the same form and the same argument from one's own experience to the treatment of others, applied to words in 99 and to harm in general in 318. (The first line of 318 is misprinted in this edition, but the verse is recoverable.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"99 asks what could lead someone who knows the good that sweet words do to use harsh ones (வன்சொல்). 784 says friendship is not for laughing together but for going ahead to rebuke when a friend oversteps (மிகுதிக்கண் மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). The friendship chapter supplies a case where sharp words have a purpose, so 99's question is best read as aimed at needless harshness and not at every hard word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:831","type":"parallel_domain","note":"100 pictures someone who has ripe fruit at hand and takes the unripe: unpleasant words chosen while pleasant ones exist (இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்). 831 defines folly as taking hold of harm and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). Harsh speech in 100 is an instance of exactly the choice 831 names as folly, which fits the chapter's closing argument that harsh words are a bad bargain for the speaker as well as wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:1306","type":"same_image","note":"100 says speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are available is like taking the unripe fruit when the ripe is there (கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந்தற்று). 1306, a speech in the Book of Love, says that love without serious and light quarrels is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). The ripe/unripe pair is used differently: in 100 the ripe fruit is simply the right choice, while the lover treats fruit that is too ripe and fruit that is too raw as two ways of missing the right point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:211","type":"agrees","note":"101 says that for help given without any prior help (செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவி) even earth and heaven are hardly a match (ஆற்றல் அரிது). 211 says obligation wants no return (கைம்மாறு வேண்டா) and asks what the world does for the rain in exchange (என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ). The same verb ஆற்று marks both: one verse looks from the receiver who cannot repay, the other from the giver who does not ask to be repaid.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with செய்யாமல், 'without (the other) having done anything first'. 101 applies it to help: unprompted help is beyond repayment. 313 applies it to hatred: even toward those who hate you though you did nothing (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), doing hurt brings sorrow with no escape. Read together, the unprovoked good creates a boundless debt while the unprovoked wrong still gives no licence to retaliate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:102","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"102 says help done at the time of need (காலத்தினால் செய்த நன்றி), though small, is greater than the earth. 788 makes that timeliness the definition of friendship: it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand going to a slipping garment. 102 values the help by its moment; 788 says what kind of helper acts in that moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:813","type":"parallel_domain","note":"103 praises help from those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்), calling its good greater than the sea. 813 uses the same weighing verb for the opposite case: friends who weigh what they will get (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) are put level with thieves. The pair shows the book judging help and friendship by one test, whether the giver was calculating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:109","type":"develops","note":"104 says a millet of good is taken as a palmyra by those who know its worth. 109 says even harm like killing (கொன்றன்ன இன்னா) vanishes when one thinks of a single good the person did (ஒன்று நன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்). Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 109: because the small good is held as large, that one good can cancel the later harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses use the phrase மாசற்றார் கேண்மை, the bond of the stainless: 106 says do not forget it (மறவற்க), 800 says keep close to it (மருவுக). Each then adds a second command about a kind of friend, 106 never to abandon those who were a support in distress, 800 to drop the unsuitable (ஒப்பிலார்) even at a cost. The chapter on choosing friends repeats the gratitude chapter's rule and supplies its other half, whom to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:806","type":"agrees","note":"106 commands: do not abandon (துறவற்க) the friendship of those who were a support in distress. 806 states it as a description: those who keep within the bounds will not abandon (துறவார்) the tie of long-standing friends even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்). The same verb is used in both, with the direction reversed: 106 is loyalty owed for help received in one's own trouble, 806 loyalty kept when trouble comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"107 says the friendship of one who removed one's distress is remembered through seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும்). 398 says learning gained in one birth (ஒருமைக்கண்) is a protection through seven (எழுமையும்). The seven-birth span is the book's figure for what outlasts a single life, used here for a debt of gratitude and there for learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:799","type":"parallel_domain","note":"107 says people will remember (உள்ளுவர்) through seven births the friendship of those who wiped away their distress. 799 says the bond with those who abandoned one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when thought of (உள்ளினும்) at the hour of death. Both are about how conduct in another's hard time is remembered: the helper for lives to come, the deserter up to the last moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:113","type":"same_image","note":"108 says a wrong done to you should be forgotten that very day (அன்றே மறப்பது). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day (அன்றே ஒழிய விடல்). The shared அன்றே gives both the same urgency: neither a grievance nor an unfair gain is to be kept overnight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"109 says that even harm like killing disappears when one recalls a single good the person once did (அவர் செய்த ஒன்று நன்று உள்ள). 807 says those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love even when the friend does ruinous things (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). Both have the same 'even if they do grave harm' (செயினும்) clause; what outweighs the harm is a remembered favour in one and long affection in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:207","type":"same_image","note":"110 says those who kill any good may have an escape (உய்வுண்டாம்), but there is none for one who kills a good done to him (செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு). 207 has the same shape: those who incur any enmity (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும்) escape (உய்வர்), but the enmity of one's deeds follows and kills. Both use 'everything else can be survived' to single out one thing, ingratitude in 110 and evil deeds in general in 207.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:896","type":"same_image","note":"110 says there is escape (உய்வுண்டாம்) for other wrongs but none (உய்வில்லை) for killing gratitude. 896 says one burned by fire may survive (உய்வுண்டாம்), but those who go on offending the great will not (உய்யார்). The same உய்வு formula marks what each chapter treats as its unforgivable offence, so a reader should take 'only this has no escape' as the book's way of stressing gravity and not as a strict ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:528","type":"qualifies","note":"111 asks that fairness be kept alike across the divisions of people, on Parimelazhagar's reading toward enemy, stranger and friend. 528 says a king should not look on all in common (பொதுநோக்கான்) but according to rank or merit (வரிசையா நோக்கின்), and many will then stay by him. Together they show that impartiality in 111 does not mean identical treatment: one may not favour by relationship, but one should distinguish by desert.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:541","type":"parallel_domain","note":"111 says the virtue called தகுதி is good if one keeps to what is due in each case (பகுதியால் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுக); Parimelazhagar takes பகுதி as enemy, stranger and friend. 541 defines a king's justice (முறை) as inquiring, showing favour to no one (யார்மாட்டும் கண்ணோடாது) and acting after consideration. The householder's impartiality of 111 reappears as the procedure of the ruler's court.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to his descendants. 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 thus limits 112 to the usual course of things; Parimelazhagar explains the exceptions by deeds of earlier births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:171","type":"agrees","note":"112 says a fair person's wealth is a support to his posterity (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). 171 states the reverse: if one covets others' goods without fairness (நடுவின்றி), the family perishes (குடிபொன்றி) and guilt comes at once. Both tie நடுவு to what happens to one's line, 112 as promise and 171 as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:328","type":"parallel_domain","note":"113 says to give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்), even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 328 says that, however great the gain that brings good (நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும்), gain that comes by killing (கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கம்) is lowest to the worthy. The same concession, 'even if it is good and large', is refused in both; only the forbidden means differs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"115 and 118 end with the same line, கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி, 'not bending is the ornament of the worthy'. In 115 the bending is of the heart under loss and gain (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை); in 118 it is leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like a balance. The repetition joins two sides of நடுவு: steadiness under one's own fortunes and impartiality between others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"115 says loss and increase both occur, and the worthy keep the heart from bending because of them. 169 gives the hard case: the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright are ruined (செவ்வியான் கேடு), which calls for reflection. 169 shows the situation in which 115's advice is tested, when fortune seems to reward bending.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"116 says that if one's heart leaves fairness and does wrong (நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்), one should know 'I will perish' (கெடுவல்யான்). 204 says do not plot another's ruin even by a lapse, for அறம் will plot the plotter's ruin (சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). Both say that harm intended for others returns as one's own ruin; 116 makes it the wrongdoer's own knowledge, 204 gives the agent as அறம்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"117 says the world (உலகம்) will not count as ruin the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even if they have every good quality'. They conflict if உலகம் in 117 means people in general; Parimelazhagar avoids this by reading உலகம் there as the high-minded (உயர்ந்தோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"117 says the low state of a person who stands fair is not held to be ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது). 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called சால்பு. Both deny that poverty lowers a person of character; 117 says it for fairness in particular, 988 for worth as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:118","b":"k:561","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 describes a balance that first makes itself level (சமன்செய்து) and then weighs (சீர்தூக்கும்). 561 says a king is one who examines a case fittingly (தக்காங்கு நாடி) and punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது); Parimelazhagar glosses the examining as done 'standing in the middle' (நடுவாக நின்று). The two steps of the scale, being level and then measuring, match the two steps of just punishment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"119 says rectitude is the absence of crookedness in speech (சொற்கோட்டம்), provided there is none within (உட்கோட்டம்). 279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute (யாழ்கோடு) is good, so judge people by what their deeds do. Both use straight and bent to separate appearance from substance: 119 will not take straight words as proof without a straight mind, and 279 will not take outward shape as proof at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:295","type":"agrees","note":"119 makes unbent speech count as rectitude only if the mind too is unbent (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்). 295 praises one who speaks truth together with the mind (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) above those who do austerity and give. Both attach the same condition to good speech, agreement between word and mind, in the fields of fairness and of truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 says true trade for traders is to look after others' things as their own (பிறவும் தமபோல்). 315 asks what use understanding is if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). The same 'as one's own' (தம்போல்) test is applied to goods in the market and to suffering in the chapter on not hurting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:1015","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 asks traders to care for what belongs to others as for what is theirs (பிறவும் தமபோல் பேணி). 1015 says the world calls those who feel shame at others' blame and their own (பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார்) the dwelling of shame; Parimelazhagar reads this as weighing the two alike. Both define a virtue, fairness in one and shame in the other, by removing the difference between oneself and others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:50","type":"agrees","note":"121 says restraint brings one among the immortals (அமரருள் உய்க்கும்); 50 says one who lives on earth as one should will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both promise a place among the gods to a person living an ordinary household life well, without renunciation, and 121 names restraint as the quality that does it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"121 ends அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும், and 168 ends with envy, called a பாவி, which destroys fortune and தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும். The same closing verb phrase, 'drives and leaves one there', is used for lack of restraint and for envy, so the two vices are given the same power to deliver a person to a bad place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:31","type":"develops","note":"31 asks what gain is greater for a living being than virtue (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு); 122 says there is no gain for a living being beyond restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு) and tells one to guard it as wealth. 122 reuses chapter 4's formula almost word for word and applies it to one particular virtue, so restraint is given the rank the earlier verse gave to virtue as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:363","type":"same_image","note":"122 tells one to guard restraint as பொருள், wealth, since nothing is a greater gain; 363 says there is no fine wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) here like not wanting, and nothing equal to it there either. Both verses count an inner holding-back as the real wealth and both use the 'nothing equals it' form, once for the householder's restraint and once for the renouncer's freedom from craving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:715","type":"same_image","note":"123 calls restraint செறிவு and says that, recognised, it yields distinction; 715 calls not speaking first among elders a செறிவு and ranks it the best of good things (முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared word shows the general restraint of chapter 13 turning up in the assembly as a concrete act, holding back one's speech before those who know more.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"123 says that if one knows and then restrains oneself in the proper way (அறிவறிந்து ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்), that restraint is recognised and yields distinction; Parimelazhagar takes அறிவறிந்து as knowing that restraint is itself what knowledge is. 834 gives the negative: no fool is greater than one who has studied, understood and taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்காப் பேதை). Together they say knowledge is finished only when it turns into restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:29","type":"same_image","note":"29 speaks of those who have climbed and stand on the hill called good qualities (குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be ascetics; 124 says the restrained person's stature is greater than a mountain (மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது), and Parimelazhagar reads நிலை there as the householder's state. On his readings the renouncer stands on a hill while the householder who stays restrained in his own station is said to overtop one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain's. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kundri seed (குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர்). The mountain stature of 124 holds only while the 'not shifting' holds; 965 states how little it takes to lose it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"124 praises one who is restrained and does not shift from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது); 989 says those called the ஆழி of worth do not shift even when the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both measure a person by immovability, 124 against a mountain and 989 against the turning of an age.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:125","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"125 treats bowing (பணிதல்) as an excellence, a second wealth for the rich; 985 says bowing is the strength of those who get things done and the weapon by which the worthy turn opponents around (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). The same act is valued as character in the Book of Virtue and as effective power in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமையுள் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து); 398 says the learning one gains in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமைக்கண் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). The closing formula is identical, so sense-restraint and learning are set side by side as the two acquisitions that carry across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"126 says restraint of the five in one birth protects through seven; 835 says a fool can, by what he does in one birth (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire he will sink in through seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக்கு அழுந்தும் அளறு). The one-birth and seven-births arithmetic runs in both directions: one life is enough to secure the next seven or to ruin them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"127 says whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue, or they will grieve through a fault of speech (நாகாக்க காவாக்கால் சோகாப்பர்); 305 says if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, or anger will kill you (சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால் தன்னையே கொல்லும்). The sentence frame காக்க / காவாக்கால் is the same, and in both the harm of the unguarded thing falls on the person who failed to guard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:128","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good turns out not good (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் ... நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as the person's other merit turning bad. 965 says even hill-like people sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed. Both hold that a single small lapse is enough to cancel a large standing good, in speech in one verse and in honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:159","type":"develops","note":"129 says the scar left by the tongue never heals; 159 says those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of people who exceed all bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற்பவர்) are as pure as renouncers. 129 gives the reason 159's praise is so high: what the forbearing person takes without retaliation is the one kind of wound the book says does not close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:1049","type":"same_image","note":"129 sets a fire-burn, which heals, against the tongue's scar, which does not; 1049 says one can even sleep in the middle of fire but cannot close an eye in the middle of poverty (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). The book repeatedly takes fire as the measure of bearable pain and places a non-physical hurt beyond it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"130 says அறம் itself watches for the right moment and enters the path of one who has guarded against anger, learned, and is restrained (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து); 204 says that if you plot another's ruin, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both verses virtue is an agent that acts on a person, seeking out the restrained and planning against the malicious.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"130 has virtue looking for its chance and coming along the path to the restrained person; 594 has prosperity asking the way and going to the one of unwavering energy (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்). The same figure, a good that seeks out the person fit for it instead of being pursued, is used for virtue in the first book and for worldly gain in the second. The edition's text of 594 has lost its first letter (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்), but the reading is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life because it gives excellence (ஒழுக்கம் உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்); 1017 says those ruled by shame will give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both place a quality of character above life itself; 1017 states as what such people actually do what 131 states as a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"133 makes conduct the thing that constitutes good family (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை); 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாக). If குடிமை in 133 means that conduct alone makes one well-born, 951 runs the other way and makes birth the source of the conduct. The conflict disappears on Parimelazhagar's reading of 133, where conduct only preserves or forfeits a rank that birth has already given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:1019","type":"agrees","note":"133 says a lapse from conduct turns into low birth; 1019 says a lapse in கொள்கை burns one's lineage (குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின்), and Parimelazhagar glosses கொள்கை there as ஒழுக்கம். Both say family standing is lost through the individual's failure in conduct, one calling the result a low birth and the other a burning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:134","b":"k:560","type":"same_image","note":"134 treats forgetting the Veda (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்) as a loss that can be made good; 560 lists among the effects of a king who does not protect that those of the six occupations forget their books (அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்). The same forgetting appears as a private, repairable lapse in 134 and as a sign of public ruin in 560, while 134 keeps conduct as the loss that cannot be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"135 takes it as given that the envious have no gain (ஆக்கம்); 169 says the gain of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் ... நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 concedes that the case 135 calls non-existent can occur and needs explaining, which weakens the simile's premise; Parimelazhagar softens it by adding 'if they occur'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:170","type":"develops","note":"135 argues by comparison: as there is no gain in the envious, there is no elevation in one without conduct (அழுக்காறுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம் போன்று இல்லை). 170 states the premise on its own: none have grown great through envy (அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை). 135 takes chapter 17's claim as already known and builds its point about conduct on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:136","b":"k:164","type":"same_image","note":"136 says the strong do not slacken in conduct, knowing the harm that comes of a lapse (இழுக்கத்தின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கறிந்து); 164 says people do no wrong out of envy, knowing the harm that comes of the wrong path (இழுக்காற்றின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). The second lines are nearly the same words, and both verses ground good behaviour in foreseeing consequences, which 136 counts as strength (உரவோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:137","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"137 says that from conduct people reach high standing and from a lapse they reach எய்தாப் பழி, which Parimelazhagar takes as blame that is not properly theirs, laid on them because the lapse makes it believable. 117 says the world will not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in the right by being fair (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்). In both the world's verdict follows character more than facts: it discounts the upright person's fall and charges the lapsed person with more than he did.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:138","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"138 calls good conduct the seed of good (நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம்) and says bad conduct always gives suffering; 361 calls craving the seed that produces unending birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). The seed figure is used for a cause whose yield comes later and keeps coming, once for a good crop and once for a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says speaking evil things even by a slip is not possible for those who have conduct (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல் ஒல்லாவே); 199 says those of stainless sight do not say meaningless things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). Both claim that a settled quality rules out even the accidental lapse of speech, and they trace that quality to conduct in one case and to clear understanding in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:959","type":"agrees","note":"139 makes the absence of evil speech a test of having conduct; 959 says that as a sprout shows the ground it lay in, the words from the mouth show the family of the one born in it (குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat what comes out of the mouth as the evidence of what a person is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:1044","type":"qualifies","note":"139 says a person of conduct cannot speak evil even by a slip; 1044 says that even in the well-born, poverty brings the slackness from which degrading words are born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்). 1044 names a condition, want, under which the slip of speech that 139 calls impossible does happen to good people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"140 makes accord with the world the test that learning must pass; 280 says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). In both, the world's standard outranks a formal qualification, learning for the scholar and outward marks for the ascetic. Parimelazhagar takes உலகம் in both verses as the worthy and not people at large.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:832","type":"develops","note":"832 defines the folly of all follies (பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை) as setting one's love on what is not proper to one (கையல்லதன்கண் காதன்மை). 141 names one case of exactly that: the பேதைமை of desiring (பெட்டு ஒழுகும்) a woman who is another's. Read together, 141 is an instance of the general definition that 832 gives much later.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:910","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly (பேதைமை) of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found (இல்) in those who have seen aram and porul; 910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman (பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை) is never found (இல்) in those with a deliberating mind. Both end on 'பேதைமை ... இல்' and treat the lapse as a failure of understanding that knowledge excludes, once about another's wife in the Book of Virtue and once about subservience to one's own in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:142","b":"k:150","type":"agrees","note":"142 says that among all who stand outside virtue (அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம்) none is a greater fool than the one who stands at another's door (பிறன்கடை); 150 says that even one who does not keep to virtue and does wrong (அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும்) does well not to desire another's woman. Both verses rank within the class of the non-virtuous, and both put this one offence at the bottom, 142 by naming the worst case and 150 by naming the one abstention still worth having.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"143 counts the betrayer of a trusting household as no different from the dead (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 214 says only the one who knows what is fitting lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). In both, being alive is measured by conduct and not by breath, once for a wrong committed and once for a duty to others left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:926","type":"same_image","note":"143 says those who go on doing evil in the home of people who trust them (தெளிந்தாரில்) are 'no different from the dead' (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 926 uses the same construction for sleepers (துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர்) and extends it to drinkers. Parimelazhagar explains 926 by the absence of awareness and 143 by the loss of aram, porul and inbam, so the same formula of living death is applied to a moral failure in one verse and to loss of consciousness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the millet's measure (தினைத்துணை). In 433 those who are ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவார்) take a fault of a millet's size as large as a palmyra; in 144 the man entering another's home does not reflect even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). The pair shows the same measure from opposite sides: the careful person enlarges the smallest fault, and the adulterer does not give the smallest thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"144 asks what greatness comes to, however great a person is (எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம்), if he enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). 965 says even those like a hill (குன்றின் அனையார்) sink if they do a lowering deed as small as a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not outweigh a degrading act; 965 gives the general rule and 144 one named act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands without perishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்). 145 says the blame of the man who violates another's home also stands forever without dying (எஞ்ஞான்றும் விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). Taken literally 145 names a second undying thing, so the pair shows the book treating lasting blame as the counterpart of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:284","type":"parallel_domain","note":"145 says one who crosses into another's home thinking it easy (எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான்) gets blame that stands forever without dying (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). 284 says a love grown strong in theft (களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல்) yields at harvest a distress that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). The same logic, a desire for what is another's that looks light at the start and has a permanent result, is applied to another's wife and to another's goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:146","b":"k:171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the man who crosses into another's home: enmity, sin, fear and blame (பகை பாவம் அச்சம் பழி). 171 says that one who covets another's good property without fairness finds his family ruined and fault brought on at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). Coveting another's wife and coveting another's goods are both given immediate, compound consequences; Parimelazhagar reads the பழி of 146 as blame on the family (குடிப்பழி), which brings it closer to the குடி of 171.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"Both verses use the formula 'the one called a householder' (இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான்). 41 defines him by what he supports: he is the steady help of the three on their good path. 147 defines him by what he refrains from: he lives the household life with aram (அறனியலான்) who does not desire the womanhood of one who belongs to another (பிறனியலாள் பெண்மை நயவாதவன்). Together they give a positive and a negative test for the same title.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:49","type":"develops","note":"49 says that what is called aram is household life, and that it is good if there is nothing in it that another blames (பிறன்பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). 147 says the householder living by aram is the one who does not desire another's woman. 147 can be read as naming a specific condition under which the household life of 49 stays free of others' blame; chapter 15 itself (145, 146) names பழி as the result of the offence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:282","type":"parallel_domain","note":"148 praises not even looking at (நோக்காத) another's wife; Parimelazhagar glosses this as not taking her into the mind. 282 says that even thinking in the heart (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods (பிறன்பொருளை) by stealth is wrong. In both chapters the prohibition is moved back from the act to the look or the thought, for another's wife and for another's property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"150 says not desiring another's woman is good even in one who practises no aram and does wrong; 297 says that if one can keep to not lying, then not doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை) is still good. Both isolate a single restraint and say it holds its value without the rest of virtue. Set side by side, the book makes this kind of claim for more than one virtue, so each is better read as emphasis than as a ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"151 says bearing with those who scorn us is the foremost thing (பொறுத்தல் தலை). 579, in the chapter on kindly regard in the Book of Wealth, says that showing regard and bearing (கண்ணோடிப் பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை) even with those whose nature is to hurt us is the foremost quality. The same ranking, with the same words பொறுத்தல் and தலை, is given for the householder and again for the ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 152 as telling one to bear a wrong even when one is able to punish it (தாம் ஒறுத்தற்கு இயன்ற காலத்தும்). 301 states that condition outright: only the one who holds back anger where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான்) truly holds it back, and where it cannot, it makes no difference. 301 supplies the reason the commentator's condition matters: forbearance toward those one cannot hurt is not yet forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:488","type":"qualifies","note":"152 says to bear transgression always (என்றும்) and better still to forget it. 488, advising a king on timing, says: on seeing enemies, bear them (சுமக்க); when their end is seen to have come, the head goes down (கிழக்காம் தலை). In 488 the bearing is temporary and is kept in mind until the moment to act, where 152 asks for a bearing with no end that passes into forgetting. The Book of Wealth thus limits to a tactic what the Book of Virtue states without condition.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:444","type":"same_image","note":"153 says the strength within strength (வன்மையுள் வன்மை) is bearing with fools (மடவார்ப் பொறை); 444 says the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is to live so that those greater than oneself become one's own. Neither verse puts strength in force. One finds it in enduring those below one in understanding and the other in attaching oneself to those above.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:841","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses name a 'poverty within poverty' (இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and give different answers: in 153 it is turning away a guest, in 841 it is lack of understanding (அறிவின்மை), with other lack not counted as lack by the world. The same superlative formula serves hospitality in the Book of Virtue and intelligence in the Book of Wealth, which shows the formula working as emphasis within each chapter's topic.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:864","type":"same_image","note":"154 ties நிறை to forbearance: to keep நிறையுடைமை, live guarding பொறையுடைமை. 864 pairs the same two things in the negative, describing the man who does not leave off anger and has no நிறை (நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன்) as easy prey for anyone. The commentators gloss நிறை differently in the two places (Parimelazhagar: சால்பு in 154; Pope, following him, has keeping secrets in 864; Manakkudavar on 154 has a conduct of self-guarding), but in both verses its presence goes with restraint of anger and its absence with unchecked anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"154 says that one who wants நிறையுடைமை never to leave should guard forbearance; Parimelazhagar glosses நிறை here as சால்பு. 987 asks what use சால்பு is if one does not do pleasing things even to those who have done one harm (இன்னாசெய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two say the same thing from both ends: forbearance keeps சால்பு in place, and சால்பு that does not show in the treatment of offenders is worth nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"155 says the wise do not count as anything those who punish in return (ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையார்) and keep those who forbear like stored gold. 314 keeps the word ஒறுத்தல் and gives it a new content: the way to punish those who have done harm is to shame them by doing them good (அவர்நாண நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்). 314 thus shows the only form of 'punishing' that 155's judgment leaves open. (The Parimelazhagar text displayed for 314 in this edition belongs to 315.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:549","type":"qualifies","note":"155 says those who punish (ஒறுத்தார்) are not counted as anything. 549 says that guarding the people and removing wrongdoing (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain on a king but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). Parimelazhagar restricts 155 to a harm done to oneself, and 549 concerns a ruler acting for his subjects, so 549 marks the limit of 155: the low regard for punishing applies to personal retaliation and not to the office of justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 says those who forbear have renown until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்), against one day's pleasure for those who retaliate; 145 says the man who violates another's home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). The adjacent chapters use the same accounting, a brief gratification weighed against a permanent reputation, with புகழ் on one side and பழி on the other. Manakkudavar takes பொன்றுந் துணையும் as until the person's own death, Parimelazhagar as until the world ends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:173","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 sets the one day's joy of those who retaliate (ஒருநாளை இன்பம்) against the lasting renown of those who forbear. 173 says those who want the other joy (மற்றின்பம்) will not do what is not aram out of desire for a small joy (சிற்றின்பம்). Both argue against a wrong by the shortness of the pleasure it buys, for retaliation in one verse and for coveting in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:856","type":"agrees","note":"156 grants the retaliator one day's pleasure and gives the forbearing renown to the end. 856 says that the life of one who says it is sweet (இனிது) to prevail in hostility is close to failure and ruin (தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து). Both admit that getting the better of an opponent tastes sweet and both say the sweetness is brief; the verse in the Book of Wealth names ruin where the verse in the Book of Virtue names only the loss of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"157 says that though others do what is improper to one (திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும்), it is good not to do what is not aram. 312 says that even when someone has done harm out of hatred, not doing harm in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the settled principle of the stainless. The same rule appears in the householder's chapter on forbearance and in the ascetic's chapter on not harming, which makes it common to both ways of life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read நோநொந்து in 157 as grieving for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoers. 319 states the mechanism behind that expectation: if one does harm to others in the forenoon, harm comes to oneself in the afternoon of its own accord (தாமே வரும்). On the commentators' reading 157 presupposes 319: the injured person pities the offender because the offence will return on him, and for the same reason does not add a wrong of his own. Kalaignar instead takes the pain in 157 as the injured person's own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157: though others do one wrong, not doing what is not aram is good (அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று). 852: though someone does unbearable things intending division (பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), not doing harm with hostility in mind is the chief thing (இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). The 'even if they do ... not doing' structure and the refusal to answer in kind recur in the Book of Wealth's chapter on hostility, where the reason given is political prudence as well as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"158 says to overcome those who do excessive wrong out of arrogance (மிகுதியான்) by one's own worthiness (தகுதியான் வென்றுவிடல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as forbearance. 985 says the strength of the able is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both describe yielding as the means of prevailing and use the vocabulary of contest, வெல்லல் and படை, for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:986","type":"qualifies","note":"158 calls forbearance toward the arrogant a victory (வென்றுவிடல்). 986 says the touchstone of சால்பு is to accept defeat (தோல்வி ... கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equals. The same conduct toward an overbearing lesser person is named winning in one verse and taking a loss in the other; 986 adds that it has to be felt and accepted as a defeat, which guards against reading 158 as a way of scoring over the offender. Manakkudavar on 158 says explicitly that forbearing is not defeat but is itself the victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"158 says one defeats the overbearing by one's own forbearing worth. 1327, spoken by the man in the Book of Love, says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as is seen in the union that follows. The paradox that giving way is the real victory appears in ethics and again, in a dramatised speech, in love; the second is a lover's remark and not a moral rule, but the shape of the thought is the same.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:48","type":"agrees","note":"159 says those who endure (நோற்கிற்பவர்) the harsh words of transgressors are as pure as renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from aram has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). Both apply the ascetic's verb நோல் to the householder and set him level with or above the ascetic; Parimelazhagar on 159 adds that these are people who remain in household life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses play on இறந்தார் and துறந்தார். 310: those who go beyond bounds in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). 159: those who endure harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்) have the purity of renouncers (துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை). In both, a householder's control over anger, his own or his response to another's, earns the standing of renunciation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:389","type":"parallel_domain","note":"159 praises those who endure harsh words (இன்னாச்சொல்) from people who overstep. 389 says the world rests under the parasol of the king who has the character to bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). The same capacity is a private virtue in one verse and a quality of rule in the other. Parimelazhagar takes the bitter words of 389 to be the rebukes of advisers, so there the speaker means well, unlike the transgressors of 159.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 defines the form of tavam as two things: bearing the pain that comes to one (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) and doing no harm to living beings. 160 uses the same verb for both the faster and the one who endures harsh words (நோற்பார்). By 261's definition the person in 160 who bears abuse and does not retaliate already meets both parts of tavam, which explains how 160 can rank him ahead of those who fast.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"160 ranks those who fast behind those who bear harsh words; 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). In both a virtue of everyday speech, enduring it in one case and keeping it truthful in the other, is placed above formal religious practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:161","b":"k:135","type":"same_image","note":"135 pairs the same two words: as the envious have no ஆக்கம், so one without ஒழுக்கம் has no உயர்வு. 161 fuses them, asking that freedom from அழுக்காறு be taken as ஒழுக்காறு. The rhyme ஒழுக்கு/அழுக்காறு is used in both to set envy and conduct side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:162","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals being free of envy toward anyone (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை). 363 says there is no excellent wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) like வேண்டாமை, here or there (அஃதொப்பது இல்). The same formula ranks an absence, of envy in the householder book and of wanting in the ascetic book, above every positive possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:141","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found in those who have seen அறம் and பொருள். 163 says the envier of பிறனாக்கம் is one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். In adjacent chapters a wrong attitude to what is another's is measured against the same pair, virtue and wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"163 says the one who does not cherish another's prosperity (பிறனாக்கம் பேணாது) and envies it is called one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். 1079 shows the same reaction in the கீழ்: on seeing others clothed and fed (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின்) he is quick to find a fault in them. The later verse gives the behaviour that the earlier one judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:304","type":"parallel_domain","note":"304 asks whether there is any other enemy than anger (சினத்தின் பகையும் உளவோ பிற), which kills smile and joy. 165 says the envious need no enemy since envy itself brings ruin. Two of the four things 35 excludes from virtue, anger and envy, are each described as the person's own enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings one's end (குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை). 165 says the same of one fault: envy suffices to ruin its holder without any ஒன்னார். The king's general rule in Book 2 has a specific case already stated in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"165 says envy is enough for the envious: it brings ruin even when enemies fail (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). 448 says a king without people who rebuke him perishes even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe ruin that needs no outside enemy, once in a private vice and once in a ruler's unguarded state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:527","type":"develops","note":"527 says crows do not hide food but call the others and eat, and that ஆக்கம் belongs only to those of that nature (அன்ன நீரார்க்கே). 166 gives the opposite disposition and result: one who grudges giving leaves his சுற்றம் without food or clothing. Both tie a household's kin and its prosperity to the attitude taken toward sharing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:1079","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the phrase உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம். In 1079 the base man sees others' clothing and food and finds fault with them; in 166 the kin of one who envies what is given (கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான்) perish without clothing or food. The pairing shows what is envied returning as what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:167","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess (செய்யவள்) grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வை). 617 names both sisters by residence: மாமுகடி lives in sloth, and தாமரையினாள் in the effort of the unslothful. The same pair of goddesses marks envy in Book 1 and sloth in Book 2 as conditions that hand a person over from fortune to misfortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays in what is right through fairness. 169 lists the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) as something to be pondered. 117 limits how that கேடு should be counted: the upright person's decline is not regarded as real ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:171","b":"k:604","type":"parallel_domain","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness makes the family perish and brings faults at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). 604 says of the slothful that the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). The same double penalty, on the குடி and in குற்றம், is attached to greed in Book 1 and to sloth in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"172 says those ashamed of unfairness (நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்) will not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had (படுபயன் வெஃகி). 113 gives the instruction that goes with it: gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) is to be dropped that very day, even if it brings only good. 172 describes the character and 113 the rule it follows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:164","type":"parallel_domain","note":"164 says people do no wrong out of envy because they know the harm that comes of the wrong path (ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). 172 says people do nothing blameworthy out of coveting because they are ashamed of unfairness (நாணுபவர்). The neighbouring chapters use the same sentence shape, ending in செய்யார், with knowledge of consequences as the check in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:173","b":"k:754","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that comes without wrong and with knowledge of the means (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்) yields both அறன் and இன்பம். 173 says the small joy got by coveting is refused by those who want the other joy. Together they show the choice in 173 is not between wealth and joy: wealth rightly got delivers the joy that coveting forfeits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"174 says those of faultless sight who have conquered the senses will not covet on the thought 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார்). 205 forbids doing evil on the same plea (இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க) and adds that doing so makes one poor again. Both name poverty as the excuse and reject it; 205 gives the consequence that 174 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"656 says that even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 174 says the thought of being without does not make the self-controlled covet. 656 pushes the same refusal to its hardest case, where the want is another's and not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1040 says the good woman called Land laughs on seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 174 has the same words, இலமென்று, as the thought that does not move the self-controlled to covet. The plea of having nothing is refused twice: it excuses neither taking from others nor sitting idle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"175 asks what fine and wide understanding comes to (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு என்னாம்) if one covets and acts senselessly toward everyone. 315 asks whether anything comes of understanding (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own. Both test அறிவு by how its owner treats other people, in coveting and in causing hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"834 says no one is a greater fool than the person who has studied, understood and taught others and yet does not restrain himself (தானடங்கா). 175 says subtle and wide understanding is worth nothing if its owner covets and does வெறிய things. Both hold that learning which does not govern conduct does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:204","type":"develops","note":"204 says not to plot another's ruin even by a lapse (சூழற்க), because அறம் will plot the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). 176 says that one who plots wrongs for wealth (பொல்லாத சூழ) will perish (கெடும்). The shared verb சூழ் places the ruin at the stage of planning, before any act; 204 names the agent of that ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:285","type":"agrees","note":"285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. 176 says one who stands on the path desiring அருள் (அருள்வெஃகி) perishes if he desires பொருள் (பொருள்வெஃகி) and plots wrongs. Both verses set அருள் and பொருள் as rival objects of one desire and say the second drives out the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:113","type":"qualifies","note":"177 argues from the outcome: gain got by coveting does not turn out well when enjoyed. 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness is to be given up that very day even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 113 removes the dependence on outcome, so the prohibition would stand even where 177's prediction failed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:284","type":"same_image","note":"177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting, since at its ripening (விளைவயின்) the fruit is hard to make good. 284 says the love grown strong in stealth yields, at its ripening (விளைவின்கண்), a sorrow that does not die. Coveting in the householder book and theft in the ascetic book are both judged by the harvest image, where the gain looks sound until it ripens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 opens with the same prohibition, வேண்டற்க: do not want gambling even if you win, because the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. 177 says வேண்டற்க of the gain got by coveting, whose fruit turns out badly. Both warn against a gain that is real at the moment of taking and harmful afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:178","b":"k:283","type":"develops","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure even as it seems to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்). 178 says the way to keep wealth from shrinking is not to covet another's goods. 283 gives the negative side of the same claim: what is added by taking another's property is not a lasting addition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:167","type":"develops","note":"167 says the fair goddess grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister. 179 says திரு goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. The two neighbouring chapters use the goddess in opposite directions: she leaves the one who resents others' goods and comes to the one who does not want them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:594","type":"same_image","note":"179 says திரு, knowing the way (திறன் அறிந்து), goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. 594 says ஆக்கம் asks the way (அதர்வினாய்) and goes to the one of unwavering drive. In both, fortune is a traveller that seeks the person out; Book 1 gives non-coveting as what draws it and Book 2 gives energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:692","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those kings. 180 says not wanting others' goods yields victory, while coveting without thought yields destruction. The courtier's rule in Book 2 follows the same logic as 180: refraining from what belongs to another is what secures gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:181","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"181 holds that not backbiting is still good in a man who otherwise does wrong (அல்ல செயினும் ... இனிது). 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). Both single out one restraint of speech and weigh it against the whole of the rest of conduct, though 297 goes further and treats it as sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:820","type":"develops","note":"182 calls it worse than denying virtue to slander someone when absent and smile at him when present. 820 tells one not to come near those who are friendly in the house and blame you in the assembly (மனைக்கெழீஇ மன்றில் பழிப்பார்). 820 turns the moral verdict of 182 into practical advice about which friendships to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 ranks death above a life kept up by backbiting and pretence. 230 says nothing is more bitter than death (சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை), yet even it is sweet when one cannot give. Each verse measures a fault or lack by setting death beside it and finding death the better; the pairing shows how the book uses that comparison for both a wrong done and a good left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 says that dying yields more of the gain the books of virtue speak of than living by backbiting and falsehood (பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்). 967 says that rather than live by following those who scorn you (வாழ்தலின்), it is better to be called ruined as you are. Both use the comparative 'than living thus' to say that a kind of life can cost more than its end, once for the slanderer's two faces and once for lost honour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:197","type":"parallel_domain","note":"184: though you speak harshly to his face (சொல்லினும்), do not speak (சொல்லற்க) the heedless word behind his back. 197: let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must (சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக), it is good that they not say what lacks use. The two neighbouring chapters use the same concessive move, granting a lesser fault of speech in order to isolate the one the chapter is about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"184 concedes speaking without kindly regard to a person's face (கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும்) and forbids only the word said in his absence that does not look to what follows (முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்). 795 goes further and tells one to seek out friends able to speak so that one weeps and to rebuke (அழச்சொல்லி ... இடித்து). What 184 merely tolerates, hard speech face to face, 795 treats as a mark of the friend worth choosing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:193","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 says that a man's heart does not really speak virtue is seen from the meanness of his backbiting (புறஞ்சொல்லும் புன்மையாற் காணப்படும்). 193 says that expansive talk of useless things itself declares the speaker to be without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). In both a kind of speech is treated as evidence about the speaker, whatever he professes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 reads a man's inner lack of virtue from his backbiting. 959 says that as the sprout shows what lies in the soil, the words of the mouth show the family one is born in (காட்டும் குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat speech as the visible sign of something hidden, the heart in one case and lineage in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"186 says the one who tells another's blame (பிறன்பழி கூறுவான்) will have his own faults sorted through and told (தன்பழியுள்ளும் திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப்படும்). 319 says that hurt done to others in the forenoon comes back on oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்). The same return of the deed appears for speech and for injury; in 186 the return is carried out by other people's tongues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"186 warns the teller of others' faults that his own will be told. 980 says greatness covers another's weakness while smallness only tells faults (சிறுமைதான் குற்றமே கூறிவிடும்). 186 gives the consequence of the habit and 980 gives its diagnosis: fault-telling is what smallness does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"187 says those who do not know how to make friends by words that bring laughter (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) will part even their own kin by words that divide (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layers (இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் ... சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). The two state the same link between speech and keeping one's people, from its negative and positive sides, in the Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"187 treats speaking so that others laugh (நகச்சொல்லி) as the way friendship is made. 784 says friendship is not for laughing (நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல்) but for going ahead to rebuke when the friend oversteps. 784 limits 187: pleasant talk may begin a friendship, but it is not what the friendship is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"188 describes people whose habit is to broadcast (தூற்றும்) the faults of those near them. 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum because they carry any secret they have heard to others (கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). 1076 places the habit of 188 in the Kural's portrait of the base person.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those whose way is to spread even the faults of people close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers (என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). In 1165 the woman asks what those who can bring grief within friendship (நட்பினுள்) would become in enmity. Both argue from conduct toward intimates to what must follow toward others; in 1165 it is a lover's complaint, not a general teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"189 speaks of the backbiter as a weight the earth bears (வையம் ... பொறை). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The figure of a person as dead weight on the ground is used in both for someone who fails others in ordinary dealings, by malicious speech in one and by lack of regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"189 wonders that the earth bears the backbiter's weight at all. 990 says that if the worthy decline in worth, the great earth will not bear its load (இருநிலந்தான் தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை). Together they show the image used at both ends: the earth strains under the bad man, and is able to carry its load only while the worthy stay worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 wishes that people would see their own faults as they see a stranger's. 930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk, does not think of his own lapse when he drinks (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 930 is a concrete instance of the failure 190 names: the other man's fault is seen clearly and one's own identical fault is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:193","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"193 says that a man's spreading out useless matter in speech itself announces that he is without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). 959 says the words of the mouth show the family one comes from, as a sprout shows the soil. In both the speech testifies about the speaker; 193 makes the talk itself the subject of the verb 'says', which Parimelazhagar notes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"195 says useless talk costs people of fine nature their distinction (சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும்). 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 supplies a reason for 195: a fault that would pass unnoticed in others is conspicuous in those held high.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"195 says that if people of fine nature (நீர்மையுடையார்) speak useless things, their distinction and the regard it earned leave them. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not protect the eminent from a small lapse; 195 applies this to idle speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"196 calls the habitual idle talker chaff, a husk with nothing in it. 406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist and are like barren saline land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களரனையர்). Both take a farming image of something that looks like the useful thing and gives no yield, and both turn on பயன், what a person produces.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:193","type":"qualifies","note":"193 says that useless talk at length shows the speaker to be without நயன், so the two faults go together. 197 separates them and ranks them: speech without நயன் may be let pass in the worthy, speech without use may not. Read together, 197 makes uselessness the graver of the two; Parimelazhagar draws the same ranking from சொல்லினும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:697","type":"parallel_domain","note":"197 lets the worthy say even what lacks நயன் but holds that they should not say what lacks use (பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 697 tells one who serves a king to say what is wanted and never to say what is without use even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்); Parimelazhagar glosses வினையில as matters without benefit. The rule against useless speech is kept as firmly at court as in private ethics, and neither verse lets circumstances excuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"199 says what those of stainless sight will not say. 258 says those whose sight has parted from fault (செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார்) will not eat flesh that has parted from life. The book repeatedly states a restraint as what people of clear காட்சி simply do not do; here the same form covers speech and diet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the phrase 'என்னும் செருக்கு'. In 201 it is 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு', which Parimelazhagar glosses as a delusion (மயக்கம்) and Manakkudavar as an exhilaration (களிப்பு) that the good fear; in 180 it is 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல்). The same word names a heady state to be dreaded in one verse and a state worth having in the other, depending on what it rests on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:244","type":"qualifies","note":"201 makes fear of evil deeds the mark of the excellent. 244 says that for one who sustains living beings and practises அருள் there are no deeds his own life need fear ('தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்'). The fear 201 commends is aimed at one's own possible deeds, and 244 names the condition under which nothing of that kind remains to be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"201 says those practised in evil do not fear 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு' while the excellent (விழுமியார்) do. 428 gives the general rule in the chapter on understanding: not fearing what is to be feared (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சாமை) is folly, and fearing it is 'அறிவார் தொழில்'. Read together, the fear in 201 is a working of knowledge and not timidity; Parimelazhagar on 428 lists sin, blame and ruin among the things to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"202 says evil deeds are to be feared more than fire ('தீயினும் அஞ்சப்படும்') because they yield evil. 129 sets fire against the tongue: a wound burned by fire heals, the scar burned by the tongue does not ('ஆறாதே நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு'). In both, fire is the harm that ends, used to measure a harm that lasts; Parimelazhagar on 202 makes this explicit, saying fire cannot follow one into another time, place or body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"202 ranks evil deeds above fire as a thing to fear. 896, in the Book of Wealth, says one burned by fire may still survive ('எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்') but those who keep wronging the great will not. The same comparison, fire as the survivable danger, is applied once to one's own deeds and once to the anger of the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"203 calls it the chief of all understanding to refrain from doing evil even to those who hate one ('செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்'). 312 says that not hurting in return even one who has hurt you out of hatred ('கறுத்து இன்னா செய்த அக்கண்ணும்') is the settled stand of the stainless. The same rule is called knowledge (அறிவு) in the householder's chapter and purity (மாசற்றார் கோள்) in the ascetic's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"203 asks only for restraint: do no evil even to enemies. 987 asks what worth (சால்பு) is good for if it does not do pleasant things even to those who did one harm ('இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்'). The Book of Wealth's ideal of the சான்றோர் goes one step beyond the householder's rule, from not returning harm to returning good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:204","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, virtue plots one's own. 319 states the return in terms of time: harm done to others in the forenoon brings harm to oneself in the afternoon, of itself ('பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்'). 319's 'தாமே' leaves the return impersonal where 204 gives it an agent, and Parimelazhagar on 204 adds the same point about speed: virtue begins plotting at the very moment the plotter does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"205 rules out poverty as a ground for evil deeds. 656 takes the hardest case: even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry ('ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்'), one should not do what the worthy condemn. Parimelazhagar notes that 656 sets aside a general rule of the dharma texts allowing wrong to feed one's dependants, which shows how far 205's principle is taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"205 quotes the plea 'இலன் என்று' as an excuse for wrongdoing. 1040 quotes the same plea, 'இலமென்று', as an excuse for sitting idle, and says the good woman called Land laughs at those who make it. The book hears 'I have nothing' twice as a pretext, once for evil and once for sloth, and rejects it both times.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:320","type":"agrees","note":"206: one who does not want afflicting things to overpower him ('நோய்ப்பால தன்னை அடல் வேண்டாதான்') should not do evil to others. 320: all pains rest on those who caused pain, so those who want freedom from pain cause none ('நோய் செய்யார் நோயின்மை வேண்டுபவர்'). The claim and its form (நோய், வேண்டு-) are nearly the same, stated once for the householder in the chapter on fearing evil deeds and once among the ascetic virtues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:208","type":"develops","note":"207 says the enmity of deeds does not leave and follows behind to kill ('வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்'). 208 gives the image for it: the ruin of evildoers is like a person's shadow that does not leave and stays at the foot ('வீயாது அடி உறைந்தற்று'). The repeated 'வீயாது' ties them, and both Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar say 208 illustrates what 207 asserted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"207 treats one's evil deeds as an enemy ('வினைப்பகை') worse than any outward one. 434, advising the king, says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin ('குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை') and so guarding against fault should be one's aim. The two verses, one in the Book of Virtue and one in the Book of Wealth, both move the real enemy from outside to one's own conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208: the ruin of those who did evil is like a shadow that never leaves a person. 36: virtue done now is, at the time one perishes, a companion that does not perish ('பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை'). Both say a person's deeds stay with him; the good deed stays as a companion and the evil deed's ruin as a shadow at his heel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208 uses a simile of something that cannot fail to happen: ruin stays with the evildoer as his shadow stays at his foot. 307 does the same for anger: the ruin of one who treats anger as a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which cannot miss ('கை பிழையாதற்று'). Both verses take 'கேடு' as their subject and end on a simile of certainty, one for evil deeds and one for anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:843","type":"develops","note":"209 assumes that doing evil is acting against oneself: one who loves himself stays away from it. 843 says the suffering that those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies ('செறுவார்க்கும்') to inflict. 843 describes the person who fails 209's test, and Parimelazhagar's comment there names poverty, blame and sin as what such people bring on themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:116","type":"agrees","note":"210: know ('அறிக') that one who does not turn aside to do evil has no ruin. 116: if one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know ('அறிக') 'I shall perish' ('கெடுவல் யான்'). They state the same law from its two sides with the same imperative, 210 for the one who keeps straight and 116 for the one who leaves the middle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"210 promises that the one who does no evil will not come to ruin. 117 says the world will not count as ruin ('கெடுவாக வையாது') the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 117 allows that the upright may be brought low and changes what counts as 'கேடு', which limits how literally 210's promise can be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"211 says duty-giving seeks no return (கைம்மாறு). 221, opening the next chapter, defines giving as giving to those who have nothing, and says everything else has the nature of 'குறியெதிர்ப்பை', a measured loan to be returned. 221 turns 211's point into a test: a gift that looks to a return is not giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"212 says all the wealth produced by exertion ('தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருள்') is for doing 'வேளாண்மை', benefaction. 613 says the proud distinction called benefaction ('வேளாண்மை என்னும் செருக்கு') rests in the quality called exertion ('தாளாண்மை'). The same two words are paired from opposite ends: in the Book of Virtue helping others is the purpose of earning, and in the Book of Wealth effort is the basis that makes helping possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"212 says wealth is for benefaction, with 'தக்கார்க்கு' read by Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as the worthy who receive it and by Parimelazhagar as the worthy who own it. 1006 describes the failure of this purpose: one who neither enjoys his great wealth nor gives anything to the worthy ('தக்கார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்') is an affliction (ஏதம்) to it. 1006 uses 'தக்கார்க்கு' plainly of recipients, which supports the Manakkudavar reading of 212.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"213 ranks ஒப்புரவு above anything to be had in the gods' world or this one. 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world ('மேலுலகம் இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று'). Both detach the worth of helping others from heaven as a reward: in 213 heaven holds nothing better, and in 222 the good stands even without heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213: neither in the world of the gods nor here ('புத்தேள் உலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்') is it easy to find another good like ஒப்புரவு. 363: there is no excellent wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either ('ஈண்டில்லை ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்'). The same two-world formula crowns benevolence in the householder's section and desirelessness in the renouncer's; taken literally the two superlatives compete, and the pairing shows each section naming its own highest good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 'ஒத்தது' in 214 as the way of the world (உலகநடை): one who knows and follows it lives. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world ('உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்') lack understanding however much they have studied. On that reading both make accord with the world the test of a person, of being alive in 214 and of having understanding in 140; Manakkudavar instead takes ஒத்தது as ஒப்புரவு itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:217","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"217 makes a tree the figure of a generous man's wealth: a tree that is medicine and does not fail ('மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரம்'). 576 makes a tree the figure of people who have eyes but no kindly regard: they are like trees joined to the soil ('மண்ணோடு இயைந்த மரத்தனையர்'). The book uses the tree in two ways, for what gives to all who come to it and for what stands unmoved, so the image alone carries no fixed value.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:218","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"218: even at a time without means ('இடனில் பருவத்தும்'), those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு. 955: even when what they have to give has shrunk ('வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும்'), an old family does not part from its character. The Book of Virtue credits this constancy to insight into duty ('கடனறி காட்சி'), and the Book of Wealth credits it to lineage ('பழங்குடி').","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:89","type":"parallel_domain","note":"219 redefines poverty for the benevolent man: it is not lacking goods but being unable to do good with them. 89 redefines it from the other side: poverty in the midst of having ('உடைமையுள் இன்மை') is the folly of not tending guests. In both verses poverty is measured by what one does for others and not by what one holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"220: if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth getting even by selling oneself ('விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). 780: a death at which the eyes of one's lord fill with tears is worth getting even by begging ('இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). The same closing formula takes something normally shunned, ruin or death, and declares it worth a degrading price, once for the benefactor and once for the soldier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:1080","type":"same_image","note":"220 speaks of a man selling himself ('ஒருவன் விற்றுக்கோள்') as a price worth paying for the ruin that benevolence brings. 1080 says the base are fit for one thing: when something befalls them, to sell themselves quickly ('விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து'). Self-sale is honourable in the first as the cost of giving and contemptible in the second as an escape from one's own trouble.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1054","type":"tension","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it were called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1054 says that asking from those who do not know concealment even in a dream is itself like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). They conflict if கொளல் is read, with Parimelazhagar, as accepting alms without exception; on Kalaignar's reading of கொளல் as taking goods from others by whatever fair means, the two address different acts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"222 holds receiving to be bad whatever is said in its favour. 1061 says that not asking is worth a crore even from those dear as one's eyes who give gladly without hiding (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும் இரவாமை). Both refuse the best case that could be made for receiving: 222 the claim that it is a good path, 1061 the excellence of the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:205","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the quoted plea 'I have nothing': 223 has இலன் என்னும் எவ்வம், 205 has இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க. In 205 the plea is an excuse for doing evil and is answered with the warning that one will have nothing all over again; in 223 it is a plea the well-born do not voice. In both the book refuses to let want serve as a reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's first reading, 223 says the well-born do not speak to others the beggar's degrading word 'I am poor'. 1044 says that even in the well-born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும்) poverty produces the lapse from which degrading words are born (இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு). The conflict depends on that reading of 223; if 'இலன்' is the giver's refusal to an asker, as Pope takes it, the two speak of different utterances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:90","type":"same_image","note":"In 224 the giver watches the asker's face and is uneasy until it turns glad. In 90 the guest watches the host's face and wilts, like the anicham flower when smelled, if that face changes (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). The face is the measure of the exchange in both, read in opposite directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"224 says the unease of being asked lasts until the asker's face is glad. 228 names what follows as a pleasure in its own right, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம், which Parimelazhagar explains as the joy the giver has from the receiver's rejoicing. 228 then uses that pleasure as a charge against those who hoard and lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:1057","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 gives the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face (இன்முகம்). 1057 gives the asker's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). The two describe one moment from both ends, and the asker's gladness in 1057 is what ends the giver's discomfort in 224.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:226","b":"k:228","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the act of putting wealth away (வை). In 226 feeding the destitute is the வைப்புழி, the place of deposit; in 228 the hard-eyed 'keep and lose' what they own (வைத்திழக்கும்). The chapter sets a store that holds beside a store that is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:85","type":"agrees","note":"227 promises that the one who shares his food will not meet hunger. 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests and eats what is left even needs sowing (வித்தும் இடல் வேண்டும் கொல்லோ). Both state as a rule that feeding others first secures one's own food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"227 praises habitual shared eating (பாத்தூண்). In 1107 the man compares his beloved's embrace to living in one's own home and eating one's own food after sharing it (தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The lover's simile takes the householder's sharing of 227 as its standard of pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:598","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of a pleasure the hoarder never learns, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம். 598, which Parimelazhagar applies to kings, says those without inner drive never reach the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pleasure of giving is missed through hardness in the one verse and through lack of energy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"228 says only that the hoarder keeps his goods and loses them. 1009 says how: bright wealth gathered by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The later verse fills in the 'losing' of 228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:527","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229 condemns those who eat alone to build up their own wealth. 527 says crows do not hide their food but call and eat (காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும்), and that gain belongs only to those of that nature. The rule against eating alone appears in the ethics of giving in 229 and as advice on keeping kin and prosperity in 527.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:968","type":"parallel_domain","note":"230 holds death sweet for one who can no longer give. 968 asks whether a life of tending the flesh is any medicine once greatness has lost its dignity (பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து). In both a life is not worth keeping once the thing that gave it worth is gone: giving in 230, honour in 968.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"1041 says that the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty itself (இன்மையின் இன்மையே இன்னாதது). 230 opens by saying nothing is more painful than death (சாதலின் இன்னாதது இல்லை), then reverses it for the person who cannot give. Taken together, 230's second half brings it into line with 1041: the want that stops giving is placed above death in bitterness.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"231 ends 'ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு' and 31 ends 'ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு': both use the figure of profit to a living being. 31 gives the place to virtue in general, which yields distinction and wealth; 231 gives it to renown that comes from giving. The shared formula shows 231 naming one particular form of the gain that 31 states broadly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"231 says that apart from living with renown through giving there is no profit for a living being (அது அல்லது ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). 122 says no gain for a living being is greater than restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). Read literally the two exclusive claims cannot both hold; the conflict disappears if each is taken as the emphasis its own chapter gives its subject.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:296","type":"tension","note":"232 makes giving the subject of all praise. 296 says there is no renown like not lying (பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை). They conflict if both are read as naming the single greatest source of renown; Parimelazhagar's gloss on 296, that truthfulness is the cause of renown in this life, keeps the claim just as strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:387","type":"parallel_domain","note":"232 says the world's speech settles as praise on the giver. 387 says that for one able to give and protect with sweet words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்கு), this world is as his own word would have it. In the chapter on kingship the same giving that earns the world's words in 232 earns its compliance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"233 says only renown stands undying. 156 says those who punish have a day's pleasure, while those who forbear have renown பொன்றுந் துணையும், which Pope and Parimelazhagar take as until the world ends. 156 measures a virtue's reward by the lasting quality that 233 attributes to renown, and gives it a source other than giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:234","b":"k:966","type":"same_image","note":"234 joins earthly renown (புகழ்) to honour in the gods' world (புத்தேள் உலகு). 966 names the same pair as the two things lost by following those who scorn you: it brings no renown and does not carry one to the gods' land (புகழ் இன்றால் புத்தேள் நாட்டு உய்யாதால்). In both verses renown here and the gods' world there are counted together, as gained in 234 and forfeited in 966.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:117","type":"agrees","note":"235 says a loss can be an increase. 117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்) the low state of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. Both deny that the decline of a good person's fortunes is a real loss, 235 from the side of renown and 117 from the world's judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"235 calls rare a death that is really a coming into existence. 780 says that if one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death (சாக்காடு) is worth getting even by begging. The soldier's death in 780 is an instance, in the domain of war, of the death 235 says only the skilled achieve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:362","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of தோன்றுதல் as birth, 236 treats being born with renown as good and not being born as only the better alternative for those without it. 362 says that if one wants anything it should be not to be born (வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை), with no exception. The conflict depends on the birth reading; on Kalaignar's reading, where 236 is about entering a field of activity, the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 236 as: if born human, be born with the qualities that lead to renown. 972 says birth is alike for all living beings (பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும்) and distinction differs through the difference in what they do. On the birth reading, 972 limits 236: no one is born with renown, so the 'appearing with renown' has to be made by deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"236 says: if you appear, appear with renown, and for those without it not appearing is better than appearing (தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று). 1003 says the appearance (தோற்றம்) of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown (இசை வேண்டா) is a burden to the land. Both judge a person's coming into the world by renown, and 1003 names the misers as a case of those who 'appear' without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:237","b":"k:1060","type":"parallel_domain","note":"237 tells those without renown to blame themselves and not their scorners. 1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி). In both the grievance against another person is turned back to one's own condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:1004","type":"develops","note":"238 says it is a disgrace to leave no legacy of renown. 1004 asks what the man whom no one wants (நச்சப்படாதவன்) supposes will remain as his எச்சம்; Parimelazhagar explains that he is unwanted because he has never given. 1004 applies 238's standard to the miser and finds nothing left.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"In 239 the land bears (பொறுத்த) a body without renown and yields less. 189 asks whether it is out of regard for virtue that the earth carries the weight (பொறை) of one who speaks ill of a person behind his back. Both picture the earth as bearing a moral load, and they differ in that 189's earth endures while 239's earth declines.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says the land's rich yield shrinks (வண்பயன் குன்றும்) under a person without renown. 560 says the yield of cows shrinks (ஆபயன் குன்றும்) if the guardian does not guard. The same phrase ties natural yield to human conduct, a private person's in 239 and the king's in 560.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1003","type":"agrees","note":"239 says land that has borne a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) will decline in its yield. 1003 says the appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the land (நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both make the person without renown a weight the earth carries; 239 adds the cost to the earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:970","type":"develops","note":"240 says that to live without renown is not to live. 970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of those with honour who will not live when disgrace comes (இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்). 970 shows a person acting on 240's equation, and the result is the renown 240 demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"240 uses the formula வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் for those who live without blame. 1033 uses the same formula for those who live by eating what they plough (உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்), with all others following and bowing for their food. The phrase 'they alone live' is awarded on two different grounds, a good name in 240 and self-reliance in 1033.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:72","type":"same_image","note":"72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves, while the loving belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). 251 describes the reverse movement: another's body (பிறிது ஊன்) taken to build one's own. Read together, the body is the measure in both: love gives it over, and the eater of flesh takes another's.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:315","type":"develops","note":"315 asks what understanding has achieved if one does not guard another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). 251 sets the same pair, self and other (தன் ஊன் / பிறிது ஊன்), the wrong way round: the other's flesh serves one's own. 315 gives the general test that 251's rhetorical question about அருள் applies to eating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:968","type":"same_image","note":"251 asks how one who eats another's flesh to enlarge his own (தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்கு) can exercise அருள்; 968 asks whether a life that nurses the flesh (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) is any medicine once dignity is lost. Both use ஊன், one's own flesh, for a body tended at the cost of something higher: அருள் in one verse, honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:247","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses explain அருள் by putting it beside பொருள்: 247 says that world is not for those without அருள் just as this world is not for those without wealth, and 252 says holding அருள் (அருளாட்சி) is not for flesh-eaters just as holding wealth (பொருளாட்சி) is not for those who do not guard it. 252 takes the comparison one step on: அருள், like wealth, is something that can be lost through carelessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"252 takes it as given that holding wealth is not for those who do not look after it (பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை). 376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however anxiously it is guarded, and what is one's own will not leave even if carried out and poured away (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). The conflict holds if ஆட்சி is read as possession, as Manakkudavar and Pope read it; Parimelazhagar reads it as getting the benefit of wealth, which softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"253 says the mind of one who has eaten a creature's body with relish does not turn to good (நன்னூக்காது), like the heart of one holding a weapon. 285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. In both, what the mind is fixed on, flesh or another's property, leaves no room for அருள்; Parimelazhagar glosses 253 the same way, as a mind that looks only to the meat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:254","b":"k:246","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use பொருள் in an unusual, moral sense alongside அருள். 246 says those who leave அருள் and do what should not be done have left பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்); 254 calls eating flesh பொருளல்லது. Parimelazhagar takes பொருள் in 254 as virtue (so that பொருளல்லது is sin) and in 246 as the lasting good one should have done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று), does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 255 gives hell a mouth (அண்ணாத்தல்), which on Parimelazhagar's reading has swallowed the eater of flesh and will not open again. In both the language of eating is turned on the human: the one who spares life is not eaten, and the one who eats is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:835","type":"same_image","note":"255 says that if one eats flesh, அளறு (the mire, hell) will not open its mouth (அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது), which Parimelazhagar explains as not opening to let him out again. 835 says a fool can in one birth make the அளறு he will enter and sink in for seven (எழுமையும் தான் புக்கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both use the same rare word for a hell one makes for oneself and does not get out of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:1059","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1059 asks what distinction givers could have if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). 256 says none would bring flesh to sell if the world did not kill for eating. Both reason from a counterfactual about one party to the existence of the other: askers make givers possible, and eaters make sellers of flesh possible.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:257","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"575 says that without kindly regard the eye is perceived as a wound (புண்ணென்று உணரப்படும்); 257 says flesh would not be eaten if people perceived it as another creature's wound (புண்ணது உணர்வார்). Both use புண் with உணர் to strip something of its usual standing, and both are about compassion: the eye without it is a sore, and food seen with it is a sore.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:258","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"174 says those of faultless vision who have conquered the senses (புலம்வென்ற புன்மையில் காட்சியவர்) will not covet, even thinking 'we have nothing'. 258 says those whose vision is free of fault do not eat flesh. Both attach an abstention that costs something, from another's goods or from meat, to காட்சி, and 174 adds the mastery of the senses that 258 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). 259 says not killing one creature and eating it is better than a thousand sacrifices. Both rank a plain restraint, not lying and not killing, above the religious practices that carry the most prestige.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing; 260 says all living beings fold their hands (கைகூப்பி) and worship the one who does not kill and refuses flesh. Both state what the non-killer receives in return, and both put it as a response of life itself: living beings honour him, and the taker of life leaves him alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:261","b":"k:157","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157 says that though others do you wrong, it is good to feel the pain and not do what is contrary to virtue (நோநொந்து அறனல்ல செய்யாமை). 261 defines தவம் by the same two parts: bearing pain, and causing none to living beings. The chapter on forbearance asks of the householder, in dealings with those who wrong him, what 261 gives as the whole form of austerity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:273","type":"develops","note":"262 says தவம் comes to those who already have தவம், and that for those without it taking it up is futile; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read this as merit from earlier births. 273 pictures a person without strength (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes on the form of the strong: a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. 273 shows what the futile undertaking of 262 looks like in practice. (The edition's text of 262 lacks the word அவம் at the start of the second line.)","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:373","type":"parallel_domain","note":"373 says that however many subtle books one studies, the understanding that is truly one's own still prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). 262 says austerity too succeeds only for those who already have it. Both hold that a discipline taken up now, study or austerity, yields only according to what the person brings to it; Parimelazhagar refers both to what earlier births have fixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"262 says that for those without தவம், undertaking it is futile. 620 says those who toil on without flagging or delay will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). If the தவம் one must already have is merit from former births, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it, then 262 makes present effort useless without a prior endowment, which is what 620 denies. On Kalaignar's reading, where it means present firmness and self-control, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 names the householder as the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை), along with the destitute and the dead. 263 asks, with a wry கொல், whether the rest have forgotten தவம் out of a wish to provide for renouncers (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). It turns the duty stated in 42 into an explanation, or an excuse, for why householders do no austerity themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:48","type":"qualifies","note":"263 speaks of the others, householders on Parimelazhagar's reading, as having forgotten தவம் while they provide for renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads 'keeps on their way' as supporting ascetics. On 48's account the provider has not forgotten austerity: his life already contains more of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:1036","type":"agrees","note":"1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) cannot keep their state. 263 assumes the same dependence: renouncers need others to supply their needs (துப்புரவு). Both verses note that renunciation rests on people who have not renounced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"29 says the anger of those who stand on the hill of good qualities cannot be withstood even for a moment (கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது), on Parimelazhagar's and Pope's reading; Kalaignar takes it to mean their anger does not last a moment. On the first reading it agrees with 264 that renouncers hold a power to ruin those who oppose them, though 264 makes it a matter of intention (எண்ணின்) and 29 of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:261","type":"tension","note":"261 says the form of தவம் is just this: bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை அற்றே). 264 says crushing foes (ஒன்னார்த் தெறல்) and raising friends come through தவம், if one so intends. Read plainly, austerity defined by harmlessness is credited three verses later with the power to destroy. Parimelazhagar eases this by taking the foes as enemies of virtue and by reading எண்ணின் as implying that ascetics do not normally think of such things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:899","type":"agrees","note":"899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), even the king breaks midway and loses his rule. Parimelazhagar reads them as people of high vows, and Pope likewise. This is the power of 264, ஒன்னார்த் தெறல் through austerity, seen from the side of the king who must not provoke it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:362","type":"qualifies","note":"265 says austerity is practised because through it whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted. 362 says that if one must want, one should want not to be born, and that this comes by wanting not to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்). 362 narrows what the வேண்டிய of 265 should be; Parimelazhagar reads 265 itself as referring to results in the next life and release, while Manakkudavar takes it to include enjoyments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share a formula: வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால் (265), what is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, and எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்துப (666), what is thought of is obtained as it was thought. 265 gives this as the reason austerity is striven for; 666 makes it depend on the planner's firmness (திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்). The promise made for austerity in the book of virtue is made for resolute action in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:348","type":"agrees","note":"348 divides people the same way: those who have thoroughly renounced have reached the head, and the rest, confused, are caught in the net (மயங்கி வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றையவர்). 266 says those who do தவம் do their own work and the rest act in vain, caught in desire (ஆசையுட் பட்டு). Both use பட்டு / பட்டார், being caught, for everyone outside the ascetic's path; Parimelazhagar supplies 'the net of desire' in his gloss on 266.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:308","type":"same_image","note":"308 says that even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன), not being angry is good if one can manage it. 267 says pain that burns (துன்பஞ் சுடச்சுட) makes those who can endure it shine like gold in fire. Both picture suffering as fire applied to a person; 308 asks only that it be borne without anger, and 267 says what the bearing produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:630","type":"parallel_domain","note":"267 says that for those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine (ஒளிவிடும்), as gold shines the more it is heated. 630 says that if one takes what hurts as pleasure (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்), there comes a distinction even one's enemies admire. The ascetic's gain from pain in the first book has a worldly counterpart in the second: in both, pain borne in the right way adds to the person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:620","type":"parallel_domain","note":"620 says those who toil on without flagging will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்); 269 says even getting past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்) is within reach of those with the power of austerity. Each uses the particle உம், 'even', to name the one thing thought unbeatable and then says sustained effort beats it: labour in the book of statecraft, austerity in the book of virtue. (The edition's text of 269 ends with a stray letter, தலைப்பட்டவர்க்குல்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:616","type":"parallel_domain","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and the absence of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). 270 says the have-nots (இலர்) are many because few practise austerity (நோற்பார் சிலர்). Both trace poverty to something people fail to do, but they name different things: present exertion in the chapter on labour, and austerity here, which the commentators take to bear fruit across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:842","type":"agrees","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's தவம் (பெறுவான் தவம்). 270 says the have-nots are many because those who practise austerity are few. Both explain who gets and who lacks by austerity, and 842 shows the idea used in passing in the second book, as an accepted explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"271 says the five elements laugh within (பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்) at the concealed conduct of a deceitful mind; 293 says that after a lie about what one's own heart knows, that heart itself burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both place the witness to a hidden fault inside the person; 293 moves it from the body's elements, which only laugh, to the heart, which punishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"In 271 the five elements laugh within at a hypocrite's hidden conduct; in 1040 the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing'. In both a part of the material world is the one that laughs at a human pretence, and Parimelazhagar glosses the laugh in 1040 too as inward (தன்னுள்ளே).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); 272 says a sky-high appearance (வானுயர் தோற்றம்) does nothing when the heart is in known fault. 272 applies the general rule of 34 to the specific case of the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:273","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 praises the one who guards the five senses with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்); 273 likens one of strengthless nature (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes the form of the strong to a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. Parimelazhagar reads the missing strength in 273 as the power to bring the mind under control, which is what 24 names as the mark of the real renouncer, so 273 shows the outward form of 24 without its substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 gives the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை); 274 speaks of doing 'what is not it' (அல்லவை) under cover of தவம் and compares this to a hunter snaring birds. Read together, the hunter simile shows the hypocrite doing the very thing, harm to creatures, whose absence 261 makes the definition of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"274 has the hunter hidden in the bush as the image of evil done under cover of austerity; 828 says a weapon hides even in an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and likewise in his tears. In both a gesture of piety is the cover for harm, once in ascetic life and once in false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:655","type":"same_image","note":"275 says the concealed conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many harms with the cry எற்றெற்று; 655 says do not do what will make you grieve saying எற்று ('what is this I have done'). The same exclamation of regret marks the test in both, in the renouncer's conduct and in the minister's action; Manakkudavar, however, takes the cry in 275 as other people's, not the doer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... துணை); 276 says none are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving as if they had renounced (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்). Parimelazhagar takes the deceived to be the givers, so the pairing shows what the false renouncer exploits: the duty of support that 42 lays on the householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:1198","type":"same_image","note":"276 ends 'வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்': none are harder than those who live by posing as renouncers. 1198, spoken by the woman in separation, ends with the same words: none in the world are harder than those who go on living without a kind word from the one they love. The shared formula uses வன்கண் in two senses, the cruelty of the deceiver in 276 and the grim endurance of the one left behind in 1198.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:706","type":"qualifies","note":"706 says the face shows what is strongest in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it (நெஞ்சம் கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்); 277 says there are people red outside and black inside. 277 limits 706: the face may betray a passing feeling to a skilled reader, but a settled outward appearance can be the opposite of the inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"277 says the world has people who look outwardly like the red kunri seed but are inwardly black like its tip (புறம் ... அகம்); 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய ... அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சர்). The same outside/inside split is described for the false ascetic and for the false friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"277 uses the kunri seed for its two colours, red body and black tip; 965 uses it for its size, saying even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri (குன்றி அனைய). The same seed serves both verses as a figure for a small dark flaw in something that otherwise looks fine, by colour in one and by measure in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is upright, so take people by the nature of their deeds (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்); 505 says one's own deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). The same rule appears as a test for ascetics in the book of virtue and as a test for choosing officers in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"279 warns against reading uprightness from shape, with the arrow and lute as examples; 667 says do not scorn on seeing someone's form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), because the great chariot has its small linchpin. One verse guards against being impressed by form and the other against despising it, and both replace form with function.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:281","b":"k:178","type":"parallel_domain","note":"281 tells one who wants not to be scorned to guard his heart from stealing anything at all (கள்ளாமை காக்க தன் நெஞ்சு); 178 says that what keeps one's wealth from shrinking is not coveting the goods in another's hand (வெஃகாமை ... பிறன்கைப் பொருள்). Each gives restraint toward others' property as the means to something one wants for oneself, standing in 281 and one's own wealth in 178; Parimelazhagar reads 281 differently, as about one esteemed for not despising release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:317","type":"agrees","note":"282 says even thinking in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods by stealth is wrong; 317 says the highest thing is not to do, with the mind (மனத்தானாம்), anything ill to anyone at any time in any degree. Both place the fault at the level of intention before any act, for theft in one and for harm in general in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure while seeming to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்); 660 says making wealth by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்து) and feeling secure in it is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. The same claim, that wrongly got wealth looks held and is being lost, is made to the renouncer in one book and to the minister in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:368","type":"develops","note":"368 says those without craving have no sorrow, and where craving exists sorrow comes on and on without fail (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்); 284 says desire hardened in stealing (கன்றிய காதல்) yields sorrow that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). 284 is the general law of 368 applied to one desire, with the same stress on sorrow that does not end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"284 speaks of காதல் grown in களவு and means desire for stealing, which ends in undying sorrow; in 1092 the man says the small look her eyes steal (கண் களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) is more than half of love's pleasure. The same word களவு is condemned as theft of goods in the book of virtue and prized as the stolen glance in the book of love, where it is a lover's speech and not a teaching about theft.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:251","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says அருள் with love is not found in one who watches to take another's goods; 251 asks how one who eats another creature's flesh to swell his own can hold to அருள் (எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்). Both test a claim to அருள் against a practice of taking from another for oneself, property in one case and the body in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:757","type":"qualifies","note":"285 opposes having அருள் in view to having பொருள் in view; 757 says the child அருள், born of அன்பு, is reared by the wealthy foster-mother பொருள். 757 limits how far 285 can be taken: the opposition holds for goods taken from others by watching for their lapses, not for wealth as such, which 757 makes the support of the same அருள் and அன்பு that 285 names.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:286","b":"k:474","type":"parallel_domain","note":"286 says those with hardened desire for stealing cannot stand and walk within அளவு (அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார்); 474 says the one who does not live in accord, does not know his measure (அளவறியான்) and admires himself perishes quickly. On Kalaignar's reading of அளவு in 286 as a limit one lives within, the two apply one idea, conduct kept inside a known measure, to the renouncer and to the king; Parimelazhagar instead takes அளவு in 286 as the reasoned measuring of the soul and its ends, on which reading the link is only verbal.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:175","type":"agrees","note":"287 calls stealing a dark knowledge (களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை) that is not found in those set on the strength called அளவு; 175 asks what a fine and wide understanding (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு) comes to if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone. Both deny that cleverness in the service of taking others' goods counts as real understanding.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:422","type":"develops","note":"422 defines understanding as what does not let the mind go wherever it has gone, but turns it from evil toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது அறிவு); 287 grants stealing the name அறிவு only with the qualifier கார், dark. By 422's definition the thief's skill fails as அறிவு, which explains why 287 marks it as darkened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:288","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 locates virtue in the mind being without stain; 288 says virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு just as concealment stands in the heart of those who know stealing (நெஞ்சத்து அறம் ... நெஞ்சில் கரவு). 288 adds to 34 that the heart is occupied either way: what one practises, measure or theft, decides whether virtue or deceit takes up residence there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:289","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealing do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்); 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without trace. Both make ignoring அளவு the cause of ruin, in the renouncer's conduct and in the management of means; Parimelazhagar reads அளவல்ல in 289 as wrong thoughts and not as excess of spending, so the likeness is of pattern, not of subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"290 gives the two outcomes of stealing and not stealing: loss of உயிர்நிலை, and the world of the gods (புத்தேள் உலகு) not failing; 121 gives the two outcomes of restraint and its lack: a place among the immortals (அமரருள்) and thick darkness. Each closes or opens its chapter with the same two-sided reward and penalty attached to a single virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"290 says that for those who steal the உயிர்நிலை fails, while for those who do not steal the world of the gods does not fail; 255 says உயிர்நிலை rests in not eating flesh, and that hell will not open its mouth for one who eats it. Both verses hang உயிர்நிலை, the standing of life in the body on Parimelazhagar's gloss, on one abstention and pair it with a fate after death; Manakkudavar reads the word in 290 as release itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:291","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"291 defines truth (வாய்மை) not as reporting what happened but as speech with no harm in it at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). 292 draws the consequence: even a falsehood stands in truth's place (பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த) if it yields faultless good. Read together, the second verse is the first's definition applied to its hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:113","type":"tension","note":"292 makes good results decisive: a falsehood that yields faultless good (நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்) counts as truth. 113 says gain got by leaving fairness is to be dropped that very day even if it yields nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). The conflict holds only if both are read as answers to whether a good outcome can license a breach of a norm; 113 is about gain and 292 about speech, and 292's புரைதீர்ந்த (free of fault) may already exclude what 113 forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:293","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"In 129 a burn from fire heals inside but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not; in 293 the liar's own heart burns him (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both use burning for the lasting damage of speech: the harsh word burns the hearer, the false word burns the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:294","b":"k:119","type":"parallel_domain","note":"294 says one who lives without falsehood in the heart (உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யாது) is present in the hearts of all the world. 119 says rectitude is speech without bend, provided there is surely no bend within (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை). In truthfulness and in impartiality alike, straight speech is counted only when the inside matches it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:48","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 sets the truthful speaker above those who practise austerity and giving. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). In both, the ascetic is the standard of comparison and an ordinary life rightly lived is placed above it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:296","b":"k:297","type":"develops","note":"296 says not lying gives all the virtues (எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்); Parimelazhagar takes எய்யாமை as 'without wearying the body'. 297 then concludes that one who keeps steadily to not lying does well even without doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). The second verse depends on the first: the others can be left undone because this one already supplies them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:601","type":"same_image","note":"299 names not lying the one true lamp for the worthy (பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு). 601 calls lineage an undimming lamp (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) that nevertheless goes out when the grime of sloth covers it. The lamp figure marks what a person's standing depends on, and 601 adds that such a lamp can be put out by neglect.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"301 counts restraint of anger only where the anger could have its way. 153 says the strength of strengths is bearing with fools (வன்மையுள் வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை). Both locate the real test of forbearance where one has the upper hand and nothing outside compels it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"302 says anger is bad where it cannot take effect, and where it can, nothing is worse (செல்லிடத்தும் இல்அதனின் தீய பிற); Parimelazhagar reads the two places as the stronger and the weaker. 861 advises avoiding opposition to the strong and pressing enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). They conflict if 302 is taken as a rule for everyone; if the anger chapter is addressed to ascetics, as Parimelazhagar holds, and 861 to a king, they are advice for different people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:895","type":"develops","note":"302's first half says anger where it cannot take effect is bad, which Parimelazhagar explains as harm coming back from the stronger party. 895 states that harm at its extreme: those a king of fierce might is angry with (வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) will not survive wherever they go. The chapter on not offending the great fills in what 302 leaves as a bare 'bad'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with an excess done to you, and that forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 303 uses the same verb for the anger itself: forget anger toward anyone (மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும்), since evils are born from it. One forgets the offence and the other the anger at it; 303 adds the reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"303 says evils are born from anger (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). 360 names anger (வெகுளி) with desire and confusion as the three whose very names must perish for pain to perish. What 303 says of anger as a source of evils, 360 places in a full account of the roots of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"304 counts the death of laughter and joy (நகையும் உவகையும்) as the harm that makes anger the worst enemy. 999 says that for those not capable of laughing (நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு) the great world lies in darkness even by day. 999 describes the state that 304 says anger produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it adjoins (சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி), burns even the safe raft of one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 526 says no one on earth has more people around him than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). The two give the loss and the gain of the same fact: kin stay with the one who is free of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"306 calls one's people a safe raft (ஏமப் புணை) that anger burns. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love certainly exists and the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. The same phrase names what keeps a person afloat; in one verse a passion destroys it and in the other a passion leaves the speaker without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:312","type":"develops","note":"308 says that even if someone does harm like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்), not being angry is good, if one can manage it (புணரின்). 312 says that even when someone has done hurt in hatred, not doing hurt in return is the settled stand of the stainless (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை). 308 governs the feeling and 312 the act that would follow from it; 308 alone concedes that it may not be possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"308 asks for no anger even under harm like fire. 852, in the chapter on hostility in the Book of Wealth, says that even if someone does what cannot be borne, meaning division, not doing harm in return is best (இன்னா செய்யாமை தலை). The same advice, with the same 'even if ... செயினும்' form, is given as virtue in one book and as policy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:487","type":"tension","note":"309 praises one who does not think of anger in the heart at all (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly on the spot but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). They conflict if both describe how a wise person treats anger inwardly, one removing it and the other keeping it for later; the conflict lessens if 309 is for the ascetic, as Parimelazhagar reads it, and 487 for a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:540","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all one has thought of is gained at once (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்) if one never thinks of anger in the heart. 540 says gaining what one has thought of is easy (உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிது) if one can keep thinking of it. Both promise the same result in nearly the same words and set a condition on the mind: one removes a thought, the other holds one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 tells one to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை). 311 refuses wealth that would require hurting others (பிறர்க்கு இன்னா செய்யாமை). Both grant that the gain is real and still rule out the means, one in the householder's ethics and one in the renouncer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:755","type":"parallel_domain","note":"755, in the chapter on making wealth, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்) should be let go and not embraced. 311 makes not hurting others the settled stand of the stainless even at the cost of wealth. The condition that 311 states for the pure is repeated in the Book of Wealth as a rule for acquisition itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:312","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"852, on hostility, says that even if someone does unbearable things intending division, not doing hurt in return out of hostility is the highest course (இகல்கருதி இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). 312 says not hurting in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the stand of the stainless. The same rule, in nearly the same words, is carried from personal purity into the politics of faction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:313","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"313 says that doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing to them (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), brings sorrow with no escape (உய்யா விழுமம்). 207 says people escape any enmity (உய்வர்) but the enmity of their own deeds follows and kills (வினைப்பகை வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்). The shared root உய் shows the reasoning: an enemy can be survived, one's own retaliation cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says bearing an excess done to you is good and forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 314 ends on விடல்: do good and let it go. Parimelazhagar takes this விடல் as forgetting both the hurt and the good done in return, which would make 314 the fuller form of 152's advice; the Tamil itself says only 'do and leave'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:158","type":"develops","note":"158 says to defeat those who did wrong out of arrogance by one's own worthiness (தம் தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as forbearance. 314 goes a step further: do them a kindness so that they feel shame. Both keep the vocabulary of contest (winning, punishing) while replacing retaliation, and 314 adds the active good that 158 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:317","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 317 makes the highest thing the absence of any mind-born ill toward anyone (மனத்தானாம் மாணா செய்யாமை தலை), in a chapter whose first two verses speak of the மாசற்றார். 317 gives 34's general claim a specific content: the unstained mind is one that harbours no hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:209","type":"parallel_domain","note":"209 says that if a person loves himself (தன்னைத்தான் காதலனாயின்) he should not come near evil deeds, however small. 318 asks why one who knows what hurts his own life does hurt to other lives. Both reason from self-regard, by different routes: 209 from the doer's own interest, 318 from the doer's own knowledge of pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the rule: what one has perceived as painful, do not do to another (துன்னாமை வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்). 318 turns it into a question: one who knows what hurts his own life (தன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னாமை தானறிவான்), why (என்கொலோ) does he hurt other lives? The second verse treats breaking the rule as something needing explanation; Parimelazhagar suggests delusion as the implied answer. (The edition's text of 318 has typos in its first line.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:208","type":"same_image","note":"208 says the ruin of those who do evil is like a shadow that never leaves and rests at the foot (நிழல் ... அடி உறைந்தற்று). 319 puts the same inevitability in time: forenoon and afternoon (முற்பகல், பிற்பகல்). One verse uses space and the other the day's two halves to say that consequence is attached to the deed; the day figure also fits a shadow lengthening after noon, though the text does not say so.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:320","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"320 says all pains fall on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 169 admits that the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright meet ruin, and says only that this is to be pondered (நினைக்கப் படும்). 169 concedes cases that 320's rule does not show on its face; Manakkudavar's reading of 320, that present pain comes from hurt done earlier, and Parimelazhagar's of 169, that such cases are to be examined for causes, are the way the commentators reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:349","type":"develops","note":"349 says the moment attachment ends births are cut off, and otherwise impermanence is what is seen (மற்று நிலையாமை காணப்படும்). 331 faults the understanding that takes the unlasting for lasting. 349 explains why the error in 331 matters: for one still attached, impermanence is the standing condition, and Parimelazhagar on 331 likewise ties the error to attachment and rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:351","type":"parallel_domain","note":"331 calls lowest the paltry understanding that takes what does not last for lasting (நில்லாதவற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்). 351 says a joyless birth comes from the confusion that takes what is not real for real (பொருளல்லவற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும் மருள்). The two verses are built on the same sentence frame; 331 applies the misperception to duration and 351 to reality, and 351 adds the consequence, rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:112","type":"tension","note":"332 says great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) arrives and leaves like a theatre crowd, with no exception stated. 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude does not decay and is a support to his descendants (சிதைவின்றி எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). If 332 is read as a claim about all wealth, 112's durable wealth contradicts it; the conflict eases if 332 is taken, with Parimelazhagar, as wealth that stays only while its cause in good deeds lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:376","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however it is guarded, and what is one's own will not go even if thrown out (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). 332 says wealth comes and goes like a crowd at a show. 376 puts a cause behind 332's picture: coming and going follow one's allotted portion; Parimelazhagar reads 332 the same way, the crowd coming for the dance and leaving when it ends.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:592","type":"agrees","note":"332 likens the coming of great wealth to a crowd gathering at a dance hall and its going to that crowd dispersing. 592 says possession of goods does not stay and slips away (பொருளுடைமை நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்), while possession of inner drive is real possession. The impermanence of wealth taught to the renouncer is restated in the Book of Wealth, where it becomes a reason to value energy and not a reason to withdraw.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:437","type":"agrees","note":"437 says the wealth of the miser who does not do with it what should be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது) perishes without anything surviving. 333 says wealth does not stay and should be turned at once into what does. The Book of Wealth gives the same instruction negatively: unused wealth is simply lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"334 presents each day as a blade cutting life, with no one exempted. 326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது) of one who lives by not killing. Read literally, 326 exempts the non-killer from the daily loss of life that 334 treats as universal; the conflict depends on taking 326 as a promise about lifespan and not as praise of the vow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:342","type":"parallel_domain","note":"342 says if you want what renunciation brings, renounce while there is still something (உண்டாகத் துறக்க), which Parimelazhagar takes as 'while there is time'. 335 says to hurry to good deeds before the death rattle. On that reading the next chapter applies the same urgency to renouncing that 335 applies to good deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says this world has the 'greatness' that one who was here yesterday is not here today (நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை). 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except high renown (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்றில்). 233 agrees with 336 about everything else and names one exception; the renouncer's chapter offers none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:235","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says a person who was yesterday is not today (இன்றில்லை). 235 speaks of a death that is really existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு), possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains as the body of fame surviving. The two verses use the same pair, உள and இல், in opposite directions: 336 for the plain fact of death and 235 for the rare death that leaves something that is.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:338","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"338 pictures the body as a குடம்பை, shell or nest, that the bird leaves empty; 340 pictures it as a lodging (துச்சில்) where life stays as a tenant without a home of its own (புக்கில்). Both make the body a dwelling and life its occupant. 338 shows the departure, and 340 asks why there has to be one; Manakkudavar takes the permanent home to be liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:339","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"339 presents death and birth as alternating like sleep and waking, which both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read as a cycle that keeps turning. 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). 356 limits 339: the waking that follows each death is the rule only for those who have not seen the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"341's doubled யாதனின் யாதனின் ... அதனின் அதனின் allows release thing by thing: each thing left stops hurting. 343 demands that everything wanted be let go together (வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு). Parimelazhagar notices the difference under 341, calling giving up everything at once the chief way while granting that giving things up one by one still removes the pain each brings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:626","type":"parallel_domain","note":"341 says that whatever one has left (யாதனின் நீங்கியான்), from that one has no pain (நோதல் இலன்). 626, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, asks whether those who never guarded wealth saying 'we have got it' (பெற்றேமென்று ஓம்புதல் தேற்றாதவர்) would grieve saying 'we have lost it' (அற்றேமென்று). The renouncer's rule reappears in the book of wealth as a habit of mind for someone who still owns things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:629","type":"develops","note":"341 ties freedom from pain to having left the thing itself (நீங்கியான்). 629 says one who does not crave the pleasure in pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not suffer the pain in pain; Parimelazhagar reads this as someone who goes on experiencing the pleasure without wanting it. The pairing shows the same result claimed for an inward letting go, without the outward leaving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 tells one to do virtue without saying 'we will see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது). 342's உண்டாகத் துறக்க is read by Parimelazhagar as 'renounce while there is time', i.e. in youth, so the joys last long; Manakkudavar instead reads it as 'if you want gain for your life'. On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on renunciation repeats, for renouncing, the earlier rule against postponing virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a person who runs household life by the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்). 342 says that after renouncing there are many things to be had here, and urges renouncing if one wants them (வேண்டின் ... துறக்க). The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Pope does: on that reading 46's rhetorical 'what is gained?' gets the answer 'much' in 342.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:369","type":"agrees","note":"342 says that after renouncing, many things come about here (ஈண்டு இயற்பால பல), which Parimelazhagar takes as joys in this life. 369 says joy is unbroken even here (இன்பம் இடையறாது ஈண்டும்) once craving, the sorrow of sorrows, is gone. Both place the reward of letting go in this world (ஈண்டு), not only after death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"6 describes God as the one who quenched the five whose gates are the senses (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்) and promises long life to those who keep to his path. 343 lays the same thing on the renouncer as a duty (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை). The renouncer is asked to do what the invocation names as the mark of the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"126, in the householder's chapter on restraint, speaks of drawing in the five like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), which protects through seven births. 343 uses a harder verb for the renouncer: the field of the five must be killed or crushed (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை), and every wanted thing dropped at once. Read together they show two degrees of the same discipline, withdrawal that can be reversed and destruction that cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:263","type":"qualifies","note":"344 makes possessionlessness the nature of austerity. 263 asks whether the others have forgotten austerity out of a wish to provide for those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). The pairing shows that the ascetic's having nothing is sustained by householders who keep possessions in order to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1036","type":"qualifies","note":"344 says having nothing at all is natural to the vow (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கு ஒன்றின்மை) and that owning anything brings back confusion. 1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up what is desired' have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's owning nothing rests on others who produce food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1050","type":"qualifies","note":"344 treats having nothing (ஒன்றின்மை) as the proper state of the vow. 1050 speaks of those who have nothing to enjoy (துப்புரவில்லார்) and yet do not fully renounce (துவரத் துறவாமை), calling them death to salt and gruel. Lacking things is therefore not the same as renouncing: poverty without the inner giving up is counted as a burden, not as austerity.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:73","type":"qualifies","note":"73 says the bond of the precious life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is the way that came to be joined with love, so embodiment is given a purpose. 345 says that for those undertaking to cut off births (பிறப்பறுக்கல் உற்றார்க்கு) the body itself is excess and other ties (தொடர்ப்பாடு) pointless. The value 73 gives the body-bond holds within the life of love; 345 marks where, for the renouncer, it stops holding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:627","type":"parallel_domain","note":"345 says even the body is too much for one seeking to end birth. 627, in the book of wealth, says the great do not take trouble as trouble, knowing the body is a target for suffering (இலக்கம் உடம்பு இடும்பைக்கு). Both start from the body as a liability, but 627 uses that view to keep steady in worldly trouble while 345 uses it to argue for giving up every tie.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"50 says one who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வானுறையும் தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). 346 promises the one who ends 'I' and 'mine' a world higher than the gods' (வானோர்க்கு உயர்ந்த உலகம்). Set side by side the two verses give a scale: the good householder reaches the gods, the renouncer goes beyond them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"346 says one who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' (யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு) enters a world above the gods. 72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others even to their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). The householder's love already loosens 'mine', and even the body as 'I', by giving it to others; renunciation does it by cutting the notion itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:438","type":"parallel_domain","note":"347 uses பற்று for attachment in general and says its holders are held by griefs. 438, on a ruler's faults, names miserliness as 'the clinging heart' (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) and says it is not to be counted with any other fault. The same word and the same refusal to let go appear in the domain of wealth, where Parimelazhagar glosses it as not releasing goods when they ought to be released.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றினைப் பற்றி விடாஅதவர்க்கு). 940 says that as a gambler loves the game more with each loss, the life grows more fond with each suffering it undergoes (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the object of that fondness to be the body. 940 supplies what 347 leaves unexplained: suffering does not loosen the grip but tightens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:276","type":"qualifies","note":"348 sets those who have fully renounced above everyone else. 276 says none are harder-hearted than those who have not renounced in the heart (நெஞ்சின் துறவார்) and live by deceiving as though they had (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து). The praise in 348 therefore applies to renunciation of the heart; the outward form without it is placed among the worst.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:365","type":"develops","note":"348 gives the prize only to those who renounce thoroughly (தீரத் துறந்தார்). 365 says those called 'freed' are those freed of craving, and the others are not freed in that way (மற்றையார் அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்), whatever else they have given up. 365 specifies what 348's 'thoroughly' requires: the giving up must reach craving, not only things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:349","b":"k:361","type":"develops","note":"361 says craving is the seed that yields unceasing birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). 349 says the moment attachment is cut off, that cuts off birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). One verse names the cause and the other states what follows when the cause is removed; Parimelazhagar makes the same cause-and-effect point under 349.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:359","type":"develops","note":"350 says to hold to the hold of the one who has none. 359 says that if one knows the support and lives so that supports perish (சார்புணர்ந்து சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains due to come will not come. The two are built alike on a repeated word, பற்று and சார்பு, and Parimelazhagar reads the சார்பு that is known as the true reality on which all rests, so 359 states the result of the practice 350 prescribes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:351","b":"k:1002","type":"same_image","note":"351 says an inglorious birth comes from the delusion that takes what is not real for real (மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு). 1002 ends with the same words: the delusion of thinking everything comes by wealth, and so hoarding without giving, produces an inglorious birth. The book of wealth applies the metaphysical formula to one concrete error, treating money as the thing that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:352","b":"k:243","type":"parallel_domain","note":"352 has darkness leave and joy arise for those who see truly. 243 says those whose hearts are joined with compassion do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 243's wording supports Parimelazhagar's reading of இருள் in 352 as a dark world of suffering, and shows the book promising escape from it by compassion as well as by knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 says one who lives on earth (வையத்துள்) as one should is placed among the gods who dwell in the sky. 353 uses the same pair, earth and sky (வையத்தின் வானம்), and says the sky is the nearer of the two for one who is clear of doubt. The householder gets there by right living and the knower by certainty, and in both the sky is brought within reach of someone still on earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"353 says that for those who have left doubt and become clear (ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு) the sky is nearer than the earth. 702 says one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை) should be held equal to a god. In both, knowing free of doubt lifts a person toward the divine, once in the knowledge of reality and once in a courtier's reading of minds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:2","type":"parallel_domain","note":"2 asks what learning has achieved (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல்) if the learned do not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge. 354 says command of the five senses has no benefit (பயமின்றே) for those without perception of the real. Both verses grant an attainment and then deny its fruit when the final step is missing, learning without worship in one and discipline without insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:126","type":"qualifies","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection for seven. 354 says the attained fivefold awareness yields no fruit without true perception; Parimelazhagar reads ஐயுணர்வு as the mind withdrawn from the senses and held under control, and the missing fruit as release, while Manakkudavar reads it as knowing all that the five senses can know. On Parimelazhagar's reading sense-restraint earns protection across births, as 126 says, but not the end of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:857","type":"parallel_domain","note":"355 makes seeing the true thing the definition of understanding. 857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார்); Parimelazhagar takes it there as the substance of the texts on right policy that bring success. The same phrase marks, in the book of wealth, what a mind bent on quarrel cannot perceive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:398","type":"develops","note":"398 says the learning acquired in one birth protects through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). Learning in the book of wealth is an asset carried across births; learning that ends in seeing the real is said to end the births themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:357","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"356 speaks of those who learn and see the true thing; 357 adds the mind's own work: if the mind examines and knows with certainty what is (ஓர்த்து ... உள்ளது உணரின் ஒருதலையா), one need not think of birth again. Parimelazhagar reads the two as successive means, hearing instruction in 356 and reasoned examination in 357. The printed text here has உணரான், but Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's glosses read உணரின், which the sense requires.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"351 says a joyless birth comes from the delusion (மருள்) that takes the unreal for real. 358 goes a step further and calls birth itself a folly (பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை); Parimelazhagar explains this as naming the effect in place of its cause. The chapter moves from delusion as the cause of birth to birth and delusion spoken of as one thing, removed together by seeing the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"358 speaks of 'the folly called birth' departing when the real is seen. 835, in the chapter on folly in the book of wealth, says a fool can in one birth's doing make the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both tie பேதைமை to the chain of births, 358 as its root and 835 through the fool's deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:5","type":"parallel_domain","note":"5 says the two kinds of deed do not attach (சேரா) to those devoted to the Lord's praise. 359 says the pains due to attach will not attach (சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்) to one who knows the support and lives so that supports perish; Parimelazhagar takes these as the fruits of stored deeds still awaiting experience, and Manakkudavar takes சார்பு itself as the hold of deeds. Devotion in 5 and knowledge with practice in 359 are credited with the same effect on karma.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:347","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold on to attachment. 359 gives the other case: for one who knows the support and lives so that supports fall away (சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains that were due do not come to cling. 347 states the bondage and 359 the way out, in the same language of things that cling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:4","type":"agrees","note":"360 says pain perishes when desire, anger and confusion perish down to their names (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் ... நாமம் கெட); Parimelazhagar glosses the three as wanting, aversion and ignorance. 4 describes God as the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no suffering. The state 360 sets as the goal is the nature 4 gives to the Lord, with the same result, the end of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"360 lists desire, anger and confusion as what must perish for pain to end. 431 lists three faults whose absence gives a ruler's prosperity its dignity: arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as pride, anger and lust. A similar triad, sharing anger and, on that gloss, desire, is the obstacle to release in one book and to good rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 364 defines purity as the absence of craving (அவாவின்மை). Taken together, 364 names the stain that 34 leaves unnamed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:275","type":"develops","note":"275 warns that the hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' (பற்றற்றேம் என்பார்) brings many harms. 365 says who may rightly be called freed (அற்றவர் என்பார்): only those freed of craving. 275 shows the claim made falsely; 365 gives the inner criterion that the claim has to meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, one of which is craving (அழுக்காறு அவா வெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல்). 366 says craving is what deceives a person (வஞ்சிப்பது அவா), and that fearing it is itself virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). 366 takes one item from the list in 35 and gives the reason for singling it out: it works by deceit, so it needs constant watch.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says fearing what should be feared is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்) and leaves the object open. 366 names an object, craving, and calls the fear of it virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). The general rule about right fear in the book of statecraft gets a specific content in the renouncer's chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:1075","type":"same_image","note":"Fear and craving appear together in both verses with opposite roles. In 1075 fear is the whole conduct of the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), with a little more if there is something they crave (அவாவுண்டேல்); Parimelazhagar takes the fear as fear of punishment. In 366 fear is directed at craving itself and is called virtue. The pairing separates fear that serves craving from fear that guards against it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"266 says those who do austerity do their own work, and the others, caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு). 367 says that if craving is cut off thoroughly, the deed that does not fail (தவாவினை) comes in the way one wishes. They state the same link from opposite sides: desire makes action futile, and its removal makes action unfailing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all that one has thought of is reached at once if one does not think of anger in the heart (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்). 367 says the unfailing deed comes as one wishes (தான் வேண்டுமாற்றான் வரும்) once craving is cut. Each verse removes one passion, anger or craving, and promises that what one intends then arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:341","type":"develops","note":"341 says a person has no pain from each thing he has left (யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல் அதனின் அதனின் இலன்), taking objects one by one. 368 says those without craving have no sorrow at all (அவாஇல்லார்க்கு இல்லாகுந் துன்பம்). 368 moves from the objects given up to the craving behind them, so the relief is complete instead of item by item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:347","type":"agrees","note":"347 says sorrows hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள்). 368 says that where craving exists sorrow keeps coming without fail, one after another (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்). Both describe suffering as continuing for as long as its cause is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான்) and calls hardship natural does not come to sorrow (துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). 368 says those without craving have no sorrow. The renouncer's doctrine reappears as practical advice for a person at work in the world, where Parimelazhagar relates it to the pains of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:369","b":"k:854","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share one sentence frame. 369: joy is unbroken if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes (அவாவென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). 854: it yields the joy of joys if the sorrow of sorrows called hostility perishes (இகலென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). The same title, sorrow of sorrows, is given to craving in the book of virtue and to hostility in the book of statecraft, each as the worst thing in its own field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"370 says that giving up craving gives, in that very state (அந்நிலையே), a nature that does not shift (பேரா இயற்கை). 349 says the moment attachment ends (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே) it cuts off birth. Both make the result immediate on the cause ending, with no further step between.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"370 says craving has a nature that is never filled (ஆரா இயற்கை அவா). 940 gives a picture of that: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும்), a living being's love grows with each suffering (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar takes this as love of the body. 940 shows why satisfying craving cannot end it, which is why 370 says it has to be given up.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:617","type":"qualifies","note":"617 says the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth (மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி) and the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the one without sloth. 371 says sloth (மடி) and its opposite are themselves produced by the fate of loss and the fate of gain. In 617 fortune depends on whether one is slothful; 371 adds that whether one is slothful depends on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:618","type":"tension","note":"371 says tireless effort arises from a fate of gain and sloth arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 618 says lacking good fortune (பொறியின்மை) is no one's disgrace, but lacking effort after learning what should be known is a disgrace (ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி). If sloth itself is produced by fate, as 371 says on a plain reading, the line 618 draws between blameless fortune and blameworthy idleness is hard to hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ்); Parimelazhagar adds that this happens even to one who has understanding. The protection 429 promises rests on understanding, and 372 says understanding itself can be taken away by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:859","type":"agrees","note":"372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை). 859 says that when gain is coming a person does not look at hostility (இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால்), and to bring ruin on himself he looks to excel at it. In both, approaching gain or ruin shows first as a change in the person's own judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:823","type":"parallel_domain","note":"823 says that even after learning many good things it is hard for the unworthy to become good in heart (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும் மனநல்லர் ஆகுதல் மாணார்க்கு அரிது); Parimelazhagar applies this to enemies. 373 says that after much study a person's own understanding still prevails. The same limit on what books can change is stated once for understanding and once for the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:169","type":"parallel_domain","note":"169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்), and gives no answer; Parimelazhagar says the wise look for the earlier deed behind it. 374 notes a like mismatch between wealth and understanding and gives it as the two-fold nature of the world. One verse separates wealth from goodness and the other separates it from wisdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:408","type":"develops","note":"374 states as a general fact that wealth and clear understanding are separate lots. 408 judges one result of that: wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). 408 takes for granted the mismatch that 374 explains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:375","b":"k:483","type":"tension","note":"483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the right means and knowing the time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). 375 says that in making wealth all that is good turns bad and bad turns good (நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம்); Parimelazhagar takes the good and bad things to be time, place, means and action, reversed by fate. On that reading the favourable conditions 483 relies on do not secure the result, and the two conflict. Kalaignar reads 375 differently, as good and bad deeds turning out contrary to intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:376","b":"k:659","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however carefully it is guarded (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if thrown out. 659 says all that is taken with others' tears leaves with tears, and what is of the good kind (நற்பாலவை) pays later even if lost. Both say some goods stay and some go whatever one does; 659 gives a test the person controls, the way the wealth was gained, where 376 gives only fate's allotment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:377","b":"k:1005","type":"qualifies","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்), and treats this as the miser's own failing. 377 says even those who have gathered crores can enjoy them only as the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் ... துய்த்தல் அரிது). Both separate owning from enjoying; 377 places part of enjoyment outside the owner's choice, which limits the blame in 1005.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:378","b":"k:344","type":"develops","note":"344 says having nothing is natural to austerity (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை). 378 looks at those who already have nothing through poverty and says they would renounce, but for what fate makes them undergo. Parimelazhagar makes the connection: they have already lost what a renouncer gives up, and still suffer because their intent differs. Having nothing is the condition of austerity in 344, and 378 shows it is not enough by itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:626","type":"agrees","note":"379 asks why those who see things as good when good comes should grieve (அல்லற்படுவது எவன்) when it does not. 626 asks whether those who never clutched at wealth saying 'we have got it' would grieve (அல்லற்படுபவோ) saying 'we have lost it'. Both are rhetorical questions with the same verb and the same symmetry between gain and loss. 626 adds the practical point that grief at loss depends on how one held the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:380","b":"k:619","type":"qualifies","note":"380 says fate is the strongest thing and gets ahead of any plan. 619 grants that a thing may not come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), and adds that effort still pays the wage of the body's labour (மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்). 619 accepts the limit that 380 states and marks out what remains for effort within it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:823","type":"qualifies","note":"391 treats right conduct as what should follow flawless learning. 823, on false friends, says that even after studying many good things (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும்) it is hard for the unworthy (மாணார்) to become good at heart. It limits 391: study does not by itself produce the standing-by-it, which is why 391 has to command it separately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:834","type":"develops","note":"391 asks for two things in order: learn without flaw (கசடறக் கற்க), then stand in keeping with it (நிற்க அதற்குத் தக). 834 names the person who does the first and skips the second: one who has studied, understood and even taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்கா) is the greatest of fools. The pair shows that the second half of 391 is not an afterthought: learning without it is classed as folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:392","b":"k:445","type":"same_image","note":"392 makes numbers and letters the eyes of the living. 445 says a king goes about with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with care. Both treat sight as something acquired from outside oneself, through learning in one case and through chosen advisers in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 denies real eyes to the unlearned. 418 says ears not pierced by listening (கேள்வியால் தோட்கப்படாத செவி) are of a deaf kind even though they hear. The next chapter repeats the pattern for the other sense: an organ counts only when it has been trained by learning or listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:643","type":"develops","note":"394 describes the learned as people whose company others want again after it ends. 643 defines speech as what binds those who heard it and makes even those who did not hear want it (கேளாரும் வேட்ப). 643 gives the means, speech, for the effect 394 describes.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"394 says the work of the learned is to meet so that others are glad and to part so that others think of them (உள்ளப் பிரிதல்). 839 says, ironically, that friendship with fools is very sweet because parting from them (பிரிவின்கண்) brings no pain at all. Both judge a person by what the parting leaves behind; the fool is the case where it leaves nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"395 praises learning in the stance of one who lacks before one who has. 724 tells even the learned to state what they have learned before the learned and take more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). The asking stance of 395 is thus not only for beginners; it continues among the learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:1066","type":"qualifies","note":"395 uses the poor standing before the rich (உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல்) as its figure for the learner, and on Parimelazhagar's reading those who learned standing so are foremost while those who would not are lowest. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even asking water for a cow. Read together, the posture the book calls degrading when the object is goods is the one it honours when the object is learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:397","b":"k:753","type":"parallel_domain","note":"397 says that for the learned any land is one's land and any town one's town (யாதானும் நாடாம் ஊராம்). 753 says the unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind (எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று) and cuts away the darkness there. Learning and wealth are each credited with making foreign places open to a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:399","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"399 assumes the learned person's learning gives the world joy. 650 says those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others grasp it (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom without scent. It adds the condition 399 leaves unstated: the world's delight depends on being able to convey the learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:241","type":"parallel_domain","note":"400 gives the title of real wealth to learning. 241 gives it to grace (அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம்), with the reason that wealth of goods is found even among the base. The same move appears in the Book of Virtue with a different winner, and 241's reason, who can hold it, differs from 400's, whether it can be lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:411","type":"same_image","note":"400 says learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth and other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல மற்றையவை). 411 says the wealth of the ear is the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துளெல்லாம் தலை). Adjacent chapters use the same 'true wealth' figure for study and then for listening, without ranking the two against each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:401","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"401 says holding forth in an assembly without full book-learning (நிரம்பிய நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்) is like playing dice without a marked board. 728 says those who have learned many things are still of no use if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. Together they give both halves: speech without learning has nothing to stand on, and learning without speech yields nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:402","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"402 compares the unlearned man's desire to speak (சொற்காமுறுதல், as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it) to a woman without breasts desiring womanhood. 727 compares the learning of one who fears the assembly to a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி among enemies. Both use a figure of lacking what one's role requires, and they cover the two opposite failures: wanting to speak without learning, and having learning without the nerve to speak. Kalaignar reads 402 differently, as desiring to hear the unlearned man's words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:715","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 715 says the best of all good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). 715 turns the rule 403 gives the unlearned into a general virtue of the assembly that binds capable speakers too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:729","type":"qualifies","note":"403 counts silence before the learned as the unlearned person's merit. 729 says those who have studied and understood yet fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). Silence is therefore not good in itself: it is right for the one with nothing to say and a failing in the one who has something.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"404 says that however good an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்) may be, those with understanding do not accept it. 423 says understanding is seeing the true substance of a thing from whoever's mouth it is heard (யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும்). If 404 means the wise set aside what the unlearned say even when it is good, it conflicts with 423's rule to judge the matter and not the speaker; on Parimelazhagar's reading, that they only decline to credit him with real brightness because an occasional hit is accidental, the conflict is smaller.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:636","type":"develops","note":"404 says native brightness without learning is not accepted by the wise. 636 says nothing over-subtle can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 636 states positively what 404 implies: natural intelligence gains its force when joined to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:717","type":"parallel_domain","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing collapses once he converses. 717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines out (விளங்கும்) among those able to examine words without fault. Conversation with the competent is the test in both, with opposite results for the unlearned and the learned.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:1046","type":"same_image","note":"405 says the unlearned person's self-regard gives way when he engages and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar supplies that the talk is with a learned person. 1046 says that even when the poor speak of good matters well understood, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The same closing phrase marks two ways speech fails: in 405 for want of learning in the speaker, in 1046 for want of standing with the hearers, though the content is sound.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:572","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 grants the unlearned existence only and compares them to land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both reduce a person lacking a quality to mere existence and express it through land, as barren soil in one and as weight on the ground in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:730","type":"same_image","note":"406 says the unlearned are no more than 'they exist' (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are like barren saline ground that yields nothing. 730 says those who, fearing the arena, cannot convey what they have learned are, though they exist, equal to those who do not (உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர்). The same bare-existence wording puts the learned who cannot speak in nearly the place of the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"407 says good looks without understanding are worth what a clay doll's are. 667 says not to scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), since the great chariot has its small linchpin. Both deny that appearance measures a person, 407 for the handsome and empty, 667 for the slight and capable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"407 compares the good looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding to a well-decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 1020 compares the movements of those with no shame within to a wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) made to seem alive by its string. Both use a doll for a human form missing its inner part, understanding in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:558","type":"parallel_domain","note":"408 says wealth is worse than poverty when it sits with the unlearned (வறுமையின் இன்னாதே). 558 says having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை) under a ruler who does no justice. Both invert the usual ranking of wealth over poverty under a stated condition: the holder's lack of learning in one, the king's injustice in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"408 says wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good. 837 shows what happens when a fool gets great wealth: strangers feast and his own people go hungry. 837 gives one concrete form of the harm; Manakkudavar likewise reads 408 as harm done to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:133","type":"parallel_domain","note":"409 says the unlearned, though born high, do not have the worth (பாடு) of the learned born low. 133 says having conduct is good family, and lapse from it becomes low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Both make birth give way to something acquired, learning in 409 and conduct in 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). 420 calls those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear மாக்கள், and asks what it matters whether they die or live. The next chapter carries the comparison on, from those who have not studied to those who do not even listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 makes book-learning the difference between people and beasts. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and that the distinction of human beings is having shame (நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). The two verses give different answers, learning and shame, to what sets humans apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 says the unlearned differ from the learned as beasts from people, though all are human in form. 1071 says the base look exactly like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a likeness the speaker has seen nowhere else. Both deny full human standing to some who have the human shape, for lack of learning in one and for baseness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"412 treats listening as food for the ear (செவிக்குணவு) and lets the stomach have a little (சிறிது) only when that food is absent. 420 turns the same ear-and-mouth pairing into a judgment: for those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear (செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்), living and dying come to the same. The chapter's ranking of the two appetites starts as a rule for mealtimes and ends as a measure of a life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:946","type":"parallel_domain","note":"412 allows the stomach only a little (சிறிது), and Parimelazhagar explains the word by saying heavy eating multiplies disease and desire. 946, in the chapter on medicine, says well-being stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்) and disease with the heavy eater (கழிபேர் இரையான்). The same restraint at the table is asked for in the name of listening in one verse and in the name of health in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:413","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"413 makes listeners equal to the oblation-fed (ஆன்றாரோடொப்பர்). 702 says one who knows without doubt what is inside another (அகத்தது உணர்வான்) should be taken as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடொப்பக் கொளல்). In both, a human being is ranked with the gods for a faculty of taking in what others carry: their teaching in one, their unspoken thought in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:414","b":"k:403","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 414 addresses the same person (கற்றிலனாயினும்) and tells him to listen (கேட்க). The silence that 403 only recommends as damage control becomes in 414 the means of gaining what he lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:139","type":"develops","note":"415 says the words from the mouth of those with conduct (ஒழுக்கமுடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்) are like a staff where the ground is slippery (இழுக்கல் உடையுழி). 139 says of the same people (ஒழுக்கமுடையவர்க்கு) that speaking evil with the mouth, even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்), is not possible. 139 gives the reason 415 needs: those who do not slip in what they say are the ones whose words can steady someone who is slipping.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:447","type":"parallel_domain","note":"415 presents the words of people of conduct as the staff that keeps a person upright on slippery ground. 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையார்). Both verses locate safety in another person's speech; 447 applies it to a king and makes the helpful words explicitly unwelcome ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says well-grounded listeners do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும்). 139 says that for those who have conduct, speaking evil even by a slip (வழுக்கியும்) is not possible. The same guarantee, speech that holds even under error, is attached to listening in the Book of Wealth and to conduct in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says those whose wide listening rests on their own close inquiry (இழைத்துணர்ந்து ஈண்டிய கேள்வியவர்) do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லார்). 199 says those of stainless vision, free of delusion (மருள்தீர்ந்த மாசறு காட்சியவர்), do not say empty things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). The two verses have the same shape, a kind of person whose speech stays sound even when the mind lapses, and they credit it to different sources: listening in one, clear vision in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:418","b":"k:577","type":"parallel_domain","note":"418 denies real hearing to ears that listening has not opened. 577 says those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்), and those who have eyes do not lack it. The form of argument is the same, an organ is counted as present only when it is used for its higher purpose, applied to instruction in 418 and to kindness in 577.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:95","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says being humble and sweet-spoken (பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல்) is a person's ornament, and nothing else is. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to come by without fine listening. 95 holds humble speech up for everyone, and 419 adds a condition: it has to be prepared for by listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:844","type":"develops","note":"419 says humble speech is hardly to be had without fine listening. 844 defines வெண்மை, shallowness of understanding, as the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pair links lack of instruction and self-praise from both ends: 419 says the uninstructed will not speak humbly, and 844 says boasting of one's understanding is itself the mark of lacking it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:214","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 treats those without the ear's taste as people whose death and life are the same. 214 says only one who knows what is fitting truly lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is counted among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). The book repeatedly denies real life to those who lack one defining quality; here the quality is listening, and in 214 it is knowing one's duty to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like saline ground that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 420 says of those who have no taste for what the ear takes in that their dying or living makes no difference (அவியினும் வாழினும் என்). The chapters on not learning and on listening reach the same verdict, a life that merely exists; Manakkudavar reads 420, as Parimelazhagar reads 406, as about being of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 asks what it matters whether they die or live, those மாக்கள் who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for all living beings (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல), and the distinction of மாந்தர் is having shame. Both verses treat eating as what humans share with every creature and put human worth elsewhere, in the ear's taste for one and in shame for the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"421 names understanding as the tool that guards against ruin (அற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி). 781 asks what guard is as hard for hostile action to get past as friendship (வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு). The Book of Wealth offers two protections that are not walls or weapons, one within the person and one between persons, and praises each in the vocabulary of defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 says one who guards the five with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்) is a seed for the highest ground; the goad makes the senses elephants, as Parimelazhagar spells out. 422 gives understanding a similar job, not letting one go where one has gone (சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா), which Parimelazhagar explains with a rider directing a horse. 422 goes a step past 24's holding back: it also sorts good from evil and steers toward the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"422 says understanding does not let one go wherever the mind has gone, but draws away from evil and directs toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). 787 says friendship removes one from what ruins and sets one on the path (அழிவினவை நீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து). The two verses use the same pair of actions and the same verb of steering (உய்); what understanding does for a person from inside, the friend does from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"424 makes it part of understanding to put things so that hearers take them in easily. 650 says those who cannot lay out what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom with no scent. 650 gives the failure case of 424's first half: learning that cannot be conveyed is counted as lacking something essential. The first word of 650 is misprinted in this edition, though the image is clear from the commentary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"424 says understanding sees the fine meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள்); Manakkudavar takes this as seeing what they mean without their spelling it out. 701 praises one who knows what is intended by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 701 carries the second half of 424 one step further, from reading what lies within words to reading what is never put into words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"115 says loss and increase are not absent from life, and that not bending in the heart (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Parimelazhagar records a second reading of 425, in which the opening and closing are expanding in wealth and shrinking in want. On that reading the two verses say the same thing, that a person should stay the same through gain and loss, one under the heading of fairness and the other of understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:544","type":"same_image","note":"425 says embracing the world is brightness (உலகம் தழீஇயது ஒட்பம்). 544 uses the same verb twice: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு) of the king who rules embracing his people (குடிதழீஇ). In 544 the embrace is returned, which gives a concrete reason why 425 counts drawing the world close as intelligence. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' in 425 to the high-minded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:782","type":"parallel_domain","note":"425 says understanding is what has neither opening nor closing (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது), which Parimelazhagar reads as steadiness in friendship with the high-minded, unlike a pond flower that opens and shuts. 782 says the friendship of the wise fills like the crescent moon and that of fools shrinks like the moon after full. Both verses judge attachment by a natural cycle; 782 praises the bond that grows, while 425 asks for one that does not swing in either direction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:850","type":"develops","note":"426 makes accord with the world part of what understanding is. 850 says one who says 'it is not' of what the world says is (உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான்) will be set down as a demon on earth. 850, in the chapter on shallow understanding, extends 426 from conduct to belief and states the cost of breaking with the world's view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:372","type":"qualifies","note":"427 makes knowing what is to come the mark of having understanding, as if it were a trait of the person. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப்படுக்கும் இழவூழ்) and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ்). Read with 372, the division in 427 is not fixed: the same person may see ahead or fail to, depending on what fate is at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"427 defines those with understanding as those who know what is going to be (ஆவதறிவார்). 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 429 gives the use of the foresight that 427 only defines: knowing beforehand matters because it lets one guard beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:461","type":"develops","note":"427 separates the wise from the unwise by whether they know what will be (ஆவது). 461 tells one to act after weighing what will be lost, what will come of it (ஆவதூஉம்), and the gain that follows later. 461 turns the foresight of 427 into a procedure, a reckoning of outcomes made before acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:428","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"428 makes fear of the right things the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). 1075 says fear is the whole basis of proper behaviour among the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Together they show that fear in itself does not separate the wise from the low; what separates them is that the wise fear what deserves it and the base behave only for fear of being punished.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:435","type":"agrees","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead have no affliction that comes to shake them (அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்). 435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like a straw-heap before fire. They are the positive and negative statements of one rule; 435 stands in the chapter on faults, where Parimelazhagar supplies 'fault' as what comes, while 429 speaks of harm in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:442","type":"develops","note":"429 credits freedom from shock to an understanding that guards against what is coming. 442 tells a ruler to cherish and keep those who can remove the trouble that has come and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). 442 shows that the foresight of 429 need not be the ruler's own: it can be secured by choosing the right people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:591","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 defines having by understanding: the wise have all, and the unwise have nothing whatever they own. 591 uses the same turn for energy of mind: it is what makes someone 'one who has' (உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம்), and those without it do not really have what they have (உடையது உடையரோ). The book redefines possession in the same words for two different inner qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). 841 says lack of understanding is the lack among lacks (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை), and the world does not count other lack as lack. 841, opening the chapter on shallow understanding, restates 430 from the side of poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:431","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"431 lists செருக்கு first among the faults whose absence gives prosperity its dignity. 180 names a செருக்கு it approves of, 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல் ஈனும்); Parimelazhagar there glosses the word as wealth. Read together they separate arrogance from a pride that consists in refusing to covet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:531","type":"develops","note":"432 lists 'மாணா உவகை' among a ruler's faults; Parimelazhagar takes it as joy beyond measure. 531 says why such joy is dangerous: the slackness that comes from the elation of great joy (சிறந்த உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு) is worse than excessive anger. 531 supplies the mechanism for the fault 432 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:1028","type":"agrees","note":"432 calls honour that has passed beyond the fine a fault in a ruler. 1028 says that for one raising his family, idling and minding his மானம் brings ruin (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). Both treat a misplaced sense of honour as a fault, one in the king and one in the head of a house.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"433 describes how those ashamed of blame weigh a small fault of their own. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up raised high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 adds the public side: in a person of standing a small fault is seen large by others too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"433 says the blame-fearing treat a millet-sized fault as palmyra-sized. 965 says even people like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. 965 gives the outward fact, that a tiny act does lower the great, which makes the inward magnifying in 433 an accurate estimate and not an exaggeration.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:434","b":"k:304","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin. 304 asks whether there is any other enemy (பகையும் உளவோ பிற) than anger, which kills laughter and joy. 304 applies to one named fault the claim 434 makes of fault in general: the real enemy is inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:435","b":"k:535","type":"agrees","note":"435: the life of one who doesn't guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand (முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான்) will later grieve over his own error. The same failure is given two outcomes, sudden destruction in 435 and late regret in 535.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"437: the wealth of one who clings to it without doing what should be done with it (செயற்பால செய்யாது இவறியான்) perishes without lasting. 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who keep what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) don't know the joy of giving. Both assume hoarded wealth is lost anyway; 228 adds what the hoarder missed in the meantime.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"437 says wealth perishes when its owner does not do the things that ought to be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது ... கெடும்). 466 says one perishes by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). The miser of 437 is a case of 466's second half: ruin by omission.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"437 says the miser's wealth perishes because he does not do what should be done with it; Parimelazhagar lists these as virtue, increase of wealth and enjoyment. 1006 says one who neither uses his wealth himself nor gives to the deserving (தான்துவ்வான் ... ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்) is an ஏதம், a harm, to great wealth. 1006 spells out the two uses left undone and turns the miser himself into the wealth's affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:432","type":"qualifies","note":"432 lists இவறல் as one of three faults in a ruler, beside excessive honour and excessive joy. 438, on Parimelazhagar's reading, takes it out of any such list as greater than the rest (எற்றுள்ளும் எண்ணப்படுவதொன்று அன்று). Manakkudavar reads 438 differently, as saying miserliness is not to be valued as anything, and on that reading the two verses simply agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:1002","type":"develops","note":"438 says the miserliness called the clinging heart (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) is not to be counted among any others; Parimelazhagar takes this to mean it exceeds all other faults. 1002 names what lies behind it, the delusion (மருள்) that everything comes by wealth, so that one clings without giving (ஈயாது இவறும்), and gives its result as an inglorious birth. 1002 supplies a cause and a consequence for the fault 438 ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:474","type":"develops","note":"439 commands: never admire yourself (வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை). 474 gives the outcome for one who doesn't live in accord with others, doesn't know his measure and admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்): he perishes quickly. Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 439, saying that self-admiration keeps one from knowing place, time and strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:652","type":"same_image","note":"439 ends 'நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை', do not desire a deed that yields no good. 652 ends with the same phrase and extends it: one must always avoid a deed that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை). The rule is given in 439 as part of a ruler's guarding against faults and in 652, on Parimelazhagar's reading, as a rule for ministers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:866","type":"develops","note":"440: if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without the love being known (காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்), his enemies' designs come to nothing. 866, from the enemy's side, says one of blinding anger and excessive desire (கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is an opponent whose enmity is worth having. Together they show the reasoning: a visible passion is an opening for an enemy, which Parimelazhagar states in his note on 440.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:441","b":"k:795","type":"parallel_domain","note":"441 tells a ruler to choose, after weighing, the friendship of those who know virtue and are mature in understanding (கேண்மை ... தேர்ந்து கொளல்). 795 tells anyone to examine and take the friendship (நட்பு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்) of those able to make one weep, to rebuke, and to teach the right way. The same instruction, to select friends for their capacity to guide, is given for the king's advisers and for ordinary friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:435","type":"develops","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes will burn like straw. 442 tells the ruler to cherish and take as his own those whose nature is to remove the affliction that has come and to guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). The foresight 435 demands of the ruler is, in 442, something he obtains through the people he keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"442 describes the people a ruler should keep: they remove the trouble that has come and guard in advance against more. 787 describes friendship in similar terms: it removes what leads to ruin (அழிவினவை நீக்கி), sets one on the path, and shares the suffering when ruin comes. The great adviser and the friend are given the same protective work, though 787 adds a sharing of suffering that 442 does not ask of the adviser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:443","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of rare things (அரியவற்றுள் எல்லாம் அரிது). 781 asks what is as hard to make as friendship (செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின்), and what guard is as hard for an enemy's work to get past. Both rank a bond with others as the hardest thing to obtain, and 781 adds that it is also the strongest protection, which is what chapter 45 goes on to claim for the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:891","type":"parallel_domain","note":"444: to conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself are one's own is the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 891: not slighting the power of the able is the chief of all the safeguards of those who guard themselves (போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). The two verses use the same ranking formula for the two sides of dealing with the great, winning them as the highest strength and not offending them as the highest protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"444 places the chief strength in attaching oneself to those greater than oneself; Parimelazhagar adds that this means following their lead. 985 says the strength of those who accomplish things is to bow (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). Both locate strength in deference and not in standing alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"445 tells the ruler to choose his counsellors after deliberation (சூழ்வாரைச் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்), since they serve as his eyes. 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after considering a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). Both put the selection of the people one deliberates with before the deliberation itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:581","type":"same_image","note":"445 says a ruler proceeds with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with thought. 581 says a ruler should hold spies and the respected treatise as his eyes (மன்னவன் கண்). The king's 'eyes' are advisers in one verse and intelligence plus learning in the other, and in both he sees through something other than himself. This follows Parimelazhagar's reading of 445; Manakkudavar takes its second line differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:639","type":"develops","note":"445 says counsellors must be chosen with care because the ruler sees by them. 639 states the cost of choosing badly: a minister at one's side who plots harm (பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி) is worse than seventy crore enemies. 639 shows what the care in 445 protects against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"446 says enemies can do nothing against one who keeps the company of the worthy. 460 says there is no support greater than good company (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை) and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company. 460 states as a general rule what 446 applies to a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:868","type":"agrees","note":"446: against one who can conduct himself as having the worthy for his company (தக்கார் இனத்தனாய்), enemies have nothing left to do. 868 gives the reverse case: one with no good qualities and many faults is without company (இனன் இலன்), and that is a protection to his opponents. Both verses use the word இனம் and both make a ruler's exposure to enemies depend on who stands with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"447 speaks of the ruler who keeps rebukers. 638 gives the matching duty of the one beside him: even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). 638 extends the idea to the case where the king is not the willing listener 447 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:784","type":"parallel_domain","note":"447 values supporters for their rebuking (இடிக்கும்) and says a ruler who has them cannot be ruined. 784 defines friendship by the same act: it is not for laughing but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps (மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). Rebuke is treated as the purpose of the bond in both statecraft and friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:449","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"449 says there is no standing (நிலை) for those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its base. The image of a structure that needs a prop is applied to a king in one verse and to a household in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"450 says abandoning one's tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than the enmity of many. 800 says to hold close the friendship of the stainless (மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மை) and to be rid of the friendship of the unfit even at a price. 800 gives the same instruction for friendship and adds its other half, which bonds to drop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:816","type":"parallel_domain","note":"450 weighs losing the good against the enmity of many and finds it ten times worse. 816 weighs close friendship with a fool against being a stranger to the wise and finds the second ten million times better (கோடி உறும்). Both rank relationships by a multiplied comparison, and in both the quality of the person outweighs whether the relation is friendly or hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொள்பவன்) is more wretched than the mad. 450 uses the same phrase as its measure: letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than that (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). Read together, what 873 treats as an extreme of folly becomes the smaller of two harms in 450.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"451 says greatness fears small company (சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை) while smallness takes it for kin. 201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear them but the excellent do (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). In both, what marks the better person is fear of the thing the worse person is at ease with, once about company and once about deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"452 says water takes the nature of the land, and understanding the nature of one's company (இனத்தியல்பு). 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால் காட்டும்) and speech shows the family one was born in. Both treat a person as carrying the mark of the ground they stand in, 452 for the company kept and 959 for birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:1323","type":"same_image","note":"452 uses water changing with the nature of the land it meets (நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர் திரிந்து) for understanding taking on the nature of one's company. In 1323 a lover calls the pair those joined as water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார்), and Parimelazhagar explains that image by water taking on the land's nature. The same picture of water absorbed into soil is a caution about company in one place and a figure of union in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:453","b":"k:505","type":"tension","note":"453 says perception comes from the mind, but the word that one is 'such a person' comes from one's company (இன்னான் எனப்படும் சொல் இனத்தான் ஆம்). 505 says the touchstone of greatness and smallness is each person's own deeds (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as that and nothing else. If 453 is read as saying what a person really is, and not only what people call them, the two name different tests of character: company or deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 455 says purity of mind comes resting on purity of company. The book thus gives inner purity two supports, one's own truthfulness and the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"456 says no deed fails to turn out well for those whose company is pure (இனந்தூயார்க்கு இல்லை நன்றாகா வினை), and Parimelazhagar gives the reason: their deeds are thought through with good company. 462 states that mechanism directly: nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:457","b":"k:651","type":"qualifies","note":"457 says goodness of mind gives gain (ஆக்கம்) and goodness of company gives all praise as well (இனநலம் எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்). 651 has the same two-step form but says goodness of companions gives gain and goodness of action gives all that is wanted (துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் தரூஉம் வினைநலம் வேண்டிய எல்லாம் தரும்); Parimelazhagar there reads companions as giving wealth only. Company holds the higher place in 457 and the lower in 651, where one's own action outranks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:458","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"458 says that even for the worthy who have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a safeguard (ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). 448 says a king without the guard of people who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்) perishes even with no one to ruin him. Both use the same word for protection (ஏமம்), and 448 shows what the company protects against: one's own unchecked faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:459","b":"k:243","type":"develops","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined to அருள் do not enter the dark world of pain (அருள் சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க்கு இல்லை இருள் சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம் புகல்). 459 agrees that the next world comes from goodness of mind (மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமை) and adds that even this is made secure by goodness of company. The afterlife that 243 rests on the heart alone is given a second support in 459.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:32","type":"parallel_domain","note":"460 is built on the form 'nothing beyond X as support, nothing beyond Y as affliction' (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை). 32 uses the same form and the same word ஊங்கு for virtue: no gain beyond virtue, no ruin beyond forgetting it (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை). The pairing shows good company being given, in the book on statecraft, the kind of unqualified ranking the opening chapters give to virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:450","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no support greater than good company and nothing that afflicts more than bad company (தீயினத்தின் அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்). 450 says letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). 460 names keeping bad company as the worst harm and 450 names losing good company; both rank these above open enemies, and Parimelazhagar glosses 460's second half as enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"461 lists the outcomes to be weighed before acting: loss, gain and later yield. 471, opening the next chapter with the same imperative, lists the strengths to be weighed: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). The second supplies what the first's calculation of gain and loss depends on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:676","type":"develops","note":"461 tells one to weigh what will be lost, what will be gained and the later yield, and then act (அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ... ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்). 676 has the same closing command and also three items, but different ones: what it takes to finish, the obstacles, and the great yield at completion (முடிவும் இடையூறும் ... படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்). 461 is an account of cost and return; 676 keeps the return and adds the course of the work itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:462","b":"k:568","type":"agrees","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company and thinking it through themselves (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). 568 gives the negative case: the fortune of a king who does not think matters through with his circle (இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன்) and then rages will shrink. Both make deliberation with one's இனம் the condition of success, stated once as promise and once as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:932","type":"parallel_domain","note":"463 says the wise do not undertake what loses the principal in hope of gain. 932 asks whether gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred (ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்), have any way of living well. The gambler is the plain case of the venture 463 rules out, a small gain pursued at the cost of what one already had.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:172","type":"parallel_domain","note":"464 says those who fear the harm called disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவிலதனைத் தொடங்கார் ... அஞ்சுபவர்). 172 has the same form: those ashamed of unfairness do not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain (பழிப்படுவ செய்யார் நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்). In both a fear or shame is what holds a person back, in 172 from a wrong act and in 464 from an unexamined one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says not fearing what should be feared is folly and fearing it is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்). 464 gives one such fear and what it does: fear of disgrace (இளிவென்னும் ஏதப்பாடு) keeps people from beginning what they have not made clear. The general rule of 428 is applied in 464 to the start of an undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:473","type":"develops","note":"465 says rising without full deliberation (வகையறச் சூழாது எழுதல்) benefits the enemy. 473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on in enthusiasm and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). 473 names one of the things left unconsidered, one's own strength, and shows the cost to oneself where 465 shows the gain to the enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"465 says setting out without thinking through every aspect is a way of setting one's enemies in a planting bed (பகைவரைப் பாத்திப் படுப்பது); Parimelazhagar reads this as establishing them in ground where they grow. 718 says speaking before those who understand is like pouring water into the bed of a growing plant (வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர் சொரிந்தற்று). The same garden bed stands for fostering growth in both, wanted in 718 and brought on oneself in 465.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:879","type":"same_image","note":"465 pictures a rash attack as planting the enemy in a bed where he will grow. 879, in a chapter on enemies, says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, since when hardened it wounds the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க). Both treat the enemy as a plant whose growth is the danger: 879 says to stop it early, 465 warns against tending it by one's own haste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"466 says one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானும் கெடும்). 510 says trusting someone unexamined and doubting someone already examined and trusted both bring unending trouble (தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Each verse sets an error of excess beside an error of defect and gives them the same result, once for action and once for trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:672","type":"develops","note":"466 says both doing the unfit and leaving the fit undone bring ruin. 672 gives the same two-sided rule for timing: delay what should be done slowly, and do not delay what must be done without delay (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை). 672 carries 466's point, that holding back is no safer than acting, into the question of when to act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:467","b":"k:509","type":"parallel_domain","note":"467 fixes the order for action: think first, then resolve, and no thinking postponed until after. 509 fixes the same order for trusting people: trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக). In both the examination belongs wholly before the commitment, and the commitment is then not to be reopened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:616","type":"qualifies","note":"616 says effort produces fortune and lack of effort brings want (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்). 468 says exertion that is not made by the right way (ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்) will give way even if many stand guarding it. 468 puts a condition on 616's praise of effort: it has to follow the proper method, and neither strain nor helpers make up for that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:640","type":"parallel_domain","note":"468 says effort without the right method fails whatever support it has (பலர் நின்று போற்றினும் பொத்துப்படும்). 640 says those without practical capacity leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order (முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர் திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்). The two are complementary failures: effort without method in 468, and method on paper without the ability to carry it out in 640.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"469 says doing good can itself be a mistake if the character of the person is not taken into account. 987 asks what worth has achieved if it does not do what is sweet even to those who have done one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்), which makes the recipient's conduct irrelevant. The conflict holds if 469's நன்றாற்றல் is read as doing good in general, as Pope and Kalaignar take it; Parimelazhagar narrows it to a king's use of gifts and soft words toward other kings, and on that reading the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"469 says there is error even in doing good when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word, while the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல் கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 is an instance of the difference in character 469 says must be known first: the approach that works on one kind of person does nothing with another.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"470 says to think and do what will not be scorned, because the world does not accept what does not accord with oneself (கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு). 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world lack understanding, however much they have studied (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்). Both make the world's acceptance a test that sound judgement has to meet, 470 for a ruler's choice of action and 140 for conduct in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"470 tells the one who acts to avoid what the world will scorn and not accept. 280 tells the ascetic that neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if he gives up what the world has condemned (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்); Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' there as the high-minded. The same measure, what the world rejects, governs a king's policy in one verse and a renouncer's conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:250","type":"qualifies","note":"471 makes comparing strengths the test of whether to act. 250, in the chapter on compassion, uses the same comparison as a brake: when going against those weaker than oneself (தன்னின் மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து), one should think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Being the stronger party permits action in 471, while 250 asks the stronger party to remember what that position feels like from below.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"471 tells one to weigh four strengths before acting: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலி, தன்வலி, மாற்றான் வலி, துணைவலி ... தூக்கிச் செயல்). 675 gives a second checklist with the same closing command, five things to think through until the darkness clears: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன் ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). Read together, 471 measures the parties to a contest and 675 adds the circumstances of the act, and only the deed (வினை) is on both lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:861","type":"develops","note":"471 says to weigh one's own strength against the opponent's before acting (தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). 861 states what follows from that weighing when choosing an enemy: avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursue enmity with the weaker (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). 861 is 471's comparison turned into a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"472 says nothing fails for those who know what they can do and what must be known and proceed resting in that (ஒல்வது அறிவது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company and thinking it over themselves (தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்). The promise is the same in both and the condition differs: knowledge of one's own capacity in 472, counsel and deliberation in 462.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:483","type":"parallel_domain","note":"472 promises that nothing fails for one who knows his capacity (ஒல்வது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the means and knows the time (அருவினை என்ப உளவோ கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). The chapters on knowing strength and knowing time each make the same claim for their own subject, which suggests each is meant as one necessary condition, not a sufficient one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:594","type":"qualifies","note":"594 says gain asks the way and goes to one whose ஊக்கம் does not waver (அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையான் உழை). 473 says many who did not know their own strength set out on ஊக்கம் and broke midway (வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). The same word names the quality praised in chapter 60 and the cause of failure here, so 473 limits 594: drive pays only when it is matched to a measured strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against letting a task perish in the middle of doing it (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), because the world gives up on those who leave work unfinished. 473 describes how that happens: people start on enthusiasm without knowing their strength and break midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 612 demands finishing; 473 puts the remedy before the start, in measuring one's strength.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:899","type":"same_image","note":"473 says many who did not know their strength broke midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 899 uses the same figure for a king: if those of exalted resolve are angered, even the king breaks midway and loses his kingship (இடைமுரிந்து வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்). Parimelazhagar reads 473 as starting something against those stronger than oneself, and 899 gives one such case, a ruler who misjudges the power of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"474 lists three failings that bring quick ruin: not living in accord (அமைந்தாங்கு ஒழுகான்), not knowing one's measure (அளவறியான்) and admiring oneself. 863 lists the traits of one who is easy for an enemy, and two of them match: he does not know (அறியான்) and does not fit in with others (அமைவிலன்). The chapter on what makes a good enemy to have describes, from the other side, the man 474 says will perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"474 says the one who admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்) perishes quickly. 978 uses the same phrase to define smallness: greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In 978 self-admiration is a mark of character, and in 474 it is a miscalculation of strength with a practical result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:667","type":"same_image","note":"475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if too many are piled on (பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும்). 667 says not to scorn people for their appearance, since there are those who are like the linchpin of a great rolling chariot (உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு அச்சாணி அன்னார்). Both use the axle of a vehicle to say that size misleads: the light thing can break it and the small thing holds it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"475 says a light load breaks the axle when there is too much of it (அப்பண்டம் சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). Parimelazhagar takes the verse as an indirect statement that a strong king who makes enemies of many because each is weak loses his strength when they combine. 873 says this outright: one who, alone, takes on the enmity of many (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்) is more foolish than the mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:889","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 says that feathers, light as they are, break an axle when piled too high. 889 says that enmity within, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. Both warn against judging a danger by the smallness of its unit, 475 because small things add up and 889 because of where the small thing sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:596","type":"qualifies","note":"596 says that whatever one thinks of, one should think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்), and that even if this is thwarted it is as if it were not. 476 says that those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and push beyond it (அஃதிறந்து ஊக்கின்) end their lives. 596 sets no ceiling on aspiration, while 476 sets one on action: there is a point past which striving higher is fatal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:858","type":"parallel_domain","note":"476 says pushing on past the tip of the branch (இறந்து ஊக்கின்) ends one's life. 858 uses the same verb of hostility: leaning away from it is gain, and if one pushes on to excel in it (மிகல் ஊக்கின்), ruin pushes on in turn (ஊக்குமாம் கேடு). In both, the drive that the book elsewhere praises becomes the cause of ruin when it is carried past a limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:943","type":"parallel_domain","note":"477 says to give knowing the measure, for that is the way of guarding wealth while bestowing it (அளவறிந்து ஈக அது பொருள் போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி). 943 says to eat knowing the measure once the last meal is digested, for that is the way to carry the body far (அளவறிந்து உண்க அஃது உடம்பு பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). The two verses have the same build, a command to know the measure followed by 'that is the way', applied to wealth in one and the body in the other. Both verses are printed with அறவறிந்து in this edition; the reading அளவு is from Parimelazhagar's gloss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:463","type":"agrees","note":"478 says a small income does no harm if spending does not exceed it. 463 says those with understanding do not undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain (ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை ஊக்கார்). Both put keeping what one has ahead of getting more: 478 for the regular flow of income and spending, 463 for a single venture.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:512","type":"qualifies","note":"512 says the work should go to one who widens the sources of income (வாரி பெருக்கி) and increases the yield. 478 says that even if the channel of income is narrow (ஆகாறு அளவிட்டிதாயினும்) there is no ruin, as long as the channel of outgo is not wider (போகாறு அகலாக்கடை). 512 values enlarging income, and 478 adds that safety depends on the relation between the two channels and not on the size of the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:947","type":"parallel_domain","note":"479 says the life of one who lives without knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) perishes. 947 says that one who eats a great deal without regard to the measure of his digestive fire (தீயளவன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்) gets disease without measure (நோய் அளவின்றிப் படும்). Ignoring a measure brings ruin of wealth in one verse and of health in the other. 479 is printed with a corrupted character in அளவறிந்து in this edition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:218","type":"qualifies","note":"218 says those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a time without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்). 480 says ஒப்புரவு that does not weigh the limit of one's means (உளவரை தூக்காத) quickly destroys those means. The Book of Virtue praises not letting scarcity stop one's helpfulness, and the Book of Wealth, speaking of a ruler's resources, makes the weighing of means a condition of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:481","b":"k:500","type":"parallel_domain","note":"481 says the crow beats the owl by day (பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை), and Parimelazhagar notes that the owl is the stronger bird. 500 says a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). In both a weaker animal defeats a stronger one through a circumstance, time in 481 and ground in 500, which is how the two adjacent chapters make their cases.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:617","type":"parallel_domain","note":"482 pictures fortune (திரு) as something that leaves unless tied, and names timely action as the rope (தீராமை ஆர்க்கும் கயிறு). 617 pictures her as living in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள் தாமரையினாள்), with her dark elder sister living in sloth. Both treat fortune as a visitor kept by conduct, and they name different conduct: timing in 482, steady effort in 617.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"482 says that acting in accord with the season (பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) is the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. 1028 says those who raise their family have no season (குடிசெய்வார்க்கு இல்லை பருவம்), and that idling and thinking of one's dignity ruins it. The two conflict if பருவம் in 1028 means the right moment for action; Parimelazhagar instead takes it there as seasons of heat, rain and cold used as an excuse to put work off, which removes the conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:483","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"483 names two conditions that make a hard deed possible, means and time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன்). 675 keeps 483's pair and sets it inside a fuller list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"484 says that even if one has the world in mind, it comes to hand when one considers the time and acts by the place (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும் காலம் கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்). 485 reverses the same words: those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, considering the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது ஞாலம் கருதுபவர்). 484 states the condition for acting and 485 its other side, that until the time comes the ambitious do not move.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:497","type":"develops","note":"484 says the world comes to hand if one considers the time and acts by the place (இடத்தாற் செயின்). 497, in the next chapter, uses the same phrase: one needs no support but fearlessness if one thinks everything through and acts by the place (எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்). 484 names place as a second condition beside time, and the following chapter, with 497, takes it up as its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:610","type":"parallel_domain","note":"484 says a king who considers time and place can gain even the world (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும்). 610 says a king without sloth gains at once all the expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot (மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான் தாஅயதெல்லாம்). Both promise the whole earth, one to timing and the other to unremitting effort, so the two chapters make the same claim for different qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"485 praises waiting for the right time. 671 says deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (அத்துணிவு தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 671 marks the limit of 485: waiting that is fixed on the time (காலம் கருதி) is part of the plan, and holding back after the decision, for no such reason, is a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:672","type":"agrees","note":"485 says those who aim at the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது). 672 says to take one's time over what should be done slowly (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால) and not to delay what should be done without delay. Both treat waiting as a decision that fits some tasks, and 672 adds the second half that 485 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:486","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"486 says the holding back of a person of energy is like a fighting ram stepping back in order to charge (பொருதகர் தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து). 827 says not to trust humble words from an enemy, because the bending of a bow means harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்). Both use a drawing back that comes before a blow: 486 recommends it to oneself, and 827 teaches one to see it in an opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:152","type":"tension","note":"152 says always to bear with an excess done to one, and that forgetting it is better still (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம் பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). Both hold back the outward response, and they differ on what happens inside: 152 wants the wrong forgotten, 487 wants the anger kept. The conflict holds if both are read as general advice; 152 belongs to the householder's virtues and Parimelazhagar takes 487 as addressed to kings facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"301 says that the one who truly guards against anger guards it where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான்); where it cannot, guarding or not makes no difference. 487 has the bright hold back anger now, when showing it would not serve, and keep it inside for the right time. On the reading that 487's anger is to be released when it can succeed, it describes the restraint that 301 says does not count, and withholds the one that does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"151 says that bearing with those who scorn one, as the earth holds up those who dig into it (அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போல), is the highest thing. 488 uses a verb of carrying for the same act, bearing with those who would harm one (சுமக்க), and makes it a means: one bears them until their end comes and their head goes down. The two conflict if both are read as answers to how one should treat those who wrong one, since forbearance is the goal in 151 and a way to the enemy's fall in 488. 151 is addressed to the householder and 488 to a king, which is the other way to read them.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:879","type":"qualifies","note":"488 says to bear with enemies until their end comes (இறுவரை காணின்). 879 says to cut down a thorn tree while it is young, because once it has hardened it destroys the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க ... காழ்த்த இடத்து). 879 limits 488's patience: waiting serves when time works against the enemy, and not when the enemy is growing stronger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"26 says the great are those who do what is hard to do, and the small are those who cannot (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 489 uses the same phrase and adds a condition of time: hard deeds are to be done at the moment a rare opportunity arrives (செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 26 places the ability in the person; 489 says it also depends on a moment that is itself hard to come by (எய்தற்கரியது).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:333","type":"parallel_domain","note":"489 says that when what is hard to get has come, one should at that very point do what is hard to do (இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 333 says wealth has the nature of not staying, and that on getting it one should at once do what stays (அதுபெற்றால் அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்). Both reason from something that will not last, an opportunity in 489 and wealth in 333, to acting at once, and 333 turns the king's rule of timing toward lasting good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து), and like its strike when the moment is right. 126 says that one who can draw in the five senses like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்) in one birth has protection for seven. Both teach restraint through an animal that draws itself in. The tortoise's withdrawal is the discipline itself, and the heron's is half of a pair that ends in a strike (குத்து).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:274","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 holds up the heron, which stays still until the moment and then strikes, as a model for acting on enemies. 274 says that doing wrong under cover of austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush to snare birds (புதல்மறைந்து வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த்தற்று). The pattern of stillness that hides a coming strike is the same in both. It is recommended to the king waiting for his time, and it is the figure of fraud in one who wears the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says those who fear disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவு இலதனைத் தொடங்கார்). 491 gives the same ban on beginning (தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும்) a concrete test: until the place has been seen (இடங்கண்ட பின் அல்லது). Read together, knowing the ground is one specific form of the clarity 464 demands before any start.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:747","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 491's முற்றும் இடம் as a place from which the enemy can be encircled, and tells the king neither to act nor to scorn the foe before finding one. 747 defines a fort from the other side as what cannot be taken even by encircling (முற்றியும் ... பற்றற்கு அரியது). The shared siege verb shows attacker and defender reasoning about the same ground; Manakkudavar instead reads 491 as 'the place where the task can be completed', which would loosen this link.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:492","b":"k:741","type":"agrees","note":"492 says that even for those with fighting strength (முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பினவர்க்கும்) the advantage of joining a fort (அரண்சேர்ந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) yields many gains. 741 says a fort is of value to those able to go on the offensive (ஆற்றுபவர்க்கும்) as well as to those who guard themselves in fear. Both deny that a fort is only for the weak: the chapter on place and the chapter on forts make the same point about the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:861","type":"qualifies","note":"861 advises avoiding opposition to the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursuing enmity against the weaker. 493 says the weaker side can still become strong and win when it knows the ground and protects itself. 493 therefore sets a condition under which 861's rule about relative strength does not settle the outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"493 says even those without strength win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves (போற்றிச் செயின்). 878 says the enemy's pride dies when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Self-guarding plus knowledge is the common recipe; 878 generalises 493's 'place' to knowing the way of acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:494","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"494 says those who had planned (against you) lose their plan (எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர்) when you advance knowing the place and hold close to it. 440 says the enemies' scheme becomes void (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்) when a ruler enjoys what he loves without letting the love be known. In both, the opponent's design is defeated before any fight by removing what it relied on: bad ground in one case, a known weakness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:500","type":"develops","note":"495 shows a strong animal losing outside its own element: the crocodile out of water is killed by others. 500 gives the sharper case, where a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). Together they state both halves: one's own ground makes one win, and the wrong ground lets even a much weaker creature win.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:496","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"496 says chariots with strong wheels do not run on the sea and sea-going ships do not run on land (கடலோடா ... நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து); Manakkudavar takes it to mean that instruments must be made to suit the place. 675 lists means and place together (கருவி ... இடனொடு ஐந்தும்) among the five things to think through before acting. 496 shows why the two items in 675's list cannot be weighed separately: a tool is only a tool in its own medium.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:534","type":"develops","note":"497 says that one who thinks everything through and acts by the place needs no support but fearlessness (அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை): Parimelazhagar explains that forest or hill forts are of no use to a frightened mind. The pair shows the two sides of one claim: place is enough given courage, and place is worthless without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:875","type":"qualifies","note":"497 says no ally is needed (துணைவேண்டா) beyond fearlessness when the plan is complete and the ground right. 875 advises one who has no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்) to make one of the enemies a pleasing ally. 875 shows that the book does not treat being without allies as generally safe; 497's claim holds only under its stated condition of complete planning and the right place.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:473","type":"parallel_domain","note":"473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on out of zeal and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 498 says the large army's zeal (ஊக்கம்) is destroyed when it enters the small army's ground. In both, drive that outruns a sober measure, of one's strength in one chapter and of the ground in the other, is what gets broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:744","type":"develops","note":"498 says the drive of a commander with a large army is destroyed (உறுபடையான் ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்) if he enters ground suited to a small army. 744 defines a fort as having little to guard and much room, and as destroying the drive of the foe who comes against it (உறுபகை ஊக்கம் அழிப்பது அரண்). The shared phrase shows that a fort is the built form of 498's principle: ground chosen so that numbers stop counting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:499","b":"k:495","type":"develops","note":"495 gives the image: the crocodile wins in its own deep water. 499 states the human case without an image: even people with no fortress and no great standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to engage on the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 applies the crocodile's lesson to an invader's estimate of a weak-looking people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:513","type":"develops","note":"501 names four tests, virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life (அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம்), by which a person is examined before being trusted (தேறப்படும்). 513 names four qualities, love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire, in whoever has which trust lies (நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு). The first four are trials applied from outside (Parimelazhagar describes staged temptations); the second four are the traits such trials are meant to find.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:690","type":"develops","note":"501's fourth test is fear for one's life (உயிரச்சம்). 690 defines the envoy as one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short in securing his lord's good. The envoy chapter shows what passing that fourth test looks like in service.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"502 makes birth in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து) a ground for trust alongside freedom from fault. 133 says having good conduct is itself good birth, and a lapse in conduct becomes low birth (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம் இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). On 133's definition, 502's first criterion cannot be checked apart from its second: family does not vouch for a person whose conduct fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"502 places trust in one born of good family, free of faults, with a shame that recoils from blemish (குடிப்பிறந்து ... வடுப்பரியும் நாணுடையான்). 794 says the friendship of one born of good family who is ashamed of blame on himself (குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணுவானை) should be had even at a price. The same two criteria, birth and shame, are used to choose a king's officer and to choose a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"502 lists good family first and shame last among the grounds for trust. 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature (இயல்பாகச் செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு) only in those born to a good house. 951 supplies the reasoning 502 leaves unstated: family is asked for because it is taken to be where shame comes naturally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"503 says hard learning and freedom from fault still leave some ignorance when one looks closely. 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own inherent understanding still prevails (தன் உண்மை அறிவே மிகும்). Both set a limit on what study can change in a person, one in the chapter on choosing officers and one in the chapter on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"503 observes that even in those who have learned hard things and are free of fault, it is rare on examination to find no blankness (இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு). 504 gives the rule that follows: examine the qualities and the faults and judge by what predominates (மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்). Parimelazhagar on 504 makes the bridge explicit, saying no one in the world has only qualities or only faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:793","type":"parallel_domain","note":"504 tells the king to examine a person's qualities and faults (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி) and take whichever predominates. 793 tells one to know a person's qualities, family, faults and kin (குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் ... இனனும்) before forming a friendship. The same inventory is used in both domains, but only 504 says how to decide when the inventory is mixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"504 lets the predominant side decide what a person is. 965 says even those like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). 965 names a kind of fault, the degrading act, that is not outweighed by the mass of a person's merits, which limits the simple weighing of 504.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:986","type":"same_image","note":"505 says a person's own deeds are the touchstone (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்) of greatness and smallness. 986 asks what the touchstone of worth is (சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின்) and answers: accepting defeat even from those not one's equal. Both use the assayer's stone for testing people; 505 names the general test, deeds, and 986 picks one particular deed as the decisive one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"506 treats having no attachment (பற்றிலர்) as a reason not to trust a person, since such a one does not fear blame. 350 tells the renouncer to hold to the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான்) in order to let attachment go. The same word is a disqualification in the chapter on choosing officers and the goal in the chapter on renunciation; the contexts differ, so this is a contrast of valuation across the books and not a contradiction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:502","type":"develops","note":"502 puts trust in one who has a shame that recoils from blemish (நாணுடையான்). 506 warns against trusting those who are cut off (அற்றார்), because they have no attachment and are not ashamed of blame (பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி); Parimelazhagar takes அற்றார் as those without kin, Manakkudavar as those without conduct. The two verses are the positive and negative of one criterion, and 506 adds a cause: shame depends on having ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:515","type":"agrees","note":"507 says trusting the ignorant with affection as the ground (காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்) yields every kind of folly. 515 says work is not to be assigned to someone because he is dear (சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற்றன்று), only to one who knows, endures and can do it. Both exclude affection as a ground of appointment, one at the stage of trust and the other at the stage of assigning work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:836","type":"develops","note":"507 warns that trusting those without understanding out of fondness brings all folly on the one who trusts. 836 describes the result from the other side: when a fool who does not know how takes up a task (கையறியாப் பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்), it fails and he ends in fetters. 836 shows the outcome that 507's warning anticipates.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"508 and 510 share their closing line (தீரா இடும்பை தரும்) and their first fault, trusting without examining. 510 adds a second fault of equal weight: doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Read after 508, 510 shows that the chapter's warning is not simply 'trust less': misplaced doubt is punished in the same words as misplaced trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"508: one who trusts an outsider without examining (தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான்) gets trouble that does not end, extending to his descendants (வழிமுறை தீரா இடும்பை). 792: the friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends in a grief that kills him (கடைமுறை தான்சாம் துயரம்). The same structure, unexamined acceptance followed by lasting harm, is stated for a king's officer and for a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:518","type":"develops","note":"509 says trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்). 518 says that after examining a person's fitness for the work (வினைக்குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை), make him fully entitled to it (அதற்குரியனாகச் செயல்). 518 turns 509's second half into an act: the trust that follows examination is shown by handing over real authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:588","type":"qualifies","note":"509 says that once a person has been examined, what is to be trusted should be trusted. 588 says even what a spy has found and brought back should be checked through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்). For intelligence reports the book keeps verifying after the agent is chosen, which bears on 509's phrase தேறும் பொருள்: Manakkudavar reads it as the matters in which the person is reliable, not everything.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"509 orders examination first and commitment after. 791 says nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, because for those who keep friendship there is no letting go once it is made (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). Friendship follows the same sequence as appointment, and 791 states the reason the order matters: the commitment is hard to reverse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:519","type":"agrees","note":"510 says doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to his work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). Both make the ruler's unfounded suspicion of a proven servant a cause of his own loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 speaks to the ruler: doubt of one already trusted brings trouble that does not end. 693 speaks to the courtier: guard against grave faults, because once suspicion has arisen it is hard for anyone to clear it (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The two verses describe the same event, a ruler's suspicion, as lasting damage from each side of the relationship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:422","type":"parallel_domain","note":"511 says the man to employ is one whose nature, having looked into good and bad (நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி), has taken to the good (நலம்புரிந்த தன்மையான்). 422 defines understanding as not letting the mind go wherever it goes but turning it from bad to good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). The test for a king's officer is the book's general definition of அறிவு applied to a person at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the king to examine a man's qualities and faults and judge by what predominates (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி ... மிகைநாடி). 511 uses the same verb நாடி, but now it is the candidate who examines good and bad and settles on the good. The king's weighing of the man in chapter 51 becomes, in chapter 52, a check on how the man himself weighs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:682","type":"agrees","note":"513 places trust in one who has love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire (அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை). 682 opens with the same pair, அன்பறிவு, and adds considered eloquence as the three things an envoy cannot lack. The two lists share a core of loyalty and knowledge and then differ by office: the officer handling affairs needs steadiness and no greed, the envoy needs speech.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:505","type":"develops","note":"505 makes each person's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). 514 says tests of every kind before the appointment still leave many who change once in the work. Both put the decisive evidence in action, and 514 adds that the action that counts is the work itself, after trust has been given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:510","type":"qualifies","note":"510 says that doubting a man one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 514 says that even when tested in every way (எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்), many people turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). Read together, 514 limits 510: testing does not settle the matter, so the ban on doubt cannot mean a ban on watching how the man changes in office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:520","type":"develops","note":"514 observes that many who passed every test turn different through the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்). 520 tells the king to look into the one doing the work every day (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). The chapter's closing instruction answers the problem 514 raises: since office changes people, examination has to continue after appointment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:515","b":"k:640","type":"develops","note":"515 requires for a task one who knows and endures and can carry it through (அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு). 640 says that those without practical ability (திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்), though they plan in proper order, do things that never reach completion. 640 shows what 515's last requirement guards against: knowing is not enough without the capacity to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"516 asks the king to examine the doer, examine the deed, and act seeing how they fit the time (செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்). Deed and time are in both lists; 516 adds the one item 675 lacks, the person who will do it, which is the concern of a chapter on employing people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:949","type":"parallel_domain","note":"516: examine the doer, the deed and the fit with the time, then act (காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 949: the trained physician acts after considering the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time (உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும் ... கருதிச் செயல்). Both end in செயல் after a three-part assessment of person, matter and time, the same procedure in statecraft and in medicine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:510","type":"agrees","note":"517 says that once the king has reasoned 'this man will finish this by this means', he should leave it with him (அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்). 510 says that doubting one who has been examined and trusted brings endless trouble. Leaving the task with the man is the practical form of 510's rule: examination comes first and is then followed by a real handing over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:520","type":"qualifies","note":"517 tells the king to leave the task to the chosen man (அவன்கண் விடல்). 520, in the same chapter, tells the king to look into the one doing the work daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக), because the world stays straight only if he does. The delegation in 517 is therefore not a withdrawal of attention; Parimelazhagar's note on 520 says the checking should be done privately so that the man's standing is not damaged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:700","type":"qualifies","note":"519 warns the king against taking amiss the familiarity of a hard-working officer (வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மை). 700, addressed to the courtier, says that familiarity which does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை) brings ruin. The king must tolerate the liberty and the servant must not presume on it; each verse limits the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:808","type":"parallel_domain","note":"519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to the work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). 808 praises those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை). The duty not to reinterpret a close person's conduct appears in kingship and in friendship; Parimelazhagar reads 519 as the king being turned by the words of those who resent the officer, which is the situation 808 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"520 says that if the one doing the work does not bend, the world will not bend (கோடாமை கோடாது உலகு). 546 says victory comes from the king's sceptre, provided it does not bend (கோலதூஉங் கோடாதெனின்). கோடாமை, not bending, is the mark of the just sceptre, and 520 applies the same word to the officer, on whose straightness the world's straightness is made to depend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"520: let the king look daily into the one doing his work (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). 553: the king who does not look daily and do justice loses his land daily (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன் நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The same phrase of daily examination is used for officers and for justice, and both tie the state of the land to that routine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:584","type":"develops","note":"520 requires the king to examine the worker daily. 584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்), then his kin and his enemies. 584 supplies the means by which the daily examination of 520 is carried out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"521: kin keep honouring old ties (பழைமை) even when all is lost. 806: those who keep within the bounds do not give up, even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who have stood with them from of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு). Loyalty to a long tie when fortune is gone is claimed as the mark of kin in one chapter and of true old friends in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:1047","type":"tension","note":"521 says that honouring the old tie even when a man's means are gone (பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமை பாராட்டுதல்) is found among kin. 1047 says that one in poverty will be looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). The conflict holds if 1047 is read as Pope reads it, of poverty in general; the verse's own qualifier அறஞ்சாரா, poverty unconnected with virtue, which Parimelazhagar and Subramuniyaswami keep, narrows it and reduces the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:522","b":"k:884","type":"qualifies","note":"522 says that if a man gets kin whose love does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின்), they give many gains (ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்). 884 says that if hidden enmity of unreformed mind appears, it gives many ills that turn his circle bad (இனமாணா ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்). The two verses have the same shape with opposite content, and Parimelazhagar notes that 'whose love does not lapse' in 522 is said to exclude the inner enmity of chapter 89: kin are an asset only on that condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"524 says the fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்). 837 says that when a fool gets great wealth, strangers eat their fill and his own people go hungry (ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர்). 837 shows the failure of exactly the use 524 names: wealth that does not reach one's own people has missed its purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:526","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"525 names giving and pleasant speech as what surrounds a man with kin. 526 keeps giving, now great giving (பெருங்கொடை), and replaces pleasant speech with not cherishing anger (பேணான் வெகுளி), and raises the result to a following unmatched on earth. The pair is not a repetition: the second verse moves from a positive manner to the removal of what drives people away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"527 ties gain (ஆக்கம்) to those who, like the crow, call others before eating. 227 says the disease of hunger does not touch one who is habituated to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both hold that sharing food protects the sharer's own fortune, stated in the Book of Virtue as freedom from hunger and here as prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:1035","type":"same_image","note":"527 praises the crow for not hiding (கரவா) its food and for calling others to eat. 1035 says those who live by eating what their own hands produce do not beg and give to those who ask without hiding (கரவாது). Not concealing what one has, கரவு being the book's word for the withholder's fault in chapters 106 and 107, is the shared mark of the crow and of the farmer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:542","type":"same_image","note":"528 ends: many will live looking to that (அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்). 542 says the world lives looking to the sky and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (கோல்நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி). The phrase 'live looking to' makes the king's conduct something people depend on as they depend on rain; 528 uses it for his recognition of each person's worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"528 says that if the king looks at people not in common but according to rank (பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்), many will live looking to that. 972 says birth is alike for all, but distinction is not alike, because of the difference in what people do (சிறப்பொவ்வா செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமையான்). 972 gives a basis for the differential regard of 528: Parimelazhagar reads வரிசை there as each person's merit, and 972 locates such difference in conduct, not birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:530","type":"qualifies","note":"529 says estranged kin return of themselves once the cause of discord is gone. 530 turns to one who left and has come back for a reason (காரணத்தின் வந்தானை) and tells the king to attend to it and accept him only after reflection (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). The pair separates two kinds of return, one that follows from the king's own correction and one prompted by the returner's interest, and prescribes welcome for the first and caution for the second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"529 says those who were one's own and left will return to kinship when the cause of the discord is absent (அமராமைக் காரணம் இன்றி வரும்). 633 lists among a minister's abilities reuniting those who have parted (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 529 says how that reunion comes about: the ground of the estrangement has to be removed, which Parimelazhagar takes as a fault on the king's own side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 gives the rule: trust no one without examining, then trust (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 530 applies examination to a man who was already one's own, left, and came back for a reason: he is to be received after reflection (எண்ணிக் கொளல்). Leaving resets the earlier trust, so the test of chapter 51 has to be done again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:830","type":"parallel_domain","note":"530 says the king should attend to the reason for which a man who left has come back, wait, and accept him after thinking it over (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, one should be friendly in face and withhold the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). Both deal with someone who comes over after having been apart and both withhold immediate trust, but 530 ends in acceptance of the kinsman while 830 ends in letting the former enemy go.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"531 states the danger: slackness from the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger. 539 gives the practice against it: when one grows strong in one's own joy (மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து), call to mind those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:601","type":"parallel_domain","note":"532 says heedlessness kills fame (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை). 601 says the undimmed lamp called family goes out when the grime called sloth creeps over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்). In both, an inner lapse, not an outside enemy, destroys a standing built up over time.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"532 uses as its comparison that constant poverty kills understanding (அறிவினை நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). 1046 describes one form of it: even when the poor speak good matter well understood, their words and meaning go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar says nobody listens, so their learning yields nothing. Read together, the 'killing' of understanding in 532 includes its losing all effect in the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they possess (என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்). 534 says the heedless have no good (பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு இல்லை), which Parimelazhagar reads as: even with every kind of wealth, they get no benefit from it. The same logic of having without having is applied to lack of understanding in one verse and to lack of vigilance in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:741","type":"tension","note":"534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை). 741 says a fort is of value both to those who march out and to those who, in fear, protect themselves (அஞ்சித் தற்போற்றுபவர்க்கும் பொருள்). The conflict holds if 534's first half is taken as a plain claim about forts and the fearful; Parimelazhagar's readings ease it, since he takes 534 as fear in the mind making a fort useless and 741 as the weaker side prudently taking refuge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:750","type":"agrees","note":"750 says a fort, whatever its excellences, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). 534 says the fearful have no fort and the heedless have no good (நன்கு). Both hold that an outward advantage counts for nothing without the inner quality needed to use it, and both use the fort to say so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:429","type":"agrees","note":"429 gives the positive case: those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 535 gives the negative: one who did not guard beforehand later grieves over his own fault (தன்பிழை பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்). Together they show foresight treated as part of understanding and its absence as heedlessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:655","type":"develops","note":"655 tells one not to do deeds over which one will grieve, saying 'what have I done' (எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க). 535 uses the same verb, இரங்கு, for the grief of one who failed to guard in advance. The pairing shows regret following from omission in 535 as it follows from commission in 655.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:536","b":"k:582","type":"develops","note":"536 says nothing equals unfailing vigilance toward everyone at all times (யார்மாட்டும் என்றும்). 582 says it is the king's work to know quickly everything that happens to everyone at all times (எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்). The same universal scope appears in both, and 582 gives the general vigilance of 536 a concrete form in intelligence gathering.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says one must not lose heart on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்), since effort gives the greatness needed. 537 says nothing is beyond doing as 'hard' (அரிய என்று ஆகாத இல்லை) when done with unforgetting care. Each denies that difficulty is a reason to give up, and each names its own remedy: effort in 611, sustained attention in 537.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:466","type":"develops","note":"466 says one is ruined by not doing what is fit to do (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). 538 says what has been praised must be carefully done, and those who slight it and do not do it (செய்யாது இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு) have nothing through seven births. 538 takes the omission half of 466 and extends its cost beyond this life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"835 says a fool can make, in one birth, the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). 538 says those who neglect what is praised have no good through seven births (எழுமையும் இல்). Both attach a seven-birth consequence to conduct in this life, folly in one and neglect of duty in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"250 says: when you move against those weaker than you, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). 539 says: when you grow strong in your own joy, think of those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). Both prescribe a deliberate act of recollection at the moment of feeling powerful, for the sake of compassion in 250 and of vigilance in 539.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk (களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்), does not reflect on the slackness of his own drinking (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 539 tells one elated with joy to reflect (உள்ளுக) on those whom negligence ruined. Both correct an intoxicated state by looking at its effect in other people, and 930's word சோர்வு is the one 531 uses for the slackness of joy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:552","type":"develops","note":"551 compares the oppressive king to murderers and finds him worse. 552 compares the king who demands wealth to a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over' (வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென்றது). Together the two opening verses of the chapter set the unjust ruler beside common criminals, first the killer and then the robber.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:756","type":"qualifies","note":"756 names what is the king's wealth: what comes unowned, customs duty, and tribute from enemies (உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும் தன் ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருளும்). 552 treats a sceptre-holder's 'asking' (இரவு) of his people as highway robbery; Parimelazhagar notes that the word இரவு marks it as something other than the due tax. 756 shows the lawful sources against which 552's demand is measured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"381 lists the six things a king must have, among them people and resources (படைகுடி கூழ்...). 554 says a king who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together (கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும்). The two things injustice costs him in 554 are two of the six that make a king in 381.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:568","type":"parallel_domain","note":"568 says the fortune of a king who does not think things through with his circle and then rages in anger will shrink (எண்ணாத வேந்தன் ... சிறுகும் திரு). 554 says a king who acts without reflection (சூழாது) and bends the sceptre loses wealth and people. In both, the king's loss is traced to acting without deliberation, shown in anger in 568 and in injustice in 554.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:567","type":"same_image","note":"567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 555 says the tears of the afflicted are a tool that wears down wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). Both describe the effect of a king's cruelty as slow abrasion by a tool, using the same verb தேய்க்கும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"828 says a weapon hides within an enemy's folded hands, and his shed tears are the same (படையொடுங்கும் ... அழுத கண்ணீரும் அனைத்து). 555 calls the tears shed by the afflicted the weapon that wears away wealth (அழுத கண்ணீர் ... படை). Both join the same two words, tears and weapon: in 828 the tears conceal a weapon, and in 555 the tears are the weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:556","b":"k:563","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a king of cruel sceptre who goes on doing what terrifies will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 556 says kings endure through just rule (மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை) and without it their lustre does not endure. Both tie how long a king lasts to how he rules; Parimelazhagar takes what endures in 556 to be the king's fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:557","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1192 a woman says that the tender care a lover gives the one who loves him (வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி) is like the sky giving rain to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்று). 557 says a king's lack of அளி is to living beings what lack of rain is to the earth. The same word அளி and the same rain comparison describe dependence on a ruler in one book and on a beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"55 says that when a wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). 559 says that when a king bends justice, the sky will not rain (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). In both, rain responds to a person's keeping or failing the duty of their station, the wife's in the household and the king's in the realm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 559 says the sky holds back rain when the king bends justice, and the next verse, 560, uses the same verb for cows' yield (ஆபயன் குன்றும்). In each, nature's yield falls in response to a human moral failing, an individual's in 239 and a ruler's in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"19 says that if the sky does not give, giving and austerity will not remain in the wide world (தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா). 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, cows' yield declines and the six-occupation men forget their book. In both, religious and learned life depends on a provider, the rain in 19 and the king in 560, and fails when the provider fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:561","b":"k:579","type":"qualifies","note":"561 makes the king the one who punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து), while 579 calls it the highest quality to show kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும் ... பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை). The same root ஒறு sits on opposite sides. Parimelazhagar separates the cases: 561 concerns wrongs done to weaker subjects, 579 wrongs done to the ruler himself, so forbearance is limited to injuries that are the ruler's own to forgive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"562 asks for a light stroke after a stern show (மெல்ல எறிக); Manakkudavar takes this to mean the penalty should fall short of what the offence deserves. 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. The pairing marks where gentleness stops: for those 550 calls கொடியார், the book accepts the severest penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"562 tells those who want lasting prosperity to raise the rod sharply but strike gently (கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக). 567 names what happens when the stroke is not gentle: punishment past the bound (கையிகந்த தண்டம்), with harsh words, is a file that wears down the king's strength. 562 gives the practice and 567 the cost of abandoning it; the 'long prosperity' (நெடிதாக்கம்) of one is the 'conquering strength' (அடுமுரண்) eroded in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:563","b":"k:548","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a cruel-sceptred king who keeps doing what terrifies (வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோலன்) will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 548 says a king who is not easy of access and does not inquire and do justice will sink and perish of himself (தானே கெடும்). Neither verse needs an enemy to bring the king down: in both the misrule is itself the cause of the fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others (மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்). 564 gives the reverse case: where people say 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன்), the king quickly perishes. Both verses measure a reign by what is said about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"564 says the king of whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல்) are spoken will quickly perish. 555 says the tears of those who cannot bear their affliction are the weapon that wears away wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). In both, what destroys the ruler comes from the subjects' suffering, as speech in one verse and as tears in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"565 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) of an inaccessible, grim-faced man is as if seen by a demon; Parimelazhagar explains that no one approaches him, so the wealth is of use neither to him nor to others. 1000 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) gained by one without courtesy (பண்பிலான்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Outside the court the same judgment holds: the holder's manner ruins wealth that is sound in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:566","b":"k:577","type":"develops","note":"566 says that if the ruler is harsh of speech and 'eyeless' (கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes at once; Parimelazhagar takes கண் here to stand for கண்ணோட்டம். 577, in the next chapter, states the equation directly: those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்). The single word in 566 is the idea the following chapter spells out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:567","b":"k:888","type":"same_image","note":"567 calls harsh words and excessive punishment a file (அரம்) that wears down (தேய்க்கும்) the king's conquering strength. 888 says a family with enmity inside it wears away (தேயும்) like metal rubbed by a file (அரம்பொருத பொன்போல), its strength ground down. In both the file stands for slow loss of strength from within, by the king's own conduct in one and by a house's own members in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:568","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it touches, burns even the safe raft called one's people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 568 shows this at court: the king who neglects his circle (இனம்) and then turns his anger on them (சினத்தாற்றிச் சீறின்) loses his fortune; Parimelazhagar adds that the frightened ministers leave him. The general warning in the book of virtue becomes a specific danger for a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before a fault arrives (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 569 applies the same logic to war: the king who has not prepared his defence beforehand is overtaken when the battle arrives (செருவந்த போழ்தில்) and perishes. Both place the failure in the time before the danger, not in the moment of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:534","type":"qualifies","note":"569 says a king who has made no defence (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will be seized by fear when war comes and quickly perish. 534 says that for the fearful there is no fort (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are no use to a frightened mind. One verse says lack of a fort produces fear, the other that fear cancels a fort, so the fort is necessary but not sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வாரை). 570 shows the opposite court: the harsh sceptre gathers the unlearned around it (கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும்), because, as Parimelazhagar says, such a ruler admits only those who will go along with him. The two verses together make a ruler's choice of companions a sign of the kind of rule he runs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:557","type":"agrees","note":"557 says that a king's lack of tender care (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him what lack of rain is to the earth. 571 states the positive side: the world exists because kindly regard exists; Parimelazhagar reads this of kings. In both verses the ruler's kindness is treated as a condition of life for the people, not as an ornament only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"571 says this world exists because the great beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on people of courtesy, and would otherwise sink into the earth and perish (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்). Both use the same construction, உண்டு உலகு, to say that a human quality and not power keeps the world going; 571 says it in a chapter on kings and 996 of people in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:983","type":"develops","note":"983 lists kindly regard (கண்ணோட்டம்) with love, shame, obligingness and truth as the five pillars that hold up worth (சால்பு). 572 gives it a wider load: the conduct of the world rests in it (கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல்). The quality that is one support of personal character in 983 is the support of social life in 572.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (அஃதிலார் உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). 1003 says the very appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the earth (தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை). The phrase is identical, and in both the people called a burden are those who are of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"79 asks what all the outer limbs can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும் ... அன்பிலவர்க்கு). 573 asks the same kind of question of the eye that lacks kindly regard. In both a visible organ is counted useless without the inner quality it should serve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"573 asks what use an eye is without kindly regard (கண்என்னாம் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்), as a melody is no use that does not fit the song. 705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read another's sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). Both judge the eye by what it does for another person, kindness in one verse and understanding in the other, and not by sight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like barren saline land. 574 says eyes without kindly regard only seem to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து) and do nothing more. Both verses grant bare existence and deny any use, of unlearned persons in one case and of unkind eyes in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:578","type":"develops","note":"574 says eyes without kindly regard in due measure (அளவினால் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்) do nothing but seem to be in the face; Parimelazhagar takes அளவினால் as excluding excessive indulgence. 578 says what the measure is: kindly regard shown without ruining the task (கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்). The one word of limit in 574 becomes the whole subject of 578.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 asks whether shame is not an ornament (அணி) to the worthy, and whether without it their proud walk is not a disease (அஃதின்றேல் பிணி). 575 has the same shape: kindly regard is the eye's ornament (அணிகலம்), and without it (அஃதின்றேல்) the eye is a sore (புண்). In both the missing virtue does not leave the thing plain but turns it into an affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:577","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 says those who are called 'people with eyes' are the learned (கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர்). 577 says those without kindly regard are eyeless and those who have eyes do not lack it (கண்ணுடையார் கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்). The same title, கண்ணுடையார், is awarded on two different tests, learning and kindly regard; the verses do not conflict, but a reader should see that the book defines 'having eyes' twice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:579","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with excess against you (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even when you are able to punish'. 579 is that case in the ruler, who has the power to punish and is asked to bear with those who harm him. 152 goes a step further than 579 by preferring forgetting the wrong to bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:805","type":"develops","note":"805 advises that when friends do something painful, one should take it as either folly or great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க). 580 describes people who drink poison they have seen poured. 805 supplies a way of construing a friend's injury that makes the conduct in 580 intelligible, though 580's own stated motive is the wish for நாகரிகம் and not a charitable reading of the giver.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:520","type":"agrees","note":"520 tells the king to look daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக) into the one doing his work, since the world stays straight if he does; 582 makes constant, quick knowledge of everyone's affairs the king's own occupation (வேந்தன் தொழில்). Both treat continuous inquiry, not occasional inspection, as the core of rule; 582 widens the object from the king's officers to everyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:553","type":"develops","note":"582 makes it the king's work to know quickly, at all times (எஞ்ஞான்றும் வல்லறிதல்), all that happens to everyone; 553 says a ruler who does not inquire daily and do justice (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா) loses his land day by day. 553 gives the purpose and the cost: the daily knowing of 582 exists to feed daily justice, and its absence is paid for in the kingdom itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:583","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"583 denies victory to a ruler who has things spied out but does not discern their meaning (ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா); 423 defines understanding as seeing the true substance of whatever is heard from anyone (அப்பொருள் மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது). The pairing shows the spy chapter applying the general rule about hearsay to intelligence: collecting reports is not yet knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says that doubting a person one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்) brings unending trouble; 584 has spies examine everyone (அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது), including the king's own officers. The conflict holds if standing surveillance of appointed officers counts as the ஐயுறவு that 510 forbids; if spying is read as routine inquiry and not suspicion of a particular person, the two can stand together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:514","type":"develops","note":"584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்); 514 observes that even people trusted after every test often turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). 514 supplies the reason for watching one's own appointed officers: the testing done before appointment does not settle how they behave afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"584 puts the king's own kin (தம் சுற்றம்) among those a spy examines, alongside his enemies (வேண்டாதார்); 882 says not to fear enemies who are like a sword but to fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). The chapter on internal enmity explains why 584 does not exempt relatives from scrutiny.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:686","type":"same_image","note":"585 requires of a spy an unsuspected appearance, not flinching before eyes (கண்ணஞ்சாது) and never letting anything out; 686 requires of an envoy that he not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar glosses in both verses as the angry look of the other side. The open agent and the covert one are given the same nerve; what differs is that the envoy must speak so it goes home, and the spy must give nothing away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:689","type":"parallel_domain","note":"585 ends with the spy's ability never to let anything spill (யாண்டும் உகாஅமை); 689 wants the bearer of a king's message to be a hard man whose mouth does not let slip a damaging word (the edition prints வாய்சேரா; Parimelazhagar reads வாய் சோரா). Both make control of what leaves the mouth under pressure the test of the king's agents abroad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:276","type":"tension","note":"276 says none are more hard-hearted than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving in the likeness of renouncers (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்); 586 has the spy take the guise of renouncers (துறந்தார் படிவத்தராகி) to get into places and investigate. The conflict holds if 276 condemns the false ascetic guise as such; Parimelazhagar narrows 276 to cheating donors, and on that reading the spy's disguise in the king's service falls outside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:695","type":"qualifies","note":"695 tells those who serve a king not to listen in on or press after anything when a secret (மறை) is in progress, and to hear it only when he releases it; 587 makes the ability to hear concealed things (மறைந்தவை கேட்க வற்றாகி) the definition of a spy. The pairing marks a boundary: prying into secrets is a skill the king directs at others, and a fault when his own attendants turn it on him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"587 defines the spy as able to learn what was concealed and to hold what he has learned without doubt (ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 702 ranks with a god the one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை). Both prize certain knowledge of what others hide, one gained by inquiry among informants and the other by reading a person's mind from signs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:587","type":"qualifies","note":"587 requires the spy to be free of doubt about what he has learned (அறிந்தவை ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 588 tells the king to check even such a report (தந்த பொருளையும்) through another spy. The spy's certainty is a quality of the source and not a warrant for the king, who is to keep doubting until reports agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:589","type":"develops","note":"588 says that even what one spy found and brought should be confirmed through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால்); 589 adds that spies should be run so that one does not know of another, and that what three say in agreement is to be accepted (மூவர் சொற்றொக்க தேறப்படும்). 589 turns the second opinion of 588 into a procedure: sources kept independent, and a threshold of agreement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:509","type":"agrees","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining and, after examining, to trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக); 589 says to run spies unknown to one another and to accept what three say in agreement (தேறப்படும்). Both give the same two-step rule, test first and then commit, 509 for persons and 589 for reports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without others knowing of his love (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' schemes come to nothing; 590 forbids showing favour to a spy where others can know of it. Both treat a ruler's visible attachments and rewards as information that others can use against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:663","type":"parallel_domain","note":"590 warns that a king who honours a spy so that others know of it (சிறப்பறிய) has himself let the secret out (புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை); 663 says a deed should be done so that it shows only at the end, since showing midway brings lasting trouble (இடைக்கொட்கின்). In both the actor's own conduct, not an enemy's skill, is what exposes the undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:591","b":"k:1005","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்); 591 asks whether those without energy really have what they have. Both separate holding goods from having them, 1005 by the test of use and 591 by the test of the holder's drive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"333 says wealth has the nature of not staying (அற்கா இயல்பிற்று) and concludes that on getting it one should at once do what stays; 592 also says goods do not stay (நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்) and concludes that the lasting possession is the inner drive (உள்ளம் உடைமை). Starting from the same fact, the Book of Virtue points to enduring deeds and the Book of Wealth to an inner resource that can make wealth again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"617 says the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்); 594 says prosperity finds its own way to the person of unwavering energy. Both personify fortune as choosing where it lives by the person's exertion, and neither leaves it to chance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:595","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says a sand-well yields water as far as it is dug, and understanding as far as one has learned (தொட்டனைத்து... கற்றனைத்து); 595 says a water-flower's stem is as long as the water is deep, and a person's height as great as their inner drive (வெள்ளத்தனைய... உள்ளத்தனையது). Both use a water image and the same 'as much as' construction to state a strict proportion, one for learning and knowledge, the other for drive and stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:596","b":"k:619","type":"parallel_domain","note":"619 says that even if a thing cannot come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), effort pays the wage of the body's strain; 596 says a high aim keeps its worth even when it is thrust aside, which Parimelazhagar takes as being denied by fate. Both hold that what fate withholds in outcome does not cancel the value of the striving or the aim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"774 shows a warrior who has thrown his spear at an elephant pulling out the spear lodged in his own body and laughing; 597 shows the elephant itself holding firm when struck by arrows, as the image of those who do not give way in ruin. In both, a wound taken is the occasion that shows firmness, and the Book of Wealth draws the general rule in 597 that the war chapter acts out in 774.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:614","type":"develops","note":"598 says the person without drive does not attain the pride of generosity; 614 says the beneficence of one without effort comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி. 614 adds what happens when such a person tries anyway: the means are present but the capacity to use them is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:599","b":"k:763","type":"parallel_domain","note":"599 says an elephant, though huge and sharp-tusked, fears when a tiger attacks; 763 says a sea of roaring rats perishes when the cobra merely breathes. Both animal pairs teach that bulk or numbers do not decide a contest against an opponent with spirit, 599 of a person's drive and 763 of an army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"997 says those without human courtesy are like trees even if sharp as a file (மரம்போல்வர்); 600 says those without abundance of drive are trees, differing only in having human form (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). The tree marks the person who lacks what makes one human, but the two verses name different lacks: fellow-feeling in 997, inner drive in 600.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:601","b":"k:753","type":"same_image","note":"601 calls the family a lamp that does not dwindle (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) which dies when the grime of sloth spreads over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர). 753 calls wealth a lamp that does not fail (பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம்) which goes where its owner intends and cuts the darkness. The same 'X என்னும் ... விளக்கம்' figure is used twice in the Book of Wealth, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a நந்தா விளக்கு; one lamp is shown dispelling darkness abroad, the other being put out from within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1022","type":"develops","note":"602 states the negative condition for a family's standing: remove sloth. 1022 states the positive one: the family lengthens (நீளும் குடி) through long work in two things, effort and mature knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்). Read together, absence of sloth is necessary but 1022 adds knowledge beside effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1028","type":"develops","note":"602 tells those who want their family to be a family (குடியைக் குடியாக வேண்டுபவர்) to live treating sloth as sloth, or killing it (மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல்). 1028 says those who build the family have no season to wait for, and that if they idle and think of their dignity it is ruined (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). 1028 gives 602's rule its practical form: for the family-builder sloth shows up as waiting for the right time and standing on dignity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"605 treats sloth and its companions as something the ruin-bound choose and desire (காமக் கலன்). 371 says sloth itself arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 371 puts a cause behind what 605 describes as a preference, which matches 605's own word கெடுநீரார், people whose nature is already to perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:920","type":"parallel_domain","note":"605 lists delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep as the craft that those of a ruin-bound nature desire (கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்). 920 lists double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has cast off (திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு). Both verses give a short catalogue and tie it to people already marked for ruin, leaving open whether the listed things cause the ruin or are its sign.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:1068","type":"same_image","note":"605 makes four habits a boat (கலன்) that the ruin-bound love to board; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it as a vessel that drops its passengers mid-sea, though Parimelazhagar notes others read கலன் as an ornament. 1068 makes begging an unsafe boat (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of refusal. In both a way of living is a vessel trusted by the person in it and bound to fail him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:591","type":"agrees","note":"606: even when the holdings of those who own the earth come to them (படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும்), the slothful rarely reach an excellent fruit. 591: what makes one 'a possessor' is energy; do those without it possess even what they possess (உடையது உடையரோ)? Both deny that having things amounts to benefiting from them when the inner quality is missing. Parimelazhagar notes another reading of 606 in which the earth-owners' support, not their wealth, is what comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"606 says the greatest possessions yield the slothful no excellent fruit (மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது). 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). The two share the 'ஆகியக் கண்ணும் / அமைந்தக் கண்ணும்' concession: resources do not work without the action of those who hold them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:1040","type":"agrees","note":"607: the slothful end up hearing words of scorn (எள்ளுஞ் சொல்). 1040: seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing', the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்). Both give idleness the same penalty, being laughed at, once from people and once from the personified earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:608","b":"k:616","type":"same_image","note":"608: if sloth lodges in one of family standing it puts him into slavery to his enemies (அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்). 616: absence of effort puts one into want (இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). The identical closing phrase makes sloth an agent that ushers a person into a condition; the neighbouring chapters thus name two destinations, poverty and subjection, and Parimelazhagar links them in his note on 616, where want leads to destruction by enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:604","type":"qualifies","note":"604 says that for the slothful the family dies and faults multiply (குற்றம் பெருகும்). 609 says the fault is destroyed (குற்றம் ... கெடும்) once a man changes his sloth. 609 makes 604's decline reversible; Parimelazhagar goes further and takes 609's faults to include ones that arose from other causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"609: when a man ends the rule of sloth over him (மடியாண்மை மாற்ற), the fault that came into his குடியாண்மை is gone; Parimelazhagar splits the word into family and manhood, Manakkudavar reads it as the management of the family. 1026 says good manhood is making the management of the house one was born in one's own (நல்லாண்மை ... இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1026 supports reading the two terms of 609 together: manhood is measured in the household, and sloth is what unseats it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:606","type":"qualifies","note":"610 gives the unslothful king the whole measured world. 606, in the same chapter, says that even when the wealth of earth-owners arrives the slothful get no excellent fruit from it. Together they separate two things: sloth does not only block gaining the world, it also empties the world of value when it is handed over.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:611","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"611: do not flag saying 'it is hard' (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), for effort will give the greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). 26: those who do what is hard to do are great, those who cannot are small (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 26 defines greatness by hard deeds as if it were a fixed difference between people; 611 says the greatness needed is itself produced by trying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:670","type":"agrees","note":"612: guard against the work failing in the doing, because the world has left those who left the work's need undone (வினைக்குறை தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு), on Parimelazhagar's reading; Manakkudavar takes the second line the other way, that the world does not leave those who finish. 670: whatever other firmness they have, the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action (வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு). Both make the world's regard answer in kind to how a person treats the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:674","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against abandoning a task midway (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்). 674 says the remainder of a task, like the remainder of an enmity, burns like the remainder of a fire (தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்). 674 supplies a reason 612 does not give: the unfinished part is not inert but goes on doing damage.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:613","b":"k:1035","type":"parallel_domain","note":"613 says the standing of a benefactor rests on labour. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their hands produce do not beg and give to those who beg without hiding anything (கைசெய்தூண் மாலையவர்). The farming chapter shows 613's general claim in one occupation: the person who works with his hands is the one in a position to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"614 says the generosity of a man without effort comes to nothing. 219 says that for a man of good feeling, being poor just is not being able to do the helpful things he would do (செயும்நீர செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு). 219 shows the same blocked intention from the inside and without blame, so 614's scorn falls only on the case where the lack of means comes from not working.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:901","type":"agrees","note":"615 praises the one who wants the work (வினைவிழைவான்) and not pleasure. 901 says those who desire the wife do not reach the excellent fruit, and that this is the very thing those who desire action do not want (வினைவிழையார் வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது). Both set the desire for action against a desire for enjoyment and use nearly the same word for the man of action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:1030","type":"develops","note":"615 makes the work-loving man a pillar that props his kin (துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. 1030 states the cost of the absence of the figure 615 praises, with the same verb ஊன்று.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"616: effort makes fortune (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்) and lack of effort brings one into want. 371: unflagging effort arises from a fate of gain, and sloth from a fate of loss (ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை ... போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 616 makes effort the cause of wealth; 371 makes fate the cause of effort, so on 371's account the causal chain in 616 starts one step earlier and is not in the person's hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:594","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort produces fortune. 594 says increase asks the way and goes to the one of unwavering drive (அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையானுழை). Both place the source of prosperity in the person's exertion, and 594 pictures it as prosperity doing the seeking. The first word of 594 is truncated in this edition (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:618","b":"k:988","type":"parallel_domain","note":"618 separates what is no disgrace (having no luck) from what is (not working). 988 says want is no disgrace to a person who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று). Both refuse to count an outward lack as shame and move the test to something in the person, effort in one and character in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:1024","type":"same_image","note":"620 ends 'தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்', those who labour without delay, and says they see fate's back. 1024 ends with the same phrase, 'தம்குடியைத் தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்க்கு', and says that for them the outcome arrives by itself without deliberation (சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும்). The same people are described twice, once as defeating an opposing power and once as finding the result come unsought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:621","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"621 says to laugh when trouble comes (இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக), since nothing presses it back as well. 774 shows a warrior who, having thrown his spear at an elephant, pulls out the one lodged in his own body and laughs (மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்). The battlefield verse acts out the advice of 621: the laugh comes at the moment of injury, and the injury is turned to use.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:309","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same play of உள்ளம் (mind) and உள்ளுதல் (to think): in 622 a flood of suffering perishes when the wise person thinks with the mind (உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்), and in 309 all one has thought of is gained if one does not think anger in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்). Read together they make a single act of thought decide the outcome, once by what is thought and once by what is left unthought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that arrives to shake them; 622 says that for the person of understanding (அறிவுடையான்) even a flood of suffering already present is ended by thinking. The pair gives understanding two jobs, forestalling trouble and dissolving the trouble that came anyway, which Parimelazhagar takes in 622 to be trouble from fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:623","b":"k:261","type":"parallel_domain","note":"623 says those who do not suffer at suffering (இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படாஅதவர்) make suffering itself suffer; Parimelazhagar places this in the middle of an undertaking. 261 makes bearing the pain that comes (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) half of the form of தவம். The same endurance is a means to finishing work in one book and the definition of austerity in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:597","type":"agrees","note":"624 likens the person who pulls through every obstruction to a draught ox (மடுத்தவாயெல்லாம் பகடன்னான்) and says the trouble he meets is itself troubled. 597 says the strong do not give way where things break down (சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார்), like an elephant standing firm under arrows. Both make the same claim through a working or fighting animal; the ox adds forward motion to the elephant's holding of ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:620","type":"develops","note":"624 says trouble is troubled by one who hauls on like an ox through every blocked place. 620 carries the claim to its limit: those who labour on without flagging (தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்) see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). What 624 says of obstacles in the road, 620 says of the power behind them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"625 says that though troubles come stacked (அடுக்கி வரினும்), those of one who is not undone (அழிவிலான்) are themselves in trouble. 989 says those called the shore of worth do not shift even if the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both define a person by not moving under pressure, one in the chapter on a king's undertakings and one in the chapter on character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:1045","type":"qualifies","note":"1045 states as plain fact that within the one suffering called poverty many sufferings arrive and settle (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்), and offers no remedy. 625 addresses exactly such piling up (அடுக்கி வரினும்) and says it fails against one who is not undone. Set side by side, 625's confidence is conditional on the person, while 1045 describes what the stacking is like for those in want.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:4","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629 makes freedom from pain depend on not desiring pleasure when it is present (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 4 describes the Lord as one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet never have இடும்பை. The same link between having no pull toward or away from things and having no suffering appears as a divine attribute in Book 1 and as a human discipline here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"629 says one who does not crave pleasure in the midst of pleasure does not suffer in the midst of pain. 115 says loss and increase are both part of life and that not letting the heart bend (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Both ask for the same evenness across good and bad turns; 115 treats it as fairness of mind, 629 as protection against pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:669","type":"develops","note":"669 says to do with resolve the deed that yields joy even though pain comes thick at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும் ... இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை): pain now, joy later. 630 goes a step further and has the person take the pain itself as joy (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்). Parimelazhagar reads the pain in both as the strain of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:810","type":"same_image","note":"630 measures the reward by the enemy: a distinction one's foes desire or esteem (ஒன்னார் விழையும் சிறப்பு). 810 uses the same measure with the same verb: those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them (விழையார் விழையப்படுப). Each verse marks an unusual constancy, under pain or toward friends who have erred, by saying that even the hostile regard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"641 sets the good of the tongue above every other good. 411 calls the wealth of the ear the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துள் எல்லாம் தலை). The book gives the top place once to listening and once to speaking, in chapters addressed to the learner and to the minister.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:982","type":"same_image","note":"The two verses end with the same formula, 'அந்நலம்/பிறநலம் யா/எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று', used in opposite directions. In 641 the goodness of the tongue (நாநலம்) is not contained in any other goodness, which Parimelazhagar takes to mean it exceeds them all; in 982 every goodness other than the goodness of character (குணநலம்) is not counted among goodnesses at all. One chapter ranks eloquence highest for a minister, the other ranks character alone for the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:128","type":"develops","note":"642 says ruin as well as gain comes from speech and asks for vigilance against slips. 128 gives the scale of the risk: if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல்), the good turns to no good (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). 128 supplies the reason why 642 treats a lapse (சோர்வு), and not only deliberate bad speech, as the danger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:643","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 643 as speech that binds those who have accepted one as a friend (கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும்) and makes even those who have not want friendship. 187 gives the failure of this skill: those who do not know how to make friends with pleasing talk (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) part even their own kin with divisive talk. On that reading the two verses are the positive and negative of one ability, speech as what holds or breaks alliances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:469","type":"parallel_domain","note":"644 says to speak after knowing the திறன் of those concerned. 469 says that even in doing good there is error when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). Both make a good thing, a word or a deed, depend on fitting it to the particular person receiving it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:696","type":"develops","note":"644 asks for speech fitted to its hearers and says no virtue or gain exceeds it. 696 applies this to a single hearer, the king: know his mood, consider the time, and say what is needed in a way he will want (குறிப்பறிந்து காலம் கருதி ... வேட்பச் சொலல்). The general rule for a minister's speech becomes a specific rule for speech to power.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:711","type":"develops","note":"644 says to speak knowing the திறன், which Parimelazhagar takes as the relative standing of speaker and hearers. 711 gives the method: those who know the stock of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining (அவையறிந்து ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக). Chapter 72 works out as a practice what 644 states as a principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:645","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"645 says to speak a word only after knowing that no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 467 says to think and then commit to a task, and that saying 'we will think after committing' is a fault (எண்ணித் துணிக). Both put the testing before the irreversible step; 645 applies to an utterance the rule 467 gives for action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"646 pairs speaking well with drawing the benefit from others' words. 724 sets that pair in the assembly of the learned: make what you have learned go home to them (கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி) and take what is more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). 724 adds what 646 leaves open, that listening is how the speaker's own knowledge grows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:686","type":"agrees","note":"647 lists three things that make a person hard to beat in a contest: able to speak, free of lapses, unafraid (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்). 686 defines the envoy with nearly the same set: learned, not afraid of hostile eyes (கண் அஞ்சான்), speaking so it goes home, and knowing what fits the time. The envoy chapter puts the general portrait of 647 into the one post where it is tested before an enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:872","type":"develops","note":"647 says no one can easily defeat a fearless, unfaltering speaker in a contest (இகல் வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). 872 gives the advice that follows for the other side: take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow if you must, but not of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை). Parimelazhagar takes the latter to be ministers, the people 647 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:648","b":"k:1046","type":"qualifies","note":"648 says the world listens readily to skilled, pleasing speakers. 1046 says the words of the poor go slack even when they speak good matter that they have understood well (நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் ... சோர்வு படும்). 1046 limits 648: how the world receives speech depends on the speaker's means as well as his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:193","type":"agrees","note":"649 treats wanting to say many things as the sign of someone unable to say a few well. 193 says spread-out talk on useless matters (பயனில பாரித்துரைக்கும் உரை) itself announces that the speaker lacks நயன். In both, length of speech is evidence against the speaker; 193 judges his character, 649 his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"649 faults many words and praises a few faultless ones; the next verse faults those who cannot expound what they have learned so that others understand (உணர விரித்துரையாதார்). Manakkudavar reads the pair together: speak briefly, but also expand where it is needed. 650 keeps 649 from being taken as a rule of brevity alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:685","type":"develops","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words (மாசற்ற சில சொல்லல் தேற்றாதவர்) long to say many. 685 makes brevity the envoy's first mark: he speaks compactly (தொகச் சொல்லி), drops what is harsh, and speaks so as to please. 685 shows the positive skill whose absence 649 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:650","b":"k:1274","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use a flower's scent (நாற்றம்) for what is inside a person. In 650 the flower is fully open and gives no scent, like the learned person who cannot convey what he knows; in 1274 the scent is held inside a closed bud, like the unspoken meaning inside the girl's budding smile. The two uses mirror each other: an open display with nothing reaching others, and a closed one whose content the lover already perceives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:651","b":"k:455","type":"qualifies","note":"651 sets வினைநலம் above துணைநலம்: support gives only ஆக்கம், good action gives all that is wanted. 455 says purity of mind and purity of action (செய்வினை தூய்மை) both come leaning on purity of company (இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்). Read together, the action that 651 ranks above one's support is itself said to depend on the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:962","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who say 'ஆஅதும்' (we shall rise) to stop doing what dims their ஒளி. 962 says those who want சீர் together with பேராண்மை will not do 'சீரல்ல' even for the sake of சீர். 962 takes the harder case 653 leaves open: the dimming deed may itself be a route to fame, and it is still refused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:971","type":"develops","note":"653 treats ஒளி as something the ambitious ('ஆஅதும் என்னுமவர்') must protect by refraining from certain deeds. 971 defines ஒளி as 'உள்ள வெறுக்கை', abundance of spirit, and இளி as saying one will live without it. Together they give ஒளி two sides: it is lost by what one does (653) and it consists in what one aspires to (971).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654 says those of unshaken sight do nothing degrading even in distress (இடுக்கண் படினும்). 174 says those of 'புன்மையில் காட்சி' who have conquered the senses will not covet on the plea 'இலம்' (we have nothing). Both verses name a clear-sighted person (காட்சியவர்) and deny that need is an excuse; 174 applies it to coveting, 654 to action generally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654: clear-sighted people do not do what is degrading even when trouble comes. 218: those of 'கடனறி காட்சி' do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ... ஒல்கார்). One verse says hardship does not make them do wrong, the other that it does not make them stop doing good; the two are the negative and positive halves of the same steadiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:655","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"655 asks that one not do what will be regretted afterwards. 467 says to think and then resolve (எண்ணித் துணிக), and calls it a lapse to say 'we will think after resolving'. Both put the reflection before the act: 467 as a rule of planning, 655 as a rule about the purity of the deed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:961","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids the blameworthy deed even when the need is a mother's hunger. 961 says to give up things that would diminish one (குன்ற வருப விடல்) even if they are indispensable (இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்). Both deny that necessity licenses a degrading act; 961 states the principle abstractly under honour, 656 gives it a concrete and painful instance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:1047","type":"same_image","note":"656 uses the mother who bore one (ஈன்றாள்) as the strongest pull toward wrongdoing for the sake of relief. 1047 says that one in poverty not joined with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும்). The mother marks the limit case in both: 656 holds conduct firm against her need, while 1047 says poverty without virtue loses even her regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:408","type":"parallel_domain","note":"657: the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) is better than wealth got by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). 408: wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமை). The same comparison of good people's poverty with bad people's wealth is made from each side, once about how wealth was got and once about who holds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"657 ranks the utter poverty of the worthy above blame-laden wealth. 988 says poverty (இன்மை) is no disgrace (இளிவன்று) to one who has the firmness called சால்பு. 988 supplies the ground for 657's ranking: for the சான்றோர் poverty carries no இளிவு, whereas the wealth in 657 carries பழி.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"658: forbidden deeds done without shunning them bring anguish even if they succeed (முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்). 113: give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness, even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). Both concede success in advance and still reject the act, so the objection is not that such means fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:177","type":"agrees","note":"658 warns that the gain of a forbidden deed turns to பீழை later. 177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting (வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம்), because at the time of enjoying it the fruit is hard to make good (விளைவயின் மாண்டற்கு அரிதாம் பயன்). Both place the harm at the stage of the result and not at the stage of getting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"658 says forbidden actions bring anguish even when they are accomplished. 931 says not to want gambling even if one wins (வென்றிடினும்), since the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. The gambling verse gives an image for 658's claim: the success itself is what carries the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:112","type":"agrees","note":"The second half of 659 says what comes from good ways (நற்பாலவை) yields later even if it is lost (இழப்பினும் பிற்பயக்கும்). 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security for those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). Both give rightly got wealth a benefit that outlasts the moment, 659 for the person later and 112 for descendants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:660","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"660: wealth made by deceit is water kept in an unfired clay vessel. 1000: great wealth obtained by one without character is good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both use a faulty கலம் for wealth that cannot be kept, but the fault differs: in 660 it is how the wealth was made, in 1000 it is the holder's character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:34","type":"parallel_domain","note":"661: firmness in action is a person's firmness of mind (மனத்திட்பம்); everything else is something other (மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற). 34: being spotless in mind (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல்) is all of virtue; the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற). The two verses have the same shape, reducing a whole domain to a state of mind and dismissing the remainder as 'பிற', once for virtue and once for effective action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:600","type":"agrees","note":"661 locates firmness in action in the mind and sets other things aside. 600 says a person's real strength (உரம்) is abundance of spirit (உள்ள வெறுக்கை), and those without it are trees. Both place strength within the person; Parimelazhagar's note on 661 lists the outer things meant by 'மற்றைய' as the firmness of army, fort and allies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 662 keeps that first rule (ஊறொரால்) but adds a second for when harm arrives anyway (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 662 thus allows that foresight is not always enough; Parimelazhagar reads the second case as a task spoiled by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:442","type":"parallel_domain","note":"662 gives two rules as the way of those who have studied the matter: avoid harm (ஊறொரால்) and do not give way once it has come (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 442 tells a king to cherish those who remove the trouble that has come (உற்றநோய் நீக்கி) and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்). The same before-and-after pair appears as the doer's own discipline in 662 and as the quality to look for in advisers in 442.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:664","b":"k:819","type":"develops","note":"664: saying is easy for anyone; doing as one has said (சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்) is hard. 819: the bond with those whose deed is one thing and word another (வினைவேறு சொல்வேறு பட்டார்) is bitter even in a dream. 664 states the gap between word and deed as a difficulty of execution; 819 judges the same gap in a friend as a fault of character.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"666: what one has thought of is obtained as thought, if one is firm. 309: all one has thought of is obtained at once (உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்), if one does not think of anger in the heart. The same promise is attached to two different inner conditions, one positive (firmness) and one negative (absence of anger).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:667","b":"k:1032","type":"same_image","note":"667: do not scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), for there are people like the linchpin (அச்சாணி) of a great rolling chariot. 1032: those who plough are the ஆணி of the world's people, because they bear all who cannot plough and take up other work. Both use the pin that holds the wheel for someone small in appearance on whom everything turns; 667 leaves the person unnamed and 1032 names the farmer. Parimelazhagar supplies the smallness in 667 from the simile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 says to think and then resolve on a task, and that saying 'we will think after resolving' is a lapse. 668 takes up from that point: once the task has been seen without confusion (கலங்காது கண்ட), act without wavering (துளங்காது). Together they give a sequence in which doubt belongs before the decision and not after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:671","type":"agrees","note":"668: in a task one has seen clearly and decided on (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), act without wavering and without delay (தூக்கங் கடிந்து). 671, opening the next chapter, says deliberation ends in reaching resolve (துணிவு) and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). The same rule closes the chapter on firmness and opens the chapter on method: once the thinking is done, delay is the fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"669 says to carry through the worthwhile deed despite the pain it brings at first. 611 says one must not slacken on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), since effort gives the greatness needed. Both refuse the difficulty of a task as a reason to hold back; 611 speaks of effort and 669 of resolve (துணிவு).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"669: do with resolve the deed that yields joy (இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை), even though pain comes close at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும்). 615: one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினை விழைவான்) is a pillar who wipes away his kin's துன்பம். Both set இன்பம், துன்பம் and வினை against each other; 669 justifies bearing pain by the joy the deed yields, while 615 describes a doer who is not after pleasure at all and whose work removes others' pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:670","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"670: whatever other firmness they have attained (எனைத்திட்பம் எய்தியக் கண்ணும்), the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action. 750: whatever excellences it has (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்), a fort is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண்). The two chapter-closing verses are built alike and make the same point, that other strengths count for nothing without the capacity to act, once of a minister and once of a fort's defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:490","type":"agrees","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து) and like its strike when the moment is right (சீர்த்த இடத்து). 672 gives the same double instruction without the image: take time over what is to be done slowly, and do not delay what must not be delayed. Both hold that waiting and speed are each correct in their place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"671 calls it a fault when a resolved deed lingers in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 672 immediately limits this: there are deeds to be done slowly, and over those one should take time (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால); only the deed that must be done without delay must not be slept on. Delay is therefore wrong by the nature of the task, not in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"485 says those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர்). Manakkudavar reads the second half of 673 in that sense: when the deed is not possible, keep it in mind and act on seeing the place where it becomes possible. Parimelazhagar reads 673 differently, as open force where one is stronger and one of the other three expedients where one is not, so the link holds on Manakkudavar's reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:677","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு) and thinking it over themselves. 677 narrows whose counsel counts for a particular task: the one who knows that deed from inside (அவ்வினை உள்ளறிவான்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be someone who has done it before. General consultation in 462 becomes, in 677, learning the mind of the person with experience of the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:678","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"678 says to get one deed done by means of another, as one binds an elephant with a rutting elephant (யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று). Manakkudavar takes this as setting an enemy's own enemy against him. On that reading 875 is the case worked out: one who is alone with two enemies should take one of them as an ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க அவற்றின் ஒன்று). Parimelazhagar reads 678 more generally, as making a task in hand the means to another like it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:875","type":"agrees","note":"875 says that one without allies and facing two enemies should take one of them as a pleasing ally. 679 says that winning over ஒட்டார் is more urgent than favours to friends; Manakkudavar takes ஒட்டார் as one's enemies, Parimelazhagar as those not allied with one's enemy. On either reading both verses treat alliance as something to be made out of the hostile or uncommitted when one is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 says to bear with enemies on meeting them (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க), for when their end comes their head will go down; Parimelazhagar glosses சுமக்க as bowing. 680 has the small bow to the great to obtain terms. 488 adds what 680 does not say: that such bowing can be a way of waiting for the stronger side's fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"861 says to avoid standing against the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). 680 describes what that means for a weak party: those of small domain, fearing the trembling of their own side (உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சி), bow to the greater and accept terms if they can get them (குறைபெறின் கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). 861 gives the rule and 680 the settlement that follows from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"967, on honour, says it is better to be called ruined as one stands than to live by going behind those who are hostile to one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்று வாழ்தலின்). 680 says the small will bow to the great and take what terms they can. The conflict depends on reading 967's ஒட்டார் as covering a stronger enemy; Parimelazhagar limits it to following scorners for a living, but he also remarks on 680 that bowing is not the wish of those with honour, which is why the verse says 'they will accept' as a report of worldly practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:502","type":"agrees","note":"502 places trust in one born in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து), free of faults and with a shame that recoils from blemish. 681 makes birth in a fit family (ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) one of the envoy's three marks. Family is used in both as evidence about a person before he is given a charge, for trust in general in 502 and for the embassy in 681.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:692","type":"develops","note":"681 asks of the envoy 'qualities that kings desire' (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை) without saying what they are. Manakkudavar glosses the phrase as not desiring what the king desires, which is the content of 692 (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை), and Parimelazhagar says this quality is obtained from the following chapter on conduct around kings. 692 therefore supplies, on the commentators' reading, what 681 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:992","type":"same_image","note":"The first line of 681 and of 992 is the same: அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல். 992 says these two are what is called courtesy (பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு); 681 lists them and adds a third, the qualities kings desire (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை), as the nature of one who carries a message. The envoy's qualification is thus the book's general definition of courtesy with a courtly requirement added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:683","b":"k:722","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who can make what they have learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்). 683 uses the same construction for the envoy: he must be strong in the treatises among those who know treatises (நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன்). Both measure learning by standing among the learned, not against the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:407","type":"qualifies","note":"684 counts presence (உரு) among the three things an envoy needs. 407 says the fine looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 407 supplies the limit on 684's உரு: appearance counts only in the union (செறிவு) with understanding and learning that 684 itself demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen intelligence together with the treatises (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு). 684 requires of the envoy understanding and well-examined learning (அறிவு ... ஆராய்ந்த கல்வி) held together. Parimelazhagar glosses the first term in each verse as natural intelligence and the second as acquired learning, so both verses ask for the two in combination, for the minister and for the envoy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:685","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells one who serves a king to say what is needed and not disliked, in a way that is wanted (வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்). 685 has the envoy remove what offends and speak so as to please (தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி) while securing good for his own side. The same manner of speech is prescribed toward one's own king in 696 and toward a foreign king in 685; in both the pleasing manner serves what has to be said.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:686","b":"k:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says to make what one has learned go home before the learned (கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி). 686 uses the same two terms for the envoy: having learned (கற்று), he speaks so that it goes home (செலச்சொல்லி). In both, learning is complete only when it can be delivered to a demanding audience, the assembly in 724 and a hostile court in 686.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:675","type":"parallel_domain","note":"675 says to act after thinking through resources, means, time, deed and place (காலம் ... இடனொடு ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). 687 uses the same terms for speech: consider the time, know the place, think it through and then speak (காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து எண்ணி உரைப்பான்). The envoy's speech is planned by the same checklist the book gives for any deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells the courtier to read the king's mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) before speaking. 687 has the best envoy know what is due, consider the time (காலங் கருதி) and know the place before he speaks. The phrase காலங் கருதி is common to both, so the discipline for speaking to one's own king is the same as for speaking to another's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:712","type":"agrees","note":"712 says those who know how words work should discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), understand well and then speak; Parimelazhagar takes இடை as the receptive moment of the assembly. 687 asks the envoy to consider the time, which Parimelazhagar likewise glosses as the listener's receptive state (செவ்வி). Both make the listener's readiness a condition of speaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truth (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 688 names தூய்மை first among the envoy's marks and then requires வாய்மை along with all three. 298 gives the reason the two words belong together: truthfulness is how inner purity shows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:383","type":"parallel_domain","note":"383 names three things that never leave a ruler: promptness, learning and resolve (தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்). 688 has the same frame for the envoy who repeats the king's message: purity, support and resolve, these three (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்). துணிவுடைமை is the term common to king and envoy; the other two differ with the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:501","type":"develops","note":"501 says a man is to be trusted after being tested by virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life. 688 lists the faithful envoy's marks as purity, support, resolve and truthfulness (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை ... வாய்மை). Parimelazhagar explains தூய்மை as being clean in respect of wealth and pleasure and துணிவு as not holding back for fear of harm, which makes 688's qualities the passes of 501's tests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says that for people of good conduct it is not possible to speak evil with the mouth even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்). 689 asks the same of the envoy in his own field: a firmness whose mouth does not let slip a word that brings blemish (வடுமாற்றம் வாய்சோரா). What Book 1 treats as a result of character, 689 makes a condition for carrying the king's word. (This edition prints வாய்சேரா; the commentaries read வாய்சோரா.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 says to guard against a lapse in speech (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு) because both gain and ruin come from it. 689 is that rule at the point where the stakes are the king's: the envoy must not let a word that discredits slip from his mouth (வாய்சோரா); Parimelazhagar adds that the slip in question would come from fear for himself. The verb சோர் is shared, and 689 names the hardness (வன்கண்) that prevents the lapse 642 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:638","type":"agrees","note":"638 says that even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side owes it to him to say what is good for him (உறுதி ... கூறல் கடன்). 690 says the envoy secures the lord's good (உறுதி) even at the cost of his own life. Both use உறுதி for the king's real interest and hold the servant to it under threat, from his own king in 638 and from a foreign one in 690.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:723","type":"qualifies","note":"723 says those who will die among enemies are easy to find and those who do not fear in an assembly are rare (பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர் அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 690 asks the envoy to face death (இறுதி) while speaking in a hostile court. 723 separates the two kinds of courage and rates the second as rarer; the envoy of 690 needs both together, which indicates how much the verse asks.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:778","type":"parallel_domain","note":"778 says warriors who do not fear for their life when battle comes (உறின் உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர்) do not lose their distinction even if their lord is angry. 690 says the envoy is one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short and secures what is good for his lord (இறைவற்கு உறுதி). The envoy's office is given the soldier's standard, with both verses naming the lord (இறைவன்) as the one served.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:896","type":"develops","note":"691 compares the king to a fire one warms at from a measured distance. 896 says one burned by fire may still survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார் பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகுவார்). Read together, the fire in 691 understates the danger: 896 ranks the offended great above fire, though Parimelazhagar takes its பெரியார் as those great in austerity, not kings.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:1104","type":"same_image","note":"691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings (இகல்வேந்தர்) to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor closing in (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). In 1104 a lover asks where she got a fire that burns when he withdraws and is cool when he comes near (நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும் தீ). The king's fire burns at close range and the lover's reverses that rule, so the court calls for a kept middle distance and the love speech for none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:692","b":"k:179","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those same kings; Parimelazhagar takes விழைப as the enjoyments, adornment and precedence proper to a king. 179 says Fortune herself comes to the wise who, knowing virtue, do not covet (அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும் ... திரு). Both make not-wanting the route by which wealth arrives, once as court prudence and once as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:693","b":"k:891","type":"agrees","note":"693 says those who would guard themselves should guard against the gravest things (போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as faults a king cannot forgive. 891 says not slighting the power of those able to act is the chief of all the guarding done by those who guard themselves (போற்றுவார் போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). Both use போற்றல் for self-protection and locate its first object in one's dealings with the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:694","b":"k:715","type":"parallel_domain","note":"694 says that in the presence of the great one should suppress whispering and shared smiles (செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்). 715 calls the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு) the best of good things. Court and assembly get the same rule of holding speech back before superiors; 694 extends it to private asides and laughter, which Parimelazhagar says the king would take as aimed at himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"695 treats the king's secret (மறை) as something to be heard only when he lets it go. 1076 says the base are like a beaten drum because they carry to others the secrets they have heard (தாம் கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). The two give the two ends of handling a secret: restraint in acquiring it in 695, and the failure to hold it in 1076.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:696","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"696 opens with குறிப்பறிந்து, knowing the king's mood, as the first condition of speaking to him. 701 praises the one who looks and knows what is meant without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்) as an ornament to the earth. The skill 696 names in passing is taken up as the subject of the next chapter, beginning with 701.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"697 says to speak what is wanted and never to say what has no use, even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்). 200 gives the general rule: say words that have use, do not say words that have none (சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்). The court verse applies the household rule and tightens it, since here even the king's own request does not license useless talk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:667","type":"parallel_domain","note":"698 forbids belittling a king on the grounds of youth or kinship (இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார்). 667 says one must not scorn by outward form, since the great rolling chariot depends on its linchpin (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும்). Both reject judging a person by a surface measure, whether age, family relation or size, and point instead to the function he carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:801","type":"qualifies","note":"700 says the familiarity (கெழுதகைமை) that does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதி) brings ruin. 801 defines old friendship (பழைமை) as friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties of intimacy (கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு). The liberty that defines friendship between equals is thus withdrawn where the other party is a king, as Parimelazhagar reads 700.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:805","type":"tension","note":"700 says intimacy that does what is improper brings ruin (பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்). 805 says that when friends do something hurtful one should understand it as folly or as great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்). The conflict holds on Manakkudavar's gloss of 700, which speaks of the nature of friendship in general; on Parimelazhagar's, 700 concerns a king and the two verses address different relationships.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:701","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"701 praises the one who knows what is meant by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 1100, in the love chapter that has the same title குறிப்பறிதல், says that when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use (வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல). Reading without speech is a minister's rare skill in 701 and the ordinary condition of two lovers in 1100.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"703 urges the king to pay any price for those who read signs. 445 says that since counsellors serve as the ruler's eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக ஒழுகலான்), he must choose them with care. 445 gives the reason behind 703: the king sees through his advisers, so an adviser who can read what is unsaid is worth what Parimelazhagar lists as wealth, land, elephants and horses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"703 says to give whatever it takes from one's resources and secure those who read a sign by a sign (யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 794 says the friendship of one of good family who is ashamed of blame should be had even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு). The shared formula marks the two kinds of person the book thinks worth paying for, the perceptive adviser and the friend with a sense of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"704 stops at saying that those who cannot grasp the unspoken are 'different' (வேறு) from those who can. 410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). Parimelazhagar glosses the வேறு of 704 with this very comparison, the idea being that they are animals, so 410 states outright what 704 leaves implied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"704 says those who grasp what is meant without being told and the rest are alike in limbs (உறுப்போ ரனையரால்) and yet different. 993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people (உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால்), and true likeness is likeness of character. Both deny that the shared body makes people alike, with perception the dividing line in 704 and பண்பு in 993.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"704 says readers of the unspoken and others share the same limbs but are different (வேறு). 1071 says the base look just like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a resemblance the speaker has seen nowhere else. The same figure of outward human likeness hiding an inner difference serves a point about intelligence in 704 and about moral baseness in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 says eyes that cannot read another's meaning are of no use. 393 says only the learned have eyes, and the unlearned have two sores in the face (முகத்திரண்டு புண்ணுடையர் கல்லாதவர்). In both the physical eye is treated as worthless without a trained capacity behind it, learning in one and the reading of signs in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). 574 asks what eyes without measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் ... கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்). Each denies the eye its name unless it does more than see, perceiving another's mind in 705 and looking kindly in 574.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"706 says the face shows what fills the heart, as crystal shows what is beside it. 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground, and the spoken word shows the lineage of the one born in it (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும்). Both rest on காட்டும்: something visible discloses a hidden source, mood in the face and family in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 830 advises that when an enemy turns friend one should befriend with the face and let go in the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). If the face always runs ahead of the mind, as 707 says, the control 830 recommends should not be possible; the two agree only if 707 describes the unguarded face.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:1277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"707 says the face is ahead of the person in showing pleasure or anger (தான் முந்துறும்). In 1277 a woman says her bangles knew before she did that her lover had left (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). Both give the body a knowledge that arrives before the mind's own, a general observation in 707 and a lover's remark on her thinning arms in 1277.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:708","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"708 says that if one has people who look within and understand what has happened, it is enough to stand looking them in the face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). 1100 says that once eyes agree with eyes, spoken words are of no use. Both make speech unnecessary between the right pair; Parimelazhagar adds for 708 that going on to speak would lessen both parties, a concern for dignity the love verse does not have.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:710","b":"k:796","type":"same_image","note":"710 says the measuring rod (அளக்குங்கோல்) of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye. 796 says ruin has one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length (கிளைஞரை நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்). The book uses the measuring rod twice for gauging other people, the eye taking a present reading of a mind in 710 and adversity testing loyalty over time in 796.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:711","b":"k:721","type":"develops","note":"Both verses end with the identical line சொல்லின் தொகையறிந்த தூய்மையவர். 711 commands such people to know the assembly before speaking; 721 says that, having known the kinds (வகையறிந்து), they do not let their speech slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). The repeated line makes 721 the outcome of 711's instruction: knowing the assembly is what removes the fear that causes slips.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:712","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"712 asks the speaker in an assembly to discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), which Parimelazhagar glosses as the hearers' readiness to listen. 696 asks the same of one speaking to a king: know his mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) and say it so that it is wanted. The timing rule is the same before an assembly and before a single ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:713","b":"k:401","type":"agrees","note":"713 says those who take up speaking without knowing the assembly (அவையறியார்) do not know the kinds of speech and have no ability at all. 401 says holding forth in an assembly (கோட்டி கொளல்) without full learning is like playing dice with no marked board. Each names a different missing precondition for speaking in an assembly, knowledge of the hearers in 713 and knowledge of the books in 401, and each treats the speech that results as empty.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:425","type":"tension","note":"714 tells the speaker to change with the audience, bright before the bright and blank before the blank. 425 says understanding is what does not open and close (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது அறிவு). If 425's second half is read generally, as steadiness of bearing, the two disagree about whether the wise adapt their display; on Parimelazhagar's reading, where 425 is about constancy in friendship with the world, the conflict disappears.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:844","type":"same_image","note":"714 uses a colour pair for intelligence: be bright (ஒள்ளியர்) before the bright, and take the colour of white plaster (வான்சுதை வண்ணம்) before the blank (வெளியார்). 844 uses the same pair: whiteness (வெண்மை) is the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம்). Read together, the learned person of 714 puts on whiteness knowingly before those who, by 844, are white because they think themselves bright.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:419","type":"agrees","note":"715 praises the restraint (செறிவு) of not speaking first among elders. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to have for those without fine listening. Both treat modest speech as a mark of what a person has learned, and 419 supplies the source 715 leaves unstated: the habit of listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 gives the general rule: since gain and ruin come from speech, guard against a lapse in it (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு). 716 applies it to one setting, a slip before the widely learned, and measures the damage by comparing it to falling from the path (ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar reads as a renouncer's lapse from the good path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:957","type":"parallel_domain","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those who have taken in wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). In both, the weight of a fault depends on where it is seen, the standing of the witnesses in 716 and of the person in 957, and not only on its size.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:717","b":"k:404","type":"qualifies","note":"717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines among those able to examine words without fault (சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து). 404 says the wise do not accept an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்), however good it is. The same expert hearers decide both cases: before them real learning becomes visible and untaught cleverness is not accepted.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:728","type":"qualifies","note":"719 and 728 share the phrase நல்லவையுள் நன்கு செலச் சொல்லு-. 728 says those who cannot speak tellingly in a good assembly are of no use though they have learned much; 719 tells those who can do so not to speak in a mean assembly even by forgetting (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க). Together they set the limits: silence in a good assembly is a failure, and silence in a mean one is required.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:849","type":"agrees","note":"719 forbids speaking good things in a mean assembly. 849 gives a reason in another chapter: one who shows something to a person who does not see becomes himself unseeing (காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்), while that person goes on seeing as he saw. Parimelazhagar makes the same point on 719, that both assemblies would then look down on the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"720 says holding forth before those not of one's kind is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). 1000 says great wealth in the hands of one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both describe a fine liquid ruined by where it lands, and so place the loss in the receiver and not in the thing given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"721 says those who know the body of words and the kinds of assembly do not let their speech slip before the capable (வாய்சோரார்), which Parimelazhagar reads as not slipping out of fear. 647 lists the same qualities, able to speak, without slips, without fear (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்), and says such a person is hard for anyone to defeat. 647 states the payoff of the fearlessness that 721 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:716","type":"develops","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those of wide learning is like falling from the path. 721 answers from the next chapter: those who know words and know the assembly do not slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). One verse gives the cost of the slip and the other says what prevents it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who make what they have learned go home before the learned (கற்ற செலச் சொல்லுவார்) are called learned among the learned. 424 defines understanding with the same verb: saying things so that they go in easily (எண்பொருளவாகச் செலச்சொல்லி) and seeing the subtle meaning in what others say. Both measure learning or understanding by whether it reaches the hearer, not by what the speaker holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"722 gives the title 'learned among the learned' to those who can convey their learning to the learned. 650 gives the reverse case: those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like flowers that bloom in clusters without scent. Together they treat communication as part of learning itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:762","type":"tension","note":"723 calls those who die amid enemies எளியர், easy to find. 762 says the hardness that does not fear wounds when the king falters is rare except in an old army (தொல்படைக்கல்லால் அரிது). If 723's first half is read as saying battle courage is common, the two disagree about how rare it is; if 762 is limited to courage in defeat and with reduced numbers, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:872","type":"parallel_domain","note":"723 says those who will die amid enemies are easy to find, and those who do not fear within an assembly are hard to find (அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 872 says one may take on the enmity of those who plough with the bow but not of those who plough with the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). Both rank the word above the weapon, 723 by how rare the courage is and 872 by how dangerous the opponent is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:724","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"724 joins two acts: convey your learning (செலச்சொல்லி) and take in what those more learned have. 424 joins the same two as its definition of understanding: say things so that they go in (செலச்சொல்லி) and see the subtle meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள் காண்பது). What 424 calls understanding, 724 presents as what a fearless speaker gains from an assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:391","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both verses open with the command to learn. 391 says learn without flaw and then stand in keeping with it (கற்க கசடற ... நிற்க அதற்குத் தக); 725 says learn with measure so as to give a fearless reply (மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு). 391 gives learning its end in conduct, and 725 gives it a second end in public speech.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"725 says to learn by the proper path and with measure (ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க) in order to reply in the assembly without fear; Parimelazhagar takes அளவு as the science of logic. 645 says to speak a word only after knowing no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 725 names the training that makes 645's test possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:534","type":"parallel_domain","note":"726 asks what a sword is to those who are not hard-eyed, and what books are to those who fear a subtle assembly (நுண்ணவை அஞ்சுபவர்க்கு). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை), which Parimelazhagar explains as a fortress being of no use to them. In each, fear cancels an instrument that is sound in itself: sword, books, fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"726 says books are nothing to one who fears a subtle assembly. 647 says the one who can speak, does not slip and does not fear (அஞ்சான்) is hard for anyone to defeat, and Parimelazhagar takes that fearlessness as fearlessness of the assembly. 647 states positively the condition whose absence 726 says makes learning useless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:140","type":"parallel_domain","note":"728 and 140 have the same form: 'though they have learned much'. In 140, those who have not learned to live in accord with the world are without understanding though they have learned much (பலகற்றும்). In 728, those who cannot speak well in a good assembly are without use though they have learned many things (பல்லவை கற்றும்). Each names a practical ability without which the amount learned does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:395","type":"qualifies","note":"395 calls the unlearned the lowest (கடையரே கல்லாதவர்). 729 uses the same word to put someone beneath them: those who have studied and understood and still fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). 729 thus limits 395's ranking, since learning that is not voiced does not lift a person above the bottom rank and, as Parimelazhagar explains, leaves them with only the labour of having studied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:730","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"730 counts those who cannot voice their learning as equal to those who are not, though they exist. 214 says one who knows what is fitting truly lives and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both apply the figure of a living person counted as dead or absent to someone who gives nothing to others, wealth in 214 and learning in 730; Parimelazhagar reads 730's இல்லார் as the dead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:239","type":"develops","note":"731 sets unfailing yield and worthy people (தக்கார்) side by side as marks of a land. 239 says land that bears a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) declines in its blameless rich produce (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 239 makes a causal claim where 731 only lists: the land's yield depends on the quality of the people it carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"731 makes unfailing yield (தள்ளா விளையுள்) one of three things that must come together (சேர்வது) for a land. 545 says rain and yield (பெயலும் விளையுளும்) gather (தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as it should be wielded. Read together, the yield that defines a land in 731 is traced in 545 to just rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:1032","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar takes விளையுள் in 731 to stand for the cultivators, since the verse lists it beside two kinds of people (தக்கார், செல்வர்). 1032 calls those who plough the linchpin (ஆணி) of the world because they carry all who go off to other work. 1032 gives the reason the cultivators come first in 731's list.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:15","type":"develops","note":"732 says a land yields greatly because ruin is rare there (அருங்கேட்டால் ஆற்ற விளைவது). 15 says rain is what ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and also what raises up the ruined. Parimelazhagar's list of the கேடு meant in 732 begins with too much rain and no rain, so 15 names the chief source of the ruin that 732 wants to be rare.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:736","type":"develops","note":"732 asks that ruin be rare (அருங்கேடு) so that the land yields abundantly. 736 goes a step further: the foremost land does not know ruin, and its richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 732 states the ordinary standard and 736 the highest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:552","type":"qualifies","note":"733 defines a land as one that bears burdens arriving all at once and still agrees to give the king his whole due (இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது). 552 says that asking (இரவு) by one who stands with the sceptre is like a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'. The land's willingness to pay in 733 is not a licence for the king to demand: Manakkudavar reads 733's burdens as levies beyond the fixed due, which is the situation 552 condemns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"In 733 the land bears the load that comes upon it (பொறை ... தாங்கி). In 990 the great earth will not bear its load (தாங்காது ... பொறை) if the worthy decline in worth. The same words describe a land's capacity to carry weight, and 990 makes that capacity depend on the character of its best people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:734","b":"k:735","type":"develops","note":"734 lists what must not reach a land from outside or by nature: hunger, disease and the enemy who destroys (செறுபகை). 735 lists what must not exist inside it: many factions (பல்குழு), the inner enemy that lays waste (உட்பகை) and murderous bands that harass the king. The two verses divide the threats into external and internal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"735 says a land has no murderous bands that harass the king (வேந்தலைக்கும் கொல்குறும்பு). 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. 550 gives the ruler's means of producing the condition 735 describes; Parimelazhagar on 735 likewise says these three are to be removed by the king and the inhabitants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"735 requires a land to be without the inner enemy that lays waste (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை). 889 says inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவு), holds ruin within it (உள்ளதாங் கேடு). 889 explains why 735 demands its complete absence and not just its reduction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:736","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"736 calls foremost the land whose richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 955 says an old family does not part from its character even when what it has to give has fallen away (வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் ... பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று). Both use the same 'even when' construction to test excellence by what remains after loss, one for a land and one for a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:737","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"737 names a strong fort (வல்லரண்) among the limbs (உறுப்பு) of a land, with its waters and hill. 381 names the fort (அரண்) and the people or land (குடி) as two of the six things a king must have, which Parimelazhagar calls his six limbs (அங்கம்). 737 nests one of the king's limbs inside another, which fits the order of the chapters: land, then fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:738","b":"k:734","type":"agrees","note":"738 lists five ornaments (அணி) of a land: freedom from disease (பிணியின்மை), wealth, yield, happiness and protection (ஏமம்). 734 defines a land by three absences: severe hunger, unceasing disease (ஓவாப் பிணி) and destructive enemies. The two lists match as positive and negative: yield answers hunger, பிணியின்மை answers disease, and ஏமம் answers the enemy, while 738 adds wealth and happiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"739 says lands are those whose riches come without being sought (நாடா வளத்தன). 594 says prosperity itself asks the way and goes (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்) to the person of unwavering drive. Both picture wealth arriving unsought, but 594 makes it the result of the person's energy, where 739 makes it a property of the land.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:1040","type":"qualifies","note":"739 praises land whose riches need no seeking. 1040 says the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle (அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 1040 limits 739: the land has riches to give, but not to those who do nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:558","type":"develops","note":"740 says a land's excellences bring no benefit (பயமின்று) without accord between it and its king. 558 says that under the sceptre of a king who does not do justice, having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை). 558 shows one way the wealth praised in this chapter turns useless or worse under the wrong ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"740 says that a land with every excellence is of no use without accord with its king. 770 says that though an army has plenty of steadfast men (நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும்), there is no army where there are no leaders (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). In both, a body that is complete in its own parts is counted as nothing without its head.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:569","type":"develops","note":"741 says a fort matters to those who fear attack and look to their own safety. 569 says a king who has not made his defences (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will, when war comes, be struck with fear and quickly perish (வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்). 569 states the cost of ignoring 741: without a fort prepared beforehand, fear has nowhere to turn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:742","b":"k:737","type":"same_image","note":"742 says a fort has clear water (மணிநீர்), open ground, a hill (மலை) and a forest of fine shade. 737 lists as limbs of a land the two waters (இருபுனல்), a well-placed hill (வாய்ந்த மலை), the water that comes from it, and a strong fort. The same features of the terrain, water and hill, are counted first as a land's resources and then as a fort's defences, and 737 already names the fort as part of the land.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:743","b":"k:421","type":"parallel_domain","note":"743 defines the fort as a wall complete in four measures: height, breadth, strength and difficulty of approach (உயர்வு அகலம் திண்மை அருமை). 421 calls understanding the tool that guards against ruin and an inner fort that enemies cannot destroy (செறுவார்க்கும் உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்). 421 moves the fort inside the person: the quality 743 seeks in masonry, that it cannot be broken into, 421 claims for understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:745","b":"k:499","type":"develops","note":"745 says a fort is hard to take, holds stored food (கொண்ட கூழ்த்து) and is easy for those inside to hold their position in (அகத்தார் நிலைக்கு எளிது). 499 says that even people with no good defences or standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to take on in the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 shows that the insiders' advantage of position, which 745 asks the fort to give, already belongs to home ground without walls.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:762","type":"parallel_domain","note":"746 wants in a fort good men who help at the place where it would otherwise fall; Parimelazhagar glosses இடத்து as the point of destruction by those outside. 762 says the hardness that does not fear a wound when things give way and the force is reduced (உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண்) is rare except in an ancient army. Both measure fighting men by what they do at the moment of collapse, in the fort and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"746 says a fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place (இடத்துதவும் நல்லாள்). 1030 says a family with no good man to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. The same word நல்லாள் names the person on whom a fort and a family each depend at the moment of danger; goods alone hold up neither.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:764","type":"parallel_domain","note":"747 says a fort is hard to seize by encircling, by assault without encircling, or by subversion (அறைப்படுத்தும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as undermining those inside through people they trust. 764 defines an army as one that does not break and cannot be subverted (அறைபோகாதாகி). The same word அறை marks treachery from within as a danger that fort and army must both be proof against, beside open force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:748","b":"k:493","type":"agrees","note":"748 says a fort is where those who hold it keep their hold (பற்றாற்றி) and defeat even those strong enough to besiege them; Parimelazhagar notes that the smaller force does not just endure but wins. 493 says even those without strength become strong and win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves. 748 is 493's rule about place applied to the fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"749 says a fort has its distinction from what is done in action (வினைமுகத்து வீறெய்தி) so that enemies fall at the front of battle. 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has, it is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). 750 turns 749's praise into a condition; Parimelazhagar says the two verses together show that a fort cannot do without its defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:767","type":"parallel_domain","note":"749 places a fort's distinction in the actions by which enemies are brought down as soon as battle is joined (முனைமுகத்து); Parimelazhagar explains these as recognising the attack begun and choosing the acts that defeat it. 767 says an army is one that knows the way to bear the battle coming at it (தலைவந்த போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து) and advances. Both ask for trained response to the enemy's opening move, from the walls and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"750 says a fort's excellences do not exist for those who lack excellence in action. 727 says the treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is like a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி amid enemies (பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள்). In both a good instrument, a fort or learning, is worth nothing in the hands of someone who cannot act when the test comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"751 treats wealth's power to give standing to persons of no account as its distinction. 241 notes the same fact, that material wealth is found even among the base (பொருட்செல்வம் பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள), and uses it to rank wealth below grace, the 'wealth among wealths'. What 751 counts in wealth's favour, 241 counts against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:988","type":"qualifies","note":"752 reports that all scorn the poor and all honour the rich. 988 says lack is no disgrace to one who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 752 describes how the world behaves; 988 denies that the scorn is deserved where character is present, which Parimelazhagar's gloss on 752 (scorned 'even if they have every good') makes pointed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1046 gives a particular case: even when the poor understand a good matter well and say it, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The general contempt of 752 is shown reaching what a poor person says.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1047","type":"develops","note":"752's 'everyone' (எல்லாரும்) scorns the have-nots. 1047 carries that to the limit case: one in poverty not joined to virtue is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல). The pairing shows how far 'everyone' reaches, with 1047 adding the condition அறஞ்சாரா.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:759","type":"agrees","note":"753 says wealth cuts away darkness (இருளறுக்கும்), which Parimelazhagar takes to be enmity. 759 says no steel is sharper than wealth for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). Both use the verb அறு for what wealth does, once as a lamp and once as a blade; on Parimelazhagar's reading of 753 they make the same claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:39","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that came by knowing the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 39 says only what comes through virtue is pleasure (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்). 754 fits wealth into 39's rule: wealth produces real pleasure only when it was itself acquired without wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"754 limits its promise to wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 659 says what was taken making others weep leaves with weeping, while what came by good ways pays later even if lost (பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பாலவை). Both make the manner of acquisition decide what wealth yields.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"755 says a gain of wealth that does not come with grace and love should be let go, not embraced (புல்லார் புரள விடல்). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day even if it brings only good (அன்றே யொழிய விடல்). Both end on the same command, விடல், for gain acquired the wrong way, one naming love and grace as the test and the other fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"755 tells one to refuse wealth that comes without grace and love. 1009 says what becomes of such wealth: bright wealth amassed by setting love aside and not looking to virtue (அன்பொரீஇ ... அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய) is taken by others. 1009 supplies a consequence for the wealth 755 rejects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:247","type":"agrees","note":"757 says the child grace, born of love, is reared by the nurse called wealth (பொருளென்னும் செல்வச் செவிலி). 247 sets the two side by side: that world is not for those without grace, as this world is not for those without wealth. Both pair அருள் with பொருள்; 247 keeps them parallel, while 757 makes grace depend on wealth to grow.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:755","type":"qualifies","note":"757 says grace is raised by wealth. 755, in the same chapter, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாரா) is to be let go. The dependence runs both ways: grace needs wealth, but only wealth that itself came with grace and love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"758 says doing a task with something in one's own hand (தன்கைத்தொன்று உண்டாக) is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop, which Parimelazhagar explains as acting without fear or strain. 449 says those without capital have no profit (முதலிலார்க்கு ஊதியம் இல்லை). Both make resources in hand the precondition of an undertaking; 758 adds the safety they give.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:593","type":"same_image","note":"758 praises acting with wealth in hand (கைத்து உண்டாக). 593 says those who hold steady energy as what is in their hand (ஊக்கம் ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடையார்) do not grieve that they have lost their gains. The same word கைத்து is used for money in 758 and for drive in 593, which treats energy as the holding that matters when the money is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:546","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 says wealth is sharper than any steel against foes. 546 says it is not the spear that gives victory but the king's sceptre, if it does not bend (வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது). In both the literal weapon is set below something else, wealth in one and just rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"759 says to make wealth, since no steel is sharper for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). 878 says the pride found in enemies dies (மாயும் பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு) when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Both aim at the enemy's செருக்கு; Parimelazhagar glosses 878's தற்செய்து as enlarging oneself, and 759 names wealth as the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 calls wealth the sharpest steel (எஃகு) against enemies' pride. 985 says bowing is the weapon (படை) by which the worthy turn their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Each names a non-military thing as the real weapon against foes: resources for the ruler, humility for the person of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"760 makes wealth the route to the other two aims, virtue and pleasure. 31 makes virtue the source of distinction and wealth (சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Each verse, speaking from within its own book, puts its own aim first as the one from which the others follow.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:754","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in full, the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easy to have together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 754 says the wealth that yields virtue and pleasure is wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 754 supplies the condition; Parimelazhagar reads it into 760's ஒண்பொருள் as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:1002","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says abundant wealth makes virtue and pleasure easy to get. 1002 calls it a delusion to hoard without giving on the thought that everything comes from wealth (பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும் மருள்), and says it leads to a low birth. 760's claim holds for wealth that is made and used; held back by a miser, the same belief is called delusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:381","type":"agrees","note":"761 says a conquering army, complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds, is the chief of all a king's wealth (வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 381 lists the six things a king must have and puts the army first (படைகுடி கூழ் ...). The placing in 381's list and the ranking in 761 agree.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:759","type":"same_image","note":"761 counts the army as a form of the king's wealth (வெறுக்கை). 759 counts wealth as a weapon, the sharpest steel against foes (எஃகு). The two neighbouring chapters describe each thing in terms of the other: wealth is a weapon and the army is wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:764","type":"develops","note":"762 says steadfastness in defeat is rare except in an old army (தொல்படை). 764 defines an army by courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்), along with not collapsing and not being turned by subversion. 764 makes a requirement of what 762 observes, and both use வன்கண் for the quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says the hardness that does not fear harm in a time of decline (தொலைவிடத்து) is found only in an old army (தொல்படை). 806 says those who keep to the bounds do not give up, even in decline (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who stood by them of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார்). Both use the same two words to tie loyalty in bad times to length of standing, in an army and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says only a long-established army stays firm when it is reduced and the king is faltering. 955 says an old family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its nature even when its means of giving have shrunk. In both, age of standing is what keeps character intact under loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:873","type":"qualifies","note":"763 says a crowd of weak enemies is nothing to one strong fighter. 873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்). 763 holds only where the one is a cobra and the many are rats; 873 is the warning for a lone person who assumes that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:764","b":"k:766","type":"develops","note":"764 says an army has courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்). 766 lists the four things that protect an army: valour, honour, walking the excellent path of those before (மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு) and தேற்றம். 766 spells out the inherited quality of 764 as following the path of earlier warriors, which is how Parimelazhagar reads வழிச்செலவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:768","b":"k:750","type":"tension","note":"768 says an army gains stature through its array (படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்) even if it lacks striking power and endurance; Parimelazhagar takes படைத்தகை as splendour of appearance. 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with people who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). They conflict if 768's பாடு is read as real military worth; if it means only repute, 768 is a concession and 750 still stands.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"769 names a resentment that will not leave (செல்லாத் துனி) among the things an army must be free of to win; Parimelazhagar says it arises from degrading treatment. 567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 567 gives a cause on the king's side for the condition 769 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"769 says an army wins if it is free of dwindling, of resentment that does not leave, and of poverty (வறுமை). 759 says to make wealth because it is the sharpest weapon against foes. Parimelazhagar links them from both sides: under 769 he says desertion and want arise when the king does not pay, and under 759 that making wealth brings a large army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:770","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"770 says an army does not exist without leaders. 1030 says a family with no good person to prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. Both make a group's survival depend on a capable person at its head, in the army and in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:763","type":"agrees","note":"771 dismisses the enemy by pointing to the many (பலர்) who faced his lord and became stones. 763 asks what it matters if a host of rats roars like the sea, since they perish when the cobra merely breathes (நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்). Both say numbers on the other side count for nothing against one real fighter; Parimelazhagar reads 763 as the hero not fearing a crowd of non-heroes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:894","type":"agrees","note":"771 has a warrior warn enemies not to stand before his lord (என்ஐமுன் நில்லன்மின்), since many who did now stand as memorial stones (கல்நின்றவர்). 894 states the rule behind the warning: for the unable to do harm to the able is like beckoning Death with the hand (கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்று). The battlefield taunt and the political maxim give the same counsel, one from the strong side and one addressed to the weak.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:1088","type":"same_image","note":"771 is a warrior's boast about a might that turns opponents into memorial stones. In 1088 a lover says his பீடு, which even enemies who had not met him feared in battle (ஞாட்பினுள் நண்ணாரும் உட்கும்), has broken before her bright forehead. The Book of Love borrows the same figure of the warrior whose name alone frightens foes, here as a speaker's way of measuring her effect on him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"772 prefers the failed throw at an elephant to the successful shot at a hare, and Manakkudavar takes it with 771 as saying the warrior should send the weak away and fight those who can oppose him. 861 advises the reverse: avoid meeting the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and keep up enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). The conflict holds if 772 is read as a rule for choosing opponents and 861 as Parimelazhagar reads it; it is the warrior's pride against the king's policy. Kalaignar's gloss of 861 turns it into fighting the strong, which would remove the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:987","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 makes help to a foe in trouble the cutting edge of valour; Parimelazhagar illustrates it with the king of Ayodhya telling his disarmed enemy to go and come back tomorrow. 987 asks what use worth (சால்பு) is if one does not do what is sweet even to those who did one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே). The ideal of the worthy person in the chapter on சால்பு appears here as the height of a soldier's manhood.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:686","type":"parallel_domain","note":"775 asks whether it is not a rout (ஒட்டு in this edition, which Parimelazhagar glosses as turning the back) for the fierce if the eye glaring at the enemy closes in a blink when a spear is thrown. 686 requires the envoy to be one who does not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the hostile king's angry stare; 585 says the same of the spy. Holding one's gaze under threat is a test in the court as on the field.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:1129","type":"same_image","note":"775 treats a single blink (இமைப்பின்) before a flung spear as equal to flight. In 1129 the woman says she will not blink because her lover, who lives in her eyes, would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). Both make the refusal to blink a measure of devotion, to valour in one and, in the speaker's conceit, to the beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"777 says that for those who want fame that circles the earth and do not want life (வேண்டா உயிரார்), tying on the war-anklet is an adornment. 235 says a ruin that is growth and a death that is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains by the body of fame outliving the body of flesh. 235 gives the reasoning for the warrior's choice in 777.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"777 describes warriors who prefer fame (இசை) to life. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both rank a value above staying alive; in one it is public renown won in battle, in the other an inner restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:779","b":"k:772","type":"develops","note":"779 asks who could fault for failure (பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற்பவர்) those who die so as not to break what they vowed (இழைத்தது இகவாமை); Parimelazhagar explains that dying before the vow is accomplished still counts as keeping it. 772 says the spear that missed (பிழைத்த வேல்) the elephant is better to hold than the arrow that hit a hare. Both verses turn on பிழை, a miss, and deny that it is a disgrace when the attempt was great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:781","b":"k:815","type":"qualifies","note":"781 praises friendship as the best protection. 815 says the poor friendship of small people, which gives no safety even after it is made (செய்து ஏமம் சாரா), is better not had than had. The protection claimed in 781 belongs only to friendship with the right people; made with the wrong ones it guards nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:782","b":"k:811","type":"develops","note":"782 contrasts friendships that grow and friendships that wane according to the kind of person. 811 says that with people without character, however ardent they look, it is better for the friendship to shrink than to grow (பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது). The waning that 782 observes in bad friendships is in 811 what one should want for them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:811","type":"qualifies","note":"783 says that with people of character (பண்புடையாளர்) the bond grows sweeter with practice. 811 says that with those who lack character (பண்பிலார்), friendship is better shrinking than growing, even if they seem to drink one up with affection. The reward of long practice in 783 depends on பண்பு; without it, more closeness is worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"784 gives rebuke as friendship's purpose. 795 turns it into a test for choosing friends: examine and take the friendship of those able to speak so that one weeps, to rebuke (இடித்து) and to teach what is proper. What 784 says a friend is for, 795 tells the reader to look for beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:792","type":"tension","note":"Manakkudavar reads 785 as saying many days of acquaintance are not needed to judge a friendship, because for those of the same understanding (உணர்ச்சி) one meeting gives the claim. 792 says the friendship of one who does not examine again and again (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான்) ends in grief that kills him. On Manakkudavar's reading the two differ on whether friendship can safely be formed quickly; on Parimelazhagar's, 785 only says that feeling, more than contact, is what makes the bond, and there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship in which only the face smiles is not friendship (முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று), and that friendship in which the heart within smiles is. 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா). The face and heart contrast that defines true friendship in 786 becomes in 824 the mark of a false friend to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:787","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"787 ends with the friend who suffers along in ruin (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பது). 799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when remembered at the hour of death. 799 shows the cost of the failure of the third duty that 787 lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:788","b":"k:1023","type":"same_image","note":"In 788 the hand goes at once to the slipping garment (உடுக்கை). In 1023, for one who says he will raise his family, தெய்வம் tucks up its garment (மடிதற்று) and goes ahead of him. Both use a gesture with one's clothing for help that comes unasked and promptly, from a friend in one and from the divine in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:449","type":"parallel_domain","note":"789 defines the height of friendship as a firm state of propping the other up (ஊன்றும் நிலை). 449 says those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு) have no standing (நிலை), as those without capital have no profit. 789 describes the supporter and 449 the one who needs supporting, in the chapter on a king's reliance on the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"789 places friendship's throne in supporting the friend without wavering wherever it is possible (ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை). 818 says to let slip without a word the friendship of those who make difficult a task they could do (ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்றுபவர்). The same word for what lies within one's power sets the standard in 789 and identifies the friend who fails it in 818.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:790","b":"k:978","type":"parallel_domain","note":"790 says friendship looks shabby (புல்லென்னும்) even when friends dress it up in words (புனையினும்) such as 'they are this to us and we are that to them'. 978 says greatness always bows while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In both, the verbal ornament is the sign of the lesser thing: real friendship, like real greatness, does not advertise itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791: inquire first, since after befriending there is no leaving (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 467: think and then resolve on a task, and to say 'we will think after resolving' (துணிந்தபின் எண்ணுவம்) is a lapse. The same order of thought before an irreversible step is applied to friendship and to action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"791 grounds the need for inquiry on the claim that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 800 tells one to leave the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார், even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக), and Parimelazhagar takes this as the remedy for a friendship taken up unknowingly. If வீடில்லை is read as a flat 'there is no release', the two conflict; if it describes only what a true friend will not do, 800 is the exception the chapter allows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:453","type":"develops","note":"793 includes a person's இனன், his circle, among the things to learn before befriending him (Parimelazhagar takes it as kin). 453 says that the word 'he is such a one' comes from a person's company (இனத்தானாம் இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்). 453 gives the reason the circle belongs on 793's list: it is where a person's character is read from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:794","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"794 picks out as a friend the well-born person who is ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவான்). 951 says that integrity and shame together are found by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை ... செப்பமும் நாணும்). 951 gives the book's reason for 794 pairing birth with shame as the test.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:795","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"795 says to choose as friends those who can rebuke (இடித்து) and bring one to tears over a wrong. 448 says a king without those who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத) perishes even with no one to ruin him. The rebuker counts as a safeguard both for the private person choosing friends and for the king choosing his support.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:450","type":"qualifies","note":"797 counts leaving the friendship of fools as gain (ஊதியம்). 450 says that letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Whether breaking a tie is profit or harm depends on who is at the other end of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"797 says leaving a fool's friendship is gain. 816 says that being a stranger to the wise (அறிவுடையார் ஏதின்மை) is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool (பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பு); Pope and Subramuniyaswami render ஏதின்மை as enmity. Both verses put a fool's friendship below having no tie at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:839","type":"same_image","note":"797 calls it gain to give up and leave the friendship of fools (பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). 839 uses the same phrase, பேதையார் கேண்மை, and says it is very sweet because there is no pain in parting from it. Read together, 839 is the ironic version of 797: the one good thing about such a friendship is the leaving of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:106","type":"qualifies","note":"798 says not to take the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார்). 106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு துறவற்க). The two prohibitions, கொள்ளற்க and துறவற்க, are complementary: how a friend behaves in trouble decides both whom not to take and whom not to drop.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"798 begins: do not think of what makes the spirit shrink (உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ). 596 gives the positive form: in all one thinks, think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்). Both treat the direction of one's thoughts as something to be chosen for the sake of the spirit; 798 then sets fair-weather friends beside the shrinking thoughts as things to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:814","type":"same_image","note":"798 uses ஆற்றறுப்பார் for friends who fail one in distress. 814 uses the same expression of the untrained horse that fails its rider in battle (அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா) and says solitude is better than such people as one's own. 814 supplies the picture for 798's word: support that holds until the moment it is needed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:293","type":"same_image","note":"799 says the thought of false friends burns the heart (உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). 293 says that after a lie one's own heart burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). In 293 the heart burns for one's own fault, in 799 for another's desertion, so the same inner fire is given two different causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:1207","type":"same_image","note":"799 ends உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்: even to think of the deserter's friendship burns the heart. 1207 ends with the same words in the mouth of the parted woman, for whom even remembering her lover burns. The phrase is used once for betrayal and once for separation in love; in both the pain comes from memory of a bond, not from a present injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"794 says the friendship of the well-born man who fears blame is worth getting even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 800 says the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார் is worth leaving even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). The chapter uses the figure of payment in both directions, for acquiring a good friend and for getting free of a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:818","type":"develops","note":"800 says to leave the friendship of the unfit even at a cost. 818 says how to leave one kind of bad friend, those who make hard work of what they could do: without speaking of it, let the friendship slip (சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்). 818 adds a manner of leaving to 800's instruction to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:981","type":"develops","note":"802 calls it the duty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கடன்) to take a friend's liberties well. 981 says that for those who take on worthiness knowing what is due, all good things are கடன். 802 is one instance of 981's general statement, with the same word கடன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:803","b":"k:790","type":"develops","note":"803 asks what use long friendship is if one does not accept a friend's liberty as though one had done it oneself (செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை). 790 says friendship looks shabby even when friends describe each other as 'they are this to us, we are that to them'. Both deny a separation of 'mine' and 'theirs' between friends: 790 in how they speak of each other, 803 in whose act an act is counted to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:804","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"804 says that when friends act without asking, out of intimacy (கெழுதகையாற் கேளாது), it is welcomed. 788 compares friendship to the hand of a man whose garment slips, removing the distress there and then (ஆங்கே). The hand in 788 does not ask leave before acting; 804 states the same thing from the receiving side, that such unasked action is to be wanted and not resented.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:109","type":"parallel_domain","note":"805 says a hurtful act by a friend is to be understood as folly or as great intimacy (பெருங்கிழமை). 109 says that even an injury like killing is cancelled when one thinks of a single good the person did. Both teach a way of reading an injury so that the bond survives it; 109 does it by remembering a past benefit, 805 by a charitable account of the cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"187 says that those who cannot make friends by pleasant speech will divide even their own people by divisive talk (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 808 describes the friend on whom that does not work, who does not listen to reports of a friend's lapse. 187 gives the talebearer's side and 808 the hearer's refusal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"808 speaks of those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளா); Parimelazhagar takes this as not accepting it even when others report it. 510 says that doubting one who has been trusted after examination (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. Both rule out reopening a settled trust, in friendship and in a king's appointments. The second half of 808 is read in opposite ways by Parimelazhagar (the day of the lapse is a day well spent) and Kalaignar (the days are wasted), so the link rests only on the first half.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:809","b":"k:791","type":"develops","note":"791 says that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (வீடில்லை). 809 says the world wants (விழையும் உலகு) those who do not leave (விடாஅர்) the friendship of friends of unbroken long standing. What 791 gives as a reason for caution before befriending, 809 presents as what earns a person general regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:811","b":"k:1266","type":"same_image","note":"In 1266 the waiting wife says that when her husband returns she will drink him in (பருகுவன்) so that her pain ends. 811 uses the same verb for friends who look as though they would drink one up (பருகுவார் போலினும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as from excess of love. The pair shows 'drinking' as the book's figure for consuming affection, sincere in the wife's mouth and only an appearance in the bad friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:799","type":"qualifies","note":"812 treats the friendship of those who leave when the gain ends (அறின் ஒரூஉம்) as a matter of indifference, gained or lost. 799 says that the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when recalled at the hour of death (உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). Read together, losing such a friend is no loss, yet having trusted one is not painless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:800","type":"develops","note":"Both verses name the ஒப்பிலார். 812 says it makes no difference whether their friendship is had or lost; 800 goes further and says to leave it even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). Parimelazhagar explains the word differently in each place: here as those who fit with no one because they look only to their own gain, there as those who do not accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:839","type":"agrees","note":"839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting from them brings no pain (பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்றில்). 812 asks what it matters whether one gains or loses (பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்) the friendship of those who befriend when there is profit and leave when there is none. Both measure a worthless friendship by the same test: its loss costs nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:912","type":"develops","note":"813 sets friends who weigh what will come to them (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) beside 'those who take what they get' (பெறுவது கொள்வார்), whom Parimelazhagar identifies as courtesans, and thieves. 912 describes those women directly: they weigh the profit and then speak of character (பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும்). The later chapter spells out the comparison 813 makes in a phrase, with the same verb தூக்கு for the calculating look.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:814","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"788 defines friendship by the moment of need: like the hand of a man whose garment slips, it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது). 814 pictures the reverse, a horse that gives way in the battle itself (அமரகத்து). Both locate the test of a friend at the instant of crisis, one by the hand that acts unasked and one by the mount that fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:815","b":"k:106","type":"develops","note":"106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு). 815 says the friendship of the small, which gives no safety (ஏமம்), is better not obtained at all. The same measure, support in trouble, decides in one verse which friends must be kept and in the other which should never be acquired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:819","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin burns the heart even to think of at the time of death (அடுங்காலை உள்ளினும்). 819 says the bond with those whose words and deeds differ is painful even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). Each verse extends the harm of a false friend beyond ordinary waking life, to the deathbed in one and to sleep in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:820","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what people whose habit is to broadcast even the faults of those close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers. 820 warns against the bond with those who are friendly in private and blame one in public. Both take the public exposure of an intimate as the mark of a person no one should be near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:487","type":"parallel_domain","note":"487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but watch for the time and burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). 821 warns that those who keep close without inner consent will strike when they see the right place. The patience the book recommends to a ruler in 487 is the same conduct it tells him to expect from a false friend in 821.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:883","type":"agrees","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within, because at the moment of weakness (உலைவிடத்து) it cuts like the tool that cuts the potter's clay (மட்பகை). 821 says the friendship of those who join without inner accord (நேரா நிரந்தவர்) is an anvil (பட்டடை) for striking once they find the right opening (சீரிடம் காணின்). Both verses use a craftsman's tool for the hidden enemy and both place the blow at the moment that suits him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"882 says not to fear enemies who are like swords but to fear the bond with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 822 says the friendship of those who seem one's own and are not (இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார்) will turn. Both identify the enemy in the guise of kin as the dangerous one; 882 gives the instruction and 822 the reason, that such a bond does not stay what it looks like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:920","type":"same_image","note":"920 lists two-minded women (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்) with liquor and dice as the company of those fortune has left. 822 compares false friendship to women's minds that become other (வேறுபடும்). The divided or shifting female mind serves in both as the book's figure for a tie that cannot be relied on, and 920 suggests which women the figure has in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:974","type":"tension","note":"822 uses women's minds as its example of something that changes (மகளிர் மனம்போல வேறுபடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this of women in general and supports it with a line from the Valaiyapathi. 974 takes single-minded women (ஒருமை மகளிர்) as its model of the constancy that greatness needs. On Parimelazhagar's general reading of 822 the two figures conflict; if 822's மகளிர் is limited to the courtesans of chapter 92, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining them and, having examined, to trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 825 says that those who are not at one with you in mind are not to be judged trustworthy by their words in any matter (சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று). 825 adds to the rule of examination a statement of what cannot count as evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:890","type":"agrees","note":"890 says that life with those who are not in accord (உடம்பாடு இலாதவர்) is like living with a snake in a hut. 825 says those whose minds are not settled with yours (மனத்தின் அமையாதவர்) are not to be trusted on their words. Both make inner agreement, not outward closeness or speech, the thing that decides whether a person near you is safe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:709","type":"agrees","note":"709 says the eye itself tells of enmity and friendship (பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know how to read it. 826 says an enemy's words are soon recognised even when they sound like a friend's. Both hold that hostility shows through the surface; 709 makes this depend on a skilled reader, a condition 826 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:828","type":"qualifies","note":"826 says the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through although they say good things as friends do. 828, in the same chapter, says a weapon can lie hidden in hands joined in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்) and in an enemy's tears. 828 limits the confidence of 826: the speech may give itself away, but gestures of submission and grief can still conceal the intent.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 advises that on meeting an enemy one should bear him, which Parimelazhagar glosses as bowing, until his end is in sight (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை). 827 says not to accept an enemy's bowing speech (சொல்வணக்கம்), because the bending of a bow signals harm. The humility 488 teaches as a tactic is the humility 827 teaches one to distrust in the other side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:985","type":"qualifies","note":"985 says that bowing (பணிதல்) is the strength of those who accomplish and the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 827 says that an enemy's verbal bowing should not be accepted, since it points to harm as a bent bow does. Bowing is a means of working on an adversary in both; 985 praises it in the worthy, and 827 says that from an enemy it must not be taken as goodwill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 says to bear an enemy when you meet him until his final limit comes, and then his head goes down. 829 says to keep the false friend pleased and hold him in the appearance of friendship while it dies inwardly. Both advise outward accommodation of an enemy who cannot yet be opposed openly, in the chapter on timing and in the chapter on false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while bitter within. 829 says that those who make much of you outwardly and despise you (மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளுவார்) should be made to smile and be embraced within the friendship so that it dies (நகச்செய்து நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லல்). The response 829 prescribes has the same form as the conduct 824 warns against; Parimelazhagar notes that it is allowed as royal policy although such doubleness is otherwise unfitting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:830","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 advises bearing with enemies when one meets them until their end is in view. 830 advises a friendly face toward enemies at a time when, as Parimelazhagar explains, they cannot be openly put aside. Both treat outward civility toward an enemy as a matter of timing and not of feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:422","type":"agrees","note":"831 defines folly as taking up what harms and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 422 defines understanding as the reverse movement: turning away from evil and steering toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). Read together, the two definitions are mirror images, so folly in 831 is not a lack of information but the wrong choice between two things in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:797","type":"same_image","note":"831 says folly is to let gain go (ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 797 uses the same two words to say what gain is: giving up and leaving the friendship of fools (ஊதியம் என்பது ... பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). The fool lets gain go, and for everyone else letting the fool go is the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:925","type":"parallel_domain","note":"831 says folly is accepting harm and letting gain go. 925 gives a concrete case in drinking: handing over wealth to receive ignorance of one's own body (பொருள்கொடுத்து மெய்யறியாமை கொளல்), which it traces to கையறியாமை, not knowing what to do. The drinker's purchase is 831's exchange carried out literally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:832","b":"k:439","type":"agrees","note":"832 names as the worst folly a liking (காதன்மை) for what is not fit for one. 439 gives the matching instruction: do not set your desire on a deed that yields no good (நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை). Both locate the error in the wanting, before any deed is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"833 lists four absences as the fool's work: no shame, no inquiry, no tenderness, no care for anything (நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும் பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்). 428 defines folly as not fearing what should be feared and calls fearing it the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). Both define folly as a missing response where one is due, and both set it out as a characteristic தொழில்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:865","type":"parallel_domain","note":"833 describes the fool by four negations. 865 describes the ideal enemy to have by a similar string: he does not look to the way, does not do what is fitting, does not look to blame, has no character (வழிநோக்கான் ... பழிநோக்கான் பண்பிலன்), and so is sweet to his foes. The same profile that 833 treats as a moral portrait is, in the chapter on enmity, a strategic weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"833 puts shamelessness (நாணாமை) first among the fool's traits. 1018 says what that trait costs: if one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, the fault is one that virtue itself would be ashamed of (அறம் நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads it as virtue leaving him. 1018 supplies the consequence that 833 leaves unstated.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:130","type":"agrees","note":"130 praises the one who guards against anger and, having learned, is restrained (கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான்): virtue itself seeks him out. 834 names the one who has read, understood and taught but is not restrained (ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் ... தானடங்கா) the greatest of fools. Both treat learning and அடங்கல் as a pair in which the learning is judged by the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:373","type":"develops","note":"834 presents a puzzle: a person who has studied, understood and taught, and still does not restrain himself. 373 offers the chapter on fate's account of such a person: however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்), which Parimelazhagar takes as the folly given by fate. 373 explains how the fool of 834 is possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:919","type":"same_image","note":"835 calls the fool's destination an அளறு, a mire or hell, into which he enters and sinks (புக்கழுந்தும்). 919 calls the shoulders of women who draw no line the அளறு in which base men sink (பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு). 919 uses for one present indulgence the image that 835 uses for the result of a whole life of folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:468","type":"agrees","note":"836 says that when a fool who does not know the way of doing (கையறியாப் பேதை) takes up a task, the task fails (பொய்படும்) and he himself ends in fetters. 468 says effort not made by the right means will collapse (பொத்துப் படும்) even with many standing by to protect it. Parimelazhagar glosses both verbs as புரைபடும்; 468 states the rule about method and 836 adds the cost to the doer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:925","type":"same_image","note":"836 describes the fool as கையறியா, not knowing how to act. 925 uses the same compound, கையறியாமை, as the cause of paying money to lose awareness of oneself in drink. In both verses the word names a practical ignorance that ends in the person binding or losing himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"837 speaks of great wealth that has come to a fool (பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக்கடை) and benefits the wrong people. 1000 says great wealth obtained by one without character (பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Parimelazhagar notes on both verses that the verb marks wealth received and not made; both hold that the holder's defect ruins what the wealth could do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"837 says a fool's great wealth feeds strangers (ஏதிலார்) while his own go hungry. 1009 says wealth amassed by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The miser and the fool arrive at the same result by opposite routes, one by holding wealth back and one by letting it go to the wrong people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:838","b":"k:408","type":"agrees","note":"838 likens a fool's possession to drunkenness added to derangement. 408 says fortune lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). Both deny that wealth is a good independent of its holder; 838 gives the image and 408 the comparative judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"840 treats the fool's mere entry (புகல்) into the company of the worthy as a defilement; Parimelazhagar adds that the assembly itself is lowered by it. 403 allows that even the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 403 leaves a condition under which presence is acceptable, though its subject is the unlearned and 840's is the fool.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"840 likens a fool entering an assembly of the worthy to setting an unwashed foot on a bed (கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்று). 720 likens speaking before those not of one's own kind to nectar spilled in an unclean yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). The two are mirror images of the same mismatch: the unclean thing brought into the clean place, and the precious thing let fall in the unclean place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:70","type":"parallel_domain","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing but the receiver's தவம். 70 says a son's service to his father is to make people ask what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான் கொல்) to have him. Both use the same figure, explaining a good by the stored merit of the one who benefits; 70 uses it as praise of the son and 842 to deny the giver any credit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:92","type":"qualifies","note":"92 treats giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்) as a real good, surpassed only by a glad face and sweet speech. 842 takes the same act, giving with a glad heart (நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல்), and says that in a person without understanding it reflects only the receiver's merit. 842 limits 92's valuation: the glad gift counts for the giver only when understanding is behind it, which is how Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"843 says the suffering those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies to inflict (செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது). 165 says that for the envious, envy is enough: it brings ruin even when enemies fail to (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). Both measure an inner defect against an outside enemy and find the defect the more effective.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"843 says the fool harms himself beyond what an enemy could. 434 says one's own fault is the enemy that brings ruin (குற்றமே அற்றந் தரூஉம் பகை) and is therefore the thing to guard against. 434 gives the general principle of which 843 is the case of the person who cannot do the guarding.","grade":"sound","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:847","type":"develops","note":"843 states generally that those without understanding torment themselves (தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை). 847, in the same chapter, names one way it happens: the person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip and so does great harm to himself by himself (பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு). 847 is a specific instance of 843's claim, with the same stress on தானே.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:404","type":"develops","note":"844 says the claim to ஒண்மை is itself what வெண்மை is. 404 says that however good an unlearned person's ஒட்பம் may be, those with understanding do not accept it (கொள்ளார் அறிவுடையார்). 404 shows the other side of 844's self-assessment: the brightness one credits to oneself is not credited by those able to judge.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"844 locates want of understanding in the pride (செருக்கு) of saying 'we are bright'. 978 says smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து) while greatness bows. Both make self-admiration the identifying mark of the deficient state, one for understanding and one for stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:128","type":"parallel_domain","note":"845 says one false claim to learning casts doubt on what one has mastered without flaw. 128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good comes to nothing (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). Both describe a single lapse spreading backward over what was sound, in knowledge and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:391","type":"same_image","note":"391 tells one to learn what is to be learned without flaw (கற்க கசடற) and then stand by it. 845 uses the same phrase for what a person has truly mastered (கசடற வல்லதூஉம்) and says that carrying on as if one knew what one has not learned (கல்லாத மேற்கொண்டொழுகல்) brings doubt even on that. Pretence undoes the standing of the very learning 391 asks for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:405","type":"develops","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing gives way once he meets others and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்). 845 describes a further loss for one who has some real learning: by taking on what he has not learned, he puts even his genuine mastery in doubt (ஐயம் தரும்). 405 is the exposure of a claim; 845 is the damage the exposure does to what was true.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:278","type":"parallel_domain","note":"846 asks whether covering one's nakedness is not itself small-minded when one's faults are left uncovered (தம்வயின் குற்றம் மறையா வழி); Parimelazhagar takes covering faults to mean removing them. 278 speaks of many who, with stain in the mind, bathe and go about concealed (மனத்தது மாசாக ... நீராடி மறைந்தொழுகு). Both set a bodily propriety, clothing or bathing, against an inner fault it does nothing about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:1012","type":"develops","note":"846 treats clothing the body as trivial beside dealing with one's faults. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). 1012 gives the reason behind 846: dress is common to all, and what marks a person is shame at fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 847 as a person who receives rare instruction and lets it run out without taking it in, harming himself. 418 says ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear (கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே). On that reading both describe teaching that reaches a person and does not enter; 847 adds that the loss is self-inflicted.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:1076","type":"parallel_domain","note":"847 says a person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip (அருமறை சோரும்) and so harms himself. Manakkudavar takes மறை as a secret let out to others, a reading Parimelazhagar reports and rejects. On that reading the verse meets 1076, where the base are like a beaten drum because they carry every மறை they hear to others; 1076 notes the harm to others, 847 the harm to the leaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"448 says a king without people who rebuke him will perish even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பாரிலானுங் கெடும்). 848 takes the case one step further: a person who has people to direct him and still does not act on it, and cannot work it out alone. 448 makes having correctors the safeguard; 848 shows the safeguard is useless to one who will not be corrected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"848 calls the life of one who neither heeds nor understands a disease until it leaves (போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்); Parimelazhagar takes this as a burden hard for the earth to bear, while Kalaignar takes the disease as the person's own. 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a weight on the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two share the figure of a person whose mere being alive is an affliction to the world.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:638","type":"parallel_domain","note":"848 describes one who does not act when told and does not see for himself (ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான் தேறான்), and calls that life a disease until it departs. 638 describes a king in the same two terms, one who destroys what the knowing say and does not know himself (அறிகொன்று அறியான்), and says the minister must still tell him what is sound. The same double failing appears in both; 848 gives the verdict on the person and 638 the duty of those beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"849 ends with the unseeing person continuing to see as he saw (தான்கண்டவாறு). 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). Both hold that what is brought from outside, a teacher in 849 and books in 373, does not displace the understanding a person already has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:929","type":"parallel_domain","note":"849 says one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up the unseeing one, while that person goes on having seen it his own way (கண்டானாம் தான்கண்டவாறு). 929 says giving reasons to a drunk man is like searching with a flame for someone sunk under water (கீழ்நீர்க் குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று). Both describe instruction that cannot reach its target, and in both the effort reflects on the one making it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"850 condemns the one who denies what the world affirms. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) lack understanding however much they have studied. Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' in both as the high-minded many, so both make their consensus a test that private cleverness does not override.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:187","type":"same_image","note":"851 names the fault as பகல், splitting apart, and its chapter closes (860) by setting நகல் against இகல். 187 uses the same pair of roots: those who do not know how to make friends by நகச்சொல்லி will பகச்சொல்லி and separate even their kin. The chapter on backbiting already holds the contrast between dividing and laughing together that the chapter on hostility is built on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:886","type":"develops","note":"851 defines இகல் as the disease (நோய்) that spreads பகல், the ill nature of division, among all living beings. 886 takes the same division to its end inside one's own circle: if ஒன்றாமை arises among ஒன்றியார், escaping death (பொன்றாமை) is hard at any time. The general diagnosis of 851 becomes a specific warning about a ruler's own people; Parimelazhagar reads 886 of a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"852 ranks not harming a provoker as தலை, the best course. 203 uses the same ranking word: the head of all understanding (அறிவினுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is not to do evil even to those who hate you (செறுவார்க்கும்). Together they show the text treating non-retaliation as a matter of good sense as well as of virtue; Parimelazhagar on 852 explains that retaliating feeds the enmity while refraining lets it die out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:853","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"853 says that removing the painful disease of hostility gives a brightness that neither fails nor is flawed (தவலில்லாத் தாவில் விளக்கம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as lasting fame. 156 says those who punish have one day's pleasure while those who forbear have praise until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்). Both make enduring renown the result of declining the quarrel, one in the householder's ethics and one in public life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:854","b":"k:629","type":"qualifies","note":"854 offers இன்பத்துள் இன்பம், the joy of joys, as what follows when hostility goes. 629 uses the same two phrases differently: one who does not crave pleasure within pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not feel pain within pain (துன்பத்துள் துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). Read together, the joy 854 promises is a result of removing a cause of suffering, and 629 warns against making such joy an object of desire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"855 says no one sets out to defeat a person who can lean away from hostility. 874 goes a step further: the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்). 855 describes safety gained by not answering hostility; 874 describes actively turning the enemy into a friend and gives it a public value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"855 asks who would be inclined to overcome those able to lean away when hostility confronts them (இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை). 985 says the strength of the capable is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both treat yielding as what disarms an opponent; 985 states it as a trait of the சான்றோர், 855 as a skill (வல்லார்), which Parimelazhagar says is hard for kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"855 holds that the one who gives way before hostility cannot be beaten. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' sulk the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. The speaker in 1327 is a lover talking about play-quarrels, so this is the same pattern of winning by yielding in a different setting, not a doctrine shared with 855.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:856","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"856 says the life of one who declares it sweet to excel in hostility (இகலின் மிகல் இனிது என்பவன்) is close to failure and ruin. 307 says the ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth (சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன்) is as sure as the hand of one who strikes the ground. In both the fault is the valuing of the passion, and both attach certain or imminent ruin to it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:857","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"423 defines understanding as seeing the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு) in whatever one hears from anyone. 857 says those of harmful understanding who are attached to hostility (இகல்மேவல் இன்னா அறிவினவர்) do not see the மெய்ப்பொருள் that carries victory. So 857 names hostility as one thing that turns அறிவு against its defining task; Parimelazhagar narrows மெய்ப்பொருள் here to the content of the treatises on statecraft and says hostility clouds the understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"860 says all ills come from hostility, and from friendly gladness (நகல்) comes 'the pride called good conduct' (நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 180 has the same shape and the same closing phrase: coveting without thought yields destruction, and 'the pride called not-wanting' (வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு) yields victory. In both the verse sets a ruinous impulse against its absence and calls that absence a செருக்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses in both places as wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:303","type":"agrees","note":"860 says இகலான் ஆம் இன்னாத எல்லாம்: every painful thing arises from hostility. 303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because the birth of evils comes from it (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). The Book of Virtue makes anger the source of evils and the Book of Wealth makes hostility the source, with nearly the same construction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"860 credits நகல், glad friendliness, with producing good conduct and its dignity. 999 describes those without the capacity: for people not able to laugh with others (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the wide world lies in darkness even by day. 860 gives what நகல் yields and 999 what its absence costs; Parimelazhagar reads நகல் in both as rejoicing in company with others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"862 asks how a man with no love, no worthy allies (ஆன்ற துணையிலன்) and no strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. 449 states the principle: those without capital have no profit, and those without the prop of supporters (மதலையாஞ் சார்பிலார்) have no standing. 862 applies to a contest with an enemy what 449 says of a ruler's position in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"862 describes the man without love, allies or strength as unable to resist an enemy. 873 judges the man in that position who nevertheless provokes: one who, alone (தமியனாய்), takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad. 862 gives the weak position seen by an attacker; 873 gives the advice that follows for the one who is in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:534","type":"agrees","note":"863 puts fear first among the traits that make a man easy for his enemy. 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are of no use to one with fear in his mind. Both say that fear cancels whatever other protection a person has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:869","type":"develops","note":"863 lists fear and ignorance among the weaknesses that make a man easy prey (அஞ்சும் அறியான்). 869 keeps just those two and states them from the other side: joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are without understanding and afraid (அறிவிலா அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்). The chapter moves from describing the weak enemy to stating what the attacker gains from him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"864 says one who does not leave off anger (நீங்கான் வெகுளி) and has no நிறை is easy for anyone, at any time, anywhere. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, because unguarded anger kills oneself (தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்). 305 states the self-destruction as an ethical fact; 864 shows how it works in a contest, by making the angry man open to any opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"864 says the man who cannot leave anger is easy for anyone to overcome. 526 gives the opposite case: no one on earth has more people at his side than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). Put together, anger decides whether a man stands with many around him or exposed to all.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:866","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"866 says the enmity of a man of blind anger and excessive lust (காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is to be welcomed. 360 names the same two, with delusion, as what must perish for suffering to perish (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம்). What the chapter on true knowledge treats as obstacles to release, the chapter on enemies treats as what makes an opponent beatable; Parimelazhagar says such men destroy themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:800","type":"parallel_domain","note":"867 advises paying to have a certain man as an enemy rather than keep him near. 800 advises paying to be rid of a certain friend: give something (ஒன்று ஈத்தும்) and leave the friendship of those who are not a match. Both accept a cost to end a harmful closeness; the commentators differ on who the man in 867 is, Parimelazhagar taking him as one who begins a task and acts unsuitably, Kalaignar as one who stays beside you.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the ruler to examine a man's qualities, then his faults, and judge by what predominates (மிகை நாடி மிக்க கொளல்). 868 describes the result when the tally is all on one side: with no qualities and many faults (குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின்) he is left without companions, and that is a support to his opponents. The test used for choosing servants in 504 is applied by 868 to the ruler himself, with his enemies as the ones who benefit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"868 says a man without qualities and with many faults comes to have no one with him (இனனிலனாம்). 525 gives the positive case: one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in rows (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). Both make a person's following a product of his character; 868 adds the military consequence of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:869","b":"k:772","type":"tension","note":"869 says joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are ignorant and afraid. 772 says holding the spear that missed an elephant is sweeter (இனிது) than holding the arrow that hit a forest hare. One locates the pleasure of fighting in the easy opponent and the other in the hard one; the conflict holds if both are read as claims about what a fighter should want, and eases if 869 is a king's reckoning of advantage and 772 an individual warrior's sense of honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:871","b":"k:867","type":"tension","note":"871 says enmity, a thing without good in it (பண்பிலதனை), is not to be wanted even in jest (நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று). 867 says the enmity of one who stays close and does unfitting things must certainly be got, even by paying for it (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற). The conflict holds if 871 is read as a rule about all enmity; Parimelazhagar narrows it to enmity that brings no benefit, which would leave room for 867.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:872","b":"k:129","type":"parallel_domain","note":"872 says that even if you take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow (வில்லேர் உழவர்), you should not take on that of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). 129 says a wound burned by fire heals within, but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not. Both rank harm done by words above harm done by a physical instrument, one in statecraft and one in personal conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:873","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"873 states the folly of standing alone (தமியனாய்) against many enemies. 875 gives the working rule for the smallest such case: with no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்), make one of them a pleasing ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க). 875 turns 873's condemnation into a remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:985","type":"develops","note":"874 praises the one who takes enmity as friendship (பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்) without saying how it is done. 985 names a means: bowing (பணிதல்) is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Parimelazhagar glosses this as ending the opponents' enmity, so 985 reads as the method behind 874.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பண்புடையாளன் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on those of character (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்), and would otherwise sink into the earth. Both make பண்பு the thing the world stands on; 874, in the chapter on enmity, specifies it as the capacity to turn enemies into friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:875","b":"k:633","type":"parallel_domain","note":"875 advises a ruler caught alone between two enemies to make one of them an ally. 633 lists among a minister's abilities dividing (பிரித்தல்), which Parimelazhagar explains as separating the enemy's supporters from him, and rejoining those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). The advice to the king in 875 is the skill required of the minister in 633.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:876","b":"k:691","type":"parallel_domain","note":"876 says that whether or not one has trusted the enemy before, in a time of ruin (அழிவின்கண்) one should neither join him nor break from him (தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்); Parimelazhagar explains that joining lets him destroy from inside and breaking makes him add to the ruin. 691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor coming close (அகலாது அணுகாது). Both prescribe a deliberate middle distance from a dangerous party, once toward an enemy and once toward one's own king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"877 says not to let softness show before enemies (மேவற்க மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து); Parimelazhagar takes மென்மை as lack of strength, which enemies watch for. 440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without that love being known (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' designs come to nothing (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்). In both, what an enemy can use is what he is allowed to learn about one's inner state, weakness in one verse and desire in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:550","type":"same_image","note":"879 speaks of those who remove the thorn tree with the word களையுநர், weeders. 550 compares a king's putting the cruel to death to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). Both picture the removal of dangerous people as a cultivator's weeding; 879 adds that timing matters, since the weed grown hard injures the weeder's hand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"880 states the cost of not breaking the pride of one's enemies (செயிர்ப்பவர் செம்மல் சிதைக்கலாதார்): one cannot be counted as living. 759 names the instrument for that task: wealth, the sharpest steel for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும் எஃகு). One verse gives the necessity and the other the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:522","type":"qualifies","note":"881 says the nature of one's own people is bad if it does harm (தமர்நீரும் இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்). 522 says kin whose affection does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம்) yield many gains that keep branching; Parimelazhagar notes that this wording is chosen to exclude inner enmity. The condition written into 522 is the case 881 describes, so each verse marks the limit of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:1309","type":"same_image","note":"881 says even shade and water are bad when they do harm (நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா), and so is the nature of one's own people when it does harm. In 1309 the man says water is sweet in the shade (நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே), and sulking is sweet only with those who love. Both take shade and water as the standard of what is pleasant and then make the pleasantness depend on a condition, which in both cases is the real disposition of those closest to one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"882 uses the verb of fear for kin-like enemies (அஞ்சுக கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 824 uses it for deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப்படும்). The chapter on false friendship and the chapter on inner enmity reach the same instruction: what is to be feared is hostility presented as closeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"882 contrasts the enemy who is like a drawn sword (வாள்போல் பகைவர்) with the one who looks like kin, and fears the second. 828 says a weapon hides even within an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. In 882 the visible sword is the safer one; 828 shows where the other sword is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:883","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within and guard oneself (உட்பகை அஞ்சித் தற்காக்க), or it will destroy one. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger (தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க); unguarded, anger kills oneself. The same formula of self-guarding against something lodged inside is applied in one verse to people within the household or court and in the other to a passion within the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:885","b":"k:735","type":"parallel_domain","note":"885 says an inner enmity that comes in the form of kinship (உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை) brings many ills of a deadly kind (இறல்முறையான் ஏதம்) to the ruler. 735 defines a land as one without many factions, without ruinous inner enmity (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை) and without murderous marauders who harass the king. The danger 885 describes for the king's person and household is, in 735, something whose absence is part of what makes a country a country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:886","b":"k:764","type":"agrees","note":"886 says that if disunity arises among those who are one with a ruler (ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின்), it is always hard for him to escape perishing. 764 defines an army as one that is not destroyed, cannot be undermined from within (அறைபோகாதாகி) and has inherited hardness. Both treat the loyalty of one's own side as a condition of survival, stated once as a warning and once as a requirement.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 uses the casket and its lid for a family that fits together outwardly while divided within. 277 uses the kunri seed for false ascetics, red on the outside (புறங்குன்றி கண்டனையர்) and black within like its tip. Each verse chooses an everyday object to show an outside that does not match the inside, one for kin and one for renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 says a family with inner enmity is not joined, though it joins like a casket and its lid (செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship is what makes the heart within smile (நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). Both deny the name of union to a joining that is only on the surface, in the family and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"889 holds that inner enmity is ruinous even at the size of a split sesame seed. 433 says those who are ashamed of blame take a fault the size of a millet seed (தினைத்துணை) to be as large as a palmyra. 433 describes the attitude that 889 calls for: treating the small thing at its eventual size.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"889 says inner enmity carries ruin even when it is as small as a split sesame seed. 879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young (இளைதாக), since grown hard it destroys the cutter's hand. The two neighbouring chapters, on open and on inner enmity, make the same point that hostility should be judged by what it will grow into; only 879 goes on to say what to do about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"889 says that inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. 965 says even those like a hill will be lowered if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). Both use a tiny seed as the measure of something that brings down what is large, a family or kingdom in 889 and a person's standing in 965.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"891 makes not slighting the able the chief safeguard; 861 tells the ruler to avoid standing against the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). The chapter on enmity gives as a tactical rule what 891 gives as a rule of self-protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:450","type":"agrees","note":"892 says disregard of the great brings unending trouble (பேரா இடும்பை); 450 says letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Both chapters on the great rank a bad relation to them above ordinary dangers, 450 by a number and 892 by permanence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:976","type":"develops","note":"892 warns that going on without cherishing the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகின்) brings, through them, ills that do not leave. 976 says the intent to cherish and win the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணிக் கொள்வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு) is simply absent from the minds of the small. The shared verb பேணு ties them: 976 names who it is that fails to cherish, 892 what the failure costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"893 states as irony what 861 states as a plain command: offending those with the power to destroy (அடல்வேண்டின் ஆற்றுபவர்) is a way to seek one's own ruin, and 861 says to avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:922","type":"parallel_domain","note":"893 says: if you want ruin (கெடல்வேண்டின்), go ahead without listening and wrong those able to destroy. 922 says: do not drink, but if anyone will, let it be those who do not want the regard of the worthy (எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). Both forbid by an ironic permission addressed to whoever wants the bad result, once about offending the powerful and once about liquor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"894 says the unable doing harm to the able (ஆற்றுவார்க்கு ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்) is like beckoning Death with the hand. 250 asks one about to go against someone weaker (மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து) to think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன்). Both reason from the weak facing the strong: 894 uses it as prudence in the Book of Wealth, 250 turns the same experience into a ground for compassion in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"895 says those who draw the anger of a king of fierce might (வெந்துப்பின் வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) cannot go on existing anywhere they go. 691 gives the working rule for those who serve such kings: like people warming at a fire, neither move off nor come close (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). 895 states the danger, and 691 the conduct that lives beside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:899","type":"develops","note":"895 makes the king's anger inescapable for those under him; 899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), the king himself (வேந்தனும்) breaks midway and loses his kingship. Within the chapter the pair sets up an order of power: the king who destroys others can himself be destroyed by the anger of the great. Parimelazhagar takes வேந்தன் in 899 as Indra.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:207","type":"tension","note":"207 says those who incur any enmity whatever survive it (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும் உய்வர்), and only the enmity of one's own evil deeds follows and kills. 896 says those who keep offending the great do not survive (உய்யார்). If எனைப்பகை is taken literally as every human enmity, the two conflict; the conflict eases if 207's phrase is read as a rhetorical foil for the enmity of deeds, or if the harm in 896 is understood as itself the fruit of a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:444","type":"qualifies","note":"444 says living so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the head of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 897 gives the other side: a fully appointed life and great wealth count for nothing (என்னாம்) once the worthy great are angered. Together they make one's standing with the great, and not one's possessions, the deciding strength.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"897 asks what a life excellent in every part and lofty wealth (வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும்) come to if the worthy of great stature are angered (தக்கார் செறின்). 900 says even those with support beyond measure (இறந்தமைந்த சார்பு) do not survive if those of the highest distinction are angered (சீரார் செறின்). The two close on the same word செறின் and between them cancel a person's own assets and then his outside supports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"898 plays on குன்று (hill) and குன்ற (to diminish): if the hill-like intend diminishment, the seemingly permanent vanish. 965 uses the same play: even the hill-like (குன்றின் அனையாரும்) will diminish (குன்றுவர்) if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri seed. In 898 the hill-like bring others low; in 965 they themselves can be brought low by their own act, so height gives power over others but no protection from one's own lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"900 says that even those with support beyond measure will not survive (உய்யார்) if those of the highest distinction are angered (சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்). 29 says the anger (வெகுளி) of those standing on the hill of good qualities is hard to ward off (காத்தல் அரிது) even for a moment. The Book of Wealth applies to kings and their resources what the opening chapters said in praise of renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone at all (யார்மாட்டும்), because every evil is born from it. 900 treats the anger of the most distinguished (சீரார் செறின்) as a force no support can save one from, and Parimelazhagar and Pope take these as people of great austerity. If they are ascetics, the chapter on not being angry would have them without anger; the conflict depends on that identification and on reading 900 as accepting such anger, where it may only be warning the offender of what follows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:901","b":"k:45","type":"qualifies","note":"45 says that when household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), these are its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்); Parimelazhagar takes the love as that owed to one's wife. 901 says those who fix their desire on the wife (மனைவிழைவார்) miss the excellent fruit (மாண்பயன்). The pair marks a line between love within the household, which is its fruit, and விழைவு that lets the wife rule the man, which loses it; Parimelazhagar reads 901 as men who follow the wife's lead for pleasure's sake.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"903 calls a man's stooping before his wife (இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த) இயல்பின்மை, something against the natural order, which shames him among the good. 55 praises the wife who rises worshipping her husband (கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்). The two show that the book fixes the direction of deference in marriage: it is a merit in the wife and a disgrace in the husband.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:978","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை), and elsewhere the book calls bowing good for everyone. 903 names one bowing that brings shame: lowering oneself before one's wife (தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை), which Parimelazhagar explains as arising from fear. The pair separates humility that comes from the absence of pride from submission that comes from fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:665","type":"same_image","note":"904 denies வீறு (distinction) to the action of the wife-fearing man: வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று. 665 says the firmness in action (வினைத்திட்பம்) of those who have attained distinction (வீறெய்தி மாண்டார்) reaches the king and is held in regard. The same phrase வீறு எய்து names what capable action normally earns and what, in 904, fear of the wife withholds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"904 says the man who fears his wife gets no distinction from his command of action (வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று). 1026 defines good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) as making the mastery of one's house one's own (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்), which Parimelazhagar takes as leading one's family. Both build on ஆண்மை as mastery: the man of 904 has given up mastery at home, and his mastery of work earns no honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:908","type":"agrees","note":"905 says the man who fears his wife fears to do good to the good; 908 says those who act as she wishes (பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) neither meet their friends' needs nor do good (நன்றாற்றார்). Both state the cost to others: the wife-ruled man fails the good in 905 and his friends in 908.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1088","type":"parallel_domain","note":"906 says that even if they live like the gods (இமையாரின் வாழினும்), men who fear their wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no dignity (பாடிலர்). In 1088 a lover exclaims that his might (பீடு), feared by enemies in battle, has broken before her bright forehead alone. Both show male standing giving way before a woman's bodily feature; the Book of Wealth counts it a disgrace, while in the Book of Love it is a lover's speech in the moment and carries no judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1011","type":"qualifies","note":"1011 ranks shame at one's deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்) as true shame and sets aside the shame of fair-browed women (திருநுதல் நல்லவர் நாண்) as another kind. 907 says a woman who has shame (நாணுடைப் பெண்) has more greatness than the manhood of one who lives doing a woman's bidding. So the woman's modesty that 1011 treats as the lesser kind still outranks, in 907, manhood that has given up command.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"907 pairs நாண் and ஆண்மை: manhood that obeys a woman is worth less than womanhood with shame. In 1134 a lover says the fierce flood of desire carries off the raft made of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). Both treat shame and manliness as the two things a man loses together under a woman's hold; 907 states it as a judgment, 1134 as a lover's own complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"907 gives greatness to the woman with shame over the submissive man's manhood (பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து). 1137, a speech in the Book of Love, says nothing has more worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்). In both, a woman's restraint is measured against a man's lack of it and ranked higher.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:141","type":"same_image","note":"908 describes men who live as the woman wishes, பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்; 141 speaks of the folly of living in desire for another man's woman, பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை. The same pairing of பெட்டு (desire, wish) with ஒழுகு (to conduct oneself) describes a life steered by desire in both, in 141 the man's own desire for a forbidden woman and in 908 the wishes of his own wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:788","type":"qualifies","note":"788 defines friendship as removing a friend's distress on the spot (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand of a man whose garment slips. 908 says those who live as the woman of the fine brow wishes (நன்னுதலாள் பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) do not see their friends' needs through (நட்டார் குறைமுடியார்). 908 names a condition under which the duty of 788 goes unmet: the man's actions are no longer his own to direct.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:754","type":"parallel_domain","note":"754 says wealth that comes by right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன் ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 909 lists the same three goods as missing in men who do a woman's bidding (பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்). Where 754 treats the three as flowing from one sound source, 909 treats them as shut off by one fault.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made abundant wealth the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easily had together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 909 says that deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits (அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்) are all absent in those who do a woman's bidding; Parimelazhagar and Pope take பிறவினை as pleasure. Both use the three aims as the measure of a life: 760 shows how they are gained together, 909 a way they are lost together.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:910","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"910 says those with deliberation and firmness of heart never fall into the folly of attachment to a woman. 917, in the next chapter, says it is those without a firm heart (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace with their minds on other things. Both locate the safeguard in the man's own நெஞ்சம், stated positively in 910 for the wife-ruled man and negatively in 917 for the client of courtesans.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"911 warns that the இன்சொல் of women who want a man's wealth brings him harm. In 1258 a woman calls the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her lover, a 'thief of many deceptions' (பன்மாயக் கள்வன்), the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Both treat gentle words as an instrument that undoes the hearer, though 1258 is a lover's complaint in the Book of Love, not a warning the book gives in its own voice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:912","b":"k:103","type":"same_image","note":"103 says help given by those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்) proves, when its worth is weighed (நயன்தூக்கின்), greater than the sea. 912 uses the same pair of words in the same order: the women weigh the gain (பயன்தூக்கி), and the man is to weigh their conduct (நயன்தூக்கி) and not join them. Weighing the return is what spoils a relationship in both verses, and weighing worth is the observer's proper response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:913","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"80 says the seat of life follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). 913 likens the false embrace (பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம்) of women who want wealth to embracing an unknown corpse in a dark room (ஏதில் பிணம்). 913 applies 80's premise to a particular case: a body offered without love is, for the one who holds it, a dead one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says that a loving nature with grace in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's unguarded moment. 914 opposes those who examine அருட்பொருள் to women for whom பொருள் is the only thing of value. In the chapter on theft and in this one, the same pair, aiming at grace or aiming at goods, sorts people into two kinds that do not mix.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have native acuteness together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 915 says those whose knowledge is made excellent by goodness of intellect (மதிநலத்தின் மாண்ட அறிவினவர்) do not touch the favours of women common to all; Parimelazhagar reads this too as natural intellect perfecting acquired knowledge. The pairing shows the same compound of mind being credited in statecraft with seeing through stratagems and here with keeping a man from a private vice.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:1311","type":"same_image","note":"915 calls the women பொதுநலத்தார், those whose favours are common to all, and says the wise will not touch them. In 1311 a wife tells her husband that all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), calls him பரத்த, and refuses his chest. The reproach of being 'common' is turned on the man there, and it is a sulking wife's charge in the Book of Love, not a judgement the book makes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women, and that the guard kept by their own நிறை, firmness, is the chief one. 917 says it is men without a heart of நிறை (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace while their hearts tend other things. The same inner firmness that 57 names as a wife's real protection is here named as what a man lacks when he goes to such women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:864","type":"parallel_domain","note":"864 says the man who does not let go of anger and has no நிறை (நிறையிலன்) is easy prey for anyone, at any time and place. 917 says those without நிறைநெஞ்சம் are the ones who go to women whose hearts are set on something else. In both the lack of self-command is what exposes a man, to enemies in 864 and to women who want what he has in 917.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:910","type":"agrees","note":"910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman never exists in those whose hearts hold deliberation (எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத்து இடனுடையார்). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an affliction to those who lack examining understanding (ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு). The neighbouring chapters, on subservience to a wife and on courtesans, give the same safeguard: a mind that thinks the matter through.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:1082","type":"same_image","note":"918 says that for those without discerning understanding the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses as the touch of a spirit-woman who takes life through desire. In 1082 a lover says that her returning his look is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) arriving with an army. The same figure of the woman as an afflicting spirit is a lover's praise in the Book of Love and a warning in 918.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:936","type":"same_image","note":"920 says double-minded women, liquor and dice are the companions of those whom Fortune has removed from herself (திருநீக்கப்பட்டார்). 936 says those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling (சூதென்னும் முகடி) do not eat their fill and suffer. Both verses describe a vice through the two sister goddesses: in 920 the vices attend the man Tiru has already left, in 936 one of them is Mugadi herself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:974","type":"same_image","note":"920 names women of two minds (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்), which Parimelazhagar explains as a mind that joins and does not join a man at once. 974 says greatness, like women of one mind (ஒருமை மகளிர்), exists only by guarding oneself. The two phrases are opposites, and where single-mindedness is 974's model for keeping greatness, doubleness in 920 goes with dice and drink as the company of the unfortunate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:653","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who mean to rise to leave off any deed that dims their lustre (ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை). 921 says those who live in love of liquor (கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகுவார்) are never held in awe and lose their lustre (ஒளியிழப்பர்). 921 gives a named instance of the kind of act 653 warns against in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:939","type":"parallel_domain","note":"939 says that clothing, wealth, food, lustre (ஒளி) and learning do not come near a man who takes to gaming. 921 says those devoted to liquor lose their ஒளி and are not feared. The adjoining chapters on drink and dice both count the loss of ஒளி, the standing that makes a man regarded, among the costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:922","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"922 forbids drinking and adds that if anyone drinks, it should be those who do not want to be counted by the worthy (சான்றோரான் எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). 656 forbids any act the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை), even to relieve one's own mother's hunger. In both the judgement of the சான்றோர் is the measure of conduct; 922 states it as the price a drinker must be willing to pay.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:924","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"924 says shame turns away from the drinker. In 1288 the speaker tells her lover that his chest is to her as liquor is to those who have enjoyed it, wanted even though it does harm that brings disgrace (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The simile in the Book of Love takes for granted what 924 teaches, that drink disgraces, and uses it to describe a desire that persists in spite of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:925","b":"k:838","type":"same_image","note":"838 says a fool getting something into his hand is like a deranged man getting drunk (மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்று). 925 says paying out wealth to buy oblivion shows ignorance. 838 uses drunkenness as the image for what a fool does with property, and 925 describes drunkenness itself as a fool's use of property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:927","b":"k:271","type":"same_image","note":"271 says the five elements laugh inwardly (அகத்தே நகும்) at the hidden conduct of a man with a deceitful mind. 927 says those who drink and droop are seen through and always laughed at by their own town (உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர்); Parimelazhagar takes கள்ளொற்றி as drinking in concealment. In both, concealed conduct has a witness that laughs, the elements within the body in 271 and the neighbours who infer what is going on in 927.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"293 says not to lie about what one's own heart knows, for the heart will burn the liar afterwards. 928 concerns one such lie, 'I have never been drunk' (களித்தறியேன்), about something the speaker's heart knows, and says that drink brings the hidden thing out. 293 gives the inner penalty for this kind of lie and 928 adds an outward one, exposure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:1161","type":"parallel_domain","note":"928 tells the drinker to stop saying he has never been drunk, because what he hid in his heart shows all the more at that moment (ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்). In 1161 a woman says she would hide her pain, but like spring water for those who draw it, it increases (ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்). Both end on மிகும்: the attempt to conceal an inner state is answered by its overflowing, in drink and in love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 says to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 931 uses the same concession, 'even if you win' (வென்றிடினும்), and still refuses the gain, calling it a swallowed hook. In both the apparent success of a wrong source is conceded and set aside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:385","type":"qualifies","note":"385 defines a king as one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it and apportion it (இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலும் காத்தலும்). 933, which Parimelazhagar reads of a king, says both the gathered wealth and its sources (பொருள் ஆயம்) leave him if he keeps at the dice. One habit undoes the gathering and the guarding that 385 makes the mark of rule.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"933 says that if one keeps calling the rolling dice-throw (உருளாயம்), wealth and its income (பொருளாயம்) go and fall outside (புறமே படும்); Parimelazhagar takes 'outside' as into enemies' hands. 1009 says the wealth a miser gathered without love, self-regard or virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The gambler and the hoarder, by opposite habits, both end with their wealth in strangers' hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:934","b":"k:1045","type":"develops","note":"934 says nothing gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and ruins standing (சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும்). 1045 says that within the one affliction called poverty many sufferings arrive (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்). Read together they form a chain: gambling brings harms on the way to poverty, and poverty then brings its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:437","type":"same_image","note":"935 says those who clung (இவறியார்) in pride to dice, gaming hall and skilled hand became people with nothing (இல்லாகியார்). 437 uses the same word of the miser: the wealth of one who clings (இவறியான்) without doing what should be done perishes. இவறுதல், holding on and not letting go, ends in loss in both verses, whether the thing held is money or the game.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"539 advises thinking, when swollen with one's own joy, of those who were ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). 935 supplies such a memory: those who took pride (தருக்கி) in the dice and came to nothing; Parimelazhagar reads them as kings of former times and names the Pandavas. 935 does for gambling what 539 asks a ruler to do in general.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess, grudging the envious man, shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்). 936 names that sister, முகடி, as gambling itself, covering those who play. Envy hands a person over to misfortune; gambling, in 936, is misfortune under another name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:937","b":"k:1043","type":"parallel_domain","note":"937 says time spent in the gaming hall ruins long-held wealth and character (பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்). 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's தோல் (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்), which Pope takes as dignity of speech. The two sentences have the same shape: an inherited standing and a personal quality lost at one stroke, with gambling as a cause and poverty as a cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:248","type":"develops","note":"938 lists what gambling does: it destroys wealth (பொருள் கெடுத்து), makes one take up lying, destroys compassion (அருள் கெடுத்து) and drives one through misery. 248 ranks two of these losses: those without wealth may flower again one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished. By 248's measure the third item in 938's list is graver than the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:757","type":"develops","note":"757 calls அருள் a child born of love that is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called பொருள். 938 has gambling destroy both பொருள் and அருள். Parimelazhagar assigns the losses in 938 to separate causes (losing, winning, resentment), but 757 suggests a link between them: with the nurse gone, the child is harder to raise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:166","type":"parallel_domain","note":"166 says the kin of one who envies a gift perish without anything to wear or eat (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் இன்றி). 939 begins its list of what gaming keeps away with the same pair, clothing and food (உடை ... ஊண்). Envy and gambling are both measured by the loss of the plainest necessities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"939 names five things, among them learning (கல்வி), that 'அடையா' the one who takes to gaming. Parimelazhagar glosses this as 'do not come to him', but Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take the five as departing from him. On that second reading 939 conflicts with 400, which calls learning the wealth that cannot perish (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்); on Parimelazhagar's reading there is no conflict, since the gambler never acquires it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:1145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1145 a lover says love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking is wanted more each time one is drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்). 940 has the same 'each time ... the more' construction with another vice, the dice, as the comparison (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்). The lover uses the figure to praise his state, while 940 uses it to describe an attachment fed by its own losses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:941","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"941 says the three humours counted by the learned, beginning with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short (மிகினும் குறையினும்); Parimelazhagar refers the excess and shortfall to food and activity. 466 says one is ruined by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செயக் கெடும் ... செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Both verses locate harm on two sides of a right amount, in the body and in action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"943 says to eat knowing the measure once digestion is complete, as the way one who has a body can carry it long (நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) seems to exist and then perishes; Parimelazhagar refers that measure to one's wealth. The same phrase governs both the stomach and the purse. (This edition prints 'அறவறிந்து' in 943; Parimelazhagar's gloss reads அளவு அறிந்து.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:946","b":"k:420","type":"parallel_domain","note":"946 says disease stays with the one of very great feed (கழிபேர் இரையான்), as pleasure stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்). 420 asks what it matters whether those live or die who know taste only by the mouth and not by the ear, and calls them மாக்கள். Both verses use a word with an animal sense for the person ruled by the mouth: இரை, feed, and மாக்கள்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealth do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்). 947 says the one who eats beyond the measure of his fire gets disease beyond measure. Chapter 29 uses அளவு as a moral measure and chapter 95 as a bodily one, and in each case overstepping it brings its own penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"947 says that if one eats greatly without discerning the measure of the fire within (தீயளவு), disease arises without measure (நோயளவின்றிப் படும்). 475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if they are piled too high (சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). In both a fixed capacity, the digestive fire or the axle, is what excess breaks, whatever the quality of the load.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 tells the healer to look for the root of the disease (நோய்முதல்) before the remedy. 360 does this for suffering in general: when desire, anger and confusion perish even in name, the நோய் perishes. The chapter on true perception treats pain as chapter 95 treats illness, by naming causes and removing them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:504","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 sets out an order of enquiry: look into the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so that it works (நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி ... வாய்நாடி). 504 has the same threefold நாடி for judging a person: examine the qualities, examine the faults, examine which predominates, and take that. Diagnosis of a patient and assessment of a candidate are given the same grammar of step-by-step examination before a decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:637","type":"agrees","note":"637 says that even when one knows the method of doing a thing, one should act knowing how the world goes (செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்); Parimelazhagar glosses the method as what the treatises teach. 949 addresses the one who has learned (கற்றான்) and still requires him to consider this patient, this illness and this moment. Both hold that book learning has to be fitted to the particular case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip (இழுக்கார்) in conduct, truthfulness and shame. 133 says having conduct is itself குடிமை and that a lapse (இழுக்கம்) turns into low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Read together, 952's claim is close to a definition: whoever slips has by 133 already left the class of the well-born, a point Parimelazhagar makes on 133 while keeping it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:983","type":"parallel_domain","note":"952 lists three things the well-born keep: ஒழுக்கம், வாய்மை, நாண். 983 lists five pillars that hold up சால்பு: love, shame (நாண்), obligingness, kindly regard and truthfulness (வாய்மை). Two of the three marks of lineage reappear as supports of personal worth, so the same core is described once as inherited and once as a structure a person stands on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"953 names a smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning (நகை ஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை) as the share of a true lineage. 92 takes three of these and ranks them: being sweet-spoken with a glad face (முகனமர்ந்து இன்சொலன்) is better than giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்). The list in 953 is flat; 92 shows the book elsewhere weighing its items against one another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"953 puts giving and not scorning (ஈகை ... இகழாமை) side by side among the marks of a good family; Parimelazhagar takes all four as the way one meets the poor who come. 1057 gives the receiver's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt (இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவார்), the asker's heart rejoices within. The pairing shows why 'not scorning' sits next to 'giving' in the list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"954 says the well-born will not do diminishing things (குன்றுவ) even if they get crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும்). 311 says the stainless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்). Both use the same 'even if they gained' (பெறினும்) form to set a fixed refusal against a large reward, once for family standing and once for non-harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"954 says no amount of wealth makes the well-born do what lowers them. 657 says the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) ranks above gain reached by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). Both rank standing above wealth; 657 states the comparison outright, in the context of a minister's clean action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"954 says the well-born do not do things that diminish (குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்), whatever the gain. 965 uses the same word to say what follows if they do: even those like a hill sink (குன்றுவர்) if they do a diminishing thing (குன்றுவ) the size of a kundri seed. 954 gives the rule and 965 the cost of breaking it, at a scale far below crores.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:1043","type":"tension","note":"955 says an ancient family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its character when its means shrink. 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's speech (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்). They conflict if 1043 is read as plain poverty undoing lineage; the conflict eases on Parimelazhagar's reading, which stresses that 1043 calls poverty a craving (நசை), so it is the wanting, not the lack, that ruins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"956 says those who mean to keep to a spotless line do not do what lacks worth (சால்பில செய்யார்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'. 656 says one should not do what the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை) even if one must see one's mother hungry. Both set a standard of worthy conduct above need, 656 naming the hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:660","type":"develops","note":"956 says those resolved to live by their spotless lineage will not do unworthy things by taking to deceit (சலம் பற்றி). 660 uses the same word for wealth-making: securing wealth made by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள் செய்து) is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. 956 rules deceit out on grounds of family honour; 660 adds that its gains do not hold anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"958 says that if lack of affection (நாரின்மை) shows in a man, people will doubt his lineage (குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப்படும்). 992 names having love and being born in a seasoned family (அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) as the two things that make up courtesy. 992 pairs love and birth as partners; 958 shows that when the first is missing the second is no longer believed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"960 says one who wants lineage must want humility toward everyone (யார்க்கும் பணிவு). 125 says bowing is good for all (எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல்) and is a further wealth for the rich. Both make humility general, and each singles out the highly placed, by family or by wealth, as those it most becomes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"960 asks for humility toward all (யார்க்கும் பணிவு) from anyone who wants to keep a lineage. 963, in the next chapter, limits when bowing is called for: in abundance one must bow (பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல்), but in reduced circumstances one must stand high (சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). The humility of 960 is thus not meant to cover bending under want.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:1019","type":"same_image","note":"960 pairs நலம் with shame and குலம் with humility: want நலம், then want நாணுடைமை; want குலம், then want பணிவு toward all. 1019 uses the same pair in negative form: a lapse in principle burns one's குலம், and settled shamelessness burns one's நலம் (நலஞ்சுடும் நாணின்மை). The two verses share the குலம்/நலம் pairing and agree that நலம் depends on shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:131","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 961's இன்றி அமையா as things without which one would not survive, so the verse puts standing above life. 131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life (உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்) because it gives excellence. The same ranking, a value kept ahead of survival, is made for honour in one chapter and for conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:113","type":"parallel_domain","note":"962 refuses unworthy acts even when they would bring renown (சீரினும்). 113 says to let go at once of gain that departs from fairness even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்). Both have the form 'even if it yields the thing you want, not by this means', applied to fame in one and to profit in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:652","type":"qualifies","note":"652 says to avoid any action that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை), which makes renown one of the tests of an act. 962 says those who want renown together with great manliness will not do unworthy things even for renown (சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யார்). 962 therefore narrows 652's test: bringing fame does not by itself make an act fit to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:680","type":"tension","note":"963 says that in small and shrunken circumstances one must hold oneself high (சிறிய சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). 680 says those whose domain is small (உறைசிறியார்), fearing their own people's trembling, will bow to the greater and accept terms (கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). They conflict if 963's 'reduced state' includes weakness of position before a stronger party; if 963 is only about poverty and personal bearing, 680 is separate advice on statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:988","type":"develops","note":"963 asks for an upright bearing (உயர்வு) when one's means have shrunk. 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called worth (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 988 supplies the ground for 963's demand: want does not itself lower a person, so there is no reason to stoop in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:124","type":"develops","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his position (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain. 964 uses the same word for the opposite case: those who descend from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்த) are like hair off the head. Together they give the two ends of one scale, both measured by whether a person keeps to their நிலை.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"964 says people who have come down from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை) are like hair fallen from the head. 973 says those who are not low are not low even when placed low (கீழிருந்தும் கீழல்லார் கீழல்லவர்). They conflict if 964's நிலை means outward rank or station; on Parimelazhagar's reading it means the high standard the well-born hold themselves to, so the fall is their own doing and the verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"966 asks what is gained by going and standing behind those who scorn you (இகழ்வார் பின் சென்று நிலை). 1033 says only those who eat by their own ploughing truly live, and all others bow to eat and go behind (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). Both use 'going behind' another as the picture of a dependent life, and 1033 names the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1053","type":"qualifies","note":"966 says standing in the train of those who scorn you brings neither renown nor the gods' land. 1053 says that even asking has a certain beauty (ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when one stands before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று). What 966 rejects is therefore not dependence as such but dependence on people who despise the one who comes to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:967","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 prefers being called ruined to a life got by following those who reject you. 230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). In both, a life that cannot be lived on one's own terms, without honour or without the power to give, is ranked below death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"968 asks whether a life that tends the flesh is a medicine (மருந்தோ) once greatness has lost its dignity; Parimelazhagar takes this as a medicine against dying. 82 says that eating while the guest waits outside is not to be wanted even if the food were the medicine of deathlessness (சாவா மருந்தெனினும்). Both raise the idea of an immortality-medicine only to rank it below a duty, honour in one and hospitality in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"968 questions the worth of a life that merely feeds the body (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) when dignity is gone. 1017 says those governed by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் ... நாண் துறவார்). The chapters on honour and on shame reach the same ordering of life and self-respect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:779","type":"parallel_domain","note":"969 says those like the கவரிமா give up life when their honour is at stake (உயிர் நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்). 779 asks who could reproach as failures those who die so that what they vowed is not broken (இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவார்). Dying to avoid going back on what one stands for appears as a matter of civil honour in 969 and as a soldier's vow in 779.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who do not live on in disgrace. 235 says that a ruin which is growth and a death which is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are hard to reach except for the skilled. 235 gives the general idea that 970 applies: a death can leave a person present in fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:971","type":"same_image","note":"970 sets disgrace against radiance: the world worships the ஒளி of those who will not live when இளி comes. 971 defines the same two words: one's ஒளி is abundance of aspiration, and one's இளி is saying 'we will live without it' (அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்). In both, disgrace is a way of going on living, and radiance belongs to those who refuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:981","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"981 says that for those who know what is due (கடன் அறிந்து) and take on worth, every good thing is கடன்; Parimelazhagar glosses this as the good qualities being natural to them. 218 uses the same phrase, கடனறி காட்சியவர், for people who do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 218 shows one concrete case of 981's general claim: a duty that is felt as one's own does not wait on circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"982 says the worthy have only the goodness of character (குணநலம்), and that other goodness is not counted among goodnesses; Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் to be the goodness of the bodily limbs. 79 asks what all the outer limbs (புறத்துறுப்பு) can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (அகத்துறுப்பு). Both verses rank an inner quality over the body's outward parts, one for worth and one for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"982 denies that any goodness other than character counts for the worthy. 407 says the handsome looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both dismiss outward நலம் that has nothing inside it, though 407 measures by understanding and 982 by character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"983 calls its five qualities the pillar planted to bear worth (சால்பு ஊன்றிய தூண்). 615 calls the man who wants the work and not pleasure a pillar that wipes away his kin's troubles and props them (துடைத்து ஊன்றும் தூண்). The same figure, with the same verb ஊன்று, is used once for qualities holding up a character and once for a person holding up a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:1013","type":"develops","note":"983 lists five pillars that bear worth: அன்பு, நாண், ஒப்புரவு, கண்ணோட்டம், வாய்மை. 1013 singles out one of them: as all lives are fixed on the body (ஊனைக் குறித்த), சால்பு is fixed on the good called shame (நாண் என்னும் நன்மை குறித்தது). Read together, shame is one support among five in 983 and the seat of worth itself in 1013.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"985 says the strength of those who accomplish is bowing (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes. 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration. Both attach bowing to the strong and not the weak; 985 adds that it has an effect on opponents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"986 says the touchstone of worth is accepting defeat (தோல்வி கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equal. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. Both treat yielding as the better outcome; 1327 is a lover's remark about a sulk, while 986 states a test of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:998","type":"agrees","note":"987 says worth is useless unless it does good even to those who did harm. 998, in the next chapter, says failing in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness (நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்) is lowness (கடை). Both verses use the particle 'even to' (-க்கும்) to extend the virtue to hostile people, one for சால்பு and one for பண்பு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1064","type":"develops","note":"988 calls சால்பு a firmness (திண்மை) that keeps poverty from being a disgrace. 1064 describes that firmness at work: the worth that will not consent to begging even when no means remain (இடமில்லாக் காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு) is too large for all space to hold. 1064 shows what the poor but worthy person of 988 does and does not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"990 says the earth cannot carry its burden (பொறை) once the worthy lose their worth. 572 says those without kindly regard are, by merely existing, a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The same figure is used from two sides: the worthy are what lets the earth carry its load, and people without கண்ணோட்டம் are dead weight on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:548","type":"parallel_domain","note":"991 and 548 share the word எண்பதம், easy access. In 548 a king who is not accessible (எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா) and does not examine and do justice falls and perishes by himself; in 991 being accessible to all makes courtesy easy to attain. The quality whose absence ruins a ruler is the one whose presence makes a person courteous.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:97","type":"same_image","note":"994 says the world honours the character (பண்பு) of those whose usefulness (பயன்) comes from doing good with fairness (நயனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த). 97 uses the same four words of speech: words that yield benefit (பயன்) and do not part from பண்பு produce நயன் and bring forth நன்றி. Parimelazhagar glosses நயன் as justice in both, so the cluster describes good speech in 97 and a good life in 994.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"994 praises people of நயன் whose doing good makes them useful (பயனுடையார்). 216 says wealth falling to one who has நயன் (நயனுடையான்) is like a fruit-bearing tree (பயன்மரம்) ripening in the middle of the town. 216 supplies the picture for 994: the person of நயன் is of பயன் to everyone within reach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"995 says courtesy is present even in enmity (பகையுள்ளும் பண்புள) in those who understand others. 874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of courtesy who treats enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடையாளன்). 874 takes the step beyond 995, from staying courteous toward an enemy to turning him into a friend; Parimelazhagar reads 874 as a matter of policy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"995 warns that இகழ்ச்சி, scorn, wounds even as a joke. 953 lists not scorning (இகழாமை) with a smile, giving and sweet words as the four marks of a true family. 953 names the trait as a mark of good birth, and 995 gives the reason for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"998 calls it lowness (கடை) to fail in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness. 579 calls it the highest thing (தலை) to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose nature is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்). The verses mark the two ends of the same scale: good conduct toward the hostile is தலை, and its failure is கடை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:852","type":"agrees","note":"998 requires courtesy even toward those who do unfriendly, unkind things. 852 says that even if someone does what one cannot bear, intending division (பகல் கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), it is best not to do harm in return out of hostility. 852 gives the minimum, no retaliation, and 998 asks for more, that courtesy itself not lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"999 says that for those not able to laugh (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the great world lies in darkness even by day; Parimelazhagar takes நகல் as mixing with others and being glad at heart. 523 says the life of one who does not mingle freely (அளவளாவு இல்லாதான்) is like a tank filling without a bank. On Parimelazhagar's reading both describe the cost of not mixing with people, as lost wealth in 523 and a darkened world in 999.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). 1008 says the wealth of a man nobody seeks out is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of the village (நச்சு மரம் பழுத்தற்று). Both say a good thing is made useless or harmful by the character of its holder; Parimelazhagar reads 1000 as wealth that benefits no one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:229","type":"develops","note":"229 says eating alone in order to fill up one's store (நிரப்பிய தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging; 1001 takes the case one step further, the man who does not even eat it (அஃதுண்ணான்), and calls him dead. Read together they grade the misuse of wealth: unshared use is wretched, no use at all is not living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1002","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account (பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்). 1002 names nearly the same thought, பொருளானாம் எல்லாம், as what the miser thinks, and says that when it leads to not giving (ஈயாது இவறும்) it is delusion. The chapter on making wealth is thus limited here: the claim holds only for wealth that is given and used.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"114 says whether people were worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம்; 1004 asks what எச்சம் the unwanted man imagines for himself. Parimelazhagar takes the word as offspring in 114 and as what remains after death, i.e. renown, in 1004, so the pairing shows the same test of a life by what it leaves, applied with two senses of the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்); 1005 says the same of those who neither give nor enjoy, even with crores. The same form of argument, that owning is not having unless some further quality is present, is used for understanding in one place and for use and giving in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1006","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth got by a man without courtesy is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று); 1006 says the man who neither uses nor gives is ஏதம் to பெருஞ்செல்வம். In both the wealth is good in itself and the holder is what spoils it, which is how Parimelazhagar reads 1006 (others take the wealth to be a misery to him).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"226 says ending the ruinous hunger of the destitute (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where one who has got wealth stores it. 1007 says the wealth of one who does nothing for the destitute (அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான்) is like a very beautiful woman growing old alone. 226 says where wealth is kept safe; 1007 shows what happens to wealth kept anywhere else: it passes its time unused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:1039","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says land whose owner stays away sulks like a wife (இல்லாளின் ஊடிவிடும்); 1007 likens ungiven wealth to a beautiful woman who ages alone (தமியள் மூத்தற்று). Both picture a good left unattended as a woman without her partner, for farmland in one verse and for money in the other; Parimelazhagar notes for 1007 that the wealth itself loses its fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1009","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in plenty (ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு) the other two, virtue and pleasure, come easily. 1009 uses the same word, ஒண்பொருள், for wealth amassed by dropping love, denying oneself and not looking to virtue, and says others will take it. So the promise of 760 fails when the two ends it names are given up in the earning; Parimelazhagar reads 760 as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:219","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 1010 says the brief want (சிறுதுனி) of the rich who have renown is like the rain cloud running dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). 219 says the poverty of a man of நயன் is just his not being able to do what he is used to doing. Both treat the giver's poverty as a loss to others, not to himself, and 1010 adds by its image that it passes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"201 says the excellent fear the intoxication called evil deed (விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்); 1011 defines shame proper as shame on account of deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்). Fear and shame are given the same object, one's own wrong action, in two chapters, and Parimelazhagar glosses நாணுதல் elsewhere (433, 172) as அஞ்சுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1011 mentions the other shame, that of women, without describing it. In 1251 a woman in the Book of Love describes it from inside: shame is the bolt (நாணுத்தாழ்) on the door of her restraint, which the axe of desire breaks. This is a speech in character, but it shows what the shame 1011 calls பிற is: a guard on conduct in love, not a recoil from a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people; the likeness that counts is of character (பண்பொத்தல்). 1012 says the bodily needs are shared by all and shame is what sets people apart. Both refuse to take what is physically common as the measure of a human being.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"80 says the seat of life goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை) and without it a body is bones wrapped in skin; 1013 says worth depends on shame as life depends on flesh. Both use the life-and-body relation to say a quality is what a thing lives by, love for the person in 80 and shame for சால்பு in 1013. Parimelazhagar notes a variant ஊணை ('food') in 1013, which Manakkudavar follows; the link rests on the reading ஊனை.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:118","type":"agrees","note":"118 (with 115) calls not leaning to one side the ornament of the worthy (கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி); 1014 calls shame the ornament of the worthy (அணியன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு). The same phrase names two inner restraints, and neither is an outward decoration.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"980 says greatness covers others' exposure (அற்றம் மறைக்கும்) and smallness only speaks their faults. 1015 gives the feeling behind that conduct: being ashamed of another's disgrace as of one's own. One who feels the other's பழி in this way has no reason to tell it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"1015 praises one who feels shame even for others' disgrace; 1018 describes one who does not feel shame at what makes others ashamed (பிறர் நாணத்தக்கது தான் நாணான்). The two set out the ends of a scale inside the chapter: shame that extends beyond oneself, and shame that fails even for one's own act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women and the guard kept by their own firmness (நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) is best; 1016 says the high have shame for their fence (நாண்வேலி). In both the effective guard is a quality inside the person and not an outer barrier, for a wife's fidelity in one and for the conduct of the high in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"311 says the spotless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction. 1016, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says the high take shame as their fence and do not want the wide world (வியன்ஞாலம் பேணலர்) where the two conflict. Both state a refusal of great gain at the price of a restraint; the other reading he reports, that they will not want the world without first having the fence of shame, gives the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"421 calls understanding an inner fortress enemies cannot destroy (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்); 1016 calls shame a fence (வேலி), which Parimelazhagar explains as keeping blame and sin from entering. Both give an inner quality the work of a defensive structure, one against ruin and one against disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:130","type":"develops","note":"130 says virtue watches for the right moment and comes by the path to the one who guards against anger and has learned restraint (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து). 1018, as the commentators read it, has virtue ashamed to come near the shameless. Together they give the two directions of the same figure: virtue seeks out the restrained and shrinks from those without shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin; 1018 says that if one is not ashamed, அறம் is ashamed. In both, virtue is an agent that answers the person's act in the terms of that act, with a penalty in 204 and with withdrawal in 1018.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1019","b":"k:952","type":"develops","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip in three things: conduct, truthfulness and shame (ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும்). 1019 says what happens when two of those three fail: a lapse in conduct burns the family, and shamelessness that stays burns every good. The chapter on good family gives the mark and 1019 gives the cost of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"1021 says no greatness has more dignity (பீடு) than that of the one who says 'I will not stay my hand' (கைதூவேன்) from the work. 611 says not to slacken at a task's difficulty because effort itself gives greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). Both locate பெருமை in persistence rather than in rank or result; 1021 applies it to the work of raising one's family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"1021 praises the resolve never to stop work once begun (கைதூவேன்). 612 gives the other side: guard against the work dying in mid-course (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), since, in Parimelazhagar's reading, the world gives up on those who give up their unfinished work. The pair shows the same refusal to stop as honour in one verse and as a condition of the world's regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:604","type":"agrees","note":"1022 says the family (குடி) lengthens through long-continued work that has both effort (ஆள்வினை) and mature knowledge. 604 states the reverse case: for those sunk in sloth, without worthy exertion (மாண்ட உஞற்றிலவர்), the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). Together they make one claim from both sides: the fortunes of a குடி follow the exertion of its members.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"1022 pairs effort with full knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்) as the two things that extend a family. 618 uses the same pairing when it assigns blame: lacking fortune is no disgrace, but lacking effort after knowing what should be known (அறிவறிந்து ஆள்வினை இன்மை) is. In both verses knowledge and effort are named together as what a person can be held to, as against fortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"1023 has தெய்வம் tying up its garment (மடிதற்று) to go before the family-builder. 617 says the dark goddess lives in sloth (மடி) and the lotus goddess in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாள்). Both put divine favour on the side of the one who works; 1023's மடி (cloth) also echoes the மடி (sloth) that 617 and the chapter on sloth condemn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:773","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 says fierceness is called great manliness (பேராண்மை), and that helping a foe in distress (ஊராண்மை) is its edge. 1026 says good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) is making oneself the one who manages the house one was born in (இல்லாண்மை). Each verse takes ஆண்மை and coins a second compound that turns it toward care for others; Parimelazhagar notes that 'நல்' in 1026 is there to set it apart from the manliness of war.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1027","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1027 says that as in battle the load falls on the stout-hearted (வன்கண்ணர்), so among kin the burden (பொறை) falls on those able to carry it. 770 says an army has no existence without leaders, however many steady men it has (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). 770 is the military fact that 1027 uses as its simile: numbers do not carry a force or a family, a few capable people do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 describes one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as the pillar who wipes away his kin's sorrows. 1029 looks at that same person from the inside: his body seems to hold nothing but suffering. 1029 states the cost of the role that 615 praises; Parimelazhagar takes the question as noting that pleasure never reaches him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1032","b":"k:42","type":"parallel_domain","note":"42 says the householder is the support (துணை) of renouncers, the destitute and the dead. 1032 says the ploughmen bear (பொறுத்து) everyone who does not plough. Both verses identify one group whose ordinary work holds up all the others: the householder in the Book of Virtue, the farmer in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:967","type":"develops","note":"1033 describes everyone but the farmer as those who bow, eat and walk behind others (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). 967 says it is better to be called ruined than to live by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின்சென்று வாழ்தல்). Both treat 'going behind' another for a living as a lesser life; 967 judges it by honour, and 1033 names farming as the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:389","type":"same_image","note":"389 says the world rests under the umbrella (கவிகை) of a king who can bear bitter words. 1034 says those who have the shade of grain will see the shade of many umbrellas come under their own umbrella (தங்குடைக்கீழ்), which Parimelazhagar takes as their own king's. Both use the royal umbrella's shade for dominion, but 389 credits the king's character and 1034 credits the farmers' harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules justly (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு). 1034 says those who hold the grain bring many kings' umbrellas under their own. The dependence runs in both directions: in 545 good rule yields harvest, and in 1034 harvest yields the king's wider rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1061","type":"develops","note":"1061 says that not begging (இரவாமை), even from those who give gladly without concealing (கரவாது), is worth ten million times more. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their own hands produce do not beg (இரவார்) and give to askers without concealing (கரவாது ஈவர்). 1035 names the livelihood that makes the ideal of 1061 possible, and puts the farmer on the giving side of the same exchange.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1037","b":"k:1038","type":"qualifies","note":"1037 says that if ploughed soil is dried until a measure of dust (தொடிப்புழுதி) falls to a quarter (கஃசு), the crop thrives without even a handful of manure. 1038 says manuring is better than ploughing (ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல்). The two set tillage and manure against each other from opposite sides: thorough working of the soil can replace manure, yet as a rule manure counts for more than ploughing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1038","b":"k:1147","type":"same_image","note":"1038 treats manure (எரு) and water (நீர்) as what makes a crop grow. In 1147 the woman says her love-sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and her mother's words as water (கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல் நீராக). The Book of Love takes the two inputs that 1038 gives as farming advice and uses them as a figure for what feeds her passion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:553","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says that if the owner (கிழவன்) stays away and does not go to it, the land turns against him and sulks like a wife. 553 says the king who does not look into things and do justice every day loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). In both, land is lost through the owner's or ruler's lack of personal attention; Parimelazhagar reads 1039 too as requiring a daily visit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1039 compares the land to a wife (இல்லாள்) who sulks when her owner stays away. 1040 calls the land a good woman (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள்) who laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. The chapter closes with two verses that make the land a woman who responds to how a man treats her, by sulking in one and by laughing in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1039 describes neglected land with the Book of Love's words for a lovers' sulk: it resents and sulks like a wife (புலந்து ... ஊடி விடும்). 1304, in the chapter on sulking, says that not appeasing one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. The figures run in opposite directions: the land is compared to a sulking woman in 1039, and the neglected sulking woman to a plant in 1304.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:616","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and lack of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்திவிடும்). 1040 pictures the Land laughing at people who claim poverty while sitting idle (அசைஇ இருப்பார்). Both hold that such poverty comes from not working; 1040 states it as mockery from the land that would have paid them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:171","type":"develops","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness ruins the family (வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றி). 1043 names poverty itself a craving (நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை) and gives it the same effect on ancient descent. Read together, 1043 treats poverty as harmful to a lineage through the desire it breeds, the mechanism 171 states for covetousness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1045","b":"k:1056","type":"develops","note":"1045 says many sufferings arrive within the one affliction called poverty (நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள்). 1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the afflictions of poverty perish together (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). The next chapter answers this verse in its own terms: the sufferings that come as a group also leave as a group.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"1047 says the person in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by his own mother. 117 says the world does not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in virtue (நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு). The qualifier அறஞ்சாரா in 1047 marks the boundary 117 draws: estrangement follows poverty without virtue, while poverty with virtue is not held against a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1048","b":"k:532","type":"same_image","note":"1048 is a poor man's cry: will the want that as good as killed me yesterday come today too (நெருநலும் கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு)? 532 uses the same noun and verb in a simile, constant poverty killing understanding (நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). Both treat நிரப்பு as something that kills and that returns daily; Parimelazhagar glosses both with a man who fills his stomach each day only with great pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says one can even sleep in the midst of fire, but in want there is no closing the eyes (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). 896 says one burned by fire may yet survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்). Both measure a non-physical danger by granting that fire can be endured and denying the same of the thing compared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:1136","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says that in poverty the eyes cannot close by any means (யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது). In 1136 the man says his eyes will not close because of the girl, even at midnight (படல் ஒல்லா ... என் கண்). The same sign, eyes that cannot close, marks want in the Book of Wealth and longing in the Book of Love; 1136 is a lover's speech, so this is a shared figure and not a shared teaching.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"1051 says ask when you find those fit to be asked (இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின்), and if they withhold, the blame is theirs. 1067, in the chapter on the dread of begging, says: if you must beg, do not beg from those who conceal (கரப்பார் இரவன்மின்). The two chapters take opposite attitudes to begging but give the same rule about whom to ask.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:224","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 speaks from the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1052 speaks from the asker's side: asking is a pleasure if what is asked comes without suffering (துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்). Each verse makes the pain or pleasure of one party depend on how the exchange goes for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1052 says even asking is pleasure to a person (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்) when the thing comes without pain. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking, even asking water for a cow (நாவிற்கு இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்). They conflict if 1052 is read as commending asking under good conditions; 1052 speaks of pleasure and 1066 of disgrace, so the conflict depends on taking a disgraceful act as unable to be a pleasant one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"218 says those whose sight knows what is due (கடனறி காட்சியவர்) do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even when they lack means. 1053 uses the same description, கடனறிவார், for the people before whom asking has a beauty; Manakkudavar glosses them as those who know ஒப்புரவு. The pairing identifies the fit giver of the begging chapter with the person 218 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:708","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says there is a beauty even in asking while standing before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று இரப்பும்). 708 says that if one has people who look within and understand, it is enough to stand looking at their face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). Parimelazhagar reads 1053 the same way: standing before such people is enough and nothing need be said, which is why the asking keeps some grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1061","type":"qualifies","note":"1054 says asking from those who do not know concealing even in a dream is like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). 1061 takes the same givers, who give gladly without concealing and are dear as one's eyes (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார்), and says not asking even of them is worth ten million times more (இரவாமை கோடி உறும்). 1061 limits the praise in 1054: the best case of asking still ranks below not asking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:571","type":"parallel_domain","note":"571 says the world exists because the beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 1055 uses the same word and construction: asking goes on because the unconcealing exist (உண்மையால்). In both, a practice or the world itself is said to rest on the existence of a disposition in some people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:1059","type":"develops","note":"1055 says askers take up standing before others because people who do not conceal exist in the world (கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால்). 1059 says givers would have no renown if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). The two verses state the dependence in both directions: asking exists because of givers, and givers' standing exists because of askers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1063","type":"tension","note":"1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the affliction of poverty perishes at once (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). 1063 says nothing is harder than the hardness of thinking 'we will end the affliction of want by begging' (இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்து தீர்வாம்). They conflict on whether asking can end poverty's distress, if 1056 is read as a general claim; Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 1063 as blaming the choice of begging over effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1068","type":"qualifies","note":"1056 gives the good outcome of asking: meeting those without concealment ends the afflictions of want. 1068 gives the other outcome: the unsafe boat called asking splits when it strikes the rock called concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்தாக்க). Both make the result of asking turn on கரவு in the person asked, so 1068 states the risk that 1056 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1057","b":"k:228","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of the giver's joy, the pleasure of giving and being glad (ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம்), which hoarders do not know. 1057 speaks of the asker's joy, the heart glad within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது), when the giving is free of scorn. The same verb உவ marks gladness on both sides of one act of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"1058 says that without askers the world would be puppets coming and going, which makes askers part of a living world; Manakkudavar draws the conclusion that asking is therefore not to be held low. 1062 says that if one must live even by begging, let the maker of the world wander and perish (பரந்து கெடுக உலகியற்றியான்). They conflict on whether a world that contains begging is as it should be; the conflict depends on reading 1058 as approval and not only as a remark about what givers need.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1060","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி); Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as witness that means are hard to come by for anyone. 250 says that when going against someone weaker, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Both restrain a person by turning him to his own experience as evidence of the other's position.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:616","type":"develops","note":"616 says effort makes fortune and lack of effort brings want in (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்); Parimelazhagar applies it to kings. 1063 calls it hardness (வன்மை) to think poverty's affliction can be ended by begging, and Parimelazhagar explains that the proper path, effort, is being set aside. 1063 applies 616's rule to the poor man and names begging as the wrong substitute for effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:1068","type":"develops","note":"1063 calls hard the thought that poverty can be ended by begging (இரந்து தீர்வாம்). 1068 gives a picture of why: begging is a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits when it strikes the rock of concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்). Manakkudavar says 1068 states the cause of poverty not ending, which makes it the reason behind 1063's claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"218 says those who see their duty do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 1064 says worth does not consent to asking even at a time without means (இடமில்லாக் காலும்). The same test, what a person does when resources are gone, is applied to giving in 218 and to asking in 1064: the good neither stop giving nor start begging.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it is called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1066 says that even if one asks for water for a cow (ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும்), nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both take the cow as a case of asking for the sake of virtue. Both verses deny that a good purpose removes what is wrong with asking or receiving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"656 says that even if one must see one's own mother hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 1066 says that even asking water for a cow is degrading to the tongue (நாவிற்கு இளிவந்தது). Each picks the most sympathetic motive available, a hungry mother or a thirsty cow, and holds that it does not excuse the act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:1053","type":"tension","note":"1053 says that even asking, standing before those of unconcealing heart who know their duty, has a certain beauty (இரப்பும் ஓர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து). 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even for a cow's water. They conflict if 1066 is read as covering every act of asking; on a narrower reading 1053 speaks only of asking from the right giver, which 1066 does not mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1067","b":"k:1077","type":"develops","note":"1067 asks beggars not to beg of those who conceal (கரப்பார்). 1077 describes such people from the other side: the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) for anyone but those whose fist would break their jaw. 1077 shows what asking a concealer comes to, and why 1067 pleads against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:306","type":"same_image","note":"306 says anger burns even the safe raft called one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that breaks on concealment. The same word for safety (ஏமம், ஏமாப்பு) marks the vessel in both: one's people are the safe one in 306, and asking is named the unsafe one in 1068.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of concealment; the commentators supply poverty as the sea it was meant to cross. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. Both use the figure of a sea that must be crossed and a vessel that fails, 1068 as a teaching about begging and 1164 as a lover's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:224","type":"agrees","note":"224 says being asked is unpleasant (இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1069 says the heart melts at the thought of asking. Taken together they say asking is painful on both sides, for the one asked in 224 and for the one who asks or contemplates it in 1069, and that only the gift ends the pain.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"1057 says that on seeing those who give without scorn, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளம் உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). 1069 says that at the thought of asking the heart melts (உள்ளம் உருகும்), and at the thought of concealment even what remains is gone (உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்). Both follow the heart (உள்ளம்) through the act of asking; 1057 gives its state before a good giver and 1069 its state before a refusal. Parimelazhagar takes the heart in 1069 as the speaker's, while he reports others take it as the asker's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing' (இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை), and giving, are found in one of good family; Pope takes the words as the giver's excuse, while Parimelazhagar first takes them as the asker's plea. 1070 says the asker's life leaves when the word is spoken (சொல்லாட); Parimelazhagar takes this as the concealer saying 'no'. On Pope's reading of 223 both verses are about the word of refusal, which the well-born never say and which 1070 says kills the hearer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even dying is sweet when giving is not possible (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). 1070 says the asker's life goes at the word of refusal (சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்) and asks where the concealer's life hides (யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ). 230 gives the giver's view that life without giving is not worth keeping; 1070 puts that standard to the one who can give and refuses, and wonders that his life stays.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:600","type":"parallel_domain","note":"600 says those without inner drive are trees, and their being human in form is the only difference (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). 1071 says the base resemble people exactly (மக்களே போல்வர்). Both treat the human shape as something that can be present without what makes a person, lacking drive in 600 and lacking character in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:201","type":"develops","note":"201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear, and the excellent do fear, the intoxication called evil deed (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). 1072 says the base are more fortunate than those who know what is good, because they have no distress in the heart (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). 1072 puts 201's fact in mock praise: Parimelazhagar says the verse scorns by implication, and that the point is their not fearing blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"839 says the friendship of fools is very sweet (பெரிதினிது), because parting from it gives no pain. 1072 says the base are fortunate (திருவுடையர்), because their hearts have no distress. Both verses praise a bad thing for the absence of a pain that only something of value would cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1072","type":"agrees","note":"1072 calls the base fortunate (திருவுடையர்) for having no distress of heart, and 1073 calls them like the gods (தேவர் அனையர்) for doing as they please. Both give praise that is meant as blame; Parimelazhagar says so of each. The two together describe one condition, no inner check in 1072 and no outer one in 1073.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"1073 says the base are like the gods because they too act as they please (மேவன செய்தொழுகலான்). 1075 says that where the base show proper conduct (ஆசாரம்) it is fear, and beyond that a little if there is something they desire. 1075 marks the limit on 1073's freedom: their doing as they please stops only at fear or gain; Parimelazhagar says doing what they like is their nature and conduct is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1074","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). 1074 says the base man, on seeing those worse than himself, outdoes them and swaggers (மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும்); this follows Parimelazhagar's reading of அகப்பட்டி as those of looser conduct, while Manakkudavar reads the word quite differently. On Parimelazhagar's reading 1074 shows where the self-admiration of 978 finds its material: in comparison with someone lower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1016","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1016 says the high do not care for the wide world unless they have shame as their fence (நாண்வேலி). 1075 says the conduct of the base is fear (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Each verse names what keeps a kind of person within bounds: shame from inside for the high, fear from outside for the base.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1075 says the base behave properly out of fear. 1078 says the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 applies the rule of 1075 to giving: what fear does for their conduct, force does for their generosity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"221 says giving to those who have nothing is what giving is (வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகை), and all other giving has the nature of a loan to be returned. 1077 says the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) except for those whose bent fist would break their jaw; Parimelazhagar sums up that they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them. The base man's giving in 1077 is the reverse of 221's definition: it goes only to the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1077 says the base give only to those who would break their jaw. 1078 states the same with a contrast and a figure: the worthy are of use at a word (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர்), and the base, like sugar-cane, when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும்). Parimelazhagar treats the two verses as a pair on how the base give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:216","type":"parallel_domain","note":"216 says wealth with a person of நயன் is like a fruit-bearing tree ripening in the middle of the town (பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்று). 1078 says the base are like sugar-cane (கரும்புபோல்), of use only when crushed. Both use a plant's yield (பயன்) as the figure for how a person's wealth reaches others: the tree offers its fruit in the open, and the cane gives nothing until it is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:223","type":"develops","note":"1078 says the worthy are of use as soon as they are told (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர்). 223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, belong to one of good family; Parimelazhagar records a second reading on which the giver gives before the asker has to say it. On that reading 223 goes one step beyond 1078: the worthy of 1078 give at a word, and the well-born of 223 give before the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:918","type":"same_image","note":"In 1081 the man cannot decide whether she is an அணங்கு, a choice peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women (மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு) is called an அணங்கு for those without discerning sense. The same word names a spirit that overpowers a man in both books: here it is a lover's wonder, there a warning, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in 918 as a divine woman who takes life through desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1082","type":"develops","note":"1081 only asks whether she is an அணங்கு. In 1082, once she returns his look (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்), he says it is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) who has also brought an army. The doubt of the first verse becomes a felt assault in the second, and Parimelazhagar reads 1082 as spoken after he has settled that she is human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"In 1081 his heart reels (மாலும் நெஞ்சு) because it cannot place her among goddess, peahen and woman. In 1112 he scolds the same heart for being confused at flowers (மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே), as if her eyes were like what many can see. The confusion moves from what she is to what could be compared with her, and by 1112 he treats it as the heart's error.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:775","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 775 a warrior whose glaring eye (விழித்த கண்) blinks when a spear is thrown counts that as running away. In 1082 the man looks, and her look answering his (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்) is like an army brought against him. Both treat meeting another's gaze as combat, one on the battlefield and one at first sight in love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1082 says her returning look is like an attacking spirit come with an army (தாக்கணங்கு தானைக்கொண் டன்னது): the look only wounds. 1091 finds two looks in her eyes (இருநோக்கு), one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (நோய்நோக்கு ... அந்நோய் மருந்து). The next chapter adds the healing side that 1082 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1113","type":"same_image","note":"1082 makes her answering glance an armed host (தானை). 1113, listing her beauties, calls her kohl-lined eyes spears (வேலுண்கண்). The eye as weapon is a fixed figure of the man's praise, used first as his alarm and later as a plain item of description.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"765 defines an army as the force that can stand together even if Death comes raging at it (கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும்). In 1083 the man says he now knows what is called Death (கூற்று): it has womanly grace and large warring eyes (பேரமர்க் கட்டு). Both set கூற்று in a battle; the Book of Wealth imagines standing against it, and the lover says he has met it in her eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:1226","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1083: 'I did not know before (பண்டறியேன்) what Death is; now I know'. 1226: the woman did not know (அறிந்ததிலேன்), while her husband had not left, that evening brings sickness. In both a lover says love has taught what was only a word before, the man at first sight and the woman in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:279","type":"parallel_domain","note":"279: the arrow is straight but cruel, the lute bent but sweet, so judge by what a thing does (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்). 1084 sets a girl of womanly gentleness (பெண்டகைப் பேதை) against eyes that look as if they eat lives; Parimelazhagar takes அமர்த்தன as 'were at odds' with her nature. Both verses turn on a form that does not match its effect, one as a rule for judging ascetics and one as a lover's surprise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death that eats life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 1084 says the eyes of this womanly, artless girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them (கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றம்). The lover gives her eyes the standing epithet of Death, which the previous verse (1083) has just named.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:1221","type":"same_image","note":"1084: her eyes have a look that eats the life of those who see them (உயிருண்ணும்). 1221: the woman tells evening it is not evening but a time that eats the life of those once wed (மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும் வேலை). The same phrase marks what kills each lover: for the man her eyes at first sight, for the woman the evening hour in his absence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"In 1085 the doe (பிணை) is one of three things her look might be, beside Death. In 1089 her doe-like artless look (பிணையேர் மடநோக்கு) and her modesty are ornament enough, so added jewels are pointless. The same doe glance is first one part of a threat and then her natural adornment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1085 asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe, and says it has all three (இம்மூன்றும் உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar comments that it gives pleasure and pain together. 1091 states that plainly as two looks, one the sickness and one its cure. The puzzled question of 1085 is resolved into a doubleness he can name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1087","type":"develops","note":"1086 wishes her brows would screen her eyes (மறைப்பின்), for then the eyes would not cause him trembling pain. 1087 says the cloth over her breasts is like the face-cloth over a rutting elephant's eyes (கட்படாம்). Both are about a covering over something that harms him: one he wishes for and one he sees, and Parimelazhagar says of the second that the cloth keeps them from killing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1086 the man says her eyes cause him trembling pain (நடுங்கஞர்) and wishes them screened. In 1171 the woman asks why her own eyes weep, since they showed him to her and so gave her this unending sickness (தண்டாநோய் தாம்காட்ட). Each lover names eyes as the cause of the pain; for him it is hers, for her it is her own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1087","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"1087: the cloth over her firm breasts is the face-cloth on a rutting bull elephant (கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம்). 24: the renouncer guards the five senses with the goad of firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்), a goad being the elephant-driver's tool. Both draw on the dangerous elephant held in check; the ascetic chapter makes the senses the elephant, and the lover makes her covered breasts the elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"1088 reports the first loss: his battlefield strength breaks at the sight of her forehead. 1134 says the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). The later verse generalises what 1088 exclaims, with manliness now lost along with shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1088: his might (பீடு), feared even by enemies in battle, has broken (உடைந்ததே) before her bright forehead alone. 1258: the woman says the soft words of that deceiving thief are the weapon that breaks her womanly firmness (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Each lover uses the language of war and breaking for the loss of the quality they prized in themselves, his strength and her restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:95","type":"parallel_domain","note":"95: humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and nothing else is (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1089: what use are foreign ornaments (ஏதில தந்து அணி) to one who has a doe's artless look and modesty? Both deny that added adornment counts beside an inborn quality, 95 as a rule of conduct and 1089 as a lover's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"1090 uses liquor as the lesser pleasure that love outdoes. 1288 uses it as an exact likeness: 'your chest is to us as liquor is to those who are drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shaming harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா)'. The later verse grants what 1090 leaves unsaid, that the pull likened to drink goes on despite disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1102","b":"k:948","type":"qualifies","note":"948 tells the healer to examine the disease, then its root (நோய்முதல்), then the means that subdue it (அது தணிக்கும் வாய்), as three separate things. 1102 first grants the ordinary rule, that the medicine is other than the disease, and then says love is the exception, where cause and remedy are the same person. The lover's verse marks love's sickness as a case the method of 948 does not cover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1103","b":"k:213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213 says that in the world of the gods or here (புத்தேளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்) it is hard to find another good like ஒப்புரவு, doing one's part for others. 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed one's world is as sweet as sleeping on the beloved's shoulders. Both measure an earthly thing against heaven and prefer it; in the Book of Virtue it is a duty, and here it is a lover's own claim about his pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:265","type":"parallel_domain","note":"265: austerity is practised here because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). 1105: her shoulders are like each desired thing at the moment of desiring it (வேட்ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே). Both describe desire met exactly as it arises, promised to the ascetic through தவம் and claimed by the lover in her embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says the objects of all five senses are found in her alone. 1105 says her shoulders are like whatever one craves, at the time one craves it. The first claim is about range, that she holds every kind of pleasure; the second adds timing, that she answers each wish as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"64: gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children is far sweeter than ambrosia (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதே). 1106: her shoulders are made of ambrosia (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன) because their touch revives his life. Parent and lover both reach for அமிழ்து to measure the nearness of someone of their own; the parent ranks the child's gruel above it and the lover equates her with it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:1124","type":"agrees","note":"1106: his life sprouts afresh whenever he touches her. 1124: she is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she leaves (வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ... சாதல் அதற்கன்னள்). Both tie his உயிர் to her presence; 1124 states the other half, which 1106's 'each time' (உறுதோறு) implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:229","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229: eating alone to fill one's own store (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging. 1107: eating one's own after sharing it, in one's own house, is the model of sweetness, and the embrace is like it. Read together they give both halves of one judgement on eating: bitter when hoarded, sweet when shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"1108 praises the embrace with no gap. In 1187 the woman says she lay embracing, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once pallor was on her thick enough to scoop up. The woman's verse gives a reason from her side why the unbroken embrace is the sweet one: the least separation brings the pallor of parting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1196","type":"agrees","note":"1108 specifies 'two who desire' (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே): the close embrace is sweet to both. 1196: love on one side only is painful, and like a carrying pole it is sweet on both ends (இருதலையானும் இனிது). Both make sweetness depend on the love being mutual, 1108 in fulfilment and 1196 from the woman who lacks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1109 counts sulking (ஊடல்) among the fruits of love. 1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run a little long is like a little too much salt (சிறிது மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்). The later verse sets a limit on the first item of 1109's list: it is a gain only in measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1326","type":"develops","note":"1109 names quarrel, making up and union together as rewards without ranking them. 1326 ranks them: as digesting is sweeter than eating, sulking is sweeter than union (புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது). The later verse takes one item from 1109's list and gives it first place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as a gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம்), life loves itself more with each suffering (உழத்தொறூஉம்). 1110: each closeness shows love to be more than he knew, as each learning shows ignorance. Both describe an attachment that grows through its own repetition; the gambling chapter gives it as a warning and the lover as a delight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:1145","type":"agrees","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்று), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1110: love is like finding one's ignorance each time one learns, each time he is close to her. Both say love grows by repetition and does not wear out; 1110 takes its likeness from knowledge and 1145 from drink, and they differ in what is repeated, union or exposure by gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1111","b":"k:1289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham (நின்னினும் மென்னீரள்); 1289 says love itself is more delicate than a flower (மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம்) and few catch its right moment. The same comparative, softer than a flower, is applied first to the beloved's body and later to the experience of love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:906","type":"same_image","note":"1113 praises her as bamboo-shouldered (வேய்த்தோளவட்கு). 906, in the chapter on men ruled by their wives, says those who fear the wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no standing even if they live like the gods. The same stock epithet of beauty is admired in the Book of Love and named in the Book of Wealth as the thing an unmanned husband fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"1113 lists her features as metaphors: body a tender shoot (முறி), teeth pearls, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears (வேலுண்கண்). 1084 had already noticed the mismatch this list contains: her eyes, with a look that eats the life of those who see them, are at war (அமர்த்தன) with her gentle womanly nature. Read together, the spear in the inventory of soft things is the same contradiction 1084 names outright.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 warns that even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if the load is piled too high (அச்சிறும்). 1115 says she has worn anicham flowers without removing the stalks (கால்களையாள்), so no good drums will sound for her waist; Parimelazhagar explains that the waist will break under the weight and get the funeral drum. Both make the lightest possible load the breaking one, as counsel on overreach in 475 and as playful hyperbole about her slenderness in 1115.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham. 1115 pushes the claim to a comic limit: the unremoved stalks of that same flower (அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள்) are too heavy for her waist. The flower that was the standard of softness becomes a burden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1081","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1081 the man, at first sight, cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). In 1116 the confusion is moved to the sky: the stars cannot tell the moon from her face (அறியா) and wander from their places (பதியின் கலங்கிய). The lover's own bewilderment about what she is becomes, in the praise chapter, a bewilderment he attributes to the stars.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1117","type":"qualifies","note":"1116 says the stars cannot distinguish the moon from her face. 1117 immediately denies that the two are alike: the moon fills out from loss and has a spot, and her face has none (மறுவுண்டோ). Parimelazhagar reads 1117 as mocking the stars for missing an obvious difference, so the second verse withdraws the equality the first seemed to grant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"782 uses the moon's phases for friendship: with the wise it fills like the crescent, with fools it shrinks like the moon after full. 1117 mentions the same cycle, a moon that shines only after filling its lost parts (அறுவாய் நிறைந்த), as a defect that her face does not share. The waxing that is a hopeful figure in 782 counts in 1117 as evidence of the moon's inconstancy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1118","b":"k:1210","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses address the moon with வாழி மதி. In 1118 the man tells it that it could have his love if it could shine like her face; Parimelazhagar hears the blessing as mockery. In 1210 the woman, left behind, asks it not to set so that her eyes may find the man who has gone without leaving her heart. The moon is a rival to be dismissed in the time of union and a helper to be begged in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1119 the man tells the moon that to resemble her face it must not appear for many to see (பலர்காணத் தோன்றல்). In 1311 the woman refuses the man's chest because all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்). Each lover applies the same standard from the other side: what is shared with every onlooker is no longer one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1120","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 opens the chapter by ranking her above the anicham in softness. 1120 closes it by making the anicham and swan's down hurt her feet like the nerunji fruit, a thorn (மாதர் அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்). The flower moves from being less soft than she is to being painful to her. Parimelazhagar sets the verse in a refusal to take her on a desert elopement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:64","type":"parallel_domain","note":"64 says the gruel one's children have stirred with their small hands is far sweeter than amrita (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிது). 1121 says the moisture at her white teeth is like milk mixed with honey (பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்று). In both, affection makes something bodily and ordinary taste better than the finest food, for a parent in one verse and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:1258","type":"same_image","note":"1121 calls her பணிமொழி, the woman of soft, low speech, in a verse about the sweetness of her kiss. In 1258 the woman uses the same word for his speech: the soft words (பணிமொழி) of that thief of many deceits are the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve. The quality he savours in her is the one she names in him as a weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1123","b":"k:1126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1123 the man orders the image in the pupil of his eye (கருமணியிற் பாவாய்) to leave, since otherwise there is no room for the woman he loves. In 1126 the woman says her lover never leaves her eyes (கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார்) and is too subtle to be hurt when she blinks. Both lovers place the other inside the eye; he has to make room for her, and she has him there already.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 says their bond is like that of body and life. 1124 draws the consequence: together she is to his life what living is (வாழ்தல்), and when she leaves she is what dying is (சாதல்). The comparison in 1122 becomes a statement of what separation will mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1151","type":"agrees","note":"In 1124 the man says her leaving is death to his life (சாதல் அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து). In 1151 the reply to his announced journey is that news of his quick return should be told to those who will be alive then (வாழ்வார்க் குரை); Parimelazhagar gives the line to her friend, Manakkudavar to the woman. Each side says that separation is not survivable, he at a brief parting after a meeting and her side when he leaves on a long journey.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1129","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1126 she says her lover does not leave her eyes and is not hurt even if she blinks (இமைப்பின் பருவரார்), because he is so subtle. In 1129 she will not blink, knowing he would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). The same conceit is used both ways within one chapter: blinking is harmless in the first verse and a risk in the second, which shows these are playful defences of him and not one consistent picture.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1218","type":"agrees","note":"1126 says he never leaves her eyes though the town thinks him gone. 1218 says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder and when she wakes he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து). Parimelazhagar frames both as her defence of him against the charge of absence: she answers by locating him somewhere inside herself at every moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1205","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1128 his presence in her heart is something she protects, to the point of avoiding hot food. In 1205 the same presence is a complaint: he keeps her out of his own heart and yet is not ashamed to come into hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). The later verse adds what 1128 leaves out, the question whether the dwelling is mutual.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1128 says that since her lover is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தார் காதலவராக) she fears eating anything hot, knowing it would burn him. 1249 uses almost the same opening, உள்ளத்தார் காதலவராக, to ask her heart whom it goes out looking for when he is within it. The playful premise of 1128, said to defend him, is used in 1249 to rebuke her own restless heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1130","type":"develops","note":"1129 and 1130 end with the same line, ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர். 1129 gives the town's ground for blame, her sleeplessness, which she explains as refusing to blink. 1130 states her answer directly: he lives gladly in her heart always (உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும்), while the town says he lives apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1220","type":"agrees","note":"In 1129 she does not blink for fear of losing sight of him, and for this the town calls him a stranger without love (ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்). In 1220 the people of the town say he has left her in waking life, and she asks whether they do not see him in dreams (கனவினால் காணார்கொல்). In both she sets the town's outward evidence against her inner experience of his presence, first in the eye and then in the dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1130","b":"k:1204","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1130 she is sure that he lives in her heart, and gladly (உவந்துறைவர்), against the town's talk. In 1204 she affirms the same thing, that he is always in her heart (எந்நெஞ்சத்து ஓஒ உளரே அவர்), and then asks whether she too has a place in his (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). The confidence she shows the town in 1130 does not answer the doubt she admits in 1204: his presence in her heart says nothing about her place in his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 calls the palmyra horse the only safeguard for those who suffer in love. 1137 praises womanhood that suffers a sea of desire (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and still does not mount it (மடலேறா). The same verb உழந்து marks the same suffering, so 1137 limits 1131: the remedy is one a woman forgoes, and forgoing it is counted as worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1164","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 has the man say that for those worn down by love there is no strength that is a safeguard (ஏமம்) except the palmyra horse (மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி). In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it does not (ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்). The one ஏமம் he names is a man's recourse; her verse shows a sufferer for whom even that is missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 the woman tells her heart to give up either desire or shame, since she cannot bear the two together (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண்விடு). 1132 is that choice made: the man keeps desire and puts shame aside (நாணினை நீக்கி) to ride the palmyra horse. Read together, 1247 states the dilemma and 1132 shows one way out of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1026","type":"same_image","note":"1026 defines நல்லாண்மை as making oneself fit to manage the house one was born into (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1133 uses the same word for what the lover had before (நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and has now exchanged for the palmyra horse. Set beside 1026's definition, the lover's loss is of the capacity that makes a man of use to his family, not only of pride.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088 the man, on first seeing her, says his might (பீடு), which enemies dreaded in battle, has broken before her bright forehead. In 1133 he says the shame and good manliness he once had are replaced by the palmyra horse. The later verse carries the first collapse through to its end: what broke at first sight is now given up publicly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1254","type":"agrees","note":"In 1133 the man says he once had shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and today has the palmyra horse. In 1254 the woman says she used to say she had restraint (நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன்), yet her desire passes concealment and comes out in public. Each speaker sets a former self-possession against its loss to love; his loss is நாண் and ஆண்மை, hers is நிறை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger burns the safe raft that is one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 1134 says the flood of love sweeps off the raft that is shame and good manliness (என்னும் புணை). In both a passion destroys the thing figured as the raft that keeps a person afloat: anger in the chapter on not being angry, desire here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1251","type":"agrees","note":"1134 has the flood of love carry away the raft called shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). 1251 has the axe of desire break the door called restraint, bolted with the bar of shame (நிறையென்னும் நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). Both make shame a built defence that love's force destroys, one in the man's voice and one in the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1135","b":"k:1183","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man says the girl with small bangles gave him (தந்தாள்) the palmyra horse and the grief that evening brings. In 1183 the woman says he took her grace and shame and gave in return (கைம்மாறா ... தந்து) sickness and pallor. Each lover describes the affliction as a gift from the other, and in both verses shame is part of what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1136","b":"k:1252","type":"agrees","note":"In 1136 the man's eyes will not close (படல் ஒல்லா) for the girl, so even at midnight (யாமத்தும்) he thinks of riding the palmyra horse. In 1252 the woman says desire, having no pity, rules her heart at its work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both use midnight, when all else rests, as the proof that love allows no rest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1138","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 asks whether there is a bolt that can shut love in (அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ்), since the small tears of the loving make it known. 1138 says காமம் breaks through secrecy and appears in public. The Book of Virtue says of அன்பு what the Book of Love says of காமம்: neither can be kept hidden, and each publishes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1139","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"In 1139 the woman's love, thinking 'not everyone knows' (அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றே), goes reeling through the street (மறுகின் மறுகும்). In 1180 she says the town easily learns the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the secret is given away by the lover herself and not by the town: by her love going about in 1139, by her eyes in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:877","type":"agrees","note":"877 advises not telling one's pain to those who do not know it (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). 1140 shows the reason from inside: those who have not suffered what the speaker suffered (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா) laugh at her in plain sight. The statecraft maxim and the lover's complaint agree that pain gets no hearing from those who have not felt it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:1219","type":"agrees","note":"In 1140 the woman says the unknowing laugh where she can see, because they have not undergone what she has (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு). In 1219 she says those who blame him for not being gracious in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Both explain the town's judgement by its lack of the lover's experience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says that because gossip has risen the precious life holds on (அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்). 1213 says the woman's life exists because she sees in dreams the one who gives nothing in waking life (காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). In each a lover's life is kept going by something that only stands in for the beloved: the town's talk in one, a dream in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1263","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says life stays because the gossip has risen. 1263 says the woman still exists by longing for his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). Both verses name the one thing that keeps life from leaving; in 1141 it comes from outside, from the town, and in 1263 from her own hope.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1142","b":"k:1150","type":"agrees","note":"In 1142 the man says the town, not knowing how hard the flower-eyed woman is to reach (அருமை அறியாது), has given him gossip (அலரெமக்கு ஈந்தது). In 1150 the speaker says the town is raising the very talk 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை எடுக்கும் இவ்வூர்). Both treat the town's gossip as an unintended service; Parimelazhagar reads ஈந்தது as help given, and reads 1150 as the friend counting on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1143","b":"k:1213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1143 the man says the gossip the town knows is like having obtained what he has not obtained (பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து). In 1213 the woman sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking life, and lives by it. Both take a substitute for union as if it were union: public talk for him, a dream for her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1144","b":"k:1147","type":"agrees","note":"In 1144 the man says his love thrives through gossip (கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம்) and without it would lose its nature and go slack. In 1147 the woman says the town's gossip is manure and her mother's words water to 'this sickness' (நீளும் இந்நோய்). Both say gossip feeds love; he calls what grows காமம் and welcomes it, she calls it நோய்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:1281","type":"qualifies","note":"1145 takes drinking as the model for love: each exposure makes love sweeter as each bout makes drink wanted. 1281 says being intoxicated at a thought and glad at a sight belong to love and not to liquor (கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). So the likeness in 1145 has a limit the book itself states: liquor works only when drunk, love without contact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1146","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"In 1146 the woman saw him one day only, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon (திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண்டற்று), which Parimelazhagar glosses as spreading everywhere. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows high, like the spot on the moon in the sky (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). Both use the moon as the thing whose blemish everyone sees: a brief event or a small fault becomes public because of where it sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1147 makes the sickness of love a crop, manured by gossip and watered by a mother's words, that grows long (நீளும் இந்நோய்). 1227 makes the same இந்நோய் a flower that buds in the morning and opens in the evening. In both the woman describes her lovesickness as a plant with its own growth, fed in 1147 and timed in 1227.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1144","type":"develops","note":"1144 says love thrives by gossip and would droop without it. 1148 puts the same point as a rebuttal of those who expect gossip to end the affair: it is ghee on fire. 1148 gives the image and the opposing view that 1144's plain statement lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1260","type":"same_image","note":"1148 says that to think of putting out love with gossip is like thinking to put out fire with ghee (நெய்யால் எரி நுதுப்பேம்). 1260 asks whether those whose hearts are like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன) can say they will hold out in a sulk. Both use fat meeting fire for love: in 1148 the fat feeds the fire, in 1260 the heart is the fat that melts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1149","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"In 1149 the woman asks whether she can be ashamed at gossip (அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ) now that he has left her. In 1297 she says she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்), caught in a heart that cannot forget him. In both her shame gives way, in 1149 because his leaving has made it pointless and in 1297 because her own heart overrides it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1150","b":"k:1143","type":"agrees","note":"1150 says the town is raising the gossip 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை), and the lover will grant what is asked if he wishes. In 1143 the man asks whether the gossip the town knows is not his to have, since it is like gaining what he has not gained. Both sides welcome the talk: for him it stands in for union, and in 1150, on Parimelazhagar's reading, it is the ground for asking him to take her away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:1253","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same words, மறைப்பேன்மன் ('I would hide it'), and both say the attempt fails: in 1161 the pain rises like spring water under baling, in 1253 desire shows itself without her intending it, like a sneeze (குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல்). Read together, one figure says concealment makes it grow and the other that it escapes her will altogether.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1162","b":"k:1200","type":"develops","note":"1162: she cannot hide the sickness, and telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). 1200: she tells her heart that it means to tell its pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) and might as well fill up the sea. Parimelazhagar reads both as about sending him word; 1200 adds futility to the shame that 1162 gives as the obstacle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1017","type":"develops","note":"1017: those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). 1163 shows that ranking from inside: she drops neither love nor shame, and it is her life, the pole (உயிர் காவா), that takes the strain; Parimelazhagar adds that the life will snap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1164","b":"k:10","type":"parallel_domain","note":"10: the great sea of birth is swum by those who reach the Lord's feet and not by others (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர்); Parimelazhagar supplies the feet as the raft. 1164: the sea of love exists and the raft to swim it does not (அது நீந்தும் ஏமப்புணை). The same structure of a sea crossed only with a support appears in the first chapter and in a separated woman's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1165","b":"k:805","type":"qualifies","note":"1165: she asks what those who bring grief in friendship would do as enemies; Parimelazhagar says this is aimed, in a sulk, at the friend who has not sent a message. 805 advises that when friends do something hurtful (நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்) it should be understood as folly or as great intimacy. 805 offers the charitable reading that her question refuses.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:629","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629: one who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not come to pain within pain (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 1166: the pleasure of love is a sea and its pain when it afflicts is larger still (துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது). The woman's experience fits the link between relishing pleasure and suffering pain that 629 states as a rule in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:1137","type":"same_image","note":"1137 speaks of suffering a love like the sea (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and praises womanhood that still does not mount the palmyra horse. 1166 uses the sea twice over: love's pleasure is a sea, and when it afflicts (அடுங்கால்) the pain is greater than that. The sea is the book's measure for love, and 1166 says the measure is exceeded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1136","type":"agrees","note":"Both lovers are awake at midnight, with the same word யாமத்தும். The man in 1136 says his eyes will not close and even at midnight he thinks of mounting the palmyra horse; the woman in 1167 says that even at midnight she alone is there (யானே உளேன்), swimming without sight of shore. Sleeplessness in the dead of night is given to both sides.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1164","type":"develops","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and there is no raft to swim it. 1167 continues the figure: she has swum the fierce flood (காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்தி) and sees no shore (கரை காணேன்), and at midnight there is only herself. The second verse turns the missing raft into an actual, endless swim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1168","b":"k:1222","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1168: the night has put all living beings to sleep and, she says with pity (அளித்து), has no companion but her. 1222: she tells the evening it looks sad-eyed (புன்கண்ணை) and asks whether its partner is as hard as hers. In both she treats a time of day as a fellow sufferer and puts her own state onto it; Parimelazhagar says as much of 1222.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase கொடியார் கொடுமை, 'the cruelty of the cruel one', for the absent man. In 1235 her shoulders, their old beauty faded, declare it; in 1169 she uses it as the measure the long nights exceed (கொடுமையின் தாம் கொடிய). 1169 moves the charge of cruelty from the man to the time itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1269","type":"agrees","note":"1169: the nights that pass so long these days (நெடிய கழியும் இரா) are crueller than the cruel one's cruelty. 1269: for those who pine with a set day of return in mind, one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்). Both say that time stretches for the one who waits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"In 1167 she herself swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore; in 1170 it is her eyes that swim in flood water (வெள்ளநீர் நீந்தல), which is her tears. The chapter's water figure for love ends as literal water in the eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1244","type":"develops","note":"1170: if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in a flood of tears. 1244 turns the wish into a request: heart, take the eyes along when you go (கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி), for they consume her in wanting to see him. Both rest on the idea that the heart can travel to him and the eyes cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1091","type":"same_image","note":"1091 (the man): her kohl-lined eyes hold two looks, one that gives the sickness and one that is its cure (நோய்நோக்கு ... மருந்து). 1171 (the woman): her own eyes showed him to her and so she came to know an unabating sickness (தண்டாநோய்), and she asks why they weep. Eyes are the source of love's sickness in both, the other's eyes for the man and her own for the woman, and only his verse has a cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1244","type":"agrees","note":"1171: she asks why her eyes weep, since they showed him to her (தாம் காட்ட யாம் கண்டது). 1244: the eyes consume her in their wish to see him (அவர்க்காணல் உற்று). In both she speaks of her eyes as agents apart from herself, with wants of their own that she pays for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:535","type":"parallel_domain","note":"535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand will grieve afterwards over his own error (முன்னுறக் காவாது ... பின்னூறு இரங்கிவிடும்). 1172: the eyes that looked without foresight now toil in misery (பைதல் உழப்பது). Parimelazhagar's comment on 1172 uses the same terms, that those who do not guard against what is coming can only bear it when it arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"792: the friendship of one who does not take it after examining again and again ends in grief that kills him (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை). 1172, on Parimelazhagar's reading: the eyes looked that day without examining and understanding what would follow (தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய) and now suffer. The rule about forming ties without scrutiny is applied to a look. Manakkudavar reads the first phrase differently, as having looked with knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1173: the eyes looked hastily of themselves (கதுமெனத் தாம் நோக்கி) and now weep of themselves, which is laughable. 435: the life of one who does not guard before a fault comes is destroyed like straw before fire (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்). Parimelazhagar explains the laughter in 1173 with that same phrase: it is laughable to those who guard before it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:1140","type":"same_image","note":"1140: the ignorant laugh where she can see it (நகுப அறிவில்லார்), because they have not suffered what she has. 1173: she finds her own eyes' plight fit to be laughed at (நகத்தக்கது உடைத்து). Laughter at a lover's suffering comes from outsiders in one verse and from the sufferer herself in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1174","b":"k:320","type":"parallel_domain","note":"320: all pain falls on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 1174: the eyes that fixed an inescapable sickness in her (உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து) have themselves dried up and cannot weep. Parimelazhagar reads 1174 in these terms: having done her harm first, harm came to them of itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:1166","type":"develops","note":"1166 says love's pain, when it afflicts, is greater than the sea (அதனிற் பெரிது). 1175 repeats the measure as an epithet, a love-sickness the sea cannot hold (கடலாற்றாக் காமநோய்), and goes on to assign blame for it to her eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1176","b":"k:1173","type":"develops","note":"1173 finds it laughable that the eyes looked of themselves and weep of themselves. 1176 goes a step further: it is sweet (ஓஒ இனிதே) that the eyes which gave her this sickness have fallen into it too (தாஅம் இதற்பட்டது). The sequence moves from mockery of her eyes to satisfaction at their suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1177","b":"k:1174","type":"develops","note":"1174 reports that the eyes have dried up and cannot weep (நீருலந்த). 1177 wishes exactly that on them: may the eyes that longed and melted to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone (உள்நீர் அறுக). What one verse states as their condition the other pronounces as a curse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1179","b":"k:1295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1179: when he does not come the eyes do not sleep, and when he comes they do not sleep (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின் துஞ்சா). 1295: the heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting (பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்). The same two-sided bind is said of the eyes and of the heart, and Parimelazhagar explains 1179 by the fear of parting that 1295 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1271","type":"agrees","note":"1180 (the woman): the town can easily learn the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums. 1271 (the man): though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control (கரப்பினும் கையிகந்து) and have something to tell. Both lovers say her eyes disclose what she means to conceal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1160 speaks, with irony, of the many who agree to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards (அரிதாற்றி ... பிரிவாற்றிப் பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்); Parimelazhagar glosses அரிது ஆற்றி as consenting to the parting. 1181 is the woman who did consent (நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன்) and now cannot bear the result. Read together, 1181 shows the cost of the consent that 1160 doubted anyone could really give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1162","type":"develops","note":"In 1162 she cannot hide the sickness, and telling the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1181 the question widens to யார்க்கு உரைக்கோ, to whom can I tell it at all, because she herself agreed to his going; Parimelazhagar adds that telling a self-made sorrow would itself be blame. The pair traces how the possible hearers for her complaint run out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1182","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"1182 has the pallor ride over her body with the pride of saying 'he gave me' (அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால்). 1183 uses the same verb of giving for an exchange: he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor (நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து). The pallor that boasts of being his gift in one verse is entered as his payment in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says obligation wants no return, கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு, and points to the rain, which the world cannot repay. 1183 uses the same word bitterly: as கைம்மாறு for the grace and modesty he took, he gave sickness and pallor. The word that names the return a good giver does not ask for is here the name of a bad bargain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1125","type":"same_image","note":"1125 and 1184 open with the same words, உள்ளுவன் மன்யான். The man in 1125 says he would remember her qualities if he ever forgot them, and he does not know forgetting; the woman in 1184 says she thinks of him and yet turns pale. The same claim of constant thought is his proof of love and her complaint that such thought gives no protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1250","type":"develops","note":"In 1184 she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits (உள்ளுவன் ... உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and calls it deceit (கள்ளம்) that the pallor comes all the same. In 1250 she tells her heart that by keeping in it the man who left, she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்). 1250 states as cause and effect what 1184 finds baffling: the faithful thinking does not hold the pallor off.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1277","type":"develops","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the shore had parted from her (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1185 the pallor spreads while he is still in sight. Both make the body register the parting faster than the mind or the facts do; 1277 pushes it to before the event.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1278","type":"agrees","note":"1185 points there to the lover going and here to the pallor spreading (உவக்காண் ... இவக்காண்); Parimelazhagar takes it as an earlier occasion when he had gone only a short way. 1278 says he left only yesterday and her body has been pale seven days (நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார் ... எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து). One verse measures the pallor's speed in space and the other in time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 describes those who, with goods in view, watch for another's lapse (பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்), and denies them a loving nature. 1186 gives pallor the same posture: it watches for the weak moment (அற்றம் பார்க்கும்), which Parimelazhagar notes is a figurative use of 'watch'. The book's picture of the thief waiting for inattention is the picture the woman uses for what takes her colour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:1238","type":"agrees","note":"1186 is the woman's account: pallor watches for a gap in her husband's embrace (முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும்) as darkness watches for a lamp to fail. 1238 is the man's memory of the same thing: he loosened the arms that held her and her forehead turned pale (முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது). The two speakers confirm each other from either side of the embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1187","b":"k:1239","type":"agrees","note":"In 1187 she lay embracing him, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once the pallor was thick enough to scoop up. In 1239 the man recalls that a cool breeze cutting between them in the embrace (முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழ) was enough to pale her eyes. Both verses, from the two partners, set the threshold of separation at the smallest physical gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1190","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 complains that the talk fixes on her pallor and not on his leaving. 1190 says it is good to get the name of pallor, provided they do not spread talk of his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்); Parimelazhagar sets it as her reply when the friend has blamed him. The complaint of 1188 should therefore not be read as a wish to have him blamed: in 1190 she accepts the very imbalance she resents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1220","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 says people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1220 the townspeople do say he has left her in waking life (நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர்), and she answers that they must not see him in dreams. These are speeches at different moments, but together they show her objecting both when the town blames only her and when it blames him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1181","type":"develops","note":"1181 puts the consent on herself: நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன், I agreed to his absence. 1189 names him as the one who brought her to agree (நயப்பித்தார்) and says let her body pale as it will if he stands well; Parimelazhagar reads நன்னிலையர் as free of fault, while Manakkudavar reads the condition as his suffering too. On Parimelazhagar's reading the two verses share out the responsibility and she keeps the cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"In 1189, on Parimelazhagar's reading, she accepts the pallor so long as the man who persuaded her is held blameless. In 1236 her arms thin and her bangles slip, and what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). In both she ranks his good name above her own wasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1232","type":"qualifies","note":"1190 hopes that her pallor can be talked of without his நல்காமை being talked of. In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes themselves seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same word நல்காமை shows the limit of her wish: the pallor is itself the report against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"1190 says being named for her pallor is good if people do not broadcast his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்). 1236 says the hurt is in hearing him called cruel (கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து), more than in the slipping bangles. Both verses make the talk against him, and not her own disfigurement, the thing she cannot bear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1191","b":"k:1108","type":"agrees","note":"1108, spoken in union, says the close embrace is sweet to two who desire each other (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே). 1191, spoken in separation, says those loved by the ones they love (தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர்) have the stoneless fruit. Both place love's sweetness in the desire being mutual, using the same verb வீழ்; one says it from inside and the other from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1193","b":"k:598","type":"same_image","note":"598 says those without drive never reach the pride of saying 'we are generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 1193 says the pride of saying 'we shall live' (வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு) suits only those loved by the ones they love. The same construction makes a justified pride depend on a prior condition: inner drive in the one case, being loved in return in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1194","b":"k:1191","type":"develops","note":"1191 says those loved by the ones they love have 'obtained' (பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே) love's stoneless fruit; Parimelazhagar ties the word to earlier good deeds. 1194 gives the negative: even those who are esteemed are கெழீஇயிலர் if the one they love does not love them, which Parimelazhagar glosses as lacking good deeds and Manakkudavar as being without what they desire. The pair states the same condition from both sides, and shows the commentators reading it as fortune and not merit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1195","b":"k:1245","type":"qualifies","note":"1195 asks what the one she loves can do for her when he has no love for her (தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1245 asks her heart whether a man who does not respond when she draws near (உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்) can be dropped as one who hates her (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ). The second speech limits the first: finding no benefit in an unreturned love does not make her able to give it up.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:118","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 praises being level like the scale-beam and not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை) as the ornament of the worthy. 1196 applies the same test to love: borne on one end (ஒருதலையான்) it hurts, and on both it is sweet. Evenness between two sides is a mark of justice in the Book of Virtue and a condition of happiness here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1252","type":"develops","note":"1252 says the thing called desire has no eye, that is no pity (கண்ணின்று), since it works her heart even at midnight. 1197 asks whether Kaman, who stays and acts on one person only, does not see her pain and misery (காணான்கொல்). The complaint moves from the feeling to the god, and both use sight as the test of his mercy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1308","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1308 the man asks what use suffering is when there is no lover who knows 'this one is suffering' (அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). In 1197 the woman asks whether Kaman fails to see her suffering. Both speakers treat pain as needing a witness, and each finds the one who ought to notice absent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1198","type":"develops","note":"1198 calls hard those who live without receiving a sweet word from the beloved (இன்சொல் பெறாஅது). 1199 defines how little would count: though he gives no grace, any report of him is sweet to her ears (அவர்மாட்டு இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு). Parimelazhagar reads இசை as any word at all, even that he is not coming, and links the two verses on that basis; Kalaignar takes இசை as his praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"In 1213 her life continues because in dreams she sees the man who gives no grace in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை). In 1199 the man who gives no grace (நல்கார் எனினும்) still sweetens her ears by any word of him. In both she is sustained by a substitute for his presence, a dream or a report, while saying outright that he withholds himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1200","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and the raft to cross it does not (காமக் கடல் ... ஏமப் புணை இல்). 1200 tells the heart to fill up the sea (கடலைச் செறாஅஅய்) instead of telling its pain to the unloving; Pope takes this sea as her own sorrow, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the sea that torments her. On Pope's reading the two verses share the sea as the measure of her grief, with crossing it and filling it both out of reach.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses turn on the word உள்ளினும். In 1201 the man says remembering gives unending joy (தீராப் பெருமகிழ்); in 1207 the woman says that even while she remembers, her heart burns (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the separation burning her despite the memory. These are two speakers in two moods, so it is a limit on the first claim and not a contradiction in the book's teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1281","type":"agrees","note":"1201 (the man, for Parimelazhagar) says love is sweeter than toddy because it gives great joy that does not end even when only thought of (உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ்). 1281, spoken by the woman, makes the same comparison: elation at a thought and delight at a sight belong to love and not to toddy (கள்ளுக்கு இல் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). The pairing shows the same judgment given from both sides of the couple, built on the same verb உள்ளுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1209","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 says love is sweet in every way since no pain comes from thinking of the one you love (நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று இல், as the commentators read this edition's garbled line). In 1209 the woman's dear life is wasting because she thinks hard on his lack of tenderness (அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து). The pairing shows that the sweetness of 1202 depends on what is remembered: the shared days, not the present neglect. Manakkudavar in fact reads 1202 as conditional, 'if no pain comes'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1296","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 claims that thinking of the beloved brings no pain. In 1296 the woman says that when she sat alone and thought (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart stayed only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய); Parimelazhagar specifies that what she thought of was his cruelties. The two speeches mark out the cases in which remembering comforts and in which it wounds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1204","b":"k:1205","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). 1205 gives the answer as an accusation: he has put a guard on his heart against her (தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார்) and yet comes unceasingly into hers. Manakkudavar reads 1205 as the woman, having doubted, now deciding that he does not think of her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1205","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1205 complains that he keeps her out of his heart while entering hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). 1291 states the same imbalance to her own heart: his heart stays his (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why is hers not hers? In 1205 she blames him for the unequal exchange, and in 1291 she blames her own heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1206: she exists only by remembering the days she had with him (உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்), and asks what else she could live by. 1213: her life exists because she sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking (கனவினால் காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). Both make survival in separation depend on a mental substitute for his presence, memory by day and dream by night.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1263","type":"develops","note":"1206 says she lives by looking back at the days of union (உள்ள உளேன்). 1263 says she still lives by longing for his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). The same verb உளேன் is supported in one verse by the past and in the other by the expected future; Parimelazhagar on 1206 notes that the other supports, such as a messenger from him, are missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1159","type":"develops","note":"1207 says that even as she remembers, her heart is burned (உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1159 describes the same burning as a property of love's sickness: fire burns only when touched, and cannot burn when one moves away as love does (விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ). 1159 gives the general paradox of a fire that burns at a distance, and 1207 shows it at work inside memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1262","type":"agrees","note":"In both verses the woman rejects the idea of forgetting him, which Parimelazhagar says the friend had advised in each case. 1207 asks what she would become if she forgot (மறப்பின் எவனாவன்); 1262 answers concretely that if she forgot today (இன்று மறப்பின்) her beauty would go and the bangles slip from her arms. Remembering hurts, but both verses hold that forgetting would be worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1199","type":"develops","note":"1199 says that though the one she longs for gives nothing (நல்கார் எனினும்), even a word about him is sweet to her ear. 1208 measures what an ungiving lover leaves her more sharply: his whole gift (சிறப்பு) is that he does not object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar takes 1208 as scorn of the friend's promise that he will come and be kind, whereas Manakkudavar takes the not-being-angry as real grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1320","type":"same_image","note":"The verses share the words நினை and காய். In 1208 the woman says that however much she thinks of him he is not angry (எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார்), and that this is all the honour he does her. In 1320 the man reports that when he merely sits thinking and looks at her she is angry (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும்) and asks whom he is thinking of. The absent lover tolerates everything because he is not there to care, while the present one is questioned about every thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 says that if her eyes could go where he is, as her mind does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in tears. 1210 looks for a way round that limit: on Parimelazhagar's reading, since both of them look at the moon, her eyes meet his there (கண்ணினால் காண). Manakkudavar reads it differently, as a complaint that the moon will not set and let her sleep and see him in a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1212","type":"develops","note":"In 1211 the dream arrives as the carrier of his message (காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவு). In 1212 she wants to use the dream in the other direction: if her eyes would sleep, she would herself declare to him that she survives (உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன்). Parimelazhagar says this is spoken when she was thinking of sending a messenger, and that she would tell him in full what she would hold back from one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1268","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses end on the word விருந்து, a feast. In 1211 the woman (for Parimelazhagar) asks what feast she can make for the dream that came bringing her lover's messenger (யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து). In 1268 the man, away on the king's work, looks forward to the evening feast at home once the king has won (மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து). The real feast waits on his return, and meanwhile she can offer hospitality only to a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1179","type":"agrees","note":"1212 makes everything depend on a condition: if her eyes would sleep when she begs them (யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சின்). 1179 says why the condition fails: her eyes do not sleep when he does not come and do not sleep when he does (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா). Set beside chapter 122, this shows that the dream which consoles her in the following verses is not available to her at will.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1213","b":"k:1198","type":"qualifies","note":"1198 says no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without getting even a kind word from the one they love (வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்). 1213 has the woman living on though he gives her nothing in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை), and explains it: her life exists because she sees him in dreams (கனவினால் காண்டலின்). The second verse answers the first's charge of hardness by naming what keeps her alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1214","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 regrets that her eyes cannot travel to where he is as her mind can. In 1214 the dream does the travelling: it seeks out the man who gives nothing in waking life and brings him (நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு), and so there is pleasure in the dream. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the dream goes to the country he went to, finds him and delivers him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1215","b":"k:1201","type":"qualifies","note":"1201 says love gives a joy that does not end even in recollection (உள்ளினும் தீரா). 1215 says that what she saw in waking was sweet then, and the dream too is sweet just while it is seen (கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது). Manakkudavar glosses both pleasures as lasting for that moment; Parimelazhagar takes her point to be that dream and waking are therefore equal for her. On either reading the woman's sweetness is tied to the moment of seeing, where the man's persists in thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"1169 calls the long nights of separation crueller than the cruel man himself (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1216 wants the night's state never to end, with waking itself abolished (நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின்). The difference is sleep: the night is an enemy when she lies awake and a refuge when it brings the dream, and Parimelazhagar accordingly has her call waking a villain (பாவி).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1329","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1216 the woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover would not leave her in the dream (கனவினால் காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்). In 1329 the man, in the middle of a lovers' quarrel, wishes the night would lengthen so that he can go on pleading (நீடுக மன்னோ இரா). Both wish away the end of the night and both close on the wistful particle மன்; hers is said of a dream meeting and his of a real one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:819","type":"parallel_domain","note":"819, on bad friendship, says the tie with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). In 1217 the lover who gives nothing in waking life hurts her in her dreams as well (கனவினால் ... பீழிப்பது). Both take the dream as the last place a person's unreliability should reach, and both find that it reaches there: one verse in the book of polity, the other in a lover's complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:1213","type":"qualifies","note":"The two verses open with nearly the same words, நனவினால் நல்காதவரை and நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார், and reach opposite verdicts on the dream. In 1213 seeing him in dreams keeps her alive; in 1217 she asks why the cruel one torments her in dreams (எம்மைப் பீழிப்பது). Parimelazhagar places 1217 after she has woken and found him gone, so the dream comforts while it lasts and wounds once it has ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1217","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses describe the same event: he is with her in sleep and gone on waking. In 1217 this is his cruelty; Parimelazhagar explains the torment as his being on her shoulder in the dream and vanishing when she wakes. In 1218 she tells it in his favour: asleep he is on her shoulder (தோள்மேலர்), and awake he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து), so he never leaves her at all. Parimelazhagar says 1218 is her defence of him after the friend had spoken against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1218 says that on waking he quickly becomes one who is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர்). 1249 tells her own heart that the lover is inside it (உள்ளத்தார் காதலவர்), so there is no one to go out looking for. Both use his dwelling in her heart to deny that he is absent; 1218 adds the dream so that both night and day are covered.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:66","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses have the same construction: an opinion is held only by those who lack an experience. In 66, 'the flute is sweet, the lute is sweet' is said by those who have not heard their own children's prattle (மழலைச்சொல் கேளாதவர்). In 1219 those who blame the man for giving nothing in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (கனவினால் காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Parental love in the first book and a lover's dream in the third both discredit a judgment by pointing to what its holders have not known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:1236","type":"develops","note":"1236: with bangles and arms slackening, what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). 1219 goes from that pain to a rebuttal: those who blame him (நோவர்) are people who have not seen a lover in their dreams. Parimelazhagar gives both verses the same occasion, the friend speaking ill of the man, and says she puts her reply in 1219 on strangers out of displeasure with the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 calls Death 'the life-eating' (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) and says it does not advance on the days of one who does not kill. 1221 gives the same epithet, உயிருண்ணும், to the evening that comes on women parted from their husbands. The pairing shows the woman addressing evening in the book's own language for Death; Parimelazhagar's note on 1229 says she thinks of evening as கூற்றம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:334","type":"parallel_domain","note":"334 says what shows itself as a 'day' (நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி) is a blade that saws through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. 1221 has the parted woman tell the hour it is not evening (மாலையோ அல்லை) but a thing that eats the life of those once wed (உயிருண்ணும் வேலை; Parimelazhagar takes வேலை as a final hour, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as a spear). Both unmask a unit of time as something that consumes life, one as a general truth of impermanence, the other as one lover's experience of separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1223","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1223 says the evening that used to come budding with chill and looking wan (பனி அரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை) now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing (துனி அரும்பித் துன்பம் வளர). 1227 says her sickness buds in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swells through the day and opens in the evening. Both use அரும்பு, budding, for her pain, and together they give evening as both what brings the bud and when it opens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1224","b":"k:1169","type":"develops","note":"1169 says the long nights now passing are crueller than the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1224 says that where her lover is absent (காதலர் இல்வழி) evening comes like ஏதிலர், pitiless strangers or foes, on a killing ground (கொலைக்களத்து). Both make a stretch of time into a cruel agent set beside human cruelty, and 1224 names the condition: it is so only in his absence.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 states a rule: hurt done to others in the forenoon (முற்பகல்) comes back to oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல்), of itself. In 1225 the woman asks what good she did to morning (காலைக்குச் செய்த நன்று) and what enmity she did to evening (மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை). Her question assumes the same logic of return, that suffering answers something one did, and finds no deed of hers to explain evening's hostility.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1228","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1258 the woman says the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her deceiving lover is the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). In 1228 the herdsman's flute is a weapon that kills (கொல்லும் படை). Both call a gentle sound a படை, one because it draws her to him and the other because it announces the evening without him.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1229","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1168 the night has put all living beings to sleep (மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி) and she alone is its companion, so her suffering is solitary. In 1229 she says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind (மதிமருண்டு; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take this as her own mind), the whole town is bewildered and suffers (பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும்). The two speeches picture the world around her in opposite ways, asleep and untouched in one and sharing her distress in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1209","type":"agrees","note":"In 1209 the woman says her dear life is perishing (விளியும் என் இன்னுயிர்) as she thinks much on the lack of kindness of one who said they were not separate. In 1230 her life, which had not died till now (மாயா உயிர்), is dying in the dim evening as she thinks of the man whose nature is wealth (பொருள்மாலையாளரை உள்ளி). Both say that thinking of him is what wears her life away, and each names him by a reproach: his broken word in one, his preference for wealth in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1263 she says he left desiring victory with his own resolve as companion (உரன் நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்), and that she still lives by desiring his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). In 1230 thinking of the man given to wealth makes her life die. Both name him by the aim he left for, but in one speech the thought of him keeps her alive and in the other it kills; these are two moments of the same speaker, not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 says love cannot be bolted in, because the small tears of the loving make it public (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்). 1232 says her eyes, pale and running with tears (பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்), seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same mechanism, tears that disclose, works in both; in 71 they reveal the weeper's love, in 1232 they also accuse the absent lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:1180","type":"agrees","note":"In 1180 the woman says the town easily learns the secret of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes seem to be telling of his unkindness. Both treat the eyes as announcing what she would keep hidden; the woman says it of herself and the friend says it to urge her to bear up, as Parimelazhagar frames it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1157","type":"agrees","note":"In 1157 the woman asks whether the bangles slipping past her wrist do not proclaim (தூற்றாகொல்) that the lord of the shore has left her. In 1233 the shoulders that swelled on the day of union (மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்) seem to announce fully that he has parted (தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்). In both her body makes the separation public without her speaking; 1233 adds the contrast with how the same shoulders looked at union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1277","type":"same_image","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted (தணந்தமை ... முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1233 her shoulders announce that he has parted, with the same word தணந்தமை. In 1277 the body is first to know of the parting, and in 1233 it is the one that tells others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1235","type":"develops","note":"1234 and 1235 share the line தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள், shoulders whose old beauty has faded. 1234 describes the loss: with her partner gone (துணை நீங்கி) they have lost their fullness and the bangles slip. 1235 turns the same shoulders into testimony that declares the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்), so the repeated line moves from description to accusation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1262","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1262 the woman tells her friend that if she forgets him today (இன்று மறப்பின்), her beauty will leave her shoulders and her ornaments will slip off (கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). In 1234 the beauty has faded and the bangles are slipping because her partner is gone (துணை நீங்கி). Read together, the bodily loss follows his absence whether she remembers or forgets, though in 1262 she holds that remembering is what prevents it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1188 the woman complains that people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he has left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1235 the wasted shoulders themselves declare his cruelty. One speech says the visible change brings talk about her alone, and the other says the same kind of change speaks against him; the difference is in who is reading the body, the town or the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1235 the friend, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says the shoulders with bangles lost and old beauty faded declare the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்). In 1236 the woman answers that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what pains her is the saying that he is cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). The second verse limits the first: the body's evidence stands, but she refuses the charge drawn from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1236","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1169 the woman herself speaks of the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை) when she says the long nights are crueller still. In 1236 she is pained when he is called cruel (கொடியர்). The pair shows that she uses the word in her own lament and does not accept it from another's mouth; these are different moments of a dramatised speaker, not a contradiction in teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1237","b":"k:1162","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1162 she says she cannot hide this sickness, and that telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1237 she asks her heart whether it can gain honour by telling him of her wasting shoulders. The two give opposite values to the same act of telling him, shame in one and possible honour (பாடு) in the other; in 1237 it is the heart and not she herself who would carry the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1182","type":"same_image","note":"In 1182 the woman says pallor spreads and rides over her body with the pride (தகையால்) that he was the one who gave it. In 1240 the man says the pallor of her eyes felt distress (பருவரல்) on seeing what her forehead had done. Both give the pallor feelings of its own, pride in one and distress in the other, so that பசப்பு is spoken of as a party to the love and not only its symptom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1238","type":"develops","note":"1238 says that when he loosened the embracing arms her forehead turned pale. 1240 says the pallor of her eyes itself came to distress (கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே) on seeing what the bright forehead had done (ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு). 1240 depends on 1238 for what the forehead did; Parimelazhagar explains that the eyes' pallor was abashed that the forehead had paled sooner than they did, at a smaller cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1241","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicines for other ailments are things other than the ailment (பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிற), but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). In 1241 the woman, apart from him, asks her heart to think of any medicine at all (எனைத்தொன்றும் ... மருந்து) for her sickness. Read together, 1102 explains why her heart has nothing to offer: the only cure is the absent person who caused the illness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1195","type":"agrees","note":"1195 asks what those we love can do for us if they do not love in return (தாம் காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1242 draws the conclusion and turns it on her own heart: since he has no love (காதல் அவரிலர் ஆக), its suffering is folly (நோவது பேதைமை). The first states the uselessness of one-sided love, the second names persisting in it as foolishness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1308","type":"agrees","note":"1308, which Parimelazhagar gives to the man during a sulk, asks what use suffering is where there is no lover who recognises that one suffers (நோதல் எவன் ... அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 1242 has the woman tell her heart that suffering is folly when he has no love (நோவது பேதைமை). Both speakers, from either side of the relationship, make pain depend for its point on the other's loving awareness of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1204","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர் நெஞ்சத்து). 1243 gives her own bleak answer: a compassionate thought of her does not exist in the one who caused the sickness (பரிந்துள்ளல் பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்). The question of 1204 has by chapter 125 become a settled premise for scolding her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1296","type":"agrees","note":"1243 asks the heart why it sits and suffers in thought (இருந்துள்ளி என் பரிதல்) when he has no pitying thought. 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்) her heart is there only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய). Both describe sitting and remembering as self-inflicted harm, and each blames the heart for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says that though he disregards her and does only as he pleases, her eyes will not rest without seeing him (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்). 1244 says the eyes, wanting to see him (அவர்க் காணல் உற்று), eat her. Both make the eyes an appetite of their own that her judgment of him does not govern.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"807, on old friendship, says that those whose bond came by the way of love do not cut off love even when ruinous things are done to them (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). 1245 asks whether she can let him go as an enemy merely because he does not return her closeness. The logic of long friendship in the Book of Wealth, that injury does not license abandonment, reappears as a lover's rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"1245 asks the heart whether one can abandon as an enemy (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not respond when she draws close (யாம் உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்). 1292 complains that the heart, even having seen that he is not close (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்), goes to him on the thought that he will not be hostile (செறாஅர் என). The two share the pair உறாதவர் / செறு, and show the same refusal to treat coldness as enmity, once owned by her and once blamed on the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1286 says that when she sees him she sees no faults, and when she does not see him she sees nothing but faults (காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). 1246 calls the heart's anger in his absence false because it never survives the sight of him (காதலர்க் கண்டால் புலந்துணராய்). 1286 states the pattern plainly; 1246 uses it to discredit the anger felt while apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1294","type":"agrees","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot even sulk and then be reconciled (புலந்துணராய்), so its present anger is a false heat (பொய்க்காய்வு காய்தி). 1294 makes the same charge: the heart will not first raise a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), so no one will plan with it. In both the heart is accused of being incapable of the sulk it pretends to.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1247 leaves open which of desire and shame is to go (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). 1251, opening the next chapter, reports the outcome: the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint that shame had bolted (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் ... நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). The choice she offers her heart in 1247 is settled in 1251 by force, not by decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967, on honour, says that being called ruined is better than living by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின் சென்று ஒருவன் வாழ்தலின்); Parimelazhagar takes this of following scorners for gain. 1248 calls her heart a fool for going behind the one who left without grace (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய் பேதை). The standard of honour from the Book of Wealth is the one she applies to her own heart, though in love she cannot enforce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:1293","type":"agrees","note":"1248 calls the heart a fool for going after the man who left (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய்) while grieving that he gives no grace. 1293 asks the heart whether it follows him as it likes (பெட்டாங்கு அவர் பின் செலல்) because the ruined have no friends. Both speak of the heart 'going behind him' as a desertion of her that she can see but not stop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know how to forget, and that even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1250 says that holding him in her heart will cost her yet more beauty. Both count remembering him as a harm to herself, while 1207 adds that forgetting is not available to her.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1262","type":"tension","note":"In 1250 she says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close (துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா), she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்); Parimelazhagar frames this as a case for forgetting him. In 1262 she tells her friend that if she forgets him today, beauty will leave her and the bangles slip from her arms (இன்று மறப்பின் ... கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). The two speeches give opposite accounts of whether remembering or forgetting costs her beauty; both are a speaker's words in the moment, and the conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of the settings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1252","b":"k:1167","type":"agrees","note":"1167 says she swims the flood of desire and sees no shore, and even at midnight only she is awake (யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்). 1252 says desire has no pity (கண் இன்று), for it sets her heart to work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both mark midnight, when Parimelazhagar notes all others rest, as the hour that shows desire does not release her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1318","type":"same_image","note":"1253 likens desire to a sneeze that appears without one's intending it; Parimelazhagar glosses that it cannot be held in. In 1318 the man does stifle a sneeze (தும்முச் செறுப்ப), and she weeps that he is hiding from her that his people are thinking of him. The sneeze is in both an involuntary sign of what is within, and 1318 shows that even suppressing it is read as a disclosure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"1180 says it is not hard for the town to get the secret of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). 1254 says her desire goes past concealment into the public place (மன்று படும்). 1180 supplies a means by which the exposure described in 1254 happens: her own eyes announce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:1245","type":"develops","note":"1245 asks whether she can abandon as hostile (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not answer her love. 1255 turns that private question into a general statement: not following the hostile (செற்றார் பின் செல்லா) is a dignity unknown to those with love's sickness (காமநோய் உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று). The word செற்றார் links them, and the second explains why the first could only be a rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1168","type":"same_image","note":"In 1168 she calls the night அளித்து, pitiable or kindly, because having put every living thing to sleep it has no companion but her. In 1256 she uses the same word of her grief (அளித்தரோ ... துயர்), which Parimelazhagar takes as scorn. In both she speaks to her tormentor in the language of grace and pity, which is how these monologues voice bitterness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1255","type":"develops","note":"1255 states in general terms that the lovesick do not know the dignity of not going after one who is hostile. 1256 applies it to herself with irony: how gracious (அளித்தரோ) is this grief, which wants her to go after the one who is hostile (செற்றவர் பின் சேறல் வேண்டி). The pair moves from a maxim about lovers to her own case, and Parimelazhagar reads அளித்து as mockery.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 she cannot bear desire and shame together and asks her heart to give up one (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). In 1257 the matter is settled in his presence: when he does what she longs for, shame is simply not known to her. The conflict that is unbearable in his absence does not arise once he is there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"1297 says that, caught up with a foolish heart that cannot forget him, she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்). 1257 says she knows nothing of a thing called shame (நாண் என ஒன்றோ அறியலம்) when the one she cherishes does out of desire what she longs for. The two verses give the same loss of shame from two moments, separation in 1297 and reunion in 1257.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:555","type":"same_image","note":"555 asks whether the tears of those who suffer under a ruler are not the weapon that wears away his wealth (கண்ணீர் அன்றே செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). 1258 has the same form of question: are his humble words not the weapon that breaks our womanhood (பணிமொழி அன்றோ ... உடைக்கும் படை). In both a soft thing, tears or gentle speech, is named as the real weapon against something that looks strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"985 says that bowing is the strength of those who accomplish things and the weapon by which the worthy turn their opponents (பணிதல் ... மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 1258 calls her lover's lowly speech (பணிமொழி) the weapon that breaks her firmness. Humility as a படை is praised in the ethics of the worthy, and in the love poems the woman sees the same device used on her and calls its user a thief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1259 narrates one occasion: she went away to sulk and embraced him when she saw her heart joining him. 1260 turns it into a general question about people whose hearts melt like fat in fire, and whether 'we will stand in a sulk' (ஊடி நிற்பேம் எனல்) is even open to them. The second verse gives the reason for the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1287","type":"agrees","note":"1287 asks what use it is to sulk when one knows the sulk will prove false (பொய்த்தல் அறிந்து என் புலந்து), like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off. 1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்) can ever say they will hold out in a sulk. One image is of being swept away and the other of melting, and both conclude that the sulk cannot be sustained.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:776","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 776 the warrior takes up his days (தன் நாளை எடுத்து) and files every day without a wound as wasted; in 1261 the wife marks off the days of his absence (நாளொற்றி) until her finger wears down. Both keep a ledger of days, the soldier's reckoned by wounds received and the waiting wife's by absence endured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:1269","type":"develops","note":"1261 shows the woman's finger worn (தேய்ந்த விரல்) from marking the days since he left (அவர்சென்ற நாளொற்றி); 1269 says that for those pining with the return day in mind (வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்குபவர்க்கு) one day passes like seven. 1269 gives the felt arithmetic behind 1261's tally: the days she counts are each lived as many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 praises the man who wants the work and not pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as a pillar to his kin; in 1263 the wife describes her husband as gone desiring victory with his own resolve for company (உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாக), which Parimelazhagar glosses as not desiring enjoyment. 1263 shows the same man from inside the house: she stays alive only by desiring his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்), the same verb நசைஇ set against his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"In 1263 she waits for the man who left wanting victory; in 1268, which Parimelazhagar assigns to the man away on the king's service, he wishes the king would engage and win (வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன்) so that he can join his wife for the evening. On that reading the pair gives both ends of one separation: her longing for his return and his impatience for the work to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:476","type":"same_image","note":"476 warns that one who has climbed to the tip of a branch (நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார்) and pushes further loses his life; in 1264 her heart, thinking of his return, கோடுகொடு ஏறும், which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read as climbing a tree's branches to look for him (Parimelazhagar reads it as swelling up like a tree putting out branches). On the climbing reading the same image marks reckless overreach in the book on statecraft and eager hope here.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"In 1170 her heart goes to where he is, and she wishes her eyes could follow (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்); in 1264 her heart rises thinking of his return (வரவுள்ளி). Both make the heart the part of her that moves toward him while she stays put; on Manakkudavar's reading of 1264 it even climbs to do the seeing her eyes cannot.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"In 1187 pallor seizes her the moment she shifts a little in his embrace (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்); in 1265 she says the pallor on her shoulders will leave once she has seen him to her eyes' fill (கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின் நீங்கும்). Together they give pallor's rule in the Book of Love: it comes with the slightest distance and goes only with his presence, which Parimelazhagar underlines by noting that merely hearing of his return will not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says her eyes will not rest without seeing her husband (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்) even though he does as he pleases; 1265 prays to see the same கொண்கன் until her eyes are full. Both make the eyes' need to see him the thing that must be satisfied before anything else can mend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says all five senses find their objects in her; in 1266 she will 'drink' him (பருகுவன்), which Parimelazhagar explains as drinking that nectar through all five gateways of sense. On his reading 1266 is the woman's version of 1101, with the five-sense enjoyment compressed into a single verb.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1102","type":"agrees","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicine for a disease is normally something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து); in 1266 the woman says that if he comes for one day she will drink him in so that all her sickness is destroyed (பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட). Each lover names the other as both the cause and the only cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists sulking, reconciling and uniting (ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல்) as the rewards of those joined in love; 1267 shows the woman, before he has even returned, unable to choose between sulking and embracing and wondering whether to combine them. What 1109 states as a sequence of pleasures, 1267 shows as one impatient wish for all of them at once, which is how Parimelazhagar explains its place in this chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1269","b":"k:1278","type":"same_image","note":"1269 says one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்) for those awaiting the return day; in 1278 she says he left only yesterday (நெருநற்று) and she has been pale for seven days (எழுநாளேம்). Both use seven days to measure how separation distorts time, stretching a day of waiting in 1269 and running ahead of the departure itself in 1278.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:71","type":"agrees","note":"71 asks if there is any bolt to shut love in, since the small tears of the loving announce it (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்); 1271 says her eyes tell something despite her concealment. The claim that affection cannot be locked away and that the eyes give it out appears in the book on virtue as a general truth about அன்பு and in the Book of Love as a scene.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:709","type":"parallel_domain","note":"709 says the eye itself tells enmity and friendship (கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know its kinds; in 1271 the man tells her that though she hides it, her eyes have slipped her control and have something to say (உண்கண் உரைக்கல் உறுவது). The minister's skill of reading the eye in the book on statecraft is the lover's skill here, with the same verb உரை for what the eye does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1272","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1272 says this girl has a great measure of womanly nature (பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது), which Parimelazhagar takes as artlessness beyond the usual; 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading is womanhood added to womanhood (பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்து). The chapter's first observation about her is given its content at the end: her womanliness is her saying it with the eyes and not in words.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:1274","type":"develops","note":"1273 compares her unspoken meaning to a thread that shows through the gems (திகழ்தரு); 1274 compares it to the scent inside a closed bud (முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்). Parimelazhagar distinguishes them: the first lies within yet is visible outside, the second is not visible outside at all, so the pair moves from a sign that can be seen to one that can only be inferred.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1274","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1227 describes love's sickness as budding in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swelling through the day and flowering at evening; 1274 places an unspoken meaning inside the bud of her smile (நகைமொக்குள்). Both use the bud for a feeling that is already there and has not yet opened, pain in the one and a hidden intention in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1091","type":"agrees","note":"1091 finds two looks in her eyes, one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (அந்நோய் மருந்து); 1275 finds in her covert behaviour a medicine that ends great sorrow (உறுதுயர் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து). In both the remedy is not anything she says but something to be read in a sign she gives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"1092 values the small look her eyes steal (கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) at more than half of love; 1275 says her கள்ளம், her stealth, contains a medicine. Theft is the figure for her indirect signalling in both, at first meeting in 1092 and after reunion in 1275.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1279","type":"develops","note":"1275 says the stealth with which she acted and withdrew (செய்திறந்த கள்ளம்) holds a medicine for great sorrow; 1279 states what she did (அஃதாண்டவள் செய்தது): she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet. Parimelazhagar ties the two explicitly, taking 1279's அஃது to point back to 1275, so that the silent gesture is the 'stealth' and its meaning, going with him, is the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"827 warns not to take an enemy's bowing words at face value, because a bow's bending signals harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்); in 1276 the woman reads his unusually great tenderness (பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல்) as a sign of coming absence. The same inference, that excess of kindness points to its opposite, is advice about false friends in one book and a wife's reading of her husband in the other, though she alleges no enmity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1278","b":"k:1277","type":"agrees","note":"1277 says her bangles knew of his parting before she did; 1278 says her body has been pale for seven days though he left only yesterday. The two adjacent speeches make the same point with different signs: her body responds to his intention to leave before the leaving is an acknowledged fact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1093","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1093 reports a wordless act and reads it: she looked, and having looked bowed her head (நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள்), and that (அஃது) was water poured on their bond; 1279 reports that she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet, and that (அஃது) is what she did there. Both verses consist of a sequence of looks followed by அஃது, leaving the meaning to be drawn from the gesture, at courtship in 1093 and at the threat of parting in 1279.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"1234 describes separation's effect: the shoulders lose their fullness and old beauty and the bangles slip (பைந்தொடி சோரும்… தோள்); in 1279 she looks at bangles and soft shoulders (தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி) and then at her feet. Read with 1234, her first two glances name what will happen if she is left, and the third, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it, names her alternative of walking with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1053","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says that even asking has a beauty of its own (இரப்புமோர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when done before those who know their duty and do not withhold; 1280 says her asking (இரவு), done with the eyes, has a womanliness of its own (பெண்மை உடைத்து). The word for begging is the same, and both grant it a grace under conditions, though the chapter on begging means alms and 1280 means a lover's plea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"1137 says nothing has greater worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்); 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading (கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு) is womanhood upon womanhood. Both praise as specifically womanly the refusal to declare desire openly, 1137 by what she will not do and 1280 by the silent means she uses instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1281","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"In 1281 love is set above liquor: it delights at a thought and a look, which liquor cannot do. In 1288, in the same chapter, his chest is itself like liquor to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), wanted though it does shameful harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The chapter uses the one image both ways, first to say love exceeds drink and then to say it compels like drink.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"433 says those ashamed of blame treat a fault as small as a millet as if it were a palmyra (தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம்... பனைத்துணையாக் கொள்வர்). 1282 uses the same two measures in the other direction: with love as large as a palmyra, even a millet of sulking over his fault is to be avoided. In 433 the small fault is magnified; in 1282 the small grievance is to be dropped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount and is spoiled only by being prolonged a little (உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி). 1282 says that when love is full to a palmyra's measure, not even a millet of sulking is wanted (தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும்). Read together, 1282 states a condition, காமம் நிறைய வரின், under which the measured sulk that 1302 recommends falls to nothing; Parimelazhagar takes 1282 as the woman voicing her own haste in the form of general advice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"1257 says she knows nothing of shame when the one she cherishes does, in desire, what she wants (பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்). 1283 reuses the two words with the sense turned round: even if he does only what he himself wants, without cherishing her (பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும்), her eyes will not rest without seeing him. The second verse removes the condition of the first: her yielding does not depend on his doing what pleases her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1246","type":"develops","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot stay in a sulk, so its present anger is false (பொய்க்காய்வு). 1284 is that prediction coming true: she set out to sulk and the heart forgot it and went to unite (அதுமறந்து கூடற்கண் சென்றது).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1294","type":"develops","note":"1284 reports to her friend that her heart forgot the sulk and went to unite. 1294 gives the consequence: since the heart will not first sulk and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), who will consult with it about such things again (நின்னொடு சூழ்வார் யார்). The report of the heart's desertion in one chapter becomes the quarrel with the heart in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 asks what harm could come if people saw their own faults as they see strangers' faults (ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்). 1285 says she cannot see her husband's fault when he is before her, as the eye cannot see the kohl-stick touching it. In both, what is nearest is what goes unseen; 190 treats this as a failing to correct, and 1285 as a fact about love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1285 gives the image: the eye does not see the stick that is lining it, and she does not see his blame when she sees him (பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து). 1286 states the other half that the image implies: when she does not see him she sees nothing but his faults (காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). Parimelazhagar's gloss on 1285 already supplies this, saying the eye has seen the stick at every other time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1286","b":"k:1297","type":"develops","note":"1286: seeing him, she sees no faults; not seeing him, she sees nothing else. In 1297 she calls her heart மடநெஞ்சு, and Parimelazhagar defines that மடமை as exactly this reversal: remembering when he is absent, and forgetting when he is present, faults that ought to be remembered at sight and forgotten in absence. On his reading 1297 names and blames what 1286 only describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1287","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"1134: the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness (காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும்). 1287: why sulk knowing it will prove false, like those who leap into a stream knowing it will carry them off (உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய்பவர்). The same water and the same verb, உய்த்தல், are used by the man of his restraint in 1134 and by the woman of her sulk here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்), the living being loves life more with each suffering. 1288: liquor is wanted by the drunk even as it does them shameful harm, and so is his chest. Both explain an attachment that grows through injury by a vice from the same group of chapters (dice in 940, liquor in 1288).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:1145","type":"same_image","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1288: his chest is as liquor is to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shameful harm. Both take the drinker's returning appetite as the likeness for love; 1288 adds the harm and the shame that 1145 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1289 says few attain love's செவ்வி, its right moment or condition. 1306 gives a figure for missing it on either side: without துனி and புலவி love is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). Parimelazhagar places 1289 in a sulk that will not yield to appeasement, so the fineness it speaks of is the same fineness of timing that 1306 puts in terms of ripeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1327","type":"develops","note":"1290 remembers her sulk ending because her eagerness to embrace was greater than his. 1327 states the rule the man later draws: in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won, as is seen in the union (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). Her giving way in 1290 is a case of the defeat that 1327 counts as victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1291","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"72: the loveless belong wholly to themselves, and the loving belong to others even in their bones (அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்). 1291: his heart is for him (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why, heart, are you not for me (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). The woman's complaint is 72's distinction seen from the inside: the one who loves no longer owns her own heart, and the one who does not love keeps his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1292","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the lovesick cannot know. 1292 shows it: her heart goes to him (சேறி) though it has seen that he is not attached to her. 1255 gives the general statement and 1292 the instance, addressed to the heart that does the going.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1293 has the woman quote the same worldly observation as a saying, கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார் இல், and ask her heart whether that is why it leaves her and follows him. The pairing shows a hard fact from the chapter on wealth being used in the Book of Love as a taunt, with the woman as the ruined one and her heart as the fair-weather friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:787","type":"qualifies","note":"787 defines friendship as turning a friend from ruin and, when ruin comes, sharing the suffering (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு). The saying in 1293, that the ruined have no friends, describes how the world behaves and not what friendship is; by the standard of 787 her heart, which goes after him as it pleases (பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்), has failed as a friend. The woman's question, என்பதோ, shows she is quoting the saying to reproach, not endorsing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:798","type":"develops","note":"798 warns against taking the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு). In 1293 the one who deserts in distress is her own heart, which cannot be declined as a friend. The verse turns the advice of the friendship chapters into a predicament with no remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1260","type":"agrees","note":"1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat in fire can say they will hold out in a sulk (புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்). 1294 tells the heart that it will not sulk first and enjoy afterwards, so no one will plan such things with it. Both say the intention to sulk cannot survive her own heart; 1294 adds that she will stop consulting it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1330 says sulking is the pleasure of love, and the pleasure of sulking is to come together and embrace (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்). 1294 has the woman want exactly that order, first make a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), and complain that her heart will not do it. The book's closing statement of how a sulk should work is, in 1294, a plan she cannot carry out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:293","type":"parallel_domain","note":"293: do not lie about what your heart knows, for after the lie your own heart will burn you (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). 1296: thinking alone, she finds her own heart is there to eat her. In both the tormentor is one's own heart and the setting is solitude with what one knows; 293 makes this the working of conscience, and 1296 gives it as the experience of the abandoned lover, with no fault of hers involved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know forgetting, and even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart consumes her. Both make thinking of him in his absence the source of the pain; in 1207 the heart is what is burned, in 1296 it is what does the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame's sake and do not give up shame for life's sake (நாண்துறவார்). In 1297 the woman has forgotten shame because her heart cannot forget him, and Parimelazhagar glosses the shame as dearer to her than life, the valuation of 1017. The pairing sets the rule from the chapter on shame beside a speaker in the Book of Love who holds that rule and reports failing it; it is her confession, not a contrary teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1132","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1132 the man says his body and life, unable to bear it, will mount the palmyra horse, with shame set aside (நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து). In 1297 the woman says she has forgotten shame through her heart's inability to forget him. Each sex is shown losing நாண் to love; the man sets it aside to act in public, and the woman finds it gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 says it is better to be called ruined where one stands than to live by following those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின்). In 1298 the heart, described as loving life (உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு), counts scorning him as the disgrace and goes on dwelling on him. The chapter on honour ranks honour above life; the heart in 1298 keeps the vocabulary of disgrace (இளிவு) but, out of attachment to life, applies it so as to stay with the one who slighted her. This is the heart's reasoning as she reports it, not a rival doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1184","type":"same_image","note":"1184: she thinks of him and what she speaks of is his merit (உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and still the pallor comes. 1298: her heart thinks of what pertains to him (அவர்திறம் உள்ளும்), judging that to scorn him would be a disgrace. The phrase அவர் திறம் with உள்ளுதல் appears in both; in 1184 it is her own loyal practice that goes unrewarded, and in 1298 it is something her heart does that she stands apart from.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1255","type":"qualifies","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away is unknown to the lovesick (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை). In 1298 her heart reasons the opposite way about where disgrace lies: it thinks that to scorn him would be a disgrace (எள்ளின் இளிவாம் என்று எண்ணி) and so keeps thinking of his side. 1298 shows the lovesick heart not merely ignorant of that dignity, as 1255 has it, but supplied with its own argument from honour for doing without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 says virtue, done without delay, is the companion that does not die when one dies (பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை). 1299 says that in grief there is no companion if one's own heart is not one. Both verses ask what stays with a person when other support is gone; Book One answers virtue, and the lover's verse names the heart and finds it missing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1263","type":"same_image","note":"1263 describes the man as having left with his own heart or resolve for companion (உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்). 1299 asks who can be a companion in grief when one's own heart is no companion (நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). The heart as துணை is what he had on setting out and, on Parimelazhagar's assignment of 1299 to the man, what he lacks when she will not be reconciled; Manakkudavar gives 1299 to the woman.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1300","type":"develops","note":"1299 asks who will be a companion in grief when one's own heart is not (தாமுடைய நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). 1300 repeats the frame, தாமுடைய நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி, and draws the further step: then it is a small matter that strangers are not one's own. The pair moves from the loss of the nearest help to an inference about everyone else, and Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, where the first eight verses of the chapter are the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:188","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those who spread even the faults of their closest (துன்னியார்) will do to strangers (ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). 1300 argues that when one's own heart is not one's own, strangers (ஏதிலார்) not being one's own is a slight thing. Both reason from the nearest relation to ஏதிலார்: if the bond fails at the closest point, nothing can be expected further out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:882","type":"parallel_domain","note":"882 says do not fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). 1300 finds the same reversal inside one person: the heart that should be தமர், one's own people, is not, and beside that the distance of outsiders hardly counts. The lesson of the chapter on enmity within, that the danger is in what is closest, is applied in 1300 to the heart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1291 opens the chapter with the woman asking her heart why it is not hers when his heart is his own (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). 1300 closes it with the general form: when one's own heart is not one's kin (நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி), it is nothing that strangers are not. Parimelazhagar gives the first to the woman and the last to the man, so the chapter begins and ends with the same complaint from each side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1301 has the friend urge the woman to hold back her embrace and sulk (புல்லாது இராப் புலத்தை) so they can watch his pain 'a little' (சிறிது). 1302 states the limit that word implies: sulking is like salt in right measure, and letting it run on (நீள விடல்) is like salt in excess. Parimelazhagar reads the சிறிது of 1301 as already pointing to this rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1303","type":"develops","note":"1301 proposes not embracing (புல்லாது) in order to see his அல்லல்நோய். 1303 uses the same two words from the other side: leaving the one who sulks unembraced (புல்லா விடல்) is like giving அல்லல்நோய் to those already in distress (அலந்தாரை). Read together, the pain that 1301 treats as a brief test is the pain 1303 says must not be left standing.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1329","type":"agrees","note":"In 1301 the friend wants the sulk kept up so that his suffering can be seen for a while. In 1329 the man himself wishes she would go on sulking (ஊடுக மன்னோ) and the night grow long so that he may keep pleading (யாம் இரப்ப). Both sides treat the sulk as something to prolong on purpose, hers to watch and his to beg.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1302 gives the measure: a little sulking seasons, a little too long spoils. 1306 gives the two failures by name: without துனி and புலவி, love is like கனி and கருக்காய், which Manakkudavar reads as overripe fruit and unripe fruit. Together they make sulking a matter of degree, judged by taste, in both verses through a food image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1303","b":"k:1304","type":"agrees","note":"1303 says that leaving unembraced those who have sulked (புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்) is like giving sharp pain to those already suffering (அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்று). 1304 says that not appeasing those who sulk (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). The pair states one claim twice, once as added hurt and once as a finishing cut. Parimelazhagar takes both as the wife's taunt sending the man back to his other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1304","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares leaving a sulking lover unappeased to cutting an already withered creeper at its root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). 1030 says a family with no good man to prop it falls when trouble cuts it at the foot (இடுக்கண் கால் கொன்றிட வீழும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as an axe at the tree's base. Both picture a weakened plant that needed support and got the cut instead, once in love and once in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1321","type":"agrees","note":"1305, on Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man find beauty in her sulk even though he is good and blameless. 1321 gives her side: he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is what makes sulking possible. Both verses detach the sulk from any real offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"1305 says that for men good in their qualities (நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு) the beauty (ஏர்) is the sulking in flower-eyed women; Parimelazhagar reads this as 'even for the faultless, sulking is sweet'. 1325 says the same in plainer terms: though without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft shoulders of the one they love has something in it (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). Manakkudavar reads 1305 differently, taking நல்லவர் as the other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1330","type":"agrees","note":"1306 puts it negatively: without quarrels, serious and light, love is fruit at the wrong stage. 1330 puts it positively: sulking is the pleasure of love (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்), and the pleasure of sulking is the embrace that follows. The chapter 133 verse states as a conclusion what 1306 argues by image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1152","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1307 finds a pain inside sulking (ஊடலின் உண்டு ஆங்கு ஓர் துன்பம்): the thought of whether union will be delayed or not. In 1152 the woman says union itself holds the misery of fearing separation (பிரிவு அஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு). In both, a state counted as pleasant carries worry about what comes next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 calls sulking the pleasure of love, provided the embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). 1307 fastens on that proviso: because one cannot be sure the union will not be long delayed (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்), the sulk has its own pain. 1307 is the reservation built into 1330's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:877","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1308 asks what use it is to suffer where there is no lover who knows 'this one is hurt' (நொந்தார் என்று அஃது அறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 877, in the chapter on enemies, advises: do not voice your hurt to those who do not know you are hurt (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). The same roots நோ and அறி carry one rule in love and in statecraft: displayed pain is wasted on someone who does not perceive it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1200","type":"agrees","note":"In 1308 suffering is pointless when the other does not know or care that one suffers. In 1200 the woman tells her heart that reporting its great pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) is like trying to fill in the sea. Man and woman reach the same judgment from opposite sides of the couple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1309","type":"develops","note":"1308 says the man's hurt is useless without a lover who recognises it. 1309 gives the general form: as water is sweet in shade, sulking is sweet only with those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது). Parimelazhagar assigns both to the man, in a sulk that she is not answering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1309","b":"k:1323","type":"agrees","note":"1309 limits the sweetness of sulking to those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே). 1323 asks whether any heaven matches sulking with those joined like water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார் அகத்து). Both say the value of the sulk depends on the closeness of the two, and both use water to say it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1310 calls the heart's wish to join with one who neglects it mere craving. In 1255 the woman says the dignity of not going after those who have turned away (செற்றார் பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the love-sick do not know. Both admit that desire overrides self-respect toward an indifferent partner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"In 1310 the man, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's intent to join with one who leaves him to wither in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு) is just craving (அவா). In 1292 the woman scolds her heart for going to him even after seeing that he is not close to her (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்). Each blames the heart for running to someone who has shown no care.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares the unappeased sulker to a withered creeper (வாடிய வள்ளி) cut at the root. 1310 speaks of being left to dry up in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவார்). The wife's warning and the man's complaint use the same picture of a plant left without water; Parimelazhagar places 1310 in a sulk she does not answer.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1312","b":"k:1253","type":"same_image","note":"In 1312 the woman says he sneezed while she was sulking, knowing (அறிந்து) she would have to say 'live long' and so speak to him. In 1253 the sneeze is the example of what happens without intention: desire comes out குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல். Parimelazhagar notes that 1312 treats a natural sneeze as a deliberate one, which is the subtlety the chapter title names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"1311 gives her stated ground: every woman takes him in with her eyes (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). 1313 shows the same worry applied to a trifle: a wreath must have been put on to be shown to another woman (ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டிய சூடினீர்). Her jealousy in both is about who sees him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1321","type":"develops","note":"In 1313 the man reports that even wearing a wreath makes her angry (காயும்): 'you wore it to show some woman'. In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet his way of showing tenderness can bring on a sulk. 1321 supplies from her mouth what the man's complaints in chapter 132 imply: the grounds are invented.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1314","b":"k:645","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1314 he says 'we love more than anyone' (யாரினும் காதலம்) and she turns it on him: 'than whom, than whom?' (யாரினும் யாரினும்). 645 advises speaking a word only after knowing there is no other word that can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). The love scene shows that rule failing in a comic way: his own word, repeated back, is the one that beats him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:107","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1315 a promise limited to this birth (இம்மை) makes her weep. 107 says the good remember through all seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர்) the friendship of those who removed their distress. Her complaint measures his promise against the span the book itself uses for lasting bonds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1314","type":"agrees","note":"In 1314 'more than anyone' is heard as implying other loves; in 1315 'we will not part in this birth' (இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம்) fills her eyes with tears, because she hears a parting in the next. Parimelazhagar describes the move in 1315: she takes a plain statement as a hinting one. Both verses turn a strong assurance into an offence by what it leaves unsaid.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1316 has her treat remembering as proof of forgetting. In 1207 the woman, alone, says she does not know forgetting (மறப்பறியேன்) and that even remembering burns. The standard she holds him to in 1316, never having forgotten at all, is the one she claims for herself in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1312","type":"develops","note":"In 1312 she suspects his sneeze was a trick to get the customary 'live long' out of her. In 1317 she gives that blessing (வழுத்தினாள்) and at once takes it back (அழித்து அழுதாள்) with a new suspicion. One sneeze is read two ways in the chapter, as a stratagem and as proof of another woman.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1318","b":"k:1317","type":"develops","note":"In 1317 sneezing brings the charge that some woman is thinking of him. In 1318 he holds the sneeze back (தும்முச் செறுப்ப) and she weeps anyway: 'are you hiding from me that your people think of you?' (நுமர் உள்ளல் எம்மை மறைத்திரோ). The two verses close both exits: sneezing and not sneezing are both faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1319 even his effort to appease her makes her angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்): 'you must be like this with others too'. 693 warns those who serve kings that once suspicion has arisen, clearing it is hard for anyone (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The court maxim and the bedroom scene describe the same trap: reassurance offered to a suspicious mind is taken as more evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1304 says not appeasing one who sulks (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. In 1319 he does appease her (தன்னை உணர்த்தினும்) and that too is a fault. Taken together, the man is blamed in chapter 131 for not doing what chapter 132 shows cannot be done to her satisfaction.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 her objection is to other women's eyes on him (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). In 1320 it is to his eyes on her, which she thinks are really on another woman; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both explain that she supposes he is matching her limbs against someone else's. The chapter opens and closes on the look as the place where she locates unfaithfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1313","type":"agrees","note":"1313, 1319 and 1320 share the frame '…even if I do X, she is angry (…இனும் காயும்), saying …'. In 1313 the act is wearing a wreath; in 1320 it is only sitting and looking at her (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும்), which she takes as comparing her with someone (யார் உள்ளி நோக்கினீர்). The series runs from adornment down to a silent look, so that no act of his is left free of suspicion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 the woman refuses his chest because all women 'feast on it in common with their eyes' (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), a sulk with a stated ground. In 1321 she grants there is no fault at all and says his tenderness itself brings the sulk on; Parimelazhagar explains this as her thought that others too might receive that same tenderness. On his reading the jealousy of 1311 survives in 1321 with its ground removed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is able to produce a sulk. In 1325 the man says that even when men are faultless (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft arms of the one they love 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The chapter opens each speaker's side with the same concession: the sulk it praises needs no offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the rare word சிறுதுனி for a brief bad spell in something good. In 1010 the short want of the renowned rich is like the rain-cloud going dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது); in 1322 the small pain that sulking brings may make his good tenderness droop (நல்லளி வாடினும்), yet it gains worth (பாடு பெறும்). In each the dryness is temporary and belongs to a giver whose giving is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1322 says tenderness may wilt (வாடினும்) under the small pain of a sulk and still come to greatness. 1304 uses the same verb for the limit: not reconciling one who has sulked is like cutting an already wilted creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதலரிந்தற்று). The wilting is harmless only if the sulk is ended; Parimelazhagar reads 1304 as the woman speaking of his other women.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1306 says love without துனி and புலவி is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று), so some quarrel is needed. 1322 names the amount: only the 'small துனி' that arises in a sulk (ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி), which leaves his tenderness greater even if it droops for a while. Parimelazhagar on 1306 takes துனி as the full-grown quarrel and புலவி as the young one, so 1322's சிறு marks the tolerable dose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"828 warns that a weapon lies hidden inside an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. 1324 finds a weapon (படை) inside the lovers' sulk, which Parimelazhagar explains as the other's bowing and humble words. Submission as a concealed weapon is a danger to be detected in false friendship and, in love, the means by which the sulk is ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1251 says the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint bolted with shame (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும் ... கதவு). 1324 uses the same verb for the resolve to keep sulking: a weapon arises that breaks the heart (உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை). What was a breach forced during separation in 1251 becomes, inside the sulk, a breaking that leads to the embrace that does not let go (புல்லி விடா).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1103","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same phrase, 'the soft arms of the one they love' (தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்). In 1103 sleeping on them is set against the world of the lotus-eyed one; in 1325 being kept away from them (அகறலின்), though one is faultless, 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The repeated phrase shows the book's last chapter finding a pleasure in the very absence of what the first union praised.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1325 says that being kept from her arms by a sulk has a pleasure of its own (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). 1307 says sulking also holds one pain: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல் என்று). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1325 after the making up and 1307 during the sulk, so the pleasure of 1325 is one judged in hindsight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1319","type":"develops","note":"In 1319 the man reports that even when he tries to appease her she is angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்), saying he is like this with others too: a complaint that he is blamed without cause. In 1325 he grants the same situation, a man without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்) kept from her arms, and finds something to enjoy in it. The groundless sulks listed in chapter 132 are re-read as a pleasure in chapter 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:944","type":"parallel_domain","note":"944 instructs: know that the earlier meal is digested (அற்றது அறிந்து) and eat only when thoroughly hungry (துவரப் பசித்து). 1326 says the digesting of what was eaten is sweeter than eating (உணலினும் உண்டது அறல் இனிது), and so sulking is sweeter than union. The chapter on medicine states as a rule of health what the man uses as his simile; Parimelazhagar on 1326 draws the same line, that one who eats when hungry eats more and with more relish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1326 ranks sulking above union: ஊடல் is sweeter than புணர்தல் for love. 1330 calls sulking love's pleasure but adds that the pleasure of the sulk itself lies in being able to come together and embrace (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). The closing verse makes the sulk's sweetness depend on the union that 1326 placed below it, as digestion has value for the next meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 says not to want gambling even if you win, since what is won (வென்றதூஉம்) is like the hook a fish has swallowed. 1327 says that in a sulk the loser is the winner (தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). The two verses reverse the apparent result of a contest in opposite directions: the gambler's win is a loss that shows later, and the lover's loss is a win that shows later in union (கூடலிற் காணப்படும்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:802","type":"same_image","note":"802 says intimate liberty is a limb of friendship and that 'being உப்பு to it' (அதற்கு உப்பாதல்) is the duty of the worthy; Parimelazhagar reads this as being pleasant toward that liberty. 1328 uses உப்பு for the savour of union after a sulk, which Parimelazhagar again glosses as sweetness. In both, salt stands for the relish that makes something a close companion does, a liberty or a sulk, enjoyable and not an offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:1238","type":"same_image","note":"In 1238 the man recalls that when he loosened the arms that embraced her, her forehead turned pale (பசந்தது ... பேதை நுதல்). In 1328 he recalls union so intense that her forehead sweated (நுதல் வெயர்ப்ப). Her forehead is the place where he reads both the first sign of separation and the fullness of union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1052","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1052 says even begging is a pleasure to a person if what is begged for comes without pain (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்). In 1329 the man wants the sulk and the night prolonged so that he may beg (யாம் இரப்ப), treating the pleading itself as something to enjoy. Begging, which chapter 107 calls degrading, is here a pleasure because the giver is not in doubt, which is the condition 1052 sets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1307 names the one pain within sulking: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்). 1329 wishes for that delay, asking that she keep sulking and the night grow long (ஊடுக ... நீடுக). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1307 during a sulk and 1329 after one has ended well, so the delay feared in the moment is desired in retrospect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says a sulk is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run long (நீள விடல்) is like a little too much. 1330 makes the sulk love's pleasure only if embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). Parimelazhagar reads 1330's condition through this measure: the sulk must be ended at the right point, between a quarrel too ripe and one too young, and that is hard to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 says the sulk's own pleasure comes if the lovers get to unite and embrace (பெறின்). 1304 states the other case: not reconciling one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a wilted creeper at the root. The conditional in 1330 is a real one, and 1304 shows what a sulk is when the condition fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:1","b":"k:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both books make wealth's coming and going a spectacle: the Kural's crowd gathering and dispersing at a show, the Naladiyar's feaster turned beggar.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:2","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from wealth's instability: act on it at once (Kural 333) or share it from the day it comes (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:3","b":"n:21","type":"same_image","note":"The king on the elephant's neck under the royal umbrella appears in both. Here he falls when his deeds turn; in 21 he simply dies like everyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:4","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds before death stops them. The Kural pictures the hiccup that silences the tongue; this quatrain the angry approach of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:5","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject 'later': the Kural says virtue is the companion that does not perish when you do, and this quatrain says those who gave first escape Death's road.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:6","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"Kural 269 says those with the power of austerity can leap past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்); this quatrain says no one here has leapt past Death and escaped. The conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's literal reading of 269; Kalaignar's figurative 'conquering death' softens it. Mudaliar cites 269 here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:8","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest புல்லறிவு; this quatrain shows the புல்லறிவாளர் at it, with wealth that flashes and vanishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:9","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that wealth neither enjoyed nor given is as good as none: in the Kural such a man has nothing, here he is thought to have lost it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:10","b":"k:1009","type":"agrees","note":"Both say wealth amassed by denying oneself (Kural தற்செற்று, here உடம்பு செற்றும்) and neglecting virtue goes to others. The Naladiyar adds the honey bee as witness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Here the wise renounce the world even in childhood. Kural 46 asks what a person living household life by virtue would gain by going the outer way. The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Parimelazhagar and Pope do, and துறந்தார் here as world-renunciation, as Shanmuganar does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"Kural 342 says renounce while there is time; this quatrain pushes the right time back to infancy, because age is certain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:17","b":"n:44","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the spear-like eye of conventional praise and undo it: 17 by age, which makes it a staff-guided eye, 44 by anatomy, which makes it a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:19","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both quote the procrastinator's words, 'we will know it later' (மற்றறிவாம் here, அன்றறிவாம் in the Kural), and reject them. The Naladiyar's reason is that the young die too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"k:38","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வீழ்நாள் படா- for a day not lost. In the Kural it is the good deed done daily; here it is the sun that never fails to rise and so measures out a life. The same words point to opposite sides of the account.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"n:7","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the sun the measuring vessel of one's days; in 7 Death does the measuring and eating, in 22 the measure alone is warning enough to do ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:23","b":"n:6","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum sounds in both: in 6 it may sound tomorrow for the hoarder, in 23 it replaces the wedding drum on the same day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:26","b":"k:340","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat the life as a temporary occupant of the body, a lodger in the Kural and a dancer in a skin bag here, so the body is not its home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:27","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"Both join seeing impermanence to ending the round of births; on Mudaliar's reading of தடுமாற்றம், the wise here resolve to end what Kural 349 says ends when attachment ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:28","b":"n:34","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rare body must be used to win its fruit before it goes; 34 names that fruit as virtue and adds the sugar-cane image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:29","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 336 says one here yesterday is gone today; this quatrain shrinks the span to a sentence, stood, sat, lay down, gone, and draws the command to act now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:30","b":"k:338","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the life leaving the body as a bird leaving its nest. The Kural makes it a comment on body and life; the Naladiyar applies it to kinsfolk, who leave the body behind for their family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:31","b":"k:37","type":"agrees","note":"Both read present fortune as the visible fruit of virtue: the Kural's palanquin rider and bearer, the Naladiyar's insider and the one at the gate. The Naladiyar adds that the insider's place is lost when the merit is not renewed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:32","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 32 says no ruin exceeds forgetting virtue (மறத்தல்); this quatrain stages the heart that forgets virtue (அறமறந்து) for wealth and asks it what it will do now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:33","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the arrival of past deeds' fruit an occasion for equanimity; the Naladiyar goes further and makes that acceptance the way out of rebirth's confusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:34","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane. In the Kural the base yield only when crushed, a reproach; here the body should be crushed for its juice, a counsel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:35","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both say virtue gathered in time stays when the body perishes; the Kural calls it an undying companion, the Naladiyar the jaggery kept when the cane refuse burns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:36","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on not knowing when death comes: the Kural mocks those who plan crores of things without knowing they will live another moment, and this quatrain answers with Death standing behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:37","b":"n:22","type":"qualifies","note":"22 urges ஒப்புரவு before one's days end; 37 warns against an ஒப்புரவு done only for the body. Together they mark the word's proper object as others, not oneself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:38","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a small good large: the Kural's millet-sized help counted as a palmyra by those who know its worth, the Naladiyar's banyan seed. The Naladiyar places the growth in the worthiness of the recipient.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:39","b":"k:334","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 334 says the day looks like a thing but is a saw cutting life, to those who see it; this quatrain describes those who do not see it, and rejoice at each day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:40","b":"k:968","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue that keeping the body alive at the cost of dignity buys nothing, since the body does not last: the Kural asks if it is a medicine, the Naladiyar makes the bargain conditional on a body that never dies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:41","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the body a புக்கில், a lodging; Mudaliar even glosses with துச்சில், the Kural's word. The Kural uses it to show the life has no home of its own; the Naladiyar to show the body is a mean hut not worth desiring.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:42","b":"k:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 80 says a body without love is bone wrapped in skin, implying love makes it more. The Naladiyar treats every body as filth wrapped in skin and allows no such exception.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:44","b":"k:1114","type":"same_image","note":"The water-lily as a figure for the eye appears in both. In Kural 1114 the lover says the lily would bow before her eyes; here the renunciant calls those who make that comparison eyeless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:45","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1113's lover calls her teeth pearls and her eyes spears; this quatrain calls those who praise teeth as pearls untaught and petty, and answers with bones in the burning ground. The conflict holds if the Kural's Book of Love is read as valuing the lover's praise, not only as dramatised speech.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:48","b":"k:913","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 913 likens the embrace of women who sell themselves to embracing a corpse in the dark; the Naladiyar extends the corpse to every admired body, which the Kural does not.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:49","b":"n:50","type":"same_image","note":"In both the skulls laugh and teach: in 49 they speak to all the living, in 50 they cure the infatuated in particular.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:50","b":"k:345","type":"agrees","note":"Both conclude that the body is not worth valuing: for those ending births even the body is excess in Kural 345, and those cured by the skulls here count themselves as nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:51","b":"n:93","type":"develops","note":"Both make good fortune last only as long as the merit behind it: in 51 evil returns when good deeds are exhausted, and in 93 wealth leaves when the deeds that sent it run out (வினையுலந்தக்கால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar quotes Kural 266 here: those who do austerity do their own work (தங்கருமம்), and the rest waste themselves. 52 names the waste as endless scholarship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:397","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 397 asks why anyone stays unlearned until death. 52 calls lifelong study of grammar and astrology madness. They are compatible only if the Kural's learning is not the instrumental lore 52 mocks; read together, 52 limits which study is worth a life.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"n:135","type":"develops","note":"Mudaliar points to 135, where learning has no shore and life is short, so one must choose what to learn. 52 draws the harder conclusion that endless study is folly when death is near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:53","b":"k:342","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge renouncing while there is time: 342 says renounce while it is still possible, and 53 has the wise renounce without delay once they see that everything fails to last.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:54","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Kural 46 asks what one would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue. 54 says the wise, seeing the pain in household pleasure, keep off the household way. Both answer whether household life is a path the wise should stay on, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict holds unless 54 is read as describing only the fools' pleasure-seeking household, not a virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:55","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press for the good before the body fails: 335 says do good before the death-rattle stops the tongue, and 55 urges the heart to set out now that youth is gone and age and disease are near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:56","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"Kural 52 says a household is nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 56 starts from the same case and adds that she can't be put away, then draws a conclusion the Kural never draws: marriage itself is to be shunned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:57","b":"k:267","type":"agrees","note":"Both make hardship the test of austerity: 267 has the ascetic shine like gold in the fire, and 57 counts as blessed only those who hold their vows when unbearable troubles strike.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:58","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar glosses the pity of Kural 157 (நோநொந்து) by quoting this quatrain: grieving that the wrongdoer may fall into the fiery hell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:59","b":"k:24","type":"agrees","note":"Both make control of the five senses the way to release: 24 calls the one who guards the five with firmness a seed for the highest ground, and 59 says the one who steers their craving reaches வீடு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"n:54","type":"agrees","note":"The two share a line and an argument. 54 ends with leaving household life and 60 with refusing to cling to pleasure as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"k:629","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie freedom from pain to not craving pleasure: 629 says one who does not want the pleasure within pleasure feels no pain within pain, and 60 has the higher ones see the pain in each pleasure and let it go.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:61","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both find the model of bearing scorn in something below us: 151 in the earth that holds up those who dig it, 61 in the fly that climbs on one's head.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:62","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Kural 969 praises those who, like the yak, give up life when honour is lost. 62 says those set on finishing their work don't give up life whenever disgrace comes. Both answer whether disgrace is a reason to die. The conflict holds if 969's மானம் covers the humiliation 62 has in mind; 62 limits itself to those with a task to complete.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:63","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both make speech a burn. In 129 the tongue's burn on the hearer never heals; in 63 careless words burn the speaker without end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:64","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with the ignorant the strength of strengths; 64 shows the excellent doing it and the base failing at it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"k:301","type":"agrees","note":"Both count restraint only where it costs something: 301 says one who checks anger where it could take effect truly checks it, and 65 that true patience belongs to one with the power to punish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"n:91","type":"agrees","note":"65 calls the gift of one without wealth the true gift; 91 promises open doors in the next world to those who give gladly even when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:66","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both praise bearing the bitter words of those who overstep. 159 likens such people to renunciants in purity; 66 credits their lineage with the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:67","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"Both make not returning harm to one who has harmed you the mark of the good. 67 adds that this is not weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:68","b":"k:29","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make the anger of the good brief. Kural 29, in Parimelazhagar's reading, adds that even while it lasts it can't be withstood; 68 stresses only that it cools of itself, leaving out its force.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:69","b":"k:987","type":"agrees","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it doesn't do good even to those who did harm; 69 says the well-born do exactly that, even to the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:71","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"Both make getting away from fools a gain: 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship profit, and 71 advises slipping away rather than talking with one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"n:64","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same situation in nearly the same words (நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்): 64 shows the excellent not growing angry, and 72 adds that the world counts retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"k:155","type":"agrees","note":"Both make public regard the measure: 155 says people count those who retaliate as nothing and keep those who forbear like gold, and 72 that the world takes retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:73","b":"k:784","type":"agrees","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke a friend who oversteps, not to laugh together. 73 values those harsh words above an outsider's pleasant ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:74","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the phrase 'fear what should be feared' (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சு-): 428 makes it the work of the wise, and 74 lists it among the marks of those who live free of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"n:76","type":"tension","note":"Adjacent quatrains on a friend who wrongs you: 75 allows quiet withdrawal once one can bear no more, and 76 forbids abandoning a close friend and says to blame oneself. The conflict holds if both concern the same wrong; it weakens if 75's தேற்றா ஒழுக்கம் is the friend's general misconduct rather than hurt done to oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid broadcasting a close friend's faults with the same verb (தூற்று-): 188 condemns those who spread even their intimates' faults, and 75 says to withdraw without doing so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:76","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that close friends are not cast off for the harm they do: 807 says those bound by love keep it even when friends bring ruin, and 76 says to blame oneself rather than leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that if one must ask, ask only the right people: 1067 pleads with beggars not to ask those who conceal, and 78 forbids telling one's want to the graceless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1064","type":"agrees","note":"1064 praises the worth that won't consent to begging even with nothing left; 78 implies the same ranking, since only those who lack the resolve to die tell their need at all.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:79","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both put blamelessness above the strongest pull: 656 forbids a deed the worthy would blame even to feed one's starving mother, and 79 forbids blameworthy pleasure even if it would never end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:80","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse to let ruin excuse wrongdoing: 205 says not to do evil because one is poor, and 80 says not to plot the ruin of the worthy even when one is ruined oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:82","b":"k:146","type":"develops","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the adulterer: enmity, sin, fear and blame. 82 keeps the same four and pairs them with four goods he loses: virtue, renown, friendship and greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:84","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife great manliness (பேராண்மை); 84 calls the act unmanliness (ஆணின்மை).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:86","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on the look (நோக்கு): 148 praises the manliness that does not look at another's wife, and 86 asks why a married man would look at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:88","b":"k:1134","type":"tension","note":"In 1134 the flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness. In 88 the strong, fearing shame, keep desire hidden until it subsides. Both make claims about whether shame can hold desire, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict depends on reading 1134 as a general claim; it is a lover's plea in the Book of Love, while 88 states a rule about the strong.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:89","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both contrast an outer burn with an inner one: in 129 the fire's wound heals but the tongue's scar does not, and in 89 arrow, fire and sun burn the body while desire burns the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"Both say desire burns where fire can't: 1159 says fire burns only when touched while love burns at a distance, and 90 that fire can be escaped in water but desire can't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1049","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same figure in another domain: 1049 says one can sleep in the midst of fire but not of poverty, and 90 that one can escape fire but not desire. Each makes fire the lesser affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:91","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that giving does not wait for wealth: 218 says those who know their duty don't falter in helping even when means are gone, and 91 rewards those who give gladly when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:92","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue from impermanence: 333 says wealth does not stay, so do what lasts at once, and 92 says death, age and disease are near, so share while wealth is in hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:93","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that guarding keeps wealth: 376 says what is not one's own won't stay however it is guarded, and 93 that wealth leaves when its merit runs out however tightly held. 376 names fate; 93 names past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:94","b":"k:270","type":"qualifies","note":"Both explain the many poor by what was not done: 270 by the few who practise austerity, and 94 in Mudaliar's reading by the failure to give in a former birth. They give different causes for the same fact without denying each other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:95","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"Both put a number on not begging: 1061 says it is worth ten million times more even when givers are glad, and 95 that it is worth twice as much as giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the giver's wealth in a fruiting tree at the town's centre, where all can reach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"1008 keeps the tree in the town's centre but makes the miser's wealth a poison tree fruiting there; 96 moves the miser's barren palm out to the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:211","type":"develops","note":"211 makes the rain the model of help that seeks no return. 97 sets rain and the good's giving side by side as the two things the world can't survive without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:19","type":"qualifies","note":"In 19 giving depends on rain: if the sky fails, giving and austerity cease. In 97 the two are parallel supports, each needed on its own.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:98","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Both say giving to those who can repay is not giving but lending: 221 calls it a measured return (குறியெதிர்ப்பை), and 98 a profitable loan (பொலிகடன்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:99","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both turn on small things adding up: in 475 peacock feathers, piled high enough, break a cart's axle, and in 99 small gifts fill the bowl. One warns, the other encourages.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:100","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"Both make giving the source of lasting praise: 232 says all that speakers praise rests on those who give to the asker, and 100 that the word 'they gave' is heard through three worlds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"k:208","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a deed's consequence inseparable from its doer, with different images: the Kural's ruin stays at the heels like a shadow, and the Naladiyar's deed goes looking like a calf for its mother.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"n:120","type":"develops","note":"101 says the deed finds its doer among many; 120 says it is the only thing that goes with him at death. Together they make the deed both pursuer and sole companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:102","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from impermanent wealth: do the lasting good at once. 102 adds beauty, youth and standing to the list and condemns the life that does nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:103","b":"k:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that enjoyment is measured out in advance: the Kural even for those who amass millions, the Naladiyar for all who want prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 104 says that even sages cannot remove what is bound to befall. 269 says that those who attain the power of austerity can even leap past death. Both concern whether ascetic power can overcome what is coming; the tension holds if உறுவர் are ascetics and 269's கூற்றம் is fated death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"104 says no one can remove what is destined, as no one can make rain fall; 620 says those who strive without flagging see even fate's back. The tension depends on reading 620 as effort overcoming fate, as Pope and Kalaignar do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:105","b":"n:3","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the great brought low and give the same cause: in 3 the elephant-riders fall when their deeds are spent, and in 105 the palm-sized become millet-sized through former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:106","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that the unlearned truly live: the Kural says they can only be said to exist, like barren land, and the Naladiyar says Death will not even take them, as husks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:107","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"107 makes begging the outworking of the beggar's own former deeds, so the order that produces it is just. 1062 calls down ruin on whoever ordained that anyone should live by begging, treating it as an unjust order. The tension depends on 107's vinai being the beggar's own deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:108","b":"k:834","type":"qualifies","note":"Both describe one who knows and still does wrong. The Kural calls him the greatest fool; the Naladiyar traces the act to former deeds, which explains it without removing the blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:109","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a person's will has no say in what comes: in the Kural, care cannot keep what is not one's own; here, wishing cannot keep off what is due.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:110","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both end a chapter on fate by asking why anyone grieves when the bad arrives, since it is as fixed as the good.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes killing a kindness received the sin with no escape. On both commentators' reading, 111 names something worse: a promise held out and then broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"223 praises never uttering 'I have nothing' and giving instead. 111 allows that saying 'no' is blameless when one truly cannot give; the fault lies in false promising.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:112","b":"k:514","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy and unworthy never fall from their natures at any time. 514 says that many people, though trusted after every test, change through the nature of the work. The tension holds if 514 means people really change, as Parimelazhagar says, rather than that the tests misjudged them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:113","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"113 observes that kin vanish in trouble; 796 turns that into a benefit, saying ruin is a rod that measures one's people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"k:760","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that whoever secures wealth gets the other two aims of life as well. The Naladiyar adds the cost of failing: suffering like being boiled.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"n:1","type":"qualifies","note":"114 makes wealth the key to virtue and pleasure; 1, opening the book, shows the rich reduced to begging for gruel and says wealth should not be counted on. Wealth secures the other two only while it lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:115","b":"k:1046","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the poor are not heard however sound their words. The Naladiyar adds the other half: the rich are heard even when unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:116","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe study that leaves a person unrestrained. The Kural calls such a person the greatest fool; the Naladiyar says it is their nature, as bitterness is the gourd's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"117 says to scorn those who scorn you; 151 says that bearing with them, as the earth bears those who dig it, is the highest thing. Both concern the same situation. The tension depends on reading 117 as advice, as Mudaliar, Shanmuganar and Pope do, and not as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"n:72","type":"tension","note":"Within the Naladiyar: 72 says that when unworthy people speak improperly, bearing it in silence is fitting and the world thinks retaliation shameful. 117 says to scorn the scorners first. The tension holds if 117's scorners and 72's speakers are the same kind of person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:118","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"118 says virtue is one in nature whatever form its ways take. 34 says what that one thing is: a spotless mind, everything else being show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:120","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"36 makes virtue the companion that does not die with you. 120 says the only thing that goes along is the deed, which includes bad deeds; so the companion can also be a burden.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 121 makes household life itself the sorrow in which those who fail to renounce end as corpses. 46 asks what anyone would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue for the other path. The tension depends on Mudaliar's gloss of துக்கம்; on Shanmuganar's it is weaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:251","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating flesh. The Kural asks how one who fattens his flesh on others' can have grace; the Naladiyar makes his belly a burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:122","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both say harm done to others returns to the doer. The Naladiyar makes the return copy the harm: those who caged birds wear irons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:123","b":"k:330","type":"agrees","note":"Both explain a wretched diseased body as the result of having taken life. The Naladiyar names the creature and the disease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a person takes on the nature of their company, the Kural with water changing with the soil and the Naladiyar with ghee turning to flame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:112","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy never fall from their nature, as jaggery is never bitter. 124 says even the upright turn to cruel deeds when they join the wicked. Both concern whether good character can be changed; the tension holds if 124's கோடார் are truly good, as Mudaliar's நடுவுநிலைமையுடையார் implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:139","type":"develops","note":"124 shows company corrupting the upright; 139 shows it improving the unlearned, like a new pot taking on the scent of flowers. The same mechanism works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:125","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the waxing and waning moon for good and bad friendship. The Kural sets the wise against fools, and the Naladiyar the great against the small.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:126","b":"k:792","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn of friendship formed without examination; the Kural says it ends in mortal grief, the Naladiyar finds a snake where sandal was expected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"127 says no one can know another's mind, because minds and deeds differ. 706 says the face shows what is uppermost in the heart, as crystal shows what is near it. The tension depends on taking 127 as a general claim rather than a warning about deceivers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:702","type":"qualifies","note":"702 grants that someone may know another's mind without doubt, but calls such a person equal to a god. 127 says no one has that power, which agrees that it is beyond ordinary people.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:128","b":"k:821","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friendship without inner consent. The Kural calls it an anvil waiting for the right moment to strike; the Naladiyar says it becomes a stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a bright sword in the wrong hands. In the Kural it is learning held by one who fears the assembly; in the Naladiyar it is one's own strength lost through association with the unlearned.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer distance from fools to closeness: the Kural rates the enmity of the wise far above a fool's intimacy, and the Naladiyar says the good course is to get away from the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:130","b":"k:61","type":"tension","note":"61 says no gain is known greater than children who learn what should be known. 130 rebukes the heart for pining for children and says there is no gain for the soul but good done. The tension softens if 61 speaks of worldly gains and 130 only of the soul's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:131","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that outward beauty is worth anything without learning: the Kural compares it to a well-made clay doll, and the Naladiyar refuses it the name of beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:400","type":"agrees","note":"Both say learning does not perish when other possessions do. The Naladiyar spells out the ways it differs from wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:398","type":"qualifies","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 132 says learning lasts as long as its owner lives; 398 says learning gained in one birth protects through seven. Shanmuganar's reading, that it lasts through fame, is closer to the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank the low-born learned above the unlearned, the Kural by comparing them to the high-born unlearned and the Naladiyar with salt from brackish soil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"n:142","type":"qualifies","note":"133 sets the low-born learned in the highest place; 142 says excellence, gentleness and good conduct belong only to the high-born, whatever wealth others gain. Read together, learning can raise one's rank but, in 142, not those qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:134","b":"k:67","type":"agrees","note":"Both make learning the best thing a father gives his children: the Kural as a place at the front of the assembly, the Naladiyar as an inheritance that cannot be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:135","b":"k:391","type":"agrees","note":"Both insist on learning what is to be learned. The Naladiyar adds the reason: learning is endless and life is short.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:136","b":"k:423","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the worth of what is taught does not depend on who teaches it: the Kural says to see the truth in whatever is heard from anyone, and the Naladiyar says to learn from the learned whatever their caste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:137","b":"k:394","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the meeting of the learned a delight. The Kural describes how they part; the Naladiyar ranks their gathering above heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:138","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that friendship with people of character grows sweeter with time, the Kural like a book reread and the Naladiyar like cane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:139","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say understanding takes on the nature of one's company, the Kural with water and soil and the Naladiyar with water and flowers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:140","b":"k:356","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the purpose of learning the perception of what is true, which leads out of rebirth on Mudaliar's reading of 140. The Naladiyar adds that worldly learning cannot do this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:141","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both use an animal that will not go against its nature in extremity: the yak that dies when it loses a hair, and the lion that will not eat grass. The Kural speaks of honour, the Naladiyar of the principles of the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:142","b":"k:952","type":"agrees","note":"Both name three qualities the well-born never lose. The Kural's are conduct, truthfulness and shame; the Naladiyar's are fullness of character, gentleness and conduct, and it adds that wealth cannot buy them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:144","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a fault in the well-born is seen by everyone, the Kural like the spot on the moon and the Naladiyar as blame spread by many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both call people who lack a human moral quality trees or wood: in the Kural those without courtesy, however sharp, and in the Naladiyar the low-born who lack the four fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:951","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a moral fear or shame is found naturally only in the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"Both list four qualities of a good lineage, and both include giving and sweet speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"146 says that good company, sweet speech, giving and goodness of mind are found only among the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the deeds one does. The two disagree about whether excellence follows birth or deeds. The tension holds if 972's distinction means worth of character, as Pope and Kalaignar take it, and if 146's qualities belong to that worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:147","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"219 says that, for a good man, poverty is being unable to do what should be done. 147 says the well-born still do it even in hardship, so for them poverty in that sense never fully arrives.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:148","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"The two share the phrase ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்: those who know their duty, or the well-born, do not slacken in serving others when they lack the means. The Naladiyar adds the half-eclipsed moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:149","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base do not give even when they could: in the Kural they won't shake off their wet hands for anyone but those who would break their jaws.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:150","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the well-born keep their generous nature when their means have shrunk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the spot on the moon. In 957 the fault of the well-born shows as plainly as the moon's spot; in 151 the noble differ from the moon in being unable to live with the spot at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both make honour a matter of life and death: in 969 the honourable give up life like the yak that loses its hair, and in 151 the noble waste away at a single stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:152","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"Aim high regardless of outcome: 596 says a lofty aim, even if thwarted, keeps its worth, and 152 says the arrow that misses a lion beats the one that kills a jackal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:153","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Want does not license wrongdoing. 656 holds even if one's mother starves; 153 holds even when one's own body is reduced to sinew.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"785 says long acquaintance is not needed for friendship, only shared feeling; 154 shows the worthy treating a single meeting as an old bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:791","type":"tension","note":"791 warns that nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, since the true friend cannot later withdraw. 154 praises binding someone seen once as if an old friend. The tension holds if 154 means any passer-by; Pope's parenthetical '(worthy) men' softens it, but the Tamil has no such qualifier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with fools the strength of strengths; 155 shows that strength as patient listening to an ignorant speaker so as not to shame him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"403 puts the burden on the unlearned: they are good if they stay silent before the learned. 155 takes the case where they do not, and puts the burden on the learned to endure it kindly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane, to opposite ends. In 1078 the base are like cane, giving only when crushed; in 156 the well-born are like cane, still sweet however crushed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"159 makes bearing harsh words from the insolent as pure as renunciation; 156 makes it the mark of good birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:157","b":"k:139","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the virtuous cannot speak evil even by a slip: வழுக்கியும் in 139, தள்ளியும் in 157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife both virtue and complete conduct; 158 makes that blindness one of three abstentions that leave nothing more to teach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:181","type":"agrees","note":"Both give one abstention near-sufficient moral weight: 181 says it is good to be called one who does not backbite even if one does wrong, and 158 counts silence in backbiting among the restraints that make virtue-teaching unnecessary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:224","type":"qualifies","note":"224 admits that being asked is painful until one sees the asker's glad face; 159 describes the excellent as treating the prospect of being asked as good from the outset.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"1057 is the asker's side: the heart rejoices at givers who do not scorn. 159 is the giver's side: the excellent honour those who may ask instead of scorning them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:160","b":"k:443","type":"agrees","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of all things; 160 calls meeting the well-born a treasure cave, against the base who chase the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:700","type":"parallel_domain","note":"700 warns a minister that doing improper things on the strength of long familiarity with the king brings ruin. 161 makes the same point about presuming on the forbearance of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:896","type":"agrees","note":"896 says one may survive fire but not a lasting offence against the great; 161 says the damage of offending them cannot be undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:162","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls winning the great the rarest of things. 162 imagines that rarity handed over free and wasted by people who cannot use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:163","b":"n:165","type":"develops","note":"Both locate worth in the judgment of the worthy. 163 dismisses the verdict of the base, and 165 dismisses one's own verdict on oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:895","type":"agrees","note":"895: those who have drawn a fierce king's anger exist nowhere, wherever they go. This matches Mudaliar's reading of 164, where the angered great are kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"900: even those with overwhelming support perish if the eminent are angered. This matches the reading of 164 in which the great are the morally great and the fortress is no defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:165","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself by admiring itself; 165 gives the words of that self-admiration and denies it the name of esteem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:166","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"The same claim with a different measure of time: 782 has the waxing and waning moon, 166 the shortening and lengthening shadow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:167","b":"k:691","type":"qualifies","note":"691 advises those who serve kings to stay neither too near nor too far, like people warming at a fire. 167 observes that those who stay close are the ones who enjoy a king's wealth, without the warning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:839","type":"tension","note":"839 says, with irony, that friendship with fools is sweet because parting from them brings no pain. 168 says parting even from the undiscerning brings great sorrow. The two make opposite claims about the pain of parting from fools, if தெரிவிலார் is read as fools, as Mudaliar's gloss அறிவிலார் has it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:816","type":"qualifies","note":"Both end with கோடி உறும். 816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool, a ranking of companions. 168 drops the ranking: mixing with no one is ten million times better.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:168","b":"n:174","type":"tension","note":"168 concludes that not mixing with anyone is far better because of the pain of parting. 174 says no one would hate even painful birth if they could keep friendship with the noble. One makes withdrawal the remedy for life's pain, the other makes companionship the remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:169","b":"k:38","type":"agrees","note":"Both count days: 38 says letting no day fall idle without good blocks the road of births, and 169 says the good have no day without learning, visiting the great or giving. Both use படாஅ for days that do not occur.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness as bowing: பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை in 978, பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை in 170.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"226 says removing the hunger of the destitute is where one should store wealth; 170 says only those who relieve their dependants' distress are truly wealthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:171","b":"k:455","type":"agrees","note":"455 says purity of mind and deed both lean on purity of company; 171 shows company acting on past deeds as well, clearing the faults of youth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 calls the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; 172 closes its six precepts with taking in the words of the great every day.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both commend bearing others' harsh words; 159 ranks those who do it with renunciants, 172 lists it among the basic precepts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:173","b":"k:362","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat birth as the thing to escape: 362 says if one must want, want not to be born, and 173 seeks out those who know birth is bitter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"n:173","type":"qualifies","note":"173 declares birth bitter from the start. 174, placed next to it, grants that and adds that friendship with the good makes birth something no one would resent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"k:460","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no greater support than good company. 174 raises the stakes: good company is what reconciles a person to being born at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:175","b":"k:452","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water changed by what it joins. In 452 water takes on the nature of its soil, as a mind takes on its company; in 175 sewage takes the name of the sacred river, as the lowly take the standing of the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:176","b":"n:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the moon's dark spot. In 151 the moon tolerates its flaw and the noble cannot; in 176 the flaw is worshipped because it sits in the moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:177","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"Milk and its mixture, in opposite directions: in 1000 good milk is spoiled by a bad vessel, as wealth by a graceless owner; in 177 water is absorbed into milk, as the small into the great.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:178","b":"k:446","type":"agrees","note":"446: enemies can do nothing to one who walks with the worthy. 178 gives the image of grass the plough cannot uproot because it grows against a stump.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:179","b":"k:458","type":"agrees","note":"458 says that even the worthy, whose minds are good, are protected by good company. 179 gives the converse: their worth is wrecked by bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"k:453","type":"agrees","note":"453 says what a person is called comes from their company; 180 says the pure in mind are scorned because of theirs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"n:177","type":"develops","note":"The same mechanism run both ways: in 177 the mean are hidden by the great, in 180 the pure are blackened by the bad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:181","b":"k:1050","type":"agrees","note":"1050 says the destitute who do not renounce merely consume salt and gruel. 181 has someone who can no longer give conclude that renunciation is the fitting course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest understanding; 182 gives the example of the householder who thinks himself settled for good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic way. 182 calls those content in home life fools and praises those who see its supports as passing. The conflict holds on Mudaliar's reading, where the failing supports are household attachments themselves; if 182 targets only heedlessness, the two are compatible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:183","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds now because the body may fail without warning: 335 before the death-hiccup stops the tongue, 183 because the body changes without cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a village water source for the generous: 215 compares a wise man's wealth to a full town tank, 184 compares the great in decline to a spring-fed well that still serves in drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"218 says those who know their duty do not slacken in serving others even when they have no means; 184 says the great keep giving in decline.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:184","type":"same_image","note":"Consecutive water images for giving in decline: a spring-fed well in 184, a dry riverbed that yields to digging in 185.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:153","type":"develops","note":"Both end with செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை. In 153 the poor worthy do their duty within their means; in 185 the once-rich keep doing it after ruin, for fewer people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955: even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family keeps its nature. 185 says the same of the ruined giver who still gives to a few.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:186","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the moon's spot; 186 says the same with a brand on a white bull, and adds that the crimes of the small go unseen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:187","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816: being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than intimacy with a fool. 187 goes further: even enmity with the wise brings dignity, while intimacy with the mean brings grief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it cannot do good even to those who do harm. 188 says to meet foes with terror and the false with falseness. The conflict is direct on Mudaliar's reading of சலவர் as liars; on Pope's reading the third clause is milder, but the clause on foes still conflicts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:830","type":"agrees","note":"830 advises friendship of the face, not the heart, with a foe who turns friendly. 188 likewise answers each person in their own coin rather than with uniform goodness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"360 says suffering ends when desire, anger and delusion perish. On Shanmuganar's reading of the three faults, 190 offers a practice that removes them: feeding others before oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"227: hunger does not touch one who habitually shares food. 190 says sharing the first-served food keeps the worthy from sorrow till the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:191","b":"k:615","type":"agrees","note":"615 calls the one who prefers work to pleasure a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles. On Shanmuganar's reading of 191, kin rest easy on what the energetic one provides.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:192","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says effort brings the greatness the task requires; 192 shows it as a twig hardening into a post strong enough for an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"618 says bad fortune is no disgrace but failing to work is; 193 tells the knowledgeable not to scorn humble work when fortune is thin.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"n:198","type":"qualifies","note":"198 says the lion-hearted would die rather than do anything disgraceful; 193 says even the tiger eats a frog when hungry. Read together, humble work is not disgrace, and dishonourable deeds are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"k:619","type":"agrees","note":"619 says effort pays its wage even when fate denies the goal; 194 makes persistence through failure the very definition of manliness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"n:152","type":"agrees","note":"Both begin from success or failure (இசையும் எனினும் இசையாதெனினும் in 152, இசையாதெனினும் in 194) and judge a person by how they act regardless of outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972: birth is alike for all beings, and distinction differs by the work done. 195 says good and bad caste are mere words and lineage is made by austerity, learning and effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"951 says integrity and shame exist together naturally only in the well-born. 195 says good and bad lineage are words without substance. Both concern whether birth carries real moral qualities, and they answer opposite ways unless 951's இற்பிறந்தார் is read as a lineage made by conduct, as 195 defines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading of 179, the worthy are formed by the family they are born into, as paddy by its soil; 195 says good and bad lineage are only words. On Mudaliar's reading of குலம் in 179 as one's company, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:663","type":"agrees","note":"663 says true manliness lets a deed be known only when finished, since revealing it midway brings grief; 196 says the perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:701","type":"agrees","note":"701 calls one who reads what is unsaid an ornament to the world; 196 says the world is under the sign of those who read others' drive from their features.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a structure eaten at the base and propped up. In 1030 a family whose foot is cut by trouble falls if no good person props it; in 197 the son props the decaying father like banyan roots.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"70 says a son repays his father by making people wonder what penance the father did. 197 gives a more practical repayment: covering the father's weakness when he falters.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656: even seeing one's mother starve, do not do what the worthy condemn. 198: even dying in want at home, the strong do not do what brings disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both set death against dishonour: 969's yak dies when it loses its hair, 198's lion-hearted would die in want rather than be disgraced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:199","b":"k:601","type":"agrees","note":"601 says the lamp of lineage dies when sloth covers it; 199 says high birth is worth nothing without great manliness, like a flower that has lost its scent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same claim: 1065 says even watery gruel got by one's own effort (தாள்தந்தது) is the sweetest food, and 200 says water won by one's own effort (தாளாண்மை) becomes nectar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"n:296","type":"develops","note":"In 200 the Mutharaiyar are patrons whose feasts the base enjoy; in 296 the poor who never go begging to the rich are themselves called Mutharaiyar. The patron's name moves from giver to the self-reliant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:201","b":"k:521","type":"develops","note":"521 says that only kin keep honouring old ties when a man has lost everything; 201 describes what that loyalty does inside the sufferer, likening it to a mother forgetting the pains of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:202","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the good man a fruiting tree that the many enjoy. 216 says his wealth is like a tree ripening in the middle of town; 202 adds the shade tree and insists he toils for it himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:203","b":"k:1027","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the burden of those who depend on a household on its capable members: 1027 compares them to the brave in battle, 203 to a branch that never fails to bear its own fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:204","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both contrast a friendship that holds or grows with one that soon fails; 782 uses the waxing and waning moon, 204 the ascetic's unswerving path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:205","b":"k:790","type":"agrees","note":"Both distrust speech that labels a relation, and their wording is close (இனையர், இன்னர்/இன்னம்). 790 says friendship looks shabby when friends describe what they are to each other; 205 says the best people never sort others into ours and theirs.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Both say that plain gruel (கூழ், புற்கை) shared with one's own is sweeter than the finest food. The Kural says it is sweeter than ambrosia when one's children's little hands have stirred it; 206 says it is sweeter than a golden-dish feast from those who are not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"n:217","type":"same_image","note":"The kin chapter and the chapter on testing friendship use the same contrast of poor food given with love and rich food given without it. 217 ends in the bitter strychnos fruit, 206 and 210 in neem.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:207","b":"k:90","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that hospitality without warmth spoils the meal for the guest. 90 says a guest withers like the anicham flower at an averted face; 207 says the punctual feast in an unloving house tastes of neem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:208","b":"k:799","type":"agrees","note":"Both single out those who leave when ruin comes: 208 compares them to tongs that let go in the fire, and 799 says the memory of such friends burns the heart at the hour of death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:209","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 defines friendship partly as sharing the suffering in ruin; 209 says a friendship that shares only joys can do nothing even in the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"n:138","type":"same_image","note":"A near doublet: both compare friendship with the learned to eating sugarcane from the tip and the other kind to eating it from the root. 138 ends on those without character or kindness, 211 on those without sweetness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say friendship with the right people grows sweeter with use. 783 compares it to a book whose delight grows with study; 211 to sugarcane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"n:245","type":"tension","note":"212 relies on family as the only workable guarantee that a friend won't change, since minds can't be known. 245 says people are not like their இனம் but like their minds. The tension holds if 245's இனம் includes family, as Mudaliar's 'இனத்தார்' allows; if it means only the company one keeps, the two are about different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"k:793","type":"qualifies","note":"793 tells one to know a man's qualities, family, faults and connections before befriending him. 212 keeps family but doubts the rest: the mind can't be known, so family is the one dependable ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:213","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both prize love that survives injury from the friend: 807 says those bound by love don't stop loving even when the friend ruins them, and 213 pictures the dog wagging its tail with the spear in its body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:214","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a shared heart, not meeting or long acquaintance, makes friendship. 214 adds the reverse case: long absence doesn't break it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:215","b":"k:425","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a flower's opening and closing for inconstant friendship. 425 says understanding is not opening and closing; 215 contrasts tree flowers that stay open with pond flowers that close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:216","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"104 says those who know its value count a millet-sized help as palmyra-sized. On Mudaliar's reading the palmyra in 216 stands for the best friends, who form a great friendship from one small help.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"n:187","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer hostility from the right people to friendship with the wrong. 187 says the enmity of the wise brings dignity; 219 says enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool. 219 goes further: open enmity is better than a fool's friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"k:791","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue for testing before befriending because a formed bond can't be cleanly undone. 791 gives the reason as the friend's own loyalty; 220 gives it as the pain of parting, even from a snake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"n:168","type":"agrees","note":"Both say parting from a bad bond still hurts. 168 concludes that it is best to mix with no one; 220 concludes that one must test long before mixing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"n:75","type":"qualifies","note":"75 says to bear a friend's unkind conduct as far as one can and, if one cannot, to withdraw without speaking ill. 221 offers no such exit: keep them and keep their faults inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for a charitable account of a friend's wrongs. 805 says to put them down to folly or great intimacy; 221 says every good thing has its husk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:222","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both make forbearance toward those who damage you an image from the land: the earth bears those who dig into it (151), and the farmer re-dams the stream that keeps breaking through (222).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:223","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use an old friendship is if one doesn't accept a friend's liberties as one's own acts. 223 puts the same demand as a maxim: one person's patience keeps two in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:224","b":"k:799","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a wrong friendship as burning the heart. In 799 it is the memory of friends who deserted one; in 224 it is a friend one cannot leave who lacks good nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:225","b":"n:224","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent quatrains turn fire both ways: in 224 a bad friend is a fire lit to burn the heart, and in 225 the fire that burned the house is still needed daily, so the friend who hurt one is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:226","b":"n:76","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid casting off a close friend for the hurt they cause. 76 says to blame oneself, since even beasts find parting hard; 226 says no one cuts off the hand that poked their eye.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"980 says greatness hides others' faults and smallness only tells them. 227 applies this to friends and ranks the teller below the wrongdoer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what those who spread the faults of their intimates would do to strangers. 227 makes the same judgement on those who publish a friend's wrongs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:228","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both reinterpret a friend's hurtful act. 805 reads it as folly or intimacy; 228 says it becomes good once held in the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:229","b":"k:830","type":"qualifies","note":"830 says to befriend a former enemy with the face, let go in the heart, and then drop the friendship. 229 also hides an inner judgement behind outward regard, but toward a friend who has proved false, and it never says to drop him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:230","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"504 tells one to examine a person's qualities and faults and take what predominates. 230 forbids that examination once the friendship is made, so the two divide the labour: examine before, never after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:230","b":"n:238","type":"same_image","note":"Both are first-person curses with the same frame: may I go where the worst betrayer of a friend goes, while the world laughs. 230 names the one who did not keep a friend's secret, and 238 the one who violated a friend's wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"k:812","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friends who stay while there is something to gain. 812 asks what it matters whether one keeps or loses them; 231 shows them putting up with discomfort until their business is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"n:284","type":"agrees","note":"284 has crowds gathering like crows while wealth lasts and no one asking after you when it goes; 231 shows the same self-interest from inside the friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:232","b":"k:811","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the friendship of the worthless is barren even at its fullest. 811 says it is better shrinking than growing; 232 says even at its height it is like failed rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"n:137","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the company of the learned against heaven. 137 doubts heaven could be sweeter; 233 makes the comparison explicit and adds that bad company is one of the hells.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the company one keeps at the extremes: no greater support than good company and no greater affliction than bad (460), heaven and hell (233).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:234","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe a friendship that seems to grow and then shrinks: the moon past full (782), fire in straw (234).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:235","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"818 says to let slip the friendship of those who make difficulties of what they could do. 235 names the same delay, with empty promising, and says it brings suffering even to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"n:177","type":"qualifies","note":"177 says the smallness of the small stops showing when they join the great, as water in milk looks like milk. 236 says their deeds stay different though they grow up beside the great. Read together, association changes how the small look, not what they do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say an outside good doesn't change an inner nature: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they study, and 236 says the same of their friendship with the large-natured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:237","b":"k:825","type":"agrees","note":"Both make inner accord the condition of friendship. 825 says not to trust the words of those not one with you in mind; 237 says friendship with them is painful.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:238","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 says friendship shares the suffering when ruin comes. 238 raises the stake to one's own life and curses the friend who holds back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:239","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"797 calls leaving the friendship of fools a gain. 239 describes the reverse exchange, leaving the discerning for the petty-minded, as ghee swapped for neem oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:244","type":"qualifies","note":"240 says the discerning who take up with bad company are ruined; 244 says the good-natured don't turn bad in mind though their company is bad. On Mudaliar's reading of 240 as ruin, the two fit: such men may keep their minds and still lose everything else, as 180 says of the sandal tree in a forest fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:179","type":"agrees","note":"Both say bad company ruins the good: 179 compares it to a storm wrecking a ship, and 240 to a cobra mating with a viper.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:773","type":"agrees","note":"773 calls fierceness valour and kindness to a foe who has fallen (ஊராண்மை) its keen edge. 241 says the worthy feel shame to attack a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:879","type":"tension","note":"879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, before it can wound the cutter; 241 says the worthy don't attack an enemy at his weakest. Both concern whether to strike a foe while he is weak. The tension holds if 879's thorn tree is an enemy, as Parimelazhagar reads it in the chapter on enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"n:148","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the eclipse snake and the moon. In 148 the moon still lights the world while the snake holds half of it, an image of the nobly born in poverty; in 241 the snake spares the crescent, an image of the worthy sparing a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:963","type":"tension","note":"963 says humility suits prosperity and standing tall suits adversity; 242 says restraint is the ornament of the poor and that living without humility shames one's family. Both concern how the poor should bear themselves. The tension depends on taking 963's உயர்வு as not bowing, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, and 242's அடக்கம் as bowing; if 242 condemns only living beyond one's means, they fit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says humility is good for all and an extra wealth for the rich. 242 makes it the ornament of the poor.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:243","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that origin decides worth: 972 says all are alike in birth and differ by their deeds, and 243 says the next world comes from oneself, not from one's region.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:243","b":"n:195","type":"agrees","note":"195 calls good and bad caste mere words and puts worth in austerity, learning and effort; 243 makes the same claim about the region of one's birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"179 says goodness perishes when it comes near bad company, as a storm wrecks a ship; 244 says the good-natured do not turn bad in mind though their company is bad. Both concern whether bad company corrupts the good, and the commentators read 244's இனம் as company, which makes the conflict direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"k:458","type":"qualifies","note":"458 says that even those with full goodness of mind are strengthened by good company. 244 says the good-natured hold firm without it, even among bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:245","b":"k:452","type":"tension","note":"The same image of water leads to opposite claims. 452 says water changes with the soil, so a person's understanding takes on the nature of their company (454 adds that it only seems to be in the mind); 245 says sweet water rises by the sea, so people are as their minds, not their kind. The tension holds if 245's இனம் means the people around one, as the image of surroundings suggests. Shanmuganar's 'not only by their group' softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:246","b":"n:215","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn friendship that comes and goes. 215 contrasts the tree flower with the pond flower; 246 says people of steady mind don't leave and rejoin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"n:168","type":"tension","note":"168 says parting even from those without discernment brings great pain, so it is best to mix with none; 247 says that parting from the undiscerning is how the pain parts. Both concern whether leaving the undiscerning hurts, and they share the phrase தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா. The tension rests on reading 247 as both commentators and Pope do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie joy to good company and affliction to bad: no greater support than good company and nothing more afflicting than bad (460).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"n:192","type":"agrees","note":"192 says a pliant twig becomes a post for tethering elephants, and so does a life if a man makes himself without slackening; 248 says a person alone raises or lowers himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 248 makes the self the agent of rising and falling.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:249","b":"n:193","type":"agrees","note":"Both permit the capable to stoop for a purpose: the tiger eats a frog when there is no other meat (193), and the great walk behind the unlearned for a task (249).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:249","b":"k:967","type":"qualifies","note":"967 says it is better to be ruined than to live by following those who scorn you. 249 allows following the unlearned for the sake of a task, which marks a limit on that pride: following for a purpose is not following for a living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"n:114","type":"develops","note":"114 names the three ends of life and says gaining the middle one, wealth, brings the other two. 250 pictures a life that completes all three without hindrance, as a ship reaching port.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"k:754","type":"agrees","note":"754 says rightly gained wealth yields virtue and pleasure. 250 counts the life that achieves work, pleasure and virtuous giving together as a voyage completed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"Both call want of understanding the true poverty. Kural 841 adds that the world does not count other lack as lack; 251 adds the image of jewels on one who lacks the essential thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:402","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a person lacking the marks of a sex to picture a lack that ornament cannot cover: the unlearned wanting to speak (Kural), the senseless rich in their jewels (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate wealth from wisdom. The Kural states it as two natures of the world under fate; 252 makes a comic myth of the same fact, the two goddesses quarrelling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"n:266","type":"develops","note":"252 explains in jest why Fortune avoids the learned; 266 turns on her and curses her for going to the base and leaving the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:253","b":"k:405","type":"agrees","note":"Both show the unlearned exposed in company. In the Kural his standing wilts as he speaks; here he is asked to read and answers with a stick.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:403","type":"tension","note":"Kural 403 says the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned; 254 says that even sitting quietly among the wise, such a man is a dog sitting there. Same situation, opposite verdict. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's plain reading of 403 (silence keeps them from exposure and may lead them to learning), not if 403 is taken as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:840","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the fool's mere presence among the worthy a defilement: unwashed feet on a bed (Kural), a dog sitting among them (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:255","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well not to speak in a mean assembly. 255 adds the reason, that the hearers would not take the meaning in, and pictures the low talking freely there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:256","b":"k:642","type":"agrees","note":"Both use சோர்வு for the lapse in speech to be guarded against. The Kural says it because gain and ruin come from speech; 256 shows the learned guarding against it by silence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:257","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Good teaching spilt where it is wasted: nectar spilt in a dirty yard (Kural), mango juice pressed into a pig's trough (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:258","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say teaching cannot override what a person already is: in the Kural one's own understanding prevails over many books; here understanding cannot be driven into a body without merit. On the commentators' reading both rest on past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:260","b":"n:255","type":"develops","note":"Both use புன்கோட்டி, the petty assembly. 255 shows the low talking in such assemblies; 260 shows how one is founded, by a man who rejects the learned and finds his like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:261","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture the ungiving man's wealth as a fruiting tree no one can use: a poison tree in the middle of town (Kural), a wood-apple near at hand that the bats ignore (here). Parimelazhagar also stresses that the man is near the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture wealth as drinking water. The Kural's wise man's wealth is the full village tank; here the rich man is the undrinkable sea and the giver the small sweet well.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:264","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that being rich and being wise are separate fortunes. 264 makes the sharper point that the senseless live in silk while the wise are passed over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:265","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"Kural 169 says the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright are to be pondered. 265 gives the answer such pondering reaches: the fruit of old deeds, and nothing else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:267","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"Both invite wonder at poverty staying with the good and wealth with the unworthy. Neither verse explains it; Parimelazhagar and 265 explain it by past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:266","b":"k:179","type":"tension","note":"Kural 179 says Fortune knows the way and goes to the wise who understand virtue; 266 says she leaves the good and goes to the worthless. Both speak of the goddess choosing whom to join. The tension holds if 179 is a claim about how fortune actually goes, as Parimelazhagar takes it, and not only a promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:270","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not make one learned without understanding: the Kural names that understanding as living in accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"n:275","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the sea against a small well. In 263 people seek out the sweet well as they seek out givers; in 275 they wait at a nearly dry well, as the poverty of the worthy is better than wealth that forgets the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:271","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating alone to keep one's store full. The Kural ranks it more bitter than begging; 271 shuts the door of the next world on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:272","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject putting virtue off: the Kural's அன்று அறிவாம் என்னாது ('without saying we'll see to it later') answers the very words of 272's delayers, பின் அறிதும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:273","b":"k:1001","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the man who stores wealth, does not use it and dies: the Kural says he achieved nothing, 273 that his own wealth laughs at him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:274","b":"k:1007","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the miser's wealth to a beautiful young woman, with opposite outcomes: in the Kural she grows old alone, unused; here she is enjoyed in due season by a stranger. Unused or lost, the miser has none of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:276","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, whatever they hold. 276 makes it a joke in which the speaker owns the miser's wealth as much as he does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty. 277 names troubles poverty spares, but only in comparison with the ungiving rich, so it limits 1041 rather than contradicting it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:278","b":"n:274","type":"develops","note":"274 says the miser's wealth goes to a stranger; 278 follows it to the heirs, who repeat his avarice, so the wealth does no good in either generation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:279","b":"k:1078","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base yield only when crushed: like sugar-cane (Kural), like a cow milked by force (here). Both use கொல்ல for the crushing. The Kural's worthy give at a word; here the giver yields like a cow to its calf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:280","b":"k:754","type":"tension","note":"Kural 754 says wealth gained by right means and without wrong yields virtue and pleasure; 280 says wealth at every stage, earning included, is the dwelling of suffering. Both speak of wealth as such and its yield. The tension holds if 280 is about wealth in general, as its wording suggests, and not only the miser's, as the chapter setting might imply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"n:280","type":"agrees","note":"277 lists the miser's troubles of guarding, burying and clutching; 280 generalizes them into a sequence of earning, guarding, dwindling and loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"Both say everyone scorns those who have nothing and honours the rich. 281 adds that noble birth does not help: the penniless are lower than a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:841","type":"tension","note":"Kural 841 says the world does not count lack of wealth as real lack; 281 says the world counts those who lack it lower than a corpse. Both describe how the world regards the poor. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of 841's second half as a claim about the world's judgment; Kural 752 sides with 281, so the Kural itself is divided.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:283","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say the poor lose their own people. Kural 1047 limits it to poverty without virtue, which even a mother treats as a stranger; 283 makes no such exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:284","b":"k:527","type":"same_image","note":"The same bird with opposite morals: the Kural's crows call their kin to share their food, an image of the generous; here crows crowding a carcass are the flatterers round the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:285","b":"k:1043","type":"agrees","note":"Both say poverty destroys the standing of one's ancient family at a stroke. 285 adds valour and learning to what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:287","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1046 says the words of the poor lose force even when they understand well. 287 goes further: the understanding itself loses its sharpness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:286","b":"n:288","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar says 288 repeats 286: better to leave the village than live there unable to give. The agreement holds on his reading of 286 as advice to a poor man, not Shanmuganar's reading of it as scorn for a miser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank begging above a life that does not give: the Kural the hoarder eating alone, 288 the poor man who stays home unable to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:1050","type":"tension","note":"For the destitute who cannot give, Kural 1050 says the fitting course is to renounce completely, the body included on Parimelazhagar's reading, rather than live off others' salt and gruel. 288 recommends going away and living by begging. Same person, opposite remedies; here the Jain book is the less renunciatory one. The tension depends on Parimelazhagar's reading of 1050.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:289","b":"n:1","type":"agrees","note":"Both show fallen fortune through food: those who once refused a second helping beg for gruel (1), and hands that wore bracelets cook unsalted greens (289).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:290","b":"n:283","type":"agrees","note":"The same image and conclusion, bees leaving a flowerless plant and the poor left without kin; Mudaliar notes the repeat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:291","b":"n:298","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the honourable mind a fire lit by the rich: a forest fire at their arrogance (291), a forge fire at their contemptuous pity (298).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse 'going behind' those who scorn (Kural இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று, here இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று). The Kural says it brings neither praise nor heaven; 292 says the honourable go to those who understand unasked.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"Both allow asking before the right people: the Kural finds a beauty in asking those of open heart who know their duty, and 292 sends the honourable to those who grasp the need before it is spoken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:293","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1057 delights in givers who give without scorn; 293 shows the giver who feeds the poor at the back door with contempt, and draws the matching conclusion: forget such friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:295","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both set an absolute limit: the worthy will not do what brings blame, not to save a starving mother (Kural) and not to save their own lives (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:296","b":"n:270","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rich who do not give are poor and the poor who do not beg are rich. 296 adds a name for the honourable poor, the great Muttaraiyar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:297","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"Both make fear of blame the mark of the highest: the Kural's பழிநாணுவார் see a millet-sized fault as a palmyra; here only blame frightens the best, while lower people fear hunger and pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Each book gives honour an animal that dies rather than compromise: the yak that will not live once its hair is gone (Kural), the tiger that starves rather than eat prey fallen on its left (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:966","type":"develops","note":"Kural 966 says that following those who scorn you does not lead to the gods' land. 300 goes further: even if heaven were in hand, the excellent would refuse it at honour's cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:301","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask why a person of worth should stand behind those who look down on him. 966 counts the loss in renown and in heaven; 301 in clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids deeds the wise condemn even to relieve one's mother's hunger. 302 makes the same choice for one's own hunger, and grounds it in the shortness of life rather than in the judgement of the wise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both make death and rebirth as brief as an ordinary act of the eyes, and Mudaliar cites 339 here. The Kural uses the image to show impermanence; 302 uses it to make blameless hunger the cheaper choice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:303","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"1053 finds a beauty even in asking before unconcealing givers who know their duty. 303 narrows this further: the worthy approach only givers who first welcome them in, so asking becomes hospitality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:304","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for dignity when circumstances shrink; 304 shows it, with fortune and the gods both gone and the worthy still refusing to bow to the hoarding rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 1061: not asking even of givers dear as one's eyes, who give without holding back, is far better. Both use the same phrase, கண்ணன்னார்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1069","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the heart melt (உள்ளம் உருகும்) at the thought of begging. 1069 goes on to the one who refuses; 305 to the beggar at the moment of taking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:306","b":"k:1063","type":"qualifies","note":"Both reject begging as a cure for poverty. In Parimelazhagar's reading the Kural's alternative is effort; 306's is to want less and consent to hardship. They differ on the remedy, not on the refusal, and the difference shows the Naladiyar's turn toward renunciation.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:307","b":"k:1057","type":"tension","note":"1057 speaks of beggars rejoicing when they meet givers who give without scorn, which assumes such givers exist. 307 says the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born. The conflict holds only if 307 is taken literally; Mudaliar's explanations treat it as overstatement or wordplay.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:308","b":"k:1070","type":"agrees","note":"In both, the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal. 1070 turns the point against the refuser; 308 stays with the asker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:309","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"1066 finds nothing more degrading to the tongue than asking, even for water for a cow. 309 also places the disgrace in the spoken request rather than in dependence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:310","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use old friendship is if a friend's liberties aren't accepted as one's own. On Shanmuganar's reading, 310 shows the cost of that failure to the friend who relied on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:311","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well in a good assembly not to speak in a mean one; 311 describes the mean assembly in detail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:312","b":"n:315","type":"develops","note":"312 warns that the bad scholar will rise to fight; 315 shows the cost to whoever stays to argue: his teeth in his hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:313","b":"k:649","type":"agrees","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words long to say many; 313 shows the same many-worded speaker in debate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:314","b":"k:403","type":"agrees","note":"403 says even the unlearned are good if they stay silent before the learned; 314's fool fails at exactly that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:315","b":"k:857","type":"agrees","note":"857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the truth that brings victory; 315's disputants want victory and will not accept the truth (மெய் கொள்ளார்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:316","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"In 69 a mother rejoices more than at his birth when she hears her son called சான்றோன். In 316 the சான்றோர் themselves grieve for the mother of a fool. The two verses are mirror images.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:317","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"317 separates a book's text from its meaning; 318 separates those who keep the text from those who grasp the meaning and teach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:318","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"650 likens those who cannot explain what they learned to flowers without scent; 318 sets them apart from scholars who make others understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:319","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"318 says true scholars make a book's meaning clear to others; 319 sets out what that explanation must contain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 says the learned of low birth have a worth the unlearned of high birth lack. 320 says that however much they learn, those without good birth cannot do what the well-born do. The conflict depends on reading இற்பிறப்பு as birth into a good family, as both commentators do, and on counting the capacity 320 denies as part of the worth 409 gives to learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"992 makes birth in a good family one of the two parts of courtesy; 320 credits good birth with the restraint to overlook another's petty understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"n:322","type":"same_image","note":"Both use milk-rice whose taste goes unknown: the ladle that stirs it and the dog that prefers leather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 makes the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; in 321 the learned value such words while the fool mocks them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:322","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe hearing that isn't hearing: 418's ear unpierced by instruction is deaf though it hears, and 322's listeners lend an ear and still do not hear (செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:323","b":"k:420","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the same verdict, 'what matter whether they die or live'. The Kural aims it at those who taste with the mouth and not the ear; 323 at those who see death coming and do no good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:324","b":"k:999","type":"agrees","note":"999 says the world is dark even by day for those who cannot laugh with others; 324 asks why anyone would refuse to smile with those he meets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:325","b":"k:158","type":"agrees","note":"158 tells one to defeat those who wrong one out of arrogance by one's own forbearance; 325 shows that defeat in public, though it doesn't name forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:326","b":"n:332","type":"develops","note":"326 shows the cost of putting off virtue until old age; 332 names the usual excuse, that the household's needs come first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:327","b":"k:1006","type":"agrees","note":"1006 names the same two failures, not using one's wealth and not giving to the deserving. 327 adds a third: not taking the good path that protects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:328","b":"k:335","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the end, when the tongue no longer works. 335 urges good deeds before that moment; 328 shows the gesture that comes too late.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:328","b":"n:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock the words of postponement: பின்னறிவாம் here, மற்றறிவாம் in 332.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:329","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"333 says wealth doesn't stay, so do lasting deeds as soon as you have it. 329 shows people who think of such deeds only when their means are gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:330","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"336 states the general fact that one here yesterday is gone today. 330 makes it the death of one's own loved ones and still finds people unmoved.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:331","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"337 mocks those who cannot know they will live another moment yet plan in crores; 331's turtle is a picture of that ignorance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:332","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says to do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம்); 332 mocks the household version of the same words (மற்றறிவாம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:333","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading the two make the same claim: learning without knowing how to live with the world is still ignorance. On Mudaliar's reading, where உலகம் is the worlds after death, the link does not hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:334","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare a person without human quality to an inert thing, a tree in 997 and a stone in 334. 334 goes further and ranks the stone above the person for its usefulness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:335","b":"n:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the fool's urge to speak in an itching tongue (சுனை): in 313 the itch to debate, here the itch to abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:336","b":"k:967","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn against following those who do not accept you. 967 counts the cost in honour; 336 in futility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:337","b":"n:304","type":"qualifies","note":"304 says the worthy will not stand behind the hoarding rich; 337 says people at large do. 304 describes the exception to 337's rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:338","b":"n:327","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the goods a fool's life lacks: 327 enjoyment, giving and the good path; 338 adds love and renown.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:339","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 rates being a stranger to the wise far above close friendship with a fool; 339 says friendship with the undiscerning is painful even if it brought the whole earth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:340","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration; 340 shows the self-praiser mocked as mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not override what a person already is. In Parimelazhagar's reading 373 credits the lasting understanding to fate; 341 credits it to base nature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"n:345","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a domestic animal that keeps its habit despite good feeding: the hen to its rubbish heap, the dog to others' leftovers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:342","b":"k:976","type":"agrees","note":"976 says the small have no thought of cherishing the great and taking them as guides; 342 shows the base man inventing excuses not to go to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading 343 matches 973: being placed high does not make the base high, nor does it change the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:977","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, prosperity turns the base man to a new course, as 977 says distinction falling to the unworthy turns into excess.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 104 with the same millet and palm, and Mudaliar cites it here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"105 says a benefit's measure is the worth of the one who receives it; 344's second half shows that measure at zero, with palm-sized help counting for nothing among the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness admires itself; 346 shows both, with an emperor's wealth on one side and a fraction of a coin on the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for humility in abundance; 346's worthy keep it even with an emperor's wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:347","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 347 and 350 apply it to the base who have grown rich, whose wealth cannot hide what their deeds show.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:348","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"1079's base man finds fault on seeing others well fed and clothed; 348's rejoices when others suffer. They are the same envy seen from both sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:349","b":"k:810","type":"agrees","note":"810 praises those who do not change toward old friends; 349 gives the worthy that constancy and shows the base without it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:350","b":"n:347","type":"agrees","note":"The two share their closing lines word for word: 347 makes the point with an object, the jewelled sandal, and 350 with an animal, the young bullocks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:352","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not change what a person is: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they learn, and 352 says those without fine discernment never gain it from flawless books.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:353","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"184 ranks harsh words to a man's face above careless words behind his back. 353 doesn't let face-to-face speech off: the base are shameless precisely because they list a man's faults standing beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:354","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading of 354 as a figure, both make self-display the mark of the small: 978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration, and 354's wantons adorn and vaunt themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base give only to force: 1077's base won't shake their wet hands except for those who would break their jaws, and 355's chisel only moves when struck.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"1078's sugar-cane yields only when crushed, and 355's chisel cuts only when struck: two tools of labour for the same claim about the base.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:356","b":"k:108","type":"agrees","note":"108 tells one to keep a good done in mind and forget the bad that very day. 356 says the worthy do exactly this and the base do the reverse, dwelling on abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:357","b":"k:109","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one kindness outweigh great injury: in 109 a wrong as painful as killing vanishes at the thought of one good, and in 357 the worthy bear a hundred faults for one kindness. 357 adds the base, for whom the scale runs the other way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:358","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955 says an old family doesn't part from its nature even when its means shrink, and 358 says the well-born do in poverty what the base won't do in wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:359","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock plans made without regard to life's shortness: 337's unwise think crores of thoughts without knowing they'll live a moment more, and 359's dreamers say 'today, now, soon' and perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:360","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture people who have the means of feeling and none of the feeling: 576's men have eyes but no kind regard and are like trees in the soil, and 360's rich live in wealth but stay dry as the netti's pith.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:361","b":"k:53","type":"agrees","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth and what is present if she lacks it. 361 gives the second half a picture: every grandeur present, and the house a wilderness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:362","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say guards can't keep women chaste. 57 concludes that a woman's own steadfastness is the best guard; 362, on Mudaliar's reading, doubts that most women have it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:363","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"52 says a life has nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 363 names three such failings and calls each a weapon that kills the husband.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic's way. 364 calls returning to married life a fault like stoning oneself, and Mudaliar takes it as the wise telling one to give household life up. The tension holds on Mudaliar's reading; if the verse is only about remarriage after a wife's death, as Shanmuganar's gloss allows, it is narrower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"n:56","type":"develops","note":"56 explains why the learned call marriage கடி, a dread; 364 opens by citing that name, and Mudaliar points back to 56.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"k:47","type":"tension","note":"47 puts the one who lives household life according to its nature first among all who strive; 365 puts austerity first and household life in the middle. Parimelazhagar limits 47's 'all who strive' to those in the third stage, not full renunciants, which softens the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"n:366","type":"develops","note":"Two ranked lists of lives side by side. Both put enjoyment of what one has in the middle and craving at the bottom, but the top changes from austerity to learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:367","b":"k:959","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the sprout to read what lies behind it: in 959 the sprout shows the soil as speech shows the lineage, and in 367 the paddy breeds true as a son's mind follows his father's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:368","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"169 calls the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright a thing to ponder. 368 describes that world: the worthy fallen and the base risen, the umbrella turned upside down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:369","b":"k:788","type":"agrees","note":"788 says friendship removes distress at once, like a hand catching a slipping garment. 369 says a heart that doesn't respond so is not worth keeping alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:370","b":"n:371","type":"same_image","note":"The same frame, word for word in places, with two images of a love that lasts only while it is fed: the flood that ends with the rain, and the lamp that ends with the oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:371","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the sweet words of women who desire wealth, not the man, bring harm. 371 gives the mechanism: their love burns only while the money feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:372","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both are the dupe's own speech recalling a vow of inseparable love, a death pact in 372 and a promise never to part in 376, broken when his money ran out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:374","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"912's women weigh the profit before they speak of their virtue; 374's women weigh only wealth, so the poor man is poison and the oil-presser with money is sugar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:375","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"920 names இருமனப் பெண்டிர், double-minded women, among the company of the ruined; 375's eel, showing one face to the snake and another to the fish, pictures the double mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:376","b":"n:378","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the fighting ram's horn, which twists away, for the courtesan's love turned to hostility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:377","b":"k:918","type":"agrees","note":"918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு to those without discernment; 377's wild cow, whose lick pleases and then kills in Mudaliar's gloss, is the same danger, with public ridicule added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:378","b":"k:914","type":"agrees","note":"914 says those who seek the wealth of grace won't touch the base charms of women who count only wealth; 378 says those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace them, which Mudaliar takes as the path to liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:379","b":"k:913","type":"agrees","note":"913 compares the false embrace to holding a stranger's corpse in a dark room; 379 says the women belong to no one but their bodies. Both deny that anyone is present in the embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:380","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"917 says those without firmness of heart embrace women whose hearts are on other things; 380 says those full of sin don't grasp the guile of women whose minds are with another man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:381","b":"k:57","type":"agrees","note":"On the commentators' reading, both place a wife's chastity in her own keeping: 57 calls the guard of her own steadfastness the best, and 381 praises the woman whose conduct leaves no one pursuing her. On Pope's reading, 381 instead credits the absence of temptation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:382","b":"k:51","type":"qualifies","note":"51's good wife lives within her husband's means; 382's good wife keeps her duties of hospitality even when the means are one pot of water and the guests could drink the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:383","b":"n:361","type":"develops","note":"The two halves of one claim: 361's mansion without a good wife is a wilderness, and 383's leaking hut with one is the only true home. Mudaliar's gloss on 383, that other houses are forests, makes the pairing explicit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"k:1302","type":"agrees","note":"1302 says sulking is right in measure like salt and spoils if prolonged; 384's good wife sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:385","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"385's wife never loses her shyness with her husband however often they meet; 1257's woman knows nothing of shame when her beloved does what she desires. The second describes desire overriding reserve after separation, so the two set limits on each other rather than conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:387","b":"k:1311","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives refuse a chest that other women have shared: 1311 because all women feast on him with their eyes, and 387 because he comes from the courtesans unbathed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:388","b":"n:390","type":"same_image","note":"The same speech to the minstrel in the same frame, with two images of the neglected side: the unstruck face of the drum and the sapless end joints of the sugar-cane.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:389","b":"n:387","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives read the courtesans' embraces on the husband's chest, unwashed in 387 and smeared with sandal in 389.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Both make sulking love's salt: 1302 measures it, with too much spoiling the dish, and 391 says love without it has no salt at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1306","type":"agrees","note":"1306 says love without quarrels, serious and light, is like over-ripe or unripe fruit; 391 says that union and sulking together are love's way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"n:391","type":"agrees","note":"The good wife of 384 sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly; 391 gives the reason, that without sulking love has no salt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"In both, a sound of the season becomes a killing instrument for the separated woman: the rain's thunder is a funeral drum in 392, and the herdsman's flute a weapon in 1228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"n:364","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum appears in both: in 364 it should teach a man to give up married life, and in 392 the thunder sounds like it to a woman whose husband is away. The same sound serves renunciation in one book and love in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"k:1229","type":"agrees","note":"Both make evening the bewildering hour of separation: 393's மம்மர்கொள் மாலை, and 1229's evening that confuses the mind of the whole town.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"n:397","type":"same_image","note":"Two evening scenes in one frame: the day's work falls silent, and the woman casts off her garland and weeps for the absent man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:394","b":"k:1261","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the woman count the days of absence on her fingers, and Mudaliar cites 1261 with 394. In 1261 she speaks; in 394 the man imagines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:395","b":"k:1086","type":"same_image","note":"Both make her brow a bow that guards her eyes: in 1086 the brows, if they would only screen her eyes, would spare him his pain, and in 395 they frighten off the kingfisher.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:398","type":"qualifies","note":"The mother of 396 doubts that feet which shrank from soft dye can bear the stony path; the girl of 398 answers that the strength comes with the man, as riding comes with the horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:399","type":"develops","note":"Both are the mother's speech after her daughter's flight: 396 fears for the girl's feet on the way, and 399 rereads the farewell embrace she didn't understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:400","b":"k:1225","type":"develops","note":"1225's woman asks what she did to morning and to evening, looking for the cause of her pain in the hours. 400 runs through such suspects and acquits them, blaming only the road her lover took for wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"ac:1","b":"k:131","type":"develops","note":"Kural 131 puts ஒழுக்கம் above life because it gives excellence; Acharakkovai 1 says what conduct grows from, naming eight inner virtues as its seed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural's command to guard conduct gets a source: the conduct to be guarded is not a set of rules but the outgrowth of gratitude, patience and the rest.","la":"It makes rule-bound conduct the fruit of eight ethical virtues, so a ritual code is founded on ethics before any ritual rule is given.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:2","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 133 says conduct is noble birth and its lapse is low birth; Acharakkovai 2 makes good birth one of eight rewards that unfailing conduct earns.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both tie birth to conduct, but the Kural collapses birth into present conduct while the Acharakkovai keeps birth as a real rank that conduct earns, so the same link can dissolve or confirm hierarchy.","la":"It sets birth among worldly rewards such as wealth, land and health, so birth becomes a prize of conduct rather than its definition.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:3","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai 3 lists sacrifice among four acts to be guarded and kept fruitful; Kural 259 rates abstaining from killing one creature above a thousand sacrifices.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ritual code keeps sacrifice as a thing of value needing protection by conduct, while the Kural prices it below a single act of non-killing, so the two books weigh the same rite very differently.","la":"It groups sacrifice with fees, penance and learning as goods that conduct preserves, where the Kural sets sacrifice against non-killing and ranks it lower.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:4","b":"ac:16","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 has one rise worshipping father and mother; verse 16 names five elders, parents among them, to be worshipped as gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Verse 16 supplies the rank that verse 4's daily act presupposes, and shows the dawn rite honours only two of the five elders owed god-like worship.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:5","b":"s:1004","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 5 bars one in எச்சில் from touching brahmins and other sacred things; Sivavakkiyar asks why water in the mouth is called எச்சில் while the Veda churned in the same mouth is not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar turns the category the Acharakkovai uses to protect the brahmin against the brahmin's own recitation, showing that the purity rule depends on exempting the sacred mouth from it.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:6","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"On the reading that புலை names the outcaste person, Acharakkovai 6 makes the sight of a birth-group polluting; Kural 972 says birth is alike for all living beings and distinction comes from deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The conflict holds only if புலை is a person and செய்தொழில் means conduct, so the pairing shows how the caste force of each verse rests on one disputed word.","la":"It sets a birth-group beside a dog and the heavenly bodies as one class of barred sights, a ranking by birth the Kural's verse denies.","gr":3},{"a":"ac:7","b":"s:1442","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 7 classes sexual union and eating as kinds of எச்சில்; Sivavakkiyar says the pure body itself came from எச்சில், and honey and milk are எச்சில் too.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar takes the Acharakkovai's own classing of union as எச்சில் to its end: if union pollutes, every body and every food is born of pollution and the category cannot separate clean from unclean.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:8","b":"s:1423","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 8 bars recitation while in எச்சில்; Sivavakkiyar says the mantra one recites is itself the mouth's எச்சில்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One text guards recitation from impurity and the other calls recitation impure in itself, so the rule that protects the mantra is the Siddhar's evidence that purity of the mouth is a fiction.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:8","b":"ac:7","type":"develops","note":"Verse 7 defines the four kinds of எச்சில்; verse 8 says what they bar and what keeping them yields.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the book's method: a definition followed by its prohibitions and a reward in learning, so purity rules are presented as a discipline of the scholar.","gr":4},{"a":"ac:10","b":"k:298","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 298 says water gives only outer purity and inner purity is seen through truth; Acharakkovai 10 lists ten occasions when water must remove impurity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural concedes the outer purity that the Acharakkovai regulates but makes it the lesser half, so the ten baths answer a question the Kural treats as minor; they are different things, not a contradiction.","la":"It turns the Kural's single clause on outer purity into an exact list of the occasions that call for a bath, among them dream, hair-cutting and contact with low people.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:10","b":"s:1177","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 10 makes bathing the removal of impurity on ten occasions; Sivavakkiyar addresses those who bathe daily and whose dirt never goes, asking where the dirt really lies.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar denies the premise the bathing list rests on, that the impurity is where water can reach, whereas the Kural only ranks it below inner purity.","gr":4},{"a":"ac:12","b":"k:1066","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1066 says asking even water for a cow degrades the tongue; Acharakkovai 12 says the worthy do not beg sandals even when they lack them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Acharakkovai brings the Kural's limit case of begging down to one's own bodily need and sets it inside a list of purity rules, so not begging becomes part of bodily propriety.","la":"It places the refusal to beg beside rules against touching oil and others' cloth, treating begging as a kind of defilement.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:13","b":"ac:6","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses bar the sight of புலை, verse 6 to one in எச்சில், verse 13 to one who has oiled the body.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bar on seeing புலை recurs under two different ritual states, which shows it is a standing rule of the code rather than a feature of one kind of impurity.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:15","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai 15 says one who scorns brahmins perishes; Kural 134 says a brahmin's birth itself is ruined when his conduct fails.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural adds the condition the Acharakkovai leaves out: reverence owed to the brahmin by birth lapses with his conduct, so the two books disagree on whether birth alone commands it.","la":"It sets brahmins with the elements, the cow, the sun and the moon as cosmic things owed reverence, a rank the Kural makes conditional on conduct.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:16","b":"pn:183","type":"develops","note":"Acharakkovai 16 makes the teacher one of five elders to be worshipped as gods; Purananuru 183 says learning is worth serving and paying the teacher, and that learning outweighs both seniority and the order of birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Puram 183 gives the reason for the teacher's place and also cuts against the list: the elder brother the Acharakkovai reveres yields to the wiser younger, and a learned man of the lowest order draws the highest to him.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:20","b":"k:942","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Kural 942 regulates the meal by digestion, eating only after the last meal is spent; Acharakkovai 20 regulates it by direction, posture, silence and worship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books divide the same act between body and rite: the Kural cares when and how much one eats, the Acharakkovai how one sits and where one looks, and neither addresses the other's concern.","la":"It makes the meal a sequence of bodily disciplines, facing east, stillness, silence and worship, none of which concerns the food itself.","gr":4},{"a":"ac:21","b":"k:43","type":"develops","note":"Kural 43 names five the householder must tend (ancestors, gods, guests, kin, self); Acharakkovai 21 names five who must be fed before the householder eats: guests, elders, the cow, caged creatures and children.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural's general duty to five becomes a sequence at the meal, and the circle of dependants widens from kin and gods to animals and captives, while the self drops off the list.","la":"It turns a list of duties into an order of serving, and it puts the cow and the caged creature beside guests and elders as claimants on the same meal."},{"a":"ac:21","b":"n:94","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 94 asks for daily giving before eating, even a grain's worth, to anyone; Acharakkovai 21 names who must be served before the meal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar makes giving before eating a matter of amount, however small, while the Acharakkovai makes it a matter of persons, so the two together fix both how little suffices and to whom it is owed.","la":"It replaces the Naladiyar's unnamed recipient with five named dependants of the house, human and animal."},{"a":"ac:21","b":"ac:39","type":"develops","note":"Verse 21 names five to be fed before one eats; verse 39 bars cooking for oneself alone and requires the house offering to be fed first.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the code puts others before the eater at both ends of the meal: in cooking (39) and in serving (21), with the house offering added to the living claimants."},{"a":"ac:22","b":"ac:30","type":"develops","note":"Verse 22 bars laying the bed along the roof-ridge; verse 30 bars the head to the north and the corners when lying down.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the bed is governed by two separate direction rules, one tied to the house's ridge and one to the compass, so the code fixes sleep by both the building and the quarters."},{"a":"ac:23","b":"k:946","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 946 grounds not eating to excess in health and joy; Acharakkovai 23 bars excess even when the food is choice, among rules of posture and place.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same restraint has two grounds: in the Kural it pays in pleasure and freedom from disease, in the Acharakkovai it is decorum, binding even when the food tempts, so moderation is both prudence and conduct.","la":"It sets overeating beside eating lying down, standing, in the open and on the bed, so excess becomes a breach of form like a wrong posture rather than a danger to health."},{"a":"ac:25","b":"ac:20","type":"develops","note":"Verse 20 sets the eater's direction, posture and silence; verse 25 sets the order of the food by taste, sweet first and bitter last.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Verse 20 governs only the eater's body, and verse 25 completes the meal code by governing the food's sequence, so the rule reaches what is eaten as well as how."},{"a":"ac:31","b":"t:231","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai 31 forbids passing between brahmins (பார்ப்பார்) as between gods; Tirumanthiram 231 denies the name of brahmin to those who lack truth, knowledge, restraint and devotion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Acharakkovai's deference attaches to the birth-name with no condition, while the Tirumanthiram makes the name itself depend on inner qualities, so the second would void the rule's deference for a brahmin who lacks them."},{"a":"ac:32","b":"ac:34","type":"develops","note":"Verse 32 lists ten places barred for spitting and excretion; verse 34 says where these are allowed, a screened or secluded place, and sets the stake at refusing even Indra's throne.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give the rule's two sides, the barred places and the permitted one, and verse 34 adds that the screening is also an act of mind, so the code of bodily acts reaches into intention.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:37","b":"k:920","type":"develops","note":"Kural 920 names double-minded women, toddy and dice as the company of those Fortune has abandoned; Acharakkovai 37 bars even looking at another's wife, toddy, theft, gambling and killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's sanction is loss of fortune in this life, while the Acharakkovai adds public scorn and hell, and it shifts the sexual fault from the courtesan to another's wife, so the same cluster of vices is judged on different grounds.","la":"It adds theft and killing to the Kural's three, so pleasures and crimes stand in one list, and it bars them at the level of sight."},{"a":"ac:37","b":"el:12","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 12 says it is good neither to desire nor to look at five places, among them the killing ground and the gambling ground; Acharakkovai 37 bars looking at five things, among them killing and gambling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both works bar the eye, but the Elathi bars the places where killing and gambling happen while the Acharakkovai bars the acts themselves and gives hell as the reason, so the ban on looking is applied once to sites and once to deeds."},{"a":"ac:37","b":"ac:38","type":"develops","note":"Verse 37 bars looking at five wrongs; verse 38 bars even thinking of four more.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the two verses move the ban inward from the eye to the mind, and both name hell as the end, so the code treats sight and thought as stages of the same fault.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:38","b":"k:282","type":"develops","note":"Kural 282 says that even thinking of stealing another's goods is wrong; Acharakkovai 38 bars thinking of seizing, along with lying, slander and envy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's thought-crime is confined to theft, and the Acharakkovai extends the same inward standard to speech faults and envy and names its sanctions, suspicion, hell and the deity's wrath.","la":"It groups seizing with lying, slander and envy, so faults of hand, tongue and heart are all forbidden at the stage of thought."},{"a":"ac:39","b":"k:229","type":"develops","note":"Kural 229 calls eating alone from one's hoard worse than begging; Acharakkovai 39 bars cooking for oneself alone and requires the house offering to be fed first.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural condemns eating alone by its bitterness, while the Acharakkovai moves the bar back to the hearth, so the fault begins when the pot is set on the fire for oneself.","la":"It sets the bar on solitary cooking beside a purity rule for the kitchen and an offering rule, so sharing becomes part of the ritual order of the house."},{"a":"ac:40","b":"k:987","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 987 asks one to do good even to those who have done harm; Acharakkovai 40 asks only that one not wound elders and young kin at their meal, even if they have done great harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Kural's universal demand, the Acharakkovai's rule is narrower in scope (kin, at the meal) and lower in demand (not harming rather than doing good), so the code fixes a minimum where the Kural sets an ideal.","la":"It joins forbearance to a seating rule (not sitting above them), so restraint toward kin is part of the precedence of the table."},{"a":"ac:42","b":"s:1098","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 42 treats the menstrual days as days of no touch and sets the twelve days after the bath apart for the wife; Sivavakkiyar 134 says the monthly pollution is what grows into the body once the months stop, so no birth is free of it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ritual rule and the Siddhar's attack both join menstruation to conception, but one draws a line of pollution across the cycle while the other uses the same cycle to deny that any body stands outside pollution."},{"a":"ac:43","b":"ac:42","type":"develops","note":"Verse 42 names the days a husband keeps to his wife after her bath; verse 43 lists hours and calendar days on which the couple do not come together.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together the two verses make marital union a matter of the ritual calendar, with days prescribed by the wife's cycle and others barred by the moon, the gods and the hours."},{"a":"ac:47","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"Kural 560 says that when the guardian fails to guard, those of the six duties forget their books; Acharakkovai 47 lists affliction to the land's guardian among the days brahmins do not recite.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both tie the reciting of the Veda to the king's state: the Kural through long neglect that makes the texts forgotten, the Acharakkovai through a single day of the king's distress that halts recitation at once.","la":"It sets the king's affliction among moon days, earthquake, thunder and impurity, so the ruler's distress counts as a cosmic disturbance rather than a failure of governance."},{"a":"ac:48","b":"k:43","type":"develops","note":"Kural 43 makes tending ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself the householder's chief duty; Acharakkovai 48 names the days of gods, ancestors, wedding, festival and sacrifice as days for aram and for feeding guests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's standing duties to gods, ancestors and guests are turned by the Acharakkovai into dated occasions, showing how a general householder's duty becomes a festival calendar.","la":"It replaces the Kural's five persons owed care with five kinds of day, so the duty is counted by occasion, and the guest is fed as an addition to the day's aram."},{"a":"ac:49","b":"k:124","type":"develops","note":"Kural 124 praises the restrained man who does not stray from his station (நிலை); Acharakkovai 49 says dress, gait, slack speech and rebuke must fit one's standing (நிலைமை), learning, manliness and family.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's 'not straying from one's station' is shown in concrete outward marks, and the Acharakkovai's addition of family as a measure shows how that station can be read as rank of birth.","la":"It names four outward behaviours, two of them faults, and four measures they must fit, so keeping one's station becomes a fitting of dress, walk and speech to learning and lineage."},{"a":"ac:50","b":"k:988","type":"develops","note":"Kural 988 says poverty is no disgrace to one with the firmness of worth; Acharakkovai 50 says the learned do not mock or deride the poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The disgrace of poverty is denied from two sides: the Kural says the poor man of worth carries none, and the Acharakkovai says the learned onlooker must not lay it on him.","la":"It sets not deriding the poor among the habits of the learned listener, beside not blaming and not sleeping among crowds, so respect for the poor becomes part of learned decorum."},{"a":"ac:51","b":"ac:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 6 bars a falling star from the sight of one in எச்சில்; verse 51 bars a falling star, lightning, harlots' finery and the low sun from anyone who wants to keep his radiance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same sight is barred on two grounds in the book, once as a matter of pollution and once to guard one's lustre, so the gaze rules do not rest on pollution alone."},{"a":"ac:52","b":"k:196","type":"develops","note":"Kural 196 calls the man who keeps up useless talk chaff among people; Acharakkovai 52 says those of unshaken mind never speak useless words, deceit, loose talk, abuse or backbiting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural judges the empty talker by what his talk shows him to be, while the Acharakkovai names the cause of not talking so, a steady mind, so useless speech appears as a symptom of an unsettled one.","la":"It makes useless speech one of five kinds with deceit, wanton talk, abuse and backbiting, all kept off by a single inner steadiness, where the Kural treats useless talk on its own."},{"a":"ac:52","b":"n:189","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 189 says the pure in heart stay unshaken when slandered; Acharakkovai 52 says those of unshaken mind never backbite.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together the two verses make one steady mind both unmoved by slander and unwilling to slander, so steadiness works in both directions of backbiting.","la":"It sets backbiting last in a list of five faults of speech that the same unshaken mind avoids, where the Naladiyar shows only that mind receiving slander."},{"a":"ac:54","b":"k:90","type":"develops","note":"Kural 90 says a guest wilts when the host's face changes; Acharakkovai 54 puts a smile first among five honours given with food to a guest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural says what the wrong face does to a guest, and the Acharakkovai sets the right face at the head of a sequence of welcome, so the host's smile is the first act of hospitality, before word, water, mat and bed.","la":"It orders the welcome from face to word to body to rest, so the smile the Kural defends by a negative image becomes the first of five steps that accompany food."},{"a":"ac:56","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work one does; Acharakkovai 56 says one does not leave the work of one's ancestral line even when famine makes one poor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"If செய்தொழில் in the Kural is occupation, the two clash: the Kural ranks people by their work and not their birth, while the Acharakkovai fixes the work by birth, so rank by work becomes rank by descent.","la":"It sets the bar on leaving hereditary work after plain rules of safety in fire, rain and forest, treating loyalty to one's descent as prudence of the same kind."},{"a":"ac:58","b":"ac:16","type":"develops","note":"Verse 16 names the king, teacher, mother, father and elder brother as the குரவர் to be worshipped as gods; verse 58 ends by keeping the குரவர் on one's right.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Verse 16 says the elders are to be worshipped like gods, and verse 58 gives one bodily form of that worship, the circling to the right otherwise paid to the divine."},{"a":"ac:60","b":"k:715","type":"develops","note":"Kural 715 calls not speaking first among elders the best restraint; Acharakkovai 60 says that on the road with eminent elders one does not ride, wear sandals or shade oneself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's deference to elders lies in speech, the Acharakkovai's in the body on the road, so respect is shown both by holding back the word and by sharing the elder's hardship.","la":"It turns deference into three bodily refusals, of mount, sandals and shade, which the Kural's single rule about speaking first does not cover."},{"a":"ac:61","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 134 says a brahmin's birth is ruined when his conduct fails, though a forgotten Veda can be relearned; Acharakkovai 61 says the four-Veda knowers, come by heaven's order, are honoured as one's own elders.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural makes the brahmin's standing hang on conduct and treats the Veda as recoverable, while the Acharakkovai grounds his honour in the Veda and a heavenly origin and names no condition of conduct, so the two disagree on what earns the rank.","la":"It places the Veda-knowers inside the circle of one's own elders, so reverence for a birth-group is folded into filial reverence, which the couplet never does."},{"a":"ac:62","b":"ac:16","type":"develops","note":"Verse 16 names the five peerless elders and says to worship them as gods; verse 62 names the three acts of ஆசாரம் owed to elders.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Verse 16 gives the rank and verse 62 the bodily acts by which god-like worship of elders is performed, so the book's worship of elders turns out to be bowing, timely attendance and rising."},{"a":"ac:72","b":"ac:16","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 16 says the elders are to be worshipped as gods; verse 72 says that in a great house or a temple one does not bow even to them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The god-like worship verse 16 owes to elders gives way where a greater object of worship is present, so the book ranks reverence by place and the elder's claim is not absolute."},{"a":"ac:64","b":"ac:61","type":"develops","note":"Verse 61 honours the four-Veda knowers as one's own elders; verse 64 puts பார்ப்பார் first among eight to whom one yields the road.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Verse 64 turns 61's honour into a gesture on the road and sets the brahmin at the head of a line of the burdened, the sick and cows, so rank by birth is enacted with the same act as care for the weak."},{"a":"ac:65","b":"k:126","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 126 says that drawing in the five senses like a tortoise protects for seven births; Acharakkovai 65 says the five are hard to hold even for the worthy, so they must not stay alone with their nearest kinswomen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural trusts restraint of the senses as a power one can gain, the Acharakkovai doubts it even in the worthy and replaces it with a rule that removes the occasion, so self-mastery is backed by avoidance.","la":"It names mother, daughter and sister as the case, so the doubt about restraint is pressed to the women least suspected, a limit the couplet never tests."},{"a":"ac:66","b":"k:700","type":"develops","note":"Kural 700 says intimacy that does the improper because 'we are old friends' brings ruin; Acharakkovai 66 names கழிகிழமை, overstepping intimacy, among the things those who would last in service avoid.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the danger of presuming on an old bond; the Acharakkovai puts that danger among gate, gift and dress, showing that presuming on intimacy is one of several small encroachments on a lord's dignity.","la":"It sets excessive intimacy beside anger at the gate, carelessness at gifts and dressing like the lord, so the couplet's single fault becomes one of a family of encroachments."},{"a":"ac:68","b":"k:692","type":"develops","note":"Kural 692 says not desiring what kings desire brings lasting gain through them; Acharakkovai 68 opens with not taking pleasure in what the great take pleasure in.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the rule and its reward; the Acharakkovai shows what it looks like in the house, joined to not bringing low men in and not slighting the house's child, so not rivalling the lord's pleasures is part of guarding his household.","la":"It joins the rule to two others about the great man's house, so restraint of desire is placed among household courtesies rather than left as a maxim."},{"a":"ac:68","b":"k:698","type":"develops","note":"Kural 698 says not to slight one as younger or as kin but to act by the light he stands in; Acharakkovai 68 says not to speak slightingly even of an undiscerning child.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the young one the Kural warns against slighting can be a mere child of the great house, so the rule against belittling youth reaches down to the child who cannot yet understand.","la":"It makes the child's undiscerning state the extreme case, not only youth, and ties the rule to the place where one is not intimate."},{"a":"ac:69","b":"pz:281","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 281 says that even when an angry king does what one does not want, one should not act on one's resentment, for no one wakes a sleeping tiger; Acharakkovai 69 says one does not sulk or contradict even if the great one says the crow is white.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi bars acting on resentment when the king wrongs one; the Acharakkovai extends the bar to speech and face when he speaks falsely, so the attendant's silence covers both injury and untruth.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:69","b":"n:164","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 164 says those who anger the great do not escape even in a fortress, as the snake in its crevice dreads thunder; Acharakkovai 69 lists the small signs of displeasure one must not show before the great.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar gives the reason the Acharakkovai's courtesies are owed: the great one's anger reaches anywhere, so the rule not to sulk or contradict is a defence, not only a manner.","la":"It breaks the single danger into a list of guarded acts, sulking, facing, private talk and contradiction, so the quatrain's dread becomes a code of gestures.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:71","b":"n:170","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 170 says the fitting quality of one who has some excellence is restraint; Acharakkovai 71 says those of good family do not spread out their wealth, learning, lustre and virtue before the lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar makes restraint the proper dress of excellence in general; the Acharakkovai names the place where it is tested, before the lord, and the risk of failing it, being taken for an enemy.","la":"It names four excellences, wealth, learning, lustre and virtue, as alike to be kept silent before the lord, and gives a reason, looking like an enemy, that the quatrain lacks."},{"a":"ac:71","b":"ac:63","type":"develops","note":"Verse 63 bars speaking one's learning before elders; verse 71 bars displaying learning and three other excellences before the lord.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same silence about one's learning is owed in the house to elders and at court to the lord, so the book treats display of learning as a breach of rank in both spheres."},{"a":"ac:73","b":"k:694","type":"develops","note":"Kural 694 says to suppress whispers and shared smiles before the great; Acharakkovai 73 bars laughter, yawning, hawking and sneezing before them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural bars the laugh as a sign that may be misread; the Acharakkovai puts it with yawning and sneezing, which carry no meaning, so the presence of the great governs the body itself and not only what it signals.","la":"It joins a meaningful act, laughter, to involuntary ones, so the couplet's guard against misread signs becomes a guard on the body as such."},{"a":"ac:75","b":"k:694","type":"develops","note":"Kural 694 says to suppress whispers before the great; Acharakkovai 75 says that in the house of the great one does not take in whispered words, nor look at the women, nor let the garment slip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural forbids whispering in the presence of the great; the Acharakkovai also forbids receiving a whisper, so the listener is as guilty as the speaker.","la":"It sets the whisper beside the gaze on the women and the slipping garment, so the couplet's rule on speech becomes one of several guards on eye, ear and body in the great house.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:76","b":"k:696","type":"develops","note":"Kural 696 says to speak knowing the mood and the time, what is needed and not disliked; Acharakkovai 76 says to speak at the right moment with few syllables that hold the whole sense.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks for the right time and the right matter; the Acharakkovai adds the right length, so speech before the great is judged by moment, content and brevity together.","la":"It lists four faults of manner, haste, going on, falsehood and sprawl, before giving the positive rule, so the couplet's advice is defined against its failures."},{"a":"ac:78","b":"k:695","type":"develops","note":"Kural 695 says not to listen in or press for anything, but to hear a secret when it is released; Acharakkovai 78 says not to take counsel with others or listen in, and if near, to stand turned away as if busy with something else.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the inner restraint; the Acharakkovai gives the posture that makes it visible, standing turned away, so not listening must also be seen not to listen.","la":"It adds a staged posture of attention elsewhere to the bar on listening, which the couplet does not have."},{"a":"ac:80","b":"ac:6","type":"develops","note":"Verse 6 lists புலை among five sights barred in எச்சில்; verse 80 bars addressing புலையர் by kinship terms, and both close on நன்கறிவார்.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"Verse 80's plural personal form with a verb of address shows the book uses this root for persons, which weighs toward reading புலை in verse 6 as the outcaste person rather than an impure thing.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:81","b":"k:695","type":"develops","note":"Kural 695 tells the king's servant not to listen in or press after secrets; Acharakkovai 81 names the palace places, the back gate, the assembly and the women's quarters, where looking in is barred, and exempts those whose office lies there.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the courtier's discretion is spatial as well as verbal: what the Kural states as restraint of curiosity the Acharakkovai maps onto doors and rooms, and ties to office rather than to trust.","la":"It turns a general rule about secrets into three named places and adds the exception for those whose work lies there."},{"a":"ac:82","b":"k:700","type":"develops","note":"Kural 700 warns that intimacy which, presuming on old ties, does what is improper brings ruin; Acharakkovai 82 applies the same warning to sitting near the women, even with great intimacy (மிகக்கிழமை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair show the period's fear of presumed closeness reaching its sharpest point in the women's place, where the Acharakkovai grounds the bar not in the king's anger but in a claim about women's changing liking.","la":"It names the one occasion where even the closest intimacy gives no right and adds a reason drawn from women rather than from the ruler."},{"a":"ac:83","b":"pt:77","type":"agrees","note":"Acharakkovai 83 says those who would live long do not tell the size of the king's army; in Patitruppattu 77 the poet, asked by travellers how large Poraiyan's army is, says he could not count it and describes its elephants.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the courtier's rule of silence and the bard's praise give the same answer to the same question: no figure is told, and the bard turns the forbidden number into the uncountable."},{"a":"ac:84","b":"k:698","type":"develops","note":"Kural 698 says the king is not to be slighted as young or as kin; Acharakkovai 84 sets the king among the snake, fire and the lion and says slighting any of them as young, weak or familiar brings harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks respect for the light the king stands in, while the Acharakkovai asks it because he can harm, so the pair show two grounds for the same deference, the office and the danger.","la":"It ranks the king with a snake, fire and a lion, so his claim to respect is made one with their power to harm."},{"a":"ac:84","b":"tk:42","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 42 says the vellala family behaves toward brahmins as toward fire even when familiar with them; Acharakkovai 84 says fire, like the king, harms one who slights it as familiar.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two books use one image, fire that burns the familiar, for two superiors, the brahmin and the king, showing that the period's rule against contempt bred by familiarity covered ritual and political rank alike."},{"a":"ac:85","b":"k:692","type":"develops","note":"Kural 692 says not desiring what kings desire brings lasting prosperity through them; Acharakkovai 85 says that even great charity, weddings, enterprise and houses must not outdo the king's, or lasting wealth (மன்னிய செல்வம்) is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing turns the Kural's general restraint into a ceiling on display, and shows that in the period even a subject's virtue, charity included, could not be allowed to outshine the king.","la":"It names four kinds of display, charity first among them, so the limit is placed on virtue as well as on luxury."},{"a":"ac:88","b":"k:103","type":"develops","note":"Kural 103 praises help given by those who do not weigh the return; Acharakkovai 88 says those who would live rightly do not even speak of the return on help they gave.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's giver does not reckon the return before giving; the Acharakkovai adds that he does not mention it after, so the pair carry the refusal to weigh from the act into speech.","la":"It groups silence about one's help with not faulting food and not praising one's own vows, so the rule becomes part of a discipline of the tongue about one's own goods."},{"a":"ac:89","b":"k:379","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 379 asks why those who welcome good fortune grieve at bad, on the ground of fate; Acharakkovai 89 says those of true vision do not grieve over what has passed or what is beyond their control.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural grounds calm in fate, whereas the Acharakkovai limits it to the unattainable, the past and the uncontrollable and grounds it in vision, so the pair show calm resting either on what governs events or on seeing which events one can still change.","la":"It splits the Kural's single good and bad fortune into three cases, each scoped to what cannot be changed."},{"a":"ac:90","b":"s:1003","type":"tension","note":"Acharakkovai 90 sets rules toward the பார்ப்பார், from whose hand a cow is not taken, and the புலை, to whom leavings are not held out; Sivavakkiyar asks what a Paraiyar woman and a Brahmin woman are, since no mark on flesh, skin or bone divides them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read against each other, the rule that ranks two birth-groups by what may pass between their hands meets a denial that the body carries any rank, so the purity rule is shown to depend on a mark the Siddhar says is not there."},{"a":"ac:91","b":"sp:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 4 says a proud raised neck (ஏக்கழுத்தம்) in one who has nothing to be proud of draws the laughter of the good; Acharakkovai 91 says a raised neck (ஏக்கழுத்து) is blamed even in the forest, since there are always those older than oneself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Sirupanchamoolam faults the raised neck when it lacks ground, but the Acharakkovai faults it anywhere, even unseen, so the pair separate pride as absurdity from pride as disrespect to elders.","gr":2},{"a":"ac:92","b":"n:52","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 52 calls those who prattle endlessly (தொலைவில்லா) of grammar and astrology the greatest fools; Acharakkovai 92 tells one to fix the day of a rite from the unfailing (தொலைவில்லா) அந்தணர், whose word never errs.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word, தொலைவில்லா, marks the Naladiyar's endless and foolish astrology and the Acharakkovai's unfailing reckoners, so the two books take opposite views of consulting the day before acting.","la":"It sets the reckoning of days in a contrast between two birth-groups, so the question becomes whose reckoning to trust, not whether to reckon."},{"a":"ac:92","b":"k:30","type":"tension","note":"Kural 30 says those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous, kind to every living being; Acharakkovai 92 trusts the word of the அந்தணர் and distrusts that of the புலையர் on the day of a rite.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural derives the name அந்தணர் from conduct, while the Acharakkovai uses it as a birth-group set against the புலையர், so the pair show the same word serving as a moral title in one book and a caste rank in the other.","la":"It pairs the அந்தணர் with the புலையர் as opposites, a contrast of birth that the Kural's definition by kindness does not make."},{"a":"ac:95","b":"k:57","type":"tension","note":"Kural 57 asks what use the guarding of women by confinement is, since the best guard is their own steadfastness; Acharakkovai 95 lists the wife among four things a man guards like gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural moves the guard of a wife inside her, while the Acharakkovai keeps it in the husband's hands beside his body and his deposits, so the pair show two views of where a wife's keeping lies.","la":"It puts the wife in one list with the man's body, a deposit and his savings, so she is guarded as a possession."},{"a":"ac:95","b":"tk:62","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 62 names the man who wrongly keeps a deposit placed with him as one who loses his line; Acharakkovai 95 lists a deposit among four things to be guarded like gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One verse says what keeping a deposit asks and the other what breaking it costs, and together they show the deposit as another's property that the period held as close as one's own body and family."},{"a":"ac:96","b":"ac:1","type":"develops","note":"Acharakkovai 1 names eight virtues as the seed from which ஆசாரம் grows; Acharakkovai 96 says ஆசாரம் comes in any case to those who hold to their own work like the ant, the weaver bird and the crow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book that opened by rooting conduct in inner virtues nears its close by rooting it in diligence, so ஆசாரம் is given two sources, character and steady work, and the second is learned from animals."},{"a":"ac:97","b":"k:694","type":"develops","note":"Kural 694 tells one to suppress whispers and shared smiles in the presence of the great; Acharakkovai 97 says one speaks before the great only with folded hands or a covered mouth and does not look into their eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural forbids the small private signs that the great may read as against them, and the Acharakkovai prescribes the posture of the mouth, hands and eyes, so together they show restraint before the great as a whole bodily discipline.","la":"It names the hands, the mouth and the eyes in turn, so the Kural's ban on two signs becomes a full posture of address."},{"a":"ac:98","b":"el:12","type":"agrees","note":"Elathi 12 says it is good neither to desire nor to look at five places, among them the killing ground and the ground where men gamble; Acharakkovai 98 says the wise do not enter the gamblers' hall or the ground of unceasing din, and that entering brings harm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books treat the gambling place as a place to avoid, but the Jain Elathi bars even desiring or seeing it alongside the killing ground, while the Acharakkovai bars entering it for the harm it brings, so the pair show a moral and a prudential reason for the same avoidance."},{"a":"ac:100","b":"ac:1","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai 1 makes eight virtues the seed of ஆசாரம்; Acharakkovai 100, the last verse, names nine persons freed from it: the stranger, the destitute, the old, the young, the bereaved, the frightened, one who has eaten, one on the king's work and the bridegroom.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read as the book's frame, the pair show the code bounded at both ends: it grows from virtues that bind everyone, but its rules lapse for those whom ignorance, weakness, fear, grief or duty prevent from keeping them."},{"a":"ac:100","b":"el:35","type":"agrees","note":"Elathi 35 promises kingship to one who feeds the renouncers, the powerless, the unknown, the dead or bereaved (இறந்தார்), the destitute and the young; Acharakkovai 100 frees from ஆசாரம் the stranger, the destitute, the old, the young and one who has lost a life.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two books name nearly the same weak persons, but the Elathi makes them claims on the giver's food while the Acharakkovai lifts the rules of conduct from them, so the vulnerable are owed help in one code and excused in the other.","gr":3},{"a":"el:2","b":"k:260","type":"develops","note":"Kural 260 says all creatures worship the one who neither kills nor eats flesh; Elathi 2 says the one who has the right to kill and does not, and is not deluded by flesh, rises above gods as well as men.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Jain ethics raising the stake: the Kural's worship by the spared creatures becomes rank above the gods, and the abstention counts most in the one who has the power to kill.","la":"It puts non-killing and refusing flesh first in a list with not tormenting, not deceiving and not swerving, so the two abstentions of 260 become the head of a five-part code rather than its whole."},{"a":"el:3","b":"k:263","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 263 chides householders who have forgotten penance in providing for renunciants; Elathi 3 says outright that penance is easy and giving is hard.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with 263, the verse reverses the ranking the Kural's chiding implies: if giving is the harder task, the householder who gives instead of doing penance has chosen the steeper road.","la":"It sets penance and giving as the first of several easy-hard pairs, so the ranking of the two is stated as a rule of difficulty rather than left to a rhetorical question."},{"a":"el:4","b":"k:391","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 391 says learn flawlessly and then stand by what is learned; Elathi 4 says that where six kinds of kindly conduct are seen, the books of learning may be let go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes conduct the completion of learning, while Elathi makes it a substitute: together they show two orders, learning that leads to conduct and conduct that makes the books unnecessary.","la":"It names the six acts that do the books' work (relieving distress, not scorning, shunning the base, relieving grief, avoiding blame, desired speech), so the conduct that replaces learning is specified, which 391 leaves general."},{"a":"el:6","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"Kural 30 calls those who wear cool kindness toward every living being the அந்தணர்; Elathi 6 calls one who has six possessions a mother to all living beings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both give a title for care toward every creature, but Elathi grounds it in six restraints, keeping out lies and flesh among them, so universal care is built from abstentions, not only from a cool temper.","la":"It breaks the single quality of 30 into six possessions, two of them shutting out falsehood and flesh, and gives the outcome a mother's name instead of a status word."},{"a":"el:7","b":"k:86","type":"develops","note":"Kural 86 says the host who tends the guest and watches for the next becomes a guest of the gods; Elathi 7 says the gods hasten to receive as guest one who gives sweet speech, mingling, a seat and good food without harsh words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet promises the reward and Elathi spells out the hospitality that earns it, so the pair shows the heavenly reciprocity resting on manner of speech as much as on food.","la":"It itemises hospitality into speech, mingling, a seat and food, with harsh words removed, a grading from word to meal that 86 does not make."},{"a":"el:8","b":"sp:69","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 69 counts the one who agreed to a killing among five who share its sin; Elathi 8 opens its list with not consenting and not killing as two separate acts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two Jain lists show consent treated as its own act of killing: one assigns it a share of the sin, the other makes refusing it a virtue in its own right."},{"a":"el:9","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 says relieving the hunger of the destitute (அற்றார்) is where one should store wealth; Elathi 9 says giving food, shelter and clothing to the destitute (அற்றார்கட்கு) makes one live as a learned man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the gift a bank for wealth and Elathi makes it a source of wisdom, so the same act toward the destitute is repaid in two different coins.","la":"It widens the gift from food to food, shelter and clothing and adds giving without regard to the receiver's learning or kinship, which 226 does not mention."},{"a":"el:11","b":"k:367","type":"develops","note":"Kural 367 says that if one cuts desire fully, the deeds that do not fail come; Elathi 11 echoes it (அவாஅறுப்பின் ஆற்ற) and adds the fate of one who does not cut it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-verbatim echo shows Elathi reading the couplet and supplying its other half: what 367 leaves unsaid, the failure to cut desire, is rebirth under the five senses.","la":"It sets the one who cuts desire beside the one who does not, so the couplet's promise becomes one branch of a choice with a stated penalty."},{"a":"el:11","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 24 guards the five with the goad of firmness, implying elephants without naming them; Elathi 11 names the five elephants (ஐங்களிறு) that toss the one who does not cut desire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Elathi makes explicit the elephants the couplet only implies, and shows what happens when the goad is missing: the senses carry their rider into the hole of rebirth.","la":"It turns the goad image into a narrative of failure, the rider tossed by the five, which the couplet's image of control does not show."},{"a":"el:13","b":"sp:68","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 68 says long bathing at a ghat does not remove the blemish; Elathi 13 counts not bathing (ஆடாமை, on our reading) among the six marks of ascetics.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On this reading the two Jain lists move from denying bathing any cleansing power to making its refusal a mark of the renunciant, the reverse of the bathing rules of the Acharakkovai."},{"a":"el:14","b":"k:834","type":"develops","note":"Kural 834 calls the greatest fool one who studies, understands and teaches but does not restrain himself; Elathi 14 says that for one who keeps six restraints the books are beside the point.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together the two state the same principle from opposite ends: learning without restraint is folly, and restraint without learning already has what the books are for.","la":"It names the restraints (no lying, no flesh, no toddy, no evil, no base company, no reviling) that 834 leaves as a single self-restraint, and puts flesh and toddy among them."},{"a":"el:14","b":"el:4","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 lets the books go where six kindly acts are seen; verse 14 puts the books outside one who keeps six abstentions.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated close shows the chapter treating conduct over books as a refrain, and the two lists divide that conduct into acts of kindness (4) and acts of refusal (14).","gr":3},{"a":"el:15","b":"tk:55","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 55 names a friendship that does not keep a precious secret (அருமறை) as one of three spies; Elathi 15 counts not letting a precious secret slip to sweet talkers as good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the secret's danger from both sides: Thirikadugam names the friend who leaks it as a spy, and Elathi locates the leak in the listener's trust of pleasant speech."},{"a":"el:16","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"Kural 42 makes the householder the support of those who renounced, those who have nothing and the dead; Elathi 16 names drawing near to renunciants, giving to those who have not renounced, speaking well of the dead and tending their kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse reads like a gloss on 42: it keeps the three figures and turns the dead into the living kin they leave, so the householder's duty to the dead becomes care of a household.","la":"It turns the three dependants of 42 into four acts and adds the kin of the dead, so the list says how the support is given, not only to whom.","gr":2},{"a":"el:17","b":"k:632","type":"develops","note":"Kural 632 names a minister excellent in resolve, guarding the people, learning and labour; Elathi 17 names guarding the people, resolve and strength in learning, then adds secret counsel, keeping off sloth and good company.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two share three of their qualities in near-identical words (குடி, வன்கண், கற்றல்/நூல்வன்மை), and Elathi's additions show a minister judged also by the company he keeps and by his secrecy.","la":"It adds secret counsel and good company to the Kural's core, so ministerial fitness extends from capacity to discretion and associations.","gr":3},{"a":"el:18","b":"k:920","type":"develops","note":"Kural 920 makes double-minded women, liquor and dice the company of those Fortune has abandoned; Elathi 18 lists indulgence, waste, hunting, toddy, gambling, harsh words, harsh punishment and unrestraint as what a king must guard against.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's three ruinous companions reappear inside Elathi's longer list of the king's vices, which shows the Kural's general warning taken up into a royal code where it secures the kingdom against rivals.","la":"It places the Kural's three vices among the seven of kingship (adding hunting, waste, harsh words and harsh punishment) and closes with unrestraint as their sum, carrying the warning into the king's domain."},{"a":"el:19","b":"n:158","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 158 says one deaf to others' secrets, blind to a neighbour's wife and dumb in evil backbiting needs no teaching of virtue; Elathi 19 names the same deafness to secrets and dumbness to evil speech, with not killing and not lying, as the marks of one greater than a king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared images of deafness and dumbness show the two Jain works using one figure for restraint, and Elathi's addition of non-killing turns the Naladiyar's man who needs no teaching into a rank above kings.","la":"It sets the Naladiyar's restraints of the senses after non-killing, not causing killing and not lying, so sense restraint is ranked behind non-violence and truth in a seven-part list."},{"a":"el:20","b":"pn:43","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 43 recalls the Chola ancestor who entered the scales for a dove; Elathi 20 praises one who delights in giving even when it is like cutting off a limb.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read with Puram 43, Elathi's simile carries the memory of the king who gave his own flesh: the heroic deed of an ancestor becomes the measure of ordinary giving for anyone."},{"a":"el:21","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 53 counts goods that fail (home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth, strength) as the reason to renounce; Elathi 21 counts the same failing goods together with what arrives, disease and old age.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two show that the case for release can be argued from loss alone or from loss and arrival, and Elathi's version gives the body's afflictions equal weight with the goods that slip away.","la":"It pairs what leaves with what comes in one list of five, so the argument moves from fading goods to arriving afflictions, which the quatrain does not count."},{"a":"el:24","b":"n:52","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 52 has the best reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do their own work; Elathi 24 lists disease, birth, old age, death, sorrow and want and names the work as the way of release.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the quatrain's unnamed 'own work' is given a name and its four evils gain birth and want, so the round of births itself becomes the thing to escape.","la":"It adds birth and unending want to the Naladiyar's four, making the list the cycle of births rather than the ills of one life."},{"a":"el:23","b":"el:22","type":"develops","note":"Both verses list what Death disregards and close with a book as the remedy; 22 names the defences Death does not fear, 23 the occasions and qualities he does not look at.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the chapter's argument that no strength, wealth, virtue or auspicious time stops Death, and that the Jain answer in both is scripture, not effort or rite."},{"a":"el:26","b":"k:686","type":"develops","note":"Kural 686 names the envoy as learned, fearless of hostile eyes, persuasive and knowing the time; Elathi 26 has the same four and adds a tested beauty of mind and fame known to the world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the envoy's qualities carried from the Kural into a later list that makes reputation itself a qualification, so the envoy is chosen partly for being known.","la":"It sets world-known fame as a sixth excellence beside the Kural's four, ranking renown with skill as something an envoy must already have."},{"a":"el:27","b":"k:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 35 defines virtue as avoiding envy, craving, anger and harsh speech, mixing faults of mind and of speech in one four; Elathi 27 names three faults (coveting, anger, wrong view) and assigns them to the mind alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Set side by side, the Kural's single list is seen sorted in Elathi by the faculty that sins, and wrong view, a doctrinal error, enters a list the Kural keeps to passions.","la":"It sorts faults by faculty, mind here and speech and body in the next two verses, and adds wrong view to the passions, a sorting the couplet does not make."},{"a":"el:27","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 360 names desire, anger and confusion as the three whose end ends pain; Elathi 27 names coveting, anger and wrong view as the three faults of the mind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books build a triad of inner faults with anger at its centre, but where the Kural ends in confusion, a lack of seeing, Elathi ends in wrong view, a positive false belief, which shows the Jain stress on right doctrine.","la":"It replaces confusion with wrong view and puts the triad inside a scheme of ten faults across mind, speech and body.","gr":2},{"a":"el:28","b":"el:27","type":"develops","note":"27 names three faults of the mind, 28 four faults of the mouth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the two verses (with 29) show the work laying out a full ten-part scheme of faults by faculty, mind three, speech four, body three, spread over consecutive verses."},{"a":"el:29","b":"el:28","type":"develops","note":"29 names three faults of the body and closes the scheme begun in 27 and continued in 28.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"With 29 the ten faults are complete, and the pairing shows that the counting form can carry one doctrine across several verses rather than finishing in each."},{"a":"el:29","b":"sp:18","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 18 lists not lying, not stealing, not desiring women and not eating flesh as what ends rebirth; Elathi 29 names killing, theft and lust as the body's faults.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken together, Elathi's body-faults are seen in Sirupanchamoolam as conditions of escaping rebirth, and the sister Jain work adds flesh-eating to the bodily code Elathi frames by faculty."},{"a":"el:31","b":"el:30","type":"develops","note":"30 names six kinds of legal son and 31 names the remaining six.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Only read together do the two verses give the twelve kinds of son of the law-books, showing the work importing a Sanskrit legal classification whole into Tamil verse."},{"a":"el:32","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"Kural 50 says one who lives on earth as life should be lived is placed among the gods; Elathi 32 says one who keeps six silences about himself and others and gives before eating is god of gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair fills the Kural's undefined 'as life should be lived' with particular restraints of speech and the act of giving, and raises the reward from a place among the gods to their head.","la":"It spells out the good life as six named silences, mostly about one's own birth, wealth and years, joined to giving before eating."},{"a":"el:34","b":"k:86","type":"develops","note":"Kural 86 says the host who tends guests will be a welcome guest to the gods; Elathi 34 says the gods seek with love one who gives what fits and keeps from angry and boastful speech.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books put the giver's reward in the gods' welcome, and Elathi adds that restraint of speech and anger, not hospitality alone, is what the gods are drawn to.","la":"It sets the giving beside five restraints of speech and temper, so the gift alone does not earn the welcome."},{"a":"el:35","b":"sp:70","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 70 promises kingship to those who give to prisoners and to people keeping fasts; Elathi 35 promises rule of the earth to one who gives food to renouncers, the needy and the young.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two Jain lists share the reward of kingship for feeding, but Sirupanchamoolam's recipients are mostly ascetics fasting by rule while Elathi's are mostly the needy, which shows the same promise attached to two different kinds of worthy receiver.","gr":2},{"a":"el:36","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 calls removing the hunger of the destitute the place to store wealth; Elathi 36 names whom to feed (the lame, the blind, the mute, the kinless, the unlearned) and says the gods will desire such givers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair turns the Kural's general 'destitute' into named kinds of person who cannot earn, and moves the giver's return from stored wealth to the gods' regard.","la":"It names five kinds of need, bodily, social and of learning, and puts the unlearned among the disabled as people owed food."},{"a":"el:37","b":"k:335","type":"develops","note":"Kural 335 says to hurry to good deeds before the death rattle stops the tongue; Elathi 37 lists the pleas Death ignores, worship among them, and names penance as the remedy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the urgency of the Kural's couplet sharpened into a Jain argument in which even worship fails before Death and only penance done in time avails.","la":"It counts six failed appeals to Death, including worship, so ritual is set aside before penance is named."},{"a":"el:38","b":"n:52","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 52 calls those who prattle endlessly of grammar and astrology the greatest fools; Elathi 38 says the great penance is not removed by letters, numbers or hymns but reached by conduct.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, both Jain works set letters and number-lore against the real work, and Elathi names that work as conduct within penance where the quatrain leaves it as one's own task.","la":"It puts praise and hymns beside letters and numbers as three substitutes for conduct, adding the devotional to the learned ones the quatrain mocks."},{"a":"el:39","b":"k:664","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 664 says speaking is easy and doing as spoken is hard; Elathi 39 lists four easy-hard pairs and ends that winning is easy and the word is hard.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same easy-hard form ranks speech oppositely in the two books, because Kural 664 means announcing a plan while Elathi means the right and truthful word, so the pair shows how much 'speech' changes with what kind of speaking is meant.","la":"It sets four easy-hard pairs in a row, death against character and victory against the word, so speech is ranked among the hard things the Kural keeps for deeds."},{"a":"el:39","b":"el:37","type":"tension","note":"37 treats Death as the overpowering thing no plea escapes; 39 calls dying easy beside the hard work of character.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Within one chapter the work views death from two sides, as the terror that drives penance and as the easy thing beside lasting virtue, and the pair shows death's weight depends on what it is measured against.","gr":5},{"a":"el:40","b":"n:92","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 92 sets death, age and disease before the reader and urges sharing food while wealth is in hand; Elathi 40 joins choosing the way out of impermanence with removing others' hunger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the ethics group tying the feeding of the hungry to the escape from impermanence, and Elathi places it among inner disciplines like not doubting and not wavering.","la":"It sets removing hunger beside non-harm, steadiness and the choice of release, so feeding becomes one of the marks of one bound for heaven.","gr":2},{"a":"el:41","b":"k:232","type":"develops","note":"Kural 232 says all that speakers say amounts to praise resting on those who give to askers; verse 41 says the books of the path will recite the worth of one who gives to the resourceless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates fame in what people say; the verse places it in the scriptures themselves, so giving earns a place in the texts that teach giving.","la":"It makes giving the last of six marks after five refusals (the mean, flesh, falsehood, theft, tight-fistedness), so the praise in the books is earned by a whole discipline and not by the gift alone."},{"a":"el:42","b":"k:260","type":"develops","note":"Kural 260 says all beings worship the one who neither kills nor eats flesh; verse 42 widens the refusal to consenting to killing and to the company of killers, and rewards it with kingship on a killing elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's reward is the reverence of the spared creatures; the verse pays the same abstention with conquest, showing a Jain work content to reward non-killing with a war elephant.","la":"It splits the couplet's one refusal into three degrees of complicity (killing, consenting, keeping killers' company) and then joins them to feeding, so non-killing is graded by distance from the act."},{"a":"el:43","b":"k:908","type":"develops","note":"Kural 908 says those who act as a woman wishes fail their friends and do no good; verse 43 sets not trusting women's words beside feeding great ascetics, both as a giver's marks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the period rule against heeding women is tied in both works to giving: the Kural says it stops good deeds, and Elathi sets it beside the meal for ascetics it protects.","la":"It puts distrust of women's words among gambling, deceit and disputes, as one more distraction a giver must refuse, where the Kural's chapter treats it alone as a fault of the husband."},{"a":"el:44","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Kural 322 calls sharing one's food and sustaining many lives the chief of all virtues; verse 44's giver, who gives white rice before eating, is reborn a king whose lot is to share and eat.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks sharing first among virtues; the verse makes it also the shape of the reward, so the king of the next birth is the sharer of this one on a larger scale.","la":"It sets sharing after truthfulness, refusing flesh, not reviling and clearing the road, so the couplet's chief virtue closes a list that joins speech, diet and public work."},{"a":"el:45","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"Kural 342 says to renounce while there is time; verse 45, on the reading that விடுப்பான் is letting the kingdom go, says the giver will rule, conquer and quickly give it up.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On that reading the verse makes kingship a stage on the way to renunciation, a reward meant to be left, where the couplet speaks of renouncing what one already has.","la":"It sets renunciation at the end of a reward sequence (rule, conquest, release) earned by six marks of conduct, so the couplet's counsel becomes the last step of a karmic career."},{"a":"el:46","b":"k:922","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 922 says not to drink and, if one does, to be one who does not care for the esteem of the worthy; verse 46 bars not only drinking but looking upon drinkers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats drink as the drinker's own loss of standing; the verse extends the bar to the sight of drinkers, so the rule reaches the company one keeps.","la":"It sets three degrees of avoiding toddy (revelry, drinking, seeing drinkers) beside three duties of the table, so abstention is joined to hospitality as one portrait of the householder."},{"a":"el:48","b":"k:527","type":"develops","note":"Kural 527 says crows do not hide but call out and eat, and gain belongs to those of that nature; verse 48 counts announcing the meal in one's house before eating as a mark of the future king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse gives the crow's call a human form, a spoken rice-call (சோற்றரவம்) in the house, and shows the couplet's image was a practice before it was a figure.","la":"It plays the rice-call against two other noises the householder avoids (strangers' clamour, hostile talk), so the sound he makes is defined by the sounds he refuses."},{"a":"el:48","b":"n:271","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 271 says sharing what is cooked before eating is true cooking, and the door of the next world shuts on those who shut the door and eat; verse 48 has the householder call out the meal and rewards him with kingship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give the same act opposite outcomes: the closed door and closed heaven in the Naladiyar, the public rice-call and a sword-held kingdom in Elathi, so the meal's openness decides the next life in both.","la":"It makes the open meal one of five household marks, with keeping guests and cherishing kin, where the quatrain argues the single act with a punishment."},{"a":"el:49","b":"iv:23","type":"qualifies","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 23 calls giving gold with cows to brahmins foremost sweet; verse 49 names gold and herds among great gifts and sets with them food with ghee for great ascetics.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same gifts of gold and cattle are aimed at brahmins in Iniyavai and joined to a meal for ascetics in Elathi, showing the two codes differ on who is the worthy recipient."},{"a":"el:51","b":"sp:65","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 65 says one who digs a tank, sets its bank, clears a road, breaks a field and digs a shared well goes sweetly to heaven; verse 51 rewards tanks, wells, shelters, water-booths and halls with a life of music, dance and love.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Two Jain works price the same public water works differently: heaven in one, sensual pleasure and the akam play of union and sulking in the other."},{"a":"el:52","b":"sp:72","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 72 says a mouthful given unasked to the weak, the old, those fasting to death, the sick and those who lost their land makes the giver a king; verse 52 makes kings of those who feed people who lost house, eyes, wealth, paddy or herds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two works state one rule, food to the ruined earns kingship, but Elathi defines need wholly by loss while Sirupanchamoolam includes the dying ascetic, so they draw the circle of the needy differently."},{"a":"el:53","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 says removing the hunger of the destitute is where a man with wealth should store it; verse 53 names six kinds of destitute to be fed in the house and pays the store back as command of armies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's metaphor of storage is made literal: the verse says what the stored wealth returns as, an army in a later life.","la":"It breaks the couplet's one class of the destitute into six (the indebted, unprotected, empty-handed, lame, old and young), so need is named by its causes."},{"a":"el:54","b":"sp:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 7 says the பார்ப்பான் is one who shares his food; verse 54 lists பார்ப்பார் first among those to be fed, beside the hungry and ascetics.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One Jain work defines the brahmin by giving food, the other names brahmins by birth as receivers of it, so the same word marks a giver's virtue in one and a recipient's rank in the other."},{"a":"el:55","b":"sp:71","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 71 calls delivering a child, caring for the pregnant and taking up an infant great virtue; verse 55 counts feeding and curing new mothers, the pregnant and infants as acts that earn great wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both Jain works make the birth-room a field of merit; Elathi adds food and cure to the care and names a reward where Sirupanchamoolam cites the old books."},{"a":"el:57","b":"sp:75","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 75 says those who cured the diseases of five kinds of sufferer live now without disease; verse 57 says those who fed and cured six diseases become kings.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two works pay the same act of healing differently: Sirupanchamoolam returns it in kind as health, Elathi converts it, like its other gifts, into rule.","gr":2},{"a":"el:59","b":"k:441","type":"develops","note":"Kural 441 counsels choosing the friendship of those mature in virtue and knowledge; verse 59 sets taking the words of the excellent among a learner's acts and makes them the making of a blameless king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks to a king choosing counsellors; the verse shows the same habit begun as a learner's discipline of bowing and study, which yields the king.","la":"It sets taking counsel between bowing and close study, so heeding the great is one of six habits of learning rather than a separate policy of state."},{"a":"el:60","b":"k:460","type":"develops","note":"Kural 460 says nothing brings more affliction than bad company; verse 60 names the afflictions, sin, blame, enmity, death, ruin and fear, and says they cling in both worlds like a curse.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's 'affliction' is left general; the verse itemises it and extends it beyond this life, so bad company is a karmic cost and not only a social one.","la":"It counts the evils that follow from bad company and joins worldly ones (blame, enmity) to otherworldly ones (sin), which the couplet's single word does not separate."},{"a":"el:61","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 360 names three, desire, anger and confusion, whose very names must perish; Elathi 61 names six defects of the soul.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Together they show the Jain list keeping desire and anger but trading the Kural's confusion for pride, avarice, hatred and partiality, so the root faults shift from error of mind to faults of self-regard.","la":"It doubles the Kural's three into six and adds pride and avarice, which 360 does not name, so the passions are counted as the Jain stains of the soul rather than as a triad of the mind."},{"a":"el:62","b":"el:12","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 12 says it is good neither to desire nor to look at the killing ground, the gaming place and the like; Elathi 62 says those of conduct do not go to dances, festivals, weddings, killing grounds or the battlefront.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two verses show that the book's bar is on spectacle as such: 12 keeps the eye from scenes of violence, and 62 puts weddings and festivals under the same bar and names the cost of going."},{"a":"el:63","b":"el:9","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 9 says those who give food, shelter and clothing to the destitute live as learned men; Elathi 63 says those who give food, clothing, stylus and book to students live amply in this life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the book twice making learning or its fruit the reward of a gift, and 63 narrowing the gift to the tools of study, so that the giver shares in the learning he supplies."},{"a":"el:64","b":"k:2","type":"develops","note":"Kural 2 asks what learning has achieved if the learned do not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge; Elathi 64 orders taking the Exalted one as lord, reciting his book, restraint and penance into a path to release.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes worship the condition of learning, and the verse turns that condition into the first step of a sequence that ends in release, so worship, study and penance become stages rather than separate duties.","la":"It sets worship first in a ranked series of four steps ending in release, where the couplet only binds learning to worship."},{"a":"el:65","b":"k:269","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 269 says that even leaping past death is possible for those who have the power of austerity; Elathi 65 says that one who would see Death's strength cut down must study the all-knowing one's book and do a penance that harms nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the verse names the conditions the Kural leaves out: the austerity that defeats death must be guided by scripture and must injure no creature, so non-harm becomes part of the power over death.","la":"It turns the Kural's bare power of austerity into a sequence of conditions (scripture, undistracted mind, harmless penance), each needed for the same victory over Death."},{"a":"el:66","b":"k:369","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 369 says that when craving, the sorrow of sorrows, perishes, joy is unbroken; Elathi 66 describes the high state only by six absences, sweet sleep among them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the goal as unbroken joy, while Elathi refuses even sleep's sweetness to it, so the pair shows two ways of naming release: as fullness of joy or as the cessation of every state, pleasant or painful.","la":"It defines the goal by a list of negations that strikes out goods (light, speech, sleep) along with ills, which the couplet's single positive term does not do."},{"a":"el:67","b":"el:66","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 66 describes the high state by six things it lacks; Elathi 67 names the seven low states one by one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the two verses map both ends of the same cosmos: the summit is known only by negation, while the depths are given names and an order, so the book makes ruin more nameable than release.","gr":2},{"a":"el:68","b":"k:72","type":"develops","note":"Kural 72 says the loving belong to others even to their bones; Elathi 68 names dying for the beloved first among six marks of love.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states love's self-surrender as a claim about ownership, and the verse spells out what that surrender does in practice, from giving life and wealth down to grieving at a parting.","la":"It ranks love's marks from the gift of life down to feeling at union and parting, so the bones of 72 head a list that also counts speech and grief as love."},{"a":"el:70","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"Kural 263 asks whether the others have forgotten penance in wanting to provide for those who renounced; Elathi 70 says the one who gives alms, food and clothing to ascetics will rule earth and sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet treats provisioning ascetics as the reason householders left penance aside, the verse makes that provisioning its own road to heaven, so the giver gains what the ascetic seeks.","la":"It lists the gifts in kind (alms, begged food, food, clothing) and adds a reward over two worlds, which the couplet's question leaves unstated."},{"a":"el:71","b":"k:43","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 43 names five to be tended (ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself); Elathi 71 names six to be fed: wayfarers, burden-bearers, the sick, kin, those of the sky and those of the other side.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Beside the Kural's five, the Elathi list drops the self and widens the circle to the stranger, the labourer, the sick and the adversary, so the household's duty is measured by need more than by bond or rite.","la":"It counts the sick, the porter and the adversary as equal claimants on the household's food, which the couplet's ancestral and kin-centred five do not include."},{"a":"el:72","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"Kural 351 says a joyless birth comes from the confusion that takes the unreal for real; Elathi 72 traces not understanding to fault, karma, birth and suffering, and says it turns in a circle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names ignorance as the cause of birth, and the verse adds the middle steps and closes the chain into a cycle, so birth becomes both the fruit and the renewer of ignorance.","la":"It sets ignorance at the head of six linked steps and closes them into a circle, where the couplet names only cause and result."},{"a":"el:73","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 46 asks what a man who lives household life in virtue would gain by going the outside way; Elathi 73 calls household life and great penance two noble ways of acting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks the household above renunciation, while the verse sets the two side by side as one kind of noble life defined by their relation to attachment, so the pair shows Elathi declining the Kural's ranking.","la":"It pairs the two ways under one name (வினைவாழ்க்கை) and defines each by how it handles attachment, where the couplet sets them against each other."},{"a":"el:74","b":"n:131","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 131 says the beauty of hair, garment border and saffron is not beauty and that learning's beauty is; Elathi 74 says the beauty of waist, shoulders, gait, modesty and neck is not beauty, and that of numbers and letters is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar dismisses adornment, while Elathi dismisses the body itself and even modesty, so the pair shows the same verdict for learning reached against two different rivals, what one wears and what one is.","la":"It replaces the Naladiyar's adornments with six parts and graces of the body, modesty among them, so the rejection reaches a virtue as well as looks."},{"a":"el:74","b":"k:392","type":"develops","note":"Kural 392 calls numbers and letters the two eyes of living beings; Elathi 74 calls the beauty of numbers and letters the only beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse takes the Kural's very pair, எண் and எழுத்து, and moves it from the sense of sight to the standard of beauty, so learning is both how one sees and how one is seen.","la":"It sets the pair of 392 against six bodily beauties, so learning wins a contest the couplet never stages."},{"a":"el:75","b":"sp:33","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 33 says only he who leads one to the higher state is a teacher; Elathi 75 says a teacher's fitness lies in knowing the six schools and refuting a false teacher's word.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"One verse defines the teacher by where he leads, the other by what he can defend, so together they give the Jain teacher's two tests: the goal he serves and his mastery of rival doctrine."},{"a":"el:76","b":"sp:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 35 says giving brings enjoyment, penance heaven and knowledge release; Elathi 76 says giving brings enjoyment, penance heaven and penance that does not cling brings release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two Jain works share the first two rungs word for word but differ on the third, knowledge in one and a deeper penance in the other, so the pair shows a live question within the group about what finally frees."},{"a":"el:77","b":"el:64","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 64 says release comes to one who takes the Exalted one as lord, studies his book and fully performs his penance; Elathi 77 says such penance brings release in three, five or seven births.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the verses give the path and its timetable: 64 says what leads to release, and 77 says release is not immediate but measured in births.","gr":2},{"a":"el:78","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 221 says giving to those who have nothing is true giving; Elathi 78 names six kinds of the destitute: the motherless child, the widow, those without livelihood, the ruined trader, those who lost their wealth, and the blind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet defines giving by the recipient's want, and the verse names who the wanting are and how they came to want, so the general rule gets its cases.","la":"It names each recipient by what was lost (mother, husband, trade, wealth, sight), so the Kural's வறியார் become a list of the newly helpless rather than the poor as a class."},{"a":"el:79","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"Kural 642 says gain and ruin both come from speech, so lapses must be guarded; Elathi 79 says death, ruin, enmity, sorrow, joy and exile all come by the tongue.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two outcomes are spread into six, from joy to death and exile, so the pair shows speech held responsible for the whole range of a life's fortunes, not only for profit and loss.","la":"It splits the Kural's gain and ruin into six named fates, adding death and banishment, so the stakes of speech rise to life and country."},{"a":"el:80","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 says relieving the hunger of the destitute is where the wealthy should store their wealth; Elathi 80 says those who feed outcastes, the wounded, the blind, the immobile, the fallen and the eminent in need will live as kings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet calls feeding a store of wealth, and the verse names the stored return (living as kings) and widens the hungry to include the outcaste, so the gift crosses lines of birth the couplet does not mention.","la":"It names the hungry one by one and puts the outcaste first, so feeding is extended across rank as well as need."},{"a":"iv:1","b":"k:395","type":"develops","note":"Kural 395 has the learner yearn like the poor before the rich; Iniyavai goes further and calls learning sweet even if it must be had by begging.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the poverty a simile for the learner's humility, while the verse makes it literal, so together they show the tradition moving from 'learn as if a beggar' to 'learn even as a beggar'.","la":"It puts learning got by begging first among four sweet things, ahead of the assembly and the company of the great, so the list ranks learning above the dignity it costs.","gr":2},{"a":"iv:2","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 46 asks what one gains by leaving a household run in dharma; Iniyavai 2 agrees while the household goes well but makes renunciation the highest sweetness when it does not prosper.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's rhetorical question turns out to carry a condition: household life wins only while it succeeds, and the verse names the failure that opens the outer path.","la":"It orders household and renunciation as first and highest under a stated condition, a ranking with a hinge the couplet's question does not supply."},{"a":"iv:4","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 251 asks how one who eats flesh to swell his own flesh can have grace; Iniyavai 4 uses the same phrase and calls abstaining foremost sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet condemns the act by what it costs the eater's compassion, the verse praises the abstention as a pleasure, so the pair shows non-eating of flesh argued both as a moral loss avoided and as a gain enjoyed.","la":"It ranks abstaining from flesh 'foremost' beside the sight of an elephant army and a riverside village, so a renunciant's rule is counted among a ruler's and a villager's delights."},{"a":"iv:4","b":"in:22","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 22 is this verse's mirror: kings without elephants, eating flesh to swell flesh and a town cut off by a forest river are painful where elephant armies, abstaining and a town on the river's bank are sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Side by side they show the two books built as paired catalogues, and the forest river item shows the method is not a simple negation: the same river is sweet as a bank and painful as a barrier."},{"a":"iv:5","b":"k:323","type":"develops","note":"Kural 323 ranks not killing first and not lying after it; Iniyavai 5 also puts not killing foremost and closes with not speaking evil of anyone.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both books place non-killing at the head of a short ranked list and a restraint of speech behind it, so the pair shows the Kural's ranking reused as the frame of a king's list, where justice of the sceptre is slotted between.","la":"It sets non-killing in a king's list beside unjust honours and the straight sceptre, so a universal vow is read as the first duty of rule.","gr":2},{"a":"iv:6","b":"k:33","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 33 bids one do dharma as far as one can and without ceasing; Iniyavai 6 calls dharma done to the measure of one's strength foremost sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets the measure as a demand, the verse as a pleasure, so the pair shows the capacity clause working two ways: it limits what is owed and it is what makes the act sweet rather than a burden.","la":"It joins measured dharma to hearing good counsel and choosing worthy guards, so doing good is ranked first among the supports of a life rather than stated alone."},{"a":"iv:7","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 134 says a forgotten Veda can be relearned but a brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct fails; Iniyavai 7 names brahmins' keeping of the Veda as very sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse praises the recitation the couplet treats as recoverable, and the couplet ranks conduct above it, so a reader sees the Kural demoting the very possession the later list holds up as the brahmin's excellence.","la":"Its own third item puts restraint above even a father, so within the list conduct also outranks the ties of birth, which brings it closer to the couplet than its first item suggests."},{"a":"iv:8","b":"in:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Innaa Narpathu 28 calls painful the gallop of a spirited horse ridden by one who has not learned; Iniyavai 8 calls sweet the strength of the spirited horse one rides.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same spirited horse is a pleasure or a pain according to the rider's training, so the pair locates the sweetness not in the horse but in the skill that can bear its strength."},{"a":"iv:10","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"Kural 57 says confinement is useless and a woman's own நிறை is her best guard; Iniyavai 10 calls putting away women who lack நிறை sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet moves the guard inside the woman, and the verse states the consequence when that inner guard is missing, so together they show நிறை made both the woman's defence and the ground on which she may be sent away.","la":"It sets dismissing such a wife beside living free of debt and avoiding the mean-minded, so the rule is filed among the household's protections, not among the virtues of marriage."},{"a":"iv:11","b":"k:1065","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1065 says nothing is sweeter than thin gruel won by one's own effort; Iniyavai 11 says nothing is nobler than refusing to eat from unworthy hands even unto death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both close on the same 'nothing above it' form, but the couplet praises the food one earns and the verse the food one refuses, so the pair shows the two halves of a single code of eating with dignity.","la":"It caps a list about not living off the road and not taking crookedly with a refusal unto death, so the list climbs from avoiding gain to giving up life."},{"a":"iv:12","b":"k:726","type":"develops","note":"Kural 726 asks what use treatises are to those who fear the subtle assembly; Iniyavai 12 calls sweet the learning of one who does not fear the clamorous assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet denies the fearful scholar the worth of his books; the verse grants the fearless one sweetness, so the pair shows courage before the assembly as the condition on which learning pays.","la":"It sets fearless learning between a healthy child and lasting wealth, so learning appears as one of the household's goods kept safe from its typical failure."},{"a":"iv:12","b":"in:35","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 35 names the sickness that infants suffer as painful; Iniyavai 12 names a child living without sickness as sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books say the same thing from its two sides, and the pairing shows that the child's health is the first item in the sweet list but the third in the painful one, so each book ranks it by what surrounds it."},{"a":"iv:13","b":"k:969","type":"develops","note":"Kural 969 likens those who die when honour is touched to the yak that dies once its hair is lost; Iniyavai 13 calls not living after honour is lost foremost sweet.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet treats the act as a matter of nature, the verse as a pleasure chosen first, so the pair shows dying for honour justified both as instinct and as preference.","la":"It ranks death before dishonour ahead of restrained living and flawless wealth, so honour is set above the two things that would make a long life comfortable."},{"a":"iv:14","b":"k:66","type":"develops","note":"Kural 66 implies children's babble is sweeter than flute and lute; Iniyavai 14 calls hearing it sweeter than amrita.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet measures the child's voice against the best of art, the verse against the food of the gods, so the pair shows the babble outranking both human and divine sweetness.","la":"It sets the babble beside the child's first steps and then beside steadiness when one's deeds come home, so the household's joys and the courage to suffer are counted together."},{"a":"iv:15","b":"in:38","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 38 calls painful the folly of following after another man's wife; Iniyavai 15 calls sweet the dignity of not looking after her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two lines use nearly the same words and say the same thing from two sides: what Innaa names as folly in the doer, Iniyavai names as dignity in the one who abstains, so the act is judged by its effect on the man's standing.","gr":2},{"a":"iv:15","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"Kural 148 calls not looking at another's wife great manhood and full conduct; Iniyavai 15 calls not looking after her a dignity that is very sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the restraint a heroism, the verse a pleasure, and the verse narrows the act to the backward glance, so the pair shows the restraint tested at the smallest motion of the eye.","la":"It sets the restraint beside rain on parched crops and elephants roaring at a king's gate, so a private abstention is ranked among the large public pleasures of the land."},{"a":"iv:16","b":"k:722","type":"develops","note":"Kural 722 says those who make their learning go home before the learned are the learned among the learned; Iniyavai 16 calls setting out one's learning before the learned very sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the act the test of reputation, the verse makes it a pleasure, so the pair shows the same performance valued as proof to others and as delight to oneself.","la":"It joins speaking before the learned to joining one's betters and giving without asking, so the list ranks learned speech first among acts of standing among superiors."},{"a":"iv:17","b":"k:679","type":"develops","note":"Iniyavai 17 nearly quotes Kural 679 (doing good to friends; winning over those not allied), but reads நாட்டார்க்கு, the land's people, and calls both sweet where the couplet ranks them by urgency.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's ranking is one of speed, the verse's one of sweetness, and the verse adds granaries and many allies, so the pair shows a counsel of timing turned into a picture of a secure ruler.","la":"It adds a third item, full granaries and true allies, so the couplet's two acts of alliance become part of a ruler's provisioning."},{"a":"iv:19","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 221 says giving to those who have nothing is the only true gift; Iniyavai 19 calls giving great wealth 'where it is fit' sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes need the test of a gift and the verse makes fitness of the recipient the test, so the pair shows two criteria in the tradition for where wealth should go.","la":"It places giving after making wealth without lack, and after truthfulness and humility, so giving is set as the last stage of a well-conducted life, not as an act prompted by another's need."},{"a":"iv:19","b":"in:32","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 32 calls painful the talk of those who speak behind one's back; Iniyavai 19 calls sweet living without backbiting one's friends.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"They state one thing from two sides, the victim's pain and the speaker's restraint, and together show backbiting judged both by what it does to the hearer and what refraining does for the speaker."},{"a":"iv:1","b":"iv:37","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 1 names the speech of women with pearl-like smiles as sweet; verse 37 names knowing such women as poison as sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within one book the same object is sweet to hear and sweet to know as poison, which shows the book addressing the householder in one verse and the would-be renouncer in another rather than holding one view of women."},{"a":"iv:22","b":"in:39","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai calls giving with knowledge of one's income sweet; Innaa 39 counts the liberality of one with nothing to give as painful. They say the same thing from two sides.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read as a pair, the twin books fix one measure for giving from opposite ends: the pleasure lies inside one's income and the pain begins outside it."},{"a":"iv:22","b":"n:65","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 65 calls the gift of one without wealth the true fruit of giving; Iniyavai calls giving within one's income sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the ethics group divided on where giving is best: the quatrain prizes the gift that strains one's means, while this verse prizes the gift that stays within them.","la":"It sets measured giving beside independence from others and refusal of indulgence, so giving is ranked as one form of self-limitation rather than a test of sacrifice."},{"a":"iv:23","b":"in:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Iniyavai calls giving cows and gold to brahmins foremost sweet; Innaa 21 counts the speech of a brahmin without Vedic recitation as painful, and giving to the never-content as painful.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Together the twin books make the brahmin a privileged recipient but not an unconditional one: the Innaa line ties his standing to recitation, a condition this verse leaves out."},{"a":"iv:24","b":"n:65","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 65 calls true patience that of a strong man able to punish; Iniyavai calls sweet the austerity of one who wants to win and does not rage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place the worth of restraint where the urge to strike is live, and the pair shows the group treating anger held back in a contest as a form of penance.","la":"It names the restraint நோன்பு, a word of austerity, and sets it beside endurance and freedom from craving, so mastering anger is filed with ascetic practice."},{"a":"iv:25","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 343, in the chapter on renunciation, says the field of the five senses must be crushed and all desires let go at once; Iniyavai calls curbing the craving of the five senses foremost sweet, with no renouncer named.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows sense-restraint moving from the renouncer's whole programme in the Kural to a first pleasure open to anyone, its demand softened from abandoning to curbing.","la":"It ranks sense-restraint first and pairs it with keeping away from the unlearned and the unsteady, so the inner gate and the outer company are guarded together."},{"a":"iv:26","b":"n:111","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 111 says refusing what one cannot give is no fault, but letting an asker's hope (நசை) die after keeping him waiting is worse than ingratitude; Iniyavai calls not killing the hope (நசை) of those who come sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both judge a giver by what happens to the asker's hope, the quatrain condemning its slow killing by false promise and the verse praising its keeping, so honesty to the hope matters as much as the gift.","la":"It ties keeping askers' hope to a spirit that will not live without regard and a bond that hides nothing, so generosity is grouped with self-respect rather than with promise-keeping."},{"a":"iv:26","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 966 asks why one should stand behind those who scorn one; Iniyavai calls very sweet the spirit that will not live where it is held in no regard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet argues from the loss of praise and heaven, while the verse calls the refusal a pleasure in itself, so the pair shows honour defended first by reckoning and then by taste.","la":"It places the refusal to live without regard between two items on giving, so the self-respect of the giver and of the one who will not cringe are made one temper."},{"a":"iv:27","b":"k:969","type":"develops","note":"Kural 969 says those like the yak give up life when honour is lost; Iniyavai calls not living on when dishonour comes foremost sweet.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The verse names as a pleasure what the couplet presents as the nature of a rare kind of person, so the pair shows an admired temperament turned into something anyone may prefer.","la":"It sets dying rather than living dishonoured between the giver's bearing and right receiving, so honour is framed by how one gives and how one takes.","gr":2},{"a":"iv:28","b":"k:1066","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1066 says even asking water for a cow is the most degrading thing for the tongue; Iniyavai calls not going about asking others to relieve one's distress foremost sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair values not asking from both sides, as disgrace avoided and as the first of pleasures, and the verse's ஆற்றாமை widens it from asking for goods to asking for relief.","la":"It ranks not asking first and follows it with remembering death and holding to right speech under ruin, so refusing to beg sits among the disciplines of a man facing loss."},{"a":"iv:28","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"Kural 113 says to let go at once of gain that comes from leaving fairness; Iniyavai calls sweetest the discernment that speaks no wrong even when one's fortune is being destroyed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet refuses unfair gain and the verse refuses unfair speech even to escape ruin, so the pair carries fairness from getting to speaking under loss.","la":"It puts this item last as the sweetest of three, after not begging and remembering death, so integrity under ruin outranks the other two."},{"a":"iv:29","b":"k:596","type":"develops","note":"Kural 596 says whatever one thinks, think of rising; Iniyavai calls sweet the zeal born of thinking of rising, using the couplet's own word உயர்வுள்ளி.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse borrows the couplet's phrase and names what the thought produces, zeal, so the pair shows aspiration as the root and ஊக்கம் as its fruit.","la":"It brackets the thought of rising between leaving the base and not scorning the lowly, so aspiration is hedged on both sides by conduct toward others."},{"a":"iv:29","b":"tk:77","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 77 opens with கயவரைக் கையிகந்து வாழ்தல் and makes it a mark of those whose family has no stain; Iniyavai 29 opens with கயவரைக் கைகழிந்து வாழ்தல் and calls it sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical line shows a saying circulating between the counted works, and the different predicates show each bending it: a sign of good birth in one, a pleasure open to anyone in the other."},{"a":"iv:30","b":"in:40","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa 40 counts seizing a deposit (அடைக்கலம் வவ்வுதல்) as painful; Iniyavai calls the goodness that does not seize a deposit the finest. They say the same thing from two sides.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Iniyavai line adds the temptation the Innaa line omits, the thought that no one knew of it, so the pair shows the deposit as a test of honesty without witnesses."},{"a":"iv:30","b":"k:104","type":"develops","note":"Kural 104 says those who know the fruit of a kindness count a millet's worth as a palm; Iniyavai calls living with the fruit of a kindness weighed very sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says how to weigh a kindness and the verse says living by that weighing is a pleasure, so the pair shows gratitude as a habit of measuring that becomes a way of life.","la":"It joins gratitude to honest speech in the assembly and keeping a deposit, so the three become one virtue of rendering to each what is owed."},{"a":"iv:31","b":"k:417","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 417 says those whose listening is gathered on close study do not speak folly even when they misunderstand; Iniyavai says even those of excellent learning by listening should speak only after examining.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet trusts deep listening to protect speech even through error, while the verse still asks the learned to examine first, so the pair shows the counted work refusing learning as a guarantee.","la":"It ranks examined speech with protecting refugees and doing one's duties even on borrowed money, so careful speech is made a duty owed to others."},{"a":"iv:31","b":"k:220","type":"develops","note":"Kural 220 says loss through ஒப்புரவு is worth buying even by selling oneself; Iniyavai calls doing what is to be done even by borrowing sweet, if செய்வன covers duties of help.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's extreme, selling oneself, appears here in a household form, taking a loan, so the pair shows the ethics group pricing duty to others in debt.","la":"It sets duties done on credit beside protecting those who came for refuge, so borrowing to do one's part is grouped with protection rather than prudence.","gr":2},{"a":"iv:33","b":"pn:330","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 330 praises one man who, sword raised, stopped a king's army from passing him as the shore stops the sea; Iniyavai calls checking the army of great kings in the crowded battle sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru makes the stand one hero's glory, and the counted verse files it with civic zeal and tireless effort, showing heroic memory folded into a general ethic of exertion."},{"a":"iv:34","b":"k:800","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 800 says to leave the friendship of the unfit even at the cost of a gift; Iniyavai calls sweet leaving the friendship of the worthless even when they embrace one.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet counts the cost of leaving in goods and the verse in affection refused, so the pair shows two prices a man may pay to be free of an unworthy friend.","la":"It closes a list that begins with keeping off the roads at night and speaking without slips, so ending a bad friendship is grouped with other kinds of self-guarding."},{"a":"iv:35","b":"k:588","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 588 says to confirm what one spy reports through another (ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றி); Iniyavai calls sweet for kings learning facts ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றி.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse's compressed phrase echoes the couplet's, and read through it the item can mean checking one spy by another, not merely using one.","la":"It sets spying beside full inquiry before judgment and sharing with all beings, so intelligence is ranked with justice and care as one royal pleasure."},{"a":"iv:36","b":"k:167","type":"develops","note":"Kural 167 says the goddess shows the man of twisted envy (அவ்வித்து அழுக்காறு) to her elder sister; Iniyavai calls not speaking out of twisted envy foremost sweet, in the same words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet punishes envy held and the verse praises envy unspoken, so the pair shows the ethics group separating the feeling from its voicing and treating restraint of the tongue as a partial cure.","la":"It puts restraint of envious speech first, ahead of conquered anger and refusing to snatch, ranking a fault of the tongue above faults of temper and hand."},{"a":"iv:37","b":"n:11","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 11 says the wise, knowing grey hair will come, renounced in childhood; Iniyavai calls knowing youth to be old age sweet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain draws the conclusion, early renunciation, that the verse leaves implicit, and the verse calls that foresight sweet rather than grim.","la":"It joins the foresight of age to seeing desire as poison and to the comfort of safe kin, so two renunciant insights are framed by a household one."},{"a":"iv:38","b":"k:524","type":"develops","note":"Kural 524 says the fruit of wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin; Iniyavai says the feast for guests of one whose kin stand by him is sweet beyond measure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes kin the use of wealth and the verse names the guest-feast as the moment that use shows, so the pair links kinship to hospitality.","la":"It ranks the kin-supported feast above the fighter's weapons and allies with எத்துணையும் ஆற்ற, so the house's hospitality outranks martial strength."},{"a":"iv:39","b":"k:1060","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1060 says the one who asks must not be angry; Iniyavai calls it foremost sweet that one who lives by alms does not clamour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place restraint on the receiver rather than the giver, and the pair shows the ethics group laying a duty on the needy as well as on the rich.","la":"It moves the rule from askers in general to the one who lives by alms and joins it to calm in a borrowed lodging, so it becomes part of a renouncer's poverty.","gr":3},{"a":"iv:39","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 340 calls the soul a lodger in a borrowed corner (துச்சில்) of the body; Iniyavai calls sweet not grieving while living in a துச்சில்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read through the couplet, the verse's lodger may be the soul in the body as well as a poor man in borrowed rooms, which would make its item an image of calm about mortality.","la":"It sets the lodger's calm between the alms-eater's restraint and freedom from greedy want, so the image is read inside a renouncer's poverty."},{"a":"iv:40","b":"k:391","type":"develops","note":"Kural 391 says to learn faultlessly what should be learned and stand by it; Iniyavai calls the firm core of learning what has worth, faultlessly and over many days, the sweetest thing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse adds duration and names the result காழ், a hard core, so the pair shows flawless learning as something that hardens over time.","la":"It closes the book on learning after staying in one's town at a cost and not eating the seed-grain, so learning is grouped with the household's ways of keeping what feeds the future."},{"a":"in:2","b":"ac:6","type":"develops","note":"Acharakkovai 6 lists the dog among things a person in எச்சில் does not look at; Innaa 2 counts a fowl and a dog entering a brahmin's house as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show the dog as a standing source of pollution in the period's code, kept from the eye of the impure person in one book and from the threshold of the brahmin house in the other."},{"a":"in:2","b":"s:426","type":"tension","note":"Pattinathar says not to talk of எச்சில் and of fit and unfit, since people do not know where the impurity lies; Innaa 2 counts animals entering a brahmin's house as a pain, on the reading that the pain is ritual defilement.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Innaa locates defilement in what crosses a brahmin's threshold, while Pattinathar denies that impurity sits in outward things at all, so the pair shows the purity code and its later Siddha rejection."},{"a":"in:3","b":"iv:32","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 32 names not living under a king without care for his people as foremost sweet; Innaa 3 names living under fierce kings of bent sceptre as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books say the same thing from both sides, and the Iniyavai adds that the sweet thing is a choice (not to live there), while the Innaa's float image shows the subject who has no such choice."},{"a":"in:3","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"Kural 557 likens a king's want of kindness to the earth's want of rain; Innaa 3 sets living under a cruel king beside swimming long water without a float.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet measures a cruel king by what the land loses; the Innaa measures him by the subject's helplessness, a swimmer with nothing to hold, which turns the public loss into a private danger.","la":"It puts life under a cruel king beside the floatless swimmer and the harsh speaker's company, so tyranny is counted among the dangers a person meets rather than among the faults of a ruler."},{"a":"in:4","b":"k:861","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 861 says to avoid opposing the strong and to favour enmity with the weak; Innaa 4 counts doing harm to the very strong as painful, and provoking the fortunate as well.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Innaa keeps the couplet's first half and drops its second: the danger of opposing the strong stands without the counsel to turn on the weak, and it is widened to those who merely have fortune.","la":"It sets the harm done to the strong beside the ox-less farmer and the fleeing soldier, so the act is counted as self-inflicted loss rather than weighed as strategy."},{"a":"in:5","b":"k:663","type":"develops","note":"Kural 663 says the strength of a deed is that it is revealed only at its end, and that it brings trouble if it comes out midway; Innaa 5 counts a deed done without secrecy (மறை) as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet explains when secrecy matters (until the end), and the Innaa supplies the images for an exposed undertaking: unfenced cane and a leaking roof.","la":"It pairs the open deed with rule without justice and gives both two images of unguarded keeping, so secrecy is grouped with protection rather than with cunning."},{"a":"in:5","b":"k:548","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 548 says a ruler who does not examine and do justice perishes of himself; Innaa 5 counts a kingship that rules without முறை as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet looks at the unjust ruler's own fall, while the Innaa, by placing him beside a cane field with no fence, looks at what his subjects suffer while he still rules.","la":"It makes injustice one of four unprotected things, so the verse judges rule by the protection it fails to give rather than by the king's fate."},{"a":"in:6","b":"k:218","type":"tension","note":"Kural 218 says those who know what is due do not falter in giving even when they have no means; Innaa 6 counts giving by those in distress as painful, on the reading that இன்னா marks a thing to be avoided.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On that reading the books disagree about the poor man's gift: the Kural praises it as a mark of discernment, and the Innaa ranks it with a warrior idle in battle and a crooked man's word.","la":"It sets the distressed giver's gift beside the virtuous man's harsh word, so the verse names gifts and words that go wrong because the giver is in the wrong state, not because he lacks virtue."},{"a":"in:7","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 727 likens the learning of one who fears the assembly to a bright sword in a பேடி's hand among enemies; Innaa 7 counts a weapon held by one without strength as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet uses the powerless swordsman as a simile for useless learning, and the Innaa counts him plainly but puts beside him the speaker who cannot reply, so the verse holds both halves of the couplet's comparison as separate items.","la":"It lists the weak man's weapon and the speaker who cannot answer as two items of one kind, where the couplet joins them only as image and subject."},{"a":"in:7","b":"k:650","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 650 likens those who cannot expound what they have learned to flowers in bloom without scent; Innaa 7 counts the beauty of a scentless flower as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Innaa places the scentless flower directly beside the speech of one who cannot reply, which is the Kural's comparison spelled out as two counted pains.","la":"It turns the Kural's simile into two items, a scentless flower and a speaker who cannot answer, and adds weapon and daring as two more things without their ground."},{"a":"in:8","b":"k:784","type":"develops","note":"Kural 784 says friendship is not for laughing together but for rebuking when one oversteps; Innaa 8 counts the lack of binding affection (நார்) in friends made in laughter as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet says what friendship is for, and the Innaa names the pain of the friendship that was only laughter: it has no fibre when it is tested.","la":"It sets the laughing friend beside the fair-seeming heart without character and the ungracious friend, so the verse ranks three counterfeits of friendship together."},{"a":"in:9","b":"pn:375","type":"qualifies","note":"Innaa 9 counts the absence of patrons as the foremost pain of gift-seekers; in Puram 375 a drummer finds no patron but Ay and wishes that, with Ay gone, there be no patrons at all, lest bards praise kings of worthless wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bard in the Puram poem prefers the very lack the Innaa ranks first among his pains to service under the ungenerous, which the Innaa counts as a separate pain in its third item.","gr":2},{"a":"in:10","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 likens speaking before those not of one's circle to nectar spilled in a dirty yard; Innaa 10 counts reciting a song where no one grasps its meaning as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the waste in the hearers' unfitness, and the Innaa, by setting the song beside the poor man's generosity, shows it as a gift wasted on both sides: the giver of words has no receiver, the would-be giver of goods has nothing.","la":"It sets the wasted recital beside a lonely dark path and the penniless man's liberality, so the song without listeners is counted among gifts that cannot arrive.","gr":4},{"a":"in:10","b":"iv:22","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 22 names giving with knowledge of one's income as sweet; Innaa 10 names liberality attempted by those without wealth as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two books say the same thing from two sides, and together they make the measure explicit: giving is judged against income, sweet when it fits and painful when it outruns it."},{"a":"in:11","b":"k:890","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 890 likens living with those not in accord (உடம்பாடு இலாதவர்) to living with a snake in a hut; Innaa 11 counts the embrace of a wife without உடம்பாடு as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves 'those not in accord' open, and the Innaa narrows the same word to the wife, placing the discord at the closest point of the house, the embrace itself.","la":"It sets the discordant wife beside the petty friend, the intemperate companion and the creditor's eye, so marriage is counted among the bonds that can turn to pain."},{"a":"in:13","b":"k:890","type":"develops","note":"Kural 890 likens living with those not in accord to living with a snake in a hut; Innaa 13 counts a house where people like a disease live as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's image is sudden danger and the Innaa's is slow sickness, so the pair shows two ways a house of the wrong people harms: the bite and the wasting.","la":"It sets the diseased house among the king's and courtier's pains, so a household trouble is counted beside a bell-less elephant and submission to proud kings."},{"a":"in:15","b":"iv:32","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 32 names as sweet the purpose of an undertaking (கருமப் பொருள்) as those who know its surroundings explain it; Innaa 15 names the same thing explained by the unlearned as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same phrase is sweet or painful in the two books depending only on who speaks it, which shows that both make the counsellor's knowledge, not the undertaking, decide its worth."},{"a":"in:16","b":"k:392","type":"develops","note":"Kural 392 says numbers and letters are the two eyes of living beings; Innaa 16 sets the good looks of a man without eyes beside accounts kept by one who does not know numbers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Innaa places the literal blind man next to the innumerate one, which makes the Kural's metaphor into two counted pains of one kind: both are blind.","la":"By putting eyelessness and innumeracy side by side as items 3 and 4, the list enacts the couplet's equation of number with sight without stating it."},{"a":"in:16","b":"k:1001","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1001 says one who hoards great wealth and does not eat it is as good as dead; Innaa 16 counts storing great wealth without enjoying it as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet judges the hoarder as already dead, and the Innaa, by setting him beside the handsome blind man, makes him one who owns a good he cannot use.","la":"It pairs unused wealth with unseeing beauty, so the hoard is grouped with gifts that their owner cannot enjoy rather than with failures of giving.","gr":2},{"a":"in:17","b":"k:840","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 840 likens a fool's entering the assembly of the worthy to putting an unwashed foot on a bed; Innaa 17 counts a fool's entering among the worthy (சான்றோருட் பேதை புகல்) as painful, in nearly the same words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the image of defilement and the Innaa gives the worthy their mark, ஆன்றவித்த (full and self-subdued), which is what the fool's entry offends.","la":"It sets the fool's intrusion beside a penance without self-subduing, so both are counted as the unsubdued entering where self-mastery is required.","gr":2},{"a":"in:17","b":"k:656","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 656 says not to do what the worthy condemn even to relieve one's mother's hunger; Innaa 17 counts abandoning the mother who bore one as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural uses the mother's need as the hardest case in which a wrong still may not be done, and the Innaa counts her neglect as a pain in its own right, so together they bound the duty: it is weighty, but it does not license blame.","la":"It sets the mother's neglect beside a fool among the worthy and a false penance, so care for her is counted among the duties of a person's standing rather than as a test case.","gr":2},{"a":"in:17","b":"iv:34","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 34 names not going about at night as foremost sweet; Innaa 17 names going about when it has grown dark as very painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same rule appears from both sides with matching intensifiers (முன் in the Iniyavai, பெரிது in the Innaa), so both books rank night travel above their other items."},{"a":"in:18","b":"k:600","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 600 says a man's உரம் is the abundance of his உள்ளம் (inner drive); Innaa 18 counts the உள்ளம் of a man who has உரன் lying idle as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines strength as drive, so the two cannot come apart; the Innaa names the case where they do, a strong man whose mind sleeps, and counts it as a pain.","la":"It sets the idle strong man beside the man who faces a warrior's chest, so lost strength and rash strength are counted together."},{"a":"in:19","b":"k:409","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 409 says the unlearned born high are worth less than the learned born low; Innaa 19 counts lack of learning in one born to a good family as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make learning the test of birth, but the Kural overturns the ranking while the Innaa keeps it, counting the unlearned noble as a failed seed and union outside family rank as another pain.","la":"It sets the unlearned noble beside a seed that fails to sprout and a union without family standing, so birth is kept as the frame that learning fulfils, not one it overturns."},{"a":"in:19","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all living beings and distinction comes from the work they do; Innaa 19 counts union where there is no family standing as painful, on the reading that குலம் is birth rank.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On that reading the two disagree on whether family rank is a ground of worth: the Kural denies it at birth, and the Innaa makes its absence a reason a union is a pain.","la":"It places union outside family rank beside the unlearned noble and the immodest woman, so birth, learning and women's conduct are counted as one order of standing."},{"a":"in:19","b":"tk:84","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 84 joins people of a long line who lack the excellence of learning to poverty; Innaa 19 counts lack of learning in one born to a good family as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books set the unlearned man of good family beside a failed source of growth (the badly made tank inlet, the unsprouting seed), so the two together show a shared image: lineage is a resource that learning must make flow.","gr":2},{"a":"in:20","b":"k:13","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 13 says that if the sky fails, hunger torments the world though it is ringed by water; Innaa 20 counts the failure of the rain's plenty as painful to a town.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of the world and its hunger, and the Innaa of the town and its plenty, flanked by the koel's ill-timed call and a sluggish ox, so the failure is set in the farmer's season rather than the cosmos.","la":"It sets failed rain between the out-of-season koel and the weak ox, so drought is counted as one of the farming year's mistimings and lacks."},{"a":"in:20","b":"in:4","type":"develops","note":"Innaa 4 counts the soil's moisture passing unused for ploughmen without oxen; Innaa 20 counts ploughing with a sluggish ox as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book grades the ploughman's lack across two verses: with no ox the wet season is lost, and with a sluggish ox it is wasted, so the ox decides whether the rain is of use."},{"a":"in:21","b":"tk:18","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 18 names old age and great disease as two separate things to fear more than thieves; the Innaa line joins them as one pain, sickness that comes once one is old.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two dreads compound: the Innaa line shows disease and age not as two risks but as one in which each makes the other worse."},{"a":"in:22","b":"k:251","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe flesh-eating as feeding flesh with flesh (ஊனைப் பெருக்குதல், ஊன் பெருக்கற்கு); the Kural asks how such a person can hold அருள், the Innaa line ranks the act as the first of pains.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows one image doing two jobs: in the Kural it proves the loss of compassion, in the Innaa verse it is a pain ranked above even soured honey and kingless armies.","la":"It ranks flesh-eating first (முன்) among things that have lost their proper working, a king without elephants, honey gone sour, a cut-off town.","gr":5},{"a":"in:23","b":"k:1076","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural explains the image the Innaa line only names: the base are like a beaten drum because they carry every secret they hear to others.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"With the Kural's gloss, the drum-like talker in the Innaa list is seen as a breach of keeping, matching the wall-less gate and the fouled ford beside him.","la":"It sets loose talk beside a gate with no wall and a ford fouled by washing, so the talker becomes one more failed guard."},{"a":"in:23","b":"k:262","type":"develops","note":"Kural 262 says penance comes to those who have it and is futile for those without; the Innaa line names what such a man lacks, நிறை, self-restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Innaa line fills the Kural's circular condition with content: the 'தவம் one must already have' is the inner restraint without which penance is a pain.","la":"It names the missing quality (நிறை) and files the empty penance with other things whose holding power is gone."},{"a":"in:24","b":"in:23","type":"develops","note":"Verse 23 makes guarding the gate of a wall-less old town painful; 24 makes living in an old town without protection painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second verse moves the pain from the guard's futile labour to the townsman's daily exposure, so one weakness is felt by both its defenders and its dwellers.","gr":4},{"a":"in:25","b":"in:24","type":"develops","note":"After the town without wall (23) and without protection (24), verse 25 names the old town without கட்டு, bonds of order.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Across three verses the town's weakness moves from its stone to its order, teaching that a town is unsafe for lacking ties as much as for lacking walls.","gr":2},{"a":"in:25","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"Kural 788 defines friendship as clearing a friend's distress at once, like a hand catching a slipping garment; the Innaa line counts the mere sight of a friend's distress as a deep pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the feeling behind the Kural's reflex: the friend's hand moves so fast because seeing the trouble already hurts.","la":"It sets the friend's trouble against the enemy's pride in the next line, so friendship's pain is measured against its mirror in enmity."},{"a":"in:26","b":"k:892","type":"develops","note":"Kural 892 says neglecting the great brings ills through them; the Innaa verse counts doing evil to the great as painful and opens with letting go of a bond with them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show neglect and injury of the great as stages of one fault, the Innaa list naming the first step, loosening the bond, that the Kural leaves implicit.","la":"Its first and last items both concern the great, so harming them is shown as the end of a course that begins with letting go of them."},{"a":"in:26","b":"iv:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Iniyavai 28 calls not going about crying for relief from one's distress foremost sweet; the Innaa line calls telling one's suffering to those who do not care painful. They agree from two sides, but the Innaa line narrows the fault to an uncaring listener.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair separates self-restraint in grief from the choice of listener: the Iniyavai praises not complaining at all, the Innaa only counts complaint to the indifferent as a pain."},{"a":"in:27","b":"iv:37","type":"qualifies","note":"Iniyavai 37 calls knowing youth as old age sweet; Innaa 27 calls entering old age within youth painful. They do not disagree: one is foresight of age, the other its untimely arrival or imitation.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they keep the counsel to remember age while young from turning into premature decline, the sweet awareness and the painful condition being kept apart."},{"a":"in:28","b":"k:401","type":"develops","note":"Kural 401 likens holding an assembly without full learning to a board game with no board; the Innaa line names the same act, கல்லாதான் கோட்டி கொளல், as painful.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Innaa verse adds exposure to the Kural's emptiness: paired with the unlearned man on a prancing horse, holding court becomes a display that can throw him.","la":"It frames the verse with the unlearned man, mounting a spirited horse at the start and holding court at the end, two displays he cannot carry."},{"a":"in:28","b":"k:1046","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1046 says the words of the poor go slack even when well understood; the Innaa line counts the very grace of speech in a poor man's mouth as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair moves the loss from the listeners to the speaker: the Kural notes that no one heeds, the Innaa line that fine speech which cannot land is itself a pain.","la":"It sets poverty beside want of learning and skill, so wealth and learning appear as the two things that give speech its weight."},{"a":"in:29","b":"k:61","type":"develops","note":"Kural 61 prizes above all children who come to understanding (அறிவறிந்த மக்கட்பேறு); the Innaa line counts getting children without it (அறிவறியா மக்கட் பெறல்) as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Innaa line states the other branch of the Kural's condition, showing that its praise was never of children as such but of children with sense.","la":"It sets senseless children beside a snake charmed without knowledge and a secret in a careless ear, as power held without the knowledge to handle it."},{"a":"in:29","b":"in:23","type":"develops","note":"29 counts a secret heard by one who cannot hold it; 23 counts the talk of those who are like a beaten drum.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two lines describe one leak from both ends, the careless hearer and the broadcasting talker, so the book shows a secret failing first in the ear and then on the tongue."},{"a":"in:30","b":"k:890","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 890 makes living with those not in accord like living with a snake in a hut; the Innaa line names the house with a hidden snake plainly, as a bodily danger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed together, the Innaa line gives the literal fear on which the Kural's figure rests, so the reader sees why discord at home is felt as close, quiet and constant danger.","la":"It keeps the snake-house literal beside a high leap, a raging elephant and a tiger's path, four plain dangers with no moral item."},{"a":"in:31","b":"k:573","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 573 asks what use a melody is that does not fit the song, and an eye without kindly regard; the Innaa verse opens with a lute not set to its mode and ends with the enmity of those without coolness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both pass from music out of tune to a person out of tune, which suggests a shared idea of kindness as attunement, with the Innaa verse adding enmity as its opposite.","la":"It spreads the Kural's single analogy over four items, three instruments or reckonings out of true and then a hostile temper."},{"a":"in:32","b":"k:184","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 184 grants that harsh words to the face are better than careless words behind the back; the Innaa line names the backbiter as one who will not speak to the face (முன்னை உரையார்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both rank the face above the back, and the Innaa wording makes the backbiter's fault a refusal of the open speech the Kural concedes, so cowardice is seen inside the malice.","la":"It sets backbiting beside failing to guard oneself and bad company, as ways a person is undone without seeing it."},{"a":"in:32","b":"iv:34","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 34 calls leaving the friendship of worthless people, even when they cling, sweet; the Innaa line calls association with those without goodness painful. The same thing from two sides.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give both the cost and the remedy: the Innaa names the pain of the tie, the Iniyavai the sweetness of cutting it even when the other holds on."},{"a":"in:33","b":"k:929","type":"develops","note":"Kural 929 says giving reasons to a drunk is like searching with a torch for a man under water; the Innaa line says the business a drunkard himself speaks of is painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they close talk with the drunk in both directions: reasons cannot reach him, and his own account of affairs cannot be relied on.","la":"It sets the drunk's counsel beside walking on thorns and the deceitful's company, as pains that come through trusting the wrong ground.","gr":3},{"a":"in:34","b":"n:341","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 341 says the base man, though books of weighty meaning are opened for him, goes his own way like a hen scratching rubbish; the Innaa line says even an excellent book is a pain to one who does not want it.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The pair moves the loss from the learner to the teaching: the Naladiyar shows the base mind wasting the book, the Innaa line the book itself suffering a reader who does not want it.","la":"It sets the unwanted book beside counsel spoken to the unruly and friendship with the despised, all goods given where they do not fit."},{"a":"in:35","b":"in:27","type":"develops","note":"27 counts beauty in those without means as painful; 35 counts a handsome man who is a fool as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet twice makes beauty a pain when what should go with it is missing, wealth in one verse and sense in the other, so beauty is judged by its company."},{"a":"in:36","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"Kural 799 says friendship with those who desert one in ruin burns the heart even at death; the Innaa verse closes its run of poverty's pains with the friendship of those who abandon one at the time of ruin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures the desertion by the burn it leaves; the Innaa verse shows it as the last support lost, after the wish to give, the rich city and others' doors have already hurt.","la":"It places the desertion at the end of a falling householder's sequence, so betrayal is the last stage of poverty, not a separate fault."},{"a":"in:36","b":"k:218","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 218 says the discerning do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even without means; the Innaa line counts a man without wealth desiring to be liberal as painful. Not a tension on our reading: the Innaa line names a cost, not a fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the pain hidden inside the Kural's praise: giving steadily in want is admirable, and the wish to give without means is also an ache.","la":"It sets the giving wish among the pains of want, a rich city around one and eating at others' doors, so it is seen from inside poverty.","gr":5},{"a":"in:36","b":"iv:22","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 22 calls giving with knowledge of one's income sweet; Innaa 36 calls longing to be liberal without wealth painful. The same rule seen from two sides: giving should match means.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two books set a measure on liberality, the sweet side naming the rule and the painful side the cost of breaking it."},{"a":"in:39","b":"n:65","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 65 says the gift of one without wealth is the true fruit of giving; the Innaa line counts liberality in one who has nothing to give as painful. On the reading that the Naladiyar's giver has little and the Innaa's has nothing, they meet at a limit rather than conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they mark where giving from want stops being the highest giving and becomes pain: the point where nothing is left to give.","la":"It files moneyless liberality with a stone in the chewed areca and broken patronage, as an act that breaks in the middle."},{"a":"in:39","b":"pn:101","type":"qualifies","note":"In Purananuru 101 Avvai tells her heart that whether Athiyaman's gift is delayed or not it is sure, since he greets her on the hundredth visit as on the first; the Innaa line counts a poet held back by the giver as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the pain the Innaa line names and how it is borne: a patron's constant welcome makes the delay of the poet's send-off endurable.","gr":3},{"a":"in:39","b":"in:36","type":"develops","note":"36 counts a man without wealth longing to be liberal; 39 counts liberality practised by one who has nothing to give.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The poet counts the moneyless giver twice, once in the wish and once in the act, so the pain is not only frustrated desire but the giving itself carried on without means."},{"a":"in:37","b":"k:301","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 301 says only restraint of anger where it can take effect counts; the Innaa line counts rancour held against the small as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, anger toward the weak is both the Kural's true test of restraint and the Innaa verse's common failure of it, the same ground seen as trial and as fall.","la":"It sets rancour toward the weak beside a scentless flower and a fool under questioning, so cruelty is filed with exposures of one's own lack.","gr":2},{"a":"in:37","b":"in:29","type":"same_image","note":"29 counts jumping into water without knowing the stakes; 37 counts going down into water without knowing the ford.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet uses the water twice with two kinds of ignorance, of what lies beneath and of the way in, showing knowledge of place as the condition of entering."},{"a":"in:38","b":"k:141","type":"agrees","note":"Both call desire for another man's wife பேதைமை; the Kural says it is absent in those who have seen aram and porul.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Innaa list explains the shared word by its company: the folly is that of a venture beyond one's strength, like a valourless king in battle, which those the Kural describes would never attempt.","la":"It sets the folly beside a valourless king in battle, a bareback ride on a fierce horse and unskilled work, so it is ranked as reckless overreach.","gr":4},{"a":"in:40","b":"iv:30","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai 30 calls the goodness that does not seize a deposit, thinking 'who knew of it then?', the sweetest; Innaa 40 calls seizing a deposit painful. The same thing from two sides.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Iniyavai line names the thief's private reckoning that no one saw; set against it, the Innaa line shows the betrayal of trust as painful whether or not anyone knew.","gr":2},{"a":"in:40","b":"tk:62","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 62 names the one who wrongfully keeps a deposit among three who live on with their line lost; the Innaa line counts seizing a deposit as painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Thirikadugam names the consequence, loss of descendants, that the Innaa line's bare 'painful' leaves unspoken, so the ethics works together give the act and its price."},{"a":"mk:1","b":"k:134","type":"develops","note":"Kural 134 says a brahmin can relearn a forgotten Veda but his birth is ruined if conduct fails; saying 1 ranks conduct above recitation for all people.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking of conduct over recitation, made for the brahmin whose role is recitation, is here lifted off birth entirely and given to everyone the frame line addresses.","la":"It sets the ranking first in a ten of rankings and removes the brahmin, so conduct over study becomes the opening rule for all people rather than a warning to one varna."},{"a":"mk:1","b":"mk:8","type":"develops","note":"Both sayings place learning below something else: conduct in 1, serving the learned in 8.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Within one ten learning is twice ranked second, once to conduct and once to reverence for those who have learnt, so the book never lets learning be the highest good."},{"a":"mk:2","b":"k:562","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 562 tells the ruler to raise the rod sharply but bring it down lightly; saying 2 ranks being held in awe above being loved, on the plainer reading of கண்ணஞ்சப் படுதல்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural allows awe only as a measured show that must not become harm, while the saying ranks awe over love outright and for anyone, so the pair shows how far the measure matters.","la":"It ranks awe directly against affection, a contrast the couplet lacks, and carries it beyond the king to every person the frame addresses."},{"a":"mk:4","b":"k:300","type":"develops","note":"Kural 300 says nothing seen as true is better than truthfulness; saying 4 sets truthfulness specifically above liberality.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's absolute claim for truth is tested against the one virtue its rival tradition prizes most, open-handed giving, and truth still wins.","la":"It names the rival virtue that truth outranks, liberality, where the couplet compares truth with everything at once.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:5","b":"n:55","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 55 laments youth gone and disease and old age coming; saying 5 ranks freedom from disease above youth.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The quatrain counts youth's passing and illness's coming as one loss that urges renunciation, while the saying pulls them apart and says health, not youth, is the greater good.","la":"It ranks two goods the quatrain mourns together, making health a separate and higher thing than youth."},{"a":"mk:6","b":"k:1014","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1014 calls shame the ornament of the worthy; saying 6 ranks shame above beauty or good qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's metaphor of shame as an ornament becomes a literal ranking: shame outranks the beauty that ornaments are meant to serve.","la":"It sets shame against beauty in a direct comparison, turning the couplet's figure into a ranking among goods."},{"a":"mk:7","b":"k:54","type":"develops","note":"Kural 54 asks what is greater than a woman with the firmness of chastity; saying 7 names lineage as one of the things chastity outranks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural leaves chastity's superiority open-ended, the saying sets it against birth itself, so a woman's fidelity is made to count for more than the family she comes from.","la":"It pits chastity against lineage, one specific good of birth, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"mk:8","b":"k:395","type":"develops","note":"Kural 395 says the foremost are those who learnt while yearning before teachers like the poor before the rich; saying 8 ranks serving the learned above learning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes humility before the learned the posture of learning, while the saying ranks that reverence above the learning it leads to, so the means is put above the end.","la":"It ranks the service of the learned against learning itself, where the couplet only makes the one the manner of the other."},{"a":"mk:8","b":"sp:28","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 28 says the fit pupil serves his teacher with love; saying 8 ranks serving the learned above learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One counted work names service of the teacher as the mark of a fit pupil and the other ranks it above learning itself, so the group treats reverence for the learned as prior to knowledge."},{"a":"mk:10","b":"k:782","type":"develops","note":"Kural 782 says the friendship of the good waxes like the moon and that of fools wanes after being full; saying 10 ranks not dwindling above a large beginning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's moon image is about friendship; the saying states its principle bare, for any course of fortune or bond, and keeps only the half that matters: holding, not starting great.","la":"It strips the image and the domain of friendship and ranks endurance over a large start as a general rule."},{"a":"mk:11","b":"mk:12","type":"develops","note":"Line 11 reads great birth from kindness; line 12 reads kindness from giving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken in order the two lines make a chain of evidence, so in this ten noble birth is finally proved by the act of giving."},{"a":"mk:11","b":"mk:16","type":"agrees","note":"Line 11 says a great house shows in kindness; line 16 says a small house shows in pride.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the ten treating rank of birth as real yet legible only in conduct, with kindness and pride as its two marks."},{"a":"mk:11","b":"k:959","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 959 reads lineage from a person's speech; the line reads great birth from kindness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both read birth from a sign, but the sign moves from what is said to what is felt, so the Muthumozhikkanchi's test is harder to counterfeit.","la":"Set in a ten of signs, the line makes kindness the test of birth and the next line makes giving the test of kindness, a chain the couplet does not build.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:13","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"Kural 796 says ruin is the rod that measures one's people; the line names help as the mark of a friendship that does not fail.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the occasion of the test and the line gives its measure, so together they say what ruin reveals: who still helps.","la":"It places the friendship test among tests of birth, learning and ability, so friendship is judged by the same rule of outward proof as every other claimed virtue."},{"a":"mk:13","b":"tk:37","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam 37 says the extent of friendship shows in backbiting; the line says a good friendship shows in help.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two give friendship a positive and a negative test, help given and slander withheld, so neither alone measures it."},{"a":"mk:14","b":"k:717","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 717 says learning shines among those who can examine words; the line says learning is known by the learner's own discernment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural needs a qualified audience for learning to appear, while the line finds the proof inside the learner, in how he sees.","la":"By setting learning between friendship and eminence in a run of signs, it treats learning as one more inner fact read from outward evidence rather than as display before an assembly.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:16","b":"k:978","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 978 says smallness adorns itself with self-admiration; the line says birth in a small house shows in pride.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same mark of pride serves the Kural as a sign of small character and the Muthumozhikkanchi as a sign of small birth, which shows the ten tying character to family.","la":"Paired in the ten with line 11, it sets the small house against the great one, a contrast of birth the couplet, which speaks only of character, does not draw.","gr":4},{"a":"mk:16","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 195 says good and bad lineage are only words and that lineage is made by austerity, learning and effort; the line treats a small house of birth as a fact that pride reveals.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show two answers in one group: the Naladiyar dissolves birth rank into achievement, while the Muthumozhikkanchi keeps rank and reads it from conduct.","la":"Its pairing with line 11 sets great and small houses as fixed ranks with opposite marks, which is exactly the distinction the quatrain calls empty words."},{"a":"mk:17","b":"k:287","type":"develops","note":"Kural 287 calls stealth a dark knowledge absent in those who love measure; the line names trickery as what shows a man to be a thief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural places theft in a kind of cunning, and the line turns that cunning into the evidence, so the thief is known by his method before any taking.","la":"Among signs of good birth, kindness and learning, it gives the one sign of a crime, so the ten's method of reading people from acts reaches the detection of a thief."},{"a":"mk:18","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"Kural 642 says gain and ruin come from speech and bids one guard against its lapse; the line says a lapse in speech reveals every other lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats a slip of the tongue as a cause of loss, while the line treats it as evidence, so the guarded tongue both protects and conceals.","la":"Set beside line 19, it makes speech and understanding the two windows through which all other failings are seen, a pairing the couplet lacks."},{"a":"mk:18","b":"mk:19","type":"develops","note":"Lines 18 and 19 share one frame: a lapse in one place reveals lapses elsewhere, first in speech and then in understanding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated frame shows the ten holding that faults do not stay single: one visible slip is read as the index of hidden ones."},{"a":"mk:19","b":"k:421","type":"develops","note":"Kural 421 calls understanding the tool and inner fortress that guards against faults; the line says a lapse of understanding exposes the other lapses.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the fortress image explains the line: once understanding gives way, the faults it guarded behind it come into view.","la":"It moves understanding from a guard to a sign, placing its failure among the ten's ways of reading a person."},{"a":"mk:20","b":"k:505","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 505 makes one's own deeds the single touchstone of greatness and smallness; the line makes deeds the test of manly ability only.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural uses one test for all worth, while this ten assigns a different sign to each quality, kindness for birth and giving for kindness, and keeps deeds for ability.","la":"As the last of ten paired signs, it shows deeds as one test among several, each matched to its quality, rather than the one touchstone the couplet names."},{"a":"mk:23","b":"mk:24","type":"develops","note":"24 reverses the terms of 23: the rarity of the great is not blamed, then the greatness of the rare.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The chiasm makes greatness and rarity two faces of one value, so a reader learns that complaining of a thing's cost and grudging its esteem are the same error."},{"a":"mk:23","b":"k:975","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the great accomplish deeds that are hard; the line says the hardness of great things is not to be complained of.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the link between பெருமை and அருமை from both ends: the great man takes on difficulty, and the onlooker should not resent that great things are difficult.","la":"Paired with its mirror line 24 in a ten on what is not blamed, it turns the couplet's praise of the great into a rule for those who judge them and their works."},{"a":"mk:25","b":"k:619","type":"develops","note":"The Kural grants effort its wage even when the end fails; the line adds that the unfinished work itself is not censured.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet secures the striver's inner reward and the line secures his standing among others, so an honest shortfall costs neither the wage nor the name.","la":"Set among excuses for nature, poverty and foreign custom, the shortfall becomes one of the things beyond a person's power that the ten exempts from blame, which the couplet on effort does not frame."},{"a":"mk:25","b":"k:612","type":"qualifies","note":"On the reading that the world abandons those who leave a task short, the Kural blames the shortfall that this line excuses.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The pair divides the unfinished task in two: abandoning it in the middle draws the world's blame, while a work that simply could not be done in full does not.","la":"The ten's predicate puts the onlooker's verdict first, where the couplet speaks to the worker, so the line adds the case the couplet leaves open, a deficiency not chosen."},{"a":"mk:26","b":"in:3","type":"develops","note":"Innaa Narpathu counts living under fierce kings of bent sceptre as painful; the line says one living in such a land does not blame the king.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the pain of misrule and the silence of its subject belong to one situation: the subject suffers it and still holds his tongue, which is prudence, not approval."},{"a":"mk:26","b":"k:558","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says having is worse than poverty under a ruler who does no justice; the line says the subject there does not voice blame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names what the subject loses under misrule and the line names what he must also give up, the open word against the king, so the cost of a bent sceptre falls on speech as well as property.","la":"Among the ten's cases of withheld blame it makes silence under an unjust king a rule of the subject's conduct, a side of tyranny the couplet, which speaks of the king's guilt, does not state.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:27","b":"k:805","type":"develops","note":"The Kural reads a friend's hurtful act as folly or great intimacy; the line extends forbearance to good kin who fail to do what they should.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair carries the Kural's charity of interpretation from friends' acts to kinsmen's omissions, and keeps its limit: in both, the tie must first be a good one.","la":"It moves the couplet's rule from friendship to kin and from a wrong done to a duty left undone, and sets it beside excuses for the poor and the small."},{"a":"mk:28","b":"k:426","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says understanding lives as the world lives; the line applies that to the customs of an unknown country.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the couplet's conformity turning outward: wisdom not only follows the ways of one's own world but withholds blame from a world whose ways it does not know.","la":"It names the thing the couplet leaves general, ஆசாரம் of a தேசம், and so makes custom local and the judge's ignorance a reason for restraint."},{"a":"mk:28","b":"ac:20","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai fixes the posture and direction of the meal as ஆசாரம்; the line treats ஆசாரம் as varying by country and not to be faulted where it is unknown.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding them together shows that one ethics work treats ஆசாரம் as binding rule while another grants it is a country's custom, so the rule code is local even in the eyes of its own literature."},{"a":"mk:29","b":"k:218","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says those who know their duty do not slacken in helping even without means; the line says the poor man who is not generous is not blamed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair separates the ideal from the verdict: the Kural praises the poor man who still helps, while the line forbids others to condemn the one who cannot, so excellence is not made a debt.","la":"In a ten on withheld blame the line speaks to the onlooker rather than the giver, a standpoint the couplet on ஒப்புரவு never takes."},{"a":"mk:29","b":"n:95","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says if poverty makes giving impossible, not begging is worth twice giving; the line says the poor man's lack of generosity is not blamed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the poor man's position from both sides: others owe him no reproach for not giving, and he keeps a virtue of his own in not asking.","la":"The quatrain gives the poor man a substitute duty; the line instead binds the onlooker, placing the poor man beside the untrained and the small as people whose shortfall is excused."},{"a":"mk:30","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says greatness hides another's weakness while smallness tells faults; the line says even the excellent do not blame the conduct of the small.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows greatness proved on the very people who invite contempt: not publishing a fault is hardest and most telling when the faulty are the small.","la":"Closing a ten of withheld blame, it turns the couplet's portrait of greatness into the last case of a rule, with சிறந்தோரும் marking the eminent as those for whom restraint is due."},{"a":"mk:31","b":"k:657","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 657 sets the utter poverty of the worthy above wealth won under blame; Muthumozhikkanchi 31 sets the wealth of the blameworthy level with poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks clean poverty above tainted wealth, while the line only levels them, and holding both shows a scale with two steps: tainted wealth is already worth no more than want, and the worthy man's want is worth more still.","la":"It makes tainted wealth one of ten things measured against a plain lack (hunger, solitude, refusal), so blame is shown to empty wealth of what wealth is for, not merely to rank below virtue."},{"a":"mk:32","b":"k:773","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 773 calls தறுகண் great manliness and gives it kindness to the fallen foe as its edge; the line says that தறுகண்மை in excess (கழி) is no better than the பேடி's lack of it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural refines valour by adding mercy as its edge, and the line limits it by measure, so the pair shows two separate checks on courage: mercy to the fallen and restraint in excess.","la":"Set in a ten where each thing is equated with its seeming opposite, it makes the over-bold and the coward one kind, a turn the couplet's praise of valour does not make."},{"a":"mk:33","b":"k:1012","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1012 says food and clothing are common to all living beings and shame is the mark of humans; the line says a life without shame is no better than hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets shame above food as what makes a person human; the line turns the same order into a verdict on the shameless man, whose full table counts for no more than an empty one.","la":"It measures the lack of shame in the coin of food, the very thing the couplet put aside as common to all, so the ranking is applied as a judgement on a life rather than stated as a definition."},{"a":"mk:33","b":"n:268","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 268 sees the shameless feasting at home while those with shame walk far and eat scraps; the line counts a shameless life as no better than hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain records the outward fact that shamelessness feeds well, and the line answers it by counting that full stomach as hunger, so together they give the complaint and its reply.","la":"The line states as a measure what the quatrain shows as a scene, setting the shameless life among ten counterfeit goods that equal their lacks."},{"a":"mk:34","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1057 says the asker rejoices deep within on meeting givers who do not scorn; the line says giving without regard is no better than withholding.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the worth of a gift in the asker's inner joy at being spared scorn, and the line draws the conclusion from the giver's side: without that regard the gift is as good as refused.","la":"It puts the careless gift beside the shameless life and the false helper, ten things that keep the outward form of a good while lacking its substance, which the couplet, focused on the asker, does not do."},{"a":"mk:35","b":"k:664","type":"develops","note":"Kural 664 says speaking is easy for all and doing as one said is hard; the line counts an undertaking left undone as no better than folly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the gap between word and deed as a difficulty, and the line names its cost to the man who leaves it open: he is counted a fool, so failure to perform becomes a matter of judgement, not only of strength.","la":"By measuring the broken undertaking against சிதடி, it moves the couplet's point from firmness in action to the standing of the one who fails, among other things judged as their opposites.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:37","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 110 says there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him; the line counts withdrawing regard after receiving as no better than cruelty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks ingratitude as the sin without remedy, and the line names what it is in act: the ungrateful man, who strikes no one, is reckoned as cruel as one who does harm.","la":"It classes ingratitude with கொடுமை, an act of harm, where the couplet ranks it among sins by its consequence, so the list says what kind of wrong it is rather than how grave."},{"a":"mk:38","b":"k:816","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool; the line counts a fool's support as no better than solitude.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet sinks the fool's friendship far below even estrangement from the wise, while the line only equates it with having no one, and the pair shows the range of the same judgement from mild to extreme.","la":"It measures the fool's company against solitude, the measure the same ten uses for unshared pleasure in 40, so a bad companion and a joy kept to oneself are shown to leave a person equally alone."},{"a":"mk:38","b":"mk:40","type":"develops","note":"Both lines close on தனிமையின் துவ்வாது: a fool's support and pleasure enjoyed alone are each no better than solitude.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two make solitude the measure from both sides: one may be alone though accompanied, by a fool, or alone though joyful, by not sharing, so company is defined by what is shared and not by who is present."},{"a":"mk:40","b":"k:229","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 229 says eating alone to fill one's own store is more bitter than begging; the line says enjoying pleasure alone is no better than solitude.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet ranks solitary eating below begging, a loss of goods and honour, while the line counts any unshared pleasure as loneliness, so the pair shows the same fault judged once as social shame and once as the self's own isolation.","la":"It widens the couplet's solitary eating to every pleasure kept to oneself and pairs it within the ten with the fool's company, both measured by solitude."},{"a":"mk:41","b":"mk:42","type":"develops","note":"41 denies the name of spouse to one who does not act knowing the other's nature; 42 denies the name of household life to a house whose wife is not excellent.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read as written, the ten opens with a condition on the husband before it measures the house by the wife, so the household is made to fail from either side and not from the wife alone."},{"a":"mk:41","b":"tk:96","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam 96 names the wife who knows her husband's hint as one at whose word rain falls; saying 41 denies the name of spouse to one who does not act knowing the other's nature.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same virtue, knowing the partner's nature, is a power over the rains in one work and the bare minimum of being a spouse in the other, so it is both the ceiling and the floor of marriage."},{"a":"mk:42","b":"k:52","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 52 says that if the wife lacks household excellence the life is nothing whatever else it has; saying 42 says the same in the அல்ல form.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's claim that a wife's excellence decides the household survives the move into a book of bare definitions, where it becomes one of the tests by which a thing earns its name.","la":"It sets household life beside kin-friendship, giving, learning and penance as things that lose their very name when one inner condition fails, so the wife's excellence becomes a defining condition of the same kind as aram in giving.","gr":3},{"a":"mk:42","b":"n:56","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 56 says that even a wife without good qualities cannot be put away, and that this is why marriage is called கடி; saying 42 says a house whose wife is not excellent is no household life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Put together, the two say that the life which the saying denies the name of household is one the man still cannot leave, so 'not a life' names a bond that persists as a hardship.","la":"Its list denies such a house the name of a life outright, where the quatrain keeps the marriage and counts its cost."},{"a":"mk:43","b":"mk:11","type":"develops","note":"Saying 11 says birth into a great house is known by ஈரம; saying 43 says kin-friendship without ஈரம் is no kin-friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book uses the same moistness of heart twice, as the sign of good birth and as the condition of kin's bond, so blood and lineage are both made to depend on a tenderness that birth alone does not give."},{"a":"mk:43","b":"k:522","type":"develops","note":"Kural 522 says kin whose love does not lapse bring ever-branching gains; saying 43 says a kin bond without tenderness is not a kin bond.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural values kin by the gains their unfailing love brings, while the saying moves the test inward and denies the name of kin to those without tenderness, before any gain is counted.","la":"It puts the kin bond in a run of definitions by inner condition (spouse, house, friend, learning), so kinship is ranked with friendship as a bond made by feeling, where the couplet treats kin as a source of wealth."},{"a":"mk:44","b":"k:231","type":"develops","note":"Kural 231 says giving and living with renown is the only gain of life; saying 44 says the man whose hand never loosens is no mountain of renown.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural joins giving and fame as the one profit of living; the saying shows the same bond from its failure, that the closed hand loses the standing of one's name, on the reading of சொன்மலை as renown.","la":"It states the bond negatively and puts the miser among other false holders of a name (the unfeeling kinsman, the friend whose heart does not consent), a contrast of claim and substance the couplet lacks."},{"a":"mk:45","b":"k:786","type":"develops","note":"Kural 786 says friendship that smiles in the face is not friendship and friendship that smiles in the heart is; saying 45 says one whose heart does not agree is no friend.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both deny the name of friendship by the same 'is not' form, and the saying narrows the Kural's heart-smile to consent, so the friend is one whose heart has come into agreement, not only one who feels warmth.","la":"It sets the false friend beside the false spouse, kinsman, learning, giving and penance, so the Kural's one test for friendship becomes a general method: each bond or practice is named by an inner condition."},{"a":"mk:45","b":"mk:46","type":"develops","note":"Both sayings turn on நேர்தல்: the friend whose heart does not agree, the learning gained without consent or submission.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Placed side by side, the two make friendship and learning both depend on an act of assent, so the pupil's submission to learning is the same act as the friend's agreement of heart."},{"a":"mk:46","b":"k:395","type":"develops","note":"Kural 395 praises those who learned yearning before the learned like the poor before the rich; saying 46 says learning without நேர்தல் is not learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks the humble learner highest; the saying goes further and denies that learning without that assent or submission is learning at all, on the reading of நேராமல் as not submitting.","la":"It turns the couplet's ranking into a definition and puts learning beside friendship and penance as things that exist only when entered into by consent or undertaking."},{"a":"mk:46","b":"mk:8","type":"develops","note":"Saying 8 ranks serving the learned above learning; saying 46 denies the name of learning to what is learnt without submission.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Across two tens, the book first puts reverence for teachers above learning and then makes it the condition of learning, so reverence moves from the greater good to the thing without which learning does not exist.","gr":5},{"a":"mk:47","b":"k:619","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 619 says that even where fate denies the end, effort pays the wage of bodily strain; saying 47 says strain that does not bring one to live is not strain, on the reading of வருத்தம் as effort.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural promises effort a wage even in failure, while the saying refuses to count strain that brings no living, so the pair sets a floor (some wage) against a definition by result (a life gained).","la":"It names effort among things judged by an outcome or manner beside giving and penance, a measure by result the couplet explicitly sets aside."},{"a":"mk:48","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 221 says giving to those who have nothing is giving and all else is a measured return; saying 48 says what is not given in aram's way is not giving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both redefine giving by denying the name to gifts that look like it; the Kural fixes the test by recipient and expected return, while the saying fixes it by the road of aram and leaves its content open.","la":"It pairs the test of giving with a matching test of penance in the next line (அறத்தாற்றின் / திறத்தாற்றின்), so the householder's gift and the ascetic's vow are judged by one rule of right manner, which the couplet does not do."},{"a":"mk:48","b":"mk:34","type":"develops","note":"Saying 34 reckons giving without care for the receiver no better than refusing; saying 48 says giving not along aram's way is not giving.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book judges giving twice by its manner, once by care for the receiver and once by aram's road, so a gift's worth never rests on its size in either ten."},{"a":"mk:49","b":"mk:48","type":"develops","note":"The two lines are built as twins: அறத்தாற்றின் ஈயாதது ஈனையன்று, திறத்தாற்றின் நேர்லாதது நோன்பன்று.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The twin form makes the giver's gift and the ascetic's vow two cases of one rule, that an act is named by the way it is done, so the householder's and renouncer's codes are tested alike.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:49","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"Kural 261 defines தவம் as bearing pain and harming no living being; saying 49 says a vow not taken up in its proper way is no penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines penance by its content, endurance and harmlessness, while the saying defines it by how it is undertaken, so the pair shows two separate tests a vow must pass.","la":"It sets penance as the twin of giving in a matched pair of lines, putting the ascetic's vow under the same test of right manner as the householder's gift, which the couplet does not."},{"a":"mk:49","b":"tk:96","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 96 names the ascetic who carries out what he has undertaken in the proper way as one at whose word rain falls; saying 49 says a vow not undertaken in its proper way is no penance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One work denies the name of penance to a vow wrongly undertaken; the other promises rain to the ascetic who completes his vow rightly, so between them the proper manner is both the condition of penance and the source of its power."},{"a":"mk:50","b":"pn:195","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 195 tells grey-haired elders in useless age that Death will bind them and urges them at least to refrain from harm; saying 50 says age without knowledge of the next birth is not old age.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poet shames old men by the coming of Death, while the saying denies them the name of age unless they know what follows death, so the heroic warning about the end becomes a doctrine of rebirth."},{"a":"mk:50","b":"mk:39","type":"develops","note":"Saying 39 reckons disgraced old age no better than anger; saying 50 says age that does not know rebirth is not old age.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book judges old age twice and never by years: once by the disgrace it carries, once by what it knows of the birth to come, so elderhood is a moral and a spiritual standing."},{"a":"mk:51","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 61 knows no gain like children who come to know (அறிவறிந்த); the line names the getting of children the greatest gain with no condition.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's praise hangs on a single qualifier the line drops, so the pair shows the Kural deliberately narrowing a common saying that children are the greatest gain.","la":"Set first in a ten of superlatives that ranks fame as the greatest legacy (58) and giving as the greatest distinction (60), the line makes offspring one of several 'nothing greater' goods, so its supremacy is one claim among rival ones rather than the couplet's single ranking.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:51","b":"in:29","type":"tension","note":"Innaa Narpathu 29 counts begetting children without understanding (அறிவறியா மக்கட் பெறல்) among painful things; the line calls the getting of children, unqualified, the greatest gain.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read plainly, the two works give the same act opposite values, and only the qualifier about the child's understanding, which the Kural supplies, reconciles a supreme gain with a pain.","gr":4},{"a":"mk:52","b":"k:213","type":"develops","note":"Kural 213 says nothing here or in the gods' world is as good as ஒப்புரவு; the line says nothing is a more fitting acquisition than knowing it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks the duty itself above all goods; the line ranks the knowledge of the duty, so that merely learning what one owes the world already counts as the best thing one can gain.","la":"Placed in a ten of gains, losses and disgraces, the line turns ஒப்புரவு from a good done for others into an acquisition of the doer's own, set beside children and fame as things one gets."},{"a":"mk:53","b":"mk:54","type":"develops","note":"53 calls a desire that can come to pass the best desire; 54 calls a desire that cannot the worst.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Together the two lines judge desire by its outcome rather than its object, a scale on which the same wish is best or worst depending only on whether it can be fulfilled.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:54","b":"tk:28","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 28 names the man who sits craving what he does not have (இல்லது காமுற்று இருப்பான்) as one who pounds husk; the line calls unattainable desire the worst desire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thirikadugam gives the image of the wasted labour and the line gives the verdict, so the pair says why futile wanting is worst: it spends effort with nothing to show."},{"a":"mk:55","b":"tk:45","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 45 says the man who without love hides what he can (இயைவ கரப்பான்) from those who ask falls into hell; the line uses the same words and calls the act the greatest cruelty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two works share the phrase for withholding what one can give, and between them name its moral kind (cruelty) and its fate (hell), so refusal is treated as a harm done to the asker, not just a lack of virtue."},{"a":"mk:55","b":"k:1069","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1069 says the heart melts at the thought of begging and is destroyed at the thought of concealment; the line makes concealment the greatest cruelty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet weighs concealment by what it does to the asker's heart, the line by what it makes the concealer, and both rank it below begging itself, which 59 calls the worst disgrace.","la":"In a ten that also names begging the worst disgrace (59) and giving to askers the highest distinction (60), the line completes a three-way scoring of one exchange (asking, refusing, giving) that the couplet treats only from the asker's side."},{"a":"mk:55","b":"mk:37","type":"qualifies","note":"37 sets turning one's regard away from those one has taken from as no better than cruelty; 55 sets hiding what one can give as the greatest cruelty.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The book uses cruelty as the measure twice, once as a floor for ingratitude and once as a summit for refusal, so withholding from an asker ranks as worse than the ingratitude it is compared with."},{"a":"mk:56","b":"k:214","type":"develops","note":"Kural 214 says one who knows what is fitting lives and anyone else is counted among the dead; the line calls being without understanding the worst death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the living death a matter of not knowing ஒப்புரவு; the line widens it to any lack of understanding and ranks it below bodily death, while 52 in the same ten keeps the couplet's other half, knowing ஒப்புரவு as the best gain.","la":"The ten splits the couplet's antithesis across two separate lines (52 and 56), so knowing one's duty and lacking understanding become a highest gain and a worst death in a series of superlatives."},{"a":"mk:57","b":"k:363","type":"develops","note":"Kural 363 calls not wanting the finest wealth here and hereafter; the line calls wanting the greatest poverty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes desirelessness a wealth and the line makes desire a poverty, so together they move both wealth and poverty out of possessions and into the state of wanting.","la":"Placed two lines before begging is named the worst disgrace (59), the line sets desire as the poverty that leads to asking, a causal sequence the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"mk:57","b":"n:306","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 306 says a mind racked by desire (காதல் கவற்றும் மனம்) sends a man to beg of strangers when he could end his want by asking of himself; the line calls desire the greatest poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain shows the mechanism the line only names: it is desire, not lack, that drives a man to beg, so the poverty that matters is the one inside.","la":"The line states the root in four words, as one of ten superlatives, where the quatrain needs a scene and a remedy; set beside 59, the ten's list makes desire and begging the worst poverty and the worst disgrace.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:58","b":"k:238","type":"develops","note":"Kural 238 calls it a disgrace not to obtain the legacy called renown (இசையென்னும் எச்சம்); the line, using the same two words, calls renown the greatest legacy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes renown the minimum below which a life is disgraced; the line makes it the maximum above which there is no legacy, so the pair fixes fame as both the floor and the ceiling of what one leaves.","la":"In a ten that opens with children as the greatest gain (51), the line separates the legacy of fame from the gain of offspring, two senses that the couplet's எச்சம் leaves folded together."},{"a":"mk:58","b":"mk:51","type":"qualifies","note":"51 names the getting of children the greatest gain; 58 names renown the greatest எச்சம், a word that also means offspring.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Within one ten the two superlatives divide the field: children are the greatest gain in life, but what survives a person is ranked as fame, not progeny."},{"a":"mk:59","b":"mk:60","type":"develops","note":"59 makes begging the deepest disgrace; 60 makes giving to those who beg the highest distinction.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ten scores one exchange twice, from both sides, so the asker's lowest point is the giver's highest, the same thing seen as shame and as honour."},{"a":"mk:59","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1066 says nothing degrades the tongue more than asking, even for water for a cow; the line says no disgrace goes beyond begging.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet tests the claim at its hardest case, a worthy cause and a tiny request, while the line states it bare, so the pair shows the superlative holds without exception for need or motive.","la":"Coupled with 60, the line's list sets the asker's disgrace beside the giver's distinction, so begging is judged inside an exchange rather than alone as the couplet judges it."},{"a":"mk:60","b":"pn:204","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 204 says giving at 'take' is high and refusing a gift is higher still (கொள்ளேன் என்றல் அதனினும் உயர்ந்தன்று); the line says no distinction exceeds giving to those who ask. The tension holds on the plain reading of the line's 'nothing greater'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bard's poem ranks the proud refusal of a gift above the gift, where the ethics line puts the giver at the summit, showing the puram ethic of honour placing the poet's self-respect above the patron's generosity."},{"a":"mk:60","b":"k:231","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 231 says giving and living with renown are the only profit of life; the line ranks giving to askers as the highest distinction.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet treats giving as a means to renown, the only profit; the line narrows the gift to one made to those who ask and makes its distinction an end, so the asker's presence is what makes the gift supreme.","la":"The ten sets giving to askers directly after begging as the worst disgrace (59), so the giver's distinction is ranked against the asker's shame, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"mk:62","b":"n:68","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 68 grants that the worthy of standing (சீர்கொண்ட சான்றோர்) feel anger and says it cools of itself; the line says that a man of great standing (சீர்) being without anger is false.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Together they move the virtue of the eminent from having no anger to letting anger subside, since one says such a man will feel it and the other says how his anger differs from the low man's.","la":"Set beside the drunkard's lapses and the greedy judge, it counts anger as a thing that comes with high standing as surely as lapse comes with drink, a turn the quatrain's contrast of the worthy and the low does not make."},{"a":"mk:63","b":"k:930","type":"develops","note":"Kural 930 asks whether the drinker, seeing another drunk, does not think of the lapse (சோர்வு) of his own drinking; the line says a drinker without lapse is false.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet hopes the drinker may see his own lapse in another man, and the line states that lapse as certain, so the pair shows the Kural's appeal resting on a fact the drinker refuses: that his lapse is not occasional but sure.","la":"It makes the drinker's lapse one of ten impossibilities with the greedy judge and the impure ascetic, a type-judgement on a kind of man rather than the couplet's question put to one drinker."},{"a":"mk:63","b":"in:33","type":"develops","note":"Innaa Narpathu 33 counts as painful the matter of business a drunkard speaks of; the line says a drinker free of lapses is false.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Innaa line names the pain of dealing with a drinker's affairs and the Muthumozhikkanchi line gives its cause, the lapse that is certain in him, so one reports the effect and the other the reason no business with him is safe.","gr":4},{"a":"mk:64","b":"k:484","type":"develops","note":"Kural 484 says even the world comes to hand (கைகூடும்) if one weighs the time and acts by the place; the line says gain coming to hand (கையுறல்) for one who does not know the time is false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes knowing the time enough for even the greatest gain, and the line makes it necessary for any gain, so together they give timing both halves of a condition: without it nothing comes to hand, with it even the world can.","la":"It states as an impossibility, beside nine others, the negative half that the couplet's promise leaves unsaid, and drops the couplet's second condition of place.","gr":4},{"a":"mk:65","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"Kural 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming are not shaken by affliction; the line says one who does not know what is coming cannot guard himself.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet joins knowing and guarding in one phrase and promises calm; the line pulls them apart and makes knowing the prior condition, so the pair shows that the Kural's guarding is itself a kind of knowledge, not a separate defence.","la":"In a ten that sets each act against the lack that makes it false, it isolates foresight as the single lack that undoes self-protection, where the couplet praises understanding in general."},{"a":"mk:67","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"Kural 978 says greatness always bows and smallness adorns itself in self-admiration; the line says one who cannot bear lowliness cannot rightly seek greatness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet describes greatness as bowing once it is had, and the line makes the bearing of low estate the road to it, so together they show humility as both the way to greatness and its mark once reached.","la":"Paired in its ten with its own mirror (68), it turns the couplet's contrast of two characters into a test of endurance applied to both high and low estate."},{"a":"mk:67","b":"mk:68","type":"qualifies","note":"67 denies greatness to one who cannot bear lowliness; 68 swaps the two words and denies lowliness to one who cannot bear greatness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read as a pair, the two lines make the capacity to bear one's station, high or low, the single condition of moving between them, so neither ambition nor humility is genuine in one who cannot endure the other state."},{"a":"mk:69","b":"k:541","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 541 defines justice as inquiry, no favour to anyone, holding to the ruler's part and acting after thought; the line says justice done by one who craves wealth is false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's definition is a procedure and says nothing of the judge's appetites; the line adds a condition on the judge himself, so the pair shows that the four steps can be kept in form and still be false if greed sits behind them.","la":"It sets greed for wealth among ten lacks that falsify an act, so justice is judged by the character of the one who does it, not by the steps the couplet lists.","gr":3},{"a":"mk:70","b":"k:262","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 262 says penance comes to those who have penance and is futile for those without it; the line says penance done by one who is not pure is false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's condition for penance is circular, penance belongs to those who already have it, and the line names what that prior possession is, purity, so the pair turns the Kural's riddle into a stated precondition.","la":"In a ten that names the one lack that falsifies each act, it gives the precondition of penance a name, வாலியன், where the couplet leaves it as penance itself."},{"a":"mk:70","b":"tk:80","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 80 counts penance taken up by one without restraint of heart (நிறை) as seed sown on scrub; the line says penance by one who is not pure (வாலியன்) is false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two name different lacks, restraint of heart and purity, that make penance void, and one calls it barren where the other calls it false, so together they show the penance of the unfit failing both as fruit and as truth."},{"a":"mk:71","b":"pn:27","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 27 reports as hearsay (என்ப ... எனக் கேட்பல்) that those whom poets praise reach a sky-car no charioteer drives; the line states flatly that heaven is easy for those who desire fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroic poem hedges its heaven as something heard and grants it to the man others praise, while the maxim drops the hedge and grants it to the man who wants fame, so the pair shows a court belief hardening into a saying and its condition moving from the poet's praise to the seeker's own desire."},{"a":"mk:72","b":"k:856","type":"develops","note":"Kural 856 says the life of one who finds it sweet to excel in hostility is close to failure and ruin; the line says that a hard battle comes easily to one who loves contention.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they give two links of one chain: the relish for rivalry brings battle near, and the man who relishes it has ruin near, so both texts measure the quarrelsome man by what lies close at hand rather than by what he chooses.","la":"In a ten where each desire draws its own easy consequence, battle is set beside disease for the glutton and blame for the lecher as the natural yield of an appetite, which the couplet, inside a chapter on hostility alone, does not do."},{"a":"mk:73","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 230 says nothing is more bitter than death, yet even death is sweet when giving cannot be done; the line says giving to the asker's wish comes easily to those who love kindness.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The line explains from the giver's side what the couplet asserts as a ranking: for one whose heart is moist, giving is so natural that being unable to give becomes, as the Kural says, worse than dying.","la":"It puts the ease of giving down to a disposition (ஈரம்) and sets it in a ten of desires and their yields, so generosity appears as the fruit of a love of kindness, where the couplet only ranks giving above life."},{"a":"mk:74","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum because they carry secrets they hear to others; the line says spreading secrets comes easily to those who love tale-bearing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet traces the spilling of secrets to a fixed baseness of nature, while the line traces it to an appetite for slander, so the pair shows that the drum-like betrayer is made by a taste he indulges, not only born base.","la":"Set among ten desires and their easy outcomes, it gives the betrayal of secrets a cause in a named desire (குறளை), where the couplet gives only an image and a class of person.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:75","b":"k:630","type":"develops","note":"Kural 630 says that one who takes what hurts as pleasure gains a distinction his enemies admire; the line says pleasure comes easily to those who take on hardship.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet pays the man who embraces hardship in esteem, the line pays him in pleasure itself, so together they show the old claim that welcomed toil returns both honour and the very joy that was given up.","la":"Paired in the ten with its mirror line 76, it turns the couplet's single reward into one half of a reversal law: what is chased flees and its opposite arrives, a symmetry the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"mk:75","b":"mk:76","type":"develops","note":"75 says pleasure comes easily to those who embrace hardship; 76, reversing the same two words, says pain comes easily to those who crave pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side the two lines make one law from two sides: desire turns its object into its opposite, so the way to pleasure runs through accepted pain and the chase of pleasure ends in pain."},{"a":"mk:76","b":"n:60","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 60 says fools crave pleasure though suffering mounts, while the higher ones see the pain in each pleasure and do not cling; the line says pain comes easily to those who crave pleasure.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Naladiyar finds pain lodged inside pleasure itself and so counsels detachment, while the line lays the pain on the craving and not on the pleasure, which leaves room, as its mirror 75 shows, for pleasure that comes to one who did not chase it.","la":"Its pairing with 75 makes pleasure attainable by the non-craving, a turn the renunciant quatrain does not allow, since it treats every pleasure as the seed of pain."},{"a":"mk:77","b":"k:946","type":"develops","note":"Kural 946 says joy stays with the one who eats knowing when to stop, as disease stays with the one who eats hugely; the line says grave disease comes easily to those who love food.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet ties disease to the act of eating beyond measure, the line ties it to the love of food that precedes the act, so the pair moves the cause of illness back from the meal to the appetite.","la":"In the ten it stands beside blame for the lecher (78) and pain for the pleasure-seeker (76), so gluttony's disease is one instance of a general rule that a desire brings its own harm, where the couplet speaks in a chapter on medicine."},{"a":"mk:78","b":"k:146","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 146 says enmity, sin, fear and blame never leave one who crosses into another's home; the line says grievous blame comes easily to men who lust after women, naming no other's wife.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural fixes blame on a single transgression against another man's house, while the line widens it to the roving desire itself, so the pair shows blame attaching first to the trespass and then, in the maxim, already to the appetite before any trespass.","la":"Among the ten's desires and their easy harms, the line gives blame as the natural yield of a craving, like disease for the glutton, where the couplet lists blame as one of four penalties for a specific act."},{"a":"mk:79","b":"k:227","type":"develops","note":"Kural 227 says the evil disease of hunger hardly touches one accustomed to பாத்தூண், sharing before eating; the line says பாத்தூண் comes easily to those who willingly bear a burden.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives what the habit of shared eating protects against, the line gives what makes the habit easy, so together they show shared food as both the fruit of shouldering a charge and the guard against the hunger the miser fears.","la":"It traces the Kural's habit to a prior disposition to bear a load and sets it among good desires with good yields (fame, kindness, hardship), a cause the couplet does not give.","gr":3},{"a":"mk:80","b":"mk:38","type":"develops","note":"38 reckons the support of a fool no better than being alone; 80 says that for those with no support, violent killing comes easily.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Joined, the two lines make an argument the book never states: a fool's support leaves one as good as alone, and to be without support is to be easily killed, so a foolish protector exposes a person to the same danger as none."},{"a":"mk:81","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"Kural 559 says that when the king bends justice the sky bends and withholds rain; the line says the land of a king without justice is poor.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet gives a mechanism, the failed rain, and the line gives only the result, a poor land, so together they show the period's chain from royal injustice through the weather to the country's want.","la":"It sets the unjust king's land first in a ten of things that fail for lack of their ground, so misrule becomes the type of all such emptiness rather than a cosmic exception."},{"a":"mk:81","b":"mk:26","type":"develops","note":"Line 26 says those living under a king who keeps no justice do not openly censure him; line 81 says his land is poor.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the subjects keep silent about an unjust king but the land itself shows the injustice through its poverty, so the blame the people withhold is spoken by the country."},{"a":"mk:82","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 13 says aged men who cling to desire in the house have no path to safety; the line calls a very old woman's desire poor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain condemns aged desire because it bars release, and the line condemns it with no thought of release and names a woman, so the pair shows one judgement on old age held on ascetic grounds and on plain worldly fitness.","la":"It places the old woman's desire among ten misfits of condition (a sick man's pleasure, an old man's ornament), judging it as unfitting rather than as a danger to the soul."},{"a":"mk:82","b":"mk:86","type":"agrees","note":"Both lines set an aged body against a good of youth: desire in an old woman (82), ornament on an old man (86).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ten applies the same verdict to both sexes, desire for the woman and adornment for the man, so the pairing shows how the period divided what each was faulted for in age."},{"a":"mk:83","b":"k:890","type":"develops","note":"Kural 890 likens living with those not in accord to sharing a hut with a snake; the line calls joining with a hostile intimate poor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet stresses the danger of living beside the hostile, the line the futility of allying with them, so together they show the enemy within is both a threat and a bad bargain.","la":"It moves the couplet's fear into a reckoning of worth, counting such a union among things that are simply poor, which the snake image does not do."},{"a":"mk:83","b":"tk:4","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 4, item 3, counts taking into one's hand a man who once raged against one among the acts of one bound for death; the line calls joining with a hostile intimate poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thirikadugam calls the embrace of a former foe deadly and Muthumozhikkanchi calls it worthless, so the same act is judged by its danger in one list and by its yield in the other."},{"a":"mk:84","b":"mk:5","type":"develops","note":"Line 5 ranks freedom from bodily disease above youth; line 84 calls the pleasure of a sick man poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The first ten ranks health above youth and the ninth shows why, since disease empties even pleasure, so health appears as the ground other goods stand on.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:85","b":"k:1308","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1308 asks what use suffering is where no lover knows of it; the line calls sulking poor where one is not cherished. Both turn on an absent beloved's care (இல்லா வழி, போற்றாவழி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of grief with no one to notice and the line of sulking with no one to appease it, so together they show that every display of hurt in love has worth only before someone who cares.","la":"It takes a lover's sulk out of the Kural's love book and sets it among public ills like misrule and empty anger, treating it as one more act that fails without its ground."},{"a":"mk:86","b":"in:27","type":"develops","note":"Innaa Narpathu 27, item 3, calls beauty in those without means painful; the line calls ornament on an aged body poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One list makes beauty depend on wealth and the other makes adornment depend on youth, so together they show that beauty in these works is never good alone and fails when either support is gone."},{"a":"mk:87","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 likens speaking before those not of one's set to nectar spilled in a drain; the line calls speech poor where words do not carry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the waste in the hearers' unfitness, the line in the word's lack of force, so together they show that speech fails either from the audience or from the speaker's standing.","la":"It puts futile speech beside futile anger (89) and futile sulking (85) in one ten, making each a case of an act without the power to land.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:89","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"Kural 301 says restraint of anger counts only where anger can take effect; the line calls anger poor where it inspires no awe.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says restraining powerless anger is no virtue, and the line says indulging it is no gain, so together they show that anger without power is worth nothing whether held back or let loose.","la":"It judges anger by its effect, as one of ten things emptied by a missing condition, where the couplet judges the restraint of anger as a virtue.","gr":3},{"a":"mk:90","b":"in:21","type":"develops","note":"Innaa Narpathu 21, item 2, calls going to eat with those who do not share their food painful; the line calls going where there is no friendship poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Innaa Narpathu names the particular case, the stingy table, and the line states the general rule behind it, so together they show the visit's worth lying in the host's bond, not in what is offered."},{"a":"mk:92","b":"mk:10","type":"develops","note":"Line 10 ranks not dwindling later above swelling at first; 92 says the one who wants to swell does not leave off gathering renown.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two lines say that greatness is kept from shrinking only by an unbroken run of creditable deeds, so the unslackening of the last ten is the answer to the dwindling feared in the first."},{"a":"mk:93","b":"mk:8","type":"develops","note":"Line 8 ranks revering the learned above one's own learning; 93 makes unbroken reverent service the condition of learning at all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The first ten treats reverence as a value above learning and the last treats it as the road to learning, so the book holds both that service is the means and that it outranks the end."},{"a":"mk:93","b":"k:395","type":"develops","note":"Kural 395 says the learned learnt by standing before those who know as the poor stand yearning before the rich; 93 says the would-be learner never drops வழிபாடு.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the learner's posture as a beggar's longing, and the line gives it as worship that never lapses, so the pair shows humility in learning as both a hunger and a sustained service.","la":"In a ten that pairs each goal with the practice its seeker never drops, reverence is set beside toil for plenty and penance for steadfastness, so it reads as the price of learning, where the couplet uses it to rank the learned above the unlearned."},{"a":"mk:94","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"Kural 265 says penance is pursued here because it gains whatever one wishes, as one wishes; 94 names one such wish, to stand firm, and says its seeker never leaves off penance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes penance a means to any wish at all, while the line ties it to one wish and requires it without a break, so the pair moves from penance's open promise to its continuing cost.","la":"It sets penance among nine other practices each tied to its own goal, so penance becomes one specialised means among learning, toil and justice rather than the universal means the couplet makes it."},{"a":"mk:95","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 671 says deliberation ends in resolve and that delay after resolving is an evil; 95 says the one who wants a good life never leaves off deliberation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"For a single task the couplet closes deliberation once resolved, and for a whole life the line keeps it always open, so the pair shows deliberation ending task by task while never ending as a habit of living.","la":"It carries சூழ்ச்சி from the minister's work on one undertaking, where the couplet places it, to the making of a whole life, by setting it in a list of lifelong practices."},{"a":"mk:96","b":"k:619","type":"develops","note":"Kural 619 says effort yields its wage of bodily strain even where fate will not grant the end; 96 says one who wants plenty does not shun வருத்தம்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The line makes toil necessary for plenty and the couplet makes it rewarding even against fate, so the pair gives both halves: there is no abundance without strain, and strain is never wholly unpaid.","la":"Among goals and their unbroken practices, the list makes toil the specific price of surplus, beside pain as the price of pleasure, a pairing of goal and cost the couplet's defence of effort against fate does not make."},{"a":"mk:97","b":"k:628","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 628 says the one who does not desire pleasure and takes suffering as natural is never distressed; 97 says the one who wants pleasure does not shun pain.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both bind pain to the pursuit of pleasure, but the couplet frees the man who stops desiring pleasure while the line binds the man who keeps desiring it, so the pair gives the two exits from the same bond.","la":"Placed beside its mirror in 98, the line makes pleasure and pain each the cost of the other, a symmetrical turn the couplet, which speaks of only one direction, lacks."},{"a":"mk:98","b":"mk:97","type":"develops","note":"97 says the seeker of pleasure does not shun pain; 98 reverses the terms, so the seeker of pain does not hold back from pleasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken as a pair the two lines say the same truth from both sides: pleasure and pain are tied so closely that whoever chases either is pursuing the other."},{"a":"mk:98","b":"n:60","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 60 says fools long for pleasure while suffering mounts, and the higher ones see the pain in each pleasure and do not cling; 98 names the unchecked pleasure-seeker as one who in effect wants pain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The quatrain describes the wise seeing pain inside pleasure, and the line describes what follows when that is not seen, so the pair joins the renouncer's insight to the verdict it passes on the one who lacks it.","la":"The line compresses the quatrain's contrast of fool and sage into a single reversed formula, and its mirroring with 97 makes the point by form alone, without the quatrain's turn to renunciation.","gr":2},{"a":"mk:99","b":"k:547","type":"develops","note":"Kural 547 says the king protects the world and just order (முறை) protects the king when he keeps it without fail; 99 says the one who wants safety does not leave off acting by just order.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet explains why the king's safety lies in justice and the line states it as an unbroken practice, so the pair shows justice as the ruler's own shelter and not only a duty owed to the ruled.","la":"It puts the king's justice in a list where each seeker keeps a practice for his own end, so just rule is framed as what the ruler does for his own security, the same way the learner keeps reverence."},{"a":"mk:99","b":"pn:35","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 35 counsels Killivalavan that in a land of just rule, where the humble can reach the king when they seek முறை, rain comes when sought, and that if he protects his people the unsubmitting will bow; 99 states the bare rule that security needs just order.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows to a named Chola what just rule looks like and what it brings, the king reached by the humble and the enemy bowing, and the line reduces the same counsel to a maxim any ruler can carry.","gr":3},{"a":"mk:100","b":"k:1097","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1097 says small words without real anger and hostile looks are the signs of those who are close while seeming distant; 100 says the one who wants love does not give up hinted speech.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The line says the lover speaks by hint and the couplet shows how hints are read, even harsh ones turned inside out, so the pair gives both the speaker's practice and the hearer's skill.","la":"It closes a ten of public goals and their practices with love and its indirection, so hinting stands beside justice and toil as a discipline, where the couplet treats it as a sign within the lovers' scene.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:0b","b":"tk:0","type":"same_image","note":"Both invocations of the group open on Vishnu's stride across the earth: Thirikadugam counts it among three deeds of the feet, Nanmanikkadigai among four deeds of the 'son'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two invocations show the counted form fitted to the god before it is fitted to conduct: each book praises him in its own number, three deeds or four.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:0b","b":"k:610","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural uses the strider of the earth as the measure of what a king free from sloth gains; the invocation worships the stride itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same three steps serve the Kural as a measure of a diligent king's reach and the invocation as an act of the god, so the myth is shared ground between worship and statecraft.","la":"It puts the stride among three other cosmic deeds (swallowing the earth, lifting the hill, breaking Sho), so the step is one of the god's acts rather than a yardstick of gain."},{"a":"nm:1","b":"n:305","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says true living is never to ask even of the dearest; Nanmanikkadigai forbids only taking so that the hand of those one should not receive from is above one's own.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against the quatrain's ban on all asking, the verse draws the line at the giver: receiving is shameful when the hand above is one that should not be.","la":"It sets the rule on receiving beside rules against contempt for the lowly and rage at the humble-born, so the shame of the raised hand is one of four checks on rank-pride."},{"a":"nm:2","b":"k:969","type":"develops","note":"Kural 969 compares men who die when honour is lost to the yak that dies when its hair is gone; Nanmanikkadigai has the asunam die at the drum and nobility die at an unworthy word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet says honour's loss kills its owner, and the verse adds that a single unfit word is enough to kill nobility, so the threat is speech, not only deed.","la":"It sets nobility's death beside three natural deaths (the asunam, the strong, the seeding bamboo), so the word that kills it is ranked with the drum and the bamboo's own fruit as a fatal cause."},{"a":"nm:3","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"Kural 796 calls ruin a rod that measures one's people; Nanmanikkadigai says the use of kin is known by being ruined, after the washing of gems, riding of horses and burning of gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show ruin as an assay: the couplet names its single benefit, and the verse sets it in the craftsman's sequence of tests, where the tester pays the cost.","la":"It makes testing kin by ruin the last of four assays with gem, horse and gold, so the kin test is the one that burns the tester, not the thing tested.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:4","b":"n:133","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar prizes salt born in barren ground above rice from fertile fields and puts the learned of low origin in the highest place; Nanmanikkadigai says agil, orpiment and pearls come from mean sources and no one knows the family a good woman is born in.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books use a precious thing from a poor source to cut worth loose from birth, the Naladiyar for the learned man and Nanmanikkadigai for the good woman.","la":"Its three natural instances (spurge, deer's belly, sea) build to a question rather than a command, so the reader is left unable to trace goodness to a family."},{"a":"nm:4","b":"k:959","type":"tension","note":"Kural 959 says a person's words show the lineage he was born in, as a sprout shows the soil; Nanmanikkadigai asks who can know the family a good woman is born in.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the couplet treats the person as evidence of the family, while the verse denies that inference: goodness, like agil from the spurge, does not reveal its stock.","la":"Its three images of precious things from unlikely sources make the point by pattern: if agil comes from the kalli, the source cannot be read from the product."},{"a":"nm:5","b":"k:247","type":"develops","note":"Kural 247 says this world is not for those without wealth as the other is not for those without அருள்; Nanmanikkadigai says the way of virtue is born of அருள் and everything is born of wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pair of அருள் and பொருள் appears in both, but the verse turns the couplet's two worlds into two sources: one of virtue, one of everything else.","la":"It sets the pair after the gem from stone and delight from the beloved's word, and closes on wealth as the source of all, which makes the couplet's balance end in wealth's favour."},{"a":"nm:7","b":"n:280","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 280 says earning, guarding, losing and ruin of wealth are all suffering; Nanmanikkadigai says those who plan to make wealth and those who guard it get no sleep.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says wealth is suffering at every stage; the verse names the suffering as sleeplessness and gives it to the thief and the lover as well.","la":"It ranks the earner and guardian of wealth with the thief and the lovesick as sleepless men, so lawful wealth shares its unrest with theft and longing."},{"a":"nm:7","b":"tk:73","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 73 names the man who says he will make bright wealth by begging as one who lives daring the hard; Nanmanikkadigai says the one who plans to make bright wealth gets no sleep.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two books use the same phrase for the plan to make bright wealth and both make it a hardship: Thirikadugam a daring, Nanmanikkadigai a sleepless night."},{"a":"nm:8","b":"k:304","type":"develops","note":"Kural 304 says anger kills smile and joy and is the worst enemy; Nanmanikkadigai says before the angry everything fails to show and is ruined.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names what anger kills in the angry man; the verse widens it to everything before him, so anger blinds as well as destroys.","la":"It sets anger's ruin after three things that do not show before the learned, the resolved and the wrongdoer, so anger closes a list of what each kind of person cannot see."},{"a":"nm:9","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1014 calls shame the ornament of the worthy; Nanmanikkadigai calls modesty the ornament of a woman's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural gives shame to the worthy of either sex as their ornament, while the verse assigns it to women's beauty, so the pair shows the virtue narrowed into a gendered adornment.","la":"It puts women's modesty in a row of ornaments with paddy for land and lotus for pond, which makes the virtue something that decorates a seen beauty, where the Kural makes it a guard of conduct.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:9","b":"tk:52","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 52 calls modesty the ornament of a woman in love and learning the ornament for the next life; Nanmanikkadigai calls modesty the ornament of women's beauty and virtue the ornament for the world one goes to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both short works pair a woman's modesty with an ornament for the next life, so the gendered ornament and the otherworldly one are a shared pairing of the group, not one poet's choice."},{"a":"nm:9","b":"nm:15","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 9 calls virtue one's own ornament for the world one goes to; verse 15 says that if one wants what goes along with oneself, one should do virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the book virtue is twice the one thing that crosses death, once as an ornament worn and once as a companion that goes along."},{"a":"nm:10","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word and the base only when crushed like cane; Nanmanikkadigai binds the base with iron and the worthy by courtesy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of what the two yield; the verse of how they are held, so the pair moves from extracting good to restraining harm with the same contrast.","la":"It puts the base and the worthy in a row with the raging elephant and the great serpent, so each kind of creature has its own binding and courtesy becomes a bond.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:11","b":"k:108","type":"develops","note":"Kural 108 says it is not good to forget a good done and good to forget at once what is not good; Nanmanikkadigai asks one not to be embittered by harsh acts and to hold the good done as good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both pair forgetting injury with remembering help; the verse goes on to one's own anger and ruinous deeds, so the inward work follows the outward.","la":"It follows the couplet's pair with two rules about oneself (letting anger go, destroying the self-ruining deed), so forgiving others is set beside disciplining oneself."},{"a":"nm:12","b":"k:29","type":"develops","note":"Kural 29 says the anger of those who have climbed the hill of virtue is hard to withstand even for a moment; Nanmanikkadigai says sages give harm through their penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns of the ascetic's anger; the verse names its weapon, penance itself, and sets it with the snake's tooth and the elephant's tusk.","la":"It ranks the sage's penance with the snake's tooth, the elephant's tusk and the sulking woman's face as four instruments of harm, so holy power is classed as a natural weapon.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:13","b":"k:968","type":"develops","note":"Kural 968 asks whether feeding the body is a medicine once dignity is lost; Nanmanikkadigai says hunger is better than eating with dignity undone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet questions the worth of a life fed without honour; the verse states the choice as a meal: go hungry rather than eat so.","la":"It places the choice among three other preferences of the plain over the spoiled (drum over untuned lute, a firm woman over worthless men, fire over parting), so hunger is one of four lesser goods."},{"a":"nm:13","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1159 says fire burns only when touched, but love burns when one moves away; Nanmanikkadigai says entering fire is better than parting from those one is bound to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both weigh separation against fire and find separation the worse, the couplet in a lover's voice and the verse as a general rule of attachment.","la":"It carries the lover's comparison into a list of plain preferences, so parting from one's own becomes a general loss, not only love's."},{"a":"nm:14","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"Kural 525 says one who gives and speaks sweetly is ringed by kin; Nanmanikkadigai says the throng of kin rises in the pit of sweet speech and liberality grows in the bed of plenty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives two causes of a crowd of kin; the verse splits them, puts each in its own soil, and pairs them with the bad crops of harsh speech and poverty.","la":"It makes each disposition a crop of its condition, two good and two bad, so kin-gathering is shown with its opposite, concealment from harsh speech."},{"a":"nm:14","b":"n:306","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 306 says poverty can be ended by asking oneself rather than begging from strangers; Nanmanikkadigai says begging springs up quickly in the pit of poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse describes begging as poverty's quick crop; the quatrain answers that the crop need not grow if one begs of oneself, so the pair sets the natural course against the discipline that stops it.","la":"It sets begging as the last of four growths from their soils, so it appears as a natural consequence where the Naladiyar treats it as a choice."},{"a":"nm:15","b":"k:36","type":"develops","note":"Kural 36 says to do virtue now, since it is the companion that does not perish when one does; Nanmanikkadigai says that one who wants what goes along with oneself should do virtue.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both make virtue the one companion past death, and the verse puts it as a means to a wish, beside fame for lasting here and giving up anger for winning.","la":"Its 'if you want X, do Y' frame sets virtue as the means for one wish among four, with begging as the ironic means to pain, so the virtues become instruments chosen by their ends.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:15","b":"nm:11","type":"develops","note":"Verse 11 says one must always let go of one's anger; verse 15 says one who wants to win should give up anger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two verses together give the rule and its reason: anger is let go not only as a duty but because giving it up is how one wins."},{"a":"nm:15","b":"nm:14","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 says begging rises quickly in the pit of poverty; verse 15 says one who wants pain should rise to beg.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the book gives begging both a cause and a fruit: it grows from poverty and it yields pain.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:16","b":"k:725","type":"develops","note":"Kural 725 says to learn by the proper path so as to answer without fear in the assembly; Nanmanikkadigai says the learned man crosses the deep of the assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the purpose of learning as fearless reply; the verse calls the assembly a deep like the sea and a battle line, which shows why that fear is real.","la":"It ranks the learned man's crossing of the assembly with the sailor's sea, the horseman's army and the ascetic's penance, so speaking before an assembly is made as perilous as war or the sea."},{"a":"nm:16","b":"tk:7","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 7 names the hard learning of one who fears assemblies as a mute's dream; Nanmanikkadigai says the learned man crosses the deep of the assembly.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same claim is put from two sides: learning that fails in the assembly is useless, and learning is what carries a man across it."},{"a":"nm:17","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 53 lists home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth and strength as not lasting, so the best renounce them; Nanmanikkadigai gives friendship, youth, wealth and life each its own end.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says these things do not last; the verse says what each ends in, and three of the ends (falsehood, old age, excess) come from the thing itself.","la":"It assigns each thing its own end, so wealth ends in excess and friendship in falsehood rather than all ending alike, and it adds friendship, which the quatrain does not name."},{"a":"nm:18","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"Kural 523 says one who does not mingle with kin lives like a tank filling with no bank; Nanmanikkadigai says casting off kin is the gain of ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet shows the loss in an image of wealth spilling away; the verse names it ironically as ruin's prosperity, closing a list of what builds.","la":"It sets casting off kin after three true sources of prosperity (a good wife, uprightness, a king's good name), so the last item undoes the predicate by calling ruin a gain."},{"a":"nm:18","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"Kural 386 says the land of an accessible king who is not harsh of speech is spoken of above others; Nanmanikkadigai says the land's prosperity is people saying 'this king is good'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the king's conduct that earns praise; the verse makes that praise itself the land's wealth.","la":"It sets the king's good name among the prosperity of house and work, so the realm's gain is one of a household series, measured by speech."},{"a":"nm:18","b":"nm:20","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 18 says excellent women are the prosperity of a house; verse 20 says the lack of a bright-browed wife lays a house waste.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book states the same claim from both sides, prosperity with a good wife and waste without one, so the house is defined by the wife's presence."},{"a":"nm:19","b":"nm:16","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 16 says the learned man crosses the deep of the assembly; verse 19 says that if the learned man shakes, the meaning shakes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The book qualifies its praise of the learned: he carries the meaning across the assembly only while he holds firm, and his faltering loses it.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:20","b":"k:406","type":"develops","note":"Kural 406 likens the unlearned to barren saline ground; Nanmanikkadigai says a body without learned understanding is waste to oneself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both use waste land for the unlearned, but the couplet says they yield nothing to others while the verse says the body is waste to its owner.","la":"It sets the unlearned body after a house without a wife, a region without friends and an assembly without elders, so the ignorant self is one of four places laid waste by an absence.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:20","b":"tk:10","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 10 names an assembly with no elders to settle quarrels among three things that yield no good; Nanmanikkadigai calls lack of elders waste to the assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both short works make elders the thing an assembly cannot do without, and Thirikadugam gives the reason the verse omits: elders settle disputes."},{"a":"nm:21","b":"n:177","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 177 says water mixed with milk becomes milk, so the small are hidden by joining the great; Nanmanikkadigai says the vessel spoils the milk, as bad company ruins a lineage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same mixing image runs both ways in the group, the good absorbing the small in one and the container corrupting the good in the other, so company is shown to work in both directions.","la":"It sets the spoiled milk among four kinds of ruin (disunity, falsehood, bad company), so mixing is counted as a cause of loss, not of concealment.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:22","b":"nm:28","type":"develops","note":"Verse 22 says learning gives eyes cleared of darkness; verse 28 traces learning step by step through lessened ignorance to the path of truth and the higher world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the single sight learning gives in 22 becomes in 28 the first link of a chain that ends beyond this world."},{"a":"nm:23","b":"k:805","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 805 tells one to read a friend's hurtful act as folly or intimacy; Nanmanikkadigai says friends, even angry, do nothing that makes one tremble.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet expects friends to wound and supplies a charitable reading, while the verse sets a limit no anger crosses, so the pair marks how far a friend's hurt may go.","la":"It ranks the friend with the elephant and the beaten child as bonds that hold under strain, against the enemy who never stays, a contrast the couplet lacks."},{"a":"nm:24","b":"nm:37","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses open with laughter among friends: in 24 its good grows there, in 37 it is sweet there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book twice makes friendship the condition of laughter, once as growth and once as pleasure, so laughter is never praised in itself but only in company."},{"a":"nm:25","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"Kural 428 calls not fearing what should be feared folly; Nanmanikkadigai says that if one fears anything, it should be fearlessness itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet sorts objects of fear, while the verse turns fear on the fearless disposition, so the danger moves from the thing feared to the temper of the man.","la":"It makes the fear of fearlessness the first of four checks on oneself, with not falling short, not swerving for wealth and trusting friends, so fear opens a short code of integrity."},{"a":"nm:25","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"Kural 510 says doubting one already trusted brings endless trouble; Nanmanikkadigai says not to pry into a task left with friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the general error of doubting the trusted, and the verse turns it into a working rule about checking up on a delegated task.","la":"It puts non-interference with friends' work beside refusing a crore against one's conscience, so trust in friends is counted as part of one's own integrity."},{"a":"nm:26","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Kural 256 argues that buyers cause the killing, since no one would sell flesh if none bought; Nanmanikkadigai counts buying and eating flesh as a fault in its own right.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet needs an argument to make the buyer guilty, while the verse simply lists buying and killing as two faults, showing the group's code taking the Kural's conclusion as settled.","la":"It sets the buyer's fault beside the killer's and beside rearing beings to torment them and improper speech, so flesh-buying becomes one of four plain faults rather than the end of an argument."},{"a":"nm:26","b":"sp:12","type":"agrees","note":"Both lists put killing and eating flesh beside a fault of speech: Nanmanikkadigai saying what should not be said, Sirupanchamoolam speaking evil of a girl without inquiry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two counted works independently join a speech fault to the code of non-killing, so harmful words are filed with harm to bodies across the group."},{"a":"nm:27","b":"k:542","type":"develops","note":"Kural 542 says the world looks to the sky and the people to the sceptre; Nanmanikkadigai keeps both and adds the infant at the breast and Death looking to the world's end.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse borrows the couplet's two dependencies and closes them with Death's grim watch, so the image of living by looking to a giver ends in the one watcher who takes.","la":"It adds the infant's dependence on milk and inverts the series with Death, so the king's sceptre is one giver among nurturers set against a taker."},{"a":"nm:28","b":"k:398","type":"develops","note":"Kural 398 says learning gained in one birth protects through seven; Nanmanikkadigai traces how learning reaches the other world step by step.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states that learning outlasts a life, and the verse supplies the stages by which it does so, ignorance lessening into insight, insight into the true path, the path into fame and the higher world.","la":"It turns the couplet's single benefit into a chain of four causes, so the reward beyond this life is earned through intermediate changes in the learner."},{"a":"nm:29","b":"k:421","type":"develops","note":"Kural 421 calls understanding the tool that guards against ruin; Nanmanikkadigai says understanding is found standing where trouble befalls oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives understanding its use as a guard, and the verse gives the place where it is proved, one's own adversity.","la":"It sets understanding among things known by where they settle (water in a hollow, sin under blame, desire in a crowding), so understanding is defined by its location in trouble."},{"a":"nm:30","b":"k:1041","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty except poverty itself; Nanmanikkadigai names poverty the most painful of all things.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves poverty incomparable, while the verse places the hand that never refuses right after it, so the extreme of want is paired with the extreme of giving.","la":"It makes the pain of poverty one of four superlatives and follows it with the generous hand, so the list supplies the remedy the couplet leaves out.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:31","b":"k:871","type":"develops","note":"Kural 871 says enmity should not be wanted even in jest; Nanmanikkadigai says enmity sprouts from a man's own prior intent and then becomes a seed of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns against wanting enmity, and the verse explains why: it grows from one's own intention and multiplies into more pain.","la":"It sets enmity beside fire from smoke and joy from laughter, so laughter seeds joy while intent seeds enmity, a contrast to the couplet's 'not even in jest'."},{"a":"nm:32","b":"n:334","type":"same_image","note":"Nanmanikkadigai likens the hard-hearted to stone; Naladiyar 334 ranks stones above base people because stones at least serve whoever uses them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses use stone in opposite ways, as hardness to be blamed and as plain usefulness to be praised, so the base man is lower than the very thing the hard-hearted resemble.","la":"It makes stone one of four likenesses (disease, ornament, weeds), so the image names a kind of person rather than ranking people against things."},{"a":"nm:33","b":"k:134","type":"tension","note":"Reading என்செயினும் with line 1, Nanmanikkadigai holds the brahmin's birth best whatever he does; Kural 134 says a brahmin's birth is ruined when his conduct fails.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On that reading the two make incompatible claims about whether conduct can forfeit a high birth, which shows the group divided on birth against conduct.","la":"It ranks brahmin birth alongside the mother, prosperity and youth as unequalled goods, so birth is treated as a fixed endowment like youth, not a standing to be kept."},{"a":"nm:33","b":"s:1003","type":"tension","note":"Nanmanikkadigai ranks the brahmin's birth above all others; the Siddhar verse asks whether a number is marked on flesh or bone to tell a Paraiyar woman from a Brahmin woman.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set together, a birth-rank stated as plain fact meets a later denial that birth leaves any mark on the body, showing the claim the ethics work takes for granted being contested."},{"a":"nm:33","b":"nm:40","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 33 ranks brahmin birth highest; verse 40 says fortune does not abide by rank or kind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book ranks births yet denies that birth secures fortune, so within one work high birth is honoured but given no hold on prosperity."},{"a":"nm:34","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"Kural 443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of rare things; Nanmanikkadigai says great things are reached through great men, as iron splits iron.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet prizes the great as companions, and the verse explains the worth by a law of like working on like, so the great are needed because only they can reach great ends.","la":"It proves the claim with two physical cases (iron on iron, water rinsing after water), so the moral rule is given the force of a natural one."},{"a":"nm:35","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum that proclaims secrets; Nanmanikkadigai says the elation of the base blares through the town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet shows the base unable to keep others' secrets, and the verse shows them unable to keep their own joy quiet, so loudness marks them in both.","la":"It sets the base man's elation against the warrior's before kings and the giver's before the poor, ranking kinds of joy by where they show."},{"a":"nm:38","b":"k:260","type":"develops","note":"Kural 260 says all creatures fold their hands to the one who neither kills nor eats flesh; Nanmanikkadigai says others' not eating flesh is the refuge of living beings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both speak from the side of the creatures, the couplet as their worship and the verse as their shelter, so abstaining from flesh is a protection given, not only a purity kept.","la":"It puts abstention beside givers as the beggar's refuge and valour as an army's, so refusing flesh is counted among the supports others depend on."},{"a":"nm:40","b":"k:1253","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1253 has a woman say her desire shows like a sneeze though she would hide it; Nanmanikkadigai says desire cannot be hidden however hard one tries.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lover's confession in the couplet becomes in the verse a general law, so a moment of love poetry is used as ethical knowledge.","la":"It sets desire beside fortune and Death as things no one controls, so its showing is ranked with impersonal forces rather than one woman's weakness."},{"a":"nm:41","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"Kural 376 says what is one's lot will not stay though guarded nor go though thrown away; the verse says the same of life itself at birth and at death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's law of lot, said of possessions, is extended to the life that holds them: being born and dying are the first goods one cannot keep or refuse.","la":"It sets life's coming and going beside Fortune's swelling and ruin's vanishing, so birth, death, gain and loss become one set of things outside command."},{"a":"nm:42","b":"k:761","type":"develops","note":"Kural 761 ranks a complete, wound-fearing army first among a king's wealth; the verse says a king's glory withers without one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the army is both the king's chief possession and the root his fame grows from, so losing it is not a loss of goods but a drying-up of renown.","la":"It puts the king's army in a row with war for warriors, roots for trees and the sluice for the neytal, so the army becomes the hidden supply of glory, not a holding."},{"a":"nm:43","b":"n:101","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 101 pictures an old deed seeking out its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; the verse calls one's former deed one's mother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two images run the bond in opposite directions: in the Naladiyar the deed hunts the doer like a calf its mother, here the deed is the mother who bore the doer.","la":"It ends a list that redefines stranger, beloved and father, so karma takes the place of the mother in a whole remade family."},{"a":"nm:44","b":"k:812","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 812 dismisses the friendship of misfits who come with gain and leave without it; the verse says friends' friendship perishes when the good changes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet blames a bad kind of friend, while the verse makes the fading of friendship with advantage a natural law, like a crop without water, so the fault may lie in friendship itself.","la":"It sets friendship's decay beside a crop dying without water, so the end of interested friendship reads as nature, not as a vice of some."},{"a":"nm:44","b":"k:1019","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1019 says lasting shamelessness burns every good; the verse says fortune perishes the moment shame is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse makes the couplet's slow burning instant (போழ்தே, the very moment), so the loss of shame is shown as a sudden rather than gradual ruin.","la":"It puts the loss of shame first in a list of four ruins of the same form, so shame stands with water for crops as the thing whose absence ends goods at once."},{"a":"nm:45","b":"k:1308","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1308 asks what use suffering is where there is no lover to know it; the verse says a sulk dies in one who does not understand sulking.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both put the life of oodal in the partner's understanding, and the verse adds that the same is true of gratitude, guests and song, so the lover's plight is one case of a general rule.","la":"It sets the sulk beside a kindness, a guest and a song, so love's quarrel becomes one of four gifts that die in an incompetent receiver."},{"a":"nm:45","b":"k:90","type":"develops","note":"Kural 90 says a guest wilts when the host's face changes; the verse says a guest dies before those without the wish to receive.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the guest's wilting in a visible face, the verse in the host's absent desire, so the face is shown as the sign of a want of welcome that kills even before it shows.","la":"It sets the guest beside gratitude, song and sulk, so hospitality is a gift that needs a fit receiver, like art."},{"a":"nm:46","b":"k:707","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 707 asks whether anything is as knowing as the face, which reaches first in joy or anger; the verse says nothing tells the intention beforehand as the face does.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the face faster than speech, and the verse sets this speed against slower signs, scent, weighed words and the mind, so priority in time is what makes the face the best witness.","la":"It ranks the face above three other signs (scent, words, mind), making the couplet's claim a contest won rather than a lone assertion."},{"a":"nm:47","b":"k:19","type":"develops","note":"Kural 19 says giving and penance will not last if the sky does not give rain; the verse says rain does not fall where there are no ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes penance depend on rain and the verse makes rain depend on penance, so together they describe a circle in which ascetic and sky sustain each other.","la":"It makes rain one link in a chain that ends with king and householder, so the couplet's dependence becomes a whole social order resting on the house."},{"a":"nm:47","b":"tk:96","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 96 names the knowing wife, the ascetic who keeps his vow and the good king as three at whose word rain falls; the verse makes rain rest on ascetics, ascetics on the king, the king on householders.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Both books make human virtue the cause of rain, but Thirikadugam sets three causes side by side while the verse lines them up, so one gives co-equal powers and the other a chain of dependence ending in the household.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:49","b":"n:111","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 111 says saying 'I have none' of what one cannot give is no disgrace and is the way of the world; the verse says saying 'nothing' to beggars is hard for anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain frees refusal from blame and the verse says it is still hard to say, so together they separate guilt from difficulty: refusal is no fault but costs everyone.","la":"It ranks refusal as the hardest of four hard things, the only one hard for anyone, whereas the quatrain only judges it."},{"a":"nm:49","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing' and giving belong only to the well-born; the verse says saying 'nothing' to beggars is hard for anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the refusal to refuse a mark of birth, the verse makes the reluctance to refuse universal, so lineage is no longer needed to explain it.","la":"It sets refusing beggars among difficulties tied to kinds of person (the eminent, the kin-born, the stingy) and frees this last one from any kind."},{"a":"nm:50","b":"k:942","type":"develops","note":"Kural 942 says the body needs no medicine if one eats after the last meal is digested; the verse says food burns a body that takes no pleasure in it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the rule for eating, the verse the damage when the body cannot take food, so the pair shows food as medicine or fire depending on the body's state.","la":"It sets food beside words, past deeds and an army, all things meant to serve that turn and burn their holder."},{"a":"nm:51","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"Kural 423 says understanding is seeing the true substance of whatever is heard from whomever; the verse says the meanings of the many books are things to be examined.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the test for any utterance, and the verse applies it to the many books themselves, so even the written tradition must be sifted, not merely received.","la":"It puts sifting the books last in a sequence of right responses (deride contempt, ponder counsel, join dharma), so examination is the final act of a learner's conduct."},{"a":"nm:52","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 259 ranks not killing one creature above a thousand sacrifices; the verse says there is beneficence equal to sacrifice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both measure an ethical act against sacrifice, but the Kural puts non-killing above it and the verse puts beneficence level with it, so sacrifice stays the standard in one and is outranked in the other.","la":"It makes beneficence equal to sacrifice as a tank can hold a river and a song reach the Veda, so the claim is one of a set of lesser things rising to the greater."},{"a":"nm:53","b":"tk:12","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam 12 defines the vellalan as one who does not eat while a guest waits; the verse defines him as one who owns oxen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both define the same birth-group name by a mark, but one by hospitality and the other by owning the plough-ox, so the period's ethics offered rival tests, conduct or possession, for who deserved the name."},{"a":"nm:53","b":"k:56","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 56 says she is a woman who guards herself, cares for her husband and keeps their good name without flagging; the verse says the woman of household life has wisdom with her beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make பெண் a title to be earned, but the Kural earns it by guarding and serving while the verse asks for wisdom joined to beauty, a different test of the same name.","la":"It puts the wife among definitions of the farmer, the brahmin, the cock and the commander, so a woman's title is one of a set of role-names each tied to a mark.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:54","b":"k:460","type":"develops","note":"Kural 460 says nothing brings more affliction than bad company; the verse says the bad delighting in the bad is ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns the good against bad company, the verse looks at the bad among themselves, so ruin comes not only from joining them but from the pleasure they take in each other.","la":"It closes a list of fitting delights (kings in elephants and horses, good men in modest women) with a delight that is ruin, so the last item undoes the pattern."},{"a":"nm:55","b":"k:61","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 61 says nothing that can be gained matches children who come to know; the verse says no wealth is brighter or fuller than children.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet's praise is conditional on the children's understanding while the verse gives it unqualified, and the verse places it among other firsts, so children are the first of wealth as the mother is first above the gods.","la":"It ranks children as one of four superlatives (eye among organs, husband among kin, mother above gods), so their worth is placed in a hierarchy of bests.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:56","b":"in:30","type":"same_image","note":"Innaa Narpathu 30 counts a house where a snake lurks hidden as painful; the verse likens the small-minded who hide what has befallen them to an anthill where a snake dwells hidden.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Innaa line names the hidden snake as a danger to live beside, and the verse turns it into a person, so the reader learns that the secretive man is that danger."},{"a":"nm:57","b":"k:348","type":"develops","note":"Kural 348 says those who wholly renounce reach the head and the rest are caught in the net; the verse says those who delight in dharma are born again and those who renounce are of heaven.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's net becomes in the verse rebirth earned by dharma itself, so even the righteous householder is among those still caught, while renunciation alone leaves.","la":"It sets renunciation after the rule that deeds follow their doers, so release is the end of a sequence on karma rather than a standalone praise.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:58","b":"k:336","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 336 says the world's greatness is that one who was here yesterday is gone today; the verse says the born and the dying always exist.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural looks at the individual who vanishes and the verse at the pattern that stays, so impermanence of persons is shown to sit within a permanence of kinds.","la":"It places birth and death beside the sun and moon, disease and labour, and givers and takers, all fixtures of the world, so mortality becomes one more constant."},{"a":"nm:59","b":"iv:4","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 4 names not swelling one's flesh by eating flesh as foremost sweet; the verse says the one who eats sweetly is the one who eats without killing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two state one claim from two sides: Iniyavai calls the abstention sweet, and the verse calls the meal sweet only if it involves no killing, so sweetness belongs to the eating, not the food."},{"a":"nm:59","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 251 asks how one who eats flesh to swell his own can hold to grace; the verse redefines the sweet eater as one who eats without killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet argues against flesh-eating from grace, the verse from the word 'sweet' itself, so the claim moves from the eater's heart to the meaning of a good meal.","la":"It sets the non-killing eater among redefinitions of the shunned, the lonely and the pleasant, so diet is judged by the same test of conduct as social standing."},{"a":"nm:60","b":"n:303","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 303 says the worthy go before no one except those who embrace them and say 'come in, eat'; the verse says begging from one who does not know one's plight becomes enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says whom the worthy may approach and the verse says what happens if they approach the wrong one: the request turns into enmity.","la":"It sets this cost beside the giver's fame, the taker's name and the laughter of going before strangers, so asking is weighed by its consequences on both sides."},{"a":"nm:61","b":"k:85","type":"develops","note":"Kural 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests and eats the leftovers even needs sowing; the verse says hospitality thrives when vessels are spread and guests fed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet claims the host's field prospers without seed; the verse supplies the mechanism by analogy, since fire grows by the ghee it consumes and the cow by the milk the calf takes, so the host's outflow is itself what increases.","la":"It sets guest-feeding beside fire fed with ghee, gods given praise and a cow let to its calf, so hospitality becomes one case of a general law that things thrive by being drawn on."},{"a":"nm:62","b":"k:62","type":"develops","note":"Kural 62 says blameless children of good character keep evils from a parent for seven births; the verse says a man's blamelessness is seen in his children.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes good children a protection for the parent and the verse makes them the evidence of the parent's worth, so read together the child both shows and secures the father's blamelessness.","la":"It puts children among four kinds of evidence (ruin for friendship, wealth's movement for prosperity), so the child becomes a sign to be read rather than a blessing to be had."},{"a":"nm:64","b":"k:409","type":"develops","note":"Kural 409 says the unlearned of high birth lack the worth of the learned of low birth; the verse says that in one family the learned youth is praised over the unlearned elder.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet reverses the rank of birth for learning; the verse reverses the rank of age within a single family, so together learning overturns both of the inherited orders a household runs on.","la":"It prefaces the reversal with one fire worshipped in a lamp and scorned in firewood, so the elder and the youth are shown to be one substance valued by what it does."},{"a":"nm:65","b":"k:84","type":"develops","note":"Kural 84 says the fair goddess dwells gladly in the house of one who tends guests with a glad face; the verse says a host of fortune comes when the bright lady looks on one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet tells what keeps the goddess in a house and the verse what her look does, so together hospitality is the cause and the goddess's glance the means by which fortune gathers.","la":"It ranks her glance with water for seed and wealth for desire, then adds that her grief undoes it, so fortune is shown as conditional where the couplet shows only her staying."},{"a":"nm:65","b":"k:167","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 167 says the fair goddess, grudging the envious man, shows him to her elder sister; the verse says that if the bright lady is grieved the fortune perishes.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The verse states only that her displeasure ruins, and the couplet names one thing that displeases her, envy, so the pair turns a bare warning into a cause and its effect.","la":"It sets the loss of fortune as the reversal of its own previous item, so the list moves from gaining to losing with one goddess at both ends."},{"a":"nm:66","b":"n:200","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 200 says the base fill themselves on rice and fried dishes a great patron gives, while to the self-reliant water won by effort is nectar; the verse says all the low delight in rice from paddy.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both books define the base by being satisfied with rice, but the quatrain sets against them those who live by their own effort and the verse those who delight in their due standing, so the opposite of the base is labour in one and honour in the other.","la":"It sets the low man's rice in a series with the tiger's flesh and the cattle's grass, so appetite for food is made a creature's nature, and the high are marked off by a delight that is not eaten.","gr":4},{"a":"nm:67","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"Kural 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming meet no affliction that shakes them; the verse says deeds that recoil (அதிர்க்கும்) show themselves only later, except to those who knew beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the verb அதிர் for the shock of what comes, and the verse adds that the shock is the delayed result of one's own deed, so the couplet's foresight becomes foresight of one's own acts rather than of outside events.","la":"It joins foresight to freedom from craving in one list, so knowing beforehand and wanting nothing are set as the two guards of a deed."},{"a":"nm:67","b":"k:367","type":"develops","note":"Kural 367 says that if craving is cut off thoroughly, unfailing deeds (தவாவினை) come as one wishes; the verse says the deeds of those without craving do not fail (தவாவாம்).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The verse repeats the couplet's promise in its own words but sets beside it that the heart of those who have gained is ever craving, which shows why the couplet's condition is hardest for the one who already has.","la":"It sets the desireless deed against the craving of those who have obtained, a contrast between two kinds of person that the couplet, which speaks of one, lacks."},{"a":"nm:68","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 228 asks whether hard-eyed hoarders who keep and lose do not know the joy of giving; the verse ranks those without wealth above those who have it and only guard it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet says the hoarder misses a joy; the verse goes further and ranks him below the man who has nothing, so wealth kept unused is shown to be worse than poverty, not merely less than giving.","la":"It frames the point as a ranking and repeats it with the poor over the hoarder, then sets forbearance over reviling in the same form, so hoarding is placed among vices of the same weight as abuse.","gr":4},{"a":"nm:68","b":"k:159","type":"develops","note":"Kural 159 says those who bear the harsh words of transgressors are as pure as renouncers; the verse ranks those who bear above those who rise up and revile.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet measures the forbearer upward against the renouncer and the verse measures him against the reviler beside him, so forbearance is placed both by its reward and by the act it refuses.","la":"It sets forbearance in a series of 'better than' rankings with poverty over hoarding, so restraint of anger and restraint of possession are judged by one standard.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:69","b":"k:706","type":"develops","note":"Kural 706 says the face shows what is strongest in the heart, as crystal shows what is near it; the verse says a man's face tells the desire within him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says the face shows whatever is strongest and the verse names that thing as desire (வேட்கை), so the pair makes the face chiefly a betrayer of wants.","la":"It ranks the face with a son revealing his father and rain revealing a land, so the face becomes one case of reading a hidden source from its outflow."},{"a":"nm:69","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"Kural 70 says the son's help to his father is that people ask what penance his father did; the verse says a son tells of his father's goodness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the praise a debt the son repays; the verse states it as a fact of inference, so read together the son repays his father by being the evidence others read.","la":"It places the son among natural signs (the face, the field, the sky), so the father's repute is shown to be inferred as surely as rain is from cloud."},{"a":"nm:70","b":"tk:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam 10 says a town without teachers yields no good; the verse says a town is good if many live in it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse makes a town good by its numbers and Thirikadugam makes it worthless without teachers, so the pair shows the group measuring a town both by size and by learning, and a populous town can still fail the second test."},{"a":"nm:70","b":"k:83","type":"develops","note":"Kural 83 says the life of one who tends guests daily does not fall into ruin; the verse says a family gains glory by the food it gives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet answers the fear that giving food ruins a house; the verse claims more, that it is the source of the family's distinction, so feeding is shown as both safe and the very thing that ranks a house.","la":"It ranks the family's food-giving with a bull's herd and a town's people, so a house's glory is made to come from those it sustains.","gr":5},{"a":"nm:71","b":"k:33","type":"develops","note":"Kural 33 tells one to do virtue without stopping as far as one is able; the verse says the doing of virtue is according to one's strength.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet uses capacity as a floor (do all you can) and the verse uses it as a measure (each gives by his strength), so read together capacity both limits what is owed and forbids doing less.","la":"It sets virtue with a rider's horse, a tank's sluices and a household's spending, so the measure by capacity becomes a principle of proportion across unlike things.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:71","b":"k:478","type":"develops","note":"Kural 478 says there is no ruin if the outflow is not wider than the inflow, using the image of channels; the verse says sluices are as wide as the dug tank and people live according to their means.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both picture a household as water and channels, and the verse adds that the outlet should be cut to the size of the tank, so the couplet's rule of balance becomes a rule of construction made when the tank is dug.","la":"It sets the sluice beside the measure of virtue by strength, so spending and doing good are governed by the same proportion."},{"a":"nm:72","b":"k:99","type":"develops","note":"Kural 99 asks why one who sees the good of sweet speech would use harsh words; the verse says the harsh delight in harsh words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's question assumes harsh speech is a mistake; the verse answers that it is a pleasure to the harsh, so the pair shows that reasoning against harsh words will not reach those whose nature enjoys them.","la":"It places harsh speech after units of time and the clear-minded, so a speaker's words are shown as following from his nature as watches follow from naazhigai."},{"a":"nm:73","b":"k:415","type":"develops","note":"Kural 415 says the words of the good are like a staff on slippery ground; the verse says the learned man rises if he stumbles while the fool breaks his leg.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the staff to the hearer as another's words; the verse puts the support inside the learned man himself, so learning is shown as the staff once it has been taken in.","la":"It sets the learned and the fool side by side in the same fall and then names the cause, a means made with single-minded care, which the couplet's image does not explain."},{"a":"nm:74","b":"k:410","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 410 says others are to the learned as beasts are to people; the verse says those who love the learned are like the learned.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet draws a line between the learned and all others; the verse lets those who love the learned cross it, so the pair shows the divide is passable through devotion rather than study alone.","la":"It closes a list in which each group is lifted to the rank of another (poets to gods, townsfolk to kin, servers to parents), so loving the learned becomes one of several ways nearness confers rank."},{"a":"nm:76","b":"k:27","type":"develops","note":"Kural 27 says the world lies with one who discerns the five sense-kinds; the verse names three senses with their objects and then thought.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ends its list at the five senses; the verse adds thought as a further faculty with its own object, done in company, so the pair shows the ethics group placing counsel beyond the discernment of sense.","la":"It pairs each faculty with its object and makes the last one collective (தொக்கிருந்து), a step from individual sensing to shared deliberation that the couplet lacks.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:77","b":"n:2","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 2 says wealth turns like a cart wheel and urges sharing its food at once; the verse says no one has passed through prosperity without ruin.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain draws a counsel of giving from wealth's turning and the verse draws none, so set together they show the same observation used once as an argument and once as a bare fact.","la":"It compresses the impermanence of life, strength, youth and wealth into four negations in one verse, where the Naladiyar gives them separate chapters."},{"a":"nm:77","b":"n:11","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 11 says the wise renounced in childhood knowing grey hair would come; the verse says no one has gained unaging youth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain turns the loss of youth into a reason to renounce early; the verse states only that the loss is universal, so the pair shows the Naladiyar's renunciation resting on a premise the other book states without that conclusion.","la":"It sets youth among strength, life and wealth, all lost alike, so the loss of youth is not singled out as the Naladiyar's chapter makes it."},{"a":"nm:78","b":"k:986","type":"develops","note":"Kural 986 says the touchstone of worth is accepting defeat even from those not one's equal; the verse says nobility is known by helpfulness toward anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make the test of worth one's conduct toward those who are no match for one (துலையல்லார், யார்கண்ணும்), the couplet by yielding and the verse by helping, so the pair shows nobility tested where nothing is owed.","la":"It sets the sign of nobility with the signs of a man, a pool and toddy, so worth is known by its outflow as a pool by its water."},{"a":"nm:79","b":"k:127","type":"develops","note":"Kural 127 says whatever else one fails to guard one should guard the tongue; the verse says the tongue cuts friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns of the suffering an unguarded tongue brings to its owner; the verse names a particular loss, friendship, so the pair shows what is at stake in the couplet's general warning.","la":"It sets the tongue beside the fool who lets go and the resolve that fails, so speech is listed among the things that break bonds."},{"a":"nm:80","b":"k:303","type":"develops","note":"Kural 303 says to forget anger toward anyone, since evils are born from it; the verse says that if one destroys anything, it should be anger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks for anger to be let go of; the verse turns destruction itself toward anger, so the pair shows the same urge to destroy being redirected rather than given up.","la":"It frames each counsel as the best object of a verb (give food, lift fallen kin, destroy anger), so the destructive impulse is given a proper target like the giving and the lifting."},{"a":"nm:80","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 says relieving the destroying hunger of the destitute is where one with wealth should store it; the verse says that if one gives, one should give food.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says where wealth is best stored and the verse which gift is best, so together food given to the hungry is both the best gift and the safest store.","la":"It ranks food first among gifts by setting giving beside letting go, lifting and destroying, each with its one right object.","gr":5},{"a":"nm:81","b":"tk:84","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam joins people of a long line without learning to poverty; Nanmanikkadigai says lineage and family standing die beneath want of learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Thirikadugam names the outcome, poverty, and Nanmanikkadigai the death of the family's standing itself, so together they make unlearning ruin both the purse and the name of an old house."},{"a":"nm:81","b":"in:27","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu calls beauty in those without means painful; Nanmanikkadigai says beauty and youth die beneath poverty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books say the same thing from two sides: Innaa names the sight of beauty in want painful, Nanmanikkadigai says the beauty itself does not survive it."},{"a":"nm:81","b":"k:409","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 409 ranks the learned low-born above the unlearned high-born; Nanmanikkadigai says lineage dies beneath want of learning.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Where the Kural keeps high birth as a rank that learning outweighs, the quatrain makes the rank itself die without learning, a stronger claim about birth.","la":"It puts lineage in a row with beauty, paddy and a draught ox, so birth becomes one more thing that needs a support beneath it to live."},{"a":"nm:82","b":"k:397","type":"develops","note":"Kural 397 says every land and town becomes one's own, and asks why one stays unlearned; Nanmanikkadigai gives the belonging to the good and the renouncer, and ends with the man of wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ties belonging everywhere to learning alone; the quatrain shows virtue, renunciation and plain wealth also open every town, and names the base for whom none opens.","la":"It splits the Kural's single case into four holders, three by virtue or vice and the last by money, and the last item ironically undoes the first."},{"a":"nm:82","b":"pn:192","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 192 opens with every town as ours and everyone our kin, as the stance of one who sees through the wise; Nanmanikkadigai says every town belongs to those with something in hand.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The heroic poem makes belonging everywhere a philosophical stance; the ethics verse observes that wealth secures the same welcome, which sets the ideal against how towns in fact receive people."},{"a":"nm:83","b":"sp:10","type":"same_image","note":"Sirupanchamoolam counts the spider's eggs, the crab's young and abuse to the tongue as each one's own death; Nanmanikkadigai has the plantain's fruit and the unlearned man's own words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both books use a creature killed by its own offspring to say that speech kills the speaker, so the image of self-born death is a shared stock of the ethics group, applied each time to the tongue."},{"a":"nm:83","b":"k:77","type":"develops","note":"Kural 77 says dharma burns the loveless as the sun burns the boneless; Nanmanikkadigai calls dharma death to those who do wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read beside the plantain killed by its own fruit, the quatrain makes dharma a death the wrongdoer grows himself, which the Kural's sun, an outside force, does not show.","la":"It sets dharma's punishment among deaths that come from within the victim, so retribution becomes self-made rather than imposed."},{"a":"nm:84","b":"pn:187","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 187 says a land is good only where its men are good; Nanmanikkadigai says kings gain glory by the land they rule.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair runs the credit in opposite directions: the Purananuru makes the land's worth depend on its people, the ethics verse makes the king's glory depend on the land, so his glory rests on men he does not make good himself."},{"a":"nm:85","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"Kural 523 says life without mingling with kin is a tank with no bank; Nanmanikkadigai calls leaving one's kin striving after ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows the loss as waste, wealth spilling away; the quatrain names leaving kin as an effort, so ruin is something one works at rather than suffers.","la":"It sets abandoning kin as the one wrong striving after three right ones, so the last item reverses the verse's praise of effort."},{"a":"nm:85","b":"k:30","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 30 says those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous, kind to every creature; Nanmanikkadigai names striving always after good as the mind of the அந்தணர்.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural derives the name from the conduct; the quatrain lists the அந்தணர் with women and kings as a station with its proper striving, so read together they show the word moving between a status and a virtue.","la":"It puts the அந்தணர் in a row of stations, each with its own work, which turns the Kural's definition into a duty of a group."},{"a":"nm:86","b":"k:284","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 284 says a love ripened in stealth yields undying sorrow; Nanmanikkadigai says not to steal because harsh things follow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both give the reason against theft as its consequence rather than its wrong, so the ethics books argue the point by outcome, not by rule.","la":"It sets theft beside rejecting the worthy, befriending the small and making enemies, so theft becomes one of four ways of making trouble for oneself."},{"a":"nm:86","b":"k:871","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 871 says enmity should not be wanted even in jest; Nanmanikkadigai says one must not take up enmity with anyone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the ban on enmity is absolute in both books: the Kural closes the gap of jest, the quatrain the gap of the opponent, with anyone at all.","la":"It sets avoiding enmity next to avoiding friendship with the small, so both who one befriends and who one opposes are guarded."},{"a":"nm:87","b":"k:302","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 302 calls anger bad where it cannot avail and worse where it can; Nanmanikkadigai bids anger be curbed where it cannot avail.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain stops where the Kural's first half stops and, after bidding pride against enemies, leaves anger where one is strong unmentioned; the Kural's second half closes that gap.","la":"It sets curbing anger beside a call to be proud against enemies at the right time, so the anger left out is partly licensed by the list.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:87","b":"k:787","type":"develops","note":"Kural 787 says friendship turns one from ruin and sets one on the path; Nanmanikkadigai says to increase friends by leading them to the good.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes what a friend does; the quatrain turns that guidance into the way friendships multiply, so guiding friends is also how one gains more of them.","la":"It makes leading friends to the good a means of increase, set against diminishing food and anger, which turns a duty into a gain."},{"a":"nm:88","b":"k:605","type":"develops","note":"Kural 605 calls delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep the pleasure-boat of those bound for ruin; Nanmanikkadigai says sloth stays with those who are to perish.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books use the same phrase for the ruined (கெடுநீரார், கெடுவார்); the quatrain sets sloth's dwelling beside a tethered elephant, so sloth is pictured as a thing that stays, not a boat that carries one off.","la":"It puts sloth in a row with cruelty, women's shame and a tethered elephant under one verb, staying, so vice and restraint both become things that hold a person."},{"a":"nm:89","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"Kural 978 says greatness always bows and smallness adorns itself; Nanmanikkadigai says bowing is always the ornament of the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural plays bowing against self-adornment; the quatrain makes bowing itself the ornament, and sets it beside the Veda and victory, so humility is the learning proper to the worthy.","la":"It ranks humility among the proper excellences of each station, the andhanar's Veda and the king's just victory, making it the worthy man's own accomplishment."},{"a":"nm:90","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"Kural 57 asks what use confinement is and says a woman's own steadfastness is the best guard; Nanmanikkadigai says a woman without a bond goes as she pleases though guarded.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural moves the guard inside the woman; the quatrain adds desire that flows on though blocked and murder that clings though hidden, so the failure of guarding becomes a general law about inner nature.","la":"It sets the guarded woman beside desire and a hidden killing, so the claim widens from women to everything that outward control cannot hold."},{"a":"nm:90","b":"k:1253","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1253 has a woman say her desire breaks out like a sneeze though she would hide it; Nanmanikkadigai says desire goes where it was going though obstructed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the experience in a lover's voice; the quatrain states it as a law beside a hidden killing, which shows the ethics books treating akam's confession as a fact about desire in general.","la":"It turns a lover's admission into one of four laws of what cannot be stopped or hidden, placed next to murder."},{"a":"nm:91","b":"k:575","type":"develops","note":"Kural 575 calls kindly regard the eye's only jewel and an eye without it a wound; Nanmanikkadigai says where the gentle eye of the worthy grows dharma grows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural judges the eye by its kindness; the quatrain gives each kind of eye its harvest, and makes the hard eye a source of strength in one line and of sin in another.","la":"It sets the gentle eye among four eyes, two of them hard, so kindly regard is ranked against daring and cruelty rather than praised alone.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:92","b":"k:218","type":"tension","note":"Kural 218 says those who know what is due do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even when without means; Nanmanikkadigai calls liberality at a time without means a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two disagree about giving when poor: the Kural praises it as knowledge of duty, the quatrain counts it a fault of timing, so the ethics books split over whether means limit generosity.","la":"It sets giving beyond one's means among faults of wrong timing, learning missed in youth and anger without kin, so liberality is judged by its season."},{"a":"nm:92","b":"in:10","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu calls the liberality of those without means painful; Nanmanikkadigai calls it a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two words differ: painful to see in Innaa, a fault in Nanmanikkadigai, so the same act is judged both by its cost to the giver and by its wrongness, and both books stand against the Kural 218 view."},{"a":"nm:92","b":"k:306","type":"develops","note":"Kural 306 says anger burns the raft of one's own kin; Nanmanikkadigai calls anger when one has no kin a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they make kin and anger work both ways: anger destroys kin, and without kin anger has nothing to stand on, so the angry man loses the very support his anger needs.","la":"It counts anger without kin as a fault of wrong circumstance, beside youth without learning and giving without means."},{"a":"nm:93","b":"k:67","type":"develops","note":"Kural 67 says a father's good to his son is to place him in front in the assembly; Nanmanikkadigai calls letting children grow unlearned an evil.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the father's duty as a gift; the quatrain states its neglect as an evil placed beside killing, which raises the weight of the duty.","la":"It ranks neglecting a child's learning with killing everywhere, so a parent's omission joins the gravest wrong in the list.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:93","b":"k:133","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 133 says conduct is noble birth and failing in it becomes low birth; Nanmanikkadigai says lineage is evil when its principle is destroyed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The quatrain goes past the Kural's demotion to call the lost lineage itself an evil, and its கொள்கை, a held principle, names something narrower than conduct in general.","la":"It sets fallen lineage beside killing, unlearned children and shameless beauty, so birth is one more good turned evil by a loss."},{"a":"nm:94","b":"k:541","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 541 says justice is investigating, showing favour to no one and acting after consideration; Nanmanikkadigai defines justice as having no favour toward anyone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same phrase (யார்மாட்டும் / யார்கண்ணும், no favour) defines justice in both, and the quatrain adds inner inquiry for the discerning ruler where the Kural asks for investigation, so the ruler's scrutiny is turned on himself as well.","la":"It sets the definition of justice beside definitions of conduct as learning and of wealth as enjoyment with dharma, making justice one of the ruler's set of defined virtues.","gr":2},{"a":"nm:94","b":"ac:1","type":"qualifies","note":"Acharakkovai 1 names learning as one of eight seeds of ஆசாரம்; Nanmanikkadigai says ஆசாரம் is learning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book of ஆசாரம் makes learning one root among eight and goes on to ritual rule, while the quatrain makes learning the whole of it, so the word is defined far more narrowly and less ritually here."},{"a":"nm:95","b":"k:288","type":"develops","note":"Kural 288 says deceit stands in the heart of those who know stealth; Nanmanikkadigai says the thief knows the misery of concealment.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows deceit settled in the thief; the quatrain shows it as his torment, which only he knows, so the thief is both the home and the sufferer of his concealment.","la":"It sets the thief's misery among sufferings known only from inside, the drunkard's, the bird's and the many-wived man's."},{"a":"nm:96","b":"k:139","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 139 says those with conduct cannot speak evil even by a slip; Nanmanikkadigai says deceitful words do not appear in the mouths of the learned even if they die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural rules out the accident, a slip; the quatrain rules out the extremity, death, so between them neither chance nor pressure draws evil speech from the good.","la":"It mirrors each good mouth with a bad one, the learned and worthy against the graceless and base, so the same kinds of word are shown present and absent.","gr":4},{"a":"nm:97","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 likens holding forth before those not of one's kind to nectar spilled in a gutter; Nanmanikkadigai says even a learned man has no standing before the unlearned and foolish.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures the waste of the speech; the quatrain measures the loss to the speaker, and pairs it with the unlearned man's loss before the fit assembly, so standing is shown to depend on the match of speaker and hearers.","la":"It makes the learned man's failure the mirror of the unlearned man's, so the list shows a mismatch failing in both directions."},{"a":"nm:97","b":"pz:14","type":"agrees","note":"Pazhamozhi 14 says fine points of learning spoken before the unlearned turn bad; Nanmanikkadigai says a learned man before the unlearned has no standing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi says the words go bad, Nanmanikkadigai that the man loses standing, so the group counts the damage to both the teaching and the teacher.","gr":3},{"a":"nm:98","b":"n:347","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 347 says a sandal of gold and gems is still for the foot, so the base are known by their deeds though wealthy; Nanmanikkadigai says the base, though bound and polished, show their dirt.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's base are dressed up by wealth, the quatrain's are scrubbed by force, and both fail, so neither fortune nor correction changes their nature.","la":"It sets the base against a gem that stays itself though soiled, so the base become the reverse of the gem, keeping their dirt as it keeps its worth."},{"a":"nm:99","b":"k:784","type":"develops","note":"Kural 784 says friendship is for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps; Nanmanikkadigai likens friends' rebuking words to the lute.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states rebuke as the purpose of friendship; the quatrain says how it should be heard, as music, so the duty of the friend is matched by the response of the one rebuked.","la":"It sets the friend's rebuke among likenesses of company good and bad, so rebuke is counted as one of the pleasures of good company."},{"a":"nm:99","b":"nm:103","type":"develops","note":"Verse 99 likens a house without a good woman to a ruin; verse 103 calls the wife the light of the house.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The book says the same thing from both sides, ruin without her and light with her, and 103 continues the chain to sons, learning and understanding, so her worth is the first link, not the whole."},{"a":"nm:100","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 380 asks what is stronger than fate, which arrives first whatever one devises; Nanmanikkadigai says deeds overrun one when earlier penance was small.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes fate's power depend on how much penance was stored before, which limits the Kural's unqualified claim for fate.","la":"It sets karma's overrunning beside water spilling from a small tank and enemies overrunning the weak, so fate becomes one more force that breaks a container too small for it."},{"a":"nm:101","b":"k:754","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 754 says rightly gained wealth yields both dharma and pleasure; Nanmanikkadigai gives pleasure to wealth and dharma to softened grace.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural lets good wealth bear dharma, the quatrain assigns dharma to grace alone, which separates the two that the couplet joins.","la":"It lays out a table of causes in which wealth and grace sit side by side with different fruits, so dharma is pointedly not among wealth's results."},{"a":"nm:101","b":"nm:104","type":"develops","note":"Verse 101 says reviling yields reproach; verse 104 says harsh speech yields a reviling mind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the book places the reproach first in others' mouths and then in the speaker's own mind, so harsh speech damages the speaker twice."},{"a":"nm:102","b":"k:503","type":"develops","note":"Kural 503 says even the learned and flawless are rarely without blankness when examined; Nanmanikkadigai says no one knows everything and no one knows nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural speaks of the best; the quatrain adds the worst, so knowledge is never total and ignorance never total, which leaves everyone open to examination.","la":"It pairs no one all-knowing with no one all-ignorant, and the same for virtue and fault, so the list denies both extremes."},{"a":"nm:102","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"Kural 504 bids one weigh virtues and faults and take what predominates; Nanmanikkadigai says no one has faults that hide all virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain supplies the ground for the Kural's method: since no one is pure fault, there are always virtues to weigh against the faults.","la":"It makes the mixed nature of people a general law beside the limits of knowledge, which is what the Kural's weighing needs."},{"a":"nm:103","b":"k:60","type":"develops","note":"Kural 60 calls a home's excellence its blessing and good children its ornament; Nanmanikkadigai makes the wife the light of the house, sons hers, learning theirs and understanding learning's.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural stops at children; the quatrain continues the chain to learning and understanding, so the household's worth is finally measured by knowledge.","la":"It turns the couplet's pair into a chain of four lights, each lit by the next."},{"a":"nm:104","b":"sp:89","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 89 has nearly the same lines: sweet speech makes kin, harsh speech enmity, gentle speech grace, and grace release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The one variant matters: Sirupanchamoolam has harsh speech make enmity (பகைமை), Nanmanikkadigai a reviling mind (வசைமனம்), which moves the damage from one's relations to one's own mind."},{"a":"nm:104","b":"k:96","type":"develops","note":"Kural 96 says sweet speech of good things makes dharma grow; Nanmanikkadigai traces gentle speech through a mind of grace to release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural carries sweet speech to dharma in this life; the quatrain carries it through grace to வீடு, so speech becomes the first step of release.","la":"It makes speech the start of a chain ending in release, with harsh speech as the branch that turns the mind to blame."},{"a":"pz:1","b":"nm:92","type":"develops","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 92 lists not learning in youth as one of four faults; Pazhamozhi 1 says why it is a fault: the chance does not return in old age.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse names the fault and moves on; the proverb supplies its logic, that learning is a toll only collectable while the road is still ahead.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:1","b":"n:135","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 135 says learners have few days and so must choose what to learn; Pazhamozhi 1 locates those days in youth and says that once past they cannot be used at all.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Together they make two limits on learning, a short life that forces choice and a closing season of youth after which no choice remains.","la":"its proverb of toll and wages turns the quatrain's shortness of days into a deadline, a payment that lapses once the work is past."},{"a":"pz:2","b":"k:1110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1110 uses the learner's growing sense of ignorance as a simile for love; Pazhamozhi 2 states the same thing of learning itself and makes it the engine of study.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural borrows from learning a fact Pazhamozhi takes as a method: the lament 'I have not learnt' drives the next round of pondering and labour.","la":"it gives the simile's base in its own right and adds the practice it drives, reflection on one thing at a time, which the love couplet has no room for."},{"a":"pz:2","b":"nm:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 28 says that as one learns ignorance lessens, step by step toward truth; Pazhamozhi 2 says the earnest learner finds himself the more unlearned at each step.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, ignorance shrinks in fact while the learner's sense of it grows, so the Nanmanikkadigai's ladder is climbed only by one who feels farther from the top."},{"a":"pz:3","b":"n:140","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 140 says worldly learning without the books of wisdom is clamour that removes no confusion; Pazhamozhi 3 compares learning that leaves confusion to a lamp bought that gives darkness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says which learning fails to clear confusion; the proverb adds the cost, that such learning is a price paid for darkness.","la":"its lamp-buying image frames learning as a purchase to be tested before paying, which the quatrain's judgement of book against book does not."},{"a":"pz:4","b":"k:397","type":"develops","note":"Kural 397 says every land and town is the learned man's own; Pazhamozhi 4 says the same and closes with the proverb that he needs no food for the road.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states belonging and asks why anyone stays unlearned; Pazhamozhi turns belonging into maintenance, the learned eat wherever they go.","la":"the proverb carries the couplet's claim of belonging into the traveller's practical case, provision for the journey."},{"a":"pz:5","b":"k:414","type":"tension","note":"Kural 414 offers listening to one who has not studied, the ஆயினும் keeping study first; Pazhamozhi 5 says outright that hearing is better than learning.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two rank the same pair in opposite order: the Kural makes hearing the fallback for the unlearned, Pazhamozhi makes it the cure for the learned man's narrowness.","la":"its frog-in-the-well image gives a reason for ranking hearing above study, that one's own text is a single well, which the couplet lacks."},{"a":"pz:5","b":"k:411","type":"develops","note":"Kural 411 ranks the wealth of the ear first among all wealth; Pazhamozhi 5 ranks hearing above learning itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks hearing against wealth, Pazhamozhi against study, so together they set listening at the head of both outer and inner goods.","la":"it moves the ranking from wealth to learning and names what hearing cures, the well-frog's belief that its own water is the only water."},{"a":"pz:6","b":"k:698","type":"develops","note":"Kural 698 tells those who serve a king not to belittle him as young; Pazhamozhi 6 shows elders doing just that and the young Chola answering them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the rule to the courtier; the story shows the king's side, meeting contempt for his youth with a disguise and a sound judgement.","la":"it supplies the episode and the cause of the king's competence, his family's craft, which the courtier's rule leaves unstated."},{"a":"pz:6","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 195 says austerity, learning and effort make a lineage; Pazhamozhi 6 says a lineage gives half its craft without learning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair disagree on which way the gift runs: for the Naladiyar learning makes the family, for Pazhamozhi the family half-supplies the learning.","la":"its named royal story stakes the claim for inherited capacity on a king's judgement, against the quatrain's denial that birth names anything.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:6","b":"nm:64","type":"qualifies","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 64 says the world passes over the elder who grew old unlearned and praises the young one who learned; Pazhamozhi 6 also has a young man outdo his elders, but by birth, not study.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both set the able young against the doubting old, but the counted work credits learning and Pazhamozhi credits lineage, so youth's claim rests on two different grounds."},{"a":"pz:7","b":"k:717","type":"develops","note":"Kural 717 says the learning of the learned shines among those able to examine words faultlessly; Pazhamozhi 7 says it is visible only to the very learned and closed to common people.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural names the audience where learning shines; Pazhamozhi adds that outside it learning is not dimmer but unseen, as a snake's feet are hidden from all but snakes.","la":"its proverb makes judgement of learning a matter of kind knowing kind, and shuts out the common people the couplet does not mention.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:8","b":"pz:7","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 7 says only the learned know the learned; 8 says the goodness of the good is known only by those better than they.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the claim tightens from peer to superior: learning can be judged by equals, but goodness only by one who exceeds it."},{"a":"pz:9","b":"n:256","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 256 says the learned keep silent for fear of a lapse while others chatter, like green and dry palm leaves; Pazhamozhi 9 says those ignorant of the learned man's restraint praise themselves, like a pot that is not full.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the learned man's motive for silence; Pazhamozhi names what the noisy lack, knowledge of that restraint and attention to themselves, so the two halves of the contrast each get a cause.","la":"it narrows the others' chatter to self-praise and traces it to heedlessness, which the leaf image leaves unexplained.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:9","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"Kural 978 says greatness bows while smallness adorns itself with self-admiration; Pazhamozhi 9 ties self-praise to not knowing the restraint of the learned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets self-admiration against greatness as a trait; Pazhamozhi makes it a symptom of shallow learning, so boasting becomes evidence of what one has not learnt.","la":"it moves the claim from greatness in general to learning and gives the full-pot proof that the deeply filled make no noise."},{"a":"pz:10","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"Kural 957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the spot on the moon; Pazhamozhi 10 uses the same image for the misdeed of a learned man known in his town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The image of the moon's spot passes from birth to learning, so the two together make visibility of fault the cost of any eminence, inherited or earned.","la":"it carries the couplet's image from lineage to the learned who live conspicuously, and makes their breach of their own texts the fault seen."},{"a":"pz:11","b":"k:414","type":"develops","note":"Kural 414 offers listening as the help left to one who has not studied; Pazhamozhi 11 names study and listening as the two routes and condemns the man who has taken neither.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show listening as the fallback the Kural concedes and Pazhamozhi makes the minimum, so the man without even that has only luck left.","la":"it sets study and hearing side by side as two routes and adds a proverb that strips credit from any lucky hit.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:11","b":"n:270","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 270 says those with understanding are as the learned though they have not studied; Pazhamozhi 11 says one who has neither studied nor listened does not understand clearly and is right only by chance. The conflict holds on the reading that 270's உணர்வுடையார் need no study or hearing at all.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair splits on whether native understanding can stand in for learning: the Naladiyar lets inner sense make a man learned, Pazhamozhi treats an untaught man's right judgement as a windfall.","la":"its proverb turns the untaught man's success into chance, the very case the quatrain credits to inner understanding.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:12","b":"k:405","type":"develops","note":"Both say the unlearned man's standing falters (சோர்வு படும்) when he speaks; Pazhamozhi 12 places it before the learned and gives the cause through its proverb.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the faltering bare and leaves the hearer unnamed; Pazhamozhi supplies the learned hearers and the mechanism, the questions every claim must meet.","la":"the proverb explains why the speech falters: among the learned every statement must survive a question it has never faced.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:12","b":"pz:13","type":"develops","note":"The two verses open with the same line on the unlearned man's fine point; 12 has it falter when spoken, 13 has it count for nothing when unspoken.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they form a dilemma: speaking exposes the unlearned man's insight and silence erases it, so without learning there is no way for it to count.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:13","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 403 calls the unlearned very good if they keep silent before the learned; Pazhamozhi 13 says silence leaves their insight unshown and so gives no ground for thinking themselves good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ironic praise of the silent unlearned is limited by Pazhamozhi: silence spares them shame but cannot make them one of the good.","la":"its proverb makes silence a nullity, so the goodness the couplet grants the silent unlearned is goodness with nothing in it.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:13","b":"n:254","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 254 says the unlearned man among the wise is a dog sitting if silent and a dog barking if he speaks; Pazhamozhi 13 says his unspoken insight is nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both refuse the unlearned any good way to be present among the learned, the Naladiyar by contempt for his person, Pazhamozhi by an argument that an unshown insight is no insight.","la":"it argues from the proverb on speech rather than from an insulting image, so the denial rests on how insight is shown, not on what the man is.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:14","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 likens holding forth before those not of one's own group to nectar spilled in a dirty yard; Pazhamozhi 14 says learned speech before the unlearned turns good to bad, as a city is a forest to one without kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both measure an audience by kinship (தங்கணத்தார், தமர்), but the Kural's nectar is only wasted while Pazhamozhi's good words become harmful, which makes the wrong audience an active danger.","la":"its proverb casts the speaker, not the speech, as the one stranded, a man among strangers in a city.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:14","b":"n:255","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 255 has the worthy keep their learning unspoken, even when asked, where it will not be taken in; Pazhamozhi 14 gives the cost of speaking there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar gives the practice, silence even when asked, and Pazhamozhi gives the reason, that good words among the unlearned turn bad.","la":"it names the outcome the worthy avoid, the reversal of good into bad, which the quatrain leaves implicit.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:15","b":"k:719","type":"develops","note":"Kural 719 says those who speak well in a good assembly should not speak in a mean one, even by slip; Pazhamozhi 15 calls fine speech among the unlearned the worst thing for a man and a lapse of conduct.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Pazhamozhi moves the Kural's rule of assembly into the field of ozhukkam, so speaking to the wrong audience is a fault of character that lowers the speaker, not only a tactical error.","la":"it ranks the act as the worst of things for a man and proves it with the conduct maxim, which the couplet does not invoke.","gr":4},{"a":"pz:15","b":"k:137","type":"develops","note":"Kural 137 says conduct brings high standing and lapses bring blame; Pazhamozhi 15 uses the same ozhukkam and izhukkam pair as a proverb to judge speech among the unlearned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the Kural's conduct couplet circulating as a proverb and being applied to a case the couplet never names, the choice of whom to speak before.","la":"it applies the conduct maxim to a particular act, eloquence in the wrong company, and so widens what counts as a lapse.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:16","b":"k:402","type":"develops","note":"Kural 402 likens the unlearned man's desire to speak to a desire for womanhood in one without breasts; Pazhamozhi 16 likens his challenge to the learned to a lame man wanting to play with an elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both frame the unlearned man's ambition as desire without the faculty, but Pazhamozhi moves it from wanting to speak to picking a fight, where the lame man's wish also courts danger.","la":"its image adds danger to incapacity, since an elephant can crush the lame man who wants to play.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:16","b":"n:312","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 312 has the bad scholar, worsted in assembly, slap his shoulders and rise to fight; Pazhamozhi 16 has the unlearned provoke the learned and rise itching for a contest.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Naladiyar shows the fight as what follows defeat, Pazhamozhi as what the unlearned man starts; together they mark aggression as the unlearned man's response to learning before and after the contest.","la":"its proverb judges the challenge hopeless from the start, while the quatrain warns the disciplined to keep away.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:17","b":"k:711","type":"develops","note":"Kural 711 bids the speaker know the assembly and examine the matter before speaking; Pazhamozhi 17 names what is to be known, the hearers' desire about the matter, and calls speech that misses it a losing case.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, knowing the assembly turns out to mean reading its appetite: the Kural asks for discernment, and Pazhamozhi says that discernment is of desire, so a true speech the hearers do not want is already lost.","la":"its bee image and proverb make the hearers' want the measure and recast unsuited speech as a defeat carried into the contest."},{"a":"pz:19","b":"k:715","type":"develops","note":"Kural 715 praises the restraint of not speaking first among elders; Pazhamozhi 19 applies the same rule of order to a dispute two have begun, where the later speaker pushes ahead of his turn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Kural's rule of precedence is also a rule of relevance: speaking out of turn is not only immodest but misses the point at issue, as the bandage misses the torn knee.","la":"the proverb gives a reason the couplet lacks: speech before its turn lands on the wrong place."},{"a":"pz:20","b":"k:729","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 729 holds the learned who fear an assembly of the good lower than the unlearned; Pazhamozhi 20 advises the learned to fear the unlearned who bandy words among them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's demand for fearlessness in assembly has an exception: before the good, fear is a failing, but before the shameless unlearned, against whom even gods are powerless, wariness is wisdom.","la":"it names the one opponent in an assembly a learned man should fear, and its proverb grounds that fear in shamelessness rather than skill."},{"a":"pz:22","b":"n:314","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 314's fool, with one overheard sutra, walks unabashed (நாணாது) into the gathering of the good and shows his petty understanding; Pazhamozhi 22's man, knowing neither honour nor shame, sits among the wise and questions them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both trace the intrusion to a lack of shame rather than of learning alone, and Pazhamozhi adds that the shameless man need not even recite: questioning the wise is enough to make him laughable.","la":"it pairs மானம் with நாண் as what is missing and turns the exposure into ridicule through its proverb."},{"a":"pz:23","b":"n:255","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 255 praises the worthy who will not speak their learning even when asked, knowing it would not be taken in; Pazhamozhi 23 shows the chatterer scorning those whose learning keeps their tongues in check.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair exposes the cost of the worthy's silence that the Naladiyar praises: the low read restraint as emptiness and mock it, so the learned man's silence protects his learning but not his standing.","la":"it sets the restrained learned and the chattering intruder in one scene, so the silence the quatrain commends is seen from the scoffer's side."},{"a":"pz:24","b":"k:719","type":"develops","note":"Kural 719 tells the able speaker not to speak in a mean assembly even by slip; Pazhamozhi 24 condemns the man who does the reverse, silent before the good and boastful before the mean.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair fixes both halves of the rule: the Kural forbids good speech wasted on the mean, Pazhamozhi shows the inverted vice, where the mean assembly gets self-praise and the good assembly gets nothing.","la":"it joins silence among the good and self-praise among the mean as one fault and gives it a single image of cowardice."},{"a":"pz:24","b":"k:727","type":"develops","note":"Kural 727 likens the learning of one who fears the assembly to a bright sword in a பேடி's hand amid enemies; Pazhamozhi 24 likens the same fear to a man who strings his bow at home and shoots a pot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two martial images complete each other: the Kural shows the weapon useless in the field, Pazhamozhi shows it used where no enemy is, so fear of the assembly is both impotence before the learned and bravado before the mean.","la":"it adds the self-praise in a mean assembly to the fear the couplet names, so the coward's display is seen alongside his failure."},{"a":"pz:25","b":"k:720","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 720 likens holding forth before those not of one's group to nectar spilled in a dirty yard; Pazhamozhi 25 likens counsel spoken among dull men to sifting flour in the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two waste images differ in what is lost: the Kural's nectar is fouled where it falls, Pazhamozhi's flour simply vanishes, and Pazhamozhi names the hearers as fools rather than outsiders, so the loss comes from incapacity, not from difference of group.","la":"it narrows the couplet's 'those not of one's group' to the dull and complacent and makes the counsel itself (உறுதி), not display, what is lost."},{"a":"pz:25","b":"in:10","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 10 counts, as its first painful thing, reciting a song where none grasp its meaning as painful; Pazhamozhi 25 says counsel spoken among dull men is lost like flour sifted in the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The counted work names the speaker's pain and the proverb names the speech's loss, so together they show that speaking to those who cannot take it in hurts the speaker and achieves nothing for the hearer."},{"a":"pz:18","b":"pz:19","type":"develops","note":"Verse 18 forbids answering back so that an exchange becomes a two-sided quarrel; verse 19 forbids pushing ahead of one's turn in an exchange two have begun.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read in sequence the two verses give a small order of debate in assembly: do not turn a speech into a contest by retort, and do not break into a contest out of turn."},{"a":"pz:26","b":"n:131","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 131 says the beauty of hair, garment border and saffron is not beauty and only learning's is; Pazhamozhi 26 keeps the ornaments but makes understanding the garment they must follow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain discards outward adornment, while the Pazhamozhi verse keeps it and ranks it, so outer graces are not worthless but parasitic on the inner one.","la":"its string of jewels, sandal and garlands is not dismissed but placed in an order, ornaments after the cloth, which the quatrain's flat denial lacks."},{"a":"pz:26","b":"k:846","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 846 calls it folly to cover one's nakedness while leaving faults uncovered; Pazhamozhi 26 makes understanding the cloth without which ornaments are absurd.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both use clothing to name what must be covered first, and together they make the senseless man's lack a nakedness that neither dress nor jewellery can hide.","la":"it sets gems, gold, sandal and garlands on the naked body, turning the couplet's single garment into a whole toilette that is wasted without understanding."},{"a":"pz:27","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"Kural 410 sets the learned against others as men against beasts; Pazhamozhi 27 says a thousand of the senseless together do not equal one excellent man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the gap one of kind; the Pazhamozhi verse draws the consequence that numbers cannot close it, so a crowd of fools adds up to nothing.","la":"its moon and many stars turn the couplet's two-term comparison into one against a multitude, adding the argument from number."},{"a":"pz:28","b":"k:848","type":"develops","note":"Kural 848 describes the man who neither does what he is told nor sees for himself; Pazhamozhi 28 says the senseless cannot be set firm and gives as the condition that learning has not gone in. The link rests on an uncertain reading of 28.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the double failure of the unteachable, and the Pazhamozhi verse places its cause earlier, in learning that never entered, so telling fails because nothing is there to receive it.","la":"its image of the drum that answers a spoken cue supplies the responsive learner the couplet leaves out, so the fool is measured against an instrument."},{"a":"pz:29","b":"k:678","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 678 and Pazhamozhi 29 close on the same proverb, binding an elephant with an elephant; the Kural applies it to using one task to achieve another, Pazhamozhi to winning a wise man through a wise man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows one proverb serving two ethics: in the Kural a rule of method for tasks, in Pazhamozhi a rule of persons, that only an equal can draw in an equal.","la":"it moves the elephant image from the ordering of tasks to the gaining of people, and names the kings who practise the capture."},{"a":"pz:30","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 says speaking before those not of one's own kind is nectar spilt in a dirty yard; Pazhamozhi 30 says the discerning do not even enter among those who have no regard for them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet counts the waste after one has spoken in the wrong company; the Pazhamozhi verse moves the judgement earlier, to not going in at all, and makes that avoidance itself a mark of understanding.","la":"its jackal that keeps out of the reapers' sight turns the couplet's rule of speech into a rule of where to be seen, with the danger as well as the waste."},{"a":"pz:30","b":"k:500","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the jackal as the creature that reads its ground: in Kural 500 it kills the elephant stuck in mud, in Pazhamozhi 30 it keeps out of the reapers' sight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the jackal's cunning has two faces, striking where the ground favours it and vanishing where it does not, and the Pazhamozhi verse gives the second to the wise.","la":"it turns the Kural's lesson of place in war into a lesson of social company, applied to the discerning person rather than the commander."},{"a":"pz:32","b":"n:105","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 32 says those of wide strength cannot be hidden, as no hand hides the sun; Naladiyar 105 shows the palm-tall great shrinking to millet size and hiding their brightness through former deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair limits the proverb's confidence: no man can cover real greatness, but the Naladiyar shows it dwindling from within through old deeds, so it is safe from suppression, not from fate.","la":"its moss and raised hand make concealment a deliberate act by others, which separates it from the self-hiding the quatrain attributes to deeds.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:33","b":"k:400","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 400 calls learning the imperishable wealth and says other things are not wealth; Pazhamozhi 33 grants that jewels and riches do return to the fortunate, and ranks understanding above them because it never leaves.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural denies other goods the name of wealth, the Pazhamozhi verse concedes their worth and their power to come back, and rests understanding's superiority on its independence from fortune.","la":"it names the rival goods, priceless jewels and wealth, and gives them a real but conditional permanence, so the ranking is argued rather than asserted."},{"a":"pz:33","b":"pz:26","type":"develops","note":"Both set understanding against ornaments: in 26 ornaments come after the cloth of understanding, in 33 priceless jewels come and go with fortune while understanding stays.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the chapter makes jewels doubly inferior to understanding, dependent on it for their sense and on fortune for their keeping.","la":"33 adds time to 26's order of dressing, so the ornament is not only secondary but unstable.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:32","b":"pz:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both prove excellence by a light in the sky: in 27 the moon that the many stars cannot equal, in 32 the sun that no hand can cover.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two sky proverbs give the excellent man two properties at once, that a crowd cannot outshine him and a hand cannot put him out.","la":"32 turns 27's contest of brightness into one of concealment, so the image moves from comparison to suppression."},{"a":"pz:34","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 133 makes conduct itself the substance of noble birth and its lapse low birth; Pazhamozhi 34 keeps old lineage as a good already held and makes conduct an addition upon it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together they show two models of lineage: in the Kural birth is earned by conduct, in Pazhamozhi conduct enriches a birth already counted as excellent.","la":"its proverb of milk upon ghee makes conduct a second richness laid on lineage, a layering the couplet's identity of the two removes."},{"a":"pz:34","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 195 says good and bad lineage are words with nothing behind them; Pazhamozhi 34 treats excellent descent from an old line as a real distinction. The conflict holds on reading 34's விழுத்தொடையர் and தொல் வந்தார் as birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the ethics group divided on whether lineage is a fact before conduct: the Jain quatrain empties the word, the proverb collection banks on it.","la":"its abundance image treats birth and conduct as two goods to be summed, where the quatrain allows only austerity, learning and effort to make a line."},{"a":"pz:35","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"Kural 423 says wisdom is seeing the true substance of what is heard from whatever mouth; Pazhamozhi 35 forbids scorning a good word from whatever mouth and proves it by low sources of prized things.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the indifference to the speaker as a test of wisdom; Pazhamozhi shows its price, even the strictest abstainer taking what a dog has caught.","la":"it gives three low sources of good things, the spurge, the crow and the dog, and ends on a breach of a birth-group's diet as the extreme case."},{"a":"pz:36","b":"k:505","type":"develops","note":"Kural 505 makes one's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; Pazhamozhi 36 tells the hearer not to be like the small and to walk by his own conduct, which none knows apart from him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the touchstone; Pazhamozhi places it inside the man, so the one who must test the deeds first is the doer.","la":"it sets the small man's three failures (not watching himself, not knowing his forebears, not joining the right path) before turning the touchstone on the hearer."},{"a":"pz:37","b":"k:849","type":"develops","note":"Kural 849 says one who shows things to the unseeing becomes unseeing himself while the other keeps what he saw; Pazhamozhi 37 says even the subtly learned cannot turn back one who has taken a wrong course.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural mocks the would-be teacher; Pazhamozhi removes the blame from him, since even the wisest fail when the pupil's resolve is fixed like a drum's sound.","la":"it raises the adviser to the great and the keenly learned to show the failure is not his, and its drum gives the cause: a mind sounds only what it has resolved."},{"a":"pz:38","b":"k:436","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 436 says a ruler who removes his own fault and then sees others' has no fault; Pazhamozhi 38 uses the same pair of own fault and others' fault to mock anyone who corrects others first.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Held together, the Kural's condition for a king's just correction becomes in Pazhamozhi a rule for anyone, and the reversed order is made ridiculous rather than unjust.","la":"it carries the king's condition into common life and adds the goat that cures others' wind and not its own, turning a rule into a jest."},{"a":"pz:39","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"Kural 656 forbids a deed the worthy condemn even to feed one's starving mother; Pazhamozhi 39 forbids a scarring deed even to save oneself from ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural tests the rule against duty to another, Pazhamozhi against self-preservation, and gives the reason both leave out: the word about the deed outlasts stone.","la":"its proverb supplies the reason behind the refusal, the lasting report, which the couplet leaves as a bare command."},{"a":"pz:39","b":"k:129","type":"develops","note":"Kural 129 says the scar (வடு) burned by the tongue never heals; Pazhamozhi 39 says one must do nothing that leaves a scar (வடு) on oneself, since a word does not wear away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair turns the tongue's unhealing scar both ways: the Kural's falls on the one spoken to, Pazhamozhi's on the one spoken of, and both last for the same reason.","la":"it moves the scar from the wound of harsh speech to the reproach a deed earns, and sets it against stone for duration."},{"a":"pz:40","b":"n:189","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 189 says the pure in heart stay unshaken by cruel slander like a flame in a lamp; Pazhamozhi 40 says undeserved reproach is a disease that, untreated by excellence of character, destroys a man.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Naladiyar makes the pure immune to slander; Pazhamozhi denies immunity and asks for treatment, so worth must act against blame rather than merely survive it.","la":"its medical frame of disease, medicine and wasting gives reproach a course and a cure, where the quatrain gives only the steady flame."},{"a":"pz:40","b":"pz:41","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 40 makes reproach a disease that wastes the man it strikes; 41 says great reproach does not exist for those who do not heed it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show that blame wounds only those who care for their name, so the disease of verse 40 is itself a mark of the man worth curing."},{"a":"pz:42","b":"n:189","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 189 sets the pure heart unshaken against cruel slander; Pazhamozhi 42 says the evil tongue never runs short and spares not even a god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi shows how far slander reaches, even to Mal's saving act, which is why the Naladiyar's steadiness must hold against slander that cannot be avoided.","la":"its example of a god called a cowherd shows slander turning a saving act into a slight on rank, a case beyond the quatrain's general slanderer."},{"a":"pz:43","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"Kural 320 says all pains rest on those who caused pain; Pazhamozhi 43 uses the same word, நோய், in its proverb and applies it to the man who hurts someone because they will forgive.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the rebound of pain is independent of the victim's response: forgiveness on one side does not stop the return of pain on the other.","la":"it names the excuse the couplet does not, that the victim will bear it, and makes gods and the steadfast fall under the same rule."},{"a":"pz:44","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"Kural 316 forbids doing to another what one has felt to be painful; Pazhamozhi 44 gives the same self-measure as its proverb and names what blocks it, the sense of the other as different (வேற்றுமை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the self-measure of pain is the remedy and the sense of otherness is the disease: the Kural gives the rule, Pazhamozhi names what makes people break it.","la":"it adds the cause of the harm, a felt difference from the other, and narrows the case to harm with no gain at all.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:45","b":"n:213","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 213 holds up the dog that wags its tail with its master's spear in its body as the loyal friend; Pazhamozhi 45 says even a dog shut in and beaten bites its owner.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same animal marks the limit of loyalty: the Naladiyar praises the dog's endurance of injury, Pazhamozhi shows that cornering and steady abuse break even that endurance.","la":"its proverb adds the condition, the shut door and repeated beating, under which the quatrain's model of loyal endurance fails."},{"a":"pz:45","b":"pz:49","type":"same_image","note":"Both proverbs of this heading turn on a dog's bite: in 45 the abused dog bites its master, in 49 the worthy man does not bite back the dog that bites him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heading uses the dog from both sides, as the weak who will strike back when cornered and as the base whom the worthy will not answer, so the same bite warns the strong and restrains the worthy."},{"a":"pz:46","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"Kural 319 says hurts done to others in the forenoon come back in the afternoon; Pazhamozhi 46's proverb keeps the forenoon and afternoon but has the man only see (கண்டான்) another's ruin, and addresses the heart.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they move the rebound from the deed to the inner wish: the Kural times the return of harm done, Pazhamozhi applies the same timing to a cruel thought voiced in the heart.","la":"addressing the rebuke to the heart and using 'saw' for 'did' extends the couplet's rule from acts to wishes."},{"a":"pz:46","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 204 says that if one plots another's ruin, virtue plots the ruin of the plotter; Pazhamozhi 46 says one who looks on another's ruin sees his own soon after, and rebukes the heart for a cruel word.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make inner intent enough to set the return in motion, but the Kural names virtue as the agent while Pazhamozhi names no agent and only the speed of the return.","la":"it sets the counsel inside a dialogue with one's own heart and swaps the Kural's agent, அறம், for a bare law of time."},{"a":"pz:47","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"Kural 555 makes the tears of those who cannot bear their affliction the weapon that wears away a ruler's wealth; Pazhamozhi 47 uses the same phrase of tears wept unable to bear it, and makes them Death to any strong oppressor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the victims' tears pass from wearing down a king's wealth to killing the oppressor, and from the king's subjects to four named kinds of the defenceless anyone strong might torment.","la":"it lists four kinds of victims, the ill-favoured, the friendless, the poor and those taken as foes for a word, where the couplet names none."},{"a":"pz:47","b":"k:894","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 894 says the weak doing harm to the strong is beckoning Death; Pazhamozhi 47 says the strong tormenting the weak find the victims' tears fall on them as Death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair makes Death the outcome on both sides of an unequal fight: the weak who strike the strong invite it by their act, the strong who strike the weak receive it from their victims' tears.","la":"it reverses the couplet's direction, making the strong the doer and the weak's tears, not the strong man's arm, the bearer of Death.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:48","b":"k:892","type":"develops","note":"Kural 892 says disregard of the great brings ills through the great, with the conduct itself as agent; Pazhamozhi 48's image of the hand hurt by pinching rock makes the same point that the great need not act.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The proverb's mountain gives the couplet's grammar an image: the great do nothing and the attacker's hand is hurt by its own act against something harder, however easy the attack looks.","la":"it adds the apparent ease of the attack (நற்கெளி தாகி விடினும்) and a proverb that shows why the ease deceives."},{"a":"pz:49","b":"n:70","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 70 and Pazhamozhi 49 share the proverb that no one bites back a dog that bites; the Naladiyar applies it to low words, Pazhamozhi to harm done (நலிதல்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared proverb carries non-retaliation from insult to injury: the Naladiyar asks the high not to answer low words, Pazhamozhi asks the worthy not to answer actual torment.","la":"it moves the proverb from speech to deed and names the offender's lack of worth (நீர்த்தகவு இல்லார்) as the reason the worthy hold back.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:49","b":"k:312","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 312 says not hurting in return even those who hurt in hatred is the stand (கோள்) of the stainless; Pazhamozhi 49 uses the same word, கோள், for the worthy's refusal to retaliate but grounds it in the offender's likeness to a dog.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two give the same rule different grounds: the Kural states non-retaliation as purity of the stainless, Pazhamozhi bases it on the offender's lowness, so the restraint becomes a mark of rank.","la":"its proverb ranks the offender with a dog, a contempt the couplet does not express, and limits the case to offenders lacking worth.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:50","b":"k:158","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 158 says those who wrong one out of excess are to be defeated by one's own rightness; Pazhamozhi 50 says the superior should not rise against the low who wrong him at all, since it is a great weapon spent on a jackal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet still frames forbearance as a way of winning, while Pazhamozhi treats the contest with the low as not worth entering, so forbearance becomes economy of the superior's strength rather than victory.","la":"its proverb sets a measure of proportion, great means against a small target, that the couplet's language of victory does not.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:50","b":"pz:49","type":"develops","note":"Both verses forbid the worthy to answer the low in kind; 49 says it is not their way, 50 says it wastes their strength.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Side by side the two give two separate reasons for the same restraint: retaliation would lower the worthy to the dog's act (49), and it would spend great means on a little jackal (50)."},{"a":"pz:51","b":"n:68","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 68 says the worthy's anger cools of itself like heat taken on by boiled water; Pazhamozhi 51 uses the same heated water to say the well-born's heat, while it lasts, cannot burn the house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the boiled-water image answers two questions: the Naladiyar says the worthy's anger does not last, Pazhamozhi says it does no harm even at its height.","la":"its proverb shifts the image from the cooling of the heat to the house that survives it, so harmlessness is added to brevity."},{"a":"pz:51","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from deeds; Pazhamozhi 51 credits freedom from anger to those excellent by birth (பிறப்பினால் மாண்டார்). The tension holds on reading பிறப்பு in 51 as lineage rather than nature.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two accounts of where restraint comes from: the Kural makes worth follow what one does, while Pazhamozhi makes a virtue of conduct the mark of a good birth.","la":"it sets the great by birth against the very small and makes forbearance the sign that separates them, the ranking the couplet refuses."},{"a":"pz:52","b":"n:71","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 71 says to speak nothing to a fool and slip away; Pazhamozhi 52 gives the same withdrawal as the answer to a man of unquenchable anger and grounds it in his untrustworthiness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show that withdrawal, not endurance, is the counsel for two cases, the fool and the man of lasting anger, and Pazhamozhi supplies the reason: neither can be trusted with one's words.","la":"it carries the quatrain's withdrawal from the fool to the man of lasting anger and adds a proverb that names distrust as the ground."},{"a":"pz:52","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"Kural 510 warns against trusting one not examined; Pazhamozhi 52 names unquenchable anger as a sign that a man fails the test and is to be avoided.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing turns the Kural's general rule on examined trust into a usable test, since a man whose anger never cools shows by it that he is never clear and so not to be trusted.","la":"it names one observable trait, anger that never cools, as the mark of the untrustworthy that the couplet leaves unspecified.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:53","b":"k:158","type":"develops","note":"Kural 158 says to defeat those who wrong one in excess by one's own fitness; Pazhamozhi 53 says the long feud dies of itself as grass does when paddy is grown.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states that rightness defeats excess and Pazhamozhi shows how: like a crop crowding out weeds, one's own good work starves the enmity without any act against the enemy.","la":"its farming proverb gives the mechanism, crowding out rather than fighting, that the couplet's language of victory leaves unstated."},{"a":"pz:53","b":"sp:15","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 15 calls bearing a wrong greatness and counts living so that enmity ends as good; Pazhamozhi 53 says how the enmity ends: by tending one's own good rather than by force.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse names ending a feud as a good to aim at, and Pazhamozhi shows that the aim is reached by not aiming at the enemy at all, since the feud withers on its own."},{"a":"pz:54","b":"k:995","type":"develops","note":"Kural 995 says scorn hurts even in jest; Pazhamozhi 54 adds that the hearer takes the jest as abuse and the harm returns on the jester, like a rat burnt by the lamp it seized.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural looks at the pain a jest gives the hearer, Pazhamozhi at the anger it rouses and the cost to the jester, so together they show a mocking word wounding both ends.","la":"its proverb turns the couplet's concern for the hearer into a warning about self-harm, the rat burnt by its own theft.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:55","b":"n:72","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 72 bids silence before the unfit words of those not one's equals because the world counts a retort as disgrace; Pazhamozhi 55 bids the same silence because silencing strangers is as futile as bolting a public hall.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair gives two different grounds for one silence, reputation in the Naladiyar and futility in Pazhamozhi, which shows the counsel can rest on prudence alone without appeal to honour.","la":"its proverb replaces the quatrain's appeal to the world's judgement with the plain impossibility of shutting an open hall."},{"a":"pz:56","b":"k:185","type":"develops","note":"Kural 185 says backbiting exposes the meanness of the backbiter; Pazhamozhi 56 says the target of hired lies has no cause for grief, since the charge is false.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two verses show slander's weight falling wholly on the speaker: the Kural says it exposes him, Pazhamozhi that it leaves the innocent target nothing to grieve.","la":"it adds the slanderer's motive, food from one's enemies, and the victim's side, which the couplet on the backbiter does not consider."},{"a":"pz:57","b":"n:70","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 70 says no one bites back a raging dog; Pazhamozhi 57 says no one gores back a cow. Both make a beast of the insulter to show that retaliation is beneath a person.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two images differ in what they make of the insulter: the Naladiyar's biting dog is vicious, Pazhamozhi's goring cow is merely artless, so the unlearned are excused where the low are condemned.","la":"it puts a cow in the dog's place and the unlearned in the place of the low, softening the judgement on the offender."},{"a":"pz:57","b":"k:153","type":"develops","note":"Kural 153 calls bearing with fools the strength of strengths; Pazhamozhi 57 says the learned do not retort to the unlearned, for no one gores back a cow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural calls forbearance toward fools a strength, and Pazhamozhi gives the ground of that strength: a learned man who retorts makes himself the equal of an animal, so restraint keeps the rank between them.","la":"its proverb makes the fool a beast whose offence cannot be returned, a ranking the couplet's paradox of strength does not draw."},{"a":"pz:58","b":"pz:51","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 51 says the well-born's heat, like hot water, cannot burn the house; 58 says that abuse returned is the very act of setting the house on fire.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"Read side by side, the two verses draw the line between heat felt and heat spoken: anger held inside does no harm, while anger turned into a retort burns down one's own house."},{"a":"pz:58","b":"n:63","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 63 says unguarded words burn the speaker without end; Pazhamozhi 58 says returned abuse sets fire to one's own house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place the burn of an angry word on the speaker, and Pazhamozhi widens it from the speaker's self to his household, the house as the thing consumed.","la":"its proverb names the house, not the speaker's mind, as what the retort burns, widening the harm beyond the self."},{"a":"pz:59","b":"k:987","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 987 says worth is useless unless it does good even to those who did harm; Pazhamozhi 59 says worth (சால்பு) comes from enduring such people rather than punishing them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair sets two thresholds for nobility: Pazhamozhi grants சால்பு to one who merely endures the wrongdoer, while the Kural asks that the wrongdoer also be done good.","la":"it sets enduring against punishing and gives each its fruit, renown against nothing, where the couplet asks a third thing, doing good."},{"a":"pz:59","b":"n:65","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 65 says true patience is that of the strong who could punish all; Pazhamozhi 59 says punishing those who harmed one, though one could, brings no gain.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both assume the power to punish, and together they show that forbearance counts as a virtue only when retaliation is open, and then gains what punishment cannot.","la":"it adds the outcome of the forgone punishment, no gain, and of the forbearance, renown and nobility, to the quatrain's test of strength."},{"a":"pz:60","b":"k:891","type":"develops","note":"Kural 891 ranks not slighting those able to act as the highest safeguard; Pazhamozhi 60 says calling the great small is neither wisdom, grace nor knowing, and brings harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural values the restraint as self-protection; Pazhamozhi adds that the slight is also a failure of understanding, so the man who belittles the great is both unsafe and wrong about them.","la":"its threefold denial (not wisdom, not grace, not knowing) strips the slight of every merit before naming its harm, and the proverb says the slighted greatness itself survives."},{"a":"pz:60","b":"n:147","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a damaged great building that keeps its function: Naladiyar 147's decayed house still has a dry corner, Pazhamozhi 60's assembly hall stays a hall when cut into. The reading of 60's proverb is uncertain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same image works from two sides: the Naladiyar uses it for what the well-born still do in hardship, Pazhamozhi for how others must still regard the great when their standing is damaged.","la":"it turns the quatrain's image of the well-born man's own constancy into a warning to those who would belittle him.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:61","b":"k:893","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 893 says, in grim irony, that one who wants ruin should offend the able; Pazhamozhi 61 has the small man offend the great because his senses are dazed and he cannot see his death.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural frames offending the great as a choice of ruin, Pazhamozhi as blindness: held together they show the offence as willed in form and unseen in fact, the offender choosing what he cannot see.","la":"its proverb replaces the couplet's mock instruction with the dazed traveller, making the offender's fault a failure of sight rather than of will."},{"a":"pz:61","b":"n:64","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 64 says the excellent do not flare up when the low confront them with unfit words; Pazhamozhi 61 says the small who confront the great with insolence walk to their death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair separates the great man's calm from the offender's fate: the great may not burn with anger, yet the insolent small man is lost all the same, so his safety never rested on their temper.","la":"it moves the scene from the great man's response to the offender's end, with the proverb's dazed traveller as the measure of his danger."},{"a":"pz:62","b":"k:894","type":"develops","note":"Kural 894 likens the weak doing harm to the strong to beckoning Death by hand; Pazhamozhi 62 likens the unlearned provoking the great to goading an elephant from behind an erukku shrub.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's weak man calls his death openly; Pazhamozhi's thinks he is hidden, so the pair adds a second folly to the first: misjudging one's cover as well as one's strength.","la":"the proverb adds the false shelter, the thin erukku, which the couplet's open gesture lacks, and names the provokers as the unlearned."},{"a":"pz:62","b":"n:164","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 164: a snake in a rock crevice still dreads thunder, and those who anger the great are not safe in a fortress; Pazhamozhi 62: the provoker hides behind an erukku shrub and goads an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two hiding places run from the strongest to the weakest, a rock crevice and a fortress against a shrub, and both fail, so no shelter of any strength protects against the great.","la":"its proverb makes the shelter absurdly thin and the offender the one who starts the charge, where the quatrain's snake only fears."},{"a":"pz:63","b":"k:152","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 152 says to bear with excess always (என்றும்) and better to forget it; Pazhamozhi 63 asks whether a man who errs again after a first fault will be borne, and answers with the plantain that bears once.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against the Kural's 'always', Pazhamozhi sets a count: forbearance covers the first offence, and the repeated one is outside it, so the pair raises whether forbearance is unlimited or once given.","la":"the plantain proverb puts a number on forbearance, one bunch and no second, where the couplet sets no limit."},{"a":"pz:63","b":"n:161","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 161 warns not to offend even the faultless counting on their forgiveness, since once they turn away no one can undo it; Pazhamozhi 63 says a second offence after a first is not borne.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain warns against trading on forgiveness at all; Pazhamozhi shows when the trade fails, at the second offence, so together they mark forgiveness as real but single.","la":"it fixes the point at which the quatrain's turning away comes, the repetition, through the plantain that fruits once."},{"a":"pz:64","b":"k:898","type":"develops","note":"Kural 898 says those who seem to stand forever vanish with their line (குடியொடு) when the hill-like are slighted; Pazhamozhi 64 says the house (குடி) of the crooked perishes while the great only tremble and grieve.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the offender's line the casualty, but Pazhamozhi has the great grieve rather than strike, so the ruin of the family comes from the offence itself and not from the great man's wrath.","la":"it makes the offenders the great man's own long-standing people and gives the great dread and grief, where the couplet gives them only height."},{"a":"pz:64","b":"k:806","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 806 says those who keep bounds do not give up an old bond even when ruin comes; Pazhamozhi 64 has the great keep feeling for long-standing associates who go astray, yet their house perishes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the limit of old loyalty: the great do not cast the old associate off, they grieve for him, but their constancy cannot stop the ruin his crooked conduct brings.","la":"it sets the loyal great man's grief beside the old associate's ruin, so the couplet's constancy is shown to be powerless to save."},{"a":"pz:61","b":"pz:62","type":"develops","note":"Both have the small or unlearned offend the great and end in an image of unseen danger: 61 a dazed man who does not see his death, 62 a man who thinks an erukku shrub hides him from an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two proverbs name the two blindnesses of the offender: he does not see the danger at all, or he sees it and wrongly trusts a cover."},{"a":"pz:65","b":"n:340","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 340 says learning and birth win honour when others praise them but bring mockery when a man praises them himself; Pazhamozhi 65 condemns self-praise of a deed not yet accomplished.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they give two grounds against self-praise: the quatrain's man owns real merits and is still mocked for voicing them, while Pazhamozhi's man claims a result that does not exist yet, so the second fault is falsehood on top of vanity.","la":"its proverb of selling uncaught birds adds the time element the quatrain lacks: praise claimed before the work is done is a sale of what one does not have."},{"a":"pz:66","b":"n:219","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 219 ranks abuse above praise of qualities one does not have; Pazhamozhi 66 says praise must be pushed away even when it comes from one's own kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes unfit praise worse than abuse in general; Pazhamozhi carries the refusal to the hardest case, the affectionate praise of one's own people, and gives the reason in the image of a jewel that is one's own and still does not fit.","la":"the proverb's own-but-unfitting jewel names the source the quatrain leaves open, one's own kin, and makes the test fit rather than giver."},{"a":"pz:66","b":"pz:65","type":"develops","note":"65 forbids praising oneself before the deed; 66 forbids settling into praise even from one's own people.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two adjacent verses close off praise from both the self and the circle nearest the self, leaving only praise that fits the deed and comes from beyond one's own people as worth keeping."},{"a":"pz:67","b":"n:346","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 346 says the base man who gains a tiny fraction thinks himself Indra; Pazhamozhi 67 says those who extol such self-proclaimers call a hearth dog a tiger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain describes the base man's inflated self-image; Pazhamozhi turns to the bystander who repeats it, so the pair shows the boast needs a flatterer to circulate and that the flatterer shares in the absurdity.","la":"it moves the fault from the boaster to the one who praises him and concedes that such praise is harmless, a qualification the quatrain does not make."},{"a":"pz:68","b":"k:1047","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1047 says a man in poverty is looked at as a stranger even by his own mother; Pazhamozhi 68 calls it a painful wrong for a man to set a poor kinsman apart among his many kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural records estrangement from kin as one of poverty's miseries; Pazhamozhi names the kinsman who does the estranging as at fault, so the same fact appears once as the poor man's suffering and once as the kin's wrong.","la":"its proverb of a line drawn within a row makes the poor kinsman's exclusion a breach of the group's own unity, which the couplet states only as the poor man's loss."},{"a":"pz:69","b":"pz:72","type":"develops","note":"69 says the noble's light is his own and the covering will not cling; 72 says it is the discerning who see the gem through the mud.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, worth that survives misfortune needs two things: its own shine and an eye that can find it under the dirt, so the verse after 69 hands the burden from the noble to the beholder."},{"a":"pz:70","b":"n:300","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 300's tiger starves rather than eat a kill fallen on its left side; Pazhamozhi 70's tiger starves rather than graze grass. Both make the tiger's hunger the test of the excellent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two tigers refuse different things, one a proper meal improperly placed and one a meal beneath its kind, so honour is shown both in rejecting the base and in rejecting the ill-fitting.","la":"its proverb ties the refusal to poverty (ஒற்கம்) and to holding one's station, where the quatrain ties it to honour weighed against heaven."},{"a":"pz:70","b":"n:193","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 193 says a starving tiger will eat even a small frog and urges taking lowly work in need; Pazhamozhi 70 says the starving tiger will not graze grass and that the exalted keep their station in want. The conflict holds on reading 193 as counsel to stoop for a while.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same starving tiger is made to argue both ways, so the pair shows where the line falls: the tiger may stoop to small meat but not to grass, stooping within one's kind but never out of it.","la":"it makes the tiger's refusal the proof that poverty must not move the high from their station, the opposite lesson from the same animal."},{"a":"pz:71","b":"k:964","type":"tension","note":"Kural 964 says those fallen from their position are like hair fallen from the head; Pazhamozhi 71 says the great keep their worth when their standing is gone, like the timber of a fallen mansion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair splits on whether worth lies in place or in substance: the Kural's fallen hair is worthless off the head, the Pazhamozhi's timber is still building stuff off the ruin.","la":"its salvage image gives the fallen great a further use, where the couplet's hair has none once it leaves its place.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:72","b":"n:347","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 347 says a gem-set golden sandal is still for the foot, so the base are known by their deeds though rich; Pazhamozhi 72 says a gem rolled in mud is still a gem to those who know.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they make one rule from both directions: wealth does not lift the base and mire does not lower the noble, so neither fortune changes what a person is.","la":"it gives the case the quatrain leaves out, the noble brought low, and adds that only the discerning see the worth through it.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:73","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"Kural 409 says the unlearned, though born high, have not the worth of the learned born low; Pazhamozhi 73 says the well-born, though they learn nothing, are far better than those who know other things.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two books rank the same pair, birth and learning, in opposite order, so the ethics group does not agree on whether lineage can stand in for study.","la":"its proverb makes the well-born the very source of what learning would bring them, so instruction is superfluous rather than merely outweighed."},{"a":"pz:74","b":"pn:200","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 200 presents Pari's daughters as the children of the man who gave his tall belled chariot to a jasmine creeper that never sang for him; Pazhamozhi 74 cites the same gift as hearsay of old givers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroic poem uses the chariot to the creeper to claim a husband for the daughters, while the ethics verse turns the same act into a maxim that knowing folly adorns the worthy, so the patron's deed becomes a proof text.","la":"it sets Pari's chariot beside a second giver's mantle and names both as foolishness, a judgement the bardic poem never makes.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:74","b":"pn:142","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 142 says Pekan is foolish (மடம்) only in giving, like rain on salt land, never foolish in battle; Pazhamozhi 74 says knowing foolishness (அறிமடம்) is an ornament to the worthy, citing the mantle given to a peacock.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem already calls Pekan's giving மடம் and limits it to the gift; the ethics verse adds அறி, knowing, and so makes the patron's folly a chosen virtue rather than a lapse.","la":"it pairs Pekan's mantle with Pari's chariot as one kind of act and gives the whole class a name, அறிமடம்."},{"a":"pz:75","b":"n:325","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 325 says it is a wonder if one who reviles another before an assembly survives when the reviled stays silent; Pazhamozhi 75 says the worthy say no wounding word in an assembly because any speaker is answered.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the rare case, the reviled keeping silence, and the proverb the ordinary one, the retort, so between them reviling in company is shown to rebound whether it is answered or not.","la":"its proverb extends the rule to the highest speaker, Mal himself, so no rank exempts a reviler from reply."},{"a":"pz:76","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"Kural 293 says not to lie about what one's own heart knows, for the heart will burn its owner; Pazhamozhi 76 makes the king his own witness (தனக்குக் கரியாவான் தானாய்) who judges his hidden fault and cuts off his hand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the heart the witness that punishes a lie; the Pazhamozhi shows a king acting on that witness and carrying out the sentence on his own body, so self-witness becomes self-justice.","la":"its proverb names the opposite motive, 'no one will see', and the story shows the inner witness overruling it in a king with no accuser."},{"a":"pz:76","b":"n:226","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 226 asks whether anyone cuts off his own hand because it poked his eye, to say one does not cast off a friend for a hurt; Pazhamozhi 76 praises a king who did cut off his own hand for its fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same act is absurd as a response to a friend's offence and noble as a response to one's own moral fault, so the pair separates forgiving others from judging oneself.","la":"its story turns the quatrain's absurd hypothetical into a real and admired deed, by making the fault the king's own."},{"a":"pz:77","b":"n:189","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 189 says the pure in heart stay unshaken by cruel slander, like a lamp's inner flame; Pazhamozhi 77 says the worthy do not join slanderers who pose as protectors but turn away at once like a crow from a bow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the inner response to slander, steadiness, and the Pazhamozhi the outer one, flight, so the worthy are shown both unmoved by the slanderer and unwilling to stay in his company.","la":"it names the slanderer's disguise, speaking as if guarding one, and answers it with withdrawal rather than endurance."},{"a":"pz:78","b":"k:174","type":"develops","note":"Kural 174 says those of faultless sight who have conquered the senses will not covet on the plea 'we have nothing'; Pazhamozhi 78 says the worthy do not covet others' goods even when hunger bends them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural rules out need as an excuse by the coveter's self-mastery; the Pazhamozhi makes the distance between the worthy and another's goods a fact of nature, like sea and forest, so non-coveting appears as character, not effort.","la":"its proverb turns the couplet's conquest of the senses into a fixed separation that needs no struggle."},{"a":"pz:79","b":"pz:80","type":"develops","note":"79 says the fault of the exalted never stops being spoken of, like a brand on a grey bull; 80 says a single fault of the exalted is seen by all, like a lamp on a hill, while the small man's many go unseen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together, the fault of the great is exposed in both dimensions, lasting for all time in 79 and seen across all space in 80, which is why the heading asks more of them than of the small."},{"a":"pz:80","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"Kural 957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the spot on the moon; Pazhamozhi 80 says one fault of the exalted is a lamp on a hill while the many faults of the small never show.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows the fault against the bright moon; the Pazhamozhi adds the other side of the scale, that the small man's long-matured faults stay hidden, so visibility, not guilt, is what height changes.","la":"it sets the one fault of the great against the many of the small, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"pz:81","b":"k:955","type":"develops","note":"Kural 955 says an old family does not part from its nature when what it can give has shrunk; Pazhamozhi 81 says a man of a giving house keeps up ஒப்புரவு without wealth, and adds that he does so though untended and unfed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats the shrunken means as the only test; Pazhamozhi adds neglect, so the inherited nature is shown to hold without either resources or nurture.","la":"its proverb of the unfed calf that still becomes a bull makes the claim one of nature against upbringing, which the couplet does not raise."},{"a":"pz:81","b":"n:141","type":"same_image","note":"Both use grass and a beast to show that the well-born keep their nature in hardship: the lion will not chew creeping grass, the bull's calf grows into a bull though it grazes where there is no grass.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same pasture image argues in two directions: the Naladiyar's lion refuses what is beneath it, while Pazhamozhi's calf needs nothing from the pasture at all, so lineage is shown both as refusal and as self-sufficient growth.","la":"it turns the image from what the well-born will not do to what they become regardless, and ties it to the act of ஒப்புரவு rather than to principle in general."},{"a":"pz:82","b":"k:220","type":"develops","note":"Kural 220 says ruin through ஒப்புரவு is worth buying even by selling oneself; Pazhamozhi 82 names the concrete form of that cost, borrowing to do what is due, and the excuse it refuses, waiting for a better time.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the Kural's startling price, selling oneself, as the far end of an ordinary practice the worthy actually follow: going into debt rather than postponing a duty.","la":"its pun on கடன் as debt and duty makes the cost and the obligation one word, which the couplet keeps as ruin set against gain."},{"a":"pz:83","b":"n:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 80 forbids eating from the hands of those one should not eat from and speaking words mixed with falsehood; Pazhamozhi 83 grants that the worthy may have eaten from another's hand and says they speak the truth even so.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar guards truth by refusing the obligation that might bend it; Pazhamozhi allows the obligation and makes truth hold against it, a stronger and riskier claim.","la":"it sets the worthy man's truth beside the traitor's borrowed lie in battle, a contrast of two men under obligation that the quatrain's separate refusals lack."},{"a":"pz:84","b":"pz:85","type":"develops","note":"Both proverbs rest on a public commitment: the man who strapped on the drum beats it, the man who made friends before the village feeds them; 84 applies this to those who came for shelter, 85 to friends who prove faulty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together the two verses ground loyalty not in the worth of the person protected but in the protector's own visible pledge, so the other's unworthiness is ruled out twice as an excuse."},{"a":"pz:84","b":"pn:203","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 203 tells a king that refusing a returning suppliant with 'you took before' is cruel and that protecting suppliants is his duty; Pazhamozhi 84 says those who took in seekers do not cast them off as worthless.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem closes the excuse of past giving and the ethics verse closes the excuse of the seeker's unworthiness, so together they show the protector's duty answering two different pretexts for withdrawal."},{"a":"pz:85","b":"k:806","type":"develops","note":"Kural 806 says those who keep within bounds do not give up an old friendship even when it brings ruin; Pazhamozhi 85 says the worthy do not change toward cherished friends who lack good qualities, because the friendship was made before the village.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes constancy a mark of the man who keeps bounds; Pazhamozhi adds a reason outside him, the village that witnessed the bond, so loyalty becomes a public obligation as well as a private virtue.","la":"its proverb names the friend's bad character outright and answers it with feeding as a duty, a concrete act the couplet does not specify."},{"a":"pz:86","b":"k:579","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 579 ranks highest the disposition to show kindly regard and bear with those whose way is to hurt; Pazhamozhi 86 sets that regard at one moment, when an open and dangerous enemy is in distress.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pairing fixes when the Kural's demand is hardest: not under the enemy's attack but at his fall, when sparing him is a choice and he may still bite.","la":"the snake proverb names the enemy as still dangerous and the assembly of the worthy as the place he is spared, a setting and a risk the couplet leaves out."},{"a":"pz:87","b":"pz:88","type":"develops","note":"Both verses set the high-born beside those small by birth: in 87 the great go to the small and depend on them, in 88 the great take the small into their company.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the edition's heading praising the great for crossing the birth-rank in both directions, as dependents and as patrons, while leaving the ranking itself in place."},{"a":"pz:88","b":"k:451","type":"tension","note":"Kural 451 says greatness fears small company and only smallness takes it as kin; Pazhamozhi 88 praises great families who conduct themselves as kin with those lesser than they. The conflict holds on the reading that 88's சிறியார் are the same small people the Kural warns against, not merely those of lower rank.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two split on whether keeping company with the lesser degrades the great or raises the lesser: the Kural fears contamination, Pazhamozhi sees the flower lending itself to the string.","la":"its proverb reverses the direction of influence that the couplet assumes, making the great the ones who pass on their quality."},{"a":"pz:88","b":"k:960","type":"develops","note":"Kural 960 says one who wants lineage must want humility toward all; Pazhamozhi 88 shows the great families in the act, keeping kin-like company with those below them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states humility as the condition of lineage; Pazhamozhi gives its form and its effect, the lesser carried along like string with the flower.","la":"it turns the couplet's general humility into one practice, keeping the lesser as kin, and adds what the lesser gain from it."},{"a":"pz:89","b":"k:657","type":"develops","note":"Kural 657 sets the utter poverty of the worthy above wealth gained under blame; Pazhamozhi 89 sets the poverty of the great above the wealth the small have reached, calling that wealth shameful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural condemns wealth for how it was got; Pazhamozhi condemns it for who holds it, so the pair shows two grounds on which wealth can rank below poverty.","la":"the old-ghee proverb concedes the great man's decline and ranks him above the fresh lesser thing anyway, a turn the couplet's plain ranking lacks."},{"a":"pz:89","b":"n:275","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank the poverty of the worthy above others' wealth through an image of what is drunk or eaten: the Naladiyar's small well beside the sea, Pazhamozhi's old ghee beside buttermilk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The images argue differently for the same ranking: the Naladiyar's well is preferred because it can be used, Pazhamozhi's ghee because its kind does not change with age, so one rests the claim on use and the other on nature.","la":"it measures the worthy against the small rather than against those ignorant of the next world, making the ranking one of character rather than of foresight."},{"a":"pz:90","b":"k:458","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 458 says that even the worthy, good of mind in full, are protected by good company; Pazhamozhi 90 reverses the same phrase to say the base, good of company in full, never become good of mind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the house view that company acts on the mind has a limit: it guards a good mind already there and cannot make one in the base.","la":"its salt-sea image and inverted wording set an exception beside the couplet: for the base, company runs one way only and leaves the mind as it was."},{"a":"pz:90","b":"k:452","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 452 says water changes with the land it runs on, as understanding takes the nature of company; Pazhamozhi 90 uses water the other way, the fresh flood that cannot sweeten the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same water image proves opposite things depending on which is larger: the Kural's water yields to the soil, while the base man is the sea that absorbs every good influence unchanged.","la":"it turns the couplet's image around, making the base the vessel that alters what flows in rather than being altered."},{"a":"pz:90","b":"pz:106","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 90 denies that good company changes the base; 106 denies that a patron's favour and prosperity change them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the chapter closes both routes of reform it considers, the influence of good companions and the gift of fortune, so the base man's nature is fixed against both."},{"a":"pz:91","b":"n:262","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 262 says the wise do not seek the base however rich, as no one picks cactus buds; Pazhamozhi 91 says those who live as kin of the worthy do not go over to the small though they are called superior.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar grounds the refusal in judgement of the base, Pazhamozhi in belonging: settled among the worthy, one does not change sides, and the sweetness on offer is jaggery in sand.","la":"it gives the refusal a cause the quatrain lacks, the bond of living as kin of the worthy, and admits the small some sweetness while denying it is edible."},{"a":"pz:93","b":"n:351","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 351 says the petty-minded grow more practised in wrong as they age; Pazhamozhi 93 says some grow old without understanding, like trees that flower without fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they deny that age brings wisdom from two sides: the Naladiyar says age deepens folly, Pazhamozhi that it can simply pass without yield, so telling the fool is one more seed that will not sprout.","la":"it sets old age among the failures of plant growth, flower without fruit and seed without sprout, so the old fool becomes a natural kind of barrenness."},{"a":"pz:94","b":"k:849","type":"develops","note":"Kural 849 says the one who shows things to the unseeing becomes unseeing himself while the other keeps what he saw; Pazhamozhi 94 says not to counsel the obstinate, who keep what they took up.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes the failed lesson and Pazhamozhi draws the rule from it, adding the cause: the obstinate are already dyed, so the teacher has no blank cloth to work on.","la":"its indigo proverb explains why the lesson fails, a first colour that has set, and turns the couplet's observation into a counsel not to speak.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:95","b":"k:510","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 510 says that both trusting without examining and doubting one already trusted bring endless trouble; Pazhamozhi 95 says that once you have seen a man speak evil you should never trust him again.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi names the case where withdrawing trust is not the fault the Kural warns of: one evil word plainly seen is taken as proof of a nature, as neem stays bitter whoever eats it.","la":"it adds a specific condition, evil speech plainly seen, that overrides the couplet's warning against doubting the trusted."},{"a":"pz:96","b":"n:350","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 350 says small bullocks fed cut grass never learn the yoke, and the base, though rich, are known by their deeds; Pazhamozhi 96 says forest men of cruel deeds granted a place in the country do not behave well.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's care is feeding and wealth, Pazhamozhi's is a ruler's grant of settlement; together they show the period's ethics denying reform by provision whether the provider is a herdsman or a lord.","la":"it moves the claim from private wealth to a ruler's act of settlement and pins it on a people of a named way of life.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:97","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1075 says fear is the conduct of the base; Pazhamozhi 97 says the base do not care about the great blame that falls on them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair separates two fears: the base may be held in line by fear of punishment, as the Kural says, but blame, which works on others through shame, has no hold on them at all.","la":"its proverb of eating fearlessly at a house of mourning shows what the base do not fear, the judgement of others, which the couplet leaves out.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:100","b":"k:1078","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1078 says the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed; Pazhamozhi 100 says those who have been punished go on doing evil, growing a span for every cubit cut.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Pressure may wring use out of the base, as the Kural says, but Pazhamozhi denies that it changes them: what is cut back grows again.","la":"its regrowth image separates getting use from the base by force and reforming them by punishment, which the couplet runs together."},{"a":"pz:101","b":"k:1078","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1078 says the base yield use only when crushed; Pazhamozhi 101, on the reading that அலப்பின் means 'if pressed', says the base do wrong when pressed and also when left alone. The conflict depends on that reading of line 1.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two disagree on whether pressure works on the base at all: the Kural makes it the one way to get good from them, Pazhamozhi makes it one more occasion for harm, like the jackal that feeds in any year.","la":"it sets the pressed and the unpressed case side by side and finds harm in both, removing the couplet's one remedy."},{"a":"pz:102","b":"n:260","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 260 says the base man kicks away the subtle teaching of the learned and sets up a petty assembly with one like himself; Pazhamozhi 102 says those who refuse the good words of the keen-minded and act harshly are known to everyone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar follows the refuser into a small circle of his own kind; Pazhamozhi follows him into the village's notice, so refusing counsel ends both in a narrowed company and in public notoriety.","la":"it adds the consequence the quatrain lacks, harsh action that makes the fool as known as a rogue bull."},{"a":"pz:103","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work one does; Pazhamozhi 103 says those who come down in a base line have no one of good character among them. The conflict holds on the reading of 972's செய்தொழில் as conduct or work rather than hereditary occupation.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair puts the period's two accounts of character side by side: the Kural makes worth follow deeds and denies birth a role, Pazhamozhi makes it follow descent so firmly that a whole line is written off.","la":"its monkey proverb treats a line of descent as a species, the very ranking by birth that the couplet rejects."},{"a":"pz:104","b":"k:507","type":"develops","note":"Kural 507 says trusting the ignorant out of fondness brings every folly; Pazhamozhi 104 says clearing out the fit to raise up the useless base is like felling the jackfruit to plant the kanjirai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural warns against the wrong appointment, Pazhamozhi counts its double cost: the base raised and the fit removed, a sweet tree lost and a bitter one planted in its place.","la":"its orchard proverb pairs the promotion with a removal, so the folly is an exchange of good for bad rather than a single mistaken trust.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:105","b":"n:345","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 345 says a dog fed from a golden bowl still eyes leftovers, so honouring the base does not change their deeds; Pazhamozhi 105 says giving the small the honour due to the great is like laying a seat for a dog.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the honoured dog, but the Naladiyar faults the dog, which stays a dog, and Pazhamozhi faults the one who honours it, who has also taken the honour from the great.","la":"it moves the blame from the honoured base to the giver and adds the loss to the great whose due was given away."},{"a":"pz:106","b":"n:347","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 347 says the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by their deeds, as a golden sandal is still for the foot; Pazhamozhi 106 says the worthless whom a patron lifts out of distress into faultless prosperity do not become men of good family.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar says wealth does not change deeds; Pazhamozhi adds that it does not change rank either, even when the wealth is a patron's gift and is used without fault.","la":"it names the patron's favour as the source of the wealth and denies the base the standing of குடி, a claim of rank the quatrain does not make."},{"a":"pz:107","b":"n:70","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 70 tells the high not to answer the low, as no one bites back a biting dog; Pazhamozhi 107 sets the base man's slighting talk as a dog barking at the moon.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the dog moves from biting to barking out of reach: the Naladiyar asks the great to refrain from answering, and Pazhamozhi shows the insult never reaches them anyway.","la":"its proverb puts the insulted great man out of the dog's range, so restraint is backed by the insult's harmlessness, which the quatrain does not claim.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:107","b":"pz:122","type":"develops","note":"107 has the base set the great level with themselves in speech; 122 has the unrestrained rise while the worthy withdraw.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses give the same inversion at two scales, one man's insolent equal talk and a whole order where stone floats, and show the first as the seed of the second."},{"a":"pz:108","b":"n:329","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 329 says the small-minded think of the next world only in want and illness; Pazhamozhi 108 gives the base man who denies it outright and urges the fill of desire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair sets two degrees of the same neglect, forgetting the next world when able and denying it as a creed, and Pazhamozhi's proverb names the cost the Naladiyar leaves implied, brick eaten in tears.","la":"it quotes the denier's own words and answers them with an image of the trade he offers, where the quatrain only describes the forgetter's timing."},{"a":"pz:108","b":"k:850","type":"develops","note":"Kural 850 says one who says 'it is not' to what the world says is will be reckoned a demon; Pazhamozhi 108 names one such denial, of the next world, and its practical upshot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves blank what is denied; the pairing fills it with the next world and shows the denier's error as a bad exchange, not only a social stigma.","la":"it fills the couplet's blank with a named denial and a proverb that judges it by what is lost, not by the world's verdict."},{"a":"pz:109","b":"sp:16","type":"develops","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 16 says that before the worthless anger is good and nobility bad, as good meat is wasted in a biting dog's mouth; Pazhamozhi 109 says good done to the graceless base puts the doer in danger.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Holding them together shows the two halves of one counsel: Sirupanchamoolam says kindness to the base is wasted, Pazhamozhi that it is also dangerous, the tiger's face being closer than the dog's mouth.","gr":4},{"a":"pz:110","b":"k:1078","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1078 says the base yield their use only when crushed like sugar-cane; Pazhamozhi 110 asks whether the wicked ever come under control because they have suffered and answers no. The conflict holds on the reading that the Kural's crushing and Pazhamozhi's suffering are both pressure applied to the base.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair splits on whether pressure works on the base: the Kural finds something sweet to be pressed out, Pazhamozhi finds clay that no binder will make into a wall.","la":"its proverb puts the fault in the material itself, so that no admixture of hardship helps, against the couplet's cane that yields under force."},{"a":"pz:110","b":"nm:98","type":"agrees","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 98 says the base, though bound and scoured, show their dirt; Pazhamozhi 110 says suffering does not tame the wicked, as unfit clay makes no wall.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both deny that working on the base changes them, and together they cover the two methods of correction, scouring from outside and mixing in from within, each failing on the material."},{"a":"pz:111","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from deeds; Pazhamozhi 111 lays the quarrelsome temper on those small by lineage. The conflict holds on the reading that குலத்துச் சிறியார் ranks by birth rather than naming the mean ones of a family.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Kural, the verse shows the ethics books divided on whether baseness is inherited: the couplet ranks by deeds, Pazhamozhi traces a vice to the family one comes from.","la":"it attaches a specific behaviour, turning on peacemakers, to a birth group, the very link between birth and worth the couplet denies."},{"a":"pz:111","b":"n:68","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 68 says the anger of the low keeps spreading and never dies; Pazhamozhi 111 shows it spreading onto those who come to part the fighters.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi gives the Naladiyar's spreading anger its concrete path, from the quarrel to the peacemakers themselves, so that intervention becomes one of the ways it spreads.","la":"its proverb shows the attempt to settle the quarrel as what overturns it, a turn the quatrain's general claim lacks.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:112","b":"k:888","type":"develops","note":"Kural 888 says a family with inner enmity wears itself down like gold rubbing a file; Pazhamozhi 112 says provoking the defiant within one's own house is pulling the axle-pin from inside the cart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show inner enmity as both a slow wearing and a sudden collapse, and Pazhamozhi places its cause with the householder who provokes it, not with the enmity alone.","la":"its proverb makes the head of the house the agent of the ruin, sitting inside what he dismantles, where the couplet speaks of the family as a whole."},{"a":"pz:113","b":"k:451","type":"develops","note":"Kural 451 says greatness fears small company while smallness takes it at once as kin; Pazhamozhi 113 says fostering and living with the shameless is binding worms into a wound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet explains why the great fear such company, and the pairing supplies what they fear: harm that grows under one's own care, sealed in by the very bandage of fellowship.","la":"its proverb makes one's care for the base the means of one's injury, a mechanism the couplet's fear leaves unstated."},{"a":"pz:113","b":"pz:109","type":"develops","note":"109 warns against doing good to the graceless base; 113 against fostering and living with the shameless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order the two verses rise from a single favour to a shared life, and the danger rises with them from a tiger's sudden bite to a wound eaten from within."},{"a":"pz:114","b":"k:127","type":"develops","note":"Kural 127 says those who do not guard the tongue suffer from the fault of their words; Pazhamozhi 114 shows the hiding fool betrayed by his own evil speech, like the frog in the sand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi adds what the Kural does not consider, that the unguarded tongue undoes even a man who has hidden himself, so concealment is no substitute for restraint of speech.","la":"its proverb sets hiding against speaking and lets speech win, a contrast the couplet's single command lacks."},{"a":"pz:115","b":"k:182","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 182 ranks backbiting with a false smile as worse than open wrongdoing; Pazhamozhi 115 asks why blame the backbiter at all, since he is past disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking condemns the backbiter most severely, and Pazhamozhi warns that this condemnation does him no harm, since a man without shame is beyond the reach of blame.","la":"its proverb turns from judging the backbiter to judging the use of blaming him, adding a limit of blame the couplet does not raise."},{"a":"pz:116","b":"nm:10","type":"agrees","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 10 says the base are bound with iron after goading while the worthy are bound by courtesy; Pazhamozhi 116 says the qualityless cannot be restrained by words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they mark a limit of persuasion: courtesy binds the worthy, but words are wind to the base, so only force is left, as the Nanmanikkadigai says outright."},{"a":"pz:116","b":"n:70","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 70 says the high do not answer the low with their own mouths; Pazhamozhi 116 says it is hard to restrain loose talkers by speech at all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's refusal to answer the base is a matter of dignity, and Pazhamozhi adds a practical ground: answering is useless, since words cannot catch wind.","la":"its proverb gives a reason of futility beside the quatrain's reason of rank."},{"a":"pz:117","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"Kural 448 says a ruler without rebukers perishes even with no one to ruin him; Pazhamozhi 117 says one who will not take the words of the great and does not guard himself makes others into thieves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ruin needs no enemy; Pazhamozhi adds that the unguarded man's ruin creates its own agents, so the thief is partly his making.","la":"it carries the couplet's claim from the king to anyone and its proverb shifts guilt from the thief to the man who would not heed counsel."},{"a":"pz:120","b":"k:821","type":"develops","note":"Kural 821 calls the friendship of those close without inner consent an anvil for striking when the chance comes; Pazhamozhi 120 calls the arrogant, concealing men one has honoured gravel in a sandal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two images of hidden ill will differ in timing: the Kural's waits for its moment to strike, Pazhamozhi's galls at every step, so the false intimate is shown as both a threat and a constant wearing.","la":"its proverb turns hidden hostility into a present, continuous injury, where the couplet looks to a single future blow."},{"a":"pz:121","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"Kural 510 says that doubting one already trusted brings endless trouble; Pazhamozhi 121 says raising a man to intimacy and casting him off without cause is a deed of smallness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural counts the cost of withdrawing trust to the one who withdraws; Pazhamozhi judges the character of the act, a child's play with a grove, so the error is both costly and mean.","la":"its proverb names the fault as whim and smallness, a moral judgement beside the couplet's prudential one."},{"a":"pz:122","b":"n:368","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 368 says the old wealthy and the worthy decline while concubines' sons and the base flourish, the world upside down; Pazhamozhi 122 says the worthy withdraw while the unrestrained rise, the gourd sinking and the stone floating.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both read social inversion through an image, but the Naladiyar mixes birth and wealth into its ranks while Pazhamozhi measures only by speech and restraint, showing the complaint can be made on conduct alone.","la":"it drops the quatrain's birth and wealth markers and ranks by conduct, and its proverb calls the inversion impossible in nature, not merely a turned umbrella."},{"a":"pz:123","b":"n:348","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 348 lists the base as harsh, without kindly regard and scornful of all; Pazhamozhi 123 names the same traits and adds the absence of fear of evil deeds and a verdict of hell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain describes and stops; Pazhamozhi takes nearly the same portrait and passes sentence, so the pairing shows the description turning into a judgement.","la":"its proverb closes the portrait with a verdict, making such men hell's own, where the quatrain gives only the list."},{"a":"pz:124","b":"k:1122","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1122 calls the bond between the lover and the girl the bond of body and life, using the word நட்பு; Pazhamozhi 124 says friends take each other as a whole body, more than the god who took Uma as half.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both measure the closest bond by the body, but the Kural's lovers are body and life, two in one frame, while Pazhamozhi uses the divine half-woman to claim that friendship joins two wholes completely, setting friendship above even the god's marriage.","la":"its proverb from the god's half-body adds a ranking the couplet lacks: friendship is placed above the divine marriage, not only likened to lovers."},{"a":"pz:124","b":"pz:132","type":"develops","note":"124 says friends take each other as a whole body; 132 says the faultless take a friend's fault as their own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The union pictured in 124 as one body is given its moral cost in 132: if the friend is one's own whole body, his faults are one's own too.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:125","b":"k:819","type":"develops","note":"Kural 819 calls bitter the bond with those whose words and deeds differ; Pazhamozhi 125 says between true friends the words and their meaning are one, and twisting them is betrayal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural tests the speaker for a gap between word and deed; Pazhamozhi tests the hearer for a gap between word and meaning, so the demand for oneness falls on both sides of a friend's speech.","la":"its proverb of acceptance turned to weapon carries the fault to the listener who re-tells, a party the couplet does not consider."},{"a":"pz:125","b":"pz:130","type":"develops","note":"125 condemns re-telling a friend's words in a twisted sense; 130 says a chosen friend is not blamed among many.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two verses govern what one says of a friend to others: neither distort his words nor publish his faults, so a friend's speech and his failings are both kept from the crowd."},{"a":"pz:126","b":"n:76","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 76 says one should not abandon those one has mingled with, since parting is hard even for beasts; Pazhamozhi 126 uses nearly the same words and closes with the proverb that parting hurts even from a demon.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain grounds staying in patience with a friend's hurts, while Pazhamozhi drops the hurts and adds that parting is painful even from a demon, so the reason to stay becomes the bond itself, whatever the companion is worth.","la":"its proverb adds a claim the quatrain lacks: the pain of parting does not depend on the friend being good."},{"a":"pz:126","b":"n:220","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 220 says parting after closeness hurts even from a deadly snake, so test friends first; Pazhamozhi 126 says parting hurts even from a demon, so do not abandon those one has joined.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same image of a painful parting from a dangerous companion yields opposite advice: the quatrain draws caution before the bond, Pazhamozhi draws loyalty after it, and together they show the image argues for testing first and keeping afterwards.","la":"its proverb is used after the bond is formed, where the quatrain uses the same thought before it, so the pair divides friendship into two stages.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:127","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"Kural 788 says friendship removes distress at once, like the hand that catches a slipping garment; Pazhamozhi 127 says a friend's distress is not removed by words, as dew does not fill a tank.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the speed and instinct of true help; Pazhamozhi names what does not count as help, kindly words, and its dew-and-tank proverb shows that even sincere sympathy falls short of the deed.","la":"its proverb adds the negative case the couplet leaves out: words, however ungrudging, are dew and not water for the tank."},{"a":"pz:127","b":"n:369","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 369 says those whose hearts do not ease the pain a dear one tells them had better leap from a cliff; Pazhamozhi 127 says the remedy for a dear one's distress is not accomplished by words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes a heart moved by a friend's pain the minimum of friendship; Pazhamozhi sets the bar higher, since feeling expressed in speech still leaves the tank empty, so the pair moves from compassion to remedy.","la":"its proverb ranks deed above sympathy where the quatrain asks only that the heart be moved.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:128","b":"k:791","type":"tension","note":"Kural 791 says nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry; Pazhamozhi 128 says there is no need to insist on worthy friends and one should take anyone's friendship, since a befriended dog brings in a hare.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On a plain reading the two disagree on whether a friend must be tested first: the Kural fears the bond one cannot leave, while Pazhamozhi counts the profit even a low friend brings, so the pair shows two grounds of valuing friendship, by its risk and by its yield.","la":"its proverb measures a friend by what he brings in, a ground of judgement the couplet does not use, and so drops the inquiry the couplet requires."},{"a":"pz:128","b":"n:213","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 213 says hold close those like a dog, which wags its tail with its master's spear in its body, and leave those like an elephant; Pazhamozhi 128 says a befriended dog brings in a good hare.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both take the dog as the model friend but for different virtues: in the quatrain it is loyal even when wounded, in Pazhamozhi it is useful as a hunter, so the same animal serves a test of character and a reckoning of gain.","la":"its proverb keeps the quatrain's dog but changes its merit from loyalty to yield."},{"a":"pz:128","b":"iv:34","type":"tension","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 34 names as sweet leaving the friendship of worthless people even when they embrace one; Pazhamozhi 128 says one should take friendship whoever's it is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Within the same group of works the counted verse and the proverb verse take opposite sides on befriending the unworthy, which shows the group does not speak with one voice on choosing friends."},{"a":"pz:129","b":"n:310","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 310 says that where old acquaintance stands as a post (பழமைகந் தாக) one may ask what intimacy allows; Pazhamozhi 129 speaks of friends who lean on old acquaintance as a post (பழமை கந்தாக) and closes that a span of respect beats a cubit of friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two use the same phrase of old acquaintance as a tethering post: the quatrain lets one lean on it to ask for help, while Pazhamozhi warns that leaning on it while dropping care wears the friendship out, so the post is a support with a limit.","la":"its proverb ranks a little respect above much intimacy, a measure the quatrain does not apply to the same post of old acquaintance."},{"a":"pz:129","b":"k:801","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 801 defines old friendship as friendship that does not cut down the liberties intimacy gives; Pazhamozhi 129 says a span of respect is better than a cubit of friendship when intimacy is presumed upon without care.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks the friend to bear the liberties of intimacy; Pazhamozhi sets a limit from the other side, that intimacy which forgets care and respect is worth less than a little regard, so the liberties of old friendship do not cancel the duty of regard.","la":"its proverb ranks two measures, respect and friendship, where the couplet only defines one."},{"a":"pz:130","b":"n:227","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 227 says the worthy see no fault in those they have joined, and those who bring out a friend's wrongs are lower than he; Pazhamozhi 130 says those who chose friends after sifting do not blame them among many, as no one tells a shameful dream.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain ranks exposing a friend as worse than his wrong; Pazhamozhi gives the reason through the dream: a friend chosen by oneself is part of oneself, so publishing his fault is publishing one's own shame.","la":"its proverb of the shameful dream adds why the exposer is shamed, which the quatrain asserts without a reason."},{"a":"pz:130","b":"k:820","type":"develops","note":"Kural 820 warns against those friendly at home who defame one in the assembly; Pazhamozhi 130 says those who chose friends after sifting do not blame them among many.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes the false friend from the victim's side; Pazhamozhi describes the true friend from the speaker's side, and the pair makes the public gathering the place where a friendship is proved or broken.","la":"its proverb gives the true friend's reason for silence, the shameful dream one does not tell, where the couplet only names the false friend's fault."},{"a":"pz:131","b":"k:808","type":"develops","note":"Kural 808 praises those whose intimacy is strong enough not to listen to reports of a friend's lapse; Pazhamozhi 131 says one does not burn against a friend one has made even for faults seen with one's own eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural guards the friend against hearsay; Pazhamozhi goes further, to faults one has witnessed, and grounds the restraint in having made the friend oneself, as one does not watch one's own calf die.","la":"its proverb of the dying calf extends the couplet's case from reported faults to seen ones and gives the reason, that the friend is one's own."},{"a":"pz:131","b":"el:15","type":"agrees","note":"Elathi 15 counts not burning against those dear as one's eyes (கண்போல்வார்க் காயாமை) among five good things; Pazhamozhi 131 says one does not burn against (காய்ந்தீயார்) a friend one has made even for faults seen with one's own eyes.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The counted verse lists the restraint as one good among five; Pazhamozhi sets the hardest condition on it, faults seen with one's own eyes, and argues it by the calf one would not see die.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:132","b":"n:223","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 223 says it is right to bear with friends even when they do great wrong and closes with ஒருவர் பொறையிருவர் நட்பு; Pazhamozhi 132 closes with the same proverb after saying the faultless take a friend's fault as their own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two works close on the same proverb but frame it differently: the quatrain asks the injured friend to bear the wrong, while Pazhamozhi has the faultless friend own it as 'our fault', so the one who bears becomes the one who shares the blame.","la":"it applies the shared proverb to a different act, owning the fault rather than enduring the injury.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:132","b":"k:803","type":"develops","note":"Kural 803 asks what use long friendship is if one does not accept a friend's liberty as if one had done it oneself; Pazhamozhi 132 says the faultless take a friend's faults as their own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks one to treat a friend's liberties as one's own acts; Pazhamozhi carries the same identification to his faults, so what began as tolerance of intimacy becomes sharing of blame.","la":"its proverb of one's patience as two's friendship extends the couplet from liberties to wrongs."},{"a":"pz:133","b":"in:26","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 26 counts telling one's own trouble to those who do not care (பரியார்க்குத் தாமுற்ற கூற்று) as painful; Pazhamozhi 133 says one should tell one's want to one moved with care (பரிந்து) who will remove it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two say the same thing from two sides, the painful telling to the uncaring and the fitting telling to the caring healer, so together they make the listener's care the condition of disclosure."},{"a":"pz:133","b":"n:369","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 369 condemns those whose hearts do not ease the pain dear ones tell them; Pazhamozhi 133 says the sufferer should tell his want to one who will remove it, as the sick do not hide disease from the physician.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain speaks to the friend who hears; Pazhamozhi speaks to the friend who suffers and tells him to choose a hearer able to cure, so the pair completes the exchange of telling and easing from both ends.","la":"its proverb of patient and physician adds the sufferer's duty to disclose, which the quatrain assumes."},{"a":"pz:134","b":"n:284","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 284 says a thousand gather like crows while there is wealth and none asks after one in want; Pazhamozhi 134 says a thousand eat at a man's house while he lacks nothing, and in the Kali age the ruined have no friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two share the thousand who feed on the prosperous, but Pazhamozhi places the desertion in the Kali age, turning the quatrain's account of poverty into a mark of a fallen time.","la":"its proverb with the Kali age frame adds a cause in the age itself, where the quatrain gives only the man's change of fortune."},{"a":"pz:134","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"Kural 796 says ruin has one benefit, that it measures one's people to full length; Pazhamozhi 134 says in the Kali age the ruined have no friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats ruin as a rod that sorts true friends from false; Pazhamozhi reports what the rod finds in this age, nothing at all, so the couplet's consolation becomes a bleak verdict.","la":"its proverb gives the result of the measuring the couplet describes, the thousand eaters reduced to none."},{"a":"pz:134","b":"nm:62","type":"agrees","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 62 counts that want of close friends is learned in ruin (கெழியின்மை கேட்டா லறிக); Pazhamozhi 134 says the ruined have no friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse makes ruin a means of knowing; Pazhamozhi adds the crowd of eaters who vanish and puts the fact in the Kali age, so a test of friends becomes a judgement on the time."},{"a":"pz:135","b":"n:110","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 110 says what was fixed at birth does not shrink or grow and so asks why grieve at ruin; Pazhamozhi 135 turns the same fixed term against a friend turned murderer, who cannot shorten it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Together they show the fixed-term doctrine working in two directions: the Naladiyar uses it to quiet grief over loss, while Pazhamozhi uses it to quiet fear of betrayal, making fate a shelter against human malice.","la":"its proverb pictures Death as the guard of a life until its portion is eaten, so the fixed span becomes protection from a plotting friend rather than a reason for calm at ruin."},{"a":"pz:135","b":"nm:40","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture Death as an eater: Nanmanikkadigai 40 says it does not take its food at anyone's bidding, Pazhamozhi 135 that it guards a man until his eating is done.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Put together, Death's refusal to eat at command explains the Pazhamozhi consolation: no plotter can order Death to take a man early, so the same appetite that cannot be bribed cannot be hurried either."},{"a":"pz:136","b":"n:218","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 218 asks what use is a close friend who gives not even a fly's foot of help; Pazhamozhi 136 names the friend kept as a reserve who gives none at need and adds that he first set one in danger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair separates two failures the word 'useless friend' joins: the Naladiyar's friend merely gives nothing, while Pazhamozhi's raises one into peril and then withdraws, so the worst friend is the one whose help began.","la":"the ladder proverb adds the stage the quatrain lacks, being helped up and then stranded, which makes a half-given help worse than none.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:137","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"Kural 788 says friendship clears distress there and then, like the hand catching a slipping garment; Pazhamozhi 137 shows a friend who chose penance over battle, help offered when the festival is over.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with the Kural's 'there and then', the Balarama story shows that timing is the whole of friendship's help, so even a holy act done for the friend fails if it replaces help at the moment of need.","la":"it sets an epic case and a proverb of lateness against the couplet's simile of speed, and ranks the friend's present help above pious works done on his behalf."},{"a":"pz:137","b":"n:238","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 238 is a vow to put one's life in a distressed friend's hands; Pazhamozhi 137 condemns the friend who went off to penance instead of fighting beside his friend.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow and the story are the two halves of one rule: the Naladiyar says what the friend owes in the hour of danger, Pazhamozhi shows a famous figure who withheld it and calls his religion a late shoulder.","la":"it supplies a named failure for the quatrain's oath, so the duty to risk one's life is shown being broken by a pious withdrawal rather than by cowardice."},{"a":"pz:138","b":"k:839","type":"tension","note":"Kural 839 calls the friendship of fools very sweet because parting from them brings no pain; Pazhamozhi 138 closes with the proverb that the friendship of fools is painful afterwards. The tension holds if the Kural's line is read plainly rather than as irony.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two place the pain of a fool's friendship differently: the Kural finds none at the parting because there was no real bond, while Pazhamozhi finds pain later because the fool believes slander and breaks the bond.","la":"it ranks friends by how they take slander, highest against lowest, and so names the cause of the later pain, which the couplet does not."},{"a":"pz:138","b":"n:189","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 189 says the pure in heart stay unshaken by cruel slander like a shielded flame; Pazhamozhi 138 makes the same steadiness the mark of the highest friend, who finds no rift even when looking, against the lowest, who believe.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair moves resistance to slander from a private virtue to a test of friendship: the Naladiyar's steady heart protects itself, Pazhamozhi's protects the bond between two other people.","la":"it sets the steady man against his opposite and names what the credulous one's belief costs, the friendship's later pain."},{"a":"pz:139","b":"n:229","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 229 says that when those taken as one's own prove otherwise, one should honour them all the more and keep it within; Pazhamozhi 139 calls heart's friendship given to the shameless moonlight shed in the forest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Naladiyar's counsel to keep honouring the false friend meets its limit in shamelessness: where the other has no shame within, the honour reaches no one, and Pazhamozhi counts it wasted.","la":"its proverb names the outcome the quatrain leaves out, that continued regard for the shameless lights an empty forest."},{"a":"pz:140","b":"k:805","type":"tension","note":"Kural 805 says that when friends do what hurts one should read it as folly or great intimacy; Pazhamozhi 140 says one should not act as one who knew them of old but break with them as they broke.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two give opposite rules for the old friend who turns unfriendly: the Kural asks for a generous reading of the hurt, Pazhamozhi denies old acquaintance any claim and prescribes the break as a cure.","la":"its proverb treats breaking off as a medicine, so ending the friendship becomes a remedy rather than a failure of the patience the couplet asks."},{"a":"pz:140","b":"n:226","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 226 asks whether anyone cuts off his own hand because it poked his eye, so a friend who hurts should not be cast off; Pazhamozhi 140 says breaking with one who breaks is the remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The images decide the rule: the Naladiyar makes the friend one's own limb, so cutting him off is self-harm, while Pazhamozhi makes the broken friendship an ailment, so the cut is the cure.","la":"the medicine proverb answers the quatrain's body image with one of cure, turning the same separation from mutilation into healing."},{"a":"pz:141","b":"n:375","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 375 uses the eel that shows one end to the snake and the other to the fish for women of double conduct; Pazhamozhi 141 calls the man who courts both friends and enemies a brand burning at both ends.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both books picture double dealing as one body with two ends, but the eel profits by fitting each side, while the brand burns whoever holds it, so Pazhamozhi makes the two-sided man a danger to both sides rather than a clever survivor.","la":"it moves the two-ended image from the courtesan to the go-between among friends and foes, and its proverb names the harm to the holder that the eel image does not."},{"a":"pz:140","b":"pz:139","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 139 counts love given to the shameless as wasted; 140 goes on to say what to do when a friend turns unfriendly: break with him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the two verses move from loss to action: once the love is seen to be moonlight in a forest, the next counsel is to stop shedding it and answer the break with a break."},{"a":"pz:142","b":"k:505","type":"develops","note":"Kural 505 makes one's own deeds the only touchstone of greatness and smallness; Pazhamozhi 142 says one part seen of a person, like one ladle from the pot, suffices to judge the whole.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they turn the Kural's touchstone into a method of sampling: one need not watch all a person's deeds, since a single act examined stands for the whole as one ladle stands for the pot.","la":"its cooking image and proverb add the claim that a small sample is enough, which the couplet's touchstone does not say."},{"a":"pz:143","b":"k:271","type":"develops","note":"Kural 271 says the five elements within laugh at the deceiver's hidden conduct; Pazhamozhi 143 says the wrongdoer who thinks his deed screened by a mountain is pierced by others' eyes, sharper than arrows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair names two witnesses the hider cannot escape, his own body within and other people's eyes without, so concealed wrong is seen from both sides at once.","la":"it moves the witness from the body's elements to the onlookers and gives the wrongdoer's false belief, the mountain, that the proverb answers."},{"a":"pz:144","b":"k:824","type":"tension","note":"Kural 824 warns of deceivers whose faces smile while their hearts are bitter, so the face hides malice; Pazhamozhi 144 says the deceitful are known on sight because the face tells what the heart intends even when they conceal it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they disagree on whether a deceiver's face can be trusted to betray him: the Kural fears the face that masks, the Pazhamozhi trusts the face to leak, so the reader must ask whether the skill lies in the face or in the one who reads it.","la":"its flood image claims the sign comes before the deed and cannot be held back, a confidence the couplet denies."},{"a":"pz:144","b":"k:706","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 706 says the face, like crystal, shows what is strongest in the heart; Pazhamozhi 144 applies this to deceit held under concealment (ஒளிப்பினும்).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair extends the Kural's claim from feeling that shows freely to intent that is actively hidden, and holds that concealment does not stop the face's showing.","la":"it narrows the couplet to கள்ளம் and adds the condition of hiding, with the flood's dampness as the sign that runs ahead."},{"a":"pz:145","b":"k:708","type":"develops","note":"Kural 708 says it is enough to stand and look at the face of those who read the inward; Pazhamozhi 145 says for those who know faces the face suffices to know intention, as a mirror shows one's unseen limbs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural speaks of the petitioner who needs only show his face to a perceptive master, and the Pazhamozhi gives the reason it suffices: the face works as a mirror that makes the unseen inward visible.","la":"its mirror image supplies the mechanism the couplet assumes, and its proverb frames face and mind as offspring and source."},{"a":"pz:146","b":"n:127","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 127 asks who can examine another's mind, since minds and deeds differ; Pazhamozhi 146 opens with nearly the same words (ஒருவர் ஒருவர்தம் உள்ளத்தைத் தேரும்) and grants the difficulty, but answers that people take the colour of their kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two share the question almost word for word and part on the answer: the Naladiyar leaves the mind unknowable because conduct misleads, while the Pazhamozhi finds an outside mark, lineage, that it trusts more than conduct.","la":"its proverb turns the Naladiyar's closing despair into a method, making kin the visible plumage of a hidden nature."},{"a":"pz:146","b":"k:959","type":"develops","note":"Kural 959 says a sprout shows the soil and speech shows the lineage one was born in; Pazhamozhi 146 runs the inference the other way, using lineage to read the hidden mind, as land shows in a bird's colour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Kural lineage is what is inferred from speech, and in the Pazhamozhi lineage is the evidence from which the mind is inferred, so the pair shows kin serving as both sign and signified in this ethics.","la":"its bird image matches the Kural's soil image but makes kin the key to another's mind rather than the thing to be found.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:147","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 holds birth alike for all living beings and distinction to come from the work done; Pazhamozhi 147 says one's own birth-group (தஞ்சாதி) shows through any concealment, as in the keeper's daughter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair sets two views of what makes a person's standing side by side: for the Kural deeds make the difference, for this verse birth leaves a mark no change of house can hide.","la":"its exemplum and proverb make birth an ineradicable trait shown in a habit of eating, where the couplet makes birth equal and conduct the divider."},{"a":"pz:147","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 195 calls good and bad caste mere words and makes lineage from austerity, learning and effort; Pazhamozhi 147 says one's own சாதி breaks through concealment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the same group of ethics works the Naladiyar denies that caste has substance while the Pazhamozhi treats it as a nature that betrays itself, so the group holds no single view of birth.","la":"its story gives birth an observable trait, which is just what the quatrain denies birth has."},{"a":"pz:148","b":"k:454","type":"develops","note":"Kural 454 says understanding seems to be in the mind but lies in one's company; Pazhamozhi 148 makes company the test by which others, sages included, are known.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's claim about where understanding lives becomes, in the Pazhamozhi, a rule of evidence: if a person's nature is in their company, then the company is what an observer should read, even of a sage.","la":"it extends the couplet to the judging of முனிவர் and sets company above witnesses and inquiry as proof.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:148","b":"pz:146","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 146 reads a hidden mind by kin; Pazhamozhi 148 reads it by the company kept, without inquiry or witnesses.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Within one heading the book offers two outward marks of an inner nature, the group one is born into and the group one chooses, and 148 adds that the second applies even to renouncers who have left their kin."},{"a":"pz:149","b":"k:442","type":"tension","note":"Kural 442 bids one cherish as support those who remove an affliction that has come and guard against one to come; Pazhamozhi 149 says one need not seek helpers at all, since each is his own physician.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural's helper is a physician to the king, and Pazhamozhi takes the same healing image back for the self, so the two disagree on whether one's trouble can be cured from outside.","la":"its proverb turns the couplet's healer into the striver himself, denying that another can do one's own work."},{"a":"pz:150","b":"k:410","type":"tension","note":"Kural 410 sets the unlearned to the learned as beasts to men; Pazhamozhi 150 calls wise the man who finishes his task even if he has learned nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read together, the two give rival measures of a person: the Kural ranks by what one has studied, Pazhamozhi by what one has completed, and on its reading the unlearned doer outranks the idle learned.","la":"its proverb moves rank from learning and age to finished work, a criterion the couplet does not admit."},{"a":"pz:151","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81 says household life exists for tending guests and doing them good (விருந்தோம்பி வேளாண்மை); Pazhamozhi 151 uses the same pair of words and sets battle valour and freedom from sloth beside them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the Kural's purpose of the household needs a means it does not name: hospitality is sustained only by toil, so giving to guests stands with valour as a fruit of effort.","la":"it puts hospitality, beneficence and sword-valour in one series closed by toil, making giving an outcome of effort rather than the end of the household alone."},{"a":"pz:152","b":"k:611","type":"develops","note":"Kural 611 forbids slackening because a task seems hard; Pazhamozhi 152 forbids it because a single effort seems too small to help.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they close both exits from effort: the Kural answers the excuse that the task is too great, Pazhamozhi the excuse that one's part is too small.","la":"it names the opposite excuse to the couplet's and gives the cost, not surviving even the day."},{"a":"pz:153","b":"n:194","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 153 promises that the unwearied never fail to reach their aim; Naladiyar 194 makes manliness standing firm even when the thing does not succeed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair splits the reward of effort: Pazhamozhi gives the friendless striver success as his warrant, while the Naladiyar holds that persistence is the measure even when success does not come.","la":"it ties effort to the excuse of having no kin and guarantees success, where the quatrain detaches manliness from success."},{"a":"pz:154","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"Kural 471 bids one weigh the strength of the deed, one's own, the opponent's and the allies' before acting; Pazhamozhi 154 weighs oneself, one's helpers and the gain, using the same verb தூக்கி.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi adds the gain to the Kural's weighing of strengths and says what follows without it, so the reckoning is shown to be of purpose as well as of power.","la":"it replaces the enemy's strength with the gain in the reckoning and adds, through its proverb, the formless result of unweighed action."},{"a":"pz:155","b":"pn:39","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 39 tells a Chola that, with ancestors who destroyed the hanging fort, killing is not his own fame; Pazhamozhi 155 recalls the same Chola feat of the fort in the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem treats the hanging fort as inherited glory a descendant cannot claim, while the ethics verse turns it into a lesson anyone can use: the fort fell to force applied by the right means."},{"a":"pz:156","b":"pz:153","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses forbid pleading 'I have no kin' as a reason to fail; 153 answers it with unwearied effort, 156 with the image of the long-armed man's great shoulders.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the heading's single counsel argued two ways, by the promise of success and by the sign of strength in one's own body, so the lack of kin is answered both by outcome and by capacity."},{"a":"pz:157","b":"k:691","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 691 tells those who attach themselves to kings to stay like fire-warmers, neither far nor near; Pazhamozhi 157 calls those who seek wealth through kings a crow on a pestle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural offers a way to keep a safe place by the king, and Pazhamozhi doubts that such a place can be kept: the courtier's perch moves with every stroke.","la":"its proverb makes court dependence itself unstable and sets it against wealth won by effort, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"pz:158","b":"k:517","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 517 says to examine who will finish a task by what means and then leave it to him; Pazhamozhi 158 shows the task handed out of sloth to those without firm grip, while the master too lets go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's letting go is safe only after examination; Pazhamozhi shows the same handing-over done from sloth, so delegation is a virtue or a ruin depending on why it is done.","la":"it names the motive, sloth, and the double loss of grip, which the couplet's rule of choosing assumes away.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:159","b":"pz:149","type":"develops","note":"149 says no one does for another what that other must do; 159 applies it to the man who waits to have his task done later by someone else.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"159 supplies the evidence for 149's flat denial: the helper one waits for will not even heat water, so he will not leap into fire for one."},{"a":"pz:160","b":"n:110","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 110 says what was set in the early mark neither shrinks nor grows and comes as due; Pazhamozhi 160, on the reading that the smearer rewrites the leaf, says a faulty written fate can be remade.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give opposite answers to what one should do before a fixed fate: the Naladiyar counsels calm acceptance, Pazhamozhi effort that rewrites the leaf, and both reject grieving or idle worship.","la":"its proverb casts fate as a palm-leaf document that a later hand can correct, against the quatrain's mark that cannot change."},{"a":"pz:160","b":"k:620","type":"develops","note":"Kural 620 says those who toil without flagging see even fate's back; Pazhamozhi 160 says folded hands before the one who fixed fate end no trouble.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi names the rival of effort that the Kural leaves unstated, pious resignation, and replaces the Kural's battle image with a scribe correcting a leaf.","la":"it sets worship of the fixer of fate against effort, making resignation itself the error."},{"a":"pz:161","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"Kural 612 warns against letting a task die midway, since the world's regard follows how one deals with unfinished work; Pazhamozhi 161 says what broke off unfinished has no increase and what does not exist has no name.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi states as a law of naming what the Kural states as social regard: the half-done work does not earn a poor name but no name at all.","la":"its four parallel clauses rank the finished, the broken-off and the capable, ending in the proverb that the undone has no name."},{"a":"pz:163","b":"k:814","type":"develops","note":"Kural 814 prefers solitude to allies like an untrained horse that throws its rider in battle; Pazhamozhi 163 describes those who stir their own side into a quarrel and flee first.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's ally fails at the decisive moment; Pazhamozhi adds that the same man often made the battle, so the desertion is the second half of a single betrayal that begins with words.","la":"its proverb of the broken food-bowl names the fault as ingratitude to one's own support, where the couplet only weighs such company against being alone."},{"a":"pz:164","b":"k:672","type":"develops","note":"Kural 672 sorts tasks into those to be done slowly and those to be done at once; Pazhamozhi 164 says work with intimates must be carried patiently, since haste ruins it.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves open how to tell the slow task from the urgent one; Pazhamozhi supplies one test, the closeness of the people involved, and the cost of getting it wrong, that the work turns into trouble.","la":"it places the slow kind of task in the circle of one's intimates and adds the condition of keeping to the spoken word, which the couplet does not name.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:165","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 504 tells the employer to examine both good qualities and faults and take what predominates; Pazhamozhi 165 says not to voice an unsteady helper's faults but to take the benefit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's weighing of faults happens in the employer's mind; Pazhamozhi adds that once the helper is in use, saying the faults aloud is itself a fault, so the examination must stay silent.","la":"its proverb of the holed appam turns the weighing into a rule of speech: the faults are counted in choosing and not counted again in the using."},{"a":"pz:166","b":"k:1078","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word and the base only when crushed like cane; Pazhamozhi 166 says to win help by affection, as forcing it is milking a cow with an arrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two divide the means by the person: affection draws service from the ordinary helper while force kills what it wants, and the couplet marks the base as the exception who yield only under pressure.","la":"its paired images of the calf let in and the arrow loosed rank gentle means over force for ordinary dealings, which the couplet, speaking only of two extremes, does not do."},{"a":"pz:169","b":"n:355","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the chisel that will not cut even into a tender shoot unless struck; the Naladiyar applies it to the base, Pazhamozhi 169 to any agent left to do a task.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar makes the unstruck chisel a mark of base character, but Pazhamozhi uses the same saying of the employer's duty to everyone he employs, so the image shifts blame from the worker's nature to the master's need to keep directing.","la":"the quatrain uses the chisel to sort people into kind and harsh-ruled; the Pazhamozhi makes it a rule for the master after the handing over, a stage the quatrain never reaches."},{"a":"pz:169","b":"pz:166","type":"qualifies","note":"166 says service should be won by affection, not forced; 169 says even entrusted work needs striking like a chisel.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two adjacent counsels separate winning a helper from running him: affection is the way to gain his will, but once the task is his, the master's prompting is still needed, so gentleness at the start does not excuse absence later."},{"a":"pz:170","b":"n:345","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 345 says a dog fed from a golden bowl still watches for others' leftovers, so honouring the base does not change their deeds; Pazhamozhi 170 says setting the base on a task and sitting back is setting a cat to guard fish heads.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain states the base man's fixed appetite; Pazhamozhi turns it into a charge against the employer, whose fault is not only the choice but sitting idle afterwards, so the base nature becomes the master's loss.","la":"it moves the quatrain's observation into the employer's domain and adds the second fault of leaving the agent unwatched.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:171","b":"k:503","type":"develops","note":"Kural 503 says even those who have studied hard books and are flawless are rarely without some blankness; Pazhamozhi 171 says even one who has learnt and is rid of faults should not get a task without clear practical knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verbal echo (learned and faultless) makes the pair a step: the couplet admits learning leaves gaps, and Pazhamozhi draws the employer's conclusion that learning and faultlessness alone do not qualify a man for a task.","la":"it adds the decision the couplet leaves open, that the work should wait rather than go to the learned but incapable.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:172","b":"k:515","type":"develops","note":"Kural 515 says work is not to be given to anyone but one who knows and can do it, merely because he is favoured; Pazhamozhi 172 says not to ask whether a man is kin but to look only at learning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet bars favour as a ground; Pazhamozhi names the commonest favour, kinship, and adds a concession the couplet lacks, that even a disobedient learned agent costs no more than the salt fed to a working ox.","la":"its proverb weighs the risk of the learned stranger against kin and finds the risk cheap, a cost-reckoning the couplet does not make."},{"a":"pz:173","b":"pz:168","type":"develops","note":"168 says even one's own cart needs grease, so an agent takes his small share; 173 says wealth always clings to those who handle it, so careless handlers must not be chosen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two verses accept the handler's share as unavoidable and then limit it: the master budgets for the grease but chooses men who care for the work, so the cut stays a காணி and does not become flight with the goods."},{"a":"pz:174","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"Kural 510 says doubting one who has been trusted brings endless trouble; Pazhamozhi 174 says that if an appointee proves bad he should be let go without public exposure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns against doubt of a trusted man; Pazhamozhi takes the harder case where the doubt is proven, and still counsels quiet removal, so the concern in both is the damage done by open distrust.","la":"it adds the case the couplet does not face, the appointee who has truly failed, and the manner of removing him."},{"a":"pz:175","b":"k:520","type":"develops","note":"Kural 520 tells the ruler to look daily into those who do his work; Pazhamozhi 175 says raising untrustworthy men and leaving them far off is setting a tortoise loose in a pond and asking it to return.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet prescribes daily oversight; Pazhamozhi shows what its absence looks like, an agent out of sight and in his own element, and adds that the fault is compounded when the agent lacked honesty of mind from the start.","la":"its proverb joins two errors the couplet keeps apart from choosing, raising the wrong man and sending him beyond sight."},{"a":"pz:176","b":"k:664","type":"develops","note":"Kural 664 says speaking is easy for anyone and doing as spoken is hard; Pazhamozhi 176 says the bystander's subtle advice is easy while the doer who enters without inner knowledge perishes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets saying against doing in one person; Pazhamozhi splits them between two, the adviser at the edge of the board and the player in it, so the cost of easy speech falls on someone else.","la":"its dice-board proverb puts the speaker and the doer in different seats, which the couplet's single subject does not."},{"a":"pz:176","b":"k:677","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 677 says the doer should learn the mind of one who knows the task from inside; Pazhamozhi 176 warns against the bystander who speaks fine words but does not know the task's hidden state.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet tells the doer to take counsel; Pazhamozhi adds which counsel to refuse, that of the onlooker who talks subtly from outside, so the test of an adviser is inner knowledge, not subtlety.","la":"it sets the false adviser beside the true one the couplet names, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"pz:167","b":"n:350","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 350 says small bullocks never learn to draw the cart however well fed; Pazhamozhi 167 says forcing work from a slack man is ploughing with an idle old bullock.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says care cannot supply a missing capacity; Pazhamozhi adds that pressure cannot either, so between them neither feeding nor driving makes the unfit beast or man do the work.","la":"it turns the quatrain's verdict on the base into a rule for the employer about sloth and the use of force."},{"a":"pz:177","b":"k:506","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 506 warns against trusting those cut off from kin, since ties make a person accountable; Pazhamozhi 177 says kinship or friendship is no ground for trusting anyone with one's weak point.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show ties as a necessary but not sufficient condition of trust: the kinless are not to be trusted, yet kin are not to be trusted with one's exposed point either, since even a god acted for his own ends.","la":"its proverb grounds the limit in a universal claim proved from a god's conduct, so the couplet's reliance on attachment meets a rule that self-interest outruns every bond."},{"a":"pz:178","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum that proclaims every secret heard; Pazhamozhi 178 likens the choosing of a mean confidant to putting the stick to the drum.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The drum image moves from the base man to his chooser: the Kural blames the drum for sounding, Pazhamozhi blames the one who sets the stick to it, so the leak begins in a bad choice of confidant over worthy kin.","la":"it sets the mean confidant against kin rich in excellence, turning the couplet's description of the base into a fault of selection by the one who confides.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:180","b":"k:1076","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1076 makes carrying on (உய்த்து) secrets the mark of the base; Pazhamozhi 180 says that even pleasant friends cannot refrain from passing on (உயவாது) what they heard, then restricts the rule to the base.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with the Kural, the verse shows the trait the couplet confines to the base is near-universal, so keeping secrets from the base is a minimum measure, not a sufficient safeguard.","la":"it widens the couplet's claim from the base to everyone, even agreeable friends, before narrowing it again in the proverb, a turn the couplet lacks."},{"a":"pz:180","b":"tk:55","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 55 counts a friendship that does not keep a weighty secret as a spy; Pazhamozhi 180 says that even among pleasant friends no one keeps back what he heard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the leaking friend is not a rare traitor but the common case, which is why a counted work can number him among the spies inside one's circle."},{"a":"pz:181","b":"k:847","type":"develops","note":"Kural 847 says the man without understanding who lets slip the precious secret (அருமறை) harms himself; Pazhamozhi 181 says telling the precious secret (அருமறை) to the small-hearted is cotton carded on a palm-top.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair names one concrete way the secret slips: handing it to a small heart, and the image shows why the harm the Kural calls self-inflicted cannot be recalled once done.","la":"it opposes the noble keepers to the small-hearted hearers, adding to the couplet's lone fool a ranking of who can hold a secret at all."},{"a":"pz:182","b":"k:509","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 509 bids one trust no one without examination; Pazhamozhi 182 applies this even to the affectionate and says the unexamined truster perishes.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pairing extends the Kural's rule to the case where it is hardest to keep, affection, and fixes the stake at death rather than mere trouble.","la":"it ranks the hostile, who need no testing because they are plainly not to be told, below the affectionate, who must be tested, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"pz:179","b":"n:127","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 179 permits telling a secret once the hearer's love is known; Naladiyar 127 asks who has the power to know another's mind, since mind and deed differ.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Held together they show the condition Pazhamozhi sets may never be met: if love cannot be read from conduct, the counsel to wait for known love becomes a counsel of near-total silence.","la":"the Naladiyar quatrain denies the knowledge of minds that Pazhamozhi's proverb of the hunting trail presupposes can be had."},{"a":"pz:183","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"Kural 510 says trusting without examining brings endless trouble; Pazhamozhi 183 gives the case of a ruler who gave without weighing the one who came and lost the world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the couplet's untested trust covers generosity too: the open-handed king who does not weigh the asker suffers the very trouble the Kural assigns to careless trust.","la":"its story turns the couplet's abstract fault into a royal gift, so the failure to examine is shown in the act the ethics books most praise, giving."},{"a":"pz:185","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"Kural 423 asks for the truth of whatever is heard from any mouth; the proverb of Pazhamozhi 185 asks that even what one saw be weighed before it is spoken.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair extends testing from what one hears to what one has seen and from judging to speaking: the Kural doubts others' report, the proverb doubts one's own eyes as a ground for testimony.","la":"it moves the test from the hearer's understanding to the witness's speech before a king, adding the shame that makes a worthy man hold back."},{"a":"pz:187","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the millet grain against the palmyra: in Kural 104 a millet-sized kindness is reckoned palm-sized, in Pazhamozhi 187 a millet-grain's gain near at hand is preferred and the palm is the tree too slow to feed its planter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same millet-and-palm scale serves two judgements of value: the Kural enlarges the small out of gratitude, Pazhamozhi prefers the small because it is near and certain, so the image teaches valuing the small for moral and for prudential reasons.","la":"it makes the palm stand for delay rather than size, adding time to a scale the couplet uses only for magnitude."},{"a":"pz:191","b":"k:99","type":"develops","note":"Kural 99 asks why one who sees the sweetness sweet speech yields would use harsh words; Pazhamozhi 191 asks the same of those who can tell good speech from mean, and answers that only mean speech brings trouble.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two rest one choice on two motives: the Kural on the good that sweet speech brings, Pazhamozhi on the danger harsh speech brings back to the speaker, so the case for sweet speech is both gain and safety.","la":"its proverb turns the couplet's rhetorical question into a rule about risk, that sweet speech never endangers, which the couplet does not state."},{"a":"pz:191","b":"tk:24","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 191 says sweet speech never brings anyone into trouble; Thirikadugam 24 names a courtesan's sweet words as a mire that sinks those who step in.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the proverb holds for the speaker, not the hearer: one's own sweet speech is safe, but another's sweet speech may be bait, so sweet speech protects the one who uses it and can trap the one who trusts it."},{"a":"pz:194","b":"k:277","type":"agrees","note":"Both close on உடைத்து, 'the world holds such': Kural 277 those bright outside like the kunri seed and black within, Pazhamozhi 194 thieves like peacocks who seem attached by day and rob by night.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi moves the Kural's hidden-black ascetic into the household: the false outside belongs to dependants who live with one, and the betrayal is a theft of food and clothing in the hours of sleep.","la":"it gives the couplet's single colour image a sequence of acts, night theft and daylight show of attachment, which makes the deceit something one can watch for."},{"a":"pz:194","b":"tk:51","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 51 says deceivers stay deceivers even when they keep close and that examination shows them; Pazhamozhi 194 shows deceivers who keep close, appearing attached by day while stealing by night.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair says both that closeness proves nothing and what the examination must look for: the gap between the day's show of attachment and the night's acts.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:195","b":"k:426","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 426 says understanding lives as the world lives; Pazhamozhi 195 closes on running with the town's way, but first asks for repeated private scrutiny of what one has seen without heeding others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that going with the world is the end of a private test, not a substitute for it: Pazhamozhi puts one's own repeated examination before conformity, as the Kural's chapter sets 423 beside 426.","la":"it ranks the counsels in order, not abandoning dependants, not leaving the renouncers' path, examining for oneself, and only then conforming, so conformity comes last."},{"a":"pz:196","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"Kural 759 calls wealth the sharpest steel for cutting down enemies' pride (செருக்கறுக்கும்); Pazhamozhi 196 says wealth given cuts off (அறுத்தல்) the senseless better than a spear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows how the Kural's metaphor works in practice: wealth wounds not by being held but by being handed over, so the sharpest blade is a payment that leaves the opponent nothing to resist.","la":"its proverb sets the gift and the spear side by side and ranks the gift above force, a contrast the couplet only implies."},{"a":"pz:197","b":"k:753","type":"develops","note":"Kural 753 calls wealth an unfailing lamp that cuts darkness wherever one goes; Pazhamozhi 197 says the rich see well even in the sea, while the same affairs bring the poor to grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two make wealth a means of sight as well as of power: the Kural's lamp travels to any land, and Pazhamozhi carries it into the sea, the place where sight should fail, and adds the poor man for whom the same road is dark.","la":"it sets the rich man's ease against the poor man's trouble in the same affairs, a contrast the couplet does not draw.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:198","b":"n:283","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 283 says bees do not go to the hill where the kanthal has not flowered, so the poor have no kin; Pazhamozhi 198 says no one who dug a tank sought out the frogs, so the rich need not seek those who need them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two nature images state one law from both sides: people are drawn by what can be had, so the rich are sought without seeking and the poor are left without kin, and Pazhamozhi presents as a comfort what the Naladiyar presents as a grief.","la":"it states the attraction from the side of the owner, who may wait, where the quatrain states it from the side of the poor man who is abandoned."},{"a":"pz:199","b":"k:752","type":"develops","note":"Kural 752 says all honour the rich and all scorn the poor; Pazhamozhi 199 says even the compassionate praise the rich and gives the reason in its proverb, that two take from the fortunate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the fact; Pazhamozhi names whom 'all' includes, even those whose compassion should look to need, and explains the praise by interest, since everyone shares in what the rich hold.","la":"it divides the Kural's 'all' into the compassionate and the rest and supplies a motive, a reason the couplet does not give."},{"a":"pz:200","b":"n:280","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 280 counts guarding wealth among its bitter sufferings (காத்தலும் ஆங்கே கடுந்துன்பம்); Pazhamozhi 200 says the wealth one guards guards its owner in return, like the thicket and the tiger. The tension holds on the plain reading of both: the Jain quatrain treats guarding as a cost, the proverb as a mutual defence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two books judge the same act of keeping wealth oppositely: for the Naladiyar the guard is one more place of suffering, for Pazhamozhi it is what makes wealth a shelter, so the dispute is whether wealth is a burden on its owner or a defence of him.","la":"its proverb makes guarding reciprocal, which turns the quatrain's one-way burden into an exchange of protection."},{"a":"pz:201","b":"k:478","type":"develops","note":"Kural 478 says a small income brings no ruin if outgo is not wider; Pazhamozhi 201 says a small income gathered daily grows great, like drops into a flood.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair gives two answers to the small income: the Kural makes it safe by limiting what goes out, Pazhamozhi makes it great by what accumulates over time, so a narrow channel is both a limit to respect and a source that time can enlarge.","la":"it adds the daily repetition and a second image of gathered light, turning the couplet's balance of channels into growth by accumulation."},{"a":"pz:202","b":"k:667","type":"develops","note":"Kural 667 says not to despise anyone for their form, since the great chariot has its linchpin; Pazhamozhi 202 uses the same words (எள்ளாமை வேண்டும்) of one whose small beginnings are known in town, since the market calf may become a bull.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two give different grounds for the same rule: the Kural's small linchpin matters now by where it stands, while Pazhamozhi's calf matters later by what it grows into, so contempt can err about function or about time.","la":"it moves the rule from bodily size to known origin or capital, and grounds it in growth over time rather than in present function."},{"a":"pz:203","b":"pz:201","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 201 says small drops gathered make a flood; Pazhamozhi 203 says the licks of a jackal do not dry the sea, so petty thefts do not hurt great wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Side by side, the two verses show that the book's arithmetic of small amounts runs only toward growth: daily drops add up to a flood, but daily thefts are not allowed to add up to loss, because the scale of the whole decides which small things count."},{"a":"pz:204","b":"k:1043","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1043 says poverty destroys at once one's ancient descent and dignity; Pazhamozhi 204 says the great known to the land, though fallen poor, keep their standing, since no dog sniffs at a tiger's head. The tension depends on reading the couplet's தொல்வரவு as inherited standing, as its marks do.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two disagree on whether poverty can strip inherited greatness: the Kural says it takes descent and dignity together, while Pazhamozhi holds that renown already known to the land outlasts the wealth that won it.","la":"its proverb of the dog and the tiger's head adds the onlookers, the mean who do not dare, where the couplet speaks only of what the poor man loses."},{"a":"pz:204","b":"pz:89","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 89 says the poverty of the great is better than the wealth of the small, as old ghee never sinks below buttermilk; Pazhamozhi 204 says the fallen great are not insulted by the mean.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two verses build the book's case that greatness is not measured by wealth: 89 ranks the poor great above the rich small in worth, and 204 adds that others still treat them so, since the tiger's head is safe from dogs."},{"a":"pz:205","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"Kural 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming are not shaken by affliction; Pazhamozhi 205 applies that foresight to one's own wealth and to the prosperity of one's kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's foresight is a general shield against calamity; the snail that stores water shows it as stockpiling for a known dry season, so foresight becomes provision for kin as well as self.","la":"it joins one's own wealth and the kin's prosperity as one object of forethought, which the couplet leaves unnamed, and gives the snail as the instance."},{"a":"pz:206","b":"k:508","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 508 warns that trusting an outsider (பிறன்) without examining him brings lasting trouble; Pazhamozhi 206 refuses even eminent men and kin the trust of one's goods until they are tested.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural's danger is the untested stranger; Pazhamozhi removes the exemption kinship might seem to give, so testing applies inside the family too, and adds that the loss of entrusted wealth cannot be reversed.","la":"it names the two pretexts for trust, standing and kinship, and sets both aside, which the couplet does not, since it speaks only of the outsider.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:208","b":"n:280","type":"tension","note":"Both speak of guarding ஈட்டிய ஒண்பொருள், earned bright wealth: the Naladiyar calls that guarding bitter suffering and wealth the dwelling of suffering, while Pazhamozhi 208 counts guarding it oneself a duty and handing it carelessly to others a folly.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"On the same phrase the Jain quatrain makes custody of earned wealth a burden to be shed, by giving it away, while Pazhamozhi makes it a charge never to be delegated, so the two disagree on whether keeping earned wealth is worth its care.","la":"its crow proverb makes the careless handing over the fault, where the quatrain's list of sufferings makes the guarding itself the fault."},{"a":"pz:209","b":"n:350","type":"same_image","note":"Both use grass fed to a bullock that will not work: the Naladiyar's small bullocks never take the yoke however much cut grass they eat, and Pazhamozhi 209's grass is fed to a sluggish ox.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar uses the ox to show that the base cannot be improved by feeding, while Pazhamozhi uses it to show the feeder's loss, so the one image condemns the base man's nature in one book and the owner's choice of keeper in the other.","la":"it moves the image from the base man who has come into wealth to the wealth a householder hands to a man of no family, making the image a counsel on custody."},{"a":"pz:209","b":"k:1000","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1000 says great wealth got by a man without courtesy spoils like good milk in a faulty vessel; Pazhamozhi 209 says wealth placed in the hands of a man not of good family is grass fed to a sluggish ox.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural blames the holder's character for spoiling his own wealth, while Pazhamozhi turns this into a rule for the owner who chooses a keeper, and makes birth rather than courtesy the test.","la":"it carries the couplet's claim from the man who owns wealth to the man to whom wealth is entrusted, and its proverb makes the loss a waste with no return rather than a spoiling."},{"a":"pz:210","b":"k:535","type":"develops","note":"Kural 535 says the man who slipped by not guarding beforehand grieves afterwards over his own error; Pazhamozhi 210 says neglecting what one had and searching to get it back later is catching a peacock with butter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves the negligent man with regret, while Pazhamozhi follows him into his attempt to undo the loss and shows the attempt itself as absurd, so the cost of neglect is wasted effort as well as grief.","la":"its proverb mocks the method of recovery, a stage the couplet does not reach, and fixes the neglect on wealth one already had."},{"a":"pz:206","b":"pz:208","type":"develops","note":"206 bars entrusting wealth to untested kin and eminent men; 208 bars handing it carelessly to worthless men; both turn on wealth put into another's hands.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together, the two verses show the danger of entrusting wealth from both sides: the keeper may be untested though respectable, or plainly base, and in each case the owner's own lapse is the fault.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:212","b":"pz:211","type":"develops","note":"211 says wealth let out of hand does not come back; 212 says a lender who let a loan lie and then asks for it is met with the borrower's anger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"212 supplies the mechanism behind 211's maxim: lent wealth fails to return not only by loss but because asking for it turns the borrower hostile, so the loan costs the friendship as well as the goods."},{"a":"pz:212","b":"sp:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 9 says a debtor's tongue dies when he once sees his creditor; Pazhamozhi 212 says the borrower asked to repay grows angry as if at an enemy.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Sirupanchamoolam gives the debtor's shame and Pazhamozhi his anger, and Pazhamozhi ties the anger to the lender's long delay, so shame is seen to turn into resentment once a loan has lain unclaimed."},{"a":"pz:213","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"Kural 113 bids one let go at once any gain that comes from leaving fairness, even if it brings good; Pazhamozhi 213 says the good do not even desire such gain, and gives the reason in the proverb.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's command assumes the unfair gain may truly bring good; the proverb denies even that, so renouncing it becomes good sense rather than sacrifice.","la":"its proverb and the image of the onrushing wave add a mechanism, that the gain's apparent growth is the first stage of its loss, which the couplet leaves out."},{"a":"pz:213","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 659 says what is taken with tears goes with tears; Pazhamozhi 213 says evil gains perish as they seem to thrive.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two make ill-gotten wealth self-cancelling twice over: it leaves the way it came, and its swelling is the very shape of the leaving.","la":"it sets the claim as a choice the good make in advance, refusing to want such wealth, where the couplet only forecasts its end."},{"a":"pz:214","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1008 likens the wealth of the unloved to a poison tree fruiting in the middle of town; Pazhamozhi 214 likens the wealth of the irresolute to a mango that fruits and draws the stick.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same fruiting tree cuts two ways: in the Kural the fruit is useless to the town, in Pazhamozhi it is coveted and brings blows on the tree, so wealth harms either its neighbours or its holder.","la":"it moves the fault from the owner's being unloved to his lacking tested resolve, and turns the tree from an emblem of uselessness into a target."},{"a":"pz:214","b":"k:1009","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1009 says others take the wealth gathered by giving up love and virtue; Pazhamozhi 214 says wealth draws attackers when its holder lacks firmness, though the wealth itself is good.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Losing wealth to others has two causes in the pair: a vice in how it was gathered and a weakness in who holds it, so even rightly gained wealth is not safe in weak hands.","la":"it adds a condition the couplet lacks, that the wealth may be நன்மையின் மாண்ட and still be lost, because the danger lies in the owner's want of resolve."},{"a":"pz:215","b":"n:278","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 278 says the miser's heirs do not give once the wealth is theirs; Pazhamozhi 215 says that the miser's kin too cling and grow tight while he lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, miserliness is shown as a household trait passed sideways to kin in life and down to heirs after death, not a private vice.","la":"it pairs not giving with not enjoying and sets the kin's clinging in the same breath, so the hoard is shown corrupting the whole circle around it."},{"a":"pz:216","b":"k:1005","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores; Pazhamozhi 216 says the drum-proclaimed wealth of such a man is a dog's coconut.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural says he has nothing; the proverb sharpens this to having something he cannot open, so the miser is not poor but locked out of his own possession.","la":"its proverb replaces the couplet's abstract 'nothing' with an image of possession without access, and the drum carries the claim to royal wealth."},{"a":"pz:217","b":"n:9","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 9 says the man who only guards his wealth without using or giving it will be thought of as one who has lost it; Pazhamozhi 217 says the non-giver's wealth is a loss like losing an eye with its beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both verses answer the miser's fear of loss by calling his keeping the real loss, and the proverb adds that it is a double loss of use and of looks.","la":"it grounds the claim in the fall of rulers of the whole earth, an argument from impermanence the quatrain does not make, and turns the miser's own word இழவு against him."},{"a":"pz:217","b":"k:1007","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1007 likens the non-giver's wealth to a beautiful woman growing old alone; Pazhamozhi 217 likens it to losing the eye together with its beauty.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both images make unused wealth a beauty wasted, but the Kural's beauty fades unseen while Pazhamozhi's is destroyed with the organ, so one is waste by neglect and the other by injury.","la":"its proverb replaces slow fading with sudden destruction, matching its frame of kings who vanished like a festival dance."},{"a":"pz:218","b":"k:228","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 228 asks whether the hard-eyed hoarders do not know the joy of giving; Pazhamozhi 218 says whether such hoarders fear sin or not makes no difference.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair close the hoarder's two exits: he lacks the joy of giving, and his fear of sin, which might have stood in for it, counts for nothing without the act.","la":"its proverb of the blind eye, the same shut or open, rules out piety without giving, a point the couplet's appeal to pleasure does not reach."},{"a":"pz:218","b":"pz:219","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 218 says the miser's fear of sin is idle; 219 says that those who give nothing toward the next birth keep their wealth and die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second verse supplies the consequence the first only implies: a fear of sin that does not give leaves the next birth unprovided.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:219","b":"k:1002","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1002 says the delusion of clinging without giving brings a மாணாப் பிறப்பு; Pazhamozhi 219 says those who give nothing for the coming birth hoard by attachment and die.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the bad birth as the fruit of hoarding; Pazhamozhi makes giving the provision for that birth, so not giving is a failure to pack for a journey.","la":"it frames giving as something owed to the birth that follows (படரும் பிறப்பு) and names the attachment (பற்று) that holds the wealth, and its proverb sets outer guarding against inner loss."},{"a":"pz:219","b":"n:273","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 273 says the fool who hoards and dies is mocked by his own stored wealth; Pazhamozhi 219 says those who keep their wealth and die guard the outer fence while the inner is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses use the same phrase of keeping and dying and give the hoarder two judges, his wealth's mockery and his own lost next birth.","la":"it adds the next birth as the thing lost, where the quatrain stops at the mockery of the wealth that outlived him."},{"a":"pz:220","b":"n:269","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 269 likens the giving of fools to rain poured into the sea while the paddy withers; Pazhamozhi 220 says leaving the hunger of kin and lovers unrelieved while helping strangers is like the black palmyra.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make misdirected giving a vice, not a virtue: the Naladiyar faults giving where there is no need, Pazhamozhi names the need passed over, the hunger of one's own kin.","la":"it names who is passed over, those who came with love and one's kin, where the quatrain gives only the image of the parched field."},{"a":"pz:221","b":"n:261","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 261 says no one counts on the wealth of the worthless, as bats shun the cracked wood-apple; Pazhamozhi 221 lists the faults of the ungiving and calls leaving them leaving an old field's stack.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain says people do not approach such wealth; Pazhamozhi adds a counsel, that walking away costs nothing, so shunning the miser becomes a choice, not an instinct.","la":"it lists the miser's faults side by side (no giving, no strength, harsh speech, no help in trouble), making stinginess one of a cluster of failures."},{"a":"pz:222","b":"iv:26","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 26 names as sweet the worth of not killing the hope of those who come in desire; Pazhamozhi 222 shows the hope-killer at work, feigning kindness and then withholding.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse names the virtue bare; Pazhamozhi shows its opposite as a staged deceit, the gift dangled like grass tied at a beast's neck."},{"a":"pz:222","b":"pz:225","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 222 shows a man who seems about to help and then dashes the asker's hope; 225 says an honest refusal is no fault but that killing hope forfeits the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the pair say the wrong is not in refusing but in raising hope and then crushing it, and 225 names the price of what 222 depicts."},{"a":"pz:223","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, belong to the well-born; Pazhamozhi 223 condemns pleading 'nothing' to those long close when one could help.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures the plea of poverty against birth; Pazhamozhi measures it against an old bond, so the same words are a fault of lineage in one and a wound to friendship in the other.","la":"it adds who is asking, old intimates, and its proverb of the hacked tree says what the false plea does to the bond, which the couplet does not consider."},{"a":"pz:224","b":"k:1067","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1067 begs all beggars not to beg of those who conceal; Pazhamozhi 224 says those who beg of hard-minded men get nothing, like seeking alms in a monastery.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the warning; Pazhamozhi gives the reason in an image, that the hard rich man yields no more than renouncers who own nothing.","la":"its proverb equates the hard-hearted rich with those who themselves live on alms, a comparison the couplet does not draw.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:224","b":"n:279","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 279 says the base yield only when strong men press them, like a cow milked by force; Pazhamozhi 224 says those who stand and beg before the hard-minded get nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together, the hard-hearted are moved by force but never by entreaty, so the beggar's mode of asking is the one sure to fail.","la":"it fixes the asker's posture, standing before them (முன்னின்று இரந்தார்), as the thing that yields nothing, where the quatrain sets yielding gladly against yielding under force."},{"a":"pz:225","b":"n:111","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 111 opens with nearly the same two lines as Pazhamozhi 225: refusing what one cannot give is no disgrace and the way of the world; it then says keeping the asker waiting till hope dies is worse than killing gratitude, where Pazhamozhi says the hope-killer has no world to go to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared lines show one maxim closed two ways, measured against ingratitude in the Naladiyar and against the next world in Pazhamozhi, so the same fault is given a moral rank and a cosmic penalty.","la":"the two verses share their first lines; what Pazhamozhi adds is its proverb, which turns the Naladiyar's comparison of faults into a sentence on the hope-killer's afterlife."},{"a":"pz:225","b":"pn:196","type":"agrees","note":"Purananuru 196, a bard to the Pandya Nanmaran, says that saying yes to what one can and no to what one cannot are both proper ways of a giver's friendship, while false promises and false refusals wound the asker and lessen the giver's fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bard's complaint to a king and the ethics verse state the same rule about refusal, which shows the norm of the honest 'no' is older than the ethics works and was first argued from a suppliant's side."},{"a":"pz:225","b":"k:1070","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1070 says the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal; Pazhamozhi 225 says refusing what one cannot give is no disgrace, and that the fault lies in keeping the asker in hope and then not sharing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural weighs every refusal by the asker's death of spirit; Pazhamozhi exempts the honest refusal and reserves the blame for the one who holds out hope and withholds.","la":"it divides refusal into the honest 'I have none' and the hope kept alive and killed, a distinction the couplet, which condemns concealment as such, does not make."},{"a":"pz:226","b":"k:224","type":"develops","note":"Kural 224 says being begged from is painful until one sees the asker's glad face; Pazhamozhi 226 says all that is given to those who do not rejoice in what was done is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair make the receiver's gladness the completion of the gift: the Kural says it ends the giver's pain, Pazhamozhi that without it the gift itself is wasted.","la":"it turns the couplet's psychology of the giver into a judgement on the recipient, setting the ungrateful who will not stand by one in need as the wasted object of giving."},{"a":"pz:226","b":"n:98","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 98 says to give to those who cannot repay is duty and giving to those who repay is only a loan; Pazhamozhi 226 says giving to those who do not rejoice in kindness is all loss.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they say what a gift may and may not ask in return: no repayment, but gladness, so a recipient who cannot repay is fit and one who will not rejoice is not.","la":"it adds a condition the quatrain lacks, the recipient's joy in the kindness, without retracting its rule against seeking return."},{"a":"pz:227","b":"k:619","type":"tension","note":"Kural 619 grants that the deity may deny the end but says effort still pays the wage of bodily strain; Pazhamozhi 227 asks what effort can do at all once the deity has settled the end.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Both start from the same concession, that தெய்வம் can deny the goal, and part over what is left: the Kural keeps a wage for effort, while Pazhamozhi leaves it nothing, so the pair locates the precise point where the two works divide on effort and fate.","la":"its proverb of a pit dug in vain makes effort's labour itself the loss, where the couplet saves that labour as a wage.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:228","b":"n:108","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 108 says that when the knowing do blameworthy things it comes from their past deeds; Pazhamozhi 228 says the works of renowned wise men go by their fortune, so fate defeats understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two verses show fate overriding knowledge in two places, in the wise man's conduct (the Naladiyar) and in the outcome of his works (Pazhamozhi), so knowing what is right secures neither right action nor success.","la":"it moves the quatrain's moral puzzle to the success of undertakings and seals it with a proverb that sets fate against understanding outright."},{"a":"pz:229","b":"pz:230","type":"develops","note":"Both proverbs turn on உறற்பால, what is bound to befall: 229 proves it by the moon's harm, 230 by an elephant that went to a man of Karuvur.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together the same word covers both directions of fate, harm that strikes the highest and good that finds a man who did not seek it, so உறற்பால in this chapter is neutral lot, not misfortune only."},{"a":"pz:230","b":"n:109","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 230's proverb repeats almost word for word Naladiyar 109's last line, that wanted or not what is bound to come can hardly be kept from touching one; the Naladiyar applies it to unwanted evil, Pazhamozhi to an elephant that went to a man of Karuvur.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a saying shared between the two works, and that Pazhamozhi re-aims it: the quatrain's line is a warning that unwanted evil cannot be fended off, and the elephant story makes it a promise that unsought good cannot be missed.","la":"it takes the quatrain's closing line as a proverb and backs it with a named episode, turning a general warning into a story of fortune arriving."},{"a":"pz:231","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"Kural 376 says what is not one's own will not stay though guarded, nor what is one's own leave though thrown away; Pazhamozhi 231 shows a palmyra guarded all night whose fruit still falls to the one it is due to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing turns the couplet's abstract 'guard' into a watchman shouting through the night under a tree, and adds a second person the couplet lacks, the passer-by who gets the fruit, so the loss of the guarder is someone else's unearned gain.","la":"it gives the couplet a scene with two people, guard and receiver, so fate's allotment is seen as a transfer, not only a keeping or a losing.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:232","b":"k:463","type":"develops","note":"Kural 463 says the wise do not undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain; Pazhamozhi 232 says those who did no great good deeds before cannot have great wealth later, for without capital there is no profit.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same trader's logic of capital and profit is prudence in the Kural and fate in Pazhamozhi, where the capital is earlier good deeds, so the pair shows the merchant's rule being moved from a counsel about risk to an account of merit.","la":"its proverb replaces the couplet's money capital with good deeds done before, so the capital cannot be raised now by any present skill.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:233","b":"n:93","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 93 says wealth gathers when its time to gather comes, whether one gives or withholds; Pazhamozhi 233 says the same of those who serve kings, by the vengai that flowers only on its day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses the timing of wealth to remove the miser's excuse, and Pazhamozhi uses it to remove the courtier's hope, so the same rule frees the giver to give and tells the servant of kings his service cannot hasten his reward.","la":"it carries the quatrain's rule from the giver's house to the king's court and gives it the image of a tree that flowers only on the right day."},{"a":"pz:234","b":"pz:239","type":"develops","note":"234 says the five got out of the lac house because there is no place without escape for one due to escape; 239 says the Chola's son survived burning and came to rule, for nothing is beyond those who keep their life.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both proverbs follow an escape from fire, one from the epic and one from Tamil royal memory, and the second carries the story past survival to kingship, so the chapter makes surviving the fire the first step of a fortune still unfolding."},{"a":"pz:235","b":"k:375","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 375 says that in making wealth the favourable can turn bad and the unfavourable good; Pazhamozhi 235 says that for those who are to prosper even what was judged bad turns out good, for the fortunate have no evil day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves it open which way a thing will turn, and Pazhamozhi decides it by the person: the same reversal runs only toward good for one with fortune, so the pair shows the couplet's instability resolved by whose lot it is.","la":"it makes the reversal depend on the person's fortune and ties it to the reckoning of good and bad days, which the couplet does not mention."},{"a":"pz:236","b":"k:618","type":"develops","note":"Kural 618 says lacking fortune is no one's disgrace; Pazhamozhi 236 says that men who reach a generous king and still get nothing are not to be called dull or small, since gain comes by one's lot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's general absolution becomes a defence of a particular figure, the empty-handed petitioner at court, and the pair shows the Kural's line on blame used to protect a man from the court's contempt rather than to urge him to work.","la":"it places the couplet's point at a king's court and adds a proverb of earned luck, so the failure is ascribed to unearned penance, not only excused.","gr":4},{"a":"pz:236","b":"n:31","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 31 shows those who did no penance before clinging to the outer door of a house, unable to enter; Pazhamozhi 236 says for those who did penance curry falls even into their rice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two verses give both ends of one belief about past penance and present food: one man shut out at the door looking in, the other finding food falling into his plate, so the pair draws the full scale between beggary and luck as the fruit of earlier penance.","la":"its proverb shows the reward side that the quatrain leaves implied, and sets it at a king's court rather than at a rich man's house."},{"a":"pz:237","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"Kural 620 says those who toil without flagging see even fate's back; Pazhamozhi 237 says effort is needless for the fortunate and fruitless for the unfortunate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both name ஆள்வினை and fate together and give opposite verdicts on the same contest, the Kural letting tireless labour outrun fate and Pazhamozhi denying labour any effect in either lot, on the plain reading of both.","la":"it splits people into two lots and denies effort a role in each, where the couplet speaks of the single toiler who prevails."},{"a":"pz:237","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 371 says the fate of gain produces tireless effort and the fate of loss produces sloth; Pazhamozhi 237 says the fortunate need no effort and the striving of the unfortunate is fruitless.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural keeps effort as fate's instrument, while Pazhamozhi makes it idle on both sides, so the pair shows two ways of subordinating effort to fate: as its product, or as beside the point.","la":"it pairs the two lots as the couplet does but cuts effort loose from them, so the fortunate prosper without it and the unfortunate strive in vain."},{"a":"pz:238","b":"n:33","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 33 says the fool breaks down when the fruit of past deeds arrives, while those who see it as old desert are freed of confusion; Pazhamozhi 238 says those who do not see their suffering as their old deed rage at the foes who cause it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain turns the knowledge of old deeds against grief, and Pazhamozhi turns it against anger at enemies, recasting the foe as a servant carrying out an order, so the same doctrine is shown ending both despair and revenge.","la":"its proverb of the servant who burns the town on orders gives the enemy a role, the agent of one's own deed, that the quatrain does not consider."},{"a":"pz:239","b":"pp:2:130-148","type":"develops","note":"Porunaratruppadai says Karikalan received his inheritance while still in the womb; Pazhamozhi 239 tells of a Chola's son who survived burning, mounted the great elephant and in the end ruled justly, a prince tradition identifies as Karikalan.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"If the traditional identification holds, the same accession is told once as a birthright fixed before birth and once as a survival against fire, and the pair shows the ethics work turning a praised royal memory into proof that a living man can still come to anything.","la":"it uses the heroic memory not to praise the king but as a proverb's evidence, so the Chola's rise becomes a general lesson about survival."},{"a":"pz:240","b":"n:110","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 110 says that since what was set at the start cannot change, there is no reason to grieve when ruin comes; Pazhamozhi 240 says those who know what is to be done do not fear their distress when fearful things come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both ask for calm before what fate brings, but the quatrain grounds it in the fixity of the lot while Pazhamozhi grounds it in knowing what to do, so the pair shows two routes to the same composure, resignation and readiness.","la":"it rests calm on knowing one's course of action, with an image of an elephant sheltering its mate, rather than on the quatrain's sum that cannot be altered."},{"a":"pz:241","b":"k:541","type":"develops","note":"Kural 541 defines justice as acting without favour to anyone; Pazhamozhi 241 names the hardest case of that 'anyone', one's own people.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's யார்மாட்டும் is abstract; the pairing shows where impartiality is actually tested, at the king's own kin, and names the failure there as softness of eye.","la":"its proverb turns கண்ணோடாது into a portrait of the king who fails it, the soft-eyed man who cannot punish, so the rule gains a named vice the couplet does not give."},{"a":"pz:241","b":"pz:256","type":"qualifies","note":"241 demands that the king turn hard-eyed and punish; 256 praises rulers who are not quickly angered even by great harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two separate severity from haste: the king must punish wrong without favour, but the rulers people shelter under are those slow to anger at harm done to them."},{"a":"pz:242","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki cites the Chola who crushed his own son under the chariot for a cow's grief; Pazhamozhi 242 tells the same episode and adds that the worthy had hidden the deed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kannaki uses the episode to shame a king who killed without inquiry; Pazhamozhi makes it a rule about age, so one exemplum serves both as indictment and as maxim."},{"a":"pz:242","b":"pz:6","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 6 has a young Chola who judges well despite his youth; 242 has a son not spared despite his youth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two Chola stories make youth irrelevant from both sides of the court: it neither disqualifies the judge nor excuses the accused."},{"a":"pz:243","b":"k:118","type":"develops","note":"Kural 118 praises leaning to no side like a level balance; Pazhamozhi 243 applies that to the king's dealing with rich and poor and pictures the lean as milk and water.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's balance shows only that something tilts; the pairing names who gains and loses, the rich getting milk and the poor water from one royal source.","la":"it moves the worthy man's balance into the king's court and names the two parties, rich and poor, that the couplet leaves unnamed."},{"a":"pz:244","b":"pn:184","type":"agrees","note":"Pisirandhaiyar tells the Pandya to take his due like an elephant fed paddy in balls, not one loosed in the field; Pazhamozhi 244 gives the same rule of revenue through the bee that takes and leaves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem warns one king of the ruin a greedy collection brings on him and his land; the ethics verse lifts the warning into a general rule of royal taking that should leave no mark at all."},{"a":"pz:245","b":"pz:246","type":"develops","note":"245 says even prosperous subjects should not bear excess levy; 246 says a king's favours funded by excess levy are like feeding a creature its own crest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two close both escapes for over-taxing: the subjects' prosperity does not justify it, and the king's later generosity does not redeem it."},{"a":"pz:246","b":"k:552","type":"develops","note":"Kural 552 likens a sceptred king's demand to a robber's 'hand it over'; Pazhamozhi 246 follows the extorted wealth into the king's gifts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet stops at the taking; the pairing shows the robbery disguised as largesse, so a king can be both robber and giver at once.","la":"it sets refuge-seekers harmed, crooked rule, excess levy and later favour in one sequence, so the extortion of 552 is followed to its use."},{"a":"pz:247","b":"mk:26","type":"develops","note":"Muthumozhikkanchi 26 says those under an unjust king do not openly censure him; Pazhamozhi 247 says they have no recourse at all.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair gives the reason for the silence: the people do not speak against the unjust king because, with the protector himself the taker, there is no one higher to cover them."},{"a":"pz:248","b":"pz:250","type":"develops","note":"248 says a king who lets things slip is scorned, since all scorn the weak; 250 says an unjust king is scorned by his own allies and yet needs some severity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they place the king between two contempts, for weakness and for injustice, and make a small severity within just rule the way between them.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:249","b":"k:763","type":"develops","note":"Kural 763 says a host of rats roaring like the sea perish at the cobra's breath; Pazhamozhi 249 says a gathered enemy army is nothing to a firm-minded king, one stone for a thousand crows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the strength in the cobra alone; the pairing traces it to the king's undivided mind, so numbers fail against resolve, not only against force.","la":"its condition, a king not divided in mind, gives the source of the one's power over the many that the couplet's image takes for granted."},{"a":"pz:250","b":"k:562","type":"develops","note":"Kural 562 tells the king to raise the rod sharply and bring it down lightly; Pazhamozhi 250 says kings need a little harshness, since even a cool stick raises the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both refuse a choice between mildness and terror, and the pairing shows two ways to split it: the couplet splits the act into threat and blow, the quatrain sets a small severity inside just rule.","la":"it adds the cost of failing at the other end, that even allies scorn a king who is not just, so severity is fenced by contempt on both sides.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:251","b":"pz:253","type":"develops","note":"251 says no rival word can rise under a king's command, as no tree grows under a tree; 253 says keeping would-be leaders on equal footing is living with a snake.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"251 states the fact of single authority and 253 the danger of breaking it, so together they explain why a king keeps the ambitious beneath him.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:252","b":"pz:253","type":"qualifies","note":"252, on a doubtful reading, says a ruler can unmake the men he raised; 253 warns that men raised to equal standing are a snake in the room.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, 253 limits the confidence of 252: the raised follower stays in the ruler's hand only while he is not raised to the ruler's level."},{"a":"pz:253","b":"k:890","type":"develops","note":"Kural 890 says living with those not in accord is like dwelling with a snake in a hut; Pazhamozhi 253 uses the same image for a king who keeps aspirants to leadership on his own level.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves open who the discordant companions are; the pairing names them in a court, ambitious men the king himself has raised, so the snake is one he brought in.","la":"it carries the household image into the king's domain and makes the king's own act of raising men the cause of the danger."},{"a":"pz:255","b":"k:507","type":"develops","note":"Kural 507 says trusting the ignorant out of fondness brings every folly; Pazhamozhi 255 says giving high dignity to the unrestrained is putting a firebrand in a monkey's hand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet counts the folly as the king's; the proverb shows it burning outward, since the monkey with fire harms everything near it, not only the one who handed it over.","la":"it moves the fault from ignorance to want of restraint and adds wealth to the trust given, so the harm is power and means in an unruly hand."},{"a":"pz:256","b":"k:155","type":"develops","note":"Kural 155 says the world keeps the forbearing like wrapped gold; Pazhamozhi 256 says the slow to anger rule the world and people shelter under them like a food tree.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the forbearing esteem; the pairing turns esteem into rule, so forbearance is shown as a ground of authority and not only of reputation.","la":"its image reverses the couplet's direction: the forbearing are not kept by others like gold but keep others under their shade."},{"a":"pz:257","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"Kural 443 says making the great one's own is the rarest of things; Pazhamozhi 257 shows Lanka's younger prince joining Rama and gaining Lanka.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks the act as rare; the pairing gives its reward in one epic case, and shows the bond with the great outweighing even the bond with a brother.","la":"it proves the couplet's claim by a story, and the story's cost, leaving a brother, is something the couplet's general claim does not face.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:258","b":"k:717","type":"develops","note":"Kural 717 says the learning of the learned shines among those who can examine words faultlessly; Pazhamozhi 258 says the fierce king's council shows a counsellor's breadth of learning as the flood shows the mountain's rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they make the assembly a gauge as well as a stage: the Kural needs fit judges for learning to shine, and the Pazhamozhi adds that what pours out there is the measure of what was stored unseen.","la":"its flood-and-rain proverb turns the couplet's claim about where learning shines into an inference from visible effect to hidden quantity, and sets it in a king's dangerous court."},{"a":"pz:259","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"Kural 638 makes it the duty of the one at the king's side to tell him his good even when he destroys counsel; Pazhamozhi 259 says the king's faults are laid on those beside him, since no doll is unlike its maker.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair gives the duty of plain counsel its sanction: because the world reads the king as his counsellors' making, the one who stays silent shares the guilt of what the king does.","la":"its doll-and-maker proverb adds the consequence the couplet leaves unsaid, that blame for the king's wrongs passes to his attendants.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:260","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"Kural 445 says counsellors are the ruler's eyes and must be chosen with care; Pazhamozhi 260 says one who has not made many around him his eyes is helpless in distress like a lute with cut strings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the eyes the Kural asks a ruler to choose are shown at the moment they are missing: in a crisis the king without them still has his state but can make no response, as a stringless lute keeps its body and loses its sound.","la":"it adds the number, many rather than one, and the crisis in which the lack is felt, with an image of form left without function.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:261","b":"k:441","type":"develops","note":"Kural 441 bids one weigh their worth and take the friendship of the wise who know virtue; Pazhamozhi 261 says to test by discussion those who weigh good and ill and hold them firm, as bull rubs against bull.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair names the means of the Kural's weighing, testing in set speech, and its fruit, a support that runs both ways as two bulls prop each other.","la":"its bull proverb makes the tie between chooser and chosen mutual, where the couplet speaks only of what the chooser gains."},{"a":"pz:262","b":"k:442","type":"develops","note":"Kural 442 bids one cherish those who can remove present affliction and guard against what has not come; Pazhamozhi 262 describes guardians restrained in mind, speech and body and says without them life perishes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the Kural's guarding helpers are, for the Pazhamozhi, first of all men disciplined in all three channels, so the ascetic's threefold restraint becomes the qualification of the king's protectors.","la":"it sets the guardians' power beside their threefold self-restraint as its ground and closes with the stark cost of lacking them."},{"a":"pz:263","b":"n:73","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 73 asks whether the harsh words of those who love are worse than strangers' smiling sweet words; Pazhamozhi 263 says good ministers press unwanted good on the king and check his evil, so elders' words are painful at first.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair carries the friend's welcome harshness up to the throne and adds time: the bitter word is painful only at first, and the minister must also push the king toward good he resists, not only rebuke.","la":"it moves the claim from friends to minister and king, adds the forward push to unwanted good beside the check on evil, and gives the time-order of pain then profit."},{"a":"pz:263","b":"k:448","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 448 says a ruler without the guard of those who rebuke perishes even with no enemy; Pazhamozhi 263 says the elders' counsel that saves him is painful at first.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair names the cost of the Kural's safeguard: the rebuke that keeps the king from ruin must be borne as pain before it is known as protection, which is why kings are tempted to do without it.","la":"it adds the cost to the hearer, painful first, which the couplet's plain statement of necessity leaves out."},{"a":"pz:264","b":"k:696","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 696 bids one know the king's mood and the time and say what is needed and not disliked; Pazhamozhi 264 says the wise, finding the king's favourable moment, will even use guile, as mothers soothe children with the moon.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Together they show how far reading the king's mood can go: the Kural stops at saying the welcome needful thing, while the Pazhamozhi lets the counsellor treat the king as a child to be steered by a pleasing deception.","la":"its mother-and-moon image adds deceit to the couplet's tact and casts the king as a child, a ranking of counsellor over king the couplet avoids."},{"a":"pz:265","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"Kural 691 says those who serve quarrelsome kings should be like men warming at a fire, neither too far nor too near; Pazhamozhi 265 says the experienced never even think of what would anger the mighty king, for nothing escapes his force.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the fire's danger from inside the servant's mind: the Kural regulates distance, and the Pazhamozhi says the only safety is never to give the king's anger cause, even when he is in the right.","la":"its doubled proverb of deer before tiger and straw before wind adds that once the king's anger rises there is no escape at any distance, so the caution moves from posture to thought."},{"a":"pz:265","b":"pz:263","type":"tension","note":"Pazhamozhi 263 praises ministers who afflict the king to tell him his good and stop his evil; Pazhamozhi 265, in the same heading, says the experienced never think of anything that would anger him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Side by side the two verses of one heading leave the counsellor between duty and survival: the good minister must hurt the king to save him, yet the king's anger is a tiger no one escapes, so the heading gives no single rule."},{"a":"pz:266","b":"pn:290","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 290 has a warrior's king give him toddy before drinking himself, and the warrior will take the spear meant for the king; Pazhamozhi 266 turns the toddy already drunk into the reason a king's man cannot shrink from his task.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the heroic custom of the king's toddy hardening into a courtier's proverb of obligation: what the war poem offers as honour freely returned, the ethics verse states as a debt that forbids hesitation."},{"a":"pz:267","b":"pz:266","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 266 says those who have eaten the king's food cannot shirk his work; 267 says those who have not yet been rewarded cannot refuse it either, since the one who commands will feed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together the two verses close both exits from service: the servant who has been paid owes the work, and the servant who has not been paid owes it too, so reward never becomes a condition the servant may set."},{"a":"pz:272","b":"n:167","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 167 says those who keep close to kings enjoy their wealth without needing to be worthy; Pazhamozhi 272 says whomever the king esteems the people esteem as well.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows royal nearness conferring not only goods but public standing: the Naladiyar's unworthy favourite gets the king's wealth, and Pazhamozhi adds that he gets the town's regard too, poured on from the king like ghee from a filled pot.","la":"its proverb of the ghee-filled vessel makes honour a thing that passes from container to container, adding the public as a second recipient that the quatrain's image of shade-giving trees does not have."},{"a":"pz:273","b":"k:363","type":"develops","note":"Kural 363 says there is no fine wealth like not wanting, here or hereafter; Pazhamozhi 273 uses the same word வேண்டாமை to say that a king's servant who asks nothing even in hardship is given all he wants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the renouncer's highest wealth being turned into a courtier's method: the Kural's not-wanting is its own reward, while Pazhamozhi makes it the means by which a king's servant is given everything, so the same virtue serves release and preferment.","la":"it carries the couplet's claim from the chapter on cutting off desire into service at court and adds the case of hardship (அல்லுறினும்), where not asking is hardest.","gr":4},{"a":"pz:274","b":"pz:273","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 273 says the king's servant should ask the king for nothing; 274 says he should not seek help elsewhere either, for that is like sucking the cow's throat while the udder is there.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together the verses leave the servant no one to ask: not the king, whom he must not petition, and not others, who are a dry source, so all his provision must come unasked from the master he serves."},{"a":"pz:275","b":"k:696","type":"develops","note":"Kural 696 asks the king's counsellor to know the mood and the time before saying what is needed; Pazhamozhi 275 says those who speak to kings at the right moment achieve everything, and that a hunchback inside is better than a minister outside.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the couplet's timing depends on access: one who knows the right moment is useless unless he is in the room when it comes, so the menial with the inner rooms can outdo the minister the Kural addresses.","la":"its proverb ranks a hunchbacked attendant above a minister, adding a ranking by nearness that cuts across office, which the couplet's advice on timing does not consider."},{"a":"pz:276","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"Kural 691 asks those who serve kings to be like people warming at a fire, neither too far nor too near; Pazhamozhi 276 warns the favourite who answers the king's fierce love with his own and acts without perceiving what lies hidden.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show that the danger of being too near comes most from the king's own affection: the Kural's fire burns those who press close, and Pazhamozhi shows the courtier being drawn close by love and forgetting to keep distance.","la":"it gives the too-near case a cause the couplet leaves out, the king's love itself, and closes it with a proverb of self-betrayal."},{"a":"pz:277","b":"k:698","type":"develops","note":"Kural 698 forbids belittling a king as young or as close kin; Pazhamozhi 277 forbids mocking a king who has lost his greatness and his kin and stands alone, for no one slights a snake as small.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair extends the Kural's bar on belittling from youth and kinship to ruin and isolation, and the proverb's இளைது echoes the couplet's இளையர: in both, smallness of age or fortune is no licence, but Pazhamozhi grounds this in the power still in him, the Kural in the light of his station.","la":"it moves the couplet's case from a young king in his strength to a fallen king without kin, and gives a reason by fear of the snake where the couplet gives one by the dignity of the office.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:277","b":"n:241","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 241 says the worthy feel shame and do not advance on humbled enemies, as the eclipse snake does not seize the crescent moon; Pazhamozhi 277 says those who serve a fallen king do not mock him, as no one slights a young snake.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set side by side, the two snakes give two different grounds for sparing the fallen: in the Naladiyar the strong one is the snake and holds back from shame, in Pazhamozhi the fallen king is the snake and is spared from fear, so restraint before the weak may be nobility or prudence.","la":"it turns the quatrain's image around, making the humbled one the snake, so the same restraint is grounded in the danger of the weak rather than the shame of the strong."},{"a":"pz:280","b":"k:700","type":"develops","note":"Kural 700 says the intimacy that does improper things on the ground of old acquaintance brings ruin; Pazhamozhi 280 says those trusted for their truth who turn to falsehood against the king fell the palm onto themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair names two kinds of capital a servant can spend against himself: the Kural's long familiarity and Pazhamozhi's reputation for truth, and in both the very ground of trust becomes the instrument of the fall.","la":"its palm that falls on the one who fells it makes the ruin self-inflicted by the servant's own act, where the couplet says only that the intimacy brings ruin."},{"a":"pz:280","b":"pz:270","type":"same_image","note":"Pazhamozhi 270 has the ripe palm fruit fall at the foot of its parent tree, and 280 has the felled palm fall on the feller; both use a falling palm for a deed returning to its doer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two palm images show one law of return working both ways in the king's service: work done in measure comes back like ripe fruit to its tree, and treachery comes back like a felled trunk on the axeman."},{"a":"pz:281","b":"pn:73","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 73 has Nalankilli say the fool who scorned him will hardly escape, like a blind man stumbling on a sleeping tiger; Pazhamozhi 281 tells the king's wronged servant not to wake the sleeping tiger by acting on his grievance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the same sleeping tiger from both sides: the Chola king boasts of being the tiger that the careless rouse to their death, and the ethics verse teaches the king's own servant to treat that tiger as a standing danger to be left asleep."},{"a":"pz:282","b":"n:164","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 164 says those who have angered the great do not escape even in a strong fortress, as the snake in its crevice dreads the thunder; Pazhamozhi 282 asks why those who cannot protect themselves are angry with kings at all.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair runs the danger the other way: the Naladiyar speaks of those who have provoked the great and find no shelter, Pazhamozhi of those who themselves feel anger at the king, and both conclude that against royal power the weak have no refuge.","la":"it shifts the quatrain's case from another's wrath to one's own anger, and its kongu tree makes the refuge something the angry man climbs himself."},{"a":"pz:283","b":"k:699","type":"develops","note":"Kural 699 says the clear-sighted do not do the unacceptable on the thought that they are in favour; Pazhamozhi 283 says that when a king bears an offence with visible displeasure, going on to offend again is a boil on a hump.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they mark two false grounds for presuming: favour in the Kural and forgiveness in Pazhamozhi; the ethics verse adds that the king's patience, shown with displeasure, is a warning the offender misreads as licence.","la":"it adds a sequence the couplet lacks, a first offence borne and then repeated, and a proverb of harm piled on harm."},{"a":"pz:284","b":"n:189","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 189 says the pure in heart stay unshaken by cruel slander like a flame in a lamp; Pazhamozhi 284 says the wise do not join those agitated by slander spoken to the king, but work out how he may see through it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair moves steadiness under slander from one's own mind to the court: the Naladiyar's pure heart is not moved when slandered, and Pazhamozhi's wise courtier is not moved when another is slandered before the king, and also helps the king see clearly.","la":"it gives the steady one a task beyond composure, clearing the king's judgement, and sets him against a crowd of stilt-dancers moved by alarm."},{"a":"pz:285","b":"k:750","type":"develops","note":"Kural 750 says a fort with every excellence is nothing for those without excellence in action; Pazhamozhi 285 names the missing excellence as courage and adds allies to the fort as a support that fails without it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the outer defences of a ruler, walls and allies alike, are shown to depend on one inner thing, and the Pazhamozhi names that thing as freedom from fear.","la":"it sets strong allies beside the fort as a second outer prop and lets the proverb make fear the single cause that empties both."},{"a":"pz:286","b":"k:879","type":"develops","note":"Kural 879 says to cut the thorn tree while young; Pazhamozhi 286 keeps the timing and adds a second step, stripping the young enemy of his friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair gives two parts of one tactic: the Kural's when (before the thorn hardens) and the Pazhamozhi's how (leave the tree alone so it cannot become a forest).","la":"it joins early action to isolation from allies, a pairing the couplet's single tree image does not make."},{"a":"pz:287","b":"k:489","type":"develops","note":"Kural 489 says to do the hard deed at the very moment the rare chance comes; Pazhamozhi 287 applies this to answering an enemy who has struck, and calls delay folly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's general rule of the moment is given a hostile case, where the passing chance is the attacker himself, gone like a snake once it has bitten.","la":"it turns the couplet's abstract rare moment into a concrete one with a cost, the snake's fang that cannot be taken once it has fled."},{"a":"pz:287","b":"pz:286","type":"develops","note":"Both verses urge acting at once against an enemy; 286 speaks of an enmity still young, 287 of one that has already struck.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they cover enmity before and after its first blow, and say that in neither case does waiting help, once because the enemy grows, once because he escapes.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:289","b":"tk:24","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 24 makes the bowing of hardened enemies a mire; Pazhamozhi 289 says favours shown to enemies set against reconciliation do not take.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two verses look at a false peace from both sides: the enemy's submission cannot be trusted, and one's own kindness cannot change him, so neither party's gesture ends a settled enmity."},{"a":"pz:290","b":"nm:60","type":"develops","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 60 counts going before those who are not one's own as laughable; Pazhamozhi 290 says why, with the child crying to be carried by those who would not let it follow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted line names the act as laughable; the proverb shows what makes it so, the asking for closeness from people who deny even distance.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:291","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"Kural 269 says that even leaping past Death becomes possible for those who have gained the power of penance; Pazhamozhi 291 says that even those with the greatness of penance have never clapped Death on the back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two take opposite views of what penance can do against death: the Kural lets austerity overcome it, while the Pazhamozhi, using the demon great in penance who fell, denies that it protects the one who provokes the great.","la":"it sets the claim inside a story of a penitent who was destroyed in war, so the power of tapas is tested by a case rather than asserted."},{"a":"pz:291","b":"k:892","type":"develops","note":"Kural 892 says disregard of the great brings ills that do not leave; Pazhamozhi 291 gives an exemplary case, the demon who took enmity with the great without heed and was caught in battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's general warning gains a named example and a sharper premise, that the offender's own might, even might won by penance, does not change the outcome.","la":"it adds the exemplum and the point that the offender was himself great, which the couplet's plain contrast of the great and the heedless leaves out."},{"a":"pz:292","b":"n:178","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 178 says enemies' anger cannot reach the weak who have joined good protection, like grass by a stump the plough avoids; Pazhamozhi 292 says the small who attack such dependents only rouse the protector.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same situation is seen from both sides: the quatrain promises the protected weak their safety, and the Pazhamozhi warns their attacker that striking them wakes the great one behind them.","la":"its bear and wild dog image shows the attacker's fate, which the quatrain's stump and plough, concerned only with the sheltered grass, does not."},{"a":"pz:292","b":"pz:291","type":"qualifies","note":"291 warns against enmity with the great themselves; 292 extends the warning to enmity with those who depend on the great.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read in sequence, the reach of a great man's protection is shown to cover his dependents, so the danger of provoking him begins one step before he is touched."},{"a":"pz:293","b":"k:861","type":"develops","note":"Kural 861 says to avoid opposing the strong; Pazhamozhi 293 says what the weak should do when the strong are already against them: flee far and live by any means.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet only advises against the contest; the Pazhamozhi carries the advice into defeat and allows any means of escape, since survival keeps future good open.","la":"it adds the step of flight and the proverb's promise of later fortune, which the couplet's balanced rule for strong and weak lacks."},{"a":"pz:293","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Kural 969 praises those who, like the yak, give up life when honour is at stake; Pazhamozhi 293 tells the weak to survive by doing anything at all.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give opposite answers on whether life may be bought with loss of standing: the Kural's man of honour dies rather than live shamed, while the Pazhamozhi counts staying alive as the greater good.","la":"its என்செய்தே யாயினும் and the promise of the bangle make survival a positive aim, where the couplet makes life itself forfeit to honour."},{"a":"pz:294","b":"k:894","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 894 says that when the weak do harm to the strong it is like beckoning Death; Pazhamozhi 294 says the small inflaming the victorious ends in no good.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's image makes the attack fatal; the Pazhamozhi's bees warring with the mountain make it pointless as well, an assault that cannot even reach its target.","la":"it adds futility to danger: the bees' quarrel with the hill shows the attack does nothing to the strong, not only that it destroys the weak.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:295","b":"tk:4","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 4 names living openly before enemies and taking by the hand one once enraged as the doings of a man about to die; Pazhamozhi 295 says those who swagger and enrage the steadfast become crows' food.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both verses treat a way of behaving toward enemies as a sign of death already on its way, the counted work by naming it, the Pazhamozhi by showing the boaster's end as carrion."},{"a":"pz:296","b":"k:874","type":"tension","note":"Kural 874 says the world rests on the greatness of one who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship; Pazhamozhi 296 says old enmity never becomes friendship. On the reading that 874 means turning enemies into friends, the two conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two disagree over whether enmity can be converted: the Kural praises the man who makes a friend of a foe, while the Pazhamozhi holds that the old foe, like a tuber, only waits for rain.","la":"its tuber image gives a reason for the denial, that enmity lies dormant and revives with its season, which the couplet's praise does not face."},{"a":"pz:296","b":"iv:17","type":"tension","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 17 names winning over those who are not one's allies as sweeter than doing good to the land; Pazhamozhi 296 says an old enemy never becomes a friend and should not be kept close.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the ethics group itself the two verses disagree on the reconciled enemy, one ranking his winning over among the sweetest things and the other counting him a sprout that will rise against one."},{"a":"pz:297","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"Kural 882 says not to fear sword-like enemies but the bond with kin-like ones; Pazhamozhi 297 gives the reason as distance, the flood afar against the debt in one's own house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the ranking of the two dangers; the Pazhamozhi explains it, since the open enemy works from outside and far off while the false friend consumes the house from within.","la":"it sets a flood, a natural force, against a household debt, so the ranking turns on nearness rather than on the enemy's strength."},{"a":"pz:298","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"Kural 882 says fear the bond with kin-like enemies more than sword-like ones; Pazhamozhi 298 counts the false intimate's harm in two worlds and ranks the open enemy above him by the proverb of blindness and dim sight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows why the kin-like enemy is worse: the open foe is a known lack one guards against, while the false friend's harm reaches even one's standing in the next world.","la":"its rhetorical question and proverb turn the couplet's warning into a ranking of two harms, with the open enemy placed above the false friend as blindness above clouded sight."},{"a":"pz:298","b":"n:219","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 219 ranks enmity above the friendship of the undiscerning; Pazhamozhi 298 ranks open enmity above the attachment of those who do not care for one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain blames the friend's lack of understanding, the proverb verse his lack of care, so two different faults in a friend both make plain enmity the better lot.","la":"it sets the ranking within strategy of enmity rather than among a list of lesser evils, and grounds it in harm done here and hereafter.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:299","b":"k:880","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 880 says those who do not break the pride of hostile men are as good as dead; Pazhamozhi 299 sets the limit, that once the enemy is killed further rage is waste.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they bound hostility at both ends: one must break the enemy, but anger that outlives the enemy is water poured on a reaped field.","la":"its harvest proverb adds an end point the couplet lacks, measuring anger by whether anything is left for it to do."},{"a":"pz:299","b":"n:241","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 241 says the worthy are ashamed to advance on a humbled enemy; Pazhamozhi 299 says rage after the enemy's death is fruitless.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain stops the worthy from pressing a fallen enemy out of shame, the proverb verse out of futility, so honour and plain economy reach the same restraint.","la":"it gives a practical reason, waste, beside the quatrain's reason of honour, and moves the case from a humbled enemy to a dead one."},{"a":"pz:300","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"Kural 861 says avoid opposing the strong but favour enmity against the weak; Pazhamozhi 300 says even if the great fight the strong, enmity with the low brings them nothing. The tension holds if புன்பாட்டவர், men of low standing, are the Kural's மெலியார்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural measures an enemy by strength and picks the weak, Pazhamozhi measures by worth and warns that the petty, though small, can flood one like a light shower.","la":"its proverb gives the low enemy a capacity for harm the couplet denies the weak, so the choice of enemy turns on standing and risk, not strength alone."},{"a":"pz:300","b":"n:187","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 187 says even enmity with the wise brings dignity while intimacy with the mean brings grief; Pazhamozhi 300 says enmity with the low brings the great nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two say from opposite sides that the worth of an enemy is the measure of the quarrel: a wise foe honours one, a petty foe only costs.","la":"it adds the hazard the quatrain leaves out, that the petty enemy can still do great harm, as a light shower brings a flood."},{"a":"pz:302","b":"pz:300","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 302 counsels silence before the strong and pressing the weak; 300 says the great gain nothing from enmity with the low.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together the book separates the weak, who may be overrun, from the low, who are not worth fighting, so strength and standing are two different measures of an enemy."},{"a":"pz:302","b":"in:4","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 4 counts doing harm to the very strong as painful; Pazhamozhi 302 counsels keeping silent before the strong.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The counted verse names the pain of provoking the strong, the proverb verse the policy that avoids it and turns force on the weak instead, so the two give cause and remedy.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:303","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"Kural 873 calls one who alone takes on the enmity of many worse than mad; Pazhamozhi 303 tells the man facing enemies not to trust himself and to rely on numbers, even of poor allies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet forbids the lone stand, the proverb verse supplies its positive rule, that number outweighs merit in war, so worthless allies are still worth having.","la":"it adds what the couplet lacks, a ranking of number above quality in allies, and a warning against self-admiration."},{"a":"pz:304","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"Kural 875 says one alone against two enemies should take one as an ally; Pazhamozhi 304 says against a stronger enemy one should find someone to harass him from behind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes an ally out of an enemy one already has, the proverb verse recruits a new enemy for one's enemy, so both turn the enemy's own surroundings into one's defence.","la":"its ram-and-dog proverb adds the tactic of a rear threat, which halts an advance without meeting it head on, something the couplet's alliance does not name."},{"a":"pz:305","b":"k:887","type":"develops","note":"Kural 887 says a family with inner enmity is not joined though it fits like box and lid; Pazhamozhi 305 shows how an enemy creates that enmity, by flattering one brother that the two together need no one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes inner enmity from inside the house that suffers it, the proverb verse from the hand that plants it, so the pair shows both the seam and the wedge.","la":"it supplies the act of sowing discord, with its quoted boast and bone-among-dogs image, which the couplet's picture of a split family leaves unsaid."},{"a":"pz:305","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"Kural 633 names the minister's skill of dividing others; Pazhamozhi 305 shows one way of dividing, by giving allied brothers something to quarrel over.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists division as a skill of office, the proverb verse shows it worked through flattering speech, so the minister's art is seen in practice.","la":"it turns the couplet's bare word பிரித்தல் into a scene with a speaker, a boast and its effect."},{"a":"pz:306","b":"pz:304","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 304 sets someone on a stronger enemy's rear; 306 incites an outside power against one's natural enemy and keeps it friendly by favour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses together show a graded method: first find a rear threat, then keep the third power warm and let it take the burn, as the monkey's hand stirs the fire."},{"a":"pz:307","b":"pz:303","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 303 says nothing is mightier than numbers; 307 says one capable man kills the hundred because division is in everyone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book's two proverbs meet: numbers win in the field, but a host can be undone by one man who works its divisions, so number is strength only while it is united."},{"a":"pz:307","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"Kural 889 says inner enmity as small as a split sesame seed holds ruin; Pazhamozhi 307 says division is in everyone and an able man can use it to kill a hundred.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns the house of its own small crack, the proverb verse tells the enemy to widen it, so the same fact of universal division is a danger at home and a weapon abroad.","la":"it moves the couplet's warning into offence and gives a scale to the ruin, one able man against a hundred."},{"a":"pz:308","b":"k:883","type":"develops","note":"Kural 883 says to fear the enemy within and guard against it; Pazhamozhi 308 says the discerning gain by winning over the enemy within their enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the Kural tells one to fear at home the proverb verse tells one to seek in the enemy's camp, so inner enmity is a single force judged by whose house it is in.","la":"its doubled proverb, liquor by liquor and thorn by thorn, gives the principle that like cures like, which the couplet's potter's-cutter image lacks."},{"a":"pz:308","b":"k:879","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 879 says cut a thorn tree while young before it wounds the hand; Pazhamozhi 308 removes a thorn with a thorn.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both read the enemy as a thorn, but the Kural deals with it by timing, cutting early, and the proverb by means, setting one thorn against another.","la":"it adds a second way of handling the thorn, by its own kind, beside the couplet's way of cutting it young.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:309","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"Kural 874 praises one who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship; Pazhamozhi 309 says an oppressor whose victim does not fall should come over to him in friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes turning enmity into friendship a mark of character, the proverb verse makes it a move forced by a failed attack, so the same turn can be virtue or prudence.","la":"it adds the occasion and the motive, a missed arrow and the need for guard, which the couplet's praise of character does not give."},{"a":"pz:310","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"Kural 633 names three skills of the minister, dividing others, holding one's own and reconciling; Pazhamozhi 310 names four means of ending enmity, sweet words, gifts, division and measured force.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two lists overlap only in division: the Kural's are skills of joining and parting, the proverb verse's a sequence ending in force, so they show two ways of ordering statecraft.","la":"it puts the means in an order, words and gifts first and force last and in due measure, which the couplet's three skills do not rank."},{"a":"pz:310","b":"pz:196","type":"agrees","note":"Pazhamozhi 196 says the senseless are best cut off with wealth, a gift's thrust being stronger than a spear's; 310 puts giving second among the means of ending enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The earlier verse gives the gift as a weapon by itself; 310 sets it in a sequence with words, division and force, so the book's praise of the gift becomes one stage of a method."},{"a":"pz:317","b":"pz:311","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 311 praises the worthy who, having weighed it, meet the foe whether they or the enemy win; 317 calls attacking without being the enemy's match a harm, like striking a stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the weighing in 311 does not license fighting any foe: courage indifferent to outcome is admired, but 317 bars the charge against a clearly superior enemy, so the chapter separates accepting risk from courting certain defeat."},{"a":"pz:313","b":"pz:311","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the silent fighter against the talker: in 311 those who rage within the fort with many words, in 313 the idle who talk and blame the king while the doers stand silent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the chapter's double charge against talk in war: it is wasted when made from safety, and it becomes slander when the talker turns it on the king."},{"a":"pz:312","b":"k:764","type":"develops","note":"Kural 764 makes an army one that cannot be turned by subversion (அறைபோகாது); Pazhamozhi 312 asks whether one who would give his body for the king could join his enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the quality, incorruptibility, as a property of the army; Pazhamozhi grounds it in the single soldier's willingness to die, so loyalty becomes the consequence of a readiness to give the body rather than a separate virtue.","la":"its two-bulls proverb gives a reason the couplet lacks: enmity so settled that the two cannot even share a ford, so desertion is shown as unnatural rather than only forbidden."},{"a":"pz:315","b":"n:346","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 346 says the excellent never speak a boastful word even with an emperor's wealth; Pazhamozhi 315 asks the warrior who has killed the strutting foe not to tell his own valour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's restraint concerns wealth and the base man's swagger; Pazhamozhi carries it to the battlefield, where the deed is real and the praise of others is due, so silence is asked even of one who has earned praise.","la":"it moves the rule from wealth to valour and gives the reason in a proverb, that the deed shows itself as a theft cannot be bundled, where the quatrain gives only the contrast of excellent and base."},{"a":"pz:317","b":"k:473","type":"develops","note":"Kural 473 says many who did not know their strength rose on zeal and were broken midway; Pazhamozhi 317 says that even capable men who rush on an unequal foe come to harm, like a hand struck on stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet blames ignorance of one's own strength; Pazhamozhi adds that being able (ஆற்றுவாரேனும்) is not enough, since what counts is the match (நிகர்) with the enemy, so the weighing is relative, not absolute.","la":"it shifts the measure from the self's strength to the comparison with the enemy and gives the image of injury to the striker, which the couplet's broken-midway leaves general.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:318","b":"k:664","type":"develops","note":"Kural 664 says speaking is easy and doing as one spoke is hard; Pazhamozhi 318 asks the man in the council of kings not to talk in turn and leave but to resolve to act, undeterred by the enemy's elephants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the difficulty in doing; Pazhamozhi names what makes doing hard in war, fear of a strong enemy, and refuses the wait for a safer moment, so resolve is tied to accepting risk.","la":"it sets the contrast in the royal council before war and adds the waves proverb, which answers the excuse of waiting that the couplet does not consider."},{"a":"pz:319","b":"k:772","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 772 says carrying a spear that missed an elephant is sweeter than an arrow that hit a hare; Pazhamozhi 319 scorns petty men who keep quiet before the strong and overrun the weak.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both measure valour by its target rather than its success, and Pazhamozhi names the opposite type the couplet only implies: the man who chooses hares, silent before the guard and pitiless to the helpless.","la":"it adds the social side the couplet lacks, the bully's two faces toward strong and weak, and the razor image of sharpness wasted on what offers no resistance."},{"a":"pz:320","b":"k:534","type":"develops","note":"Kural 534 says the fearful have no fortress; Pazhamozhi 320 says the incapable are caught through fear though shut behind walls, like a timid bird that betrays itself in the dark.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states that walls do not help the fearful; Pazhamozhi shows how, since fear itself gives them away, as the bird's start reveals it, so the failure is exposure from within, not weak defences.","la":"it supplies the mechanism through the bird image, the fear that makes its holder visible, which the couplet's flat 'no fortress' does not give."},{"a":"pz:323","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"Kural 428 calls not fearing what should be feared folly and fearing it the work of the knowing; Pazhamozhi 323 denies that the fool's daring, resting on ignorance of dangers, is daring, and grants that knowing brings great fear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes fear a mark of wisdom in general; Pazhamozhi carries it to the soldier and draws the hard consequence, that the brave man is one who knows the danger and so feels the fear, making courage a matter of holding against it rather than lacking it.","la":"it moves the claim into the soldier's domain and turns it against a kind of courage, ruling out the fearless fool as brave, which the couplet does not say."},{"a":"pz:325","b":"k:768","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 768 says that even without striking power or endurance an army gains stature by its படைத்தகை; Pazhamozhi 325 uses the same word for men of good name who lack the drive to march on the enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats bearing as a real asset of a weak army; Pazhamozhi turns it on men, so that bearing without ஊற்றம் becomes their measure, and the concession reads, on this reading, as a limited or ironic praise.","la":"its list of generosity, conduct and intelligence as the virtues of reputation sets the warrior's drive apart from them, showing that the civil virtues do not make a fighting man."},{"a":"pz:326","b":"pn:341","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 341 tells of a father who will not give his young daughter even when the king pleads and takes up arms with his tiger-like kin, torn between her wedding tomorrow and death; Pazhamozhi 326 says even the girl's brothers will refuse the king and the only outcome is battle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem holds the refusal as a tragic choice and grieves for the town's beauty; the ethics verse keeps the heroic memory but removes the dilemma, so the refusal becomes a fixed rule of a proud house and war the only remedy, said with a kitchen proverb."},{"a":"pz:327","b":"k:247","type":"develops","note":"Kural 247 grants as plain fact that this world is not for those without wealth; Pazhamozhi 327 brings that fact into the house and sets money beside a woman's modesty and the body's food as a precondition of the household's work.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's premise about wealth and this world becomes a rule of the house: money is one of three things a household life cannot do without.","la":"its run of three 'not without' clauses ranks money with modesty and food, so wealth stands as one of the household's basic needs rather than a worldly power."},{"a":"pz:330","b":"k:85","type":"develops","note":"Kural 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests even needs sowing; Pazhamozhi 330 gives the same unfailing yield to the house, but credits the hospitality to the wife who reads the husband's unspoken sign.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the promise of endless plenty for the hospitable house resting in practice on the wife, whose silent understanding of her husband is what keeps the guests fed.","la":"its proverb of a water-lift in the sea replaces the unsown field with an inexhaustible source, and it adds the wife's reading of signs, which the couplet does not mention."},{"a":"pz:331","b":"k:67","type":"develops","note":"Kural 67 names the father's good to his son as setting him first in the assembly; Pazhamozhi 331 makes the father's task the child's conduct and warns that a child badly formed afflicts the one who formed him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they give the father's duty a reward and a penalty: the well-formed son is set before the assembly, the ill-formed one turns on the father like a made thing become a demon.","la":"its proverb of the doll that becomes an afflicting spirit to its maker adds the cost of failure, which the couplet's statement of the duty leaves unsaid."},{"a":"pz:336","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"Kural 57 says confinement is no use and a woman's own நிறை is the best guard; Pazhamozhi 336 restates this and adds the obverse, that a woman without that restraint is not amended by force however hard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet shows only that the inner guard is better; the verse makes the outer guard useless in both cases, needless for the restrained and powerless over the unrestrained.","la":"its dog's-tail proverb supplies the second case the couplet does not state, so the claim covers both the woman with restraint and the woman without it."},{"a":"pz:336","b":"nm:90","type":"agrees","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 90 says a woman without a bond goes as she pleases though guarded; Pazhamozhi 336 says the same of confinement and closes it with the dog's tail.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse sets the woman beside desire and hidden murder as natures that outrun control; the proverb makes the same point by a household image of a crookedness force cannot straighten."},{"a":"pz:333","b":"a:146","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 146 the wife refuses the bard sent to plead for the husband who goes to the courtesans' street; Pazhamozhi 333 sets the same refusal of the bard in four lines and closes it with the proverb that a feigned sleeper cannot be woken.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ethics work takes the akam wife's refusal of the bard and turns it into a general judgement, that a husband who strays knowingly is beyond persuasion, where the Sangam poem left the charge in images and pity.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:335","b":"k:693","type":"develops","note":"Kural 693 tells the king's servant that once suspicion has arisen nobody can clear it; Pazhamozhi 335 carries the same irreversibility into a friend's house, where the town's rumour leaves no witness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows one rule about suspicion working at court and in the town alike: since it cannot be undone, the only defence is avoiding the occasion, even for the innocent.","la":"its flood-and-dam proverb gives the couplet's 'hard for anyone' a cause, the swell of public talk, and moves it from the king's suspicion to the neighbours'."},{"a":"pz:329","b":"n:141","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 141 says the well-born keep their principles though their clothes are rags; Pazhamozhi 329 says the household should keep a bright outward show even in ruin, since the world reads want from dress.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two divide dignity in poverty between inner principle, which must never fall, and outward appearance, which the Naladiyar lets go and the Pazhamozhi insists on keeping for the world's eyes.","la":"its proverb about the clothed never being asked if they have eaten adds the social reason for outward show, which the quatrain's lion image of inner nature ignores."},{"a":"pz:337","b":"k:90","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 90 warns the host that a guest wilts at a changed face; Pazhamozhi 337 turns the duty on the guest: the great should not stay with the poor, whom hospitality strains beyond their means.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, hospitality has two delicate parties: the guest who wilts at a cold look and the poor host who would wound himself to feed a great guest.","la":"its sparrow proverb names the host's cost, which the Kural's chapter on hospitality never counts, and makes restraint a guest's virtue."},{"a":"pz:338","b":"k:92","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 92 ranks sweet speech above glad giving; Pazhamozhi 338 says a gift of food, clothes, medicine and shelter is spoiled if the sweet word is withheld at parting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, sweet speech is not only the higher of two separable goods but the finishing part of a gift, so its absence cheapens what was given.","la":"it names the costly gifts one by one and then the missing word, so the proverb measures the meanness by the gap between a slaughtered buffalo and the spice.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:338","b":"el:7","type":"develops","note":"Elathi 7 counts sweet speech with mingling, a seat and good food in the host's gift; Pazhamozhi 338 isolates the word given at the guest's leaving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Elathi joins the sweet word to food in one welcome, and Pazhamozhi shows the welcome can fail at its last moment, when the departing guest is dismissed without it."},{"a":"pz:339","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"Kural 280 says shaving or long hair are not needed if one gives up what the world blames; Pazhamozhi 339 also sets aside the sect's marks and says what penance is: an even mind under the blade and the sandal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural removes the outward marks by a negative test, and Pazhamozhi supplies a positive one, equanimity toward harm and honour, as the substance the marks pretend to.","la":"its yoke-pin proverb gives the inward test an image of balance the couplet lacks, centring penance on a mind pulled equally both ways."},{"a":"pz:339","b":"s:311","type":"agrees","note":"The Siddhar song says forest, rags and begging bowl gain nothing without inner love; Pazhamozhi 339 says the sect's mark is not penance without an even mind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both deny that garb makes the ascetic, but they put different things in its place, love for the Lord in the Siddhar and Jain-style equanimity in Pazhamozhi."},{"a":"pz:340","b":"tk:62","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 62 names the man who wrongly keeps a deposit among three whose line is lost; Pazhamozhi 340 traces how keeping begins, by delaying the return in the hope that the owner will not claim it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the sin and its root: the Thirikadugam punishes the kept deposit, and Pazhamozhi locates the fault already in stalling when the owner asks."},{"a":"pz:340","b":"k:283","type":"develops","note":"Kural 283 says wealth got by stealth perishes even as it seems to grow; Pazhamozhi 340 applies the same doom to a deposit held back, saying base wealth does not stay.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pazhamozhi counts the withheld deposit as stealth without any taking, so the Kural's rule on stolen gain reaches a thing that came into one's hand lawfully.","la":"it moves the couplet's claim from theft to trust, the thing placed in one's hand, and adds the time of return as the test."},{"a":"pz:341","b":"sp:63","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 63 says small gifts given without calling them of small use bear great fruit; Pazhamozhi 341 says nothing is of no use to something, since even a crab saved a man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two refuse the judgment 'too small to matter' from both sides, of the gift one gives and of the companion one keeps."},{"a":"pz:341","b":"pz:136","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 136 says those cherished as a store against bad days fail one in trouble; Pazhamozhi 341 says even the smallest companion may save one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within one book help is shown to come from where it is not counted on, the crab, and to fail where it was banked on, the cherished friend."},{"a":"pz:342","b":"n:121","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 121 sets flesh-eaters among those who do not turn to renunciation; Pazhamozhi 342 says one who has renounced and then eats flesh undoes the whole crossing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar makes flesh-eating a mark of the unrenounced, and Pazhamozhi adds that renunciation once achieved can still be sunk by it.","la":"its proverb of the sea and the hoofprint ranks the craving overcome against the small appetite kept, a contrast of scale the quatrain does not draw."},{"a":"pz:342","b":"pz:343","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 342 addresses renouncers who eat flesh; 343 addresses those who kill in rage and lack grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The adjacent verses carry the bar on flesh from the renouncer's path to anyone without grace, and give it two roots: leftover appetite and anger."},{"a":"pz:343","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 251 asks how one who eats flesh can hold to grace; Pazhamozhi 343 traces the flesh-eater back through killing to rage, and says he did not walk in grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the incompatibility of flesh and grace, and Pazhamozhi gives its order in a life: anger first, then killing, then eating.","la":"its chain of rage, killing and eating sets the diet among its causes, and the curd proverb casts grace as the good thing spoiled."},{"a":"pz:343","b":"tk:36","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 36 names the flesh-eater who claims compassion; Pazhamozhi 343 says those who eat flesh do not walk in grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both deny the meat-eater's claim to grace, one by exposing his self-serving reasoning and the other by tracing the habit back to anger."},{"a":"pz:344","b":"k:104","type":"develops","note":"Kural 104 says those who know the fruit take a millet's good as a palm; Pazhamozhi 344 asks the knowing to plan always for the good of one who gives them a measure of grain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural enlarges a small kindness in the mind, and Pazhamozhi turns that into lasting service toward a provider, framed as a return no trade can match.","la":"it moves the couplet from a single favour to a standing tie of maintenance, and its trade proverb puts gratitude in the language of profit."},{"a":"pz:345","b":"n:357","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 357 says the worthy bear many faults from one who did them a kindness; Pazhamozhi 345 has the giver bear the anger of the kinsman he fed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair runs patience both ways along a gift: the receiver bears the benefactor's faults in the Naladiyar, and the giver bears the receiver's rage in Pazhamozhi.","la":"it reverses the quatrain's direction and grounds the forbearance in kinship made by giving, with the ghee proverb as its sign."},{"a":"pz:346","b":"k:204","type":"develops","note":"Kural 204 says that if one plots another's ruin, aram plots the plotter's; Pazhamozhi 346 says plotting against one's benefactor is cutting the branch one sits on.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves the return to aram, while Pazhamozhi needs no agent: the benefactor is the plotter's own support, so the ruin is self-inflicted at once.","la":"it narrows the couplet to the one who cared for the plotter and joins others to the plot, and its proverb makes the retribution mechanical."},{"a":"pz:347","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 110 says there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him; Pazhamozhi 347 names one form of it, joining the benefactor's enemies and backbiting him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks ingratitude as beyond remedy, and Pazhamozhi gives it a concrete shape in speech and alliance, likened to burning the house that fed one.","la":"it specifies the ingrate's act as backbiting in company with the estranged and weighs it by the past meals, which the couplet does not name."},{"a":"pz:347","b":"pz:346","type":"develops","note":"Both close the heading with ingratitude; 346 is plotting ruin, 347 is backbiting in league with enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two proverbs split ingratitude's harm: cutting one's own branch ruins the traitor, burning the house one ate in ruins the benefactor."},{"a":"pz:352","b":"k:521","type":"develops","note":"Kural 521 says honouring old ties when all is lost is found only in kin; Pazhamozhi 352 extends it to kin who were hostile before, and sets them against outsiders who were favoured.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show that kin loyalty in ruin does not rest on remembered kindness: even a hostile kinsman stands behind, so the bond is of family, not of debt.","la":"its contrast of ஒருகுடியார் with பிறர் treated as gold gives the couplet a counter-case, the favoured outsider who still will not come in."},{"a":"pz:352","b":"n:113","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 113 says that in trouble few kin claim the relation; Pazhamozhi 352 says those of one family do not part from a man in trouble.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the same test, trouble, the two works give opposite verdicts on kin, one of shared family loyalty and one of kin as fair-weather clingers, so the ethics group has no settled view.","la":"it narrows kin to ஒருகுடியார் and puts the desertion on outsiders, where the quatrain puts it on kin themselves."},{"a":"pz:348","b":"n:203","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 203 says the great never refuse to bear those who depend on them; Pazhamozhi 348 says bearing them is useless unless their trouble is felt as one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair adds an inner condition to the duty of support: carrying dependants as a branch carries fruit is not enough if their trouble is not felt as one's own.","la":"its proverb of pounding husk turns support without fellow feeling from a virtue into wasted labour."},{"a":"pz:350","b":"n:201","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 201 says a man's distress vanishes on seeing kin who ask after him; Pazhamozhi 350 says good kin are those who remove distress by deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the comfort of kin has two measures, being asked after and being helped, and the Pazhamozhi verse ranks the deed as what makes kin good.","la":"it defines good kin by their act (வினைமரபின் நீக்கும்) and makes them a remedy at hand, where the quatrain heals by presence alone.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:350","b":"nm:3","type":"develops","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 3 says the use of kin is known when one is ruined; Pazhamozhi 350 says what that use is, removing the distress, and calls it a medicine at home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counted verse gives the test of kin and the proverb verse its content, so ruin both reveals kin and is the thing good kin cure."},{"a":"pz:351","b":"k:529","type":"develops","note":"Kural 529 says kin who left return when the cause of discord is gone; Pazhamozhi 351 says kin who seem never to help will help at last, as rain falls after holding off.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the return of kin wait on removing a cause, while the rain proverb makes it as sure as weather, so delay in kin's help is no proof of its absence.","la":"its rain proverb gives kin help the certainty of a natural cycle and sets it against strangers who never help.","gr":2},{"a":"pz:353","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"Kural 448 says a ruler without those who rebuke him perishes; Pazhamozhi 353 says hurtful counsel should be heard from kin, whose treading, like a mother's, does not lame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair moves the needed rebuker from the king's council into the family and gives a reason the couplet lacks for bearing him: the bond guarantees the hurt does no harm.","la":"its mother-bird proverb explains why rebuke is bearable, which the couplet only declares necessary."},{"a":"pz:354","b":"n:208","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 208 says true intimates enter the fire with you like the smith's poker; Pazhamozhi 354 says one taken as kin follows wherever his kinsman goes, as thread follows the needle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both use a craftsman's tool for loyalty, but the poker proves itself in danger and the thread by following, so kin loyalty is shown as obedience to another's course whatever it costs.","la":"its needle and thread make loyalty a matter of following the kinsman's lead, where the quatrain's smithy tools sort friends by their behaviour in the heat."},{"a":"pz:355","b":"k:524","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 524 makes living surrounded by kin the fruit of wealth; Pazhamozhi 355 says even brothers living long in one dwelling is painful.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair separates being surrounded by kin from sharing a roof with them: the Kural's ideal holds only at a distance, for even brothers wear on one another at close quarters.","la":"its proverb that even the goddess of fortune is a bad housemate puts a limit of nearness and time on the couplet's praise."},{"a":"pz:355","b":"nm:49","type":"qualifies","note":"Nanmanikkadigai 49 says living apart from one's companions is hard for the well-born; Pazhamozhi 355 says living long together in one place, even for brothers, is painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two verses name a real dilemma of kinship: separation is hard and long cohabitation painful, so kin are best close but not under one roof."},{"a":"pz:356","b":"k:938","type":"develops","note":"Kural 938 lists what gambling destroys: wealth, truth, compassion; Pazhamozhi 356 shows by the Bharata that dice between kin destroy kinship itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's loss of compassion becomes, in the epic case, brothers' sons turned into strangers and enemies, so the worst stake at dice is the bond with the other player.","la":"its Bharata story names a loss the couplet's list leaves out, the player's kin turned ஏதிலர்."},{"a":"pz:349","b":"n:191","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 191 says kin live at ease on what their relative gives, like crops below a tank; Pazhamozhi 349 says the patron's trust in them means nothing unless he sees they do not go in want.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair makes provision the substance of the kin bond: the quatrain shows kin living on the giver, and the proverb verse denies that trust without the meal is any bond at all.","la":"its proverb that all is false but cooked food puts feeding above every other proof of kinship, which the quatrain assumes without arguing."},{"a":"pz:358","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 226 makes relieving the hunger of the destitute the place to store wealth; Pazhamozhi 358 denies the hoard is a reserve and calls aram done at the fit place the reserve for the time of need.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn the language of saving onto giving, but Pazhamozhi widens the deposit from the hungry poor to any aram placed with discernment, and its proverb names when the store is drawn on, at the time of exhaustion.","la":"it joins enjoying wealth, giving it on both sides and choosing the fit occasion as one use set against the hoard, where the couplet names a single act."},{"a":"pz:361","b":"k:335","type":"develops","note":"Kural 335 urges good deeds before the hiccup stops the tongue; Pazhamozhi 361 shows those who wait until illness to begin aram and find it of no use.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets the deadline at the death rattle; Pazhamozhi sets it earlier, at the first sight of illness, and says aram begun then fails because the means is not to hand, as the stone is not when the dog comes.","la":"its dog-and-stone proverb adds that the late giver lacks the means as well as the time, a cause the couplet's deadline does not give."},{"a":"pz:364","b":"k:96","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 96 says sweet speech of good things makes அல்லவை wane and aram grow; Pazhamozhi 364's proverb says that when aram is done, அல்லவை leave, provided it is given with discernment of the fit place.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two share the pair aram and அல்லவை but run the cause differently: in the Kural speech grows aram and wears down evil, in Pazhamozhi aram itself, placed with discernment, drives evil out, so aram is the fruit in one and the agent in the other.","la":"it sets good hereafter, growth of wealth and the going of evil together as fruits of one well-placed aram, with discernment as their condition, where the couplet names one fruit of speech."},{"a":"pz:365","b":"n:92","type":"agrees","note":"Both use death, old age and disease as the spur: Naladiyar 92 to share wealth while it is in hand, Pazhamozhi 365 to do aram while one can.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same triad backs sharing wealth in the Naladiyar and the use of a rare human birth in Pazhamozhi, which ends the triad with death as a stupor that puts one to sleep, so the urgency rests on the rare chance as well as on the threat.","la":"it puts the rarity of human birth before the triad of disease, old age and Death, joining a reason of opportunity to the quatrain's reason of threat.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:365","b":"n:37","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 37 says the gains of human birth should not be spent serving the body; Pazhamozhi 365 says the hard-won human birth should be used for aram as far as one can before disease, age and Death arrive together.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar argues from what human birth is for; Pazhamozhi adds the deadline, so the rare birth has both a purpose and a closing time, and aram is measured by capacity, ஆற்றும் துணையும்.","la":"it couples the rarity of the birth with the three that end it, which the quatrain, speaking of gains and the body, does not name."},{"a":"pz:366","b":"k:612","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 612 warns against letting a task die midway; Pazhamozhi 366 applies this to aram and says an aram broken off midway is worse than one never begun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural guards any task against abandonment; Pazhamozhi makes the rule sharper for aram, ranking the half-done gift below not giving at all, and puts the remedy in the planning that keeps many from being burdened.","la":"it ranks the abandoned aram below never beginning, a ranking the couplet does not make, and ties firmness of plan to sparing others the burden."},{"a":"pz:367","b":"k:33","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 33 asks for aram without ceasing, as far as one is able; Pazhamozhi 367 allows that one may not manage it on many days and asks for it on some days with the best things.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural measures aram by constancy within capacity, Pazhamozhi concedes the lapse and moves the measure to the quality of the gift, so a few days of the best is accepted where the couplet asks for every day.","la":"it trades frequency for quality, some days with the best things, which the couplet's 'without ceasing' leaves no room for."},{"a":"pz:368","b":"n:280","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 280 counts guarding wealth among its bitter sufferings; Pazhamozhi 368 says why guarding is vain: those who eye wealth outnumber those who keep it, as birds take grain under the watcher's eye.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar finds custody painful; Pazhamozhi finds it futile, since the covetous are always more than the keepers, and under its heading on aram this futility is the argument for giving wealth rather than keeping it.","la":"its pairing of grain and wealth makes the watcher's failure an image of all custody, where the quatrain lists custody as one of four pains."},{"a":"pz:368","b":"pz:208","type":"qualifies","note":"Pazhamozhi 208 counts guarding earned wealth oneself a duty and handing it to the worthless a folly; Pazhamozhi 368 says that even wealth guarded with care is outnumbered by those who eye it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together the book's two verses on custody limit each other: 208 says keep it yourself, 368 says keeping it never wins, so custody is a duty under the wealth heading that the aram heading turns into a reason to give."},{"a":"pz:370","b":"k:249","type":"develops","note":"Kural 249 likens aram done without அருள் to a muddled man's sight of truth; Pazhamozhi 370 calls அருள் the root of aram and its abandonment a loss of the greater for the lesser.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows graceless aram as an empty act; Pazhamozhi names grace as aram's root and adds the cost of leaving it, the hare given up for a crow, so the fault is a bad exchange as well as a blindness.","la":"its hare-and-crow proverb ranks grace above whatever aram is done without it, a ranking of goods the couplet's simile of sight does not give."},{"a":"pz:371","b":"k:276","type":"develops","note":"Kural 276 calls those who deceive as if renounced while unrenounced in heart the hardest of all; Pazhamozhi 371 extends false practice to aram as well as penance and says it binds the deceiver in both worlds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural judges the false renouncer by his cruelty to others; Pazhamozhi turns the harm back on him, a rope he ties on himself, and carries the blame into the next life, so the fraud is self-ruin as much as an injury.","la":"it pairs penance with aram as the two things that can be falsely kept and this life's blame with the next, which the couplet, speaking only of renunciation, does not."},{"a":"pz:371","b":"tk:23","type":"same_image","note":"Thirikadugam 23 calls giving, faultless conduct and a mind free of possessions a rope that binds good deeds; Pazhamozhi 371 calls penance and aram practised falsely a rope that binds oneself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same rope image holds merit fast in Thirikadugam and holds the person fast in Pazhamozhi, so whether giving and penance are a rope that secures good or one that ties up the doer turns on sincerity.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:363","b":"n:342","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 342 shows the base man refusing with excuses when urged toward the faultless; Pazhamozhi 363 says those who will not take up aram should be made to take it, as a mother feeds a refusing infant.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Naladiyar treats refusal of the good as a mark of baseness and leaves it there; Pazhamozhi treats the refuser as a child to be fed by force, so refusal becomes a ground for compulsion rather than contempt.","la":"its mother-and-infant image adds a remedy and a motive, the other's lasting good, to the quatrain's bare portrait of refusal."},{"a":"pz:359","b":"n:328","type":"develops","note":"Both name the road after death with செல்வுழி: Naladiyar 328 mocks the fool who does not tie on its provisions; Pazhamozhi 359 shows the worthy making great wealth a protection for it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar sets clutching wealth against provision for the road; Pazhamozhi's cane both chewed and boiled into jaggery lets the same wealth serve now and keep for later, so present use and provision are not opposed.","la":"its cane-and-jaggery proverb holds enjoyment and provision in one use of wealth, where the quatrain sets the grip on wealth against the provision."},{"a":"pz:372","b":"n:98","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 98 calls giving to those who will give back a profitable loan; Pazhamozhi 372 shows the same giving as bait cast to land a larger fish.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar names the return-seeking gift a loan between equals; Pazhamozhi adds the disproportion, a small gift aimed at a large catch, so the fault is not only expecting return but calculating a profit on it.","la":"its angler's image adds the ratio of small outlay to large gain that the quatrain's loan does not state."},{"a":"pz:373","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1009 says others take wealth amassed without love or virtue; Pazhamozhi 373 names the taker, the seizing king, and sets the small gift to the destitute against the hoard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves the taker as 'others'; Pazhamozhi makes him the king who drives the hoarder out, and pairs the lost hoard with rain in the wilderness, so the choice is between feeding royal power and feeding the destitute.","la":"it sets two ends of wealth side by side, the hoard going to the king and a single gift going to the starving, which the couplet's one fate does not contrast."},{"a":"pz:374","b":"k:215","type":"develops","note":"Kural 215 likens the wise man's wealth to water filling the village tank; Pazhamozhi 374 uses the tank to say discerning giving from long-gathered wealth does not quickly drain it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's tank is wealth open to all; Pazhamozhi's tank is wealth that lasts though drawn on, so the same image answers the giver's fear of exhaustion, on condition that a capable man gives with discernment.","la":"it adds to the tank image the time it took to fill and the skill of the one who gives from it, so lasting depends on how the wealth was gathered and how it is given."},{"a":"pz:375","b":"k:1052","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1052 says asking is a pleasure if what is asked comes without suffering; Pazhamozhi 375 calls excellent the giver who reads the asker's mind and gives what he wants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural conditions the asker's pleasure on getting without pain; Pazhamozhi locates the means in the giver, who spares that pain by reading the need from the asker's manner and giving the fit thing, not stones for the bowl.","la":"its begging-bowl proverb adds that the gift must match the need, a requirement on what is given that the couplet does not make."},{"a":"pz:376","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 218 says those who know what is due do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even without means; Pazhamozhi 376 says those long used to giving still relieve dependents when they have nothing to give.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural gives the reason, knowledge of what is due, and Pazhamozhi gives the source, long habit of giving, so the giving that survives poverty is both understood and practised into the body like a horse's training.","la":"its thin-but-harnessed horse adds that the capacity shows only when put to work, a point the couplet's bare statement lacks."},{"a":"pz:376","b":"n:141","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 141 says the well-born in rags keep their principles, as a starving lion will not eat grass; Pazhamozhi 376 says the habitual giver, wasted, still relieves his dependents, as a thin great horse shows in harness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use a noble animal reduced by want; the Naladiyar's lion shows what the noble will not do, Pazhamozhi's horse what he still does, so poverty is shown to test both refusal and service.","la":"it turns the quatrain's refusal into a positive act, relieving others' distress, so the proof of nobility in want becomes giving rather than abstaining."},{"a":"pz:377","b":"k:220","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 220 grants for argument that ruin may come by ஒப்புரவு and calls it worth buying with oneself; Pazhamozhi 377 denies that anyone has ever become poor by giving.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural meets the objection by accepting the cost; Pazhamozhi meets it by denying the cost exists, so the two show two answers to the fear that giving ruins, one heroic and one reassuring.","la":"its proverb flatly denies the premise the couplet entertains, and places the claim of ruin in the mouths of the takers rather than the giver."},{"a":"pz:378","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who hoard and lose do not know the joy of giving; Pazhamozhi 378 asks whether those who hide wealth fearing it will dwindle have not seen the well refill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both ask the hoarder 'do they not know', but the Kural points to the joy he misses and Pazhamozhi to the fact he misjudges, that drawing renews the source, so the hoarder is wrong about both pleasure and arithmetic.","la":"its well proverb supplies the hoarder's own reason, fear of dwindling, and answers it with a fact of nature the couplet does not use."},{"a":"pz:378","b":"pz:374","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 374's tank does not quickly run dry; 378's well springs fresh each time it is drawn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The tank says giving does not soon exhaust stored wealth; the well says drawing on it renews it, so within one chapter the claim rises from endurance to replenishment."},{"a":"pz:379","b":"k:595","type":"develops","note":"Kural 595 says a water-flower rises to the water's depth and a man's height to his mind; Pazhamozhi 379 uses the same image to say a patron's gifts are great by the measure of his worth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures a man's rise by his inner drive; Pazhamozhi applies the image to giving, so the size of a gift becomes a reading of the giver's worth and not of the suppliant's rank.","la":"it carries the image from striving to giving and sets it against the foolish view that gifts follow the receiver's rank, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"pz:380","b":"pz:379","type":"develops","note":"379 says a patron's gifts are great by his own worth; 380 says givers themselves know the measure of their worth and need no prompting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"379 states that the giver's worth sets the gift; 380 adds that the giver knows that measure himself, so the true patron gives unprompted and the poet need not name or press him."},{"a":"pz:381","b":"pn:116","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 116 shows Pari's daughters after his fall, in a thorn-fenced hut counting salt carts; Pazhamozhi 381 shows Pari's daughter giving a bard gold in a drought.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru remembers the daughters as the dispossessed; Pazhamozhi keeps one of them as a giver who feeds a bard even when rain fails, so the ethics work turns the elegy of Pari's house into proof that his giving outlived his fortune."},{"a":"pz:381","b":"pz:376","type":"agrees","note":"376 says habitual givers relieve dependents even with nothing left; 381 names Pari's daughter giving gold in a drought.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"376 states the rule in general with a horse; 381 gives it a named case from the most famous house of givers, so the chapter pairs the principle with its exemplar."},{"a":"pz:383","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 221 calls every gift but one to the destitute a measured loan; Pazhamozhi 383 says even lavish giving with no eye to return is wage labour if it looks to fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural strips the name of giving from gifts that expect a material return; Pazhamozhi strips it also from gifts that expect only fame, so the wage can be praise as well as goods.","la":"it adds fame as a hidden return and names lack of discernment as the giver's fault, which the couplet's loan does not cover."},{"a":"pz:384","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1051 says that if the worthy giver withholds, the blame is his and not the asker's; Pazhamozhi 384 shows a never-refusing giver hiding what he has because many ask one thing, and puts the fault on the crowd of askers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural places the blame for withholding on the giver; Pazhamozhi names a case where withholding comes from pity for those who would be turned away, so the blame moves to the number of askers.","la":"its proverb adds the askers' number as a cause of withholding, a condition the couplet's single asker and single giver leave out.","gr":3},{"a":"pz:385","b":"n:126","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 126 likens attaching to one thought worthy who is not to opening a casket for sandal and finding a snake; Pazhamozhi 385 likens seekers who attach to patrons unable to satisfy them to axle grease that soaks away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's failed attachment brings danger, Pazhamozhi's brings slow waste, so the pair shows two costs of attaching without examining: harm from the unworthy and loss to the incapable.","la":"it moves the quatrain's failed choice of a friend into the suppliant's choice of a patron, and names the seeker's own great desire as the cause."},{"a":"pz:386","b":"pz:373","type":"develops","note":"373 says the concealed hoard ends as provision for seizing kings; 386 says wealth that escapes its guard and is seized by a great king leaves those who did not give with no remedy.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"373 sets the hoard's end against the gift's use; 386 adds the hoarder's helplessness after the seizure, so the king's taking appears in the chapter twice, as the hoard's fate and as the moment past remedy."},{"a":"pz:387","b":"k:122","type":"develops","note":"Kural 122 says to guard restraint as wealth, since life has no greater gain; Pazhamozhi 387 sets self-possession above uncounted wealth, good birth, royal property and a king's praise, for both worlds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's metaphor of restraint as wealth becomes a contest here: self-mastery is weighed against real wealth, lineage and court honour and wins in this life and the next, so the figure is cashed out as a ranking.","la":"it names the rivals that restraint outranks, wealth, birth, royal goods and royal recognition, and extends the claim over both lives."},{"a":"pz:388","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"Kural 126 says drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a safeguard (ஏமம்) for seven; Pazhamozhi 388 mocks those who do not guard the five early and so make no safeguard (ஏமம்) for the road after the body.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the same pair, guarding the five and the safeguard it earns, but the Pazhamozhi adds a deadline: the couplet promises a return across births, the proverb shows the loss of one who begins only when the body is falling.","la":"it joins the guarding of the five to the 'three' taken up, puts the act in youth (சிறுகாலை), and turns the couplet's promise into a jibe at the late starter."},{"a":"pz:389","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Kural 46 asks what one gains by going the outer way if household life is lived in virtue; Pazhamozhi 389 asks what use is a body that does not renounce once friends, peace, marriage and the house are achieved.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two rhetorical questions face each other: on the reading that the Kural's 'outer way' is renunciation, it makes a virtuous household sufficient, while the Pazhamozhi makes the finished household only the stage before the renunciation that alone gives the body its use.","la":"it lists the household's tasks (friends made, enemies quieted, a wife joined, a house set up) as completed, so household life becomes a sequence that ends, not a sufficient path."},{"a":"pz:389","b":"pz:390","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 389 says the body that does not renounce after the household is useless; 390 says those who choose neither household nor renunciation lose both.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two verses give an order and a warning: household life first, renunciation after, and the one fault both condemn is the man who stops in between, holding neither."},{"a":"pz:390","b":"n:365","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 365 ranks striving in austerity first and dwelling with loved ones as the middle way; Pazhamozhi 390 condemns those who stand in the middle, choosing neither household nor its absence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The word 'middle' moves: for the Naladiyar the middle is the household, a lesser but honest life, while for the Pazhamozhi the middle is indecision between the two lives, and only that is worthless, so neither life is shamed, only failing to commit.","la":"it drops the ranking of the two lives and puts the fault in the gap between them, with the image of a pole-carrier who drops both loads."},{"a":"pz:391","b":"n:6","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 6 says no one here has leapt clear of Death and escaped (கூற்றம் குதித்துய்ந்தார் ஈங்கில்லை) and urges the wealthy to give; Pazhamozhi 391 closes on the same words as a proverb after a list of wealth, lustre, strength, beauty, youth and birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same saying about leaping Death serves two ends: the Naladiyar draws from it a duty to give wealth now, the Pazhamozhi uses it to cancel every advantage of fortune and birth at once, showing the saying was a common proof for different counsels.","la":"it sets six goods (wealth, lustre, strength, beauty, youth, birth) before the saying, so the proverb strikes a whole inventory, where the Naladiyar names wealth alone."},{"a":"pz:391","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 53 lists home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth and strength as impermanent and says the best renounce them; Pazhamozhi 391 lists wealth, lustre, strength, beauty, youth and birth and says they give no escape from Death.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Two nearly identical lists of six goods lead to different conclusions: the Naladiyar argues from their passing to renunciation, the Pazhamozhi only to their powerlessness before Death, leaving the remedy to the verses around it.","la":"it adds birth (இற்பிறப்பு) and lustre to the goods that fail and drops home and repute, so lineage too is placed among the things Death ignores."},{"a":"pz:394","b":"n:130","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 130 asks the heart how long it thinks it will live while it clings to home and pines for children; Pazhamozhi 394 bids the heart not to die speaking fondly of the house, since wives and children are many bonds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both speakers argue with their own heart about house and children, and together they show the form: the Naladiyar shows the heart its brief span and the good deeds that alone remain, the Pazhamozhi pictures the deathbed scene of one still talking of home.","la":"it names wives and children together as many bonds in a proverb, and sets the scene at death, where the quatrain asks a question about years."},{"a":"pz:395","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"Kural 345 asks what further tie (தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல்) is left to those cutting off birth, when even the body is excess; Pazhamozhi 395 asks the same question (தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல்) of those who left children and wealth, and answers with the elephant whose tail sticks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Pazhamozhi repeats the Kural's very question, and its proverb supplies what the couplet's 'even the body is excess' leaves unpictured: the danger after renunciation is not the great ties but a small remnant that holds the whole back.","la":"it names what was given up, dear children and earned wealth, and adds the image of the stuck tail, which ranks the last small tie as the real obstacle."},{"a":"pz:397","b":"k:359","type":"develops","note":"Kural 359 says one who knows the support and lives so that supports perish is freed from clinging pains; Pazhamozhi 397 shows a flame living on wick, bowl and ghee and says that, its supports gone, the self cuts off birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's abstract play on சார்பு gets a working model in the lamp: the flame is not destroyed by being blown out but goes out when what feeds it is gone, so release is shown as starvation of support, not an act of force.","la":"it lists the flame's three supports, wick, bowl and ghee, and adds the water proverb, so the couplet's one word becomes a concrete chain of dependence."},{"a":"sp:1","b":"sp:payiram","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 1 offers the book as rain that ends a great famine; the payiram calls the author's hand rain that ends the famine of not learning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the famine pun is the book's own gloss on its title: the five roots cure, and the rain they bring is learning.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:1","b":"sp:20","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1 names killing, lying, meat and theft as the four the book sets aside; verse 20 lists theft, gambling, bad company, harmful speech and meat as what frees one from a flesh body.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The preface's four abstentions are, by verse 20, the price of release from rebirth, so the book's opening ethic is already a soteriology."},{"a":"sp:2","b":"k:247","type":"develops","note":"Kural 247 says that world is not for those without grace as this world is not for those without wealth; verse 2 gives enjoyment to wealth and dharma to grace, then spells out grace as three refusals.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet only assigns grace its world, the verse says what grace does here: it refuses blame, sin and tale-bearing, so grace is chiefly a restraint of deed and tongue.","la":"It pairs wealth and grace as the first two items and then gives grace three more, so the list tips the balance the couplet keeps even."},{"a":"sp:3","b":"tk:1","type":"agrees","note":"Both counted works place the chaste wife first among things that heal: Thirikadugam calls her shoulders part of a medicine, Sirupanchamoolam calls her amrita.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both works the wife's chastity heads a list whose other items are public goods (worthy company, learned friends; land, king, soldier), so the household virtue is ranked as a good of the realm."},{"a":"sp:4","b":"k:402","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 402 likens the unlearned man's wish to speak to a woman without breasts desiring womanhood; verse 4 sets the unlearned man's subtlety beside the pride of a woman lacking both ears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two share an old figure of the unlearned as a body lacking its marks, but the verse splits the simile into two separate objects of laughter and adds a third, the poor man's boast.","la":"It turns the couplet's single simile into a list of three pretensions, so the woman's pride is no longer a figure for the unlearned but a ridiculous thing of its own."},{"a":"sp:4","b":"pz:13","type":"develops","note":"Pazhamozhi 13 says the fine point an unlearned man has found is hard for him to show, so he should not rate himself among the good; verse 4 says that same subtlety makes the good laugh.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the pair gives both the cause and the effect: the unlearned man's insight cannot be shown, and so to the learned it is merely comic."},{"a":"sp:5","b":"k:150","type":"develops","note":"Kural 150 says that even one who does wrong does well not to desire another's wife; verse 5 promises fortune to one who does not desire her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet values the restraint even without other virtue; the verse makes it the gate to fortune, so the same act is moved from a minimum of decency to a source of gain.","la":"It sets not desiring another's wife beside penance, giving and learning as four means to four ends, so this restraint is ranked as one of the means of a whole life."},{"a":"sp:6","b":"k:546","type":"develops","note":"Kural 546 says victory comes from the unbending sceptre, not the spear; verse 6 makes the unbending its beauty and gives the army elephants and the soldier courage.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets the sceptre against force; the verse keeps both, giving force its own beauties and joining the sceptre's straightness to truthful speech by one word, கோடா.","la":"It gives the sceptre the same quality as unbending speech and sets it among the army's and soldiers' beauties, so justice is ranked with force instead of against it."},{"a":"sp:7","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"Kural 30 says those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous who wear kindness to every creature; verse 7 says the பார்ப்பான் is the one who shares his food.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both define a priestly title by conduct rather than birth, and the verse makes the conduct a concrete daily act, sharing food, where the couplet names a disposition.","la":"It defines the brahmin in a row with the ascetic, the leader and the worthy man, so the birth title is treated as one more role earned by an act."},{"a":"sp:7","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 134 says a பார்ப்பான் can relearn the Veda but his birth is ruined when conduct fails; verse 7 defines the பார்ப்பான் by sharing food alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet keeps birth and makes conduct its condition; the verse drops birth from the definition, so the verse goes further than the Kural in detaching the title from birth.","la":"By listing the brahmin with titles anyone can earn, the list removes the birth the couplet still assumes."},{"a":"sp:8","b":"k:575","type":"develops","note":"Kural 575 calls kindly regard the eye's only jewel; verse 8 calls it the eye's beauty and gives four other members their beauties.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet stops at the eye; the verse runs the same 'X's beauty is Y' through feet, reckoning, song and king, so the last two beauties are other people's praise.","la":"It sets the eye's beauty in a series that ends with a king judged by his people, so kindly regard becomes the first of a scale from the body to rule."},{"a":"sp:9","b":"k:403","type":"develops","note":"Kural 403 says the unlearned are good if they can keep silent before the learned; verse 9 says the unlearned man's tongue dies before those who know the good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes silence a choice that redeems; the verse makes it a shame that strikes, so the same silence is a virtue in one and a punishment in the other.","la":"It sets the unlearned man's mute tongue beside the killer's, the false witness's and the debtor's, so ignorance is ranked with guilt as a cause of shame."},{"a":"sp:9","b":"k:1066","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1066 says nothing degrades the tongue like begging, even water for a cow; verse 9 says the well-born man's tongue dies when he has eaten and knows it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both place the shame of dependence in the tongue; on our reading of the verse, eating from the wrong hand silences the well-born as begging degrades the asker.","la":"It sets the well-born man's shame beside the debtor's, so the list treats owing and being fed as one kind of silencing."},{"a":"sp:10","b":"k:127","type":"develops","note":"Kural 127 says to guard the tongue above all, or suffer through faulty words; verse 10 says abuse is death to the tongue as its own eggs are to the spider.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns of the speaker's suffering; the verse's animals show why: the harm grows from the speaker's own body, as a creature is killed by its own young or horns.","la":"It sets abusive speech last after four creatures killed by their own parts or offspring, so the tongue's ruin is shown as self-made."},{"a":"sp:11","b":"in:39","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 39 calls liberality in a man who has nothing to give painful; verse 11 calls the generosity of a man without food laughable.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two name the same thing from two sides: painful to the would-be giver and laughable to the onlooker, so empty generosity fails both inwardly and in public."},{"a":"sp:11","b":"k:1014","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1014 says shame is the ornament of the worthy and without it a proud walk is a disease; verse 11 says the worth of a shameless man is laughable.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both deny worth without shame, but the verse makes the failure social ridicule where the couplet makes it a sickness of the man himself.","la":"It sets shameless worth among four other claimed virtues without ground (vow, generosity, soldiering, poetry), so shame is shown as the ground the others also need.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:12","b":"k:254","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 254 says grace and its absence come down to not killing and killing, and eating that flesh is not right; verse 12 calls killing poison and eating meat a cruel poison.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet derives the wrong of eating from killing; the verse ranks eating as the worse poison, so the eater is held more guilty than the killer.","la":"It grades the two acts with நஞ்சு and கொடுநஞ்சு, a ranking the couplet does not make."},{"a":"sp:12","b":"in:22","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 22 calls eating flesh to swell one's flesh painful before all else; verse 12 calls enjoying meat a cruel poison.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both counted works single out meat-eating with a stronger word than their other items (முன்னின்னா, கொடுநஞ்சு), so the ranking is shared across the group."},{"a":"sp:13","b":"in:25","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 25 opens with seeing a friend's troubles as painful; verse 13 opens with distress among friends as painful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both works put a friend's distress first among painful things, but Sirupanchamoolam pairs it with not giving, so the pain is also a call to help."},{"a":"sp:14","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"Kural 55 praises the wife who worships her husband, not a god, and commands the rain; verse 14 makes walking her husband's way a woman's virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the wife's devotion cosmic power; the verse sets the same following among son, kin, soldier and subjects, as one case of keeping one's place.","la":"It lists the wife's following with the son's, the soldier's and the people's, so the rule for women is presented as one form of a general duty of following."},{"a":"sp:14","b":"k:542","type":"develops","note":"Kural 542 says the people live looking to the ruler's sceptre as the world looks to the sky; verse 14 calls living by the sceptre the virtue of the king's land.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of the king's duty to the people's need; the verse turns it into the people's virtue, obedience to the sceptre.","la":"It counts living by the sceptre among household and military duties, moving it from the king's chapter into the subject's code."},{"a":"sp:15","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"Kural 152 says bear with excess and better still forget it; verse 15 calls bearing a wrong greatness and brooding on harm smallness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks forgetting above bearing; the verse names brooding as the opposite of forgetting and calls it smallness, so it supplies the failure the couplet leaves implied.","la":"It sets greatness and smallness as a contrasting pair and adds ending enmity as a third good, a contrast the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"sp:16","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"Kural 303 says forget anger at anyone at all, since evils are born of it; verse 16 says anger is good and nobility bad before people without worth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading of verse 16 the two disagree about the same act: the Kural allows no exception to forgoing anger, while the verse makes the base an exception.","la":"It inverts three house virtues at once (anger, nobility, pride) for one audience, so the exception is a rule, not a lapse."},{"a":"sp:16","b":"n:70","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 70 says no one bitten by a dog bites it back, so the high do not answer the low in kind; verse 16 uses a barking dog to say goodness to the base is wasted and anger is good.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the dog for the base man and draw opposite counsels: the Naladiyar asks for restraint, the verse for anger and pride.","la":"It sets anger and pride as good beside nobility as bad, so the list reverses the Naladiyar's ranking of forbearance above response."},{"a":"sp:16","b":"in:40","type":"qualifies","note":"Innaa Narpathu 40 calls fierceness in a man of self-restraint painful; verse 16 says anger is good before people without worth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair marks a limit: fierceness is unfitting in the restrained man in general, but Sirupanchamoolam carves out the base as the one place it serves."},{"a":"sp:17","b":"sp:18","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 17 names lying with women and keeping house as the fruits of the unrenounced body; verse 18 says not turning to women who want him is a way out of rebirth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book grants the householder's body its fruits but sets the higher reward, not being born again, on giving up exactly those fruits."},{"a":"sp:18","b":"k:260","type":"develops","note":"Kural 260 says all creatures worship the one who neither kills nor eats flesh; verse 18 says one who does not eat flesh out of craving will hardly take a body again.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's reward is honour in this world; the verse's is release from the body, so the same abstention is given a Jain soteriological fruit.","la":"It joins refusing meat to truth, honesty and chastity, each tested by its strongest temptation, as joint conditions of release."},{"a":"sp:18","b":"sp:20","type":"agrees","note":"Verses 18 and 20 both close a list of abstentions, meat last, with the promise that one will not take a body again.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book states the same Jain promise twice in three verses, with meat as the final condition each time, which shows how central diet is to its idea of release."},{"a":"sp:19","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"Kural 410 says others are to the learned as beasts are to people; verse 19 calls the learned gods and the unlearned ghouls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet uses one step, beast to human; the verse widens the scale to god and ghoul and saves the beast for the boaster, so learning is placed on a three-step ladder of beings.","la":"It sets learning beside early earning and against nostalgic boasting, ranking each as god, ghoul, wretch or ox."},{"a":"sp:23","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 53 says the best renounce home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth and strength on seeing they do not last; Sirupanchamoolam 23 says those who rely on form, youth, beauty, family and lineage and do not renounce young never see the unshaken state.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives impermanence as the reason to renounce; the verse adds a deadline, youth itself, and a second obstacle, pride of birth, so delay and lineage join transience as what keeps a man from release.","la":"It sets family and lineage (குடி, குலம்) in the same list as the body's goods and makes youth both an item and the time limit."},{"a":"sp:24","b":"n:363","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 363 names three bad wives (the defiant, the one who skips the kitchen, the one who withholds food) as weapons that kill the husband; Sirupanchamoolam 24 names five doings of a bad wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's faults all lie inside the house and against the husband, the verse's all lie outside it (drink, absence, other houses, prying, bad company), so together they show the period's norm for a wife policing both her service and her movements.","la":"Its five faults face outward from the house, where the quatrain's three are failures of service within it."},{"a":"sp:25","b":"pn:195","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 195 tells aged worthies that the axe-bearing one will bind them, so if they cannot do good they should at least avoid harm; Sirupanchamoolam 25 sets four commands under the certainty of Death's coming.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both rest counsel on the certainty of death, but the Sangam poem bargains down to a minimum (at least do no harm) while the ethics verse turns the same ground into four positive and negative rules and adds a reckoning after death."},{"a":"sp:27","b":"sp:20","type":"develops","note":"Verse 20 lists not stealing, not gambling and not befriending the black-hearted among five abstentions; verse 27 says the friend of outsiders picks up toddy, theft and gambling.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Verse 20 puts bad company beside theft and gambling as one more abstention; verse 27 shows it as their cause, so the book's ban on bad friends is a ban on the way the other vices are learned.","gr":4},{"a":"sp:28","b":"pn:183","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 183 says learning is good even at the cost of serving the teacher, giving him wealth and accepting the lower place; Sirupanchamoolam 28 says the true teacher seeks no gain and the fit pupil serves with love.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam king accepts payment and service as the price of learning; the Jain verse keeps the pupil's loving service but denies the teacher any gain, and adds that learning without casting off the three faults is a sickness."},{"a":"sp:30","b":"n:135","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 135 says learning has no shore and life is short, so the wise choose what to learn; Sirupanchamoolam 30 says no one has learned the whole of the treatises, and no one knows all or nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain draws from the boundlessness of learning a method of choosing; the verse draws from the same limit a judgment of persons: no one is complete in knowledge or virtue, and no one wholly lacks knowledge either.","la":"It sets the limit of learning beside matching limits on knowing, not knowing and faultlessness, so the shoreless field becomes one case of a rule that no person is complete either way."},{"a":"sp:31","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"Kural 261 defines tapas as bearing the pain that comes and harming no life; Sirupanchamoolam 31 keeps both, adds three more, and says penance so made is sweet, not lowly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet defines penance by two ethical acts; the verse keeps them, turns bearing pain into forgetting it, and meets an objection the couplet does not raise, that penance degrades the one who does it.","la":"It extends the couplet's two marks to five, adding no wrong to another and the giving up of desire and anger, and closes with a verdict on how the penance feels.","gr":5},{"a":"sp:32","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 134 says a brahmin who forgets the Veda can relearn it but one whose conduct fails loses his birth; Sirupanchamoolam 32 says the brahmin is the one who sacrifices.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both tie the birth term பார்ப்பான் to something done rather than to birth alone, but the Kural ties it to conduct and the verse to the rite of sacrifice, two different tests for the same status.","la":"It puts the brahmin's defining act in a row with a woman's chastity, a scholar's listening, a poet's mind and the father's gift, so each kind of person is named by one mark."},{"a":"sp:32","b":"k:418","type":"develops","note":"Kural 418 says ears not pierced by listening are deaf though they hear; Sirupanchamoolam 32 says the one who listens is the flawless great scholar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet condemns the unlistening ear; the verse makes listening the very definition of the great scholar, ranking it above reading or composing.","la":"It sets the listener beside the poet, whose excellence is in his mind, so hearing and composing are named as two separate excellences."},{"a":"sp:33","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"Kural 255 says the standing of life lies in not eating flesh and hell will not release the one who eats; Sirupanchamoolam 33 says the one led upward who never begins on flesh is sure to go there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet threatens the meat-eater with a hell that does not open; the verse promises the abstainer a sure passage, and ties the abstention to the teacher who leads him, so diet becomes the proof of being led.","la":"It ends a list of true titles (liberal man, merchant, teacher) with abstaining from flesh as the condition of the pupil's passage."},{"a":"sp:34","b":"k:247","type":"develops","note":"Kural 247 says that world is not for those without grace as this world is not for those without wealth; Sirupanchamoolam 34 says enjoyment comes of wealth and dharma of melted grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet weighs grace against wealth by the worlds they open; the verse gives each its fruit (enjoyment, dharma) and calls the grace melted, so grace is a softening of the heart, not a possession.","la":"It sets the wealth and grace pair at the end of a chain of five causes and effects, so enjoyment and dharma are results like blame and homage."},{"a":"sp:34","b":"sp:2","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 says enjoyment belongs to the man with wealth and dharma to the man with grace; verse 34 repeats the pairing as cause and effect.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book states the wealth and grace pairing twice; in verse 34 it becomes causal and the grace is qualified as melted, so the second statement tightens the first."},{"a":"sp:35","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world; Sirupanchamoolam 35 assigns enjoyment to giving, heaven to penance and release to knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet frees giving from any reward; the verse keeps a reward but ranks it lowest, so both deny that giving is the way to heaven, one by indifference to reward and one by a ladder of rewards.","la":"It ranks giving, penance and knowledge on one ladder of fruits, enjoyment, heaven, release, which the couplet never builds."},{"a":"sp:35","b":"sp:5","type":"develops","note":"Verse 5 says one who wants to be rid of the body does penance and one who wants standing gives; verse 35 says penance brings heaven and only knowledge brings release.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Read together, the two verses disagree on what ends embodiment: verse 5 credits penance, verse 35 reserves release for knowledge and gives penance only heaven.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:36","b":"n:131","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 131 says beauty of hair, garment and saffron is not beauty and the beauty of learning is; Sirupanchamoolam 36 says beauty of hair, chest, nails, ears and teeth is not beauty and speech fitted to learning is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both deny outward beauty to praise learning, but the quatrain rests learning's beauty on fairness of heart, while the verse rests it on speech that fits the books, so one makes it inward and the other audible.","la":"Its list is all of the body (hair, chest, nails, ears, teeth), not adornments, and it turns on speech rather than learning itself."},{"a":"sp:37","b":"k:1033","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1033 says all who do not live by their own ploughing bow to eat and walk behind; Sirupanchamoolam 37 says the worthy do not eat food got by bowing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the farmer as the one man free of bowing for food; the verse makes refusing such food a rule of the worthy whatever their trade, so the Kural's economic point becomes a point of self-respect.","la":"It sets the refusal beside not sleeping in a friend's house and not taking lost goods, so eating by bowing is ranked with small breaches of dignity and honesty."},{"a":"sp:38","b":"k:323","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 323 ranks not killing first and not lying after it; Sirupanchamoolam 38 calls not lying, wealth and not killing each good without ranking them.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Set beside the couplet, the verse declines the Kural's ranking of the two vows and admits wealth among the goods between them, a Jain list that values non-killing without making it the single highest good here.","la":"It lists not lying, wealth and not killing side by side with wealth in the middle, where the couplet orders the two vows and leaves wealth out."},{"a":"sp:39","b":"k:507","type":"develops","note":"Kural 507 says trusting the ignorant out of fondness brings every folly; Sirupanchamoolam 39 says giving treasury, accounts and stores to men without understanding is setting a crow to guard rice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the fault in the master's fondness; the verse measures the appointee (understanding that does not match his size) and shows the result as the guard eating what he guards.","la":"It names the particular offices of trust and closes with the crow image, where the couplet speaks only of trust in general.","gr":5},{"a":"sp:40","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"Kural 773 says fierceness is great valour but help to a foe in trouble is its cutting edge; Sirupanchamoolam 40 names five helpless enemies whom true soldiers never approach.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes mercy to the fallen the edge of valour as a principle; the verse turns it into signs a soldier can read on the field, and makes the restraint the definition of a true soldier.","la":"It turns the couplet's one principle into five recognisable signs of surrender or helplessness."},{"a":"sp:40","b":"pn:9","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 9 praises a Pandya who warns cows, brahmins, women, the sick and the sonless to take shelter before he shoots; Sirupanchamoolam 40 names five who give up in battle and must not be approached.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam king protects those who never fight by warning them before battle; the verse protects those who stop fighting during it, so together they give both ends of the period's code of sparing in war."},{"a":"sp:41","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"Kural 148 calls it manhood not to look at another's wife; verse 41 puts another's wife last in a list of five women men avoid.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's one rule becomes a whole map of forbidden women, so the Kural's virtue of restraint is turned into a period code drawn by age, menses and vow.","la":"It sets another's wife among a girl, a woman past her menses, a female ascetic and an unguarded woman, so adultery becomes one case of a wider bar by bodily state and vow."},{"a":"sp:42","b":"k:55","type":"tension","note":"Kural 55 praises the wife who does not worship a god but rises worshipping her husband; verse 42 counts worship of the deity, beside the one who brings her fortune, as part of a wife's excellence.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read flatly, the Kural sets the husband in place of the god while this verse asks the wife to worship both, so the two works divide on whether her devotion may go beyond the house.","la":"It sets the deity's worship as a fifth duty beside income, kin and guests, making religion one household task among others rather than the one the husband displaces."},{"a":"sp:42","b":"k:51","type":"develops","note":"Kural 51 defines the wife by the home's excellence and living within her husband's means; verse 42 opens with knowing the house's ways in proportion to income.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both works put income first in defining a wife, and the verse spells out what the couplet's 'excellence of the home' includes: kin kept content, guests and worship.","la":"It ranks budgeting first and then lists the duties it must pay for, so the couplet's measure of means becomes the base of a household order.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:43","b":"n:52","type":"tension","note":"Verse 43 tells one to seek the man who knows five parts of astrology and have him fix the wedding day; Naladiyar 52 calls those who prattle of astrology the greatest of fools.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two Jain-era ethics works disagree on the worth of the same learned craft: one treats astrology as the expert's knowledge a marriage needs, the other as a madman's distraction from death.","la":"It breaks astrology into five technical parts, treating it as a craft to be mastered, which is exactly what the quatrain mocks as endless prattle."},{"a":"sp:44","b":"n:386","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 386 says beauty in a modest woman is like learning in the wise or a sword in a hero's hand; verse 44 says that without modesty and four other qualities even Lakshmi's beauty cannot be held.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The pair state one claim from both sides: modesty makes beauty serviceable, and beauty without it is ungovernable, so beauty in both works has worth only as the instrument of a virtue.","la":"It names four qualities beside modesty and sets them against beauty at its divine limit, a ranking the quatrain's three similes leave implicit."},{"a":"sp:45","b":"k:542","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 542 says the people live looking to the king's sceptre as the world looks to the sky; verse 45 says the king rises only as the field bank, water, paddy and people rise beneath him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the people depend on the king, while the verse runs the dependence upward from a mud ridge, so read together they show the dependence runs both ways.","la":"Its chain of five causes puts the king at the end of a ladder of rises beginning with a field bank, a causal order the couplet does not trace."},{"a":"sp:45","b":"k:1034","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1034 says farmers see many kings' umbrellas under their own; verse 45 traces a king's rise to the rising of the field's water and paddy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's claim that farming underwrites kingship is given its mechanism: the height of the crown is measured from the height of the field bank.","la":"It sets out each link from field to throne in order, where the couplet states only the end result."},{"a":"sp:46","b":"k:44","type":"develops","note":"Kural 44 says a household that fears blame and shares its food never loses its line; verse 46 counts not blaming and sharing with all among the marks of a good house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two conditions of a lasting house reappear inside a longer list that adds care for dependants and for the calf, so the house's sharing is carried to its animals.","la":"It places sharing beside not milking a calf to death, so household generosity is extended to the livestock, which the couplet does not reach."},{"a":"sp:47","b":"k:328","type":"develops","note":"Kural 328 says gain that comes by killing is the lowest for the worthy however great; verse 47 ends that those who know will not kill whatever they gain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse takes the couplet's refusal of gain by killing and widens killing to the hope of the needy, the kin's meal, one's generosity and fame, so neglect becomes a kind of slaying.","la":"Its pun on கொல் puts killing a life beside killing a hope or a reputation, making non-killing a rule for social conduct as well as for life."},{"a":"sp:48","b":"k:742","type":"develops","note":"Kural 742 names water, open ground, hill and shady forest as a fort; verse 48 names deep water, forest, saline waste, a lofty hill and the people, and makes having them the rule for crowning a king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fort of the couplet gains a fifth wall made of people, and its possession becomes the test for who may be made king rather than a feature of a stronghold.","la":"It adds the people as a fifth defence and turns the list into a qualification for kingship, which the couplet's list of terrain does not do."},{"a":"sp:48","b":"tk:100","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam 100 names a loyal army, a fearless fortress and great treasure as the limbs of kings; verse 48 breaks the fortress into five and makes it the ground of being crowned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One counted work treats the fort as one limb among three, the other spells it out as five, with the people among them, so the two together show the fort as both a royal possession and a living defence."},{"a":"sp:49","b":"k:1039","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1039 says land left by an owner who does not go to it sulks like a wife; verse 49 counts negligence first among five ruins of the ploughman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's single fault of absence is set at the head of a list that adds pride, unfinished work, quarrels and a broken tie with the craftsman, so a farm fails socially as well as by neglect.","la":"It sets negligence among social and moral causes of ruin, where the couplet names absence alone."},{"a":"sp:50","b":"sp:12","type":"develops","note":"Verse 12 calls killing and eating flesh poison among five poisons; verse 50 calls not killing good and killing bad, then weighs illiteracy and anger on the same scale.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the book, killing is the fixed first term of two lists, and in each the author sets lesser faults (overpowering the weak, slander, illiteracy, anger) beside it, so its scale of harms keeps killing as the measure."},{"a":"sp:51","b":"k:211","type":"develops","note":"Kural 211 says duty seeks no return, as the world gives nothing back to the rain; verse 51 calls it good to host guests without reckoning a return.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's principle of giving without return is carried to the guest at the door, and the verse sets it next to fasting and not eating from others' hands, so giving freely and taking nothing are one ideal.","la":"It pairs the host who expects no return with the man who will not eat from another's hand, so the list joins the giver's and the receiver's sides of the same refusal."},{"a":"sp:52","b":"k:60","type":"develops","note":"Kural 60 calls good children the ornament of a home's excellence; verse 52 counts bearing children first among a wife's medicines that make the husband yield.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Children, an ornament of the house in the couplet, become in the verse the wife's first hold over her husband, so the same good is read for its effect within the marriage.","la":"It sets childbearing beside beauty, accord and restraint in food as means of holding the husband, a use of children the couplet does not name."},{"a":"sp:52","b":"sp:44","type":"develops","note":"Verse 44 names five qualities without which a woman cannot be held by her husband; verse 52 names six by which a wife holds him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses look at the same marriage from opposite ends: what lets a husband keep a wife within bounds and what lets a wife bring a husband to yield, so the book treats control as running both ways."},{"a":"sp:53","b":"k:57","type":"tension","note":"Kural 57 asks what use guarding by confinement is and puts a woman's own steadfastness first; verse 53 names five men of her family as the ones who guard her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read as a claim about what keeps a woman, the couplet moves the guard inside her while the verse places it in her husband, brother, uncle, son and father, so the two disagree on where chastity is kept.","la":"It names the guard as five kinsmen, a list of external keepers that the couplet's single inner guard sets aside."},{"a":"sp:54","b":"sp:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 53 names five kinsmen who guard a woman; verse 54 says she falls if she herself does not guard her restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side in the book, the two verses split the guard between the men of her family and her own நிறை, so the book holds both views the Kural sets against each other."},{"a":"sp:55","b":"n:139","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 139 says the unlearned who keep company with the learned gain sense daily; verse 55 says the unlearned who stay outside the learned's company gain only laughter.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The quatrain gives the remedy and the verse gives the cost of refusing it, so together they make company, not learning alone, the line between gaining sense and being mocked.","la":"It grades rewards (gold, wealth, fame, laughter) by kind of learning and company, setting the unlearned at the bottom of a ladder the quatrain does not draw."},{"a":"sp:56","b":"k:969","type":"develops","note":"Kural 969 says men of honour give up life when honour is at stake, like the yak; verse 56 makes perishing, for the man of honour, the last of five acts of wisdom.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Death for honour, a matter of nature and pride in the couplet, is counted here as wisdom beside forgiveness and care for living beings, so it is framed as discernment rather than temper.","la":"It sets dying for honour beside revealing good, forgetting wrongs and helping living beings, so the list makes it one act of wisdom among gentler ones."},{"a":"sp:57","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"Kural 471 says to weigh the strength of the deed, oneself, the opponent and allies before acting; verse 57 says the minister acts after measuring his own state, the work, the opponents' state and the order of things.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's four-fold weighing is given to the minister and its fourth term changes from allies to the matchless order of things, so prudence moves from counting forces to reading circumstance.","la":"It moves the reckoning to the minister and replaces allies with the order of things, a different fourth term than the couplet's.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:58","b":"k:501","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 501 says a man is trusted after being tested by virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life; verse 58 says the trusted man has wealth, enjoyment, fearlessness, grace at death and rare dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet uses wealth, pleasure and fear as temptations to test a man, the verse treats wealth, enjoyment and fearlessness as qualities he must have, and adds grace, so the same terms change from tests to credentials.","la":"It turns the couplet's four tests into five possessions and adds grace that survives death, which the couplet does not name."},{"a":"sp:59","b":"k:1038","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1038 ranks manuring above ploughing and guarding above watering; verse 59 lists storing straw, cattle, tools and manure as care that comes before the plough is set.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put the plough after other care, and the verse widens the couplet's ladder to the farmer's stores and animals, then turns the whole into an image for the scholar's finer plough.","la":"It sets manure among the farmer's other provisions and closes with the reciter of texts, so the farming list becomes a figure for study."},{"a":"sp:59","b":"sp:49","type":"develops","note":"Verse 49 names five ruins of the ploughman; verse 59 names the care he gives before he ploughs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book gives the farmer's life as a pair of lists, what ruins him and what he must tend, so negligence in 49 is answered item by item by the provisions of 59."},{"a":"sp:61","b":"k:890","type":"develops","note":"Kural 890 likens living with those not in accord to living in a hut with a snake; the verse names five such housemates and calls them a disease.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's unnamed discordant housemates become a roster of the house (kin, wife, servant, son, neighbour), so the inner enemy is located in the very people one cannot leave.","la":"It names five separate relations of the house and makes them one kind, a disease within, where the couplet leaves 'those not in accord' unnamed."},{"a":"sp:62","b":"k:215","type":"develops","note":"Kural 215 likens the wise man's wealth to a village tank full of water; the verse makes the gift of water itself the first of five charities.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's tank is a figure for wealth all may draw on; the verse makes it literal and the lowest step of a scale that ends in founding temples and halls.","la":"It ranks the charities from a drink of water to founded buildings and puts a condition on charity at home, which the couplet's single image lacks.","gr":6},{"a":"sp:62","b":"sp:65","type":"develops","note":"Both verses list works for others' use of water, land and shelter; 62 promises great enjoyment, 65 heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they mark a scale of public works and fruits: water, shade and halls bring enjoyment, while tanks, roads and wells bring heaven itself."},{"a":"sp:63","b":"n:99","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 99 says never call a gift too little, for like the ascetic's bowl it fills slowly; the verse lists the pittances (a handful, begged salt, a rag) given without calling them small.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain argues from the receiver's slowly filled bowl and the verse from the poor giver's actual pittances, so the same rule on small gifts is seen from both ends of the gift.","la":"It names five concrete pittances, salt the giver himself begged and a torn rag among them, so the rule is tested on gifts a beggar could make."},{"a":"sp:63","b":"sp:64","type":"develops","note":"63 says small gifts not called small bear great fruit; 64 names the melting of the giver's heart as what makes a small gift grow like a hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the first verse sets the gift's size aside and the second names what replaces it as the measure, the melting of the giver's heart."},{"a":"sp:64","b":"n:38","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 38 says a small gift grows vast when it reaches worthy hands; the verse says it grows like a hill when the giver's heart melts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both say a small gift grows huge but place the cause differently: the Naladiyar in the worth of the receiver, Sirupanchamoolam in the melted heart of the giver.","la":"It gives five images of melting as the measure of the giver's heart, a condition on the giver that the quatrain does not state.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:65","b":"pn:18","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 18 urges the Pandya Nedunchezhiyan to build up tanks where the land dips, for those who bind water win wealth hereafter, sole rule and fame; the verse makes tank-digging one of five works that take any doer to heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The duty the Sangam poet urged on one king becomes in the ethics work a merit open to any man of means, and the promised reward narrows from rule and fame to heaven."},{"a":"sp:65","b":"tk:16","type":"agrees","note":"Thirikadugam 16 counts one who digs a well of drinking water without stint among three who gain a body that does not die; the verse counts a shared well among five works leading to heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two counted works reward the same well differently, one with a deathless name in this world and the other with heaven, so the group divides on where the fruit of public works falls."},{"a":"sp:66","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"Kural 280 says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; the verse lists five ascetic forms and says that in the Kali age renunciation is false and the household alone true.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet discounts the ascetic's marks but keeps renunciation, while the verse, for its own age, discounts renunciation itself, so one critique of outward form reaches opposite ends.","la":"It names five ascetic forms where the couplet names two, and ties its judgement to an age (கலி), which the couplet does not.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:66","b":"k:49","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 49 says that what is called virtue is household life; the verse ends that in the Kali age household life alone is true.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both favour the household, but the Kural grounds it in what virtue is and the Jain verse in the corruption of the age, so one preference has a timeless and an historical ground.","la":"It reaches the household's primacy by eliminating a list of ascetic forms, a route the couplet does not take.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:66","b":"sp:68","type":"develops","note":"66 rejects the outward marks of ascetics; 68 rejects four austerities and keeps only restraint of the five senses.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two verses strip ascetic display in two moves, first the marks on the body and then its austerities, leaving only restraint of the senses.","gr":3},{"a":"sp:67","b":"k:240","type":"develops","note":"Kural 240 says those who live without blame are the ones who live; the verse names the means of a life the worthy bless, beginning with remembering one's birth-house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the verdict of blame and fame on a life and the verse the means of earning the good verdict, so memory of lineage becomes a guard on reputation.","la":"It sets lineage, self-hold and awareness of others' regard as causes of the good name the couplet only judges, ending on two opposed speeches of others."},{"a":"sp:68","b":"k:343","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 343 says the field of the five must be crushed and all desire let go; the verse sets restraint of the five and wanting nothing against four rejected practices.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The verse supplies what the couplet leaves out, the practices that do not count, so restraint of the senses is defined against bathing and bodily austerity rather than only stated.","la":"It sets the couplet's restraint of the five against four rejected practices, a contrast the couplet lacks."},{"a":"sp:69","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Kural 256 says that if none killed to eat, none would sell flesh; the verse divides the sin among killer, consenter, taker, cook and eater.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ties the eater to the seller by demand; the verse spreads the sin along the whole chain, so even one who only consented or only cooked is guilty.","la":"It names five links of the chain and makes them one guilt, where the couplet names only the eater and the seller."},{"a":"sp:70","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"Kural 225 ranks the strength of enduring hunger behind the strength of removing it; the verse names kinds of fasters and promises kingship to those who feed them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks feeder over faster, while the verse honours both, making the faster the fit receiver and the feeder the one rewarded, so the ranking becomes a partnership.","la":"It names kinds of fasting (alternate days, crescent to full moon) and sets the prisoner among them, and attaches kingship as the reward, which the couplet does not.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:71","b":"sp:73","type":"develops","note":"71 counts delivering a child, caring for the pregnant and taking up an infant as great virtue; 73 sets out the care of pregnancy, birth and infancy step by step.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two verses make care of mother and newborn a sequence of virtues, from sheltering the pregnant to medicine for the child, which the Kural does not treat."},{"a":"sp:72","b":"pn:219","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 219 addresses a warrior wasting his body as he sits to die, with many sitting with him; the verse counts those who sit facing north among the needy to be fed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The act the Puram honours as heroic resolve the Jain ethics verse counts as a state of need beside age and disease, so the dying hero becomes a receiver of care."},{"a":"sp:74","b":"sp:69","type":"develops","note":"69 argues against flesh from the shared sin of the chain of killing; 74 argues for not eating and not killing from the pains of bearing and rearing a life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book gives two grounds for one abstention: the guilt that runs from killer to eater, and the cost of bringing any life into being.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:75","b":"n:123","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 123 says the old deed of breaking crabs' legs returns as a disease that rots the fingers; the verse says those who once cured disease now live free of it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, disease and health are both the return of old deeds, cruelty coming back as rotting fingers and healing as a body without illness, so the law runs both ways.","la":"It names five kinds of sufferer cured and makes healing a merit whose fruit is health, where the quatrain gives only the punishment."},{"a":"sp:76","b":"sp:70","type":"qualifies","note":"70 promises kingship to those who feed fasting ascetics; 76 makes feeding the hungry a duty even to the low who are not ascetics.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The book first rewards gifts to ascetics who fast, then widens the duty past them, so the receiver's worth governs the reward but not the obligation to feed.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:76","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 221 says giving to those who have nothing is true giving; the verse makes feeding the hungry a duty even when they are low and not ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet defines giving by the receiver's need; the verse tests that against rank and lets need prevail over lowness and lack of austerity.","la":"It sets feeding the hungry among duties of impartiality and restraint of anger, and names the low receiver the couplet does not."},{"a":"sp:77","b":"sp:72","type":"agrees","note":"72 promises kingship for one mouthful given to the weak, the old and the dying; 77 promises a glad life to the end for gifts to the wounded, blind and lame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two lists of the helpless overlap but name different rewards, showing the book attaches a distinct fruit to each list rather than one fixed reward for giving.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:78","b":"k:227","type":"develops","note":"Kural 227 says hunger does not touch one used to sharing his food; the verse says one who shares his food in a famine lives beyond eighty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet promises the sharer freedom from hunger; the verse puts the sharing in a famine, when it costs most, and promises long life instead.","la":"It ranks famine-sharing with fearless fighting and giving before eating as one road to long life, joining the householder's and the soldier's merits."},{"a":"sp:79","b":"k:691","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 691 tells those who join warring kings to be like men warming at a fire, neither far nor near; the verse sets the warring king among things the wise do not befriend, beside the elephant and the tiger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet teaches the courtier how to stand near the dangerous king; the verse, for one not bound to serve, says not to go near, so one danger yields a rule of distance and a rule of avoidance.","la":"It ranks the warring king with a loosed elephant and a prowling tiger, so the king is one more natural danger, not a master to be served."},{"a":"sp:79","b":"tk:19","type":"same_image","note":"Thirikadugam 19 counts one who grips the tusk of a killing elephant among three who hang by a thread; the verse shuns the place where a fierce elephant is let loose.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both counted works use the killing elephant as the type of avoidable danger and set human follies beside it, so folly with people is measured against folly with beasts."},{"a":"sp:80","b":"k:454","type":"tension","note":"Kural 454 says understanding seems to be in the mind but lies in one's company; the verse says blame and fame come from company but understanding from the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two divide the same pair the opposite way: the Kural puts understanding in company, while Sirupanchamoolam keeps it in the mind and gives company only reputation.","la":"It splits the effect of company by kind, reputation (பழியும் புகழும்) against understanding (மதி), a division the couplet does not make.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:80","b":"n:245","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 245 says people are not as their kind but as their minds; the verse keeps understanding in the mind while granting company power over blame and fame.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"With the Naladiyar the verse forms a Jain answer to the Kural's chapter on company: mind, not kind, decides what a person is, though the verse concedes that company decides their name.","la":"It concedes to company what the quatrain does not, blame and fame, while agreeing that the mind decides understanding."},{"a":"sp:80","b":"n:180","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 180 says people spotless in mind are scorned for their company, as sandal burns with the scrub; the verse says blame and fame come from company.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain shows the cost the verse only states: a spotless mind does not save one from the blame of one's company.","la":"It states the rule in two halves, company for name and mind for understanding, where the quatrain gives only the half on reputation."},{"a":"sp:81","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"Kural 92 ranks a glad face and sweet speech above giving with a glad heart; the verse joins soft words and a melting gift in one man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural separates sweet speech from giving and ranks speech higher; the verse refuses the choice and makes the soft word the manner in which the gift is given.","la":"It places soft speech and the melting gift side by side as the last two of five marks, after inner restraints, so speech and gift become one closing act rather than rivals."},{"a":"sp:82","b":"k:134","type":"develops","note":"Kural 134 says forgotten Veda can be relearnt but a brahmin's birth is ruined if conduct fails; the verse asks what a man of five vows lacks without the scriptures.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both rank conduct above scriptural recitation, but the Kural keeps the brahmin and his birth in view while the verse makes the point for any man who keeps five lay vows.","la":"It names what the conduct is (five vows against attachment, theft, adultery, slander and killing) where the couplet speaks only of conduct in general.","gr":2},{"a":"sp:82","b":"sp:18","type":"develops","note":"Verse 18 promises no further birth to one who does not lie, steal, desire women or eat flesh; verse 82 lists nearly the same vows and sets them above scripture.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the same vows earn two fruits in this book: release from rebirth in one verse, and freedom from needing the scriptures in the other."},{"a":"sp:83","b":"n:279","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 279 makes a cow milked by force the image of the base giver; the verse names milking by a stuffed calf-skin as a cruelty the good refuse.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses forced milking only as a figure for grudging gifts; the verse shows the literal act was itself judged wrong, so the image carried moral weight before it became a figure.","la":"It sets the milking trick in a list that ends with killing creatures, so a small deceit on an animal is ranked with taking life, which the quatrain's figure never claims."},{"a":"sp:84","b":"k:694","type":"develops","note":"Kural 694 says to suppress whispers and shared smiles before the great; the verse lists five things not wanted where elders sit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two signs are about what a ruler might read into them; the verse widens the rule to elders generally and adds faults of judgment such as favouring friends.","la":"It adds counsel, partiality to friends, enmity and song to the couplet's laughter, and cites the old treatises as its authority, turning a courtier's caution into a code of the assembly."},{"a":"sp:85","b":"sp:86","type":"develops","note":"Verse 85 names five subjects that make a first-rank learned man; verse 86 names five arts that make a middle-rank one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they grade learning in tiers: doctrine, logic and release make the first rank, while arithmetic, music and crafts make only the middle."},{"a":"sp:87","b":"sp:88","type":"develops","note":"Verse 87 calls five men dogs for lacking shame, friendship and reverence or for serving another; verse 88 names shame, friendship, the soldier and reverence as what comes easily.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The second verse answers the first word for word, so the pair shows the same qualities as failures in one man and as natural ease in their proper holders."},{"a":"sp:89","b":"k:98","type":"develops","note":"Kural 98 says sweet speech free of pettiness brings joy here and hereafter; the verse runs sweet speech through kinship and grace to release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural promises joy in two worlds; the verse names the mechanism, speech producing grace and grace producing release, and sets harsh speech's enmity beside it.","la":"Its items are links in a chain rather than peers, so the list becomes a ladder from manners to release, a sequence the couplet does not draw."},{"a":"sp:90","b":"k:160","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 160 grants greatness to those who fast but ranks them after those who bear harsh words; the verse says refusing food befits a rich man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks fasting as a whole; the verse makes its worth depend on who fasts, fitting most where food is plentiful and the refusal costs something.","la":"It pairs each virtue with the person in whom it costs most (penance with youth, fasting with wealth, beauty with chastity), a matching of virtue to station the couplet lacks."},{"a":"sp:92","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household for the outside way; the verse says household life is better than a mean dharma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's question reaches toward renunciation itself; the verse limits the claim to a poor or false dharma, a narrower comparison from a Jain text that honours the ascetic.","la":"It adds the fruit of the household, that feeding the virtuous returns as the means to feed again, which the couplet's rhetorical question does not name."},{"a":"sp:93","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world; the verse sets giving with good company and steady hearing and promises the high state.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural cuts giving loose from reward by a hypothetical; the verse binds it back to a destination, showing the two works disagree on whether the fruit is part of the reason.","la":"It pairs giving and company each with its opposite and caps them with hearing as a penance, so giving becomes one step on a road to the high state."},{"a":"sp:94","b":"s:368","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar verse asks what use ash and bathing are without learning to be born otherwise; the verse ranks worship on the Ganga's bank lowest, below restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar dismisses ritual bathing outright, while the ethics verse keeps Ganga worship as a merit but ranks it beneath restraint of presence and speech.","la":"Its ranking into foremost, high and lowest places the rite in an order of merits rather than rejecting it."},{"a":"sp:95","b":"k:114","type":"develops","note":"Kural 114 says the worthy and unworthy are seen by what each leaves behind; the verse says the rightness of five kinds of person is known when they die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both defer judgment of worth past the life; the verse applies the rule to five stations and names death as the moment of proof.","la":"It runs the test through wife, soldier, ascetic, trader and arbiter, so one rule of posthumous judgment is made to cover every station of life."},{"a":"tk:0","b":"k:610","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the god who measured the world with his foot: the invocation worships the stride; the Kural makes all it spanned the reward of a king without sloth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same Trivikrama stride serves the ethics group both as an object of worship and as a measure of worldly gain, so the mythic foot carries devotion in one book and royal ambition in the other.","la":"It sets the stride beside two other kicks of the same feet, so the measuring becomes one of three acts that together are the feet worshipped.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:1","b":"k:794","type":"develops","note":"Kural 794 says a friend of good family who shrinks from blame is worth buying; Thirikadugam makes lasting company with the worthy of an old family a medicine.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet gives the price of such company and the verse its use: one pays for it because it cures.","la":"It makes the old family's worthy one of three remedies with a chaste wife and a learned friend, so lineage-company is ranked with marriage as a cure.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:1","b":"k:417","type":"develops","note":"Kural 417 says men of deep heard learning do not speak folly even when mistaken; Thirikadugam says friendship with such men clears the tangle of words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says kelvi keeps its owner's own speech clear; the verse adds that it clears others' confusion, so the virtue passes to the friend.","la":"It turns a trait of the learned into a benefit for those who befriend them, and sets it among remedies.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:2","b":"k:134","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Kural 134 says a brahmin can relearn the Veda but is ruined if his conduct declines; Thirikadugam asks every gentle person not to let his character decline, and lists following the Veda-knowers as a separate item.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the same verb குன்று applied to conduct in both, but the Kural ranks conduct above Vedic learning inside one birth, while the Thirikadugam keeps them as two equal items for everyone.","la":"It moves the rule of undeclining conduct from the brahmin to all gentle people and places Vedic guidance beside it, not above it.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:3","b":"k:451","type":"develops","note":"Kural 451 says the small take small company as kin; Thirikadugam names living as kin of the unlearned as a ruin that comes from ignorance.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet explains who seeks low company (the small); the verse names why (ignorance) and what follows (ruin), giving the act a cause and a result.","la":"It sets low company beside wife-beating and entering a house with petty men, so all three are failures of a householder's judgment.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:4","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"Kural 882 says to fear the enemy who seems like kin; Thirikadugam counts taking by the hand a man formerly enraged against one as the conduct of one about to die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet warns against the hidden foe in general; the verse names the concrete act, taking back a former enemy, and states its outcome as death.","la":"It puts this misplaced trust beside living in sight of foes and walking unarmed among cattle, all three plain exposures to known danger.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:5","b":"k:90","type":"develops","note":"Kural 90 says the guest withers at the host's changed face; Thirikadugam counts arriving worn as a guest in a strange town as a road to suffering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures hospitality by the host's face; the verse looks from the guest's side and counts dependence on strangers as itself a hardship, which is why the host's face matters so much.","la":"It sets guesthood beside an unused ford and an unwilling woman, three ventures whose danger lies in the unknown other side.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:5","b":"k:1191","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Kural 1191 calls the love of those loved back the seedless fruit; Thirikadugam counts embracing women who do not desire one among roads to suffering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam couplet praises mutual desire from inside love; the ethics verse states the reverse as a rule of conduct, so the need for mutual desire is affirmed in both kinds of poetry.","la":"It moves the claim from a lover's lament into counsel, and likens union without desire to entering water at an unused ford.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:6","b":"k:773","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Kural 773 makes ஊராண்மை the cutting edge of valour, help to a foe in distress; Thirikadugam defines ஊராண்மை by modesty under honour, patience under scorn and unreturned dharma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows one word moving from the battlefield, where it is mercy to the fallen enemy, into everyday conduct, where it is a pride made of self-effacement.","la":"It breaks the Kural's single heroic act into three civil ones and calls their sum a pride.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:7","b":"k:727","type":"agrees","note":"Both say learning held by one who fears the assembly is useless: the Kural likens it to a sword in a coward's hand, Thirikadugam to a mute man's dream.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two images name different failures: the sword is a power left unused, the mute's dream an experience that cannot be told, so together they say fear of the assembly blocks both action and communication.","la":"It sets the timid scholar beside an incapable heir to wealth and a trapped fish, three goods held that cannot be brought out.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:8","b":"k:439","type":"develops","note":"Kural 439 says never admire oneself; Thirikadugam names lineage, learning and victory as things that need not speak of themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the rule; the verse gives a reason for it in the three strongest cases of worth, each of which has its own witness (an assembly, a king), so self-praise is not only wrong but redundant.","la":"It picks the three things most tempting to boast of and attaches to each the audience that proclaims it.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:9","b":"k:450","type":"develops","note":"Kural 450 ranks leaving the company of the good as ten times worse than many enemies; Thirikadugam counts leaving the company of the great as what fools love.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet measures the harm of leaving good company; the verse says why people still do it: fools want it, so the harm is chosen.","la":"It sets leaving good company beside adulterous desire and base resolve, making all three expressions of a fool's preferences.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:10","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Kural 322 makes sharing food the chief of all precepts; Thirikadugam counts living beside those who do not share their food as something that yields no good.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet praises the sharer; the verse shows the cost of the non-sharer falling on his neighbours, so sharing is a public good of a settlement, not only a private virtue.","la":"It sets the stingy neighbour beside a town without teachers and an assembly without elders, three lacks that make a place barren.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:11","b":"k:127","type":"develops","note":"Kural 127 says whatever else one fails to guard, guard the tongue; Thirikadugam singles out the unguarded talk of a man who is not drunk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Adding 'not drunk' removes the one excuse for loose speech, so the verse sharpens the couplet: unguarded speech when sober is chosen, not lapsed into.","la":"It makes careless speech one of three social intrusions (uninvited watching, untrusted visiting) that offend the whole town.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:12","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81 says all household life exists for caring for guests and doing வேளாண்மை; Thirikadugam defines the வேளாளன் as one who does not eat while a guest waits.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books use the vel- word for help to guests; the verse makes it a definition of a person, showing that in this group the name is earned by hospitality, not given by birth or land.","la":"It sets the vellalan beside the debt-free man and the grateful man as three kinds of kin worth having.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:12","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 110 says there is no escape for one who kills a benefit received; Thirikadugam defines the man of principle as one who does not forget.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet condemns ingratitude as unforgivable; the verse turns gratitude into a mark by which one chooses kin, making it a reason to bind oneself to a person.","la":"It makes gratitude a qualification for kinship, alongside freedom from debt and hospitality.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:13","b":"k:544","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 544 says the world embraces the feet of the king who embraces his people; Thirikadugam names such a king as hard to find anywhere.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet states the reward of the caring king as if it were the order of things; the verse adds that such kings are rare, so the world's embrace is seldom earned.","la":"It sets the good king beside a well-conducted youth and a flawless ascetic, three rarities each at odds with his station.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:15","b":"k:813","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 813 says friends who weigh their gains are no better than thieves; Thirikadugam counts the one who clings by fortune and watches for profit as not to be befriended.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural shames the calculating friend by comparison; the verse adds when he shows himself, at the turn of fortune, which is also when the liar and the hollow man show themselves.","la":"It groups the calculating friend with the liar and the one who claims equality in one's fall, three who fail at reversal.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:16","b":"pn:18","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Purananuru 18 tells a Pandya that those who join water to land give bodies and life; Thirikadugam says one who digs a drinking well without stint gains an undying body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem credits the builder of water works with the lives of his people; the ethics verse credits the well-digger with his own undying body, so the same act of providing water is rewarded outward in one and inward in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:16","b":"k:233","type":"develops","note":"Kural 233 says only lofty fame stands undying; Thirikadugam names fame as one of three ways to an undying body, with a chaste wife and a public well.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes fame the only deathless thing; the verse widens the routes to it, counting a marriage and a work of public water as equally lasting.","la":"It turns the Kural's single route to deathlessness into three, two of them not fame-seeking acts at all.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:16","b":"tk:1","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1 makes a wife chaste as Arundhati a medicine; verse 16 makes the man who won a chaste wife deathless.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the book the chaste wife is given two predicates, cure and deathlessness, so she is valued both for what she does for the husband now and for the name she leaves him.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:17","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1134 has love's flood sweep away the raft of shame and manliness; Thirikadugam has three men let go of the raft of learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both use the raft against a flood, but the Kural's raft is carried off by force while the Thirikadugam's is let go by choice, which makes the ethics verse a charge of fault, not a lament.","la":"It shows one raft dropped at three stages of life (old age, marriage, renunciation), not one lover's moment.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:18","b":"n:92","type":"agrees","note":"Naladiyar 92 sets death, old age and disease before the reader and tells him to share before eating; Thirikadugam names old age, separation and disease as things to fear more than thieves.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The quatrain moves from the threats to a remedy (give now), while the verse stops at the threat and calls it theft, so the pair shows a list that diagnoses beside a quatrain that prescribes.","la":"It adds the separation of the joined to old age and disease, putting an akam grief among the bodily dreads.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:19","b":"k:146","type":"develops","note":"Kural 146 says enmity, sin, fear and blame never leave one who enters another's home; Thirikadugam puts the man at another's door by night between one who runs at a killing elephant and one who plays with a cobra.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists the moral consequences; the verse's company of items makes the danger bodily and immediate, like courting an elephant or a snake.","la":"It frames adultery as reckless risk to life by placing it among two plain acts of physical folly.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:19","b":"tk:9","type":"develops","note":"Verse 9 calls lust for women not one's own what fools love; verse 19 says the man at another's door by night hangs by a thread.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within the book the same act is first a fool's desire and then a mortal danger, so the book judges it both by the wanting and by the result.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:20","b":"k:603","type":"develops","note":"Kural 603 says the family of the slothful fool perishes before him; Thirikadugam counts falling into sloth while one's dependants go hungry as painful to all.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the end of sloth, the family's death; the verse gives its moment, idling while those bound to one hunger, so the harm is seen as it begins.","la":"It sets this sloth beside dependence on others and being scorned as unlearned, three humiliations of a man who fails his station.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:21","b":"iv:22","type":"qualifies","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu calls giving with knowledge of one's income sweet; Thirikadugam fixes the share at a quarter of what comes in.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show that 'knowing one's income' had a number attached: the income is known so that a fixed fourth can go out."},{"a":"tk:21","b":"sp:15","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam calls brooding on harm done to one smallness; Thirikadugam calls not searching out what was done in a quarrel a distinction.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair gives one act two names from both sides, and it supports reading Thirikadugam's doubtful line as forgiving injuries done to one rather than forgetting one's own deeds."},{"a":"tk:21","b":"tk:31","type":"develops","note":"Both verses name learning the good after surveying many things and set it beside giving from the household's income.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The book twice pairs selective learning with measured household giving, so the pairing is a doctrine of the work and not a chance of one list."},{"a":"tk:22","b":"k:361","type":"develops","note":"Kural 361 calls craving the seed of unceasing birth; Thirikadugam makes craving one of three whose seed, once destroyed, ends birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The seed image is shared, but Thirikadugam widens what the seed is made of from an inner craving to include an outward act, false speech.","la":"It sets craving beside attachment and false speech, so a lie becomes one of the seeds of rebirth, which the couplet does not say."},{"a":"tk:22","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 360's triad whose end ends pain is desire, anger and confusion; Thirikadugam's triad whose end ends birth is attachment, craving and false speech.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Comparing the two triads shows the Kural placing the root of suffering wholly in the mind, while Thirikadugam counts a fault of speech among the roots of rebirth.","la":"It replaces anger and confusion with false speech, so the counted causes of bondage include an act of the tongue and not only states of mind."},{"a":"tk:23","b":"k:341","type":"develops","note":"Kural 341 says each thing one leaves removes the pain it would bring; Thirikadugam says a mind that has left many things is a rope to merit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives detachment a negative yield (pain removed); the verse gives it a positive one (merit bound), so letting go both frees and gains.","la":"It sets the detached mind beside the gift and honourable conduct, so the renouncer's letting go and the householder's giving are one kind of rope."},{"a":"tk:24","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 911 says the sweet words of women who want wealth, not love, bring harm; Thirikadugam names a courtesan's sweet speech a sinking mire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kural names the harm; the verse names the mechanism, a pleasing surface over a hook, and its effect, a slow sinking rather than a blow.","la":"It groups the courtesan's speech with bait on a hook and an enemy's bowing, so it becomes one of three lures whose sweetness hides the danger."},{"a":"tk:24","b":"k:931","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the fishhook: Kural 931 likens gambling winnings to the hook a fish swallows; Thirikadugam names the frog wrapped round a hook as bait.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ethics group uses the hook for any pleasure that is the means of capture: the Kural shows it from inside the fish after the gain, Thirikadugam at the bait before it.","la":"It turns the hook from an image of gambling gains into one of three snares beside flattery by a courtesan and by a foe."},{"a":"tk:24","b":"k:828","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 828 says a weapon hides in an enemy's folded hands; Thirikadugam calls the bowing of hardened enemies a mire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names what the homage hides, a weapon, and the verse what it does to the one who trusts it: he sinks.","la":"It puts an enemy's homage beside a courtesan's sweet words, so political and sexual flattery are reckoned one kind of trap."},{"a":"tk:25","b":"k:915","type":"develops","note":"Kural 915 says those of refined understanding do not plunge into the charms of common women; Thirikadugam says the learned do not eat from such a woman's hand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's avoidance is of her body; the verse's is of her food, so the learned man's refusal reaches even to accepting maintenance from her.","la":"It groups her with the arrogant petty man and the robber of a charity, so the bar falls on all who live by a wrong means, not on sex alone."},{"a":"tk:26","b":"tk:34","type":"develops","note":"Both close by naming three who are called the world: verse 26 a considerate guest, a non-killer and a gentle teacher; verse 34 the brahmin, the just king and orderly people.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The book defines 'the world' twice, once by three private virtues and once by three estates, so the word means both good persons and an ordered society."},{"a":"tk:26","b":"k:325","type":"develops","note":"Kural 325 makes the one who refrains from killing the chief of all renouncers; Thirikadugam counts him among those who are the world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural honours non-killing at the head of renunciation; the verse honours it in ordinary social life, so it is a summit in both ways of living.","la":"It sets the non-killer beside a considerate guest and a teacher of good conduct, taking non-killing out of the renouncers' ranking into everyday company."},{"a":"tk:27","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 113 says to let go at once of gain that leaves fairness, even if it brings good; Thirikadugam counts not speaking partially, whatever one gains, as the work of the pure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets fairness against gain; the verse calls the same refusal a purity, so impartial speech is a cleanliness and not only a justice.","la":"It makes impartiality one of three purities with bathing before meals and nobility unto death, joining a bodily rite to two inner virtues."},{"a":"tk:27","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"Kural 298 divides outer purity, made by water, from inner purity, seen in truth; Thirikadugam lists bathing before meals with impartial speech and unfailing nobility as the work of the pure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet separates the two purities; the verse joins them in one list and implies that a claim to purity needs both at once.","la":"It counts the water-purity and two inner purities as one work without ranking them, where the couplet ranks them by kind."},{"a":"tk:28","b":"iv:24","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu calls sweet the austerity of one who wants to win and does not rage, and not craving what one lacks; Thirikadugam says the one without austerity who rages to win and the one who craves what he lacks pound husk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two works state the same pair from opposite sides in nearly the same words, so restraint in contest and in craving is both a sweetness and the only labour that bears grain."},{"a":"tk:29","b":"iv:36","type":"develops","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu calls sweet not snatching what one sees and desires; Thirikadugam asks for no desire for a thing even once it is in one's hand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair grades the restraint: not seizing what is seen is the lower step, not wanting what is already held is the higher.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:30","b":"k:532","type":"develops","note":"Kural 532 says heedlessness kills fame as chronic want kills understanding; Thirikadugam says one who has put away heedlessness in settled wealth keeps lasting fame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the threat to fame in general; the verse says when it strikes, when wealth seems secure.","la":"It places the vigilant rich man beside the courteous giver and the man free of envy, so fame rests on three habits of the wealthy.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:30","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1057 tells of the seeker's joy at givers who do not scorn; Thirikadugam gives lasting fame to the man who does not scorn those who seek him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same courtesy pays both parties: the seeker gets inner joy and the giver lasting renown.","la":"It turns the seeker's joy into the giver's renown and ranks the courtesy with vigilance and freedom from envy."},{"a":"tk:31","b":"k:322","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 322 crowns sharing food and sustaining life as the head of all the authors gathered; Thirikadugam crowns three things as the head of all that is heard: selective learning, unbroken sharing household life, and making wealth by effort.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Sharing food is the head in both, but Thirikadugam shares the crown with the learning that guides the giving and the earning that supplies it.","la":"It divides the couplet's single crown among three, adding learning and self-made wealth to the act of sharing."},{"a":"tk:32","b":"k:424","type":"develops","note":"Kural 424 defines understanding partly as seeing the subtle meaning in others' words; Thirikadugam makes taking the meaning from subtle words a debt the learned owe.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet describes a capacity; the verse turns it into an obligation that comes with learning.","la":"It joins the duty of subtle hearing to two duties of speech, silence when idle words are asked for and speech only to the not-base."},{"a":"tk:33","b":"n:197","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 197 says a son props a failing father as aerial roots hold up a termite-eaten banyan; Thirikadugam says a good minister holds the people like the banyan's aerial roots.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same image of support from below serves the family and the state: the minister is to the kingdom what a son is to an ageing father.","la":"It sets the root-like minister between the sceptre-fearing people and the firm guard as one of a king's three supports."},{"a":"tk:33","b":"k:542","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 542 says the people live looking to the king's sceptre as the world looks to rain; Thirikadugam names people who live in awe of the sceptre as a support of the king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sceptre has two faces to the people, hope in the Kural and fear in Thirikadugam, and the verse counts that fear as an asset of rule.","la":"It makes the subjects' awe of the rod one of three things a king keeps, with a minister and a guard."},{"a":"tk:34","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"Kural 560 says the six-duty brahmins forget their texts when the king fails to guard; Thirikadugam sets the brahmin who has paid his debts beside the just king and orderly people as the world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the brahmin's ritual learning depend on good rule; the verse makes it a coordinate pillar beside the king.","la":"It lists priest, king and people as three parts of one order rather than as cause and effect."},{"a":"tk:34","b":"k:541","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 541 defines justice as investigating (ஓர்ந்து), showing no favour and acting after consideration; Thirikadugam names the king who weighs matters (ஓர்ந்து) and does not bend justice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both put weighing first in justice, but the verse makes the just king only one third of what makes a world.","la":"It places the just king between the dutiful brahmin and the orderly people, so justice is one estate's part of a shared order."},{"a":"tk:35","b":"n:135","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 135 says learning has no shore and life is short; Thirikadugam praises one who has seen the shore of the texts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, mastering a body of texts (நூல்) is possible while learning (கல்வி) itself has no end, so the two claims are about different things.","la":"It ranks the one who reached the texts' shore with a steady mind and freedom from delusion as three who stand on truth."},{"a":"tk:36","b":"k:256","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 256 says no one would sell flesh if the world did not kill to eat; Thirikadugam names the man who says the killing happens without his consent as self-deceived.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse names the buyer's excuse in his own words, and the couplet supplies the answer: the purchase is the cause.","la":"It puts the eater who disclaims the killing beside the flesh-eater who claims compassion and the sacrificer, so three justifications are one kind."},{"a":"tk:36","b":"sp:69","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 69 says the one who consented, the buyer, the cook and the eater share the killer's sin; Thirikadugam mocks one who says the deed is done without his consent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both answer the disclaimer by spreading guilt along the chain: Sirupanchamoolam lists the chain, Thirikadugam ridicules the man who denies his link in it."},{"a":"tk:36","b":"k:259","type":"develops","note":"Kural 259 ranks not killing to eat above a thousand sacrifices; Thirikadugam names one who kills in a desired sacrifice as one who knows only his own view.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural weighs sacrifice against abstention; the verse drops the weighing and calls ritual killing a self-deception.","la":"It groups the sacrificer with two flesh-eaters, so ritual killing is reckoned with ordinary meat-eating rather than above it.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:37","b":"k:188","type":"develops","note":"Kural 188 asks what those who spread the faults of their intimates will do to strangers; Thirikadugam says the measure of friendship shows in backbiting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet reasons from near to far; the verse makes backbiting the gauge by which one learns how far a friendship really reaches.","la":"It sets backbiting as a test of friendship beside tests of lineage and of authority, three signs by which hidden worth is known."},{"a":"tk:38","b":"k:431","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 431 says the prosperity of those without arrogance, anger and smallness is dignified; Thirikadugam names arrogance, anger and the smallness of coveting as an army that breaks wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same triad is the condition of wealth's dignity when absent and the force that destroys wealth when present.","la":"It turns the couplet's three absent faults into one hostile army and defines the third, smallness, as coveting many things."},{"a":"tk:38","b":"tk:40","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 38 makes swelling anger part of an army that breaks wealth; verse 40 makes the strength that wears anger thin the work of the wise.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book measures anger by quantity, swelling or thinning, and ties the first to lost wealth and the second to old wisdom."},{"a":"tk:39","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 920 names double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has left; Thirikadugam names the same three as the work of men without goodness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both name the same three vices, and the verse adds what they share: each is a stupor (மயக்கம்).","la":"It calls each item a மயக்கம், so paid sex and dice are made forms of intoxication alongside drink."},{"a":"tk:39","b":"k:913","type":"develops","note":"Kural 913 likens the false embrace of women bought with wealth to embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room; Thirikadugam calls it seeking a base stupor (புலைமயக்கம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's corpse and the verse's புலை both make the bought embrace a contact with something vile, one by image, one by name.","la":"It sets the bought embrace in a series of three stupors with drink and dice."},{"a":"tk:40","b":"k:310","type":"develops","note":"Kural 310 says those who renounce anger equal those who renounce all; Thirikadugam names the strength that wears anger thin as the work of the anciently wise beside household duties.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural lifts quelling anger to the level of renunciation; the verse keeps it as a householder's strength (விறல்).","la":"It places anger's control with conduct toward women and sharing food, so it is counted among household virtues rather than set equal to renunciation."},{"a":"tk:41","b":"k:955","type":"develops","note":"Kural 955 says an old family does not part from its nature even when its means of giving shrink; Thirikadugam makes the worth that keeps family standing from falling, even when shaken, one of three things chief among all that is heard.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet observes that old families hold firm; the verse turns that observation into a thing to be taught and ranked first, so constancy of lineage becomes the content of instruction, not only its result.","la":"It ranks family constancy with renown from giving to the distressed and with daily widening of friendship, so lineage is made one of three practices that head all teaching."},{"a":"tk:42","b":"k:691","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 691 tells those who serve kings to be like men warming at a fire, neither near nor far; Thirikadugam tells the vellala family to behave toward brahmins as toward fire even when familiar.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ethics group uses one fire image for two powers: the Kural for the king, the Thirikadugam for the brahmin, so the cultivator's caution toward a birth-group is modelled on the courtier's caution toward the throne.","la":"It sets the fire-caution among a farming family's marks of beauty, between refusing gambling wealth and loving the plough, so a rule of birth-rank becomes part of a household's grace."},{"a":"tk:42","b":"k:1033","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1033 says only those who live by ploughing truly live; Thirikadugam makes love of farming the beauty of one family, the vellala.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives ploughing to anyone who will live independently; the verse ties its honour to one birth-group, so the same praise of farming reads in one book as a way of life open to all and in the other as a family's mark.","la":"It joins love of farming to two other rules for the same family, so the plough becomes one of three marks of a caste-occupation rather than a universal claim.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:42","b":"tk:12","type":"develops","note":"Verse 12 defines the vellalan as one who does not eat while a guest waits; verse 42 names three other marks of the vellala family's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the book defines the vellala by four conducts, hospitality, refusing gambling gain, caution toward brahmins and love of the plough, so the title is built from conduct and occupation together."},{"a":"tk:43","b":"k:127","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 127 says guard the tongue whatever else goes unguarded; Thirikadugam makes restraint of the mouth only the first of three restraints, yielding purity, below restraint of deed and of heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural puts the tongue first as the one thing to guard; the verse ranks it lowest of three, so what the Kural sets as the minimum the verse treats as the first rung toward release.","la":"It turns restraint into an ascending ladder, mouth, deed, heart, with a rising fruit at each rung, a ranking the couplet does not make."},{"a":"tk:43","b":"k:121","type":"develops","note":"Kural 121 says restraint leads among the immortals; Thirikadugam gives the heavenly state only to restraint of deed and reserves release for restraint of heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's single reward of restraint, a place among the gods, becomes in the verse the middle of three rewards, so heaven is placed below release.","la":"It splits the Kural's one restraint into three with three fruits, purity, the divine state and release, ranking them."},{"a":"tk:44","b":"k:82","type":"develops","note":"Kural 82 says eating alone while a guest waits outside is not to be wished even if the food is ambrosia; Thirikadugam calls a day on which one ate without a guest a sickness to the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet rejects the guestless meal before eating; the verse counts its cost afterwards as an illness, so the same refusal is given both a motive and a symptom.","la":"It sets the guestless day beside the unembraced night and the morning without giving, so hospitality becomes one of three duties that together fill the householder's whole day."},{"a":"tk:44","b":"tk:12","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 12 says the vellalan does not eat while a guest waits; verse 44 says a day eaten without a guest is a sickness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The book treats the guestless meal twice, once as a loss of the title vellalan and once as a sickness of the strong, so the same fault is a loss of rank and of health.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:45","b":"k:1067","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1067 begs beggars not to beg of those who conceal; Thirikadugam sends the one who without love hides from askers what he could give into hell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks to the beggar and protects him from the concealer; the verse speaks of the concealer and names his punishment, so the two together cover both sides of the refused request.","la":"It sets concealment from askers between tormenting the unable and forgetting death, so withholding is ranked with cruelty and heedlessness as a road to hell."},{"a":"tk:45","b":"n:20","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 20 urges keeping Death's stealth in mind and taking provisions in time; Thirikadugam names not reflecting on Death's certain coming as one of three things that lead to hell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes remembering death prudent; the verse makes forgetting it a sin with a penalty, so heedlessness is turned from folly into guilt.","la":"It places forgetting death beside two acts against others, so an inward lapse is judged on the same scale as cruelty and concealment.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:46","b":"tk:14","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 names youth, folly and meanness as three the wise do not approach; verse 46, with the same closing line, names an unsound horse, a fierce elephant and the place of an angry man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the book's 'the wise do not go near' covers both inner dispositions and outer dangers, and the angry man of verse 46 stands at the join, a human fault treated like a dangerous beast."},{"a":"tk:47","b":"k:57","type":"tension","note":"Kural 57 says the guarding of women by confinement achieves nothing and their own steadfastness is the best guard; Thirikadugam counts women among three things whose guarding must not be slighted. The tension holds on reading காப்பு in 47 as the householder's watch over women.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two books disagree on who keeps a woman's virtue: the Kural moves the guard inside her and calls custody useless, the Thirikadugam keeps it with the man as with his wealth and food.","la":"It ranks women with earned treasure and good food as goods to be watched, which is the custodial view the couplet sets aside."},{"a":"tk:48","b":"k:159","type":"develops","note":"Kural 159 says those who bear the harsh words of transgressors are as pure as renouncers; Thirikadugam says the one who takes abuse as sweet speech lives having seen the true thing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet praises bearing abuse as endurance; the verse goes further and has abuse heard as sweetness, so the pair shows two grades, enduring the bitter and no longer tasting it as bitter.","la":"It sets taking abuse as sweet beside taking gruel as ghee-rice and bitterness as sugar, so patience in speech becomes one case of a general reversal of taste."},{"a":"tk:48","b":"k:1065","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1065 says even thin gruel is sweetest when earned by one's own effort; Thirikadugam says the one who values gruel as ghee-rice has seen the truth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural makes gruel sweet because it is one's own; the verse makes it sweet because the seer no longer ranks foods, so one ground is self-respect and the other indifference.","la":"It puts the gruel among three reversals of speech and taste, so its sweetness comes from a way of seeing, not from how it was earned."},{"a":"tk:49","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"Kural 52 says if the wife lacks household excellence the life is nothing whatever else it has; Thirikadugam names a wife who wears the house down by falsehood as one of three who are no security for this life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet lays the failure of the household on the wife alone; the verse sets beside her the disobedient junior and the abusive head, so the pair shows the house can be ruined from three places.","la":"It distributes the household's ruin across junior kin, the head and the wife, rather than resting it on the wife as the couplet does."},{"a":"tk:50","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"Kural 559 says the sky withholds rain when the king bends justice; Thirikadugam names the plundering king as one of three malign planets that stop the rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ties rain to the king alone; the verse adds deceitful men and a transgressing wife, so the power to cause drought, a royal burden in the Kural, is spread over common people and the household.","la":"It sets the king beside commoners and a wife as equal causes of drought, widening the couplet's royal sanction to the whole society."},{"a":"tk:50","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"Kural 55 says the wife who worships her husband makes it rain at her word; Thirikadugam says the wife who crosses the bounds of the house cuts the rain off.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two books give the period's belief in both directions, a chaste wife commands rain and a transgressing one stops it, so the wife's conduct is treated as a lever on the weather.","la":"It sets the wife's transgression beside the king's plunder and men's deceit, so her fault is ranked with misrule as a cause of drought."},{"a":"tk:51","b":"k:822","type":"develops","note":"Kural 822 says the friendship of those who seem kin but are not shifts like a changing mind; Thirikadugam says deceivers stay deceivers even when they keep close.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet stresses that false friendship changes; the verse that the false friend's nature does not, so the pair separates the shifting surface of conduct from the fixed nature beneath it.","la":"It sets the deceiver against the true ally who stays an ally even when estranged, so the list gives a test of constancy in both directions."},{"a":"tk:52","b":"k:575","type":"develops","note":"Kural 575 says kindly regard is the ornament of the eye and without it the eye is a sore; Thirikadugam opens with the same ornament and says it is found only with modesty and learning in those of worth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet judges the eye by kindly regard; the verse says that regard is not found alone but in the same people as modesty and learning, so it becomes a sign of a whole character.","la":"It assigns three ornaments to three wearers, the eye, a woman and the next life, and says all three gather in one kind of person."},{"a":"tk:52","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1014 calls modesty the ornament of the worthy; Thirikadugam calls it the ornament of a woman in love.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural gives naanam to the saandror without regard to sex; the verse gives it to the woman, so the same virtue is, in the two books, a mark of nobility and a gendered duty.","la":"It sets the woman's modesty between the eye's kindness and learning for the next life, giving each ornament its own wearer."},{"a":"tk:53","b":"k:402","type":"develops","note":"Kural 402 likens the unlearned man's wish to speak to a breastless woman's desire; Thirikadugam sets the unlearned man's eager talk beside a blind man's wife's beauty and lute-music heard by one who cannot tell its modes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's image locates the lack in the speaker; the verse's neighbours locate it in a missing receiver, so the pair shows unlearned speech failing both for want of substance and for want of anyone able to take it.","la":"It groups unlearned speech with beauty unseen and music unheard, making it one kind of waste with them rather than a lone figure of incapacity."},{"a":"tk:55","b":"k:584","type":"develops","note":"Kural 584 says a spy examines everyone, the king's servants and kin first; Thirikadugam names a secret-leaking friend, a woman without regard for honour and a rancorous servant as those called spies.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet turns the king's surveillance inward on his own circle; the verse shows why from the other side, since those closest, friend, woman and servant, are the ones who leak.","la":"It names the household's own informers by kind, giving concrete cases of the inward danger the couplet only implies.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:56","b":"k:131","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 131 says conduct gives excellence and must be guarded above life; Thirikadugam makes joining the road of inherited conduct the third of three things that keep the path of excellence open, after letters and the service of parents.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books tie ஒழுக்கம் to விழுப்பம், but the Kural makes conduct its single source, while the verse makes it the last step of a sequence that begins with learning letters and serving parents.","la":"It orders conduct in time, after literacy and filial service, so excellence is reached by a sequence rather than by one virtue."},{"a":"tk:56","b":"k:392","type":"develops","note":"Kural 392 calls numbers and letters the two eyes of living beings; Thirikadugam makes learning the letters first the opening step on the path of excellence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet values letters as sight; the verse gives them a place in time, first, before the parents and inherited conduct, so literacy becomes the start of a moral sequence.","la":"It makes letters the first of three ordered steps (முந்தை ... பிற்பாடு), a sequence the couplet does not have.","gr":3},{"a":"tk:57","b":"in:31","type":"agrees","note":"Innaa Narpathu 31 says singing to an untuned lute is very painful; Thirikadugam says song not measured to the beat is painful to hear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One verse faults the pitch and the other the beat, so together they show the period held music to two measures, tuning and time, either of which failing makes song painful.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:58","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"Kural 525 says one who gives and speaks sweetly is surrounded by kin layer on layer; Thirikadugam makes giving by old ties, embracing kin and gathering one's people with close words the work of the king's younger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives anyone the means to gather kin; the verse assigns that work in a royal house to the junior prince, so kin-keeping becomes a delegated office of state.","la":"It moves the kin-gathering acts into the royal household and gives them to one office, the king's younger."},{"a":"tk:59","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"Kural 523 likens the life of one who does not mingle with kin to a tank filling with water but with no bank; Thirikadugam says the wealth of one who does not help his kin perishes while seeming to exist.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's tank shows the water running off; the verse adds that the loss is hidden, the wealth still seems to be there, so the tank looks full while it drains.","la":"It groups kinless wealth with untended farming and a drinker's family name as three goods lost while still in sight."},{"a":"tk:59","b":"k:1039","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1039 says land the owner does not visit sulks like a wife; Thirikadugam says the farming of one who does not tend the green crop at ripening perishes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks for the owner's steady presence; the verse names the moment when absence is fatal, the time of ripening, so neglect is located in time.","la":"It sets the untended harvest beside kinless wealth and a drinker's lineage, making all three cases of loss that looks like having."},{"a":"tk:60","b":"k:1041","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty except poverty; Thirikadugam names want as the sorrow proper to human birth, beside the ghost's hunger and the beast's fear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes poverty incomparable among human pains; the verse explains its place: each kind of birth has its own sorrow and want is the human one.","la":"It sets human want beside the hunger of ghosts and the fear of beasts, so poverty becomes the human share of a sorrow common to all births."},{"a":"tk:60","b":"n:174","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 174 grants that birth is bitter but says no one would resent it given lasting friendship with the good; Thirikadugam lists the sorrow of each kind of birth with no remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse itemises the bitterness of birth by kind of being; the quatrain names the one thing that makes it bearable, so the pair holds the diagnosis and the consolation apart.","la":"It divides the bitterness of birth into hunger, fear and want, by kind of birth, where the quatrain speaks of birth whole."},{"a":"tk:61","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"The Kural gives guarding against what is coming as a mark of understanding; the verse makes it one of a great minister's three daily practices.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Foresight that the Kural praises as a private wisdom becomes, in a minister, an office: one prevents the blow by knowing the enemy king's state every day.","la":"It binds foresight to self-discipline and daily watch on hostile kings, so prevention is shown as the minister's working method rather than a trait."},{"a":"tk:62","b":"iv:30","type":"agrees","note":"Iniyavai Narpathu 30 names as sweet the three virtues whose breach Thirikadugam 62 counts: weighing a kindness, no crooked word in the assembly, not seizing a deposit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two books count the same three acts of trust from both sides, sweetness for keeping them and loss of heirs for breaking them, so the triad was a fixed set in the period's teaching."},{"a":"tk:62","b":"k:112","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the upright man's wealth is a strength to his descendants; Thirikadugam says the man who speaks crookedly in the assembly loses his descendants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, fairness and its breach are both paid in the next generation: the Kural's promise to the heirs of the just has its dark twin in the heirless partial judge.","la":"It sets partial speech beside ingratitude and a seized deposit, so unfairness is classed as a breach of trust with a shared penalty."},{"a":"tk:63","b":"tk:64","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 64 makes tending guests the duty by which a chaste wife is a friend; verse 63 counts a bangled wife who dreads guests among things that yield no good.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two adjacent verses give the same rule from both sides: hospitality is the wife's first duty, and failing it alone makes her a loss to the house.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:64","b":"k:60","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls good children the ornament of a house's excellence; the verse makes bearing children of an old family the act by which the wife is mistress of the house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Children move from ornament to office: what the Kural treats as the crown of a good home, Thirikadugam treats as a debt the wife owes in order to hold her title.","la":"It turns three household goods into three roles of one woman, friend, mother and mistress, and frames them all as debts of chastity.","gr":4},{"a":"tk:65","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural names desire, anger and confusion as the triad whose very names must perish; the verse counts fear, craving and anger as self-consuming.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Fear takes the place of confusion: Thirikadugam treats terror as a passion of the same kind as craving and anger, burning the one who holds it, which the Kural's triad leaves out.","la":"It replaces confusion with fear and gives the triad an image of self-frying in one's own ghee, so the harm is shown as self-inflicted."},{"a":"tk:66","b":"k:554","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the king who bends the sceptre loses wealth and people together; the verse counts the bent-sceptred king among things not joined with fitness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Besides the ruin the Kural predicts, the unjust king is also absurd, like a ring on a man with no servant: injustice is shown as a failure of seemliness as well as of rule.","la":"It sets the unjust king beside a husbandless woman's ornament and a servantless man's ring, so royal injustice is judged as an empty sign of rank."},{"a":"tk:67","b":"n:363","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 363 calls three kinds of wife weapons that kill the husband; Thirikadugam 67 counts the wife who stands against her husband with bad servants and faulty kin as old karma that devours him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar blames the wife as an agent, while Thirikadugam spreads the harm across the whole household and calls it past karma with no remedy, shifting from blame to endurance.","la":"It widens the Naladiyar's three bad wives to wife, servants and kin, and explains them as karma ripening in old age."},{"a":"tk:67","b":"sp:61","type":"agrees","note":"Sirupanchamoolam 61 counts kin, wife, servant, son and neighbour as five household diseases; Thirikadugam 67 counts wife, servants and kin as devouring old karma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two counted works single out the same household cast as a chronic affliction; Sirupanchamoolam calls it disease and adds son and neighbour, Thirikadugam calls it karma and adds old age."},{"a":"tk:68","b":"k:311","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls not hurting others the settled stand of the stainless; the verse names doing no hurt to any creature as purity itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's stainless people are defined by harmlessness, and Thirikadugam draws the conclusion that harmlessness simply is purity, setting it against ritual senses of the word.","la":"It sets purity as harmlessness beside giving to the poor and a life shaped by impermanence, as the three marks of those who know the good.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:68","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says wealth does not stay, so do at once what stays; the verse lists giving to the needy and the path of one who considers impermanence side by side.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural derives giving from impermanence as a single argument; Thirikadugam presents them as two coordinate marks of the wise, so awareness of transience is a conduct in its own right.","la":"It lists giving and awareness of impermanence as separate items with harmlessness, rather than deriving one from the other."},{"a":"tk:69","b":"k:87","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural measures the fruit of hospitality by the guest's worth; the verse prizes the guest who comes in distress after losing his way.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Against the Kural's ranking of guests by merit, Thirikadugam values the guest by his need: the lost and suffering stranger is the rare prize.","la":"It counts the lost guest among hard-to-get goods with a working ox and a maiden, so the guest is valued as a gain to the house, not a cost."},{"a":"tk:76","b":"tk:69","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 69 counts the lost, suffering guest as hard to come by; verse 76 counts the guest who arrives on a rainy day as hard to tend.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same book holds both sides of hospitality: the needy guest is a rare prize and also the hardest burden, so the value and the strain of the guest come together."},{"a":"tk:70","b":"k:215","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens the wise man's wealth to a village tank filling; the verse calls the man who dug a charitable tank and grove one of the truly rich.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's simile is made literal: the public tank is not only like a good man's wealth, its digging is counted as his wealth.","la":"It redefines the rich as three givers, of a tank, of Vedic learning and of shared food, so wealth is measured by what one has made common."},{"a":"tk:71","b":"k:1010","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens the brief want of the renowned rich to rain-clouds running dry; the verse counts the poverty of men with the manliness to give as a sight the eye cannot bear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both treat a giver's poverty as a public loss rather than a private one; Thirikadugam adds the witness's pain, so the giver's want hurts those who watch.","la":"It sets the giver's poverty beside two shameful sights, so the generous man's want is ranked as a kind of indignity."},{"a":"tk:72","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"The Kural tells one to let go that day of gain that comes from leaving fairness; the verse says ill-gotten wealth itself fears the man who keeps dharma in mind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks for an act of renunciation, while Thirikadugam makes it a property of the person: for the dharma-minded, wrong gain never comes near enough to refuse.","la":"It sets dharma beside steadiness and valour as three strengths that make things fear their holder, a ranking of inner strengths the couplet lacks."},{"a":"tk:73","b":"k:1063","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls nothing harder than the hardness that would end poverty by begging; the verse counts the man who would build wealth by begging among three who dare the hard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Thirikadugam pushes the Kural's case further: begging not to escape poverty but to grow rich, and it ranks this with crossing the sea as a reckless venture.","la":"It sets begging-for-wealth beside trusting a roving woman and a sea voyage, so the folly is judged as a physical gamble."},{"a":"tk:74","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the asker's heart rejoices at givers who give without scorn; the verse counts the man who laughs at one asking for a gift among those who hold to nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows the asker's side and Thirikadugam the scorner's: mocking an asker is not just meanness but a sign of a man without any principle.","la":"It sets mocking a beggar beside killing to eat and presuming on the great, so contempt for the asker is ranked with flesh-eating as a lack of rule."},{"a":"tk:74","b":"k:254","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says killing is the absence of grace and eating the flesh is not right; the verse counts the man who lives by killing and eating among three who keep no rule.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural argues from grace, Thirikadugam from discipline: flesh-eating is condemned on both grounds, in one as cruelty and in the other as want of a firm rule.","la":"It sets killing to eat among faults of social conduct, so non-killing is read as part of self-rule rather than of compassion alone."},{"a":"tk:75","b":"n:386","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 386 uses the learning of a man with understanding and bright wealth in a generous man's hands as similes for a modest woman's beauty; Thirikadugam 75 counts the same two as solid heartwood.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same paired figures served two works with different ends: the Naladiyar borrows them to praise a woman's beauty, Thirikadugam keeps them as goods in their own right with a heartwood image.","la":"It treats the two figures as things in themselves, joined by a leader who knows fit unions, under one tree image."},{"a":"tk:75","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens wealth in a just man's hands to a fruit tree ripening in town; the verse likens wealth in a generous man's hands to a tree hardened without hollow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use a tree for the good man's wealth, but the Kural praises its fruit given to all and Thirikadugam the soundness of its wood, so the same image teaches use and durability.","la":"It sets wealth beside learning and a fit leader as three sound trees, ranking generosity with understanding."},{"a":"tk:76","b":"k:917","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says men of unsteady heart touch women who join with them while their hearts are elsewhere; the verse counts the courtesan whose heart is elsewhere yet never fails in business as hard to keep.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both note the courtesan's divided heart; Thirikadugam adds that her business never fails, so the danger is a reliable trade without love.","la":"It sets the courtesan beside a rain-day guest and a defiant servant, so she is counted among household burdens rather than vices."},{"a":"tk:77","b":"k:956","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says those who resolve to live by a spotless lineage do nothing unworthy through deceit; the verse says three positive habits are found only in those of spotless family.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural shows lineage as a restraint on wrongdoing; Thirikadugam shows it as a source of positive conduct, so a pure line both forbids and produces.","la":"It names three habits of a good family, avoiding the base, constancy in friendship and guarding against blame, where the couplet gives one restraint."},{"a":"tk:78","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says inner purity is seen through truth; the verse counts purity, truth and evil unspoken in mind and word as the ascetic's practice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural ties purity to truth; Thirikadugam gives each its own fruit, boldness and beauty, and adds silence of the mind as a third discipline.","la":"It separates purity and truth into two items with different rewards and places them in the ascetic's domain."},{"a":"tk:80","b":"tk:78","type":"develops","note":"Verse 78 names the practices of those strong in penance; verse 80 counts penance taken up by one without restraint of heart as wasted seed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses together give the condition and its failure: penance bears fruit with purity and restraint of mind, and is seed lost in a thicket without them."},{"a":"tk:80","b":"k:262","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says penance comes to those who have it and is futile for others; the verse counts penance taken up by one without restraint as seed cast in a thicket.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Thirikadugam names what the Kural leaves circular: the missing quality is restraint of the heart, so the futility is located in the will.","la":"It ranks futile penance with unjust headship and unruly beauty, so the failure is shown as a general rule of gifts without their inner condition."},{"a":"tk:80","b":"k:407","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says beauty without subtle understanding is a clay doll; the verse says beauty in one who does not perfect his conduct is wasted seed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two place the condition of beauty in different places, learning for the Kural and conduct for Thirikadugam, so beauty needs both mind and conduct to count.","la":"It moves the test of beauty from understanding to conduct and sets it with rank and penance."},{"a":"tk:79","b":"k:905","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the man who fears his wife fears to do good to the good; the verse calls the man who lives in fear of his wife an ox whose eating grieves the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the harm, Thirikadugam the scorn: a wife-fearing husband is useless to others and also a beast, set beside the shameless and the lapsed ascetic.","la":"It ranks the wife-fearing husband with the blame-fearless man and the lapsed ascetic, as three who should not even be fed."},{"a":"tk:81","b":"k:920","type":"develops","note":"Kural 920 names double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has left; Thirikadugam names the courtesan, the gambler and the money-dealer themselves as sinking in craving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ruins the customer of these trades; the verse drowns the traders too, so vice is shown to consume both sides of the bargain.","la":"It replaces liquor with the money-dealer, so all three are people who live by others' wants and are judged by their own greed rather than by the harm they do."},{"a":"tk:81","b":"n:371","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 371 says a harlot's love ends when the hand is empty, like a lamp without oil; Thirikadugam says the courtesan herself sinks in the sea of craving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain explains why her love fails (it follows money); the verse names the cost to her, so the same greed that makes her love brief also drowns her.","la":"It sets the courtesan beside the gambler and the usurer, making her one of three money-trades rather than a figure of false love."},{"a":"tk:82","b":"k:720","type":"develops","note":"Kural 720 says holding forth before those not of one's group is nectar spilled in a drain; Thirikadugam counts not saying what the unreceptive will not accept, even when provoked, as part of a good person's path.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the reason for silence before the wrong audience as waste; the verse makes that silence a mark of standing, kept even under provocation.","la":"It sets restraint before the unreceptive beside being acknowledged by the worthy and the intimate, so the silence counts as one of three signs of a good person's standing."},{"a":"tk:83","b":"k:112","type":"develops","note":"Kural 112 says the wealth of the upright does not decay and supports those after them; Thirikadugam says the upright are like rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet keeps the yield of rectitude within the upright man's line; the rain image sends it outward to all, so rectitude is shown both lasting and shared.","la":"It sets the upright against two images of dissolution (salt in water, friendship under a lie), so rectitude stands as the one thing in the list that gives instead of melting.","gr":2},{"a":"tk:83","b":"tk:15","type":"develops","note":"Verse 15 says the man who lives by lies is not to be befriended; verse 83 says a lie dissolves friendship's thriving shoot as water dissolves salt.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One verse keeps the liar out of friendship at the start and the other shows a lie undoing friendship already grown, so the book makes falsehood fatal to friendship at both ends."},{"a":"tk:84","b":"k:408","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 408 says wealth in the hands of the unlearned is more painful than poverty among the good; Thirikadugam counts the unlearned among those already joined to poverty.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the unlearned rich man is poor twice: the couplet makes his coin a grief and the verse makes him poor in what he never took in, so poverty is a lack of intake as well as of money.","la":"It sets the unlearned with a tank whose inlet fails and an infant not fed full, so lack of learning becomes a vessel that cannot fill, a kind of poverty the couplet does not name."},{"a":"tk:85","b":"k:585","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 585 asks a spy to have an appearance that raises no suspicion and to let nothing spill; Thirikadugam asks him to be of a despised kind, to forget nothing he hears and to report the hidden matter fully.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse makes the couplet's unsuspected look specific: contempt is the cover, because people do not guard their words before the despised, and it adds the report that the couplet's silence serves.","la":"It pairs the disguise with memory and accurate report, so the spy's work is a sequence (hear unseen, keep, deliver) where the couplet gives only concealment."},{"a":"tk:87","b":"tk:9","type":"develops","note":"Verse 9 lists what utter fools love, beginning with moving away from the company of the great; verse 87 lists who are utter fools, including the man who enters a company remote from learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses share the predicate முழுமக்கள் and both define the utter fool by the company he leaves or joins, so the book makes the choice of company the first test of folly."},{"a":"tk:89","b":"k:1001","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1001 says one who stores great wealth and does not eat it is dead; Thirikadugam says one who buries wealth without enjoying it is born yet unborn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet counts the hoarder dead at the end; the verse counts him never born, so between them the hoarder's life is cancelled at both ends.","la":"It sets the hoarder beside the graceless heart and the overreaching harsh tongue, so hoarding becomes one of three refusals (of grace, of use, of measure in speech) that unmake a birth."},{"a":"tk:89","b":"n:273","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 273 says the fool who neither enjoys nor gives his wealth is mocked by that wealth and by the world's compassion; Thirikadugam counts the graceless heart and the buried wealth as two separate items.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain fuses the failure of compassion and the failure to use wealth into one mockery; the verse separates them, showing the Naladiyar's single fault as two distinct ways of being unborn.","la":"It counts the graceless heart and the buried treasure as two items with harsh speech as a third, where the quatrain treats compassion only as the hoarder's judge."},{"a":"tk:90","b":"k:243","type":"develops","note":"Kural 243 says those whose hearts are joined with grace do not enter the painful world of darkness; Thirikadugam names gracious speech, earning for giving and study for dharma as three ways not to reach the dark world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet locates the escape from the dark world in the heart; the verse carries grace into speech and adds two practical means, so the escape becomes a matter of how one talks, earns and studies.","la":"It turns grace from a state of heart into a manner of speaking and sets it beside wealth and learning used for their ends, so three daily practices replace one inner condition."},{"a":"tk:90","b":"tk:89","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 89 names the graceless heart, buried wealth and harsh speech as unmaking a birth; verse 90 names earning for giving, studying for dharma and gracious speech as the way past the dark world.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Verse 90 answers 89 item for item on wealth and speech, so the book shows the same two goods (wealth, words) as either cancelling a life or saving it, depending on use."},{"a":"tk:92","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"Kural 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction differs by what one does; Thirikadugam counts the man not born in an excellent line as born yet unborn, on the reading that விழுத்திணை is a high birth-group, not conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ethics group splits on whether lineage can void a life: the couplet places worth in work, the verse places a birth condition beside illiteracy and boasting as equal disqualifiers.","la":"It puts low birth in one row with two faults one can mend, so birth is treated as a fault of the same kind as conduct, which the couplet denies."},{"a":"tk:92","b":"tk:89","type":"develops","note":"Verses 89 and 92 share the predicate 'born yet unborn'; 89 lists failures of grace, use of wealth and speech, 92 failures of birth, letters and speech.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both lists include a fault of speech, but 92 adds birth and literacy where 89 has grace and wealth, so the book's 'unborn' joins conditions one chooses with a condition one cannot."},{"a":"tk:92","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"Kural 410 says others stand to those learned in the shining books as beasts stand to people; Thirikadugam says one who knows not a single letter is born yet unborn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet lowers the unlearned to beasts; the verse goes further and denies them birth, and sets the bar at one letter rather than at the great books.","la":"It sets the illiterate beside the low-born and the boaster, pairing a lack of letters with a lack of lineage as one kind of void life."},{"a":"tk:93","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"Kural 360 says pain perishes when the very names of desire, anger and confusion perish; Thirikadugam names a world passing in darkness, anger that speaks without knowledge and desire for worthless things as what belongs to a creature in folly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse gives each of the Kural's three its mark (confusion as a darkened world, anger as speech without knowledge, desire as love of the worthless), so the couplet's bare names gain definitions.","la":"It describes each member of the triad by its effect and assigns all three to the fool, where the couplet only names them as what must be destroyed."},{"a":"tk:94","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 57 asks what use guarding women by confinement is and makes their own steadfastness the chief guard; Thirikadugam counts the man who neglects guarding his woman among those without right conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet moves a woman's guard inside her; the verse keeps an outer guarding as the husband's duty and faults him for neglecting it, so the group holds both views of where the guard lies.","la":"It ranks neglect of guarding a woman with courting the unfriendly and faulty speech, so it becomes a lapse of the man's own conduct, not a question of the woman's virtue."},{"a":"tk:95","b":"k:225","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, though it ranks below relieving others' hunger; Thirikadugam counts hunger that eats away wisdom as one of three forces that drive out good deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The verse shows why enduring hunger is a strength in the couplet: hunger is an army against merit, so bearing it is holding a line, and relieving it removes the enemy itself.","la":"It sets hunger in one army with desire and anger, so a bodily need is ranked as a moral enemy beside two passions."},{"a":"tk:95","b":"tk:93","type":"develops","note":"Verse 93 gives anger and desire to the creature in folly; verse 95 sets anger and desire beside hunger as an army that drives out good deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the book treats anger and desire both as the condition of folly and as the force that removes merit, and swaps the third member, delusion, for hunger."},{"a":"tk:96","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"Kural 55 says that if the wife who rises worshipping her husband says 'rain', it rains; Thirikadugam gives the same rain-command to the wife who knows her husband's mind, the ascetic true to his undertaking and the king who shuns cruelty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the power over rain to the wife alone; the verse shares it with the ascetic and the king, so it becomes the reward of anyone who keeps the line of his station.","la":"It names three holders of the rain-power from household, renunciation and rule, turning the couplet's single wonder into a rule about keeping one's station."},{"a":"tk:97","b":"ac:16","type":"develops","note":"Acharakkovai 16 names the king, teacher, mother, father and elder brother as the peerless elders to be worshipped as gods; Thirikadugam counts refusing the five elders' command as the act of those without goodness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Acharakkovai supplies who the ஐங்குரவர் are and the positive rule; Thirikadugam names the breach and ranks it with lying in friendship and casting off a chaste wife, setting obedience to elders in one ethic with loyalty to friend and wife."},{"a":"tk:97","b":"tk:86","type":"develops","note":"Verse 86 calls letting go of the raft of chastity and the loss of friendship's grace an enmity; verse 97 names forsaking a chaste wife and false dealing in friendship as acts of the graceless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Verse 97 names plainly the husband's act of forsaking a chaste wife beside a breach of friendship, which supports reading 86's letting go of chastity as his act rather than hers."},{"a":"tk:98","b":"tk:96","type":"develops","note":"Verse 96 gives the rain-command to the wife, the ascetic and the king; verse 98 makes the fire-priests, the just king and the obedient wife the seed of the month's three rains.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the book's rain list is fixed on king and wife but varies its third member between ascetic and fire-priest, and moves from command to seed, from a single word to a monthly cause."},{"a":"tk:98","b":"k:560","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, the cows' yield fails and the men of six duties forget their books; Thirikadugam sets the fire-priests' dharma and the king's just rule side by side as seeds of rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes priestly learning depend on the king's protection; the verse makes priest, king and wife coequal causes of the rains, flattening the Kural's order of dependence into a joint one.","la":"It adds the wife who follows her husband as a third cause beside priest and king, joining the household to the ritual and royal orders as one seed of rain."},{"a":"tk:99","b":"tk:90","type":"develops","note":"Verse 90 names giving, study for dharma and gracious speech as the way past the dark world; verse 99 names forsaking the learned, self-will and unchecked wrongdoing as barring the good world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Study is on one list and the abandonment of the learned on the other, so the book makes learning the hinge between the dark world and the good one."},{"a":"tk:100","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 381 names six limbs of a king (army, people, resources, ministers, allies, fort); Thirikadugam names three: a loyal army, a fort fearless of numbers, and uncountable treasure.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Thirikadugam's king needs only the three material limbs and drops people, ministers and allies, a narrower and more martial account of what makes a king than the Kural's.","la":"It selects three of the six and qualifies each (loyal, fearless, uncountable), so the list sharpens the quality of each limb where the couplet only counts kinds."},{"a":"tk:86","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1134 has the lover say the flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness; Thirikadugam names letting go the great raft of chastity out of desire as an enmity that brings fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make a virtue the raft that desire tears away, but the love couplet voices it as a lover's helpless loss while the ethics verse judges the same release as a culpable fault.","la":"It sets the abandoned raft of chastity beside a loosened bond of love and friendship's lost grace, so the release becomes one of three broken bonds that turn into enmity."},{"a":"tk:88","b":"n:52","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 52 says the best reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do their own work; Thirikadugam names disease eating one, Death consuming life and wealth passing without worth as what unsettles the raft.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain draws the conclusion, do your own work now, while the verse shows what that work is threatened by, so the pair gives both the danger and the answer to it.","la":"It adds the passing of wealth to disease and Death as a third force shaking the raft, so loss of goods is ranked with bodily ruin where the quatrain counts only the body's fates."},{"a":"tk:91","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"Kural 539 says to think of those ruined by negligence when one is swollen with joy; Thirikadugam counts speaking heedlessly at a time of gain as a sign of the body that does not last.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats elated negligence as a danger to be remedied by memory, while the verse reads the same heedless word as a mark of mortality, so forgetting at a gain is shown to be forgetting that one will die.","la":"It sets heedless speech at a gain beside the cry at the end and shame at a withering body, so negligence is ranked with dying itself as a sign of a perishable life rather than as a tactical fault."}]});
